\textbf{ABSTRACT}
\par
Information economics models evaluate the value of information under the assump-
\par
tion that decision makers wish to maximize their expected payoff. This assumption has
\par
been criticized for not being realistic enough since decision makers usually consider more
\par
than one business objective and might be satisficers rather than optimizers.
\par
This paper attempts to apply an information economics model to decision situations
\par
where two business performance criteria, expected payoff and risk, are considered. In order
\par
to overcome the difficulty of unknown trade-off between the two criteria, one criterion
\par
is used as an objective to be optimized, while the other is set as a constraint. This may
\par
be interpreted as a combination of optimizing and satisficing approaches. It is shown how
\par
an information system can be evaluated in terms of both expected payoff and risk. The
\par
model suggests the trade-off between the two criteria as an additional trait of an informa-
\par
tion system. In the last part of the paper, a numerical example illustrates how a compara-
\par
tive evaluation of information structures \textbf{\emph{is }}performed when risk minimization and expected
\par
payoff maximization are concurrently used as business performance criteria.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Subject Arem: }}Decision \textbf{\emph{Analysis, In formation Economics, Risk and Uncertainty, and}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Statistical Decision Theory.}}
\par
\textbf{INTRODUCTION}
\par
MIS literature generally agrees that the value of an information system is not
\par
an absolute value but should be assessed in light of the decisions supported by
\par
the particular system. Therefore, it is proposed that the first step in information
\par
systems design should be the analysis of business objectives and decision areas
\par
\textbf{[14]. }The information system may then be evaluated (at least in principle) accord-
\par
ing to its contribution to the achievement of business objectives. Since organiza-
\par
tions usually have multiple objectives, information systems should be evaluated
\par
in multidimensional terms.
\par
Information economics models evaluate information systems under the as-
\par
sumption that decisions are made to optimize (net) expected utility which is based
\par
on payoff distribution \textbf{[12] }[ \textbf{131. }The value of information discussed in such models
\par
is usually measured in terms of the difference in the value of an optimized objec-
\par
tive function obtained with or without additional information [3] \textbf{[5] [lo]. }One
\par
may view this approach as a natural offspring of classical utility theory \textbf{[16] [24],}
\par
where factors of payoff and risk are incorporated in a utility function of one
\par
dimension.
\par
This approach has been criticized as being overly simplistic. In particular, it
\par
is claimed that organizations usually have more than one objective \textbf{[21] }and that
\par
in many cases decisions are made in order to obtain a satisfactory, rather than
\par
\textbf{3132 \emph{Decision Sciences }}[Vol. \textbf{15}
\par
an optimal, outcome. Simon described this as a satisficing behavior and viewed
\par
it as a result of bounded rationality \textbf{[19] [21]. }Some quantitative models of deci-
\par
sion making can be interpreted as a representation of satisficing decision making
\par
\textbf{[la]. }In particular, we refer to Charnes and Cooper's models of goal program-
\par
ming and stochastic programming \textbf{[4]. }Their approach incorporates a number of
\par
objectives into a single optimization model by using some objectives as constraints
\par
while optimizing others.
\par
Our paper seeks to extend the scope of information economics from a case
\par
of a single optimized criterion to a case of decision making under two business
\par
objectives: expected payoff and risk. One objective is optimized while the other
\par
is used as a constraint to be satisfied. The terminology and framework applied
\par
are those of Bayesian decision theory and information economics; much emphasis
\par
is laid on an attempt to assess the value of information.
\par
By splitting the risk from the payoff (as is commonly done in finance theory
\par
\textbf{[ll]), }the analysis provides some insights. First, the trade-off between risk and
\par
payoff is made clearer; second, it becomes possible to allow for thresholds in the
\par
levels of risk and payoff, namely, to assume that the decision maker will not tolerate
\par
risk beyond a certain level or payoff below a certain value. In mathematical pro-
\par
gramming terms, the split facilitates the setting of constraints on the space of feasi-
\par
ble solutions. In the context of information economics, the split helps in identify-
\par
ing the contribution of each factor to the value of information. To achieve these
\par
purposes, the paper develops a model through the following steps:
\par
\textbf{1 .}
\par
A normative definition of the concept ``business policy'' is coined.
\par
\textbf{2.}
\par
A derivation of quantitative models for specific business policies is pro-
\par
vided.
\par
\textbf{3.}
\par
\textbf{A }derivation of a business strategy from the business policy is presented.
\par
(Business strategy is defined as a set of decision rules-namely what ac-
\par
tions should be taken under various situations signalled by the informa-
\par
tion system.`)
\par
\textbf{4. }Evaluation of information systems is made in terms of the values of vari-
\par
ous quantitative measures obtained by applying the decision rules.
\par
It is shown that the approach of optimizing one criterion and maintaining
\par
a satisfactory level of the other results in a mixed strategy (rather than a pure one).
\par
The term mixed strategy refers to a situation where the decision maker, in reac-
\par
tion to a certain informational signal, has to select randomly an action between
\par
two recommended ones where the probability of selection is provided by the model's
\par
solution. This situation is not very common in games against nature \textbf{[23]. }It arises
\par
here because the decision maker ``hesitates'' between an action that is riskier but
\par
promises better expected payoff and another action that is less risky but also less
\par
attractive payoff-wise.
\par
Under these circumstances, an information system can be evaluated by several
\par
quantitative measures of different dimensions. In particular, the multidimensional
\par
