\textbf{EVALUATING AN INFORMATION SYSTEM UNDER TWO CRITERIA}
\par
Having introduced a second business criterion-namely, risk-we now turn
\par
to the evaluation of an information system under both criteria-expected payoff
\par
and risk-together. This section is confined to a verbal discussion while the next
\par
section outlines analytic models.
\par
\textbf{A }major obstacle to extending the information economics approach to more
\par
than one objective is the difficulties associated with models of decision making
\par
under multiple objectives, particularly the lack of known trade-offs among objec-
\par
tives. Various procedures to deal with decisions under multiple objectives are pro-
\par
posed in the literature, especially for linear models. One class of approaches relies
\par
on the interaction between the decision maker and an optimization model, where
\par
knowledge about the decision maker's preferences is required (see \textbf{[9] }and [26]).
\par
The approach presented here requires that the decision maker state an ac-
\par
ceptable level for one criterion (aspiration level) while the other criterion is used
\par
to evaluate feasible decisions constrained by the predetermined aspiration level.
\par
This approach conforms with the idea of ``lexicographical ordering with aspira-
\par
tion level'' \textbf{[8, }p. 871.
\par
Using expected payoff and risk as criteria, an information system can be eval-
\par
uated under this scheme in terms of the best value of the objective and minimal
\par
(or maximal) value of the constraint which still provides for feasibility. Also, the
\par
shadow price of the constraint depends on the particular information structure
\par
in use. It reflects, thus, a ``natural'' trade-off between the two criteria. Therefore,
\par
it may serve as an additional, meaningful evaluator of information.
\par
Another perspective for the suggested approach is obtained by discussing it
\par
in the context of bounded rationality, which was introduced by Simon [21] as an
\par
alternative to the full rationality approach. The difficulties that make the full ra-
\par
tionality approach unrealistic can be categorized into three types. First, the deci-
\par
sion maker usually has a limited knowledge about environmental conditions, pos-
\par
sible courses of action, and possible outcomes \textbf{\emph{(data problem). }}Second, the com-
\par
putational demands to find the optimal decision may be prohibitive, at least in
\par
the time frame provided for making the decision \textbf{\emph{(computational problem). }}A third
\par
problem is the preference ordering of possible outcomes. Since decisions are usually
\par
judged according to more than one criterion, it may be impossible to identify the
\par
best decision (ordering or \textbf{\emph{ranking problem). }}This problem is especially important
\par
in organizational contexts where decisions usually have multiple objectives [6] [20].
\par
Simon proposed that, due to these difficulties, decision making is satisficing,
\par
i.e., finding a course of action that is ``good enough'' \textbf{\emph{[19, }}p. 2051, rather than
\par
optimizing, i.e., finding the best course of action. In such a behavior the decision
\par
maker tries to achieve a given aspiration level in each objective. This can be de-
\par
scribed as choosing feasibility over optimality.
\par
The satisficing approach \textbf{may }result in replacing the utility function by a much
\par
simpler one (e.g., \textbf{satisfactory/unsatisfactory }[ \textbf{19, }ch. \textbf{14]), }thus reducing the data
\par
problem. It may result in changing the objective function [21] or limiting the feasible
