space, thus reducing the computational problem. It may solve partially or wholly
\par
the ranking problem by using some objectives as constraints.
\par
It will be seen in the next section that the suggested models incorporate some
\par
elements of the bounded rationality approach. First, replacing the payoff func-
\par
tion \textbf{\emph{(v) }}by a simpler zero-one value function (6) reduces the data problem because
\par
it is easier to designate outcomes as good or bad than to place an exact value on
\par
them. Second, using one criterion as a constraint while optimizing the other is
\par
a way to alleviate the ranking problem.
