\textbf{A MATHEMATICAL MODEL}
\par
In this section we present the mathematical formulation for the two-objective
\par
information evaluation model. This formulation will be employed in an example
\par
in the next section to demonstrate the multidimensional evaluation of informa-
\par
tion systems. We shall also see that the approach leads to a special type of mixed
\par
decision rules as opposed to the pure decision rules obtained from optimization
\par
of a single criterion.
\par
We shall discuss in detail only the chance of loss criterion: the chance of devia-
\par
tion criterion can be handled mathematically in exactly the same manner. The dif-
\par
ference lies only in the implications of the results on the business strategy to be
\par
selected.
\par
We begin by referring to the single-criterion optimization model.
