\textbf{Trade-off Between Risk and Aspiration Level}
\par
Let us consider first the minimal level of risk that can be obtained by each
\par
of the three information systems; the results for various aspiration levels are sum-
\par
marized in Table 2. From this table one can conclude that with the ``bad'' infor-
\par
mation system the business can never do better than break even and even this only
\par
with .67 probability (i.e., \textbf{1 \emph{.OO }}- \textbf{.33) }for an unbiased market and with \textbf{.75 }proba-
\par
bility for the biased one. If the maximal allowed risk is 10 percent, then only \textbf{\emph{P,}}
\par
performs satisfactorily for both markets. In order to enable higher aspiration levels
\par
one has to consent to higher risk. For example, using \textbf{\emph{P I , }}an aspiration level of
\par
\textbf{6,000 }can be obtained only with \textbf{25 }percent risk.
\par
These results can be presented in two alternative ways, depending on the pref-
\par
erence of the decision maker (according to the business policy employed):
\par
\textbf{1. }Risk-bound evaluation: The policy dictates an allowed risk while the ob-
\par
tained aspiration levels are compared. For example, if the acceptable risk
\par
is 10 percent, then \textbf{\emph{P, }}satisfies a possible aspiration level of \textbf{\emph{2,000 }}while
\par
\textbf{\emph{P2 }}only 0.
\par
\textbf{2.}
\par
Aspiration-level-bound evaluation: The policy imposes a desired aspira-
\par
tion level while the risk bound is examined. For example, for \textbf{\emph{Uo }}= \textbf{2,000}
\par
the risk obtained by \textbf{\emph{P, }}is almost half that obtained by \textbf{\emph{P2.}}
\par
Using only the policy parameters \textbf{\emph{(U0,a) }}for evaluation may be viewed as a
\par
satisficing approach since no optimum is required.
