\textbf{The Impact of the Information System on Business Criteria}
\par
So far, the classical criterion of expected payoff was not even employed. This
\par
analysis was of satisficing nature only, and it was shown to yield quite decisive
\par
results.
\par
Table 3 is a comparison of the three information systems for the various aspira-
\par
tion levels. Table 3 demonstrates that risk can be much more sensitive than the
\par
expected payoff to the information system. For example, at \emph{Vo }= 0 in a biased mar-
\par
ket \textbf{\emph{(rl), }}the maximal expected payoff drops only by \textbf{22 }percent when \textbf{\emph{P2 }}is re-
\par
placed by \textbf{\emph{P3, }}while the minimal possible risk deteriorates by a factor of \textbf{2.8.}
\par
\textbf{A }high shadow price (marginal cost) implies a high sensitivity of the expected
\par
payoff to the acceptable risk level. This indicates that with some information sys-
\par
tems a minor change in policy may have a considerable effect on the business per-
\par
formance. Decision makers should be aware of this effect.
\par
In a similar way a marginal cost can be assigned to the aspiration level \emph{(Vo).}
\par
For example, a change from 2,000 to 6,000 will change the expected payoff at
\par
the minimal possible risk (which is not at the same level, of course) from \textbf{5,125}
\par
to \textbf{3,500. }It should be noted that information systems cannot always be compared
\par
for the same risk level. For example, there is no overlap between the feasible risk
\par
bounds of \textbf{\emph{P, }}and \textbf{\emph{P2 }}at the aspiration level of \textbf{Vo=2,000.}
\par
It is also seen that while minimal risk and maximal expected payoff change
\par
in the same direction, the risk shadow price may vary in any direction. It can be
\par
noticed that the shadow price varies sometimes dramatically with the replacement
\par
of the information system. Once an information system is selected, the risk shadow
\par
price may be perceived by the decision maker as a natural trade-off between the
\par
two operational criteria.
\par
\textbf{CONCLUSIONS}
\par
The classical normative models of information evaluation usually assume one
\par
kind of business objective-expected payoff maximization. The business policy
\par
described in this work incorporates an additional objective into the traditional
\par
model through a risk constraint imposed on the feasible decision space. It can be
\par
viewed as a special case of the multiple-objective approach and as a model which
\par
is closer to bounded rationality concepts than the classical payoff maximization
\par
model.
\par
It has been shown that this approach leads to several criteria for information
\par
system evaluation; in particular, we note the criterion of minimal possible level
\par
of risk. The major conclusion is that when information systems are evaluated in
\par
terms of both risk and expected payoff, then in certain cases risk may play a role
\par
that is not as important as that of expected payoff.
\par
The approach in the work has some relation to the notions of bounded ra-
\par
tionality and satisficing. First, it was shown that when the business objective of
\par
expected-payoff maximization is replaced by risk minimization, the payoff matrix
\par
is replaced by a simpler binary matrix as suggested in Simon's satisficing approach
\par
15405915, 1984, 1, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1540-5915.1984.tb01195.x by Cory Whitney - Universit\"ats-U Landesbibliothek Bonn , Wiley Online Library on [03/05/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License\textbf{\emph{Decision Sciences }}[Vol. \textbf{15}
\par
[ \textbf{19, }pp. \textbf{196-2061; }thus the data problem. which is a major cause for bounded ra-
\par
tionality, is reduced. Second, it was exhibited how information systems can he
\par
compared even without carrying \textbf{out }an optimization. For the risk-constrained pol-
\par
icy this implies a reduction of both the data and the computational problem. For
\par
the alternative policy, i.e., expected payoff constraint, it is a reduction in the com-
\par
putational problem. Finally, even when optimization is indeed employed, the con-
\par
strained model is still a method to overcome ranking problems.
\par
We view the model described above as just an initial step to make normative
\par
models of the value of information more applicable to actual situations. There
\par
are, however, several issues that should be resolved before such application can
\par
be done; in the following paragraph we mention three.
\par
First, the question arises of how to link an actual information system with
\par
evaluation models like the one described here. In our model, the information system
\par
is represented by the information structure (matrix). Hence, the problem is how
\par
to map the characteristics of the real information system into the elements of this
\par
matrix. Second, the evaluation of an information system in the present model is
\par
based on a particular two-objective model. There are other approaches for multi-
\par
objective decisions, especially those in which the decision maker's preferences are
\par
taken into account \textbf{(as }in \textbf{[9]). }Finally, the value of information in the model depends
\par
on policy parameters (risk and aspiration level). Implicitly, it means that there
\par
is no ``objective'' value of the information system, but rather the value depends
\par
on the decision maker's (subjective) preferences, especially, his or her willingness
\par
to take risks. This incurs some measurement problems. Hence there is no doubt
\par
that the area of information evaluation requires further theoretical and empirical
\par
research. [Received: June \textbf{21, 1982. }Accepted: July \textbf{19, 1983.1}
