\par
Its conceptual framework, the \&dquo;decision tree,\&dquo; permits a therapeutic man-
\par
agement problem to be separated into discrete, manageable units. The over-
\par
all structure of the problem, including all reasonable choices and their
\par
effects, form the central portion of the decision tree. Events that may some-
\par
times occur, depending on choices made and the effects of chance, are
\par
represented as probabilities. As each potential outcome is described in the
