In this paper we explicate the Markov model of prognosis as an alter-
\par
native to standard decision-analytic formalisms. The Markov model can
\par
replace a decision tree outright or can be grafted onto standard decision
\par
analysis as an equivalent to the utility structure. Of several mathematical
\par
models that could serve as adjuncts to the clinical decision making process,
\par
the Markov model is distinguished by its simplicity, its ease of use in calcu-
\par
lating prognosis, and its faithful representation of many clinical problems.
\par
In this study we describe the design and implementation of a general
\par
Markov prognostic model, and illustrate its application in a clinical decision
\par
analysis with complex utility features.
\par
The Markov Model
\par
The general problem considered here is the natural history of a chronic dis-
\par
ease, which can be viewed for an individual patient as a sequence of particu-
\par
lar states of health. Figure 1 illustrates this model. In this example a patient
\par
may be classified into one of three categories: WELL, ILL, and DEAD. At
\par
any time i, he or she resides in just one of the states. In Figure 1 this is repre-
\par
sented as a patient being in one of the three ovals in the upper row (labeled
\par
TIME i). The possible changes of state, or transitions, that occur over the
\par
fixed time interval from i to i + 1 are illustrated in Figure 1 as arrows. At
\par
TIME i + 1 the patient resides in one of the ovals in the bottom row. In the
\par
Markov model the passage of time is represented by cycles (or \&dquo;ticks\&dquo;) on an
\par
implicit \&dquo;clock,\&dquo; where i denotes the cycle count. Transitions among states
\par
occur instantaneously at each clock tick.
\par
Note that it is possible to get to the DEAD state from either of the other
\par
states, and there is of course no possible transition from the DEAD state.
\par
On the other hand, it is possible to leave WELL or ILL via a transition. In
\par
the language of mathematical modeling, WELL and ILL are termed \&dquo;Non-
\par
absorbing\&dquo; states, and DEAD is an \&dquo;absorbing\&dquo; state. Once all patients are
\par
in the DEAD state, no further transitions are possible and the process is said
\par
to have been absorbed [4].421
\par
