\par
Each change of state is represented by a single transition from one status to
\par
another. Implied in this picture is the property that the process has no mem-
\par
ory of prior states; knowing that the current status is WELL is sufficient to
\par
predict what the next state will be. This is shown on Figure 1 as specific
\par
single-step probabilities associated with the arrows. For example, the prob-
\par
ability of changing in one step from ILL to DEAD is depicted as P,d. It does
\par
not matter whether the patient has always been in the ILL state or whether
\par
the two states prior to the current ILL one were WELL. This no-memory
\par
feature of the model categorizes it as a Markov process. Furthermore,
\par
because this example has an absorbing state (DEAD), it is an absorbing
\par
Markov process. The absence of memory in the Markov process is known in
\par
mathematics as the Markovian assumption [4,5]. The assumption is quite
\par
strong: knowing only the present state of health of a patient is sufficient to
\par
project the entire trajectory of future states. In other words, all patients in a
\par
given state at a given time have the same prognosis, no matter how they got
\par
to the present state. There are few biological systems that obey the Markov-
\par
ian assumption strictly, yet its simplicity and approximate correctness make
\par
the Markov model very attractive.
\par
MARKOV PROCESSES AND CHAINS. Two types of Markov models are used
\par
in medical decision making-those in which the state transition probabili-
\par
ties are constant, and those in which the transition probabilities vary over
\par
time according to preset regular rules. The first class of models are Markov
\par
chains. These models are a subset of the more general Markov processes, in
\par
which transition probabilities are time-dependent.
\par
In terms of the calculational burden they impose, Markov chains are
\par
quite easy to use. We shall illustrate this feature of Markov chains in the
\par
clinical case example. On the other hand, Markov chains may be applied to
\par
medical problems only if the chances of moving among clinical states can be
\par
assumed to be constant. Constant transition probabilities are realistic only
\par
for diseases with a short time horizon. In more chronic conditions there is
\par
Figure 1. Three-state Markov Model.422
\par
usually the inescapable factor of increasing age. The annual mortality of the
\par
healthy population increases exponentially with age [6,7]. Inasmuch as clin-
\par
ical Markov models use death as an absorbing state, the population mortal-
\par
ity must be built into the model. For problems with short time horizons an
\par
appropriate average annual mortality rate may be used as an approxima-
\par
tion. If the disease-specific probabilities of mortality and morbidity are like-
\par
wise approximately constant, the entire problem may be modeled as a
\par
Markov chain.
\par
Longer-term or complex problems may be handled in two ways. The
\par
more general Markov process formulation may be used, with each transi-
\par
tion probability being a function not only of the state of health but also of
\par
the time (or, equivalently, of the number of cycles the process has under-
\par
gone). Conversely, an approximation may be made of the mean transition
\par
probability over the lifetime of the process, a procedure analogous to using
\par
the declining exponential approximation of life expectancy (DEALE) in
\par
classical clinical decision analysis [3]. The averaged transition probabilities
\par
may then be used in a Markov chain. The effects of using DEALE trans-
\par
formations on mortality probabilities will be discussed in Appendix A.
\par
CONSTRUCTION OF THE MODEL. The first step in building a Markov
\par
model of prognosis is the enumeration of all distinct states of health. These
\par
are shown simplistically in Figure 1 as WELL, ILL, and DEAD. These clini-
\par
cal states should be clearly defined. Importantly, they should enable estima-
\par
tion or abstraction from the literature of specific transition probabilities per
\par
unit time among the various states. An example of a poorly formulated
\par
process would be the application of a simple three-state model (such as
\par
Figure 1) to polymyalgia rheumatica. In such a simple model a variety of
\par
clinical conditions would be lumped into the ILL category. Is the patient ill
\par
due to steroid toxicity, or due to the onset of temporal arteritis, or is he or
\par
she recovering from a temporal artery biopsy? Each of these conditions
\par
would merit its own state, inasmuch as the transition probabilities would
\par
differ among them and each would engender a different quality of life when
\par
a patient is in the state. Thus states must (1) be distinguished by their prog-
\par
noses or transition probabilities, (2) be defined to correspond to standard or
\par
literature-based notions of disease, and (3) be capable of being placed in a
\par
continuous scale of relative values (see below).
\par
Having listed the possible states of health, the next step in the construc-
\par
tion of a Markov model is the definition of allowable state transitions.
\par
Figure 1 shows the allowable state changes for the sample model, drawn as
\par
arrows between possible states of health. From WELL it is possible to move
\par
to each of the other states or to remain WELL. Transitions from ILL to
\par
WELL are not allowed in this particular example but may, in general, be
\par
permitted in a Markov model. We presume then that the clinical process
\par
modeled in Figure 1 proceeds inexorably from WELL, perhaps through423
\par
ILL, to DEAD. Of course there are no possible transitions from DEAD-
\par
once dead, the patient remains dead.
\par
For Markov medical decision making we have found it useful to consider
\par
two types of states: long-term and temporary. Long-term states, such as
\par
those shown in Figure 1, are states in which it is possible to remain from
\par
cycle to cycle. That is, a transition from a long-term state to itself is allow-
\par
able. Temporary states reflect short-term events that force transition to
\par
another state in the model in the next cycle. For example, we could add a
\par
state to the model in Figure 1 labeled HOSPITALIZED, to reflect one cycle
\par
spent in the hospital. If all transitions from HOSPITALIZED were to other
\par
states, then this new addition would be a temporary state.
\par
Note that in our temporary state the probability of remaining in that
\par
state is set to zero. A less rigid effect could be achieved by setting the prob-
\par
ability of remaining in the state to a low but nonzero number (e.g., 0.2).
\par
Obviously the mean duration of time in a state (expressed as cycles), once
\par
the state is reached, equals the reciprocal of one minus the residual proba-
\par
bility. Thus, if the state represented hospitalization, then a residual prob-
\par
ability of 0.2 would imply a mean duration of 1.25 cycles, whereas a residual
\par
probability of 0.5 would imply a mean hospital stay of two cycles. A similar
\par
effect can be achieved by constructing a chain of temporary states, one lead-
\par
ing to the next with high probability and with no transitions allowed to
\par
earlier states in the chain, and with zero residual probabilities. For example,
\par
three such states might be HOSPITALIZATION, MONTH 1, HOSPITAL-
\par
IZATION, MONTH 2, and HOSPITALIZATION, MONTH 3. The length
\par
of such a temporary chain would be a measure of the duration of hospitali-
\par
zation.
\par
Next, probabilities must be associated with the state transitions. In
\par
