CALCULATION OF LIFE EXPECTANCY WITH THE MARKOV MODEL. Life
\par
expectancy, a commonly employed outcome measure for clinical decision
\par
making problems [2,3], is defined as the average future lifetime of a cohort
\par
of patients with identical clinical features [11]. The enumeration of states
\par
and the assignment of transition probabilities is sufficient to calculate life
\par
expectancy with the Markov model. Before describing the three methods by
\par
which this calculation can be made, we need to establish how the model can
\par
be used to estimate life expectancy. It is intuitive that for any Markov
\par
process with an absorbing state (e.g., DEAD) that can be reached from
\par
every other state, the probability of eventual absorption (or death) is unity.
\par
In other words, everyone dies eventually. This is proved elsewhere [4, cf.
\par
Isaiah 40:6-8]. A corollary is that the expected time before absorption of a
\par
Markov process is finite and can be calculated. Inasmuch as \&dquo;absorption\&dquo; in
\par
a clinical Markov process is death, the expected time before absorption is
\par
the life expectancy of the cohort of patients modeled by the process.
\par
The expected time before absorption can be calculated in three ways: a
\par
Monte Carlo simulation of a large series of individual patients, a probabil-
\par
istic simulation of a cohort of patients, and, for a Markov chain only, a
\par
matrix algebraic solution.
\par
Individual Monte Carlo Simulation. In this approach, patients traverse
\par
a Markov process one by one, with a random number generator determin-
\par
ing what happens to the individual at each cycle of the process. Each patient
\par
begins in the initial state or in one of a limited distribution of states of
\par
health (e.g., WELL in Figure 1). At each cycle the patient changes state
\par
according to the laws of chance, as dictated by the transition probabilities.
\par
Inasmuch as the underlying transition probabilities are point estimates
\par
derived from the literature, the type of random number generator used is
\par
immaterial. In practice, a function that generates uniform numbers on the
\par
interval [0,1] is optimal. A clock cycle length is defined, and a cycle counter
\par
increments with each transition. When the patient enters the absorbing
\par
DEAD state, the current cycle count represents the length of his or her indi-
\par
vidual life time (Figure 2). While the patient is traversing the temporary426
\par
states of the model, the length of time in each nonabsorbing state is
\par
recorded. Finally, in clinical problems with long clock cycles, it may be
\par
necessary to reduce the total time before absorption by one-half cycle,
\par
because the patient dies, on average, halfway through that final cycle. (The
\par
same correction might be made whenever moving between states with dif-
\par
ferent incremental utilities. In practice, this is only important if the differ-
\par
ence in utilities is large.)
\par
After the first person has completed the simulation (i.e., has died),
\par
another patient begins in one of the initial states and a new simulation is
\par
performed. After a large number (on the order of 104) of identical patients
\par
have been simulated, with each individual's trajectory through the Markov
\par
process governed by the laws of chance, the averaged number of cycles
\par
before absorption (death) is equivalent to life expectancy. Similarly, the
\par
averaged or expected number of cycles in each nonabsorbing state may be
\par
calculated. Of course, because we know for each simulated patient not only
\par
how long is spent in each nonabsorbing state but also when these non-
\par
absorbing cycles occur, we could simulate the effect of changes in the utility
\par
of each state over time (e.g., discounting or decreasing marginal value).
\par
Every patient need not begin the simulation in the same state. Sometimes
\par
the clinical problem is such that the patient may initially be in one of several
\par
different states of health. In such cases we distribute the initial states of the
\par
simulated patients so as to correspond to our best knowledge about the
\par
probability density distribution of the patient's initial state of health.
\par
Figure 2. Monte Carlo simulation. The lines represent the trajectory of an individ-
\par
ual patient through the Markov model. This patient is WELL for three cycles,
\par
becomes ILL in the fourth cycle, and dies in the sixth cycle.427
\par
This approach, termed a Monte Carlo simulation, is equivalent to the
\par
performance of a large number of experiments with the Markov model. Its
\par
accuracy is as good as the number of patients simulated, the quality of the
\par
random number generator used, and our knowledge about the initial state
\par
and transition probability distributions. Because each patient must be simu-
\par
lated individually, the approach is time-consuming. On the other hand,
\par
because the process is a simulation the probabilities and utilities can easily
\par
change as functions of time. Thus Markov process models with time-
\par
dependent probabilities may be studied with the Monte Carlo approach.
\par
Because patients are simulated as individuals this approach provides the
\par
greatest flexibility and detail. In particular, measures of variability around
\par
life expectancies are easy to calculate.
\par
Markov Cohort. In this method, a large number of patients are followed
\par
as a cohort. The cohort begins in an initial distribution of states, and at each
\par
cycle of the process the entire cohort is reallocated to states according to the
\par
transition probabilites. Table 1 shows the first few transitions of a Markov
\par
Cohort analysis of the simple three-state model we have been using as an
\par
example. In the cohort analysis two values are kept for each state at each
\par
cycle: the number of patients currently in the state and the total number of
\par
patient-cycles in the state. For example, at Cycle 2 the WELL state includes
\par
9000 patients; the cumulative number of WELL patient-months is 100 000
\par
+ 30 000 + 9000, or 139 000. After a sufficient number of cycles almost
\par
everyone will be DEAD. The few remaining patients (the \&dquo;tail\&dquo; of the sur-
\par
vival curve) can be treated in one of two ways: a small arbitrary amount of
\par
life expectancy can be added to each state that has remaining cohort mem-
\par
bers or the life expectancy can be truncated. The Markov cohort simulation
\par
is stopped when the remaining cohort has diminished to the point where any
\par
error introduced by summing up the experience of the remainder (dealing
\par
with the tail) is small compared to the total patient-cycles accumulated
\par
during the analysis.
\par
When a Markov cohort analysis is terminated, the total number of
\par
patient-cycles for each state is divided by the size of the original cohort,
\par
yielding the expected time that each individual member will spend in each
\par
state. Life expectancy is the sum of these expected values (see Table 1, bot-
\par
tom), possibly adjusted for differences in the utilities associated with the
\par
states. A half-cycle correction should be made for the final (i.e., tail) state if
\par
the cycle length is relatively long. The Markov cohort analysis is a simula-
\par
tion, although it does not follow patients as individuals. Time-dependent
\par
probabilities and utilities may be easily incorporated into the analysis. If the
\par
quality adjustment is done as the cohort moves through the model (and not
\par
at the end), then discounting and risk aversion can be accommodated by
\par
this technique. It cannot, however, provide information on the distribution
\par
or variance of expected values, as can the Monte Carlo technique.428
