Fundamental Matrix Solution. This approach requires constant transi-
\par
tion probabilities and thus is appropriate only for Markov chains. As shown
\par
in Figure 3, in this formulation a matrix P of single-step transition probabil-
\par
ities is constructed. The states are listed along the left and top margins of
\par
the matrix; for each cycle, the left (row) margin denotes the starting states
\par
and the top (column) margin denotes the finishing states. The appropriate
\par
transition probabilities fill the cells of the matrix; Pj is the probability of
\par
going from state i to state j in any given cycle of the chain.
\par
This matrix formulation has many useful properties [4]. Of special inter-
\par
est is the portion of the matrix containing the transition probabilities among
\par
nonabsorbing states. This section, labeled Q in the upper left of Figure 3,
\par
reflects the probability of not being absorbed, depending on the starting
\par
state of the cycle. Figure 4 illustrates a sample Markov matrix. The upper
\par
left section (Q) has four elements: 0.3, 0.5, 0, and 0.5. If an individual is in
\par
the WELL state, the probability of not being absorbed (i.e., staying alive) in
\par
one cycle is 0.3 + 0.5, or 0.8. The probability of not dying in one cycle for an
\par
individual in the ILL state is simply 0 + 0.5, or 0.5. Section Q may be
\par
manipulated in a special way: Each element is subtracted from a corre-
\par
sponding element in another 2 x 2 matrix (Figure 5) composed of ones on
\par
the diagonal and zeroes elsewhere (called an identity or I matrix). By a tech-
\par
nique of linear algebra known as matrix inversion a new matrix
\par
is constructed (Figure 6). This process is the matrix algebraic equivalent of
\par
taking the reciprocal of a transition probability, and thus bears an interest-
\par
ing analogy to the DEALE technique described elsewhere [10]. Matrix N,
\par
known as the fundamental matrix of an absorbing Markov chain, has as its
\par
elements by column the expected time in each state before absorption, given429
