\par
at each cycle the overall state-specific utility is increased by the product of
\par
the current state membership, the quality increment (from zero to one), and
\par
the current value of the utility adjustment function (i.e., the current dis-
\par
count factor). Thus, the product is really quality- and risk-adjusted patient-
\par
cycles. The fundamental matrix approach may be treated analogously-the
\par
survival values in the N matrix may be multiplied by the quality adjustments
\par
to obtain the state-specific adjusted utility. If incremental utility values
\par
change over time (as in risk aversion or discounting), then the fundamental
\par
matrix approach cannot be used explicitly.
