\par
USE OF THE MARKOV MODEL IN MEDICAL DECISION MAKING. Markov
\par
models, in their several formulations, yield risk- and quality-adjusted life
\par
expectancies. Such measures may be used in two general ways. The Markov
\par
process can be the entire analysis, if different management choices are
\par
reflected solely as different sets of transition probabilities [12] or possibly
\par
different incremental utilities. Alternatively, the results of a Markov proc-
\par
ess may provide utilities for \&dquo;tree-based\&dquo; clinical decision analyses. A deci-
\par
sion tree is constructed that has \&dquo;natural history\&dquo; as its terminal nodes or
\par
outcome states. Different pathways in the tree might imply using Markov
\par
models with different transition probabilities. Of course the entire Markov
\par
analysis can be represented equivalently as a very deep decision tree.
\par
The choice between a Markov analysis, a standard decision tree, and a
\par
combined analysis is made on the basis of ease of representation and
\par
requirements for relevant sensitivity analysis. The Markov model is concep-
\par
tually attractive (viz. Figure 1), but the transition probabilities are hidden
\par
from view. Although sensitivity analyses are possible on the parameters of
\par
the Markov process, the calculations are more straightforward and the
\par
results easier to understand if the probability is in plain view, as in a deci-
\par
sion tree.
\par
On the other hand, the Markov model is preferable for problems with
\par
repetitive chances that would require very deep and broad decision trees.
\par
