\par
Case Example: Should anticoagulation be withheld in a
\par
patient with an artifical heart valve and
\par
recent hemorrhagic cerebral infarction?
\par
A 59-year-old white female with chronic rheumatic heart disease and atrial
\par
fibrillation underwent a mitral valve replacement (Starr-Edwards, bare
\par
metal cage) seven years ago. She experienced no further mitral dysfunction
\par
and was maintained on warfarin anticoagulation since surgery without
\par
sequelae. She remained in atrial fibrillation. Her course was complicated by
\par
severe tricuspid insufficiency and chronic congestive heart failure. She had
\par
good left ventricular function but a markedly enlarged left atrium. She was
\par
admitted to another hospital with a large hemorrhagic infarction involving
\par
the right cerebral hemisphere. Anticoagulation was discontinued, and a
\par
consultation was requested to clarify the risks and benefits of continued
\par
anticoagulation.
\par
The central question was: Should anticoagulation be resumed in the set-
\par
ting of a recent cerebral hemorrhage that might be primary or might
\par
represent secondary bleeding into an embolic infarct? The patient was at
\par
risk for two potentially catastrophic events, cerebral embolization and
\par
recurrent cerebral hemorrhage. Complicating the decision was uncertainty
\par
regarding the etiology of the hemorrhagic event. Computed tomography of
\par
the head was interpreted as \&dquo;90 percent probability of primary cerebral
\par
hemorrhage,\&dquo; but the clinical story favored an embolic episode, and there-
\par
fore secondary hemorrhage.
\par
THE DECISION TREE. We approached this problem with decision analysis,
\par
using a Markov model of prognosis to measure outcomes of treatment. The
\par
decision tree in Figure 9 summarizes the model we developed. The square
\par
node at the left of Figure 9 shows the decision faced in this case: withhold
\par
anticoagulation or resume warfarin therapy. Whether or not anticoagula-
\par
tion is resumed, the patient's prognosis depends on the etiology of her
\par
stroke. Thus the decision tree shows a circular chance node on each branch
\par
of the central decision, the chances being PRIMARY or SECONDARY
\par
cerebral hemorrhage. The probability of primary hemorrhage p was esti-
\par
mated at 0.9 by the neuroradiologists at the outside hospital. A panel of
\par
radiologists suggested that the likelihood of primary cerebral infarction in
\par
this setting, with this patient's CT scan, would range from 0.8 to 0.95.
\par
At the right of Figure 9, on each of the four terminal branches of the
\par
decision tree, is a rectangular box labeled Markov Model. We need to
\par
develop four outcome assessments, corresponding to the various combina-
\par
tions of therapeutic decision and etiology of hemorrhage.
\par
MARKOV MODELS OF PROGNOSIS. Figure 10 shows the state model con-
\par
structed for this analysis. This model is similar to the one we used in an
\par
analysis of the anticoagulation decision in the bradycardia-tachycardia syn-
\par
drome [12]. There are three major states of health, WELL, DISABLED,
\par
and DEAD. There is in addition one temporary state of health, MINOR435
\par
Figure 9. Decision tree for the case example. Markov models will be used to calcu-
\par
late quality-adjusted life expectancies as utility measures.
\par
EVENT. This state occurs when the patient suffers a small, nondisabling
\par
stroke or hemorrhage that leads to medical attention but not to permanent
\par
disability. DISABLED is reserved for events that produce permanent in-
\par
capacitation. The creation of a state MINOR EVENT allows two things: A
\par
catalogue of minor strokes and hemorrhages may be kept, and a short-term
\par
utility adjustment may be made when a patient suffers a minor event. Death
\par
may occur from four causes: catastrophic stroke, catastrophic hemorrhage,
\par
underlying cardiac disease, or other causes. The latter two causes of death
\par
do not depend on the therapeutic decision made; they are constant across
\par
the four Markov models of prognosis. The chances of stroke, hemorrhage,
\par
and event-related morbidity do depend on the choice of therapy and on the
\par
etiology of the patient's initial hemorrhage.
\par
ASSUMPTIONS. In addition to the standard Markovian assumption of no
\par
memory of prior states of health, we shall make several simplifying assump-
\par
tions in this case. First, we assume that embolization and hemorrhagic
\par
events occur randomly in time and at a constant rate. This allows us to
\par
exploit all possible constructions of the Markov model.
\par
Second, we assume that fatal hemorrhage and fatal stroke are equally
\par
undesirable and indistinguishable from death occurring at the same time
\par
due to underlying cardiac disease or old age. This analysis therefore uses a
\par
single DEAD state and does not distinguish the relative undesirability of
\par
different etiologies of death, but considers only when death occurs.
\par
Third, hemorrhages are either minor or fatal. This is substantiated by the
\par
current literature [13,14]. On the other hand, embolic events may be minor,
\par
permanently disabling, or fatal. A disabling embolus diminishes the quality
\par
of a patient's remaining life, but does not affect the likelihood of future
\par
events.436
\par
Figure 10. Markov state model for the case example. See the text for details.
\par
Fourth, the major morbidity of anticoagulation is hemorrhage.
\par
Inasmuch as the patient has been maintained on warfarin for several years,
\par
this assumption appears warranted.
\par
Fifth, if the patient suffers systemic embolization, we assume that anti-
\par
coagulation is resumed immediately and the probability of re-embolization
\par
is not increased in the periembolic period. The literature suggests that the
\par
risk of a second embolus is increased for a short time after an event [15], but
\par
it does not indicate how efficacious immediate anticoagulation can be in the
\par
prevention of such re-embolization.
\par
Sixth, we assume that there is no increased risk of hemorrhage without
\par
anticoagulation. Spontaneous cerebral hemorrhage is exceedingly rare in
\par
the absence of predispositions such as anticoagulant therapy, severe hyper-
\par
tension or arteriovenous malformation [16]. Although common sense would
\par
suggest that one primary cerebral hemorrhage is a risk factor for a second,
\par
no data has been collected to verify this belief. Furlan's large population
\par
study does not mention recurrent cerebral hemorrhage when other risk fac-
\par
tors are absent [16]. This patient was on warfarin when the first hemorrhage
\par
occurred. We assume that regardless of the etiology of the first bleeding
\par
event, cessation of anticoagulant therapy will prevent recurrent hemor-
\par
rhage, but we examine the effects of this assumption with sensitivity
\par
analysis.
\par
Eighth, we shall analyze the decision using the Monte Carlo, Markov
\par
cohort and fundamental matrix approaches. For the third analysis, we use
\par
the DEALE assumption of constant population mortality rates [3]. For all
\par
analyses we assume that the mortality rate of underlying cardiac disease is
\par
constant [17].
\par
Data Used in the Analysis. In this Markov model the transition proba-
\par
bilities govern the likelihood of embolization, hemorrhage, and death due
\par
to cardiac disease or other causes.
\par
Wolf and his colleagues used data from the Framingham cardiovascular
\par
disease study to obtain a rate of cerebral embolization in the setting of
\par
mitral valve replacement and atrial fibrillation [18]. Assuming no anti-
\par
coagulation, the rate of cerebral embolization in a patient with a cage-type437
\par
artificial valve was 82 events per 1000 patients per year. Embolization in
\par
patients with primary atrial fibrillation unassociated with mitral disease was
\par
41 events per 1000 patients per year. A maximum value for the chance of
\par
cerebral embolization in a patient suffering from both conditions would be
\par
the sum of these numbers, or 123 events per 1000 patient-years (unless the
\par
diseases were synergistic and exacerbating, which is unlikely on pathophys-
\par
iologic grounds). According to a recent review by Levine [19], two-thirds of
\par
clinically detected systemic emboli are cerebral. Thus a maximum of 123 /
\par
(2/3), or 184 embolic events would be expected in 1000 patient-years of
\par
follow-up without anticoagulation. Levine also suggests that ten percent of
\par
systemic emboli are fatal and 20 percent are permanently disabling [19].
\par
The efficacy of warfarin in systemic embolization is widely recognized,
\par
although there are no controlled studies proving its worth. Uncontrolled
\par
studies and clinical experience suggest that the rate of systemic embolization
\par
on warfarin is decreased by two-thirds. Thus a maximum of 62 embolic
\par
events per 1000 patient-years of follow-up would be expected on warfarin
\par
therapy.
\par
In the case under analysis, the hazards of warfarin are related primarily
\par
to cerebral hemorrhage. The rate of cerebral bleeding depends on whether
\par
the previous hemorrhage was a primary or secondary event. If it was
\par
secondary, then we may use the literature and expert assessments of 30 to 50
\par
cerebral hemorrhages per 1000 patient-years of anticoagulant therapy, with
\par
a fatality rate of four percent [12,13]. If the initial event was a primary
\par
hemorrhage, we need to adjust that value upward. There are no large
\par
reported series of patients receiving long-term anticoagulation after intra-
\par
cerebral hemorrhage, but Lieberman and other neurologists, based on their
\par
experience, suggest a rate of 500 hemorrhages per 1000 patient-years, with a
\par
case fatality rate of 15 percent to 20 percent [14]. In selecting maximum esti-
\par
mates for embolic rate we have chosen to bias the analysis in favor of
\par
resuming anticoagulation. Maintaining this bias, we selected the lower value
\par
of 4\% x 30, or 1.2 fatal hemorrhages per 1000 patients per year, under the
\par
assumption of secondary hemorrhage, and likewise used a cerebral hemor-
\par
rhagic mortality of 15 \% x 500, or 75 per 1000 patient-years, along the
\par
PRIMARY hemorrhage branch.
\par
The contribution of severe tricuspid regurgitation and congestive heart
\par
failure to mortality is taken from the Framingham study [17]. The annual
\par
mortality rate due to such cardiac disease alone is in excess of ten percent.
\par
This \&dquo;added load\&dquo; to the patient's baseline risk has the effect of decreasing
\par
the patient's life expectancy in all Markov process models, but the effect is
\par
constant across them. Thus this excess risk decreases the absolute difference
\par
between the results of different management strategies, but does not
\par
influence the decision whether or not to anticoagulate.
\par
Finally, the patient's risk due to age, sex, and race may be incorporated
\par
in two ways. Tables of vital statistics provide the increasing annual mortal-
\par
ity rate of the healthy population [9]; for a 59-year-old female it is approxi-438
\par
mately 9.4 per 1000 per year (0.0094) and increases exponentially with age to
\par
become 0.062/year by age 80. Either mortality rate tables or a mathematical
\par
model (e.g., Gompertz) may be used in a Markov cohort or Monte Carlo
\par
analysis. For the Markov chain matrix solution, the average mortality rate,
\par
calculated using the DEALE, is used [10]. This is the reciprocal of the life
\par
expectancy of a 59-year-old female (22.6 years), or 0.044/year. Note that
\par
this value is much higher than the instantaneous age-specific mortality rate
\par
of 0.0094/year found in vital statistics tables for women of age 59, but less
\par
than that of women of age 80, because it is applied as a constant force of
\par
mortality. The exponentially increasing rate of mortality found in the tables
\par
eventually reaches and exceeds the constant mortality rate (at about age 70
\par
for females), so that the life expectancy in both formulations is the same.
\par
This comparison is discussed briefly in Appendix A and more extensively
\par
elsewhere [10].
\par
Transition Probabilities of a Markov Process. The data just summarized
\par
are incorporated into the four Markov models, as shown in Table 3. In the
\par
upper section of the table we display the data assuming increasing age-
\par
specific death rates, modeled by the Gompertz function [6]. These models
\par
will drive the Monte Carlo and cohort analyses. In the lower section of
\par
Table 3 we display the data assuming constant risk of death due to age,
\par
race, and sex. These values will be incorporated directly into the funda-
\par
mental matrix approach.
\par
There are four possible transitions from the WELL state: to WELL,
\par
MINOR EVENT, DISABLED, and DEAD. The residual \&dquo;transition\&dquo; from
\par
WELL to WELL represents no event of interest over one clock cycle. It is
\par
calculated by subtracting the sum of all risk probabilities from unity. The
\par
transition probability from WELL to MINOR EVENT is derived from two
\par
independent risks-that of a small stroke and that of a minor hemorrhage.
\par
The overall risk is the sum of the rates of each event. For example, given the
\par
decision to RESUME ANTICOAGULATION and the PRIMARY hemor-
\par
rhagic etiology (Markov Model 3, Figure 9), the risk of embolus is 62/1000
\par
patient-years. Of these 70 percent will be minor events (ten percent are fatal
\par
and 20 percent disabling). The risk of minor stroke is thus 43/1000 patient-
\par
years. The risk of hemorrhage is 500/1000 patient years; 85 percent will be
\par
minor. The overall risk of a minor event, then, is (43 + 425)/1000/year.
\par
We chose a clock cycle of one month for the Markov process (a reason-
\par
able choice-larger cycles are insensitive to the small transition probabilities
\par
in the model, smaller cycles do not accurately reflect the duration and fre-
\par
quency of clinical observation in this disorder). The risk of a minor event is
\par
468/1000/12, or 0.0390/patient-month. The probability corresponding to
\par
this rate is the exponential transform, 1 - e-0.0390, or 0.0382. If the decision
\par
is to withhold anticoagulation (Markov Models 1 and 2, Figure 9), the risk
\par
of hemorrhage becomes very small (Assumption 6). The rate of emboliza-
\par
tion increases to 184/1000 patient-years. Inasmuch as 70 percent of emboli439440
\par
are minor, the monthly transition probability from WELL to MINOR
\par
EVENT would be
\par
or 0.0106. Similar calculations give the monthly transition probabilities
\par
under the assumption that the cerebral hemorrhage is secondary.
\par
Within the upper section of Table 3 are several occurrences of a function,
\par
f(m). Because the Markov process model allows for increasing age-related
\par
mortality, we cannot give a constant value for the transition rate to DEAD.
\par
This rate, which changes with every clock cycle, has two components. The
\par
variable component is monthly age-specific mortality, which is modeled by
\par
the Gompertz mortality function. For a white female, this is shown in the
\par
center of Table 3 as 4.33 x lO-6 X eO.088 x [age]. The constant component m is
\par
the monthly mortality due to underlying cardiac disease (the load), fatal
\par
embolus, or fatal hemorrhage. The variable and constant components are
\par
combined as f(m), shown also in the center of Table 3.
\par
As an example, consider the transition from WELL to DEAD, under the
\par
decision to RESUME ANTICOAGULATION, with a secondary etiology
\par
of hemorrhage (Markov Model 4, Figure 9). The constant component of the
\par
transition rate is calculated as follows: The death rate due to underlying dis-
\par
ease is 1/12 of ten percent per patient-year, or 0.0083/patient-month. The
\par
death rate due to fatal embolus is 1/12 of 62/1000 patient-years x 0.1 (the
\par
stroke rate while on anticoagulation multiplied by the likelihood of case
\par
fatality), or 0.0005/patient-month. The death rate due to fatal hemorrhage
\par
is 1/12 of 30/1000 patient-years x 0.04, or 0.0001/patient-month. The con-
\par
stant component m is the sum of these rates, or 0.0089/patient-month. This
\par
value is inserted in Table 3 as f(0.0089), as shown.
\par
For the decision to withhold anticoagulation, the etiology of hemorrhage
\par
is immaterial to the baseline analysis. The decision tree in Figure 9 reflects
\par
the question of etiology, and it will come into play in structural sensitivity
\par
analyses. Table 3 thus shows identical values in the primary hemorrhage
\par
and secondary hemorrhage columns under the decision to withhold anti-
\par
coagulation.
\par
State-Dependent Utilities. As a starting point, inasmuch as the patient
\par
was hospitalized in another city, we have abstracted quality values for the
\par
states WELL, MINOR EVENT, and DISABLED from a related case [12].
\par
DEAD, of course, accumulates no incremental utility. WELL is defined as
\par
I/month. In the related case, ten months with discomfort due to a minor
\par
stroke or hemorrhage were equated with eight months well; the incremental
\par
utility associated with MINOR EVENT, then, is 0.8/month. Ten months
\par
with permanent disability were equated with three months well. The utility
\par
assigned to the state DISABLED, then, is 0.3/month. These values will be
\par
subjected to sensitivity analyses.441
\par
CALCULATION OF QUALITY-ADJUSTED LIFE EXPECTANCY
\par
Monte Carlo Approach. Table 4 contains the expected months in each
\par
major and temporary state, calculated using the Gompertz model of expo-
\par
nentially increasing mortality rates according to a Monte Carlo simulation.
\par
These expected values are listed for each combination of decision and etiol-
\par
ogy of the initial hemorrhagic event. Ten thousand simulations were per-
\par
formed using a mainframe digital computer, whereupon the averaged num-
\par
ber of months in each state converged toward a limit. The standard devia-
\par
tion of the number of months in each state, as well as the life expectancy for
\par
each branch of the decision tree shown in Figure 9, are also shown in the
\par
table. The greatest life expectancy, not surprisingly, occurs in the setting of
\par
a secondary hemorrhage with anticoagulation resumed (Model 4). The least
\par
life expectancy occurs in the setting of primary hemorrhage with anticoagu-
\par
lation resumed (Model 3). Quality-adjusted life expectancy, the rightmost
\par
column of Table 4, is calculated for each combination of management plan
\par
and hemorrhagic etiology by multiplying the expected months in each state
\par
by the state-specific quality factor, and then adding.
\par
The standard deviations of the expected survivals are quite large. This is
\par
true for Markov processes in general [4] ; the implications of the high varia-
\par
bility will be explored in the discussion below.
\par
Markov Cohort Approach. Table 5 contains the expected survivals (in
\par
months) in each major and temporary state, calculated using the Gompertz
\par
model of life expectancy according to a cohort analysis; the results are simi-
\par
lar to those in the Monte Carlo approach. The life expectancies calculated in
\par
Table 5 are more exact than the simulated values but do not contain any
\par
measures of variation.
\par
Markov Chain Approach. Table 6 contains the expected months in each
\par
major and temporary state, calculated by representing the data in Table 3 as
\par
Markov canonical matrices and calculating the fundamental or N matrices
\par
by inversion. Compared to the cohort formulation, the Markov chain
\par
analysis shows approximately 13 percent fewer months spent in the WELL
\par
state and 20 percent fewer months spent in the DISABLED state for each
\par
set of parameters. This occurs because the probability of death due to
\par
causes unrelated to cardiac disease in the Gompertz model is small in the
\par
early months of the simulation. The Gompertz model does not \&dquo;catch up\&dquo; to
\par
the DEALE approach until age 67. Thus the summed mortality rates due to
\par
congestive heart failure, population mortality, and catastrophic events
\par
extract more penalty early in the matrix approach [10]. The fundamental
\par
matrix thus underestimates life expectancy, as calculated using the more
\par
accurate Markov cohort analysis.
\par
Although not identical, the life expectancies and quality-adjusted life
\par
expectancies for the fundamental matrix and cohort analyses are on the442443444445
\par
same order. This patient, who if well would enjoy a life expectancy of 22.9
\par
years, can, in the best possible setting, expect only 7.8 years of life with her
\par
multiple medical problems (the N matrix calculates this as 6.7 years, a 15
\par
percent apparent reduction). With adjustments for morbidity, this is
\par
decreased further to 7.4 quality-adjusted years (6.3 years in the matrix
\par
analysis).
\par
The standard deviations calculated by the matrix approach, also listed in
\par
Table 6, are comparable to those calculated by the Monte Carlo simulation
\par
(Table 4).
\par
CALCULATION OF EXPECTED UTILITY. The values of quality-adjusted life
\par
expectancy are used as utility asessments in the decision tree shown in
\par
Figure 9, and the expected quality-adjusted survival is calculated by multi-
\par
plying the utility values by their associated probabilities of occurrence, and
\par
then adding. Using the values from the Monte Carlo analysis for the deci-
\par
sion to withhold anticoagulation, the expected utility is thus 75.43 quality-
\par
adjusted months. For the decision to resume anticoagulation, the expected
\par
utility is (89.65 x 0.1 + 58.24 x 0.9), or 61.38 quality-adjusted months.
\par
Table 7 summarizes the expected utilities for each management approach,
\par
according to the three illustrated methods of Markov analysis. Although the
\par
matrix approach underestimates life expectancy as calculated by the simula-
\par
tion, the decision analysis reaches the same conclusion as in the Monte
\par
Carlo and cohort models; in the baseline analysis, withholding anticoagula-
\par
tion provides a real quality-adjusted life expectancy benefit of 17 percent to
\par
19 percent.
\par
