Figure 14 shows a two-way sensitivity analysis of the probability of primary
\par
cerebral hemorrhage as a function of the incremental utility assigned to
\par
permanent disability (0.3 in the baseline case). As can be seen from Tables 4
\par
through 6, withholding anticoagulation produces more months with dis-
\par
ability. Thus a higher utility value assigned to DISABLED tends to further
\par
favor withholding treatment. Interpreted another way, if the patient can
\par
tolerate permanent disability, he or she would tend to favor the therapy that
\par
minimizes fatal hemorrhages, even at a cost of more serious embolizations.449
\par
Figure 13. Six-State Markov model. This model incorporates changing therapy as a
\par
result of nonfatal embolic or hemorrhagic events. Anticoagulation status and type of
\par
minor event are specified in this amended model.
\par
In another sensitivity analysis of the utility structure we examined the
\par
seventh assumption, that once permanently disabled, subsequent MINOR
\par
EVENTS generate no additional disutility. If we set the utility associated
\par
with future minor events to zero, the net effect on quality-adjusted life
\par
expectancy is less than 0.5 percent in all Markov models. This effect
\par
changes no decisions and affects the decision to withhold or resume anti-
\par
coagulation equivalently.
\par
Discussion
\par
The Markov model of prognosis is an alternative to traditional concepts of
\par
multiattribute utility. It can be developed using data from the clinical litera-
\par
ture and can be solved in various ways using a calculator or microcomputer.
\par
The Markov model generates quality-adjusted life expectancy values in a
\par
fashion similar to the declining exponential approximation of life expec-
\par
tancy (DEALE) [10]. Both techniques employ real-life units and values from
\par
the literature, a major advantage over arbitrary utility scales. Furthermore,
\par
because the expected durations of survival in transient states are explicitly
\par
calculated in the Markov model, quality adjustment is easier than in the
\par
DEALE.
\par
The Markov model introduces the concept of incremental utility, in
\par
which a patient is observed in a particular state of health over a discrete time
\par
interval (the clock cycle), and a quality or utility score is assigned to that
\par
interval. As a patient's clinical status waxes and wanes, utility is accumu-450
\par
Table 8. Six-State Amended Markov Chain Model
\par
Table 9. Fundamental Matrices for the Modified Markov Chain
\par
lated, rather than being assessed at the termination of the strategy or path,
\par
as in standard clinical decision analysis. This incremental utility can be
\par
modified using any of the methods available for decision making (risk
\par
adjustment, discounting).
\par
Leviton and his colleagues developed a Markov model of headache
\par
recurrence for patients with chronic daily headache [20]. They were able to451
\par
Figure 14. Two-way sensitivity analysis of the probability of primary hemorrhage
\par
and state-dependent incremental utility associated with disability. The threshold line
\par
separates the regions in which WITHHOLD and RESUME ANTICOAGULATION
\par
are preferable. The solid circle denotes the baseline conditions in the case example.
\par
fit experimental data closely to a Markov chain with four nonabsorbing
\par
states. There are, however, several limitations to Markov modeling. The
\par
natural history of prostatic cancer has been modeled using Markov chains,
\par
but with several forced assumptions about disease status [21]. In order to
\par
invoke the Markovian assumption, the authors permitted transitions
\par
between clinical states defined by alkaline phosphatase and acid phos-
\par
phatase levels. They assumed that sequential determinations of these
\par
enzymes gave results independent of both prior testing and clinical remis-
\par
sion status. In most malignancies, the full prior history is incorporated in
\par
decision making: \&dquo;Is this the first, second, or later relapse?\&dquo; Such data
\par
invalidate the central assumption of the Markov process, because they
\par
imply some memory of prior states of health. Some simple oncologic prob-
\par
lems may however be modeled using Markov processes that distinguish
\par
among time-related events: WELL, FIRST RELAPSE, REMISSION,
\par
LATER RELAPSE, LATE REMISSION, DEAD.
\par
It is always possible to expand a problem with finite memory into a
\par
Markov process by increasing the number of states, creating a so-called
\par
semi-Markov process. In the example case, we did just this when we con-
\par
sidered the effects of switching therapies in the case of nonfatal embolic or
\par
hemorrhagic events. Here the \&dquo;memory\&dquo; was whether or not the patient was
\par
anticoagulated at the time of the event-the issue was handled by adding
\par
two states that reflected event type and anticoagulant status. There is a452
\par
practical limit to this tactic, however-the advantage of the Markov model
\par
is its simplicity, and this advantage can be eroded by a complex state model
\par
formulation. That is, as the number of states increases, the number of
\par
transition probabilities that must be assessed grows exponentially.
\par
Another limitation to Markov modeling is the variability of a multiple
\par
parameter model. As shown in the case example, while imposing little calcu-
\par
lational burden, the matrix formulation underestimates life expectancy.
\par
This is explored further in Appendix A, where it is shown that this under-
\par
estimation does not lead to incorrect therapeutic decision making. Further-
\par
more, the standard deviations of the expected months in each nonabsorbing
\par
state are large. This implies that if the Markov model were rewritten as a
\par
deep decision tree, a huge number of pathways with different survivals
\par
would result. To worry about large variability in Markov analyses, then, is
\par
to admit that any large decision tree has numerous pathways. If we adhere
\par
to the central tenet of decision making by maximizing expected utility, than
\par
Markov analysis assists us in providing expected utilities as well as explicit
\par
measures of variability in the Monte Carlo and matrix formulations.
\par
For bedside application of Markov modeling-in a clinical decision
\par
analysis-the fundamental matrix will arrive at a decision quickly. This is
\par
especially true if only a few states are involved and if a calculator capable of
\par
matrix inversion is available. We have written a program for one calculator
\par
that permits matrix operations (the HP-15C, Hewlett-Packard, Portland
\par
Oregon). If the variance matrix V is required, this calculator will handle
\par
Markov models with up to five clinical states. If only expected survivals are
\par
of interest, this machine will calculate the fundamental matrix of an eight-
\par
state model.
\par
In cases when the actual number of months lived becomes important, the
\par
time horizon must be short for the matrix model to closely approximate the
\par
Gompertz simulation or cohort models of life expectancy.
\par
We recommend the development of Markov models of prognosis in clini-
\par
cal situations involving decision making about chronic disease. For prompt,
\par
bedside analyses the matrix formulation of the Markov model, employing
\par
constant transition probabilities abstracted from the literature using the
\par
DEALE, provides correct decisions in all cases and accurate values of life
\par
expectancy in problems having short time horizons. With access to a micro-
\par
computer [22] or at the leisure of the decision analyst, Markov cohort anal-
\par
yses or Monte Carlo simulations provide more exact calculations of quality-
\par
adjusted life expectancy. The availability of a simple formulation for prog-
\par
nostic modeling should be another solid step in the practical application of
\par
utility theory to medical decision making.
\par
Appendix A
\par
ANALYSIS OF DISCREPANCIES BETWEEN MARKOV MATRIX AND COHORT
\par
FORMULATIONS. The differences between the fundamental matrix solution453
\par
of a Markov chain and the cohort analysis using exponentially increasing
\par
population mortality rates are analogous to the discrepancies between the
\par
DEALE and Gompertz models of life expectancy reported previously [10].
\par
In fact, the DEALE itself describes a two-state Markov model with a 1 x 1
\par
fundamental matrix whose inverse is simply its reciprocal. The constant
\par
probabilities used in the Markov matrix are taken from averaged rates using
\par
the DEALE method [3]. To assess the relative and absolute accuracy of con-
\par
stant transition probabilities in Markov medical decision making, two
\par
experiments were conducted. The first was designed to test the absolute dif-
\par
ferences between matrix and cohort formulations of Markov processes. The
\par
second experiment was designed to assess whether the optimal choices in
\par
quantitative decisions analyzed with the convenient matrix formulation dif-
\par
fered notably from repeat analyses using the exponentially increasing popu-
\par
lation mortality approach.
\par
Design of Experiment 1. In this experiment Markov models with from
\par
two to five nonabsorbing states were constructed, and life expectancies were
\par
calculated using both matrix and cohort formulations. The cohort model
\par
was taken as the \&dquo;gold standard.\&dquo; Calculations were made for patient ages
\par
ranging between 20 and 80, and for diseases with transition probabilities
\par
between nonabsorbing states and to the DEAD state from 0 to 0.05/month.
\par
In all, 313 488 analyses were performed.
\par
The Markov matrices were developed as follows. One absorbing state
\par
(death) was defined. For each of the nonabsorbing states, transition proba-
\par
bilities to death and to any other nonabsorbing state were varied from zero
\par
to 0.05, in increments of 0.01, Thus the probability of remaining in the
\par
starting state was the greatest single value in every analysis (generally
\par
0.90/month or greater). This general model was developed to reflect clinical
\par
examples of Markov processes, in which the greatest single probability is
\par
often remaining in one's present state.
\par
Design of Experiment 2. This experiment utilized the results of the first
\par
experiment as outcome measures in a prototypical clinical decision. We
\par
used the same decision tree as reported previously [10], wherein a hypo-
\par
thetical clinical problem was constructed that contained branches describing
\par
immediate mortality (e.g., surgical death) and therapeutic efficacy. Figure 15
\par
shows the two Markov matrices for the problem. The chronic disease has
\par
nonabsorbing states WELL and ILL. Constant transition probabilities Pwr,
\par
Pwa, and P,d define state transitions. Additionally, there is the risk of death
\par
due to other causes and general population mortality (Pg). In the cohort for-
\par
mulation, Pg is calculated by the Gompertz model of exponentially increas-
\par
ing mortality rate. In the matrix analysis, Pg is calculated using the DEALE.
\par
If surgery is selected (upper matrix). then there is a risk of operative death,
\par
denoted as M. For the purpose of this experiment, we can call \&dquo;surgery\&dquo; a
\par
state in the Markov model, with two possible transitions, to DEAD or to454
\par
Figure 15. Transition probability matrices for Experiment 2. These matrices repre-
\par
sent a Markov decision analysis of surgical versus medical therapy for a hypothetical
\par
condition. In the upper matrix, SURGERY is a temporary state leading either to
\par
WELL or DEAD. WELL and ILL are clinical states in both models, with DEAD a
\par
common absorbing state. Transition probabilities include mortality related to age,
\par
