\par
thetical disease (P\textasciitilde,, Pwd, P1d)' Operative efficacy is denoted by E. The symbol V
\par
represents the probability of two independent events occurring together (A V B: A or
\par
B occurs). In algebraic terms the probability of A V B (Pr[AVB]) is (Pr[A] +Pr[B] -
\par
Pr[A ] Pr[B]).
\par
WELL. If surgery is survived, an operative efficacy E reduces the transition
\par
probabilities from WELL to ILL and to DEAD as shown. Operative effi-
\par
cacy was varied from 20 percent to 100 percent. In addition, the quality of
\par
life associated with the ILL state was varied from 0.1 to 0.9. In all, 22 680
\par
analyses were performed.
\par
Results. Both experiments confirmed the prior experience with the
\par
DEALE. For small values of disease-specific mortality, the matrix formula-
\par
tion underestimates life expectancy relative to the cohort formulation. The
\par
magnitude of the underestimation does not depend on the number of states
\par
directly, but on the magnitude of the excess risk of death relative to the
\par
annual mortality rate of the healthy population. Differences between the
\par
models are greatest for young patients with small disease-specific excess
\par
mortalities; when the monthly excess mortality rate is one percent or455
\par
greater, even for a 20-year-old patient the absolute difference in life expec-
\par
tancy between the cohort and matrix formulations is no greater than 14
\par
months. Comprehensive data are available from the authors but are not
\par
presented here due to space limitations.
\par
The results of the second experiment indicate that the matrix and cohort
\par
models do not lead to different therapeutic decisions, for reasonable combi-
\par
nations of operative risk and efficacy. In over 97 percent of the analyses the
\par
two models yielded the same therapeutic decision. For virtually all of the
\par
remaining situations, the discrepancy amounted to less than one percent of
\par
life expectancy. Only in cases where high operative mortality was coupled
\par
with high surgical efficacy were discrepancies greater than one year of life.
\par
The results indicate that the differences between the constant mortality
\par
approximation using the DEALE and the exact Markov cohort analysis are
\par
analogous to the simple DEALE modeling problem reported previously
\par
[10]. The addition of temporary states does not affect the performance of
\par
the model, as long as disease-specific transition probabilities remain
\par
constant.
\par
Appendix B
\par
DEMONSTRATION OF THE ANALOGY BETWEEN THE FUNDAMENTAL MATRIX
\par
OF A MARKOV CHAIN AND LIFE EXPECTANCY. The canonical form of a
\par
Markov matrix has four sections (Figure 3). Two sections, labeled I and O,
\par
signify that it is impossible to leave an absorbing state. The R section con-
\par
tains the single-step transition probabilities between intermediate or non-
\par
absorbing states and the absorbing state (or states). The Q section is a
\par
matrix of single-step transition probabilities among the nonabsorbing
\par
states.
\par
The Q matrix has information necessary and sufficient to predict the
\par
behavior of a patient entering the Markov process in one of the nonabsorb-
\par
ing states. What follows is a demonstration of the properties of the Q
\par
matrix. Interested readers are referred to Kemeny and Snell [4] for a formal
\par
proof of these properties.
\par
Matrix multiplication is illustrated in Figure 16. Rows of the multiplicand
\par
are projected across columns of the multiplier, so that the product matrix
\par
contains elements that reflect the weighted product of the elements of the
\par
original two matrices. In effect, this weighted multiplication is analogous to
\par
moving forward long a path in a decision tree. The Markov matrix is essen-
\par
tially a \&dquo;shorthand\&dquo; representation of an arbitrarily deep tree, with repeti-
\par
tive probabilities at each level of the tree. In the case of the Q matrix, the
\par
elements signify the likelihood of a patient being in each nonabsorbing state
\par
after a single cycle of the process. When the Q matrix is multiplied by itself,456
