Decision analysis requires that two equally desirable consequences should have
\par
the same utility and vice versa. Most analyses of financial decision making
\par
presume that two consequences with the same dollar outcome will be equally
\par
preferred. However, winning the top prize of \$10,000 in a lottery may leave one
\par
much happier than receiving \$10,000 as the lowest prize in a lottery. This paper
\par
explores the implications of disappointment, a psychological reaction caused
\par
by comparing the actual outcome of a lottery to one's prior expectations, for
\par
decision making under uncertainty. Explicit recognition that decision makers
\par
may be paying a premium to avoid potential disappointment provides an
\par
interpretation for some known behavioral paradoxes, and suggests that decision
\par
makers may be sensitive to the manner in which a lottery is resolved. The
\par
concept of disappointment is integrated into utility theory in a prescriptive
\par
model.
\par
YOUR BOSS tells you that he is delighted with your performance
\par
over the past year and is giving you a \$5,000 bonus. Are you pleased?
\par
If you were not expecting a bonus, you will be delighted. If you were
\par
expecting a \$10,000 bonus, you will be disappointed. The satisfaction you
\par
feel with the bonus you are given will depend upon your prior expecta-
\par
tions. The higher your expectations, the greater will be your disappoint-
\par
ment. People who are particularly averse to disappointment may learn
\par
to adopt a pessimistic view about the future.
\par
If you accept a 50-50 gamble between \$0 and \$2,000, there is a 50\%
\par
chance that you will be disappointed when the lottery is resolved. You
\par
may prefer to swap the lottery ticket for a sure \$950 not so much because
\par
of arguments about decreasing marginal value, but because doing so
\par
removes the possibility of disappointment. Of course, someone who feels
\par
that the "thrill of victory" is worth the possible "agony of defeat" may
\par
take the opposite choice.
\par
Disappointment, then, is a psychological reaction to an outcome that
\par
does not match up to expectations. The greater the disparity, the greater
\par
the disappointment. We will use the word elation to describe the euphoria
\par
associated with an outcome that exceeds expectations. Decision makers
\par
who anticipate these feelings may take them into account when compar-
