\par
disappointment and to investigate the behavioral implications for a
\par
decision maker who is prepared to make economic tradeoffs to reduce
\par
disappointment, and (ii) to compare these implications with known
\par
behavioral violations of expected utility maximization when only the
\par
amounts of economic payoffs are taken into account.
\par
This paper is a continuation of my research into the components of
\par
risk aversion. The economic explanation of risk aversion, that each
\par
additional dollar is worth slightly less due to satiation (decreasing mar-
\par
ginal value), is surely a significant factor, but perhaps not the only one.
\par
Imagine two individuals with equal wealth and identical tastes for con-
\par
sumables. One is timid, nervous, and full of self-doubt; the other is
\par
outgoing, self-confident, and with a sense of purpose. We might suppose
\par
that the latter will be less risk averse than the former. Indeed, his relative
\par
risk attitude, in the sense of Dyer and Sarin [1982], may even be risk
\par
prone.
\par
In earlier work (Bell [1982, 1983], also Loomes and Sugden [1982]), I
\par
have explored the implications of regret as a factor in risk attitude.
\par
Regret is a psychological reaction to making a wrong decision, where
\par
wrong is determined on the basis of actual outcomes rather than on the
\par
information available at the time of the decision. Just as disappointment
\par
is caused by comparing an outcome with prior expectations, so regret is
\par
caused by comparing an outcome with the payoff one could have had by
\par
making a different choice. For example, if you are given a 50-50 lottery
\par
between \$0 and \$10 and lose, you will suffer disappointment. Had you
\par
selected the same lottery over an alternative of \$4 for sure and lost, you
\par
would have suffered both disappointment and regret. For one to suffer
\par
only regret and not disappointment, the outcome of a chosen lottery
\par
would have to be exactly equal to one's expectations, but less than one
\par
could have obtained (ex post) from an alternative lottery.
\par
There are many other "reference effect" phenomena. A bonus of \$5,000
\par
may exceed one's expectations, but still lead to dissatisfaction if you
\par
learn that your colleague got a bonus of \$10,000. Perhaps the most
\par
influential reference point is the status quo of the decision maker. It has
\par
been widely observed that a decision maker will make significant eco-
\par
nomic tradeoffs to remove the possibility of a net loss on a transaction.
\par
Building a utility model that incorporates all of these effects may well
\par
be desirable not only to provide a better description of behavior, but also,
\par
to the extent that a decision maker is prepared to trade off dollars
\par
explicitly to gain a state of psychological satisfaction, for prescriptive
\par
purposes.
\par
In this paper, to avoid unnecessary complication, we will consider only
\par
the effect of disappointment (and elation) on decision making under
\par
uncertainty. We will assume throughout that the decision maker has
