1. A SIMPLE MODEL OF DISAPPOINTMENT
\par
The substitution principle is key to the derivation of expected utility
\par
theory. Though some regard it as virtually a self-evident requirement for
\par
consistency, in much the way that the transitivity principle seems so
\par
compelling, others attack it as the weak link that allows behavioral
\par
contradictions in practice. The principle asserts (see Keeney [1982] for
\par
example) that any preference ordering of two alternatives with uncertain
\par
consequences will not be affected either by substituting for any one
\par
consequence an equally desirable consequence (which may itself be a
\par
lottery) or by deleting a consequence common to both alternatives. This
\par
principle is usually the one relied upon to demonstrate inconsistency in
\par
preference orderings.
\par
We will use an example of Kahneman and Tversky [1979] to illustrate
\par
both the compelling nature of the substitution principle and why disap-
\par
pointment provides an explanation for some observed violations of it.
\par
(See also the discussion by Machina [1981 pp. 172-173].) Kahneman and
