4. SENSITIVITY TO METHOD OF UNCERTAINTY RESOLUTION
\par
In Section 2 we developed a model that captures the idea that people's
\par
reaction to decision outcomes is a function not only of the absolute value
\par
of their payoff, but also to the change in their expectations and to the
\par
likelihood of such outcomes. So far we have looked at the implications
\par
of disappointment for decision making in standard situations including
\par
violations of the substitution principle and risk proneness.
\par
Now we extend our analysis to the implications of disappointment for
\par
a class of decision situations that has received little empirical study and
\par
almost no normative analysis, the possible preference of the decision
\par
maker for different methods for resolving what are otherwise identical
\par
situations.
\par
The logical argument underlying the substitution principle fails if we
\par
agree that the method of resolution may matter. While the economic
\par
implications may not change, the psychological implications might.
\par
For example, the Irish Sweepstakes is run as a two-stage lottery. In
\par
the first stage, 20 or so tickets are drawn at random to match one for
\par
one the horses running in the Irish Derby. At the second stage, you win
\par
the lottery if you draw a horse at the first stage and if your horse wins
\par
the race. Since the prize money is significant, the disappointment from
\par
winning a horse, but not the race, would be substantial. The disappoint-
\par
ment would be greatest had you drawn the favorite. Many first stage
\par
winners are offered sizeable amounts for their tickets. The threat of
\par
losing may make such an offer very attractive. (There are competing
\par
arguments however. The severe regret that would accompany having sold
\par
what proved to be the winning ticket might be intolerable. Also, as one
\par
such first stage winner put it, "My luck has been with me so far, why
