\par
For example, suppose that you have the job of telling someone that a
\par
close friend/relative has been killed in a car crash. How do you go about
\par
it? A normal reaction is to break the news in stages. ("I have some bad
\par
news. There's been a terrible accident. I'm afraid.... ") The interpre-
\par
tation is that you are gradually lowering the recipient's expectations so
\par
that the truth does not come all at once. Your mechanic knows this
\par
method too. ("It was a bigger job than we thought. We had to rebuild the
\par
engine. And replace the chassis .... .)
\par
Breaking good news seems rather different. Suppose that you noticed
\par
your brother had inadvertently neglected to check the status of his Irish
\par
Sweepstakes ticket, and you found that not only had he drawn a horse
\par
but that the horse had won, how would you tell him the news?
\par
It is arguable whether the method of breaking news is chosen more for
\par
the benefit of the teller than the tellee, but an analysis of the disappoint-
\par
ment implications suggests that it will usually, but not always, be the
\par
case that bad news should be broken gently and good news all at once.
\par
Before our analysis begins one more assumption is required. So far, we
\par
have no explicit model of the net psychological effect of two sequential
\par
