5. SUMMARY AND CONCLUSIONS
\par
For many decisions it is apparent that any quantitative analysis must
\par
account for the various conflicting objectives of the decision maker.
\par
Psychological satisfaction, as opposed to satisfaction derived from con-
\par
sumption, is an appropriate objective that should be included in any
\par
decision analysis if the decision maker regards it as a criterion for
\par
decision. It can be argued that doing so lowers short term financial
\par
efficiency, but taking weekday vacations in the mountains also does so.
\par
Indeed the benefits may be similar; alleviation of stress may have the
\par
intangible payoff of improving the general efficiency of the decision
\par
maker.
\par
In particular, a consumer may wish to spend some of those dollars in
\par
avoiding disappointment, an aspect of risk aversion that does not seem
\par
to be reflected by a utility function over dollar assets alone. This paper
\par
does not suggest that people ought to make financial tradeoffs to avoid
\par
disappointment, nor does it assert (though I believe it to be true) that
\par
people do so. As is the case with normative analyses, it merely indicates
\par
the behavior that is the logical result of such an objective. Although the
\par
implications of this analysis are, in a number of ways, consistent with
\par
behavior observed in laboratory experiments, it would be surprising to
\par
hear that subjects become sufficiently involved with their hypothetical
\par
choices to make psychological effects primary motivators of their selec-
\par
tions. It may be that the psychological impacts of a decision are generated
\par
by the same thought process used in making a decision, namely that the
\par
value of an outcome is judged relative to various reference points such as
\par
status quo, foregone assets, and prior expectations.
\par
Disappointment, and related concepts such as regret, have important
\par
implications for the study of decision making under uncertainty. Al-
\par
though the axioms of von Neumann and Morgenstern are the corner-
\par
stones of decision analysis, they cannot be expected to hold if preference
\par
has not been calculated over all attributes of interest to the decision
\par
maker. While it has taken a study of descriptive behavior to force
\par
recognition of the importance of psychological impacts to the decision
\par
maker, it is not our intent to revise the normative theory continually
\par
until it matches empirical evidence. Such an approach would make
\par
