\par
Regret Symmetry
\par
A farmer has a crop whose value at harvest time, two months from
\par
now, is uncertain. Should he accept a recent fixed-price offer for his crop
\par
in which the buyer guarantees a specific price per bushel for the grain, to
\par
be paid at harvest time? To simplify the example, suppose that the price
\par
at harvest time will be either \$3 or \$7 per bushel with equal probability.
\par
The current offer, the only one the farmer expects to receive, is for \$5 per
\par
bushel. A traditional decision analysis shows that the fixed price offer
\par
has no risk because it is \$5 for sure, whereas waiting does have risk.
\par
How might the farmer think about the risks involved? If he sells now
\par
for \$5 and the price at harvest time proves to be \$7, he will feel that he
\par
lost \$2 by his action. If the price declines to \$3, he will feel pleased at
\par
being up by \$2. Compare that alternative with choosing to wait. If the
\par
price declines to \$3 he will regret not selling it for the \$5 and feel like his
\par
decision cost him \$2. If the price is \$7 at harvest time he will feel happy
\par
that his acumen improved his position by \$2 per bushel. With either
\par
option he is equally likely to regard himself as up or down by \$2. The
\par
alternatives are symmetric in terms of their potential for regret. In this
\par
sense the alternatives have equal risk.
\par
