4. DISCUSSION AND EXTENSIONS
\par
Utility theory is often criticized because it fails to predict actual
\par
behavior for some quite straightforward comparisons between alterna-
\par
tives with uncertain consequences. It is easy to understand why decision
\par
makers may be skeptical of expected utility analysis as a prescriptive tool
\par
when it apparently fails even for some simple comparisons. The analyst's
\par
only defense of the theory has been to offer a careful explanation of why
\par
certain of the decision maker's responses are inconsistent with the axioms
\par
of von Neumann and Morgenstern. This explanation usually leaves the
\par
decision maker unimpressed. This paper shows that some of the para-
\par
doxical behavior of decision makers is consistent with a desire to avoid
\par
post decision regret.
\par
The assessment procedures of Section 3 and the assumptions of Section
\par
1 do not explicitly involve tradeoffs between assets and regret. This fact
\par
allows assessment to proceed without an explicit declaration from the
\par
decision maker that regret avoidance is a concern. The resulting multiat-
\par
tribute utility function may offer a better chance of reflecting, descrip-
\par
tively, the decision maker's holistic appraisals of independently posed
\par
alternatives. Deriving a utility function that is descriptively accurate is
\par
an important first step for establishing a prescriptive framework for
\par
aiding the decision maker.
\par
The next step is to determine, with the decision maker, whether a
\par
regret term is an appropriate component of the analysis. Even if the
\par
decision maker agrees that regret avoidance is a goal to be traded off
\par
against final assets, he may wish to consider whether the tradeoffs he is
\par
implicitly using are appropriate. A constructive analysis might then be
\par
undertaken. Of course the decision maker may wish to eliminate the
\par
regret component entirely. Just as weather forecasters accept training to
\par
improve their probability calibration so perhaps decision makers may
\par
accept training to eliminate, as appropriate, the practice of comparing
\par
uncertain alternatives by a weighted function of value differences (Tver-
\par
sky).
\par
The approach of this paper is subject to the criticism that adding an
\par
extra variable, regret, to the utility function inevitably allows for a better
\par
fit to observed behavior. Of course, this is true. However, the appeal of
\par
this additional factor lies in the widespread recognition that regret is a
\par
factor in decision making and because it is the same property of regret
\par
(decreasing concavity of the function f) that accounts for the explanations
\par
of the paradoxes discussed. It is because of this "degrees of freedom"
\par
objection that attention in this paper is restricted to simple comparisons.
