\par
Future research will examine the role of regret in situations in which
\par
the joint probability distribution of alternative consequences is unspeci-
\par
fied and choices in which foregone lotteries are never resolved. There are
\par
other potential complications to the theory. For example, the degree of
\par
regret felt may depend on the amount of thought that went into the
\par
decision, or on how close the decision maker was to going with the
\par
alternative subsequently foregone. The level of regret felt may sometimes
\par
be related to the original status quo no matter what the outcome of
\par
foregone alternatives. More generally, this question is an example of the
\par
problem of defining regret when multiple alternatives are involved. No
\par
account is taken of mental biases identified in people's understanding of
\par
probabilities (Kahnemann and Tversky).
\par
Clearly there is a need for experimental exploration of the role of regret
\par
in decision making. This paper suggests that regret may be an important
\par
factor in resolving the apparent failure of utility theory to reflect observed
\par
behavior and moreover, that multiattribute utility theory provides a
\par
satisfactory resolution.
