The Institute Recommendations \textbf{on }auditing were based on the necessary
\par
recognition of the `inherent limitations of accounts'.
\par
Accounts are historical in character and their preparation is based largely on con-
\par
ventions, estimates, and opinions resulting from the combined judgment of directors
\par
and their advisors. There is, naturally, room for legitimate differences in estimate
\par
and opinion and there may be differences in the application of recognized accounting
\par
principles. However, unless the judgment of directors is manifestly unsound, the
\par
auditor can properly accept it provided he is satisfied that it is honestly and reason-
\par
ably informed, and results in a full and fair presentation of the accounts. \textbf{(1954}
\par
Revision, p. \textbf{6)}
\par
The auditor did not `certify' accounts. Rather he reported his opinion as to `the
\par
soundness of the view which the accounts present of the company's affairs'. He
\par
was entitled to express the opinion that the accounts are `true and correct' if
\par
the accounts are drawn u p in accordance with accepted accountancy principles,
\par
which include the soundness of accounting methods employed, the consistency in
\par
applying those methods and the truth and fairness in presentation. \textbf{(1954 }Revision,
\par
p. \textbf{7)}
\par
The role of the auditor was seen as being to ascertain and report on the
\par
conformity of accounts with `generally accepted accounting principles'. The
\par
Institute's recommendations on auditing thus reinforced previously established
\par
`accounting principles' and reaffirmed the rationale of depicting `historical \textbf{truth'.}
