A last issue of \textbf{\emph{The Chartered Accountant }}in the \textbf{1950s }contained a `seasonal
\par
message from the president': `The New Year will bring its changes, as each new
\par
year has always done, and with these changes there will be many challenges. May
\par
we be able to accept and answer them successfully' (December \textbf{1959, }p. \textbf{290).}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Confrontation }}- \textbf{\emph{the 1960s}}
\par
In August \textbf{1960 }Sydney Guarantee Ltd published a prospectus for an issue of
\par
unsecured notes, attracting subscriptions of more than \textbf{\$1 }million. In March \textbf{1961}
\par
a receiver was appointed to the company. At that stage \textbf{\$5.2 }million was owing
\par
in unsecured loans \textbf{\emph{(The Australian Financial Review }}- hereafter \textbf{\emph{AFR }}- \textbf{29}
\par
May \textbf{1962). }Five months later a winding-up order was made. By June \textbf{1962 }it
\par
was reported that the joint liquidators did not even have enough money to print
\par
and mail their report; \$8 million was `down the drain' \textbf{\emph{(The Bulletin, }16 }June
\par
\textbf{1962).}
\par
In June \textbf{1961 }it was reported that something like \textbf{\$30 }million had been raised
\par
from the public by companies which were then having difficulties in meeting their
\par
obligations; prominent among the directors of most were public accountants
\par
\textbf{\emph{(Nation, }3 }June \textbf{1961).}
\par
In February \textbf{1961 }the directors of Latec Investments Ltd announced that group
\par
net profit for the half-year ended December was \$0.5 million, more than double
\par
that for the previous year \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }23 }February \textbf{1961). }In September a new
\par
directorate announced a group loss of \textbf{\$2.9 }million for the year ended June \textbf{1961}
\par
\textbf{\emph{(AFR, }5 }October \textbf{196 1 }) . Next May shareholders were told to expect a `satisfactory
\par
profit' for the year ended June \textbf{1962 \emph{(AFR, }15 }May \textbf{1962); }in August they were
\par
told to expect a substantial loss \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }9 }August \textbf{1962). }Later, the loss was
\par
quantified as \textbf{\$6.4 }million, `the greatest loss in Australian public company history'
\par
\textbf{\emph{(AFR, }6 }September \textbf{1962).}
\par
In November \textbf{1961 }Australian Motor Industries announced a loss of \textbf{\$4.5}
\par
million, about half the shareholders' interest. Under the heading, `encircling gloom',
\par
it was reported that `A.M.I. had given fair warning that it was in trouble, but
\par
nobody expected a loss of this size' \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }9 }November \textbf{1962).}
\par
In October \textbf{1962 an }editorial in \textbf{\emph{The Chartered Accountant in Australia }}spoke
\par
warmly of the effect of the `uniform' companies legislation, which had `unearthed
\par
many of the facts' about the `true financial structures' of many firms; the share
\par
market was consequently `in a better position to value their scrip with assurance,
\par
rather than on hopeful supposition'. `The share market rightfully refuses to be
\par
disillusioned by poor performances by a handful of companies, most of which
\par
brought their troubles upon themselves' (p. \textbf{214). }Some weeks later, the press
\par
carried reports of further troubles.
\par
Yesterday was the blackest day in Australian company history. L. J. Hooker reported
\par
a revenue \textbf{loss }of \$2.2 million. Rockmans Ltd. of the Stanhill group is to be put in
