\par
Irish stated that his paper was not `an attempt to whitewash the profession'
\par
(p. \textbf{85) }He reiterated the argument that accounting reports were `technical
\par
documents', though with a new twist:
\par
. . . the balance sheet and profit and loss account are legal documents, not merely
\par
in complying with the Companies Act, but in embracing the doctrines of company
\par
law built up over the years in relation to accounts of companies. They become tech-
\par
nical documents because considerable knowledge and skill are needed to satisfy the
\par
legal doctrines and the Act. (p. 81)
\par
He did not state which `doctrines' of company law gave rise to or support for
\par
existing modes of asset measurement. Irish relied upon assertion, long an accepted
\par
mode of justification: `The balance sheet is prepared on the assumption that the
\par
company is a going concern . . . The basis of accounting in the balance sheet is
\par
cost . . .' (p. \textbf{81). }But he then identified two `groups of judgments' to be applied by
\par
accountants and auditors: judgments as to `such questions of stock values, deprecia-
\par
tion and doubtful debts' and judgments \textbf{as }to the choice of specific accounting
\par
procedures to be adopted in particular instances. `The judgment relates to the
\par
rule to be applied to the case, because there are not alternative choices - one
\par
particular rule must be selected as the most appropriate for the case' (p. \textbf{81).}
\par
The appropriate response of the profession to the criticisms following company
\par
failures was, to Irish, that of `educating the public in the proper use and significance
\par
of public accounts'. He admonished some auditors for not being `more uncom-
\par
promising in requiring adjustment of disclosure of significant items where there is
\par
a reasonable doubt and tougher about giving qualified reports' (p. \textbf{87). }The overall
\par
message from Irish was that all was well in the profession; though some members
\par
needed to put their house in order. `We can be proud of the standards of our
\par
professional work, but one bad apple, can spoil the lot - so we cannot rest on
\par
past reputation and we have had a salutary reminder of that.'33
\par
The `professional competence and integrity' of the chartered accountant was, to
\par
Irish, without question - the `degree of chartered accountant' was only `earned
\par
after passing stiff examinations', the Institute had a Royal Charter, and had `issued
\par
many valuable publications' and `fostered a great deal of post-graduate study'. As
\par
further grounds for confidence in the profession, he claimed that `no breath of
\par
criticism has been or could be levelled at probably 99 per cent of the accounts
\par
issued by companies'. Irish was `far from suggesting that even the remaining 1 per
\par
cent are justifiably attacked`. What further evidence was needed to show `that the
\par
public can confidently rely on the report furnished by reputable auditors'?
\par
If the public felt differently, then the problem was simply one of education.
\par
