\par
tions. Most are terminological (in six instances `taxation' was changed to `tax'),
\par
or alterations in citation or in allusions to statutory requirements. There are several
\par
additions to illustrative or explanatory material. Of three new clauses, one deals
\par
with the use of the term `provision', and the others relate to the separate disclosure
\par
of `asset revaluation' and `share premium reserves'.39
\par
The statement dealing with `stock-in-trade' was based on a U.K. statement dated
\par
November 1960,40 and contained even fewer alterations: only nine in the twelve
\par
pages of text. One was a change of layout; there was a change in the order of
\par
paragraphs with a consequential change in text (an allusion to a paragraph `below'
\par
was eliminated since the paragraph was \textbf{now }`above'); two references to other
\par
U.K. publications were removed; `shall' was changed to `should' (`it is necessary
\par
. . . to determine whether replacement price should be taken into account . . .');
\par
in one paragraph reference was made to `certain sections of primary industry
\par
and . . . some mining companies' rather than to `some types of business, such as
\par
. . . '; it was stated that when giving an explanation it might `possibly' be presented
\par
`by way of note'; and there appears to be one typographical enor.41
\par
There were no substantive additions to either of the recommendations in relation
\par
to the presentation of items which, from evidence already available, had been the
\par
source of some confusion in the published statements of recently-failed companies.
\par
`The widespread application in Britain over several years of the U.K. recom-
\par
mendations', wrote the Institute's president, was `proof of their soundness and
\par
acceptance'. Adoption of these recommendations in Australia was `a step forward
\par
in developing international standards'.
\par
An editorial in the Institute's journal soon afterwards welcomed the recom-
\par
mendations while making a rare reference to company failures: `It is clear that
\par
these recommendations . . . will give the investing public greater assurance that
\par
their interests are being safeguarded against the actions of incompetent and
\par
unscrupulous management.'42
