\par
spectacular financial disasters . . . have led to criticism of the accountant's role
\par
in them'. The collapse of the companies has caused `losses to investors and
\par
creditors amounting to many hundreds of millions of dollars', In nearly all these
\par
cases criticisms of accountants had been justified because of `the lack of any
\par
indication, in the published financial statements of the companies concerned, of
\par
conditions which were unsound and likely to lead to disaster' (p. \textbf{193). \emph{And }}in
\par
almost every case it was not the individual accountant or auditor that was at fault,
\par
but acceptable professional practices. `Clearly, it is the structure of the profession
\par
in the technical sense of its rules, policies or procedures that has been the chief
\par
target of criticism' (p. \textbf{193).}
\par
Keown acknowledged that the proper role of the profession is `serving the
\par
public by ensuring that proper information is provided for all persons interested
\par
in the financial aspects of business activity'. The profession could not fulfil this
\par
role unless it could:
\par
. . . produce financial information which is soundly based on a structure of rules or
\par
procedures . , . which will ensure public confidence in our profession and in the
\par
financial statements for which we take responsibility. (p. 193)
