problem, but effectively denied that the problem was within the profession's
\par
domain. They stated that companies had `brought their troubles on themselves',
\par
that `an SEC' was needed, that the problems were due to `deficiencies of manage-
\par
ment', or to `unsound business finance'. Others attacked the law for not being `more
\par
specific', or for `leaving the duties of auditors vague'.
\par
Associated with such defences was a rejection of the substance of the criticisms
\par
when applied to accounting standards.
\par
Spokesmen for the profession chose to assume that accounting techniques were
\par
satisfactory, in the face of claims that they were not. The `basic philosophies of
\par
accounting' were `fundamentally sound'; they had been proved so `over many years
\par
of experience'. This poses the question, what would be required to instill doubt?
\par
If `99\% of published reports' went `unquestioned`, was one to disregard the fact
\par
that the 99\% may have included the earlier reports of Reid Murray, Latec and the
\par
like, and agree that lack of criticism could be equated with untrammelled virtue?
\par
And what of the remaining 1\%' the 1\% which were claimed to have been `mis-
\par
leading'?
\par
Some claimed that the public did not understand the `technical limitations' of
\par
accounting reports - a problem which had been identified at least as early as 1949.
\par
The interpretation of accounts was a `matter for experts' - though experts had
\par
been among the profession's critics and the market's losers. Given that the major
\par
source of criticism of the profession stemmed from an inadequate understanding
\par
of the nature and purpose of accounting, the appropriate response was to `educate
\par
the public'. There were far more accountants calling for \textbf{a }`determined campaign
\par
to enlighten the public' than there were offering suggestions as to how it might be
\par
done. One proposal was that the Institute's 1969 statement on auditing was of
\par
`educative value', and should be widely disseminated. But there is no evidence to
\par
suggest that anything was done in this direction. Indeed, a more noticeable response
\par
was the manner in which members of the profession endeavoured to reassure their
\par
fellows. Critics had been `misinformed', or had lost sight of the `contributions of
\par
the profession'; `numberless achievements of the profession' went `unheralded'. The
\par
`truth and integrity' of the profession could be relied on. Accountants were `intelli-
