Whereas the failures of the \textbf{1930s }left the accounting profession overseas doubt-
\par
ing the wisdom of not codifying their standards, the failure of Australian com-
\par
panies in the \textbf{1960s }left the local profession doubting the wisdom of the standards
\par
they had so readily adopted from overseas. Company failures in the \textbf{1960s }in
\par
Australia had produced a confrontation between the public and the accounting
\par
profession.108 As a result the profession could no longer maintain views they held
\par
so firmly prior to the \textbf{1960s. }They could no longer maintain that public confusion
\par
about accounting reports was due to their non-understanding of the `technical
\par
limitations of accounts'; and that the profession's problem was merely one of
\par
`educating the public'. The public \textbf{\emph{was }}now aware of the technical limitations of
\par
accounts; company failures had educated them, where the profession had failed.
\par
It was not non-understanding of the `nature of accounts' that confused, but the
\par
nature of accounts. The public now understood the view that accountants held of
\par
their function - and did not accept it.
\par
By the end of the \textbf{1960s }the profession was aware that the public did not
\par
accept their view of their function; that what was in question was `stewardship',
\par
`historical truth', the `cost basis of accounts'. The profession had never confronted
\par
this situation before. Accountants had never been faced with direct questioning
\par
of their practice from the public they served. Indeed they had never questioned
\par
such matters themselves. Hence, they were ill-equipped to respond. Silence did
\par
not stay their critics; rhetoric was seemingly ignored. Appeals to authority provided
\par
no answers; nor did assertion. Denial of the problem did not remove it. Attacks
\par
on critics, or insurance against negligence merely postponed the day of reckoning.
\par
Codification and dissemination of existing `best practice' was insufficient, as this
\par
very practice was in question. The responses that the profession had learned prior
\par
to the \textbf{1960s }were insufficient either to quiet its critics, or to resolve the problem
\par
- at least, while company failures persisted.
\par
What was required was a new type of response - critical research into the
\par
profession's standards, into all that accountants had traditionally done, into the
\par
`tenets of professional faith'. Is it any wonder that the profession's response in
\par
this regard was negligible? Is it any wonder that the profession has delayed, and
\par
is delaying?
\par
A commentator at the \textbf{1949 }congress remarked: `Perhaps a few good local
\par
scandals might have stirred us out of our lethargy?'lw The profession was
\par
`stirred' by the company failures of the \textbf{1960s; }but what if the `scandals' were to
\par
cease?
