\par
Response of the Australian Accounting
\par
Profession to Company Failure in the 196Osl
\par
Accountants have weathered many storms in their progress towards professional
\par
status. From their nineteenth century beginnings in insolvency and bankruptcy,
\par
they have demonstrated their capacity to extend their range of tasks, develop new
\par
skills, and widen their technical competence. Witness, for example, developments
\par
in cost and management accounting, taxation consulting, management consulting.
\par
But from time to time financial failures, fraud, and legal proceedings have raised
\par
doubts in the mind of the public, not only about the technical skills of individual
\par
accountants, but about the aims and standards of accountants as a body, about
\par
the professionalism of accountants.
\par
Perhaps more than any other factor, company failures have tested accountants'
\par
claims to professional status, their capacity to respond to criticism, the quality of
\par
their organization and the rationale of their various practices. In the United
\par
Kingdom in the early \textbf{1930s, }the Royal Mail case was followed by considerable
\par
questioning by accountants about the desirability of explicit, common professional
\par
standards; by the establishment of committees of various accounting Institutes to
\par
consider the question; and in \textbf{1942 }by the publication of \textbf{\emph{Recommendations on}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{Accounting Principles }}by the Institute of Chartered Accountants in England and
\par
Wales? In the United States, the financial failures \textbf{of }the \textbf{1930s }and the scandal
\par
associated with the Kreuger, McKesson and Robbins, and other cases, were
\par
followed by substantial restatement by the American Institute of Accountants of
\par
earlier promulgations \textbf{on }the veriiication of financial statements; by a statement of
\par
the broad principles of financial statement preparation and presentation; and in
\par
\textbf{1939 }by the establishment of a full-time research staff (May, p. \textbf{143). }By the
\par
early \textbf{1940s }accountants \textbf{in }both countries were organizing themselves to answer
\par
public criticism as a body and, if possible, to anticipate and avoid such criticism
