\par
by developing and promulgating common standard\textasciicircum.\textasciicircum{} If criticism were to come,
\par
then it would be directed to the adequacy of the standards, not to their non-
\par
existence or non-codification; it would be directed to the profession, not to
\par
individual practitioners unless they abrogated professional standards. Accountants
\par
had responded to company failures in a way which had enhanced their claim to
\par
professional status.
\par
To this time, accountants \textbf{in }Australia had not encountered such spectacular
\par
abuses of the corporate form as had been experienced overseas. Perhaps this was
\par
why Australian accountants had shown relatively little interest in developing and
\par
enforcing standards for the presentation of financial statements. The Institute of
\par
Chartered Accountants in Australia published their \textbf{\emph{Recommendations on Account-}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{ing Principles }}in \textbf{1946, }but these had no authoritative force for the many other
\par
Australian accounting associations, nor indeed for members of the Institute. It
\par
seems that the recommendations were not widely followed in practice.4 However,
\par
in \textbf{1949 }a number of accountants recognized that there was confusion both within
\par
and without the profession as to the standards which were to govern practice, and
\par
that it was incumbent upon the accounting profession to take steps to resolve the
\par
confusion, It was time for a `Revaluation of Accounting'.
\par
In the \textbf{1960s }accountants in Australia were to experience company failures of
\par
great dimension; their practices and standards were to be called in question
\par
publicly and in law, as was their professional status. How, and how well, the
\par
profession would respond would largely depend upon the ideas and the organization
\par
it had developed in the \textbf{1950s. }How well it could respond would be determined to
\par
a great extent by the way it had geared itself for change and challenge, to the
\par
dynamism of its organizations and to the flexibility and openness of its modes of
\par
thinking.
\par
The character of the professional response is the subject of this paper.
