\par
One concern permeated the discussion at the Congress - how to enhance the
\par
professional status of accountants? The answers ran: identify and clearly state \textbf{an}
\par
area of professional responsibility; initiate social change in the public interest
\par
within this area (by submissions on tax and company law reform, for example);
\par
respond to changed social needs quickly, immediately if possible; establish, con-
\par
tinually examine, and improve the standards of practice, conduct, and competence
\par
of accountants. The promulgation of authoritative standards was seen to be a
\par
necessary condition of professional status;* the dynamism of accountants, in
\par
initiating, responding, adapting, the \textbf{key }to enhancing this s t a t \textasciitilde{} s . \textasciitilde{} Continued
\par
confidence by the public was seen as the essential prerequisite for maintaining
\par
professional status.10 By the above actions public confidence in accountants would
\par
be maintained, and there would be little need, or possibility, of legal encroachment
\par
on the profession's domain.
\par
