\par
general acceptance. Accountants were to `lead the way'.*1
\par
It was realized that some organizational `machinery' would be necessary to
\par
develop proposed standards, to establish general acceptance of standards, and to
\par
evaluate and revise existing standards. A Committee, representative of the whole
\par
of the profession, was suggested as the means for developing and revising standards.
\par
This Committee was to be supported by expert and adequate research staff, as
\par
`intensive research . . . cannot satisfactorily be conducted by small committees of
\par
practitioners' (Fitzgerald, p. \textbf{26). }General acceptance was to be attained by
\par
educative activity - by publication of proposed standards and critiques of them,
\par
by the formation of discussion groups, by a `pooling of techniques and ideas'
\par
(Trigg, p. \textbf{80). }In Australia `sufficiently rapid development and acceptance of
\par
accounting standards and their continued revision, will require a pooling of the
\par
mental and material resources of accounting institutes and teachers of accounting'
\par
(Fitzgerald, p. \textbf{26).}
\par
Discussion on the types of standards needed ranged widely: from the level of
\par
educational qualification needed to seek entry to the profession (university matricu-
\par
lation seemed to be the agreed level) ; to the registration of accountants as a public
\par
attestation of competence; to the question of the need for `higher intellectual` as
\par
distinct from `practical' training of accountants (opinion was divided as to whether
\par
a university education was necessary or even desirable); to the character of the
\par
ethical standards which should guide professional practice. In particular, it was
\par
noticed that no general standards of auditing practice had been codified or
\par
promulgated, and it was suggested that this deficiency be remedied `for \textbf{our }infor-
\par
mation and protection' (K. N. Stonier, Proceedings, p. \textbf{82).}
\par
As to standards of accounting practice, it was generally agreed that \textbf{\emph{the }1946}
\par
Recommendations of the Institute of Chartered Accountants in Australia provided
\par
an excellent base from which to develop standards. Criticisms of these recom-
\par
mendations at the Congress were mainly terminological, and were thought to be
\par
easily remedied - most `objections would possibly be met if the recommendations
\par
were reviewed by the Institute of Chartered Accountants in Australia in the light
\par
of the discussions at this Congress' (C. W. \textbf{M. }Court, Proceedings, p. \textbf{41). }There
\par
was considerable satisfaction expressed with the standards that had been developed
\par
for annual accounting reports - `when . . . pronouncements are issued [on stock-
\par
taking procedures, valuations and terminology] \textbf{and }are carried into effect . . . the
\par
balance sheet will be elevated to a stage where the prospect of further improve-
