`Towards historical truth'! There was unanimous agreement that accounting
\par
was `an historical record of stewardship', that the substantive basis of this record
\par
was `cost', that accounting reports are `historical in character'.'Z Before the
\par
congress, Yorston had written that he expected speakers to explain that `cost is
\par
the important accounting figure. That cost is significant. That cost is essential
\par
in matching the revenues of one accounting period with the costs incurred in that
\par
accounting period. Cost is necessary to ascertain amounts to be carried forward
\par
(in asset accounts) and matched against the revenues of future periods. We will
\par
have explained to us how the accounting record is mainly an historical record
\par
of facts and that financial policy is quite a distinct process'.l3 This \textbf{was }explained,
\par
\textbf{\emph{and }}affirmed at the Congress.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Consolidation }}- \textbf{\emph{the 1950s}}
\par
`The Congress will ``set the course'' of accounting in this country for many years
\par
to come' (Yorston, p. \textbf{\emph{76). }}And so it did.
\par
The sentiment expressed at the Congress was that the profession should `speak
\par
with one voice'.14 The proposed Committee on Standards was one expression of
\par
this sentiment, and joint activities by some of the participating bodies were planned
\par
and eventually undertaken. In \textbf{1952 }two of the sponsoring bodies, the Common-
\par
wealth Institute of Accountants and the Federal Institute of Accountants, merged
\par
as the Australian Society of Accountants, and in \textbf{1953 }were joined by the
\par
Association of Accountants of Australia. In \textbf{1956 }the Australasian Institute of Cost
\par
Accountants became affiliated with the Society \textbf{as }a specialist and `post-graduate'
\par
body.15 By \textbf{1960 }the Society and the Institute of Chartered Accountants in Aus-
\par
tralia were the `spokesmen' for the profession. The profession then had `two voices',
\par
not many.
