Steps were taken to implement suggestions at the Congress about the need for
\par
educative activity between members, for a `pooling of ideas'. Study groups, meet-
\par
ings, lectures and `family circle' discussions were organized. The journals of each
\par
body were advertised as a forum for debate. There is little doubt that these
\par
activities met a real need. But a perusal of lists of discussion topics reveals that they
\par
dealt with procedural or technical matters; they involved a transfer of experiences.
\par
Where the activities dealt with `standards', they were concerned with dissemination
\par
of the official view of each body as \textbf{to }`best practice', rather than critical evaluation
\par
of the standards themselves. Attention was concentrated on `establishing' standards
\par
in the minds of members \textbf{as }a basis for `general acceptance'.16
\par
Both bodies established part-time research committees immediately after the
\par
\textbf{1949 }Congress. The Institute committee produced a draft set of recommendations
\par
on `standards' of auditing practice, and these were published by the Institute in
\par
\textbf{195 1 }as \textbf{\emph{Recommendations on General Principles of Professional Auditing Practice.}}
\par
In \textbf{1953, }the Society committee published an interim research report on `Accounting
\par
Terminology'.l7 The Society regarded `endowed lectures' \textbf{as }research ventures; the
\par
Institute labelled its discussion groups as `research societies'. In \textbf{1954 }the Society
\par
decided to appoint a `full-time research officer and editor', and the Institute was
\par
`giving thought' to establishing a Research and Service Foundation with a full-time
\par
research director. By \textbf{1960 }the Foundation had been established, but had not
\par
retained a full time research director.
\par
In \textbf{1956 }the Society began to produce `Statements on Accounting' and `Tech-
\par
nical Bulletins'. In \textbf{1957 }the Institute produced its first `Research and Service
\par
Foundation Bulletin'. Only three of these publications bore upon previously pro-
\par
mulgated `accounting or auditing standards'; and then they were more extensions
\par
or reinforcements of existing standards, than revisions or evaluations.18 The others
