To this The Accountant responded, editorially:
\par
With great respect to the learned Judge, we think it should be emphasised that the
\par
cotlception of the balance sheet as `an estimate of value' in the sense implied, is not
\par
the conceptioxi of the accountancy profession or of business men or of the Stock
\par
Exchange, or indeed of Parliament, which has \textbf{so }recently laid down its requirement
\par
of a `true and fair view of the state of affairs of a company' . . . We recognise the
\par
layman's difficulty in appreciating the function of the balance sheet, but as long ago
\par
as January \textbf{1945, }the Council of the Institute, in a widely published recommendation,
\par
recorded the long-accepted principle that the amount at which fixed assets are shown
\par
in the balance sheet does not purport \textbf{to }be their realisable value or their replacement
\par
value, but is normally an historical record of their cost less amounts provided in
\par
respect of depreciation, amortisation or depletion.26
\par
Some suggestions were made that `values' in balance sheets be dated - so that
\par
no one could fail to understand that the numbers represented a past cost, albeit
\par
modified in some ways, and not a present price (Parker and Newbery, p. 223).
\par
But during the \textbf{1950s }no reconsideration of the functions of accounting or auditing
\par
was countenanced. Further education of the public was still deemed necessary.27
\par
Some practitioners noted that the view of the public about representations in
\par
accounting reports was: `the conventional historical cost basis for accounting does
\par
not exhibit a true and correct view where the fixed assets are shown at Gost with
\par
no indication as to their current real values'. But they responded that there would
\par
be no `material advantage' derived from successive revaluations.28 The few prac-
\par
titioners who attempted to analyze more deeply the `fundamental concepts of
\par
accounting' generally demonstrated an unquestioning acceptance of `stewardship' as
\par
the key idea. For example, Irish wrote:
\par
. . . accounting finds its genesis in the need to record financial relationships, in the
\par
duties of financial stewardship. Whatever else we may hope to achieve through the
\par
science of accounting, we cannot hope to displace this as a fundamental truth.
\par
The balance sheet is designed to state in summary form how funds \textbf{so }received have
\par
been expended at a particular accounting date.
\par
It is more compatible with the duties intrinsic in stewardship to conceive the balance
\par
sheet in these terms than it is to regard it as a statement of assets and liabilities.
\par
As soon as we try to justify the balance sheet \textbf{as }a statement of financial condition
\par
or regard it as assessing the worth of a business we are pushed into absurd and
\par
unreal theories. On the other hand, if we confine it to the concept of a `receipts and
\par
disposal of funds statement' we can carry our logic through to a consistent
\par
conclusion .29
