\par
Receivers were appointed to the Reid Murray group; during court hearings it
\par
was said that debenture holders were owed about \textbf{\$59 }million, against total group
\par
assets of \textbf{\$133 }million \textbf{\emph{(The Age, }18 }December \textbf{1962). }By January \textbf{1963 }there
\par
was speculation as to whether the group's assets were `worth anything like that
\par
amount' \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }15 }January \textbf{1963). }In February, Reid Murray Holdings' 220-odd
\par
subsidiaries started releasing financial statements; they revealed large losses after
\par
heavy write-downs of real estate, inventory, and write-offs of provisions for bad
\par
debts. By April, it was announced that the Victorian operations of Reid Murray
\par
would be investigated \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }9 }April \textbf{1963); }other states followed suit. In May,
\par
a winding-up order was made in respect of Reid Murray Holdings (The \textbf{\emph{Age, }17}
\par
May \textbf{1963). }Consolidated statements for the group revealed a loss for the year
\par
ended \textbf{31 }August \textbf{1961 of }more than \textbf{\$11.5 }million - `the largest deficit ever
\par
reported by an Australian Company'; at this stage the auditor's report claimed that
\par
these financial statements did not give a `true and fair view' \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }1 }August
\par
\textbf{1963).}
\par
In July, \textbf{1963 }an editorial in \textbf{\emph{The Chartered Accountant in Australia }}was again
\par
concerned with the prospects of the Australian economy - and the share market.
\par
The mood was again one of optimism:
\par
Prices will be assisted in coming months by a generally \textbf{good }crop of company
\par
profit results. Moreover, this year, there will be far fewer shocks for investors in
\par
companies of the calibre of Reid Murray, Latec, Stanhill and \textbf{so on }(p. 2).
