In December, there was a further crop of company losses. The twelfth of
\par
December was described in the press as `Black Thursday'. `Yesterday was one of
\par
the darkest days in Australia's financial history. \textbf{Six }public companies, including
\par
three in the Australian Factors group, revealed losses totalling more \textbf{than [\$7.2}
\par
million].' \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }}13 December \textbf{1963.) }Latec revealed further losses of \textbf{\$4.6}
\par
million. Chevron Sydney Ltd reported further losses of \textbf{\$1 }million. The Australian
\par
Factors group losses were \textbf{\$1 }million. Neon Signs (Australasia) Ltd disclosed a
\par
loss of \$0.5 million - \textbf{six }weeks after the company's chairman had indicated that
\par
he expected a small profit \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }}30 October \textbf{1963).}
\par
Following these reports of company losses, public criticism began to be directed
\par
at the accounting profession. Some criticisms were cautiously expressed:
\par
\textbf{One point that does emerge clearly from these results is that several companies con-}
\par
\textbf{cerned have very misleading statements of profits in the previous year's accounts and}
\par
\textbf{in interim statements last year. \emph{( A F R , }}22 \textbf{January }1963)
\par
Other were more extravagant:
\par
. . . \textbf{there must have been collusion between directors, accountants and auditors to}
\par
\textbf{what amounts to fraud.}
\par
. . . \textbf{chartered accountants in their endorsement of annual reports declared that the}
\par
\textbf{figures were fair and reasonable, when, in fact, they were not.}
\par
\textbf{There should be a Federal investigation into the whole matter with a view to intro-}
\par
\textbf{ducing legislation to protect the public against ruthless financial manipulators.}
\par
\textbf{\emph{( A F R , }Letters, }29 \textbf{January }1963)
\par
An academic was reported as describing all investment as `gambling' in `this
\par
current state of affairs'. `No accountant or skilled investment dealer (and therefore
\par
investor) could tell from present balance sheets whether they represented the facts
\par
per se or the method of presentation' \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }19 }March \textbf{1963).}
\par
Stock exchange officials claimed that `poor accounting . . . brought about these
\par
situations' \textbf{\emph{(AFR, }22 }January \textbf{1963). A }financial journalist stated that ` ``a ready
\par
acceptance of responsibility to the community'' has been lacking in too many
\par
accounting places in the recent period of company distresses' (J. C. Horsfall, The
\par
\textbf{\emph{Bulletin, }27 }July 1963).
\par
The profession responded with silence. There were no public statements, nor
\par
even comments in the professional journals. One writer later referred to `the
\par
seeming desire of the professional body to ride out the storm, to ignore the
\par
plight of shareholders, to avoid answering the implications concerning auditing
\par
standards' (The \textbf{\emph{Bulletin, }27 }July \textbf{1963).}
\par
The first official expression of concern did not come until July \textbf{1963. }The N.S.W.
\par
Division of the Institute conducted its annual conference - ironically, in a beach-
\par
resort hotel built by Latec. One session was devoted to an examination of com-
\par
pany failures. A paper was presented by R. A. Irish, titled `Should we Blame \textbf{the}
\par
Auditing Profes\textasciitilde ion?'.3\textasciitilde{} Irish had recently been appointed \textbf{an }inspector of Latec,
\par
and was the author of a text long prescribed for the Institute examinations on
\par
\textbf{\emph{32. The Chartered Accountant in Australia, }August 1963, pp. 79-96.}
\par
