\textbf{\emph{The response}}
\par
The losses incurred through company failures in the \textbf{1960s }were impressive.
\par
Reid Murray had been described as the fourth-largest listed company in Australia
\par
in \textbf{1961p' }its subsequent losses totalled \textbf{\$47.5 }million, and would have been well
\par
in excess of \textbf{\$60 }million if one took account of unpaid debenture interest (Final
\par
Report, pp. \textbf{70-1). }The Stanhill group losses were assessed at \textbf{\$47.5 }million (Third
\par
and Final Report, \textbf{p. 69), }Latec reported losses totalling \textbf{\$11 }million, and the
\par
accumulated losses in H. G. Palmer recently reached \textbf{\$53 }million (AFR, \textbf{27}
\par
January \textbf{1971).}
\par
The impact of these losses was felt throughout the community: at the time of
\par
its crash, there were 80,000 investors in Reid Murray alone \textbf{\emph{(The }}Bulletin, 8
\par
December \textbf{1969). }A considerable proportion of the losses was borne by those who
\par
had invested in fixed interest securities; \textbf{\$16.2 }million in the Reid Murray group
\par
(Final Report, p. \textbf{70), \$13 }million in the Stanhill group (Third and Final Report,
\par
p. \textbf{69), }and more than \textbf{\$17 }million in H. G. Palmer.93 Many more investors lost on
\par
the falling market in the companies' shares: the `market capitalisation' of Reid
