\par
Many of the failures followed hard on the heels of the publication of audited
\par
financial statements depicting a profitable past and an apparently sound present.
\par
The inspectors' reports later documented breakdowns in accountability. Many of
\par
the failed companies had been in a state of crisis for some considerable period prior
\par
to their ultimate collapse. Their financial statements had not only failed to inform
\par
investors - they had also been misleading. These financial statements had been
\par
prepared by accountants, signed by auditors. In the public's eye they were the
\par
responsibility of the accounting profession.
\par
It was not surprising, then, that the accounting profession and its practices
\par
were publicly criticized. The critics were not only `disgruntled investors', and
\par
ex-investors, but stockbrokers and financial journalists - persons who presumably
\par
had some knowledge of the securities industry and financial reporting. They pointed
\par
to a vast gulf between the public's expectations and the profession's performance.
\par
Accountants were quick to appreciate that their claim to professional status was
\par
in question. Indeed, they were not reluctant to dramatize the situation - the
\par
auditor was `in the spotlight', the profession was undergoing a `trial by fire'. A
\par
need was seen to `improve the status of the profession', to restore the profession's
\par
`image'. But while accountants recognized that the string of financial disasters had
\par
`focused attention on the profession', they were slow to apprehend the nature of
\par
problems confronting them.
\par
Initially the problem was seen to be the transgression or poor judgment of
\par
individual members. The attitude was taken that there was `one bad apple in every
\par
barrel'; that despite some public criticism, the profession as a whole was not
\par
deserving of opprobrium.'' A need for `effective disciplinary controls over mem-
\par
bers' was recognized. And there was a fear that the government might intervene if
\par
the profession showed itself incapable or unwilling `to maintain high professional
\par
standards'. Both the Institute and the Society affirmed that they would take
\par
effective disciplinary action against members `where the circumstances warrant and
\par
the evidence permits'.95 The most usual `circumstances' leading to the imposition
\par
of professional sanctions were either infringements of domestic rules regarding
\par
payment of subscriptions and the solicitation of work, or the commission of
\par
criminal acts where the `evidence' was provided in the course of litigation. But
\par
there seemed to be a growing feeling that professional controls should extend
\par
further, to govern the application of accounting or auditing `standards'. The
\par
