gent and courageous', and formed `one of the few thoroughly responsible sections
\par
of our community' (J. \textbf{\emph{G. }}A. Tucker, p. 377). Members of the public were assured,
\par
in the profession's journals, that `their interests were being safeguarded', that they
\par
could `confidently rely' on the reports of the profession. But the profession made no
\par
public statement in response to its critics and initiated no moves to enlighten the
\par
community into the mysteries of accounting.
\par
If the Australian public became aware that accounting reports are not always
\par
what they seem to be, it was not through a programme of education, but bitter
\par
experience.
\par
Publication of the Society's report in \textbf{1966 }was the most tangible evidence of
\par
recognition by the profession that company failures were a phenomenon warranting
\par
detailed research. Even so, the Society report avoided discussing the suggestions
\par
that accounting reports could be irrelevant and misleading. The problem had been
\par
prejudged as one of `narrowing the differences of opinion', of securing uniformity
\par
in the application of particular techniques. The `problem of accounting standards',
\par
the `broad issues of principle' were referred to a research committee and deferred
\par
for the Research Foundation. Neither body has responded to date.
\par
When the Society's report was written, it would appear that the second Reid
\par
Murray and Stanhill reports, the Latec report and the Collier Moat report were
\par
unavailable. Since January \textbf{1966 }there have been reports on Neon Signs (A'asia) ,
\par
Savoy Corporation, and further reports on Reid Murray and Stanhill. Other
\par
material has been widely publicized regarding the revealed losses of H. G. Palmer,
\par
Federated Industries, and A. G. Healing. No further examination of this material
\par
has been published.
\par
The Society's \textbf{1966 }publication considered criticisms of inspectors regarding the
\par
adoption of `particular techniques'. Criticisms of `specific principles' were also
\par
considered in a number of articles published in professional journals, or at con-
\par
ferences. The Society issued a brief pronouncement concerning the treatment of
\par
debtors, and commenced a new series of Bulletins with a discussion of the treat-
\par
ment of land development projects.98 The Research Foundation issued a booklet
\par
concerned with hire-purchase accounting, which, however, was devoted to pro-
\par
cedural matters, the matters of principle apparently being regarded as settled.99
\par
The only evidence that a systematic examination of the criticisms contained in
\par
inspectors' reports was in progress came as late as \textbf{1967 }when the Institute's
\par
annual report noted that the reports were `studied by [a] special sub-committee of
\par
each State Council' - in the context of possible disciplinary action against
\par
members.
\par
Towards the end of the \textbf{1960s }there were indications of a growing awareness by
\par
members of the profession that the problem confronting them went deeper than `a
