standards'. When the \textbf{1960s }ended, some accountants, at least, were openly
\par
stating that published accounting reports had indeed been misleading.
\par
But what problems relating to these `standards' were identified? There were
\par
calls for a `spirit of disclosure'. Revision of many `terms and phrases' was said to
\par
be long overdue. Proposals, similar to those of the \textbf{1950s, }for `plain English'
\par
reports, and for more `disclosure of details', such as the techniques used to arrive
\par
at a particular asset measure, were canvassed. The profession had long accepted
\par
that `terminology' and `presentation' could be improved.
\par
At the same time, there was much expression of dissatisfaction with statutory
\par
requirements for minimal disclosure; there was a need for the law to `strengthen
\par
the Companies Act'. Such suggestions were not seen by the profession \textbf{as }an
\par
admission of failure to `lead the way' in financial reporting - or to involve an
\par
effective abdication of the responsibility it had so jealously claimed in \textbf{1949. }The
\par
initiation of reforms was largely left to the lawyer-inspectors, the profession's
\par
submissions on new legislation being limited to giving `comments and guidance on
\par
practical matters \textbf{in }which their members have experience'. The profession took
\par
the view that it was not their function `to lay down matters of policy'.1\textasciitilde{} Even
\par
where members had `experience' of apparent anomalies which were clearly and
\par
solely within the accountants' domain, the profession displayed a remarkable
\par
tardiness.lOl A case in point was the problem of auditors of consolidated statements.
\par
The inspectors' reports described the difficulties confronting some auditors, but not
\par
until \textbf{1965 }did the profession make any attempt to meet this problem, and then
\par
only by `urging' members to `assist'. There was no concerted campaign for reform.
\par
There was no apparent research into the problem.
\par
A feature of the period \textbf{1949-69 }is the type of research conducted by the
\par
profession, and the idea which the profession seems to have had about the nature
\par
of `research'. Certainly the research conducted during the \textbf{1950s }did not involve
\par
revising, refining, or improving accounting or auditing standards. Yet such research
