a `public relations exercise'. The Accounting and Auditing Research Committee
\par
conducted the opinion poll - `What's Wrong with Financial Statements', and a
\par
`Survey of Accountancy Research in Australia'.103 The staff of the Foundation
\par
wrote several articles reporting happenings overseas.104 And after \textbf{1970 }the
\par
Foundation published a book codifying existing accounting rules.1{$^\circ$}5 But the
\par
Foundation was not used to investigate the accounting implications of company
\par
failures, to undertake research into accounting and auditing standards, or indeed,
\par
into any social problem within the profession's domain. The Foundation was not
\par
even used in adapting United Kingdom Recommendations to Australian legisla-
\par
tion.106 The activities undertaken by the Foundation were in accord with one of its
\par
aims - `the consolidation and dissemination \textbf{\emph{of }}extant accounting and auditing
\par
principles'. The other aim of the Foundation - `research into the refinement \textbf{\emph{of}}
\par
extant principles and unresolved problems of accounting and auditing' - received
\par
no attention. Either, as in the \textbf{1 9 5 0 \textasciitilde{} \textasciitilde{} }the profession had confused dissemination
\par
with research; or they had no idea of what to research, or how. What else could
\par
explain such vacillation?lo7
