A new administrative agency has vast opportunities to exer-
\par
cise creative intelligence in its initial decisions. These deci-
\par
\textbf{May 1970 Vol. 38 No. }4\emph{The Washington Lawyer}
\par
sions, made by the handful of men and women who comprise
\par
the initial staff, reverberate through time and space in a tidal
\par
wave of procedural and substantive consequences. They
\par
translate legislation into concrete programs to bring the law
\par
to bear on social or economic activities of men in society. The
\par
administrators grapple with problems which were not ad-
\par
dressed by the legislature, where ideological, theoretical and
\par
even practical implications are not clear. Their decisions are
\par
often taken in an atmosphere where the choice is "free,"
\par
where judgment is unencumbered by immediate political pres-
\par
sure or judicial precedent, where administrators can either be
\par
creative or cautious and where the larger forces of the bu-
\par
reaucracy and the courts will support their decisions, what-
\par
ever they may be.
\par
The potential scope of administrative creativity in a new
\par
agency makes it important to understand these "low visibil-
\par
ity" decisions which shape the agency, the law and the social
\par
problem which brought the agency into being. I was privi-
\par
leged, between 1965 and 1967 to be a part of this process, in
\par
the formation of the Equal Employment Opportunity Com-
\par
mission,1 first as a consultant, then as Chief of Liaison with
\par
Federal and State Agencies, as Chief of Conciliations and
\par
again as a consultant after I returned to academia. The
\par
EEOC, a five member body created by Title VII of the Civil
\par
Rights Act of 1964, struggles with discrimination in employ-
\par
ment because of race, color, religion, national origin and sex.
\par
It may investigate, determine if reasonable cause exists to
\par
believe there is discrimination and attempt to conciliate. If
