\par
of present legal education will be better equipped as lawyers
\par
if they can focus sharply on the problem of how we can best
\par
fulfill the purposes which brought the agency into being
\par
rather than on the question of whether the courts will sus-
\par
tain this course of action. The latter question is, of course,
\par
important. Administrative processes must be confined to the
\par
channels marked by due process and legislative intent. How-
\par
ever, these channels are often much wider than the lawyer's
\par
trained myopia permits him to see. Creative administration
\par
requires as wide a view of alternatives as legal limits permit
\par
and judgments between alternatives which are fitted to the
\par
task before the agency.
\par
My first direct involvement with administrative decision-
\par
making in the field of racial discrimination came in 1963 when
\par
I became a consultant to the New Jersey Civil Rights Com-
\par
mission. Its Chairman, Mr. Sidney Reitman, was critical of
\par
the work of the Division on Civil Rights, which enforced the
\par
anti-discrimination laws of the state. Utilizing my Rutgers
\par
Law School class in Social Legislation, I conducted a compre-
\par
hensive study of the Division. I found that it typified admin-
\par
istrative caution and ineptness at every turn-its procedures
\par
were incredibly sloppy, it narrowly construed a statute which
\par
the courts were prepared to construe broadly, it did not se-
\par
cure relief for the complainants or for the general class of vic-
\par
tims of discrimination. It was a failure.'
\par
In order to reach these conclusions, I developed a method
