by which to evaluate the performance of the agency. I
\par
developed a mental image, a model of how the state agency
\par
\emph{should }operate in order to have maximum impact on the prob-
\par
lems of discrimination. Into this model went the policy of
\par
maximum enforcement, the "liberal construction" of the sub-
\par
stantive provisions of the law and the evaluation of proce-
\par
dures to determine if they really implemented the policy or if
\par
other procedures would be more likely to effect this end.
\par
Applying this analysis, which is related to Programming-
\par
Planning-Budgeting (PPB) and systems analysis, but with a
\par
lawyer's bent,4 I concluded that the New Jersey agency
\par
should have broadly construed its powers and then utilized
\par
them in a tough-minded, effective enforcement program.
\par
Many of my criticisms scored the obvious lack of administra-
\par
tive energy and inventiveness. For example, the Division
\par
refused to apply the statute broadly enough to achieve
\par
legislative objectives, insisting on a series of legislative
\par
amendments instead. Thus, the problem of discrimination
\par
remained in the legislative rather than the administrative
\par
stage, as it had for a quarter of a century. This pattern
\par
could not achieve social change. As long as the administra-
\par
tion could "pass the buck" back to the legislature, the law
\par
was not being enforced. Civil rights groups accepted the
\par
premise that the statute required amendment and trouped
\par
with frustrating regularity to the state legislative halls for
\par
amendments to achieve what should have been accomplished
\par
by aggressive administration. The administrators, mean-
\par
while, had an excuse for non-performance.
\par
My New Jersey study convinced me of the futility of this
\par
game and of the importance of energetic administration. In
\par
a sense, the New Jersey Supreme Court had been calling for
\par
creative administration, but there was no response from the
\par
agency. In fact, the administrator took a defensive attitude
\par
toward my work and sought to have it suppressed or modified.
\par
Several years later, during the "black power" days of 1968,
