\par
The study was made public, along with recommendations
\par
for vigorous enforcement of the existing law and the use of
\par
creative administration of its provisions. The study came to
\par
the attention of Tom Powers, the Executive Assistant to the
\par
Secretary of Labor, when he came to Newark to consult us
\par
concerning problems of racial discrimination encountered in
\par
the construction of the new Rutgers Law School. He invited
\par
me to spend part of my forthcoming sabbatical year helping
\par
to set up the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission, of
\par
which he was to be the first Executive Director. I decided to
\par
spend six months with the Commission and the rest of the
\par
year writing on a Caribbean Island. I spent two years in
\par
Washington, contracted a lingering case of Potomac fever,
\par
never had my sabbatical in the sun, but did have the oppor-
\par
tunity to apply myself, through the EEOC, to the most press-
\par
ing domestic problem of our time.
\par
When I arrived in Washington to deal with the federal-state
\par
relations of EEOC, I found total confusion. The Act had been
\par
signed on July 2, 1964, with a one year moratorium on its
\par
substantive provisions. This year of grace was supposed to
\par
allow for "voluntary compliance" and the organization of the
\par
principle agency, the Equal Employment Opportunity Com-
\par
mission. But President Johnson confounded this objective by
\par
taking a full eleven months of that year before he appointed
\par
the Commissioners. They, in turn, had to find a staff and be
\par
ready for business in one month. As a result, the initial inter-
\par
pretations of Title VII were made quickly and informally. In
\par
those early days, before major bureaucratic organization de-
\par
veloped, the staff worked very closely with the individual
\par
Commissioners.
\par
\emph{Creative Administration and Legislative Intent}
\par
Immediately following the swearing-in of the Commissioners,
\par
a task force of half a dozen borrowed lawyers began, in a
\par
flurry of activity, to develop procedures and to render initial\emph{Administrative Creativity}
\par
\textbf{WE GEORGE WASMNGTON }LAW \textbf{REVIEW}
\par
substantive interpretations. I joined the task force initially
\par
to deal with the federal-state relations problems under Title
\par
\textbf{VII, }but rapidly broadened my involvement to include all as-
\par
pects of the work of the task force. The task force attorneys
\par
had varying backgrounds and brought different attitudes to
\par
the conference table. One view was based on personal knowl-
\par
edge of the legislative debates and cloak room conversations
\par
which had surrounded the passage of the Act. Richard Berg,
\par
who would become Deputy General Counsel, had been in the
\par
Justice Department and acted as advisor to Senator Hum-
\par
phrey during the debates in the Senate. He saw the statutory
\par
language in the context of its original informal setting (or,
\par
more precisely, what he understood to be the original informal
\par
setting). He had a clear and personal understanding of the
\par
"legislative intent," based on his involvement in compromises
\par
made in the process of putting together the Civil Rights Act
\par
of 1964. The proposals he made and his reactions to the pro-
\par
posals of others were influenced \textbf{by }his concern with keeping
\par
the bargain which he understood had been struck in the legis-
\par
lature. The statute was a compromise, and Mr. Berg intended
\par
to be reasonably faithful to this compromise. As he saw it,
\par
"legislative intent" was not a standard for review of adminis-
\par
trative action, but a reality to be observed.'
\par
Berg's view was only one example of the prevailing attitude
\par
that Title VII had been a "defeat" for the civil rights move-
\par
ment. This conclusion was based on two occurrences: first,
\par
Congress had deleted the hearing and cease and desist powers,
\par
which the "liberals" had wanted for the \textbf{EEOC, }and left en-
\par
forcement to the courts; second, a series of amendments
\par
designed to allay the fears of the conservatives over the reach
\par
of the statute had been agreed to \textbf{by }the Democratic "leader-
\par
ship conference" as a part of the decisional process which
\par
broke a long filibuster and secured passage of the \textbf{bill. }But
\par
neither of these episodes warranted the broad negative con-
\par
