\par
provided for extensive federal court jurisdiction for enforce-
\par
ment purposes.
\par
In my New Jersey study, I had developed some basic atti-
\par
tudes toward the enforcement of anti-discrimination laws. As
\par
charters of equality, they should be broadly construed, with
\par
the administration initially interpreting the laws as broadly
\par
as possible, subject to judicial restraint. Applying this view
\par
to Title VII, the statutory language should be read against the
\par
postulate that it had been difficult to achieve any civil rights
\par
legislation and that the statute was a political compromise
\par
which recognized the need for federal action to bring employ-
\par
ment discrimination to an end. Therefore, it should be lib-
\par
erally construed, and procedures should be shaped to the
\par
needs of the minorities for whom the statute had been
\par
adopted. The objective was to maximize the effect of the
\par
statute on employment discrimination without going back to
\par
the Congress for more substantive legislation.
\par
The legislative history set limits beyond which administra-
\par
tors could not go in carrying out the statutory mandate, but
\par
it did not dictate the course of administration. One of the
\par
major functions of written legislative history is to force legis-
\par
lators to operate in the open. Informal "cloak room deals,"
\par
not embodied in statutory language, reports or debates, are
\par
simply not limiting factors shaping administrative interpre-
\par
tations. This view of legislative history requires the admin-
\par
istrator to develop ideas, policies and procedures which derive
\par
from an informed understanding of the dynamics of the social
\par
problem and the role of government in its resolution. The
\par
statute and its history both set the goal and define the limits
\par
available to the agency to reach that goal. But within these\emph{Administrative Creativity}
\par
\textbf{THE GEORGE WASHINGTON LAW REVIEW}
\par
confines, the agency is free to shape its own destiny with re-
\par
spect to the problem it is required to solve.
\par
As an administrator for \textbf{EEOC, }I attempted to bring my
\par
model of an aggressive and effective, agency into reality.
\par
Having a model gave me a great advantage in the early days,
\par
because, to a greater extent than most of the other staff mem-
\par
bers, I had a basis from which to propose and evaluate sub-
\par
stantive and procedural decisions. In addition, my study of
\par
the New Jersey agency had revealed certain pitfalls to be
\par
avoided. Of course, one should not confuse energy with wis-
\par
dom, and it was sometimes comforting to know that the
\par
agency's word was not final. The courts were available to
\par
prevent serious errors. However, most of the problems con-
\par
fronting the EEOC could be solved by creative interpretation
\par
of Title VII, which would be sustained by the courts, partly
\par
out of deference to the administrators.
\par
When I went to Washington, I shared the "liberal view"
\par
that the Commission was weak and had no power. We called
\par
it a toothless tiger. I later realized that there are many
\par
sources of power, and that the weakness of the Commission
\par
might, itself, become a strong point in solving employment
\par
discrimination problems. Experience with the workings of
\par
the statute and of the government in our pluralistic society
\par
convinced me that to give the Commission the additional
\par
statutory power which the liberals believed so important in
\par
1965 would not help-but would positively harm-the drive
\par
to end employment discrimination. A \emph{more powerful }Com-
\par
mission would become a captive of those interests which
\par
were to be regulated, while the existing weak institution ena-
\par
bled civil rights groups to use the federal courts which are
\par
favorable to their demands.
\par
\emph{Developing Procedures}
\par
The source materials with which we worked in developing
\par
procedures in those early days were minimal. The Bureau of
\par
National Affairs and Commerce Clearing House had each
\par
published excerpts from the legislative history and the various
\par
changes in statutory language which had been made as the\emph{The Washington Lawyer}
\par
legislation had worked its way through the Congress.' Berg
\par
had published his article. There was nothing else. The initial
\par
interpretations were made almost exclusively by the judgment
\par
of the individual lawyers involved, supported by arguments
\par
based on these limited materials. Thus, the interpretations
\par
were basically products of the various attorneys, shaped min-
\par
imally by extrinsic aids, and leavened by the policy orienta-
\par
tion of the Commissioners.
\par
Time was of the essence. The first set of rules was rushed
\par
through in two weeks to be ready for opening day, July 2,
\par
1965. These rules have been modified slightly, but they have
\par
stood up rather well, considering the haste with which they
\par
were drawn.' The form of the rules was primarily the work
\par
of Trial Examiner Bruce Hunt, who was lent to the Commis-
\par
sion by the National Labor Relations Board. His experience
\par
in race relations stretched back to the original President's
\par
Committee of the early 1940's.
\par
Those of us who believed that the procedures should be
\par
shaped for maximum enforcement had certain objectives with
\par
respect to the rules. They should be simple, leaving the
\par
heaviest burden on the Commission, rather than on the com-
\par
plainant. They should protect the complainant from risks of
\par
retaliation and should facilitate, rather than impede, the in-
\par
vestigative, finding and conciliation efforts of the Commis-
\par
sion. Some of these objectives turned out to be internally
\par
inconsistent when applied to situations which emerged as the
\par
Commission developed. For example, a totally informal pro-
\par
cedure would have speeded the handling of cases but was
\par
incompatible with the goal of obtaining decent settlements,
\par
which required a high degree of procedural formality.
\par
With mixed feelings, overshadowed by questions as to
\par
why the President delayed to the last minute in making
\par
the appointments to the Commission, the voyage of the Com-
\par
mission was launched by the staff. Despite the air of overall
