\emph{Avoiding the "In Writing and Under Oath" Requirements}
\par
When we sat around the table in Commissioner Samuel Jack-
\par
son's anteroom, one of the first questions was posed by the
\par
opening language of section 706(a) of the Title. It began:
\par
"Whenever it is charged in writing under oath by a person
\par
claiming to be aggrieved" the Commission should investi-
\par
gate, determine if there was reasonable cause, and then at-
\par
tempt to conciliate. Failing conciliation, the individual could
\par
sue, and the Commission could recommend that the Attorney
\par
General sue in the name of the United States.
\par
The first draft of proposed regulations required that a
\par
"complaint" be in writing and notarized. Some of us objected
\par
vigorously to these requirements. Our experience in race re-
\par
lations and knowledge of sociological studies of Negro atti-
\par
tudes toward law made it clear that it was difficult enough to
\par
get any significant number of minority group members to file
\par
complaints at all.8 If the Commission were to return com-
\par
plaints which were not sworn or tell people who made oral
\par
complaints to submit them in writing under oath, the clientele
\par
of the agency would feel rebuffed, and would reject the Com-
\par
mission as it had rejected other agencies.' In addition, in
\par
some parts of the South, it was difficult for a Negro to get a
\par
document notarized.
\par
Thus, we proposed that the Commission act on an informal
\par
statement whether it was in writing and notarized or not,
\par
stopping to comply with these requirements when appropriate.
\par
The burden of writing and notarizing would be on the Com-
\par
mission, not on the complainant. The majority of the drafts-
