\par
There were two policy reasons against deferring to state
\par
criminal laws. The first was the fundamental principle that
\par
the criminal law is not well suited to enforce social legislation,
\par
especially in an area of social tension and change. The norms
\par
of the criminal law require a narrow construction of the law,
\par
proof of intent and proof of facts beyond a reasonable doubt.
\par
Prosecutors won't prosecute, juries won't convict and the law
\par
is unenforced. Far better is a civil or administrative remedy
\par
which requires conformity to a broadly construed code of so-
\par
cial legislation rather than the inapposite and cumbersome
\par
process of the criminal law. Second, if there was deference
\par
by EEOC to the criminal law, some states could keep the
\par
Commission processes at bay and thus delay the day of reckon-
\par
ing when the individual sued in the federal courts. For both
