\emph{The Compliance Process}
\par
During late summer and early fall of 1965, as Chief of Liai-
\par
son, I dealt with a myriad of details concerning the develop-
\par
ment of the compliance procedures of the Commission. Again,\emph{The Washington Lawyer}
\par
the thinking began with my study of the New Jersey Division
\par
on Civil Rights. I had been shocked by the inadequacies of the
\par
settlements which the Division had reached, and concluded
\par
that this was at least in part due to the inadequate procedures
\par
followed by the Division. 8 One basic failure was the separa-
\par
tion of the investigative and the conciliatory functions. A
\par
Division representative would go out on a complaint and try
\par
to settle the case on the spot, generally accepting whatever
\par
settlement the respondent offered. Usually settlement con-
\par
sisted of a paper promise to obey the law, and rarely did it
\par
entail any relief for the complainant. I concluded that one
\par
reason for this situation was that the Division representative
\par
was told to talk settlement before there had been any finding
\par
that there was anything to settle. Thus, the respondent could
\par
say, "Okay, I deny there is any discrimination at all, but I'm
\par
willing to make some gesture to get rid of this case." And
\par
the Division representative, interested in a good record for
\par
himself, and empowered to reach a "satisfactory adjustment"
\par
would proceed to take the settlement.
\par
I was determined that the Commission should avoid these
\par
errors. Consequently, I found myself, for the first time in
\par
my academic life in the law, stressing the importance of pro-
\par
cedure. The statute provided for the filing of a charge, an
\par
investigation and a finding of reasonable cause to believe the
\par
charge to be true, followed by efforts at conciliation. The
\par
Commission adopted the principle of separation of these func-
\par
tions. As a result, the procedures for conciliation are separate
\par
from those for investigation, and the personnel who conciliate
\par
are different from those who investigate. Between the two
\par
processes is the issuance of a written decision that there is
\par
"reasonable cause" to believe the statute has been violated
\par
which is, in turn, a predicate for conciliation. Because of my
\par
New Jersey experience, I was quite doctrinaire on this subject
\par
of separation of functions and of the need for a written deci-
\par
sion on cause to believe there was discrimination.
\par
There were errors made in maintaining such a position-a
