\emph{The Reasonable Cause Concept}
\par
In building the compliance program we took the innocuous
\par
clause in the statute which provided that the Commission
\par
could conciliate after making a finding of reasonable cause,
\par
and elevated this reasonable cause decision to a high policy
\par
level. The decision was initially written by a Commissioner's
\par
assistant and circulated to the Commission. In some state
\par
agencies, the reasonable cause decision is made informally on
\par
the scene by an investigator. But in the Commission proce-
\par
dure, the decision is made in Washington by the Commission.
\par
Those of us who "upgraded" or "dignified" the Commission
\par
decision had several objectives in mind:
\par
First, we hoped to make a body of law regarding what con-
\par
stitutes a discrimination. The incredible laxity of state FEP
\par
agencies and their failure for nearly a quarter of a century to
\par
act for law enforcement meant that there was no detailed
\par
body of law in this area. Therefore, no firm guidance was
\par
given to employers, unions and government officials as to
\par
what situations had to be corrected. Under Title VII, the
\par
federal courts would interpret the Act, but it seemed likely
\par
that busy federal judges would pay considerable attention to
\par
the concept of discrimination developed by the Commission
\par
through the decisional process. The reasonable cause finding
\par
thus became a vehicle for development of the law with respect
\par
to discrimination.
\par
Second, after I became Chief of Conciliation, in October,
\par
1965, I became intensely aware of the function of the reason-\emph{The Washington Lawyer}
\par
able cause decision in laying a foundation for conciliation.
\par
The conciliators could use the cause finding as their factual
\par
premise and concentrate the discussion on remedies. For this
\par
purpose, the decision should appear to be a lawyer-like docu-
\par
ment, not a rambling exploration of issues. I drafted the
\par
model format for the reasonable cause finding with this in
\par
mind. Had I pressed the principle of the importance of law
\par
making, I might have stressed what lawyers like to call the
\par
"creasoning" in the cause finding; that is, the written demon-
\par
stration of the connection between the facts, the law and the
\par
result in the case.
\par
Yet, in this early stage of the Commission process, the im-
\par
portant thing was judgment, not reasoning. So I deliberately
\par
left the "reasoning" step out of the decision format. The
\par
format consists of: (a) summary of charge; (b) summary of
\par
facts found; and (c) conclusion of the reasonable cause issue. 4
\par
This was a conscious choice not to bog the EEOC down in its
\par
own precedents so early in the game. I think this was wise
\par
at the time, but it too had its price. The difficulty was that
\par
in the absence of expressed reasoning processes, it was very
\par
difficult for Commission personnel to operate. Investigators
\par
don't know what facts to look for, and decision writers don't
\par
know what to write. The only cure for this was some explica-
\par
