\par
mission deeply in the precedent making and interpreting
\par
process. Two years later, as I was leaving the Commission,
\par
I spent several months with the thousands of Commission
\par
decisions, and worked them into a 250 page manual which
\par
attempted to supply some of the links of reasoning, so that
\par
one original purpose of the elevation of the reasonable cause
\par
decision could be achieved. But by this time the federal
\par
courts were beginning to render decisions, and the entire chain
\par
of reasoning was in jeopardy because the Commission staff
\par
had only four or five lawyers, and thus could not regularly
\par
file amicus briefs on behalf of the Commission in all these
