\emph{The Concept of Conciliation}
\par
Finally, the entire conciliation concept was built around the
\par
reasonable cause finding. The concept that the Commission
\par
would seek a change in the system of discrimination rather
\par
than a "buy out" of the charging party was based on the use
\par
of the reasonable cause finding, not only as a foundation for
\par
protecting an individual complainant, but also as an indict-
\par
ment of company practices in general. For this purpose a
\par
written document from the Commission level was absolutely
\par
indispensable. Without such a document, the Commission
\par
staff was reduced to pitting its judgment against that of man-
\par
agement or union officials. With such a document, the staff
\par
was in a better negotiating posture because it had an official
\par
-though non-adversary-finding of discrimination as a pred-
\par
icate for conciliation discussions.
\par
This theory of the Commission procedures prevailed. 35 In-
\par
vestigations were undertaken, reasonable cause findings were
\par
made and, in September 1965, the Commission suddenly found
\par
that it was time to begin conciliating. Relatively few people
\par
on the Commission staff had practical experience in negotia-
\par
tion, conciliation, arbitration or any other informal procedure.
\par
With my background in labor arbitration and my experience
\par
in advising Rutgers in the handling of its race relations prob-
\par
lems, I was prepared for conciliation, at a time when no con-
\par
cept of what constituted conciliation existed. Conciliation ob-
\par
viously entails informal settlement short of litigation. But
\par
this leaves open a wide range of matters. What issues should
\par
be settled, on what terms and under what conditions? There
\par
was no body of experience in this area, because the state agen-
\par
