\par
cies and the federal government contracts enforcement pro-
\par
gram up to that time had been failures. Specifically, there
\par
was no experience under the formal procedures with a written
\par
reasonable cause decision because, as I have suggested, the
\par
use of such formal procedures was novel to the field.
\par
Stated another way: There are two models of "conciliation"
\par
of a race discrimination case. In one model, which was tra-
\par
ditional, the "conciliator" attempts to cool off the situation by
\par
the minimum steps necessary. He inevitably tries to push
\par
the weaker party toward the stronger to achieve at least a
\par
temporary settlement. He has nothing to do with litigation.
\par
A second model finds the conciliator as the "settling law-
\par
yer" in a process which could lead to a formal decision on the
\par
complainants' rights. Here the conciliator is operating at the
\par
stage prior to suit but after a preliminary determination of
\par
plaintiffs' rights \emph{(i.e., }the reasonable cause finding) has been
\par
made. Conciliation is, in this view, a preliminary part of a
\par
full blown litigation process designed by Congress. This lat-
\par
ter view is the one I adopted. In implementing it as Chief
\par
of Conciliations, I prepared a memorandum on the nature of
\par
Title VII conciliation which included the following:
\par
The nature of the conciliation process under Title VII must
\par
be explored in light of (the) statutory mandate. Conciliation,
\par
while highly developed in specialized fields of human endeavor,
\par
has no single fixed meaning. Its essentials vary depending on
\par
the circumstances to which it is applied, the attitudes of the
\par
parties towards the process, and the legal context within which
\par
it takes place. The minister who talks privately to his parish-
\par
ioners in the southern town about the end of discrimination,
\par
the marriage counsellor, trying to save a family, the repre-
\par
sentative from the Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service
\par
trying to avoid a strike, the constant scurryings of diplomats
\par
attempting to avoid international conflict, and the constant
\par
dealing of thousands of lawyers and others trying to settle
\par
problems without formal proceedings, all involve "conciliation."
\par
The urge to conciliate to avoid formal proceedings is almost as
\par
fundamental as the drive to establish such formal procedures
\par
in the first place. One might suggest that as soon as a pro-
\par
gram for formal settlement of disputes is established, those sub-
\par
ject to it promptly decide to avoid it and to settle matters in-
\par
formally in terms and under conditions which may be more\emph{The Washington Lawyer}
\par
to their liking.
\par
Since conciliation has no fixed meaning, the Commission must
\par
shape the concept of conciliation under Title VII. To assist in
\par
this process it may be useful to distinguish Title VII conciliation
\par
from two other related types of conciliation, conciliation under
\par
state fair employment practices laws, and under the National
\par
Labor Relations Act.
\par
1. Conciliation under the Labor Laws
\par
Conciliation under Title VII must necessarily differ in sev-
\par
eral respects from conciliations under the National Labor Re-
\par
lations Act. The legal context is sharply different. The objec-
\par
tive of national labor policy is industrial peace within the con-
\par
text of collective bargaining. The objective of labor conciliation
\par
is to bring the parties (who usually have relatively equal bar-
\par
gaining power) to an agreement which is acceptable for the
\par
limited duration of their contract. National labor policy seeks
\par
a solution acceptable to the parties, and little more. In fact,
\par
the original understanding under the Wagner Act was that the
\par
government was not to be concerned at all about the content
\par
of the collective bargain. In this legal context, conciliation con-
\par
sists of techniques by which the clearly expressed demands of
\par
the parties can be accommodated. The nature of the bargain
\par
struck by the parties is not the concern of the conciliator or the
\par
government.
\par
Conciliation under Title VII proceeds under a different legal
\par
mandate. It is aimed not only at satisfying the immediate de-
\par
mands of the parties, but at achieving compliance with the law.
\par
It therefore requires attention to the detailed terms of the solu-
\par
tion which is not necessary under the National Labor Relations
\par
Act.
\par
2. Conciliation under some state FEP laws.
\par
Under most state FEP laws, the aggrieved party has no indi-
\par
vidual right to litigate. If settlement is not achieved through
\par
conciliation, the state agency may hold a public hearing and
\par
may, after finding discrimination, issue a cease and desist order
\par
which is then judicially enforceable. In some jurisdictions, the
\par
settlement agreements reached through conciliation have fo-
\par
cussed more on the general behavior of the respondent than on
\par
the specific rights of the complainant. While this may appear
\par
to be desirable, its consequence has been in some cases the ac-
\par
ceptance of "letters of intent" to comply with the law as a
\par
"satisfactory adjustment" of individual grievances. This ap-
\par
proach has led to the proposed remedy of the "next available\emph{Administrative Creativity}
\par
\textbf{THE GEORGE WASHIINGTON LAW REVIEW}
\par
job" rather than immediate employment in refusal to hire or
\par
discharge cases, and to limited use of back pay or other mone-
\par
tary relief.
\par
The legal context of a Title VII conciliation requires concen-
\par
tration on the rights of complainants, for the right to litigate
\par
rests with them. No settlement can bind them unless they
\par
are a party to it. Therefore, the settlement agreement, in ad-
\par
dition to facing general problems which the respondent may
\par
have, must meet the legitimate needs of complainants for pres-
\par
ent recognition of his legal rights. Title VII conciliation must
\par
provide present, visible and genuine protection for charging
\par
parties if it is to both secure their rights and protect respond-
\par
ents from the risks of suit. The outcome of a Title VII concilia-
\par
tion should be an agreement which is legally sufficient to
\par
achieve these results.
\par
\textbf{3. }Conciliation and Title VII
\par
The nature of the legal, social and economic context thus
\par
shapes the concept of conciliation under Title VII. One can
\par
visualize two models of conciliation. One is the "hat in hand"
\par
approach, urging the respondent to sin no more, to go out and
\par
do good. The other is the more matter of fact and mundane
\par
approach which suggests that, in order to avoid a law suit, the
\par
respondent must take certain specific steps which will solve his
\par
problem. It is this latter approach which is suggested for con-
\par
ciliation under Title VII. Our experience supports this ap-
\par
proach. Respondents need no preaching. They know that Title
\par
VII is the law of the land. They are not certain what acts con-
\par
stitute a violation of Title VII and are not certain what must
\par
be done to comply with it. The primary task of the conciliator
\par
is to focus on the latter point, and to persuade the respondent
\par
to take such "affirmative action" as will constitute "voluntary
\par
compliance" with the statute by recognizing the rights of com-
\par
plainants and the interests of the minority group members in
\par
being free from future discrimination and having genuinely
\par
equal employment opportunity.
\par
The first two cases for conciliation were ready in Birming-
\par
ham and Memphis. I chose Birmingham, the symbolic center
\par
of the South to the civil rights movement and to organized
\par
labor and industry. More important, it is the home of United
\par
States Steel and the Steelworkers in the South. It is sym-
\par
bolic of all the problems of discrimination in the South.\emph{The Washington Lawyer}
\par
\emph{The First Case}
\par
My first experience in Birmingham was with a small plant
\par
which employed forty workers in a continuous flow process of
\par
manufacturing sewer pipe. The plant was located on the out-
\par
skirts of Birmingham to the west. The day I arrived I was
\par
met by the investigator, John Hennigan, a devoted and intel-
\par
ligent representative, who was on loan to the Commission
\par
from the Navy Department. He was genuinely helpful. He
\par
helped me through the first of many midnight sessions with
\par
the charging parties, at an attorney's house on the west side
\par
of Birmingham, surrounded by a high fence and floodlights
\par
because of a bomb episode of the previous year. He helped
\par
me through the period of hostility and uncertainty as we
\par
gained the confidence of the charging parties so that I would
\par
have a full grasp of their problem when I went to see the
\par
company and union the next day.
\par
One thing that John did not warn me about was that despite
\par
all these years of disappointment, the faith of the Negroes in
\par
the Government ran high. Their will to believe was so strong
\par
that they wished for assurances of help from the government
\par
representative. Later, if he was unable to solve their problem,
\par
they blamed him. The feeling of dependence may be nice for
\par
the ego of the government representative, but it is a luxury
\par
in which he cannot indulge for it is unfair to build expecta-
\par
tions when delivery is uncertain. As a result of one such ex-
\par
perience, I directed the conciliators to make very clear to the
\par
charging parties that there was no certainty that conciliation
\par
would work, and that the alternative was litigation.
\par
The next morning we visited the plant to talk to the com-
\par
pany representative and the union leaders. The plant mana-
\par
ger was a Californian, new to the South, not sympathetic to
\par
the position of the white employees, but unable to see any
\par
alternative. The problems in this little plant were, in sub-
\par
microcosm, the problems of the South. Some 30 per cent of
\par
the labor force were Negroes-nine employees. They were
\par
all confined to the shipping and finishing department and
\par
were not allowed into the white production department. The\emph{Administrative Creativity}
\par
GEORGE \textbf{WASHINGTON LAW REVIEW}
\par
entry level jobs in the white department paid at least forty
\par
cents per hour more than the top rate in the Negro depart-
\par
ment but were no more difficult. Newly hired white em-
\par
ployees had seniority in the production department which
\par
the Negroes did not. The union and the company were will-
\par
ing to let the Negro employees begin to move into the white
\par
department as vacancies developed, but their seniority would
\par
be less than the junior whites who had recently been hired in.
\par
In addition, there were segregated toilet, locker and shower
\par
facilities.
\par
I began discussions by advising the manager of his alterna-
\par
tives: If conciliation failed, a private suit might be filed or
\par
the Attorney General might be asked to file suit and the Of-
\par
fice of Federal Contract Compliance might be asked to sus-
\par
pend further contracting. I said I was not there to review
\par
the facts of discrimination; this had been established in the
\par
reasonable cause finding of the Commission. I was there to
\par
help eliminate the system of discrimination if possible. This
\par
became the standard "opening statement" of Commission con-
\par
ciliators."
\par
An international vice president of the union involved
\par
"happened" to be in town and attended part of our session,
\par
along with several of the employees. The manager seemed
\par
willing, but could think of nothing to offer except allowing
\par
the Negroes to move into white jobs as vacancies developed.
\par
Since turnover was low this offer appeared to have little sub-
\par
stance.
\par
As the day of discussion wore on, someone mentioned that
\par
the men were working seven days a week. I did a double
\par
take. This was virtually unheard of.
\par
The men explained that two years previously they had won
\par
in arbitration the right to work seven days a week. By now,
\par
they were tired. I saw a solution to the problem. I suggested
\par
that \textbf{if }the men went to a five or six day week, this would
\par
36. The conciliation process is more fully described in Blunrosen,
\par
\emph{The Individual Right }to \emph{Eliminate Employment Discrimination by}
\par
\emph{Litigation, }19th Ann. Winter Meeting, Industrial Relations Research
\par
Association 88 (1966).\emph{The Washington Lawyer}
\par
immediately create openings in the production department to
\par
which several Negroes could be transferred without delay.
\par
All of the difficult legal problems involving discriminatory
\par
seniority systems and remedies for discrimination evaporated.
\par
The union was willing, on behalf of the whites, to amend the
\par
contract. The company was pleased, because they acquired
\par
more flexibility in scheduling, and three Negro employees re-
\par
ceived immediate promotions. The men were willing to have
\par
the segregation wall removed from the locker-shower facili-
\par
ties.
\par
I must try to describe my feelings of that moment. It was
\par
dazzling. I felt elated and whole, as if, after days thrashing
\par
around futilely, I had ripped away the cobwebs which bind
\par
most of life. I had a brief glimpse of a future when men
\par
would shed the "tyranny over the mind" which prevented
\par
them seeing and acting in ways to improve conditions of life.
\par
One of the reasons why I stayed with government for two
\par
years, and why, since my return to Rutgers I have found
\par
many activities, including arbitration, dull, is that I compare
\par
them to the occasional moments of creation which came in the
\par
conciliation process.
\par
\emph{The Significance of a Signature}
\par
While the union and the company amended the collective
\par
agreement to move to the \textbf{5-6 }day week, I drafted the first
\par
written conciliation agreement in the history of the Commis-
\par
sion. It took the form of a settlement agreement between the
\par
charging parties and respondent. I began with some statutory
\par
language and followed it with the details of the settlement,
\par
including a drawing of the changes which were to be made in
\par
the facilities. I discussed details over the phone with corpo-
\par
rate counsel in New England and gave a copy to the attorney
\par
for the charging parties. The principle upon which I oper-
\par
ated was that the agreement had to be signed by the charging
\par
parties to be valid. This seemed to be of the essence of any
\par
settlement under Title VII, because the statute gave the com-\emph{Administrative Creativity}
\par
\textbf{THE GEORGE WASHINGTON LAW REVIEW}
\par
plainants the right to sue. Hence they had the right to settle,
\par
and the Commission could not and should not settle their case
\par
for them. I viewed the statute as creating individual rights
\par
which were not within the authority of the Commission to
\par
destroy. Without a great deal of theorizing (that was to
\par
come later), I implicitly rejected the paternalistic idea that
\par
the Government, or the conciliator, could decide for the Negro
\par
employees that the settlement was adequate. \textbf{By }requiring
\par
their signatures, we opened a new chapter in the history
\par
of race relations. While at the time I did what seemed nor-
\par
mal, a radical change was made in the usual processes of gov-
\par
ernment. The traditional view had been that the government
\par
had the power to make settlements and to determine what
\par
constituted a satisfactory adjustment. In theory, this was
\par
because of the weakness of the victim of discrimination and
\par
the necessity for having the government protect these victims
\par
of injustice. In practice, deals were made between represen-
\par
tatives of the government and respondents which often had
\par
little relevance to the charging parties. Professor Sovern de-
\par
scribes one horrible example of action \textbf{by }the New York Com-
\par
mission in which a complainant filed a charge, and the next
\par
thing he knew, he was told it had been satisfactorily adjusted.
\par
\textbf{All }intervening steps were taken without his knowledge, and
\par
the ultimate adjustment did not benefit him at all." 2 I re-
\par
jected such paternalism. Our experience is that when govern-
\par
ment acts in a paternal way, it deprives its "beneficiaries" of
\par
any chance of decent treatment. Our treatment of Indians
\par
and of juvenile offenders, as well as our handling of race
\par
relations, demonstrates the inhumanity of government when
\par
it operates in \emph{parens patriae.}
\par
\emph{Multilateral Bargaining}
\par
So the theory emerged that the conciliation agreement had
\par
to be signed by the complainant, that there could be no ending
\par
of the case without that and that the alternative to dealing
\par
37. SovEm, \emph{supra }note \textbf{1, }at 49.\emph{The Washington Lawyer}
\par
in conciliation with the charging parties was a law suit. This
\par
principle aggravated the large employers and unions who
\par
were used to doing business with government representatives.
\par
Time and again they objected to our view that a settlement
\par
had to be acceptable to the complainants to be a settlement at
\par
all. Since many complainants were represented by the
\par
NAACP and the Legal Defense Fund, the effect of our view
\par
was to require companies and unions to sit down and negotiate
\par
with the sophisticated representatives of Negro employees.
\par
Thus emerged three-interest bargaining between labor, man-
\par
agement and civil rights interests. Time and again I was
\par
pressed by a company or union to accept a solution on behalf
\par
of the Government regardless of the views of the charging
\par
parties. After all, we were the administrators of the law, we
\par
were told. It was up to us to decide what constituted "vol-
\par
untary compliance" with the statute. At one time, one of the
\par
largest corporations and largest unions brought their top per-
\par
sonnel to Washington to press this view. After sitting before
\par
them for a full day, I decided to take the matter to the Com-
\par
mission with a recommendation that the proposal made by
\par
them be rejected. The Commission accepted this recommen-
\par
dation. Three months later, the company and the union com-
\par
menced negotiations with the representatives of the Legal
\par
Defense Fund.
\par
The theory which emerged and was implemented through
\par
conciliation was that individual rights under Title VII were
\par
inalienable, at least as far as the Commission was concerned.
\par
This meant that if respondents wished to avoid suit, they
\par
might have to sit down with representatives of the charging
\par
parties, such as civil rights organizations. Thus the civil
\par
rights organizations were given standing and status to negoti-
\par
ate, a standing and status they would not have if only the
\par
Government and the respondent decided what the result
\par
should be. Furthermore, civil rights organizations were given
\par
a seat at the bargaining table, a seat which they had never
\par
had before. Respondents could no longer ignore the civil
\par
rights interest; they had to listen and respond.\emph{Administrative Creativity}
\par
\textbf{THE GEORGE WASGTON LAW REVIEW}
\par
The simple act of asserting that a Title VII settlement re-
\par
quired the signature of the complainants had the effect of
\par
converting Title VII into a rough "duty to bargain" on the
\par
part of labor and management. If they wished a settlement,
\par
they had to negotiate with the representatives of the civil
\par
rights interests. If they refused to negotiate, or if no settle-
\par
ment could be reached, the civil rights interests could take the
\par
matters to the courts. At this time in our history, the courts
\par
are friendly to the civil rights interests and are developing a
\par
line of decisions liberally construing the anti-discrimination
\par
laws. It was therefore in the interests of labor and manage-
\par
ment to work out solutions to the problems which were posed
\par
in these Title VII negotiations. In addition, the risk of long
\par
and sometimes public involvement with the agency enforcing
\par
the anti-discrimination clause in government contracts and the
\par
possibilities of a suit by the Attorney General added impetus
\par
for settlement to these cases.
\par
In creating this "duty to bargain," we introduced into this
\par
field the concept of multilateral negotiations, a vastly ex-
\par
panded version of collective bargaining in which multiple in-
\par
terests are in fact represented. Multilateral bargaining is
\par
quite possibly one of the ways of the future in resolving the
\par
increasingly more complex disputes within our society. It is a
\par
logical extension of pluralism. If collective bargaining repre-
\par
sented the process by which the interests of labor were recog-
\par
nized in the employment scene, then multilateral bargaining
\par
represents the method by which the interests of still further
\par
organized groups can be taken into account in the informal
\par
processes by which the business of life is carried on. As we
\par
expand the scope and activities of organized groups in society,
\par
they will come to need such channels for articulation and im-
\par
plementation of their interests. The experience under Title
\par
VII may illuminate this way.
\par
Thus our decision to require the signatures of the charging
\par
party constituted at once an act of faith in the principles of
\par
individualism, a denial of the concept of paternalism which
\par
had infused the anti-discrimination laws in the past and an in-\emph{The Washington Lawyer}
\par
troduction to multilateral bargaining between labor, manage-
\par
ment and the civil rights movement. This is quite a burden
\par
for that single afternoon in a small plant in Birmingham. Yet,
\par
in retrospect, that afternoon was the crossroads where these
\par
concepts were to be born or repressed for some later emer-
\par
gence.
\par
The entire thesis required that the charging parties-and
\par
hence the civil rights interests-would themselves determine
\par
whether conciliation was successful. If we had once recog-
\par
nized that the Government could determine what constituted
\par
"voluntary compliance" with the statute, there would have
\par
been a totally different kind of process and outcome. For
\par
example, one could read the provisions of sections 706(a)
\par
and 706(e) as providing that if the Commission thought that
\par
a conciliation proposal was acceptable and constituted "volun-
\par
tary compliance" 8 with Title VII, it would never issue a no-
