\emph{The End of the First Year}
\par
My first year with the Commission saw the negotiation of the
\par
Newport News Agreement, at the end of March 1966, as the
\par
high point. In reaching that agreement the EEOC, the Justice
\par
Department, the Office of Federal Contract Compliance and
\par
the Defense Department cooperated as they never had before
\par
or have since. To negotiate with the contract compliance offi-
\par
cer on one side and a representative from the Attorney Gen-
\par
eral on the other was an exhilarating experience. The agree-
\par
ment and its administration demonstrate the potentiality in
\par
the administrative process for breaking the back of the prob-
\par
lem of employment discrimination in the country. This poten-
\par
tial has not been realized. I have written separately about the
\par
Newport News Agreement because of its importance in point-
\par
ing out how it is possible to solve rather than perpetuate the
\par
problem of discrimination.2 One of my deepest frustrations
\par
is that the concept of unified action to achieve carefully tail-
\par
ored changes in industrial relations systems to correct dis-
\par
crimination has not been adopted by the Government.
\par
In April or May, 1966, shortly before he left, Chairman
\par
Roosevelt asked me to stay on for another year. I debated
\par
long and hard in an utterly inconclusive way. I was growing
\par
tired of the bureaucracy, and was spending more time on less
\par
important matters as the administrative process unfolded. My
\par
decision crystallized on one of my many trips to Birmingham.
\par
The Negro employee who had brought the charges involved
\par
in my first conciliation had been fired under apparently dis-
\par
criminatory circumstances. I went to Birmingham to un-
\par
scramble the matter, and ultimately got him his job back. He
\par
symbolized for me the courage of all minorities who were chal-
\par
lenging their oppressed conditions and seeking justice in the
\par
work place. I decided to stay for a second year.
