\textbf{The model}
\par
The Commander Model addresses the traditional strategic management question of `How
\par
can \textbf{I , }as a general manager, develop a strategy for my business which will guide my day-to-
\par
day decisions in support of my longer-term objectives?'
\par
In this model, the role of the CEO is that of a `Rational Actor' issuing directives from the
\par
seat of power. The model assumes that an exhaustive analysis can be undertaken \emph{before}
\par
taking action, and requires that the CEO holds a considerable amount of power and has
\par
access to complete information. It relies almost exclusively on economic rationality, and
\par
considers political issues only as they involve parties outside the firm. Generally, it fails to
\par
consider the implementability of plans, either as a binary variable (`implementable' or `not
\par
implementable') or as an aspect of the strategy with a cost in time and money associated
\par
with it.
\par
The Commander Model encompasses two rubrics, the systems model and the incremental
\par
approach (Wheelwright, \textbf{1973). }In the systems model, one first identifies the objectives of
\par
the organization, then generates courses of action which might meet the objectives, then
\par
evaluates them on grounds of economic efficiency and, finally, chooses one for execution.
\par
The model has been assailed on the grounds that, under conditions of bounded rationality,
\par
the planner cannot consider all options, but this has not hindered its effective use. In the
\par
second, incremental, approach, one identifies the existing strategy, evaluates threats and
\par
opportunities facing the firm, and plans modifications to the current strategy in order to
\par
meet the changed environmental conditions. Although these two approaches clearly differ,
\par
both in their orientation toward magnitude of change and their scope of analysis, they are
\par
both commander modeIs in the sense that they assume a purposive general manager
\par
directing the firm toward objectives defined at the apex of the organization.
\par
In its most identifiable form, the Commander Model employs tools that are analytic
\par
(usually economic) in nature. \textbf{I }It is in the context of this model that one would see the use of
\par
experience curves, growth/share matrices, PIMS studies, and industry and competitive
\par
analyses.
