\par
1. \textbf{\emph{The Commander Model requires easy implementation}}
\par
Since the model does not consider implementation problems explicitly, we hypothesize that
\par
it will work best under the following conditions.
\par
The objective function of the formulator should match that of the managers who
\par
must implement the strategy.
\par
Systems currently in operation in the company must not impede the behaviour called
\par
for by the strategy. For example, a firm which historically rewards managers for
\par
growth may have difficulty implementing a harvest strategy.
\par
Where top management enjoys a great deal of centralized power, top-down strategies
\par
are more likely to succeed. Such power could come from an effective authoritarian
\par
management style, from deep personal loyalty to the CEO, or from having good
\par
access to formal and informal information on key people in the firm.
\par
The Commander Model is more likely to succeed when it is not threatening, such as
\par
when the strategy calls for only slight change and does not require layoffs.
\par
Given its centralized information requirements, the Commander Model will
\par
probably work best for firms in stable environments that have a low degree of
\par
diversity, or at the SBU level of diversified firms (where information has fewer filters
\par
to pass through).
\par
The Commander Model will be more effective if the organization is in a strong
\par
competitive position and has plenty of slack. To the extent that strategies differ in
\par
the amount of resources required for implementation, the greater the slack in the
\par
organization, the greater the likelihood that a given strategy will be implementable.
\par
\textbf{\emph{2 . The Commander Model requires good information}}
\par
The value of a strategy depends on the ability of the strategists t o obtain complete, accurate
\par
information about the firm's internal capabilities and its position in the environment.
\par
Although the strategy is developed by people near the top of the organization, much of this
\par
information enters the firm at lower levels through, for example, salesmen, purchasing
\par
agents and factory foremen. Thus, the commander model requires that this information be
\par
collected and communicated upward, efficiently and accurately.
\par
This presents two potential limitations. First, in \textbf{a }fast-changing environment, theinformation submitted to the strategists may be obsolete by the time it reaches them. Under
\par
\textbf{\emph{Strategy Implementation }}zy245 zy
\par
such circumstances, decision-making must be done by the people closer to the information
\par
sources. Secondly, even when the rate of change allows information to be assembled and
\par
transmitted upward in a meaningful time frame, the people who provide the information
\par
may feel strong incentives to conceal or mislead, if the outcome of the decision process
\par
might affect them.
\par
3. \textbf{\emph{The Commander Model requires objective planners}}
\par
In decentralized organizations, where division managers formulate plans and pass them
\par
upward, the planner no longer stands aloof from political turmoil and economic self-
\par
interest. This problem differs from the one just described; there we were concerned with
\par
biasing of information provided to central planners, whereas here we are concerned with
\par
bias in the process which transforms data into plans. The plans generated by division
\par
managers will probably be optimal from the point of view of their divisions (or the
\par
managers themselves, if incentive compensation is involved), but not from the standpoint of
\par
the corporation as a whole. Controlling this problem requires a sizeable planning staff at the
\par
corporate level, which can provide the time and expertise necessary to give divisional plans
\par
the close scrutiny they require. \textbf{zyxwvu}
\par
\textbf{4. \emph{The Commander Model splits the firm into thinkers and doers}}
\par
The division of the organization into thinkers and doers can create motivational problems. \textbf{zyAs }some studies have pointed out, people on the firing line (`doers') tend to withhold
\par
alternatives which they think have little chance of acceptance (Carter, 1971). If the general
\par
manager creates the belief that the only acceptable strategies are those developed by himself
\par
or by his planning staff, he may find himself faced with an extremely unmotivated, un-
\par
innovative group of employees. Indeed, one premise of the model, that strategies are
\par
formulated at the top of the organization and forced downward, is inconsistent with
\par
observed behaviour in many companies. \textbf{As }Bower (1970), Carter (1971) and Burgelman
\par
(1983) have observed, strategy often results from individuals taking the initiative in
\par
identifying and championing opportunities.
\par
\textbf{Why the approach persists}
\par
In the light of the limitations of the Commander Model in its pure form, why is it still
\par
prevalent in business schools and among consultants? Five factors account for its
\par
popularity. First, it does offer \textbf{a }valuable perspective to the chief executive, although its
\par
value may be limited by the implementability of the strategy, by information problems, and
\par
by the incentives operating on managers at different levels in the organization. Secondly, by
\par
dividing the management task into `planning' and `doing', the general manager reduces the
\par
number of inputs he must process simultaneously. Thirdly, the commander approach places
\par
the strategic planner in a position to influence the firm's density, an opportunity which, no
\par
doubt, appeals to many consultants and freshly minted MBAs. Fourthly, it fits the MBAs
\par
predisposition toward dealing with the quantitative and objective elements of a situation,
\par
rather than with more subjective and behavioural considerations. Finally, the separation
