of systems used for planning, performance measurement, and incentive compensation; and
\par
\textbf{(3) }the use of cultural adaptation techniques to introduce system-wide change.
\par
\textbf{Structure and staffing}
\par
Perhaps the most obvious tool for strategy implementation is for management to alter the
\par
structure of the organization and to add, remove o r shift personnel in order to lead the firm
\par
in the desired direction. This has been the traditional approach espoused by most business
\par
policy textbooks (see, for example, Andrews, \textbf{1971; }Galbraith and Nathanson, 1978;
\par
Schendel and Hofer, 1979; Uyterhoeven, Ackerman and Rosenblum, 1977). Generally, the
\par
structure of the organization should denote the skill set most critical to attaining the
\par
strategy: for example, a strategy calling for world-wide co-operation of manufacturing in
\par
order to capture cost efficiencies demands a functional organization for production,
\par
whereas a strategy calling for marketing of commodity products (e.g. Procter and Gamble)
\par
calls for a product-oriented organization.
\par
\textbf{Planning systems, performance measurements and incentive compensation}
\par
A variety of account and control tools support administrative systems for implementing
\par
strategy.
\par
\emph{Planning systems }governing capital and operating budgets can be adjusted t o elicit desired
\par
behaviours. For example, if the firm's strategy calls for investing in certain businesses and
\par
harvesting others, or for repatriating profits in one national unit to fund others, these goals
\par
should figure prominently in the capital budgeting system which approves every project
\par
with a return above an arbitrary hurdle rate. Similarly, the process for preparation and
\par
approval of annual operating budgets can be adjusted to support the kind of resource
\par
allocation decisions called for in the strategy. In addition to reviewing the planning systemsthemselves, the would-be implementer must also consider the effect of financial standards,
\par
\emph{Strategy Implementation }zy247
\par
such as cost allocations and transfer prices, which influence decision-making throughout
\par
the firm.
\par
The \emph{information systems }used by the firm to measure performance should translate the
\par
strategy into meaningful short-term milestones, so that the progress according to the
\par
strategy can be monitored. The power of such a system is enhanced considerably when
\par
integrated with the following implementation mechanism.
\par
To the extent that the desired behaviour can be translated into clear-cut numerical terms,
\par
and the outcome is sufficiently free of uncontrollable risk as to make it productive to
\par
impose this risk on the manager, then the \emph{incentive compensation }scheme can be changed to
\par
encourage the desired behaviour. At a minimum, the general manager must ensure that
\par
current compensation arrangements do not create an incentive plan in opposition to the
\par
substance of the strategic plan. \textbf{zyxwvu}
\par
\textbf{Cultural adaptation}
\par
To implement strategy more effectively, the manager can rely on techniques described in the
\par
community development literature in order to introduce change in the organization
\par
(Ahrensburg and Niehoff, 1971). These techniques include such fundamentals as (a) use
\par
demonstrations, (b) start with clearly-perceived needs and (c) enlist the support of high-
\par
credibility presenters. Related change techniques have emerged through studies of corporate
\par
culture (Gluck, 1981; Pascale and Athos, 1981; Peters, 1978). This latter research identifies
\par
ways in which the executive's actions have strong symbolic force in conveying to employees
\par
a sense of desired behaviour.
\par
\textbf{Implications}
\par
With a set of powerful implementation tools at his or her disposal, the executive using the
\par
Change Model can carry out more difficult plans in a greater variety of organizations than
\par
would be possible without these tools. Thus, in a very practical sense, this approach will be
\par
more effective than a pure commander model in many organizations; that is, the Command
\par
and Change Models are partners in the strategy formulation-implementation cycle.
\par
The cast of characters in this model differs only slightly from those participating in the
\par
Commander approach. Now the strategist, whether general manager or staff specialist,
\par
needs political skills in addition to the planning tools described in the first section. As
\par
before, the team of planner and executive must jointly possess all knowledge needed to
\par
plan, and all the power needed to implement the strategy. Now, however, the executive does
\par
not merely pass the strategy on to his subordinates; he stays actively involved through the
\par
implementation phase and probably reveals the strategy gradually, taking advantage of
\par
opportune moments (Quinn, 1977, 1978; Wrapp, 1967).
\par
However, tacking `implementation' onto `strategy' does not solve most of the problems
\par
encountered with the first model: the Change Model does not deal with problems of
\par
obtaining accurate information; it does not deal with situations where the planner faces
\par
disincentives against objectivity; and, since it still calls for imposing the strategy in top-
\par
down fashion, it does not resolve the motivational problems created by the first approach.
\par
The Change Model raises a theoretical possibility of a new kind of problem. By
\par
manipulating the systems and structures of the organizations in support of a particular
\par
strategy, the general manager may be trading off important strategic flexibility. Some of
\par
these systems, particularly incentive compensation, take a long time to design and install;
\par
once installed, they may take time to become effective. Should an unforeseen change in the248 \emph{zyxwvutsrqpL. }\textbf{\emph{zyxwvutsrJ . Bourgeois, zyxwvuIII and D. R . Brodwin }}zyxwvenvironment require a redirection of the strategy, it may be very difficult to change the
\par
firm's course, since all the `levers' controlling the firm have been set firmly in support of the
\par
now-obsolete game plan. Thus, where environmental uncertainty is high, it may prove more
\par
effective in the long-run to refrain from using some of the tools described above. For
\par
example, many high technology firms, which rely on rapid development and introduction of
\par
a continuous stream of technological innovation, avoid imposing bureaucratic
\par
administrative systems which would blunt their ability to create strategic change.
