\textbf{The model}
\par
The Collaborative Model extends strategic decision-making to the organization's dominant
\par
coalition in answer to the question, `How can \textbf{I }get my top management team t o help
\par
develop and commit to a good set of goals and strategies?'
\par
In this model, the CEO employs group dynamics and `brainstorming' techniques to get
\par
managers with differing points of view to provide their inputs to the strategic process. Given
\par
that effective top management teams will differ in their goal structures (Bourgeois, \textbf{1980), }as
\par
well as their perceptions of the environment (Lawrence and Lorsch, \textbf{1967), }the CEO will
\par
want to extract whatever `group wisdom' is inherent in these multiple perspectives. The role
\par
of the CEO is that of co-ordinator, structuring the interactions among the decision-makers
\par
in such a way that all good ideas are entertained. In a sense, a football team huddle is
\par
representative, where relevant strategic information from various positions is factored into
\par
each round of decision-making.
\par
The structuring of group interactions can take a variety of forms, most of which are task-
\par
(as opposed to affect-) oriented. For example, Arther D. Little engages its management
\par
team participants in a Q-sort method of gaining team consensus on which generic strategies
\par
`fit' their particular industry situation. Mitroff and Emshoff \textbf{(1979) }put their participants
\par
through a structured `dialectical inquiry' process which forces them to surface the basic
\par
assumptions upon which various proposed strategic alternatives are based. A third variant
\par
of the collaborative model involves \textbf{\emph{teaching }}Commander-type analytical tools to the top
\par
management team, as done by some academic consultants. (This contrasts with providing
\par
management with the analytical \textbf{\emph{output, }}such as a report, which many strategic management
\par
consultants do.)
\par
A number of corporations use this approach. General Motors formed `business teams' in
\par
