\par
job was not to make and implement strategy, but to assemble a team of competent
\par
managers-most more competent than he in their respective functional fields-which could,
\par
jointly, collaborate in the formation of strategies. When Ed Carlson took over ailing United
\par
Airlines in \textbf{1970, }he set out initially to decentralize decision-making as much as possible. In
\par
order to achieve this, he loaded his \textbf{12 }senior executives and spouses onto a jet in early \textbf{1971}
\par
and flew them to Los Angeles for a weekend of low-key meetings. These loosely-structured
\par
