\textbf{Implications}
\par
The Collaborative Model overcomes two key limitations inherent in the previous two: by
\par
capturing information carried by executives closer to the front lines of operations and by
\par
engaging several brains at once, it helps overcome both the information accuracy and
\par
cognitive limits of the Commander Model. To the extent that participation breeds
\par
commitment among the deciders, it can deal with the motivational problems encountered in
\par
both the Commander and Change Models, and, thereby, improve the probability of
\par
successful implementation.
\par
However, what the Collaborative Model gains in team commitment may come at the
\par
expense of economic rationality. In this model, strategy is a negotiated outcome among
\par
players with different points of view and, possibly, different goals. The `ideal' strategy
\par
indicated by Commander-type tools, and the `ideal' administrative system implied in the
\par
Change Model may be economically and technically rational, but may not be politically
\par
feasible.
\par
The evaluative criterion for this model would be team consensus achieved with minimal
\par
`gaming' and acrimony. Conventional wisdom would hold goal consensus as the key
\par
guarantor, although recent research would indicate that consesus on specific strategies is
\par
more critical for performance (Bourgeois, \textbf{1980). }Although the previous two models may be
\par
most efficient in simple and stable (and, therefore, more easily understood) environments,
\par
the benefits of the Collaborative Model probably start to accrue in more complex and,
\par
perhaps, less stable environments, where the chief executive or division manager is unable to
\par
perceive, assimilate and comprehend the totality of his organization's activities.
\par
This model does not necessarily overcome the gaming or fiefdom-building tendencies
\par
which often follow from conditions of resource scarcity and information assymetry. In fact,
\par
the group decision process itself, especially as it relies directly on the participants for
\par
