\textbf{The model}
\par
The cultural model takes the participative elements of the collaborative model to lower
\par
levels in the organization as an answer to the strategic management question `How can \textbf{I }get
\par
my whole organization committed to our goals and strategies?'
\par
In this model, the CEO guides his organization by communicating and instilling his vision
\par
of the overarching mission for the firm, and then allowing each individual to participate in
\par
designing his or her work procedures in concert with that mission. \textbf{So, }once the game plan is
\par
set, the CEO plays the role of `coach' in giving general direction, but encourages individual
\par
decision-making to determine the operating details of executing the plan.
\par
This model should strike a familiar chord in the minds of readers exposed to the plethora
\par
of recent `Japanese management' articles appearing in both popular and academic
\par
publications (see, for example, the \emph{Fortune }series on `Working Smarter, Burck, 1981a,
\par
1981b; Main, 1981a, 1981b). In effect, the Cultural Model represents the latest wave of
\par
