Based on our assessment of the nature of the companies generally held up as exemplars of
\par
this approach to strategic management, we have reached some tentative conclusions about
\par
the organizational characteristics for which it is most suited: the Cultural Model seems to
\par
work when power is decentralized, where there are shared goals between the organization
\par
and its participants, and where the organization is stable and growing.
\par
This last point may be key: cultural models work best where there is sufficient
\par
organizational slack to absorb the cost of installing and maintaining them. Consider some
\par
of the exemplar firms: HP, IBM, Matsushita and Intel. These tend to be high-growth firms.
\par
\textbf{As }Main describes Intel's experience, `To lessen the threat of change, Intel promised not to
\par
fire any permanent employee whose job was eliminated. The company's phenomenal sales
\par
growth, 29.3 per cent in 1980, helps absorb everyone who wants to stay' (1981b54).
\par
The cultural model has several limitations. For one, it assumes informed and intelligent
\par
people (note that most of the examples are firms in high technology industries). Secondly,
\par
the presence of a powerful culture that seems to be `working', in the sense that consistency
\par
of decisions at all levels is achieved, may lull the organization and allow it to drift and lose
\par
focus. For example, when one division of Hewlett-Packard introduced the HP-3000
\par
computer, it discovered too late that the machine it would supplant (made by a separate
\par
divison) was not much inferior and used a different operating system. (For the \textbf{3000 }to
\par
