\textbf{The model}
\par
The Crescive Model addresses some of the limitations ascribed to the previous approaches
\par
by addressing the question `How can I encourage my managers to develop, champion, and
\par
implement sound strategies?'
\par
In this model, strategy comes upward from the firing line, rather than downward from
\par
the top. The role of the CEO has moved from designer to that of premise-setter and judge.
\par
Here, the strategic problem revolves around the CEO's ability to define organization
\par
purposes (i.e. set decision premises) broadly enough to encourage innovation, and to select
\par
judiciously from among those projects or strategy alternatives that reach his attention. This
\par
is a delicate balancing act. Too broad a definition may permit diffused attention by the firm
\par
or even encourage certain counterproductive behaviours among managers (Bower, 1970);
\par
and a proliferation of proposals may produce cognitive strains for the CEO. But too narrow
\par
a definition might squash initiative, since managers tend to suppress proposals which are
\par
deemed to have a limited probability of approval (Carter, 1971). In effect, strategic
\par
management, under this model, consists of creating and maintaining a fine balance between
\par
what Burgelman (1983) calls `autonomous strategic behavior' at the sub-unit (SBU) level,
\par
and what Bales (1977) has labelled the `president's paradox' of control at the top.
\par
How can the CEO encourage a vigorous pace of innovation within the firm and still
\par
maintain an effective filter for screening out inappropriate or ill-conceived programmes?
\par
The answer to this question is, we believe, key to the next generation of strategic
\par
management. We also believe that research, for normative purposes, has only begun to
\par
address the question from a strategic (total enterprise) perspective. The works of Burgelman
\par
(1983) and Quinn (1979) represent the first steps of building on the product or technological
\par
innovation literature (which tends to focus on the functional department level, such as the
\par
R\&D laboratory), the `venturing' literature and their own empirical research to start
\par
suggesting strategic designs for nurturing entrepreneurship within large corporations.
\par
In a moment, we will suggest a model that draws from quite a different theoretical base-
\par
agency theory-that arrives at results quite similar to those of Burgelman and Quinn, i.e. a
\par
model of strategy `growing' from within the bowels of the firm (hence, our label crescive,
\par
from the Latin \emph{crescere, }to grow), but which is neither based upon our own empiricalresearch nor upon the innovation literature. Instead, we developed our thinking by first
\par
\textbf{\emph{Strategy Implementation }zyx255 }zy
\par
deducing from various pieces of what we would call `pathology-oriented' empirical
\par
research, as well as case histories and anecdotes, that such a model of corporate behaviour
\par
indeed exists. Secondly, we asked whether the behaviour we observed need be
\par
pathological-could it not represent harnessable energy? Finally, for the purposes of
\par
exposition, we put on our normative caps and took the tentative developmental steps
\par
outlined below.
\par
But, before we outline those steps, let us briefly illustrate some of the research and
\par
examples which influenced our thinking.
\par
Bower's \textbf{(1970) }study has already been cited as exemplifying sub-unit-initiated strategic
\par
behaviour. One key conclusion to be drawn from his case histories, however (see, for
\par
