\par
incredible amounts of energy to bias estimates, hide pet projects, or otherwise attempt to
\par
influence the formal capital budgeting process in order to pursue strategic initiatives
\par
identified at the local level. This behaviour is `pathological' in the sense that managers are
\par
sometimes forced to be devious in order to take advantage of the market opportunities as
\par
they perceive them. An additional key conclusion is that, as investment alternatives ascend
\par
through the corporate hierarchy, their promotion by credible champions often overrides
\par
their inherent economic justification. The ill-fated Fireguard project in Industrial Products
\par
