\textbf{The Creseive Model-a normative suggestion}
\par
The model we propose is an attempt to apply some rudiments of agency theory to strategic
\par
management. (A summary of applicable parts of the theory is given in the Appendix. A
\par
more formal view is given by Jennergren, 1980.) In a sense, the relationship between a chief
\par
executive officer and the general managers of profit centres in a diversified firm can be
\par
considered to be similar to that described by the principal/agent model of the relationship
\par
between a firm's owners and its managers. The CEO entrusts organizational resources to
\par
division managers and, particularly in diversified firms, he cannot know and understand all\textbf{\emph{Strategy Implementation 257 }}zyof the strategic and operating situations facing the divisions (Berg, 1965). Therefore, if he is
\par
to exploit the fact that they can see strategic opportunities which he cannot, he must allow
\par
them discretion in the commitment of resources. His dilemma is that he is ultimately
\par
responsible for the total organization, but must give up some control over it in order to
\par
foster strategic opportunism and achievement. His career (if not his personal wealth) is in
\par
the hands of others. How can he manage this? We have taken some tentative steps toward
\par
1. developing the Crescive Model in answer to this by identifying five propositions with
\par
interesting implications for the strategic management process.
\par
The chief executive cannot monitor all significant opportunities and threats. \textbf{zyx2. }The power of the executive to impose strategy on the organization is limited; among
\par
other things, the shortage of executive talent allows successful managers to leave if
\par
