\par
Since the chief executive relies on his subordinates to provide data and generate
\par
strategies, as well as to deliver results, he must be careful with the incentives he
\par
establishes. Subordinates can exploit the CEO's vulnerability, for example, by
\par
`lowballing' on forecasts they will be called on to meet, by withholding total efforts,
\par
or by misdirecting resources toward pet projects. Only by reviewing a manager's
\par
performance on a series of projects over a relatively long period of time can this risk
\par
be controlled.
\par
\textbf{\emph{5 .}}
\par
Strategy formation often occurs in groups and usually incorporates perceptions,
\par
rather than incontrovertible facts. However, management can take actions which will
\par
ameliorate some of the difficulties inherent in group decision-making.
\par
The model implies a description of a strategy-making process appropriate to the large
\par
divisionalized firm and offers some guidance for the chief executive trying to manage his
\par
firm strategically.
\par
A detailed consideration of each proposition follows.
\par
\textbf{\emph{The chief executive cannot monitor all significant opportunities and threats}}
\par
If the company is highly diversified, it is difficult or impossible for senior management to
\par
stay abreast of developments in all of the firm's different industries. Similarly, if an
\par
industry is shifting very quickly (e.g. personal computers), information collected at lower
\par
levels often becomes stale before it can be assimilated, summarized, and passed up the
\par
ranks. Even in more stable industries, the time required to process information upward
\par
through many management levels can mean that decisions are being made based on
\par
outdated information.
\par
As a result, in many cases the CEO must relax his expectations concerning the extent to
\par
which strategic plans can be developed centrally. Instead, an incentive scheme or `free-
\par
market' environment is established to encourage operating managers to make decisions that
\par
will further the long-range interests of the company.
\par
\textbf{\emph{The power zyxwvutof the chief executive is limited}}
\par
In the typical private corporation, the chief executive enjoys substantial but limited power.
\par
His power derives from his ability to bestow rewards, allocate resources, and reduce the258 \textbf{\emph{zyxwvutsrqL . zyxwvutsJ. Bourgeois, 111 and D. R . Brodwin}}
\par
uncertainty for members of the organization. Thus, to an extent, the executive can impose
\par
his or her will on the other members of the organization. However, the executive's power is
\par
limited by the labour market (since employees can leave and join another firm), and by key
\par
managers' control over information or over customer relationships.
\par
Whether or not executives operate by `muddling through' (Lindblom, 1959) seems to
\par
depend on their relative power in the organization. If the executive has relatively little
\par
power, all decisions must be compromises, and the outside observer will see little evidence
\par
of purposive behaviour. On the other hand, if the executive enjoys substantial power,
\par
behaviour will look more purposive, and `muddling' much less in evidence. Perhaps this
\par
explains why `muddling through' was first observed in the public sector, where the chief
\par
administrators typically have less power and autonomy than in most private firms.
\par
\textbf{\emph{The need for making time-critical decisions}}
\par
In contrast to the dictum that executives should be planning-oriented and thoughtful, most
\par
spend the majority of their work day attending to short-range problems (Mintzberg, 1975).
\par
The fundamental conflict between planning and action has much to do with the executive's
\par
role as giver-of-rewards. People bring strategic matters to him partly to secure recognition
\par
and advancement; in addition, he is responsible for managing the resources under his
\par
control. Thus, any realistic model of the strategic process must recognize that executives d o
\par
not plan much; they are bombarded constantly by requests from subordinates.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Strategy in a multi-person context}}
\par
The CEO (as well as the division general manager) relies heavily on subordinates when
\par
formulating strategies. First, he needs up-to-date information about conditions in each
\par
division. Secondly, he needs his subordinates to formulate strategies (or review strategies
\par
formulated by others) and sign off on operating goals.
\par
The CEO's dependence on his subordinate managers creates a thorny control problem: in
\par
essence, if a manager knows he will be accountable for plans he formulates or which use
\par
information he provides, he has an incentive to bias his estimates of the division's
\par
performance. The conclusions that one might draw from applying agency theory (see
\par
