\par
In this paper, we have explored five approaches to strategy implementation. Our
\par
presentation, being in caricature form as it was, simplified to the extent of implying five
\par
mutually exclusive categories. In truth, we believe these are not mutually exclusive forms,
\par
but probably modal, in the sense that any particular firm will probably engage in a variety
\par
of the models, but with different emphasis. Some firms, particularly those with `two
\par
cultures' (for example, TRW has both aerospace and automotive supply groups of \textbf{SBUs;}
\par
the former are `high flyers' and Californian, the latter `mature' and industrial Midwestern),
\par
could conceivably be bimodal.' Although we make no claim as to the empirical reality of
\par
this framework, the reaction by executives to whom it has been presented has, in fact, been
\par
one of being able to see their own firms `mapped' onto a five-point continuum, with the
\par
curve peaking at the modal form or forms (TRW, for example, peaks at both the
\par
commander-change juncture and at the crescive point).
\par
These models represent a means of thinking about the range and complexity of tools the
\par
CEO might consider when organizing his firm's approach t o strategy-making. None of
\par
these approaches is correct for all companies. Their use should depend on such factors as
\par
degree of diversification, rate of growth and change and existing culture. Clearly, the
\par
Cultural or Crescive Models would be too elaborate for some, perhaps less diversified and
\par
less complex firms.
\par
The five models also represent an increasing attention to bring implementation forward in
\par
the strategic management process. The first three models assume implementation as after-
\par
the-fact. The number of formulators are few; the rest of the organization is somehow
\par
manipulated into implementation. The remarkable thing about the Cultural Model,
\par
however, as quoted above in the Westinghouse case, is how the large amount of time
\par
invested in consensual decision-making pays off with almost instant implementation. \textbf{A}
\par
similar claim can be made for the Crescive Model-by the time the strategy alternative has
