resources. On strategic questions, the principal is typically the CEO or the division general
\par
manager (we will use the CEO in the following discussion). The second, the \textbf{\emph{agent, }}is hired
\par
by the principal to perform some task; the agent might be a division general manager
\par
(DGM) reporting to the CEO or a functional manager reporting to a division manager (we
\par
will use DGM). Agency theory offers a way to analyse systematically the task at hand, and
\par
devise incentives and measurement systems which will increase the likelihood that the agent
\par
(DGM) will execute the task successfully for the principal (CEO).\textbf{262 \emph{zyxwvutsrqL. J. }}\emph{zyxwvutsrqpBourgeois, IIIandD. R. Brodwin }zyxwv
\par
Three concepts, \emph{in formation assymetry, monitoring }and \emph{moral hazard, }play a central role
\par
in agency theory. \emph{Information assymetry }is the condition where the agent has more
\par
knowledge than the principal about the relevant decision. Assymetry occurs because the
\par
CEO needs accurate information from the DGM on which to base strategy, but he has no
\par
way to evaluate the accuracy of the information he receives. Asking the DGM t o formulate
\par
his strategy, instead of providing a portion of the input for the total organizational strategy,
\par
still presents the same problem. Information assymetries tend to be worse in diversified
\par
firms than in firms active in only a few business areas; in the diversified firms, the CEO and
\par
group managers typically lack experience in all but a few of the company's market
\par
segments. (They tend to lack the time required to `learn' each segment as well.)
\par
\emph{Monitoring }describes the extent to which the principal can observe whether the agent is
\par
performing the desired task. The principal can choose to monitor outcome or effort, or
\par
some combination of outcome and effort. When the `task' is to implement a strategy,
\par
neither type of monitoring is particularly effective: on the one hand, the effort is hard to
\par
measure. If the principal imposes on the agent a complete strategy and tells him t o execute
\par
it, he has no way of monitoring the level of effort the agent puts forth. He can count the
\par
number of hours the DGM spends at his office, but since `management' is a subtle task,
\par
such crude measures approximate only poorly the kind of effort we need to measure. On the
\par
other hand, we cannot use success or failure of a project as a proxy for effort, since
\par
outcome depends on environmental variables not under the control of the DGM. (This is
\par
especially true when the CEO imposed the plan on the manager, without his agreement on
\par
its feasibility.) If the project fails, the agent blames the external factors, and the principal
\par
cannot easily disagree.
\par
The term \emph{moral hazard }describes the fact that the agent can take advantage of the
\par
principal if he desires. The CEO faces three kinds of moral hazards: (1) the DGM will bias
\par
his estimates, if the incentives and/or sanctions make this profitable for him, \textbf{(2) }he can
\par
divert resources from intended uses in order to meet performance standards imposed on
\par
him, to the detriment of the long-run vitality of the operation (one such technique is to defer
\par
expenditures for R\&D and maintenance; more subtle techniques might involve, for
\par
instance, changing the accounting methods; in essence, the agent can usually find some way
\par
to divert resources in ways the principal will not detect) and \textbf{(3) }the DGM can withhold
\par
effort, if he knows that the CEO cannot tell the difference between failure due to
\par
insufficient effort and failure due t o such extraneous factors as competitive pressure,
\par
recession, etc. In the light of the information assymetries, monitoring problems and moral
\par
hazards described, how should the CEO structure the employment relationship with his
\par
attractive: zyxwvutsr(1) if, in order to get truthful information, the CEO absolves the DGM of
\par
division managers? Three options can be identified, although none look particularly
\par
responsibility for successfully implementing a proposed strategy, the DGM has an incentive
\par
to shirk, (2) if the CEO forces the DGM to bear risk for the success or failure of the project,
\par
he will `lowball' his estimates and perhaps divert resources, and \textbf{(3) }even if the CEO can
\par
prevent the DGM from diverting resources or biasing forecasts, then by making him bear
\par
risk for the outcome, the CEO will elicit extremely risk-averse behaviour, behaviour far
\par
more risk-aversive than the corporation would want.
\par
In conclusion, our inability to structure effective employment relationships for strategic
\par
management action has three important consequences. First, managers place a great
\par
emphasis on `commitment', since `accountability' is relatively less meaningful in the
\par
strategic arena. \textbf{As }a result, it is very important that the management system encourage
\par
individuals to step forward as champions of projects which are consistent with the overallstrategy. Secondly, the manager must be able to compromise regarding the programme he
\par
\emph{Strategy Implementation }\textbf{zyx263 }zy
\par
wishes to see undertaken. \textbf{A }second-best strategy championed by someone capable and
\par
determined may be worth more than the optimum strategy backed with only lukewarm
\par
support. Thirdly, in order to sort out the true skill level and motivation of any given DGM
\par
or other subordinate, the CEO must observe him over a long period of time, on a number of
\par
different projects. The subordinate gradually develops credibility, an important
\par
consideration in the resource allocation process.
