DECISION MAKING* 12
\par
E. H. BOWMAN
\par
Massachusetts Institute of Technology
\par
This paper reports some research, ideas, and theory about managerial
\par
decision making. The first research projects dealt with are aggregate produc-
\par
tion and employment scheduling. From this is developed the idea that man-
\par
agement's own (past) decisions can be incorporated into a system of improving
\par
their present decisions. Decision rules are developed, with the coefficients in the
\par
rules derived from management's past decisions (rather than from a cost or
\par
value model). Half a dozen test cases are used to illustrate and test these
\par
ideas (theory). Some rationale about decision making in organizations and
\par
criteria surfaces is supplied to help interpret the major ideas presented.
\par
This paper reports some research in managerial decision making, as well as
\par
the ideas and a theory stemming from this research. Presented here is a combi-
\par
nation of description and prescription. It combines the talents of the manager
\par
with those of the analyst in a method and in a theory. The method is pragmatic
\par
rather than utopian in that it offers one way of starting with the managers'
\par
actual decisions and building on them to reach a better system.
\par
A referee has summarized this paper as follows: "that managerial decisions
\par
might be improved more by making them more consistent from one time to
\par
another than by approaches purporting to give 'optimal solutions' to explicit
\par
cost models .. . especially for problems where intangibles (run-out costs, delay
\par
penalties) must otherwise be estimated or assumed." Though this is a norma-
\par
tive statement, it is derived from a number of descriptive concepts (about the
\par
world in which we live) which may be of equal interest.
\par
The paper is organized essentially in the sequence in which the research was
\par
performed and the ideas generated. The problems, research, and decision rules
\par
of production scheduling are set forth first. They introduce the cause of this
\par
paper. Next, the approach of using management's own past decisions in order
\par
to improve present decisions is described. The theory is then set forth in a more
\par
general form, after which several additional empirical tests of the theory are
\par
described. Finally, the ideas are summarized. Further questions of validity,
\par
generality, and operationality must be answered by future research.
\par
