2) Experienced managers are aware of the system variables which influence
\par
these criteria.
\par
3) Managers, in their present position through a process of natural screening,
\par
make decisions, i.e., implicitly operate decision rules, with a sense and intuition
\par
which relates the variables to the criteria imperfectly-but more erratic than
\par
biased.
\par
4) Most cost or criteria surfaces as a function of the decision variables are
\par
shallow, dish-shaped at the bottom (top) and even with bias in the manager's
\par
behavior, it is the far out (variance) examples of behavior which are really
\par
expensive or damaging.
\par
5) If manager's behavior had paralleled the decision rules with their average
\par
or mean coefficients, their experience would have been better according to the
\par
their) criteria.
\par
It seems useful to attempt an explanation of why decision rules derived from
\par
management's own average behavior might yield better results than the aggre-
\par
gate behavior itself. Man seems to respond to selective cues in his environment-
\par
particular things seem to catch his attention at times (the last telephone call),
\par
while at other times it is a different set of stimuli. Not only is this selective
\par
cueing the case, but a threshold concept seems to apply. He may respond not at
\par
all up to some point and then overrespond beyond that. It is this type of be-
\par
havior which helps explain the variance in the organization's (or its manage-
\par
ment's) behavior.
\par
Departures of the decision making behavior of management from the pre-
\par
ferred results, in this sense then can be divided or factored into two components,
\par
one which in the manner of a grand average departing from some preferred
\par
figure, we call bias (which causes a relatively small criteria loss due to the dish
\par
shaped bottom of the criteria surface), and one which representing individual
\par
occurrences of experience departing from the grand average, we call variance
\par
(which causes larger criteria losses due to the individual occurrences up the sides
\par
of the criteria dish-shaped surface). It is the latter and more important com-
\par
ponent which seems to offer the tempting possibility of elimination through the
\par
use of decision rules incorporating coefficients derived from management's own
\par
recurrent behavior.
\par
What can be done with this management coefficients theory?
\par
1) It may yield fresh insight into a management problem or a decision process-
\par
it may lead to further ideas. (This is operational in an academic world.)'3
\par
a) Several approaches to the same problem are often of benefit.
\par
b) Patterns of behavior variance against the decision rule may point to missed
\par
elements in the analysis.
\par
c) It gives us a chance to see (indirectly) the criteria through the manager's
\par
eyes (or action).
