Summary
\par
The gist of the management coefficients theory is a relatively simple notion:
\par
a) In their decision making behavior, managers and/or their organizations
\par
can be conceived of as decision rule coefficient estimators, (not that they ex-
\par
plicitly are coefficient estimators).
\par
b) It is the variance in the decision making rather than the bias that hurts due
\par
to dish-shaped criteria surfaces.
\par
c) A decision rule with mean coefficients estimated from management's be-
\par
havior should be better than actual performance.
\par
d) It may be better than a rule with coefficients supplied by traditional
\par
analysis.
\par
e) Systematic and comparative studies using this idea may lead to further
\par
ideas.
