\textbf{\emph{A. }}\emph{Background and Typology}
\par
The concept of shareholder participation in the governance of the
\par
publicly held corporation presumes the need for representative govern-
\par
ment.1 \textbf{A }large organization, whatever its mission, cannot achieve its
\par
goals \textbf{by }constituting its members into an ongoing committee of the
\par
whole. Even participatory democracies, worker-owned enterprises, and
\par
cultural revolutions accept the distinction between mass and cadre, wher-
\par
ever they may at times draw the line between the two.
\par
The opposite constraint, however, is equally valid. \textbf{A }dividing line
\par
implies the existence of residual, policy-setting, ratifying, or monitoring
\par
powers in the principal-agent relationship.2 Members' participation
\par
rights may differ in private economic firms and political units, in part
