\par
Nevertheless, each criterion has its normative importance. Moreover,
\par
there is not a necessary correlation between any one criterion and differ-
\par
ent normative ends. Voice and loyalty, as well as exit, support the values
\par
associated with the principles of allocative efficiency. Likewise, exit, as
\par
well as voice and loyalty, supports the values associated with the social
\par
and political legitimacy of the corporation.
\par
These organizing visions of efficient markets for shares and of effi-
\par
cient markets for management are of limited utility because corporations
\par
are more like people than like money. They come in a large variety of
\par
sizes and shapes, of ownership diffusion and concentration, and of pas-
\par
sive and active member involvement.' I This variety confounds simplistic
\par
efforts to impose a "rule of markets" on the upper, public end and a "rule
\par
of contract" on the lower end of what, after all, represents not a dichot-
\par
omy but a spectrum of firms. The reality of corporate diversity also has
\par
institutional implications." Legislatures may be emperors over rules,
\par
but courts are emperors over events. By definition events fall between
\par
rules whenever, as is usual, the rules are more abstract and less detailed
\par
than the events they purport to control. Since courts cannot work well
\par
with simple, dichotomous rules or models unless the courts have a rea-
\par
sonable amount of "judicial discretion,"' 12 litigation shapes and tests rules
\par
as it applies them.13
\par
The following Article is a review of shareholder participation in cor-
\par
porate governance and of shareholder relief from coerced acceptance of
\par
majority and managerial decisions. It represents an effort to provide a
\par
status report on that reciprocal relationship. It examines that relation-
\par
ship as it exists today and as it might be predicted and prescribed to look
\par
tomorrow.
