\emph{C. Disenfranchisement and the Proper Concept of Statutory Models}
\par
There appears to be a trend against maximum participation for nor-
\par
mally nonvoting shareholders in decisions about structural transac-
\par
tions.22 Both those in favor of participation and those against it use the
\par
statutory material to their end. The disenfranchisers, however, seem to
\par
be prevailing on both questions of consistency and policy. In deciding
\par
for disenfrachisement, some courts take inarticulate refuge in the literal
\par
application of an underlying enabling statute while others use literal stat-
\par
utory language in the service of their articulated version of policy.
\par
The reason for these tendencies in statutory construction, I believe,
\par
lies less in the substantive policies themselves than in the ever-increasing
\par
dysfunctionality of ever more elaborate statutes.23 The proponents of
\par
minimal shareholder participation first exploited inadvertent statutory
\par
gaps to create what originally were simply loopholes. They then quickly
\par
came to argue that those irregularities represented policy choices, choices
\par
that in turn justified the elimination of remaining protective provisions
\par
now "out of sync" with a "properly understood" statute. Examples of
\par
this evolution in the field of shareholder voting rights include the well-
\par
known Delaware approach to the de facto merger doctrine2 4 and Man-
\par
ning's treatment of the appraisal remedy. 25
\par
This kind of approach gained momentum when Delaware jurispru-
