\par
experienced neither to fear looking behind the screen of expertise nor to
\par
disparage the invisible hand that can convert even the selfish private liti-
\par
gant into an agent for the public good.31
\par
The following review of specific legal issues produces rather particu-
\par
larized critiques and suggestions. Taken all together, however, it will
\par
suggest that the perceptible current erosion of shareholder participation
\par
in corporate governance-and especially in corporate restructuring deci-
\par
sions-has profound consequences for the effective functioning of corpo-
\par
rations and for their socially perceived legitimacy. These developments
\par
should provoke concern from proponents of pure economic efficiency
\par
and from proponents of less precisely formulated corporate constitu-
\par
tional models. Thus both viewpoints are sketched and taken into
\par
account as these developments are considered in this Article.
\par
II
\par
GOVERNANCE
\par
The large topic of shareholder participation in corporate governance
\par
exists in an often overlooked procedural context, that of disclosure and
\par
information flow, which is the subject of Section \emph{A. }The major part of
\par
this Article is devoted to problems of shareholder voting rights. That
\par
topic is broken down into the special problems of special shares and the
\par
general problems of direct and indirect erosion or elimination of the vot-
\par
ing rights of residual equity holders. These developments are explored in
\par
Section \emph{B. }Section \emph{C }takes up the recent proliferation of nonvoting com-
\par
mon shares. The extent of shareholder participation also is influenced by
\par
(and premised upon) the existence of adequate dissenters' remedies,
\par
which are discussed in Section \emph{D. }Section \emph{E }reviews the most recent judi-
\par
cial inroads on the business judgment rule.
