\emph{B. The Exclusion of Shareholders from Decisionmaking}
\par
Perhaps more important than the limitations on information are the
\par
restrictions on shareholder participation itself. The central theme of this
\par
Article is the substantial erosion of shareholder participation in struc-
\par
tural decisions affecting the fate of their investments. To some degree the
\par
legislatures are responsible, but to a far greater degree it is the courts that
\par
are relegating shareholders to the questionable role of bystanders. Share-
\par
holders frequently have been left with exercising merely ceremonial func-
\par
tions in decisions affecting not only corporate operations but also the
\par
central owner-enterprise relationship itself. In many instances, courts
\par
have limited or eliminated the shareholders' ultimate authority over
\par
structural decisions via an expanded definition of "business judgment."
\par
This part critically explores this development in four separate contexts:
\par
1) the elimination, by contract and statutory interpretation, of the role of
\par
preferred shareholders in corporate reorganizations; 2) the limitation of
\par
the right of common shareholders to vote on a variety of merger-related
\par
transactions; 3) the approval of hostile-takeover defensive tactics that
\par
give management the discretionary right to trigger supermajority share-
\par
holder vote requirements; and 4) the approval of managerial commit-
\par
ments to third parties to disregard possible shareholder acceptance of
\par
statutory merger proposals.
\par
To begin this review with the treatment of preferred stock may seem
\par
suggest that nonmanagement candidates for the board of a nonprofit corporation must in all cases
\par
have superior or equal advantages in an electoral battle. We suggest only that nonmanagement
\par
candidates must receive treatment that is fair when compared to that accorded management
\par
candidates."); \textbf{\emph{see also }}Durkin v. National Bank, \textbf{772 F.2d 55, 59 (3d }Cir. \textbf{1985) }(holding that the
