\emph{L Stock Classes and Their Relation to Participation Rights}
\par
The first problem in the panoply of issues relating to shareholder
\par
participation in structural decisions arises from the categorization of
\par
shareholders on the basis of stock class and series. If enterprise-financing
\par
plans call for a more differentiated structure than simply issuing shares
\par
representing residual rights to profit and return of capital, legislatures
\par
and courts need to address governance and financial rights of the new
\par
elements of the stock structure. Corporate governance issues affecting
\par
preferred stockholders are a convenient focal point for this discussion.
\par
Preferred stock comes in many guises, and no single Platonic form
\par
underlies its variety. 6 1 Whatever its attributes (its "rights, preferences,
\par
and privileges," in the jargon), preferred stock is quintessentially a mat-
\par
ter of contract. If any deviation from the attributes of the residual com-
\par
mon stock concept is desired, the contract must specify it. Of course this
\par
is only an imaginary situation. Preferred stock not only needs to be
\par
defined by contract, it is defined by contract-by definition, one is
\par
tempted to say.62 Even so, the potentially problematical diminution of
\par
preferred shareholder participation in corporate governance stems not
\par
from the fact that participation is almost totally a matter of contract, but
\par
from the courts' occasionally dubious approach to contract interpreta-
\par
tion, especially in the shadow of the partial but incomplete regulation of
\par
some participation rights by statute.
\par
