\emph{D. The Appraisal Remedy as a Means to Exclude Equitable Relief}
\par
The element of voice-the ability to exercise or deliver control-is
\par
not the only attribute at issue in this discussion of the division of powers
\par
in corporate governance. Adequate conditions of exit-the price avail-
\par
able for cashed-out or exchanged shares in reorganizations-not only
\par
lurk in the background of the preceding discussion of control, but are
\par
directly at issue When a dispute erupts over the financial terms of a
\par
merger. Dissenters from a collective decision often are granted a second
\par
round by way of an appraisal remedy.2 " At the same time, availability
\par
of that remedy can be used as a reason to downplay the danger that
\par
loyalty problems may taint both the voting and the price-setting
\par
processes.
\par
\emph{L Introduction and Background}
\par
One explanation of the development of the appraisal remedy is that
\par
fundamental changes such as mergers originally could not be effected
\par
without the unanimous consent of all holders of voting stock. The
\par
appraisal remedy for dissenters from such transactions was the price paid
\par
for acceptance of majoritarian decisions.2 0 5 In a sense, we have arrived a
\par
century later at a new version of that bargain. Today, the merger of a
\par
controlled but only partially owned subsidiary into its dominant parent is
\par
on its way to being immunized from conflict-of-interests challenges (and
\par
the equitable relief available thereunder) in return for a generous
\par
appraisal remedy.
\par
The phenomenon of "conflict-of-interests reorganization" is preva-
\par
lent. It has particularly great potential to cause disputes because of its
\par
role in the second stage of a takeover transaction.2 0 6 Both original and
\par
second-stage conflict-of-interests mergers share the attribute that, absent
\par
legal constraints, the parent shareholder is in a position to impose the
\par
terms of the merger exchange and to force their acceptance. In the latter
\par
case, however, the adversarial posture of the dominating parent company
\par
already was signalled by the earlier takeover bid. Nontendering target-
\par
company shareholders are thus to some extent on notice that they cannot
\par
expect a solicitous attitude from the new majority shareholder. Conse-
