\textbf{CONCLUSION}
\par
State corporation law is in a state of flux, partly caused by, and
\par
partly a cause of, a crisis of nerve. In the central sector of shareholder
\par
governance rights, traditional enabling-law philosophy, excessively
\par
aggressive efficiency rationales, and powerfully focused managerialist
\par
strategy have combined to weaken an already inarticulate faith in the
\par
primacy of owner over manager.266 The efficient capital market and the
\par
market for control, however, not only are inadequate substitutes for a
\par
firm sense of the right relationship(s) between owner and manager in
\par
real-time, real-world institutional settings, 267 but are themselves savaged
\par
by many of the new arrangements discussed, above. At least some of
\par
these arrangements mock efficiency in the name of efficiency, and bury
\par
shareholder participation in the name of praising it.
\par
This Article began as a status report on current developments affect-
\par
ing shareholder participation in corporate governance. As it turned to
\par
more detailed and more critical explanation and evaluation of specific
