The following review of specific legal issues produces rather particu-
\par
larized critiques and suggestions. Taken all together, however, it will
\par
suggest that the perceptible current erosion of shareholder participation
\par
in corporate governance-and especially in corporate restructuring deci-
\par
sions-has profound consequences for the effective functioning of corpo-
\par
rations and for their socially perceived legitimacy. These developments
\par
should provoke concern from proponents of pure economic efficiency
\par
and from proponents of less precisely formulated corporate constitu-
\par
tional models. Thus both viewpoints are sketched and taken into ac-
\par
count as these developments are considered in this Article.
\par
II
\par
GOVERNANCE
\par
The large topic of shareholder participation in corporate governance
\par
exists in an often overlooked procedural context, that of disclosure and
\par
information flow, which is the subject of Section \textbf{\emph{A. }}The major part of
\par
this Article is devoted to problems of shareholder voting rights. That
\par
topic is broken down into the special problems of special shares and the
\par
general problems of direct and indirect erosion or elimination of the vot-
\par
ing rights of residual equity holders. These developments are explored in
\par
Section \emph{B. }Section \emph{C }takes up the recent proliferation of nonvoting com-
\par
mon shares. The extent of shareholder participation also is influenced \textbf{by}
\par
(and premised upon) the existence of adequate dissenters' remedies,
\par
which are discussed in Section \textbf{\emph{D. }}Section \emph{E }reviews the most recent judi-
\par
cial inroads on the business judgment rule.
\par
\textbf{\emph{A. }}\emph{Informed Participation}
\par
Informed participation is as important as participation per se. Fur-
\par
thermore, the distinction between passive participation in a simple ratifi-
\par
cation mode and active participation in the agenda-setting sense is an
\par
essential aspect of the overall topic of shareholder participation in corpo-
\par
rate governance. This section discusses developments affecting these as-
\par
pects of participation by shareholders.
\par
1. Information Requirements
\par
A preliminary, often-slighted topic is the level of information corpo-
\par
rations must provide to enable informed shareholder participation in the
\par
decisionmaking allocated to them. It is not necessary here to review the
\par
well-known federal regime of periodic reporting and reporting via the
\par
proxy materials.3" It is useful, however, to mention some recent state
\par
law developments that aim at providing adequately prepared and ade-
