\par
launched during the Carter administration has all but failed, despite the
\par
fact that the SEC is on record as considering the director-nomination
\par
process to be within its enabling authority.5 " Interestingly, some states
\par
have moved towards granting nonmanagement candidates easier access
\par
to the nomination and election machinery. For now, however, this ac-
\par
cess has been confined to the slightly different situation of nonprofit cor-
\par
porations. For these corporations, several courts have discovered-or
\par
invented-common law rights of "fair process." In other words, the
\par
courts have required fair starting positions for management and dissident
\par
slates as a procedural requirement inherent in the concept of membership
\par
corporations.59
\par
This fairness requirement has proved instrumentally difficult to
\par
adapt to the business corporation, where one share, not one member,
\par
equals one vote in the election and usually in the nomination process. To
\par
date none of the proposals for direct nomination as of right, with or with-
\par
out some threshold requirement based on percentage of ownership, has
\par
made any headway. We remain where the jawboning of the SEC and the
\par
New York Stock Exchange has left us: with a slight push towards the
\par
use of a separate nominating committee of the board, composed of
\par
outside directors, who ought to be open to nomination suggestions.6 "
