\emph{B. The Exclusion of Shareholders from Decisionmaking}
\par
Perhaps more important than the limitations on information are the
\par
restrictions on shareholder participation itself. The central theme of this
\par
Article is the substantial erosion of shareholder participation in struc-
\par
tural decisions affecting the fate of their investments. To some degree the
\par
legislatures are responsible, but to a far greater degree it is the courts that
\par
are relegating shareholders to the questionable role of bystanders. Share-
\par
holders frequently have been left with exercising merely ceremonial func-
\par
tions in decisions affecting not only corporate operations but also the
\par
central owner-enterprise relationship itself. In many instances, courts
\par
have limited or eliminated the shareholders' ultimate authority over
\par
structural decisions via an expanded definition of "business judgment."
\par
This part critically explores this development in four separate contexts:
\par
1) the elimination, by contract and statutory interpretation, of the role of
\par
preferred shareholders in corporate reorganizations; 2) the limitation of
\par
the right of common shareholders to vote on a variety of merger-related
\par
transactions; 3) the approval of hostile-takeover defensive tactics that
\par
give management the discretionary right to trigger supermajority share-
\par
holder vote requirements; and 4) the approval of managerial commit-
\par
ments to third parties to disregard possible shareholder acceptance of
\par
statutory merger proposals.
\par
To begin this review with the treatment of preferred stock may seem
\par
out of sequence; it is the treatment of voting stock in major structural
\par
transactions that underlies current debates about the division of powers
\par
between management and shareholders. On the other hand, what has
\par
happened to the explicitly contractual rights and attributes of preferred
\par
stock foreshadows the disenfranchisement dangers facing common stock
\par
in statutory-merger and takeover-bid situations. Preferred stock is in this
\par
sense a bellwether for all stock, voting as well as nonvoting. A descrip-
\par
tion and critique of recent judicial treatment of the participation claims
\par
of preferred shareholders will clarify the frame of reference for the later
\par
discussion of analogous developments in the common stock area.
\par
1. Stock Classes and Their Relation to Participation Rights
\par
The first problem in the panoply of issues relating to shareholder
\par
participation in structural decisions arises from the categorization of
\par
shareholders on the basis of stock class and series. If enterprise-financing
\par
plans call for a more differentiated structure than simply issuing shares
\par
representing residual rights to profit and return of capital, legislatures
\par
and courts need to address governance and financial rights of the new
\par
elements of the stock structure. Corporate governance issues affecting
\par
preferred stockholders are a convenient focal point for this discussion.
\par
Preferred stock comes in many guises, and no single Platonic form\textbf{\emph{CANADA-UNITED STATES }}\emph{LAW }\textbf{\emph{JOURNAL}}
\par
\textbf{Vol. 13:391 1988}
\par
