\par
Forestalling arrangements such as lockups and closed-shop agree-
\par
ments are defended primarily \textbf{by }extending to the statutory merger arena
\par
the mentioned "anti-auction" rationale, which originally was developed
\par
to constrain target management defensive maneuvers in hostile tender-
\par
offer-situations. 1 63 That rationale suggests that in time, and overall,
\par
higher offers for control will emanate from first bidders if acquisition
\par
targets agree not to solicit or even to entertain bids from later competing
\par
bidders. These second bidders may be freeriding on the first bidder's pre-
\par
sumed significant and already sunk investment in the search for and eval-
\par
uation of potential acquisition targets. It is argued that this may produce
\par
both a shortrun and a longrun type of inefficiency. In the short run, the
\par
freeriding bidder may win, though its use of the target's resources might
\par
be less efficient than would be their use \textbf{by }the stymied first bidder. In the
\par
long run, any reduction in future search investments is inefficient because
\par
the number of takeover bids may decrease, and with it their role in moni-
\par
toring the stock markets for suboptimally performing targets.'
