164 This argument for the anti-auction rule and its premise of significant search costs assumes
\par
too much. First, not all-and perhaps few-mergers find their impetus in the desire to realize some
\par
benefit only disclosed by costly research. The fact of a merger indicates nothing about whether such
\par
search expenses have been incurred. Rather, as is true of transactions of all sorts, the parties simply
\par
may have differing preexisting evaluations of the best use of the resource (i.e., its real value to them);
\par
such judgments neither necessarily require cost in creation nor guarantee that anyone involved will
\par
generate the optimal "synergistic" effect. Thus, the anti-auction rule is inappropriate in cases which
\par
generated no search costs to be protected. Moreover, one suspects it is rather easy for a first bidder
\par
to conjure up such "costs" even if the anti-auction theory is fine-tuned to turn on their actual exist-
\par
ence. To the extent the anti-auction rule is correct about search costs, it is overstated as a general
\par
legal rule because it posits such costs in all instances.
\par
Further, inasmuch as overall economic efficiency is the touchstone for the anti-auction theorists,
\par
their arguments are undercut to the extent later bidders are able to merge with the target and pro-
\par
