1. Introduction and Background
\par
One explanation of the development of the appraisal remedy is that
\par
fundamental changes such as mergers originally could not be effected
\par
without the unanimous consent of all holders of voting stock. The ap-
\par
praisal remedy for dissenters from such transactions was the price paid
\par
for acceptance of majoritarian decisions.2 {$^\circ$}5 In a sense, we have arrived a
\par
century later at a new version of that bargain. Today, the merger of a
\par
controlled but only partially owned subsidiary into its dominant parent is
\par
on its way to being immunized from conflict-of-interests challenges (and
\par
the equitable relief available thereunder) in return for a generous ap-
\par
praisal remedy.
\par
The phenomenon of "conflict-of-interests reorganization" is preva-
\par
lent. It has particularly great potential to cause disputes because of its
\par
role in the second stage of a takeover transaction." 6 Both original and
\par
second-stage conflict-of-interests mergers share the attribute that, absent
\par
legal constraints, the parent shareholder is in a position to impose the
\par
terms of the merger exchange and to force their acceptance. In the latter
\par
case, however, the adversarial posture of the dominating parent company
\par
already was signalled by the earlier takeover bid. Nontendering target-
\par
company shareholders are thus to some extent on notice that they cannot
\par
expect a solicitous attitude from the new majority shareholder. Conse-
\par
quently, courts well may be hesitant to impose on such dominant new
\par
shareholders the fiduciary duties presumably deemed appropriate in the
\par
case of an original conflict-of-interests merger proposed and pushed
\par
through by a controlling parent company shareholder in a longstanding,
\par
steady-state situation.
\par
The statutory treatment of conflict-of-interests reorganizations is
\par
relatively new. Only California has explicitly connected enabling provi-
\par
sions with the appraisal remedy. 07 Mergers effectuated by means of the
\par
surviving company's control position are treated differently from mergers
\par
between entities at arm's length. Dissenters from control-position merg-
\par
ers have the right to equitable and legal relief, and also benefit from the
