\textbf{Typically, political decision making involves the concomitant problem of deciding how to}
\par
\textbf{use advice. Advice can reduce uncertainty about outcomes, but it is often costly to obtain and}
\par
\textbf{assimilate, and is itself subject to uncertainty and error. This paper explores how a rational}
\par
\textbf{decision maker uses imperfect advice. Using only the assumption of utility maximization,}
\par
\textbf{along with a specification of exactly how knowledge and advice are "imperfect," it is possible}
\par
\textbf{to derive some of the initial assumptions of cognitive and bounded-rationality models. Also}
\par
\textbf{changes in the decision-making environment can be connected to changes in how advice is}
\par
\textbf{used, thereby providing theoretical predictions about political behavior. In particular it is}
\par
\textbf{shown here that, under certain reasonable circumstances, the rational decision maker should}
\par
\textbf{engage in selective exposure or "bolstering." These results do not depend upon any cost}
\par
\textbf{advantage or inherent value in biased advice.}
\par
\textbf{INTRODI)'CTI(N}
\par
\textbf{In making complicated decisions, political actors face the necessity of}
\par
\textbf{using advice. Given the scarcity of time and the need for expertise beyond}
\par
\textbf{the decision maker's capabilities, advisors are called upon to distill com-}
\par
\textbf{plex information about alternatives into simpler recommendations about}
\par
\textbf{the choice to be made. A subsidiary problem for the decision maker, then,}
\par
\textbf{is how to make the best use of available information, or how to choose}
\par
\textbf{advisors. The purpose of this study is to build a rational choice model of the}
\par
