\par
\textbf{use of advice, and in particular to characterize the role of biased advisors in}
\par
\textbf{this process. As we will see, a neutral or even-handed advisor is not always}
\par
\textbf{the best kind.}
\par
\textbf{How Information is Used}
\par
\textbf{Studies of political decision making in fields from voting to foreign}
\par
\textbf{policymaking examine how political actors make use of information. One}
\par
\textbf{frequent conclusion is that decision makers use information poorly, and in}
\par
\textbf{particular that they do not use it objectively. Instead, they engage in}
\par
\textbf{"bolstering," attending too much to sources that share the decision maker's}
\par
\textbf{own predispositions. Likewise, through "defensive avoidance," they give}
\par
\textbf{too little consideration to discrepant information (e.g., Janis and Mann,}
\par
\textbf{1977, p. 205; Jervis, 1976). Related phenomena, termed "selective atten-}
\par
\textbf{tion" or "selective exposure," have long been subjects of inquiry in experi-}
\par
\textbf{mental psychology (Wicklund and Brehm, 1976, pp. 170-90).}
\par
\textbf{These phenomena have often served as examples of the way in which}
\par
\textbf{political (and other) decision makers fall short of the rational ideal. One}
\par
\textbf{implication of this viewpoint is that, if we want to model and predict the}
\par
\textbf{behavior of political decision makers, we ought to rely upon cognitive and}
\par
\textbf{bounded-rationality approaches (Simon, 1947; Steinbruner, 1974). On the}
\par
\textbf{other hand, some psychologists have looked upon selectivity phenomena}
\par
\textbf{as purposeful responses to the complexity of the environment (Broadbent,}
\par
\textbf{1958; Moray and Fitter, 1973).}
\par
\textbf{The present study explores this alternative approach, using a model}
\par
\textbf{based entirely on utility maximization. As with simple, perfect-information}
\par
\textbf{models of rational choice, we use only one behavioral assumption, namely}
\par
\textbf{that the individual decision maker is a rational actor. As in the bounded-}
\par
\textbf{rationality approach, we assume that the actor does not have and cannot}
\par
\textbf{obtain complete information. Here, however, we maintain the assumption}
\par
\textbf{of utility maximization throughout. In principle, we can then predict how}
\par
\textbf{changes in the objective political world cause changes in decision makers'}
\par
\textbf{use of advice and information. In this sense, this study explores the politics,}
\par
\textbf{rather than the psychology, of political behavior. As it turns out, more can}
\par
\textbf{be explained in this manner than is commonly thought.}
\par
\textbf{The type of model used in this paper represents a significant gain in}
\par
\textbf{theoretical parsimony over the more standard bounded-rationality}
\par
\textbf{approach. Our model is grounded only on the assumption of utility maxim-}
\par
\textbf{ization; it adds no initial presumptions about ways in which the decision}
\par
\textbf{maker copes with an environment of imperfect information. Instead, we}
\par
\textbf{derive the optimal means of coping from explicit assumptions about how}
\par
\textbf{information and knowledge are imperfect. These imperfections are central}
\par
\textbf{to the bounded-rationality model as well. There, however, they are not532 JOURNAL OF POLITICS, VOL. 47, 1985}
\par
\textbf{made rigorous, and the actor's responses to them are fixed by assumption.}
\par
\textbf{The goal of a "full-rationality" model like that used here is to derive}
\par
\textbf{boundedly rational behavior in such a way that the theory itself, initially}
\par
\textbf{independent of stylized empirical facts, yields predictions about how}
\par
\textbf{behavior should respond to changes in environmental conditions.}
\par
\textbf{Using this model we will be able to say how a rational actor, in some}
\par
\textbf{simple decision problems, ought to use information. The results are some-}
\par
\textbf{times counterintuitive. There are, for example, perfectly ordinary circum-}
\par
\textbf{stances in which the rational decision maker prefers a biased source of}
\par
\textbf{information over a neutral one, even if the neutral one is just as easy to}
\par
\textbf{consult, and even though the biased information has no inherent value to}
\par
\textbf{the decision maker outside of its usefulness in making a good decision.}
\par
\textbf{When the decision maker is predisposed to a particular belief, this behavior}
\par
\textbf{includes a desire for information from sources known to share that predis-}
\par
\textbf{position. In other words, selective attention or bolstering would occur even}
\par
\textbf{in purely rational decision making, and it would vary predictably in}
\par
\textbf{response to the objective, nonpsychological characteristics of the decision}
\par
\textbf{problem.}
\par
\textbf{Breadth of Application}
\par
\textbf{The theoretical model presented below applies to a wide variety of}
\par
\textbf{real-life political situations. The executive official making a policy decision}
\par
\textbf{is the most direct application; the decision could be anything from a minor}
\par
\textbf{detail of implementation to a major regulatory edict or foreign policy}
\par
\textbf{action. Prior to deciding, the executive may consult advisors or conduct}
\par
\textbf{policy analysis. Such problems have been addressed extensively by politi-}
\par
\textbf{cal scientists such as Allison (1971), George (1980), and Jervis (1976). The}
\par
\textbf{relevant questions for our purposes are how much and what kind of advice}
\par
\textbf{or analysis the decision maker should choose, given constraints on time and}
\par
\textbf{resources.}
\par
\textbf{A similar breadth of applications exists for the decision problems of}
\par
\textbf{legislators who rely, for instance, on staff members (Malbin, 1980), and of}
\par
\textbf{judges who listen to expert testimony (Horowitz, 1977, pp. 45-51). Even at}
\par
\textbf{the level of mass political behavior, the elements of this model come into}
\par
\textbf{play (Lazarsfeld, Berelson, and Gaudet, 1944). Voters, for example, are}
\par
\textbf{uncertain about which candidate will bring about the most desirable state}
\par
\textbf{of the world. They may spend time and effort digesting more information}
\par
\textbf{from the news media, the opinions of friends, and elsewhere to improve}
\par
\textbf{their ability to make a voting decision. Weatherford (1983) has taken just}
\par
\textbf{such a viewpoint in his empirical study of economic voting, in which he}
\par
\textbf{considers "the voter as information processor" (p. 161). Kuklinski, Metlay,}
\par
\textbf{and Kay (1982) took a similar approach in their analysis of voter decision}
\par
\textbf{making on the complex issue of nuclear energy.BIASED INFORMATION IN POLITICAL ADVICE 533}
\par
\textbf{The analysis presented here fleshes out theoretically this idea of the}
\par
\textbf{political actor as a rational decision maker making rational use of imperfect}
\par
\textbf{and costly information. It demonstrates that, in all areas of political activ-}
\par
\textbf{ity, there are circumstances in which it is useful to rely on biased advice.}
\par
\textbf{More generally, it suggests a powerful way to conceptualize and analyze}
\par
\textbf{the simultaneous problems of how to use advice and how to make policy}
\par
\textbf{choices.}
\par
\textbf{Outline}
\par
\textbf{The first section below presents a model of the decision maker's problem}
\par
\textbf{and examines the properties of information sources. The following section}
\par
\textbf{is an analysis of a situation in which an initially ignorant decision maker has}
\par
\textbf{two sources of advice, only one of which can be consulted before a final}
\par
\textbf{choice between two policy alternatives is made. Even in this simple prob-}
\par
\textbf{lem, the decision maker may prefer information that is biased. The third}
\par
\textbf{section examines the same problem for a decision maker who begins the}
\par
\textbf{process with a predisposition in favor of one alternative and against the}
\par
\textbf{other. If the predisposition is strong enough, "objective" or evenhanded}
\par
\textbf{information is of no value, while information that is biased in favor of the}
\par
\textbf{already preferred alternative helps the decision maker achieve higher}
\par
\textbf{expected utility. The final section discusses what this analysis implies about}
\par
\textbf{how political advice is used and how we ought to model limited-}
\par
\textbf{information decision problems.}
\par
\textbf{Although the main sections of this paper involve a certain amount of}
\par
\textbf{algebraic and probabilistic calculation, an effort has been made to accom-}
\par
\textbf{modate the reader who is not inclined to grind through it. Each section}
\par
\textbf{begins with a nontechnical summary of the assumptions and results that}
\par
\textbf{follow. In addition, the principal results are restated verbally alongside}
\par
\textbf{each mathematical presentation. Finally, the behavior of the probability}
\par
\textbf{distributions and their updating processes, which are central to this analy-}
\par
\textbf{sis, is illustrated in diagrams.'}
\par
\textbf{Tie MODEL}
\par
\textbf{Description}
\par
\textbf{This study will address the following generic problem: a decision maker}
\par
\textbf{must choose between several alternatives. For ease of presentation, we will}
\par
\textbf{use only two alternatives, but the model's conclusions apply to any reason-}
\par
\textbf{O On the other hand, the messy details of standard calculations have in several places been}
