\textbf{tions of how well the policies would serve the goals. These advisors are}
\par
\textbf{basically truthful, but their advice is subject to certain mistakes and biases.}
\par
\textbf{The official knows generally how the biases and mistakes are generated,}
\par
\textbf{but cannot retrospectively unscramble the advice to learn exactly the}
\par
\textbf{underlying truth. Assuming that there are political and resource costs that}
\par
\textbf{prevent the official from freely consulting all possible advisors on every}
\par
\textbf{problem, what kind of advisors should be consulted before a policy choice}
\par
\textbf{is made? In particular, what sorts of known imperfections in advice are}
\par
\textbf{admissible or even desirable?}
\par
\textbf{In the model below, we abstract away the decision maker's or advisors'}
\par
\textbf{consideration of any actual physical or social outcomes of the policies. We}
\par
\textbf{refer only to the utility that will result for the decision maker, who may}
\par
\textbf{have any combination of selfish, altruistic, or organizational motives. For}
\par
\textbf{present purposes, this is a reasonable abstraction of a more general advi-}
\par
\textbf{sory process. Indeed, in some cases real political advice does not go far}
\par
\textbf{beyond predicting the quality of outcomes, rather than describing the}
\par
\textbf{outcomes in detail. The conclusions presented below apply, in principle, to}
\par
\textbf{more complex cases as well as to the simple one that is explicitly modeled.}
\par
\textbf{The model assumes throughout that the advice from any source consists}
\par
\textbf{only of a pronouncement that the outcome of an alternative will be either}
\par
\textbf{"good" or "bad." This feature represents the basic nature of advice, a}
\par
\textbf{distillation of complex reality into a simple recommendation. In order to}
\par
\textbf{analyze clearly the decision maker's learning process, we portray the}
\par
\textbf{advisor as giving an opinion about each policy alternative rather than}
\par
\textbf{merely as recommending a best policy.}
\par
\textbf{The advisor's pronouncement is assumed accurate in a probabilistic}
\par
\textbf{sense: the higher the true utility of a policy, the higher the probability that}
\par
\textbf{an advisor will predict it to be "good." However, advisors may differ in theBIASED INFORMATION IN POLITICAL ADVICE 535}
\par
\textbf{way their recommendations depend on the truth. For example, one source}
\par
\textbf{may be very unlikely to call a certain policy "good" even if that policy's true}
\par
\textbf{utility is high. Such a source is "biased" against that policy alternative. For}
\par
\textbf{another advisor, the probability of calling an alternative "good" may}
\par
\textbf{increase more rapidly with true utility; this makes the advisor almost}
\par
\textbf{certain to say "good" if the true utility is high, "bad" if it is low, and equally}
\par
\textbf{likely to say either if utility is in the middle. This advisor is more neutral, or}
\par
\textbf{less biased, than the first.}
\par
\textbf{Throughout, we make the key assumption that the decision maker, either}
\par
\textbf{through previous experience or objective data, always knows in advance}
\par
\textbf{exactly how the advisor's pronouncements depend, probabilistically, upon}
\par
\textbf{true utility. In particular, the advisors' biases are known. But the decision}
\par
\textbf{maker cannot know in advance what the advisor will say in a given case,}
\par
\textbf{and what random (or seemingly random) element may have colored the}
\par
\textbf{advisor's recommendation can never be learned.}
\par
\textbf{The remainder of this section makes precise the model just described.}
\par
