\textbf{Discussion}
\par
\textbf{The model outlined in this section is just one narrow example of a}
\par
\textbf{situation in which costly and imperfect advice is used to make a decision.}
\par
\textbf{However, it has several important features common to all such problems.}
\par
\textbf{First, new information serves only to alleviate the decision maker's uncer-}
\par
\textbf{tainty, not eliminate it. Second, a decision maker may have some beliefs}
\par
\textbf{prior to gathering advice, and if so, he places some weight on those prior}
\par
\textbf{beliefs even after hearing the new information. Third, advice consists of a}
\par
\textbf{summary or distillation of a more complicated reality unobservable to the}
\par
\textbf{decision maker. Finally, advice may be right or wrong.}
\par
\textbf{The notion of bias used here is not the only possible one. It does not}
\par
\textbf{capture everything we usually mean when we think of bias, and it involves}
\par
\textbf{some features not necessarily part of bias in the everyday sense. What the}
\par
\textbf{model does portray is a certain tradeoff in the accuracy of advice. The}
\par
\textbf{tradeoff is between accuracy about extreme conditions, when true utility is}
\par
\textbf{very high or low, and inaccuracy about more moderate conditions. In real}
\par
\textbf{life such a tradeoff often occurs when the inaccuracy appears in the form of}
\par
\textbf{bias. In this model, when an alternative is moderately good, the advisor}
\par
\textbf{biased against it still calls it bad. The alternative must be very good for such}
\par
\textbf{an advisor to admit it. But the model does not allow for simple "reverse542 JOURNAL OF POLITICS, VOL. 47, 1985}
\par
\textbf{barometer" biased advice, for bias that simply overstates by some constant}
\par
\textbf{the quality of an outcome or for bias that is not mitigated by the true quality}
\par
\textbf{of the alternative, no matter how extreme.}
\par
\textbf{This model portrays advice simply as a good or bad pronouncement}
\par
\textbf{about an unobserved utility value. Mathematically, the results appear to be}
\par
\textbf{generalizable to any situation in which the accuracy of biased advice is}
\par
\textbf{greatest for extreme values of an underlying true characteristic. In the real}
\par
\textbf{world, this condition is most likely to hold when advising consists of}
\par
\textbf{distilling a complicated reality into a simpler summary judgment. The most}
\par
\textbf{direct example of such advising is the use of experts by policymakers. But}
\par
\textbf{even nonexpert advice is often of the same nature, due to both time}
\par
\textbf{pressures placed upon the decision maker and the efficiency of having}
\par
\textbf{advisors specialize in understanding certain problems.}
\par
\textbf{Thus, although the model considerably simplifies the complicated world}
\par
\textbf{of real political advising, it captures many of the interesting features of such}
\par
\textbf{problems. The concrete results derived below are expressed in terms of the}
\par
\textbf{parameters of the present model. If even this simple model generates such}
\par
\textbf{effects, there is every reason to believe that corresponding phenomena}
\par
\textbf{manifest themselves in the more complex real world. And aside from}
\par
\textbf{presenting these specific results, the analysis carried out here serves to}
\par
\textbf{illustrate how modeling of other information and learning processes might}
\par
\textbf{be carried out in a rational choice framework.}
\par
\textbf{OPTIMAL BIAS IN SOURCES}
\par
\textbf{Description}
\par
\textbf{In this section, the decision maker faces a choice between two policy}
\par
\textbf{alternatives whose utility is initially unknown. The option is then of choos-}
\par
\textbf{ing between them immediately or consulting a single advisor and then}
\par
\textbf{choosing. The advisor offers an opinion on both alternatives, pronouncing}
\par
\textbf{each one either good or bad.}
\par
\textbf{What are the characteristics of the best advisor for this purpose? If we}
\par
\textbf{consider only advisors who are neutral toward alternative 1, the best would}
\par
\textbf{be one having a considerable bias against alternative 2. That is, other things}
\par
\textbf{equal, the decision maker using an information source biased in this way}
\par
\textbf{against alternative 2 will choose more accurately than one who uses an}
\par
\textbf{unbiased source. The size of this optimal bias can be described as follows:}
\par
\textbf{for such a source, the initially ignorant decision maker estimates that there}
\par
\textbf{is only a 14.5 percent chance that the source will pronounce alternative 2}
\par
\textbf{good, while there is a 50 percent chance that the source will pronounce}
\par
\textbf{alternative 1 good. The advantage, in terms of utility value, of using this}
\par
\textbf{source instead of an unbiased one is around 2 percent of the differenceBIASED INFORMATION IN POLITICAL ADVICE 543}
\par
\textbf{between the lowest possible utility level and the highest possible utility}
\par
\textbf{level.}
\par
\textbf{To put it another way, if the decision maker in this problem has a choice}
\par
\textbf{among several advisors each unbiased toward alternative 1, she should, if}
\par
\textbf{rational, prefer to get advice from a source with a bias against alternative 2.}
\par
\textbf{This conclusion does not depend in any way on any cost advantages or}
\par
\textbf{inherent value in the biased advice.}
\par
