\textbf{Discussion}
\par
\textbf{To understand this result intuitively, consider what the decision maker}
\par
\textbf{gains and loses in using an optimally biased advisor instead of an unbiased}
\par
\textbf{one. The biased advisor is more likely to say that alternative 1 is "good" and}
\par
\textbf{alternative 2 "bad" when in reality alternative 2 has the higher utility; this is}
\par
\textbf{a loss for the decision maker. At the same time, it becomes safer to respond}
\par
\textbf{to the information that alternative 2 is "good" by choosing 2. Indeed,}
\par
\textbf{coming from the optimally biased advisor, a signal that alternative 2 is}
\par
\textbf{"good" makes the information about 1 irrelevant, since 2 will be chosen}
\par
\textbf{regardless. The probability of one kind of error, mistakenly choosing}
\par
\textbf{1, is increased, while the probability of another kind, mistakenly choosing}
\par
\textbf{2, is reduced, and the tradeoff is a profitable one.}
\par
\textbf{Using the expression derived for V(X), we can determine what happens}
\par
\textbf{to the value of an advisor as the bias changes. For a, = 1, we already know}
