\par
\textbf{Two general kinds of results concerning the reaction of a rational deci-}
\par
\textbf{sion maker to environmental changes emerge from this analysis. First, since}
\par
\textbf{information cost directly reduces the value of information, anything that}
\par
\textbf{increases the cost of advice will decrease the use of that source in favor of}
\par
\textbf{other sources or of less information gathering. This is apparent directly}
\par
\textbf{from the expressions for v(f), the functional equation. Second, if the bias of}
\par
\textbf{an advisor increases, his value will at first increase and then begin to}
\par
\textbf{decline, but not very far. The reaction of a fully rational decision maker to}
\par
\textbf{different levels of bias may be quite out of keeping with the usual intuitive}
\par
\textbf{notions of "rational" decision-making behavior.}
\par
\textbf{The details of the results in this section depend, naturally, upon the}
\par
\textbf{particular form of the probability model used here. However, the point is a}
\par
\textbf{general one: for some combinations of functional forms in a garden-variety}
\par
\textbf{Bayesian decision model, the tradeoffs between different types of errors}
\par
\textbf{may be such that an "objective," "evenhanded," or "unbiased" source of}
\par
\textbf{information is inferior to a source with a (known) bias. This result does not}
\par
\textbf{depend upon skewed prior beliefs or favorable cost differences between}
\par
\textbf{the sources.}
\par
\textbf{SELECTIVE ATTENTION AS OPTIMAL}
\par
\textbf{STATISTICAL SAMPLING}
\par
\textbf{Description}
\par
\textbf{We turn now to the decision maker who, instead of being ignorant about}
\par
\textbf{the alternatives' payoffs, has an initial predisposition in favor of one of}
\par
\textbf{them. Suppose the decision maker believes that policy 1 will likely offer a}
\par
\textbf{good result while policy 2 will probably offer a worse result. Suppose that}
\par
\textbf{the decision maker can, if she wishes, improve on this information by}
\par
\textbf{consulting one of two available information sources. Advisor Y is unbiased}
\par
\textbf{toward both alternatives. Advisor X is biased in favor of alternative 1 and}
\par
\textbf{against alternative 2. In other words, the decision maker can choose546 JOURNAL OF POLITICS, VOL. 47, 1985}
\par
\textbf{between a "neutral" source of information and a source that she knows is}
\par
\textbf{more likely to agree, perhaps mistakenly, with her own predispositions.}
\par
\textbf{This is precisely the situation in which students of the decision-making}
\par
\textbf{process have observed selective exposure and selective attention: already}
\par
\textbf{predisposed toward one alternative and against another, the decision}
\par
\textbf{maker consults information sources likely to agree with that preconcep-}
\par
\textbf{tion, rather than consulting objective or unbiased sources. The analysis}
\par
\textbf{below demonstrates that the fully rational decision maker, aware of this}
\par
\textbf{predisposition, the biases of the sources, and the risky nature of the choices}
\par
\textbf{involved, will under specifiable conditions choose the biased over the}
\par
\textbf{objective information.}
\par
\textbf{The intuition behind this result is as follows. For the decision maker}
\par
\textbf{already strongly predisposed toward policy 1, the objective advice from Y}
\par
\textbf{will not reverse the preference between the alternatives, regardless of what}
\par
\textbf{it is (even though it might narrow the perceived difference between them).}
\par
\textbf{However, there is always the possibility (small but significant) that the}
\par
\textbf{biased advisor X will unexpectedly pronounce policy 1 bad or policy 2}
\par
\textbf{good or both. Such an unexpected finding makes a big impression on the}
\par
\textbf{decision maker; it is enough to reverse prior preferences. The information}
\par
\textbf{from advisor Y can cause no change in the decision maker's choice and is}
\par
\textbf{thus worthless. The information from advisor X can make a difference, and}
\par
\textbf{therefore has positive value.}
\par
