\textbf{Discussion}
\par
\textbf{Thus our rational decision maker chooses to consult an advisor who is}
\par
\textbf{biased toward her own point of view, even though neutral advice is}
\par
\textbf{available. Again, this is not due to any cost advantage of biased advice. The}
\par
\textbf{rational decision maker in the situation modeled here would not consult Y}
\par
\textbf{at any positive cost, but would be willing to pay a positive amount [any-}
\par
\textbf{thing less than V(X)] to consult X. Also, this choice of information does not}
\par
\textbf{depend on any consumption value derived from learning or using the}
\par
\textbf{information from source X. This rational strategy for seeking advice would}
\par
\textbf{be labeled as "bolstering" by any observer possessing all the relevant facts}
\par
\textbf{except the model of advising we have used.}
\par
\textbf{In the real world, where the choice of information-gathering strategies is}
\par
\textbf{not restricted to one consultation of a single source, rational selective}
\par
\textbf{exposure will appear in a somewhat more complicated form. Other things}
\par
\textbf{equal, we would expect the value at any time of seeking further advice}
\par
\textbf{from a source like X to be higher than that of consulting Y. Over a large}
\par
\textbf{number of decision problems, more X-type than Y-type advisors should be}
\par
\textbf{consulted. In addition, there may be many biased advisors among whom to}
\par
\textbf{choose, in which case the problem generalizes to one of choosing an}
\par
\textbf{advisor with optimal bias.}
\par
\textbf{The opportunity to consult sequentially many different information}
\par
\textbf{sources would provide an additional reason for the rational decision maker}
\par
\textbf{to engage in selective exposure beyond those derived here. A biased}
\par
\textbf{source, because of its potential to reveal an unexpected recommendation,}
\par
\textbf{offers the opportunity to avoid further information gathering if it is con-}
\par
\textbf{sulted early in the game. In fact, it is possible to construct examples, based}
\par
\textbf{on the model used in this paper, in which a rational decision maker will}
\par
\textbf{delay consulting an objective source Y in order to consult an agreeable,}
\par
\textbf{biased source X first, where X is more expensive than Y and has lower value}
\par
\textbf{in the single-consultation problem (the one analyzed above) than does Y}
\par
\textbf{(Calvert, 1982). The phenomenon is related to that found by Weitzman}
\par
\textbf{(1979) in analyzing the "Pandora paradox" of optimal sequential search.}
\par
\textbf{SUMMARY ANI) CONCLUSIONS}
\par
\textbf{Summary}
\par
\textbf{The two previous sections have demonstrated three separate reasons}
\par
\textbf{why biased information may be preferred to unbiased information, even if552 JOURNAL OF POLITICS, VOL. 47,185}
\par
\textbf{there is no cost advantage. Our discussion of optimal bias showed that,}
\par
\textbf{even for an initially undecided decision maker, the optimal level of bias}
\par
\textbf{need not be zero. This effect occurs because a biased advisor recommend-}
\par
\textbf{ing the alternative that he was supposed to have been biased against is}
\par
\textbf{likely thereby to prevent the decision maker from making a relatively large}
\par
\textbf{error. In the particular model used here, this effect more than offsets the}
\par
\textbf{possible failure of such an advisor to warn against small errors. All this}
\par
\textbf{depends, naturally, upon the particular functional forms used, but the ones}
\par
\textbf{used here are not obviously lopsided or pathological.}
\par
\textbf{Our discussion of selective attention showed that a decision maker with a}
\par
\textbf{strong predisposition toward one alternative might not benefit at all from}
\par
\textbf{having advice from an unbiased source, since regardless of its content that}
\par
\textbf{advice could not cause her to change her mind. In the real world, where one}
\par
\textbf{seldom faces a situation in which only a single observation is possible, this}
\par
\textbf{effect corresponds to the likelihood that only a large number of such}
\par
\textbf{recommendations could change the decision maker's predisposition.9 With}
\par
\textbf{a biased advisor, however, there is the possibility that a single recommen-}
\par
\textbf{dation could reverse the decision maker's preference between alternatives.}
\par
\textbf{Finally, it was noted at the end of that section that, in a setting of sequential}
\par
\textbf{sampling, the optimal information-gathering procedure might use biased}
\par
\textbf{advisors first, since this might eliminate the need for any further consulting,}
\par
\textbf{again by giving an unexpected recommendation.}
\par
\textbf{In each of the preceding sections, it was possible to derive some basic}
\par
\textbf{results about the response of a rational decision maker to environmental}
\par
\textbf{change. Having derived the value of advice as a function of bias, we can}
\par
\textbf{connect changes in bias with changes in the patterns of a decision maker's}
\par
\textbf{choice among advisors. Likewise, changes in the effective cost of informa-}
\par
\textbf{tion from a source will lead to predictable changes in the use of the sources.}
\par
\textbf{Applications in Politics and Political Science}
\par
\textbf{The results presented here suggest how useful it can be for a decision}
\par
\textbf{maker to maintain advisors whose beliefs or self-interest bias their advice in}
\par
\textbf{ways known to the advisee. This idea applies to an endless variety of}
\par
\textbf{decision-making settings and of types of advice. The use of technical}
\par
\textbf{experts by an executive policymaker is one obvious field of application, as}
\par
\textbf{is the reliance on specialized underlings by a bureaucrat or legislator. A}
\par
\textbf{related problem occurs when a policymaker chooses among several costly}
\par
\textbf{and time-consuming methods of policy analysis. Each method may have its}
\par
\textbf{9 Recent evidence from the psychology laboratory indicates that such "incongruous"}
\par
\textbf{information is indeed of primary importance to makers of limited-information decisions in a}
\par
\textbf{setting of presumptive bias. See Lingle, Dukerich, and Ostrom (1983).BIASED INFORMATION IN POLITICAL ADVICE 553}
\par
\textbf{characteristic biases, and the most neutral method might not be the best}
\par
\textbf{one, once all the relevant costs and benefits are taken into account. Even}
\par
\textbf{rational voters might have good reason to "select out of the passing stream}
\par
\textbf{of stimuli those by which they are inclined to be-persuaded" (Lazarsfeld et}
\par
\textbf{al., 1944, p. 82) and to consult the positions of biased interest groups for}
\par
\textbf{advice on the proper side to take on complex new policy issues, as Kuk-}
\par
\textbf{linski et al. (1982) found.}
\par
\textbf{The analysis presented in this paper offers at least two lessons for}
\par
\textbf{students of political decision making. First, it is not possible to infer, from}
\par
\textbf{the fact that he favors biased information, that a decision maker is not being}
\par
\textbf{instrumentally rational in deciding a policy question.'0 Information-}
\par
\textbf{gathering methods that are rational under the model propounded here}
\par
\textbf{would appear irrational to any observer using a less inclusive model of the}
\par
\textbf{learning and choosing process. Precepts such as "sensitivity to relevant}
\par
\textbf{information," "objectivity," or "maintaining receptivity" have a strong}
\par
\textbf{intuitive appeal as hallmarks of rational information gathering (Stein-}
\par
\textbf{bruner, 1974, p. 330; Janis and Mann, 1977, p. 11). But strange things can}
\par
\textbf{happen when we actually model rational decision making under imperfect}
\par
\textbf{information.}
\par
\textbf{Second, the model analyzed here provides an example of how one can}
\par
\textbf{build a theory of bounded rationality directly from initial assumptions}
\par
\textbf{about imperfect information and limited processing abilities. Apart from}
\par
\textbf{its special distributional forms and particular versions of "advice" and}
\par
\textbf{"bias," the model contains some features of more general applicability. The}
\par
\textbf{Bayesian approach provides a complete, off-the-shelf model of rational}
\par
\textbf{learning, although another well-specified theory could be substituted}
\par
\textbf{should one become available. The placing of positive weight both on prior}
\par
\textbf{beliefs and on new information is an often noted (and sometimes criti-}
\par
\textbf{cized) feature of real decision making. Perhaps most centrally, the deriva-}
\par
\textbf{tion of the ex ante value of information is a step that must precede any}
\par
\textbf{attempt to specify rational behavior under these conditions. The advantage}
\par
\textbf{of building such a model is that it yields predictions about behavior without}
\par
\textbf{first applying empirical observations, often smuggled in under the form of}
\par
\textbf{10 Our model, on the other hand, has nothing to say about the decision-making "irrational-}
\par
\textbf{ity" that occurs as a result of bureaucratic politics or any other group decision process. Authors}
\par
\textbf{such as Allison (1971) and Steinbruner (1974) criticize the "rational actor model," which tries}
\par
\textbf{to account directly for the outcomes of organized decision making by positing rational pursuit}
\par
\textbf{of a group goal. However, rational choice theorists since Arrow (1951) have realized that}
\par
\textbf{individual rationality in any decision process is completely consistent with (and may indeed}
\par
\textbf{require) group irrationality. The object of exercises such as this paper is to explain group}
\par
\textbf{decisions from a starting point of individual rationality; there are no guarantees as to the}
\par
\textbf{"rationality," in a social or group sense, of the resulting outcomes.554 JOURNAL OF POLITICS, VOL. 47, 1985}
\par
\textbf{extra, a priori assumptions about the nature of satisficing behavior or}
\par
\textbf{cognitive style.}
\par
\textbf{Conclusion}
\par
\textbf{Analysts of decision-making processes recognize that imperfect infor-}
\par
\textbf{mation about alternatives is a critical feature of real-world decision processes.}
\par
\textbf{Decision models that incorporate strategic decisions about the use of}
\par
\textbf{information, as well as risky choice, such as those of McCall (1970) and}
\par
\textbf{Rothschild (1974) on economic behavior and Shepsle (1972) on strategies}
\par
\textbf{of electoral candidates, add a layer of nontrivial complication to our}
\par
\textbf{understanding of optimal decision making.}
\par
\textbf{The multiple-source information-gathering model in this paper goes a}
\par
\textbf{step farther and shows that the optimal use of information has several}
\par
\textbf{counterintuitive features. Thus, when we draw inferences about the nature}
\par
\textbf{of decision making from our observations of how information is used, it is}
\par
\textbf{not a good idea to rely on simple, intuitive notions of rational information}
\par
\textbf{use. Depending on particular conditions of uncertainty and information}
\par
\textbf{costs, rational choice may call for seemingly irrational use of information.}
\par
\textbf{More generally, the model in this paper serves as an example of how one}
\par
\textbf{ought to theorize about the effects of imperfect information and con-}
\par
\textbf{strained analytical abilities on the behavior of decision makers. Just}
\par
\textbf{because the real world does not meet the conditions of simple rational}
\par
\textbf{decision models, the analyst cannot abandon the rigorous study of how}
\par
\textbf{goals determine choices. If one believes that cognitive abilities are}
\par
\textbf{"limited" and that rationality is "bounded," theoretical parsimony dictates}
\par
\textbf{that one try to specify those limits and bounds and derive their implica-}
\par
\textbf{tions, rather than introduce special assumptions about compensating}
\par
\textbf{behavior. This is not to deny the value of empirical studies of decision and}
\par
\textbf{cognition, but rather to distinguish between such studies and the effort to}
\par
\textbf{develop a general theory about decision making.}
