\textbf{ABSTRACT}
\par
The acquisition of knowledge, the development of the individual's knowledge bases, does
\par
not occur in a vacuum. The social context, both social structure (the perceived regularities in
\par
the social network) and social knowledge, appears to be related to the individual's acquisition
\par
of knowledge. In this article, the relationship between the social world and individual knowl-
\par
edge acquisition is explored. A general theory is postulated and then formulated as a model.
\par
The basic theory is predicated on the assumption that social interaction is the driving force
\par
behind knowledge acquisition.
\par
A selection of results pertaining to social learning will be presented. These include, but
\par
are not limited to the following:
\par
{$\bullet$} Social structure affects knowledge acquisition; e.g., individuals in tightly knit social
\par
groups develop similar cognitive structures, but not necessarily similar evaluations of
\par
those structures.
\par
{$\bullet$} Social knowledge evolves slowly and relative to shared experience.
\par
{$\bullet$} Individual knowledge acquisition is limited; e.g., individuals and groups talk past
\par
each other because of the lack of critical paths in their respective knowledge bases.
\par
\textbf{Introduction}
\par
The acquisition o f knowledge, the development o f the individual's
\par
knowledge base, does not occur in a vacuum. The social context, b o t h social
\par
structure = \emph{the perceived regularities in the network of ties between individu-}
\par
\emph{als in the society - }and social knowledge - \emph{that information which is known}
\par
\emph{by "'everyone" in the society - }appears to have an effect on the individual's
\par
acquisition o f knowledge. Briefly, the conjecture is that the social world and
\par
the world of the individual are being continuously constructed as individuals
\par
move t h r o u g h the series of tasks that constitute daily living, interacting, c o m -
\par
municating, and hence acquiring knowledge. The acquisition of knowledge,
\par
and hence cognitive development, becomes a by-product o f h u m a n interac-
\par
tion: the individual's cognitive structure is continuously constructed as the382
\par
individual moves through a series of tasks interacting with others, com-
\par
municating, and hence gathering information. The development of the social
\par
world involves tacit consensus among the individuals in the society over per-
\par
ceived regularities; in this way, social knowledge, as shared cognitive struc-
\par
ture, is also a by-product of human interaction. Social knowledge affects fu-
\par
ture interaction/communication; in part, because it provides a basis for
\par
analyzing/interpreting particular tasks. Social structure and the individual's
\par
propensity to interact develop reflexively. As individuals' knowledge bases
\par
co-evolve, their propensity to interact changes, social knowledge evolves, and
\par
social structures change. It is to the elaboration and exploration of this con-
\par
jecture that this work is addressed.
\par
Concept development is the vehicle class used to motivate this theory;
\par
the specific vehicle used to test the tenets of this theory is the development
\par
of the concept \emph{tutor }by a group of MIT undergraduates as they move through
\par
the task of selecting a new tutor for their living group, Third East.
\par
The importance of the social context to the individual's reasoning proc-
\par
ess and his ability to acquire knowledge through his interaction with others
\par
has come to be more accepted by researchers in general. And yet, as Cicourel
\par
(1974: 11) notes:
\par
It is commonplace in sociology for writers to acknowledge the ultimate importance of
\par
the interacting situation between two or more actors. The assumed relationship between
\par
structure and process, however, is often no more than an expression of faith rather than
\par
the integration of social process with social structure...
\par
By an expression of faith Cicourel seems to be referring to the fact that
\par
explicit cognitive procedures and a theory of meaning are absent from such
\par
formulations of social interaction; e.g., the work of Davis (1949) on status,
\par
Parsons (1951) on role expectation, H o m a n s (1961) on social stimulus, and
\par
Blau (1967) on social status.
\par
Despite of the lack of an explicit cognitive model for the relationship be-
\par
tween the social world and the individual world, a variety of arguments have
\par
been forwarded on the nature of this relationship. Social position affects mo-
\par
bility, career chances, and who one knows (Breiger, 1978, 1979; White, 1970;
\par
Granovetter, 1973, 1974, 1982). Culture affects learning processes and be-
\par
havior (Mead, 1962, 1964, 1978; Luria, 1978; Vygotsky, 1962, 1978).
\par
In other words, there is evidence that the social world affects what we
\par
know, what information we store, even if it does not affect the cognitive
\par
mechanism for storing, retrieving, and manipulating that information. These
\par
works point to the existence of a relationship between the social and the in-
\par
dividual world; they do not provide a mechanism for movement from the in-
\par
dividual to the social, a model of this relationship. But they do place con-383
\par
straints on such a model; i.e., it is common to treat social context as being,
\par
from the individual's point of view, potentially knowable, and constraining.
\par
This notion of a knowable and constraining social context is shared by
\par
researchers in anthropology (Mead, 1962; Levi-Strauss, 1963), psychology
\par
(Luria, 1978; Vygotsky, 1962; Lawler, 1979, 1985), sociology (Cicourel, 1974;
\par
Roloff and Berger, 1982; Forgas, 1981; Berger, 1973), and administrative
\par
sciences (Cohen and March, 1974; Cohen et al., 1972) [1]. A similar argument
\par
is put forward in ethnomethodology; man is an actor whose life is a
\par
dramaturgical unfolding of events, whose knowledge is determined by these
\par
events (Garfinkle, 1968; Cicourel, 1974). Social knowledge, sometimes re-
\par
ferred to as social background information, can be roughly described as \emph{that}
\par
\emph{information which everyone knows. }Social knowledge is one of those nebu-
\par
lous social phenomena whose existence is generally assumed, whose nature
\par
is rarely modeled, and which is presumed to affect consensus. Statements like
\par
Shibutani's (1961: 40) that "men are able to act together with relative ease be-
\par
cause they share common understanding as to what each person is supposed
\par
to do" illustrate the presumed importance of social background knowledge,
\par
and, the lack of specificity as to what it is, and how it works. Theories of
\par
social meaning tend to postulate a relationship between individual cognitive
\par
representation and tacit consensus, without defining its form [2]. Social
\par
knowledge - that information which is known simply because one is a mem-
\par
ber of the society - is also viewed as playing a critical role in the acquisition
\par
of knowledge. Such shared and hence, social knowledge is necessary in order
\par
for the individual to make decisions and perform tasks (Heider, 1958; Wegner
\par
and Vallacher, 1977). Social knowledge is assumed by members of the society,
\par
and is necessary for interpreting communications (Whorf, 1956; Rumelhart
\par
1978a; Rumelhart, 1978b, Collins et al., 1977; Charniak, 1972). Without such
\par
information the members of the groups would have no common bases that
\par
would admit discussion, let alone agreement (Charley, 1984). The pieces of
\par
social knowledge are to the tasks of everyday life as rules are to a game [3].
\par
These pieces of social knowledge can be used to help establish the individu-
\par
al's frame of reference, his cognitive structure, which is then used by the in-
\par
dividual to perform various social tasks, and hence to make decisions (Goff-
\par
man, 1974; Rumelhart, 1978a; Schank and Abelson, 1977).
\par
In this article, the relationship between the social world and the in-
\par
dividual world is explored relative to the process of knowledge acquisition.
\par
A general theory of the interrelationship between that which is social and
\par
that which is individual is postulated and then formulated as a general inter-
\par
action model, section 1. The basic theory is predicated on the assumption
\par
that social interaction is the driving force behind knowledge acquisition. In
\par
section 2 details on the relationship between the individual's propensity to in-
\par
teract and social structure are elaborated. To increase the precision of the384
\par
general theory presented in section 1, a representation scheme for knowledge
\par
is needed. This representation scheme is presented in section 3. Using this
\par
representation scheme, a model of individual and social knowledge is
\par
presented in section 4. Following this, in section 5, a more specified, albeit
\par
simple, model of knowledge acquisition, and the evolution of social knowl-
\par
edge as a function of social interaction is proposed. The interrelationship be-
\par
tween individual knowledge and social knowledge is critical to this model.
\par
And finally, in section 6, a set of hypotheses about the nature of individual
\par
knowledge acquisition and social knowledge based on the foregoing models
\par
is proposed and then tested.
\par
DATA
\par
The data base is drawn from an in-depth study of the relationship be-
\par
tween the social structure of a group of undergraduates at MIT and their
\par
cognitive conception or structure of the concept \emph{tutor }as the students moved
\par
through the task of selecting a new tutor or graduate resident, for their living
\par
group, Third East (Carley, 1984). This living group, Third East, at the time
\par
of this study was composed of 45 undergraduates, male and female, flesh-
\par
men through senior, all living in one section of a particular dormitory - the
\par
third floor in the east parallel of the East Campus dormitory. Physically,
\par
Third East is a block long hallway, in a dormitory, East Campus, formed of
\par
ten such hallways, five each in two parallel buildings.
\par
The data used are non-experimental and are based on a rich ethnograph-
\par
ic study of this group. The data include subjective interaction data, personali-
\par
ty profiles, interviews with each student separately on their perception of the
\par
concept \emph{tutor }and their perception of the entire tutor selection process at
\par
several different stages, voting procedure information, transcripts of meet-
\par
ings, actual votes, decision time flame data, and historical and cultural infor-
\par
mation on the living group.
\par
The interaction data were analyzed using network tools to extract the
\par
underlying social structure, e.g., the block modeling program CONCOR was
\par
used to locate structural groups (Breiger et al., 1975). The students were inter-
\par
viewed several times throughout the tutor selection process as to their percep-
\par
tion of the concept \emph{tutor, }as well as on other facets of the selection task.
\par
These interviews were then coded as knowledge bases, one base per person
\par
per time period (Carley, 1985a). A set of procedures, collectively referred to
\par
as \emph{Frame Technology, }that allows the researcher to relate social structure to
\par
cognitive structure was used to analyze this data (Carley, 1985b). Herein, the
\par
structural groups extracted from the interaction data and the maps [4] of the
\par
student's perception of the concept \emph{tutor }are used. Again, these maps are the
\par
result of coding the interviews with the students using a set of techniques385
\par
designed for coding, storing, and analyzing verbal data on the basis of the
\par
knowledge representation scheme forwarded.
\par
M E T H O D
\par
The hypotheses are examined using a set of procedures that allow the
\par
researcher to relate social structure to cognitive structure. The procedures
\par
represent an attempt at breaching the gap between the network tools used to
\par
look at social groups and the formalisms in artificial intelligence for
\par
representing cognitive structures. Collectively this group of procedures is re-
\par
ferred to as \emph{Frame Technology }(Carley, 1984, 1985b). The network tools used
\par
in studies of social networks include block modeling tools such as BLOCK-
\par
ER (Heil and White, 1976), and CONCOR (Breiger et al., 1975; Schwartz,
\par
1977), as well as general categorization schemes such as cluster analysis
\par
(Everitt, 1974; Gradap, 1981a, b). The purpose of these tools is to elicit the
\par
structure of the network, given a set of network ties; e.g., to elicit a set of
\par
social positions or groups given the interaction propensities of all of the in-
\par
dividuals in the society. The representation formalisms include frames
\par
(Minsky, 1975; Charniak, 1972), scripts (Schank and Abelson, 1977; Abel-
\par
son, 1976; Abelson and Reich, 1969), schemes (Anderson and Bower, 1973;
\par
Bobrow and Norman, 1976), transition networks (Collins and Loftus, 1975;
\par
Clark and Clark, 1977; Wyer and Carlston, 1979), semantic nets (Simmons,
\par
1973; Schank and Colby, 1973), and schemata (Rumelhart and Ortney, 1976;
\par
Tesser, 1977; Tversky and Kahneman, 1980). These formalisms serve as
\par
models of information in memory and as devices for representing articulata-
\par
ble knowledge. The tools in \emph{Frame Technology }allow the researcher to take in-
\par
teraction data and locate social position using network tools - specifically
\par
CONCOR \textbf{- }and to take interview or verbal data and create a coded
\par
representation of these data using representation formalisms - specifically
\par
knowledge bases [5]. Then the set of tools in \emph{Frame Technology }can be used
\par
by the researcher to combine the knowledge bases of individuals within or
\par
between social positions and to measure the resultant similarity. In this way,
\par
the tools allow the researcher to look at shared knowledge.
\par
C O M M E N T
\par
I want to stress that this article is about the social side of learning. The
\par
goal is to set out a basic model of the interrelationship between the social
\par
world and the individual's cognitive world, and then, to explore this model
\par
relative to knowledge acquisition. The focus is on the consequences and
\par
predictions of this model. The data presented are only exploratory and in-386
\par
dicative of the type of analyses that are needed to test the model. This article
\par
is not about tutor selection or what it means to be a tutor. The findings
\par
presented are not meant to be a comprehensive analysis of the learning be-
\par
havior of MIT students. A complete description of these students, and their
\par
social world can be found in Carley, 1984. Nor is this article about a new
\par
methodology. Admittedly, the methodology used, frame technology, is novel
\par
in that it permits the researcher to directly relate social to cognitive data.
\par
However, the purpose of this article is not to elucidate the use of these tech-
\par
niques nor the algorithms on which they are based. An overview of these
\par
techniques can be found in Carley, 1985b, and a more comprehensive treat-
\par
ment in Carley, 1984.
\par
\textbf{1. C o n s t r u e t u r a l i s m - T h e o r y a n d M o d e l}
\par
Individuals do not acquire knowledge in a vacuum nor is the acquired
\par
knowledge independent of the individual's current knowledge. Construc-
\par
turalism is the theory that the social world and the personal cognitive world
\par
of the individual continuously evolve in a reflexive fashion. The individual's
\par
cognitive structure (his knowledge base), his propensity to interact with other
\par
individuals, social structure, social meaning, social knowledge, and consen-
\par
sus are all being continuously constructed in a reflexive, recursive fashion as
\par
the individuals in the society interact in the process of moving through a se-
\par
ries of tasks. As the individuals move through the set of tasks that constitute
\par
daily life, they interact and, in doing so, they acquire knowledge. What
\par
knowledge they acquire and whom they interact with are factors which are
\par
intimately related. Central to the constructuralist theory are the assumptions
\par
that individuals process and communicate information during interactions,
\par
and that the accrual of new information produces cognitive development,
\par
changes in the individuals' cognitive structure. What follows is an elabora-
\par
tion of various assumptions and ideas underlying this theory.
\par
KNOWLEDGE ACQUISITION IS DONE IN THE CONTEXT OF PERFORMING TASKS
\par
The individual's life is viewed as the performance of a set of tasks, some
\par
sequentially performed and some simultaneously. These tasks run the gamut
\par
from going to a movie, to selecting a president, to choosing a toothpaste, to
\par
interpreting questions on a test. The individual's performance of a task is de-
\par
pendent on his evaluation of the information he knows about some aspect
\par
of that task. The information that the individual knows relative to some task
\par
is his frame of reference for that task. When an individual performs a partic-
\par
ular task that task's frame of reference becomes the individual's current387
\par
In the process of everyday life individuals move through a series of tasks,
\par
interact, and communicate. These tasks set the context for what information
