mation is acquired within the context of performing these tasks.
\par
R E L A T I N G T H E S O C I A L TO T H E I N D I V I D U A L
\par
The individual is modeled as a node connected to a set of other in-
\par
dividuals by a network of ties. The social world is modeled as a wholistic en-
\par
tity perceived as the patterns of regularities in the network of ties between
\par
individuals. As individuals interact, they and the social world co-evolve -
\par
see Fig. 2.
\par
The individual has two defining characteristics that uniquely identify
\par
him vis-\textasciitilde t-vis all the other individuals in the society. These are his propensity
\par
for interaction with the other members of the social unit [6] and his cognitive
\par
structure (his knowledge base) [7]. Presumably there are factors other than
\par
cognitive structure and interaction propensities that comprise a human being
\par
\textbf{- }e.g., motives and emotions. However, herein the concern is with these
\par
characteristics only to the extent to which they are articulatable, and hence
\par
communicable.
\par
The individual's interaction propensities and his cognitive structure
\par
change as the individual participates in everyday life, performing tasks, inter-
\par
acting, and hence communicating and acquiring knowledge.
\par
Note that some of the information that is communicated may be social
\par
knowledge; i.e., knowledge that is shared by members of the society. In fact,
\par
assuming that, for a particular individual, all pieces of information are
\par
equally likely to be communicated, then social knowledge is, throughout the
\par
society, the most likely information to be communicated as it is shared by
\par
"everyone." An individual can only communicate information that he
\par
knows, that is currently a part of his cognitive structure. An individual can
\par
only acquire information if it is comprehensible, given his current cognitive
\par
structure. I would suggest that there are two ways in which the individual can
\par
acquire knowledge, independent discovery or via communication. It is as-
\par
sumed that knowledge acquisition via discovery is relatively rare in compari-
\par
son with acquisition via communication. Note that if neither new informa-389
