heuristic artifact of social life. The social world evolves simply because all of
\par
the individuals in the social unit are simultaneously going through the proc-390
\par
ess of interaction and consequent knowledge acquisition even though they
\par
are performing different tasks. The co-evolution of individuals has social
\par
evolution as a by-product.
\par
Interaction is the driving force which produces change in the social
\par
world and in the individuals who are members of the social unit. Changes
\par
occur because, due to this interaction, knowledge is acquired by the individu-
\par
als and each individual's propensity to interact with others is reinforced. So-
\par
cial structure, regularities in the pattern of interaction between the members
\par
of the social unit, evolves reflexively as the individual's propensities to inter-
\par
act evolve. Social structure affects the cognitive development of each in-
\par
dividual separately, by constraining interaction [10]. Similarly, social knowl-
\par
edge, knowledge shared by members of the social unit, evolves reflexively as
\par
the individual's cognitive structures evolve. As knowledge is acquired, in-
\par
dividuals reposition themselves cognitively vis-h-vis other members of the so-
\par
ciety. As changes occur in who shares what information, cognitive reposi-
\par
tioning, the individual's propensity to interact with the other members of the
\par
social unit changes. Hence, social structure and social knowledge co-evolve.
\par
\emph{Individuals as information processors and communicators}
\par
Individuals are viewed as joint information processors and communica-
\par
tors subject to time and effort constraints. As the individuals perform tasks
\par
they are constantly gathering, storing, manipulating, retrieving, and com-
\par
municating information. Admittedly, individuals may also be doing other
\par
things than simply processing and communicating information, e.g., they
\par
may be reacting emotionally to some event or they might be trying to exert
\par
control over some situation or individual; however, for the purpose at hand
\par
the information processing view is sufficient. While the proposed view of the
\par
individual as an information processor and communicator is perhaps an
\par
oversimplification, I suggest that this view is useful in that it promotes an ex-
\par
amination of the relationship between the social world and the individual
\par
cognitive world in terms of information flow, and hence knowledge acquisi-
\par
tion. Further, if the individual is subject to time and effort constraints then
\par
task performance is important; not because of the feat but because it directs
\par
and constrains the information gathering and communication process.
\par
\emph{Initial cognitive structure}
\par
What knowledge the individual acquires is predicated on his current
\par
cognitive structure; i.e., individuals do not begin with a blank slate [11]. Rath-
\par
er, they start out with some knowledge, some set of preconceived notions
\par
about a variety of topics including ideas about the task being performed, the
\par
society in which they live, and the individuals with whom they interact. This
\par
set of ideas or knowledge forms a knowledge base. The initial knowledge391
\par
base is the set of pieces of information that the individual knows when we
\par
begin to observe his behavior. Note that this knowledge base includes the
\par
frame of reference for each of the particular tasks that the individual per-
\par
forms. Further, at any point in time we can think of the individual as focus-
\par
ing on, as having immediate recall of, that part of the knowledge base, that
\par
frame, that refers to the tasks currently being performed.
\par
The current knowledge base is used to direct the gathering of new infor-
\par
mation, and hence the further development of the knowledge base. This
\par
recursive process of \emph{knowledge-base-development information-gathering}
\par
\emph{knowledge-base-development }continues regardless of the particular task the
\par
individual is performing, or the state the individual is at in performing that
\par
task. For example, let's say that the individual is involved in the task of
\par
choosing a president. Regardless of whether it is the beginning of the election
\par
process, e.g., prior to the primaries, or at the end of the process, e.g., when
\par
you pull the lever at the voting booth, the individual will be collecting infor-
\par
mation and adding it to their knowledge base in the same fashion. When it
\par
comes down to the wire and it is time to make a decision the information-
\par
gathering knowledge-base-development process does not halt; rather, the in-
\par
dividual simply makes his decision on the basis of the information he has
\par
available to him at that time [12]. The point here is that the information-
\par
gathering process may be directed by what task is currently being performed,
\par
but it continues regardless of specific task.
\par
\emph{Cognitive structure is articulate}
\par
The individual is continuously constructing his knowledge base, his cog-
\par
nitive structure. This continual construction involves the adaptation of the
\par
existing framework, the existing knowledge base, to comprise the lessons of
\par
a new experience. As Polanyi (1962: 105) notes what is meant by this process
\par
gains a more precise meaning when the framework in question is "articulate":
\par
The distinction between assimilation of experience by a fixed interpretive framework
\par
and the adaptation of that framework to comprise the lessons of a new experience,
\par
gains a new and more precise meaning when the framework in question is articulate.
\par
The first represents the ideal, of using language impersonally, according to strict rules;
\par
the second relies on the personal intervention of the speaker, for changing the rules of
\par
language to fit new occasions... The first is a routine performance, the second a heuris-
\par
tic act. A paradigm of the first is counting, which leaves its interpretive framework -
\par
the numbers used in counting - quite unchanged; the ideal of the second is found in
\par
the originality of poetic phrasing or of new mathematical notations covering new con-
\par
ceptions. Ideally the first is strictly reversible, while the second is essentially irreversible.
\par
For to modify our idiom is to modify the frame of reference within which we shall
\par
henceforth interpret our experience; is to modify ourselves.
\par
In the constructuralist perspective all knowledge is articulatable [13],392
\par
therefore, it is potentially communicable. The individual's cognitive struc-
\par
ture, his knowledge base, is an articulate structure. The modification of this
\par
structure requires the intervention of the individual to change the rules of the
\par
language [14] to fit the new occasion; i.e., if the individual is to accept new
\par
information that is communicated to him, he must adapt his knowledge base.
\par
As will be discussed in section 4, the alteration of the individual's knowledge
\par
base implies a change in the rules of language, a change in what information
\par
is related to what, a change in the meaning of various concepts. Cognitive
\par
development, the evolution of the knowledge base, is thus a heuristic act
\par
which is irreversible.
\par
\textbf{2. Interaction and Social Structure}
\par
In order to make some predictions from the previous theory a more pre-
\par
cise model of social structure and the relationship between individual interac-
\par
tion and social structure is required. Recall that the notion of social structure
\par
presented rested on the propensity of individuals in the society to interact.
\par
Each individual has a certain propensity to interact with each of the other
\par
individuals in the society. For a particular individual, this set of propensities
\par
can be modeled as an array containing his propensity for interacting with all
\par
other individuals in the society - see Fig. 3. Collectively, these arrays, the
\par
interaction propensities for all individuals in the social unit, form a matrix.
\par
Recall that the social world is modeled as a network where the individuals
\par
are the nodes and the connections are the relationships between these in-
\par
dividuals. One type of relationship is the propensity to interact. The pro-
\par
posed matrix is a representation of that part of the social world where the
\par
network ties are propensity to interact: each individual is connected to all
\par
other individuals by an interaction propensity. Within this matrix there are
\par
particular patterns; i.e., some of the individuals will have similar patterns in
\par
their interaction propensities vis-\&-vis all of the other individuals in the socie-
\par
ty. As the individual's interaction propensities shift, these patterns change,
\par
and the pattern of actual interaction changes.
\par
The propensity of one individual to interact with another individual is
\par
a function both of the level of past interaction and the degree of shared
\par
knowledge. Since interaction leads to knowledge acquisition, as individuals
\par
interact more, they share more knowledge and so are more likely to interact.
\par
This is a case of positive feedback. A consequence is that patterns of interac-
\par
tion will tend to change slowly, barring forces outside of the social unit [15].
\par
Another consequence is that actual interaction becomes a fairly accurate
\par
predictor of future interaction, of the individual's propensity to interact.
\par
