\par
\textbf{3. T h e R e p r e s e n t a t i o n S c h e m e}
\par
To further increase the precision of the model presented in section 1 a
\par
specific model of knowledge/information is required. As previously noted,
\par
knowledge is assumed to be articulatable. I propose that knowledge is not
\par
only articulatable, but composed o f distinct pieces that are communicable
\par
[17]. The representation scheme used for knowledge/information [18] is
\par
based on the notion of a network of concepts. A variety of similar represen-
\par
tation schemes have been forwarded for the representation of information in
\par
memory; schemes (Anderson and Bower, 1973; Bobrow and Norman, 1976),
\par
frames (Minsky, 1975), transition networks (Collins and Loftus, 1975; Clark
\par
and Clark, 1977; Wyer and Carlston, 1979), semantic nets (Simmons, 1973;
\par
Schank and Colby, 1973), and scripts (Schank and Abelson, 1977; Abelson,
\par
1976; Abelson and Reich, 1969). The proposed schemes all share the notion
\par
of an ideational kernel, e.g., concepts, arranged in a network. Purely as a
\par
representation scheme, the proposed scheme is quite similar to several of
\par
these other schemes, especially augmented transition networks. The primary
\par
difference is the underlying notion that all knowledge/information, regard-
\par
less of source, is articulatable and hence can be represented using the same
\par
scheme.
\par
FACTS AND KNOWLEDGE BASES
\par
Knowledge/information is viewed as a discrete entity composed of
\par
pieces - see Fig. 4. A piece of knowledge, a piece of information, is modeled
\par
as a fact - two concepts and the relationship between them (Carley, 1985b)
\par
[19]. Some examples of facts are \emph{a Third Easter wouM be a good tutor,}
\par
\emph{Gnerds aren't friendly, }and \emph{Someone who fits in with the hall won't insist on}
\par
\emph{quietness.}
\par
I will refer to a collection of facts as a \emph{knowledge base. }The individual's
\par
knowledge base is that collection of facts that are known by the individual
\par
\textbf{- }see Fig. 4. The knowledge base, in this case, can be thought of as a
\par
representation of the individual's cognitive structure, an articulation of the
\par
individual's perception of all his previous experiences. The social knowledge
\par
base is that collection of facts which serve as background information
\par
presumably known by "everyone" in the society. Social knowledge is that
\par
knowledge to which there is tacit consensus among the members of the social\textbf{395}
