\par
INTERNAL STRUCTURE
\par
Within a knowledge base, we expect to find that some of the facts will
\par
interrelate; e.g., Jay loves Ann, Ann loves Greg, and Jay and Greg hate each
\par
other. Facts can interrelate forming a network simply by sharing concepts -
\par
see Fig. 4; i.e., pattern matching on concepts leads to the knowledge base be-
\par
ing replete with implicit networks. Networking is not always the result of pat-
\par
tern matching; i.e., some networks are directed or have a hierarchical
\par
structure.
\par
Individuals as information processors subject to time and processing
\par
constraints have a tendency to generalize, to categorize. The result of
\par
categorization is new information, new facts. These new facts have the old
\par
facts embedded within them; e.g., a new concept or category can be formed
\par
which serves as a handle for all of the generalized or embedded information.
\par
Generalization thus becomes a process of embedding one set of knowledge,
\par
e.g., a frame, within a particular concept or fact. Embedding is powerful as
\par
it provides a general framework which can be used to interpret outcomes, and
\par
which accurately predicts the general or average nature of those outcomes.
\par
Because of this tendency to generalize, the set of facts in the individual's
\par
knowledge base is not random but is interrelated, forming networks which
\par
have a hierarchical structure.
\par
\emph{The definition}
\par
Some hierarchical networks of facts are more interesting, or have special
\par
interpretations; e.g., the definition. A definition is a focused network of facts
\par
such that the focus is the concept being defined and the other concepts in
\par
the network serve to define the focus by their relationship to it and each
\par
other.
\par
The fundamental idea is one of relative definition:
\par
{$\bullet$} Concepts are ideational kernels that in isolation are devoid of
\par
meaning.
\par
{$\bullet$} Concepts can only be defined by their relationship to each other.
\par
{$\bullet$} Meaning is a function of the interrelationships between concepts in
\par
the knowledge base, and not the concepts per se.
\par
In terms of social usage, general communication, there are no absolute defi-
\par
nitions only relative ones - we do not go to the dictionary to understand a
\par
word, but understand it through the context in which it is being used. Even
\par
the definitions in the dictionary are transient and dependent on the percep-
\par
tion of tacit consensus to a set of information that collectively constitutes the
\par
meaning of a particular concept.397
\par
\emph{Frames and maps}
\par
These hierarchical networks of facts can be represented using a variety
\par
of structural models. Frames are one such structural model; maps are anoth-
\par
er (Carley, 1985b). Both frames and maps are networked knowledge bases with
\par
a hierarchical structure imposed on the network of facts. Frames and maps
\par
can be thought of as focused networks of definitions where the focus is the
\par
thing to be decided upon. Unlike the definitions previously discussed, frames
\par
and maps are complex and contain within them multiple definitions. Such
\par
Structures may be interconnected, in that they can share concepts and/or
\par
facts - see Fig. 6.
\par
For each task that an individual performs, he has a particular frame.
\par
This frame, in a hierarchical fashion, contains all of the knowledge that he
\par
knows to relate to this task. There are frames other than task-related frames.
\par
A map is a slice of a frame at a particular level of complexity. For example,
\par
in section 6 Fig. 12 is a map of the students' general perceptions of the con-
\par
cept \emph{tutor. }It is not a frame, because there are other things the students know
\par
about tutors; e.g., specific information about their current tutor or past
\par
tutors.
\par
\textbf{4. Individual and Social Knowledge}
\par
Given the foregoing representation scheme, the relationship between in-
\par
dividual knowledge and social knowledge can now be explicated. Knowledge
\par
is represented as a fact, regardless of whether that information is individual
\par
knowledge or social knowledge. The difference between social and individual
\par
knowledge is the level of consensus to that fact.
\par
INDIVIDUAL KNOWLEDGE
\par
Each individual has a knowledge base which contains all of the informa-
\par
tion that he has acquired. Within this knowledge base there is a set of frames,
\par
some of which share some of the same pieces of information. Different
\par
frames are associated with different tasks. The knowledge base and the set
\par
of interlocked frames are thus representations of the same information. In
\par
the first case we are looking at facts as facts and in the second at the structur-
\par
al relationships formed by sets of facts.
\par
\emph{Individuals as rational beings}
\par
Individuals are presumed to be rational. Rationality is defined in terms
\par
of the use and coherency of known information. Individuals are considered
\par
rational in the sense that they use primitive logical operations and optimiza-398
\par
tion to make decisions based on the information currently in their flame of
\par
reference. Rationality is thus bounded by what information the individual
\par
knows and the amount of time available to process that information.
\par
The individual is rational if he uses his flame of reference for a task to
\par
perform that task. The individual is rational if he incorporates into his frame
\par
of reference that information which is communicated to him which is related
\par
to information currently in his flame of reference. The individual is behaving
\par
irrationally if he makes a decision or comes to a conclusion, that is not based
\par
on the information he knows; e.g., deciding to farm in the desert even though
\par
one flame concerns desert farming and includes facts such as \emph{water is neces-}
\par
\emph{sary for farming }and \emph{there is nor water in the desert. }The individual is behav-
\par
ing irrationally if he rufuses to accept new information which is related to
\par
information he already knows, given processing constraints; e.g., refusing to
\par
accept the fact \emph{a dog is a mammal }even though his current flame concerns
\par
animals and includes the facts \emph{mammals are characterized by having body}
\par
\emph{hair and bearing their young live and so are dogs.}
\par
\emph{Coherent knowledge base}
\par
An aspect of rationality is that the individual's cognitive structure, his
\par
knowledge base, is presumed to be coherent on a frame by frame basis; i.e.,
\par
it does not contain a piece of information A and its logical opposite, not A,
\par
at the same time in a single frame. To an observer the individual's knowledge
\par
base may appear to be incoherent because the observer does not perceive the
\par
entire set of frames in question. For example, a mathematician might say that
\par
\emph{the sum of the angles of a triangle is always 180 degrees }and then at another
\par
time that \emph{the sum of the angles of a triangle need not be 180 degrees; }thus,
\par
seeming contradictory to the listener. In the first case the mathematician's
\par
current frame, his frame of reference, might be Euclidean geometry, and in
\par
the second case his current frame might be a frame for a non-Euclidean ge-
\par
ometry, like spherical geometry. The hierarchical nature of frames, due to
\par
levels of generalization/categorization, may make it difficult for individuals
\par
to see the inconsistencies between different frames in their own knowledge
\par
base; i.e., if the top levels of the frames deal with extremely different types
\par
of information (involve different concepts) the individual may never access
\par
both frames simultaneously or sequentially and so realize that pieces of in-
\par
formation contained at lower levels in the frame are actually contradictory.
\par
\emph{Intelligence}
\par
Intelligence is defined in terms of the manipulation of sets of informa-
\par
tion. The individual's level of intelligence is a function of his ability to cross-
\par
relate and manipulate the facts in his knowledge base, forming new networks
\par
(frames) and altering old ones. It may be difficult to distinguish between irra-399
\par
tional and intelligent behavior because in order to distinguish between the
\par
two it is necessary to observe the actors frames of reference.
\par
SOCIAL KNOWLEDGE
\par
Polanyi (1958: 216-219; 264-266) in his discussion of the pursuit of
\par
science implicitly defines social knowledge as "tacit knowledge" resulting
\par
from "tacit consensus," itself a by-product of the articulation of "co mmon
\par
experience," and, more importantly, a product of the transitivity of appraisal
\par
across a continuous network of critics. The concepts 'majority', 'relevant',
\par
and 'availability' are now redefined as relative phenomena, products of one's
\par
position in the social network. For example, if constructing the social knowl-
\par
edge for students in the social sciences: the piece of information that \emph{Piaget}
\par
\emph{is a developmentalpsychologist }might be considered a piece of social knowl-
\par
edge because it is known and consented to by most students of psychology,
\par
some in anthropology and sociology, and only a few in political science and
\par
organizational theory; and at the same time, the piece of information that
\par
\emph{Garfinkle founded ethnomethodology }may also be considered to be social
\par
because it is known and consented to by most students of sociology and an-
\par
thropology, some psychologists and political scientists, and only a few or-
\par
ganizational theorists. Note that in both cases there is a locus (or loci) of
\par
consensus, and that it differs from case to case, for each piece of infor-
\par
mation.
\par
In keeping with Polanyi, and given the foregoing representation schema,
\par
I define social knowledge in terms of shared facts. Two individuals may share
\par
information; i.e., the same piece of knowledge may be in both of their knowl-
\par
edge bases. In general, for any two individuals the intersection of their
\par
knowledge bases contains the knowledge which is social vis-\textasciitilde t-vis those in-
\par
dividuals. The more members of the social unit who share a particular piece
\par
of information the more social that knowledge. Thus, tacit consensus to a
\par
fact produces social knowledge. There is no need for explicit acknowledge-
\par
ment of the sharing of that information to confirm the information as social.
\par
However, each time the information is reiterated its presence as a piece of so-
\par
cial knowledge is reaffirmed.
\par
\emph{Social validity}
\par
Each piece of information has a particular level of social validity. The
\par
level of social validity is a function of the number of individuals in the social
\par
unit who share that piece of information. The social knowledge base is
\par
formed at a particular level of validity by taking the intersection across a set
\par
of individuals' knowledge bases for each piece of information separately.
\par
The social knowledge base contains those pieces of information that are400
\par
shared by some portion of the members of the society. It is a research deci-
\par
sion as to what level of consensus produces "social knowledge" and hence
\par
what information is in the social knowledge base. A social knowledge base
\par
produced by taking the intersection of the knowledge bases of all of the
\par
members of the social unit contains only that information which is socially
\par
the most valid. In effect, the social knowledge base is formed by taking the
\par
intersection across the knowledge bases of all of the members of this social
\par
unit - see Fig. 5. In general, whether the social knowledge base is formed
\par
by taking the intersection across all of the members of the social unit or just
\par
some sub-unit within the larger, the larger the social unit the smaller the so-
\par
cial knowledge base. For example, the amount of information shared, on
\par
average, by two individuals in the United States is smaller than the amount
\par
of information shared, on average, by two students living on Third East.
\par
The level of social validity chosen when constructing the social knowl-
\par
edge base leads to different predictions. For example, if the level of validity
\par
desired is tacit consensus by the simple majority of the individuals in the so-
\par
cial unit then there may be no individual in the society whose knowledge base
\par
contains the entire social knowledge base - see Fig. 5. Whereas, if all in-
\par
dividuals in the social unit are expected to consent then all individuals will
\par
share all of the information in the social knowledge base.
\par
\emph{Loci of consent}
\par
I suggest that different members of the society will have different per-
\par
ceptions of what knowledge is social, in part because of their structural posi-
\par
tion in the society and in part because of differences in the level of tacit
\par
consensus to a fact expected by that individual in order to attach the label
\par
"social knowledge." In the first case, for different structural groups in the
\par
society, different information will be considered to be social knowledge; e.g.,
\par
on Third East it is a piece of social knowledge that \emph{if someone makes a pun}
\par
\emph{they are thrown in the shower }whereas on other parts of campus making a
\par
pun was not such a capital offense, and in still other parts, the offender
\par
might be thrown in the moat. Each piece of information has a different locus
\par
of consent. In the second case, one individual might consider a piece of
\par
information to be social if he can presume it to be shared by the average
\par
man on the street, e.g., if he has seen it on t.v., whereas a different individual
\par
might consider a piece of information to be social only if he has personal ex-
\par
perience and if all of his acquaintances have communicated this piece of in-
\par
formation. I would suggest that most individuals probably have a naive no-
\par
tion of social knowledge as that information shared by "everyone" where
\par
"everyone" is the vast majority of members of their current group. Together,
\par
these notions are similar to Polanyi's notion of consensus across a network
\par
of critics.401
\par
Fig. 5. Social knowledge: shared facts. Social knowledge is based on tacit consensus to a set
\par
of facts, on a fact by fact basis. The more individuals in the society who have a particular
\par
fact in their respective knowledge base the higher the level of social validity attained by that
\par
fact. Social knowledge exists at different levels of social validity, different levels of agreement
\par
to the fact by the members of the social unit. In this example, simple majority has been used
\par
as the level of social validity, a piece of knowledge is in the social knowledge base just in case
\par
it is in the majority of the group members individual knowledge bases.
\par
Social knowledge has a dualistic nature - it is known and consented to
\par
by individuals who share a social tie and each piece of social knowledge is
\par
in itself a social tie. Individuals can speak of everybody knowing 'x' because,
\par
within a particular mapping of the social network (when looking at particu-
\par
lar ties) most of the individuals who are tied to that individual do in fact
\par
know and consent to that fact [20]. Further, that social fact, that piece of so-
\par
cial knowledge, in and of itself becomes a defining characteristic of that so-
\par
cial structure, it is a tie which connects individuals in that social unit. Social
\par
knowledge thus serves to reflect social structure and both knowledge and
\par
structure evolve reflexively.
\par
\emph{Potentially available knowledge}
\par
For the society, at any particular time, there is a set of potentially availa-
\par
ble information - the set of facts formed by taking the union over the
\par
knowledge bases of all of the members of the society. This set includes any402
\par
information that is known to at least one member of the social unit. All in-
\par
dividuals are assumed to have potential access to the same information.
\par
However, what knowledge they actually acquire, when they acquire it, how
\par
they value that knowledge, and whether or not they even accept it and incor-
\par
porate it into their knowledge base is affected both by their current flame of
\par
reference and their position in the social network. It is the co-development
\par
of the knowledge bases of all of the members of the social unit that leads
\par
to the development of the social knowledge base and the alteration of the so-
\par
cial network.
\par
\emph{Acritical knowledge}
\par
Since the social knowledge base is built upon a piece by piece basis, the
\par
resultant internal networks are not formed by generalization, and so do not
\par
necessarily have a hierarchical structure. Hence, there is no such thing as co-
\par
herency on a frame by frame basis, only coherency in the knowledge base in
\par
its entirety. Recall, that the individuals' knowledge bases are coherent in a
\par
frame by frame fashion, but not necessarily in entirety. Consequently, regard-
\par
less of the level of social validity used, it is possible for the social knowledge
\par
base to be incoherent. The social knowledge base may contain pieces of in-
\par
formation that are in direct contradiction to each other. For example, the so-
\par
cial knowledge base may contain both the fact that \emph{the tutor must be able}
\par
\emph{to help freshmen with their course work, }and that \emph{the tutor does not need}
\par
\emph{to be able to help freshmen with their course work. }The incoheren-
\par
cy/inconsistency of the social knowledge base is exacerbated if the level of
\par
social validity required is based on the tacit consensus of fewer members of
\par
the social unit than a majority. For example, typical cases of inconsistent
\par
social knowledge with low levels of social validity are clich6s. Thus, by virtue
\par
of its social existence external to any one individual, social knowledge is
\par
acritical.
\par
\textbf{5. K n o w l e d g e A c q u i s i t i o n - A M o d e l}
\par
The foregoing models of social and individual knowledge make it possi-
\par
ble to provide a mechanism by which the associated knowledge bases evolve.
\par
The proposed mechanism centers around the reconstruction of meaning as
\par
the individuals acquire knowledge. Recall that concepts are defined by their
\par
relationship to other concepts. Each time the individual acquires a new piece
\par
of information, incorporates it into his knowledge base, the meaning of the
\par
concepts linked by this information changes. No mechanisms for deleting in-
\par
formation are proposed. Thus knowledge acquisition becomes a heuristic act
\par
which irreversibly alters the meaning. Social meaning, shared facts relative to403
\par
a single concept, evolves, is reconstructed, as the social unit moves through
\par
a series of tasks and the individuals communicate information. Shared ex-
\par
perience leads to shared knowledge because it provides an opportunity for
\par
interaction, hence communication, focuses all individuals on frames that,
\par
while not identical nor even concerned with defining the same topic, all con-
\par
tain a great deal of information relative to the experience and hence have a
\par
higher degree of overlap (share more facts) than would any random set of
\par
frames.
\par
ACQUISITION OF KNOWLEDGE BY THE INDIVIDUAL
\par
Interaction/communication leads to cognitive development. The in-
\par
dividual's knowledge base, specifically his current frame of reference (i.e., the
\par
frame for the task he is performing) affects what information he chooses to
\par
communicate and whether or not he incorporates that information which is
\par
communicated to him into his knowledge base. In the following discussion,
\par
the concern is with what \emph{can }be accepted and what \emph{can }be communicated;
\par
not, what \emph{will }be. The difference between what \emph{can }and what \emph{will }be may be
\par
a function of emotional state, intelligence, physical handicaps, etc. Whether
\par
or not this is true, is beyond the scope of the current article. One possible
\par
interpretation of the following work is that factors such as intelligence and
\par
emotions are "noise" in the system, with a probability distribution, such that
\par
the following model is a model of what happens not only on average, but in
\par
general (e.g., Gaussian).
\par
\emph{Processing demands on knowledge availability}
\par
As an information processor with limited time and resources, the in-
\par
dividual at any one time, in order to accomplish a task, is focused on, has
\par
immediate access to, only part of his knowledge base. The requirements of
\par
task performance limit the individual, such that, while performing that task
\par
he has immediate access to that part of his knowledge base that focuses on
\par
that task, i.e., the task frame is his current frame. The level of access he has
\par
to other frames in his knowledge base is directly a function of the degree to
\par
which those frames are similar, share facts, with his current frame. Recall that
\par
the individual's knowledge base at a structural level contains a set of inter-
\par
locked frames. The degree of interlock is the number of facts the two frames
\par
share - their intersection. We can imagine a network of frames - see Fig. 6,
\par
such that two frames are linked if they intersect in terms of facts. In this net-
\par
work, there exists a path between two frames if the two frames intersect or
\par
if they are linked to frames which are linked together. The individual's cur-
\par
rent frame limits what information the individual can communicate and ac-
\par
cept at that point in time by limiting access. Specifically, if we think of access404
