\par
\emph{What information can be communicated}
\par
A n individual can not c o m m u n i c a t e a fact that he does not know. The
\par
individual can only c o m m u n i c a t e inf o r m ation in his cognitive structure; i.e.,
\par
the fact to be c o m m u n i c a t e d must be in his knowledge base. W heth er or not405
\par
the facts in a particular flame can be communicated is dependent on the de-
\par
gree of access that the individual has to that flame at that time. All facts
\par
within a particular flame are equally likely to be communicated vis-\textasciitilde t-vis that
\par
frame. The propensity to communicate a particular fact is thus proportional
\par
to the number of frames that contain that fact and the degree of access to
\par
those frames at that point in time. Thus, the individual can not communicate
\par
a fact unless it is both in his knowledge base and he has access to it, given
\par
his current flame [22]. Secondly, given time and effort constraints on com-
\par
munication, the individual is most likely to communicate those facts which
\par
are currently being reinforced (occurring in many frames) and easily accessi-
\par
ble (occurring in the current flame or one connected by a strong path).
\par
\emph{What information can be accepted}
\par
Cognitive development is seen as the alteration of the existing cognitive
\par
structure via the acquisition of knowledge. Under normal conditions, it is as-
\par
sumed that individuals can only acquire knowledge; i.e., they can not "un-
\par
acquire" knowledge [23]. A simple, but powerful principle that I would argue
\par
is necessary for defining mechanisms of knowledge acquisition is \emph{the princi-}
\par
\emph{ple of immediate comprehension: }Individuals will utilize new information to
\par
alter their cognitive structures if they can directly relate that information to
\par
information currently in their cognitive structures.
\par
Given the model of cognitive structures as a collection of facts, a knowl-
\par
edge base, then immediate comprehension [24], the ability to immediately re-
\par
late new information to information currently in one's cognitive structure,
\par
can be redefined in terms of the co-occurrence of concepts in the new fact
\par
and the facts in the current knowledge base. A fact is modeled as two con-
\par
cepts and a connection between them, and two facts are linked if they share
\par
a concept. This is simply a matter of pattern matching on the concepts (their
\par
symbols). Thus the individual can acquire information and yet have no un-
\par
derstanding of what the communicators mean by the concepts involved,
\par
Thus, a fact can be added to the individual's knowledge base just in case that
\par
knowledge base contains at least one fact to which the new fact will link. If
\par
one of the concepts in the new fact is \emph{understood }by the individual, occurs
\par
in that individual's cognitive structure, then the individual can relate that new
\par
fact to at least one other fact currently in his cognitive structure, and so can
\par
add the new fact to the knowledge base.
\par
Recall, that all concepts acquired meaning only by their relationship to
\par
other concepts (the principle of relative definition). Acquisition of knowl-
\par
edge via the principle of immediate comprehension leads to the development
\par
of the cognitive structure by modifying the meaning of the concept(s) which
\par
are contained in the new fact. When a new fact is added to the knowledge
\par
base it serves to link together two previously unlinked concepts. Hence, the406
\par
meaning of those concepts has been altered. Given that there is no mecha-
\par
nism for deleting facts, knowledge acquisition is thus a heuristic act which
\par
is irreversible. Cognitive development becomes an incremental process, the
\par
result of a gradual construction process (like putting tinker-toys together):
\par
what one learns, how one interprets information, is dependent upon one's
\par
previous understanding, one's previous knowledge base. The principle of im-
\par
mediate comprehension leads to the result that knowledge acquisition occurs
\par
one fact at a time. A fact can be added to the individual's knowledge base
\par
only if it shares a concept with at least one other fact already in the knowl-
\par
edge base. Given that there is no mechanism for "un-acquiring" facts, there
\par
will be no fact in the individual's knowledge base that is not connected to
\par
at least one other fact. Moreover, since frames are hierarchical structures
\par
built up o u t of facts through the process of generalization, there will be no
\par
frame in the individual's knowledge base that is not linked to at least one oth-
\par
er frame. Hence, between any two frames, there will always be a path; howev-
\par
er, the path may be long (number of intervening frames) and it may be very
\par
weak (the links between any two frames may be based on very few shared
\par
facts). Further, the only way to acquire new concepts, to increase one's
\par
vocabulary, is by relating the new concept to one currently in one's cognitive
\par
structure; i.e., by adding a new fact that contains that new concept.
\par
The principle of immediate comprehension limits what the individual
\par
\emph{c a n }accept; however, it does not guarantee acceptance. The individual, as an
\par
information processor subject to time and effort constraints, is more likely
\par
to acquire knowledge if that knowledge is immediately relevant. That is, if
\par
a piece of information is communicated, an individual is more likely to add
\par
that information to his knowledge base, given that he can add it, if that infor-
\par
mation directly relates to information which is easily accessible. Basically, the
\par
internal structure of the knowledge base, the network of interlocked frames,
\par
serves to inhibit l\textasciitilde nowledge acquisition.
\par
The principle of immediate comprehension applies on a frame by frame
\par
basis; e.g., the individual will not add a communicated fact to his current
\par
frame unless that fact shares a concept with at least one fact in the individu-
\par
al's current frame. The individual's propensity for accepting a particular
\par
piece of information is inversely proportional to the ease of accessing those
\par
facts which share a concept with the new fact. Given the network of frames,
\par
the individual's propensity to accept a particular piece of information into
\par
a particular frame is inversely proportional to the length of the path connect-
\par
ing the current frame and the frame in which the new piece of information
\par
is to be added. In a literal sense, the individual's currrent frame acts as his
\par
frame of reference - that body of knowledge that admits understanding and
\par
interpretation of new information - which, at the same time, allows him to
\par
understand any information that is directly related and inhibits him from un-
\par
derstandin\textasciitilde{} information that is not directly related.407
\par
Further, each time an individual has a particular fact c o m m u n i c a t e d to
\par
him he has a chance to accept it, to in co r porate it into his knowledge base.
\par
Thus, the m o re a particular fact is c o m m u n i c a t e d , the more likely it is that
\par
the individual will eventually acquire it. T h e individual's propensity for ac-
\par
cepting a particular piece of i n f o r m a t i o n is thus p r o p o r t i o n a l to the frequen-
\par
cy with which that fact is c o m m u n i c a t e d to him.
\par
