\par
EVOLUTION OF SOCIAL KNOWLEDGE
\par
The development of social knowledge, social learning, involves tacit
\par
consensus over perceived regularities. Social learning occurs as individuals'
\par
cognitive structures co-evolve. As individuals interact the number of facts
\par
that they share increases. Social knowledge evolves as more and more of the
\par
members of the society share the same facts. Social knowledge evolves
\par
on a fact by fact basis. As more individuals in the society share a particular
\par
fact, that fact increases in social validity, and becomes a piece of social
\par
knowledge. A potentially available fact becomes a social fact as more in-
\par
dividuals acquire it. Thus, social knowledge, as shared cognitive structure, is
\par
also a by-product of human interaction. However, because social knowledge
\par
requires tacit consensus among individuals, it will evolve more slowly than
\par
will the individual's knowledge base.
\par
Since there is no mechanism at the individual level for "un-acquiring"
\par
information, social knowledge will continue to grow if membership in the so-
\par
ciety is fixed and there is at least one path connecting everyone in the social
\par
unit in terms of propensity to interact.
\par
Since social knowledge requires tacit consensus by the members of the
\par
society, if the society's membership changes, social knowledge may actually
\par
decrease. For example, at one time, all of the members of the society may
\par
share a particular fact and so it has a high level of social validity. However,
\par
several years later, the society may have many new members who do not share410
\par
this fact with the original members of the society and so this fact has a lower
\par
level of social validity. Thus, social knowledge is continuously constructed as
\par
members enter and leave the society. The longer a group of individuals are
\par
together, the more they interact, the more similar their knowledge bases be-
\par
come, and the more social their knowledge.
\par
\emph{Social consensus}
\par
Social knowledge becomes a problematic and continuously renegotiated
\par
data base because who knows (and hence tacitly consents to the existence of)
\par
what information is dependent on one's position in the continuously chang-
\par
ing social structure. A fact is considered to be a part of social knowledge if
\par
it has a certain level of social validity. The social validity of a piece of infor-
\par
mation is a function of the level of tacit consensus. For each piece of social
\par
knowledge, tacit consensus to that fact by members of the society, which im-
\par
plies at least an implicit agreement to the value/existence of that piece of in-
\par
formation, is not an artifact, but a necessary prerequisite for that fact to be
\par
a part of the social knowledge base. Consensus to a set of information, which
\par
is seen to imply at least an implicit agreement to the meaning of the included
\par
concepts, under a constructural framework, is again not an artifact but a
\par
product of consensus over each piece of information, each fact, separately.
\par
Since each individual has a different knowledge base, at the social level con-
\par
cepts can only be defined probabilistically [26]. Further, since the social
\par
knowledge base is continually constructed on a piece by piece basis, meaning
\par
becomes a negotiated dynamic phenomenon that changes as the structural
\par
makeup of the social unit changes.
\par
The pervasiveness of a piece of information, the extent of its social va-
\par
lidity, is thus a function of the length of chains in the social network along
\par
which that information is known. In this case, as one moves along the social
\par
network, as one moves from one social group to another, concepts gradually
\par
shift in their social meaning, and the set of shared facts changes. Consensus
\par
to a set of facts thus serves, implicitly, to define group membership.
\par
\emph{Incomplete social knowledge}
\par
Social knowledge, as a function of shared individual knowledge, is not
\par
necessarily complete. For social knowledge to be complete, at a particular
\par
level of validity, there must not be a piece of information which is not shared
\par
by a "majority" of the individuals in the society. Where absolute validity is
\par
required, complete social knowledge would occur only if the union of the
\par
knowledge bases is equivalent to the intersection.
\par
\emph{inaccessible social knowledge}
\par
The lower the level of social validity attached to a particular fact, the411
\par
less likely it is that that fact will be accessible to the individual. Because immedi-
\par
ate comprehension is required for acquiring knowledge, and since "social
\par
knowledge" is not necessarily shared by everyone, a particular member of the
\par
social unit may not only not have a particular piece of social knowledge in
\par
his knowledge base, but he may be unable to directly access it because he
\par
does not have other information that relates to this piece of information.
\par
Even though, "everyone else" in the society may have access to this fact. Be-
\par
cause of the way the social knowledge base is constructed on a piece by piece
\par
basis, some individuals may use only part of the base, and others may use
\par
a different part.
\par
The basic idea is that if the individual is in a particular sub-group his
\par
individual knowledge base may be so different from the majority knowledge
\par
base which forms the social knowledge that he 'literally' can't comprehend
\par
it. In this case one might want to question whether or not he should even be
\par
considered a member of the social unit. The degree of overlap between the
\par
individual knowledge base and the social thus becomes a measure of sociali-
\par
zation, and social membership.
\par
The principle of immediate comprehension implies that the individual
\par
can only access a social fact in which at least one of the concepts also occurs
\par
in his current set of active concepts - those concepts in the current cognitive
\par
structure. Socialization thus becomes an incremental process during which
\par
the new member is acquainted with a new set of concepts by creating new
\par
connections between old concepts and then thus redefining old concepts in
\par
terms of new ones. And then, the new member is acquainted with those facts
\par
that can only be related in terms of the new concepts.
\par
\emph{Social existence}
\par
Social knowledge maintains an existence independent of any particular
\par
individual precisely because that knowledge is shared by many individuals.
\par
A single individual can enter or leave the society with little direct impact on
\par
social knowledge. His propensity to communicate while a member of the so-
\par
ciety will affect the development of social knowledge, even though social
\par
knowledge exists independently of groups of individuals. The social world is
\par
maintaned as a perceptual entity due to the wholistic effect of reflexive con-
\par
sensus; i.e., social knowledge is a function of group membership, and group
\par
membership is a function of social knowledge. As groups develop and dis-
\par
solve, social knowledge changes.
\par
