\par
\textbf{6. Knowledge Acquisition - Results}
\par
The foregoing model rests on the theory that social knowledge and in-
\par
dividual knowledge co-evolve as individuals interact. There are a variety of413
\par
implications, predictions, that follow from this theory. A few of these impli-
\par
cations relating to the evolution of individual and social knowledge bases will
\par
be explored. Further, the specific models of information and knowledge
\par
bases presented herein allow the researcher to explore a variety of issues rela-
\par
tive to the nature of knowledge acquisition. Herein, tools based on these
\par
models are used to look at two social communication issues - miscommuni-
\par
cation and persuasion. These tools are described in Carley (1985b).
\par
INTERACTION AND TASK SOLUTION PROMOTE KNOWLEDGE ACQUISITION
\par
According to the model, as individuals perform tasks in the course of
\par
pursuing everyday life they acquire knowledge by acquiring new facts. In the
\par
process of acquiring new facts, they may acquire new concepts, however, the
\par
potential number of facts increases faster than the number of concepts [27].
\par
In the proposed data set, it was found that during the three months of tutor
\par
selection the cognitive structures of the students on Third East did increase.
\par
Moving from the beginning to the end of the process, the average number of
\par
concepts increases from 49.27 to 52.87 and the average number of facts in-
\par
creases from 194 to 233.8. Further, as individuals within the group interact,
\par
it is expected that the more the individuals interact the more chances there
\par
are for communication and hence a transferral of information. Thus, over
\par
time it is expected that the cognitive structures of the members of the society
\par
will become more similar. And, in fact, on Third East, the Standard deviation
\par
in number of concepts/facts used decreases over time: for concepts from
\par
16.81 to 15.76, and for facts from 89.15 to 86.36. These trends indicate that
\par
shared experience and interaction during the tutor selection process, led to
\par
not only an increase in individual knowledge; but, to an increase in shared
\par
knowledge. It is important to note that, even though new information was
\par
available to the individuals during the tutor selection process, knowledge ac-
\par
quisition through interaction dominated knowledge acquisition through in-
\par
dependent discoveries - if this were not the case the standard deviation
\par
would not have decreased.
\par
SOCIAL STRUCTURE AFFECTS KNOWLEDGE ACQUISITION
\par
In a social unit whose existence is not defined by the task in question
\par
one would expect that the established pattern of interaction, the extant social
\par
structure among the members of the social unit, would affect the behavior of the
\par
members of the social unit. One might even argue that the more established
\par
the group, the greater the potential for the underlying social structure to af-
\par
fect behavior vis-\textasciitilde t-vis a particular task. When cognitive development is seen414
\par
as the result o f social interaction, then the social structure o f the social unit
\par
(as defined by the interaction pattern) will be reflected in the socio-cognitive
\par
structure (as defined by shared knowledge).
\par
In Table I the relationship between structural position and shared
\par
knowledge is presented. T he me mbe rs o f Third East were broken down into
\par
structural groups using C O N C O R (Breiger et al., 1975; Carley, 1984). Joint
\par
m a ps were constructed for each o f the structural groups. These joint maps
\par
were f o r m e d by taking the intersection o f facts that occurred in all of the
\par
g r o u p members' individual knowledge bases [28]. Th e level of intra-group in-
\par
teraction and shared knowledge are b o t h recorded. Note, the extant social
