\par
\emph{Shared position leads to shared knowledge}
\par
The first point is that social knowledge and social structure are some-
\par
what reflexive; i.e., individuals who share similar structural positions will
\par
tend to have similar knowledge bases. For example, Zaccur and Ernest are
\par
members of the same social structural group (have similar interaction pat-
\par
terns), and they tend to occupy the same socio-cognitive space vis-a-vis the
\par
concept \emph{tutor }(share facts relating to this concept). The confluence of struc-
\par
tural and cognitive ties creates a shared reality, a consensus. Despite rare di-
\par
rect interaction these two individuals construct their formulations of the
\par
concept \emph{tutor }in the same way - they both utilize non-hall-based informa-
\par
tion and evaluate information on the basis of their off-hall experiences.
\par
The second point is that, if consensus was merely the result of social
\par
training, of shared experience, and not the result of interaction, then we
\par
would expect that, in a group where the individuals did not interact with each
\par
other and yet were in similar structural positions and shared a large number
\par
of external social ties (hence experiences), that there would be a high level of
\par
consensus. However, this is not the case. Referring to Table I, we see that the
\par
Loners, specifically Zebadiah and Woodie form such a group. They have an
\par
intra-group interation level of 0 at the close knit level - both said that they
\par
rarely interacted. And they share a large number of external social ties; e.g.,
\par
they are both in R.O.T.C., don't like to drink, are violently opposed to drugs,
\par
and conservative. On the basis of shared ties we would expect these individu-
\par
als' knowledge bases would exhibit a high degree of consensus; but, they
\par
don't - chi squared for the facts is only 73.46. However, they do exhibit a
\par
high degree of concepts in common (45.24\%). This suggests that: shared so-
\par
cial ties (similar experience) result in shared vocabulary, and not necessarily
\par
shared meaning. However, given that all concepts are defined only by their
\par
relationship to other concepts, the fact that shared position leads to only a
\par
similar vocabulary means that individuals who occupy similar positions may
\par
use the same words (perhaps even with the same frequency) and yet mean
\par
something very different by the words that they use.
\par
And a third point is that structural position indicates a particular level
\par
of connectedness [29] which in turn affects not the exact information in the
\par
knowledge base, but its structure and the amount of knowledge. Note, those
\par
individuals who did not have strong ties with the hall, who did not spend a
\par
lot of time interacting with hall members other than perhaps those in their
\par
own group (e.g., the Loners and the Socially active) tend to have highly elabo-
\par
rated maps at the onset of the process (large knowledge bases: average num-
\par
ber of concepts is 68.5 and average number of facts is 295.5) and as the416
\par
process continues their knowledge bases appear to shrink. Whereas, the more
\par
hall-dominated students (Laidback and Doers), those who have strong ties
\par
and interact with hall members outside of their own structural group tend to
\par
have less elaborate knowledge bases at the onset (concept average is 43.5 and
\par
fact average is 164.3) and as the process continues their knowledge bases ap-
\par
pear to increase. This behavior is probably a function of social ties. That is,
\par
both the Loners and the Socially active do not rely on the hall for social life
\par
- they maintain, albeit for perhaps different reasons, only weak ties with the
\par
hall. Whereas the Doers and the Laidback spend a larger overall fraction of
\par
their time socializing on the hall, have strong ties with the hall.
\par
Self-dominated individuals, those with only weak ties with the hall will
\par
tend to have a wider range of social contacts, more interactions with non-hall
\par
members. This will give them a wider base of information to draw on and
\par
hence a more diversified knowledge base. Whereas, hall-dominated individu-
\par
als will spend more time interacting on the hall, have a more narrow range
\par
of social contacts, and so will have a more narrow base of information to
\par
draw on. For a task that is specific to the living group, as in the case of the
\par
tutor selection, individuals with predominantly weak and inter-group ties will
\par
actually be acquiring less information about the task than will those with
\par
strong inter-group ties: their base of potential knowledge is wider and hence
\par
less focused on the local group's task. Thus, although their non-hall oriented
\par
activities may actually give them a head start in so far as having elaborate
\par
initial conceptions about the problem, in the long run this wider base of in-
\par
teraction may actually serve to inhibit the flow of task-specific information.
\par
On the other side of the coin, hall-dominated individuals, those with
\par
predominantly strong inter-group ties will at the onset have access to only so-
\par
cial knowledge, previously agreed to rules. Without the influx of new infor-
\par
mation, this group could in fact stagnate. However, given that there will be
\par
some new knowledge, the group with strong ties will actually learn new infor-
\par
mation faster as they will start out with a larger initial set of shared knowl-
\par
edge and they will be interacting with more individuals in the social unit.
\par
Individuals are more likely to acquire a piece of information if it is already
\par
shared by a high percentage of the individuals in similar structural positions.
\par
This is a direct result of the previous rules for communication and knowledge
\par
acquisition. Individuals in similar structural positions interact with the same
\par
set of individuals to the same degree. Consequently, on average, they are like-
\par
ly to receive the same information. If a high percentage of the individuals in
\par
similar structural positions already share a particular fact then it must be the
\par
case that many individuals who communicate with this set of individuals
\par
have this fact in their respective knowledge bases. The more individuals who
\par
have a particular fact in their respective knowledge base, the more likely that
\par
fact is to be communicated. And the more often a fact is communicated to
\par
an individual, the more likely he is to acquire it.417
\par
\emph{Level of interaction determines level of shared knowledge}
\par
When members of the social unit interact there is a chance for communi-
\par
cation; and hence, a chance that the communication is successful, that infor-
\par
mation is transferred. In the simplest model, \emph{ceterius paribus, }the greater the
\par
degree of interaction between two individuals, the greater the amount of in-
\par
formation that is transferred, the greater the degree of convergence in their
\par
cognitive structures. In this way, interaction leads to the conversion of in-
\par
dividual cognitive structures into a similitude of the general conception of
\par
reality - i.e., socialization. Note that in Table I, as the level of intra-group
\par
interaction decreases, so does shared knowledge. Where the interaction levels
\par
are not homogeneous - the members of the social unit can be arrayed on
\par
the basis of the degree of interaction - interaction may actually force
\par
destabilization - divergence of shared cognitive structures. That is, it is pos-
\par
sible for the paths by which individual's cognitive structures converge to a
\par
shared cognitive structure for the social unit as a whole to differ according
\par
to the patterns of interaction. This imbalance is augmented by the fact that
\par
it is possible for the rate of convergence in shared cognitive structure for any
\par
particular sub-group to be higher than the rate of convergence for the social
\par
unit as a whole. Differences in the patterns of individual interaction propen-
\par
sities across the group form structural groups whose existence is reinforced
\par
as individuals' knowledge bases within the sub-group converge while the
\par
knowledge bases of individuals in different sub-groups diverge.
\par
In Table I we see that those cases where structural similarity breeds cog-
\par
nitive similarity are exactly those cases where structural similarity and shared
\par
interaction paths merge. That is, the higher the level of intra-group interac-
\par
tion the higher the level of shared knowledge. The model suggests that the
\par
tighter the social sub-group (the higher the internal interaction among the
\par
members of the sub-group relative to the amount of external interaction) the
\par
greater the pressure toward shared knowledge among the members of the
\par
sub-group. Further, given two tightly structured groups with little cross-
\par
interaction the result should be divergent conceptions across sub-groups.
\par
Specifically, in sub-groups that are tightly internally structured - where the
\par
members of the sub-group spend the majority of their time with each other
\par
with little external contact (e.g., cliques) - one would expect that the mem-
\par
bers of such sub-groups would portray not only similar knowledge bases, but
\par
particular facts that are not shared by non-clique members. And, in fact this
\par
is the case. For example, the Gnerds a n d the Heads represent opposing
\par
cliques with high intra-group interaction levels and low inter-group interac-
\par
tion levels (as in Fig. 3). If we look at the joint maps formed by taking the
\par
intersection of the group members' respective knowledge bases relative to the
\par
concept \emph{tutor }we see t h a t in fact there is some overlap between the two
\par
groups; but, they also have different facts - Figs. 10 and 11 [30], For exam-\textbf{418}
