The foregoing findings suggest the following ideas:
\par
{$\bullet$} Shared social structural position is sufficient for shared vocabulary.
\par
{$\bullet$} Interaction is necessary but not sufficient for shared knowledge.
\par
{$\bullet$} Coupling shared position and interaction increases the propensity
\par
for shared knowledge.
\par
S O C I A L K N O W L E D G E EVOLVES SLOWLY
\par
Given that the amount of interaction that can occur between the mem-
\par
bers of the social unit is predicated upon the amount of time that the in-
\par
dividuals have to interact, when time is a scarce resource, the potential for
\par
divergence of shared cognitive structures is at its highest. That is, given
\par
enough time, the knowledge bases Of the members of the social unit will con-
\par
verge, barring the continual influx of new information, and barring changes
\par
in group membership and assuming at least one chain of interaction that
\par
connects every individual in the society. The rate of that convergence is de-
\par
pendent on the social structure. Given enough time, even the most unlikely
\par
interaction will occur and all knowledge will eventually be transferred. How-
\par
ever, under normal limited conditions, time is a scarce resource, and extant
\par
social structure, for the most part, determines interaction, thus limiting in-
\par
formation flow.
\par
Consequently, social knowledge should evolve slowly since tacit consen-
\par
sus is required to produce social knowledge, and such consensus will not oc-
\par
cur if the extant social structure inhibits interaction and hence
\par
communication and the acquisition of knowledge by individuals in different
\par
structural groups. In order for a fact to be added to the social knowledge
\par
base there must be tacit consensus to this fact. The level of tacit consensus
\par
increases as more individuals acquire a particular fact. Given that structural
\par
position limits whom one interacts with, and that interaction is necessary for
\par
knowledge acquisition, it will generally be the case that social knowledge will
\par
evolve slowly. The more segmented the society, structurally, the slower social
\par
knowledge will evolve, etc.
\par
These processes are further exacerbated by the fact that in pursuing their
\par
daily lives, the members of the society are not necessarily working on the
\par
same task and hence are not guaranteed to even be using the same set of con-
\par
cepts, let alone facts. This phenomenon is also exacerbated by a dynamic so-
\par
cial membership. That is, the level of tacit concensus changes as individuals
\par
enter and leave the society and as the members of the society garner new421
