SOCIAL KNOWLEDGE ADMITS RAPID COMMUNICATION
\par
Within a group with a substantial common flame of reference, substan-
\par
tial amounts of social knowledge, the communication can proceed at what
\par
appears to be a rapid rate due to the large number of shared facts. The larger
\par
the number of shared facts the more disjoint the communication may appear
\par
to an outsider as much of the communication is implicit. Recall that in-
\par
dividuals with a substantial shared frame of reference can immediately com-
\par
prehend any fact that is related to any of their shared knowledge. Hence, for
\par
a member of the group to communicate a particular piece of information to
\par
the outsider may take a substantial amount of education; whereas, to com-
\par
municate it internally takes virtually none. This suggests that, what to the
\par
outsider may appear as rapid communication among group members, is sim-
\par
ply a result of communicating highly context-specific information; i.e., of
\par
communicating information connected to the shared-knowledge base. To the
\par
extent that a shared body of knowledge, a shared frame of reference, can be
\par
thought of as culture, the foregoing argument has the interpretation that cul-
\par
ture, simply by being a broad common-knowledge base, admits apparent rap-
\par
id communication among the members.
\par
In Fig. 14 the explicit knowledge shared by Hazel and Lowell is present-
\par
ed. In Fig. 15 this map has been expanded to include the implicit knowledge.
\par
Implicit knowledge is that set of facts that both individuals shared by virtue
\par
of being members of the same socio-cultural environment. In conversations
\par
with other members of the social unit individuals do not need to reiterate
\par
shared social knowledge, as it is understood. This does not mean that these
\par
individuals won't reiterate shared knowledge, just that they are less likely to.424
