\emph{Increased context sensitvity}
\par
As fewer facts are c o m m u n i c a t e d , the sensitivity o f those facts to the lo-
\par
cal context, to the shared frame o f reference increases. Consider the case
\par
where we have a group o f individuals with a large shared-knowledge base,
\par
and a virtual outsider - s o m e o n e w ho shares relatively few facts with the
\par
groups' members. In this case, if a fact is c o m m u n i c a t e d by a m e m b e r of the
\par
group, the outsider will stand little chance of c o m p r e h e n d i n g that fact; i.e.,
\par
it will be quite unlikely that one o f the concepts that comprise that fact will
\par
be in his knowledge base [32]. Basically, the tighter the group, the less likely426
\par
it is that an outsider will be able to comprehend communication between
\par
group members. The outsider does not have the context (the shared flame of
\par
reference formed out of the set of shared facts) to understand the communi-
\par
cated information; and, the more context there is (the larger the shared
\par
frame) the more likely it is that internally-communicated information will
\par
not be immediately comprehensible to outsiders. For example, a non-member
\par
of Third East can read, in interviews with the students, the fact that \emph{a gnerd}
\par
\emph{won'tfit in on the hall. }However, the non-member is not privy to the shared
\par
meaning of the concept \emph{gnerd }on Third East, and so won't know facts that
\par
members of Third East take for granted such as \emph{gnerds are not entertaining}
\par
\emph{and sociable, gnerds can tutor a large number o f students, gnerds have taken}
\par
\emph{hard courses, gnerds work too hard, }and so on.
\par
M I S C O M M U N I C A T I O N - T A L K I N G P A S T O N E A N O T H E R
\par
The external divergence and internal convergence of sub-groups modi-
\par
fies the rate of communication between members of the social unit by affect-
\par
ing the meaning of concepts. Consequently, members of the groups can think
\par
that they are agreeing, because they use the same words (concepts) and yet
\par
they are actually miscommunicating. Again, if we assume that there are a
\par
limited number of facts, then, the larger the common frame, the less likely
\par
it is that individuals will talk past each other, will miscommunicate. If we de-
\par
fine miscommunication as the incorporation of the communicated fact into
\par
a chain of facts other than the chain expected by the communicator, then as
\par
the size of the shared frame increases, relative to the size of the non-shared
\par
frame, the likelihood is decreased that a particular fact is embedded in a
\par
chain that is 'unexpected' by the communicator. Such a chain of concepts,
\par
such a network of facts is considered a "critical path" [33].
\par
The communicator's expectations will be based on his frame. If, for ex-
\par
ample, there are only 3 chains of facts in which the fact b c is embedded
\par
in the communicator's flame (A's frame), and there are 4 such potential
\par
chains in the communicatee's frame (B's frame) then clearly the likelihood of
\par
miscommunication is going to depend on the relative size of the shared
\par
frame, the set of shared knowledge.
\par
Miscommunication occurs when a fact is communicated and shared, but
\par
the meaning of at least one of the concepts is not shared. Recall, that this
\par
is possible as concepts are only defined by their relationship to other con-
\par
cepts. Miscommunication will be worse, in the sense of less expected, if the
\par
level of consensus to the meaning of the concepts that compose the fact is
\par
high.
\par
Let me illustrate this with an example of miscommunication that oc-
\par
curred on Third East. One of the individuals applying for the tutorship posi-427
\par
tion was in fact a current Third Easter. Referring back to Figs. 10 and 11, the
\par
Gnerds' and Heads' maps of the concept tutor, you can see that both groups
\par
acknowledge the fact that a \emph{Third Easter wouM be a good tutor, }and that a
\par
\emph{Third Easter wouM fit in on the hall. }A Third Easter actually applied for the
\par
tutorship position. The Heads expected that the Gnerds would rank this can-
\par
didate high when it came time for the vote; however, they did not, and the
\par
Heads were confused.
\par
This confusion is due to miscommunication. Miscommunication is par-
\par
ticularly unexpected in this case because, in the social unit as a whole, there
\par
is a dense socially-accepted meaning for the concept \emph{Third Easter }i.e., many
\par
of the facts that serve to define what a Third Easter is, are part of the social
\par
knowledge base. In Fig. 16 the shared social meaning of the concept \emph{Third}
\par
\emph{Easter }is presented. Because of the high level of shared meaning to the con-
\par
cept \emph{Third Easter }the Heads don't expect the Gnerds' behavior. However, if
\par
you refer to Fig. 10 you will see that for the Gnerds there is a negative rela-
\par
tionship between the concept \emph{current hall member }and the concept
\par
\emph{lookupableto, }and that \emph{lookupableto }is an important requirement of a tutor.
\par
The path - \emph{Third Easter - - current member - - lookupableto - }is not
\par
shared by the Heads, it is part of a critical path whose existence leads to mis-
\par
communication. The Gnerds do not accept a current Third Easter as a tutor
\par
because, although he is a Third Easter and therefore fits in with the hall, he
\par
is not lookupableto. And for the Gnerds, lookupableto is a strong re-
\par
quirement.
