P E R S U A S I O N - T H E U S E O F S O C I A L K N O W L E D G E
\par
Now, let's say that the Gnerds, Hazel and Lowell, want to convince the
\par
Heads that it is important that the tutor be someone who is lookupableto.
\par
To do this, the Gnerds would have to go through a process of educating the
\par
Heads. They would first have to successfully communicate a fact that ties the
\par
concept \emph{lookupableto }to a concept currently in the Heads' knowledge base,
\par
specifically, in the Heads' frame for the concept \emph{tutor }and then a fact that
\par
ties \emph{lookupableto }to the concept \emph{tutor, }e.g., they could communicate the fact
\par
\emph{someone who splits their time equally among the students is lookupableto}
\par
and then the fact that \emph{a tutor shouM be lookupableto. }In other words, they
\par
could try to persuade the Heads by communicating the facts that form the
\par
critical path. In principle, such communication could be successful; i.e., it
\par
does not counter the principle of immediate comprehension. However, I
\par
would suggest that such an approach will not be successful because it will not
\par
be reinforced by the social world.
\par
We have already seen that persuasion is more likely to succeed if in-
\par
dividuals are members of the same structural groups and have a high level
\par
of interaction; i.e., individuals in such groups have similar cognitive struc-
\par
tures. In that case, shared structural position reinforces communication and
\par
increases the likelihood of persuasion. Gnerds and the Heads as opposing
\par
cliques; their structural positions are so different that they reinforce the lack
\par
of communications, i.e., both groups have high levels of intra-group interac-
\par
tion (1.00 - see Table I) and relatively low levels of inter-group interaction,
\par
especially with each other. The members of these groups rarely interact, and
\par
so stand little chance of successfully communicating, persuading the other,
\par
if they rely on making their arguments by communicating just any critical
\par
path in their knowledge base. However, such persuasion could be enhanced
\par
if they relied on social knowledge. That is, the Gnerds have little chance of
\par
directly influencing the Heads as they rarely interact; however, they can in-
\par
fluence the Heads indirectly by influencing individuals who do interact with
\par
the Gnerds.
\par
Indirect influence is further enhanced if the persuader ties the fact he
\par
wishes to communicate to extant social knowledge. Social knowledge can be
\par
used to reinforce an argument and so increase the likelihood of persuasion.
\par
By tying the fact that one wishes to persuade the other of to a previously
\par
agreed upon set of knowledge, the persuader is using social knowledge to in-
\par
crease his likelihood of success. This point is illustrated in Fig. 17. Let's say
\par
that Aaron wants to persuade Zebadiah of either fact 1 - - 2, or 4 - - 5.
\par
Both facts are potentially communicable; i.e., both facts are immediately
\par
comprehensible by Zebadiah. Aaron will have more luck communicating fact
\par
1 - - 2 than fact 4 - - 5 because, even though neither of these facts is429
