tutor candidate is on the hall, then those concepts will be evoked and so will
\par
the concept \emph{lookupableto. }As more members of the society accept facts that
\par
contain the concept \emph{lookupableto, }more individuals in the society will com-
\par
municate facts that contain \emph{lookupableto }to the Heads. Consequently the
\par
Heads will eventually add the concept \emph{lookupableto. }Once the Heads have
\par
the concept \emph{lookupableto }in their map, persuasion becomes a matter of link-
\par
ing the concepts.
\par
It is interesting to note that, at MIT, tutor candidates often complain
\par
that the students do crazy things during the interviews, almost as if they were
\par
trying to test the candidates; e.g., staging water fights, asking outrageous
\par
questions, etc. The foregoing analysis suggests that what the students are do-
\par
ing is not so much testing the candidate as creating a situation that is known,
\par
via social knowledge, to evoke particular concepts that they want all of the
\par
members of their social unit to have in their knowledge bases. That is, the
\par
students are engineering situations by staging water fights and asking outra-
\par
geous questions as a means to increase group consensus.
\par
At any rate, one's ability to persuade another is thus enhanced if one can
\par
rely on extant social knowledge to do the education, and then make a minor
\par
modification in this social knowledge. This idea coupled with the principle
\par
of immediate comprehension suggests that persuasion will be the most suc-
\par
cessful if it relies on communicating a fact that links two well-established
\par
maps together. Moreover, one can make the prediction that the longer the
\par
chain of facts that is to be communicated, and the more removed the facts
\par
are from the extant social structure, the less likely the communication will
\par
succeed, the less likely that the receiver will be persuaded.
\par
\textbf{7. Synopsis}
\par
The constructuralist theory was briefly presented. This theory posits
\par
that the world of the individual and the social world are constructed as in-
\par
dividuals interact while performing the set of tasks that constitute daily life.
\par
Interaction leads to communication and thus to knowledge acquisition. Cog-
\par
nitive development, knowledge acquisition at the individual level, is the di-
\par
rect result, perhaps even a by-product of social interaction. Since interaction
\par
is part of everyday life, the individual's cognitive structures are continually
\par
constructed and updated. The co-development of the individual's knowledge
\par
bases produces social knowledge. Shared knowledge, social knowledge,
\par
changes individuals' propensities to interact. And, individuals' propensities
\par
to interact develop reflexively with social structure.432
\par
Constructuralism serves as the backdrop for an extended look at the
\par
relationship between social knowledge and individual knowledge. In the fore-
\par
going discussion little attention was paid to specifically modeling the way in
\par
which changes in knowledge affect interaction or to the relationship between
\par
interaction and social structure. These topics, while germane to the theory,
\par
are beyond the scope of the current paper, and so, tended to be glossed over.
\par
Rather, herein, the goal was to elaborate that part of the constructuralist the-
\par
ory that spoke to the case of knowledge acquisition at the social and in-
\par
dividual level. In this vein, several propositions and a few specific models
\par
were forwarded; e.g., articulatable knowledge, immediate comprehension,
\par
relative definition, tacit consensus, and the knowledge acquisition model.
\par
Based on these ideas, a few predictions were made and explored using the
\par
Third East data; e.g., increased interaction leads to increased shared knowl-
\par
edge, social knowledge is transient but evolves slowly, miscommunication oc-
\par
curs if critical paths are missing, and effective persuasion requires minor
\par
social modification.
\par
No claim is being made that the data prove the theory. Nor is there any
\par
claim that the findings are unique. Rather, the point of the foregoing analysis
\par
is that the proposed model of knowledge acquisition is not only simple, but
\par
leads to consequences that are in fact observable. Further, the proposed mod-
\par
el has valuable implications for the process of knowledge acquisition and the
\par
analysis of this acquisition; e.g., the whole notion of utilizing social knowl-
\par
edge to abet persuasion. A final point is that the representation scheme for
\par
knowledge - facts and knowledge bases - is particularly useful in locating
\par
specific instances of particular communication behavior, and hence in in-
\par
creasing the researchers' analytical power.
\par
Although this paper has been about the way individuals acquire infor-
\par
mation, not the way they perform tasks, some implications can be drawn
\par
about tasks. Tasks require the formulation of a problem and a solution; they
\par
require the individual to acquire knowledge and to define when the acquired
\par
knowledge constitutes a solution. For the individual, task performance is a
\par
heuristic act if it is dependent on the acquisition of knowledge, and not ex-
\par
clusively on the use of previously acquired knowledge. As more members of
\par
the society go through the process of formulation vis-h-vis a particular task,
\par
the task becomes a social task. The task becomes a social task in which the
\par
task formulation, the set of requisite knowledge, is negotiated by those per-
\par
forming the task. As multiple individuals pursue the same task, social knowl-
\par
edge vis-\textasciitilde t-vis the task increases. Now, consider the limiting condition -
\par
every piece of information relative to the task has a high level of social validi-
\par
ty. A piece of information at the individual level acquires the impact of a rule
\par
if that piece of information exists independent of the individual by virtue of
\par
being social, shared by others. In this case, the task can be thought of as cul-433
\par
tural. The task, its formulation and solution are part of the culture because
\par
the formulation no longer requires negotiation; i.e., the formulation has been
\par
established by previous social encounters over the same or similar tasks. Cul-
\par
tural tasks are, in a sense, solved social problems. The establishment of rules
\par
vis-h-vis the task is thus indicative of the degree of cukuralization of the task.
\par
Culturalization of information decreases the degree of individual for-
\par
mulation necessary and increases the ease of information transmittal within
\par
the society. Culturalization can also be seen as making the knowledge acqui-
\par
sition, and hence tasks, easier from the individual point of view as it
\par
decreases the need for analysis. To use a rough analogy, task solution can be
\par
seen as involving processing, storage, retrieval, and correlation; here, cul-
\par
turalization can be thought of as obviating the need for correlation, decreas-
\par
ing the processing costs, and thus reducing the task from one of formulation
\par
to one of memorization. Games have a high level of culturalization, the
\par
presidential election less so, and the Third East tutor selection even less.
\par
A final point. Given that one's understanding of another's behavior is
\par
predicated upon the belief that similar cognitive conceptions will produce
\par
similar behavior, then control of cognitive development becomes equivalent
\par
to control over social outcome. If, as postulated, cognitive development is the
\par
direct result of social interaction, then control over that interaction becomes
\par
the keystone to the establishment and maintenance of a particular social
\par
world. Limiting the individual's interactions serves to direct his cognitive de-
\par
velopment by inhibiting knowledge acquisition to just that knowledge com-
\par
municated by his contacts. At the societal level, limitations on the member's
\par
patterns of interaction can serve to create a stable social environment, and
\par
a stagnant one.
\par
