``The conceptual framework of decision analysis, as it is structured today, is rooted in the now classic ideas of von Neumann, Morgenstern, Wald, and other mathematicians who were driven by the grand vision of a rational and prescriptive theory of decisionmaking: a theory resting on a rigorously formulated axiomatic foundation and employing the full power of mathematical analysis to arrive at optimal solutions to precisely stated problems. Generally, the real world is much too complex, uncertain, and ill-defined to be a good fit for a theory in this spirit. As a consequence, many of the deeper and most sophisticated parts of the mathematical theory of decision processes have found few if any applications to the solution of realistic problems---problems in which the data are incomplete, imprecise or unreliable, and in which the goals and constraints are poorly defined and hard to quantify.'' (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 765)
\par
``an excessively utilitarian view of the role of theory and an expectation that it should be of demonstrable applicability to immediate problems and not merely a link in a longer chain that may eventually lead to a useful result. In the case of decision analysis, however, the issue goes deeper than that. Specifically, what may be argued, as we do in the following, is that the root of the problem is not the theory per se but the mathematical foundation on which it is built. There is no question that this foundation, which is essentially that of classical mathematics, is well-suited for fields such as physics, chemistry, mechanics, etc., in which one deals preponderantly with an animate systems whose behavior can be described with high precision. But, in the case of decision processes, what matters are human goals, perceptions, and emotions that do not lend themselves to quantitative analysis in the spirit of classical mathematics.'' (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 766)
\par
``human goals, perceptions, and emotions that do not lend themselves to quantitative analysis in the spirit of classical mathematics. For this reason, any attempt to construct a theory of decision analysis within the classical framework is bound to lead to a theory that is of limited relevance to real-world problems. This is why there is such a wide gap between the theory and practice in decision analysis and why the complaints of practitioners hit much closer to the mark than they do in other fields.'' (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 766)
\par
``A basic reason for the inappropriateness of classical mathematics as a foundation for decision analysis is that in mathematics, as we know it today, the concept of a set is dichotomous in nature. Thus any object is either a member or nonmember of any given set. By contrast, most of the classes that we encounter in the real world---especially in the context of decision analysis---do not have sharply defined boundaries.'' (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 766)
\par
``A basic reason for the inappropriateness of classical mathematics as a foundation for decision analysis is that in mathematics, as we know it today, the concept of a set is dichotomous in nature. Thus any object is either a member or nonmember of any given set. By contrast, most of the classes that we encounter in the real world---especially in the context of decision analysis---do not have sharply defined boundaries. Membership in such classes is a matter of degree and the transition from membership to nonmembership is usually gradual rather than abrupt. For example, the goal of an economic policy might be to reduce unemployment to the vicinity of six percent, keep the inflation rate stable, and bring the deficit down to approximately 100 billion dollars. In this goal, the classes described by the labels in the vicinity of six percent, table inflation rate, and approximately 100 billion dollars are not sets in the conventional sense of the term but sets with unsharp boundaries; that is, fuzzy sets. It is such sets---and not the crisp sets of classical mathematics---that play such a pervasive role in human reasoning, decisionmaking and, more generally, all cognitive processes that relate to concept formation, abstraction, and generalization.'' (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 766)
\par
``The introduction of the concept of a fuzzy set was motivated by the need for a systematic way of dealing with unsharp classes ---a way which is not provided by classical mathematics or probability theory. The development of the theory of fuzzy sets has led to the emergence of what is referred to by some as fuzzy mathematics [7]. This mathematics, in contrast to the traditional mathematics based on two-valued logic, is tolerant of imprecision and partial truths. Within this mathematics, a legitimate solution to a problem need not be expressible as an unequivocal and precise assertion. But what is perhaps most important about fuzzy mathematics is that it provides a language that is much more expressive than the language of classical mathematics and is better suited for the representation of assertions expressed in a natural language.'' (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 766)
\par
``In effect, by acquiring the ability to compute with fuzzy probabilities, the decision analyst can come closer to reality in the formulation and solution of practical problems involving decisionmaking under uncertainty.'' (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 766)
