``In order to acquire and use information effectively, we need to be able to evaluate the information in some manner, and various characteristics of information can be considered in the evaluation process. For instance, how accurate is the information? Does the information exhibit systematic biases, and can it be adjusted to allow for such biases? How uncertain are we about the accuracy and bias associated with a given piece of information? To what extent are different pieces of information redundant or dependent in some other way, perhaps directly or possibly indirectly because of our uncertainty about their accuracy and bias? These questions are complicated by the fact that we want to be able to deal with a variety of types of information from different sources. From statistics, the accuracy and bias of different estimates based on random sampling plans can be determined. But what if the information consists of a set of probabilities based on past data, models, or experts? The notion of refinement has been developed recently to deal with the question of accuracy of probabilities, and the notion of calibration of probabilities addresses the issue of bias [17]. Refinement, calibration, and similar characteristics of probabilities and other types of information deserve further study.'' (Cohen et al., 1985, p. 767)
