Value analysis uses a variety of procedures and theories from
\par
business, engineering, and the social sciences. The values of an
\par
individual or group pertaining to possible decisions are expressed
\par
in responses to questions such as "What are your main objectives
\par
and concerns relating to the decision problem? What attributes or
\par
measures would be appropriate for differentiating between op-
\par
tions? Why is a specific alternative either good or bad, desirable,
\par
or undesirable?" Using the responses to these questions, the
\par
analyst identifies and structures a hierarchy of values, referred to
\par
as a value tree, with general values and concerns at the top and
\par
specific criteria at the bottom [101].
\par
There are two approaches for generating value trees, namely,
\par
top-down and bottom-up. The top-down approach is deductive
\par
and analytic, while the bottom-up approach is inductive and
\par
synthetic. Neither is inherently to be preferred.
\par
The top-down approach is based on an explication of the
\par
evaluators' most general values bearing on decisions of concern.
\par
The analyst asks the evaluators for their overall values first. Next,
\par
evaluators are asked to explain the meanings of their initial value
\par
categories and their interrelationships. The discussion may indi-
\par
cate, for instance, that environmental damage is a major objective
\par
for evaluating energy policies. The analyst pursues this by asking
\par
what is meant by environmental damage, why is it important,
\par
how it is affected by energy options, and so on. The result of such
\par
an interview is a list of concerns articulated with logical princi-
\par
ples but not in a structured manner. In the interview, it is not
\par
necessary to worry about consistency or double counting; it is
\par
more important to stretch the imagination to promite a wide
\par
spectrum of values. Part of the explication process is an exercise
\par
in semantics, inducing a significant clarification of meaning and
\par
understanding.
\par
The bottom-up approach begins with the question "what are
\par
the criteria which distinguish between the evaluation objects?" It
\par
is useful to begin by generating a large list of criteria. The initial
\par
focus should not be on logical consistency or redundancy, but
\par
rather on completeness. One way to create such a list is by
\par
articulating the good and bad aspects of a few alternatives. The
\par
analyst can also draw on previous studies that evaluated similar
\par
alternatives.
\par
This initial list is then reduced by eliminating criteria that are
\par
not value relevant and expanded by including criteria for any
\par
" holes" that can be identified. Next, groups of related criteria are
\par
aggregated into a higher-level value category. This clustering
\par
process is repeated for higher level values until a small number of
\par
general value areas is identified that covers the range of the
\par
original criteria list.
\par
Having arrived at a preliminary value tree structure---either
\par
with the top-down or the bottom-up method---the analyst can
\par
appraise the tree structure by checking whether
\par
1) criteria explain the meaning of a general value category or
\par
relate in some other way (e.g., functional, means-end);
\par
2) any criterion is included in more than one higher-level
\par
category;
\par
3) the list of criteria is exhaustive; and
\par
4) any criterion is redundant.
\par
As a result, changes are inevitably made. The purpose is to
\par
create a value tree where the relationship between the lower-level
\par
criteria and higher-level categories is hierarchical and one of
\par
inclusion (by one), avoids interdependencies between higher-level
\par
value categories (by two), and creates an exhaustive (by three)
\par
and nonredundant (by four) list of criteria.
\par
Identification of objectives, structuring value trees, construc-
\par
tion of scales to measure objectives, and assessing utility and
\par
value functions offer mechanisms to focus on several value-related
\par
issues in decisions. Since values are the reason for any interest in
\par
a decision, value analysis is useful to guide all aspects of analysis,
\par
such as creating alternatives and evaluating options, bargaining
\par
and negotiation to help reach a consensus, deciding what infor-
\par
mation to collect, and communicating important aspects of a
\par
decision problem.
\par
Additional research is needed to develop a theory and proce-
\par
dures to 1) assist analysts and decision makers in the search for
\par
fundamental values, 2) structure value trees, 3) relate and in-
\par
tegrate various value trees, 4) develop consistent representations
\par
quantifying values over related value trees, 5) search systemati-
\par
cally through value structures to create better alternatives, 6)
\par
identify and help resolve conflicts among different decision-
\par
makers and stakeholders, and 7) assist in bargaining, negotiation
\par
and mediation.
