tured today, is rooted in the now classic ideas of von Neumann,
\par
Morgenstern, Wald, and other mathematicians who were driven
\par
by the grand vision of a rational and prescriptive theory of
\par
decisionmaking: a theory resting on a rigorously formulated
\par
axiomatic foundation and employing the full power of mathe-
\par
matical analysis to arrive at optimal solutions to precisely stated
\par
problems.
\par
Generally, the real world is much too complex, uncertain, and
\par
ill-defined to be a good fit for a theory in this spirit. As a
\par
consequence, many of the deeper and most sophisticated parts of
\par
the mathematical theory of decision processes have found few if
\par
any applications to the solution of realistic problems---problems
\par
in which the data are incomplete, imprecise or unreliable, and in
\par
which the goals and constraints are poorly defined and hard to
\par
quantify.
\par
In recent years there has been a great deal of discussion,
\par
debate, and soul searching regarding the applicability of the
\par
theory of decision analysis to problems in operations research,
\par
management science, and related fields, [108], [58], [9], [37], [81],
\par
[82]. Among the practitioners, there is a common feeling that the
\par
presently existing theory is often of limited value.
\par
The complain that theoreticians do not care to address real-
\par
world problems can be heard, of course, in all fields of science. In
\par
3 This section of our report has been contributed by L. A. Zadeh.766 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS, VOL. SMC-15, NO. 6, NOVEMBER/DECEMBER 1985
\par
part, such complaints reflect an excessively utilitarian view of the
\par
role of theory and an expectation that it should be of demonstra-
\par
ble applicability to immediate problems and not merely a link in
\par
a longer chain that may eventually lead to a useful result. In the
\par
case of decision analysis, however, the issue goes deeper than
\par
that. Specifically, what may be argued, as we do in the following,
\par
is that the root of the problem is not the theory\emph{ per se} but the
\par
mathematical foundation on which it is built.
\par
There is no question that this foundation, which is essentially
\par
that of classical mathematics, is well-suited for fields such as
\par
physics, chemistry, mechanics, etc., in which one deals pre-
\par
ponderantly with an animate systems whose behavior can be
\par
described with high precision. But, in the case of decision
\par
processes, what matters are human goals, perceptions, and emo-
\par
tions that do not lend themselves to quantitative analysis in the
\par
spirit of classical mathematics. For this reason, any attempt to
\par
construct a theory of decision analysis within the classical frame-
\par
work is bound to lead to a theory that is of limited relevance to
\par
real-world problems. This is why there is such a wide gap
\par
between the theory and practice in decision analysis and why the
\par
complaints of practitioners hit much closer to the mark than they
\par
do in other fields.
\par
A basic reason for the inappropriateness of classical mathe-
\par
matics as a foundation for decision analysis is that in mathe-
\par
matics, as we know it today, the concept of a set is dichotomous
\par
in nature. Thus any object is either a member or nonmember of
\par
any given set. By contrast, most of the classes that we encounter
\par
in the real world---especially in the context of decision
\par
analysis---do not have sharply defined boundaries.
\par
Membership in such classes is a matter of degree and the
\par
transition from membership to nonmembership is usually gradual
\par
rather than abrupt. For example, the goal of an economic policy
\par
might be to reduce unemployment to the vicinity of six percent,
\par
keep the inflation rate stable, and bring the deficit down to
\par
approximately 100 billion dollars. In this goal, the classes de-
\par
scribed by the labels in the vicinity of six percent, table inflation
\par
rate, and approximately 100 billion dollars are not sets in the
\par
conventional sense of the term but sets with unsharp boundaries;
\par
that is, fuzzy sets. It is such sets---and not the crisp sets of
\par
classical mathematics---that play such a pervasive role in human
\par
reasoning, decisionmaking and, more generally, all cognitive
\par
processes that relate to concept formation, abstraction, and gen-
\par
eralization.
\par
The introduction of the concept of a fuzzy set was motivated
\par
by the need for a systematic way of dealing with unsharp classes
\par
---a way which is not provided by classical mathematics or
\par
probability theory. The development of the theory of fuzzy sets
\par
has led to the emergence of what is referred to by some as fuzzy
\par
mathematics [7]. This mathematics, in contrast to the traditional
\par
mathematics based on two-valued logic, is tolerant of imprecision
\par
and partial truths. Within this mathematics, a legitimate solution
\par
to a problem need not be expressible as an unequivocal and
\par
precise assertion. But what is perhaps most important about
\par
fuzzy mathematics is that it provides a language that is much
\par
more expressive than the language of classical mathematics and is
\par
better suited for the representation of assertions expressed in a
\par
natural language.
\par
In more specific terms, the expressiveness of fuzzy mathematics
\par
derives from the possibility of employing a fuzzified version of
\par
any concept which enters into the formulation and/or solution of
\par
a problem. In the context of decision analysis, the more im-
\par
portant of these concepts are the following.
\par
1)\emph{ Fuzzy Probabilities'.} Such probabilities are imprecisely
\par
known or imprecisely defined probabilities defined in terms
\par
such as probable, likely, unlikely, highly improbable, etc. In
\par
the theory of fuzzy sets, fuzzy probabilities are manipulated
\par
as fuzzy numbers through the use of fuzzy arithmetic, rather
\par
than as second-order probabilities. The importance of the
\par
concept of a fuzzy probability stems from the fact that in
\par
most applications of decision analysis the underlying prob-
\par
abilities are not known with sufficient precision to be repre-
\par
sented and treated as real numbers. In effect, by acquiring
\par
the ability to compute with fuzzy probabilities, the decision
\par
analyst can come closer to reality in the formulation and
\par
solution of practical problems involving decisionmaking
\par
under uncertainty.
\par
2)\emph{ Fuzzy Quantifiers:} Fuzzy quantifiers are exemplified by
\par
most, several, few, almost all, frequently, usually, etc. Such
\par
quantifiers may be interpreted as fuzzy numbers that char-
\par
acterize the absolute or relative count of elements in fuzzy
\par
or nonfuzzy sets. For example, the proposition "most pro-
\par
fessors are not rich" may be interpreted as a fuzzy char-
\par
acterization of the proposition in question and may be
\par
expressed in terms of fuzzy probabilities as the following:
\par
\emph{the fuzzy conditional probability of not rich given professor is}
\par
\emph{most, where most is a fuzzy number in the unit interval.}
\par
The concept of a fuzzy quantifier plays a central role in
\par
commonsense reasoning, a type of reasoning that humans
\par
use in everyday decisionmaking. More specifically, much of
\par
commonsense reasoning involves the use of dispositions;
\par
that is, propositions that are preponderantly, but not neces-
\par
sarily, always true. For example, "birds can fly" is a dis-
\par
position, as are the propositions "overeating causes obesity"
\par
and "Swedes are taller than Spaniards." From this perspec-
\par
tive, a disposition may be viewed as a proposition with
\par
implicit fuzzy quantifiers. Thus "birds can fly" may be
\par
interpreted as "most birds can fly," "overeating causes
\par
obesity" as "most of those who overeat are obese," and
\par
"Swedes are taller than Spaniards" as "the proportion of
\par
tall men among Swedes is significantly higher than the
\par
proportion of tall men among Spaniards."
\par
Reasoning with dispositions involves a three-stage pro-
\par
cess. First, dispositional premises are converted into pro-
\par
positions by an explicitation of the implicit fuzzy quanti-
\par
fiers. Second, various so-called fuzzy syllogisms of fuzzy
\par
logic are employed to infer fuzzily-quantified conclusions
\par
from the premises. And finally, the fuzzy quantifiers in the
\par
conclusions are suppressed, leading to what might be called
\par
dispositional conclusions. In this way, one can infer, for
\par
example, the dispositional conclusion that "icy roads are
\par
dangerous" from the dispositional premises "icy roads are
\par
slippery" and "slippery roads are dangerous."
\par
3)\emph{ Fuzzy Truth Values:} One of the aspects of natural languages
\par
which we frequently take for granted is the possibility of
\par
qualifying the truth of an assertion by a fuzzy truth value,
\par
e.g., quite true, somewhat true, not very true, completely
\par
false, etc. By contrast, in classical two-valued logic---which
\par
provides the foundation for classical mathematics---a pro-
\par
position can only be true or false, with no intermediate
\par
truth values allowed. Intermediate truth values are allowed
\par
in multivalued logic. For example, in Lukasiewicz's LAleph\emph{ x}
\par
logic, the truth value can be any number in the unit interval.
\par
However, there is no provision for fuzzy truth values in
\par
LAleph ! or other multivalued logical systems.
\par
In fuzzy logic a proposition may be qualified in a variety
\par
of ways, among which are a) truth qualification via a fuzzy
\par
truth value, b) probability qualification via a fuzzy probabil-
\par
ity, and c) possibility qualification via a fuzzy possibility;
\par
e.g., quite possible, almost impossible, etc. In this way,
\par
fuzzy logic provides a mechanism for enhancing the expres-
\par
siveness of fuzzy mathematics and thereby makes it more
\par
relevant to the formulation and solution of problems in
\par
which the underlying knowledge is imprecise, incomplete, or
\par
not totally reliable.
\par
4)\emph{ Linguistic Variables:} A concept that plays an important part
\par
in extending the applicability of fuzzy mathematics is that
\par
of a linguistic variable, [104]-[107], As its name suggests,
\par
the linguistic variable\emph{ X} is a variable whose values are
\par
words or sentences in a natural or synthetic language. For
\par
example,\emph{ age} is a linguistic variable if its values are as-
\par
sumed to be young, not young, not very young, old, veryIEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS, VOL. SMC-15, NO. 6, NOVEMBER/DECEMBER 1985 767
\par
old, etc. Generally, a value of a linguistic variable may be
\par
generated from a) a primary form (e.g., young and its
\par
antonym, old); b) connectives such as\emph{ and} and\emph{ or;} and c)
\par
modifiers like very, not, more, or less, quite, rather, etc. The
\par
primary terms are, in general, context-dependent and play
\par
the role of units that are in need of calibration. The mod-
\par
ifiers and the connectives may be assumed to have a fixed
\par
meaning. In this way, the meaning of a linguistic value of\emph{ X}
\par
may be computed from the meanings of the primary terms.
\par
In general, the meaning is represented as a possibility
\par
distribution over a universe of discourse. For example, the
\par
meaning of young would be represented as a possibility
\par
distribution over the interval [0; 100]. This distribution
\par
associates with each numerical value,\emph{ u,} of\emph{ age} a number
\par
between 0 and 1 which represents the possibility that\emph{ age} is
\par
\emph{u} given that\emph{ age is young.}
\par
The importance of the concept of a linguistic variable stems
\par
from the fact that it provides a mechanism for an approximate
\par
characterization of values of variables and their interrelations.
\par
This facility plays a particularly important role in the characteri-
\par
zation of ill-defined systems, in qualitative systems analysis, and
\par
in the definition of goals and constraints which do not lend
\par
themselves to quantitative characterization [103], [110].
\par
In sum, the concept of a linguistic variable in combination with
\par
the other basic concepts of fuzzy mathematics, which derive from
\par
the notion of a fuzzy set, make it possible to formulate and solve
\par
a number of problems in decision analysis that are too complex
\par
and too ill-defined to be susceptible to solution by conventional
\par
techniques. In this way, the theory can be brought closer to
\par
reality. There will always be many problems, however, for which
\par
no theory, whether based on fuzzy mathematics or not, could
\par
provide a rational basis for solution. This is particularly true of
\par
problems relating to conflict resolution, group decision analysis,
\par
and, more generally, to large-scale systems in which the goals and
\par
the horizons are hard to agree upon and not merely hard to
\par
quantify. Thus, in the final analysis, the ultimate limitation of
\par
any theory of decision processes derives not from its mathemati-
\par
cal foundations, but from our inability to understand and eluci-
\par
date the meaning of the rationality of human behavior. This will
\par
always be a controversial issue.
\par
IV. ACQUIRING AND USING INFORMATION IN
\par
DECISIONMAKING4
\par
In a recent paper [102] promising research directions in de-
\par
cisionmaking under uncertainty were discussed. This area is very
\par
broad and interdisciplinary, and the suggestions therein are cer-
\par
tainly pertinent to future research goals and needs in decision
\par
and management science (DMS). Rather than attempt here to
\par
repeat an extensive list of research directions already available to
\par
interested readers, a somewhat more limited---yet by no means
\par
small---topic will be discussed here. That is, the acquisition and
\par
use of information in decisionmaking.
\par
Many needs encourage interest in acquiring and using informa-
\par
tion in decisionmaking. The role of information in decisionmak-
\par
ing is to help us deal with (and perhaps reduce) the uncertainty
\par
inherent in virtually all important decisionmaking problems. This,
\par
in turn, enables us to evaluate the risks associated with various
\par
courses of action.
\par
Information of many different types is generally available or
\par
can be sought out. The preparation of an exhaustive list of types
\par
of information is not feasible, but information can be classified
\par
along various dimensions. For example, we can distinguish be-
\par
tween qualitative and quantitative information and between ex-
\par
perimental and observational information. At a more detailed
\par
level, we can compare the specific forms the information can take
\par
on, such as point estimates for a variable versus probabilities for
\par
the variable versus higher order probabilities representing uncer-
\par
tainty about the probabilities for the variable.
\par
4 This section of our report was contributed by R. L. Winkler.
\par
Information can also come from different sources. At one
\par
extreme is information obtained via informal observations by the
\par
decisionmaker. At another extreme is a set of data collected
\par
through carefully designed sampling or experimentation. Often
\par
models can provide some helpful information, as exemplified by
\par
the use of output from econometric models in the decisionmaking
\par
process when the ultimate payoffs or rewards will be related to
\par
the future movement of the economy. On the more subjective
\par
side, experts are frequently valuable sources of information and
\par
can often provide information of value that simply can not be
\par
obtained in any other way.
\par
In important decisionmaking problems, information of varying
\par
types may be available from varying sources. Furthermore, there
\par
may be noise in the information-gathering process, and different
\par
pieces of information may be dependent. The study of methods,
\par
by statisticians and others, for analyzing data has emphasized
\par
processes in which the observations are assumed to be indepen-
\par
dent and identically distributed. Research is needed to provide a
\par
better understanding of processes that are not so "clean" but that
\par
may involve varying types of information of varying quality with
\par
possible redundancy. This research should include work on the
\par
evaluation of different types of information, the aggregation of
\par
information, the acquisition of information, and the communica-
\par
tion of information.
\par
In order to acquire and use information effectively, we need to
\par
be able to evaluate the information in some manner, and various
\par
characteristics of information can be considered in the evaluation
\par
process. For instance, how accurate is the information? Does the
\par
information exhibit systematic biases, and can it be adjusted to
\par
allow for such biases? How uncertain are we about the accuracy
\par
and bias associated with a given piece of information? To what
\par
extent are different pieces of information redundant or depen-
\par
dent in some other way, perhaps directly or possibly indirectly
\par
because of our uncertainty about their accuracy and bias?
\par
These questions are complicated by the fact that we want to be
\par
able to deal with a variety of types of information from different
\par
sources. From statistics, the accuracy and bias of different esti-
\par
mates based on random sampling plans can be determined. But
\par
what if the information consists of a set of probabilities based on
\par
past data, models, or experts? The notion of refinement has been
\par
developed recently to deal with the question of accuracy of
\par
probabilities, and the notion of calibration of probabilities ad-
\par
dresses the issue of bias [17]. Refinement, calibration, and similar
\par
characteristics of probabilities and other types of information
\par
deserve further study.
\par
When several pieces of new information are obtained, a de-
\par
cisionmaker should aggregate the information and combine it
\par
with any previously available information. Bayes' theorem pro-
\par
vides a procedure for this aggregation, albeit at the price of
\par
having to determine a likelihood function, which should reflect
\par
characteristics such as accuracy, bias, and dependence, as noted
\par
concerning evaluation [65].
\par
When we deal with different types of information from diverse
\par
sources and view the information-generating process as noisy and
\par
dependent, the specification of a likelihood function can be quite
\par
difficult. Moreover, the results of the aggregation can be highly
\par
sensitive to relatively slight variations in the likelihood function,
\par
which is something we may be quite shaky about in the first
\par
place. As a result, we may wish to resort to some simple ad hoc
\par
rules or informal procedures instead of a full-blown formal
\par
Bayesian analysis. An example is using a simple average of a
\par
number of estimates instead of trying to assess relative accuracy
\par
and dependence and finding a weighted average to reflect these
\par
characteristics. The robustness of simple averages makes them
\par
appealing, and more work is needed to give us an idea of how
\par
much we are likely to sacrifice or gain by using such\emph{ ad hoc}
\par
procedures. Also, what sorts of simple procedures can be devel-
\par
oped when the information available is of different types?
\par
The study of the evaluation and aggregation of information
\par
leads naturally to questions concerning information acquisition.
\par
Should we obtain information in the form of data collected from768 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS, VOL. SMC-15, NO. 6, NOVEMBER/DECEMBER 1985
\par
a sample or experiment, in the form of the output of models, in
\par
the form of judgments of experts, or some combination thereof?
\par
What can we say about the dynamics of information-gathering in
\par
complicated situations? Can approximations, decision aids, rules
\par
of thumb, computer-assisted procedures, or something else be
\par
developed to simplify the information-acquisition process? Mea-
\par
sures such as an equivalent number of independent observations
\par
[8] may be useful in contemplating the purchase of different
\par
"packages" of information, and further work regarding such
\par
measures would be useful.
\par
In situations with many interested parties, the 'communication
\par
of information is an important issue. This issue is related to areas
\par
such as organization design, group decisionmaking, competitive
\par
decisionmaking, and game theory. Under what conditions should
\par
a decisionmaker share information, and what information can be
\par
obtained from other parties (colleagues, competitors, etc.) who
\par
are involved in the decisionmaking situation?
\par
The focus here has been primarily on the types of complica-
\par
tions, such as information of different types from different
\par
sources, that often arise in the acquisition and use of information
\par
in realistic, interesting decisionmaking situations. Methods for
\par
evaluating different types of information need further study. As
\par
for aggregation of information, a formal framework is readily
\par
available. But is it practical to implement this framework, either
\par
in a formal and complete sense or in a simplified manner, in
\par
complicated situations? What happens if we are quite uncertain
\par
about many of the relevant parameters such as the accuracy of
\par
information sources or degree of dependence among sources? We
\par
need further research to sharpen our intuition about many of
\par
these issues.
\par
What types of research can be expected to be useful in the
\par
context of the issues discussed here? First, the research might
\par
draw on a number of disciplines. Some of the formal procedures
\par
for gathering and analyzing information come from the statistics
\par
and operations research/management science literature, particu-
\par
larly from the decision theory/decision analysis area. But other
\par
disciplines are certainly relevant. For example, the consideration
\par
of models takes us into the areas in which such models are
\par
developed, such as economics for econometric models, and the
\par
consideration of experts takes us into the area of cognitive
\par
psychology.
\par
Second, a distinction should be drawn between normative and
\par
descriptive considerations. From the standpoint of assisting a
\par
decisionmaker, the normative view is predominant. However,
\par
descriptive studies can also be very helpful in that they reveal
\par
potential pitfalls and they may help an individual predict what
\par
might happen in multiparty situations.
\par
Third, both theoretical research and empirical/experimental
\par
search are valuable. The development of general theoretical
\par
models and the investigation of the implications of such models
\par
(e.g., the direction and magnitude of changes in outputs as the
\par
inputs are modified, the existence of possibly surprising results
\par
and paradoxes) can help to sharpen our intuition and provide
\par
potentially useful models for possible implementation. Some em-
\par
pirical/experimental work, preferably in real-world operational
\par
settings, may help to indicate how well these models or other,
\par
perhaps simpler, approaches work in practice. Finally, although
\par
some research should eventually consider the "big picture," con-
\par
siderable gains can be expected from work concentrating on
\par
smaller issues such as the evaluation of specific types of informa-
\par
tion.
\par
V. ORGANIZATION THEORY AND ORGANIZATIONAL
\par
DESIGN5
\par
We now draw attention to a cluster of significant theoretical
\par
problems that are encountered once we begin to give advice to
\par
real decisionmakers about the "best" ways to organize for the
\par
performance of real tasks. A focus on organizational design
\par
5 This section of our report was contributed by M. D. Cohen.
\par
brings us face to face with some difficult and intellectually very
\par
significant questions. We indicate the elements that will have to
\par
be present if formalized organization theory is actually to help
\par
organizational designers and we consider some of the main
\par
clusters of difficulties that have to be overcome. A major theme is
\par
that the computer may provide a solution to some of our prob-
\par
lems, but that computer models of organizational decisionmaking
\par
are, themselves, far from being trouble-free. To begin with, we
\par
clarify what is meant by organizational design. When someone
\par
asks whether they should make or buy something used by their
\par
own organization, such as parts or advertising services, they face
\par
a design decision. So do governmental officials when pondering
\par
whether to deregulate an industry through a response to the
\par
question: "shall prices be set by governmental authorities or by
\par
market forces?" When a personnel office decides to recruit only
\par
junior level managers with graduate training, or when an office
\par
installs electronic mail facilities, these too are changes in organi-
\par
zational design. Indeed, we use an imperialistic definition in
\par
which, within a system of actors, any major alteration of capabili-
\par
ties, incentives, or relations among the actors is an act of organi-
\par
zational design.
\par
The making of an organizational design decision requires that
\par
one evaluates, at least implicitly, the expected performance of
\par
two or more design alternatives over time. We deal first with a
\par
cluster of problems that arise in evaluating performance, then we
\par
discuss a cluster of performance prediction difficulties, and fi-
\par
nally, we take up the prospects and problems of using computers
\par
to attack these two kinds of theoretical difficulties.
\par
It is no small matter to predict the performance of an organiza-
\par
tional design, as we shall see below. Even supposing that we had
\par
such a forecast of how a design would perform, we would have to
\par
face three kinds of serious problems in evaluating such a design.
\par
The first of these is the traditional problem of interpersonal
\par
comparison. If many goals are being pursued by the organization,
\par
we need to be able to determine which of them shall be applied to
\par
evaluation of performance prediction. While this issue is so
\par
important that it must be mentioned, the many associated and
\par
intricate problems are fairly familiar, which does not mean that
\par
they are solved. Thus, we can jump ahead to the second set of
\par
evaluation difficulties: intertemporal comparisons. These occur
\par
because change in organizational goals is to some degree endoge-
\par
nous. Firms, for example, have hired new managers that lead
\par
them out of traditional markets. Government regulatory agencies,
\par
such as the Civil Aeronautics Board, create staff divisions that
\par
come to the conclusion that the agency should put itself out of
\par
business. The question naturally arises concerning how the
\par
evaluation of a design should take into account the possibility
\par
that the design itself may affect the criteria of evaluation?
\par
A third set of evaluation problems may be labelled efficiency-
\par
versus-flexibility issues. These center on the observation that we
\par
may want an organizational design to do more than contribute to
\par
the efficient performance of tasks that we currently face. It may
\par
be important that the design contributes to or inhibits the organi-
\par
zation's ability to develop new capabilities. How are we, for
\par
example, to forecast the contribution a design makes to the
\par
ability of the organization to learn, and how is that to be traded
\par
off against costs in short-run efficiency?
\par
We will soon take up the question of the intellectual tools
\par
required to make some progress on these problems of evaluation.
\par
But for now, let us turn to the second general group of problems
\par
facing organization theorists who would help designers. These are
\par
problems of prediction. In one sense this includes all of positive
\par
organization theory, of course, but attention is focused only on
\par
one particular difficulty, which might be called the "model
\par
complexity dilemma". That is, how should we deal with the
\par
tension between wanting to support organizational design by
\par
making models that capture more of the complexity and detail of
\par
reality and wanting to make models that are simple enough to
\par
yield general principles that can be both taught and criticized?
\par
We know that in any real piece of organization there may be a
\par
number of actors with nonidentical capabilities and incentives,IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS, VOL. SMC-15, NO. 6, NOVEMBER/DECEMBER 1985 769
\par
that those capabilities and incentives can change over time, that
\par
the relations among the actors are complex and driven to some
\par
degree by history and personality. A good model to aid in a
\par
design forecast should be able to capture the effects of many of
\par
these complex features. But a good model should also be one that
\par
is easily communicated to students and colleagues and that is
\par
intelligible, reproducible, and extendable. The use of computers
\par
for the modeling of organizational decisionmaking offers consid-
\par
erable promise of models that are "good" in the first sense, but
\par
with serious risks that they will not be "good" in the second
\par
sense.
\par
Until very recently we have been able to model behavior that
\par
would be expected in market organizations using conventional
\par
mathematical tools, but we have been unable to obtain compara-
\par
ble representations of hierarchical authority relations or other
\par
organizational possibilities. Computer-modeling developments are
\par
now making it likely that we can borrow structures from research
\par
in artificial intelligence and cognitive science to represent organi-
\par
zational processes such as authority, planning, and learning. For
\par
example, the artificial intelligence (AI) "production system" con-
\par
struct provides an excellent formal analog of an organizational
\par
routine or standard operating procedure. Thus it may become
\par
substantially easier to predict the impact of an organizational
\par
design change on the performance of a system of standard
\par
operating procedures. The early successes at organizational mod-
\par
eling, such as Crecine's budgetary systems and Cyert and March's
\par
price-output decisions or duopoly markets, may thus be about to
\par
have some long-overdue successors.
\par
This possibility of progress on prediction problems comes with
\par
a considerable risk attached, however. That is the subject of this
\par
section on the problems as well as the promise of computer-aided
\par
theorizing. The use of the computer as a medium for theorizing is
\par
going to considerably enrich our "palette," but it is far from
\par
certain that the result will be better "paintings." Indeed, the
\par
history of design is replete with examples in which the introduc-
\par
tion of improved technology was, at least in the short run,
\par
detrimental to quality. Design theorist Christopher Alexander
\par
tells of Slovakian shawl makers whose traditions were fatally
\par
destabilized by the new possibilities inherent in analine dyes.
\par
One source of concern about computer-aided theorizing is
\par
exactly that such a Babel-of-tongues problem will now arise in
\par
the wake of an enormous increase in representational possibili-
\par
ties. To individual investigators in physics or economics it can
\par
hardly seem a blessing that conventional mathematical tools offer
\par
so few representational alternatives. Organization theory, as well
\par
as many other disciplines, will now have to evolve conventions to
\par
cope with the profusion of noncomparable models of the same
\par
phenomena.
\par
A second class of difficulties arising from the new possibilities
\par
for computer-aided theorizing is not the noncomparability of
\par
multiple models but the complexity of single ones. The limits on
\par
what equations can be solved are very stern taskmasters. The
\par
limit on what computer models can be run appears much less
\par
demanding. Thus there is little internal pressure in a computer-
\par
modeling tradition to control the number of interesting or realis-
\par
tic features that the model contains. But when it comes time for
\par
students or other scholars to work with such a model a heavy
\par
price is then paid. The model may be too complex to be success-
\par
fully transferred.
\par
These are not insurmountable barriers to the use of computer
\par
programs as a medium for theorizing about, and predicting,
\par
organizational processes. Groups of scholars will find ways of
\par
enforcing conventions on choices of representations, and re-
\par
searchers will painfully learn the limits of communicable com-
\par
plexity. When these adjustments have occurred, however, the
\par
practical possibilities for computer-aided theorizing will then be
\par
more clearly in view. They will look much more limited than our
\par
first enthusiastic hopes of the last twenty years, but they will be
\par
wider than what we had before and more easily connected to
\par
simulations and games that will have strong impact on students
\par
and practitioners. If we carefully foster research that develops the
\par
intellectual tools for computer-aided theorizing, we will be able to
\par
say a little more to designers than we have been able to say in the
\par
past about the predicted efficiency and flexibility of alternative
\par
organizational designs.
\par
VI. ISSUES OF ORGANIZATIONAL FORM FOR COMMAND
\par
AND CONTROL6
\par
Command and control is the exercise of authority and direc-
\par
tion by a military commander over forces for some mission. Thus
\par
it is a major applications area for the decision and management
\par
sciences. While direct command and control or C2 is effected
\par
through a dedicated system indirect C2 is effected through pre-
\par
planned procedures. This dedicated system is the command,
\par
control, and communications (C3) system that can be described
\par
as a composite of equipment, skills, and techniques that enables a
\par
commander to exercise continuous control of forces and weapons
\par
in all situations through provision of the information needed to
\par
make operational decisions and the means to disseminate them.
\par
The image that emerges from these definitions is that of a
\par
decisionmaker that interacts with other decisionmakers, sub-
\par
ordinates, or peers and through chains of such interactions con-
\par
trols the available resources. That is, a commander commands an
\par
organization and exercises control of forces through the organiza-
\par
tion itself. Technological change in sensors and weapons has
\par
brought about major changes in the size of the geographical area
\par
over which a tactical military organization may have influence
\par
and in the speed with which the information processing and
\par
decisionmaking functions must be executed. The complexity of
\par
the missions, the fast tempo of operations [49], the wide geo-
\par
graphical distribution of forces, and the need to coordinate
\par
diverse actions have led to the reconsideration of the organiza-
\par
tional forms and the reexamination of doctrine for tactical oper-
\par
ations.
\par
A theory for the design and evaluation of information
\par
processing and decisionmaking organizations is needed [19]. This
\par
theory must take into explicit account the role and the limitations
\par
of the human decisionmakers in an organization, as well as the
\par
existence of modern communications systems and the emerging
\par
decision support systems.
\par
A number of assumptions can be made about tactical com-
\par
mand and control; these assumptions help focus the problem and
\par
narrow its scope.
\par
First, it must be assumed that more than one decisionmaker,
\par
supported by a C3 system, is needed to carry out the tasks or
\par
missions. All decisionmakers execute a set of well-defined tasks
\par
for which they are well trained. However, each decisionmaker
\par
also has cognitive limitations. They exhibit bounded rationality
\par
[6], [87]. As a first approximation it may be assumed that the
\par
organization is a team, i.e., all the members have a common
\par
organizational objective. A more realistic theory will recognize
\par
the existence of different and sometimes conflicting objectives.
\par
The organizations are often decentralized, while the information
\par
processing and the decisionmaking are distributed. Finally, it
\par
must be assumed that the environment is hostile and that it
\par
imposes severe time constraints.
\par
Even with this narrowing of the scope of the problem, there is
\par
no existing theory or analytical framework that can address the
\par
complete problem: the design and evaluation of organizational
\par
forms. This involves the specification of the decision structure,
\par
the corresponding information structure, the protocols that define
\par
the interactions between decisionmakers, and a method for
\par
qualitative and quantitative comparison of alternative organiza-
\par
tions.
\par
One approach currently under investigation [50] is based on a
\par
generalization of the performance-workload locus [88]. Organiza-
\par
tional forms are described using the Petri-net function [73]. This
\par
allows for the unambiguous specification of the protocols de-
\par
6 This section of our report was contributed by A. H. Levis.770 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS, VOL. SMC-15, NO. 6, NOVEMBER/DECEMBER 1985
\par
fining the allowed interactions between decisionmakers. The
\par
\emph{N-}dimensional information theory may be used to calculate a
\par
surrogate for the workload of each member of the organization.
\par
Then, the set of all information processing and decisionmaking
\par
strategies the organization may use can be mapped into an
\par
\emph{(N} + l)-dimensional locus: one dimension for the workload of
\par
each one of the\emph{ N} members of the organization and one dimen-
\par
sion for the organizational performance measure. Performance
\par
specifications and workload limitations can be expressed as re-
\par
strictions on the locus. Alternative organizational forms can
\par
be analyzed and compared through their respective
\par
performance-workload loci. The approach lends itself to the
\par
development of a design methodology based on modifying the
\par
performance-workload locus to meet performance specifications
\par
and recognize the cognitive limitations of individual members.
\par
The results obtained to date, even under highly artificial as-
\par
sumptions, have demonstrated rather clearly the need for basic
\par
research in organization theory where the emphasis is not on
\par
optimum decisionmaking, but rather on the relationship between
\par
organizational structure and the limits of organizational perfor-
\par
mance. On the one side, experiments need to be carried out to
\par
establish a database for tasks, organizational structure, perfor-
\par
mance, and individual workload. The analytical framework can
\par
be used to design useful experiments. On the other side, empirical
\par
evidence is much needed to guide the direction of the analytical
\par
efforts. Models need to be calibrated, while assumptions need to
\par
be relaxed to reflect realistic operational contents.
\par
It is important that empirical and analytical research be carried
\par
out in parallel, if a theory of organizational form is to evolve that
\par
can be used for design. The command and control context,
\par
because it is a highly structured one, provides a very useful
\par
paradigm for both theoretical and empirical investigations.
\par
VII. MODELING ORGANIZATIONAL PLANNING7
\par
A new approach to organizational modeling is discussed that
\par
potentially realizes the advantages of both axiomatic methods
\par
and simulation methods of modeling. It does this by permitting
\par
axioms to serve as a nonprocedural specification of a simulation.
\par
These axioms represent ways the organization can combine its
\par
capabilities to solve a problem. Hypothesizing that organizations
\par
find solutions to problems by iterative refinement, we can use the
\par
axioms as the basis for a driver of a problem-solving simulation.
\par
Our goal is to characterize issues of computational complexity
\par
and their implications for the structure of knowledge. Our in-
\par
tended application area is organizational planning or cooperative
\par
problem solving. Based on an axiomatic representation of knowl-
\par
edge and an algebraic characterization of organizational capabili-
\par
ties, the question of computability of tasks presented to the
\par
organizations is explored. By introducing a restriction on knowl-
\par
edge representation, we can show that sequential problem solving
\par
is sufficient even though we permit concurrent activities to be
\par
carried out by members of the organization. At the conclusion of
\par
this note we speculate on the situation, where the restrictions we
\par
have introduced no longer hold and the organization may have to
\par
resort to parallelism in the problem solving process if tasks posed
\par
to the organization are to be evaluated.
\par
We view planning as an organizational problem-solving pro-
\par
cess. Focusing upon routine decisions of an operational or day-
\par
to-day nature, we take the organizational structure as a given,
\par
which serves to guide the process.
\par
The organization that we consider is assumed to be a cooper-
\par
ative organization or team. The organization is a collection of
\par
information-processing agents who have a common goal but
\par
possess varying capabilities or skills. The agents are assumed to
\par
share the goals of the organization or to accept those goals that
\par
are given to them. The plans they execute are combinations of
\par
7This section of our report was contributed by R. A. Pick and A. B.
\par
Whinston.
\par
their capabilities with those of their colleagues or subordinates.
\par
The evolution of new capabilities, creativity, organization restruc-
\par
turing, motivation, and goal conflict is beyond the scope of this
\par
model.
\par
The problem solving process is viewed as a refinement process.
\par
The agents are organized so that there is a flow of goals from the
\par
top of the organization to the bottom. During this flow, the goals
\par
are iteratively refined to be more and more concrete. The organi-
\par
zation begins with a (shared) goal, expressed as a fairly abstract
\par
statement. The problem for the organization is to find a means
\par
using its capabilities of reaching that goal.
\par
The planning method we suggest is a centralized one in which
\par
the agents receive abstract goals and refine these into more
\par
concrete goals with various coordinating mechanisms among the
\par
goals. The more concrete goals thus generated now become
\par
organizational subproblems, which can be refined further. The
\par
process halts when all goals generated match certain primitive
\par
capabilities of the organization. We call the intermediate goals in
\par
this process\emph{ plans;} the final plan achieved is called the\emph{ meaning.}
\par
The process of solving the problem is the planning process. In
\par
a sense, the organization's problem is one of restating the shared
\par
goal in terms of concrete actions that may be taken by its agents.
\par
The organization is essentially a type of compiler. In common
\par
with earlier works by March and Simon [54] and Galbraith [23],
\par
we are looking at the information processing aspects of the
\par
organization.
\par
There are at least two different approaches to analysis of
\par
organizational behavior. The first approach is the axiomatic
\par
approach; the second is the simulation approach. We approve of
\par
both methods due to their rigor and precision. We suggest a
\par
method that combines the advantages of both approaches.
\par
The axiomatic approach to modeling can be simply stated. A
\par
set of axioms to describe the organization are written down.
\par
Interesting conclusions that follow as a consequence of these
\par
axioms are then derived.
\par
One field where this approach has been used with great success
\par
is economic theory [16], [33], [56], [15]. An economy can be
\par
regarded as a type of organization in which interactions among
\par
the agents are by means of markets.
\par
The axiomatic approach has been applied to more traditional
\par
notions of organizations by Mesarovic\emph{ et al.} [59], [60], [61]. The
\par
axiomatic approach has several strong points. First, since the
\par
axioms are stated explicitly, all assumptions of the model can be
\par
verified. Second, since the axioms and conclusions are generally
\par
of a mathematical nature, the statements made by the model are
\par
precise and not subject to varying interpretations. Third, these
\par
models are typically parsimonious, so the list of axioms is gener-
\par
ally short. Fourth, the entailment process by which conclusions
\par
are derived from the axioms is subject to easy verification.
\par
Finally, the theory that goes with axiomatic models is often of
\par
intrinsic interest.
\par
Of course, the axiomatic approach has weaknesses. First, it is
\par
only useful in situations for which derivations can be made from
\par
the axioms. Second, axioms are sometimes made with the entail-
\par
ment process in mind; their meaning in terms of the phenomenon
\par
being modeled is sometimes unclear. Third, the axioms may
\par
concern objects which are not observable; they are not always
\par
subject to empirical verification. Finally, in those cases where the
\par
axioms may be verified, it seems to often be the case that the
\par
axioms are disproved but the conclusions are upheld.
\par
A simulation model is a model in which the conclusions are
\par
illustrated by using a computer to simulate the processes em-
\par
bodied in the assumptions. Simulations are generally used when
\par
the assumptions of a model are not amendable to the derivation
\par
of simple conclusions or when the model portrays a process
\par
which occurs over time. Simulations have long been used to study
\par
organizations [96], [68], [10]-[12].
\par
A major reason for using simulations is that they are often the
\par
only available method of analysis. As is the case with the axiomatic
\par
method, they provide precise results. Unlike the axiomaticIEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS, VOL. SMC-15, NO. 6, NOVEMBER/DECEMBER 1985 771
\par
method, simulation studies rarely state all of their assumptions.
\par
Also, the derivation of results is accomplished by computer code,
\par
for which verification of correctness is difficult. It is also typical
\par
for simulations to be highly situation specific. When used for
\par
organizational behavior, it is often the case that extrapolations of
\par
the results of a series of simulation experiments are made to
\par
organizations in general; it is difficult to justify these extrapola-
\par
tions. Simulation models tend to make more specific predictions
\par
than axiomatic models, and they tend to be less parsimonious
\par
and more difficult to verify.
\par
We suggest an approach which seems to allow many of the
\par
advantages of each of the above techniques. Known as equational
\par
programming [35], it is a new technique for specifying compu-
\par
tations and is based on theoretical foundations developed by
\par
O'Donnel [72]. The advantage arises from the nature of the
\par
equational interpreter. Using such an interpreter, the axioms of
\par
the system are used as the specification of a simulation. That is,
\par
the axioms (stated explicitly) are the program used for simula-
\par
tion.
\par
We may use these equations as the axiomatic basis of an
\par
organization. These equations can be studied and conclusions
\par
derived in the same way as any set of axioms used to describe an
\par
organization. These same equations can later be used, without
\par
modification, to specify a simulation of the organization's activi-
\par
ties over time using the axiomatic approach while obtaining the
\par
specificity possible with simulation. Results may be obtained
\par
both with and without the computer according to convenience.
\par
The benefits of this approach are the following.
\par
1) It supports an axiomatization of the organization. A com-
\par
plete set of axioms for the model are explicitly stated. Thus,
\par
we have the benefit of unambiguous verifiable assumptions.
\par
2) It supports a simulation of the organization. These axioms
\par
can be used to completely specify a simulation model. The
\par
model can be realized on a computer without modification.
\par
To the extent the axioms are accurate, the organization
\par
could be replaced by its axioms and an equational processor
\par
running on a computer. The simulation should be con-
\par
trasted with many simulations in that it is based upon a
\par
declarative form of knowledge (axioms) rather than a proce-
\par
dural form (programs) as is more traditional. It is this
\par
explicit statement using knowledge coded as axioms rather
\par
than procedures which makes for easy verification.
\par
3) It permits us to look at organizational planning as type of
\par
deductive reasoning. The replacement operations of a well-
\par
formed organizational model will correspond to deductive
\par
operations in mathematical logic. Thus this approach has as
\par
an underlying theme the application of logic to manage-
\par
ment. The simulation, accordingly, is not a realistic descrip-
\par
tion of behavior but is rather a sequence of steps in a
\par
rational planning process. The simulation is normative rather
\par
than positive.
\par
Thus we have a philosophy of planning and tools that may be
\par
used to explore the implications of that philosophy. The result of
\par
the refinements of the planning process is an activity expressed in
\par
organizational description language. This language permits the
\par
agents of the organization to carry out their tasks in parallel.
\par
However, most organizations have, in reality, decentralized paral-
\par
lel planning process. Modeling this leads to very deep issues of
\par
coordination due to the high complexity of planning interactions.
\par
Finding an appropriate parallel planning process is an important
\par
area of future research in decision and management science.
\par
VIII. RESEARCH NEEDS IN MARKETING8
\par
Research in marketing has progressed from early qualitative
\par
studies to sophisticated quantitative analyses of various problems
\par
8 This section of our report contributed by S. K. Sen.
\par
facing the marketing manager. In spite of this progress, much
\par
remains to be done and our main purpose here is to describe both
\par
current research and future opportunities in marketing. We also
\par
provide a brief description of new research techniques that will
\par
increasingly be used by marketing researchers. Our discussion
\par
will be organized in terms of the major decision areas in market-
\par
ing, such as product policy pricing, and advertising. We will
\par
briefly describe specific research topics in each area, and provide
\par
several references for the interested reader.
\par
There are several issues of importance relative to product
\par
policy and research in the decision and management sciences.
\par
These include
\par
1)\emph{ Market Structure:} An important issue confronting market-
\par
ing managers is how brands within a product class compete with
\par
one another. For example, does Coca-Cola compete mainly with
\par
Pepsi-Cola or does it also compete with soft drinks like 7-up?
\par
Answers to these questions have important implications for prod-
\par
uct design and pricing and considerable research is underway in
\par
this area [44], [98].
\par
2)\emph{ Innovation Incorporating Consumer Expectations'.} Models
\par
for new product introduction where word-of-mouth recommenda-
\par
tions are very important (e.g., for durables) have been an im-
\par
portant area of research in marketing. Starting with the original
\par
diffusion model by Bass [2], researchers have introduced the
\par
effects of marketing mix variables such as price and advertising
\par
[43] and the effect of competitive entry [18]. While the current
\par
work is an important first step, it does not take into account
\par
consumer expectations about the future pattern of price and
\par
product quality. Consider, for example, the personal computer
\par
market. A consumer considering the purchase of a personal
\par
computer is likely to expect the product's price to drop over a
\par
period of time and its capabilities (e.g., storage space, speed of
\par
operation, range of available software, etc.) to increase. Hence,
\par
the timing of a purchase decision is likely to be strongly affected
\par
by such expectations. It is, therefore, necessary that future re-
\par
search in this area develop explicit models of these expectations.
\par
3)\emph{ Design and Pricing of New Products Using Both Consumer}
\par
\emph{Demand and Firm Cost Data:} Product design models in market-
\par
ing [97] have typically used only consumer preference and choice
\par
data. However, the firm's technological capabilities and cost
\par
structure are also important in the pricing and design of new
\par
products. This issue is discussed by Sen [87], who points out that
\par
the economics literature [80] provides marketers with some theo-
\par
retical guidance in doing the research that is necessary in this
\par
area.
\par
4)\emph{ Pre-Test-Market Model for Durables:} For inexpensive, fre-
\par
quently-purchased products (e.g., toilet soap, deodorants, tooth-
\par
paste) there exists a set of successful models at the test market
\par
[67] and pre-test-market (i.e., laboratory simulations of test
\par
markets) levels [92], [75]. For durables, however, it is extremely
\par
costly to manufacture the new product in sufficient quantity to
\par
introduce it in several test markets. At best, a limited number of
\par
prototypes can be produced. Given these constraints, how can
\par
managers make reliable sales forecasts for new durables? This
\par
question requires the development of pre-test-market models
\par
especially designed for durables. An initial sketch of such a
\par
model is provided in [32] and [29] in the context of predicting the
\par
sales of a new automobile.
\par
5)\emph{ Product Design Under Long Production Lead Times:} Many
\par
products, such as copiers and automobiles, take a long time to
\par
manufacture and introduce into the market. For example, as
\par
much as five years might elapse between the time a new copier is
\par
designed and the time it is available for sale. Yet, the sales
\par
forecasts for the product were based on consumer data that were
\par
gathered at the time the product was being designed. By the time
\par
the product is ready to be introduced, consumer preferences may
\par
have changed and the competitive situation may have changed. If
\par
such changes have indeed occurred, the product's sales forecasts
\par
are likely to be quite inaccurate. What is required, therefore, is
\par
the development of models which explicitly take into account772 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS, VOL. SMC-15, NO. 6, NOVEMBER/DECEMBER 1985
\par
likely changes in the competitive situation and in preferences
\par
over time.
\par
6)\emph{ Defensive Marketing Strategies:} Most of the research in the
\par
new products area has concentrated on the problem of designing
\par
and successfully introducing a new product. However, very little
\par
attention has been paid to the problems faced by an existing firm
\par
in a product class where a successful new product has been
\par
introduced. How should this firm react to blunt the effect of the
\par
new entry? For example, should it cut price, increase advertising,
\par
or should it reposition its product? These issues are extremely
\par
important and are just starting to be addressed [28], [30].
\par
Pricing policy is an area that has been neglected by researchers
\par
in marketing. A good review of what is known is provided by
\par
Monroe [63]. However, one problem that has received attention
\par
lately is the problem of pricing product bundles. The bundling
\par
issue relates to products that can be sold either separately or in a
\par
bundle. A good example is a restaurant menu that allows items to
\par
be ordered a la carte or as full meals. The research question is as
\par
follows: under what circumstances is it optimal (i.e., profit maxi-
\par
mizing) to price the components separately and only allow con-
\par
sumers to buy each component individually? Or is it optimal to
\par
set a bundle price and sell the product only as a bundle? A third
\par
possibility is to price and sell the products individually as well as
\par
in a bundle. Related to these issues is the question of what price
\par
one should set for the components and the bundle. These issues
\par
are starting to be raised by economists [84] and marketers [5].
\par
The economists are approaching the problem from a theoretical
\par
perspective while the marketing work is empirically oriented. A
\par
blending of the two approaches is likely to lead to some inter-
\par
esting findings for marketing managers.
\par
Vast amounts of money are spent on promotions each year,
\par
e.g., coupons, cents-off offers, and end-of-aisle displays. Yet very
\par
little is known about when each promotion should be used, how
\par
long the promotions should run, or what is the optimal discount
\par
to provide, etc. These issues are important for both consumer
\par
promotions, which are offered to consumers by both retailers and
\par
manufacturers, and for trade promotions, which are offered by
\par
manufacturers to retailers. This is an area that is starting to
\par
receive attention and examples of early work on coupons and
\par
other promotions consist of the papers by Narasimhan [66],
\par
Neslin and Shoemaker [70], and Neslin, Henderson, and Quelch
\par
[71].
\par
Considerable research has been done by marketers in the area
\par
of advertising and Little [51] provides an excellent review of the
\par
literature. Yet, we still do not have a good positive theory of
\par
advertising. In other words, we do not have a good understanding
\par
of when different types of advertising are optimal. For example,
\par
when should we use different media (e.g., television or print),
\par
when should we use advertising that tries to create special images
\par
(e.g., for products such as perfume advertising), when should only
\par
prices be advertised, or when should advertisements stress prod-
\par
uct attributes. Casual discussions of these issues are available in
\par
the marketing literature but a formal analysis has yet to be done.
\par
However, some work does exist in economics [69] which might
\par
help marketers to develop a good positive theory of advertising.
\par
Without such a theory, it is difficult to provide managers with
\par
good guidance on advertising budgets, copy design, and media
\par
selection.
\par
Until recently, the analytical work in salesforce management
\par
used an operations research framework and a review is provided
\par
by Zoltners and Sinha [111]. More recently, however, researchers
\par
have become interested in studying various salesforce compensa-
\par
tion problems using the agency theory framework from econom-
\par
ics [27], [36], [24]. Examples of the questions being studied are
\par
when one would expect compensation to be straight salary, when
\par
it should be a combination of salary and commission, and what
\par
should be the magnitude of the salary and commission compo-
\par
nents. Interesting though preliminary research in this area is
\par
reported by [3] and [38] and a review is available in [14].
\par
Much of the earlier work on channels of distribution did not
\par
involve a great deal of modeling [94]. Recently however, there has
\par
been increased interest in modeling theoretically the relationships
\par
between different members of the distribution channel. This
\par
research gives interesting insights into the nature of optimal
\par
contracts between retailers and manufacturers [112], [57], [39],
\par
Though this research subarea is very new, it promises to be
\par
extremely important.
\par
There are also a number of general areas of interest and a few
\par
of these are cited here:
\par
1)\emph{ Industrial Marketing and the Marketing of Services'.} In gen-
\par
eral, marketing research has concentrated on products, as op-
\par
posed to services, and on consumer, as opposed to industrial,
\par
products. However, with the increasing importance of services in
\par
the American economy and the growing interest in research
\par
among industrial marketers, research in these two areas is bound
\par
to grow. See [52] and [13] for summaries of current knowledge in
\par
these two areas. Marketing research for non-profit organizations,
\par
such as symphony orchestras and operas, will also become im-
\par
portant as evidenced by the recent books by [77], [47], and [53].
\par
2)\emph{ Analysis of U.P.C. Scanner Data:} Because of the increasing
\par
usage of scanner equipment at American supermarkets, market-
\par
ing researchers are gaining access to a very rich database on
\par
products sold in supermarkets. This data will permit a detailed
\par
and careful analysis of the relative sales effects of different
\par
elements of the marketing mix (e.g., price, advertising, and pro-
\par
motion). An interesting paper in the use of scanner data is that
\par
by Guadagni and Little [25].
\par
Having discussed promising areas for decision and manage-
\par
ment science research in marketing, we now turn to some new
\par
techniques of analysis which are likely to have future value.
\par
Researchers in marketing have relied heavily on certain models
\par
from mathematical programming in operations research and on
\par
econometric techniques. These techniques will continue to be
\par
useful in the future. In addition, marketing researchers are likely
\par
to increasingly use techniques from game theory and economics.
\par
Game theory and its concepts of equilibrium [22], [64], [89] are
\par
very valuable in studying competition, which is the essence of
\par
marketing analysis. Game theoretic techniques have already been
\par
used in studies dealing with channels of distribution [McGuire
\par
and Staelin, 1983] and should be very helpful in the study of
\par
defensive marketing strategies.
\par
In addition to game theory, some techniques from economic
\par
systems analysis are likely to be increasingly useful to marketers.
\par
Models of consumer choice [4], [48], [80] will be useful in studies
\par
on product policy [31] and pricing. Agency theory [36] has
\par
already proved useful in sales compensation models [3]. Finally,
\par
the economics of information [34], [78] research is potentially
\par
very useful in studying both advertising and innovation incorpo-
\par
rating consumer expectations.
\par
There is clearly increasing interest in theory building in
\par
marketing research [109]. These theoretical developments will
\par
continue to be accompanied by what has always been a strength
\par
of marketing researchers: empirical analysis. Combined with the
\par
fact that marketing deals with real decisions faced by the market-
\par
ing manager in crucial areas such as product design and pricing,
\par
future research in marketing appears to be closely related to the
\par
more general goals in the decision and management sciences.
\par
IX. DECISION SUPPORT SYSTEMS: PERSPECTIVES AND
\par
RESEARCH OPPORTUNITIES9
\par
The concept of decision support systems (DSS) has received a
\par
good deal of attention in the computing and managerial litera-
\par
tures in the past few years. Much of our discussion thus far has
\par
relevance to this topic. But, in order to put DSS in perspective,
\par
let us remind ourselves of what we all know but sometimes
\par
forget: every manager has and uses a management information
\par
system (MIS), a combination of information sources and chan-
\par
9 This section of our report was contributed by G. Huber.IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS, VOL. SMC-15, NO.
\par
6, NOVEMBER/DECEMBER 1 9 8 5 773
\par
nels, and procedures for drawing on these sources and channels.
\par
Such systems typically consist of the trade magazines, file cabinets,
\par
sales representatives, recollections, telephones, organization
\par
charts, and the other resources and programs that the manager
\par
uses when seeking to answer a " what is?" question. Keeping this
\par
fact in mind, we see that a management MIS is the portion of a
\par
management information system that deals with computer-stored,
\par
and perhaps processed, data.
\par
There is a parallel here: every manager also has and uses a
\par
decision support system, a combination of his or her information
\par
system, and a set of decision aids for examining the desirability
\par
of possible choices. Such a system might include decision rules,
\par
policy manuals, recollections, and staff analysts among the pro-
\par
grams and resources that the manager uses when seeking to
\par
answer a "what if?" question. With this fact in mind, it follows
\par
that a DSS is the portion of the decision support system that is
\par
computer enhanced. (Conventional use of the term also implies
\par
that the relationship between the computer and the decision-
\par
maker is highly interactive, i.e., that the DSS includes a personal
\par
terminal that accesses an online processor.)
\par
DSS are basically software packages. Their components are 1)
\par
a database, 2) a model base, and 3) a software interface that
\par
allows the user to do three things: a) retrieve or enter data in real
\par
time, b) retrieve or create models in real time, and c) ask
\par
questions of the computer and obtain answers in real time. These
\par
questions are often of a cause-and-effect nature and are obtained
\par
by processing some of the data through one or more of the
\par
models.
\par
The DSS software is almost invariably accessed through a
\par
CRT-equipped terminal or desk-top computer. Input is typically
\par
made with a conventional keyboard, but touch-screen driven
\par
menus or mouse-driven pointers are not uncommon. Output is
\par
typically a numeric or graphic display on the CRT, although
\par
optional print capability is common.
\par
At this point it is useful to note that the term DSS is used in
\par
two ways. One is in reference to a specific database and a specific
\par
model base that supports a specific user in a specific set of
\par
decisions. Such custom systems are sometimes called "specific
\par
DSS." The other way in which the term is used is in reference to
\par
the high-level user-friendly language and set of procedures that
\par
allow the easy construction of a specific DSS. These "generic
\par
DSS" are what are sold commercially. Many DSS applications
\par
are associated with one-shot, ad hoc decisions, and the specific
\par
DSS employed is constructed on the spot using a generic DSS.
\par
Not all DSS users are hands-on users; many DSS users use
\par
intermediaries or "chauffeurs" to operate the input/output de-
\par
vices and carry out detailed information retrievals and analyses.
\par
What makes the user a DSS user (even if he or she is not the DSS
\par
operator) is the fact that he or she is directing the general nature
\par
of the man-machine interaction and is familiar with how the
\par
databases and model bases are being used. Typically the user is
\par
expert in the subject of the decision and is familiar with the data
\par
and model bases, perhaps by virtue of having directed their
\par
construction. Typically the user is not expert either in computing
\par
or in model-building outside of his or her own field.
\par
What a DSS is depends on whom you ask. Three professional
\par
disciplines have a special interest in decision support systems. As
\par
people from each of these have contributed to this report, the
\par
report reflects these three disciplines and their perspectives. If
\par
you ask people from these different disciplines what a DSS is,
\par
they will probably give you different answers. For example, a
\par
person whose experience is with using computers to store and
\par
retrieve data, perhaps as a designer of management information
\par
systems, will tend to tell you that
\par
DSS are basically Management Information Systems, except that they allow
\par
the user to combine data from ADP files with data from his own files and also
\par
to manipulate data interactively.
\par
These people, because of their background, will tend to view
\par
DSS as an extension of what they are familiar with and will
\par
mention what is different to them. Similarly, a person whose
\par
experience is with data analysis, for example an operations re-
\par
searcher or economic analyst, will likely tell you that DSS are
\par
basically modeling systems, except that they allow the user to
\par
create and use models interactively.
\par
In contrast, a person interested in cognitive psychology would
\par
tell you that
\par
DSS are basically external memories that help decisionmakers overcome the
\par
deficiencies of their own memories. The computer-stored data files serve as
\par
long-term memory, and the models and the processing of data through these
\par
models are substitutes for short-term memory.
\par
We should also note the view of a management consultant,
\par
especially one who is enthusiastic about DSS:
\par
DSS are a form of computing technology that implements the belief that
\par
decisionmakers themselves should be able to use the computer to aid their
\par
decisionmaking, and should not have to rely on computer experts or data
\par
analysts. The promotion of this technology and belief is called the "DSS
\par
movement".
\par
This latter view has become realistic because we have available
\par
today high-level user-oriented languages that enable users to use
\par
data bases and analytic models without the help of technical
\par
assistants.
\par
By far the largest commercial application of DSS is in the
\par
financial modeling area. At this point in time, over \$150,000,000
\par
of mini- and mainframe terminal-operated systems have been
\par
purchased. For micro systems, the number is well over half of a
\par
billion dollars. Typical applications are hierarchical budget mod-
\par
els and multiperiod cash-flow analyses. Growing areas of
\par
commercial application are in computer-aided manufacturing
\par
(CAM) and computer-aided transportation-system control. In the
\par
future, as "expert systems" become more closely associated with
\par
DSS (or vice versa), we can expect to see commercial applications
\par
in medicine and other areas of applied biological and physical
\par
science.
\par
There are many opportunities and needs for DSS research. At
\par
the present time, the development of generic DSS is taking place
\par
through the integration of knowledge from various systems and
\par
computer science specializations and from certain behavioral
\par
science specialties such as cognitive psychology. In general these
\par
supporting disciplines have available more applicable knowledge
\par
than is currently being drawn upon. Thus, there is relatively little
\par
research taking place in the area of DSS as such, even though
\par
relevant research is taking place in the supporting disciplines. For
\par
example, human factors researchers are studying the effects of
\par
different color displays, electronics researchers are improving
\par
voice recognition systems, and computer scientists and linguists
\par
are studying natural language systems.
\par
Let us turn now to the future. In the latter half of the 1980's we
\par
can expect to see an acceleration of research in DSS. One
\par
interesting development will be that the distinctions among DSS,
\par
knowledge systems, expert systems, and artificial intelligence or
\par
learning systems will fade even more than they have; we will see
\par
research directed toward developing systems having characteris-
\par
tics of two or more of these system types.
\par
Another development will be a much greater emphasis on the
\par
DSS asking questions of the user. Some of these will be straight-
\par
forward, such as when the computer is told that a certain al-
\par
gorithm is to be used, and it in turn asks the user to specify
\par
certain parameters. The more interesting developments will be
\par
directed toward having the computer ask questions so that it can
\par
determine what needs to be done (and perhaps help the user
\par
understand what it is that needs to be done). We are speaking
\par
here of the computer taking a very proactive role in problem
\par
formulation and "speculative" solution design (much as an ex-
\par
pert management consultant or psychological counselor might
\par
do). Voice recognition, natural language, and learning system
\par
research will clearly facilitate these developments.
\par
Related to this stream of research will be research concerning
\par
the development of procedures for eliciting "soft" information774 IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON SYSTEMS, MAN, AND CYBERNETICS, VOL. SMC-15, NO. 6, NOVEMBER/DECEMBER 1 9 8 5
\par
from managers and other experts for use by the computer in the
\par
service of the decisionmaker. The need for this research follows
\par
from the fact that the cognitive limitations of users often prevent
\par
them from employing information that they possess. For exam-
\par
ple, users generally are able to use their own knowledge concern-
\par
ing the probabilities of events in only a quite inefficient manner
\par
because they cannot mentally perform the computation associ-
\par
ated with the use of Bayes Theorem or a decision matrix. Subjec-
\par
tive probabilities are, of course, on the "hard" end of the "soft
\par
data" spectrum. Just how "soft" data can be and still be usable
\par
in an expert system or a DSS is a subject for future research, but
\par
is also being addressed even today.
\par
Drawing on the above efforts and on the current research in
\par
human problem solving, one of the most interesting research
\par
topics will be that of "task sharing" or "team management." By
\par
these terms, in this context we mean the sharing of decision-re-
\par
lated tasks between the two team members, the human user and
\par
the computer-resident DSS. What tasks need to be done? Which
\par
team member is most appropriate for which task (or subtask)?
\par
How can the two team members best maintain communication
\par
and coordination? We can expect to see work directed toward
\par
answering these questions. Such work will truly be research "in
\par
DSS." Again, of course, research from supporting disciplines
\par
dealing with voice recognition, natural languages, learning sys-
\par
tems and so forth will facilitate the DSS research itself.
\par
These four forecasts represent what we believe will occur, and
\par
should occur. As with all such forecasts, there is room and reason
\par
for skepticism. For those who are most skeptical, let us share a
\par
short story and an item of graffiti.
\par
In 1967 a paper describing how psychological scaling tech-
\par
niques could be used to develop the candidate-specific multiat-
\par
tribute utility functions that would allow computers to search job
\par
opening files to find employment opportunities for individual job
\par
candidates was submitted to\emph{ Management Science.} It was rejected
\par
as being too "blue-sky." "It'll never happen; you can't get
\par
humans to quantify their preferences this way," was a judgement
\par
of one referee. In 1984, the same paper would also be rejected,
\par
but the reviewers would (correctly) say "this procedure has so
\par
frequently been validated by researchers and applied by con-
\par
sultants that to describe it again would be an insult to our
\par
readers." The moral of this is that our timing may be off, even
\par
though our forecasts are not. Having computers "learn" what
\par
humans think is a research area where we can expect to see rapid
\par
advancement. The graffiti is a fitting conclusion to this effort. It
\par
was first observed near Kitty Hawk, North Carolina, circa 1903:
\par
I told Orville and Wilbur. I said, "It won't happen today, it won't happen
\par
tomorrow, and it will never happen." That's what I told Orville and Wilbur.
