\textbf{A USER-FOCUSED MODEL FOR THE}
\par
\textbf{UTILIZATION OF EVALUATION}
\par
TERRY CONNOLLY
\par
ALAN L. PORTER
\par
Georgia Institute of Technology
\par
\textbf{ABSTRACT}
\par
\emph{It isproposed that the present low levelof utilization of evaluationfindings is traceable in part to}
\par
\emph{their failure to address directly the information needs of a clearly specified decision maker. An}
\par
\emph{alternative }\textbf{\emph{model }}\emph{proceeding from such specification }\textbf{\emph{is proposed }}\emph{here, with evaluation closely}
\par
\emph{interwoven with the on-going innovation process. The modelsuggests a number of implications}
\par
\emph{for the organizational role of evalutors, for the design of evaluations, and for directions for}
\par
\emph{methodological development. In particular, we suggest that evaluation designs be assessed}
\par
\emph{against possible ``threats to utility'' as well as against the traditional ``threads to validity. ''}
\par
\textbf{INTRODUCTION}
\par
There appears to be broad agreement, and growing con-
\par
cern, that a disturbingly small fraction of evaluation
\par
studies impact actual decisions in the policy arenas to
\par
which they are addressed. Various possible explana-
\par
tions have been advanced for this: methodological
\par
weaknesses in the evaluations themselves (Cook \&
\par
Campbell, 1979); poor presentation and dissemination
\par
of findings (Hors\& Nay, Scanlon, \& Wholey, 1974);
\par
lack of attention to the policymaking time frame (Davis
\par
\& Salasin, 1977); poor organizational placement of
\par
evaluation groups (Striven, 1976); political naivete of
\par
evaluators (Berk \& Rossi, 1976); and others.
\par
In this article we argue that the crucial determinant of
\par
utilization is the extent to which an evaluation study ad-
\par
dresses the informational needs of a specific decision
\par
maker confronting or anticipating a specific decision.
\par
Clearly, most evaluators hope for an impact of this sort
\par
from their findings. However, several aspects of cur-
\par
rent evaluation practice tend to deflect the focus away
\par
from the specific decision maker, and the specific deci-
\par
sion, serving instead the poorly specified informational
\par
needs of a diffuse group of potential users. In this arti-
\par
cle, we explore a number of implications of evaluation
\par
aimed specifically, and narrowly, at the needs of a
\par
single user.
\par
The first part of the article examines the models of
\par
utilization that appear to underlie most current evalua-
\par
tion practice, and outlines an alternative explicitly ad-
\par
dressing the information needs of a focal innovating
\par
manager. Innovation and evaluation are seen in this
\par
model as two closely interlocked processes, and there
\par
appears to be considerable merit in placing both under
\par
the control of the focal manager. This proposal carries
\par
a number of implications for the design and conduct of
\par
evaluations - organizational, procedural, and meth-
\par
odological. Several of these implications are discussed
\par
in the later parts of the paper.
\par
\textbf{SOME MODELS OF THE UTILIZATION PROCESS}
\par
Probably no seasoned evaluator treats utilization as a ings, even those of the highest technical excellence and
\par
trivial problem. Merely to disseminate evaluation find- ethical care, offers little hope that they will influence
\par
An earlier version of this paper was presented at the Southern Sociological Association Annual Conference, New Orleans, Louisiana, March
\par
1978. Requests for reprints should be sent to Terry Connolly, School of Industrial and Systems Engineering, Georgia Institute of Technology,
\par
Atlanta, GA 30332.
\par
\emph{131}132 TERRY CONNOLLY and ALAN L. PORTER
\par
the decisions of relevant policymakers. Such a ``better
\par
mousetrap'' view of the utilization process is roundly re-
\par
jected by experienced practitioners:
\par
Even if you are lucky enough and smart enough to do
\par
everything right from beginning to end in terms of
\par
resources, personnel, design, avenues of influence
\par
and so on, it's not automatic from there on at all. . . .
\par
The mere production and dissemination of findings,
\par
however intellectually or methodologically compell-
\par
ing they may be, is usually not enough to sway a deci-
\par
sion, change a program, alter a budget, or change a
\par
law. (Evans, 1977, p. 39)
\par
At the symposium from which these remarks are
\par
taken (Chelimsky, 1977), many speakers urged ``pro-
\par
duct marketing'' strategies of one sort or another to
\par
bring evaluation findings to the attention of policy-
\par
makers. Such strategies include:
\par
- improved ``packaging'' (i.e., presentation in a form
\par
usable by various audiences),
\par
- ``advertising'' (making decision makers aware of the
\par
findings),
\par
- ``market segmentation'' (in the sense of addressing
\par
different versions of the ``product'' to different deci-
\par
sionmakers who can implement its recommenda-
\par
tions),
\par
- ``styling'' (eliminating jargon and technical detail, re-
\par
porting concisely in plain English).
\par
Note that these ``marketing'' strategies assume that the
\par
``product'' (i.e., the evaluation) exists and must now, as
\par
a separate activity, be brought to the attention of its
\par
potential users. We shall return to this assumption
\par
later.
\par
A more political view of the issue is provided by
\par
Lourie (1976), who outlines what might be described as
\par
an ``archive-building, trickle-down'' process connecting
\par
findings and utilization:
\par
when decision-making ``time'' comes . . . many
\par
analyses, studies, simulation models and exercises
\par
will have piled up in universities, private institu-
\par
tions, and international organizations. Some of it
\par
may have even trickled down - or up - to the ad-
\par
ministrator entrusted with the preparation of the
\par
decision and, ultimately, with its implementation. . . .
\par
Somehow, a signal for some action is initiated from
\par
the ``front-office.'' Then, gradually, some of the
\par
material is filtered to the decision-maker.
\par
The contact between policy research and decision-
\par
making seems then to occur when the decision-
\par
maker, either prompted from inside or eroded from
\par
outside, seeks ammunition with which to bombard
\par
his political targets. Within his power is the selection
\par
of existing research results. In doing so he may reject
\par
the whole for some of its parts, simply because they
\par
appeal to him in terms of their potential short-term
\par
spectacular effects. (Lourie, 1976, pp. 84-85)
\par
Such a process has two obvious weaknesses as an aid to
\par
rational policy making: the chancy linkage between
\par
evaluation findings and the decisionmaker, and the
\par
selectivity implied by the search for politically-useful
\par
weapons.
\par
This is not to suggest that evaluations are ``utilized''
\par
only when they clearly and directly impact on specific
\par
decisions. Policy and program decisions are made on
\par
the basis of many different sorts of information, and
\par
some degree of utilization can be claimed for evaluation
\par
findings that enter into this mix. Thus Weisss (1977)
\par
argues for an ``enlightenment'' function, and Parlett
\par
and Hamilton (1976) for an ``illumination'' function,
\par
for social research, in which the policy debate is en-
\par
riched by both concepts and findings drawn from the
\par
research. Similarly, Patton (1978) reports surprisingly
\par
high percentages of decisionmakers as seeing some im-
\par
pact from specific evaluation findings, though direct
\par
impact on major, concrete program decisions was rare.
\par
``The more typical impact was one where the evaluation
\par
findings provided additional pieces of information in
\par
the difficult puzzle of program action, permitting some
\par
reduction in the uncertainty within which any federal
\par
decisionmaker inevitably operates'' (p. 28).
\par
Such views offer a measure of comfort for
\par
evaluators. If we continue to produce high-quality
\par
evaluations, and market them appropriately, we may
\par
hope that our work will ultimately impact the process
\par
and outcomes of policy debates; some utilization will
\par
sometimes be achieved. However, before we modestly
\par
accept this rather humble view of evaluation as merely
\par
one instrument in the policymaking orchestra, it may be
\par
worth exploring alternative views. The remainder of
\par
this article proposes one such alternative, in which
\par
evaluation retains a direct impact on decision making.
\par
The core of this alternative is suggested by an important
\par
study by Caplan (1976).
\par
Caplan conducted interviews with some 204 in-
\par
dividuals in policymaking posts in the U.S. Goverment.
\par
His study attempted to identify what social-science in-
\par
formation gets used, by whom, for what purposes, and
\par
with what impact. His results are, perhaps, rather en-
\par
couraging, in that they suggest wider use of various
\par
social research findings than is generally assumed.
\par
Three-quarters of his respondents were able to identify
\par
two or more specific instances when they had attempted
\par
to put specific findings to use. Furthermore, the
\par
methodologies labeled as ``program evaluation'' and
\par
``field experimentation'' together account for almost
\par
one-third of the instances of use. Over half of these in-
\par
stances were on policy issues of national importance.
\par
General encouragement aside, we note several more
\par
specific findings from this study:
\par
1. Of the instances of use, 86\% were funded by the user
\par
agency and 5 1\% were conducted internally.
\par
2. This tendency toward use of internally controlledUser-Focused Model for Utilization of Evaluation 133
\par
studies was increased as the policy areas in question
\par
became more important
\par
3. Almost 80\% of the information used was generated
\par
in response to the decisionmaker's specific request,
\par
rather than being initiated by subordinates.
\par
4. As much as one-third of all information usage was
\par
applied to policy issues directly relating to the inter-
\par
nal operation of the agency itself.
\par
In short, Caplan's data suggest that social research find-
\par
ings do get used by policymakers, but that such usage is
\par
most likely when the findings are generated specifically
\par
in response to a policymaker's request and paid for by
\par
the using agency.
\par
In light of these data, it seems reasonable to
\par
hypothesize that evaluation findings are more likely to
\par
be utilized in decisionmaking if they are produced in
\par
response to a specific informational need of a spcific
\par
decisionmaker, and when the generation, timing and
\par
dissemination of results of the study are under his or her
\par
control. Conversely, utilization is less likely when
\par
evaluations are designed with no specific user, and no
\par
specific decision, in mind, and thus when generation,
\par
timing and dissemination are determined largely by the
\par
logic of the evaluation process and the needs of the
\par
evaluator. In short, we hypothesize that utilization will
\par
be high for user-focused evaluations, low for evaluator-
\par
focused evaluations. A simplified model of a user-
\par
focused evaluation process is outlined in the following
\par
section.
\par
\textbf{A USER-FOCUSED MODEL}
\par
The core of the user-focused model is a single decision-
\par
maker who is considering the use of evaluation studies
\par
to guide his or her decisions. Following Campbell
\par
(1969), the decisionmaker is considered as committed to
\par
the amelioration of some problem under his or her
\par
jurisdiction, and to have developed a list of promising
\par
and feasible candidate policy innovations that might
\par
serve this purpose. (Uncertainly as to the efficacy of
\par
each is assumed - if one knows with certainty what
\par
course of action is most effective, no further informa-
\par
tion gain can be expected from evaluation.) From the
\par
list of candidate innovations, the decisionmaker selects
\par
the most promising and asks: Is the first phase of this in-
\par
novation worth implementing? Given an affirmative
\par
answer, (s)he then asks: Is this first-phase innovation
\par
worth evaluating?
\par
We shall assume that the decisionmaker applies at
\par
least an approximate cost/benefit assessment in
\par
answering these questions. That is, the decision as to
\par
whether or not to implement a particular innovation (or
\par
partial innovation) will turn on an assessment of the
\par
benefits of success (both in problem amelioration and
\par
personal rewards), the costs of failure (again including
\par
program and personal costs), and the probability of
\par
each occurring. In most cases, the disruption and effort
\par
associated with implementation of any change must be
\par
counted as a significant cost. Similarly, the decision as
\par
to whether or not to conduct an evaluation (and at what
\par
level) will turn on an assessment of the costs of evalua-
\par
tion versus its benefits. The latter are primarily the
\par
payoff from better information (reduced uncertainty)
\par
at subsequent innovation decisions. That is, the model
\par
considers evaluation findings as a feedback mechanism
\par
whose value lies in the reduction of decision errors in
\par
subsequent decisions. Evaluations are undertaken
\par
when this value exceeds their costs.
\par
It should be noted that the issue of utilization of
\par
evaluation findings is unproblematic in the idealized ra-
\par
tional model. The decisionmaker is assumed to incur
\par
the costs of the evaluation, and to receive any benefits
\par
therefrom in terms of subsequent improved decisions.
\par
Having decided to pay for an evaluation of some phase
\par
of the intervention, (s)he is assumed to use the resulting
\par
information. Evaluation, in short, is treated as a
\par
management tool, providing the decisionmaker with in-
\par
formation upon which to base future decisions (c.f.
\par
Horst et al., 1974).
\par
It should also be emphasized that the assumption of
\par
rational cost-benefit assessments by the decisionmaker
\par
does not preclude political considerations (Graycar,
\par
1979; House, 1974). For example, a decisionmaker may
\par
well include the wishes of a superior, or an elected of-
\par
ficial, for implementation of a particular program as an
\par
important factor in undertaking a particular effort.
\par
Similarly, such factors may well enter the assessment of
\par
whether or not to undertake an evaluation: there may
\par
be little to be gained by learning the efficacy (or lack
\par
thereof) of a program which is certain to remain in place
\par
regardless of such information. (Caplan, 1976, offers a
\par
useful distinction between the ``internal'' and ``external''
\par
logics of program management: the former includes on-
\par
ly matters concerning the content, costs and benefits of
\par
the program itself, while the latter includes the wider
\par
political considerations affecting its activities. We here
\par
assume that factors of both kinds will enter into the
\par
decision maker's cost-benefit assessments.)
\par
The user-focused model is, clearly, highly over-
\par
simplified as a description of any actual or possible in-
\par
novation process. It does, however, serve to provide
\par
some conceptual clarity to a number of difficult issues
\par
in the evaluation field. Consider, for example, the
\par
following two issues, not well resolved in conventional
\par
evaluation thinking: (1) How large an ``increment''
\par
should be tried out at any given point in the innovation134 TERRY CONNOLLY and ALAN L. PORTER
\par
process?, and (2) Is evaluation of a given ``increment''
\par
justified and, if so, at what cost? The user-focused
\par
model treats these issues as intimately connected to one
\par
another. There are potential payoffs of two sorts for
\par
undertaking a given ``increment'' of an innovation:
\par
those resulting from (partial) solution of the focal pro-
\par
blem, and those resulting from the information gained
\par
by evaluation. We do not wish here to attempt any for-
\par
mal treatment of how these payoffs might be quan-
\par
tified. (A decision-theoretic treatment is outlined in
\par
Connolly, 1976; the value of information is treated
\par
generally in Howard, 1966, and Raiffa, 1968).
\par
However, the elements of such a treatment can be sket-
\par
ched. The alternative step-sizes may be severely con-
\par
strained by either the ``external'' or ``internal'' logics of
\par
the situation (Caplan, 1976). For example, political fac-
\par
tors (the external logic) may dictate that a large and visi-
\par
ble effort is required. Or, the content of the innovation
\par
itself (the internal logic) may allow only increments of
\par
certain sizes - it may simply be impossible to hire half a
\par
person, introduce part of a reform, and so on.
\par
If several possible step-sizes remain after these con-
\par
siderations, the logic of the model can be usefully
\par
brought to bear. The decisionmaker estimates, for each
\par
possible step-size, the costs and payoffs involved. The
\par
latter include both problem-solution and information-
\par
gain elements. The highest-net-payoff step-size is then
\par
chosen (incorporating evaluation costs in this assess-
\par
ment). Several different levels of evaluation effort can
\par
be considered, ranging from none, through various
\par
``quick-and-dirty'' approaches, up to full-scale,
\par
methodologic\textasciitilde ly sophisticated policy experiments. We
\par
may assume that ascending this evaluation scale will in-
\par
crease costs, while also increasing the confidence that
\par
can be placed in the results and/or the comprehen-
\par
siveness of impacts considered. The trade-off is, in
\par
principle, open to optimization or at least a reasonable
\par
approximation thereto, given the detailed facts of a
\par
specific situation.
\par
Some extreme cases may be worth considering. In
\par
some situations, no evaluation at all can be justified. If
\par
no later decision is in prospect there is no value to gain-
\par
ing information (for example, if the only feasible incre-
\par
ment is an irreversible, one-shot plunge). Also, if there
\par
is no uncertainty about the impact of the innovation, no
\par
information is gained by evaluation, so none is
\par
justified. We can also readily imagine situations, such
\par
as a small pilot project for an intendedly national pro-
\par
gram, in which the decisional stakes are high enough to
\par
justify evaluation costs of many times the project
\par
budget. Conversely, there will be large projects for
\par
which no evaluation at all can be justified. Certainly,
\par
our logic argues strongly against routinely assigning
\par
some fixed percentage of project budget for evaluation
\par
costs for all types of project. Evaluation costs are
\par
justified only by the value of the information generated
\par
- and, we argue, such information is primarily of value
\par
to the focal decisionmaker in guiding later innovation
\par
decisions in the same area.
\par
A potentially fruitful analogy can be drawn between
\par
the process of policy development and evaluation, and
\par
the process of developing a new product or process in
\par
the physical technologies (e.g., Pessemier, 1966). In the
\par
early stages of hardware innovation, laboratory
\par
demonstration and developmental testing aim primari-
\par
ly at the assessment of the promise of the idea, and at
\par
the identification of the areas where difficulties need to
\par
be resolved. When one implements a pilot demonstra-
\par
tion of a new technology, one does not want to know
\par
whether or not it works at some statistical significance
\par
level. Rather, the questions fit more closely a Bayesian
\par
model whereby the technological demonstration
\par
augments understanding along a variety of dimensions
\par
(e.g., operating performance under various conditions,
\par
cost factors as a function of scale, product marketabili-
\par
ty). Analogously, program evaluation rarely concerns
\par
the issue of ``did it work or not?'' If it did not ``work,''
\par
one still wants to know of partial successes, contextual
\par
factors, and particular causes for the lack of success.
\par
Unfortunately, our standard evaluation designs seem to
\par
focus narrowly on success or failure at a 0.05
\par
significance level. This mismatch between design and
\par
user need argues for reoriented designs.
\par
The hardware development analogy may also pro-
\par
vide some useful clues to the organizational role into
\par
which the evaluator may grow. In engineering practice,
\par
the role of the Chief Test Engineer is well established.
\par
As with the evaIuator in the role we envision here, the
\par
Chief Test Engineer is not oriented to the publication of
\par
findings in the scholarly literature, but to the generation
\par
of reliable, ``private,'' management-relevant informa-
\par
tion concerning technical performance of the product
\par
or process in question. Encouragingly, it appears that
\par
Test Engineers have largely managed to resist the in-
\par
evitable pressures to distort their findings - we would
\par
be shocked to hear of a falsified test report on a piece of
\par
equipment, for example. There may be useful guide-
\par
lines here for the evolution of the role of the profes-
\par
sional in-house evaluator.* (See also Bunker, 1978, for
\par
a broad and thoughtful discussion of organizational
\par
issues in linking social researchers and policymakers.)
\par
The ``management tool'' view of evaluation we are
\par
presenting emphasizes another crucial consideration in
\par
-.
\par
*An anonymous reviewer notes that as equipment becomes more
\par
complex and stakes higher, as in nuclear power plants and airplane
\par
manufacture, outside, independent review of test results becomes the
\par
norm. We see this as a neat extension of the present logic: a new deci-
\par
sionmaker representing the public interest requires new evaluation
\par
data to guide distinct decisions (such as certification). We can see the
\par
emergence of analogous, outside evaluator, roles, somewhat along
\par
the lines of independent auditors reviewing internal accounting prac-
\par
tices, as evaluation findings take on significance outside of direct pro-
\par
gram management. Our point here is merely that norms against
\par
falsification can be maintained despite organizational pressure in that
\par
direction.User-Focused Model for Utilization of Evaluation 135
\par
selecting an appropriate evaluation methodology: The
\par
time-frame within which decisions are to be made, and
\par
thus within which information is required. Ideally,
\par
evaluators will have an extended time-frame within
\par
which to devise rigorous designs and to carry them to
\par
completion. However, important policy issues may re-
\par
quire evaluation input on much shorter time-frames
\par
than such designs allow (Nay, Scanlon, Graham, 8\textasciitilde{}
\par
Waller, 1977). Similar demands for quick, decision-
\par
relevant information arise in the context of ``formative
\par
evaluations'' (Striven, 1965). In formative evaluation,
\par
rigor, while obviously still desirable, may be partially
\par
traded off for a fast-feedback role, in which evaluation
\par
provides at least some guidance for the host of
\par
developmental decisions that must be made as the in-
\par
fant program moves from an idea to a working actuali-
\par
ty. Again, the time-frame for decisions must be con-
\par
sidered in choosing a suitable evaluation design. More
\par
broadly, we suspect that the dichotomy between for-
\par
mative and summative evaluation hides an underlying
\par
continuum. Most formative evaluations could turn
\par
summative if the evidence is overwhelmingly hopeless;
\par
most summative decisions, formative, if the results sug-
\par
gest partial reformulation of the program would be in
\par
order (see Pondy, 1977). In the current perspective, we
\par
emphasize the formative role of evaluation in providing
\par
real-time feedback to guide managerial action. ``Sum-
\par
mative'' then merely labels, retrospectively, situations
\par
where evaluation activity has ceased because of persis-
\par
tent formative findings of either consistent failures or
\par
satisfactory success.
\par
The core of our argument thus far, then, is that the
\par
under-utilization of most evaluations to date is rather
\par
directly a consequence of their design. Designs have
\par
generally emphasized rigor of method and certainty of
\par
results, suggesting a science-like, archive-building
\par
model, rather loosely coupled to the policymaking
\par
world. As Rich (1977) suggests, there is value in such
\par
``knowledge for understanding''; but it is not identical
\par
with ``knowledge for action.'' Generation of the latter
\par
sort of knowledge requires, in our view, evaluation
\par
designs that start by consideration of the needs and con-
\par
straints of an identified, focal decisionmaker or
\par
knowledge user (see also Patton, 1978, pp. 248ff).
\par
Methodology, strategy, and tactics for archive-build-
\par
ing, knowledge-for-understanding evaluations are in-
\par
creasingly well-developed. In the remainder of this arti-
\par
cle, we wish to examine some issues that pertain to the
\par
conduct of decision-focused, knowledge-for-action
\par
evaluations.
\par
\textbf{SOME IMPLICATIONS OF USER-FOCUSED EVALUATION}
\par
\textbf{A. Privacy, Entrapment, and Dissemination}
\par
An important feature of the user-focused model as pro-
\par
posed here is that evaluation findings are, at least in-
\par
itially, made available only to the focal user, rather than
\par
being distributed widely and publicly, as in the tradi-
\par
tional model. Such a proposal is, of course, anathema
\par
to the science-like norms of traditional evaluations,
\par
where wide collegial scrutiny is a primary guarantee of
\par
objectivity and dissemination of results. However,
\par
several different lines of argument support this pro-
\par
posal:
\par
1. User-focused evaluations, as we envision them,
\par
should be oriented to the specific needs of a specific
\par
user. They need be designed around the user's infor-
\par
mation wants and willingness to commit resources.
\par
Designing for a vaguely specified general-purpose
\par
user seems likely to dilute the focus of the evaluation
\par
effort, serving many users poorly rather than one
\par
well. (See also Connolly \& Deutsch, 1980, on the dif-
\par
ficulties of specifying a single comprehensive set of
\par
evaluative criteria.)
\par
2. Control over dissemination of results eliminates the
\par
``trapped administrator'' problem (Campbell, 1969).
\par
The essence of the entrapment mechanism is that an
\par
administrator, seeing or suspecting that an innova-
\par
tion for which (s)he is responsible is failing, has both
\par
incentive and opportunity to sabotage the evalua-
\par
tion, rather than incur reputational damage. With
\par
evaluation results private to this manager, however,
\par
the incentive to sabotage (and, indeed the implicit
\par
disincentive to innovate in the first place) is re-
\par
moved. Even if the innovation fails, the manager
\par
may still salvage something from the wreckage in the
\par
way of learning from the evaluation.
\par
3. It seems likely that the normal mechanisms of
\par
organizational decisionmaking will serve, at least
\par
partially, the purposes of competitive scrutiny of
\par
results. For example, the use of evaluation findings
\par
in support of an increased budget request would
\par
bring these findings under the review of other
\par
managers competing for restricted budget alloca-
\par
tions. (Indeed, one can imagine an ``evaluative arms
\par
race,'' in which a manager, losing budget one year
\par
because of the compelling evaluation findings of a
\par
competitor, is led to use the same weapon in support
\par
of his or her own projects in subsequent years.) Such
\par
dissemination and scrutiny would be limited to
\par
positive evaluation findings, and it might become
\par
necessary to devise mechanisms by which important
\par
negative findings could be disseminated. Even
\par
without such mechanisms, however, the likely costs
\par
(non-dissemination of negative findings) may be
\par
more than offset by the gains (direct use of evalua-
\par
tion findings by the focal manager).136 TERRY CONNOLLY \textbf{and }ALAN L. PORTER
\par
\textbf{B. Threats to Utility}
\par
In conventional evaluation, the designer's central con-
\par
cern is with identifying and, if possible, obviating,
\par
threats to the validity of the findings - ``internal'' and
\par
``external'' validity in the original formulation (Camp-
\par
bell \& Stanley, 1966), more recently extended to include
\par
``construct'' and ``statistical conclusion'' validity (Cook
\par
\& Campbell, 1979). While these validity concerns re-
\par
main important in the user-focused model, some degree
\par
of relaxation is possible, and a new set of pragmatic
\par
concerns that threaten the utility of the study become
\par
relatively more serious.
\par
At least the following design criteria are brought to
\par
the fore by considering the utility of an evaluation to a
\par
focal user.
\par
1. \emph{Credibility. }While it would seem that the credibility
\par
of an evaluation should be closely correlated with its
\par
validity, we suspect that this will not be invariably true
\par
in real settings. Whether or not a manager trusts the im-
\par
plications of an evaluation will also involve the degree
\par
of trust (s)he places in the evaluator, which in turn is
\par
likely to be related to their previous interactions, the
\par
evaluator's credentials and reputation, and so on (see
\par
also Patton, Grimes, Guthrie, Brennan, French, \&
\par
Blyth, 1977). Further, credibility is related to the face
\par
plausibility of the findings, and to the manager's prior
\par
beliefs in what they should show (Cohen \& Lindblom,
\par
1979). We cannot attempt any complete specification of
\par
the process by which such credibility is to be attained. It
\par
does, however, seem clear that establishment of a can-
\par
did two-way relationship between manager and
\par
evaluator is crucial, particularly when managers cannot
\par
be expected to be expert in the arcana of research meth-
\par
odology. Evaluators should be candid in conveying the
\par
degree of trust that should appropriately be placed in
\par
particular findings, especially when design compro-
\par
mises have had to be made.
\par
2. \emph{User-Relevance. }The concern here is with the match
\par
between the evaluation answers and the user's ques-
\par
tions. ``Errors of the third kind'' - providing precise
\par
answers to the wrong questions (Mitroff \& Feather-
\par
ingham, 1974) - are anathema. Obviously, broad
\par
scope evaluations have a better chance of addressing
\par
eventual user concerns. For more narrowly focused
\par
evaluations, two-way dialogue between users and
\par
evaluators before, during and after the study is the best
\par
guarantee that the findings will be perceived as relevant
\par
(Berg, Brudney, Fuller, Michael \& Roth, 1978; Patton,
\par
1978). Matters of style and presentation - the selection
\par
of statistical techniques familiar to the user, oral versus
\par
written reporting, and so on - might also be included
\par
under this head.
\par
3. \emph{Feasibility. }The proposed evaluation must be
\par
``doable'' in the situation actually found, no matter how
\par
distasteful this is from an evaluator's viewpoint. For in-
\par
stance, political realities may limit the cooperation of
\par
program staff and clients. A retrospective evaluation
\par
may be the only option, and poor record-keeping and
\par
dispersal of key personnel may make access to critical
\par
data problematic. The nature of the program may make
\par
only certain increments of intervention possible. Such
\par
constraints will often make full-scale, randomized field
\par
experiments impossible, while leaving open possibilities
\par
of alternative designs. (On the other hand, they may
\par
preclude any serious evaluative effort, and such a
\par
possibility should be carefully considered.)
\par
4. \emph{Time-Frame. }This design criterion has two distinct
\par
aspects. First is the issue of h \emph{priori }or b \emph{posteriori -}
\par
whether the evaluation is of some activities about to
\par
take place, or of activities already in place or com-
\par
pleted. The former is, of course, highly desirable, but
\par
one can thoughtfully cope with the unplanned situation
\par
too. The second aspect of time is speed: How much time
\par
can the user provide for evaluation? The range here is
\par
very wide. Perhaps a decade is necessary to design and
\par
conduct a major field experiment, at one extreme. At
\par
the other, testimony for a critical budget hearing may
\par
be needed ``tomorrow.'' Even at this ``instant-response''
\par
end of the spectrum, some evaluative reaction may be
\par
possible, particularly if the system has been designed
\par
with a fast response capability in mind. (However, if
\par
only in the interests of maintaining credibility, the
\par
evaluator should bear in mind that the best response to
\par
the question: ``What can you get me by tomorrow?''
\par
may, in many circumstances, be ``Nothing.``)
\par
5. \emph{Cost. }Our final criterion is self-explanatory; it relates
\par
to the focal user's willingness to pay for evaluative in-
\par
formation. Our hope is that, with more flexible design,
\par
we may be able to offer bargain-priced information in
\par
some settings, extending the range of decisions to which
\par
evaluative help of some sort is available.
\par
These five criteria intimately intertwine in any real
\par
design. While validity is critical, so is utility, and
\par
designs strong in one respect are likely to suffer in the
\par
other (Porter \& Rossini, 1977). A proposed evaluation
\par
design can be eliminated from consideration if it
\par
violates acceptable thresholds on any of the criteria
\par
(e.g., the budget may be set, the time frame immut-
\par
able). More generally, particular designs can and
\par
should be weighed against all five potential ``threats to
\par
utility'' as well as against the familiar ``threats to validi-
\par
ty'' before a design is finally chosen.
\par
C. \textbf{Methodological Development}
\par
Our reading of the evaluation methodology literature
\par
suggests that the technology is well-developed only for
\par
the relatively rigorous end of the spectrum. Com-
\par
paratively little attention has been given (other than
\par
critiques and warnings) to less rigorous techniques that,
\par
within short time frames and limited budgets, will pro-User-Focused Model for Utilization of Evaluation 137
\par
duce relevant (if imperfect) guidance for hard-pressed
\par
decision makers on at least some of their critical uncer-
\par
tainties. We believe that there is a need for developing
\par
such alternative evaluative techniques. Some potential-
\par
ly fruitful directions for such development are sketched
\par
below. We suspect that most, if not all, of these techni-
\par
ques are familiar to, and probably practiced by, ex-
\par
perienced working evaluators. They are, however, little
\par
noted in the methodological literature, and thus seem
\par
worth a brief review here. In each case, there is obvious
\par
need for detailed development of the suggestion and its
\par
variants and, particularly, for field trials of the
\par
developed techniques. In the spirit of experimentation
\par
and evaluation, we offer these suggestions as potential-
\par
ly useful candidate techniques in need of evaluation in
\par
actual use.
\par
1. \emph{Pre-Planning for Evaluability. }Given a close, on-
\par
going relationship between manager and evaluator, it
\par
may be possible to implement innovations so as to
\par
enhance their evaluability at small cost. In particular,
\par
manager/evaluator teams should avoid situations in
\par
which, even if everything succeeds as well as can be
\par
hoped, evaluation could not possibly be compelling.
\par
Evaluation programs with limited resources, an-
\par
ticipating ``two-week'' requests, can prepare in several
\par
ways. Reporting requirements for the innovation in
\par
question can be established to provide descriptive infor-
\par
mation on what was done and, also, of the effects
\par
observed (to whatever extent seems appropriate).
\par
Monitoring forms could be produced and compiled to
\par
facilitate potential evaluator access (e.g., machine
\par
readable, centrally filed). This should ease quick
\par
response problems. Comparisons can be planned to
\par
provide some points of contrast where variants of the
\par
innovation are involved. By ``thinking evaluation''
\par
ahead of time, implementations can be managed to pro-
\par
vide maximal contrasts on the key points of concern. (A
\par
nice example of this general strategy is provided by
\par
Campbell and Stanley's, 1966, Design 15, in which a
\par
quite powerful design is achieved at low cost by ex-
\par
ploiting normal institutional cycles and administrative
\par
delays to obtain useful control groups).
\par
2. \emph{Process Tracing. }One invaluable way of gaining
\par
understanding of, and confidence in, project impacts is
\par
what might be called ``process training'' - that is,
\par
documenting in some detail what exactly was done, by
\par
whom, to whom, when, and so on. Nayet al. (1977, An-
\par
nex D) describe a major successful effort to use process
\par
tracing in the series of evaluations that comprised the
\par
National Evaluation Program of the National Institute
\par
of Law Enforcement and Criminal Justice. Such docu-
\par
mentation is, of course, essential to generating an ac-
\par
curate final report. It is also a vital aid to evaluation,
\par
particularly when there has been relatively little oppor-
\par
tunity to use formal evaluation designs, when the pro-
\par
ject content has changed a great deal as it developed,
\par
and when initial goals have been extensively modified as
\par
understanding of project impacts, constraints, and
\par
mechanisms has grown. In these cases, one is quite
\par
unlikely to have a clean design with a standardized
\par
package impacting a well-defined population. Good
\par
process tracing allows one to look at the impact of par-
\par
ticular parts of the project, even when the whole
\par
package failed, so that an innovator can rebuild on the
\par
partial successes rather than start from scratch (see also
\par
Weiss, 1972, pp. 323-324). It can explain why a good
\par
concept failed in a given application, thereby saving the
\par
concept for other implementations. It may also produce
\par
high confidence in the efficacy of the project, even
\par
when the formal evaluation design is weak. It also
\par
allows outsiders to start the process of assessing
\par
whether the elements of the project could be modified
\par
to work in their contexts.
\par
The basis of good process tracing is to treat each
\par
significant element of the project as a mini-project in
\par
itself, and to try to discover whether or not it is working
\par
as intended (or in some other, possibly beneficial, way).
\par
For example, for a training program with several dif-
\par
ferent elements, it may be possible to evaluate the im-
\par
pact of each. Perhaps the program included training in
\par
reading job ads, assessment of client skills, and inter-
\par
view conduct. It would be worth following up suc-
\par
cessful and unsuccessful job placements to see if clients
\par
are actually finding jobs through reading job ads,
\par
whether they are fitting their skills to the job re-
\par
quirements, and whether interviews are going well or
\par
badly. Each of these ``mini-evaluations'' may be im-
\par
perfect, relying too much on anecdotes, casual data-
\par
gathering, etc.; but if they fit together so as to show the
\par
pattern of step-by-step connection between program
\par
elements and impact on individuals, the cumulative ef-
\par
fect often allows only one reasonable explanation for
\par
observed gross program effects. Even when no gross ef-
\par
fects can be found, the mini-evaluations may suggest
\par
where improvement can be made.
\par
3. \emph{``Modus 0perandi''Inference. }An interesting refine-
\par
ment of process tracing is proposed by Striven (1974) in
\par
what he calls the ``modus operandi method'' of making
\par
causal inferences. Striven's general perspective seems
\par
close to that taken here:
\par
Granted that control-group studies (including
\par
possibly self-controls) are the method of choice for
\par
studies of social invention (Campbell, Tatsuoka,
\par
Mosteller, Meehl), we must frequently face the need
\par
to do the best we can with nonexperimental data. (p.
\par
68)
\par
He formulates the general problem as that of identify-
\par
ing the cause(s) of some specified phenomenon or,
\par
more narrowly, testing the hypothesis that the project
\par
or innovation under evaluation was, in fact, the cause.138 TERRY CONNOLLY and ALAN L. PORTER
\par
He thus sees evaluation as an attempt to identify pro-
\par
bable causes of given phenomena and argues, as we do,
\par
that a rich understanding of the process and context of
\par
the innovation are prerequisite to such identification:
\par
``cause hunting, like lion hunting, is only likely to be
\par
successful if we have a considerable amount of relevant
\par
background knowledge'' (p. 69).
\par
Such background information, Striven argues, is
\par
valuable to the evaluator in two key ways. First, it may
\par
allow construction of a ``quasi-exhausitve causal list,''
\par
identifying essentially all the possible causes of the
\par
phenomenon which are plausible under the cir-
\par
cumstances. Second, it provides information on the
\par
characteristic causal chain which connects each can-
\par
didate cause with the observed effect - that is, the
\par
``modus operandi'' of each candidate cause. \&riven's
\par
use of the term ``modus operandi,'' a borrowing from
\par
police work, suggests the inferential methodology he is
\par
proposing. A knowledgeable detective may, from in-
\par
spection of the crime and knowledge of the habits of
\par
local criminals, be able to construct a ``quasi-
\par
exhaustive'' list of suspects for a given crime, and to
\par
narrow this list by comparing the ``modus operandi'' of
\par
each suspect with the details of the crime. Inferential
\par
methodologies of this sort are, Striven argues, common
\par
to historians, anthropologists, and engineering
\par
troubleshooters, as well as detectives. They may also
\par
prove adaptable to evaluation research.
\par
An alternative argument for essentially the same
\par
mode of inference is given by Campbell (1975) in his
\par
discussion of inference from case studies. In an
\par
acknowledged reversal of his earlier trenchant criticism
\par
of case studies (Campbell, 1961; Campbell \& Stanley,
\par
1966), he suggests that such studies can, in fact, provide
\par
rather solid grounds for inference of cause and effect.
\par
As with Striven's argument, the heart of Campbell's
\par
proposal is that one achieves confident inference only
\par
by having a hypothesis sufficiently rich that it entails
\par
multiple predictions, each of which is factually sup-
\par
ported. Where Striven draws on the ``modus operandi''
\par
of each plausible cause to provide these multiple
\par
checks, Campbell proposes rich and careful theory.
\par
Both seek within the single case the multiplicity of
\par
checks that the traditional methodologist finds in large
\par
sample sizes and multiple replications.
\par
Note that process tracing/modus operandi ap-
\par
proaches can vary enormously in scope of effort. They
\par
can be relatively ``quick and dirty'' alternatives to field
\par
experiments. However, carried to greater lengths, pro-
\par
cess tracing can demand great amounts of time and
\par
resources. In return it can give evaluation insights on
\par
program formulation much richer than the ``statistical
\par
assurance of success, or lack thereof'' of experimental
\par
designs.
\par
4. \emph{Half-A-Loaf Strategies. }One of the key points about
\par
the threats to internal validity is that most are countered
\par
by randomized designs. Unfortunately, it is often not
\par
possible to randomize individuals or groups for the
\par
whole project, particularly when the project is changing
\par
rapidly and the impacts are poorly understood.
\par
However, there may be a chance to do this, at the begin-
\par
ning or even toward the end of the project, on a small
\par
sample, and perhaps only on a subset of the more easily
\par
measured impact variables. Even a tiny true experiment
\par
(i.e, with randomized groups) may produce the com-
\par
pelling unambiguous evidence for real project impact
\par
that silences the critics and makes it possible to extend
\par
the project.
\par
If randomization is totally infeasible, it may still be
\par
possible to focus on parts of a project for evaluation
\par
gains. A stepwise perspective (partial evaluation at in-
\par
termediate stages) may fit well with decision schedules.
\par
Such incrementalism is highly desirable, given the oft-
\par
mentioned time-frame issues in the use of evaluations.
\par
\emph{5. Adversarial Review. }It is apparent that the output of
\par
an evaluation study utilizing some or all of these alter-
\par
native approaches will be rather different from the con-
\par
ventional acceptance or rejection of a hypothesis at
\par
some pre-established level of statistical significance.
\par
Rather, one is likely to have a somewhat chaotic
\par
assembly of evaluation-relevant information: interview
\par
transcripts; perhaps some questionnaire, observa-
\par
tional, or archival data; reports of program par-
\par
ticipants; and so on. The distillation of such
\par
heterogeneous evidence into a clear conclusion appears
\par
a task demanding considerable skill and judgment.
\par
In approaching this task, it may well be worthwhile to
\par
arrange an adversarial review of some sort. (Such pro-
\par
cesses have been utilized in areas such as strategic cor-
\par
porate planning, as well as in legal and political settings,
\par
with encouraging results. See, for example, Mason,
\par
1969; Mitroff and Betz, 1972). In essence, the pro-
\par
cedure would be to assign advocates to make the best
\par
case possible that the focal innovation did, or did not,
\par
have the effects claimed. Both advocates would have
\par
full access to all program-relevant information, and
\par
would be charged with the evaluation of the quality of
\par
each element of the evidence as well as with the con-
\par
struction of their ``case.'' We see considerable promise
\par
in such a procedure, both for presenting to the focal
\par
manager the range of evidence bearing on the evalua-
\par
tion, and for allowing him or her to reach an assessment
\par
of the confidence with which alternative conclusions
\par
are supported. (Some reservations are, however, ex-
\par
pressed by Popham and Carlson, 1977).
\par
In closing this section, we should re-emphasize that
\par
we are claiming neither great novelty nor general
\par
superiority for any of these methodological; sugges-
\par
tions. Elements of each have been described elsewhere,
\par
and each has weaknesses not shared by the true field ex-
\par
periment, where the latter is feasible. Our case is only
\par
that, in the context of user-focused evaluations,
\par
methodological flexibility is called for. In an ideal
\par
world, evaluations would invariably yield conclusionsUser-Focused Model for Utilization of Evaluation 139
\par
of unquestionable correctness over the entire range of impacts, in our findings. These suggestions are intend-
\par
possible program impacts. In the real world, we must ed to stimulate the effort to balance validity and utility
\par
often settle for less confidence, or a narrower range of in such an imperfect world.
\par
CONCLUDING OBSERVATIONS
\par
Our purpose in this article has been primarily to ad-
\par
vance two related notions. First, evaluations can, and
\par
perhaps should, be considered in terms of their direct
\par
impact on the choices of some specific decisionmaker,
\par
with their costs being justified in terms of their benefit
\par
to that individual. Second, this perspective implies a
\par
need for evaluation procedures which, while sacrificing
\par
some of the rigor of the best current practice, may
\par
nonetheless be justified in many settings by their
\par
relevance, low cost, fast feedback, and feasibility of im-
\par
plementation. We do not wish to argue for substituting
\par
our notions for what is currently excellent practice.
\par
Rather, we wish to \emph{extend the repertoire ofevaluation}
\par
\emph{options, }in the sense of suggesting that there may be a
\par
role for the manager-focused, private evaluation as well
\par
as for the broadly focused, public evaluation; and for
\par
the ``quick and dirty'' procedure, as well as for the re-
\par
fined, full-scale study. We seek, then, to extend rather
\par
than supplant existing practice.
\par
In one sense, we are arguing for a synthesis of what
\par
Hamblin (1972) identifies as the predominant
\par
American and European approaches to evaluation. The
\par
former, in Hamblin's analysis, tends to stress validity of
\par
conclusions, de-emphasizing questions of cost and
\par
timeliness of the results, while the latter tends to pro-
\par
duce less conclusive results but in decision making real-
\par
time. In discussing this analysis, Burgoyne and Cooper
\par
(1975, p. 54) suggest that both modes of evaluation
\par
have their place, the first generating information to
\par
``check, test and improve [the system's] assumptions
\par
about things which are enduringly true,'' the second
\par
generating ``up-to-date information on what is going on
\par
in the here-and-now of the particular situation.``The se-
\par
cond class of information is the goal of the manager-
\par
focused, private evaluations we have discussed.
\par
In considering, as we do, the use of evaluation find-
\par
ings as a feedback loop in a complex social system,
\par
Burgoyne and Cooper make a further interesting point.
\par
The selection of a mode for reporting evaluation find-
\par
ings implies either an empirical assessment or a nor-
\par
mative judgment on the part of the evaluator as to
\par
where control lies (or should lie) in the social system
\par
(s)he is evaluating: ``control is createdat the point where
\par
feedback becomes available or, in more everyday
\par
terms, information is power'' (Burgoyne \& Cooper,
\par
1975, p. 54). Thus, the conventional wide dissemination
\par
of findings in shotgun manner implies that the
\par
evaluator feels that control is (or should be) widely
\par
dispersed; reporting only to senior executives implies a
\par
more centralized view of control (and, by short-
\par
circuiting existing decision processes of the organiza-
\par
tion, implies an impatience with the subtleties of such
\par
processes). We, in contrast, are somewhat conservative
\par
about existing decision processes. Private, manager-
\par
focused evaluations would tend to leave intact the ex-
\par
isting structure of organizational decision processes,
\par
while raising the level of objective reality-testing on
\par
which such processes can operate. The intent is more
\par
refinement than revolution.
\par
Such refinement, clearly, requires both that
\par
evaluative information be gathered to the extent
\par
justified by subsequent decision stakes, and that the im-
\par
perfections of the information generated be borne in
\par
mind. In this context, it is worth reflecting on the sug-
\par
gestive findings of Gilbert, McPeek, and Mosteller
\par
(1977) in a quite different evaluative context (that of
\par
assessing the efficacy of surgical innovations). They
\par
report that uncontrolled studies may produce a level of
\par
optimism about an innovation that is later found by
\par
more careful study to be unjustified: ``one must be
\par
cautious in accepting results of weakly controlled in-
\par
vestigations'' (p. 687). Given such data on the possible
\par
positive bias in non-experimental methods of evalua-
\par
tion, we must be alert to the possibilities of overselling
\par
of innovations on the strength of preliminary findings.
\par
At the same time, it may well be that a degree of such
\par
positive bias may be socially useful, in that it would
\par
tend to encourage the development of promising in-
\par
novations to the point at which full-scale trial would be
\par
justified. Obviously, we cannot explore this speculation
\par
here. We would certainly agree that information
\par
gathered by non-experimental methods should be dis-
\par
counted to some extent, perhaps to a larger extent than
\par
one would initially expect. We would, however, argue
\par
that the discount should not be 100\%. Even weak infor-
\par
mation, if recognized as such, is useful in making deci-
\par
sions. After all, it is in most cases the only sort we have,
\par
or are likely to get.
