Abstract
\par
This paper argues that policies or recommendations regarding the provision of Information for
\par
employee decision making can only be fruitfully developed within the framework of models of
\par
employee decision making. Descriptive model building, incorporating a model of the decision maker,
\par
his objectives, and the environment in which he operates, is essential for the provision of information
\par
to help employees improve their welfare. The paper develops alternative models and concludes by
\par
presenting, as a guide to future research, some tentative evidence about the decision making of one
\par
group of employee representatives in plant level negotiation in the U.K.
\par
The purpose of this paper is to consider the
\par
information needs of one potential set of users of
\par
accounting information, namely employees. We
\par
intend to add to the limited amount of research
\par
that has considered the information needs of
\par
employees and unions and thereby aid in the
\par
development of ``external reporting models which
\par
satisfy not only the needs of investors and
\par
creditors but also employees, represented by their
\par
labour unions'' (Horwitz \& Shabahang, 1971,
\par
p. 224). There has been a growing realisation over
\par
the last few years that published accounts might
\par
be used by different sorts of people, having
\par
differing objectives.
\par
We are concerned with information for
\par
employees because we believe that organisations
\par
should be accountable not only to providers of
\par
finance but also (at the very least) to those who
\par
work in them (Medawar, 1976). Employees, unlike
\par
shareholders, have little opportunity to diversify
\par
the risks associated with their relationship with an
\par
organisation. Further, we perceive current develop-
\par
ments in the U.K. that tend to emphasise the
\par
``rights'' of employees to information both about
\par
the enterprise in which they work and about the
\par
corporate sector in general.'
\par
In Belgium, the Netherlands, Denmark, Norway
\par
and Sweden there exist detailed legal require-
\par
ments about the provision of information in order
\par
to facilitate effective employee participation in
\par
decision making. In the United States the case
\par
decisions of the National Labour Relations Board
\par
which have attempted to clarify the legal
\par
obligation to ``bargain in good faith'' are
\par
influential in extending the provision of informa-
\par
tion to employees. Similarly, the International
\par
J\_abour Organisation in its Recommendation 129
\par
suggests wide-ranging information disclosure to
\par
employees: ``the information should . . . include
\par
all matters of interest to workers relating to the
