\par
If accounting is concerned with the provision of
\par
information useful for decision making, then the
\par
accounting profession will be required to satisfy
\par
the informational needs of employees. Accounting
\par
does not develop in isolation from society.
\par
Similarly the provision of information for
\par
employees will be influenced by the legal and
\par
quasi-legal developments in a particular society.
\par
Accountants should be concerned with the
\par
provision of information to facilitate effective or
\par
desirable decisions; decisions that enable the
\par
decision makers (individual or organisation) to
\par
survive and prosper in its environment. The
\par
provision of information to employees is thus a
\par
particular example of the provision of useful
\par
information for decision making purposes. The
\par
``conventional'' exhortations about accounting
\par
information would therefore seem to be appli-
\par
cable. Information must be decision relevant; the
\par
information should influence actions and improve
\par
the behaviour and performance of employees and
\par
their unions. When data is unintelligible, out-of-
\par
date or unassociated with the problem at hand,
\par
then it cannot be regarded as valuable information.
\par
It is inevitable, therefore, that a consideration of
\par
information requirements will involve considera-
\par
tion of the decisions and problems for which the
\par
information is intended to be used. Accountants
\par
need not only to use models developed by other
\par
disciplines, but also need to be able to evaluate
\par
and develop these models. Given the lack of well
\par
developed and substantial models of employee
\par
decision making, decision making as well as
\par
information provision will be central concerns of
\par
this paper.
\par
In the section that follows we will examine the
\par
criteria for choice of information and explain our
\par
preference for the decision orientated approach to
\par
employee information needs. Thereafter we survey
\par
briefly some of the deductive models of employee
\par
decision making and the associated information
\par
requirements. For our purposes, these models are
\par
limited because of their divorce from reality and
\par
we thus consider, in the next section, the role of
\par
trade unions in society and the information that
\par
they would require to fulfil that role. Subse-
\par
quently we develop an \textbf{\emph{a priori }}decision model of
\par
shop stewards and present some tentative evidence
\par
as to the decisions made and information used by
\par
that particular set of employee representatives.
\par
Finally, we summarise our conclusions and
\par
indicate possible future research strategies.
\par
DECISION ORIENTATED APPROACH TO
\par
EMPLOYEE INFORMATION NEEDS
\par
It is well established that data, if they are to be
\par
of value, must be relevant (for example, Marshak,
\par
1959; Felltham, 1968). There are, however, at
\par
least two interpretations of the concept of
\par
relevance. The ``consumer sovereignty'' approach
\par
to the provision of information is concerned with
\par
servicing the desires or wants of the decision
\par
maker. It involves asking the consumer, in this
\par
case, employees or their representatives, what
\par
information he would find useful. The resulting
\par
shopping list is compared with the information
\par
currently provided and with the information lists
\par
provided by other ``relevant'' bodies. Political
\par
bargaining or lobbying results in some consensus
\par
about disclosure.
\par
The result of such negotiations is marginal
\par
adjustment to the \textbf{\emph{status quo. }}User demands for
\par
information will be based on perceptions of the
\par
sorts of information systems that are currently
\par
available. For example, studies that test the
\par
association between decision outputs (e.g. levels of
\par
wage) and decision inputs (e.g. accounting data)
\par
provide limited guidance about choice of informa-
\par
tion as they are constrained by the current
\par
environment of the decision taker (Horwitz \&
\par
Shabahang, 1971). For example, a union official
\par
may want historic cost based information to help
\par
him formulate his claim. His demand is more likely
\par
to be based on the perception that historic cost
\par
information can be provided than on a belief that
\par
such information will be helpful to him in
\par
satisfying his need to formulate a claim advan-
\par
tageous to his members.
\par
The second, and preferred, approach to the
\par
information choice problem may be called the
\par
decision orientated approach (Sterling, 1972).
\par
Information should be provided that is required as
\par
input to the decision model which enables a
\par
decision maker to satisfy his goals and result in an
\par
improvement in his welfare. The decision
\par
orientated approach suggests that we need to
\par
consider the decisions that employees or theirACCOUNTINCINFORMATIONANDEMPLOYEEDECISIONMAKING 203
\par
\textbf{\emph{representatives should take }}in order to satisfy their
\par
\textbf{\emph{objectives. }}The three emphases in the previous
\par
sentence are worthy of further consideration.
\par
Employees have tended for social, political and
\par
economic reasons to form unions to bargain over
\par
the nature of, and rewards for, their work (Webb
\par
\& Webb, 1920; Wootton, 1955). Unions are a
\par
collectivity whose purpose is to promote the
\par
welfare of the members of that organisation; a
\par
function that can best be achieved by combination
\par
and concerted, unified action. Most employees
\par
delegate their decision making associated with
\par
bargaining to the union organisation. The nature
\par
of that organisation inevitably varies considerably
\par
between unions and between countries. We might
\par
generalise, however, to suggest that national
\par
decision making is dealt with by the national
\par
union officer and the national union organisation
\par
and regional, local and firm agreements involve
\par
union branch secretaries. A shop steward tends to
\par
be involved in firm and plant agreements as he is
\par
``a local union representative who has definite
\par
responsibility for the first stage of local negotia-
\par
tions, but is neither a full time officer nor a branch
\par
secretary with recognised negotiating rights in that
\par
capacity'' (Clegg \textbf{\emph{et al., }}1961, p. 180). In assessing
\par
the information that will be relevant to employee
\par
representatives we must therefore specify the
\par
particular representatives concerned. Further, the
\par
decision orientated approach to the provision of
\par
information suggests the need to consider the
\par
decisions that the identified representatives should
\par
take. The objectives of the decision maker are
\par
clearly relevant to this normative approach to
\par
decision making.
\par
Since we are concerned with employees, their
\par
objectives and the objectives of their representa-
\par
tives it is necessary to consider the congruence
\par
between the objectives of employees and those of
\par
the union and its officials (including shop
\par
stewards). Clearly, an assumption of goal
\par
consensus would be a naive one. Many union
\par
officials are concerned with maintaining and
\par
enhancing their careers within the labour move-
\par
ment. Nevertheless we consider that union officials
\par
are likely to act in the interests of their members.
\par
There may be occasions for disagreement about
\par
the appropriate means to achieve their members'
\par
best interests and some officials may be
\par
disproportionately concerned with their own
\par
sectional interests. Nevertheless the relationship
\par
between an official and his constituents frequently
\par
rests on a voting nexus, not a cash one. In
\par
situations where there is a longstanding dis-
\par
agreement about objectives, then either the
\par
membership leaves the union (and joins an
\par
alternative union) or the union officials leave the
\par
membership (and are replaced by officials who are
\par
more representative of the members' wishes).
\par
The objectives of any individual or organisa-
\par
tion involves both intention (as indicated for
\par
example, by official statements) and a commit-
\par
ment to satisfy that intention (as evidenced, for
\par
example, by an allocation of resources). Accord-
\par
ingly, we must look beyond official statements of
\par
objectives to the allocation of resources within
\par
unions. Indeed use of the decision orientated
\par
approach requires some consideration of what the
\par
allocations ``should be''. Considerations of ``should
\par
be'' necessitates an understanding of the role and
\par
functions of unions in our society and in changing
\par
that society.
\par
Consider the likely elements of an employee's
\par
utility function. We might suggest that economic
\par
rewards, job security and job satisfaction represent
\par
important elements of employee utility. Without
\par
evidence about the outcomes that employees
\par
regard as desirable, this list of components of a
\par
utility function may only be regarded as
\par
tentative. We suggest as an initial hypothesis that
\par
union officials therefore aim to achieve either
\par
increased pay or job security (including long term
\par
tenure of the job and safety) or job satisfaction
\par
(which may include satisfactory working condi-
\par
tions, relationships with peers and superiors as well
\par
as opportunities for achievement, recognition and
\par
self-fulfilment), or some combination of these
\par
elements.
\par
However tentative the objective function that
\par
we derive, two issues are clear. Firstly employee
\par
objectives cannot be ``boiled down'' to a notion of
\par
maximisation of wages. ``Labour is more than a
\par
commodity because it cannot be isolated from the
\par
life of the labouref (Flanders, 1968). The work
\par
situation cannot be isolated from the general life
\par
of the employee and thus social relations cannot
\par
be ignored in the specification of employee
\par
objectives. Unions and their officials have broad
\par
organisational, political, and social objectives as
\par
well as a concern to improve the economic
\par
security and status of their members (for example,
\par
see Trade Union Congress, 1966, paragraphs
\par
97-98).
\par
The second point is that the satisfaction of
\par
employee objectives may be most profitably
\par
pursued in terms of changing the economic, social204 DAVID COOPER and SIMON ESSEX
\par
and political status quo. Changes in the balance of
\par
power between classes in a society or indeed
\par
changes to the organisation of a society might be
\par
legitimate union activities. Employee representa-
\par
tives, in order to improve the welfare of their
\par
members, may be concerned with wage levels, job
\par
security, working conditions, industrial and
\par
community health, industrial democracy, national
\par
income and the quality of life. These ``political''
\par
issues are not avoided by refusing to explicitly
\par
consider them; such a failure provides tacit
\par
approval for the existing role of unions and their
\par
officials. Unlike the consumer sovereignty
\par
approach to the provision of information, the
\par
decision orientated approach focuses on satisfying
\par
the needs of the users rather than servicing the
\par
users' demands. The approach does require,
\par
however, careful consideration .of the needs and
\par
the objectives of the decision maker.
\par
These objectives cannot be pursued indepen-
\par
dently of other groups in society: suppliers,
\par
customers, providers of tinance and creators of the
\par
infrastructure for the nation are all relevant.
\par
Employee representatives cannot in the long run
\par
pursue policies that completely alienate customers
\par
or investors or any other group associated with an
\par
organisation; the organisation will cease to exist.
\par
This interdependence requires that models of
\par
employee decision making must take account of
\par
bargaining behaviour. The decision orientated
\par
approach necessitates the identification of decision
\par
models that relate action on behalf of the decision
\par
maker to his objectives.
\par
DEDUCTlVE MODELS OF EMPLOYEE
\par
DECISION MAKING AND THE ROLE OF
\par
INFORMATION THEREIN
\par
Accounting - and industrial relations - have
\par
tended to look to economics for decision models
\par
relevant to its concerns. Increasingly, however, the
\par
limitations of most simple economic models have
\par
become apparent in industrial relations - and to a
\par
lesser extent in accounting. A model is a
\par
representation of the world (or an aspect of it) and
\par
many economic models represent the world as
\par
being capable of accurate and complete representa-
\par
tion. Classical economic analysis is frequently
\par
based on a decision model that states that choices
\par
(or actions) are made by individuals so as to
\par
maximise their subjective expected utility (SEU).
\par
Such an analysis involves the identification of all
\par
possible sources of action, assigning a value (or
\par
utility) to each possible outcome associated with
\par
an action and assessing the probability of such
\par
outcomes occurring. The action with the highest
\par
expected utility is then chosen.
\par
Consider the simple (and inevitably simple
\par
minded) example of how a union negotiator might
\par
formulate a claim, as summarised in Table 1. The
\par
negotiator, in order to determine a pay claim may
\par
identify three possible courses of action: to
\par
demand a pay rise of \&8 per week, a pay rise of \&6
\par
per week or a pay rise of \&4 per week. These are
\par
seen as the only possibilities; a rise of below 24 is
\par
totally unacceptable to his members, a rise of
\par
above Q3 is totally unacceptable to the employer,2
