\par
There are basically two functions for informa-
\par
tion in this basic S.E.U. model. First a decision
\par
maker requires information to construct the model
\par
- to identify the outcomes and assess their
\par
associated utilities and probabilities. In our
\par
example, the union official would value informa-
\par
tion relating to the possible outcomes and their
\par
likelihood of occurrence. This information is likely
\par
to include details of the enterprise's ability to pay
\par
and the attitudes of his membership.
\par
The second function of information in the
\par
S.E.U. model \emph{is }to provide feedback to the
\par
decision maker in order that he may revise his
\par
model. In our example, the results of recent
\par
decisions could be combined (e.g. using Bayes
\par
theorem) with the original model specification in
\par
order to produce a more accurate model. More
\par
details, and elegant, consideration of the role of
\par
information in the S.E.U. model is contained in
\par
Demski (1972) and Feltham (1973).
\par
This type of deductive analysis can be made
\par
more sophisticated and perhaps more relevant by
\par
introducing the bargaining aspect of decision
\par
making. Game theory is concerned with decision
\par
situations where results are dependent on the
\par
inter-actions of two or more ``players'', Thus far,
\par
however, decision theorists have concentrated on
\par
analysing situations when the game is concerned to
\par
divide, by co-operative means, a fixed sum. The
\par
theory of games is largely undeveloped in the area
\par
of non-constant sum, non co-operative games (for
\par
example, see Coddington, 1968).
\par
Yet employee decision making is not likely to
\par
occur in co-operative situations where the
\par
bargaining is about the share of the cake. The size
\par
of the sum to be distributed will be a decision
\par
variable; the size of the cake will be influenced by
\par
the bargain struck.
\par
The recognition of a bargaining and inter-active
\par
aspect of decision making does, however, intro-
\par
duce a third role for information. In the non-
\par
co-operative, non-constant sum case it will often
\par
pay a player to publicise his plans. Information
\par
may be used as part of the bargaining process in
\par
order, for example, to influence other players'
\par
perceptions of the payoffs (ScheIling, 1960). The
\par
threat of a strike is intended to influence the
\par
employer's payoff expectations. Information may
\par
also be used to reduce the non-co-operative nature
\par
of the situation. The announcement of a decline in
\par
demand for a product or a cash crisis in a company
\par
may encourage co-operative bargaining behaviour.
\par
The relative sophistication of game theory and
\par
the formal elegance of the S.E.U. model cannot
\par
hide at least four difficulties of this approach.4
\par
The measurement of utility is crucial to these
\par
types of analyses. It seems inappropriate to regard
\par
monetary values as adequate surrogates for the
\par
political, sociological and psychological dimen-
\par
sions of employees' utilities. Despite the efforts of
\par
decision theorists, there is still no operational
\par
single measure of utility (see, for example, Hull ef
\par
al., 1973).
\par
The second difficulty with our normative
\par
approach is that the union official is a
\par
representative of his members and will be required
\par
to satisfy a variety of possibly inconsistent desires.
\par
Theories of choice by collectivities have been
\par
relatively unfruitful as yet. Morris (1974) and
\par
Cohen et \emph{al. }(1972) indicate the diversity of
\par
approaches. Somehow, however, the union official
\par
will have to synthesise individual desires into a
\par
practical bargaining strategy.
\par
The third difficulty which we consider is
\par
associated with the time taken to make decisions.
\par
The dynamics of the decision making process
\par
means that the probabilities of the identified
\par
outcomes, the utility associated with each
\par
outcome and the range of alternative actions will
\par
be constantly changing. Models that recognise the
\par
time dimension of the decision making process
