\par
\textbf{the set of outcomes were continuous.}
\par
\textbf{`For a comprehensive survey of these deductive approaches to bargaining theory see Foley \& Maunders (1977),}
\par
\textbf{especially Chapter 4.}206 are essential.
\par
Finally, models of decision making based on
\par
the maximisation of subjective utility seem to lack
\par
predictive ability when complex choices are
\par
involved. A great deal of evidence in psychology
\par
suggests that due to cognitive limitations, man
\par
cannot behave in the way suggested by statistical
\par
decision theory (Tversky \& Kahnemann, 1974). In
\par
particular man is a conservative processor of
\par
information, anchoring his decisions on past
\par
evidence and adjusting only marginally when new
\par
information is presented (Slavic, 1972). Organisa-
\par
tions also have limited information processing
\par
capacity (Williamson, 1967; Galbraith, 1973).
\par
DESCRIPTIVE MODELS OF EMPLOYEE
\par
DECISION MAKING
\par
The deductive models of decision making
\par
represent decision makers as sophisticated statis-
\par
ticians and their environment as exceedingly
\par
simple, capable of being completely and accurately
\par
represented and predicted. Since we are ultimately
\par
interested in the provision of information to
\par
employees and their representatives we are
\par
concerned with practical, real world phenomena.
\par
To make useful prescriptions for information
\par
provision we are therefore concerned to accurately
\par
describe the real world and how decisions are
\par
actually taken. We therefore concentrate on a
\par
descriptive model of decision making, based on the
\par
idea that individuals and organisations are
\par
intentionally rational but limited by the com-
\par
plexity of the environment in which they operate
\par
(Simon, 1957). This idea of bounded rationality,
\par
which derives from experiments to determine how
\par
man actually makes decisions, tends to emphasise
\par
the difficulties of specifying the attributes in the
\par
detail required by the S.E.U. model. Thus a
\par
descriptive model tends to concentrate on ``a
\par
description of the choice process that recognises
\par
that alternatives are not given but must be sought;
\par
and a description that takes into account the
\par
arduous task of determining what consequences
\par
will follow on each alternative'' (Simon, 1959).
\par
Observation of how decisions are actually made
\par
suggests that a decision maker will attempt to
\par
achieve a satisfactory outcome through the use of
\par
simple decision rules, ``rules of thumb'' that enable
\par
a decision maker to cope with the complexity of
\par
his environment .
\par
Models of decision making based on bounded
\par
DAVID COOPER and SIMON ESSEX
\par
rationality involve the following observations. A
\par
``problem'' requiring solution does not appear
\par
from nowhere: the nature of problem recognition
\par
needs careful consideration in any theory of
\par
decision making (Pounds, 1969). Solutions to a
\par
problem are often inherent in the way the problem
\par
is identified. A union official's recognition, for
\par
example, that the union members need more
\par
money, implies the ``solution'' of obtaining more
\par
money. Re-phrasing the problem as one of a
\par
declining standard of living opens up the solutions
\par
of price controls, tax adjustments and a general
\par
enquiry into the causes of the decline (and hence
\par
an enquiry into the issue of who benefits from
\par
decisions taken in the ``national interest'').
\par
Search for solutions and identification of the
\par
consequences that might result from any solution
\par
is normally severely limited. Decision making does
\par
not involve a ranking of all alternatives. Alterna-
\par
tives have to be found and generally the first
\par
alternative action that seems to be satisfactory will
\par
be carried out. The search for a satisfactory
\par
alternative will be based on experience of past
\par
decision making. Learning is crucial in an
\par
environment that is rapidly changing - the past, in
\par
such circumstances, is not a good predictor of the
\par
future. Adaptive decision models (and information
\par
necessary for these models) are required in rapidly
\par
changing conditions.
\par
Although general\&d models of decision
\par
making in organisations have been developed (e.g.
\par
Ference, 1970) decision models specific to
\par
employees and their representatives do not seem
\par
to be well developed. It is not clear whether the
\par
environment within which they make decisions is
\par
rapidly or slowly changing. To resolve these sorts
\par
of problems will require research into decision
\par
making by employees and their representatives.
\par
There is an urgent need, if accountants are to
\par
provide relevant information, to investigate models
\par
of bounded rationality in a complex and changing
\par
environment than have as their purpose, the
\par
improvement of the welfare of workers.
\par
For ease of exposition we may distinguish three
\par
levels or inter-connected systems in which union
\par
officials are involved in decision making designed
\par
to result in outcomes that will affect the welfare
\par
of employees. The three systems - national,
\par
industrial and plant - are clearly inter-dependent.
\par
Their relative importance in a society will depend
\par
on historical and institutional features of that
\par
society.
\par
In most Western societies, unions negotiate
