\par
We shall consider the role of the shop steward
\par
in terms of the functions or jobs he is expected or
\par
expects to fulfil (Katz \& Kahn, 1966). For each
\par
of the functions identified, we indicate the
\par
decisions likely to be taken and the information
\par
required to make these decisions. Table 2
\par
summarises the activities of shop stewards and
\par
indicates the type of information such decisions
\par
might call for.`j This table should not be regarded
\par
as an all embracing statement of roles, decisions to
\par
be made or information required. It attempts to
\par
indicate, \textbf{\emph{a priori, }}the items of information that are
\par
likely to be of importance to a shop steward.
\par
Many of these items will be used for a number of
\par
decisions and could be valuable to shop stewards.
\par
Yet, other items of information may have little
\par
value and act, at best, as confirmatory messages,
\par
and at worst, as ``noise''. In other words, the costs
\par
of information production and usage have been
\par
ignored in the table.
\par
Traditionally shop stewards have been regarded
\par
as union administrators, acting as a communica-
\par
tion channel between the union and management.
\par
The decisions associated with this role involve
\par
inter-union disputes, recruitment drives and
\par
methods of handling individual grievances. The
\par
information necessary to perform such tasks and
\par
make these decisions relates to details of union
\par
(and non-union) membership; statistics summaris-
\par
ing the membership in terms of numbers, density,
\par
category of worker and relative position compared
\par
with other unions in a large multi-union plant or
\par
firm.
\par
A key area of shop steward activity is pay
\par
negotiations. The flexibility of national agree-
\par
ments has enabled an increasing number of topics
\par
to be brought within the legitimate scope of
\par
workplace bargaining - legitimate, since it was
\par
impossible for them to be settled elsewhere. These
\par
topics included incentive payments, special pay-
\par
ment for dirty or dangerous work, merit
\par
allowances and grading for particular skills, job
\par
evaluation, overtime, clothing allowances and so
\par
on. Thus, areas which had previously been
\par
management prerogatives have come within the
\par
ambit of joint regulation. In the formulation and
\par
negotiation of a pay claim, a steward may have to
\par
decide the amount of the claim. This amount will
\par
be influenced by the amount that the firm could
\par
afford, the minimum that his members would
\par
accept, the strength of the workers' case if
\par
opposition is met, the appropriateness of incentive
\par
payments offered by management, and relative
\par
difficulty (or danger, or dirtiness) of different jobs
\par
and the bonus which a particular job merits and
\par
the time it should take. A steward would also
\par
decide on the method of presenting, and extent to
\par
which he should press, the claim.
\par
To assist his decisions, the shop steward would
\par
require information relating to the organisation's
\par
ability to pay the claim and information related to
\par
the sum that is minimally acceptable to his
\par
constituents. Ability to pay refers to the
\par
enterprise's future capacity to pay a certain level
\par
of wages and remain viable. Information relating
\par
to the future is therefore relevant. For the
\par
workplace bargaining that a shop steward is likely
\par
to be involved in, the relevant enterprise will be
\par
the plant. Information of a disaggregated nature,
\par
relating to the cost and revenue structure of the
\par
specific plant, will be relevant. Ability to pay may
\par
be regarded as the future cash flows of
\par
``the enterprise'' less those sums necessary to
\par
maintain the enterprise's operating capacity.
\par
Maunders (1977) provides further discussion of
\par
alternative measures of ability to pay. ``The
\par
enterprise'' will be the plant to the extent that the
\par
managers of the plant are autonomous to negotiate
\par
wages.
\par
The sum that is minimally acceptable to a shop
\par
steward's constituents is likely to depend on the
\par
employees' expectations regarding their future
\par
contribution to the ``value added'' of the plant,
\par
their expectations about rates of general price
\par
inflation and their expectations about their
\par
position relative to other workers in the locality
\par
and in similar plants elsewhere. Value added has
