In his role of a ``manager'', the shop steward is
\par
concerned about the efficiency of the plant's
\par
operation both in an economic and social sense.
\par
Both-the National Board for Prices and Incomes
\par
(1968) and the Government Social Survey (1968)
\par
found that shop stewards were increasingly dealing
\par
with health, safety, general working conditions,
\par
the pace and quality of work, manning, redun-
\par
dancy and recruitment. The shop steward is
\par
concerned with decisions about how the organisa-
\par
tion should be operated and the possibilities of
\par
plant closure or expansion. He is also concerned
\par
with the social performance of the organisation:
\par
he makes decisions about demands for health and
\par
safety expenditure as well as about the oppor-
\par
tunities for job satisfaction in the organisation.
\par
These types of management decision require
\par
information about economic plans (including
\par
proposed pricing, output, investment and
\par
financing policies) as well as accident, noise,
\par
turnover and absenteeism records.
\par
The messages that our u priori analysis has for
\par
the providers of information may be summarised
\par
as follows:
\par
(I) Information should be future orientated. To asses.\%
\par
an organisation's ability to pay, for example, the relevant
\par
concern in bargaining is the future ability of an
\par
organisation to pay a wage demand. To assess economic
\par
efficiency, future operating and investment plans of the
\par
organisation need to be considered. To assess social
\par
efficiency, the safety of machinery to be introduced must
\par
be evaluated. Such considerations and evaluations suggest
\par
that information should include an indication of the
\par
likelihood of various outcomes occurring in the future.
\par
(2) Information should relate to the specitic deci-
\par
sions. Thus plant level information is relevant to
\par
bargaining. Most published accounting information,
\par
however, relates to legal and not economic or sociaI
\par
entities. In short, it is not decision orientated.
\par
Organisations have been reluctant to disaggregate or
\par
``break down'' the information relating to the legal entity
\par
to information relating to the individual plants.
\par
(3) Information should relate to the environment of
\par
the organisation. Information about the level of claim
\par
that is regarded as the minimum acceptable requires
\par
consideration of wage opportunities outside the specific
\par
organisation. Future economic and social plans can only
\par
be assessed in the tight of the potential of the situation. It
\par
is highly probable that such information wiII not be
\par
purely fmanciak physical and social indicators are likely
\par
to be just as relevant to a shop steward's decisions as is
\par
financial information.
\par
Having identified as far as possible the
\par
information that might be required it seems
\par
appropriate to consider briefly the extent to which
\par
existing information supplies in the U.K. match up
\par
to these needs. Inevitably these supplies change
\par
over time and it is likely that current legislative
\par
proposals will increase the role of accounting
\par
information as a supplier of information.
\par
One source of information is the published
\par
accounts. Such accounts were never designed for
\par
use by employees and it is therefore not surprising
\par
that they have been criticised as being inadequate.
\par
For example, Maunders \& Foley (1974) have
\par
summarised the limitations of accounting reports
\par
for employee use. Accounts are frequently six
\par
months or more out of date when they are
\par
published, they relate to company rather than
\par
plant level information and they are primarily
\par
records of the past. Maunders and Foley also
\par
consider the limitations of accounting measure-
\par
ment conventions. This particular limitation will
\par
be reduced by the recommendations of the
\par
Inflation Accounting Committee (1975) and
\par
subsequent developments in the implementation
\par
of their scheme of replacement cost accounting.
\par
That Committee's concern with maintaining the
\par
operating capacity of the firm will be shared by
\par
many employees. Nevertheless, the complexity of
\par
accounts, the belief that they are susceptible to
\par
manipulation by management, their aggregated
\par
nature and their concentration on fmancial data
\par
which are only a part of likely information needs,
\par
results in the attitude that accounts are inadequate
\par
and hence, infrequently used (see Table 6).
\par
Various large companies in Britain also produce
\par
publications, often distributed to employees,
\par
which present some manpower statistics (e.g.
\par
numbers employed, labour turnover, industrial
\par
disputes and wage statistics). An increasing
\par
number of companies also produce for their
\par
workers simplified versions of their published
\par
accounts. Yet, as long as the nature of the
\par
information supplied is determined by manage-
\par
ment and the methods of collections and
\par
measurement of the various indices are poorly
\par
specified, then it is likely that the documents will
\par
be regarded as managerial propaganda.'
\par
