Some \textbf{\emph{evidence about the shop steward's role,}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{decision making and information requirements}}
\par
The discussion of the role of the shop steward
\par
has assumed that there is a progression from the
\par
specifications of functions to the specification of
\par
associated decisions and then the specification of
\par
the decisions' information requirements. We trust
\par
the logical validity of our analysis is clear; the
\par
progression has been based on the belief that shop
\par
stewards wish to improve their constituents'
\par
welfare and the decisions they will make will be
\par
influenced by that wish. Implicit in the progres-
\par
sion has been a model of how the information
\par
provided will enable the shop steward to
\par
effectively discharge his role. Our analysis can only
\par
be empirically validated by confrontation with the
\par
real world. It is therefore necessary to subject the
\par
analysis to some empirical testing. We will
\par
concentrate first on the accuracy of our
\par
description of the work of shop stewards, secondly
\par
on the information which stewards receive and use
\par
at present and thirdly on what further information
\par
shop stewards think they would like. Existing
\par
empirical work in these areas is sparse and
\par
accordingly we have been forced to restrict our
\par
analysis to data obtained from three main sources:
\par
(1) The Government Social Survey (1968)
\par
of Workplace Industrial Relations.
\par
(2) The same work repeated in 1972 and
\par
published two years later (Parker, 1974).
\par
(3) A pilot questionnaire survey covering shop
\par
stewards in the engineering industry in the
\par
Manchester area (Essex, 1975).
\par
The common features of these studies are an
\par
assessment of the factors which stewards regard as
\par
most useful in acting in their members' interests
\par
and a measure of the key areas of a shop steward's
\par
job. The first survey was based on interviews in
\par
1966 with 1200 shop stewards, randomly selected
\par
from six unions. That survey's most significant
\par
finding for our purpose is reproduced as Table 3.
\par
There was considerable consensus amongst all
\par
the parties who were interviewed (that is,
\par
stewards, foremen, works managers, and personnel
\par
officers) that the most common arguments
\par
advanced by stewards in workplace bargaining
