conditions and the nature of the job, together with
\par
comparative information, would be useful.
\par
The 1972 survey (Parker, 1974) indicates the
\par
functions of the shop steward. Table 4 indicates
\par
the range of issues of concern to shop stewards
\par
and with whom they negotiated (or whether the
\par
issue was regulated by collective agreement). It
\par
would seem that collective bargaining sets the
\par
basic guidelines for shop steward activity. Shop
\par
stewards seem to concentrate on what we have
\par
previously referred to as the ``management of the
\par
enterprise'' especially its social performance.
\par
The two Government Social Survey studies
\par
only indirectly provide relevant information about
\par
the role of the shop stewards. Accordingly we
\par
surveyed a sample of 230 shop stewards who were
\par
members of the Amalgamated Union of Engineer-
\par
ing Workers in the Greater Manchester area.' The
\par
results of this survey must be used very carefully
\par
and its conclusions regarded as tentative as the
\par
study achieved only a 24.8\% response rate and
\par
both the sample selected and the questionnaire
\par
design are limited. It would be improper to draw
\par
any general conclusions from a study concen-
\par
trating on shop stewards from one industry in one
\par
geographical location at one point in time. The
\par
possibility that shop stewards in different unions
\par
and even the same union in different locations
\par
(having varying local traditions) may perform
\par
other roles should not be overlooked. We are also
\par
unable to determine the representativeness of our
\par
own sample of engineering stewards to the
\par
population of engineering stewards in Manchester.
\par
A forced choice questionnaire, such as the one
\par
used in our survey involves the researcher coding
\par
the responses for the respondent. In other words,
\par
our image of reality has been imposed on the
\par
respondents by the questionnaire. Forced choice
\par
answers also tend to ``prompt'' the respondent,
\par
particularly when the respondent is asked about
\par
information which he has probably never received.
\par
These limitations suggest that data about the
\par
shop steward's role should also be collected by
\par
observational methods. Until such data can be
\par
compared with our own, it would be unwise to
\par
regard our survey as anything other than a highly
\par
tentative indication of the roles, decisions made
\par
and information requirements of one subset of all
\par
shop stewards. It would be erroneous to conclude
\par
that the results indicate anything other than public
\par
attitudes. The difference between these attitudes
\par
and subsequent actual behaviour has also been
\par
documented many times (see the collection edited
\par
by Thomas, 1971).
