\par
Table 7 summarises the responses of the shop
\par
stewards when they were asked to evaluate
\par
possible factors used to justify a pay claim. We
\par
might interpret items (a), (b), (d), (e) and (f) as
\par
the information the shop steward uses to assess the
\par
likely demand of his constituents. Items (c), (g),
\par
(h), (i) and (j) might similarly be interpreted as the
\par
information used to assess the enterprise's ability
\par
to pay. Note that items (i) and (j) are ranked
\par
higher when the mean rather than the total score is
\par
used.
\par
When the stewards were asked to rank the
\par
important justifying factors used for pay claims in
\par
1970, the only significant difference was that the
\par
cost of living item ranked ninth, rather than the
\par
third rank in 1975. This difference probably
\par
reflects the increased rate of inflation at the later
\par
date. The lower ranking is in line with the findings
\par
of the earlier Government Social Survey (1968).
\par
Indeed the two studies agree, by and large, on the
\par
relative importance of most factors. The major
\par
difference between our results and those of the
\par
Government Social Survey (1968) is the impor-
\par
tance of the firm's profits in our study. Perhaps
\par
during the period between the two surveys shop
\par
stewards have come to regard accounting profits as
\par
a good predictor of a firm's willingness to pay a
\par
wage claim.
\par
The final question represents a departure from
\par
earlier empirical work. Stewards were asked to
\par
assess the importance of -information which they
\par
might receive in the future. As can be seen from
\par
Table 8, the three items of information which
\par
relate to the future (namely expectations about
