\par
Together, these account for the expressed views of
\par
69.5\% of those who replied, which is a forceful
\par
case for the disclosure. Although indications of
\par
what shop stewards want is not necessarily a good
\par
indicator of what they need to effectively
\par
discharge their role, it seems that a re-orientation
\par
of accounting information would be of benefit to
\par
these potential users.
\par
The results of our survey appear to be
\par
confirmed by previous studies and in large measure
\par
correspond with the summary of shop steward
\par
decision making and information requirements
\par
presented in Table 2. Table 5 suggests that
\par
functions of pay bargaining and management of
\par
the enterprise (especially working conditions and
\par
job security) dominate the negotiating aspects of
\par
the shop steward's job. Table 6 indicates the
\par
current role of published accounting information.
\par
A comparison of Table 7 and Table 8 suggests that
\par
the information requested by shop stewards does
\par
not fully match their needs as indicated in Table 7.
\par
In general, though, our findings do not lead us to
\par
substantially modify Table 2. We should emphasise
\par
again, however, that Table 2 should be regarded as
\par
a guideline in observation of shop stewards'
\par
decision behaviour, and is not a substitute for
\par
fundamental consideration of the decisions shop
\par
stewards do take and should take to improve the
\par
welfare of their constituents.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
In this paper we have developed an approach to
\par
the identification of information relevant to
\par
employees and their representatives. The notion of
\par
relevance requires, we maintain, the identification
\par
of users' needs as opposed to his wants.
\par
information relevant to an employee representa-
\par
tive or a union official will relate to the decision
\par
model that he should be using in an attempt to
\par
improve the welfare of workers. We considered the
\par
use of a deductive model based on the idea that
\par
individuals act \textbf{SO as to }maximise their subjective
\par
expected utility. Such a model (or class of model)
\par
does not possess the realism to form the basis of
\par
information prescriptions.
\par
A model of how employee representatives make
\par
decisions and how they ought to make decisions in
\par
their membership's best interests is a pre-requisite
\par
for any recommendation about the provision of
\par
information to employees. If our analysis is
\par
accepted, then it is essential that accountants
\par
become involved in research to identify union and
\par
employee decision models. Regrettably many
\par
recent approaches (e.g. Tylecote, 1975; Climo,
\par
4976; Halfpenny \& Abell, 1977; and Tylecote,
\par
1977) concentrate on economic variables and can
\par
thus only be regarded as partial models of
\par
employee decision making.
\par
As an interim measure, and not as an alternative
\par
to the development of employee decision models
\par
and specification of information needs, the idea of
\par
providing employee representatives with access to
\par
an organisation's fmancial records has considerable
\par
appeal (see Coates \& Topham, 1974; Cooper,
\par
1977). Opening the books would temporarily
\par
avoid the need to specify decision models for
\par
employee representatives. Such a move might also
\par
allay the fears of some union officials about the
\par
deliberate distortion of information provided by
\par
management.
\par
The previous section of this paper has presented
\par
some evidence about how one type of employee
\par
representative seems to make decisions. By
\par
providing an initial specification of the types of
\par
decision making the shop steward makes, we have
\par
intended to provide some guidance for future
\par
research, perhaps based on direct observation.
\par
Finally we wish, on the basis of the empirical
\par
research presented above, to make one or
\par
two speculations about developments in account-
\par
ing practice. We have suggested that shop stewards
\par
are likely to find disaggregated data about the
\par
future performance of the plant more useful than
\par
aggregated data. Company information has limited
\par
relevance in settling local pay claims. This is most
\par
obvious in the case of multi-national companies
\par
where ``local'' pay claims may in fact be national
\par
claims. Shop stewards are also likely to find
\par
information relating to future events more useful
\par
than information relating to past events.
\par
Note however, that this information need not
\par
be financial and may not relate to the enterprise
\par
under consideration. We have suggested, for216 DAVID COOPER and SIMON ESSEX
\par
example, that information about union density in
\par
the plant may be relevant to decisions associated
\par
with all three roles of the shop stewards. If
\par
accountants are indeed information providers,
\par
then there should be little concern with his
\par
extension of their traditional function.
\par
The belief that information about the world
\par
outside the plant or enterprise will be useful to
\par
shop stewards also has implications for the
\par
function of the accountant. The provision of
\par
information about the local cost of living, job
\par
opportunities in the area and agreements made in
\par
other plants suggests that the information provider
\par
should produce reports relevant for decisions
\par
\textbf{\emph{about an }}enterprise rather than reports on an
\par
enterprise. We thus return to the idea that
\par
information for a decision model, rather than
\par
either information for a decision maker or
\par
information on a particular enterprise, should be
\par
provided by accountants.
