\par
iiExecutive Summary
\par
This report contains three related papers. The first concerns the process for
\par
making decisions regarding what research will be undertaken. The second
\par
describes a procedure for evaluating proposals. The last reports on a web site
\par
intended to bring the information gained from actual ITS implementation
\par
together so that potential ITS implementers can learn from the experience of
\par
others.
\par
A Structure for Making Decisions Regarding Caltrans/PATH Research
\par
A key determinant of PATH's success in helping Caltrans utilize advanced
\par
technology to improve the transportation system is the choice of which research
\par
to pursue. PATH research does not take place in a vacuum, but rather in the
\par
context of society's needs, technological capabilities, and research that has
\par
already been conducted. The choice of research to pursue can be thought of as
\par
being located in a cycle in which the real world influences research, which in turn
\par
influences the real world.
\par
Making good decisions regarding PATH research requires identifying
\par
transportation goals, determining where there are opportunities to move closer to
\par
these goals, determining the state of research in advanced transportation systems,
\par
and assessing PATH's particular strengths. These inform the first decision,
\par
which is to set the research agenda. This agenda is communicated to potential
\par
research program participants via a Request for Proposals. The next decision is
\par
which of the proposed projects to include in the research program. This decision
\par
is based not only on research needs but on the quality of the proposals. The
\par
procedure for review and evaluation of proposals, PREATRP, is the subject of the
\par
second paper in this report.
\par
The study found that the current process lacks clear linkage to the needs of the
\par
end users of research. The research agenda and proposals do not focus
\par
sufficiently on the benefits of the research to travelers, and lines of research are
\par
not prioritized in terms of their ultimate benefits.
\par
We recommend that the research program development:
\par
{$\bullet$} begin with explicit consideration of the goals of the transportation system
\par
and how PATH research can support those goals,
\par
{$\bullet$} give more attention to Caltrans' needs and priorities,
\par
{$\bullet$} pay more attention to moving research into practice, and
\par
{$\bullet$} bring greater rigor to the research management process.
\par
PREATRP: Procedure for Review and Evaluation of Advanced
\par
Transportation Research Proposals
\par
Proposals should be evaluated not only in terms of the elegance of the concept
\par
and method of study, but also on the product of the research. Researchersiiitend to focus on the former, while potential implementers, in this case
\par
Caltrans, focus on the latter. PREATRP was developed to address both of
\par
these criteria. It is designed for use by PATH peer reviewers and Caltrans
\par
staff. Proposals are evaluated in terms of seven criteria:
\par
{$\bullet$} Significance and relevance to PATH and Caltrans goals and
\par
mission
\par
{$\bullet$} Research methodology
\par
{$\bullet$} Quality of research plan
\par
{$\bullet$} Qualifications of research team
\par
{$\bullet$} Budget
\par
{$\bullet$} Utility of research outcome
\par
{$\bullet$} Feasibility of implementation of research outcome
\par
Within these categories are subcategories for which reviewers provide a
\par
numeric score. Reviewers are also asked to provide a recommendation
\par
regarding funding and research priority. They are also strongly encouraged to
\par
provide comments, which are passed on to the author of the proposal.
\par
Two or three academic experts and a similar number of practitioners review
\par
each proposal. Then PATH management and Caltrans New Technology and
\par
Research management separately make preliminary decisions regarding which
\par
proposals will be included in the research program for the following year.
\par
Together they make the final decision.
\par
LEAP: Learning from Evaluation and Analysis of Performance
\par
LEAP is a web site designed to support decisions regarding ITS
\par
implementation and research by making information about the performance of
\par
intelligent transportation systems more accessible. It analyzes and
\par
summarizes what has been learned from field operational tests, other research,
\par
and implementation. Information is organized to suit a range of users. For the
\par
policy maker, there are summary-level descriptions of the technology,
\par
operation, and status of the primary ITS services. For the local public works
\par
engineer or planner, there is more detailed information on specific project
\par
evaluations and research summaries. For those seeking even greater detail,
\par
references and links to additional on- and off-line resources are provided.
\par
The web site currently contains information about Advanced Traffic
\par
Management Systems (ATMS)---traffic surveillance, incident detection,
\par
incident clearance, ramp metering, traffic management centers, and electronic
\par
toll collection. It also contains information about Advanced Traveler
\par
Information Systems (ATIS), including pre-trip information, en-route
\par
information, route guidance, and rideshare matching. It includes Commercial
\par
ivVehicle Operations Systems (CVO) and Advanced Public Transportation
\par
Systems (APTS), including automated vehicle location and advanced fare
\par
payment. It also includes longitudinal collision avoidance. Sections on
\par
variable message signs, probe vehicles, and highway-rail intersection
\par
management are currently under development and will be added soon.
\par
Information is gathered from published sources: project evaluation reports,
\par
research reports, journals, and conference proceedings. Once published
\par
information on all ITS services has been posted to the site, unpublished
\par
information will be sought.
\par
A set of analytical tools to help users determine which ITS services to
\par
implement and how to implement them is planned for the future. This portion
\par
of the project draws heavily on research and design undertaken in connection
\par
with PLANiTS, a computer assisted planning tool envisioned by PATH.
\par
The web site has been visited over 8000 times during it first two years.
