viiiA Structure for Making Decisions
\par
Regarding Caltrans/PATH
\par
Research
\par
\emph{Joy Dahlgren and John Lathrop}
\par
Introduction
\par
A key determinant of PATH's success in helping Caltrans utilize advanced
\par
technology to improve California's transportation system is the choice of what
\par
research to pursue. One of the deliverables for MOU 209 is a framework for
\par
making this choice and a white paper tying this framework to overall
\par
transportation goals. This paper provides both. It begins with a discussion of
\par
the environment in which research decisions are made. This includes the
\par
goals of the transportation system, the problems and opportunities that arise
\par
within the system, the state of ITS research, and PATH's particular strengths
\par
and interests. The second part of the paper is devoted to the process by which
\par
decisions regarding PATH's research program are made. First the current
\par
process is described, including changes that have resulted or are underway as
\par
a result of MOU 209. It concludes with recommendations for further
\par
improving the process, setting these in an overall decision-making framework.
\par
The Research Cycle
\par
PATH research does not take place in isolation. It is closely tied to the
\par
problems and opportunities that are constantly arising in the world of
\par
transportation and to the research being done by others. Figure 1 attempts to
\par
capture the place of PATH research in the larger world. Basic to everything
\par
else are the goals of the transportation system. Technology and society define
\par
these goals and create opportunities to move toward them, but they also create
\par
problems that move society further from the goals.
\par
These problems and opportunities determine research priorities, which, along
\par
with the state of ITS research, PATH's particular strengths, and research
\par
being undertaken elsewhere, determine PATH's research agenda. This is the
\par
basis for PATH's Request for Proposal (RFP) to outside researchers and the
\par
proposal evaluation guidelines (described in the second paper in this report).
\par
The research agenda, along with the response to the RFP and the evaluations
\par
of proposals, determine PATH staff research and the overall PATH researchprogram. This program, in turn, guides decisions about PATH staff hiring,
\par
which adjusts PATH strengths.
\par
The program produces research products. Those that have reached the point
\par
where they can be implemented may be put into practice, perhaps with the
\par
help of the Technology Transfer Program of the Institute of Transportation
\par
Studies. Once in practice, they should mitigate transportation problems, and
\par
subsequently alter research priorities. Some other research products form the
\par
basis for further research, which then competes for priority with other research
\par
ideas. Some research does not show promise and is not pursued further.
\par
From Figure 1, it is clear that the key to the effective use of PATH resources
\par
is to set up a mechanism for clearly incorporating California transportation
\par
goals, problems and opportunities into research priorities. This calls for a
\par
more systematic structuring and analysis of those goals, problems and
\par
opportunities to use as a basis for setting PATH research priorities and
\par
agenda. Analytic tools exist for doing this, based on structured elicitation of
\par
opinions from expert panels. Such a process is described in detail in the
\par
recommendations at the end of this paper.
\par
The Environment in Which Decisions are Made
\par
This section describes the environment in which research decisions are made,
\par
represented by the boxes above the dark line in Figure 1.
\par
Transportation Goals -- Box 3 in Figure 1
\par
The California Transportation Plan calls for:
\par
{$\bullet$} Economic Vitality -- Mobility with Choice
\par
{$\bullet$} A Safe, Convenient, Reliable Transportation System
\par
{$\bullet$} Environmental Protection with Mobility and Energy Efficiency
\par
These goals spoke to the current concerns at the time the Plan was produced.
\par
The proposed goals for the 1998 additions to the Plan have a slightly different
\par
flavor, reflecting better economic times. However, underlying these goals is a
\par
more basic goal relating specifically to transportation, which will be described
\par
below.
\par
The Overall Transportation System Goal
\par
This goal is to maximize the overall societal net benefit of the transportation
\par
system. The societal net benefit is defined as the benefits to all of the parties,
\par
or stakeholders, affected by the system---individual travelers, commercial
\par
enterprises moving goods, the community as a whole, and private
\par
transportation service providers---less the costs to all of these parties. These
\par
are shown in Figure 2.
