\par
CongestionEquity -- A Constraint
\par
Equity is sometimes considered a goal. But equity is neither a benefit nor a
\par
cost. Rather, it is a measure of how benefits and costs are distributed. Here
\par
we consider it a constraint---both benefits and costs must be fairly distributed.
\par
This does not mean that improvements that damage some people can not be
\par
undertaken, only that benefits and damage should be fairly distributed across
\par
the population over the long run and that people should be directly
\par
compensated in cases where benefits and damage will not otherwise be fairly
\par
distributed. There are many long-standing policies that do this. Compensating
\par
people whose land is taken for transportation facilities is one example. Sound
\par
walls are an attempt to avoid imposing unfair noise burdens on people living
\par
adjacent to freeways. Measures taken to reduce noise near airports are
\par
designed to keep air travelers from imposing an unfair burden on nearby
\par
residents.
\par
Goals of Individual Travelers 1
\par
Access to Activities
\par
Most trips that people make are not made primarily for the fun of moving
\par
about, but in order to engage in some activity, such as work or visiting with
\par
friends. The primary goal for individual travelers is to gain access to activities
\par
they value2
\par
.
\par
Entertainment
\par
However, sometimes people \emph{do }travel just for fun. It is common to go out for
\par
walk or a bicycle ride solely for purposes of entertainment, and sometimes
\par
people go out for a drive, just for fun. People do not often go for pleasure
\par
drives during peak commute periods---it is more common on weekends.
\par
Entertainment is often a component goal of a trip taken for another purpose.
\par
People want to get somewhere \emph{and }be entertained along the way. They enjoy
\par
the solitude and comfort of their car after a busy day, the view from an
\par
airplane or automobile, the fun of a ferry ride, or the thrill of riding a
\par
1 These goals also form the basis for the development of performance measures, which are
\par
described in the Final Report for MOU 267.
\par
2 It is sometimes argued that access should be increased by more compact development rather
\par
than by more travel and that this would result in a greater increase in utility. However, people
\par
and communities choose the level of compactness that they prefer---people do this by their
\par
choice of housing location, employment, and other activities. Communities do this through
\par
their general plans, zoning, and planning decisions. If people choose to live in communities,
\par
take jobs, and engage in other activities that require a certain level of travel over the long run,
\par
this must reflect a preference for this activity pattern over one that would require less travel.
\par
This would seem to indicate that for them, more travel provides greater utility than less travel.
\par
Of course, choices that require a high level of travel may impose higher costs on others than
\par
choices that require less travel.
\par
5motorcycle. This is why people buy high-performance cars and car stereos
\par
and why Caltrans plants trees and flowering shrubs along freeways and pays
\par
attention to the visual design of overpasses and sound walls.
\par
Minimizing Travel Costs
\par
People also want to minimize their cost when they travel. These costs are
\par
discussed below.
\par
Time
\par
Transportation takes time. There is not only the time spent actually traveling
\par
but also the time spent waiting to travel and arranging to travel, as well as the
\par
extra time allowed as a buffer in case of delay. Another time-related cost is
\par
schedule delay, that is, arriving earlier than desired in order to avoid travel
\par
delay.
\par
Money
\par
Travelers spend money to purchase, maintain, and operate vehicles (including
\par
the cost of insurance and garage space) and to purchase transportation services
\par
such as transit and air travel.
\par
Accidents/Property Loss and Injury
\par
Travelers sometimes have accidents or are robbed or assaulted by other people
\par
in the course of their travel. The result can be damage to their car or other
\par
vehicle, loss of possessions, and injury, sometimes fatal.
\par
Discomfort
\par
This is a class of costs that are hard to define and measure, but that are just as
\par
real as the other costs outlined above and may be just as significant. They
\par
include cognitive and psychic costs, such as the stress due to driving itself---
\par
which can be exacerbated by difficult driving conditions caused by weather,
\par
heavy traffic, mechanical problems, or other factors---as well as by worry
\par
about taking the wrong route, being late, missing the bus, having an accident,
\par
or having an unpleasant or dangerous encounter with another person. They
\par
include physical discomfort caused by being cold or wet, being exposed to
\par
loud noises, being pushed, having a bumpy ride, or being rudely treated.
\par
Shippers' Goals
\par
Access to Markets
\par
Goods are shipped because they have more value in the destination location
\par
than in their original location. Transportation expands markets for goods. It
\par
allows producers to access more markets for raw materials, thus increasing the
\par
variety and complexity of products they can efficiently produce in a particular
\par
location. Transportation expands their market for finished goods, thus
\par
6expanding consumers' choice. allocation of resources3
\par
.
\par
In both cases this leads to a more efficient
\par
Minimizing Shipping Costs
\par
These costs are what the shipper pays the carrier4 to cover carrier costs and
\par
profit, as well as the inventory cost of the goods during shipment, which is
\par
proportional to the time elapsed during shipment.
\par
Community Goals
\par
Development
\par
The goal of transportation system improvements is often to promote or enable
\par
development. In fact, even when the original goal is simply to reduce travel
\par
time, additional development often results. Over the long term, economic and
\par
social development has been the major benefit of transportation.
\par
Transportation has enabled spatial arrangements of activities that would not
\par
otherwise have been possible. When the only transportation was by foot,
\par
people's economic and social life was limited to the other people and the
\par
goods within walking distance. With transportation, larger numbers of people
\par
and a greater variety of goods can come together, enabling economies of scale
\par
and agglomeration, which make production more efficient. The resulting
\par
increased wealth makes more resources available for additional social and
\par
cultural activities. Today, because the goods necessary for life can be
\par
transported, large numbers of people can live in metropolitan areas, thus
\par
enabling development of a richer mix of economic and social activities.
\par
Furthermore, because of transportation they can come together for these
\par
activities without having to live in close proximity.
\par
Of course, transportation only \emph{enables} development, it does not by itself \emph{cause}
\par
development. Here technology and population play the key role.
\par
Transportation facilities are built and services instituted because people see an
\par
opportunity for doing something new or something old in a different manner
\par
or in a different place.
\par
The transportation improvements that open up new routes and modes of
\par
access, such as the railroads that linked California with the rest of the United
\par
States, have the greatest developmental effect, changing the direction and
\par
scale of development. These are followed by transportation improvements
\par
that expand access within the new area or add additional modes of access that
\par
3 The provision of services is analogous to goods movement. Transportation of service
\par
providers, such as gardeners, plumbers, policemen, and teachers expands the area they can
\par
serve and benefits consumers by providing them with a wider range of services and a larger
\par
selection of service providers.
\par
4 The shipper and carrier are the same in cases where firms supply their own transportation.
\par
7enable new types of economic activity. Finally, there are improvements that
\par
are not designed to affect development, but rather to reduce costs, generally
\par
time. But of course, by reducing costs they can also influence development.
\par
In fact, each of these types of transportation improvements exerts its effects
\par
through reducing transportation costs---it is only the degree of cost reduction
\par
that differs. At this time in California, there are few opportunities for
\par
improvements of the first type. Most areas with development potential have
\par
been opened up and the only new mode on the horizon is the automated
\par
vehicle, which is still some years in the future. A few transportation
\par
improvements are of the second type, allowing expansion of development.
\par
However, many of the economic and social benefits of location and the spatial
\par
organization of activities have already been achieved. Most transportation
\par
improvements that will be made in California in the future are of the third
\par
type, designed to reduce the cost of transportation or to allow increased use
\par
without increased cost.
\par
Other Community Goals
\par
Transportation improvements are sometimes made because of other perceived
\par
benefits, such as community pride or learning about new technologies. A city
\par
may feel that its prestige is enhanced by a light-rail system. Or the goal may
\par
be to test or demonstrate a new technology. In the latter case the primary goal
\par
would be to provide information regarding the effects of the technology in
\par
order to inform decisions regarding future development of the technology and
\par
the circumstances in which it could be effectively implemented. If the
\par
technology proves useful or shows promise of being useful, this increases the
\par
likelihood that it will be used elsewhere. If not, additional ineffective
\par
experimentation can be avoided. In this case, the community served might be
\par
national or even world-wide.
\par
Minimizing the External Costs of Transportation
\par
Transportation often imposes costs on people other than the individual traveler
\par
or freight carrier. It can damage the environment, delay other travelers, or
\par
facilitate development that damages the environment or community character.
\par
Reducing these costs has been an important goal of transportation policy ever
\par
since environmental concerns gained attention and highway construction
\par
slowed in the early 1970s.
\par
Environmental Costs
\par
The use of transportation vehicles imposes environmental costs on travelers
\par
and non-travelers alike. These costs vary with location and mode. For
\par
example, the effects of emissions of pollutants and particulate matter are
\par
likely to be minimal in a sparsely populated or windy area. But in a heavily
\par
populated area or where the topography and climate favor the formation of
\par
ozone, effects can be significant. The effects of spilled fuel or oil leaks
\par
depend on how street runoff is treated. If runoff from a heavily used road or
\par
gas station drains directly into a small creek, the effects can be devastating,8but if it drains into a sewer system where it is treated, the effects will be less
\par
serious. However, in all locations, carbon dioxide emissions from the burning
\par
of carbon-containing fuel contribute to greenhouse gases in the upper
\par
atmosphere.
\par
Worn out or obsolete transportation vehicles and infrastructure, such as old
\par
tires, wrecked cars, rotting piers, and abandoned railroad tracks can degrade
\par
the environment by becoming eyesores or adding to the solid waste burden.
\par
The extent to which these are removed from public view and their materials
\par
are recycled, determines the extent of degradation.
\par
Other environmental costs include depletion of natural resources and damage
\par
to the land and water resources that are caused by the extraction, production
\par
and shipment of fuels, manufacture of vehicles, and construction of the
\par
infrastructure. These costs depend on the resources consumed, which in turn,
\par
depend on the fuel efficiency of vehicles and the design and quantity and
\par
types of materials used for vehicles and infrastructure. Social costs, such as
\par
the splitting of a neighborhood by a freeway or railroad, can also be
\par
considered environmental costs.
\par
The development enabled by transportation can consume open space and
\par
agricultural land and lead to suburban sprawl. But suburban sprawl is not
\par
necessarily a net cost. Although it is almost universally condemned, suburban
\par
living has been the choice of the majority of Californians, so apparently they
\par
believe that the benefits are greater than the costs.
\par
This definition of environmental costs encompasses the concept of
\par
sustainability---that is, meeting the needs of the present without compromising
\par
the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. The primary ways in
\par
which the current transportation system might compromise this ability are by
\par
contributing to global warming or depleting resources, including land
\par
available for transportation infrastructure and other uses.
\par
Costs Imposed on Other Travelers---Congestion
\par
An equally important type of external cost is the additional delay each user of a
\par
crowded road imposes on the other users. The more crowded the road, the
\par
greater the cost. The nature of this cost can be seen in Figure 3. The vertical
\par
axis shows the cumulative number of trips during the peak period on a
\par
particular transportation link. The horizontal axis shows the time of day. The
\par
curved line represents the number of people wanting to pass through the link5
\par
.
\par
At first the demand exceeds capacity, so a queue develops. Then demand
\par
decreases below capacity, and the queue lessens until it is completely
\par
dissipated. The horizontal distance between the two lines represents the delay
\par
for the person wanting to pass through the link at that particular time. The
\par
second, dashed curved line represents the cumulative number of trips if an
\par
5 Actually this would be a stepped line, one step for each vehicle. However, for simplicity, it
\par
is shown here as a smooth line.
\par
9Cumulative
\par
Vehicles
\par
additional traveler joins the queue at time, t. The delay to that traveler is the
\par
area labeled ``Delay \emph{experienced }by additional traveler'', but the delay that
\par
traveler imposes on other travelers is the area between the two curves.
\par
Clearly, this delay is greater than the delay he experiences. This phenomenon
\par
leads to inefficient use of the transportation infrastructure---the delay
\par
experienced by the traveler is less than the delay imposed on others. This
\par
additional delay not only increases the time costs for other travelers, it
\par
increases vehicle-hours, thus increasing carbon monoxide and hydrocarbon
\par
emissions. But the trip does not necessarily lead to a net social cost, because
\par
the benefit of the trip may exceed the total time costs resulting from the trip.
\par
Similarly, a person who uses a crowded bus or train, imposes additional
\par
crowding on the other passengers. The more other passengers there are, the
\par
greater the total discomfort he imposes. As with the automobile traveler, his
\par
cost is always less that the total cost he imposes on others.
