\par
10Goal of Private Transportation Service Providers
\par
The goal of private service providers may vary at different stages of their
\par
development or in different situations. They are likely to focus on
\par
maximizing profits, or increasing market share or maximizing revenues in the
\par
short term in order to maximize long term profits.
\par
Effects Not Considered to be Benefits
\par
Transportation jobs and investment costs, although sometimes called
\par
economic benefits, are not benefits, but rather costs, because the people and
\par
other resources used in transportation would otherwise be providing other
\par
services to society. Society is giving up those services in order to have the
\par
transportation facilities. Even if the people would be otherwise unemployed,
\par
and the government is paying them to work in transportation, society is giving
\par
up the other services that the government might have paid them to provide.
\par
The true economic benefits, beyond the benefit of reduced costs, are the
\par
development benefits. The alleged benefits are actually costs that are included
\par
in the money costs of transportation that are borne by the providers of the
\par
transportation infrastructure and services and by travelers and shippers.
\par
Goals of Public Transportation Agencies
\par
Public agencies are charged with the goal of serving the public. As indicated
\par
in Figure 2, that means they are charged with maximizing the overall societal
\par
net benefit, balanced against equity. By focusing on maximizing net benefits,
\par
they resolve the dilemma of how to serve all of the above transportation goals,
\par
many of which are in conflict, such as providing access while minimizing
\par
vehicle emissions. Determining net benefits, weighing benefits against costs,
\par
provides a mechanism for prioritizing transportation investments and
\par
determining when progress toward a certain goal is worth the cost. These
\par
benefits and costs include not only those that can be given a monetary value,
\par
but also those that can not be monetized or even quantified.
\par
Problems and Opportunities -- Box 4 in Figure 1
\par
Problems typically derive from the costs of transportation. For example, there
\par
is too much congestion, too many accidents, too much air pollution, too much
\par
driver stress, too much uncertainty regarding travel time, while on the other
\par
hand there is too little public money for increasing capacity. People do not
\par
perceive the lack of something that has not previously existed as a problem.
\par
Before the railroad was invented, people did not conceive of its lack as a
\par
problem. Similarly, people today do not see the lack of an automated
\par
highway system as a problem. But many people see it as a means to utilize
\par
the opportunities presented by vastly improved technologies for sensing,
\par
computing, and communicating to move the transportation system to a much-
\par
improved state. These are problems and opportunities in the broadest sense.
\par
They inform the overall research mission. But associated with each larger11problem or opportunity are a host of smaller problems and opportunities, the
\par
solution to or utilization of which incrementally advances research or
\par
improves system operation. For example, an automated highway will need
\par
some type of emergency vehicle access. How can this be provided without
\par
building an emergency lane and defeating the purpose of reducing highway
\par
capacity needs? This is a small problem that must be solved before the larger
\par
opportunities can be realized. With already implemented technologies, there
\par
can be refinements in use, such as more useful messages on changeable
\par
message signs, or refinements in technology, such as more robust and accurate
\par
traffic surveillance devices. Each step forward in research and
\par
implementation brings new problems and opportunities.
\par
State of ITS Research -- Box 5 in Figure 1
\par
Although PATH has been in the forefront in ITS research, particularly with
\par
respect to automated highways, many other universities are doing ITS
\par
research. Private industry, particularly the defense and automotive sectors, is
\par
developing ITS products. Engineering and management consulting firms are
\par
conducting research and developing expertise in implementing ITS systems.
\par
Federal demonstration programs and field tests are being evaluated. PATH
\par
must be aware of this research, utilize it, and exploit opportunities for fruitful
\par
collaboration. PATH must determine where redundant research is needed in
\par
order to expand, confirm or refute earlier findings and where it would be
\par
wasteful because earlier research is conclusive.
\par
PATH Strengths -- Box 6 in Figure 1
\par
Reflecting its strong linkage with academic research, PATH has tended to
\par
concentrate on developing theory, models, and tools; evaluation of
\par
implemented ITS projects; economic analysis; and designs for implementation
\par
and evaluation. However, in the Advanced Vehicle Control and Safety
\par
Systems (AVCSS) program these research skills have been combined with
\par
those of development engineers and technicians, who were able to put the
\par
research products into vehicles.
\par
PATH draws research proposals from campuses throughout the state, so it is
\par
well suited to undertake projects that are interdisciplinary in nature and broad
\par
in scope, such as the AVCSS effort.
\par
Other Research Opportunities -- Box 7 in Figure 1
\par
Although Caltrans is the primary sponsor of PATH research, PATH has been
\par
involved in several federal research efforts including evaluation of field
\par
operational tests such as TravInfo, the National Automated Highway System
\par
Consortium, and the National ITS Architecture development process. There
\par
are also opportunities to work with state and local government agencies as
\par
well as private industry.
\par
