\par
Summary Findings
\par
The basic finding that emerges from this examination of the current PATH
\par
research management process is that it lacks a clear and demonstrable linkage16to the interests of the end users, either directly or via the Caltrans operating
\par
divisions. The current, largely bottom-up, ``entrepreneurial'' research
\par
management process, with its open and frank competition for funds, is
\par
laudable for its competitive efficiency, but it is only weakly influenced by the
\par
needs of end users. If the current program was subjected to scrutiny and the
\par
question was asked: ``How is this process user-driven, or needs-driven?'' the
\par
program would be hard-pressed to come up with a good answer.
\par
This does not mean that the PATH and Caltrans staff involved in setting the
\par
research agenda do not consider end users. Many people give the process a
\par
great deal of thought and time and attempt to make it as responsive to needs as
\par
possible. The problem with the current decision-making structure is that in its
\par
early stages it is not closely tied to the overall goal of the transportation
\par
system---increasing the net benefit of the system---or to the ultimate role of
\par
the research products in addressing this goal. Because the benefits of the
\par
research products are not adequately addressed, research can not be prioritized
\par
in a sensible way. Caltrans' needs, which should also be related to increasing
\par
the net benefit of the transportation system, are not expressed in these terms---
\par
the linkage between this goal and the proposed line of research is not made.
\par
There are actually two steps to that linkage: First, Caltrans needs, and the
\par
goals, problems and opportunities of California's transportation system, must
\par
to be articulated clearly and explicitly. Second, the expected benefits of each
\par
research program must be stated in terms of those goals, problems and
\par
opportunities. The expected benefits of the research are a function of the
\par
annual benefits to be expected from the ultimate product, the role of the
\par
research in producing that product, the likelihood that the product benefits will
\par
actually be realized, and the time when such benefits would begin to be
\par
realized.
\par
Even with five meetings this year at PATH to discuss the research agenda,
\par
two things were missing in the process: 1) a thorough discussion of
\par
transportation and research problems and opportunities for research, and 2) a
\par
discussion of priorities. The RFP reflected the best judgment of PATH
\par
managers regarding these as well as the priorities of individual Caltrans NTRP
\par
managers. This is not necessarily bad, because PATH managers may have given
\par
more thought to these issues and may be best equipped to make such decisions,
\par
and presumably Caltrans managers' responsibilities reflect Caltrans needs and
\par
research priorities. Nonetheless, the process could probably have been improved
\par
by a more thorough discussion of needs, opportunities, and priorities.
\par
A Recommended Structure for Research Decisions
\par
The basic recommendation is to enhance the research management process in
\par
the direction of more clearly and demonstrably linking the funded research
\par
portfolio to the interests of the end users. The recommendation has four
\par
elements:
\par
17\textbf{1.} \textbf{2.} \textbf{Begin With Explicit Consideration of the Goal of the Transportation}
\par
\textbf{System and how PATH Research Can Support that Goal.}
\par
\textbf{Give More Attention to Caltrans Needs and Priorities.}
\par
\textbf{3.} 4. \textbf{Pay More Attention to Moving Research into Practice.}
\par
\textbf{Bring Greater Rigor to the Research Management Process}
\par
Element 1: Begin With Explicit Consideration of the Goal of the
\par
Transportation System and How PATH Research Can Support that Goal
\par
This would involve systematically eliciting from the participants at the earliest
\par
joint Caltrans-PATH research planning meetings opinions regarding Caltrans
\par
and California transportation goals, problems and opportunities, and a
\par
prioritization of them. This includes explicitly writing out each goal, problem
\par
and opportunity, and defining each of them in a clear enough way that it can
\par
be ``scaled.'' That is, so that a scale (perhaps a subjective, verbal scale6, but a
\par
scale nonetheless) can be defined clearly enough for each goal, problem and
\par
opportunity so that each research program can be assessed in terms of how
\par
well it addresses each goal, problem or opportunity. Any given research
\par
program may only address one or two goals, problems or opportunities, but
\par
that system of scaling will allow each research program to be evaluated in
\par
user-driven, needs-driven terms consistent across the range of programs being
\par
considered. There are established tools from a field called decision analysis
\par
that can be used to define and scale the goals, problems and opportunities as
\par
called for here.
\par
Once that foundation is laid, subsequent research planning meetings can be
\par
built around four types of questions:
\par
1. 2. ``Which goals, problems and/or opportunities does the proposed research
\par
project address, and how well does it address each of them?''
\par
``What constituencies would be interested in that project? That is, who
\par
would want to implement it, at the public sector and/or the private sector
\par
level, and why? If it were possible to fully inform citizens regarding the
\par
proposed research7, what degree and breadth of political support would be
\par
behind this project?''
\par
Alternatively, ``What worthwhile on-going or new projects would this
\par
research enable?''
\par
