\par
7 Our assumption is that it is \emph{not} possible to keep citizens fully informed about every decision
\par
that is made by public agencies, because these decisions are too numerous and citizens to not
\par
have time to attend to them all.
\par
183. ''Of any two projects, both addressing similar
\par
goals/problems/opportunities, which addresses them more effectively, per
\par
taxpayer dollar and/or per consumer dollar?''
\par
After the projects being considered are matched with corresponding
\par
goals/problems/opportunities:
\par
4. ``How well does this portfolio of research projects address the whole range
\par
of highest-priority goals/problems/opportunities? Are there
\par
goals/problems/opportunities being neglected while others have several
\par
projects stacked up under them? How can we re-select and/or reallocate
\par
the portfolio to address the range of goals/problems/opportunities in a
\par
more complete and/or more balanced way?''
\par
Those questions can be addressed at any level of analysis. They can be
\par
addressed on a purely non-quantitative, prose basis, or with subjective verbal
\par
scales, or with subjective numerical scales, or with more quantitative verbal or
\par
numerical scales. At each of those levels, there are established tools from
\par
decision analysis that can support the process and provide methodological
\par
defensibility. This process explicitly provides for high-risk, speculative
\par
projects that have a potential payoff commensurate with their risk.
\par
Element 2: Give More Attention to Caltrans Needs and Priorities
\par
Early Discussions with Caltrans Regarding Problems and Priorities
\par
Before the RFP is prepared and staff proposals are made each year,
\par
discussions should be held with Caltrans NTRP, Operations, District, and
\par
other staff regarding Caltrans needs and priorities, including specific problems
\par
that could be addressed by ITS and priorities for solving those problems.
\par
Then PATH staff could present ideas and research opportunities. Those
\par
discussions should be centered on the elicitation from the participants of
\par
Caltrans and California transportation goals, problems and opportunities, and
\par
a prioritization of them, as described under Element 1 above. Effort should be
\par
made to assign priorities to all research needs identified by Caltrans. It is
\par
understood that different organizational units within Caltrans and even NTRP
\par
may have different priorities, but this exercise should make differences
\par
explicit and lead to enhanced understanding within Caltrans and between
\par
Caltrans and PATH. It is critically important that the ultimate users of the
\par
research, Caltrans District staff and local agencies, be involved in these
\par
discussions.
\par
Research Outreach
\par
Since this paper was drafted, PATH and Caltrans have completed another RFP
\par
cycle and selected a 1998/99 research program. This experience and the
\par
comments of reviewers of the draft of this paper suggest that a more flexible,
\par
broad, and continuous means of eliciting research ideas is needed. The19reviewers suggested that people tend not to ``volunteer'' ideas and that there
\par
should be a staff person each from PATH and New Technology and Research
\par
to actively contact senior staff at Caltrans Districts and relevant Departments
\par
throughout the year to discuss research needs and ideas. We recommend that
\par
this approach be implemented on a trial basis as soon as possible. It would
\par
provide raw material for the discussions described above. The effectiveness
\par
of this approach should be weighed against the staff time required when the
\par
1999/2000 RFP process is completed.
\par
Research Needs Web Site
\par
Although there is a need to solicit opinions from key Caltrans staff, there is also
\par
a need for a more inclusive and broad-based discussion of transportation and
\par
research problems, opportunities and priorities. To satisfy this need, PATH's
\par
ATMIS group developed a web site on which Caltrans and PATH staff and
\par
others can post and comment on research needs, research ideas, and research
\par
opportunities. The ``Research Discussion Bulletin Board'' was established at the
\par
end of January 1998. Interested Caltrans and PATH staff seeded it with
\par
discussions intended to pique visitors' interests and motivate them to keep
\par
watching and to contribute. Caltrans New Technology and Research staff and
\par
PATH staff were invited to visit and contribute. The site functions much like a
\par
newsgroup except that the postings are not automatically sent to all subscribers.
\par
The advantage of this method of soliciting ideas over meetings of managers is
\par
that it can be much more extensive, both in terms of the people involved and
\par
time available for discussion. Ideas can be considered in more depth from more
\par
points of view. Our hope is that it can also be a means of encouraging potential
\par
implementers to become research partners and possibly for matching them with
\par
appropriate researchers. Ultimately, people in regional and local agencies and
\par
the general public might also be included.
\par
Our initial idea was to use tools from Decision Analysis, so that the site
\par
provided a structured, clear elicitation and presentation of problems, needs
\par
and opportunities. We also intended to involve PATH staff, Caltrans district
\par
staff at many levels, headquarters staff, and NTRP staff in the design of the
\par
site. But in our haste to get something implemented, we bypassed these steps.
\par
Our failure to take these steps may in part explain the fact that it has received
\par
only two postings. Clearly we need to find out if the lack of postings indicates a
\par
flawed idea, flawed execution of the idea, or simply insufficient awareness of its
\par
existence and lack of sufficient development of interesting threads of discussion.
\par
We will attempt to find the answer through our discussions with individuals and
\par
through group discussions and to modify the site, if necessary, to make it more
\par
effective.
\par
Element 3: Pay More Attention to Moving Research into Practice
\par
The 1998-99 RFP is encouraging more implementable research by giving
\par
special attention to proposals that include partnerships with implementers and
\par
provide for technology transfer. Similar encouragement should be given to20PATH staff research. Implementability has a bearing on the ultimate benefit
\par
of the research product. For example, if there are two projects with equal
\par
annual benefits once implemented, the lifetime benefits of the first to be
\par
implemented are clearly greater. Efforts should be made to include people
\par
from both the Institute of Transportation Studies Technology Transfer and the
\par
Caltrans NTRP Technology Transfer Programs in research program planning.
\par
Element 4: Bring Greater Rigor to the Decision-making Process
\par
Regarding Caltrans/PATH Research
\par
The use of decision analysis tools to facilitate the recommendations made here
\par
has already been noted, in particular the elicitation and structured-question steps
\par
of Element 1, and the structuring of the web site of Element 2. The following
\par
other decision analysis tools might also prove useful:
\par
1. 2. 3. Scenario Trees: These trees would be useful for laying out
\par
systematically how the future could unfold, including the relative
\par
likelihoods of different research outcomes. The trees would be
\par
populated by objective probabilities where available, but most likely
\par
all of the important probabilities would be subjective, elicited using
\par
expert elicitation of expert panels.
\par
Decision Trees: These trees are simply scenario trees with
\par
downstream decision nodes. These trees form the basis for
\par
developing R\&D management strategies, including ``midcourse
\par
corrections'' as the results of uncertain research, technological trends
\par
and markets gradually become known.
\par
Triage Analysis. As grim as this sounds, it would be a very effective
\par
analytic step, combining the results of the above three tools to
\par
identify which research programs would really make a difference.
\par
That is, research on technologies that are going to be developed and
\par
implemented with or without PATH research would be de-
\par
emphasized, as would be research on technologies that are apt to fail
\par
to be developed and implemented with or without PATH research.
\par
What remains is a PATH research portfolio that maximizes the
\par
effectiveness of PATH resources.
\par
4. 5. Value of Information Analysis: This analysis is a more quantitative
\par
version of triage analysis. It quantitatively parses out the incremental
\par
value of a given research program by evaluating the ultimate
\par
expected value of the R\&D decision tree first with then without the
\par
program being assessed.
\par
Adaptive Management: This process systematically manipulates the
\par
R\&D decision tree to maximize the advantages of adaptive
\par
management. That is, it identifies those actions that maximize the
\par
21reduction in uncertainty and minimize commitment of resources and
\par
puts those actions early, relative to actions where the reverse is true.
\par
PATH and Caltrans should consider investing in outside decision
\par
analysis expertise to help implement the other elements of the
\par
recommendation. The cost would be on the order of \$25,000.
\par
A Plan for Making Decisions Regarding Caltrans/PATH Research
\par
The four elements of the structure recommended above fit into the
\par
following plan, presented in chronological order.
\par
\textbf{1.} \textbf{Research Agenda Web Site -- }Caltrans and PATH staff, and
\par
perhaps others, continuously post and discuss problems,
\par
research needs, research opportunities and research ideas. The
\par
web site is structured in a way that encourages the explicit
\par
development of Caltrans and California transportation goals,
\par
problems and opportunities, and their prioritization, as called
\par
for in Element 1 above.
\par
\textbf{2.} \textbf{3.} \textbf{4.} \textbf{5.} \textbf{6.} \textbf{Outreach Team} -- A team is formed consisting of a PATH
\par
staff person and a Caltrans New Technology and Research staff
\par
person. They actively contact senior staff at Caltrans Districts
\par
and relevant Departments throughout the year to discuss
\par
research needs and ideas. This provides input to discussions on
\par
the web site and larger meetings.
\par
\textbf{Caltrans NTRP and other staff meet -- }They discuss what has
\par
been posted on the web site and what has come up in meetings
\par
with the Outreach Team, add other ideas, and then prioritize
\par
research areas, keeping in mind the ultimate expected benefits
\par
of the research. The meeting discussion forms the basis for a
\par
statement of Caltrans research needs and priorities. Again, this
\par
discussion is structured in a way that encourages the explicit
\par
development of Caltrans and California transportation goals,
\par
problems and opportunities, and their prioritization, as called
\par
for in Element 1 above.
\par
\textbf{PATH staff meet }-- They do the same, except that a statement
\par
of PATH, rather than Caltrans, research priorities is prepared.
\par
\textbf{Caltrans and PATH staff meet at the PATH Annual}
\par
\textbf{Meeting }-- Taking advantage of being together at the same
\par
venue, their joint meetings are focused around combining the
\par
results of the preceding three steps.
\par
\textbf{PATH/Caltrans retreat --} This should include Caltrans staff
\par
and staff from the Technology Transfer Program of the
\par
Institute of Transportation Studies. It might also include people
\par
from regional and local agencies. In preparation for the retreat,22PATH prepares a report on the state of current PATH research,
\par
mapping it into the system of goals, problems and opportunities
\par
and their prioritization that has been developed in the
\par
preceding four steps. This and the combined results developed
\par
in Step 4, including both the Caltrans and PATH research
\par
priorities, are sent to participants before the retreat. The retreat
\par
begins with presentations laying out the results developed in
\par
Step 5, including Caltrans and PATH research needs and
\par
priorities. These are discussed in terms of their expected
\par
benefits, and systematically subjected to the four types of
\par
questions described in Element 1 above. Consensus is reached
\par
regarding the PATH research agenda. This is made possible by
\par
proper preparation, well-organized presentations, and adequate
\par
time for deliberation. The 1997 PATH Retreat is a good
\par
model. The retreat should inform both the allocation of funds
\par
to PATH and the PATH RFP.
\par
\textbf{7.} \textbf{PATH RFP -- }This is based on the outcome of the retreat.
\par
Features designed to enhance the ultimate benefits of the
\par
proposed research are included. These would include the
\par
features from the 1998-99 RFP that proved effective and new
\par
features that showed promise in improving the quality or
\par
usefulness of the proposals.
\par
\textbf{8.} \textbf{Proposal review -- }No changes in this process are
\par
recommended, except that it be centered on the four types of
\par
questions described in Element 1 above.
\par
\textbf{9.} \textbf{Staff research }-- Some projects, because of their nature, can
\par
only be done by PATH staff and should be targeted directly at
\par
staff rather than faculty. An example is preparing the vehicles
\par
for the Automated Highway System demonstration. Except for
\par
such projects, staff research proposals should respond to the
\par
RFP, in particular filling critical gaps where there are no good
\par
faculty proposals. The mix of staff projects should be selected
\par
on the basis of the same four questions used to evaluate outside
\par
proposals.
\par
\textbf{10.} \textbf{Caltrans proposal approval --} This process would include a
\par
systematic review of the proposal using the four types of
\par
questions. Ideally, this would take place before mid-August,
\par
so that researchers would be able to compete for the most
\par
qualified student researchers. When approval is delayed, the
\par
best qualified students are often hired for other projects and are
\par
not available for new PATH research.
