----Appendix ATMIS Portion of RFP
\par
This year, PATH is introducing three themes and five special features into its
\par
research program. Proposals that are responsive to these themes or include
\par
these special features will be given special consideration.
\par
Theme 1. Decision Support for Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS)
\par
Investments
\par
Potential ITS implementers and people involved in decisions regarding funding
\par
need tools to assist in deciding which ITS projects to implement or fund. To
\par
help meet this need, PATH has developed a scheme for classifying the effects---
\par
benefits, costs, and other effects---of ITS in terms of overall transportation goals.
\par
PATH is now developing a framework for assessing these effects. However,
\par
more and better information about ITS effects is needed to fully utilize this
\par
framework. Therefore, research is requested in the following areas:
\par
A.1.1 Methods to estimate and value the effects of particular ITS services or
\par
combinations of services
\par
Methods could take many forms, including simple analytical methods or
\par
extensions of existing simulation models. Especially useful will be methods or
\par
improved methods for estimating the effects of commonly implemented services
\par
for which good models do not yet exist. Methods are also needed for valuing
\par
information (even when it does not change travel choice), assessing the effects of
\par
ITS services related to inter-modal passenger or freight movement, and
\par
estimating environmental effects, especially changes in vehicle emissions. The
\par
validity of the methods should be tested with real data whenever possible.
\par
A.1.2 Evaluation of implemented ITS projects8
\par
Such evaluations provide the knowledge base on which the above methods
\par
can be built. Information is needed on the benefits, costs and other effects of
\par
ITS projects, along with the factors that influenced them, such as the
\par
implementation environment, design and management. Evaluations involving
\par
public/private partnerships, personalized public transit, and comparisons of
\par
the relative effectiveness of various information or control strategies are
\par
especially needed. All significant effects should be included, even those that
\par
can not be monetized or quantified.
\par
8 Proposals should be made only for ITS projects that do not already have an evaluation
\par
component. For example, an evaluation program has already been established for the
\par
Southern California Showcase projects.
\par
25A.1.3 Decision Tools in Support of ITS Development and Deployment
\par
Identify existing decision support tools, or develop new tools, for developing,
\par
deploying, and operating ITS systems in cost-effective ways.
\par
Theme 2. The Model Transportation Management Center (TMC)
\par
Special attention will be given to proposals that describe the role their potential
\par
product(s) would play in a model TMC. These might include but are not limited
\par
to the following.
\par
A.2.1 Design of components of a TMC-operated system for measuring,
\par
processing, storing, and disseminating information on transportation system
\par
performance for use by TMC operators, planners, researchers, and the general
\par
public
\par
Research could deal with the entire system or with one component. interactions with other components should be noted and accommodated
\par
If the latter,
\par
A.2.2 Methods for monitoring loop detector accuracy and adjusting for error
\par
Loop detectors are the primary traffic surveillance method currently in use.
\par
Therefore methods are needed to automatically monitor the accuracy of current
\par
loop detectors and adjust for error in real time.
\par
A.2.3 Methods for optimizing incident management and other traffic
\par
management techniques
\par
Many methods for incident detection are in use and under development.
\par
However, incident verification remains a problem. Overall cost-effectiveness in
\par
reducing delay could be enhanced by developing new verification methods and
\par
by better integrating detection, verification and response strategies. Metering
\par
strategies, both onto freeways and between freeways, can also be optimized and
\par
integrated into incident response strategies.
\par
A.2.4 Origin-Destination estimation and prediction methods
\par
More accurate origin-destination estimates are needed for modeling and real
\par
time traffic management.
\par
A.2.5 The role of TMCs in inter-modal freight operations
\par
How can TMCs utilize ITS to improve commercial vehicle operations in and
\par
around ports, airports, and rail terminals? Are special commercial vehicle
\par
strategies needed or justified?
\par
26Theme 3. Partnerships
\par
ITS Applications in Inter-modal Services and Public/Private
\par
A.3.1 Benefits of ITS in Inter-modal Passenger Service
\par
Research is needed to identify and measure the benefits of ITS applications in
\par
inter-modal passenger service. Possible benefits include increased
\par
accessibility; reduced travel time, energy consumption, and air pollution (as a
\par
result of synchronized operations); smoother traffic flow; increased use of
\par
high occupancy vehicles (shuttles, buses, etc.); and somewhat decreased
\par
reliance on private automobiles. Evaluation Tools, forecasting models, and
\par
simulation model, as well as case studies may be used. Case studies to
\par
evaluation the benefit of existing system with inter-modal services may also
\par
be used.
\par
A.3.2 Market Demand for ITS Applications in Inter-modal Operations
\par
Research is needed to identify opportunities to use ITS services in inter-modal
\par
passenger and freight transport and to evaluate market demand for ITS
\par
services.
\par
A.3.3 Public/Private Partnerships To Utilize ITS in Inter-Modal Freight
\par
Operations
\par
In order to appropriately utilize ITS in inter-modal freight operations, public
\par
agencies need more information on opportunities for and constraints on
\par
public/private cooperation in implementing ITS in inter-modal operations.
\par
Issues to be investigated include: strong agency and/or industry biases toward
\par
certain modes; varying perspectives on the performance, benefits, and
\par
justifiable costs of inter-modal services; diversity of organizational objectives
\par
and expectations for inter-modal services; use of multiple standard operating
\par
procedures by operating agencies; concerns about having to share proprietary
\par
information on goods, movements; concerns about security and safe handling
\par
of goods during transfer; concerns about private sector responsibilities and
\par
potential liabilities; and difficulties in initiating and sustaining inter-modal,
\par
inter-agency and public/private partnerships.
\par
A.3.4 Public/Private Partnerships in ITS Implementation
\par
Evaluate different ways of forming and executing public/private partnerships
\par
to implement ITS. Study past and present experiences and propose
\par
approaches to better undertake and ensure the success of public/private
\par
endeavors in the research, development, and implementation of ITS.
\par
27Special Features
\par
