\par
Proposals that include in their research plan a partnership between researchers
\par
and users or implementers of the research will be given special consideration.
\par
Users or implementers could be part of the research team or they could serve
\par
on an advisory panel for the project. PATH hopes that such partnerships will
\par
encourage research that is responsive to users' and implementers' needs and
\par
that results in products that are more apt to be implemented. Proposers are
\par
encouraged to form their own partnerships, but PATH may be able to assist in
\par
identifying appropriate partners. Call Robert Tam at 510-231-5656 or e-mail
\par
rtam@uclink.berkeley.edu
\par
Feature 2. Benefit Assessment
\par
Special attention will also be given to proposals that include in their research
\par
plan a task to estimate the benefits of the deployed product(s) of the research.
\par
That does not mean that the research must result in the deployed product. But
\par
it does mean that the benefits of the research are to be estimated based on the
\par
deployed product(s) that are apt to result from that research. Estimates based
\par
