30PREATRP: Procedure for
\par
Review and Evaluation of
\par
Advanced Transportation
\par
Research Proposals
\par
\emph{Mohamed AlKadri}
\par
Introduction
\par
Research proposals are supposed to describe three research components:
\par
input, process, and output. Research input encompasses defining the problem,
\par
searching the literature, proposing a concept or a hypothesis, and establishing
\par
the need for the research. The research process encompasses how the research
\par
will be conducted, the proposed research methodology, selection of variables,
\par
analysis of data, and so on. The research output is the product of research. It
\par
takes the form of a concept, theory (proven/disproved hypothesis), set of
\par
observations and/or conclusions, set of recommendations (regarding a policy,
\par
procedures, or standards), system design, simulation model, computer
\par
algorithm, and so forth.
\par
Need for Integrated Research Evaluation
\par
The tendency in peer review of research proposals is to focus on research
\par
"input" and "process." Evaluation of the "input" and the "process" is normally
\par
done based on the proposal contents. Critiques can be supported with proper
\par
references to literature and established research practices. However, assessing
\par
the value of the research "outcome" is much more difficult. It requires
\par
beginning with the end in mind and often "guessing" the value of the research
\par
outcome for the real world. This is sometimes overestimated, underestimated,
\par
or overlooked altogether by reviewers.
\par
Caltrans New Technology and Research Program (NT\&RP) as well as PATH
\par
face this challenge in evaluating research proposals. PATH (mainly peer)
\par
reviewers are interested in new concepts, advancement to the state-of-
\par
knowledge, theory and hypothesis, and methodology of proposed research.
\par
Caltrans reviewers, on the other hand, are more interested in the research
\par
outcome, ease of technology transfer, technical and economic feasibility of
\par
implementation, extent of real-world problem solving, and measurable
\par
benefits of research in the short and long runs.31By way of solving this problem, Caltrans NT\&RP and PATH researchers have
\par
worked together on this research project (PATH MOU-209) to establish a
\par
standardized procedure to evaluate research proposals. The procedure had to
\par
be scientific, applicable to all areas of ITS, and able to evaluate technical
\par
merit as well as implementation potential. It had to use one commonly agreed
\par
upon value system with specific measures of merits. It had to encompass all
\par
possible questions formatted in a proper language. One great challenge in
\par
creating this "standardized" procedure was how to examine the significance
\par
and value of proposed research while still encouraging creativity, originality,
\par
and new concepts (with less than certain outcome) and not restricting
\par
researchers on how to conduct their research.
\par
PREATRP Research Evaluation Procedure
\par
PREATRP, Procedure for Review and Evaluation of Advanced Transportation
\par
Research Proposals, has been designed to help reviewers make a balanced
\par
judgment by taking into account all three components simultaneously. A
\par
literature review on the state of practice in research evaluation was performed
\par
1-6. Among many sources, the previous PATH evaluation form, the San
\par
Diego Association of Governments (SANDAG) Proposal Scoring System and
\par
Evaluation Sheet 4, the Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory (LLNL)
\par
standard research evaluation process 5, and the Transportation Northwest
\par
(TransNow) research evaluation procedure 6, were all consulted to develop
\par
PREATRP evaluation categories. In addition, the goals and mission of
\par
Caltrans New Technology and Research Program (NT\&RP) were used to
\par
determine the value system by which research proposals were to be evaluated.
\par
The NT\&RP Strategic Plan was used to define research priorities. The
\par
NT\&RP Program Plan was used to determine weighting scores.
\par
A draft of the first version of PREATRP was circulated to obtain reviews
\par
from practitioner and academic points of view. The feedback was generally
\par
positive (see Exhibit 2). Many suggested specific improvements, and all
\par
feasible improvements have been made in the final version currently in use.
\par
Figure 1 shows the various consulting sources and contributors to the
\par
development of PREATRP.
