33Description of PREATRP
\par
PREATRP is a procedure that has been designed to help Caltrans and PATH
\par
review ATS and ITS proposals from PATH partner campuses and other
\par
sources. It was first developed in 1995 and tested by few PATH and Caltrans
\par
reviewers to evaluate 1995-1996 proposals. It was further modified under
\par
MOU-209 and formally adopted by Caltrans and PATH as the standard
\par
evaluation procedure (and form) beginning with proposals for the 1996-1997
\par
academic year.
\par
This procedure has greatly improved the evaluation process at Caltrans and
\par
PATH. PREATRP asks critical questions and allows reviewers to score
\par
answers according to well-established evaluation criteria. The criteria are
\par
based primarily on Caltrans New Technology and Research Program mission,
\par
philosophy, stated goals, and research priorities.
\par
PREATRP contains seven categories for evaluating proposals, including
\par
technical merit, implementability, and potential return on investment.
\par
Consideration of some of these issues might sometimes be underestimated,
\par
overestimated, or completely overlooked by reviewers. The seven categories
\par
are:
\par
\emph{Significance and relevance to PATH goals \& mission}
\par
\emph{Research methodology}
\par
\emph{Quality of research plan}
\par
\emph{Qualifications of research team}
\par
\emph{Budget}
\par
\emph{Utility of research outcome}
\par
\emph{Feasibility of implementation of research outcome}
\par
Each category has a set of questions intended to evaluate specific aspectsof
\par
the proposal at a microscopic level. Each question gives the proposal a score
\par
between 1 and 5 according to the following: 1= Poor, 2= Fair, 3= average, 4=
\par
Good, 4= Excellent. Scores are left blank if 'not applicable' or 'cannot judge'
\par
is checked. Scores are tallied for each category and divided by the number of
\par
applicable questions giving it one total average score.
\par
Consistency among multiple reviews for each proposal is established by
\par
applying one standardized, not multiple evaluation criteria. With its clearly
\par
defined measures of merits, PREATRP encourages reviews to be concise and
\par
objective. At the same time, PREATRP is not restrictive in that it allows
\par
reviewers to use intuitive assessment. Reviewers are encouraged to provide
\par
written "qualitative" comments to discuss proposal aspects not captured in the
\par
quantitative section. Reviewers, if necessary, can make funding
\par
recommendations regardless of the final quantitative score so long as
\par
34recommendations are supported with proper arguments and convincing
\par
evidence.
\par
The PREATRP form fits on a two-sided sheet and has been formatted to be
\par
easy to read, understand, and use (see Exhibit 1). Reviewers have the choice
\par
of using either a simple paper scoring sheet, or an electronic spreadsheet.
\par
PREATRP tables were developed using Microsoft Excel. Raw scores can be
\par
entered directly into the electronic version. Using the electronic version, all
\par
computations are done automatically and the reviewer is able to instantly view
\par
the resulting tabular calculations, evaluation summaries, and graphics, and
\par
print them on paper.
\par
PATH Annual Proposal Evaluation and Selection Process Using
\par
PREATRP
\par
Every year, PATH develops a Request For Proposals (RFP) to solicit research
\par
in Intelligent Transportation System (ITS) areas. RFP topics are based on
\par
what research has been done in each area and on existing research gaps. The
\par
draft RFP is circulated for review by implementing agencies such as Caltrans
\par
and by university researchers. Implementers have the opportunity to update
\par
their research information needs and the researchers have the opportunity to
\par
express new research interests. Subsequently, PATH synthesizes the input
\par
from researchers and implementers to identify a revised set of research
\par
priorities.
\par
Based on the new set of research priorities, the RFP is finalized with help
\par
from Caltrans-PATH Research Coordinating Councils, and PREATRP is
\par
revised as necessary to reflect any changes to the value system by which
\par
proposals are judged. The RFP is then sent to PATH research partners and
\par
research institutions around the State. Figure 2 shows a flowchart of the
\par
process.
\par
Researchers submit their proposals in response to the RFP. Proposals are then
\par
evaluated using PREATRP. Each proposal goes through a dual evaluation
\par
process: a) by practitioners mostly from Caltrans but sometimes from other
\par
public and private agencies and b) by academic reviewers from PATH and
\par
university campuses (i.e., peer reviews). Caltrans and PATH reviewers
\par
evaluate each proposal in its entirety. Each proposal is reviewed by at least 2-
\par
3 practitioners and 2-3 academic experts. Normally, Caltrans reviews focus
\par
on proposal objectives, budget, deliverables, real-world benefits, and
\par
implementability of outcome. PATH and university reviewers focus more on
\par
the research approach, research methodology, and technical merit.
\par
Coordinators from Caltrans and PATH assemble and reconcile multiple
\par
reviews of each proposal and submit consolidated recommendations to their
\par
management.
\par
NT\&RP management, on one hand, discuss practitioners' reviews and
\par
coordinators' recommendations and make preliminary funding decisions,
\par
taking into account overall NT\&R Program needs. PATH management, on
\par
the other hand, discuss peer reviews and coordinators' recommendations and35\textbf{Research Results}
