36make preliminary funding decisions, taking into account an overall systems
\par
perspective. Preliminary funding decisions are discussed with the PATH
\par
Executive Committee and/or the AVCS, ATMIS, and Systems Coordinating
\par
Councils whose members come from academic institutions and implementing
\par
organizations. Evaluations are documented and presented to the Joint
\par
Caltrans-PATH Management.
\par
Recommended proposals undergo a further down-selection process based on
\par
relative ranking and availability of funds. Joint Caltrans-PATH management
\par
discusses technical reviews, funding recommendations, Coordinating Council
\par
evaluations, and make final funding decisions. These decisions consider
\par
combined practitioner and academic perspectives as well as macro issues such
\par
as overall program goals, needs, research gaps, priorities, budget uncertainties,
\par
partnerships, short and long term research impacts, research management and
\par
implementation strategies. Policy direction, legislative mandates, and federal
\par
and state funding potentials are also considered.
\par
Each selected proposal is processed into a Memorandum of Understanding
\par
(MOU) to execute the research. Each MOU is supposed to produce a set of
\par
deliverables, which constitute the ultimate output of the research. This output is
\par
added to the database of research results and is expected to advance and enhance
\par
the state of knowledge in ITS.
