46Decision Support for ITS
\par
Implementers -- On the Web
\par
\emph{Joy Dahlgren}
\par
Motivation
\par
In 1995, researchers with the PATH program at the University of California at
\par
Berkeley became concerned that what was being learned about intelligent
\par
transportation systems (ITS) was not easily accessible to potential
\par
implementers. As a result, implementation might not occur where appropriate
\par
and might be undertaken where not appropriate. PATH decided to design an
\par
information source that would describe ITS services, summarize what is
\par
known regarding their benefits and costs, identify deficiencies in existing
\par
research, and provide references to further research.
\par
Fortunately, the desire to create such a resource coincided with the emergence
\par
of the world wide web. It is an ideal medium to distribute ITS information to
\par
a wide audience, and the contents can be readily updated and modified as ITS
\par
develops. The site is named LEAP, Learning from the Evaluation and
\par
Analysis of Performance, and is located at
\par
http://www.path.berkeley.edu/\textasciitilde leap. The primary audience is potential
\par
implementers---local planners, public works staff, consultants, and policy
\par
makers at all levels, but the site is also designed to be assist people involved in
\par
ITS research.
\par
This paper describes the web site---its structure, sources of information, and
\par
current contents---and concludes with a discussion of planned improvements
\par
and responses of site visitors.
\par
How LEAP Works
\par
Structure
\par
