\textbf{Figure 1. Web Site Structure}The policy maker may prefer to look only at the highest level: summaries of
\par
the system or technology performance. The researcher may dig to the deepest
\par
level: evaluation methodologies and assumptions, test conditions, or research
\par
findings. Information about each ITS service includes summaries of findings
\par
as well as detailed tables and graphs that list case-by-case information.
\par
The particular information that is provided for each ITS service varies,
\par
depending on what is available and, more importantly, the state of the
\par
practice. Performance measures are more readily available for mature
\par
systems, such as electronic toll collection, than for technologies that are still
\par
being tested, such as automatic incident detection using video cameras.
\par
Ultimately, the site is expected to be useful for making technology
\par
deployment decisions and research fund allocation decisions. Information
\par
about \emph{measured} benefits and implementation costs is emphasized, because this
\par
is critical in making policy decisions. But because each ITS application is
\par
unique, additional information on the context of each application is also
\par
provided when available. For example, in discussing the performance of
\par
electronic toll collection systems, data describing the mix of modes and the
\par
traffic volumes at the toll plaza are presented.
\par
To aid decisions regarding funding allocations for research, the site contains
\par
information on the different technologies being researched and their expected
\par
performance. To the extent possible, the performance of proposed
\par
technologies is compared to that of existing systems, to better understand what
\par
gains, if any, could be achieved with these technologies.
\par
An important contribution of this site to ITS research is its assessments of the
\par
state of the practice. Gaps in the research are discussed. For implementers,
\par
barriers to implementation of a given service are noted whenever identified.
\par
The quality of evaluations is examined and sources of uncertainty in the data
\par
or methodologies are noted. Efforts are made to interpret the findings. For
\par
example, a review of the performance of incident clearance teams finds that
\par
most of their attributed benefits are savings in individual drivers' travel time.
\par
Although the total value of time saved at the system level is large, the time
\par
savings for each person are fairly small. Moreover, these benefits are
\par
calculated using a somewhat arbitrary value of time, usually assumed uniform
\par
over the population. Sensitivity analyses, testing assumptions about the
\par
distribution of the value of time over the population, about capacity
\par
reductions, or traffic levels, are rarely performed.
\par
Web site users, reading the summary of research results, the evaluation of the
\par
research itself, and the costs and benefits (effects) of the particular ITS service
\par
in various settings, can better judge what the effects of applying the
\par
technology might be in their own situations. If a user wishes to learn more
\par
about the individual evaluations or studies, he or she can consult the
\par
references associated with each service.
\par
The gathering of information from published sources is a continuous process.
\par
The site is updated as significant publications become available. In this49respect the web site provides a great advantage over published hard copy. It
\par
can be kept continually current, whereas hard copy can not be updated every
\par
time there is a small revision.
\par
Cross Referencing
\par
One of the advantages of the web is that information can be cross-referenced.
\par
For example, vehicle detection technologies used in electronic toll collection
\par
are similar to some of the technologies proposed for incident detection. The
\par
traffic surveillance technologies used for ramp metering can also be used for
\par
incident management and traveler information. In fact, the web site's
\par
organization highlights the importance of traffic surveillance as the foundation
\par
of several ITS services. Surveillance activities provide most of the
\par
information required by these services, and consequently their costs and
\par
effects are highly dependent on the current state of surveillance technology.
\par
By cross referencing information, the web site can help increase awareness
\par
that as surveillance technology advances, several ITS services may become
\par
less costly and more effective. In general, the web site's organization makes
\par
it easier to see the implications to one ITS service of advances or
\par
breakthroughs in related technologies.
\par
Seeing the connections between ITS technologies gives web site users a better
\par
picture of how ITS works and what components are necessary to implement a
\par
given technology. Again, this means that in order to implement a motorist
\par
information system, first a good traffic surveillance program must be in place.
\par
The organization of the web site, as much as the information itself, can
\par
convey this message.
\par
Links
\par
To ensure that LEAP did not duplicate existing resources, other ITS-related
\par
sites were surveyed prior to site development. While these resources provided
\par
complementary information, none overlapped with the objectives of LEAP. A
\par
page within the site provides links to many ITS-related sites, some of which
\par
are described below:
\par
\textbf{ITS America} - \emph{http://www.itsa.org}
\par
ITS America is a Congressionally-mandated organization established to
\par
coordinate the development and deployment of ITS in the United States. To
\par
aid in this goal, the site provides an activity calendar, employment
\par
opportunities, technology summaries,
\par
\textbf{ITS Deployment} - \emph{http://128.169.84.18}
\par
An online database of the ITS infrastructure deployed in urban areas of the
\par
United States
\par
\textbf{ITS Online} - \emph{http://www.itsonline.com}
\par
News, articles, and online forums related to ITS and transportation.
\par
50\textbf{Rural ITS }-- \emph{http://www.ruralits.org}
\par
A site featuring rural applications of ITS.
\par
\textbf{PATH Data Base }-- \emph{http://www.nas.edu.trb/about/path1.html}
\par
An annotated data base of over 13,000 articles and books on ITS.
\par
\textbf{Transportation Resources} - \emph{http://dragon.princeton.edu/\textasciitilde dhb}
\par
A comprehensive list of links to web-based transportation resources.
\par
\textbf{United States Department of Transportation} - \emph{http://www.dot.gov}
\par
This site describes Federal participation in ITS research and deployment.
\par
\textbf{The Bureau of Transportation Statistics} - \emph{http://www.bts.gov/smart}
\par
A digital library with transportation research papers available for download.
\par
Feedback
\par
In order to effectively develop the site for its target audience and to guide
\par
PATH research, a portion of the site is devoted to visitor feedback. Visitors
\par
can leave their name and email in a guest book, and contribute suggestions or
\par
information through online questionnaires. The feedback is summarized in
\par
greater detail later in the paper.
\par
Sources of Information
\par
To date, information for the web site has been gathered primarily from
\par
published sources: research and trade journals, conference proceedings,
\par
project evaluation reports, research reports, and other web sites. The excellent
\par
services and extensive collection of the Institute of Transportation Studies
\par
Library at the University of California, Berkeley has facilitated the
\par
compilation of information.
\par
Most information currently on the web site comes from specialized research,
\par
trade, and public sector reports. The most complete and reliable information
\par
comes from evaluations of field tests and deployed systems. However, these
\par
are generally available only for mature services, such as freeway service
\par
patrols and electronic toll collection. For services and technologies that have
\par
not been widely deployed, such as advanced incident detection or vehicle
\par
guidance, much of the information is based on analytical studies, computer
\par
simulations, or limited-scope tests.
\par
Through the literature review it has become clear that some systems are not
\par
evaluated, or that some evaluations are not published, particularly those of
\par
less successful applications. Such results should be made available, since
\par
important lessons could be learned from seemingly negative outcomes. Also,
\par
for some of the systems documented in the site, additional information would
\par
be helpful in understanding their effectiveness. One avenue to pursue is the
\par
web site's feedback page---one of the questionnaires is designed to solicit
\par
51information about evaluations not yet included in the web site. However, to
\par
date few people have volunteered such information.
\par
Current Contents
\par
The web site contains information about Advanced Traffic Management
\par
Systems (ATMS): traffic surveillance, incident detection, incident clearance,
\par
ramp metering, traffic management centers, and electronic toll collection. It
\par
also contains information about Advanced Traveler Information Systems
\par
(ATIS), including pre-trip information, en-route information, route, guidance,
\par
and rideshare matching. It includes Commercial Vehicle Operations Systems
\par
(CVO) and Advanced Public Transportation Systems (APTS), including
\par
automated vehicle location and advanced fare payment. It also includes
\par
longitudinal collision avoidance. Sections on variable message signs, probe
\par
vehicles, and highway-rail intersection management are currently under
\par
development and will be added soon.
\par
Planned Improvements
\par
Because there have been many recent additions to the web site, bringing it
\par
close to containing information on all ITS services, we plan to send an email
\par
to everyone who has signed the guest book, announcing the new additions.
\par
We will also send notices to ITS and other relevant newgroups.
\par
LEAP already has a link to the ITS Architecture web site, which provides
\par
access to all architecture documents. The evaluation web site will not
\par
duplicate this information, but will include architecture information that
\par
enhances the usefulness of the evaluatory information. It will provide
\par
information on the physical, communications, and institutional requirements
\par
of each market package or service, and also identify other market packages
\par
that utilize the same components. It will also include standards, to the extent
\par
that they have been developed. Whenever possible, the web site will use
\par
terminology from the architecture. The web site is based on what has been
\par
done or at least carefully researched, while the National ITS Architecture is a
\par
framework for ITS future development. Therefore, the web site may not
\par
contain evaluatory information on all user services. However, this
\par
information will be added as additional ITS services are implemented.
\par
Two major improvements are planned. The first is to add information about
\par
implementations for which there are no published evaluations. Managers and
\par
sponsors of these implementations will be contacted directly once the
\par
available published information has been gathered.
\par
The second planned improvement is to provide advice about ITS service
\par
implementation10. As the web site now stands, the user can look around and
\par
delve as deep as he or she likes. But other than providing information in an
\par
10 These improvements were not part of MOU 209 and are not part of MOU 356, which funds
\par
continuation of the web site development.
\par
52organized and concise form, the web site provides no help to the user in
\par
deciding what to do or how to do it. The initial conception of the web site has
\par
been expanded to include not only information but also tools for deciding
\par
where and how to implement various ITS services. The tools for determining
\par
\emph{where} to implement a particular ITS service may be hueristics, sketch
\par
planning methods, or models for estimating the effects of various ITS services
\par
in particular circumstances. Many people and organizations are trying to finds
\par
ways to model ITS benefits and costs; this is another effort in that direction.
\par
Such an analysis might be approached in two ways; in either case ITS services
\par
and transportation system needs or problems would be linked. The web site
\par
visitor might begin with an ITS service and determine what benefits and costs
\par
might result in his or her particular setting. Alternatively, he or she might
\par
begin with a particular problem and determine which ITS services or
\par
conventional improvements would be most cost-effective in addressing the
\par
problem in his or her particular circumstances. The process would begin with
\par
the web site providing information and access to models to assess the current
\par
situation. Once the user had done this, the web site would present the user
\par
with a range of possible actions to address system problems. For those
\par
selected by the user, the web site would present hueristics, sketch planning
\par
methods, or models for selecting the most promising. The appropriate scale
\par
and limits of implementation would be developed in the process. Positive and
\par
negative interactions between ITS services would be identified and taken into
\par
account. This iterative process would ultimately provide a good match of
\par
actions to mitigate problems as well as solid analytical support for justifying
\par
the actions.
\par
This may ultimately include the decision support framework currently being
\par
developed by PATH under MOU 357.
\par
This portion of the project draws heavily on research and design undertaken in
\par
connection with PLANiTS, a computer assisted planning tool envisioned by
\par
PATH. PLANiTS was initially developed before the web came into
\par
widespread use and therefore was a few years ahead of its time.
\par
For ITS actions that have been selected, advice on \emph{how }to implement those
\par
actions would be provided. This advice would draw upon the literature and
\par
the experience of previous implementers. It could include implementation
\par
checklists or sample implementation plans.
\par
In addition to providing analytical tools, PLANiTS also envisioned providing
\par
an interactive, multi-user planning environment. Once the other
\par
improvements to the web site have been completed, this might be an
\par
interesting avenue to pursue.
\par
Response
\par
Between July 1996 and July 1998, the site had over 8,000 visits. More than
\par
100 signed the guest book or returned questionnaires. Visitors' affiliations are
\par
closely divided between academic institutions, the private sector, and
\par
53government organizations. (Figure 2) Respondent occupations ranged from
\par
emergency vehicle drivers and traffic signal technicians to university faculty
\par
and private-sector consultants. Visitors originate from Australia, Canada,
\par
China, Denmark, Finland, France, Korea, Japan, Mexico, the Netherlands,
\par
New Zealand, Portugal, Sweden, United Kingdom, and the United States.
\par
(Figure 3)
\par
Overall feedback has been positive and constructive. Apart from suggestions
\par
concerning the site's layout, content, and organization, several visitors have
\par
volunteered information not reported on the site. Others have requested
\par
additional information and even asked for advice on ITS implementation.
