``Two consecutive weekly rounds of a trihospital academic nephrology division were devoted to the subject of renal biopsy in INS. All clinicians had been previously exposed to decision analysis in the literature or at grand medical rounds, but none had any formal training in the subject. At the outset, an informal questionnaire elicited the recommendations of clinicians for patients with INS and their attitudes towards decision analysis. A specific case was presented [5] and clinicians were asked whether they would recommend a biopsy prior to therapy for this patient. Then, the decision analysis for that patient was presented. Participants were given a copy of the publication and asked to read it in the intervening week. At a second session one week later, the group reviewed the model, examined its weaknesses, explored the data used in calculating the expected utilities, and discussed the results. The clinicians were subsequently asked whether they would change their minds in recommending biopsy for the case presented in the previous session and also for subsequent cases in their clinical practices. In addition, we elicited their attitudes towards the decision analysis for this particular problem and in general.'' (Detsky et al., 1987, p. 832)
