a technique which is so logical, clear, and ex-
\par
plicit have such little impact in clinical medi-
\par
cine? What's wrong with decision analysis?
\par
To help us answer these questions, we under-
\par
took the following ``qualitative study''.
\par
alent clinical outcomes (quality-adjusted life ex-
\par
pectancy). At our hospital, the majority of
\par
patients with INS undergo biopsy before treat-
\par
ment. In this ``study'', we asked if it would be
\par
possible to change the opinions of nephrologists
\par
who followed the traditional strategy by
\par
presenting a decision analysis which we believe
\par
to be fairly persuasive.
\par
\textbf{METHODS}
\par
Two consecutive weekly rounds of a tri-
\par
hospital academic nephrology division were de-
\par
voted to the subject of renal biopsy in INS. All
\par
clinicians had been previously exposed to deci-
\par
sion analysis in the literature or at grand medi-
\par
cal rounds, but none had any formal training in
\par
the subject. At the outset, an informal question-
\par
naire elicited the recommendations of clinicians
\par
for patients with INS and their attitudes to-
\par
wards decision analysis. A specific case was
\par
presented [5] and clinicians were asked whether
\par
they would recommend a biopsy prior to ther-
\par
apy for this patient. Then, the decision analysis
\par
for that patient was presented. Participants were
\par
given a copy of the publication and asked to
\par
read it in the intervening week. At a second
\par
session one week later, the group reviewed the
\par
model, examined its weaknesses, explored the
\par
data used in calculating the expected utilities,
\par
and discussed the results. The clinicians were
\par
subsequently asked whether they would change
\par
their minds in recommending biopsy for the
\par
case presented in the previous session and also
\par
for subsequent cases in their clinical practices.
\par
In addition, we elicited their attitudes towards
\par
the decision analysis for this particular problem
\par
and in general.
\par
Fifteen clinicians participated in the rounds,
\par
including six staff nephrologists and two
\par
nephrology residents. The other participants
\par
were residents in internal medicine or other staff
\par
physicians.
\par
\textbf{CASE STUDY/CLINICAL VIGNETTE}
\par
Through an informal poll taken at a recent
\par
meeting of the Society for Medical Decision
\par
Making, we identified a clinical controversy for
\par
which a high-quality decision analysis had been
\par
published: the work of Kassirer, Lau and Levy
\par
[5,6]. This analysis, concerning the decision to
\par
biopsy patients with idiopathic nephrotic syn-
\par
drome (INS) before instituting steroid therapy,
\par
was thought to have changed beliefs and prac-
\par
tices at the author's local institution and was
\par
expected to have influence in other centres.
\par
The decision analysis showed that, despite the
\par
clinical logic of performing a renal biopsy in all
\par
cases of INS to determine histology in order to
\par
direct therapy, an empirical trial of steroids
\par
could be expected to result in virtually equiv-
\par
\textbf{RESULTS}
\par
Initially, all participants said they recommen-
\par
ded a renal biopsy for the majority of patients
\par
they see with idiopathic nephrotic syndrome,
\par
and they would also recommend this for the
\par
particular case presentation. At the end of the
\par
second session, several of the housestaff had
\par
changed their opinion about the role of renal
\par
biopsy and stated that it was not necessary for
\par
the patient we presented. However, only one of\textbf{What's Wrong with Decision Analysis? }six staff nephrologists changed his recommen-
\par
dation for his patient. Some clinicians felt that
\par
the model did not capture the essence of the
\par
clinical problem, in that they used the biopsy to
\par
make the diagnosis of INS by ruling out second-
\par
ary causes such as amyloidosis. On the other
\par
hand, others in the group argued that it was
\par
very unlikely that the secondary causes would
\par
be missed by other investigations and sub-
\par
sequently discovered on renal biopsy.
\par
One of the major arguments against the con-
\par
clusion that biopsy may be of no value con-
\par
cerned the ``pure value'' of information derived
\par
from biopsy, a factor which was not considered
\par
in the model. The clinicians commented that
\par
even if the two strategies resulted in equal
\par
quality-adjusted life expectancies for their
\par
patients, the information derived from the
\par
biopsy was of value for both patients and
\par
clinicians. That is, the provision of information
\par
alone, even if it does not change clinical
\par
management, may be ``utility-increasing''. They
\par
argued that it helped with communication and
\par
genetic counselling, was of prognostic value,
\par
and was information that might prove useful
\par
should subsequent studies show other beneficial
\par
therapeutic modalities for specific histopatho-
\par
logies. At the same time, the clinicians admitted
\par
that many patients with histologies thought to
\par
be ``steroid-unresponsive'' are treated with
\par
steroids, particularly when their renal function
\par
deteriorates.
\par
A lively discussion concerning the logic of
\par
each of these arguments ensued and culminated
\par
in discussing a way of quantitating the value of
\par
the biospy information, If clinicians preferred
\par
the renal biopsy strategy when that strategy and
\par
the empiric treatment strategy resulted in equal
\par
clinical outcomes, it must be that the informa-
\par
tion derived from the biopsy alone is of some
\par
value. Would clinicians be willing to reduce life
\par
expectancy for their patients in order to obtain
\par
that information? That is, if renal biopsy
\par
resulted in a life expectancy of X months less
\par
than the empiric steroid strategy, would they
\par
still prefer the biopsy strategy? We asked them
\par
to quantify the decrement in life expectancy
\par
which they would be willing to accept before
\par
they would become indifferent in the choice
\par
between the two strategies. We gave them
\par
choices for X for 1 month, 3 months, 6 months,
\par
1 year, and 2 years. Several clinicians asked
\par
why we were limiting the trade-off to 2 years!
\par
The answers we received were: ``less than 1
\par
month, in fact less than 1 day'', ``1 month'', ``3
\par
\textbf{833}
\par
months'', ``6 months'', ``1 year'', ``at least 1
\par
year'', ``2 years'', and ``5 years''!
\par
\textbf{DISCUSSION}
\par
Decision analysis is a process with much
\par
logical appeal. From an academic perspective,
\par
the exercise of reviewing the structure of out-
\par
comes was well-received. Our discussion of
\par
the results and ability of this study to change
\par
clinical behaviour was lively, stimulating, and
\par
revealing. The discussion pointed to several
\par
reasons why the biopsy strategy would be pre-
\par
ferred. These included: the fact that the biopsy-
\par
first strategy was firmly entrenched in clinical
\par
practice and in fact is often the ``correct'' answer
\par
on certification examinations, clinicians are
\par
uncomfortable with uncertainty, internists are
\par
trained to ``make the diagnosis'' with little con-
\par
sideration of the cost and perhaps the risk, the
\par
biopsy data may contribute to our knowledge
\par
of the disease, and clinicians are uncomfortable
\par
in treating all patients with all patients with a
\par
potentially toxic therapy to which only some
\par
patients will respond. We pointed out that this
\par
latter issue was true for virtually all clinical
\par
decisions. Our inability to change the recom-
\par
mendations of five of the six staff nephrologists
\par
may have also been influenced by an ``ordering
\par
effect'' since we elicited their recommendations
\par
in the first session before presenting the decision
\par
analysis.
\par
It is useful to consider who gains and who
\par
loses from the biopsy-first strategy. Those who
\par
gain from the strategy are: steroid unresponsive
\par
patients who are spared steroids, patients with
\par
a good prognosis who are reassured through the
\par
biopsy information, and physicians who are
\par
more comfortable with the knowledge of the
\par
histopathology. However, those who lose from
\par
the biopsy strategy are: steroid responsive
\par
patients who are exposed to the discomfort
\par
(and very small risk) of the biopsy, patients with
\par
histopathologies which have so far been defined
\par
as ``steroid unresponsive'' who might in fact
\par
respond to steroids, and patients whose histo-
\par
pathology results in giving them bad news. We
\par
believe that clinicians tend to concentrate on the
\par
gains while ignoring the losses. In fact, the
\par
qualitative study described above led us to ask,
\par
``How do clinicians weigh risks and benefits
\par
when they are unwilling to do so explicitly?'' A
\par
recent paper by Feinstein may help us answer
\par
that question [7].834 ALLAN S. DETSKY \textbf{\emph{et al.}}
\par
We have found that prescriptive decision
\par
analysis models which do not consider the
\par
``pure'' value of information may be unaccept-
\par
able to clinicians because in their opinion the
\par
models lack face validity. One of the major
\par
challenges for decision analysis will be to incor-
\par
porate the pure value of information into
\par
utilities, i.e. to broaden the measurement of
\par
utilities to include this outcome. Considerations
\par
of the asymmetry between patients' and clin-
\par
icians' perspectives (i.e. assignment of different
\par
utilities to the same outcomes) may also be
\par
important. Clinicians may value pure informa-
\par
tion (i.e. information which does not specifically
\par
change management) because he or she wishes
\par
to know what the future may hold even if it
\par
cannot affect what happens to the patient, while
\par
patients may only be concerned about getting
\par
well with minimum discomfort. If there is in-
\par
deed a discrepancy in objectives, prescriptive
\par
models may yield different recommendations
\par
for clinicians and patients. An explicit decision
\par
analysis may help clinicians focus on this con-
\par
fusion over whose utility is relevant as a source
\par
of clinical disagreement over recommendations.
\par
One of the difficulties faced by the par-
\par
ticipants in our sessions was the reading of the
\par
paper. Most found the exposition to be cum-
\par
bersome and difficult to read. Clearly, our audi-
\par
ence was not well-primed for this kind of activ-
\par
ity and they had difficulty dealing with explicit
\par
quantitative processes and trades (e.g. we doubt
\par
that any clinician would be willing to give up 2
\par
or 5 years of patient's life for pure information
\par
if such information does not add to the patient's
\par
quality-adjusted survival). It might be better
\par
to elicit the value that clinicians place on this
\par
information in monetary units (willingness-
\par
to-pay), as demonstrated by Bet-wick and
\par
Weinstein [8].
\par
Some clinicians were highly skeptical of a
\par
process that was not based on actual obser-
\par
vations in real patients. One clinician used the
\par
phrase ``parlour games'' to describe the process.
\par
This impression was heightened when the dis-
\par
cussion turned to the use of sensitivity analysis
\par
or altering the model to reflect the participants'
\par
criticisms of the analysis we presented to them.
\par
This qualitative study showed us that these
\par
traditional clinicians could not be easily per-
\par
suaded to change clinical practice or opinions
\par
by the use of explicit prescriptive decision anal-
\par
yses. Perhaps this reluctance can be attributed
\par
to the foreign nature of the quantitative model
\par
(i.e. their lack of preparation and familiarity
\par
with the technique). It is possible that their
\par
reluctance lies in the conflict between subjective
\par
intuition and objective logic. We can character-
\par
ize \textbf{\emph{clinical decision making }}under uncertainty as
\par
an activity which principally involves pattern
\par
recognition and intuition. We speculate that this
\par
activity may involve the conscious and uncon-
\par
scious processing of both objective and non-
\par
objective information. This is particularly true
\par
when the clinical studies required to provide
\par
definitive answers are unavailable or the prob-
\par
lem is complex. On the other hand, \textbf{\emph{decision}}
\par
\textbf{\emph{analysis }}involves linear, explicit and logical pro-
\par
cesses. Some have popularized the terms ``right
\par
brain'' (for pattern recognition) and ``left brain''
\par
(for logic) as labels to describe these cognitive
\par
functions [9, lo]. Dawes has described the
\par
psychological resistance to the use of explicit
\par
models and the traditional reverence for intu-
\par
itive judgements \textbf{[1 1, }121. ``People and organiz-
\par
ations have to make decisions, often between
\par
alternatives that appear equally good or bad.
\par
What better way to justify than to consult an
\par
intuitive expert?. . . An expert who constructs a
\par
linear model is not as impressive as one who
\par
gives advice on a `burst' of intuition derived
\par
from `years of experience'.'' \textbf{[1 11). }Tversky and
\par
Kahneman suggest that opposition to explicit
\par
models is reinforced by the availability of exam-
\par
ples where successful intuitive prediction con-
\par
tradicts a prediction rule based on some formula
\par
[13]. Such ``exceptions to the rule'' may in fact
\par
have more impact on judgement than those
\par
based on statistical descriptions of a large num-
\par
ber of experiences [14]. These observations sug-
\par
gest to us that decision makers, whether they be
\par
in business, public policy or clinical settings, are
\par
more comfortable with ``right brain'' or intu-
\par
itive approaches than ``left brain'' models.
\par
In fact, they may be quick to point out the
\par
poor performance of predictive models (e.g.
\par
explaining a low proportion of variance) even
\par
without evidence to show that global intuition
\par
does better. In addition, decision makers
\par
may feel that repeated application of recom-
\par
mendations based on a concrete model is
\par
``dehumanizing'', depriving them of their role
\par
as a ``it-is-my-opinion-based-on-my-experience''
\par
judgement. Perhaps the audience of those
\par
who practice decision analysis perceive this
\par
activity as an attempt to replace decision
\par
making by clinicians with mechanistic algo-
\par
rithms. If so, the reluctance of that audience to
\par
accept this approach to decision making is
\par
understandable.What's Wrong with
\par
Decision Analysis? 835
\par
How then can decision analysis have substan-
\par
tial impact on clinical practice? First, investi-
\par
gators and writers in the field should carefully
\par
consider their intended audience. The choice of
\par
a target audience will affect the location choices
\par
for publishing reports (decision sciences jour-
\par
nals, health policy journals, general medical
\par
journals or subspeciality clinical journals) and
\par
the scope of teaching efforts (e.g. marketing
\par
specialized courses for the limited audience vs
\par
teaching decision analysis in a core under-
\par
graduate or postgraduate curriculum). Perhaps
\par
with limited resources available for dissem-
\par
ination and teaching, it would be more efficient
\par
for decision analysts to target their message to
\par
policy-makers, textbook writers, and ``role-
\par
model'' clinicians rather than to a broader
\par
clinical audience. This limited audience may be
\par
able to influence widespread clinical practice.
\par
Even so, it may be a formidable task to cultivate
\par
the audience of textbook writers and role model
\par
clinicians, since they are also traditional clin-
\par
icians who may be uncomfortable with decision
\par
analytic techniques. Part of the answer may
\par
come in making traditional clinicians more
\par
comfortable with probabilistic models. Perhaps
\par
our colleagues in cognitive psychology can help
\par
us understand how to use linear reasoning more
\par
effectively to influence the pattern recognition
\par
performed by most clinicians in appropriate
\par
situations, to integrate ``left brain'' and ``right
\par
brain'' processes.
\par
This leads us to ask a further question: is
\par
there something to gain by using these pre-
\par
scriptive models to influence clinical intuition?
\par
Research in decision analysis should concen-
\par
trate on the demonstration of its benefits. These
\par
may include: improving clinicians' under-
\par
standing of strategic choices and structure of the
\par
clinical problems they face, help in resolving the
\par
source of controversy between clinicians (e.g.
\par
surgeons recommending surgical therapy and
\par
physicians recommending medical therapy),
\par
structuring of future research, and hopefully
\par
improvements in clinical outcomes. If in-
\par
vestigators cannot demonstrate that decision
\par
analysis provides some benefit in health care,
\par
there will be no point in trying to determine the
\par
most effective means of disseminating its mes-
\par
sage.
\par
Decision analysts have spent considerable
\par
time improving modelling techniques and
\par
methodologies for performing their studies.
\par
However, if the field is to have substantial
\par
impact in clinical medicine, greater effort and
\par
attention must be paid to the issues we have
\par
raised here. We conclude with three major chal-
\par
lenges which we present in increasing order of
\par
importance. The first challenge is to incorporate
\par
the pure value of information (uncertainty
\par
reduction) into prescriptive models. The second
\par
challenge is to determine the most appropriate
\par
audience for decision analysis and to help this
\par
audience become comfortable with probabilistic
\par
simulation models. Meeting this second chal-
\par
lenge may require a better understanding of the
\par
interactions between decision making behaviour
\par
and formal decision analysis. The third and
\par
most important task is that research must dem-
\par
onstrate the benefits of decision analysis, for
\par
either the process of making clinical decisions
\par
or the outcomes in actual patients.
\par
If the field of decision analysis cannot meet
\par
these challenges, we believe it is in danger of
\par
remaining an esoteric exercise within academic
\par
medicine which will continue to have limited
\par
impact on clinical practice.
\par
