  doi = {10.1007/BF01874732},
  urldate = {2024-05-03},
  abstract = {This paper discusses the rationale for the use of additive models involving multiple objectives as approximations to normative analyses. Experience has shown us that organizations often evaluate important decisions with multiple objective models rather than reducing all aspects of the problem to a single criterion, dollars, as many normative economic models prescribe. We justify this practice on two grounds: managers often prefer to think about a problem in terms of several dimensions and a multiple objective model may provide an excellent approximation to the more complex normative model. We argue that a useful analysis based on a multiple objective model will fulfill both conditions - it will provide insights for the decision maker as well as a good approximation to the normative model.},
  copyright = {http://www.springer.com/tdm},
  langid = {english},
  note = {Annals of Operations Research 2(1985)39 -58 39
\par
U S I N G M O D E L S
\par
M U L T I P L E O B J E C T I V E S T O A P P R O X I M A T E N O R M A T I V E
\par
James S. DYER
\par
and
\par
John B. LARSEN
\par
\emph{College of Business Administration, The University of Texas at Austin,}
\par
\emph{Management Department, Austin, Texas 78712, USA}
\par
A b s t r a c t
\par
This paper discusses the rationale for the use of additive models involving
\par
multiple objectives as approximations to normative analyses. Experience has shown
\par
us that organizations often evaluate important decisions with multiple objective
\par
models rather than reducing all aspects of the problem to a single criterion, dollars,
\par
as many normative economic models prescribe. We justify this practice on two
\par
grounds: managers often prefer to think about a problem in terms of several dimen-
\par
sions and a multiple objective model may provide an excellent approximation to
\par
the more complex normative model. We argue that a useful analysis based on a
\par
multiple objective model will fulfill both conditions - it will provide insights for
\par
the decision maker as well as a good approximation to the normative model.
\par
We report several real-world examples of managers using multiple objective
\par
models to approximate such normative models as the risk-adjusted net present value
\par
and the value of information models. The agreement between the approximate
\par
models and the normative models is shown to be quite good. Next, we cite a
\par
portion of the behavioral decision theory literature which establishes that linear
\par
models of multiple attributes provide quite robust approximations to individual
\par
decision-making processes. We then present more general theoretical and empirical
\par
results which support our contention that linear multiple attribute models can
\par
provide good approximations to more complex models.
\par
K e y w o r d s
\par
Normatives, objectives, multiples, approximations, models.
\par
1. I n t r o d u c t i o n
\par
The purpose of this paper is to discuss the rationale for the use of models
\par
involving multiple objectives as approximations to normative analyses. Most models
\par
presented in normative economic theory conclude that the decisions of a firm can be
\par
evaluated in terms of a single criterion, dollars. However, it has been our experience
\par
9 J.C. Baltzer A.G., Scientific Publishing Company40 \emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives}
\par
that many organizations use models with multiple objectives to evaluate important
\par
decisions rather than seeking to summarize all of the information in terms of a single
\par
criterion. This practice can be justified on two grounds. First, as a practical matter,
\par
many managers prefer to think of a problem in temls of several dimensions and wish
\par
to see an analysis that separates these dimensions into more easily assessed and under-
\par
stood parts. For example, a manager may wish to consider both the expected profit-
\par
ability and the risk associated with an investment decision, and he might be dissatisfied
\par
with an analysis that presents an expected return estimate as the result. Even a more
\par
sophisticated normative analysis that computes the estimated distribution of the
\par
anticipated returns may be difficult to comprehend and to compare to distributions
\par
of other alternatives.
\par
The second rationale is that a model based on multiple objectives may provide
\par
an excellent approximation to a more complex mathematical model, and thus produce
\par
results that are highly correlated with those from a more complex normative analysis.
\par
In order to be useful in practice, an analysis based on an additive model involving
\par
multiple objectives must satisfy this criterion of providing a good approximation as
\par
well as the practical criterion of providing more insights for the decision maker. We
\par
shall argue that a good analysis will actually do both.
\par
The literature in the field of behavorial decision theory contains several papers
\par
establishing that linear models of multiple attributes provide robust approximations to
\par
the decision-making processes of individuals. These studies have been focused on situ-
\par
ations requiring judgments where it seems natural to use multiple attributes since there
\par
is no monetary basis for the decisions. A classical example is provided by Dawes [4]
\par
who investigates judgments regarding graduate school admission decisions. We argue
\par
that these results are relevant for major strategic decisions in the private sector as well.
\par
Several examples of actual applications of the use of multiple objectives to analyze
\par
strategic decisions will be cited to illustrate this claim.
\par
The outline of this paper will be as follows. The rationale for the use of ap-
\par
proximate models based on multiple objectives is presented in the following section.
\par
This rationale is illustrated with several examples. Section 3 contains a discussion of
\par
the theoretical results that justify the use of approximate models as described in
\par
sect. 2. The conclusions and a brief summary are in sect. 3.
\par
2. A p p r o x i m a t e m o d e l s
\par
Linear models involving multiple objectives or attributes are often used to
\par
approximate normative analyses in real world situations. We refer to this practice as
\par
'approximate modeling' to emphasize that the analysis is not simply an ad hoc appli-
\par
cation of subjective models, but that these models can be justified empirically and
\par
analytically as providing good approximations to normative models. We begin by
\par
discussing the PGS model, where the initials refer to 'profitability', 'growth', and\emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives }41
\par
'security'. We have seen this model used in several guises in our consulting experiences
\par
in industry. Next, we consider the linearization of the normative model for calculating
\par
the value of information associated with a test or an exploration activity, and again
\par
provide examples of its use. Finally, we summarize the application of a linear model
\par
to the problem of evaluating research proposals. This linear model approximates a
\par
multiplicative normative model given a robust set of assumptions.
\par
2.1. THE PGS MODEL
\par
Consider the case of a manager who must make a major strategic decision
\par
requiring the commitment of a signicant proportion of the firm's resources. It has been
\par
our experience that when he or she talks about this decision, it is in terms of three
\par
fundamental factors. First, there is the short-term profitability associated with the
\par
decision based on the outcomes that can be forecasted with relatively little uncertainty.
\par
Even though the manager may feel confortable comparing the alternative decisions in
\par
terms of relative magnitude on this attribute, it still may be very difficult to obtain
\par
sufficient information to conduct a traditional normative analysis resulting in a risk-
\par
adjusted net present value (NPV) estimate for each alternative. Second, the manager
\par
will often comment on the long-run consequences of choosing each alternative, some
\par
of which may be easy to quantify in principle (but not in practice) and some of which
\par
are related to more subjective issues associated with the 'corporate culture'. The impli-
\par
cations of the alternatives for the long-term growth and development of the company
\par
are the primary issues in this discussion. Third, the manager will refer to the level of
\par
risk associated with each alternative or, in a more positive manner, describe the contri-
\par
butions of each to improving the security of the organization.
\par
These three factors, short-run profitability, long-term growth, and security,
\par
have been ubiquitous in our consulting experiences with major corporations, and have
\par
been identified as the PGS model as a result. Other authors have noted this distinction
\par
as well. For example, Ruefli and Sarrazin [17] have based their theory of optimal
\par
control of strategic decisions on the notion of tailoring the short-run tactics of the
\par
firm toward the objective of meeting long-run growth goals that can be defined only in
\par
a fuzzy manner. Given that our observations regarding the PGS model are correct, we
\par
began to consider the link between this intuitively appealing approach to the evaluation
\par
of strategic decisions and the normative theory regarding the basis for a risky invest-
\par
ment decision over a long time horizon.
\par
We are interested in the conditions under which a normative model for the
\par
evaluation of risky income streams can be approximated by a linear model. Suppose
\par
we consider first the simple case of a single time period, and assume the traditional
\par
normative objective of maximizing expected utility. It is well known that this ob-
\par
jective is equivalent to a two-attribute linear model involving the mean and variance
\par
of the risky returns of the altematives when these returns are normally distributed and42 \emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives}
\par
the decision maker has a quadratic utility function. Both of these assumptions are un-
\par
realistic in practice, so the mean-variance model is often dismissed as inadequate as a
\par
normative model of risky decision making.
\par
Instead of requiring that the mean-variance model be strictly accurate as a
\par
surrogate for the normative model of utility maximization, suppose we ask when it
\par
is a good \emph{approximation }to this model. More appealing normative assumptions of
\par
constant risk aversion or decreasing risk aversion suggest that either the exponential
\par
or the logarithmic utility functions would be more appropriate for a formal analysis
\par
than the quadratic function. Tsaing [18] and others have used the Taylor series expan-
\par
sion to approximate both the exponential and the logarithmic utility functions with
\par
linear functions of the mean and the variance of the risky returns of options. For
\par
example, if the decision maker's utility function u(y) is of the exponential form
\par
