In these forms the parameters a and a determine the risk attitude of the decision
\par
maker, and these parameters appear in the coefficients of the variance terms in these
\par
two expansions. Thus, both the exponential and the logarithmic utility function can
\par
be approximated by a linear model involving the mean and the variance of the risky
\par
distributions where the weight on the variance term is determined by the risk attitude
\par
of the decision maker.
\par
Tsaing [18] gives the conditions under which these approximations are reason-
\par
able, and concludes that they will be satisfied in most real-world situations. Levy and
\par
Markowitz [14] also attempt to approximate the normative model of utility maximiza-
\par
tion with a model based on means and variances. They derive a general parameterized
\par
family of approximations that are shown to correlate very well with the normative
\par
model over a wide range of conditions. These approximations are not all simple linear\emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives }43
\par
models of the mean and variance of a distribution, but they are functions of the mean,
\par
the variance, and of the utility function evaluated at a fixed value. These results also
\par
support the argument that the normative model of expected utility maximization can
\par
be approximated closely with a function involving multiple attributes with parameters
\par
determined by the risk attitude of the decision maker. A similar point is made by
\par
Pulley [16] based on the approximation of the logarithmic utility function by a simple
\par
linear model that is shown to be an excellent surrogate in a simulation study.
\par
Relating these results to the PGS model, we can ignore the second attribute of
\par
long-term growth since we are focusing on a single period model. If we think of short-
\par
run profitability P in terms of the mean of the distribution of the risky returns, and
\par
security S in terms of its variance, we do have sufficient justification to argue that the
\par
linear model \emph{P - wS }would be an excellent approximation to the normative model of
\par
maximizing expected utility for most real-world problems. The choice of the weight w
\par
would depend on the risk attitude of the decision maker. Note that an alternative
\par
interpretation of the term \emph{wS }would be that of a 'risk premium', defined in the
\par
normative literature as the difference between the expected value of a risky alterna-
\par
tive and the certain equivalent that the decision maker would be willing to accept in
\par
its place.
\par
The intuitive extension of this argument to the multiple period case is rela-
\par
tively straightforward. As we have observed, managers often refer to the short-run
\par
consequences of a strategic decision as being relatively risk free, and conceptually
\par
aggregate them as one number. However, the longer run implications for corporate
\par
growth and success are much less certain, in part because of competitive reactions,
\par
changes in government policies, technological innovations, and other exogenous
\par
factors. Nevertheless, they can also typically rank one alternative as being better than
\par
another in terms of its 'potential' for contributing to long-term growth. Applying
\par
this concept to a normative model for evaluating a risky income stream over multiple
\par
time periods, we would separate this income stream into two parts, short-term and
\par
long-term, and treat each one separately. The basis for this separation would be the
\par
degree of certainty associated with the estimate of the returns in each time period
\par
rather than the number of time periods per se.
\par
If the short-term returns are known with certainty, they could be discounted
\par
to arrive at an estimate of their net value using the traditional NPV model and a 'risk
\par
free' discount rate. The long-term returns would be much more difficult to summarize
\par
with a single number. Instead, we might make an estimate of the expected return in
\par
each period, and then subtract a risk premium much as argued before. We could then
\par
discount these certain equivalents at a risk-free rate, and add them to our short-term
\par
estimates. These calculations would be consistent with accepted normative economic
\par
practices, but they would also lead to a result that could be summarized by the linear
\par
model P + w 1 G - \emph{w2S, }where P is the discounted sum of short-term returns, G is the
\par
discounted sum of the expected values of the long-term returns, w 1 is a discount rate\emph{44 J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives}
\par
applied to G depending on the time period used to separate short-term from long-term
\par
returns, and \emph{w2S }is the discounted sum of the risk premiums that depend on the risk
\par
attitude of the decision maker.
\par
This argument for the use of the linear P \emph{+ w 1 G - w2S }model is ad hoc, and is
\par
bases more on intuitive notions than on a rigorous analysis. Therefore, it seems appro-
\par
priate to describe several of the examples of its actual use in real-world problems in
\par
order to demonstrate that it does appear as the conceptual basis for the application of
\par
models involving multiple objectives in practice.
\par
