2.2. THE VALUE OF THE INFORMATION MODEL
\par
As part of a decision analysis of a problem, the value of perfect information
\par
can be calculated by computing the expected value of the best alternative and then
\par
subtracting the expected value of the best alternative decision made \emph{after }the elimina-
\par
tion of the uncertainty associated with an event. Although this concept is simple in
\par
theory, it is relatively difficult to implement in practice since a decision tree repre-
\par
sentation of the problem is generally associated with the analysis and the expected
\par
values must be calculated based on assessed probabilities and outcomes. Yet there are
\par
some areas of application where decisions regarding the value of the information
\par
associated with a test or a data collection activity must be made on a routine basis.
\par
The collection of all of the data required by a formal analysis of the value of this
\par
information is not a practical alternative. Two noteworthy examples include the pilot
\par
tests of social programs by the Office of Research, Demonstration, and Evaluation at
\par
the Texas Department of Human Resources (TDHR) and exploration activities focused
\par
on the evaluation of potential mineral deposits. In both of these cases we have de-
\par
veloped a simple linear model to approximate the results of the more complex norma-
\par
tive analysis.
\par
A pilot test conducted by the Office of Research, Demonstration, and Evalua-
\par
tion at the TDHR is often an implementation of a proposed social program or policy
\par
in a limited geographic area, with extensive pre-test and post-test measurements to
\par
determine impacts. These pilot tests can vary significantly in terms of costs and man-
\par
power requirements. We would like to ensure that the value of the information provided
\par
by a test justifies its costs. Further, there may be occasions when the Office of Research
\par
simply does not have sufficient resources to test every candidate hypothesis as re-
\par
quested by program managers. In such an environment, a procedure is needed for
\par
establishing priorities among these competing requests.
\par
The first task in attempting to establish a simple linear model as a surrogate
\par
for a value of information analysis is to identify the critical factors, or attributes,
\par
to be used in the model. After analyzing the formal value of information calculations,
\par
we determined these four factors to be the following:
\par
1. the accuracy of the test,
\par
2. the cost of the test,
\par
3. the truth of the hypothesis, and
\par
4. the criticality of the proposed program intervention.48 \emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives}
\par
The latter concept refers to the cost of the proposed program, as well as the potential
\par
benefits which may not be measurable in monetary terms. Loosely speaking, a large,
\par
expensive program with significant potential benefits for its target population would
\par
be rated high on the criticality factor.
\par
Our objective was to prioritize tests by rating them on a seven point scale on
\par
each of these four factors, and then using the weighted sum of these ratings as a basis
\par
for selecting the tests to be implemented. We determined from interviews that person-
\par
nel of the Office of Research were comfortable with the task of rating a proposed test
\par
on these four factors, so we only needed the weights to implement the model.
\par
These weights were determined from a discriminant analysis performed on the
\par
ratings of a number of fictitious test descriptions by TDHR managers and researchers.
\par
A discriminant analysis develops weights for several factors and a cut-off score to
\par
classify alternatives into one of two categories. In this case, we wished to classify the
\par
alternatives into the categories 'the value of the information provided by the test ex-
\par
ceeds its cost', and 'the cost of the test exceeds the value of the information'. We
\par
supplied sufficient information in the fictitious test descriptions rated on these four
\par
factors by the TDHR personnel so that we could actually calculate the value of the
\par
information provided by the test using the normative model. Thus, we had an un-
\par
ambiguous basis for classifying these cases into one of these two categories.
\par
The weights assigned to the four factors by the discriminant analysis were
\par
determined to be the following:
