\par
A second study similar in spirit but very different in the methodological ap-
\par
proach was conducted for a major energy company in order to rank mineral explora-
\par
tion projects for the assignment of limited manpower resources. We adopted the view-
\par
point that the problem of allocating manpower to exploration projects is essentially
\par
one of maximizing the expected value of the information provided by their efforts.
\par
The activities of these personnel are not directly revenue-producing ones; rather, they
\par
generate information through seismic tests and other data collection activities that
\par
alter the implicit estimates of the likelihood of discovering a commercial mineral
\par
deposit on a project. The value of this information can be estimated, at least in theory,
\par
using the normative techniques of decision analysis.
\par
We are not the first to propose this conceptual view of exploration activities.
\par
This problem was posed and analyzed in the classical textbook on decision analysis
\par
by Pratt, Raiffa and Schlaifer [15] published about twenty years ago. However, we
\par
are not aware of any successful attempts to operationalize this model as a tool for
\par
decision making. Since the value of information paradigm is theoretically sound, we
\par
conclude that it has never been implemented in this context because it fails to meet
\par
its own criterion for success; that is, the value of the information produced by the
\par
normative model is not considered to be worth the cost of the required data col-
\par
lection and data analysis activities. The challenge of implementing this model must
\par
be met by an effort to simplify the time and monetary costs associated with its use.
