\par
Rolling back the normative model in fig. 4, we obtain the following result for
\par
the value of information
\par
VI = \emph{P ( G ) [ R * P ( S / G ) - C] - E - }max(O,[R* \emph{P ( S ) - }C]),
\par
where the 'maximum' in the latter term allows for the possibility that the decision
\par
to drop a play at a cost of zero will be preferred if the expected cost of mining with-
\par
out additional exploration is negative. While even this normative model includes
\par
several simplifications of the real-world problem, including the assumption of either
\par
a 'good' or a 'bad' outcome from exploration and the assumption of a static decision
\par
process, the result is relatively complex and discourages any attempts to develop an
\par
intuitive understanding of the relative importance of the variables.
\par
In order to assist in interpreting the results and to facilitate the more de-
\par
tailed multi-attribute evaluation of projects as explained below, we developed a linear
\par
model involving the same variables in the equation for VI. The weights in this additive
\par
model were determined through a novel use of Monte Carlo simulation and regression.
\par
The first step was to determine the actual ranges for each of the variables in this model
\par
based on historical data and subjective judgments. For example, the distribution of
\par
actual mining costs C was available from historical data, while the ranges for the proba-\emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives 51}
\par
bilities were determined subjectively. We assumed a triangular distribution and ob-
\par
tained estimates of the 'lowest', 'highest', and 'most likely' values of each variable.
\par
Monte Carlo sampling was then used to obtain a set of values for each of these
\par
variables by randomly selecting a value from each of the corresponding distributions.
\par
The value of information associated with these values was calculated from the norma-
\par
tive model above. This process was repeated until a sufficient sample size was generated
\par
to perform a simple linear regression to determine the weights. The randomly selected
\par
values of the variables were treated as the independent variables, and the correspond-
\par
ing value of information calculated from the normative model was used as the de-
\par
pendent variable.
\par
After the determination of the weights in this manner, the validity of the
\par
approximate linear model was checked through another Monte Carlo simulation.
\par
Random values were again generated from the same distributions for each variable,
\par
and these values were entered into both the normative model and the approximate
\par
linear model. After a suitable sample size was obtained, the Spearman rank order
\par
correlation statistic was computed between the paired results of the two models. A
\par
result of over 0.9 was found for this statistic, and judged to be sufficient to accept
\par
the linear model as a surrogate for the normative model.
\par
One difficulty remained to be resolved. The values of these variables were
\par
judged to be too difficult to estimate directly for each of the projects under considera-
\par
tion, which could number 40 or more on an annual basis. Therefore, a linear multi-
\par
attribute model was developed to estimate each of the variables in the normative
\par
model. These attributes had already been used by exploration personnel in an ad hoc
\par
evaluation of potential projects, and were relatively easy to assess. A mapping was
\par
developed between these attributes and the variables in the value of information
\par
model. This mapping was based on the logical relationships between the attributes and
\par
the variables. For example, the attribute 'remoteness of the site' was related to the
\par
cost of mining C and the cost of exploration E, but not to the estimated returns R. In
\par
this manner, we were able to derive weights for a linear model of the attributes where
\par
the weights on each attribute were calculated from the weights on the variables and
\par
these logical relationships.
\par
This process was important to the implementation of the evaluation procedure
\par
since the final result was a simple linear model involving attributes already familiar to
\par
the users. The analysis provided a theoretically sound way of determining the relative
\par
importance weights for these attributes. This entire procedure was programmed and
\par
implemented on an IBM PC which allows new projects to be evaluated annually with
\par
only minor computational burden on the users.
\par
2.3. THE EVALUATION OF RESEARCH PROPOSALS
\par
The final example describes another study similar in spirit to the mineral ex-
\par
ploration study but with an interesting twist. In this example, practitioners were using52 \emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives}
\par
an intuitively appealing but admittedly ad hoc linear model for ranking research
\par
proposals. The formal analysis determined that the appropriate normative model
\par
should be multiplicative instead, but when the results of this normative model were
\par
compared with those of the ad hoc linear model, a Spearman rank order correlation
\par
of 0.98 was obtained!
\par
Cardus et al. [2] describe the procedure used by the Rehabilitation Services
\par
Administration (RSA) of the Department of Health and Human Services to prioritize
\par
research proposals. The agency staff would identify research issues, formulate research
\par
concepts, and then solicit proposals. The method then used by RSA to prioritize the
\par
proposals received was a simple weighted linear model based on a peer review process.
\par
Fourteen evaluation criteria were selected by RSA and weights were assigned to them
\par
for each research concept. Each peer reviewer for a project concept evaluated all of
\par
the competing proposals on each of the fourteen criteria using a seven point scale, and
\par
the weighted score for each proposal was then calculated. A final score was assigned to
\par
each proposal by averaging scores across raters.
\par
Cardus et al. developed a normative model of the proposal review decision,
\par
and determined that it should be multiplicative. For example, they argued that the
\par
appropriate model should include probabilities, such as the probability of success for
\par
the research effort, that should be multiplied by other factors. The general form of
\par
their proposed normative model is
\par
\emph{B = Ps(PuNBI + }Bs) + \emph{B v ,}
\par
where B is the expected net benefit of the project, Ps is the probability of the research
\par
being successful, Pu is the probability of the research being utilized given that it is
\par
successful, N is the number of individuals in the target population, B I is the expected
\par
benefit per person if the research is successful, B s is the indirect benefit if it is success-
\par
ful, and B F is the benefit of funding the research regardless of project success.
\par
For a given project concept, the authors noted that this model can be simplified
\par
considerably. The benefits of funding the research regardless of proposal success were
\par
judged to be constant over all proposals, so this variable B F was dropped from the
\par
model. Also, N is a constant for a given project concept, as are the maximum values
\par
of B I and B s. Thus the revised model included estimates of the proportions of the
\par
maximum individual benefits and the maximum indirect benefits that would result
\par
from the success of each proposal.
\par
In order to implement this model, Cardus et al. developed a mapping of the
\par
evaluation criteria previously used by RSA into the variables of the normative model
\par
in a manner similar to the procedure followed in the mineral exploration example.
\par
Two of these criteria were determined to be unrelated to the variables in the normative
\par
model, but were used to evaluate the adequacy of the financial management practices
\par
of the firm submitting the proposal. A new term was added to the normative model as\emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives }53
\par
a result of this insight. The rankings of proposals using the revised normative model
\par
were then compared with those from the traditional linear model previously in use by
\par
RSA. The reported Spearman rank correlations across seventeen different project con-
\par
cepts ranged from 0.88 to 1.0 with a mean of 0.98. Based on this result, it seems
\par
reasonable to conclude that the linear model developed on the basis of intuitive
\par
reasoning by the RSA provides an excellent approximation to the appropriate norma-
\par
tive model developed by Cardus et al. [2].
\par
3. T o w a r d a t h e o r y o f a p p r o x i m a t e m o d e l i n g
\par
The previous section has presented several examples of the use of linear models
\par
involving multiple objectives, attributes, or criteria that have been used to approximate
\par
normative models of a much more complex form. Justification for the use of these
\par
linear models was either based on logical arguments or on empirical comparisons with
\par
the results of the normative models. In this section we review the more general empiric-
\par
al and theoretical results that support this strategy of developing approximate models.
\par
The conclusions of this paper that approximate models based on linear com-
\par
binations of multiple objectives can be substituted in many cases for more complex
\par
normative models would not surprise a scholar in the area of behavioral decision
\par
theory. A stream of research in this area has shown that linear models of multiple
\par
attributes provide very robust approximations to the judgments made by decision
\par
makers in situations where expert judgment is required. Dawes [4] describes a classical
\par
study of the admission decision for graduate school. Applicants were evaluated on the
\par
basis of three criteria, GRE scores, undergraduate grade point average, and letters of
\par
recommendation. Implicitly, the admissions committee was asked to use these criteria
\par
to forecast the success of individuals in graduate school. Dawes showed that linear
\par
models of the criteria could be used in two contexts: to predict the success of appli-
\par
cants and to predict the judgments of the admissions committee. A startling result of
\par
his study was that the linear model of the admissions committee actually out-performed
\par
the committee in predicting the success of applicants!
\par
Dawes and Corrigan [6] cite other studies which reach a similar conclusion, and
\par
note that the contexts of the decisions in these studies all had common characteristics:
\par
each independent variable had a monotonic relationship with the dependent variable,
\par
error of measurement is a problem, and deviations from the optimal weighting do not
\par
make much practical difference. With regard to the latter point, they show that if the
\par
criteria are all scaled so that they are comparable, and if the correct signs are chosen
\par
for the weights, then the weights can be randomly generated without significantly im-
\par
pairing the performance of the model. They conclude that ' . . . the whole trick is to
\par
decide what variables to look at and then to know how to add'. Einhom and Hogarth
\par
[9] champion the view that equal weights can be used with no significant loss in the
\par
performance of linear models.54 \emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple ob/ectives}
\par
This line of research does provide strong empirical evidence that linear models
\par
can be used successfully to predict human judgments, but can they also be used to
\par
predict the results from more complex normative models? We now consider some
\par
emerging evidence regarding this important point.
\par
Bunch et al. [1] have investigated the conditions under which a linear model
\par
provides a suitable approximation to a multiplicative model. This research was in-
\par
spired by their surprising empirical results in the case of the proposal ranking study
\par
for the Rehabilitation Services Administration. They considered the simplified model
\par
\emph{PsPuPR, }where Ps is the probability that the research will be successful, Pu is the
\par
probability that the results will be useful given than it is successful, and PR is the
\par
proportion of the research objectives associated with the project concept addressed by
\par
the project proposal. As before, they assume that the criteria in the linear approxima-
\par
tion can be mapped into the values in the multiplicative model on the interval (Fy, 1),
\par
where 0 {$<$} F y {$<$} 1, and Fy is the minimum possible level of achievement of the
\par
variable \emph{Py, }where Y = S, U, and R.
\par
The results of this study demonstrate that ' . , . under certain conditions which
\par
are often met in actual practice', the simple additive model will closely approximate
\par
the multiplicative model. One such condition is that the scores on each multiplicative
\par
variable must be at least 0.8. Since most research proposals submitted to RSA and
\par
not yet rejected as obviously inferior satisfy this criterion, this insight helps to explain
\par
the high correlation between the results of their normative model and the simple linear
\par
one used previously. The second condition is that the sum of the weights on those
\par
criteria in the additive model which are 'mapped' into a variable in the multiplicative
\par
model must be proportional to the potential gain G that a proposal could make to
\par
that variable in a research activity. Loosely speaking, this condition requires a relation-
\par
ship between the sum of the weights on the criteria associated with a variable in the
\par
linear model and the \emph{range }over which it varies in the multiplicative model.
\par
Kumar and Leone [13] have conducted an extensive empirical study of the use
\par
of the regression approach to develop a linear approximation to the normative model
\par
for the value of information introduced in the discussion of the mineral exploration
\par
example. Four regression models were determined from four sets of real-world data re-
\par
garding the distributions of the variables in the normative model. A total of 243 esti-
\par
mates of the value of information were obtained from the normative model and com-
\par
pared with 243 estimates from the regression approximation for corresponding sets of
\par
values of the variables in the model. This procedure was carried out for each of the
\par
four sets of data. The Spearman rank order correlations for these four sets of data
\par
ranged from 0.63 to 0.98. However, when these correlations were computed for only
\par
the top 50 alternatives, the range was from 0.97 to 0.99. Finally, suppose the purpose
\par
of the approximate linear model is simply to choose the top 'n' alternatives for ex-
\par
ploration (as was the case in our actual application). The linear model correctly
\par
identified at least 90 percent of the top 20 alternatives as determined by the normative
\par
model, and 98 percent of the top 50 alternatives.\emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives }55
\par
Kumar [12] has continued to focus on this problem by investigating the corre-
\par
lation between the simple multiplicative model \emph{uo }and the linear model u + o. Using a
\par
Taylor series expansion, he obtained the result
\par
\emph{uv = iZvU + la u v - laul.tv ,}
\par
which shows that the best linear approximation is obtained when each variable is
\par
weighted by the mean of the other variable. More complex models can be analyzed in
\par
a similar way. For example, the Taylor series expansion of the term \emph{uo \textasciitilde{} }is approxi-
\par
mately
\par
\emph{uv = lav\textasciitilde{} u + 2 \# u \# v V - 2\textasciitilde u\#20.}
\par
These results were verified by a simulation study, and a comparison with the regression
\par
models developed as described in the exploration study showed remarkable agreement.
\par
These simulation studies have also investigated the sensitivity of the approximation to
\par
the correlation between u and o, and the magnitude of u relative to o. The results
\par
support and extend the work of Bunch et al. [1].
\par
Kleinmuntz [1 1] has obtained results which show that even the choice of the
\par
attributes in an approximate linear model may not be crucial! He begins by investi-
\par
gating the impact of omitting an attribute in an analysis. Suppose we define
