\par
An empirical investigation of these results shows that the additive models are
\par
not sensitive to the choices of the attributes under many realistic conditions, but also
\par
demonstrates some instances in which the choices are extremely important. The latter
\par
result can also be used to examine and extend the results of Kumar [12]. Suppose that
\par
Y is the multiplicative model \emph{uv }and that Y' is the additive approximation u + u. Since
\par
we can find the correlation between these two models from Kumar's results, we can
\par
use Kleinmuntz's study to determine the correlation between x + uu and x + u + u.
\par
By bootstrapping in this manner, we can find the correlations between many complex
\par
normative models involving nonlinear terms and their linear approximations.
\par
4. S u m m a r y and conclusions
\par
We have provided a rationale for the use of linear models involving multiple
\par
objectives as approximations of normative models. This rationale was introduced on
\par
the basis of logical persuasion, but was supported by both empirical and theoretical
\par
results. The fact that this practice is common in both private industry and government
\par
was documented by several examples.
\par
While the empirical and theoretical evidence do argue that these approximate
\par
models may not be sensitive to errors in the selection of the proper weights or even of
\par
the proper attributes, we should not be cavalier in our use of them. Even the most
\par
robust model can be pushed to the point where its results are meaningless. Further,
\par
there are instances when the choice of the weights can be critical, especially when we
\par
are attempting to select the 'best' alternative from a set rather than simply identifying
\par
the twenty best. Anyone who does not believe that the choice of the weights on the
\par
attributes in a linear model have any importance should attempt to convince a mathe-
\par
matical programmer that the coefficients of his objective function are irrelevant and
\par
can be selected randomly.
\par
The example of the mineral exploration problem is particularly interesting as
\par
an area for further study since the normative model was actually used to determine
\par
the weights on the linear approximation. We argue that this approach is superior in
\par
many cases to asking decision makers to assign weights subjectively to attributes,
\par
especially in complex problems. This strategy was also used in a related study by Dyer
\par
and Lund [8], and may hold promise for developing a theoretically sound rationale for
\par
assigning weights to the components of the PGS model. This marriage of the rigor of
\par
the normative model with the simplicity of an approximate linear model may be a
\par
key to implementation of many decision aids in the future.58 \emph{J.S. Dyer, J.B. Larsen, Using multiple objectives}
