FOREWORD: THE COURT AND THE ECONOMIC SYSTEM
\par
\emph{Frank H. Easterbrook*}
\par
T \textbf{HE }Supreme Court is a regulator. Much of the time the Court
\par
regulates the operations of government. It reviews what other
\par
branches have done and claims the last word. As government grows
\par
in relation to the private sector, the Court's role as governor of the
\par
government seems to grow more than proportionally.
\par
Once, the Court was principally an arbiter of disputes among
\par
private parties. Tort, contract, and statutory cases between private
\par
adversaries claimed the bulk of its time. Today, with a governmental
\par
agency often a surrogate for one of the parties, the Court still spends
\par
a great deal of time resolving similar disputes. Yet they are rarely
\par
studied as a group. While scholars write of "constitutional law" as if
\par
it were informed by one set of premises and one method of argument,1
\par
they write of economic issues as if the issues had little in common.
\par
Students of the Supreme Court's antitrust cases do not hint that
\par
admiralty and securities and standing doctrines might be derived by
\par
the same methods. The principal nonconstitutional subjects \textbf{- }ad-
\par
ministrative law, civil procedure, securities, antitrust, copyright, ad-
\par
miralty, substantive criminal law, and so on - are sorted into separate
\par
bins for separate study.
\par
My thesis is that this is wrong. A number of unifying principles
\par
influence the legal rules that govern economic conduct. These rules
\par
have changed over the last forty years, and the Court's jurisprudence
\par
has changed with them. The changes are not just matters of "con-
\par
servative" judges replacing "liberal" ones, or the reverse; there has
\par
been a substantial change in how all the Justices perceive economic
\par
issues. The Justices today are more sophisticated in economic reason-
