\par
ing, and they apply it in a more thoroughgoing way, than at any other
\par
time in our history.
\par
This is not a claim that can be "proved." I propose to offer
\par
illustrations in lieu of deductive proof. Most of my illustrations are
\par
drawn from the 1983 Term. I examine a series of cases that present
\par
economic questions - whether or not the Justices recognized or dis-
\par
cussed these questions - and contrast these decisions with opinions
\par
on similar subjects from the 1973, 1963, and I953 Terms. I propose
\par
a number of criteria by which to evaluate how well the Justices
\par
understand the workings of the economic system and the effects of
\par
legal rules.
\par
Part I shows why judges are bound to have difficulty in handling
\par
cases involving the operation of the economic sphere. These difficul-
\par
ties arise from the structure of legal dispute resolution as well as from
\par
the lack of appropriate incentives and training. Part I also introduces
\par
the three economic themes for which I look in the cases: ex ante
\par
analysis, understanding effects on the margin, and appreciating the
\par
interest-group underpinnings of statutes. Parts I through IV consider
\par
how these three economic principles permeate the Court's work.
\par
I. INTRODUCTION
\par
\textbf{\emph{A. }}\emph{Disputes and Rules}
\par
Judges both resolve disputes and create rules. For a long time
\par
courts portrayed rule creation as a by-product of dispute resolution.
\par
The court had to decide the case, and in order to show that its decision
\par
was not capricious it often had to announce a rule to govern future
\par
cases. The need to decide impelled the creation of a rule. This is
\par
appropriate for a court with mandatory jurisdiction. But the Supreme
\par
Court possesses discretionary jurisdiction, designed so that the Justices
\par
may concentrate on creating rules for the guidance of others. Today
\par
cases often are just excuses for the creation or alteration of rules.
\par
Good or bad, this is the consequence of certiorari jurisdiction.
\par
The dispute-resolution and rule-creation functions of the Court tug
\par
the Justices in different directions. Dispute resolution is backward
\par
looking, an exercise in apportioning gains and losses given the occur-
\par
rence of some known injury or conflict. The "equities" of the dispute
\par
\textbf{- }the good faith of the parties, their knowledge of the rules, and so
\par
on \textbf{- }will seem important to a fair disposition. Rule creation is
\par
forward looking, and a rule knows not its subjects. The rule seeks
\par
to induce people to become informed or change their positions. A
\par
judge often is torn between doing justice in the case - that is,
\par
adjusting the apportionment of benefits and detriments among people
\par
who, by the time of litigation, are trapped in positions no longer\emph{HARVARD LAW REVIEW}
\par
[Vol. \textbf{98:4}
\par
within their control \textbf{- }and applying a rule that creates additional
\par
benefits for people who can alter their behavior in the future.
\par
Lower courts may adhere to the traditional model of dispute res-
\par
olution with the assurance that their opinions are not a large source
\par
of rules. The Supreme Court has no such comfort. The pressure to
\par
use cases as occasions for the creation of rules grows continuously.
\par
The dockets of the lower courts expand as legal rules govern more
\par
and more conflicts. The Supreme Court cannot hear more cases,
\par
though, and it copes by deciding more things in each case. As it
\par
announces more rules and tries to explicate its reasons more fully \textbf{-}
\par
the better to govern future disputes \textbf{- }it puts greater strains both on
\par
its ability to resolve disputes and on internal harmony. The more the
\par
Court tries to say, the more grounds for disagreement.2 Justices may
\par
complain that the Court's reasons are broader than necessary to re-
\par
solve the dispute at hand (which often is true but beside the point) or
\par
even that the opinions contain hints of things to come. 3
\par
Many have written about the growth of the rule-creation function
\par
and about how constitutional decisions influence the structure and
\par
conduct of government. 4 Yet constitutional rules are unusual, because
\par
their addressees are the lower courts. The Supreme Court tells lower
