\par
the conduct of those outside the judicial system. Legislatures go on
\par
passing laws, fobbing off constitutional questions on the courts. Con-
\par
stitutional rules directed to the police or to the administrators of
\par
schools do not affect the nominal addressees of those rules. The
\par
exclusion of evidence does not harm the officer on the beat or the
\par
injunction the superintendent of schools; their salaries are secure, and
\par
a complex of immunities protects their wealth. The Court's consti-
\par
tutional decisions affect conduct (beyond that of the parties to the
\par
case) only through a series of intermediaries - those who promulgate
\par
administrative rules, those who train and supervise administrators,
\par
those who enforce the law, those who practice it, and other process
\par
providers. Incremental changes, not strong private incentives, trans-
\par
mit the effects of the Court's decisions. If the Court's decisions are
\par
unclear or its signals mixed, that is regrettable but remediable; each
\par
decision is just a nudge to the legal culture.
\par
Many of the Court's nonconstitutional decisions, though, are de-
\par
signed to influence the conduct of people outside the government.
\par
Regulation of the government, by the government, is the exception.
\par
When the Court tries to influence the conduct of actors in the economic
\par
sphere, the clarity of rules becomes more important. The tensions
\par
created by the Court's effort to serve both dispute-resolution and rule-
\par
creation functions may undercut its ability to be a constructive influ-
\par
ence. Muddy rules arising from a desire to accommodate the needs
\par
of parties caught in a web of circumstance may have effects quite
\par
unanticipated by the judges. Ambiguity and instability that are tol-
\par
erable when rules are addressed to the government may be intolerable
\par
when the rules are designed to affect private conduct.
\par
There is also a difference in the method by which judges find
\par
acceptable grounds of decision. Much legal argument is reasoning by
\par
analogys The judge may extract from earlier decisions the features
\par
he deems pertinent and determine whether today's dispute has
\par
"enough" in common with the earlier one. But that process does not
\par
offer an answer when the question is how a legal rule affects private
\par
conduct. No number of analogies will help a judge determine whether
\par
a business practice reduces output and thus raises price in violation
\par
of the antitrust laws; no earlier cases show whether the existence of
\par
video cassette recorders increases or reduces the rewards obtained by
\par
the holders of copyrights on movies; no survey of decided cases estab-
\par
lishes whether the burdens of a tax on the wholesale price of liquor
\par
are felt within or without the taxing state.
\par
Economic cases require the judge to answer questions for which
\par
neither law school, legal practice, nor the usual pre-judicial career
\par
