\par
provides the necessary skills. Of course one may say the same about
\par
business managers. Often they thrash about quite innocent of eco-
\par
nomic theory. No matter. Those who offer what consumers want \textbf{-}
\par
by design or by accident - and produce it at low cost will prosper.
\par
Rewards and punishments arise automatically in any market system.
\par
The investor who continually misunderstands the markets soon finds
\par
that he has no money left to invest; those who understand more soon
\par
control the bulk of liquid funds. The manager who lets costs get out
\par
of control or makes things no one wants finds that sales shrink. The
\par
influence of those who misunderstand or mismanage wanes. Not so
\par
with judges. There is no mechanism of selection. No judge \textbf{- }cer-
\par
tainly no federal judge - is subject to discipline for misunderstanding
\par
how markets work or for establishing rules that do the opposite of
\par
what was intended. The form of natural selection that operates in
\par
markets does not do so in courts.
\par
There is, finally, an unsettling problem that afflicts the entire
\par
judicial process: the problem of inconsistency. The law develops in-
\par
crementally. Cases pose discrete problems, and judges resolve them
\par
by voting. Sequential voting 'leads to anomalies. When stare decisis
\par
prevails, the order of decision rather than any substantive principle
\par
may determine the outcome. The Court may decide two cases differ-
\par
ently, each by a vote of 5-4, even though eight Justices believe they
\par
present the same problem. The process of sequential decision leaves
\par
arbitrary or inconsistent precedent embedded in the law. 6 Thus no
\par
theory can explain all cases. Worse for current purposes, the Justices
\par
themselves face a harder task of decision as time passes and the stock
\par
of inconsistencies grows. They cannot suddenly adopt a new theme
\par
or approach to decisionmaking; they cannot find a stable theme in
\par
their cases. The judicial process works against principled, consistent
\par
approaches.
\par
\emph{B. Criteria for Assessing the Court's Performance}
\par
\emph{on Economic Issues}
\par
The Justices may handle economic issues well or poorly. For the
\par
reasons I have covered, they are unlikely to take any principled,
\par
consistent approach to them. Before I set out the criteria by which
\par
the Justices' performance may be measured, I stop to ask whether
\par
broader forces in the legal or economic system may be driving them
\par
toward increased use of economic principles.
\par
We have little reason to expect judges' opinions to reflect any
\par
consistent perspective over the years, let alone one founded on a solid
\par
appreciation of economic principles. Private parties' selection of which
