\par
more when they expect judges to make efficient rulings, which create
\par
a surplus that can be divided among the parties. People find ways to
\par
negotiate around other areas in which judges are apt to make things
\par
worse. On another view, people challenge "bad" rules often, because
\par
they have more to gain by the substitution of better ones. If the rule
\par
is already optimal, litigation just divides fixed stakes; creation of better
\par
rules can increase the stakes subject to division. The ultimate influ-
\par
ence of decisions whether to litigate is uncertain, though, because
\par
private litigants cannot capture all of the benefits of good rules. The
\par
costs of a legal rule may be more concentrated than the gains, and so
\par
people may litigate primarily to impose those costs on others. In any
\par
event, parties' choices do not explain the content of opinions.
\par
Law schools and the popular climate may be more important in
\par
shaping the ways judges analyze cases and express positions. Law
\par
schools transmit legal culture to judges early in their careers, send
\par
new views through law clerks, exert an (indirect) influence on the way
\par
practicing lawyers view issues, and influence the positions taken in
\par
their briefs. The Court frequently deals with economic cases on the
\par
parties' terms, and the size of the stakes often produces careful briefing
\par
that reflects the latest academic thinking. Yet the dominant method
\par
of reasoning by analogy offers little help to judges faced with economic
\par
matters. Economic principles are not prominently emphasized in law
\par
schools, and when they are the professors generally transmit a vision
\par
rich in the virtues of collective action and the failure of markets.
\par
Even parts of the law that are founded on economic propositions long
\par
received little study.8 Similarly, although the terms of popular debate
\par
will have some effect - the current wave of deregulation has high-
\par
lighted problems in, and special-interest support for, some of the
\par
regulatory statutes judges administer - signals sent in this fashion
\par
are always mixed. Some new programs are born while others die,
\par
and exposing the interest-group character of some legislation does not
