\par
The caveat expressed, I lay out three fundamentals by which to
\par
assess the Court's performance in understanding and creating rules
\par
for the economic system. The first is a focus on ex ante analysis, the
\par
second an understanding of marginal effects, and the third an appre-
\par
ciation of the dynamics of the legislative process.
\par
\emph{i. Ex ante and ex post perspectives. }\textbf{\emph{- }}The nature of litigation
\par
invites judges to treat the parties' circumstances as fixed and to ap-
\par
portion gains and losses. Often the application of legal rules requires
\par
no more than that. By the time the judges see the case, it may be
\par
too late for the parties to do anything in response to a decision. Once
\par
ships have collided, the court may see nothing to do except tote up
\par
the losses and order each shipowner to pay half (the rule in admiralty).
\par
Once a manufacturer has fired a dealer, causing the dealer to lose
\par
profits, it may appear best to require the deep-pocket firm to make
\par
whole the loss. Once a firm possesses a patent and tries to extract
\par
royalties, it may seem wise to restrict the devices available to that
\par
end; the royalties lead to less use of the invention and consequent
\par
social loss, while restricting the collection of royalties has no visible
\par
social costs. Once a firm makes a tender offer for the stock of another,
\par
the investors in the target can gain if a court stops the bid long enough
\par
for an auction to develop. A party who has agreed in advance to
\par
arbitrate a dispute may contend that the particular dispute in question
\par
nonetheless should be resolved by a court; he seeks relief from the
\par
adverse consequences of his choice. Similar situations are common in
\par
commercial litigation, and the tendency to take the situation as given
\par
and divide the gains or losses is common too.
\par
When judges take the positions of the parties as given, however,
\par
they forfeit any opportunity to create gains through the formulation
\par
of the legal rule. The principles laid down today will influence
\par
whether similar parties \emph{will be }in similar situations tomorrow. Indeed,
\par
judges who look at cases merely as occasions for the fair apportion-
\par
ment of gains and losses almost invariably ensure that there will be1984]
\par
