\par
fewer gains and more losses tomorrow. When a court divides the
\par
damages between shipowners, it sends the message that one may as
\par
well be careless; it is costly to take care, yet the careful and the
\par
careless are treated alike. When a court restricts the patent holder's
\par
ability to collect royalties, it reduces the rewards anticipated from
\par
patents and thus the incentive for other people to invent. When a
\par
court declines to enforce the arbitration agreement, it makes others
\par
situated similarly to the one who avoided arbitration worse off. These
\par
people no longer can strike one kind of bargain; because they cannot
\par
agree to arbitrate, they cannot receive any compensation for their
\par
forbearance. A right that cannot be the subject of bargaining is worth
\par
less, just as eagle feathers that cannot be sold are worth less to their
\par
owners. \textbf{9}
\par
Often, discovering the correct balance between promised rewards
\par
and realized ones is exceptionally difficult. In the patent case, for
\par
example, it may be impossible to discover the "right" system of rules
\par
that gives incentives to invent yet preserves the optimal use of things
\par
that already have been invented. There is a great tension between
\par
optimal creation and optimal use of information. \textbf{10 }My point is not
\par
that creating incentives for future conduct should be the Court's sole
\par
objective in adjudicating legal disputes, but that the Court is bound
\par
to send the wrong signals to the economic system unless the Justices
\par
appreciate the consequences of legal rules for future behavior.
\par
This may appear obvious. Our legal culture favors utilitarian
\par
arguments. Lawyers routinely make "policy" arguments to courts;
\par
legislatures invoke instrumental claims to show that their statutes are
\par
"rationally related" to some objective that they attain by changing
\par
people's incentives. It is nonetheless startling how often these argu-
\par
ments collapse to claims about "fairness," which in the law almost
\par
always means some appeal to an equitable division of the gains or
\par
losses among existing parties given that certain events have come to
\par
pass. Fairness arguments are ex post arguments," and few lawyers
\par
or judges are comfortable arguing about or deciding a case without
\par
invoking the ideal of fairness. Who is for unfairness?
\par
The degree to which fairness or other ex post arguments dominate
\par
in legal decisionmaking is directly related to the court's assumptions
