\par
about the nature of the economic system. Judges who see economic
\par
transactions as zero-sum games are likely to favor "fair" divisions of
\par
the gains and losses. If the stakes are established in advance and will
\par
not be altered by courts, why should judges harshly require one party
\par
to bear the whole loss or allow another to take the gain? Yet if legal
\par
rules can create larger gains (or larger losses), the claim from fairness
\par
becomes weaker. The judge will pay less attention to today's unfor-
\par
tunates and more attention to the effects of the rules.
\par
Often only this form of attention protects the interest of increased
\par
productivity. The people who might be affected by the rules are not
\par
before the court and may not even be in the affected business (yet).
\par
The interests of prospective consumers and producers are diffuse, too
\par
much so for any one person or group to participate in the litigation.
\par
The judge is the representative of these future interests.
\par
The first line of inquiry, then, is whether the Justices take an ex
\par
ante or an ex post perspective in analyzing issues. Which they take
\par
will depend, in part, on the extent to which they appreciate how the
\par
economic system creates new gains and losses; those who lack this
\par
appreciation will favor "fair" treatment of the parties. Part II shows
\par
how the Court's economic jurisprudence has been evolving from the
\par
ex post toward the ex ante perspective.
\par
\emph{2. Incentives work on many margins. }\textbf{- }Setting out to influence
\par
future conduct is not very useful unless you know how people respond
\par
to incentives. Judges and other public figures understand the Law of
\par
Demand - if you increase the price of something, people will buy
\par
less. Judges also appreciate its generality. Write a tedious opinion
\par
(one that is more "costly" to read), and people will read less of it.
\par
But judges do not always remember why this happens: at the margin
\par
people substitute one product for another. Raise the price of beef,
\par
and people will eat less beef and more chicken. Raise the price of
\par
automobiles, and even people who "need" an automobile to get to
\par
work will buy fewer. Some will switch to other jobs and travel by
\par
bus or train; others will use a car for six years instead of three before
\par
trading it in, effectively substituting repair services for a new car this
\par
year.
\par
Decisions that appear to be dichotomous (car versus no car) turn
\par
out not to be, and even a small change in the price will induce people
\par
to change their behavior ever so slightly. On the other hand, effects
\par
that seem to be large may have no effect on behavior. If the state
\par
suddenly requires every owner of a car to pay a tax of \$i,ooo, but
\par
makes a believable promise never to levy the tax again, the ownership
\par
of cars will be unaffected. People will hold their cars and trade them
\par
in as if the tax had never been levied. Similarly, when the demand
\par
for air travel suddenly falls, so that air carriers no longer can charge
\par
enough to pay the indebtedness on their planes, they do not take the
\par
planes out of service. They may go on operating the planes for an
\par
indefinite period, "losing" money all the while according to their an-\textbf{1984]}
\par
\emph{THE }\textbf{\emph{SUPREME COURT }}\emph{-FOREWORD}
\par
nual reports, so long as the marginal revenues from carrying a load
\par
of passengers exceed the marginal costs (crew, maintenance, and fuel)
\par
of doing so. If they grounded their fleets, they would lose even more
\par
money.
\par
These points are not controversial; they are part of the elementary
\par
kit of economic ideas with which every college student (and thus every
\par
judge) is supplied. Yet the dynamics of litigation often hide marginal
\par
effects from judges. The court sees only the gross effects - averages
\par
rather than the margins on which people are trading. In a copyright
\par
dispute the person who seeks to make "fair use" of the work in
\par
question inevitably argues that even if he can use the work for free,
\par
the copyright holder will be left with "enough" reward. The would-
\par
be user invites attention to the average return rather than to the
\par
marginal effects of the decision. If the lawyer for the copyright holder
\par
simply replies that there is a legal entitlement to a particular royalty,
\par
the point about marginal effects will be obscured. A lawyer opposing
\par
a request for attorneys' fees will argue that the prevailing counsel
\par
makes a very good living, that the fees in this case would be a
\par
windfall, and that they therefore should be reduced. Again the litigant
\par
is inviting attention to the average rather than the margin. Finally,
\par
think of a dispute under the takings clause. The government argues
\par
that after the rights in question have been appropriated or extin-
\par
guished, the complainant retains many valuable rights. The govern-
\par
ment is inviting attention to average effects, while the private party's
\par
future conduct will be influenced by the change at the margin that
\par
makes investments less valuable. Litigation focuses judges' attention
\par
on the division of given stakes, on average rather than marginal costs
\par
(see Part I.A.).
\par
When the Court misses these marginal effects, the rules it designs
\par
may have unanticipated or perverse consequences. And the marginal
\par
effects may be subtle. A prohibition of tie-in sales may lead manu-
\par
facturers to redesign their products so that the new products combine
\par
the elements of the formerly separate items. This response may have
\par
the same economic effects as tying arrangements, but it may be more
\par
costly in real resources. Private rearrangements undo the legal rule,
\par
with losses all around. A decision by the Interstate Commerce Com-
\par
mission to regulate certain conduct may lead railroads to do something
\par
else that is worse than the previously unregulated conduct. If the
\par
Civil Aeronautics Board regulates the price of air travel, attempting
\par
to ensure higher profits for air carriers, it may achieve nothing of the
\par
sort. Carriers will compete for custom by offering wider seats, better
\par
meals, more frequent flights. The marginal substitution among meth-
\par
ods of competition thus may obliterate the profits. 12
\par
\emph{12 }\textbf{\emph{See }}Douglas \textbf{\& }Miller, \emph{Quality Competition, Industry Equilibrium, and Efficiency in the}
\par
\emph{Price-Constrained Airline Market, }64 AM. \textbf{ECON. }REv. \textbf{657 }(1974). For a more general assess-
\par
ment of how regulatory systems may induce regulated firms to substitute at the margin in ways\emph{HARVARD LAW REVIEW}
