[Vol. 98:4
\par
There are substitute ways to do almost anything. Ronald Coase
\par
showed that as long as people may bargain and adapt to legal rules,
\par
and the costs of transacting are negligible, people will go on adjusting
\par
and substituting until they reproduce the situation that would have
\par
prevailed in the absence of regulation. 13 If the purpose of the rule
\par
was to affect behavior, this is intolerable. Thus regulation abhors a
\par
vacuum - once you start to control some aspects of an industry, you
\par
must control all, else the subjects of regulation will evade the controls
\par
at potentially substantial costs to all. Rules, unless designed to ac-
\par
commodate the outcome of bargaining, must restrict bargains and
\par
other adaptations. This is as true of rules established by courts as of
\par
rules promulgated by agencies. If a court holds that owners of vessels
\par
absolutely warrant the safety of the ship, the owners will reduce
\par
seamen's wages (substituting health insurance for salary), install more
\par
gear (substituting capital for labor), and so on. The supposed bene-
\par
ficiaries of the warranty may not like the result. The court must
\par
either choose the optimal rule to start with or be prepared to deal
\par
with the adaptations.
\par
Good commercial judges not only know all of these things but also
\par
incorporate them in their decisionmaking. So my second inquiry is
\par
whether the Justices look at how their decisions affect incentives on
\par
the margin. Whether the Justices look at the margin (or the right
\par
margin) depends in large measure on how well they understand the
\par
ways people can substitute one product or one strategy for another.
\par
Part III shows that, on the margin, the Justices have displayed a
\par
steadily increasing sophistication.
