\emph{3. Why are there laws? }- There are two basic styles of statutory
\par
construction.14 In one the judge starts with the statute, attributes to
\par
it certain purposes (evils to be redressed), and then brings within the
\par
statute the class of activities that produce the same or similar objec-
\par
tionable results. The statute's reach goes on expanding so long as
\par
there are unredressed objectionable results. The judge interprets omis-
\par
sions and vague terms in the statute as evidence of want of time or
\par
foresight and fills in these gaps with more in the same vein. The
\par
maxim "Remedial statutes are to be liberally construed" sums up this
\par
approach.
\par
that make them and consumers worse off, see \textbf{S. BREYER, REGULATION AND ITS }REFORM
\par
\textbf{(1982).}
\par
