\par
In the other approach the judge treats the statute as a contract.
\par
He first identifies the contracting parties and then seeks to discover
\par
what they resolved and what they left unresolved. For example, he
\par
may conclude that a statute regulating the price of fluid milk is a pact
\par
between milk producers and milk handlers designed to cut back output
\par
and raise price, to the benefit of both at the expense of consumers.
\par
A judge then implements the bargain as a faithful agent but without
\par
enthusiasm; asked to extend the scope of a back-room deal, he refuses
\par
unless the proof of the deal's scope is compelling. Omissions are
\par
evidence that no bargain was struck: some issues were left for the
\par
future, or perhaps one party was unwilling to pay the price of a
\par
resolution in its favor. Sometimes the compromise may be to toss an
\par
issue to the courts for resolution, but this too is a term of the bargain,
\par
to be demonstrated rather than presumed. What the parties did not
\par
resolve, the court should not resolve either. The maxim "Statutes in
\par
derogation of the common law are to be strictly construed" sums up
\par
this approach.
\par
These maxims are useless as guides to construction; every remedial
\par
statute is in derogation of the common law. Yet this hopeless conflict
\par
of maxims does not justify despair. The appropriate treatment of
\par
statutes depends on how they come to be and what they are for. A
\par
judge cannot set about rearranging economic relations on the basis of
\par
an ambiguous statute without first resolving a question about the
\par
nature of legislation. If statutes generally are designed to overcome
\par
"failures" in markets and to replace the calamities produced by un-
\par
guided private conduct with the ordered rationality of the public
\par
sector, then it makes sense to use the remedial approach to the con-
\par
struction of statutes - or at least most of them. If, on the other
\par
hand, statutes often are designed to replace the outcomes of private
\par
transactions with monopolistic ones, to transfer the profits ("rents") of
\par
productive activity to a privileged few, then judges should take the
\par
beady-eyed contractual approach. The warehouseman does not de-
\par
liver the grain without seeing the receipt, and so too with goodies
\par
dispensed by judges. 15
\par
One of the implications of modern economic thought is that many
\par
laws are designed to serve private rather than public interests. People
\par
demand laws just as they demand automobiles, and some people
\par
demand more effectively than others. Laws that benefit the people in
\par
common are hard to enact because no one can obtain very much of
\par
the benefit of lobbying for or preserving such laws. Smaller, more
\par
cohesive groups are more effective lobbyists. These groups can obtain
