\par
Another approach is to look for the indicia of rent-seeking legis-
\par
lation: limitations on new entry into the business, subsidies of one
\par
group by another, prohibitions of private contracting in response to
\par
the new statutory entitlements. A court dealing with such laws might
\par
justifiably be unwilling to treat them as general-interest legislation
\par
and achieve more in the same vein. How much, in what way, and
\par
to what ends a judge intervenes in the operations .of the economic
\par
system thus will be influenced, if not determined, by the judge's view
\par
of the sources and functions of legislation. A judge who believes with
\par
New Dealers that markets are flawed and that "experts" employed by
\par
the government can correct these "imperfections" will take a path very
\par
different from that of a judge who believes that most legislation grows
\par
out of a struggle among interest groups to appropriate the benefits
\par
produced by other people. Different paths may be suitable for differ-
\par
ent statutes. A judge unaware that there is a fork in the road will
\par
not be able to handle either type of statute appropriately.
\par
Still a third way is to look at process. Who lobbied for the
\par
legislation? What deals were struck in the cloakrooms? Who de-
\par
manded what and who gave up what? Knowing the contending
\par
parties conveys information. So does knowing the grounds of conten-
\par
tion. A statute in which two rent-seeking groups fought to a deadlock,
\par
each unsatisfied but unable to get more, is different from a statute
\par
over which there was no serious contest among self-interested groups.
\par
The closer the contest, and the more the parties were looking to their
\par
own interests (rather than the public interest more broadly defined),
\par
the more appropriate it is to treat the statute as a contract and to
\par
decline to give either party an advantage it failed to get explicitly.
\par
Even a contract under seal must be read in light of the purposes of
\par
the parties, and a court will fill in some ancillary terms, but it will
\par
not fill in price or quantity.
\par
No statute has a single genesis. Members of Congress are not
\par
automata manipulated by today's group; they have their own goals.
\par
Most statutes are interest-group compromises only in part, and the
\par
question is, "Which part?" Some statutes are designed to implement
\par
principles of morality rather than to influence economic conduct, and
\par
these statutes cannot easily be analyzed on a continuum between
\par
public interest and private interest. Statutes may arise from close
\par
bargaining yet have net benefits for the general public; the essence of
\par
the compromise may be to delegate some questions to courts; statutes
\par
without a clear history of back-room deals may turn out to do little
\par
but transfer rents. If such statutes survive for extended periods, it
\par
becomes appropriate to infer that what happens in fact was the basis
\par
of the original plan. A court sensitive to these things must start with
\par
the bargaining behind the statute but cannot stop there. This is not
\par
the place, though, for a map of the journey it must take.\emph{HARVARD LAW REVIEW}
\par
[Vol. 98:4
\par
No doubt the Justices always have seen that statutes have support
\par
from interest groups. They are not political naifs. They even have
\par
created a constitutional right to lobby for special-interest legislation
\par
and to campaign on interest-group tickets. 17 The "new" economics of
\par
politics is an application of a far older one. Adam Smith did not view
\par
mercantilism as a way to protect the public interest,18 and since his
\par
day debates about tariffs and quotas have put interest groups front
\par
and center. So the question is not whether the Court sometimes finds
\par
statutes to be interest-group legislation, but whether it looks for this
\par
possibility pervasively. Are the Justices alert to the private-interest
\par
nature of legislation? Do they use principles of construction appro-
\par
priate to that recognition?
