\textbf{\emph{C. }}\emph{The Scope of the Inquiry}
\par
It should be apparent by now that recognition of the three prin-
\par
ciples I have addressed - ex ante analysis, assessment of effects on
\par
the margin, and appreciation of the interest-group nature of legislation
\par
\textbf{- }would have consequences for all litigation. One cannot limit the
\par
application of these themes to antitrust, intellectual property, and
\par
securities regulation - the "traditional" economic subjects. An un-
\par
derstanding of how incentives cause people to substitute will influence
\par
judicial procedure - doctrines of issue preclusion and rules of evi-
\par
dence - no less than it affects antitrust. The Court's view of the
\par
sources of legislation will influence its view of the function of litiga-
\par
tion. If statutes are bargains among special interests, they should be
\par
enforced like contracts. The appropriate plaintiffs will be those who
\par
claim "breach," not the larger class of persons affected by a bargain
\par
to which they are not "parties." Thus the Court will hesitate to infer
\par
private rights of action, and it will reject efforts by consumers to use
\par
litigation to "improve" the operation of statutes that were not designed
\par
to help consumers in the first place. On the other hand, anticompeti-
\par
tive bargains embedded in state legislation will become targets for
\par
challenge under the antitrust laws; the deference due toward a statute
