As the D.C. Circuit framed the dispute, the question was whether
\par
the Park Service had a strong reason to prevent the demonstrators,
\par
lawfully ensconced in tents, from dozing off to make a point. 2 0 The
\par
court of appeals emphasized three points: first, the CCNV's tents were
\par
already pitched; second, sleep would not add one bit to the disruption
\par
and clutter created by the little villages of tents; third, sleep would
\par
increase the effectiveness of the message conveyed by these particular
\par
demonstrators. All this being true, the court asked, how could the
\par
Park Service justify the restraint? It would suppress expression to no
\par
good end - indeed no end at all.
\par
The points made by the court of appeals are typical of ex post
\par
arguments in litigation. They start from the assumption that the
\par
parties occupy fixed positions and ask whether the distribution of
\par
entitlements in that position is fair. Here the court of appeals started
\par
from the existence of a village of tents occupied by the CCNV and
\par
asked: Why not allow sleep? The effect is small, the demonstrators
\par
few, the need great; less restrictive alternatives are available. Only
\par
knaves would say no.
\par
Yet the Supreme Court said no. It simply changed the perspective.
\par
Instead of asking, "What is the effect of allowing these few people to
\par
close their eyes?", it asked, in essence, "What is the effect of camping
\par
in the national parks near the White House?" It had no trouble
\par
accepting the constitutionality of a no-camping rule, and it saw the
\par
restrictions placed on the CCNV as a neutral application of that rule. 21
\par
Here is the critical passage: "the validity of this regulation need not
\par
be judged solely by reference to the demonstration at hand."' 22 The
\par
regulation was to be assessed by its effects in the generality of cases.
\par
To see the burden, the Court asked not what would happen if the
\par
CCNV's demonstrators took naps, but what would happen if a relax-
\par
ation of the ban on camping made similar demonstrations more at-
\par
tractive. As the implicit cost of demonstrating fell, more people with
\par
less to say would seek initial permits; those who sought permits would
\par
stay longer; those whose desire to speak was weaker than the CCNV's
\par
would come more frequently; some imposters who simply wanted to
\par
sleep and not to speak would use the parks as living quarters (perhaps
\par
to "protest" the high prices of regular hotels). The Court was barely
\par
interested in the CCNV's methods and message. It asked instead how
\par
complex patterns of behavior would change if sleeping were permitted.
\par
