\emph{THE SUPREME }\textbf{\emph{COURT - }}\emph{FOREWORD}
\par
Every line is arbitrary. Cases on either side will seem almost
\par
indistinguishable. Thus every line is vulnerable to pleas for relief
\par
"just this one time." A series of such pleas, if granted, moves the
\par
line and sets the stage for still another series of pleas directed against
\par
the new line. The proper reply, as the Court saw it, was not to
\par
entertain the pleas in the first place. In other words, the Court took
\par
an ex ante view of the dispute, bypassing for the most part the
\par
CCNV's case in order to examine how rules affect future behavior.
\par
It may have conducted the examination well or poorly; for my pur-
\par
poses the outcomes of the 1983 Term's cases are much less important
\par
than the questions the Court asked.
\par
This high-profile case is not unusual. The Court asked the same
\par
kinds of questions throughout the 1983 Term. A series of cases about
\par
intellectual property - patents, copyrights, trade secrets, and other
\par
forms of information - shows how the change has occurred.
\par
\emph{B. The Ex Ante Perspective on Intellectual Property}
\par
Problems involving intellectual property present the dichotomy
\par
between ex post and ex ante perspectives especially starkly. Once
\par
someone has created information, the cost of using the information is
\par
small. The information may be used without being used up. A movie
\par
may be shown or a song played again and again; a patented idea may
\par
be employed in product after product; a work of art may be duplicated
\par
endlessly. Because the marginal cost of using information is small or
\par
even zero, there is a strong case for establishing a system of legal
\par
rules that makes the information freely available. If someone collects
\par
a charge for use of the information (or has the equivalent right to
\par
prohibit use without payment), there will be a social loss. So, for
\par
example, if it costs \$io to duplicate a computer program, and the
\par
author insists on receiving a royalty of \$io per copy, the program
\par
must sell for \$20 or more. Some people value the program at more
\par
than \$io and less than \$20. They will not buy it; yet because the
\par
program has been written it costs only \$io to provide each one a
\par
copy. The prospective buyers lose the difference between the \$io cost
\par
and the value they place on the program's use. Society would be
\par
better off if the program sold for \$io. Any price higher than \$io
\par
creates the same sort of allocative efficiency loss as monopoly. The
\par
"overcharge" causes people to buy less and substitute something else
\par
that is less useful, takes more real resources to produce, or both.
\par
Yet from an ex ante perspective it is necessary to compensate the
\par
programmer. Even if a few people would write computer programs
\par
just for the challenge (or for their own use), they would not make
\par
those programs available to the rest of us without the promise of
\par
compensation. Without a doubt thousands of people write programs
\par
only because of the prospect of reward. Those who write increase\emph{HARVARD LAW REVIEW}
\par
\textbf{[Vol. 98:4}
\par
their productivity as the prospect of reward increases. The lower the
\par
rewards, the fewer programs there will be and the poorer will be the
\par
quality of each existing program; it takes a lot of time to perfect a
\par
program, and again the prospect of reward will influence how much
\par
time the programmer invests.
\par
The problem is that if you allow the author to collect a royalty
\par
for the effort, you create a loss by discouraging use. Higher royalties
\par
(up to the monopoly level) yield more and better programs at the same
\par
time as they yield less effective use of programs once the programs
\par
exist. The incentives that yield optimal creation will prevent optimal
\par
use, and the reverse. There is no neat solution to this problem.
\par
Proposals to substitute government grants for royalties run into the
\par
problem that the government has no very good way to decide which
\par
ideas to subsidize, or how much. The experience of the National
\par
Endowment for the Arts does not inspire confidence. 23
\par
The difficulty is perfectly general. The value of information grows.
\par
Ideas (patented or not) are the principal source of economic growth.
\par
Law, medicine, entertainment, insurance, and a dozen other industries
\par
are at the core just information. Businesses sometimes respond to the
\par
problems of creating and using information by cooperating in joint
\par
ventures or through mergers, and thus much of antitrust law turns
\par
out to be about information. 24 The securities laws, the investment
\par
community, and the stock markets are designed to elicit and use
\par
information about firms.2 5 It is hard to understate the importance of
\par
\textbf{23 }The tension between optimal creation and optimal use might be resolved if the owners of
\par
intellectual property could engage in perfect price discrimination - that is, vary the price to
\par
each buyer so that those who value the program at \$ii.oo will pay \textbf{\$1o.99, }those who value it
\par
at \textbf{\$25.00 }will pay \$24.99, and so on. The discrimination would enable the programmer to
\par
cover costs without discouraging use. But buyers rarely reveal the maximum price they will
\par
