\par
negligence is "completely incompatible with modern admiralty policy
\par
and practice. Exercising its traditional discretion, admiralty has de-
\par
veloped and now follows its own fairer and more flexible rule which
\par
allows such consideration of -contributory negligence in mitigation of
\par
damages as justice requires. Petitioner presents no persuasive argu-
\par
ments that admiralty should now adopt a discredited doctrine which
\par
automatically destroys all claims of injured persons who have contrib-
\par
uted to their injuries in any degree, however slight."'5 3 This is won-
