\par
abound with flawed assertions about the economics of information. A common one: that one
\par
firm's possession of information is a "barrier to entry" of some other firm, which might use the
\par
information to make another product, and therefore the information should be revealed. \emph{See,}
\par
\emph{e.g., }\textbf{S. }REP. No. 95-334, 9 5 th Cong., rst Sess. 30-31, 40-41 (1977). In one sense this restates
\par
the proposition that once information has been created any price for its use will lead to
\par
