\par
STIGLER, THE \textbf{ORGANIZATION }OF INDUSTRY 67-70 (1968). The use of information does not
\par
have this effect; each firm can generate its own information at comparable cost. Indeed, if the
\par
first firm to make a pesticide must provide information to others, then the latecomers will have
\par
lower costs, and the first firm will labor under the "barrier to entry." It is interesting that the
\par
Court did not embrace the flawed reasoning of the legislative debates, as it would have done
\par
in the 1963 or \textbf{1973 }Term; it simply observed that disputes about the degree to which firms
\par
should share information must be resolved by the political branches. \emph{See }Ruckelshaus v.
