\par
By the 1983 Term, however, it was difficult to find any case in
\par
which arguments about windfalls or the fair division of stakes carried
\par
much weight. In \emph{Blum v. Stenson5 7 }the lawyers for the prevailing
\par
plaintiffs in civil rights litigation, members of a legal services orga-
\par
nization, sought attorneys' fees at the same rate private counsel with
\par
equivalent experience could have charged. The defendant answered,
\par
"Windfall," to which the Court replied in essence, "Windfalls are
\par
irrelevant; attorneys should get the fees they could earn in private
\par
practice, else they will go to private practice rather than represent
\par
civil rights plaintiffs." The Court - unanimous here as in \emph{Monsanto}
\par
- ordered the plaintiffs' lawyers paid at a rate equal to the oppor-
\par
tunity cost of their time. No ifs, ands, or buts; no balancing or multi-
\par
factor equitable analysis. This is a purely economic, ex ante approach.
\par
The "equity" of the award in the given case was simply beside the
\par
point.58 There was no balancing to be done (unlike the case in \emph{Sony)}
\par
because only ex ante incentives mattered. The author: Justice Powell,
\par
who had written \emph{Bangor Punta }ten years earlier.
\par
Issue preclusion (collateral estoppel) often is described in equitable
\par
terms. Once a party has had a "full and fair" opportunity to litigate
\par
an issue, a court need not listen to the same contentions again. Issue
\par
preclusion also has an economic function. Parties who know that the
\par
first case will be dispositive have an incentive to invest in litigation
\par
to make the first decision more accurate. The threat of issue preclu-
\par
sion makes it possible for people to calculate investments in litigation
\par
so that they gain privately and society gains by increased accuracy of
\par
the legal system. When for some good reason we do not particularly
\par
want people to invest more in the first litigation - or particularly
\par
care whether that result is accurate - the equitable and economic
\par
functions of issue preclusion come into conflict. That happened in the
\par
1983 Term, offering the Court still another chance to choose between
\par
ex post and ex ante analysis.
\par
Once again it took the ex ante approach, holding in \emph{United States}
\par
\emph{v. Mendoza59 }that the United States is never bound by offensive
\par
nonmutual issue preclusion. The plaintiff in \emph{Mendoza }was a Filipino
\par
who maintained that the decision of the United States to withdraw
\par
immigration officials from the Philippines at the end of World War II
