III. THE COURT ON THE MARGIN
\par
\emph{A. Margins and Averages}
\par
The Court's efforts to influence future conduct are doomed unless
\par
it appreciates how incentives work. As Section I.B.2 shows, people
\par
look at marginal rather than average effects. They substitute among
\par
opportunities until they receive approximately the same reward from
\par
each of their activities (whether buying or doing). They buy or do a
\par
little more of one thing and a little less of something else until it is
\par
not worthwhile to make further changes. At that point the marginal
\par
gains of each activity are approximately the same. Change the returns
\par
on the margin and people alter their behavior; change the returns
\par
somewhere inside the margin and people are unlikely to alter their
\par
behavior in the desired way - if at all.
\par
The Justices used principles of marginal analysis throughout the
\par
1983 Term. \emph{Sony, Monsanto, Blum v. Stenson, }and \emph{Arthur Young}
\par
are good examples. Each rejected invitations issued by the advocates
\par
(or the lower courts) to look at average rather than marginal returns.
\par
In \emph{Sony }the lawyers for the makers of VCRs argued that the copyright
\par
holders would make "a lot" of money no matter what the outcome of
\par
the case and thus the Court should not give them the "unneeded"
\par
reward of royalties on the use of VCRs. In \emph{Blum v. Stenson }the
\par
lawyers for the state (which was required to pay the plaintiffs' attor-
\par
neys' fees) argued that a rate based on the "costs" of running a legal
\par
services office plus a "fair profit" would be enough to keep civil rights
