\emph{B. Substitution and Regulation}
\par
One implication of substitution on the margin is that firms will
\par
alter their conduct to avoid the effects of regulation. If you restrict
\par
the price they can charge, firms will change the quality of what they
\par
sell, defer delivery (rationing by queueing), or make any of a hundred
\par
other adjustments. The regulatory agency must control more and
\par
more in order to achieve the intended effect.
\par
The Court could respond to this by refusing agencies the authority
\par
to deal with these responses to regulation. It could justify the refusal
\par
by denying or overlooking the effects of the first round of regulation.
\par
It also could do so by portraying regulation as an interest-group
\par
bargain in which loopholes are as important as the substantive com-
\par
mands (a subject to which I return in Part IV). The first response
\par
has been the more common one, as the Court disregards how regulated
\par
firms act. 83 If the Court concludes, however, that Congress meant
