IV. THE \textbf{ECONOMICS }OF POLITICS \textbf{AND }THE \textbf{SCOPE }OF \textbf{STATUTES}
\par
As I explained in Section I.B.3 , the Justices' conclusions about the
\par
functioning of markets and the economics of the political process will
\par
exert a strong influence on how they read statutes. Those who see
\par
market failures, and conceive statutes as means to correct these de-
\par
fects, will read the statutes broadly and extend them to cover new
\par
cases. Those who conclude that statutes generally displace markets
\par
in order to transfer rents to political opportunists will take a more
\par
beady-eyed view. This difference will influence decisions across the
\par
board, from tort to antitrust to the implication of private rights of
\par
action. I start the examination with one of the most publicized cases
\par
of the 1983 Term, \emph{Silkwood v. Kerr-McGee Corp.,95 }which presented
\par
the conflict in especially stark form.
\par
\emph{A. Tort Law and Federal Preemption}
\par
The Atomic Energy Act of 1954 establishes a system of federal
\par
regulation reaching every aspect of the manufacture and use of ra-
\par
dioactive materials. No one can refine or use these materials without
\par
a federal license. The Nuclear Regulatory Commission's regulations
\par
govern the most minute details of the processing of materials and the
\par
