\par
between interest-group and public-interest legislation. Interest-group
\par
legislation requires adherence to the terms of the compromise. The
\par
court cannot "improve" on a pact that has no content other than the
\par
exact bargain among the competing interests, because the pact has no
\par
purpose. Recognition of the way in which economic legislation comes
\par
to be thus turns out to have the most fundamental implications for
\par
how the Justices approach their functions.
