V. CONCLUSION
\par
My theme is tendencies, not truths. No one approach can explain
\par
all decisions. The Justices were not oblivious to economic issues in
\par
the 1953 Term. There were some careful, articulate economic deci-
\par
sions in the early i96os. 15{$^\circ$} There were some lapses in the 1983 Term.
\par
The Justices operate under burdens - such as paucity of information,
\par
press of business, and the difficulties of deciding by sequential voting
\par
- that make it hard for the best among them to shine consistently.
\par
Even in economic markets, some transactions are inexplicable. To
\par
understand the Court we must attend to the larger movements and
\par
let the occasional case pass. The nature of the larger movements in
\par
economic litigation is unmistakable.
\par
One way to assess this claim is to survey particular fields of the
\par
law. The antitrust laws, for example, are written in economic terms
\par
such as "competition" and "monopoly." If the Justices miss the ob-
\par
vious economic points when dealing with antitrust matters, then a
\par
claim that the Court has recognized the subtle; implicit economic
\par
themes in the rest of its jurisprudence is hard to take seriously. If the
\par
Court was handling antitrust cases well in the 1953 and 1963 Terms,
\par
my claim that it overlooked fundamental points in other cases is
\par
harder to sustain.
\par
Yet the pattern in antitrust turns out to be similar to the pattern
\par
elsewhere. For most of the century the Court either ignored economic
\par
principles or got them backward. 15 1 In the 1953 Term the Court did
\par
not use economics at all. 15 2 In the 1963 Term the Justices usually
\par
treated economics with hostility, explaining that reductions in cost
