\emph{THE SUPREME COURT -FOREWORD}
\par
were to be mourned rather than welcomed. \textbf{1 53 }Times have changed.
\par
In the 1983 Term the antitrust opinions read like short treatises on
\par
microeconomic analysis. All of the Justices asked economic questions,
\par
such as whether the arrangements in question increased price by
\par
cutting back output.154 The 1983 Term continued a change of course
\par
in antitrust that has been under way for ten years. 155
\par
You can find the same sort of change in other substantive areas.
\par
Once, the Court viewed securities law as a part of moral philosophy.
\par
It asked whether a business practice was right or wrong, and it viewed
\par
even this question from an ex post perspective. (A practice was more
\par
likely to seem "wrong" if you overlooked how people chose the risks
\par
they would take and how they obtained explicit or implicit compen-
\par
sation for accepting those risks.) Today the Court is more likely to
\par
ask whether a business practice increases or reduces the expected
\par
returns to investors. The different perspective leads the Justices in
\par
new directions. \textbf{1 56 }The new perspective spills over from economic to
\par
constitutional issues. I have discussed a few applications; there are
\par
many others. \textbf{1 5 7}
\par
Some readers will find my thesis true but no ground for praising
\par
the Court. Those who would prefer the Court to follow a path
\par
F. Supp. \textbf{295 (D. }Mass. 1953), and Theatre Enters. v. Paramount Film Distrib. Corp., 346
\par
U.S. \textbf{537 }(I954), are the only antitrust cases of the Term. \emph{Theatre Enterprises }applied criminal
\par
law principles of conspiracy to the definition of an agreement, bypassing questions about how
\par
agreements affect markets. \emph{United Shoe Machinery, }one of the most stimulating antitrust cases
\par
ever, drew only a laconic summary disposition from the Court.
\par
\textbf{\emph{153 }}\emph{See, e.g., }United States v. Continental Can Co., 378 U.S. 441, 464 (1964); United States
\par
v. First Nat'l Bank, 376 U.S. 665, 669 (1964).
\par
\textbf{154 }The best example is NCAA v. Board of Regents, \textbf{104 S. }Ct. 2948 (1984). The Court
\par
declined to apply a rule of per se illegality to cooperation among universities, even though they
\par
were rivals in the production of football, because it recognized that the cooperation might make
\par
amateur football a better rival to professional sports and might make games more exciting on
\par
the field and thus more fun to watch. Then it searched for evidence about whether or not the
\par
arrangements in question increased output. \emph{See id. }at 2970.
\par
\textbf{155 }For demonstrations of the gradual change in the Court's application of economics to
\par
antitrust, see the articles cited in note 8.
\par
\textbf{156 }Dirks v. SEC, \textbf{103 S. }Ct. \textbf{3255 }(983), illustrates the point. The majority asked how
\par
Dirks's activities (investigating rumors about fraud at Equity Funding and then "tipping" his
\par
clients to the impending disaster so that they could sell before the big drop in price), if repeated,
\par
wotld affect investors in future cases. The dissenting Justices, citing the earlier cases, argued
\par
that Dirks's activities were simply wrong and should not be condoned.
\par
\textbf{157 }Exclusionary rule cases, once addressed in terms of "judicial integrity" or the moral
\par
standing of the police, are today treated as occasions for the assessment of the marginal deterrent
\par
effects of excluding particular categories of evidence. \emph{See, e.g., }Immigration \& Naturalization
\par
Serv. v. Lopez-Mendoza, \textbf{104 }S. Ct. \textbf{3479, }3486-90 (1984); United States v. Leon, \textbf{104 }S. Ct.
\par
\textbf{3405, }3418-22 (r984). The new perspective has been developing since 1974, \emph{see }United States
\par
v. Calandra, \textbf{414 }U.S. 338 (I974), to the dismay of both dissenting Justices and commentators,
\par
who emphasize the noninstrumental functions of the exclusionary rule, \emph{see }Kamisar, Gates,
\par
\emph{"Probable Cause," "Good Faith," and Beyond, }69 IOWA L. REV. 551, \textbf{609-I5 }(1984); White,
\par
\emph{Forgotten Points in the "Exclusionary Rule" Debate, }81 MICH. L. REV. \textbf{1273 }(983).\emph{HARVARD LAW REVIEW}
\par
[Vol. 98:4
\par
emphasizing moral rather than instrumental values find the Court's
\par
course distressing. Even those who think judges ought to recognize
\par
and deal with the economic questions implicit in the cases may think
\par
the Court's current achievement a source of small comfort. Judges
\par
must be honest agents of the political branches. They carry out
\par
decisions they do not make. Judges who appreciate the economics of
\par
legislation and the markets will be good agents as well as honest ones.
\par
Good agents reduce the costs of implementing laws. Reduce the cost
\par
of anything and you get more. So good judges make it easier for the
\par
political branches to strike compromises, to enact new laws. More
\par
laws are not necessarily better. Those who believe that much law-
\par
making is the outcome of struggles among interest groups find the
\par
prospect of additional rent-seeking legislation dismaying.
\par
A judiciary that understood a little of the economics of the legis-
\par
lative process might try to raise the costs of compromise. That is
\par
what the Supreme Court did between 189o and 1937. The proponents
\par
of substantive due process review thought judges could foil legislators
\par
who were undermining the operation of markets. That was a ques-
\par
tionable belief; if powerful groups are denied the outcome they want
\par
most, they may find other ways to accomplish their ends, ways that
\par
impose even greater losses on the rest of us. Even if judges could
\par
make wise economic decisions routinely, which is unlikely, the judi-
\par
ciary would not be able to stop the economic and political systems at
\par
a chosen point; not even the political branches, with a far stronger
\par
claim to that function, can do so. Private actors have ways of avoid-
\par
ing even the most stringent legal controls. The more fundamental
\par
point is that judges have not been charged with imposing their sub-
\par
stantive views on the economic system. Judges' claim to authority
\par
rests on a plausible demonstration that they are faithfully executing
\par
decisions made by others. 58 The consequence of honest and capable
\par
discharge of that function may be more rent-seeking legislation, or it
\par
may be more general-interest legislation. An increase in economic
\par
astuteness among judges facilitates both kinds of legislation. Which
\par
occurs is not the judges' affair.
\par
