\textbf{The Value of Monitoring for Radon in the Home: A}
\par
\textbf{Decision Analysis}
\par
\textbf{John S. Evans, Neil C. Hawkins, and John D. Graham}
\par
\textbf{Harvard School of Public Health}
\par
\textbf{Boston, Massachusetts}
\par
\textbf{In light of the recent publicity about the adverse health effects of}
\par
\textbf{radon gas, many citizens and government officials are considering}
\par
\textbf{whether or not to monitor for radon in homes. This paper presents a}
\par
\textbf{formal decision analysis of the monitoring dilemma from the per-}
\par
\textbf{spective of hypothesized homeowners. The analysis considers the}
\par
\textbf{costs of radon monitoring and control, the carcinogenic risks of}
\par
\textbf{radon exposure, the demographics of household size, and a hypo-}
\par
\textbf{thetical homeowner's knowledge of radon exposure levels---with}
\par
\textbf{and without the benefit of specific monitoring data. Since monitor-}
\par
\textbf{ing every home in the United States would be quite expensive, the}
\par
\textbf{analysis reveals some more efficient monitoring strategies that}
\par
\textbf{might be employed by citizens and government officials. While the}
\par
\textbf{paper presents a new analytic perspective in the monitoring prob-}
\par
\textbf{lem, the results should not be considered definitive. Further study is}
\par
\textbf{necessary to clarify precisely what is known about radon exposure,}
\par
\textbf{health effects, and control strategies.}
\par
Radon, a radioactive gas that can cause lung cancer, is a
\par
constituent of indoor air which is undetectable without spe-
\par
cial devices. Radon exposure is primarily responsible for the
\par
excess lung cancers of underground miners, and radon expo-
\par
sure in homes may account for a significant proportion of
\par
lung cancers in the United States. Radon gas is produced by
\par
natural radioactive decay of uranium in soil. As the gas
\par
emanates from the soil, it enters homes through cracks in
\par
foundations and cellar walls. It is so ubiquitous and poten-
\par
tially hazardous that the EPA recommends that every house
\par
in America be tested. The costs of the most common radon
\par
test methods (e.g., charcoal canister and alpha track detec-
\par
tor) are between \$10 and \$50. With some eighty million
\par
households subject to recommended radon monitoring, the
\par
costs of a comprehensive national testing program will prob-
\par
ably exceed a billion dollars.1
\par
Is it worth up to \$50 for the typical home owner to monitor
\par
for radon on a one time basis? The theory supporting our
\par
assessment of the value of radon monitoring information is
\par
explained by Raiffa2 and its application to environmental
\par
risk management problems is described by Finkel and Ev-
\par
ans.3 In this decision analytic framework, information's val-
\par
ue arises from its potential for reducing the probability and/
\par
or consequences of making "incorrect" or suboptimal deci-
\par
sions. As the degree of uncertainty is reduced by additional
\par
sampling information, the likelihood of making a subopti-
\par
mal decision is reduced, and therefore any information that
\par
contributes to uncertainty reduction has value. The extent
\par
of its value depends on the health and economic significance
\par
of the errors that will be made by making "wrong" decisions.
\par
In this analysis of the radon problem, the perspective of a
\par
well-informed agent acting in the best interests of the home
\par
owner, rather than the home owner himself, is assumed.
\par
Decision theory suggests that the value of improved infor-
\par
mation about variables such as exposure or carcinogenic
\par
potency will depend upon the range of choices available to
\par
the decision maker. If the range of choices (in terms of cost
\par
and health risk reduction) available to the decision maker is
\par
small, then information may have little value even when it
\par
virtually eliminates uncertainty. However, if the available
\par
control options are divergent---for example, no control at no
\par
cost and essentially perfect control at very large cost---even
\par
small reductions in uncertainty may have appreciable value.
\par
For any range of choices, the value of information is influ-
\par
enced by how much various sources of uncertainty contrib-
\par
ute to overall uncertainty and by how much these compo-
\par
nent uncertainties can be reduced.
\par
Uncertainty in the radon problem arises primarily from
\par
uncertainty in exposures and in estimates of radon potency.
\par
To apply the decision-analytic approach, it is necessary to
\par
develop estimates of the uncertainty in each of these param-
\par
eters and to propagate these uncertainties to estimate the
\par
overall uncertainty in risk. Elicitation of expert judgment
\par
was the method used to estimate uncertainty in key compo-
\par
nents of the analysis: 1) exposure, 2) potency, and 3) control
\par
strategy costs and efficiencies. One expert on each of these
\par
topics was selected and interviewed.
\par
Each of these elements of the analysis will be described in
\par
a separate section. The final section summarizes the value of
\par
radon monitoring for four representative households.
\par
\textbf{Elicitation of Expert Judgments}
\par
When scientific data about exposure or potency are un-
\par
available or inconclusive, expert judgment may be required
