\textbf{Exposure Analysis}
\par
Our exposure analysis focused on developing probabilistic
\par
estimates of radon exposures in homes that have not been
\par
monitored and on assessing the residual uncertainty that
\par
exists after a home has been monitored. Prior to eliciting the
\par
exposure distributions, we discussed with Dr. Nero the op-
\par
tions for monitoring radon in the home. He recommended
\par
using a track-etch detector located in the primary living
\par
space with a one-year integration period. All elicited distri-
\par
butions refer to results that would be obtained using such a
\par
sampling strategy. The assessed distributions are summa-
\par
rized in Table I.
\par
The first level of questions began at the national level. Dr.
\par
Nero assessed the national distribution of exposures to be
\par
lognormal with a median of 1 pCi/L and geometric standard
\par
deviation (GSD) of 2.8. This is the distribution he assigned
\par
to any home in the United States without any other informa-
\par
tion given. Typically, more information is available than
\par
this.
\par
The first level of information that may be available is the
\par
region of the country in which the home is located. Dr. Nero
\par
separated the country into two regions which he called the
\par
northern and southern United States. For the northern re-
\par
gion, he assessed a distribution reflecting greater exposures
\par
than in the southern region. He further refined these region-
\par
al distributions on the basis of characteristics of homes. One
\par
set of distributions was elicited for homes with basements
\par
and another for homes with crawl spaces or slab on grade
\par
construction.
\par
Information other than region of country and type of
\par
home may also be available and may lower prior uncertainty.
\par
Specifically, results of sampling in one's city or country can
\par
be used to make analogies to exposures in one's own home.
\par
Dr. Nero's estimates depended on the number of homes that
\par
had actually been monitored. If only one home had been
\par
monitored, its result would be the median of an exposure
\par
distribution with a GSD of 2.5. If five or more homes had
\par
monitoring results, their median would serve as the median
\par
of an exposure distribution with a GSD of 2.0. If fifty or more
\par
homes in the region had been monitored, the sample median
\par
and GSD would be used directly as the parameters of the
\par
analogous home's lognormal distribution.
\par
A limiting case exists when monitoring results from an
\par
adjacent neighbor are available. Dr. Nero pointed out that
\par
many factors govern how strong the analogy between adja-
\par
cent homes would be, but as a general rule, the following
\par
distributions would apply: if the two homes seem virtually
\par
identical, the measured exposure would serve as the median
\par
with a GSD of 1.5, and if the homes seem substantially
\par
different, the median would still be the measurement but
\par
with a GSD of 2.0.
