\par
sented elsewhere demonstrated that the basic analysis is
\par
relatively insensitive to discount rates ranging from one to
\par
five percent per year.11
\par
Even after the home owner has taken a sample in his own
\par
house, there is still residual uncertainty about his exposure
\par
to radon. The uncertainty varies with the level measured in
\par
the home: if the result is high ({$>$}2pCi/L) the coefficient of
\par
variation is 25 percent (GSD = 1.25), and if the result is low
\par
({$<$}0.5 pCi/L), the residual uncertainty is about 35 percent.
\par
\textbf{Ellcltation of Uncertainty in Potency}
\par
Radon levels in mines are commonly expressed in working
\par
levels (WL) and the exposures of miners are recorded in
\par
working level months (WLM). If radon is in equilibrium
\par
with its progeny, 100 pCi/L of radon is approximately equal
\par
to 1 WL and a miner exposed to this level for 170 hours is
\par
assigned a dose of 1 WLM.7 Since the only epidemiological
\par
results for assessing radon potency are those of miners, the
\par
results must be extrapolated to estimate the potency of
\par
radon in the general U.S. population, which has different
\par
doses, dose rates, attached fractions, and physiological char-
\par
acteristics than the miner population.8
\par
9
\par
-
\par
A probabilistic assessment of radon's potency (i.e., the
\par
coefficient of the linear model which reflects the excess rela-
\par
tive risk per WLM of exposure) was obtained by encoding
\par
the judgment of Dr. Hornung. The expert was asked a series
\par
of questions that followed the necessary extrapolations from
\par
epidemiological studies to the general U.S. population. First,
\par
the distribution of excess relative risk per WLM for the
\par
miners was elicited (median = 1.25 percent, GSD = 1.4).
\par
Then Dr. Hornung was asked to consider the extrapolation
\par
from high to low dose, from men to mixed populations, and
\par
from the conditions of mining to those of homes. This last
\par
extrapolation accounts for any differences in breathing rates
\par
and modes, attached fraction, equilibrium factor, and other
\par
variables. Finally, he was asked to give an estimate of poten-
\par
cy appropriate for application to the general population. His
\par
assessed distribution of excess relative risk per WLM for the
\par
general population had a median of 1.0 percent and a GSD of
\par
2.0.
