\textbf{The Risk Assessment Model}
\par
To estimate the cancer risk faced by an individual exposed
\par
to low levels of radon, it is necessary to rely on models. To
\par
estimate the cancer risk faced by typical families it is also
\par
necessary to consider the demographics of the population.
\par
Our model for radon risk assessment uses the relative risk
\par
approach, a latency period of 10 years, and a lifetime plateau
\par
period. It also assumes low dose linearity. The 1980 back-
\par
ground lung cancer (ICDA\emph{ \#} 162) mortality rates were used
\par
in the model12 as were the 1980 age-specific probabilities of
\par
death from other causes.13
\par
To compute the risks associated with specific concentra-
\par
tions of radon in the home, it is necessary to make assump-
\par
tions about the fraction of the time spent in the home and
\par
the relationship between the concentrations of radon and its
\par
decay products. We assume 75 percent occupancy14 and that
\par
the radon daughter equilibrium factor for homes is 50 per-
\par
cent.15
\par
Using potency estimates elicited from Dr. Hornung and
\par
the described model of risk, we developed estimates of the
\par
risk that would be associated with various concentrations of
