\textbf{The Decision Framework}
\par
The decision faced by the homeowner's agent is illustrated
\par
in Figure 1. He may chose to do nothing, may elect to install
\par
any one of the control strategies, or may monitor the radon
\par
levels in the home prior to making a decision about control
\par
strategies. Associated with each possible control choice is a
\par
unique combination of control costs and health risks. These
\par
are shown in brackets---\textbraceleft cost, risk\textbraceright ---at the end of each
\par
branch on the decision tree. The choice among control op-
\par
tions involves comparison and balancing of cost and risk. It
\par
is further complicated by the uncertainty the agent has
\par
about the true level of risk in the home.
\par
We assume that the homeowner's agent is a well-informed
\par
decision maker who chooses control alternatives that mini-
\par
mize total annual cost (i.e., the weighted sum of annualized
\par
control costs and monetized health risks). A value of life of 3
\par
million dollars was used in basic calculations and sensitivity
\par
analysis of the value of life was performed on values between
\par
1 million and 10 million dollars. Our analysis may have given
\par
slightly different results if the value of life year was applied
\par
rather than the value of life. A more sophisticated analysis
\par
might address the variability in the values of safety that
\par
citizens express in surveys or reveal in their everyday behav-
\par
ior.17
\par
This model is simple because it assumes risk neutrality
\par
and summarizes many attributes of the decision maker and
\par
the risk he faces in a single parameter: the value of life.
\par
Further, the value of life is assumed independent of the
\par
family's baseline level of risk. The simplicity of our assump-
\par
\textbf{1383}tions limits the broader applicability of our results, but it
\par
also provides an explicit framework for review.
\par
The total annualized costs of the null strategy and the four
\par
basic control strategies are shown as a function of risk in
\par
Figure 2. Two features of this graph are noteworthy. First,
\par
two strategies (pressurization of cellar and ventilation with
\par
heat recovery) are always dominated (i.e., not optimal for
\par
\emph{any} level of risk) and need not be considered further. Sec-
\par
ond, there are two break points that define the regions of
\par
optimality of the remaining control strategies: for baseline
\par
annual risks below 5 X 10\textasciitilde 5
\par
, the null strategy is optimal, for
\par
risks between 5 X 10\textasciitilde 5 and 1 X 10\textasciitilde 4 the 30 percent strategy
\par
(sealing cellar) is optimal, and for risks greater than 1 X 10\textasciitilde 4
\par
the 90 percent strategy (sub-slab ventilation) is optimal.
