If the risks were known with certainty, the optimal control
\par
choice could always be made, but uncertainty on the true
\par
risk leads to incorrect control choices and excess control
\par
costs. In order to reduce uncertainty in risk, the home ow-
\par
ner's agent can elect to monitor before making a control
\par
decision. In this case, he faces the costs of monitoring in
\par
addition to any control costs, but the data from a sample
\par
would reduce the uncertainty in his\emph{ a priori} estimate of risk
\par
and may improve his decision about control.
\par
Figure 3 is a reduced decision tree, including only the non-
\par
dominated control options. The total cost associated with
\par
each option has been calculated using \$3 million as the value
\par
of life. In this figure,\emph{ R"} represents the revised estimate of
\par
annual risk faced by the family unit. This revised risk esti-
\par
mate reflects the results of radon monitoring.
\par
The sequential nature of the decision process is evident in
\par
this figure. The choices that would be faced after monitoring
\par
are suggested by showing three of the continuum of potential
\par
monitoring results, 0.1,1.0, and 8.0 pCi/L. After monitoring,
\par
the control strategy chosen depends upon the result ob-
\par
tained. To evaluate the benefits of monitoring, one calcu-
\par
lates the expected cost of the decisions that would be made
\par
after monitoring and compares this with the cost of the
\par
decision that would be made on the basis of the a\emph{ priori}
\par
assessment of exposure. This difference represents the most
\par
that one should be willing to pay for monitoring. If it is
\par
greater than the cost" of testing, then monitoring is justified.
\par
If not, it suggests that the benefits of monitoring do not
\par
justify their costs. The value of information is calculated by
\par
using the applicable prior distribution of exposure elicited
\par
from Dr. Nero. Although only three monitoring results are
\par
shown, our calculations assumed at least 50 possible moni-
\par
toring results.
\par
A computer program was written to determine the expect-
\par
ed value of sample information. Estimates of information's
\par
value were derived for several scenarios which differed pri-
\par
marily in the nature and extent of information available
\par
upon which to base the a\emph{ priori} estimate of radon exposure.
\par
\textbf{Results}
\par
Our results for the value of radon monitoring follow the
\par
four representative households from their greatest prior un-
\par
certainty levels (no measurements available) to the lowest
\par
prior uncertainty levels (many similar measurements avail-
\par
able). All calculations assume a value of life of \$3 million, a
\par
real discount rate of 3\%, and the central estimates of control
\par
costs and efficiencies. The monetary values of sample infor-
\par
mation have been converted to present value to simplify
\par
comparison with the costs of monitoring, which are assumed
\par
to be about \$50.
\par
\textbf{Basic Results---No Measurements Available}
\par
If no measurements are available (Table IV), it is always
\par
worthwhile to monitor for radon. The value of monitoring is
\par
lower for the couple with children and for the elderly person,
\par
but for different reasons. Based on the prior risk estimates,
\par
the couple with children should choose 90\% control, but the
\par
elderly person should choose not to control. Because their\emph{ a}
\par
\emph{priori} decisions are relatively clear, the information provid-
\par
ed by a radon sample is of less value to these two groups than
\par
to the other family groups. The two other households, the
\par
young and middle-aged couples, have higher values of sam-
\par
ple information because their\emph{ a priori} control decisions are
\par
much less clear cut. However for all four groups the value of
\par
monitoring far exceeds its cost under the base case assump-
\par
tions and therefore monitoring is advisable.
\par
Sensitivity of value of sample information to the assumed
\par
value of life and cost of control has been determined.11 As
\par
anticipated, these two parameters strongly influenced the
\par
value of sample information. While the value of monitoring
\par
is sensitive to the assumed value of life (range explored: \$1
\par
million to \$10 million) and assumed control cost, (range
\par
explored: \$100 per year to \$500 per year for 90 percent
\par
control) the decision to monitor is not because in almost all
\par
cases the value of monitoring is much greater than its cost.
