A still lower level of uncertainty is the case where five or
\par
more regional measurements from one's city or county are
\par
available. As shown in Table V, the value of sample informa-
\par
tion now does not always exceed its cost (again assumed to be
\par
less than \$50). For all four households, there are transition
\par
points where the sample information no longer is worth its
\par
cost. The decision maker's agent would recommend against
\par
sampling in the upper right hand corner (low risk and low
\par
social costs) and the lower left hand corner (high risks and
\par
high social costs). In these two regions, the decision about
\par
