\par
lower left), but in the middle "band," a sampling result could
\par
help resolve uncertain decisions.
\par
1384 \textbf{JAPCANeighbor's Monitoring Results Available}
\par
Decisions when a neighbor in a similar home has moni-
\par
tored can be made with the lowest level of prior uncertainty.
\par
As shown in Table VI, the results are "banded" as in the
\par
previous example, but in a much more narrow fashion. When
\par
prior uncertainty is this low, monitoring information only
\par
has value in a few situations that leave the home owner's
\par
agent in a dilemma. For example, the middle-aged couple
\par
would select 0 percent control for a neighbor's result up to
\par
about 0.5 pCi/L, 30 percent control for a neighbor's result of
\par
about 1 pCi/L, and 90 percent control for a neighbor's result
\par
greater than 2 pCi/L. Whenever the neighbor's result is
\par
outside this range of critical values, the middle-aged couple
\par
would be confident with their\emph{ a priori} control decision.
\par
The sensitivity of the "banding" phenomenon to value of
\par
life and control costs is systematic. As value of life increases,
\par
the band moves diagonally toward the upper right hand
\par
corner (toward lower risks). As control costs increase, the
\par
band moves diagonally toward the lower left hand corner
\par
(toward higher risks). Nevertheless, the "band" phenome-
\par
non is not removed by changing the values of these parame-
\par
ters.
\par
\textbf{Summary and Discussion}
\par
Normative decision analysis suggests that all homes in the
\par
United States should be monitored for radon if one believes
\par
that regional or local monitoring results are either unavail-
\par
able or uninformative. Presumably, this was the assumption
\par
underlying the initial EPA policy analyses.
\par
However, if one uses available monitoring results to gener-
\par
ate probabilistic exposure analogies, more efficient monitor-
\par
ing strategies can be designed. The decisions to monitor
\par
should be guided by composition of households, their char-
\par
acteristics, especially ages at initial exposure, and the specif-
\par
ic prior estimate of exposure---the factors which determine
\par
the level of risk in any given household.
\par
Because the potential reduction in monitoring cost is so
\par
large, it is essential that that the validity of these probabilis-
\par
tic exposure analogies be explored. It seems likely that valid
\par
analogies could easily be developed for use in regions with
\par
relatively homogeneous soils such as sands and clay and
\par
possible that reasonable analogies could be developed for
\par
application to regions with bedrock near surface. As a mini-
\par
mum, before the approach is used as the basis for policy
\par
analysis, it would be necessary to elicit the judgments of
\par
several experts in each of the relevant disciplines.
\par
Although the results presented only apply to expected
\par
value decision makers and the representative families in
\par
homes with unfinished basements, the analysis could easily
\par
be expanded to include other households, home types, and
\par
more general decision criteria.
\par
The analysis presented here is too complex to be employed
\par
directly by a typical homeowner. Instead we imagine that an
\par
agent, acting on behalf of the homeowner, would evaluate
\par
risks, costs, and values, select the appropriate control strate-
\par
gy, and estimate the value of information. The homeowner's
\par
agent could be the U.S. E.P.A. or another agency of the
\par
government interested in promoting rational decisions
\par
about radon monitoring and mitigation. Such an agency
\par
might use the results of our analysis in the design of bro-
\par
chures intended to both inform homeowners about radon
\par
and assist them in making monitoring and control choices
\par
consistent with prior estimates of exposure and their atti-
\par
tudes toward risk. Additionally, the analysis could be used
\par
by such an agency in the design of national radon exposure
\par
monitoring programs to ensure that such programs are likely
\par
to produce information with an impact on control decisions.
\par
This illustrative case study demonstrates how decision
\par
November 1988 Volume 38, No. 11 analysis can be applied to policy analyses involving individ-
\par
ual decisions about monitoring and controls. It also points
\par
out that elicitation of expert judgments is a promising ave-
\par
nue for preliminary policy analyses involving uncertain
\par
risks. These methods could be easily adapted to investigate
\par
such diverse policy issues as asbestos in homes and public
\par
buildings, chloroform and other organics in homes, and pub-
\par
lic health risks from hazardous waste sites.
\par
