\textbf{The contribution of agricultural}
\par
\textbf{economics to development}
\par
T I Fenyes* \& C J van Rooyen**
\par
\emph{Agricultural economics is an applied discipline with a broadly based application}
\par
\emph{to development theory. The importance of agriculture in economic}
\par
\emph{development has led to pressing demands on the role which agricultural}
\par
\emph{economists are required to play. At all stages, a major part of the planning and}
\par
\emph{implementation input is contributed by agricultural economists. The}
\par
\emph{development of agricultural economics was therefore dynamic as well as}
\par
\emph{problem related. Within the Southern African development context, the}
\par
\emph{agricultural economist has had a long standing involvement. For example,}
\par
\emph{Professor F R Tomlinson chaired the important Tomlinson Commission in the}
\par
\emph{1950s, while Dr Simon Brand is the Chief Executive of the Development Bank}
\par
\emph{of Southern Africa. Both are agricultural economists. In general, agricultural}
\par
\emph{economics has contributed to the fields of research and training, smallholder}
\par
\emph{and rural development, project planning and management, and the}
\par
\emph{formulation, planning and analysis of macro-policy.}
\par
The importance of agriculture in the economy led to
\par
pressing demands on the role which agricultural
\par
economists were required to play. It went beyond filling
\par
the details in the framework evolved by others. In fact, at
\par
all stages, a major content to the planning was contri-
\par
buted by agricultural economists. Even at a stage where
\par
theorists faced a near-impasse, the breakthrough came
\par
from leading agricultural economists, and it made a last-
\par
ing impact on the planning process. The present status
\par
of the agricultural economist is that of an equal partner
\par
in the process of planning (Shah, 1981).
\par
\textbf{1. INTRODUCTION}
\par
Over 800 million people in various parts of the world are said to live in ab-
\par
solute poverty. Eighty per cent of them live in rural areas, with agriculture
\par
as their principal occupation. There are more than 80 million smallhold-
\par
ings of less than two hectares, most of which generate incomes below the
\par
absolute poverty level (World Bank, 1975, pp 4-5). In Africa, more than
\par
two-thirds of the economically active population live in rural areas, and
\par
about 90 per cent of the labour force is engaged in agriculture (excluding
\par
RSA and OPEC countries) (Kofi, 1977, p 100). Workers in this sector are
\par
largely self-employed, and labour is not supplemented with much addi-
\par
Professor, Department of Economics, Vista University, Pretoria.
\par
Development Bank of Southern Africa.
\par
271tional capital. The nature of agricultural production processes as well as
\par
agricultural products, and the nature of low productivity and limited mar-
\par
kets lead to the presence of institutions and practices aimed at averting
\par
risks and spreading costs in agriculture (Johnston \& Kilby, 1975, pp 27-
\par
28). Other indicators include factors such as low levels of nutrition, edu-
\par
cation and health.
\par
The underdeveloped state of traditional farming areas in the national and
\par
independent states of Southern Africa and the extent of socio-economic
\par
problems caused by mass migration to the cities (as is also experienced in
\par
developing countries world-wide) implies that at least part of the rural
\par
population of these states should be able to make a living off the land. In-
\par
vestment in agricultural programmes is therefore important and urgently
\par
required.
\par
From the foregoing remarks it is obvious that agricultural economics as
\par
an academic discipline, and agricultural economists as practitioners of
\par
this applied science should play a vital role in the theory and practice of in-
\par
tegrated agricultural and rural development and as part of an inter-
\par
disciplinary effort in development in general.
\par
\textbf{2. THE DEVELOPMENT OF AGRICULTURAL DEVELOPMENT}
\par
\textbf{ECONOMICS}
\par
A historical overview of the contribution of agricultural economics indi-
\par
cates initial involvement with institutional and reform programmes. This
\par
involvement led to preoccupation with the problems of growth and pro-
\par
ductivity in agriculture, emphasizing the efficient use of resources. In
\par
view of the perpetuation of rural poverty and backwardness in spite of
\par
some successful agricultural production efforts (especially via technologi-
\par
cal innovation), agricultural economics at present also deals with the
\par
problem of efficiency versus equity, the creation of employment, self-sus-
\par
tained rural development and basic needs approaches. In this process, the
\par
agricultural economist became "an equal partner in the process of plan-
\par
ning" (Shah, 1981) to seek solutions to the total problem of underdevelop-
\par
ment of rural areas.
\par
\textbf{2.1 Theoretical and conceptual development}
\par
During the fifties, Sir Arthur Lewis, in his now famous article on develop-
\par
ment with\emph{ unlimited supplies of labour} (Lewis, 1954), pioneered the im-
\par
portant work of agricultural economists in the post Second World War
\par
period.
\par
In the sixties, Theodore Schultz (1964) concentrated on the analysis of the
\par
\emph{transformation of traditional agriculture,} and has had considerable influ-
\par
ence on development thinking and attitudes ever since. Important con-
\par
tributions in the field of agricultural development were also made by au-
\par
thors such as Mellor, Eicher, Mosher, De Wilde and Thorbecke.
\par
To some extent, most of these contributions have tended to mirror overall
\par
growth oriented development strategies. They suggested an approach to-
\par
wards\emph{ injecting external resources and new technology,} and concentrat-
\par
ing them on a\emph{ small proportion of the} rural population without pertinent
\par
reference to broadly based welfare of equity considerations. The basis of
\par
such an approach is essentially the beliefs that:
\par
272- Rural development can be generated significantly only if new resources
\par
are brought into the rural sector
\par
- That no other, more satisfactory way of achieving rural development
\par
has yet been found
\par
- That given the limited availability of these external resources, they
\par
must of necessity be concentrated to be efficacious.
\par
Due to widespread perpetuation of rural poverty and underdevelopment,
\par
this approach has been questioned increasingly in recent years. Johnston
\par
\& Kilby (1975), and Johnston \& Clark (1982) have employed the simple
\par
and useful distinction between\emph{ bimodal} and\emph{ unimodal} strategies in rural
\par
development.\emph{ Unimodal strategies} involve farm-level investments which
\par
are finely divisible, or neutral to scale, and can be adopted easily by small
\par
farmers. Large scale projects requiring labour saving and capital inten-
\par
sive technology are, however, not promoted. The main supposed advan-
\par
tage of this small scale farmer approach is that widespread increases in
\par
rural incomes provide a large market for new. simple, labour intensive
\par
commodities and so open the gate to the long term structural changes
\par
that must accompany sustained and equitable economic development.
\par
\emph{Bimodal strategies,} on the other hand, concentrate largely on the inten-
\par
sive injection of new technology, and have been applied in large scale de-
\par
velopment projects in many African countries. Smallholder agriculture is
\par
to a very great degree neglected. This strategy may have a bigger impact in
\par
the short run and on the fraction of the population it affects, but from the
\par
point of view of long term general development and broad based participa-
\par
tion, it is. arguably, more or less sterile.
\par
During the seventies, the emphasis therefore shifted to "small is beauti-
\par
ful" and "simple is optimal" concepts (Chambers, 1980: Lele, 1977), with
\par
the newest development in the theoretical field being that of Farming Sys-
\par
tems Research (FSR). [Refer to Van Rooyen, 1984 and Rose \& Tapson,
\par
1984 for an extended reference list, as well as to the so called New House-
\par
hold Economics Theory (Low, 1984)].
\par
An interesting point about these latest developments is that they have led
\par
to the re-emergence of interest in the achievements of the Green Revolu-
\par
tion (See Farmer, 1977), as well as to Integrated Development and the
\par
Community Development movement of the 1950s and early 1960s
\par
(Holdcroft, 1978) mainly as a result of the attention now being directed to
\par
the rural poor and the productive content of some of their basic needs.
\par
The study of field of agricultural economics has also dramatically ex-
\par
panded to account for development oriented programmes. Study topics
\par
include agricultural market organisation, land economics, natural re-
\par
source development economics, rural development and welfare
\par
economics, environmental studies, production economics, agricultural
\par
development economics, agricultural and food policy analysis, opera-
\par
tional research, agricultural law, cooperatives, international trade, etc.
\par
In this section certain major developments have been briefly indicated.
\par
More specific discussion on the theory and applicable methodology, re-
\par
search and analyses will be integrated into the following sections, in
\par
which the operational development of agricultural economics will be em-
\par
phasized.
\par
273\textbf{2.3 Institutional developments}
\par
After most of the developing nations had become independent (from the
\par
1950s onwards), economic growth and development were given high
\par
priority. Particular attention was paid to institutional matters, in order to
\par
throw off the "yoke" of colonialism. For instance, it was often argued that
\par
institutional change was a prerequisite for agricultural development, and
\par
such change did indeed take place in certain countries.
\par
Changes in land ownership systems were an important sphere of change
\par
during this period. It fell to the agricultural economist to make descriptive
\par
and analytical studies of the existing land ownership situation, and to
\par
quantify the economic implications of alternative systems (Mellor, 1980).
\par
In general, little came of this, because land ownership frequently carried a
\par
political and emotional priority which could not (or would not) wait for the
\par
outcome for long term studies.
\par
Among other institutional developments were the organisation of depart-
\par
ments of agriculture and rural development, the analysis of macro-ag-
\par
ricultural situations and the organisation of co-operative movements. As
\par
a result of an ineffective marketing system, agricultural economists were
\par
often used to create, manage and advise marketing and financial institu-
\par
tions.
\par
Within the Southern African context, the Tomlinson Commission (see
\par
discussion under 2.5) is a good illustration of the early contribution of ag-
\par
ricultural economics to the problem of rural development and institu-
\par
tional reform.
\par
\textbf{2.4 Growth and surplus production in agriculture}
\par
After the initial acknowledgement that agriculture was and would remain
\par
the Cinderella sector of economic development, it became obvious by the
\par
end of the 1960s that agriculture not only could, but had to, make a sig-
\par
nificant contribution. Agricultural surpluses in particular were consi-
\par
dered to be an important goal (Hayami \& Ruttan, 1971; Mellor, 1980).
\par
From being concerned solely with institutional matters, agricultural
\par
economists gradually became more involved in efforts to increase produc-
\par
tion and in so doing to transform existing agriculture into commercial ag-
\par
riculture.
\par
During this period, the practical involvement of the agricultural
\par
economist was principally influenced by Theodore Schultz's "poor but effi-
\par
cient" hypothesis, which argues that the traditional small farmer is sim-
\par
ply not in a position to increase production through the reallocation of his
\par
resources, because they are already being used to the optimum (Schultz,
\par
1964). The source of surplus production in the agricultural sector was
\par
therefore technological renewal and innovation (also see Mellor, 1980).
\par
Agriculture had to produce on higher production functions. What was
\par
needed for this was large scale capital investment following the Harrod-
\par
Domar model (Edgemand, 1979), linked to the high-yield varieties of the
\par
"Green Revolution". Large agricultural projects were an important
\par
strategy for development, with farmer involvement organised on a cen-
\par
tralistic basis (Van Rooyen, 1983). The major emphasis during this period
\par
(the sixties and seventies) related to efforts to increase efficiency in ag-
\par
ricultural resource allocations.
\par
274\textbf{2.5 Equity and distribution of growth in agriculture}
\par
Certain dramatic successes with Green Revolution technology, particu-
\par
larly in India and other Eastern countries (Mellor, 1979), were actually
\par
misleading in more than one respect. Surpluses were produced chiefly by
\par
elite groups or within the framework of projects. Because of their relative
\par
prosperity, these groups were often in a position to deprive poorer small
\par
farmers of scarce resources, leaving these farmers worse off than before
\par
the Green Revolution (Farmer, 1978: Heyer, 1981). The so-called "urban-
\par
export" approach gave preference to production by means of capital and
\par
management intensive projects, while local small scale production and
\par
marketing initiative were often stifled (Lele, 1975). The package approach
\par
of the "Green Revolution" also demanded that agricultural production
\par
take place within the confines of a "disciplined" project, because of the
\par
high financing costs and technical inputs, among others. In general,
\par
these large scale technical interventions seldom actually succeeded in
\par
stimulating broadly based farming development, although surpluses were
\par
readily produced (Heyer, 1981; Commonwealth Development Corporation
\par
Reports, 1975-1983). Heyer (1981) also refers to the "gross failures of
\par
large-scale schemes" in the African context. The Green Revolution
\par
threatened to become a "Red Revolution" due to "islands of prosperity"
\par
within "oceans of poverty"!
\par
The lid of Pandora's box was lifted during the 1970s with the quantifica-
\par
tion of the enormously uneven distribution of wealth within agriculture
\par
and the mass poverty to which this led. It was the agricultural economist
\par
in particular who, with his newly developed empirical techniques and
\par
methods of micro-analysis, contributed to this discovery (Shah, 1981).
\par
Serious questions were then raised over the correctness and applicability
\par
of the "surplus and growth" development approach.
\par
A new problem-orientated agricultural development paradigm had begun
\par
to crystalize, with the agricultural economist acquiring a new identity as
\par
an agricultural development economist. With the help of his empirical
\par
techniques, the problems of underdevelopment could be quantified, not
\par
only philosophically and politically, but also analytically.
\par
The greatest contribution of agricultural economics during this period of
\par
growth and production was to the development of methodology, adapted
\par
to problem of less-developed agriculture. It was quickly realised that the
\par
large individual farm approach of developed agriculture was inapplicable.
\par
For data collection and farm planning purposes, a detailed analysis was
\par
made of the context in which less-developed agriculture operated. Large
\par
numbers of small farmers who worked under the same circumstances
\par
made the development of cost-effective data collection methods both
\par
necessary and possible (Collinson, 1972; Norman, 1973). The manipula-
\par
tion of farming information required methodological accommodation of
\par
farming analysis and planning techniques.
\par
Even in the 1950s, Jolly had used the "test bed" approach to analyse the
\par
problems of small farmers. During the early 1960s, the use of linear prog-
\par
ramming methods was taken over from modern agriculture (Collinson,
\par
1963). Meaningful application of these deterministic techniques required
\par
that objective functions had to be accommodated within the context of the
\par
development problem, that provision be made for risks and uncertainty
\par
275(Heyer, 1972) as well as for the influence of the household on farm de-
\par
cislonmaking (Low, 1982). Maximization of family income rather than
\par
farming profit became apparent as a more realistic goal (Dillon \& Har-
\par
daker. 1980).
\par
A further important development, given the context in which the agricul-
\par
tural economist operates, was the adaptation and development of the
\par
technique of the representative farming model (Kahlon, 1962). Individual
\par
participation and action was finally viewed as necessary within less-de-
\par
veloped agriculture, to alleviate the problems of poverty and low rural
\par
growth and production.
\par
The bread and butter contribution of the agricultural economist during
\par
the "growth and surplus" period was possibly small. In practical terms,
\par
the agricultural economist went through an identity crisis. The develop-
\par
ment of theories on agricultural development and the methodological ac-
\par
commodations in data collection and analysis, techniques of farming
\par
planning and other micro-level analyses, however, must be seen as most
\par
important contributions to economic development. In this period, the
\par
small farmer as farmer, as member of a group and as a person was "disco-
\par
vered". This discovery exposed the doleful failure of the "institutional" and
\par
"growth and surplus" orientations; in fact it opened up a Pandora's box!
\par
\textbf{2.6 A "new" development paradigm}
\par
One of the characteristics of this new development paradigm was that ag-
\par
riculture was not viewed merely as a collection of natural resources, capi-
\par
tal, labour and managerial inputs, but rather as a collection of situations
\par
or systems, each with its own properties, problems and solutions. The
\par
problem of less-developed agriculture could now be described quantita-
\par
tively in terms of mass poverty. ineffective production methods and li-
\par
mited broad basis participation in development programmes. Agricul-
\par
tural development was seen as an integral part of integrated rural develop-
\par
ment. Surplus production through the optimum exploitation of resources
\par
as well as the abolition of poverty through participation in agricultural
\par
production, was set as a goal in viable agricultural policies and strategies
\par
(Van Rooyen, 1983).
\par
One of the most important results of this new, problem-orientated de-
\par
velopment paradigm was the bi-modal, even multi-modal strategies and
\par
stratified approach to agricultural development (Van Rooyen, 1983; Vil-
\par
joen, 1983). Surpluses for growth were still important, but the struggle
\par
against mass poverty was given equal priority. Large-scale capital and
\par
management intensive projects were still considered to be important, but
\par
emphasis was placed strongly upon the development and participation of
\par
small farmers. Programmes and projects focused on the small farmer
\par
within the framework of his fanning system. (To a large extent, this em-
\par
phasis promoted the Farm Systems Research approach.) Productive
\par
employment opportunities were also of primary importance, making the
\par
agricultural sector more than just a food producer.
\par
The relationship between the households, the farming system and the
\par
non-farming environment was also exploited (Low, 1984). The insights of
\par
John Mellor deserve mention too. In his book\emph{ The new economics of}
\par
276\emph{growth} (1979), he links agricultural production via consumer behaviour
\par
to productive job creation in the rural areas, in order to accommodate the
\par
conflict between growth and equity. The key role of agriculture lies in in-
\par
creasing rural income levels through broadly based participation in de-
\par
mand oriented production, aided by labour intensive modern production
\par
methods, with the concommitant stimulation of labour intensive con-
\par
sumer goods industries.
\par
In this "new" paradigm, a further insight was necessary before the func-
\par
tion of the agricultural development economist, particularly in the African
\par
connection, could crystallize: namely, the perception of the group as a
\par
power factor. According to Judith Heyer (1981), any attempt to bring
\par
about rural and agricultural development requires a compromise between
\par
"winners" and "losers". For instance, should it be politically hazardous for
\par
a political leader or tribal chief to support an agricultural programme
\par
which would benefit certain groups at the expense of others, such a prog-
\par
ramme could be scaled down, undermined or banned. The resolution of
\par
these conflicts would make it possible for strategies, programmes and
\par
projects to be drawn up and implemented.
\par
Agricultural development economists will therefore have to give attention
\par
to the distribution of financial, economic and social prosperity between
\par
the groups involved in the development process. Without it. there exists
\par
the danger that the agricultural development economist will appear to be
\par
confined to a "have tool will travel" expert, as Collinson (1981) puts it,
\par
without being substantially involved in the important decisionmaking
\par
process.
\par
\textbf{3. AGRICULTURAL DEVELOPMENT ECONOMICS IN SOUTHERN}
\par
\textbf{AFRICA}
\par
The first notable achievement of agricultural economics in development
\par
in Southern Africa was the appointment of Prof F R Tomlinson as chair-
\par
man of the Commission for the Socio-economic development of Bantu
\par
areas within the Union of South Africa, which reported in 1955. Under his
\par
leadership, the realities of developing agriculture in the under-developed
\par
areas of Southern Africa were systimatically described and scientifically
\par
analysed. Although political forces during the late fifties severely re-
\par
stricted the implementation of some of the most important recommenda-
\par
tions, various of these recommendations are still most relevant to the pre-
\par
sent day situation.
\par
In view of these historical developments, it is not surprising that at the
\par
first congress of the Agricultural Economics Association of South Africa
\par
(AEASA), in 1962, with its theme the role of the agricultural economist
\par
and Prof Tomlinson as chairman, no paper was delivered on the role of the
\par
agricultural development economist. In 1969 an AEASA congress was
\par
held with agricultural development as its theme. It, however, also failed to
\par
clearly spell out the role of the agricultural economist within developing
\par
agriculture in Southern Africa.
\par
Since the mid-seventies, regular contributions have been included in
\par
similar congresses on the question of development* while South Africa
\par
* For example: Fenyes, 1978: Fenyes \& Van Niekerk, 1979: Van Rooyen and De
\par
Swart, 1979: Groenewald, 1981: Behrmann, 1981; Bembridge, 1984.
\par
277academics have even been invited to give their views on development in in-
\par
ternational forums. *
\par
In the practical field the agricultural economist is increasingly making a
\par
pertinent contribution. Prof F R Tomlinson recently observed that the ag-
\par
ricultural economic discipline is justly represented in development plan-
\par
ning and implementation. In this respect, agricultural economists play an
\par
important role, particularly in the management functions of public and
\par
parastatal institutions such as departments of agriculture and develop-
\par
ment corporations and, recently, the Development Bank of Southern Af -
\par
rica. They also function in development projects as well as in teaching,
\par
training and research. Agricultural economists are also involved in con-
\par
sultation and advisory services.
\par
4. AREAS OF AGRICULTURAL ECONOMIC INVOLVEMENT
\par
In the following paragraphs the contribution and potential contribution
\par
of agricultural economics and agricultural economists will be briefly
\par
examined. From this discussion, the broad scope of this discipline will
\par
emerge distinctly. Emphasis will be placed on the Southern African scene,
\par
although international experience will also be referred to.
\par
\textbf{4.1 Farmer development and micro level involvement}
\par
The development of small scale farming is regarded as unavoidable in
\par
meaningful rural development. The need for micro-economic investiga-
\par
tion and analysis in policymaking and planning has already been men-
\par
tioned. This integration is particularly Important to balance the tradi-
\par
tional "top down" project approach in agricultural development with
\par
realities as they appear at the farm level. The already mentioned\emph{ Farming}
\par
\emph{Systems Research} (FSR) approach offers the conceptual framework
\par
within which the agricultural development economist can apply well
\par
known and tested techniques such as budgets, gross margin analyses,
\par
linear programming, comparisons between farming sectors, etc. The FSR
\par
approach provides for the need to analyse the economic logic of an exist-
\par
ing farming system (Norman, 1978), as well as to judge technical innova-
\par
tion and complementary inputs such as credit, marketing facilities and
\par
extension programmes in terms of their total impact on the existing or
\par
proposed farming system (Colllnson, 1981).
\par
Relatively little agricultural economic work is still being done by means of
\par
this approach in the Southern African context (Van Rooyen, 1984). Bem-
\par
bridge (1983) analysed agricultural development problems in certain dis-
\par
tricts in Transkei with the aid of a system approach, and Kleynhans
\par
(1983) refers to the farming system in a study into the acceptance of
\par
technology by smallholder farmers in KwaZulu. The Shiela/Mooifontein
\par
maize projects in Bophuthatswana were also investigated by means of a
\par
system approach (Bembridge\emph{ et al,} 1982), whilst farming systems
\par
analysis was prominent in investigating the development potential of the
\par
Makatini Flats (Van Rooyen, 1983). In none of these examples, though,
\par
was there any question of true FSR. However, the establishment of a
\par
"Small Farmer Systems Research Centre" by the University of Fort Hare
\par
(Rose \& Tapson, 1984) holds some potential.
\par
{$\bullet$} Fenyes, 1979; Van Rooyen \& De Swart, 1979; Fenyes \& Van Rooyen, 1984;
\par
Fenyes, Vink \& Van Rooyen, 1980; Van Rooyen, De Swart \& Fraser, 1981;Bem-
\par
bridge, 1979; Behrmann, 1983.
\par
278The role of the agricultural economist at the micro level may be sum-
\par
marised as aiding the smallholder farmer. His task is thus primarily
\par
aimed at determining:
\par
- How the smallholder farmer operates, why he/she does it the way he/she
\par
does and how the system can be improved.
\par
- What the impact of technological innovation on the farming system will
\par
be. In this respect, cash flow, financial results and the creation of
\par
employment are of particular importance.
\par
- Which forces frustrate agricultural modernisation at the farm level and
\par
what institutional arrangements can be made to resolve such bot-
\par
tenecks.
\par
- What sort of conflicts can possibly arise through development actions
\par
and how they can be handled.
\par
- What total input package is required for profitable technological mod-
\par
ernisation, and in what proportions must complementary inputs be
\par
combined. A plan should, for example, provide the adequate quantities
\par
of the prescribed inputs to be available to the small farmers at the re-
\par
quired times. Credit facilities, extension actions and marketing
\par
facilities, etc, must simultaneously be organised. The argument of the
\par
correct institutional framework must therefore be addressed perti-
\par
nently.
\par
- The conditions which must be complied with to ensure that the small-
\par
holder farming family is able to maintain a minimum acceptable stan-
\par
dard of living through agriculture and/or related rural activities. For
\par
example, how large should the farming unit be, how much labour is re-
\par
quired, what will the cash flow pattern be, what additional employment
\par
can be generated via agro related activities?
\par
- How to initiate and monitor the small farmer development program-
\par
mes and projects in the light of the abovementioned points.
\par
In every one of these respects, the agricultural development economist will
\par
be able to function meaningfully only within the team context, in which
\par
he obtains the co-operation of agronomists, soil scientists, engineers, ac-
\par
countants, rural sociologists and even anthropologists, medical doctors,
\par
traders, spiritual and cultural leaders and politicians. The agricultural
\par
economist can, indeed, act as coordinator or pivot because of his training
\par
in natural as well as social sciences.
\par
\textbf{4.2 Project planning and analysis}
\par
Gittinger (1972) regarded agricultural projects as the "cutting edge" of de-
\par
velopment, and Kuiper (1972) defined a project as "a concentration of
\par
forces in a strategy of initiating improvements". Heyer (1981), however,
\par
pointed to the "gross failure of large scale schemes" in Africa.
\par
Agricultural project design, planning, analysis and choice should thus be
\par
handled with great discretion. The well trained agricultural development
\par
economist is particularly well equipped to make a meaningful contribu-
\par
tion in this respect.
\par
Scale size is often a factor which receives nowhere nearly enough atten-
\par
tion in project design. Scale size does not refer only to the physical size of a
\par
project, such as for example the size of an irrigation dam, but also the size
\par
and network of the irrigation canals, the size of the plots, the scale of the
\par
farming support services, the creation of employment opportunities, etc.
\par
279The agricultural economist should actively support the engineer and ag-
\par
riculturalist in the design of the project, particularly in the consideration
\par
of alternative scale sizes (models) (Van Rooyen, 1983).
\par
Public funds are often used as development capital in the establishment of
\par
development projects. Apart from the financial profitability of the par-
\par
ticipating groups (farmers etc), the broad economic and social impact of
\par
the projects should also be quantified as far as possible and included in
\par
the project evaluation (Little \& Mirrilees, 1977).
\par
With the assistance of FSR and planning techniques, such as linear prog-
\par
ramming and representative farming organisation models, realistic pro-
\par
jections of the net family farming income can be made. The sizes of
\par
economic farming units can thereby be determined. The realistic estima-
\par
tion of yields, input and product prices and the labour coefficient also play
\par
an important role. Agricultural economists are well equipped for these cal-
\par
culations.
\par
The quantification of the economic and social profitability of a project is
\par
often more problematical. According to the methodology of social cost-be-
\par
nefit analysis (Little \& Mirrlees, 1977; Gittinger, 1982), the benefits and
\par
costs of the project should be identified in terms of their economic and so-
\par
cial influences, and as far as possible quantified by using "shadow prices".
\par
The establishment of realistic shadow prices, again, requires teamwork
\par
with agricultural economic inputs as well as the cooperation of
\par
economists, policymakers and politicians.
\par
\textbf{4.3 Macro aspects: policy and planning}
\par
The traditional role of the agricultural economist in the implementation
\par
of policy is that of data collection and analysis ("Data gatherer", Cromarty,
\par
1981). However, the argument so far shows that the agricultural develop-
\par
ment economist ought to play a much more important and comprehen-
\par
sive role than this. The agricultural development economist dare not be a
\par
"yes man" (Schulz, 1976), but must point out the financial, economic and
\par
social implications (benefits and costs) of alternative development pos-
\par
sibilities. He should therefore insist that alternative situations be
\par
analysed and considered, before policy, strategy, programmes and pro-
\par
jects are formulated. The social cost-benefit methodology can play an im-
\par
portant role in this process.
\par
The role of the agricultural economist as data collector and processor is of
\par
particular value. The information accumulated forces decisionmakers to
\par
give recognition to the agricultural economic department. This offers the
\par
opportunity to get a "foot in the door" to participate more realistically in
\par
the decisionmaking process. An important prerequisite to meaningful ag-
\par
ricultural economic contribution on the macro level thus lies in the estab-
\par
lishment of an accurate, timely and relevant information system. Such an
\par
information system naturally also makes it possible for the agricultural
\par
development economist to apply his background of analytical training
\par
and thereby raise the quality of analysis and constructiveness of the deci-
\par
sion making process.
\par
The contribution of the agricultural development economist at the level of
\par
macro policy and planning is well captured by Kuiper (1972, p 226): "Polit-
\par
ical considerations, whether they are valid or not, play their role in the de-
\par
280cision making process, but so does economic analysis. There is no doubt
\par
that its influence will be stronger when it is based on sound data, on
\par
sound methodology and on sound reasoning. Instead of belittling its value
\par
("economic analysis") let us make the most of it".
\par
\textbf{4.4 Research and training}
\par
Important issues, identified by recent research and analysis in Southern
\par
Africa, are listed by Spies, 1982* as:
\par
i The transformation of traditional social structures and institutions.
\par
Arguments in this respect refer to
\par
- land tenure institutions
\par
- the role of cattle in the communities
\par
- the traditional allocation of responsibilities within the communities
\par
- the tribal structure (e g the power of traditional leaders) which can
\par
inhibit profitable innovations;
\par
ii The socio-economic impact of certain South African practices such as
\par
migrant labour, restrictions on human movement, the sub-division of
\par
agricultural development agencies and the historical neglect of Black
\par
agricultural development
\par
iii The quality and quantity of agricultural resources in the depressed
\par
rural areas
\par
iv The managerial quality of the human resources in depressed rural
\par
areas.
\par
As to agricultural economic training, many of the topics mentioned in 2.1
\par
are taught by departments of agricultural economics in universities in
\par
Southern Africa. Attention is also directed to instruction in agricultural
\par
development planning. The University of Stellenbosch, for example, pre-
\par
sents a two-year masters programme in agricultural and rural develop-
\par
ment. It is also illuminating that agricultural economics students have al-
\par
ready taken note of agricultural development as a potential career. In a
\par
survey (Van Rooyen, 1980), 47 per cent of all agricultural economic stu-
\par
dents showed interest in this direction for study as well as future employ-
\par
ment.
\par
In agricultural development training it is of great importance that cogni-
\par
sance be taken at the theoretical level of the problem of under-develop-
\par
ment, both at the micro and macro levels. Agricultural economists in
\par
South Africa traditionally make their contribution from a micro orienta-
\par
tion. Within the sphere of development problems, a micro orientation re-
\par
mains of great value. However, to be able to make a meaningful analysis of
\par
broader economic and social situations and become a decision-maker,
\par
serious attention should be given to training in macro economic analysis
\par
and welfare economics. Project evaluation also deserves to be offered as a
\par
subject in its own right, while courses in agricultural development plan-
\par
ning and the economic aspects of agricultural development offer funda-
\par
mental background and insight.
\par
In addition to the related references listed in the text, the following documents
\par
should also be noted:
\par
Annual reports of the departments of agriculture of the independent and na-
\par
tional states; relevant Benso and Development Bank publications and research
\par
projects concerning the development of less-developed areas in Southern Africa:
\par
Research documents from the Universities of Pretoria, Stellenbosch. RAU ,
\par
Rhodes, the Universities of the North, and of Fort Hare, to give a fe w examples.
\par
281Because of the great mobility of agricultural economists and the wide
\par
range of employment opportunities in both developed and lesser-de-
\par
veloped agriculture in Southern Africa, and because of the interest dis-
\par
played by students, universities should seriously consider prescribing the
\par
abovementioned directions of study for all undergraduate agricultural
\par
economics students. Such a training programme should not only contri-
\par
bute to avoidance of "reinventing the wheel", but also help prevent con-
\par
frontation between agricultural development economists, or at least make
\par
their differences more meaningful.
\par
However, training is not limited to the universities. An agricultural
\par
economist is naturally a university graduate, but can also make a mean-
\par
ingful contribution to the inservice training of other agriculturalists, to
\par
agricultural college training, and to the managerial training of farmers
\par
(Fenyes, Vink \& Van Rooyen, 1980).
\par
\textbf{4.5 The conditions for a meaningful contribution}
\par
From the abovementioned sections it is clear that a meaningful contribu-
\par
tion by agricultural economists requires two conditions to be met:\emph{ Firstly}
\par
it is essential that the other individuals and groups involved in develop-
\par
ment have an understanding of the professional function of the agricul-
\par
tural economist. An agricultural development economist cannot, for
\par
example, build dams or design and build roads, nor breed maize seed. He
\par
can, however, weigh up alternative agricultural development strategies,
\par
relate demand to supply and evaluate different crop programmes.\emph{ Sec-}
\par
\emph{ondly,} the agricultural development economist cannot function in isola-
\par
tion. His knowledge of technical relationships, as well as his insight into
\par
social and political factors are often restricted. The agricultural develop-
\par
ment economist is primarily a team man. Outside of the team context he
\par
can easily be viewed as irrelevant or a nuisance, whereas as a member of a
\par
team he is often the pivot around which the entire action revolves.
\par
An important question with regard to the involvement of the agricultural
\par
economist, is that of where an agricultural development economist
\par
should be institutionally positioned in order to make the optimum con-
\par
tribution. Many theories and thoughts have been expressed on this (Giles,
\par
1979). With the aforementioned pre-conditions in mind a few are men-
\par
tioned here.
\par
At the micro level the agricultural development economist must move as
\par
closely as possible to the small farmer and his operation. Theodor Dams
\par
(1981) refers here to the "dirty boots level". At the macro level the agricul-
\par
tural economist must confirm his role as a decisionmaker. The closer he is
\par
to the true decisionmakers and policymakers, thus, the better. In project
\par
evaluation and planning, the "dirty boots" level is just as important as the
\par
computer room, the drawing office and the red carpet of the decisionmak-
\par
ers and the executive.
\par
In the Southern African context it appears as if the abovementioned pat-
\par
tern is developing. A survey indicates that the institutions engaged in ag-
\par
ricultural development in Southern Africa (state, semi-state and private)
\par
have about 60 agricultural economists in their employment (Van Rooyen,
\par
1984). An analysis of questionnaires sent to these authorities reveals that
\par
their agricultural economists are engaged mainly in planning, manage-
\par
ment and monitoring of projects, the financing of small farmers for cash
\par
282crop production, the arrangement of marketing, training, and the coordi-
\par
nation of agricultural programmes, as well as research and strategic plan-
\par
ning.
\par
This analysis unfortunately also leaves the impression that often "fires
\par
were being fought" by agricultural economists rather than work of long
\par
term meaning done. The versatility as well as the potential value of the ag-
\par
ricultural development economist is, however, clearly underlined, par-
\par
ticularly when their average age of only 29 years is taken into account.
\par
\textbf{5. CONCLUSION}
\par
Agricultural economics has earned its place in Southern Africa, both in
\par
modern agriculture as well as developing agriculture. The discipline's
\par
contribution to the latter ranges from fundamental analysis to opera-
\par
tional planning and implementation.
\par
As to agricultural economic research, a clear mandate is set: Policymak-
\par
ers, planners, managers and small farmers are asking questions, and
\par
problems must be identified and solved. Applied research, for example
\par
FSR, offers exceptional opportunities in which to make a meaningful con-
\par
tribution. From this, it must be clear that in research in the field of ag-
\par
ricultural development economics there is no longer place for an ivory
\par
tower approach. In this respect it should, however, also be emphasised
\par
that until more solid micro level data are available on the basis of which
\par
macro level planning, projecting and implementation can be done, the
\par
real contribution of economists (including agricultural economists) will
\par
be limited mostly to theorising and advising government(s) and their
\par
agencies on issues related to economics.
\par
As in any operationally oriented or applied related discipline, the con-
\par
tribution of agricultural economics is illuminated most distinctly by its
\par
professionals, the agricultural economists, operating in the various fields
\par
as identified. From what has been said, it can be stated that the quality of
\par
the agricultural development economist's contribution in Southern Af -
\par
rica will be determined by the answers to the following three questions:
\par
- Will the true decisionmakers and policy administrators grant the ag-
\par
ricultural development economist his rightful place as a co-decision-
\par
maker.
\par
- Is the agricultural development economist going to be permitted to get
\par
the "mud" of the small farmer's operation on his boots, and to rub
\par
shoulders with, for example, the agronomist, the soil scientist, the en-
\par
gineer and the sociologist?
\par
- If the above questions are replied to in the positive, the further ques-
\par
tion which arises is that of whether the agricultural development
\par
economist is going to be in a position to positively utilise the confi-
\par
dence placed in him. An appropriate academic training, positive pro-
\par
fessional vision, appreciation of the problems and a healthy measure of
\par
self-criticism should enable him to fulfil his role meaningfully and with
\par
enthusiasm.
