\par
One general and popular normative scheme
\par
is Bayesian inference, a set of procedures
\par
based upon Bayes' theorem and the subjectivist
\par
interpretation of probability. These procedures
\par
show how to (a) identify the data
\par
sources that are most useful for discriminating
\par
between competing hypotheses, (b) assess
\par
the implications of an observed datum vis-avis
\par
the truth of competing hypotheses, (c)
\par
aggregate the implications of different data
\par
into an overall appraisal of the relative likelihood
\par
of those hypotheses being correct, and
\par
