Theory
\par
Definition of Probability
\par
From the Bayesian perspective, knowledge
\par
is represented in terms of statements or hy-
\par
potheses, H;, each of which is characterized
\par
by a subjective probability, P(H;), representing
\par
one's confidence in its truth (DeFinetti,
\par
1976). For example, one might be .75 confident
\par
that it will snow tomorrow. Such probabilities
\par
are subjective in the sense that individuals
\par
with different knowledge (or beliefs)
\par
may legitimately assess P(H;) quite differently.
\par
The term assess is used rather than
\par
estimate to emphasize that a probability expresses
\par
one's own feelings rather than an appraisal
\par
of a property of the physical world.
\par
Thus, there is no "right" probability value
\par
for a particular statement. Even if a very low
\par
probability proves to be associated with a true
\par
statement, one cannot be sure that it was not
\par
an accurate reflection of the assessor's (apparently
\par
erroneous) store of knowledge.
\par
The constraints on subjective probabilities
\par
emerge when one considers sets of assessments.
\par
Formally speaking, a set of probabilities
\par
should be orderly or coherent in the sense
\par
of following the probability axioms (Kyburg
\par
\& Smokier, 1980; Savage, 1954).1 For example,
\par
P(H) + P(fl) should total 1.0.
\par
Updating
\par
Additional derivations from the probability
\par
axioms lead to Bayes' theorem, which governs
\par
the way in which one's beliefs in hypotheses
\par
should be updated in the light of
\par
new information. In its simplest form, the
\par
theorem treats the implications of an observation
\par
that produces the datum (D) for determining
\par
whether a hypothesis (H) is true,
\par
