\par
bration may be traced to people's assessment
\par
procedures or to the knowledge base that they
\par
rely on.
\par
A third symptom is nonconformity, producing
\par
a probability that differs from that of
\par
"expert" assessors for no apparent reason.
\par
The existence of such consensus is most likely
\par
when there is a statistical data base upon
\par
which to base probability assessments (e.g.,
\par
public health records of mortality). In this
\par
restricted realm, the distinction between subjective
\par
and objective probabilities becomes
\par
blurred, as subjectivists would typically act
\par
as though they concur with the relative-frequency
\par
interpretation of probability, which
\par
objectivists consider to be the only meaningful
\par
one. Subjectivists would, however, never
\par
concede that frequency counts are a completely
\par
objective measure of probability, arguing
\par
that judgment is needed to establish
\par
the equivalence and independence of the set
\par
of trials from which the count was extracted
\par
and to extrapolate that frequency to future
\par
events.
\par
Another way to look at bias is in terms of
\par
the process by which assessments are produced.
\par
There is reason for concern whenever
\par
the assessors have followed procedures that
\par
are inconsistent with the rules of statistical
\par
inference and are unaware of those inconsistencies.
\par
Two well-known deviations are reliance
\par
on the availability and representative-{$\cdot$}
\par
ness heuristics when making probability assessments
\par
(Kahneman, Slavic, \& Tversky,
\par
1982; Tversky \& Kahneman, 1974). Users of
\par
the former judge an event to be likely to the
\par
extent that exemplars are easy to recall or
\par
imagine; users of the latter judge an event to
\par
be likely to the extent that it represents the
\par
salient features of the process that might produce
\par
it. Although both rules can provide good
\par
service, they can also lead the user astray in
\par
predictable ways. For example, reliance on
\par
availability induces overestimation of unusually
\par
salient events (e.g., the probability of
\par
dying from flashy, hence overreported, causes
\par
such as tornadoes and homicide; Lichtenstein,
\par
Slavic, Fischhoff, Layman, \& Combs,
\par
1978).
\par
A final process problem is the refusal to
\par
consider anything but relative frequency data
\par
when one assesses probabilities. Although
\par
subjectivists acknowledge the potential rele-
\par
vance of such data, they will not be bound
\par
to them. Indeed, a key selling point of Bayesian
\par
inference is its ability to accommodate
\par
diverse kinds of data. One can, for example,
\par
assess probabilities for a meaningful shrug or
\par
an oft:.hand comment as well as for a bead
\par
drawn from an urn or for a 40-subject experiment.
\par
The only difference is that as one
\par
moves from beads to shrugs, it becomes increasingly
\par
difficult to attest to the reasonableness
\par
of a particular assessment. An assessor
\par
who failed to seek useful and available
\par
nonfrequency evidence would, from a Bayesian
\par
perspective, be foolish. An individual
\par
who ignored nonfrequency evidence that was
\par
already on hand would be biased.6
\par
Assessing the Prior
\par
Prior beliefs arc captured by the ratio of
\par
the probabilities of the competing hypotheses
\par
prior to the collection of further evidence. As
\par
a result, the difficulties of assessing prior beliefs
\par
are by and large the sum of the difficulties
\par
of formulating hypotheses.and assessing
\par
component probabilities, as already discussed.
\par
There seem, however, to be at least
\par
two additional (and incompatible) problems
\par
that may affect this particular stage of the
\par
assessment process.
\par
One such problem is not treating the component
\par
probabilities equally. An extreme
\par
form of inequality is neglecting one of the
\par
hypotheses. When people act as though a
\par
hypothesis that is most probably true is absolutely
\par
true, they have effectively neglected
\par
its complement. In that case, hypothesis evaluation
\par
never begins, because one hypothesis
\par
is treated as fact. Even when both hypotheses
\par
are considered, one may be given deferential
\par
treatment. Skeptics, for example, may give
\par
undue weight to evidence that contradicts a
\par
favored hypothesis; they have a warm spot
\par
in their hearts for complements. On the other
\par
hand, Koriat, Lichtenstein, and Fischhoff
\par
( 1980) found favoritism for initially favored
