\emph{Copyright Information}THE DISCRIMINATING \textbf{USE }OF
\par
INFORMATION DISCLOSURE \textbf{RULES }BY
\par
THE FEDERAL TRADE \textbf{COMMISSION}
\par
\textbf{by }Robert \textbf{J. }Gage*
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Consumer ignorance unavoidably leads to unwise purchasing
\par
decisions, resulting in harm to consumers, to competing producers
\par
and to the economy as a whole. Due to their ignorance, consum-
\par
ers may be unable to assert sufficient purchasing power to stimu-
\par
late effective competition. Thus, consumers may pay higher prices
\par
for the goods and services which they purchase. In a period dur-
\par
ing which so much public discussion has been focused on the
\par
problems of inadequate income and inflationary pressures, too lit-
\par
tle concern has been devoted to improving the availability of
\par
product information, an endeavor which could indirectly increase
\par
the real income of consumers.
\par
\textbf{I. }THE \textbf{JUSTIFICATION }FOR \textbf{PUBLIC }INTERVENTION
\par
Inducing producers to provide more information about their
\par
products may be a proper pursuit of public policymakers.' The
\par
information in question, herein referred to as material informa-
\par
tion, is that information which, if known, would affect the
\par
\textbf{* }Member, District of Columbia Bar, B.A., Kent State University, \textbf{1973; J.D.,}
\par
Harvard Law School, \textbf{1977; }M.P.P., Harvard University John F. Kennedy School of
\par
Government, \textbf{1977.}
\par
\textbf{1. }This Article is addressed to the problem presented \textbf{by }the failure of the free
\par
market to provide information about consumer products. Consumer products may be
\par
thought of as those products and services which are produced, rendered or distributed
\par
for the personal use, consumption or enjoyment of individuals in or around a perma-
\par
nent or temporary household, residence or school, or those products and services
\par
which an individual may utilize in the ordinary course of events attendant to the
\par
management of his \textbf{daily }affairs or the affairs of his family. Consumer products do
\par
not include those products and services used in the production processes of a manu-
\par
facturing or similar capital enterprise.
\par
\copyright{} \textbf{1979 by }Robert \textbf{J. }Gage.
\par
\textbf{All }Rights Reserved.
\par
\textbf{10371038}
\par
\emph{UCL4 LAW REVIEW }[Vol. 26:1037
\par
purchasing decisions of a significant number of consumers.2 The
\par
essential question is whether there presently exists situations in the
\par
market in which material information is unavailable to consumers
\par
and in which, with minimal government intervention, such infor-
\par
mation could be made available at a cost substantially less than
\par
the corresponding benefit to consumers.
\par
\emph{A. Function of Information}
\par
\textbf{A }number of economic and social purposes are fulfilled \textbf{by}
\par
consumer knowledge of material information about goods and
\par
services. First, information facilitates efficient consumer choice
\par
among competing products. Such choice maximizes an individ-
\par
ual's satisfaction within a given budgetary constraint and assures
\par
an efficient allocation of societal resources.3 Information about
\par
material product characteristics can prevent consumers from
\par
purchasing goods which are relatively less useful, less safe or less
\par
effective than competing products. Without sufficient easily acces-
\par
sible information, purchasing decisions are likely to be made on
\par
the basis of'guess work, psychological and emotional manipula-
\par
tion, and a general inclination to "satisfice" rather than optimize
\par
one's expenditures.4 The key factor is that the consumer have ac-.
\par
2. Material information may be thought of as the information which a person
\par
evaluates in determining the value of a product to him. This information may be
\par
used by the consumer in determining whether he wants a type of product or in com-
\par
paring the product with its competition. Such information may relate to the good's
\par
effect on health or safety, or to its composition, use, cost, or performance, including its
\par
effectiveness in fulfilling the reasonable purposes for which it may be purchased. It
\par
may also relate to durability, reliability, warranty, or ease of repair, or be background
\par
information which might enable a person better to evaluate the need for or desirabil-
\par
ity of a product. \emph{See }text accompanying note 59 \emph{infra.}
\par
3. The economy as a whole is damaged by consumers' lack of information, as
\par
such ignorance produces inefficiencies in the allocation of resources and distribution
\par
of goods and services. To achieve pareto optimality, the efficient situation in which
\par
individual satisfaction and resource utilization are maximized, given an exogenous
\par
resource pool, it is necessary that all markets be purely competitive. Notwithstanding
\par
that a pareto optimal objective is illusory and unreachable within our present eco-
\par
nomic structure given its degree of governmental regulation, it may nevertheless be
\par
better approached if purchasers possess a high degree of knowledge about market
\par
products. The possession of perfect information is a necessary condition of pure com-
\par
petition, which is in turn an assumption of pareto optimality. For development of the
\par
technical aspects of this argument, see M. REDER, \textbf{STUDIES IN THE THEORY }OF WEL-
\par
FARE ECONOMICS (1947); I. SCITOVSKY, WELFARE \textbf{AND COMPETITION }(rev. ed. 1971);
\par
Bator, \emph{The Simple Anaytics of Welfare Maximization, }47 AM. \textbf{ECON. }REV. 22 (1957).
\par
A higher degree of consumer knowledge would induce more rational purchasing,
\par
thereby shifting demand among firms and industries. This in turn would create a
\par
more efficient allocation of resources, and further growth in the economy would
\par
permit firms to realize proper advantages of their potential economies of scale. Con-
\par
sumers, in turn, would benefit directly by capitalizing on those advantages through
\par
more competitive pricing.
\par
4. Without adequate information, \textbf{the }consumer is unable to allocate his avail-
\par
able income in accordance with his individual preferences and thereby \textbf{achieve }the1979] \emph{DISCRIMINATING USE OF RULES }1039
\par
cess to clear, objective standards for evaluating and comparing
\par
products rather than vague perceptions of their characteristics or
\par
merits.
\par
Product information also stimulates price and quality compe-
\par
tition. Such competition among established firms may be limited
\par
