II. POLICY \textbf{ALTERNATIVES}
\par
There are a number of approaches which policymakers in the
\par
public sector might take to enhance the accessibility to consumers
\par
of material information.
\par
\emph{A. Government Analysis and Dissemination}
\par
Under one approach, the government analyzes products
\par
which are available to consumers and disseminates the informa-
\par
tion generated by the analyses. Each product is laboratory-tested
\par
relative to selected standards. Such standards relate to product
\par
composition, health and safety hazard, use and performance capa-
\par
bility. Dissemination can be accomplished by placing the infor-
\par
mation on file at the Federal Trade Commissio-i's public affairs
\par
office, compiling it into industry brochures and making them
\par
available at public request, issuing media releases concurrent with
\par
new publications, or advertising test results in the popular media.
\par
Although some government testing may be desirable for a va-
\par
riety of reasons,5' testing of every available consumer product is
\par
infeasible. The vast array of products and models offered in the
\par
market coupled with the dynamics of changing design and inno-
\par
vation would make such a program prohibitively expensive. Fur-
\par
ther, requiring producers to reimburse the government for this
\par
expense would lead to incalculable administrative burdens.
\par
\emph{B. Industry Analysis and Government Dissemination}
\par
A second and closely related approach increases the flow of
\par
material information. This alternative requires producers to sub-
\par
mit for government dissemination information generated in their
\par
own tests performed according to specified government stan-
\par
dards.5 2 While this program still entails the cost of product analy-
\par
ses, any program which provides greater information to
\par
consumers would share this disadvantage. Given that many man-
\par
ufacturers already engage in extensive product testing, the overall
\par
cost of the program should be significantly less when performed in
\par
51. For example, the government may wish to test products to enhance its own
