REGULATION
\par
\emph{A. Importance of Product or Industry}
\par
The products having the most significant potential impact on
\par
consumers are those affecting personal health or safety. Losses
\par
occasioned by illness and injury include pain, suffering, medical
\par
bills, foregone earnings and opportunities, or in the gravest situa-
\par
tion, loss of life. Expenses can far exceed the cost of the item
\par
which causes the distress. Examples of such problems include the
\par
health effects caused by smoking cigarettes or inhaling toxic fumes
\par
and possible hazards emanating from the use of microwave ovens,
\par
pesticides, or explosive or flammable materials.
\par
Other products having a significant impact on consumers are
\par
those entailing the expenditure of a relatively high percentage of
\par
one's income. Such expenditure may result from the fact that the
\par
items, though infrequently purchased, are highly priced, or that
\par
the items are frequently purchased, though relatively lower priced.
\par
A further category of products which might have an impact
\par
on consumers and thus may be a proper subject for public regula-
\par
tion is products purchased by a large percentage of the popula-
\par
tion. However, the most important factor will be that a relatively
\par
high percentage of consumer spending is devoted to that product.
\par
Thus, this final category, though of interest to most consumers,
\par
may be of little significance in their household budgets. If a sig-
\par
nificant portion of the purchasers of a particular product are low-
\par
income consumers, however, the need for regulation is greater. 97
\par
