\emph{C. Effect of Information}
\par
Even if all of the previously enumerated requirements are
\par
satisfied, it still might be inappropriate for the Commission to
\par
mandate disclosure in a particular situation if the proposed disclo-
\par
sure will have little or no effect on either consumer purchasing or
\par
industry behavior. 25 The effect which the information will have
\par
on consumers is the key to disclosure. Thus, before taking action,
\par
the FTC should evaluate the disclosure proposal to determine
\par
whether the mandated information is likely to affect the market-
\par
place by altering consumer purchasing patterns, stimulating price
\par
and quality competition, or impeding deceptive advertising.
\par
Although an effort should be made to predict the likely con--
\par
sequences of the rule, it will be difficult to conduct an accurate
\par
study. One might, however, commission an experiment as was
\par
suggested by the Consumers Union during congressional delibera.-
\par
tion of the need for product testing. 26 Such an experiment would
\par
provide consumers with selected product information. By analyz-
\par
ing purchasing patterns before and after institution of the pro-
\par
gram, the consumer value of such knowledge could be
\par
determined. A simulation of this experiment also could be con-
\par
ducted by survey, analyzing tradeoff decisions made by consumers
\par
as qualitative product information is varied. Although both of
\par
these methods fail to assess market shifts directly, it should be
\par
possible to use the study data in conjunction with available data
\par
relating to the economy and develop an empirical model to pre-
\par
dict expected shifts and their consequent effect on competitive
\par
conditions.
\par
Irrespective of the method chosen, recent legislation requires
\par
that some economic analysis precede adoption of FTC rules. 27
