\par
apparatus, method, or process disclosed in this report.ABSTRACT
\par
The second volume of the report "The Effects of Risk on Prices and Quantities of
\par
Energy Supplies" presents a synthesis of developments in the economic theory of
\par
uncertainty and a discussion of the implications of the theory for energy supply.
\par
The first chapter is an overview of the economics of uncertainty with precise definitions
\par
of basic concepts such as the meaning of risk, risk aversion, and insurance.
\par
Several propositions concerning the efficient spreading of risk and the possible
\par
mechanisms for risk-bearing are described. There is also a discussion of the efficiency
\par
with which the market provides insurance and other mechanisms for absorbing
\par
risks. Detailed applications of the economics of uncertainty to particular areas
\par
of energy supply are described in volume III, but several general applications are
\par
suggested in this volume.
\par
The second chapter examines the effects of uncertainty on the decisions of firms
\par
and industrial market structure. The chapter surveys many results that are widely
\par
scattered in the literature and presents several new findings. The results of this
\par
analysis cast doubt on several propositions of classical microeconomics. For
\par
example, firms may not choose the production technique with minimum cost in the
\par
presence of risk.
\par
There are many problems which require a wider analysis than a study of an industry
\par
in isolation. The desirability of an action taken in one sector of the economy may
\par
depend on the response of the rest of the economy. The study of these economic
\par
repercussions is the subject of general equilibrium analysis. The third chapter
\par
surveys the economic analysis of uncertainty within a general equilibrium framework.
\par
The fourth and final chapter of this volume is concerned with analyzing what is
\par
meant by information and its economic value. The determinants of the value of
\par
information are discussed and a careful distinction is drawn between the value of
\par
information for private use and its value to society.
\par
The economics of uncertainty is a relatively new field, and this volume represents
\par
a current synthesis of the main results. The level of presentation assumes a knowledge
\par
of the economic theory of production and consumption under conditions of certainty
\par
and an elementary familiarity with mathematical and statistical techniques.
\par
