viiChapter 1
\par
THE PRINCIPLES OF RISK-BEARING
\par
I. INTRODUCTION
\par
Uncertainty---uncertainty about supplies of fuels, about costs of factors, about government
\par
actions---has a major effect on the supply of energy and its costs of production.
\par
Indeed, earlier writers treated the supply of risk-bearing as one of the four
\par
major factors of production, along with land, labor, and capital. Although riskbearing
\par
was probably the least understood of these factors, it seemed to be the most
\par
important factor in explaining a number of economic phenomena, e.g., the nature of
\par
firms, differential success of different economies, etc. It became increasingly
\par
clear that uncertainty was a factor distinct from the other "factors" of production.
\par
Recent developments in economic theory provide a basis for a better understanding
\par
of the precise way uncertainty affects the economy; yet, many of the conundrums
\par
which puzzled earlier writers remain unresolved.
\par
This chapter of our report represents an overview of the causes and consequences of
\par
our uncertainty to the energy sector of the economy. The production function for
\par
the energy sector has received extensive study in recent years. These studies focus
\par
on conventional inputs, the inputs of labor, capital and raw materials; but for the
\par
most part they ignore the "fourth factor". \emph{Changes} in uncertainty, however, are likely to
\par
have an important role in determining changes in costs of production and output over
\par
the next half century. The state of the art here is far less developed than that
\par
used for analyzing the role of the conventional inputs; and it is for this reason
\par
that most of this study will be v.encerned not with estimating the effect of uncertainty,
\par
but with laying the groundwork on the basis of which some estimates may be derived.
\par
An improved understanding of the effect of uncertainty is important for several
\par
other reasons. Although this study is not primarily concerned with policy questions,
\par
the framework which we develop can be used to answer a number of important policy
\par
issues facing both the industry and government. It may be useful simply to list a
\par
few of these questions.
\par
2.1-1There are a number of questions associated with the alleviation of the effects of
\par
uncertainty. One of the sources of uncertainty is uncertainty about demand,
\par
another is uncertainty about the future state of technology and about stocks of
\par
reserves of natural resources. Information about these variables is what economists
\par
call a pure public good; there is a negligible marginal cost in providing the
\par
information to additional individuals and it is difficult for any single individual
\par
or firm to appropriate the returns from acquiring the information. Thus, the market
\par
will, in general, provide an insufficient quantity of information. The consequence
\par
of this is that individuals bear an excessive amount of uncertainty. Thus,
\par
there is a prima facie case for either the government or the industry to provide
\par
this information. How much should it provide? What are the criteria by which it
\par
should decide the kind of information to acquire? Should it sell the information
\par
or give it away freely? On what criteria should firms be ''told'' to provide the
\par
costs of the information? Is some kind of benefit tax appropriate?
\par
Even apart from information problems, the classical assumptions under which it can
\par
be shown that markets possess optimality properties are not, in general, satisfied
\par
when there is uncertainty (unless there is a full set of markets for insuring
\par
against all contingencies). To some economists this suggests that there is a prima
\par
facie case for government intervention. At the present time, although we know that
\par
the economy may not be efficient, we do not know the exact nature of the biases
\par
involved, and hence we do not know what intervention, if any, would be most
\par
effective. Some examples from recent political discussions may serve to illustrate:
\par
If we know precisely the stocks of natural resources and the dates (and associated
\par
costs) of arrival of substitute and new techniques, then, we suspect, much of the
\par
controversy about whether we were acting in a sufficiently conservationist manner
\par
would disappear. Much of this discussion, in effect, addresses the question as to
\par
whether the present generation, through the market place, is making sufficient provision
\par
for some of the worst possible contingencies which might arise (e.g., no new
\par
oil being discovered and the sbustitute techniques not being developed in time).
\par
This study provides a methodology with which that question can be addressed.
\par
In the shorter run, there is the question as to whether the market provides an adequate
\par
incentive for firms and individuals to have sufficient "flexibility of demand"
\par
so that the economy can respond to a developing shortage without undue costs, e.g.,
\par
should the government encourage the introduction of convertible furnaces?; and
\par
are there other ways by which the short run elasticity of demand for fuel may
\par
be increased? Are there pricing policies which would encourage flexibility?
\par
2.1-2One of the difficulties associated with the introduction of certain techniques
\par
(e.g., nuclear energy) is the assurance of a long run supply of the fuel at a
\par
"reasonable price"; future markets for uranium would obviously be one way in which
\par
the firm could be assured of the cost of uranium. Future markets of the length required
\par
do not at present. How do we explain this and what are its consequences? Will
\par
the recent apparent failure of a firm to honor its future contracts have an effect
\par
on the demand for nuclear energy plants? Should the government provide price guarantees
\par
for inputs?
\par
Uncertainty plays an important role in explaining our industrial structure. One of
\par
the features of the energy industry which has recently been the subject of some comment
\par
is diversification of oil firms into other forms of energy. Some commentators
\par
have viewed this development with alarm, as a threat to the competitive structure of
\par
the economy. Is there an argument that can be made in favor of this diversification
\par
in terms of the consequences it has for risk reduction?
\par
There are still more questions raised by uncertainty: the future supply of energy
\par
will be largely determined by the development of new sources of energy (new methods
\par
of producing energy). At the present time there is a vast expenditure on research
\par
and development. The various research projects are all risks in varying degrees.
\par
How ought these risks be evaluated? What effect do the risks have on the rate at
\par
which we discount future costs and benefits?
\par
So far, we have provided a number of examples where there is a possible role for
\par
the government in correcting a market failure arising from uncertainty. But the
\par
government itself is a major source of uncertainty: uncertainty concerning regulatory
\par
and tax policy may have a major effect on the supply of energy, particularly in
\par
the short run. To what extent is such uncertainty intrinsic in the governmental
\par
process of a democratic country? Are there methods by which its consequences could
\par
be alleviated or procedures which might be agreed upon which would reduce it?
\par
Thus, an understanding of risk and its consequences is important to an understanding
\par
of virtually all aspects of economic activity, but particularly to a sector, such
\par
as energy, where the investments are long lived and the uncertainties appear to be
\par
large and significant.
\par
2.1-3The object of this chapter is to present a systematic overview of the economic analysis
\par
of uncertainty, particularly as it pertains to the supply of energy. The
\par
analysis is divided into the following sections:
\par
Section II develops the basic concepts, e.g., the meaning of risk and of risk aversion.
\par
The questions to which we address ourselves include the following:
\par
{$\bullet$} How can we define risk?
\par
{$\bullet$} What does it mean to say that one project is riskier than another?
\par
{$\bullet$} How can we measure the cost of risk, and on what does it depend?
\par
{$\bullet$} How can we characterize an individual's willingness to bear risks?
\par
Section II also presents some basic theorems on the effects of risk on the actions
\par
which firms and individuals undertake.
\par
How does an increase in risk affect various allocation decisions e.g., the allocation
\par
of securities within a portfolio, the investment of a firm, and savings of
\par
an individual.
\par
We also consider briefly:
\par
{$\bullet$} How does risk affect the consequences of a change in the environment
\par
on the allocation decisions, e.g., is the demand curve still
\par
downward sloping?
\par
Section III focuses on a particular set of actions of firms and individuals: their
\par
attempts to share their risks with others. We address ourselves to the mechanisms
\par
by which risks are shared and the principles which determine who bears what risks.
\par
Section IV is concerned with how uncertainty affects the actions of firms---what they
\par
do to avoid risk and reduce its effects.
\par
Finally, Section V is concerned with a taxonomy of the sources of risk, particularly
\par
as they have a bearing on the supply of energy.
\par
2.1-4II. BASIC CONCEPTS
\par
This section is concerned with defining some basic concepts which we shall use repeatedly
\par
throughout this book. These concepts characterize attitudes towards risk
\par
and the meaning of risk itself.
\par
II.1 Risk Aversion
\par
We begin our discussion by defining risk aversion. An individual is said to be risk-
\par
averse if he will not undertake a fair gamble, i.e., a risk with zero expected return.
\par
An individual is said to be risk-neutral if he is just indifferent to accepting
\par
such a risk, and an individual is a risk\textasciicircum preferrer if he prefers accepting such
\par
a risk. More formally, we let the individual's income from other sources be Y and
\par
we assume for simplicity that Y is constant. Let x be a gamble, i.e., a risky
\par
prospect, with zero expected value, i.e., Ex = 0. Then if the utility function is
\par
denoted by U, we say that
\par
if EU(Y + x) {$<$} U(Y) the individual is risk-averse
\par
if EU(Y + x) = U(Y) the individual is risk-neutral
\par
if EU(Y + x) {$>$} U(Y) the individual is a risk-preferrer
\par
(a risk-lover)
\par
What do these statements imply about the utility function U? Turning to figures 1,
\par
2 and 3, it is apparent that
\par
if U is strictly concave, i.e., U" {$<$} 0, the individual
\par
is risk-averse (Figure 1)
\par
if U is linear, i.e., U" = 0, the individual is risk-
\par
neutral (Figure 2)
\par
if U is strictly convex, i.e., U" {$>$} 0, the individual
\par
is a risk-lover. (Figure 3)
\par
The figures illustrated the case where x takes on two values, -A and +A, each
\par
with probability 0.5. Expected utility is then given by the mid point of the line
\par
joining U(Y + A) and U(Y - A). The results are immediate.
