II.7 Other Measures of Risk
\par
A number of more specific measures of risk have been employed in the literature,
\par
e.g., entropy, variance, etc. Although some of these measures may prove useful for
\par
certain specific problems, they all have the important drawback of lacking generality,
\par
in the sense that there are individuals and/or probability distributions for
\par
which analyses using the specific measure would give the wrong predictions. How
\par
important this is is a moot question; if, in a given problem, the random variables can
\par
be approximately described by normal distributions, then variance is a natural and
\par
useful measure. (Similarly, if utility functions satisfy certain restrictions,
\par
e.g., are quadratic, then mean-variance analysis is appropriate. But the utility
\par
functions for which mean-variance analysis is applicable have a number of unattractive
\par
and special properties; many qualitative properties e.g., the responses
\par
to changes in income, are opposite to what one would expect.)
\par
