III.2 Mechanisms for Risk Spreading
\par
Our economy has provided a number of alternative mechanisms for risk spreading.
\par
Ill .2.1 Insurance. The most obvious one is insurance. There is a widespread confusion
\par
in the role played by insurance. It is sometimes suggested that the function of in
\par
surance companies is risk-pooling; as we shall show later, there is some risk reduc
\par
tion from risk-pooling; but a primary function is also risk-spreading; this is particularly
\par
clear in the case of Lloyds syndicates. They are willing to insure
\par
"unique" events, i.e., for contingencies which occur sufficiently rarely that no
\par
2.1-25pooling is possible. They spread the risk among the large number of members
\par
within any syndicate, so that the risk borne by any member of the syndicate is
\par
small. For most of the types of contingencies which face the energy sector, insurance
\par
is probably not the primary means of risk-spreading. We noticed earlier that
\par
the "cost of risk" (the risk premium) was related to the size of the risk faced.
\par
Thus, the risks which are particularly important to share are of the catastrophic
\par
kind, e.g., the melt-down of a nuclear power plant. Since that is an event which
\par
is probably uncorrelated (or has a low correlation) with other income if the risk
\par
were spread among enough individuals, the risk would have a negligible cost asso-
\par
\emph{\ding{110}k}
\par
elated with it. Thus, if the market fails to provide insurance for this risk at
\par
a "reasonable" premium, i.e., corresponding to reasonable actuarial judgments about
\par
the likelihood of that event, there are strong grounds for the government to provide
\par
this kind of insurance. In providing this kind of insurance, the government
\par
is in effect spreading the risk among the entire population.
\par
III.2.2 Stock Market as a Risk Spreading Mechanism. More important than insurance for
\par
spreading the kinds of risks faced by most firms in the energy sector is the stock
\par
market. The joint stock company provides a mechanism by which the entrepreneur
\par
is able to share his risks with a large number of shareholders; in principal, the
\par
entrepreneur need not bear any risk at all. He could provide the entrepreneurial
\par
services at a fixed fee, and the risky income could be spread among a large number
\par
of shareholders. If there are enough shareholders, the risk borne by any single
\par
individual is negligible. The cost of the risk, as we noted, is then just proportional
\par
to the covariance with the rest of his income. This is important, because it
\par
means that the only risks which are important for a firm with widely held shares are
\par
those which are correlated with the remainder of income. For instance, the risks
\par
associated with drilling are, to a large extent, uncorrelated with the business cycle.
\par
If the business cycle is perfectly predicted, it will be completely uncorrelated:
\par
the main correlation comes from the variation in the profitability of drilling
\par
This discussion deals with the direct pecuniary costs of a catastrophe. The
\par
cost of bodily harm is not a diversifiable risk, but individuals can reduce the
\par
risk of harm by choosing safer occupations, residences, travel modes, etc.
\par
2.1-26arising from the variability of the price of oil and the price of inputs. But if
\par
the cycle is anticipated, there should be no cyclical variations in the net rents;
\par
they should simply increase at the rate of interest: the presence of fixed costs
\par
associated with extraction and production alters this result slightly.
\par
There are, however, important limits on the use of shares as a risk-spreading mechanism.
\par
These are associated with problems of imperfect information. Becuase the
\par
entrepreneur is more informed about the prospects of the firm than the shareholders,
\par
the shareholders may take the extent to which the entrepreneur (original shareholder)
\par
diversifies out of the firm as a signal of his judgment concerning the prospects of
\par
the firm and the firm's riskiness. For this or possibly for other reasons it is not
\par
uncommon for the original shareholders of the firm to be incompletely diversified,
\par
i.e., to have a significant fraction of their wealth arising from the given firm.
\par
This means that the firm will act in a risk-averse manner, even though, were the
\par
risk completely spread among the economy, the firm ought to behave in a risk neutral
\par
manner. The implications of this are the subject of section III.
\par
The scenario presented in the previous section, of an entrepreneur starting a firm,
\par
and succeeding to diversify out of the risk by selling his shares, though appropriate
\par
for many firms (particularly in oil exploration) is not appropriate for many
\par
very large joint stock companies. There, no single shareholder may either own a
\par
significant fraction of the firm or have a significant fraction of his income generated
\par
by the firm. But the managers of the firm, who play the primary role in deciding
\par
what risks will be undertaken, generally have a significant fraction of their
\par
income from the firm. This is exacerbated by management incentive schemes which
\par
link their pay to the fortunes of the firm (e.g., through stock option provisions).
\par
If their promotion depends on their success, not on a judgment concerning whether
\par
they made the correct judgment, then they may act in a risk-averse manner (even when
\par
the interests of the firm are to be risk-neutral, i.e., shareholders would like the
\par
firm to maximize expected profits).
\par
Although there seems to be some presumption that most managerial incentive schemes
\par
have the rewards and punishments which are a non-linear function of the success or
\par
failure of the project, in such a way that the manager acts in a risk averse manner,
\par
this need not be the case. It is sometimes hypothesized that a typical pattern of
\par
(effective) payments in some industries is that, so long as some minimum level of
\par
return is attained, compensation is independent of performance. However, if the
\par
2.1-27individual becomes very successful, e.g., if he is successful enough to "attract
\par
attention" or if he develops a new idea and becomes the "expert", then he can appropriate
\par
his "presumed" ability rents. This may lead him to act in a risk-perferring
\par
(risk-loving) manner with respect to certain categories of risks.
\par
The design of managerial incentive schemes to induce the correct response to risk is
\par
a complicated question, which we unfortunately cannot pursue here.
\par
The problems of the regulatory agency which is less informed about the technological
\par
possibilities and market condition than is the firm, can be posed in exactly an
\par
analogous way.
\par
III.2.3 Future Markets and Other Contractual Arrangements . The third category of risk
\par
sharing (or risk-spreading) arrangements is directed at more specific risks than the
\par
previous set of mechanisms. There are a variety of contractual arrangements, particularly
\par
between buyers and sellers, in which the uncertainty associated with the
\par
transaction may be borne by one party or the other or shared between them.
\par
A major source of uncertainty facing many firms is the price of their inputs or their
\par
output. These risks can be hedged by transactions in future markets, e.g., by buying
\par
an input forward, one can be sure of the cost to you of the input. But even when
\par
there is no future market (the reason for the absence of the future market is an
\par
important question) the firm can enter into specific contracts with buyers or sellers.
\par
Still these price risks are probably among the more important sources of uncertainty,
\par
affecting both the choice of technique and the development of new energy sources.
\par
The suggestion that the government provide a floor for the price of oil can be seen
\par
as a suggestion for a partial substitute for a future market, a way of mitigating
\par
the effects of the risk associated with the price of energy. But, as we shall argue
\par
later, this is not the most effective way of reducing this particular risk faced
\par
by producers. Other instruments which deal more directly with the problem would be
\par
more effective.
\par
It is perhaps worth noting the contrast between the government provision of insurance
\par
for an explosion in, say, a nuclear energy plant and the government provision of
\par
price insurance. They both represent market failures---the market has failed to provide
\par
insurance against some important contingencies. But the former insurance ought
\par
to be provided at the actuarial odds (approximately), while in the latter, the government
\par
ought to be able to make an actuarial profit.
\par
2.1-28III. 2.4 Mergers . Mergers represent a particular type of contractual agreement which
\par
is at least partly motivated by concerns for risk-sharing. Consider two firms of
\par
identical size. Then a merger is equivalent (for purposes of risk spreading) to
\par
each firm buying 50\% of the shares of the other. If the owners of the two firms are
\par
identical, then the risk borne by each stockholder is essentially unaffected (because
\par
of limited liability, there is, in fact, some increase in risk associated with such
\par
a merger). On the other hand, if there are two distinctly different groups of
\par
owners, then it is equivalent to doubling the number of individuals among whom each
\par
risk is spread, which, according to proposition 1, significantly reduces the cost of
\par
the risk.
\par
Ill. 2.5 Profit Sharing. Profit sharing represents the spreading of risk among not only
\par
capitalists and entrepreneurs, but also among workers. In a sense, any contractual
\par
arrangement between the worker and the firm has strong implications for the risks
\par
borne by the firm. An agreement concerning tenure shifts some risks concerning the
\par
individual's incompetence to perform the job well from the individual to the firm.
\par
Guaranteed annual wages forces more of the risks associated with cyclical variations
\par
in demand to be borne by the firm. Conversely, incentive pay systems typically
\par
require more of the risk to be borne by the individual. If sales go down, so does
\par
the income of the worker.
\par
III. 2.6 Risk Spreading between the Government and Firms. Both tax and regulatory policies
\par
can be viewed as a type of risk-spreading activity between the government and
\par
firms. This is particularly clear for the corporation income tax with a loss offset
\par
provision; the government takes, say, 50\% of any pure profits (returns in excess of
\par
the safe rate of interest), but through loss offset provisions, and in effect subsidizes
\par
any losses by approximately the same amount. Thus the risk is spread
\par
between the owners of the corporation and the population as a whole.
\par
Regulatory policies which allow the firm to increase the price if its costs go up
\par
have very much the same effect. A firm "gambles" on, say, nuclear generators; if
\par
the costs of uranium turn out to be much higher than anticipated, the firm is allowed
\par
to raise its price. So long as the revenues which could be raised if a monopoly
\par
price were charged are sufficiently large, then the cost of the mistake is borne
\par
largely by consumers; the risk is "spread" between the firms (because the regulatory
\par
authorities usually react to an application for a price increase only with a
\par
time lag, so that the firm must bear some of the cost), and its consumers.
\par
2.1-29The exact nature of regulatory policy has important implications for the effective
\par
degree of risk-spreading.
\par
IV. RISK AVOIDANCE AND RISK REDUCING ACTIVITIES
\par
The previous section outlined the various methods by which risks could be spread
\par
among the population, and showed how risk-spreading activities reduced the effective
\par
cost to society of risk-bearing. Still, any firm must bear a large part of the
\par
risks associated with its activities, and this leads firms to engage in a number of
\par
activities designed to reduce the risk and mitigate its effects.
\par
There are two broad categories of actions which firms undertake to reduce the risks
\par
they face: they attempt to find out about the probability distributions (they
\par
acquire information) and they alter their behavior in such a way as to alter the
\par
probability distributions (say of profits).
\par
While the preceding section was concerned with what may be thought of as financial
\par
transactions, affecting who bears the risk, the actual probability distribution
\par
of outcomes was unaffected. We have divided the affects into three categories,
\par
discussed in each of the next three subsections: they attempt to acquire information,
\par
thus reducing the risk itself (although the investment information is itself
\par
a risky investment); they attempt to take actions which "compensate" for the risk;
\par
and they attempt to avoid the risk.
\par
IV.1 Information
\par
A direct method of attacking the uncertainty is to reduce it by obtaining more information
\par
about whatever it is that one is uncertain. Some examples: exploration
\par
reduces the uncertainty aobut the stock of natural resources; market surveys and
\par
demand forecasts reduce the uncertainty about demand; indicative planning is designed
\par
to reduce the uncertainty about the plans of other firms (both competitors,
\par
demanders, and suppliers); long run government planning is designed to reduce the
\par
uncertainty about the actions of the. government.
\par
Several features of information acquisition require comment: first, none of these
\par
actually eliminates uncertainty, e.g., the forecasts of future demands are never
\par
perfect predictors. This does not, of course, mean that they do not have economic
\par
value.
\par
2.1-30The value of information is related to one's ability to change one's actions; that
\par
is, we define the value of information as what one would be willing to pay for it,
\par
prior to obtaining it. The following is a general framework for analyzing the value
\par
information. There is a set of states (possible outcomes), s. We presently have a
\par
"prior" probability distribution, giving the probability associated with each state.
\par
There is some event ("signal") which is about to occur. If event t occurs, then
\par
the probability that the true state is s is given by p . Thus, if we observe
\par
st
\par
