\par
\textbraceleft p \textbraceright{} is the "information matrix". An uninformative information matrix is one
\par
st
\par
which leaves our priors unaffected, regardless of what signal (t) occurs. For
\par
instance, if there are two states and two signals, if p =0.5 for all s and t,
\par
then the signal is completely uninformative. If, on the other hand, p\textasciicircum{} = 1,
\par
\textasciicircum 22 \textasciitilde{} an{$<$}\textasciicircum{} P'j = thsn the signal is completely informative: as soon as we
\par
observe the signal, we know the state. It is meaningful to talk, then, about one
\par
information system being more informative than another (the exact definition is
\par
fairly technical, and is omitted here). The point, however, is that any information
\par
system, ft, allows us to vary our action as a function of the signal observed, and
\par
thus, our total expected utility will be a function of ft. The value of the information
\par
is simply the amount we would be willing to pay to obtain the information
\par
(relative to having no information, ft = 0). If we could not alter our actions,
\par
information would have no value, since we would take exactly the same actions independently
\par
of the information structure. A mathematically precise definition of
\par
the value of information is given in chapter 4 of this volume.
\par
More information is obviously worth more, but it may not be worth the extra cost
\par
associated with obtaining it. It has recently been shown (Radner and Stiglitz,
\par
[1975] that it never pays to purchase a little information, i.e., the net value
\par
of information always appears as in figure 9. A little information always
\par
costs more than it is worth. The only assumptions required for this are that the
\par
utility function be continuous and that the posterior probabilities be continuous
\par
functions of the amount of information. This result has fundamental implications
\par
for the direction of information acquisition activities: it suggests that it pays
\par
to concentrate information acquisition, at least to some extent.
\par
