\par
Figure 1-9. Net Value of Information
\par
2.1-32Information is unlike other goods in another respect: it is a public good, in the
\par
sense that providing information to an additional person does not take away from the
\par
information available to the remainder (although it may reduce the economic value
\par
of that information to them).
\par
Moreover, there are often strong spillovers, e.g., a piece of information in one
\par
area may affect the costs of obtaining a piece of information elsewhere.
\par
Finally, it is often difficult to appropriate the returns to information, even apart
\par
from spillover effects. That is, if I obtain a piece of information, I sometimes
\par
can sell it (for example, the information of stock market analysis is often sold)
\par
but it is difficult to prevent resale. Even if I can prevent resale, if I take any
\par
action based on the information, and my action can be observed by others, they can
\par
infer something about the information I have received from the action I take. Even
\par
when the actions cannot be directly observed, they can often infer the action---and
\par
hence the information---from its consequences. For instance, if I discover a piece
\par
of information, that some stock is underpriced, then that will induce me to bid up
\par
the price; uninformed individuals can infer, form the rise in the price, that some
\par
participants in the market have obtained some information indicating that the stock
\par
had been underpriced.
\par
All of these are reasons why the market for information is not likely to work well,
\par
and why the market will supply an insufficient amount of information. Industry
\par
cooperatives and government subsidies for information acquisition are likely to be
\par
necessary, if the optimal amount of information is to be obtained.
\par
On the other hand, it is important to remember two qualifications. (a) There is a
\par
cost associated with obtaining information, so that beyond a point, it is better to
\par
remain uninformed (or relatively uninformed) rather than purchase (more)
\par
information. Because in bureacracies (governmental and private) there are penalties
\par
for making what turns out, ex post, to be a wrong decision, decision-makers
\par
have a strong incentive to obtain as much information as possible prior to making
\par
the decision. There may, as a result, in fact be excessive expenditures on
\par
information.
\par
(b) It is social benefits and not private benefits of information acquisition which
\par
need to be taken into account. The two may differ markedly. The private return to
\par
information often has an element of rent acquisition in it, e.g., information that
\par
2.1-33one security is better than another, or one person is more productive than another,
\par
increases the price of one security relative to another or the wage of one individual
\par
relative to another, but this is primarily a redistributive effect: the gains
\par
of one are mostly at the expense of the other. (There is some social gain, to the
\par
extent that the information leads resources to be allocated more efficiently.)
\par
Moreover, individuals, in calculating their private returns, take the distribution
\par
of prices as given; yet the information acquisition often leads to a change in the
\par
price distribution. This change in price distribution may have important welfare
\par
implication, which need to be taken into account.
\par
IV.2 Risk Reducing Activities
\par
The previous section described how, by finding out more about the probability distribution
\par
of returns, the risk faced by the entrepreneur may be reduced. In this
\par
subsection, we consider a number of ways by which firms attempt to "compensate" for
\par
risks, in effect to take actions which provide "insurance'' against a risk when insurance
\par
is not purchasable on the market.
\par
IV.2.1 Diversification
\par
If we have a set of assets (projects) which are not perfectly correlated, it always
\par
pays for us to diversify our resources among these assets. For instance, assume we
\par
2
\par
had n identically distributed assets; the own variance of each is and the
\par
covariance between any two is denoted simply by cov (and is assumed to be the same
\par
for any two assets). Then the total variance of return per dollar is
\par
cov
\par
clearly, so long as cov {$<$} an increase in n, the number of assets among which
\par
we diversify our investment, reduces the variance, with the asymptotic variance
\par
being simply cov.
\par
This result can be strengthened. Using the Rothschild-Stiglitz concept of mean preserving
\par
spreads, it is easy to show that a more diversified portfolio is less risky
\par
and will be preferred by all risk-averters.
\par
2.1-34On the other hand, the validity of the result hinges critically on there not being
\par
any returns to scale in investment in any one asset.
\par
IV.2.2 Investing in Negatively Correlated Investments
\par
We noted earlier that insurance can be thought of as a gamble, which pays off when
\par
the marginal utility of income is high. As a result, one is willing to buy insurance,
\par
even though the expected receipts are less than the premiums. Similarly, a
\par
firm may invest in a project which is negatively correlated with the returns of its
\par
other projects, even if the expected return from the project is less than that which
\par
it obtains on its other projects. For an oil company, investing in R and D for
\par
petro-chemical uses for oil may act as insurance, i.e., even if a substitute for oil
\par
as a producer of energy is discovered, this will maintain its value; it may be willing
\par
to invest in such an R and D project, even though the expected return is
\par
negative.
\par
An R and D program developing a substitute for oil might be acceptable, even if
\par
it had a negative expected return, because of its insurance value against the prospect
\par
of running out of oil.
\par
IV.2.3 Buffer Stocks (Inventories)
\par
These are a method by which firms avoid uncertainties about the price of an input
\par
required at some future date. It obviously would be more efficient to purchase the
\par
good for future delivery, at the time required, but, in the absence of such a future
\par
contract, physical storage itself provides the required insurance, obviously at a
\par
cost.
\par
Maintaining an inventory of backorders is a method by which firms insure that they
\par
can sell the output which they are producing, and enables them to smooth production.
\par
IV.2.4 Flexibility
\par
In facing a risky situation, one knows that one is going to face new information (or
\par
changing situations) as time goes on; if one had that information now, it would
\par
affect ones actions today. Knowing that one is going to obtain that information in
\par
the future leads one to take actions today which make it less costly to revise, to
\par
alter ones actions as the new information comes in, i.e., actions which enable one
\par
to respond with flexibility to the new situation. There are several aspects of
\par
flexibility to which attention should be drawn. The first we refer to as the
\par
2.1-35principle of reduced fixed committments. This says that since, by definition fixed
\par
committments (e.g., capital goods) can be altered less easily, they should be
\par
avoided. The second, we refer to as the principle of procrastination: by postponing
\par
decisions, one has greater flexibility; at the margin, it says, it is worth paying
\par
something to be able to postpone a decision. This is sometimes referred to as
\par
an option value.
\par
The general principle of flexibility can be illustrated by a firm with two alternative
\par
methods of producing. One involves very little fixed cost. The cost curve
\par
for it we assume to be a horizontal straight line. The other is the more conventional
\par
U shaped cost curve, entailing some fixed cost. Note that if there were no
\par
uncertainty, we would clearly prefer the U-shaped cost curve, since the minimum
\par
average cost is clearly lower. But if the variance of output is very high, then
\par
some of the time the average cost will be higher with the U-shaped cost curve, and
\par
if the variance is high enough, expected costs will be higher. The U-shaped cost
\par
curve is said to be less flexible than the other cost curve. With sufficient uncertainty,
\par
we always prefer the more flexible technique.
\par
It is important to emphasize that these are simply general principles, which provide
\par
an "intuitive" feel for the kinds of patterns of risk avoidance activities engaged
\par
in by firms. For instance, in certain circumstances, it may not be possible to
\par
speak of one decision as leading to greater (or less) flexibility than another, and
\par
reducing fixed committments may have ambiguous effects. Consider the case where the
\par
production function has a very low elasticity of substitution between capital and
\par
labor. For low outputs, the lower committment of fixed costs has a lower average
\par
cost, for higher outputs, it has a higher cost. If we require the firm to meet
\par
whatever output is demanded of it, clearly there is a very high penalty for under
\par
investment; thus the increased risk actually may lead to increased investment.
\par
The use of convertible boilers is an example of flexibility as a response to risk.
\par
They may be more expensive to operate (either as coal or as oil using boilers) than
\par
one designed specifically for that purpose. Again, this additional cost is just
\par
like an insurance premium: the beiefits accrue only if the contingency (high oil
\par
prices), which would drastically lower profits for a non-convertible boiler, occurs.
\par
2.1-36IV.3 Risk Avoidance
\par
The final category of responses to risk we have referred to as Risk Avoidance Activities.
\par
To the extent that one cannot insure against risks, either by undertaking
\par
financial or other actions, risk avoidance leads one to except a lower mean return
\par
with a lower risk. Whenever there is a safe (or safer) alternative to a risky activity,
\par
the more risk averse the individual or firm and the greater the risk associated
\par
with risky activities (both of these are concepts which we have defined in
\par
section II) the more resources will be diverted to the safe activity.
\par
As we noted in section II, it is not always possible to identify what is the risky
\par
activity. This becomes particularly important if there is more than one commodity
\par
(as obviously there is). For then there is a risk introduced by relative price
\par
changes. An asset which has a safe "real" return, using oil as the numeraire, will
\par
have a risky return using "other consumption" as the numeraire, and conversely. In
\par
that situation, the individual can match assets to consumption patterns, i.e., he
\par
can hold oil stocks equal in value to his (expected) consumption of oil. This we
\par
refer to as the principle of hedging. An individual with L - shaped indifference
\par
curves can be perfectly hedged, regardless of the relative price which emerges, by
\par
purchasing futures in the critical proportions of his indifference curve. An individual
\par
without L - shaped indifference curve will still trade next period, sometimes
\par
being a net supplier, sometimes a net demander. Stiglitz [1970] has defined the
\par
pure hedging position as the allocation which would be pursued by an individual who
\par
maximized his worse off state (the maximum strategy). In general, of course, unless
\par
individuals are infinitely risk-averse, they will engage in some amounts of speculative
\par
activity.
