\par
THE THEORY OF THE FIRM UNDER UNCERTAINTY
\par
I. INTRODUCTION
\par
The traditional theory of the firm assumes that the entrepreneur chooses between
\par
perfectly known and riskless alternatives to maximize profits. Powerful results
\par
can then be derived about the relationship between costs, prices, and market structure
\par
and about the incidence of sales and profits taxes. These results can then be
\par
used to draw inferences about the nature of competition and about the objectives of
\par
the management. Thus the existence of supernormal profits signals the presence of
\par
imperfect competition, as does the presence of "excees capacity", that misleading
\par
label for a firm operating under conditions of falling long run average costs. A
\par
pure profits tax will have no effect on price or output if firms maximize profit,
\par
but it may raise price if firms maximize sales revenue subject to a minimum level of
\par
post tax profits.
\par
Firms subject to rate-of-return regulation will choose excessively capital-intensive
\par
techniques, whilst competitive firms will all choose the same least cost production
\par
technique at any given moment. These propositions lie at the center of traditional
\par
micro-economics, but every one of them is suspect if the firm operates in an uncertain
\par
environment. There is a growing scepticism about the results of deterministic
\par
economics, and economists are increasingly testing the robustness of the propositions
\par
of static micro-economic theory, to the point where it now seems useful to
\par
survey the progress made, the techniques employed, and the conclusions reached. As
\par
usual, the results often depend crucially on the assumptions made, and it is important
\par
to be able to place these assumptions within some organising framework or
\par
taxonomy. This is done in the next section, which also defines the range of models
\par
to be surveyed. At the end of the detailed survey there is a brief discussion of
\par
other possible models.
\par
2.2-1II. TAXONOMY
\par
The studies to be surveyed typically analyse a single model (or at most, two or
\par
three models) which can be classified by the following characteristics:
\par
(i) Nature of commodity produced---perishable or durable.
\par
(ii) Market decision variable of firm---price or quantity.
\par
These two characteristics are related, though not in a simple way. Durable goods
\par
can be stored, and hence if the price is fixed and cannot be varied over the market
\par
period, discrepancies between supply and demand can be absorbed by inventory changes.
\par
Two separate but complementary approaches to the study of storable commodities can
\par
be distinguished in the literature. Powerful results can be obtained cheaply if
\par
intertemporal commodity arbitrage is perfect and costless, for then prices are perfectly
\par
stabilized. The consequences of perfect price stabilization have been explored
\par
in a variety of papers (Hassell, 1969; Newbery, 1976, Turnovsky, 1976; and
\par
Newbery, 1977) and are discussed in Section 14. This approach investigates the
\par
impact stabilization on market equilibrium and the consequent effects on firms,
\par
while the complementary approach is concerned to find optimal (or satisfactory)
\par
decision rules for the inventory policy of firms in a given unchanging market environment.
\par
Inventory policy as studied in the operations research literature is thus
\par
only a partial equilibrium analysis, but, by ignoring market interactions it is able
\par
to relax the assumption of perfect and costless arbitrage. This approach is a sufficiently
\par
well developed and technically complex branch of operations research to
\par
merit separate treatment, and as it is well summarized in the operations research
\par
literature (e.g.. Arrow, Karlin and Scarf, 1958, 1962) it will not be further surveyed
\par
here.
\par
For perishable goods there are three possibilities. If price can change more quickly
\par
than supply then it can adjust to equate supply and demand, and will be stochastic.
\par
If price cannot change in the short run (presumably because of information
\par
costs) then either supply will adjust (as with public transport). Most authors have
\par
concentrated on the first possibility, but Leland [1972] and Brown \& Johnson [1969]
\par
discuss the case where the price is chosen in advance and quantities adjust.
\par
Naturally the appropriate decision variable also depends on
\par
(iii) market structure---competitive, monopolistic, regulated or social welfare
\par
maximizing public utility.
\par
Competition between obligoplies introduces an additional source of uncertainty in
\par
the shape of strategic or game-theoretic choices. Since these are quite different
\par
2.2-2they are not surveyed here. Market structure affects the way in which firms react
\par
to uncertainty, but the converse may also be true. In the absence of complete insurance
\par
large firms, or vertically integrated concerns may have a comparative advantage
\par
in risk bearing over small, single process firms. If so, then riskier industries
\par
may be more concentrated. Alternatively, risk favors flexible, responsive
\par
firms, and smaller firms may be more adaptable than larger firms. Schwartzman [1963]
\par
has tested the second hypothesis, Oi and Hurter [1965] the former.
\par
(iv) Flexibility of production---describes the extent to which output decisions can
\par
be modified once the state of the world has been discovered. Most authors assume
\par
that production decisions are taken before any information about the state of the
\par
world is available, but Rothschild and Stiglitz [1971], Turnovsky [1973] and Hartman
\par
[1976] also study the case in which the fixed factor ("capacity") is chosen in advance,
\par
but the variable factor can be adjusted in the light of full information.
\par
Turnovsky [1976] has also discussed the case in which all factors can be adjusted
\par
once the market price is known.
\par
Sheshinski and Dreze [1976], and Tisdell [1968] discuss the more complex problem of
\par
choosing between technologies of differing flexibility. The idea here is that an
\par
inflexible plant may have lower minimum costs than a flexible plant, but a higher
\par
ratio of fixed to variable costs. Figure 1 illustrates this sense of flexibility
\par
for an inflexible firm I and a flexible firm F,
\par
t
\par
-------------------------------------------- >>
\par
