\par
Figure 2-1. Choice of Flexibility
\par
2.2-3The problem of choosing the optimum mixture of types of plant to provide optimum
\par
flexibility in the face of uncertain demand has much in common with the problem of
\par
choosing the best set of base and peak capacity to minimize the costs of meeting
\par
a periodically fluctuating level of demand ("the problem of the peak").
\par
(v) Source of risk---variability in demand, in input costs, or in the production
\par
process.
\par
Most industrial models are limited to uncertain demand, while agricultural models
\par
emphasize environmental uncertainties such as weather which affect the output
\par
achievable with given inputs. There are similarities between the last two sources
\par
of risk---between the uncertain costs of producing a given output and the known costs
\par
of producing an uncertain output. Analytical results depend sensitively on the distinction
\par
between demand and supply risk.
\par
(vi) Characteristics of risk---additive, multiplicative, or more complex; systematic
\par
or non-systematic.
\par
Most authors treat systematic risk for the good reason that it is then reasonable to
\par
assume that agents have learned the underlying probability distributions, and act
\par
rationally. Additive risk is easier to analyze but its separability leads to strong
\par
and therefore rather special results. Turnovsky [1976] argues in favor of multiplicative
\par
risks as a more reasonable restriction, and both he and Newbery [1976a]
\par
contrast the two. The second category overlaps with
\par
(vii) costs of information about risk.
\par
If the consequences of uncertainty are costlessly available ex post, and if the uncertainty
\par
is systematic and agents rational, then given time their decisions will
\par
converge to a rational expectations choice. If the consequences are costly to observe
\par
then producers will stop short of collecting full information and they may
\par
systematically make mistaken choices. Rothschild [1973, 1971] has discussed this
\par
problem ("the two-armed bandit") but it seems better to omit these aspects as belonging
\par
to the theory of information, which, though closely related to the theory of
\par
uncertainty, constitutes a sufficiently well articulated subject to be dealt with
\par
separately.
\par
(viii) Producers' attitudes to risk---risk-neutral or risk-averse.
\par
Large firms, quoted companies, or firms with access to a perfect supply of credit
\par
might be expected to be risk-neutral in their attitude to risk, though these arguments
\par
need closer scrutiny than most authors have supplied. Owner-managers or
\par
2.2-4farmers might well be risk-averse. As Knight [1922] has argued that profit is the
\par
reward for bearing risk producer's attitudes are clearly of interest.
\par
(ix) Equilibrium of the firm or the industry
\par
Most authors confine themselves to analyzing the equilibrium of the single firm as
\par
external circumstances such as risk, or taxes, change. Such treatments are severely
\par
partial, and need extending to the more complete analysis of industry equilibrium
\par
where market demand interacts with aggregate supply to set price and the number of
\par
firms adjusts in response to changing profits. Sheshinski and Dreze [1976] provide
\par
a good example of this approach. A more ambitious undertaking would be to model the
\par
interaction of several industries facing different kinds of risk. Stiglitz [1972]
\par
has shown that the whole concept of riskiness requires careful analysis in a multiproduct
\par
economy, while Newbery [1976b] has shown that the existence of substitutes
\par
on the supply and demand side can reverse the welfare implications of various risk
\par
reducing measures. Rothenberg and Smith [1971] have also demonstrated the importance
\par
of these wider general equilibrium effects.
\par
III. QUESTIONS ASKED
\par
The first question to ask is the effect of uncertainty on the more obvious observable
\par
variables, such as the average level of production of a firm, or of the whole
\par
industry, the average price, average profits, and the choice of mode of production.
\par
Next, some authors are interested in the effect of risk on unobservable variables
\par
such as producers' and consumers' welfare. A more subtle way of formulating the
\par
\emph{same} question is to ask whether the testable propositions of the static theory of
\par
the firm hold in the presence of uncertainty, and, if not, how they should be modified.
\par
Dreze and Sheshinski [1976] stress this approach, and emphasize the wider
\par
analytical implications for micro-economic theory. The next stage is to explore the
\par
policy implications of the reformulated theory of the firm, to investigate the
\par
incidence of taxation and the effects of regulation in the presence of uncertainty.
\par
Finally, one can ask whether the institutional structure of the industry Is likely
\par
to change in response to an increase in uncertainty, and to what extent specific
\par
institutions and types of contract can be explained as a response to uncertainty.
\par
The stock market, long term contracts, vertical integration and merger activity are
\par
obvious examples. The efficiency of these responses can also be investigated, following
\par
the lead of Diamond [1967] who demonstrates the inefficiency of the stock
\par
market except under restrictive assumptions. This last set of questions should be
\par
borne in mind, though they lie beyond the simple analysis of the effect of risk on
\par
the firm, and will not be surveyed here.
\par
