2.2-25X. WELFARE MAXIMIZING PRICE SETTING FIRMS UNDER DEMAND UNCERTAINTY
\par
If, as in Britain, public utilities are required to choose prices and investment
\par
plant to maximize welfare, the distorting effects of a crude rate of return regulation
\par
can be avoided provided that the managers of the utility have sufficient incentive
\par
to pursue the stated objective. Most writers use the expected sum of consumers'
\par
and producers' Marshallian surplus as their measure of welfare, thereby implicitly
\par
assuming away distributive problems, changes in the marginal utility, (which also
\par
reflect attitudes to risk) and general equilibrium effects. The first two assumptions
\par
can be defended if distributive justice is being pursued adequately by other
\par
means, and if consumers do not spend a large fraction of their income on the commodity
\par
supplied, but the last assumption, that general equilibrium effects can be
\par
ignored, is stronger than the authors realize. Why is demand uncertain, and will
\par
the answer affect the appropriate measure of welfare? Obviously yes, for if random
\par
changes in the price of a close substitute cause the demand uncertainty, consumer
\par
welfare will rise with a fall in the substitute price, though the area under the
\par
shifted demand curve facing our public utility will have fallen, leading to a fall
\par
in measured welfare. A full analysis of the welfare effects of risk is still
\par
