XIII. INDUSTRY EQUILIBRIUM UNDER SUPPLY RISK
\par
If input prices are risky, inputs are chosen in advance, and firms are risk-neutral,
\par
then Proposition 6 implies that homogenous firms expand output in response to risk.
\par
Similar arguments, namely that EB(w) {$<$} B(Ew), imply that average profit rises with
\par
unchanged mean price, tending to attract in new firms. Thus, for both reasons,
\par
average output price must fall. The effect on individual firm size is indeteminate
\par
in the full equilibrium context, as a fall in output price will tend to reduce firm
\par
size. All we can say is:
\par
2.2-32Proposition 17. If input prices are risky but of unchanged mean, and the industry
\par
is composed of homogenous risk-neutral firms, then output price falls and total supply
\par
expands relative to the riskless case. The opposite will occur if input prices
\par
are perfectly correlated and firms are risk-averse.
\par
Proof. Direct from proposition 6 and 7.
\par
Proposition 18. If inputs can be adjusted ex post and demand is of constant elasticity,
\par
industries of homogenous risk-neutral firms will expand and prices fall.
\par
Proof. Immediate from proposition 8.
\par
Undoubtedly similar results can be derived from the remaining proposition about
\par
individual firm equilibrium.
\par
XIV. THE EFFECTS OF INVENTORIES AND BUFFER STOCKS
\par
If inventories were costless to hold then speculation by risk-neutral agents would
\par
perfectly stabilize the price (and if inventories were costless, risk neutrality
\par
would be a reasonable assumption for income could then be perfectly stabilized at
\par
each moment in time). Naturally these are unreasonable assumptions, but they should
\par
nonetheless give a qualitative feel for cases in which prices are only partially
\par
stabilized. The first result is obvious but shows the importance of distinguishing
\par
between supply and demand risk.
\par
Proposition 19. Costless buffer stocks eliminate supply fluctuations if risk is
\par
confined to demand, but exaggerate supply fluctuations if input prices are risky.
\par
Proof. If price is stabilized and supply certain, then inputs are chosen to maximize
\par
certain profits, and need never be adjusted. Hence supply remains constant.
\par
If input prices are risky variable inputs will be adjusted so that MC = p. With
\par
no buffer a fall in input prices increases input, but the rise in industry output
\par
leads to a fall in output price, which damps the input response. If output price
\par
is stabilized this damping is absent, and the flatter the marginal cost curve, the
\par
larger will be the supply fluctuations.
\par
2.2-33In general stabilizing prices will affect average price and average supply, as
\par
should be apparent from section XIII. There is, however, another crucial difference
\par
between demand and supply risk, for while stabilizing in the former case eliminates
\par
producer risk, in the latter case it magnifies it. Since the effects of price
\par
stabilization have been dealt with elsewhere (Newbery 1976, 1977, and Turnovsky,
\par
1976) it only remains to remark that many of the results are sensitive to the nature
\par
of risk (additive or multiplicative) and the shape of the demand curve (convex,
\par
linear, or concave).
\par
XV. OTHER GENERAL EQUILIBRIUM CONSIDERATIONS
\par
The most obvious general equilibrium effect to allow for is the interaction between
\par
each firm's production decision and the industry price level. This was examined in
\par
sections XIII and XIV. The same kind of analysis could be profitably extended to
\par
allow for upward sloping industry supply schedules for inputs, allowing one to trace
\par
through the effects on input and output prices as quantities are adjusted to the
\par
presence of risk. Where the emphasis is on questions of comparative statics rather
\par
than questions of efficiency this intermediate step between partial and general
\par
equilibrium analysis is quite satisfactory---the shape and position of the supply
\par
and demand curves facing the industry are taken as given, whereas properly they can
\par
only be located after tracing through all the general equilibrium effects.
\par
By and large this procedure is probably satisfactory for comparative static results
\par
of the kind surveyed here, but it should be stressed that normative questions can
\par
rarely be treated so simply. If the aim is to discover the distributive impact of a
\par
change in risk on different groups of agents then the inquiry must be extended to
\par
other industries, for changes in relative prices will affect consumption, production,
\par
and profits elsewhere. While these are typically not the concern of comparative
\par
statics they necessarily affect welfare, and cannot be ignored. The danger of
\par
adopting an uncritical partial equilibrium Marshallian analysis of consumer welfare
\par
has already been exposed in Section X. We can tentatively summarize this insight
\par
in:
\par
Principle Industry equilibrium analysis is generally adequate for qualitative
\par
comparative statics, but is generally inadequate for welfare analysis.
\par
2.2-34The reservations or qualifications to this Principle ought to be stated immediately.
\par
In the riskless case a change in e.g., tax rates, or factor endowments will generally
\par
shift the demand and supply curves, an effect which elementary treatments
\par
attempt to capture by the distinction between short and long run curves, or ceteris
\par
paribus as opposed to mutatis mutandis. A similar distinction can sometimes be
\par
made in the industry analysis of risk, though the reason is slightly different.
\par
Any change which alters the riskiness of one industry will in general affect the
\par
risk facing other industries. Indeed, Stiglitz [1972] has shown that which industry
\par
is riskier may be only indirectly related to the original source of the risk.
\par
He shows that with two industries, one of which has a stochastic production function,
\par
the other industry may easily face more unstable profits, as relative prices
\par
respond to supply shifts of the risky industry. The change in risk elsewhere will
\par
lead to changes in the demand and supply curves facing the industry. These can be
\par
handled analytically by the device employed in Newbery [1977] where the impact of
\par
institutions which affect risk (insurance schemes, buffer stocks, etc.) is broken
\par
down into separate components, first, not allowing average outputs to change (price
\par
effects), then allowing an adjustment (quantity effects).
\par
It is probably worth noting that most of the "counter-examples" to the use of partial
\par
equilibrium analysis (Stiglitz, 1972, Rothenberg and Smith, 1971, Newbery
\par
1976b) employ a general equilibrium model with just two industries. In many ways
\par
this may be more misleading than examining a single industry facing elastic supply
\par
schedules for it means that all the burden of responding to the changes in one industry
\par
are concentrated on the only remaining industry. In general one would expect
\par
the impact to be diffused over a whole spectrum of decreasing close complements and
\par
substitutes.
\par
Rothenberg and Smith's model is a good example of the distinction between positive
\par
and normative analysis. They demonstrate that in a simple two-good, two-factor
\par
economy, the distributive impact of risk on welfare, depends on, among other things,
\par
the source of the risk (they consider randomness in labor supply and in the production
\par
function of one industry), the elasticity of substitution between the fixed and
\par
variable factor, the labor elasticities of output, the allocation of labor, and, as
\par
is common in risk analysis, the signs of various third derivatives. Their positive
\par
results about the effect of uncertainty on wage rates could probably be established
\par
qualitatively from industry equilibrium analysis given less than infinitely elastic
\par
derived demand curves for labor.
\par
2.2-35Newbery [1976b] shows that the welfare effects of price stabiliztion schemes are
\par
exactly reversed if instead of allowing the prices of other produced goods to be
\par
set on world markets and thus held constant, they are determined endogenously by
\par
consumers' Cobb-Douglas preferences. Obviously with such preferences positive results
\par
are not affected much as expenditure on each industry remains constant as
\par
prices vary, so the location of the unit elastic demand curve is unchanged by the
\par
full equilibrium response. The tentative conclusions summarized in the principle
\par
above seem justified in the treatment of risk to much the same extent that they are
\par
justified in traditional static micro-economics.
\par
XVI. OTHER MODELS
\par
This survey has deliberately concentrated on the simplest descriptive theory of the
\par
firm under various kinds of uncertainty, treating the firm as a rational, well-
\par
informed single decision-maker. This allows a unified treatment, which in turn
\par
makes it possible to contrast the implications of alternative assumptions, and
\par
thereby to test their sensitivity. Such a survey necessarily excludes approaches
\par
and models which do not fit comfortably within this unified framework, and some of
\par
the obvious omissions are mentioned here for completeness.
\par
The assumption that the firm maximizes a well-defined function of profits is a strong
\par
one, which is open to a number of rather different criticism and alternatives.
\par
First, it presupposes rational decision-making under uncertainty. Far more information
\par
is needed to compute the optimum than under certainty, for typically both
\par
the probability distribution and the shape of the relevant functions (demand or
\par
cost) are required, rather than just the price (plus, for the monopolist, the elasticity
\par
of demand). This information may not be available, and it may be difficult
\par
to solve the maximization problem, particularly for investment and inventory decisions.
\par
Mills [1962, Ch. 2] argues convincingly that this, is likely to lead the
\par
firm to adopt simple decision rules which require less than complete information,
\par
and which, though not optimal, are satisfactory in some reasonably well defined
\par
sense. There is a whole literature on this concept of "satisficing", or "bounded
\par
rationality" which is complementary to the optimizing approach adopted here. Clearly,
\par
what is needed are empirical tests to identify the decision rules adopted, of
\par
the kind Mills undertakes, and theoretical estimates of the losses of following the
\par
rules rather than optimizing. Alternatively, the situations in which the rules are
\par
optimal can be identified, as in Arrow, Karlin and Scarf [1958, 1962].
\par
2.2-36There is a related criticism of rationality derived from psychological experiments
\par
which find disturbing discrepancies between the expected utility maximizing prediction
\par
and experimental choices. Such evidence strengthens the case for examining
\par
simple decision rules, and suggests that large firms with sophisticated Operations
\par
Research departments may behave differently from small firms.
\par
Next, there is the criticism that individual risk aversion provides an incentive
\par
for risk spreading through the stock market, in which case ownership is no longer
\par
coincident with decision making. What should or does the manager maximize when the
\par
firm is owned by disparate agents? Dreze [1974] demonstrates that this is a difficult
\par
problem, and there is a whole literature on the efficiency and optimality of
\par
economics with a Stock Market which addresses this question. (See Grossmann and
\par
Hart, 1976, and references therein).
\par
The existence of institutions like the Stock Market reminds us that the market environment
\par
considered in this survey was highly simplified, and restricted to spot
\par
transactions. In the presence of uncertainty one might expect a whole range of
\par
other kinds of contracts to emerge and affect industry equilibrium. Insurance,
\par
equity finance, long term contracts and vertical integration should ideally be
\par
modelled, though the result would almost certainly be analytically intractable.
\par
Instead it seems more productive to examine each institution separately to gain
\par
some feel for their likely importance. But their omission does mean that the models
\par
studied so far are partial and incomplete. They are incomplete in another way,
\par
already mentioned, in that they largely ignore inventory and investment policy. The
\par
reasons for this have already been discussed in section II, and it is only necessary
\par
to remind the reader that the firms studied here exist in a static, if stochastic,
\par
world and make no real inter-temporal decisions.
