2.2-39Chapter 3
\par
GENERAL EQUILIBRIUM AND UNCERTAINTY
\par
I. INTRODUCTION
\par
This chapter is concerned with the analysis of the effects of uncertainty within a
\par
general equilibrium framework. We are concerned with two questions: does the
\par
general equilibrium analysis of uncertainty alter the kinds of partial equilibrium
\par
results derived in earlier chapters? And does the introduction of uncertainty into
\par
a general equilibrium model alter the conclusions of conventional general equilibrium
\par
analysis?
\par
The answer to both of these questions is yes. In particular, the basic results on
\par
the optimality of the competitive economy have to be modified when account is taken
\par
of uncertainty; and general equilibrium effects may not only alter quantitatively
\par
the partial equilibrium effects of uncertainty, but may even reverse the sign.
\par
II. THE ANALYSIS OF UNCERTAINTY WITHIN A GENERAL EQUILIBRIUM MODEL
\par
There is, at present, no generally accepted general equilibrium model of market
\par
economies with uncertainty. Three models have been developed but each of these has
\par
certain deficiencies:
\par
(a) The first well-articulated model was that of Arrow and Debreu; first they noted
\par
that a good at one date is really different from that at another; this led them to
\par
formulate the notion of "dated commodities" and they postulated that there was a
\par
complete set of markets for all commodities, i.e., future markets for all commodities
\par
extending infinitely far into the future. With this modification, all of
\par
static analysis can be extended to dynamic economies. They then noted that the same
\par
"trick" could be used to handle uncertainty: a commodity in one state (say rain)
\par
was different from what would appear to be the same commodity in another state (not
\par
rain). (It is not really, of course, that the commodity is different, but one can
\par
think of purchasing the right to consume the commodity in one state of nature and
\par
not in another.) They postulated that there was a complete set of markets for these
\par
2.3-1"contingent" commodities; this was equivalent to assuming that one could insure
\par
all risks at all dates into the future.
\par
Under these assumptions the conventional static, competitive analysis can be extended
\par
in a straightforward manner to uncertainty and the basic theorems, showing the
\par
existence and pareto optimality of competitive equilibrium can be established.
\par
The full set of markets which are required for the theorem obviously do not exist
\par
and there are strong a priori reasons why one would not expect them to. Some of
\par
these, such as transaction costs and the moral hazards associated with certain
\par
risks, have received extensive discussion in the literature. Others, arising from
\par
informational problems have only recently received much discussion. For our purposes,
\par
however, we need only observe that the assumption of the model, the existence
\par
of a full set of markets, is obviously not satisfied; a number of important empirical
\par
implications of the theory are incorrect, and the model fails to explain a number
\par
of important phenomena (see Stiglitz 1971). In particular, if the theory were
\par
correct, a manager would only need to calculate the "Arrow-Debreu prices" (the
\par
prices corresponding to output of each commodity in each state of nature at each
\par
date) and evaluate each project using those prices to determine what investment
\par
decisions should be made. There would never be any disagreements, take-over bids
\par
would never occur, etc. Were the model valid it would provide an easy way of calculating
\par
the "price of risk", i.e., of evaluating different risky projects. Indeed,
\par
were the model valid there would be little justification for a separate study of
\par
the effects of uncertainty and information on the supply of energy.
\par
(b) The next model was the more restrictive risk class model of Modigliani and
\par
Miller [1958], In their analysis of the effects of corporate capital structure on
\par
the value of a firm, they assumed that the economy could be divided into firms
\par
belonging to different risk classes. The profits of the firm within a risk class
\par
were all perfectly correlated. Then the only decision the firm had to make was
\par
the scale of output. This model has one extremely attractive feature: since there
\par
are a large number of firms producing a particular pattern of returns across the
\par
states of nature, there is a "market" price associated with a particular pattern
\par
of returns. Taking the "pattern of returns" as a composite commodity, it is immediate
\par
that we have again obtained a conventional competitive economy and, as a
\par
result, the equilibrium will be Pareto Optimal (Diamond 1967).
\par
2.3-2There are three major objections to the model. First, it assumes that the pattern
\par
of returns are exogenous; even if the firm had no choice of technique (as the model
\par
assumes), the pattern of returns would depend not only on the distribution of output
\par
but on the distribution of prices, and the latter will depend on the level of investment
\par
in the sector as a whole. Thus, as the sector's scale changes the risk
\par
class to which the sector changes; the competitive equilibrium will not in general
\par
\emph{j.}
\par
be Pareto Optimal . Second, the model does not allow us to ask questions concerning
\par
the choice of technique of firms except when every one of the possible patterns
\par
of return are already being produced by a large number of other firms. This obviously
\par
makes the model useless for asking many of the most important questions
\par
(other than simply scale of operation). Note that when there is a pattern of
\par
returns being produced by other firms there is again an identifiable "price of
\par
risk" which can be used to evaluate a project.
\par
Thirdly, the hypothesis that for every firm there is a large number of firms which
\par
are essentially perfectly correlated with it,is dubious. There may be a large number
\par
of firms with a similar distribution (e.g., identically distributed firms) but
\par
the assumption of perfect correlation is not persuasive.
\par
(c) The Monopolistically Competitive Capital Market Model (Stiglitz, 1970, 1972).
\par
This model observes that though there are many firms with very similar patterns of
\par
returns, they are all slightly different so that all firms face a slightly downward
\par
sloping demand curve for their shares. The appropriate model is thus not one of
\par
perfect competition but one of monopolistic competition.
\par
If firms are not perfectly correlated there is an incentive to create more firms,
\par
to diversify risk; if enough firms were created and if all firms were independently
\par
distributed all risk would be eliminated. A limit on this is imposed by the fact
\par
that there are fixed costs in setting up a firm. A remarkable result is that, subject
\par
to the constraint that the government does not subsidize these fixed costs and that
\par
all firms break even, the monopolistically competitive economy is Pareto Optimal
\par
has been established for a class of utility functions.
\par
Most of the work described so far has been at an abstract and fairly theoretical
\par
level, although the different models do have clear empirical implications. The
\par
only specialization of these models which has proved particularly useful for statistical
\par
purposes is the mean-variance capital asset pricing model.
\par
See Chapter 6 of Volume 4.
\par
2.3-3The model was first developed essentially as an exchange model by Sharpe [1964],
\par
Lintner [1965] and Mossin [1966] to describe the prices at which securities would
\par
trade: they established that if there is a perfectly safe asset, if all securities
\par
are evaluated in terms of the mean and variance of income generated (by the
\par
