\par
When the unanimity theorem does not hold, what is the firm to do? One suggestion is
\par
that a voting model be employed, e.g., that managers take the action which is preferred
\par
by the median stockholder. Unfortunately, equilibrium for such a model does
\par
not, in general, exist, nor is that model particularly persuasive. A more attractive
\par
model recognizes the informational asymmetries between managers and shareholders;
\par
shareholders attempt to design an incentive scheme which leads managers to
\par
take actions which maximize the welfare of the stockholders but recognize that they
\par
can have only a limited amount of control over the managers because of the information
\par
asymmetry. Most shareholders thus take no view at all on what the firm should
\par
be doing in any specific context. In such a model, take-overs play a special role;
\par
they are the means by which an individual or group of individuals who believe that
\par
the managers are not taking the appropriate action can be induced to do so. The
\par
role of take-overs is a subject which is presently under extensive study.
\par
Another implication of the price-taking model that has been found unattractive by a
\par
number of practitioners in corporate finance,is the result that in these models the
\par
financial structure of the firm (indeed any financial action) has no effect on the
\par
