\par
conventional theory says they should mean by risk. Are they confused? Should
\par
theory attempt to be consistent with common usage?
\par
For the reasons stated the two competitive theories seem clearly unsatisfactory:
\par
the monopolistically competitive model, taking into account the possibility of
\par
bankruptcy, and further developed to take account of informational problems, would,
\par
I believe, provide the best basis for the further development of the subject.
\par
III. COMPARISON OF PARTIAL AND GENERAL EQUILIBRIUM EFFECTS OF UNCERTAINTY
\par
We are used to general equilibrium effects modifying the partial equilibrium effects,
\par
partially offsetting them, but rarely are the signs reversed. In the analysis
\par
of the effects of uncertainty this presumption seems less certain. The reason
\par
for this may be seen most simply in terms of an example: the effects of a price
\par
stabilization program. The partial equilibrium effects of a price stabilization
\par
program are easy to describe. The variance of price is reduced. This may lead to
\par
a decrease in supply (if individuals were very risk-averse and if the price variability
\par
were demand-induced and if the individual is concerned about his welfare in
\par
the worst possible state, a reduction in variance means that he can reduce his
\par
effort), or a decrease in supply (if the individual is not very risk-averse the
\par
reduction in risk makes the growing of the particular crop more attractive).
\par
But this analysis assumed that the (average) output remained unchanged. What hap-
\par
\emph{\ding{110}k}
\par
pens now if output is increased? This will reduce the average price, and hence
\par
all producers may be worse off even though in the partial equilibrium analysis they
\par
are all better off.
\par
The reduction in price may reduce supply from what it would have been, but the
\par
supply may still be larger than it was initially. A simple example may make it
\par
clear that this can happen. Assume that a group of farmers has a choice of producing
\par
two different commodities, one of which has a random return, the other of
\par
which has a certain return, and assume that, as a result of risk aversion, they
\par
mix their acreage, planting some in both crops. Assume the demand curve for the
\par
safe crop is infinitely elastic. Then, as a result of a price stabilization program,
\par
the risky crop is safer; at the extreme, assume that the only source of
\par
risk is price risk so that a perfect stabilization scheme would make the formerly
\par
risky crop perfectly safe. Farmers would shift their production to the formerly
\par
risky crop, driving down the price; in the new, general equilibrium the return to
\par
the two crops would be the same and all farmers would be worse off them they were
\par
before the price stabilization program (although clearly the partial equilibrium
\par
effect would improve the welfare of farmers).
\par
2.3-6A second important argument for extending the partial equilibrium analysis to a
\par
general equilibrium analysis is that what is meant by risk can really only be understood
\par
within the context of the economy as a whole,taking into account the full
\par
possibilities of diversification and transfer of risks. Indeed, in some simple two
\par
commodity models, it is not always even possible to identify what is the risky commodity.
\par
Assume one commodity has a variable output; the other commodity's output
\par
is perfectly safe. One might be tempted to refer to the former as risky and the
\par
latter as safe since the "relative price" between the two is equally variable for
\par
both, but for the commodity whose output is variable, price will normally be inversely
\par
related, and thus reduce the "profit" risk associated with the sector. Indeed,
\par
the transfer of risk through the price system to the other sector may be so large
\par
that the apparently safe sector may act as if it were more risky, e.g., as individuals'
\par
wealth increases they may invest relatively less in it if they have increasing
\par
relative risk aversion.
\par
IV. SOME IMPLICATIONS FOR THE ENERGY SECTOR
\par
What implications does all of this have for the supply of energy? There are some
\par
important and immediate negative implications: those who argue that the market can
\par
adequately take care of risk, just as any other commodity, are, at best, reasoning
\par
by analogy---and a questionable one at that. Similarly, the argument that the
\par
appropriate rate of return for a regulated utilities is that of the same risk
\par
class (or 6 class), is also questionable.
\par
There are some positive implications: that in evaluating projects one ought to look
\par
at the covariance of returns with the market as a whole; if the price of energy is
\par
an important determinant of profitability---an R \& D project developing a substitute
\par
for oil might be socially attractive even though it would not privately be attractive
\par
for an oil producer. If the demand for oil is high, the price and profits of
\par
oil may be high, in which case the marginal utility of oil firms may be very low
\par
while the marginal utility of income as a whole (and therefore the marginal value of
\par
the project itself) will be high.
\par
Moreover, much of the risk of the energy sector may be, in effect, passed off to
\par
other sectors because of the \emph{inelasticity of} demand for energy; in the absence of a
\par
full set of securities markets this acts like an externality, and the full implications
\par
of this therefore have to be taken into account.
\par
2.3-7For example, the choice of technique on the part of energy using firms will depend
\par
on their assessment of the risks associated with the price of various forms of
\par
energy over the lifetime of their capital equipment. Thus, the risk faced by the
\par
energy producing sector depends on the actions taken by energy using sector, and
\par
conversely. The resulting market equilibrium will not in general be optimal; this
\par
is particularly important in considering "major" decisions associated with the
\par
development of new technologies.
\par
Finally, the above discussion should make it clear that in the analysis of the effects
\par
of uncertainty on the supply of energy there remains numerous important open
\par
questions. Tracing out the full implications of uncertainty in a general equilibrium
\par
context is a difficult task and although in this report we have succeeded in
\par
formulating some simple cases where these general equilibrium impacts can be traced,
\par
a fuller analysis is required. But the general equilibrium aspects which appear to
\par
be important are not those associated with large-scale programming models, nor even
\par
detailed econometric models, bur rather with a more detailed specification of the
\par
behavioral response of the firms and consumers in the economy to uncertainty, and
\par
their interactions through capital markets and commodity markets.
\par
