\par
THE VALUE OF INFORMATION
\par
I. INTRODUCTION
\par
An enormous amount of resources are allocated to obtaining and disseminating information.
\par
It is virtually impossible to obtain an accurate estimate of the expenditure
\par
on information for two reasons: much of the information comes as a joint product
\par
with other economic activities---the manager may obtain information about the
\par
quality of his workers in the process of supervising their work; information about
\par
the delivery lags associated with a particular firm follows as a by-product of
\par
ordering from the firm and it is difficult to draw the line between "informationally
\par
related" economic activities and other economic activities: all R \& D expenditure
\par
may be viewed as the production of particular kinds of information; many sales activities
\par
may be viewed as conveying information from the seller to the buyer; all
\par
supervisory functions can be viewed as a communication of information (about what
\par
the supervisor wants and the consequences of not doing what the supervisor wants)
\par
to the supervised. Whenever two individuals interact there is likely to be some exchange
\par
or communication of information. Indeed, if we view the price system as
\par
the mechanism by which information about preferences of consumers, the technology
\par
of firms and the scarcity of resources is communicated in a decentralized economy,
\par
then all those involved in making the price system work (e.g., those who run the
\par
cash register) are indirectly involved in the production and exchange of information.
\par
Thus, although there have been estimates of the allocation of resources to information
\par
related activities, it is hard to ascribe much significance to such estimates;
\par
what is clear is that much of economic activity cannot be understood in terms of
\par
the simple paradigm of producing a homogeneous commodity, such as steel, with homogeneous
\par
factors of production, with a known technology, with known prices of inputs
\par
and outputs.
\par
Perhaps more to the point, the way that information affects the allocation of resources
\par
cannot be really understood simply by treating its production as if it were
\par
like any other commodity. First, the output "information" differs from conventional
\par
commodity in several important respects; and secondly, the mechanisms by which
\par
information is produced or conveyed differs in many instances,at least,from that of
\par
conventional commodities.
\par
2.4-1There is, of course, some cost associated with obtaining information, and as a consequence
\par
firms (or other units in our society) have to make judgments about the
\par
value of information as well as the cost of obtaining it. They have to decide what
\par
kind of information to obtain and how to get it.
\par
The basic object of this chapter is to show what determines the value of a particular
\par
piece of information and to describe how one can obtain a quantitative estimate
\par
of its value, illustrating the basic principles by some examples related to the supply
\par
of energy. The chapter is divided into three parts. First, we present a general
\par
discussion of what is meant by information and its value and why information
\par
is different from other commodities. Section III illustrates how the value of information
\par
may be calculated for some specific types of information and describes its
\par
determinants. Section IV provides a review of some of the various kinds of information
\par
which are important for the energy sector; our earlier discussions lead us to the
\par
suggestion that a clearer formulation of the value of information would probably
\par
lead to a significantly different allocation of resources for obtaining information.
\par
II. THE DEFINITION OF AND VALUE OF INFORMATION
\par
"Information" has been defined in a number of different ways; for our purposes the
\par
most useful way of thinking about it is the following: "information" is anything
\par
which, in some situations at least, changes our judgments concerning the probability
\par
distribution of various events. Before relating this definition to the more formal
\par
definitions generally presented, let us see how our definition is related to the
\par
common usage of the term in several specific circumstances.
\par
(a) An employer says he wishes to know more information about an applicant. The
\par
employer has a probability distribution over the various characteristics that the
\par
individual might have (e.g., there is a 50-50 chance that he is bright and a 50-50
\par
change that he is stupid). His prior distribution, i.e., his probability distribution
\par
concerning these characteristics prior to obtaining the information,is based
\par
on previous experience with individuals with the same readily observable characteristics
\par
(age, sex, etc.). He administers an examination. Of those who pass the
\par
examination, 80\% turn out to be bright. 50\% pass the examination. Thus his
\par
posterior distribution, the distribution after he has obtained the information, is
\par
either 80-20 (if the individual passes) or 20-80 (if he fails).
\par
2.4-2Let us assume that instead there are three grades of the exam, A, B and C. If an
\par
individual obtains a B there is still a 50-50 chance that he is bright. If an individual
\par
obtains a B so that our posterior is the same as our prior, are we any more
\par
informed than we were before the exam? We might say, we are more "confident" in our
\par
probability judgments. We shall return to this problem later.
\par
(b) An oil firm wishes to have more information about the amount of oil underneath
\par
a particular tract. It has a prior distribution on the amount of oil, based on the
\par
available geological data. It drills a well. If it finds no oil, this increases
\par
slightly the probability that there is little oil in the tract. If the prior distribution
\par
has a positive probability that there is no oil, and if the firm drills a
\par
well and discovers oil, the posterior distribution will have a zero probability of
\par
zero oil. The cumulative probabilities change as in figure 1. Success shifts the
\par
cumulative probability distribution to the right, failing shifts the distribution
\par
to the left.
