II.1 Intuitive Definition of the Value of Information
\par
Why is information of value? The answer is almost obvious. How often have we
\par
heard statements such as: "If only I had known I would have Information is
\par
valuable because what is an appropriate action in one set of circumstances, would
\par
not be an appropriate action in another. But we often have to take some actions
\par
before we know what the set of circumstances is. To return to our examples:
\par
2.4-4(a) The kind of job which is appropriate for a bright person is different from the
\par
kind of job which is appropriate for a dull person. Thus, the appropriate assignment
\par
of the job (the action) depends on information concerning abilities. If we
\par
assign a dull person to a machine requiring considerable intelligence he may operate
\par
the machine incorrectly and damage it beyond repair. Thus, although eventually we
\par
would undoubtedly find out about the individual's ability (by watching him on the
\par
job) it may be important to know the information before we assign him to the task.
\par
(b) The information about the amount of oil underneath a tract will affect the
\par
amount that the firm is willing to bid for the lease for that tract. It also
\par
affects its expenditure---both the amount and pattern---on drilling.
\par
(c) Information about the total amount of oil affects the rate at which it is
\par
optimal to extract (use) the oil.
\par
(d) Information about the probability distribution of prices of oil relative to
\par
coal affects the profitability of investing in convertible boilers.
\par
Not only does information enable us to take the action which is appropriate to the
\par
particular set of circumstances, it enables us to avoid (or reduce) taking the risk
\par
avoidance activities described at length in Chapter 1. For instance, if we do not
\par
know what the ability of the individual is, we will assign all individuals to a
\par
machine which is not readily damaged but which is less productive. Lack of information
\par
about the stock of oil may lead the society to take a very conservationist
\par
position, reducing the risk of running out of oil and maintaining "options".
\par
There is an economic cost associated with taking these risk avoidance activities
\par
and with taking actions which are not well suited to the set of circumstances.
\par
Expected profits may be lower; profits may have a higher variance; expected utility
\par
may be lower (for a consumer).
\par
The value of information is measured by the amount which the firm (consumer, government,
\par
society as a whole) would be willing to pay for the information. Notice that
\par
this way of putting the matter means that what we are calling the value of information
\par
is what classical economists called the value in use, not the value in exchange
\par
The calculation of the value of information is in principle a straightforward matter
\par
Assume that a firm were risk-neutral, and hence is concerned only with its level of
\par
expected profits. We give the firm a piece of information (say, about the quality
\par
of some work) and calculate the expected profit before and after giving him the
\par
information. This determines the value of information.
\par
2.4-5In calculating the value of information we must obviously make the calculations before
\par
we know what the information is: that is, in our example we ask what is it
\par
worth to know whether this particular individual is bright or dull. There is a
\par
given probability that we will be told that the individual is bright. If we know
\par
that he is bright, job "B" is the optimal job assignment. There is a given probability
\par
that he is dull; if he is dull, job MD'' is the optimal job assignment.
\par
Profits (or expected profits, if there still remain elements of uncertainty, as
\par
there undoubtedly will) in the former case are tt ; in the latter, tt ; we thus
\par
A \emph{D}
\par
calculate the change in expected profits as a result of having the information.
\par
Later we shall present several detailed examples showing how, in principle, the
\par
value of information may be calculated. First, however, we wish to discuss several
\par
important properties and to formalize our definition.
\par
II.2 Differences between Information and Other Commodities
\par
The value of information, as we have defined it, is different from the value we
\par
associated with a conventional commodity. For a conventional commodity, what we
\par
refer to as its value is its value in exchange rather than its value in use. The
\par
essential difference arises from the fact that the value in use includes the consumer
\par
surplus, the infra-marginal returns, while the value in exchange (in a competitive
\par
economy) is related to the marginal returns. Air has a zero marginal value,
\par
although a very high infra-marginal value.
\par
Since information, for the most part, is not traded on markets there is no market
\par
price to which we can relate its value.
\par
A fortiori, the value of information is not directly related to its cost of production.
\par
(For a conventional commodity the value in exchange equals its price equals
\par
its marginal cost of production.)
\par
The absence of markets for information has another important implication: for a
\par
conventional good, the value (the marginal value) of a commodity is the same for all
\par
individuals, since all individuals purchase the commodity up to the point where its
\par
marginal value is equal to its price. Thus, in equilibrium value in exchange is
\par
equalized; but for information, neither the marginal nor the total value is necessarily
\par
equalized. Hence, we cannot refer to the value of a particular piece of information,
\par
but only to the value of that piece of information to a particular individual
\par
(government, firm, agency, etc.).
\par
2.4-6Indeed, although giving a piece of information to an individual does not subtract
\par
from the information that the giver has, it may have a significant effect on its
\par
value. For a conventional commodity, giving a unit of a commodity to someone else
\par
is not likely to have much effect on what I can do with the remaining units in my
\par
possession or the enjoument that I receive from them. Giving someone else a piece
\par
of information may have a drastic effect on what I can do with the piece of information
\par
that I have. (The distinction is somewhat artificial: if I had a monopoly
\par
over some commodity, then giving away some units of the commodity would destroy my
\par
monopoly and thus have a drastic effect on my bargaining power; it is just that
\par
monopolies over commodities are relatively rare while monopolies over particular
\par
pieces of relevant information are probably not uncommon, again for intrinsic reasons
\par
associated with differences between commodities and information.) This serves
\par
to emphasize that information and its economic value are distinct concepts.
\par
To understand why information cannot be traded like conventional commodities we have
\par
to consider why information is different from conventional commodities.
\par
The kinds of goods conventionally traced in markets are called private goods and
\par
have two essential properties: appropriability and exclusivity. If, say, a firm
\par
produces a unit of a commodity, it can sell the commodity on the market, appropriating
\par
the returns to it; and the enjoyment of the commodity by one person is at the
\par
exclusion of the enjoyment (of the same unit) of the commodity by another. If one
\par
person eats an ice-cream cone it is not available for others to eat it. If the
\par
returns to a commodity are not appropriable no private person will supply it. On
\par
the other hand, if the commodity has "appropriability" but lacks exclusivity it may
\par
\emph{j*}
\par
\emph{be} supplied in the private market, but its supply will be less that optimal (if
\par
individuals are charged, at the margin, for using the commodity). Because of this
\par
undersupply, these goods are often supplied publicly.
\par
The conventional argument that public goods will necessarily be undersupplied is
\par
incorrect if non-linear pricing schemes (essentially capturing the consumer surplus)
\par
can be employed. For the provision of the public good to present a Pareto
\par
Optimal improvement the government must in effect employ such a pricing scheme.
\par
There are some reasons why a firm could not make use of the full range of price
\par
discriminating devices that the government can.
\par
2.4-7Information lacks the property of exclusivity: if I am given a ''piece of information"
\par
it does not subtract from the information that you have. Thus, the extent of
\par
the dissemination of information should be determined simply by its marginal cost
\par
of transmission: it should be given to anyone for whom the value exceeds that cost.
\par
On the other hand, information is only partly appropriable. To put it one way, it
\par
is very hard to establish property rights on information.
\par
There are cases where information is sold: newspapers, Consumers' Reports and
\par
Investor Advisory Services are all cases where information is sold, and thus the
\par
firms producing the information can appropriate some part of the benefits. But it
\par
is often possible to obtain information without paying for it. For instance, the
\par
discovery of nylon and its production conveyed information to all other potential
\par
producers that it was, in fact, possible to produce synthetic materials with such
\par
properties. Thus, although the patent system might have protected the direct returns
\par
to nylon, the information about the possibility of making such a substance
\par
could not be appropriated at the same time. A firm that searches over a wide range
\par
of potential holders of a position and then hires a particular individual is, in
\par
effect, publicly making available the results of its research; and if the characteristics
\par
which lead to productivity in the given job are correlated with those which
\par
lead to productivity in some other job, another firm may attempt to make use of that
\par
information. (Long-term contracts might obviously enable a greater degree of appropriation
\par
of returns from this kind of information, but there are other reasons why
\par
such contracts that are binding on workers are not enforceable.) Moreover, because
\par
of its lack of exclusivity, it is very difficult to prevent resale of information;
\par
thus readers of Consumers' Reports may make the information available to non-purchasers
\par
of Consumers' Reports.
\par
There are, in addition to these problems, several aspects of information which make
\par
it unlikely that it will be supplied in competitive markets and, if supplied, make
\par
it unlikely that it will be supplied in the correct amounts.
\par
First, there is an intrinsic difficulty that, prior to obtaining the information,
\par
one does not know what it is that one is obtaining. There is a sense in which there
\par
must be uncertainty about what it is that is being purchased. (In the case of an
\par
individual who repeatedly purchases information from a given seller of information,
\par
he may form judgments about the likely value of the information to be obtained;
\par
nonetheless, he cannot know what he is purchasing.)
\par
2.4-8Secondly, every piece of information must, in a sense, be different. While giving
\par
an individual an extra unit of conventional commodity will - unless there is drastic
\par
decreasing marginal utility - still enhance the individual's welfare, telling the
\par
individual the same piece of information a second time has no effect on his welfare.
\par
(Psychologists might object to this statement for it does not properly take into
\par
account learning. Being taught the same thing twice does make a difference.) Thus,
\par
every piece of information is perforce differentiated from every previous piece
\par
acquired. Information is inherently heterogeneous in a fundamental sense. This
\par
does not mean that we cannot meaningfully talk about the process of acquiring information,
\par
for that may be a \emph{systematic} activity.
\par
Markets in which commodities are heterogeneous, rather than homogeneous, are generally
\par
not perfectly competitive, and the allocation of resources in such markets, in
\par
general, does not have the optimality properties associated with perfectly competitive
\par
markets. (This is the case, even though the markets may be highly competitive,
\par
in the conventional, non-technical, usage of the term.)
\par
But there are further reasons why markets in which information is important are
\par
likely not to be perfectly competitive. First, the value of information will (as we
\par
shall show later) increase with the scale of the firm; hence information introduces
\par
an element of increasing returns to scale into the production process. Secondly,
\par
under certain circumstances, purely competitive equilibria with imperfect information
\par
will not exist; the only possible equilibria involve imperfect competition.*
\par
The heterogeneity of information is closely related to another important problem
\par
which, until recently, represented one of the two major stumbling blocks in the
\par
development of a meaningful theory of market equilibrium with imperfect information:
\par
there is no natural unit** for measuring the quantity of information."\textasciicircum{} That is,
\par
*The arguments showing this are sufficiently complicated that we shall not present
\par
them here. See Stiglitz [1978],
\par
**Earlier writers thought of value as an intrinsic property of the commodity and
\par
much of their research was concerned with identifying this intrinsic property
\par
"value" (without success). Notice that in our formulation the value of information
\par
is not an intrinsic property of the information but depends critically on
\par
the use to which it is to be put.
\par
+The other major stumbling block arose from attempting to impose a formulation of
\par
competitive markets which worked well for conventional commodities on information
\par
which, as we have argued, is clearly different.
\par
2.4-9for conventional commodities, we can speak of the number of units of the commodity
\par
and then, quite separately, discuss its market value. Here we have loosely referred
\par
to a "piece" of information, but the only quantitative description provided so far
\par
has been its value (value in use).
\par
There have been several attempts to provide a simple quantifiable measure of information
\par
(entropy) but these do not seem to have any interesting economic properties (as
\par
we shall argue later). Economists, however, are used to dealing with variables like
\par
utility, for which there is no natural cardinalization, and it turns out that something
\par
can be said (in the nature of "more information" or "less information") even
\par
when a cardinalization is not possible. But the absence of a cardinalization of information
\par
makes it difficult to discuss meaningfully the "value of a marginal piece
\par
of information." Does information satisfy, for instance, the law of diminishing
\par
returns? Remarkably enough it can be shown that under very weak conditions an
\par
answer can be provided to this question even without a cardinalization.
\par
Even when markets are competitive, the allocation of resources to information is
\par
likely not to be optimal because there are very strong externalities often associated
\par
with information (arising, in part, from the inherent absence of perfect information
\par
markets). We gave several examples earlier: the firm which seeks out the high productivity
\par
workers may find the returns appropriated not by itself but by the worker
\par
as other firms bid for his services. In a competitive stock market informed buyers
\par
bid up the price of an undervalued security, enabling the price to convey information
\par
from the informed to the uninformed. Purchasers of Consumers' Reports who refuse to
\par
buy appliances having certain safety defects may induce the manufacturers to remedy
\par
those defects and thus convey an externality to those who have not purchased
\par
consumers' reports.
\par
II.3 Differences in Mechanisms by Which Information is Produced
\par
A final important set of distinctions between conventional commodities and information
\par
arises from the mechanisms by which information is "produced"; there are some
\par
cases where the production process is very similar to that of a conventional commodity.
\par
A group of individuals which are initially undifferentiated is subjected
\par
to an examination and the output of this process is a set of labels, differentiating
\par
in some way the individuals. But there is an important class of cases where the information
\par
production is intimately connected with (in a sense is a joint product
\par
of) other economic activities, and has important implications as to how these are
\par
2.4-10conducted. For instance, the amount of insurance which an individual purchases may
\par
convey information about the riskiness (assumed, say, to be initially unknown) of
\par
the individual; if insurance companies come to learn this, then the price charged by
\par
firms will be a function of the amount of insurance purchases (if the amount is an
\par
observable characteristic). The wage at which an individual is willing to work conveys
\par
information about his quality, and hence firms may not employ workers who are
\par
willing to work at lower wages than those it presently employs. Thus, unlike the
\par
first case, resources are not directly allocated to obtaining information; yet the
\par
attempts to obtain information by what Stiglitz and Salop have referred to as selfselection
\par
methods where decisions and actions of the individual or firm convey information,
\par
do affect the allocation of resources and can be shown to have real costs
\par
associated with them. But these costs, and the effects that the attempts to obtain
\par
information have on economic processes,cannot be adequately captured in any model
\par
treating information like an ordinary produced commodity.
\par
II.4 Implication of the Differences between Information and Conventional Commodities
\par
We argued in the previous sections that there were a number of important differences
\par
between information and conventional commodities. These differences have some very
\par
important implications: in particular, the absence of market prices for information
\par
means that the amount to be produced cannot be decided by the same kinds of criteria
\par
in a decentralized economy that can be employed for conventional commodities (e.g.,
\par
the criterion, "produce if price exceeds marginal cost" is simply not applicable);
\par
the kinds of criteria that are applicable, (does the value of the information as
\par
described above exceed its costs?) involve some fairly complicated calculations, to
\par
be described below.
\par
Moreover, private returns will, in general, differ markedly from social returns so
\par
that both the amount of expenditure on information acquisition as well as its pattern
\par
will not be socially optimal; in particular, the difficulties of appropriating
\par
the returns and the prevalence of externalities suggest that in many cases information
\par
will have to be "purchased" or subsidized by the industry or by the
\par
government.
\par
Finally, because information is not freely traded, there will exist differences in
\par
information; some of these differences in information have, in turn, important implication.
\par
In particular, the problems associated with regulation of utilities can
\par
be viewed as basically arising from the differential information (concerning the
\par
state of technology) between the regulator and the regulated firm.
\par
