III.3 Some General Properties of the Value of Information
\par
In this section we describe briefly and intuitively some of the general properties
\par
of the value of information.
\par
First, we noted that the value of information is related to the revision in actions
\par
which it induces. Thus, for instance, if some machine were to last five years and
\par
required a three-year planning and construction period, knowing the course of future
\par
prices and demands more than eight years in advance would have no greater value than
\par
knowing them just eight years in advance; the earlier information would not lead to
\par
any revision of actions.
\par
If the only available technology requires a fixed capital and labor ratio then
\par
knowing the relative price of capital and labor will have no value. If the elasticity
\par
of substitution between capital and labor (the extent to which capital can be
\par
substituted for labor) is low, then again the value of information about relative
\par
factor prices will be low.
\par
This has an important corollary. Assume initially there is no information (a formal
\par
definition is presented in the Appendix) and that our action is chosen to maximize
\par
expected utility given the absence of information. A "little bit" of information
\par
will lead to a little revision of action and hence will have very little value.
\par
More formally, one can show that the value of information as a function of the quantity
\par
of information (again defined in a very general way, as set out in the appendix)
\par
is of the form of figure 3. Thus it never pays to buy a little bit of information.
\par
Some degree of specialization in information acquisition is required.
\par
Thirdly, the value of information is related to the scale of the decision. The increase
\par
in expected profits resulting from any piece of information would in a linear
\par
(constant returns to scale) technology be simply proportional to the scale of the
\par
2.4-24firm. Thus, if for a given level of information profits are proportional to inputs
\par
with the level of information being endogenous, (expected) profits are convex function
\par
of inputs. This means that information introduces an essential aspect of increasing
\par
returns to scale in production; for there to be more than one firm in such
\par
an industry requires that there be some offsetting elements of diminishing returns,
\par
i.e., either the outputs must be a strictly concave function of inputs or the firm
\par
is risk-averse (i.e., treating "utility" as the output of the production process,
\par
there are diminishing returns).
\par
The consequences of this, of course, is that there is some presumption that the
\par
value of information for an industry (with a linear technology) exceeds that to any
\par
firm, and hence provides an argument for the provision, by the industry, of information
\par
which is of general value. (The firm would then specialize in obtaining information
\par
which is specific to its own situation, e.g., location, personnel, etc.
\par
The discussion of earlier sections should have made it clear that not only is there
\par
a presumption that \textasciicircum he market would supply too little information but the
\par
kind of information obtained may be nonoptimal. If it became known that a firm
\par
selected its managers on the basis of information concerning his general productive
\par
characteristics, being a manager at the given firm would convey a great deal of
\par
\emph{information.} Other firms would either bid away the manager or bid his wages up to
\par
the point where the firm was unable to obtain a return for its investment in information.
\par
Hence the firm is induced to obtain information which is of specific relevance
\par
to the firm and which accordingly has little value to other firms.)
\par
This presumption must be modified when account is taken of the general equilibrium
\par
effects of the actions taken by a firm. For instance, assume there were a zero supply
\par
elasticity of coal. Convertible technologies are clearly inefficient when viewed
\par
from the point of view of society as a whole. But from the point of view of a
\par
risk-averse firm,convertibility may be desirable. It would be far better to have
\par
"price insurance"; convertibility is, from the social point of view, an expensive
\par
way of obtaining price insurance for the firm. A firm may think that there is value
\par
in obtaining more information about the probability of a high price because the
\par
manager of the firm believes that inputs to the firm may be changed, i.e., the
\par
firm can obtain more coal. But by assumption that action is not possible for
\par
society as a whole. Hence, it simply cannot be said whether in general, the social
\par
value exceeds or falls short of the private value of information.
\par
A similar point has been raised in other contexts where information is important, e.g., in
\par
the work of Spence [1974] , Stiglitz [1978], and Hirschleifer [1975] where it has been emphasized
\par
that much of the private return to information is related to obtaining rents. For
\par
2.4-25instance, knowing that a security is more valuable than had previously been thought
\par
has a private value in that one can buy the security at its present low price and,
\par
when the information becomes available to the rest of the market, reap a capital
\par
gain; but obviously this information has no social value if no decision is to be
\par
based on that information, for society as a whole cannot increase its holdings of the
\par
security.
\par
IV. INFORMATION AND THE SUPPLY OF ENERGY
\par
IV.1 A Taxonomy and Some Policy Implications
\par
There are several areas in which the absence of information and attempts to acquire
\par
information have an important effect on the supply of energy. There are a number
\par
of ways in which we might organize a discussion of these various areas.
\par
(i) By the unit obtaining the information. We distinguished earlier between the
\par
value of information to a firm, to an industry and, possibly, to society as a whole.
\par
We could address ourselves to the kinds of information which are important at each
\par
level and the kinds of information which ought to be supplied by each level.
\par
(ii) By the major sources of uncertainty. In our introduction we identified several
\par
important areas of uncertainty: geological, technological, demand, supply and
\par
policy generated uncertainty. Whenever there is an element of uncertainty, it is
\par
always possible to reduce it by obtaining information; and hence our earlier discussion
\par
of the sources of uncertainty could equivalently be thought of as a taxonomy of
\par
the variables concerning which information might profitably be obtained.
\par
(iii) By the actions to be taken. For instance, the decision to undertake a particular
\par
research program may require information of various types, e.g., the demand
\par
for energy; but information concerning the demand for energy will, of course, be
\par
relevant to a number of other purposes (decisions) as well. To ascertain the total
\par
value of obtaining a particular piece of information one must consider all the actions
\par
which may be affected by it.
\par
We have chosen to organize our discussion around the actions to be taken. The discussion
\par
below is not meant to be exhaustive but merely indicate how one ought to go
\par
about thinking about what kind (and how much) information ought to be acquired.
\par
2.4-26IV. 1.1 Should the government supply geological and geophysical information to potential
\par
bidders for government leases prior to the bidding, and/or should the government
\par
encourage private firms to acquire such information? This kind of information, it
\par
has been argued, is of private value (it affects the amount bid and, if the bid is
\par
successful, it may affect the pattern and timing of drilling, but the private value
\par
is different from its social value). These questions are discussed extensively in
\par
Volume 3, Chapter 1 of this report.
\par
IV, 1.2 How much should be spent on alternative research programs , e. g. , the development
\par
of the breeder reactor, developing technologies which allow convertibility in the
\par
use of fuels at lower cost, etc.? The assessment of these alternative research programs
\par
requires various kinds of information or, more accurately, obtaining better
\par
information will allow a better allocation of resources. What one needs to ask
\par
whether the improvement in resource allocation is worth the cost of obtaining the
\par
information? This is a quantitative question, and one of the main objectives of
\par
this chapter (and indeed the entire research project), is the further development
\par
of methodologies by which this may be done.
\par
The kinds of information which are relevant for assessing alternative research programs
\par
can be divided into two broad categories:
\par
(i) Technological information concerning the likely costs of alternative research
\par
programs and probabilities of success. These are judgments, scientific and engineer
\par
ing, about which the economist may have relatively little to say.
\par
(ii) Economic information, estimates of the economic benefits and costs associated
\par
with different research programs, were they to be successful. A number of different
\par
kinds of economic considerations are relevant:
\par
(a) Supply information (prices of inputs) . The value of developing commercial nuclear
\par
reactors depends on an assessment of the supply of uranium and the supply of alternative
\par
fuels. The value of developing a cheaper method of extraction of shale of a
\par
particular type may depend on the supply of that type of shale (and again, the avail
\par
ability of other fuels).
\par
(b) The desirability of developing cheaper convertible technologies depends on the variation
\par
in relative prices expected in the future. It also depends on the supply
\par
elasticity of, say, coal.
\par
2.4-27(c) Demand Information. Obviously the value of developing cheaper methodsof producing
\par
energy depends on the estimated demand for energy; the numerous research programs
\par
attempting to forecast demands can be justified partly in these terms. For large
\par
research programs, however, what is required is not an estimate of the level of
\par
demand at the prices expected to prevail in the future but an estimate of the entire
\par
demand curve (or, at least, of the demand elasticity at the expected level of consumption);
\par
for a successful research program leading, say, to the development of a
\par
breeder reactor would probably have a nonnegligible effect on market price and
\par
hence the consumer surplus associated with the change in the price must be included
\par
in an estimate of the value of such a research program.
\par
(d) General Market Information. An assessment of the value of a research program, the
\par
benefits and costs of which extend over a number of years, requires an assessment
\par
(information about) how benefits and costs at different dates are to be weighted,
\par
i.e., appropriate intertemporal prices. How important is it to obtain a better
\par
estimate of the "correct" rate of discounting to use for such a project? This question,
\par
which repeatedly arises, is really a question about the value of information
\par
of this particular kind.
\par
IV. 1.3 When should resources be allocated to the development of a technology embodying
\par
the "latest" state of knowledge? This question is of importance because of the
\par
large fixed costs associated with developing any workable technology. If there are
\par
likely to be scientific breakthroughs which will make a technology incorporating present
\par
scientific knowledge obsolete, it obviously pays to continue with an older
\par
technology. Information about the timing of new scientific developments and the
\par
magnitude of the fixed costs are crucial.
\par
IV. 1.4 Similar issues arise concerning investment in new plant. Should a new plant be
\par
built today using existing technology, or should the construction be postponed to
\par
allow for the newer technologies which are in the process of developing? Again,
\par
both technological information (concerning the likely dates of arrival of the newer
\par
technologies and the costs associated with them) and economic information (concerning
\par
the economic costs of waiting) are required.
\par
IV. 1.5 The rate at which a resource should be extracted depends not only on the estimates
\par
of the supply of the natural resource (discussed in Chapter 2) but also
\par
on the information concerning the dates of arrival of substitutes and the costs of
\par
production. Precautionary motives will lead to holding large supplies of oil in
\par
2.4-28reserve against the contingency that the new substitute will not arrive for some
\par
time. The resolution of this uncertainty will, on average, lead to a lowering of
\par
the price of oil (even if our original estimate of the expected date of arrival of
\par
the substitute with a given expected cost of production were accurate).
\par
IV. 1.6 The problem of regulation can be viewed as essentially a problem of information.
\par
The regulator would like to make sure that the regulated firm is efficient and earns
\par
only a "fair" rate of return. As the problem is usually formulated, the efficiency
\par
issue is only addressed indirectly; the profits of the firm (rate of return on capital)
\par
are the "signals" which determine whether a case for a rate increase can be
\par
justified. The inefficiencies in production associated with using particular rules
\par
have been extensively discussed in the literature. If other independent information
\par
about the technology or the "necessary" costs of production could be obtained, regulation
\par
could be based on such estimates and thus reduce the distortionary biases associated
\par
with the use of the kinds of signals presently employed. (Where there are several
\par
units producing essentially the same commodity, an argument can be made that information
\par
about other costs of production should be the primary determinant of the
\par
price which any given firm is allowed to charge.)
\par
IV.1.7 Information and Policy Uncertainty. An important source of uncertainty
\par
concerns government policy decisions and their impacts. Little work has been done
\par
in calculating the costs of policy uncertainties and in ascertaining the value of
\par
the government's providing better information concerning those policies.
\par
V. THE VALUE OF INFORMATION CONCERNING FUTURE DEMANDS: SOME SPECULATIVE REMARKS
\par
The recent events focusing national attention on the energy sector have led to a
\par
number of large scale econometric forecasting models of the demand for energy. Such
\par
demand forecasts are a kind of information, and it may prove interesting to ask what
\par
is the value of this kind of information. Although such information undoubtedly
\par
has considerable value, how large the value is, is related to how it affects actions.
\par
If, for instance, there is no decision today which will depend on the sectoral
\par
decomposition of demand, then a disaggregated demand forecast is of value only to
\par
the extent to which the disaggregation improves the aggregate estimate. (A regional
\par
disaggregation might be more valuable if there were a central planning authority
\par
deciding on the location of plants; but even this is not persuasive since the location
\par
of demand is an endogenous variable. The provision of cheap electricity in a
\par
particular region undoubtedly does affect the demand for it there.) Similarly, for
\par
2.4-29purposes of construction of plants estimates of demand more than 30 years in the
\par
future may not be particularly relevant; for purposes of deciding on the allocation
\par
of resources for an R\&D program a longer range forecast may be of considerable
\par
value---but, as we noted earlier, for the latter purpose what is needed is
\par
neither a sectoral or regional disaggregation but what may be of considerable value
\par
is an estimate of the demand elasticity. Similarly, if the "action" which is contemplated
\par
involves designing or introducing convertible boilers: what is crucial
\par
is not an estimate of the mean level of demand and expected price, but an estimate
\par
of the voltility of demand (price).
\par
Although judgments of this kind---that one kind of information is more valuable
\par
than another---are often made, the methodologies discussed within this chapter provide
\par
a framework by which one can attempt to quantify the value of information.
\par
