2.4-30APPENDIX TO CHAPTER 4
\par
Throughout much of the discussion of the text we talked about the value of buying
\par
''more'' information. Is there a way of quantifying the amount of information? We
\par
argued earlier that no such measure exists, but that we could obtain a partial
\par
ordering, i.e., there are cases where we can say that we are unambiguously more
\par
informed.
\par
The fact that we cannot in general say that we are more informed in one situation
\par
than another is familiar to anyone who has attempted to design an examination. Assume
\par
there are three grades of students, brilliant, average and stupid. Some examinations
\par
are designed to identify the brilliant: they ask extremely difficult questions;
\par
the average and stupid students are both unable to answer these questions and
\par
hence such an examination \emph{conveys no} information about differences between the latter
\par
two groups. On the other hand, questions which are designed to separate average
\par
and stupid students often do not have the scope for allowing the brilliant students
\par
to show their brilliance. The different kinds of examination provide different information
\par
but it cannot be said that one kind is more informative than another.
\par
If we were looking for a simple measure of the degree of informativeness, there are
\par
two obvious approaches that we could take.
\par
Since, as we suggested earlier, better information enables us to suit our actions
\par
to the situation we are in, it ought to be the case that all individuals, regardless
\par
of their objective function, must prefer more information to less information (or at
\par
least be indifferent, if there is no action which is altered as a result of their
\par
information).
\par
Secondly, in the formulation presented in section II.5 we had signals which conveyed
\par
information about the states of nature. The information system was a matrix which
\par
described the probability of observing signal t if the state were s. The signal
\par
is a "message" conveying information about the state of nature. If the "message"
\par
became garbled, i.e., sometimes we thought we received message t when we really
\par
A-lreceived message t', then clearly the messages would convey less information.
\par
Hence, a second natural definition of more information is the following: an information
\par
matrix A is more informative than an information matrix B if the latter
\par
could have been generated from the former by a process of "garbling".
\par
It should be clear that our example does not satisfy either of the two definitions.
\par
The information matrix for our examination systems, assuming that one-third of the
\par
