The basic equivalence theorem for information structures states that the three
\par
characterizations of "more informative" that we have just provided, are all
\par
equivalent:
\par
An Information Matrix A is more informative that Information Matrix B if
\par
(a) Max E\textbraceleft U \textbar{} A\textbraceright{} {$>$} max E\textbraceleft U \textbar{} B\textbraceright{} for all utility functions (uses of
\par
information).
\par
(b) A represents a "finer partition" of the states of nature than B.
\par
(c) There exists a markov matrix C such that B = AC.
\par
The interpretation of the third condition is straightforward. It says that when we
\par
think we observe signal t\textasciicircum{} we are really observing signal t\textasciicircum{} with probability
\par
c... Hence the probability that signal j occurs, given that the true state is s,
\par
is the probability that the signal t\textasciicircum{} occurs given that the true state is s
\par
times the probability that we are in fact observing the signal t\textasciicircum{} when we think we
\par
are observing the signal t\textasciicircum : summed up over all possible signals.
\par
We do not present a formal proof here of the equivalence of the three definitions,
\par
but the argument is trivial. (b) implies (a) since with a finer partition of the
\par
states of nature one can do anything with which one could have done with a less fine
\par
partition, but the converse is not true. (a) implies (b) is only slightly more
\par
subtle: assume that A does not represent a finer partition of the sets of states
\par
of nature. Then there must exist a subset of states, S\textasciicircum, which are distinguished
\par
among by A and not by B, and another sub-set S2 for which the converse is true.
\par
Consider, say, a "pay-off function" such that only information distinguishing among
\par
subset has a value. Clearly then A will yield a higher expected utility
\par
than B. But there also exist pay-off functions for which only information
\par
A-5distinguishing among subsets S2 has value, and for these B yields a higher expected
\par
utility than A. Hence, if one information matrix yields a higher expected
\par
utility for all pay-off functions than another, then the former must represent a
\par
finer partition of the sets of states of nature.
\par
The equivalence between (b) and (c) is similarly easy to see. For the garbling of
\par
signals is really equivalent to not being able to identify different signals and a
\par
distinct signal can be thought of as corresponding to a subset of the states of the
\par
world.
\par
The extremes of "no information" and "perfect information" are easy to identify: the
\par
latter is the case where the posterior probability distribution is the same for all
\par
signals, while the former is the case where the states may be perfectly identified
\par
by the signals. Thus, any information matrix for which posteriors differ from priors
\par
is more informative than the no-information information matrix, and the perfect
\par
information matrix is more informative than any other information matrix.
\par
Unfortunately, most information matrices cannot be ranked. Whether one information
\par
matrix is more valuable than another depends critically on the use to which it is to
\par
be put. By imposing some further restrictions we can obtain a stronger partial
\par
ordering of information matrices. We focus our attention on situations where the
\par
pay-offs are monetary pay-offs. For a given information matrix, a given utility
\par
function and a pay-off function (which translates actions into pay-offs), there is
\par
an optimal action (dependent on the signal) and a probability distribution of payoffs.
\par
We might say that one information matrix is more informative than another if,
\par
for all concave utility functions, for the given pay-off function, the level of
\par
expected utility is higher with the former information matrix than the latter.
\par
Clearly, whenever one information matrix is more informative in the earlier sense,
\par
it is more informative in the sense just defined, but there are cases where we can
\par
rank information matrices in our new definition where we could not in our earlier
\par
definition.
