\emph{Edited by}
\par
\emph{VICTOR P. GOLDBERG*}
\par
I. \textbf{AUTHORS' SUMMARY OF }PART I
\par
\textbf{RICHARD A. POSNER: }What we ask in Part \textbf{I }is what would the world look
\par
like, in terms of judicial services, if there were no state? We don't ask this
\par
because we are interested in privatizing judicial services. Our piece is not
\par
normative in its thrust. But it is frequently a useful approach to positive
\par
analysis to ask: What would be the problems if a service which has tradi-
\par
tionally been regarded as governmental were to be provided exclusively on a
\par
private basis?
\par
Several results follow from analyzing judicial services in that way, and I'll
\par
just summarize our major results. First, there is no reason why judicial
\par
services can't be provided privately. There are problems, but nonetheless
\par
one can conceive of a system of private adjudication. In fact one observes it
\par
in many areas.
\par
If you look at the dispute-resolution side of judicial services-as opposed
\par
to rule making-the main problem is that of getting other parties to submit
\par
themselves to the dispute-resolution process. The party who anticipates los-
\par
ing the case is likely to refuse to submit. Where the state is administering the
\par
judicial system, this is not a problem because the force of the state is used to
\par
compel submission. Under a purely private system other means of inducing
\par
participation have to be relied on. An important one, both in primitive
\par
society and today, is expulsion from an organization as a sanction. And that
\par
often works.
\par
The second part of the judicial function is rule creation. It was a great
\par
surprise to us to find Professor Carrington disparaging rule creation as a
\par
function of the common law adjudicative system. As anyone who practices
\par
law knows, the rules that emerge from appellate decisions play an important
\par
role in guiding private behavior.
\par
The rule-creation function is difficult in a purely private setting. One can
\par
imagine a world of competing private judges, each of whom might issue
\par
opinions in order to get a reputation for competence and impartiality.
\par
*Professor of Economics, University of California at Davis.
\par
\textbf{\emph{323}}THE \textbf{JOURNAL }OF \textbf{LEGAL STUDIES}
\par
Nonetheless it would be very difficult to use the opinions of these ad-
\par
judicators as a source of rules, because one would never know what judge
\par
one would come up against and what rules he would apply. So we observe a
\par
strong tendency toward monopoly in the production of rules. This result
\par
would be difficult to bring about in a setting of purely private adjudication.
\par
Having examined the problems and also opportunities for purely private
\par
adjudication, we then look at some examples where adjudication has been
\par
private. We begin with some primitive societies in which there is no state in
\par
the modern sense. There is a rich descriptive anthropological literature on
\par
law in such societies. We have just dipped into it. Some very interesting facts
\par
emerge which we have summarized in our discussion of the adjudicative
\par
institutions of the Yurok Indians.
\par
It turns out that in these private adjudicative systems there are serious
\par
problems -with the creation of rules, and in general the adjudicators do not
\par
operate as rule creators. There are problems of submission to the adjudicator
\par
and of enforcement but they tend to be solved by the threat of expulsion,
\par
which is a powerful sanction in a primitive setting.
\par
We then discuss arbitration, a major modern as well as historical example
\par
of private adjudication. We are collecting material on religious courts, an-
\par
other important example of private adjudication; the sanction that makes it
\par
effective is excommunication, which represents a serious cost to members of
\par
the religious group. We have two purposes in our discussion of arbitration.
\par
First, we want to try to explain on economic grounds some of the features
\par
one observes in the arbitration system, both the rules of law governing
\par
arbitration and also the judicial rules that historically inhibited arbitration,
\par
rules we tentatively attribute to the way in which judges used to be paid.
\par
Second, and particularly relevant in light of Professor Carrington's insis-
\par
tence that we come up with suggestions responsive to the procedural reform
\par
questions that concern him, we think there is an opportunity to use arbitra-
\par
tion procedures as a benchmark for judging the performance of the public
\par
procedural system. Because arbitration is voluntarily chosen, because it
\par
must survive in a competitive environment, the procedures used in arbitra-
\par
tion provide evidence of what efficient procedures are. This conclusion de-
\par
pends, however, on a preliminary step, which we take very tentatively, and
\par
that is to show that arbitration is indeed surviving in competition with the
\par
public judicial system. If arbitration were withering, that might suggest that
\par
its procedures were inferior to those of the public judicial system. The statis-
\par
tics suggest that arbitration is at least holding its own and perhaps even
\par
improving its position in the competition with the courts. This leads us to
\par
ask whether some of the procedures in arbitration might not be appropriate
\par
models for judicial reform.
\par
For example, Professor Carrington raised the question whether pretrial
\par
discovery isn't being abused in the public court system. There are manyDISCUSSION BY SEMINAR PARTICIPANTS
\par
things that one can say-and some of these things have been said by
\par
economists--on this problem. But in addition one can look at the discovery
\par
practices in arbitration (trying to correct for differences in the type of case)
\par
and ask whether they aren't a model for optimum pretrial discovery. What
\par
one finds is that there is discovery in arbitration but that it tends to be a good
\par
deal more limited than in public adjudication. To analyze the question fully,
\par
you would have to correct for systematic differences between cases that go to
\par
arbitration and cases in the courts. Antitrust cases are not a frequent subject
\par
of arbitration, so you're not going to find a good arbitration counterpart to
\par
the large antitrust case with its immense discovery burdens.
\par
As another example of how one might use arbitration procedures as a
\par
benchmark for evaluating judicial procedures, we think it's quite striking
\par
that arbitration typically does not involve appeals. You have a single arbi-
\par
rator; he's your judge. The matter might be one of great significance with
\par
very large stakes and yet the losing party doesn't get an appeal. Now if
\par
people wanted appeals in the type of cases that go to arbitration, that could
\par
easily be built into the arbitration procedure. The American Arbitration
\par
Association could offer appellate tribunals, but it doesn't.
\par
We infer from this that the real function of an appeal is to straighten out
\par
the law rather than to correct factual errors. Since the normal arbitration
\par
doesn't involve the creation of rules and the writing of opinions, there is no
\par
real role for an appellate tribunal designed to harmonize and advance the
\par
creation of rules. There could still be a function for an appellate tribunal in
\par
correcting erroneous findings by an arbitrator, but then arbitrators would
\par
have to make findings that could be reviewed. Apparently parties to arbitra-
\par
tion don't want this and that suggests to us that the costs of appellate
\par
procedures designed simply for fact correction may be greater than the ben-
\par
efits.
\par
Also, observe the character of the adjudicator in arbitration. In a typical
\par
commercial arbitration the adjudicator is not a lawyer, is not paid, and is not
\par
a professional arbitrator. He is a businessman on a roster of arbitrators
\par
maintained by the American Arbitration Association and he delivers an ad
\par
hoc adjudication. He is a lot like a juror in the sense of being neither a
\par
regular adjudicator nor a lawyer. There is only one arbitrator, not twelve or
\par
six. The arbitrator, however, is chosen for his business expertise in the
\par
subject matter of the litigation. This suggests the possibility that one could
\par
have a very different kind of jury system in the public courts from what we
\par
have, one in which jurors were chosen with reference to their expertise in the
\par
activity being adjudicated. Professor Hazard pointed out correctly that
\par
when you have the kind of jury question that involves ascertaining what an
\par
ordinary person thinks, a standard of ordinary care, our type of jury system,
\par
which is designed to select the average man, may be a direct counterpart to
\par
the arbitration system.THE \textbf{JOURNAL }OF \textbf{LEGAL STUDIES}
\par
We were surprised to find that in general in arbitration the lawyer's fees
\par
are not paid by the losing party. Arbitration tends to follow the American
\par
approach whereby each party pays his own legal fees regardless of the out-
\par
come of the case. Many economists think that the European and English
\par
system of reimbursing the winning party's expenses out of the losing party's
\par
pockets is more efficient. Here is a bit of contrary evidence.
\par
Related to that, we find some scattered evidence that American and
\par
British arbitrators are preferred to their European counterparts, regardless
\par
of whether the parties to the litigation are English or American. Apparently,
\par
most maritime arbitration is carried out by American arbitrators using arbi-
\par
tration procedures which, apart from the points mentioned, resemble in a
\par
general way the Anglo-American procedural system. This we use as some
\par
evidence that the much criticized Anglo-American adversary system has
\par
advantages compared to the European inquisitorial system.
\par
Having discussed some private systems of adjudication we then turn to
\par
other questions. I'll just mention them.
\par
First-and this was originally to be an important emphasis of the paper
\par
but got submerged, partly because of lack of data-if dispute-resolution
\par
mechanisms are costly or ineffective, there are ways of avoiding them en-
\par
tirely. You can substitute away from disputes altogether. You can substitute
\par
all kinds of self-help remedies-for example, repossession by a creditor as
\par
opposed to suing to get the property back. The most dramatic illustration is
\par
the no-fault automobile insurance movement which is an effort to get an
\par
entire class of accidents out of the court system by eliminating the liability
\par
method of obtaining compensation and substituting a form of compulsory
\par
accident insurance.
\par
The liquidated-damages clause is an example of parties' substituting a
\par
formula of damage assessment for an adjudication over the amount of dam-
\par
ages owed. There are a lot of these devices; we haven't tried to catalog them
\par
systematically or trace their growth over time. We do suggest, however, that
\par
by looking at changes over time in the reliance placed on these substitutes for
\par
adjudication one could, at least in principle, learn something about the
\par
relative attractiveness of the courts. Presumably, if these methods are grow-
\par
ing, one reason may be increasing court fees or lawyers' fees or other obsta-
\par
cles to use of the legal system itself.
\par
We then look at competition among courts. In particular, doctrines have
\par
been developed in the judicial system that seem to operate to suppress or
\par
discourage private adjudication. We explore some of the reasons for this
\par
anticompetitive behavior. I'll mention just one example, the traditional judi-
\par
cial hostility toward the boycott. While that makes perfectly good sense
\par
where a boycott is used as a method of enforcing a cartel, there have been
\par
cases where the purpose of the boycott was simply to coerce people to use a
\par
system of private adjudication. The boycott, which is another word for\textbf{DISCUSSION BY SEMINAR }PARTICIPANTS
\par
expulsion, becomes the sanction that makes a system of private courts effec-
\par
tive. We can't understand judicial hostility to those devices on antitrust
\par
grounds and speculate that it may have something to do with the way in
\par
which judges historically were paid.
\par
II. \textbf{PANELISTS' COMMENTS ON }PART I
\par
OTTO A. DAVIS: When I read this paper I was struck by the authors' com-
\par
ment that Adam Smith had characterized the settlement of dispute as one of
\par
the functions of government. I had never really thought about this particular
\par
problem and had always accepted Smith's characterization. After all, he
\par
seems to a mere economist to have been correct in so many matters that there
\par
was no reason to question this one. The point is that I must not have looked
\par
at the world in a very careful empirical fashion, since clearly much of this
\par
business is done privately. Indeed, some of my friends make their living by
\par
settling disputes but I had. never connected that activity with Smith's state-
\par
ment.
\par
The authors distinguished very carefully, I think, between rule creation
\par
and the resolution of disputes. I don't have a big quarrel with them, al-
\par
though I thought that they gave short shrift to the process of private rule
\par
creation. I detected a statement which indicated that they did not really
\par
expect there to be much of a role for rule creation by private judges. It is true
\par
that rule creation does not seem to be a big part of private arbitration. It
\par
seemed to me, however, that the reasons they gave were not very convinc-
\par
ing. They thought there would be a great, bewildering, confusing mass of
\par
precedent produced. They also claimed that one could not really create
\par
property rights in precedent. Both reasons seemed to me to miss the essential
\par
point. If we consider the incentives of an arbitrator who is concerned about
\par
the creation of rules, then we see that he really has two conflicting incen-
\par
tives.
\par
On the one hand, he has an incentive to try to enhance his reputation by
\par
the decisions that he makes, by trying to appear extremely fair, extremely
\par
reasoned, and so forth. On the other hand, it might not escape his attention
\par
that, in his role as a rule creator, really clear statements might mean less
\par
business for his profession in the future. So, he has a conflict of interest in
\par
these two desires. It is not clear to me which one would prevail. I might note,
\par
however, this is not unlike a situation that is often faced in markets. A
\par
retailer, for example, might have an incentive to take advantage of a cus-
\par
tomer who is not informed. This desire conflicts with his other desire to have
\par
a reputation as being a good, honest merchant.
\par
Let me turn to the resolution of disputes. The authors maintain, and I
\par
tend to agree, that the private adjudication of disputes is much more likely to
\par
take place when there is an existing contractual relationship or when there isTHE \textbf{JOURNAL }OF \textbf{LEGAL }STUDIES
\par
some kind of sanction to assure that adjudication can occur. They go
\par
through a very careful analysis of the incentives of two parties to a dispute to
\par
choose an arbitrator and point out the incentives that each has. The person
\par
who thinks he might lose can easily put up a smoke screen; it's very difficult
\par
to see how the method might work, unless there is some contractual relation-
\par
ship. I found this interesting, especially their description of the American
\par
Arbitration Association and the role that this private association plays in
\par
getting around this part of the process in private adjudication.
\par
I talked to some people in the field of labor arbitration. The association
\par
provides a list and the two parties in the dispute have the right to strike
\par
names from the list. They can rule out people that might be selected to be an
\par
arbitrator. My friends in the profession told me that the process selects the
\par
least disliked person to serve as the arbitrator.
\par
It's important to look at the incentives that the arbitrator has. After all,
\par
many of them make their living in this activity. They are essentially people
\par
in the business of providing private adjudication services. One would expect
\par
that the arbitrator would have an incentive to never appear as, say, pro-
\par
management or prolabor, because that would always insure that his name
\par
was struck from the list and his livelihood would be affected. He has a great
\par
incentive to try to appear to be a reasonable kind of person. The selection
\par
mechanism, \textbf{I }think, probably is a great incentive to the arbitrator to provide
\par
a sense of fairness and reason in his decisions.
\par
I was also interested in whether there might be monopoly in this mecha-
\par
nism itself, since, after all, this is one kind of private association. So I asked
\par
my friends about the process of becoming an arbitrator. I must admit that I
\par
did not receive a clear description of the process. They said they did not even
\par
know how they became arbitrators. There may have been some self-
\par
protection in their comments. You do have to have a reputation in order to
\par
get cases to arbitrate but you have to have decided some cases in order to get
\par
a reputation. So there is an inherent dilemma there.
\par
ARTHUR \textbf{DENZAU: }I had some difficulties with the terminology in Part I.
\par
The most problematic word that I found was the word "private." I just want
\par
to explain a little bit about what caused me problems. A "private good" is
\par
