\par
of the reasons that you can have feasible adjudication within a family is that\textbf{DISCUSSION }BY \textbf{SEMINAR }PARTICIPANTS
\par
there are elements of compulsion to submit to the jurisdiction of the family.
\par
The head of the family can impose costs on the other party. Under Roman
\par
law the head of the family had the power of life and death over his wife and
\par
children. This obviously gave him the power to force them to submit to his
\par
adjudication.
\par
Even in a modern family, there are sticks and carrots which the parents
\par
can use to force resolution of disputes among the children. Because the group
\par
is such a small one, there isn't the problem of multiple inconsistent rules that
\par
would make a completely private system tend to break down in a larger
\par
community.
\par
Professor Carrington's comments illustrated most sharply the "two cul-
\par
tures" problem. It is not that Professor Carrington belongs to a different
\par
culture which has moral premises and the rest of us don't. It's that he does
\par
not see the identity between what he regards as moral premises and what we
\par
would call economic premises.
\par
He says first that coercion is a bad thing. That identified Professor Car-
\par
rington as a sturdy libertarian, a person who, I assume, is unalterably
\par
opposed to the use of taxation to support any public function (possibly
\par
excepting national defense), since taxation is a coercive method of providing
\par
a service. To the economist, coercion is undesirable (inefficient) except where
\par
necessary in order to make a consensual, noncoercive market system func-
\par
tion. I therefore think that his premise could be restated in economic terms.
\par
He alluded to the danger of the "monopoly of power"-that is, the
\par
monopoly of political power. An economist, too, would regard this as a
\par
particularly costly form of monopoly, for it would be a situation in which
\par
some person could use the power of the state to transfer the wealth of the
\par
society to himself.
\par
He next said that litigation is a waste and an unpleasant method of regula-
\par
tion, thus inciting a search for substitutes. The economist recognizes that
\par
litigation is highly costly and observes that most legal disputes never even
\par
rise to the level of hiring lawyers. Carrington's notion that there is a social
\par
interest in finding less costly methods of dispute resolution and litigation is
\par
thus congenial to economic analysis.
\par
Returning to his first point, Carrington stressed the social superiority of
\par
consensual resolution of disputes to resolution imposed by third parties.
\par
Again, that is the economist's preference. The results of voluntary agreement
\par
are the only dependable evidence of increased welfare, because two parties
\par
will not agree to a trade unless it makes them both better off.
\par
Where Professor Carrington began to deviate from the economic way of
\par
looking at things wasn't in his list of moral premises. It was in his insistence
\par
that the only things worth talking about are normative questions. Simply
\par
trying to see how the legal system operates is not very interesting, he sug-
\par
gested, at least not to someone who is concerned with reform. What I thinkTHE \textbf{JOURNAL }OF \textbf{LEGAL STUDIES}
\par
this overlooks is that you can't have durable and intelligent reform unless it
\par
is based on some knowledge about how the system operates.
\par
Thus I was surprised that Professor Carrington should be uninterested in
\par
the possible information relevant for judicial-reform purposes that might be
\par
obtained from a study of arbitration. Of course there is a difference between
\par
the cases that go to arbitration and the cases that are tried in court, but he
\par
greatly exaggerated the difference when he said that arbitration is a proce-
\par
dure resorted to by people who are in a relationship of mutual trust. Well, if
\par
you have ever dealt with an architect, you have signed a form contract
\par
which provides that in the event of a dispute the matter will be arbitrated in
\par
accordance with the rules of the American Institute of Architects. I don't
\par
think one signs that contract because one is in a relationship of greater
\par
mutual trust with the architect than that which obtains between parties to a
\par
contract that does not contain an arbitration clause. There are, then, sets of
\par
transactions, some of which go to arbitration, some to court, depending on
\par
whether or not there is an arbitration clause in the contract, which, though
\par
there may be differences between them, are highly similar samples of dis-
\par
putes. The methods used in arbitration are relevant at least to the ordinary
\par
commercial-contract litigation in the courts.
\par
As we pointed out in our paper and as Professor Denzau pointed out, you
\par
can't assume that arbitration is more efficient just because it seems to be
\par
growing relative to litigation. As both we and Denzau pointed out, there
\par
may simply be a long court queue: an unnecessary cost of litigation that is
\par
forcing people to turn to an inferior substitute. Nevertheless, with free entry
\par
into arbitration, as there is, you would expect a tendency to evolve efficient
\par
rules of arbitration. So I think there is something to be learned from arbitra-
\par
tion and Professor Carrington's dismissal of even the potential relevance of
\par
these data suggests a desire to reform the judicial system in a vacuum,
\par
without looking for empirical data where they can be found.
\par
WILLIAM M. \textbf{LANDES: }Professor Davis asked whether private arbitrators
\par
might have conflicting incentives. On the one hand, an arbitrator has an
\par
incentive to establish a reputation or brand name in order to make his
\par
services more attractive than those of other arbitrators and therefore in-
\par
crease the demand for his services. On the other hand, if he develops unclear
\par
rules, he will be able to increase the demand for arbitration or adjudication
\par
in general. This conflict of interest does not occur if, as Professor Denzau
\par
pointed out, you have competition among arbitrators. It's very much like a
\par
competitive firm that has no incentive to reduce its output on its own as a
\par
way of cartelizing the industry because the benefits primarily accrue to other
\par
firms in the industry. Cartelization requires that all firms jointly cut back
\par
their output. There is the same problem with arbitrators. There is an enor-
\par
mous free-rider problem if a single arbitrator, among a group of competitive
\par
arbitrators, attempts to make rules more confusing in order to increase\textbf{DISCUSSION BY }SEMINAR PARTICIPANTS
\par
demand. However, under conditions of collusion or monopoly, there may be
\par
incentives for arbitrators-or if adjudication was a public monopoly, a pub-
\par
lic judiciary-to make rules confusing in order to increase demand. The
\par
argument of our paper was that, if you were a monopolist, you could in-
\par
crease the demand for judicial services if you made rules confusing.
\par
Professor Denzau noted that in the usual analysis of monopoly a
\par
monopolist will not make a less durable commodity. Suppose it was cheaper
\par
for a monopolist to make a product that would last forever. He can still
\par
capture the return on this durability in the sale to the first user because the
\par
amount the first user is willing to pay will depend on the amount the second
\par
user is willing to pay for it; and, of course, the amount the second user is
\par
willing to pay will depend on the amount the third user is willing to pay, and
\par
so forth. In this way the initial seller can capture all future returns over \emph{n}
\par
periods by his sale in the first period. That would be a profit-maximizing
\par
strategy, and hence there would be no incentive for the monopolist to make a
\par
product that lasts one year and then in the second period make a product
\par
that lasts one year, and so forth.
\par
But you're not really able to do that with legal rules. Consider the example
\par
where the optimal solution is to have an infinitely durable legal rule. If you
\par
could sell that rule to the first user who in turn could establish property
\par
rights to it and sell it to the second user and so on, Professor Denzau is
\par
correct-you would then have an incentive to make a rule that lasts forever.
\par
But suppose that the only way judges are able to collect revenue, even where
\par
there is a monopoly, is through a fee system. That is, society can't establish
\par
property rights for rules and sell them. Then there would be an incentive for
\par
a monopolist to increase the degree of confusion or uncertainty of legal rules.
\par
Again, it's somewhat ambiguous, because there are offsets that depend on
\par
the degree to which people can substitute away from the monopolist to other
\par
forms of dispute resolution.
