\par
at forty-nine or fifty-one miles an hour, but clearly he was not driving
\par
fifty-five. In a system in which after the accident the parties get to shop
\par
among arbitrators and both have to agree to the choice of the arbitrator,
\par
there isn't any way in which the arbitrator with the stiffer standard is going
\par
to get that business. One way of solving the problem would be to assign
\par
arbitrators randomly, but if you did that you would perpetuate the inconsis-
\par
tent rules. If you have the fifty-five-mile-an-hour arbitrator you would be
\par
subject to one rule, if the fifty-mile-an-hour arbitrator to another. It's very
\par
hard to imagine a competitive process by which one of these rules would
\par
prevail.
\par
