\par
many ways of advertising their competence, insofar as a standard was con-
\par
cerned, they would just say "we decide cases fairly and reasonably." Because
\par
once they started particularizing their standards, that is, devising precise,
\par
specific rules, they would automatically exclude themselves from large parts
\par
of the market. The clearer the rules, the clearer it is that one of the parties is
\par
going to lose, and that party will never consent to the arbitration. The only
\par
way you can get around that is to randomize or impose some mechanical rule
\par
of assigning an arbitrator to a party. If you do that, you will perpetuate any
