more formally the analysis.
\par
Equation \textbf{(1) }defines the probability that the plaintiff will prevail as a
\par
function of the precedents in favor of the plaintiff relative to the defendant
\par
and the resource inputs of the two parties. If you like, Equation \textbf{(1) }is the
\par
dispute-resolution function of legal precedents. Figure \textbf{1, }on the other hand,
\par
summarizes the impact of precedents in providing information on the ex-
\par
pected outcome of similar disputes in the future, and thereby affecting future
\par
resource allocation. That is our S function. We've assumed that \emph{A }is the
\par
more expensive cost avoider and \emph{B }is the cheaper cost avoider, and therefore
\par
as it becomes more likely that B will be held liable, the social cost (or \textbf{\emph{S) }}from
\par
the particular activity in which \emph{A }and \emph{B }are involved would tend to di-
\par
minish. S then refers to the future costs of all participants in activityj, where
\par
those costs include whether or not the parties participate in the activity. The
\par
likelihood of \emph{B }being liable affects the incentives for the parties to participate
\par
in the activity, the level of their participation, the amount of resources they
\par
devote to accident avoidance and reducing damages, and the total number of
\par
accidents. Those costs are all subsumed in the S function since they depend
\par
on the probability that \emph{B }will be held liable.
\par
First we start with the case where no contractual relationship exists be-
\par
tween the parties and work out the trial-settlement choice. We consider the
\par
parties' decision to go to trial or settle and then examine whether there is
\par
going to be more or less litigation when an efficient rule governs the dispute
\par
in question compared to an inefficient rule, and how over time the stock of
\par
efficient and inefficient rules change. If there is no contractual relationship
\par
between the parties and the parties have no future stakes, then as long as the
\par
parties agree on the expected outcome and it costs something to go to trial,
\par
going to trial is equivalent to an unfair gamble; you would never observe a
\par
trial. The more interesting case, and the one that Paul Rubin focuses on, is
\par
where the parties have substantial and symmetrical ongoing interests in the
\par
activity. That is, they expect to be involved in the activity in the future.
\par
Their future stakes are substantial and thus they have some non-negligible
\par
interest in the particular rule or precedent that is established in this particu-
\par
lar case.
\par
The major difference between Rubin's work and ours is that we take into
\par
account the fact that the litigant who wins shifts the precedents somewhat in
\par
his favor so there is a gain to him. If the plaintiff wins, precedent shifts soDISCUSSION \textbf{BY }SEMINAR PARTICIPANTS
\par
that it is more likely \emph{B }is going to be liable in the future and therefore there is
\par
a slight reduction in the cost to \emph{A }of being involved in this activity. But
\par
alternatively if \emph{A }loses, the precedents shift toward \emph{B }and there is a gain to \emph{B}
\par
and loss to \emph{A. }In principle both \emph{A }and \emph{B }have to take account of this
\par
possibility. \emph{A, }if he wins, tends to make things better for himself in the
\par
future. If he loses, he tends to make things worse for himself. Whereas if \emph{B}
\par
wins, \emph{B }will tend to make things better for himself in the future in terms of
\par
lower future costs. If B loses, \emph{B }will tend to make things worse for himself. If
\par
the parties have symmetrical stakes in the future and assuming it is more
\par
efficient to hold \emph{B }liable, we show that if \textbf{\emph{A }}wins and the precedents shift
\par
toward A there will be a bigger reduction in total costs (S) than if B wins and
\par
the precedent shift toward B. That is, there is a net gain to both parties if
\par
precedents shift toward \emph{A.}
\par
It turns out that a necessary (but not sufficient) condition for a trial to take
\par
place is that the probability of A winning be greater than \textbf{.5. }But a probabil-
\par
ity greater than .5 implies that on balance the rule is already efficient. The
\par
precedents are such that prior to the litigation they favor \emph{A }relative to \emph{B.}
\par
What we find, therefore, is that the cases that will be litigated are those for
\par
which the existing legal rules on balance tend to favor efficiency. Since
\par
judges are following precedent, this means that over time parties like \emph{A }will
\par
tend to win and precedents will gradually accumulate more and more in
\par
favor of \emph{A. }In this set of circumstances the rule will gradually become more
\par
efficient. If, however, the probability is less than .5, the rule is inefficient
\par
(i.e., it favors the cheaper cost avoider) and there is no incentive for litiga-
\par
tion. So we get the peculiar result that efficient rules will tend to be reliti-
\par
gated and become more efficient, while inefficient rules, in effect, are not
\par
relitigated and will become dormant. Thus, if you examine a set of appellate
\par
opinions, you might infer that legal rules are efficient but this is because of a
\par
systematic bias in the set of appellate cases. Our result is more qualified than
\par
that of Rubin about the survival properties of efficient rules over time.
\par
Now let me raise two points. The first was brought to my attention by
\par
Mitch Polinsky. In our analysis we show a rising probability over time. That
\par
is, if you start out where the precedents favor \emph{A }relative to \emph{B }and \emph{.4 }tends to
\par
win more cases than \emph{B, }gradually over time the precedents build up in \emph{A's}
\par
favor and the legal rule becomes more and more efficient. Suppose, how-
\par
ever, this never reaches the limit of \emph{B }being held liable with a probability of
\par
one. Suppose also we have an infinitely long period. Since the probability of
\par
\emph{B }being held liable never goes to one, if you have an infinitely long period
\par
there is some finite probability that in fact \emph{B }is going to win. If you are given
\par
a long enough period, you are always going to put together a string of cases
\par
where \emph{B }will win, say twelve cases, even though the probability was stacked
\par
against B-type persons. If that happens, the precedents then switch over and
\par
may yield a probability of less than \textbf{.5 }and you are back then to the case of aTHE \textbf{JOURNAL }OF \textbf{LEGAL }STUDIES
\par
dormant but inefficient rule. It is a peculiar result; you may move all the way
\par
to efficiency, but as long as there is some finite probability that the decision
\par
will favor inefficiency eventually the inefficient rule will survive.
\par
George Priest suggested a second problem which does not make matters
\par
good for Priest or Rubin nor for the notion that there is a tendency over time
\par
for the common law to become more efficient. Priest raised the following
\par
point. Suppose we start out with a case where \emph{B }is going to be held liable
\par
with the probability of .9. Implicit in our analysis of marginal changes is that
\par
if \emph{A }wins that will shift the probability from, say, .90 to .91 in the future. If
\par
\emph{B }wins, the next case will just shift it back to .90. So if \emph{A }and \emph{B }get ten cases
\par
and A wins five and B wins five and we start out with the probability of .90,
\par
there would be no change in the degree to which precedent favored \emph{A }to \emph{B.}
\par
But on balance that seems a little odd because here you have a new set of
\par
circumstances-ten cases and they come out fifty-fifty. Shouldn't that in
\par
effect change the precedents and shift them in the direction of favoring \emph{B}
\par
from .10 to, say, \textbf{.15?}
\par
Or putting it another way, if you begin with the probability of \emph{A }winning
\par
of .90, so that the precedents are overwhelming in favor of A, then to shift
\par
those precedents up a little, say, to .95, you need a lot more cases in favor of
\par
\emph{A }relative to \emph{B }than you do to shift them down to, say, .85. If that is true,
\par
then there is absolutely no change in the relative stock of efficient and
\par
inefficient rules over time. I'm not sure, but I think the point has some
\par
validity. In rewriting our paper we certainly want to incorporate it; but if
\par
you take that extreme position, wherever you are today is where you will be
\par
tomorrow. That is, if precedents favor A relative to \emph{B }such that the probabil-
\par
ity of A winning is \textbf{.7, }then over time, as judges follow precedent, \emph{A }is going
\par
to win \textbf{70 }percent of the cases, \emph{B }is going to win 30 percent and you are just
\par
going to stay right where you are. There would not be any tendency to move
\par
over time to a more favorable set of precedents in favor of \emph{A }relative to \emph{B.}
\par
Going back to our original formulation, there are some other interesting
\par
results. One is that the outcome does not depend upon whether you are
\par
identified with being a member of the plaintiff or defendant class in the
\par
future, as long as you have some future stake; you don't have to be always
\par
the plaintiff or always the defendant for the results to hold. In addition there
\par
are some slight differences in the case where the parties have asymmetrical
\par
stakes. In Rubin's paper if party \emph{A }has much stronger and more important
\par
stakes in the future than party B, the precedents will tend over time to favor
\par
\emph{A }relative to \emph{B; }whereas in our analysis it doesn't quite work that way. Even
\par
though \emph{A }stands to gain something because he has a future stake, \emph{A }also
\par
stands to lose something if the precedents work against him because of his
\par
future stakes. So it's a gamble. \emph{A }has to consider how much he is going to
\par
gain and how much he is going to lose; there is a symmetrical problem. It
\par
turns out that the critical point determining whether \textbf{\emph{A }}goes forward andDISCUSSION BY SEMINAR PARTICIPANTS
\par
litigates depends upon the probability in favor of \textbf{\emph{A }}being greater than .5.
\par
That is, it has to be better than a fair gamble for \textbf{\emph{A }}to go ahead with it.
\par
Similarly if B is the dominant party, the probability has to be greater than .5
\par
that \emph{B }wins. Thus, the rule tends to move with the dominant party only
