1. Abstract
\par
It is often rightly stressed that research workers should learn how to sell their
\par
work to managers, by avoiding technical jargon, and by otherwise acquiring skill
\par
in exposition. But managers often take a step towards the research men by
\par
learning a little science. I wish to advocate that managers should learn to use a
\par
little of the language of the theory of rationality, to make judgments of in-
\par
equalities between probabilities and between utilities, and to understand what
\par
it means to be reasonably consistent. In my opinion the consequences would be
\par
a great increase in the value of operational research.
\par
A brief description of what I mean by rationality is followed by a tentative
\par
definition of "degrees of rationality". Here it seems to be necessary to make use
\par
of the notion of rationality of "types I and II". The remainder of Section 3 is
\par
concerned with an elaboration of some aspects of rationality that seem to me to
\par
be important, and either somewhat novel or not often enough emphasized in
\par
print. In Sections 4 and 5 some of the benefits and difficulties associated with the
\par
use of the theory are mentioned.
\par
2. Clarification of Title
\par
In the title of this paper the word "should" is intended to be interpreted in
\par
relation to the aims of the manager's organization rather than those of the
\par
manager himself. A good manager will have a clear understanding of the aims of
\par
his organization, and of the need to modify those aims when circumstances de-
\par
mand it.
\par
In asking how rational a manager should be, I mean both in his own de-
\par
cisions and also in his statements made to other people.
\par
