3.3 Definition of a Decision and a Conclusion
\par
Tukey [28] argued that, in addition to a decision theory, one needs a conclusion
\par
theory. But a theory of rationality is supposed to encompass them both: it is
\par
just as much a theory of conclusions as a theory of decisions and of rational
\par
actions.
\par
Tukey also complained that the works on decision theory do not define what
\par
is meant by a decision. For the sake of philosophical clarity I should like to meet
\par
this challenge and to propose a definition, especially as it has some effect on the
\par
theory of rationality. Roughly speaking, a decision is a mental event that causes
\par
an act; more precisely a decision is a conscious mental event that has a tendency
\par
to cause either an act or to cause another mental event to belong to some class rather
\par
than to another class. It is possible to assign a numerical measure to the tend-
\par
ency of an event F to cause an event E, and it is also possible to assign a meas-
\par
ure to the "change of mind" in a mental event F with respect to a class of mu-
\par
tually exclusive acts [10, 12].
\par
A conclussion is a decision to accept a hypothesis at least provisionally, where
\par
the word "hypothesis" is to be understood in a very general sense. In the par-
\par
ticular case when the hypothesis is that one course of action is better than another
\par
one, then the conclusion is liable to lead at once to a decision that is not a con-
\par
clusion, namely the decision to perform the preferable course of action. But some-
\par
times we do not have the courage of our convictions.
\par
