\par
Not all mental events are caused immediately and primarily by a conscious
\par
mental event; for if they were we could prove by backward induction that we
\par
must be conscious all the time, including before we were conceived. Our de-
\par
cisions are necessarily determined in part by unconscious mental events, by
\par
physiological events and by external forces; and it is impossible to make con-
\par
scious use of the theory of rationality for all our decisions, before the decisions
\par
are made.
\par
Whether or not our decisions have been thought out in terms of the theory of
\par
rationality, we can often learn something by analyzing our past decisions to see
\par
how consistent they were. The analysis is known as a "post-mortem."
