rational. What we want is that the utility, U, of our body of judgments should
\par
be as large as possible. Making U/V near to 1 is only one of the ways of making
\par
U large. Another method is to collect new kinds of information. But if we are
\par
confining our attention to what can be achieved with reasoning rather than by
\par
new experiments it is clear that there are only two ways of increasing U. One
\par
is to delete and modify judgments already in 3, and the other is to add new
\par
judgments to Q4. Once again a judgment of type II is required in order to decide
\par
between these two methods at any given moment.
\par
The type II principle of rationality is the recommendation to try to maximize
\par
the expected utility of your whole body of judgments at any time, allowing for
\par
the cost of trying. Part of the technique of doing this is to use the type I principle,
\par
which is that you should try to be reasonably consistent. The type II prin-
\par
ciple is the more important of the two. Implicitly everybody uses both types of
\par
rationality to some extent, but I think that a conscious appreciation of the dis-
\par
tinction between them will resolve many of the controversies in the foundations of
\par
statistics.
\par
The notion of associating a utility with a body of judgments can be applied to
\par
the special case of a single probability judgment. It is of zero utility to judge that
\par
a probability lies between 0 and 1, and clearly the utility of any probability
\par
judgment must depend on its precision as well as on other things. It is in the
\par
spirit of this paper to try to assign a definite numerical value to the precision
\par
of a probability judgment, but since the attempt is not very successful I have
\par
relegated it to an appendix.
\par
