\par
3.6 Kinds of Judgments
\par
In the above discussion of rationality there has been emphasis on judgments of
\par
inequalities between probabilities, between utilities, and between expected
\par
utilities. Although these are the most important kinds of judgments it would be
\par
misleading to leave it at that, so I shall briefly mention a few other kinds in
\par
order to emphasize their variety. (Most of the following list is quoted from an
\par
unpublished lecture given at Seattle in 1956.)
\par
Judgments about pure mathematics, for example that an asymptotic result
\par
gives a reasonable approximation for a sample of finite size.
\par
Judgment that it is reasonable on some specific occasion to show interest in
\par
a non-null hypothesis when some statistic has attained say the 5 percent level.
\par
Judgment that one can, at least for some purposes, accept the null hypothesis
\par
that certain random sampling numbers really are random.
\par
Judgment that the assumption of a normal distribution is adequate.
\par
Judgment that one hypothesis is initially more probable than another one
\par
because it is simpler, and also that it is of higher utility if approximately true.
\par
Judgment that a smoothing of a certain set of data replaces the observations
\par
by values that are closer to the expected values given the true hypothesis, with-
\par
out actually specifying the true hypothesis.
\par
Judgment that, after a confidence interval has been found, one can state that
\par
the value of a parameter lies in this interval without losing one's job as a statis-
\par
tician, in which case there would not be a long run in which to be right 95 per
\par
cent of the time.
\par
Judgment of what to do about a fiducial probability.
\par
Inequality judgment concerning a weight of evidence, i.e. the logarithm of a
\par
Bayes factor, in favour of the odds of a hypothesis.
\par
Judgment of whether to pronounce a man guilty, when serving on a jury.
\par
Judgment of how hard to try to be rational and how rational to try to be.
\par
This list is enough to show the variety of kinds of judgments.
\par
What the theory of rationality does is not to reduce the variety of judgments
\par
that should be made, but to shift the emphasis to certain kinds of judgment: to
\par
change the frequency distribution of the different kinds of judgment, and to
\par
demand a reasonable measure of consistency with the theory of subjective
\par
probability and utility.
\par
This brings one to the question of how to make judgments.
\par
3.7 How to Make Judgments
\par
The question of how to make judgments is largely answered by means of a
\par
list of kinds of judgments that are useful. The list I just gave could be extended
\par
a great deal and then used as a check list. The question is also related to why
\par
there is any need for a theory, and I should like to consider this question briefly
\par
first.
\par
3.7.1 Why Theorize? If we agree that all kinds of judgments can be used, why
\par
not simply judge the probabilities at the end of an experiment, and thereby avoid
\par
all calculations and theory? I shall give three answers to this question. First,
\par
our judgments of the initial probabilities might be more precise (in the spirit of
\par
This content downloaded from
\par
79.248.9.248 on Thu, 16 May 2024 06:46:36 +00:00
\par
All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms388 I. J. GOOD
\par
the second definition of the Appendix) than those of the final probabilities
\par
(though they may well be much less precise according to the first definition).
\par
Second, the judgments of the initial and final probabilities may be in contradic-
\par
tion. We cannot see whether they are without applying the theory of probability.
\par
If they are we should think again. Third, there are other kinds of judgment,
\par
and we can use the theory in order to draw deductions from them, and to check
\par
them for consistency.
\par
A trick for aiding the judgment, especially of initial probabilities, which I
\par
have often found useful during the last sixteen years is the device of imaginary
\par
results [3, pp. 35, 70)].
\par
8.7.2 The Device of Imaginary Results: The Backwards Use of Bayes' Theorem.
\par
A direct judgment of final probabilities after an actual experiment may be
\par
difficult. Instead we can imagine, preferably in advance of making the observa-
\par
tions, that the observations may have some very simple special values. A fa-
\par
vourite example of mine is a method of judging a lower bound for the initial
\par
probability that a man has telepathic powers. We can imagine his guessing
\par
twenty decimal digits in succession. If we would then regard the hypothesis as
\par
"odds on," then, by Bayes' theorem used backwards, the initial probability
\par
must, for consistency, be taken as at least 10-20. We can then use Bayes' theorem
\par
(forwards) in order to obtain a lower bound on the final probability in the light
\par
of some actual experiment.
\par
The device of imaginary results can also be used for making decisions. Some
\par
people find it useful to imagine what is the best and worst that can reasonably
\par
happen corresponding to the available decisions. An example of this approach
\par
is Shackle's theory of "focus outcomes", for describing the psychology of busi-
\par
ness decisions [24], but this theory does not use Bayes' theorem since the expres-
\par
sion "can reasonably happen" is not given any quantitative interpretation.
\par
A further device for making judgments is the use of probabilities of higher
\par
type, i.e. probabilities that judgments are consistent with the "true credibilities."
\par
These rather intangible probabilities can be used unofficially in order to help
\par
you to formulate your ordinary (type I) judgments, and also in other ways.2
\par
3.8 When to Make Decisions
\par
I referred earlier to the fact that one kind of judgment is the estimation of the
\par
cost of theorizing or thinking. This kind of judgment is required when deciding
\par
when to come to a decision. Putting it roughly the right time is when informa-
\par
tion is coming in slowly. More precisely, a decision should be made at a moment
\par
when the expected loss from postponing the decision exceeds the expected gain
\par
from further investigation. (If this suggestion seems obvious, so much the
\par
better.) Here the word "investigation" covers both the collection of new sta-
\par
tistics and the working out of deductions from previous evidence. This sugges-
\par
tion is not intended to imply that the decision can be made entirely by con-
\par
2 This theme was developed in more detail in lectures in Princeton and Chicago in 1955.
\par
See also [4 and 6].
\par
