79.248.9.248 on Thu, 16 May 2024 06:46:36 +00:00
\par
All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/termsHOW RATIONAL SHOULD A MANAGER BE? 389
\par
scious thinking (see Section 3.4 above), and the familiar recommendations to
\par
sleep on a problem or to take a holiday might be of equal value.
\par
The main difficulty in applying the suggestion is the great vagueness of many
\par
estimates of expected utility. It sometimes seems that the more important a
\par
decision the vaguer are our estimates. If the decisions of managers are difficult
\par
then those of "top" managers might sometimes be so difficult that they might as
\par
well decide by spinning coins, or by consulting oracles.
\par
A few benefits of rationality will now be discussed, and finally a further class
\par
of difficulties.
\par
4. Benefits of Rationality
\par
4.1 Psychological Benefits
\par
The main disadvantage of relying entirely on intuition is that, although the
\par
unconscious mind is powerful and useful, it can also play tricks on us. It can even
\par
act as if it were a conscious opponent in some people. (Perhaps these people
\par
should use the theory of games! A similar suggestion was made by W. R. Ashby,
\par
who proposed that a psychiatrist can regard his patient as an opponent.) If a
\par
man were psychologically completely healthy he would have less need of logic,
\par
because his judgments would be free from irrational bias and would be more con-
\par
sistent than those of a neurotic man. But a theory of rationality will always be
\par
required for complicated decisions and for the purpose of justifying one's de-
\par
cisions to other people.
\par
4.2 Educational Benefit
\par
The educational benefit of a theory of rationality is that it is connected with
\par
all our reasoning processes, and especially because it tends to unify various
\par
disciplines, such as mathematics, statistics, physics, economics, and philosophy.
\par
4.3 Benefits of Formalism
\par
The most convenient way of expressing the theory of rationality of type I is
\par
with the help of a formal set of axioms and rules of application. The formalism
\par
is valid even on those occasions when the theory is to be used informally. The
\par
mere attempt to apply a formal theory realistically to a practical problem tends
\par
to bring out the salient features. If the judgments are precise enough a fairly
\par
deep mathematical analysis becomes possible, and if the judgments are vague but
\par
there are enough of them to make the practical situation complicated, then the
\par
formal approach is liable to lead to qualitative clarification.
\par
4.4 Benefit to Liaison with Operational Research Workers
\par
We can hardly expect a top manager to make much use of a formal theory:
\par
he has to leave the details to his operational researchers. With luck, the work of
\par
the research men will make clear what is the right decision, or it may show that
\par
the decision is more difficult than was previously thought. Especially in the mar-
\par
ginal cases, and in those cases where the manager's own opinion is in conflict
\par
with those of his research men, it is important that the liaison between them
\par
This content downloaded from
\par
79.248.9.248 on Thu, 16 May 2024 06:46:36 +00:00
\par
All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/terms390 I. J. GOOD
\par
should be good. It is often emphasized that scientists should learn to talk in
\par
language that managers can understand. What is sometimes overlooked is that
\par
managers should learn some of the vocabulary of the scientist, plus the meanings
\par
of the words, i.e. how to use them in simple situations.
\par
As an example, consider certain provisioning problems. It is sometimes diffi-
\par
cult to estimate the costs of shortages, and consequently people have sometimes
\par
been satisfied with policies that "dominate" previously used policies. (See, for
\par
example, Thatcher [27].) These dominating policies lead to fewer shortages and
\par
also to a smaller value of the stock that has to be stored, in comparison with
\par
some given "dominated" policy. An "optimal" policy might well lead to a great
\par
increase in the savings made, if only it could be found.
\par
In order to get some idea of what the losses due to shortages are, the statisti-
\par
cian could present the manager with a list of questions such as:
\par
"Would you be prepared to pay an extra ?50,000 or ?100,000 to avoid such-
\par
and-such imaginary shortages?"
\par
"Last year you decided to save ?100,000 in working stock when you esti-
\par
mated that this would involve such-and-such additional shortages. Would you
\par
stand by this decision had the shortages been what you expected them to be?"
\par
At present the reaction of many managers to such questions would be "Your
\par
questions are merely hypothetical." But the point here is that hypothetical
\par
questions are relevant. I think that good managers will realize that there is
\par
scope for imagination, in other words for the consideration of hypothetical
\par
questions. The manager who has understood the device of imaginary results
\par
will appreciate their necessity.
\par
4.5 Benefit to Smooth Organization
\par
Will the manager who has understood the theory of rationality find it easier
\par
to run an organization smoothly without too much planning? I think the answer
\par
is probably yes, but I shall discuss only one aspect of the matter: what may be
\par
called the law of signs. It is that public and private utilities should agree in sign.
\par
It is important to hold in mind that financial incentives alone do not determine
\par
utilities: power and prestige may be equally important. Hence correct assigning
\par
of credit and blame should be encouraged although, like most things, it can be
\par
overdone.
\par
An application of the law of signs is to the problem of how to encourage an
\par
expert to make good probability judgments. For example, the expert might be
\par
asked to estimate the probability that some invention will work, or he might be a
\par
weather, stock market, or sports forecaster. This problem was considered by
\par
Good [4], McCarthy [19], and Marschak [18]. The basic idea was to pay the
\par
expert in such a way that his maximum expected benefit occurs if his estimates
\par
agree with the credibilities, and also to encourage him to collect new evidence.
\par
5. The Need to Suppress Information: a Class of Difficulties
\par
in Being Rational
\par
Part of the idea of rationality is explicitness of reasoning, but in communica-
\par
tion between people, and consequently even within a single person, there are
\par
This content downloaded from
\par
79.248.9.248 on Thu, 16 May 2024 06:46:36 +00:00
\par
All use subject to https://about.jstor.org/termsHOW RATIONAL SHOULD A MANAGER BE? 391
\par
some subjects that are more or less taboo. Examples are the detailed discussion
\par
of power, prestige, pay, and motivation. Motivations of customers have received
\par
a fair amount of attention: we require an equally intense study of the motiva-
\par
tions of people in organizations themselves, including those of managers and
\par
operational researchers. Such studies, which should be as impersonal as pos-
\par
sible, should help to overcome the taboo difficulty to some extent.
\par
Likewise, some information is "company confidential." The hindrance here
\par
is in part to the liaison between the managers of a firm and its owners, the share-
\par
holders. This is a well known difficulty, which can prevent rational discussion at
\par
Annual General Meetings of a firm.
\par
The suppression of information is a subject intimately bound up with the
\par
foundations of statistics. Although on the face of it the suppression of informa-
\par
tion is a statistical crime, it is often useful in order to be able to make precise
\par
statements for the summarization of statistical observations. It is done when-
\par
ever randomization is used in the design of a statistical experiment. The ap-
\par
proximate irrelevance of the suppressed information requires honest judgment on
\par
the part of the statistician. In order to prevent himself from cheating he can
\par
even make use of a Statisticians' Stooge, who alone is allowed to know the
\par
suppressed information. (See Good [8].)
\par
One in effect suppresses information about reality whenever one decides to
\par
make use of a particular mathematical model, such as in linear planning ("pro-
\par
gramming"). One can regard such a decision as an application of rationality of
\par
type II, in that one may have judged that the expected loss of utility in adopting
\par
the model is small. It might also be just wishful thinking, irrationality of Type II
\par
as it were. (Compare Koopman [16], p. 424.)
\par
6. Summary
\par
To summarize, I maintain that the meaning of "rationality" which I have
\par
been using is an adequate clarification of the sense in which the word is normally
\par
used, and that its essentials could easily be understood by managers. These
\par
essentials are: the notions of subjective probability and utility; the principle of
\par
rationality (maximizing expected utility); the need to use inequalities in nearly
\par
all our judgments; the fact that a measure of formalizing is possible, but that
\par
vagueness has its place; a recognition that there are two types of rationality one
\par
of which means consistency;the willingness to consider hypothetical questions;
\par
and the recognition that there are degrees of rationality of type I. When a
\par
decision is urgent a lower degree of type I rationality is reasonable, and the
\par
manager may have to accept all the responsibility, but when there is more time
\par
he should seek the advice of his practical philosophers.
\par
It could be complained that many of my arguments are difficult or impossible
\par
to formalize in a really satisfactory way, but my purpose has been not to for-
\par
malize everything, only to describe rationality as best I can.
\par
