This article is presented in three parts. The first part describes briefly
\par
the existing dispute resolution structure and presents statistical data re-
\par
flecting its operation. The second part reports in narrative form the re-
\par
sults of informal interviews with a large number of individuals from
\par
widely-differing perspectives who have substantial experience in this
\par
field. The third part presents an analysis of the research data and iden-
\par
tifies the principles that should govern consideration of any change in
\par
the present dispute resolution structure. The article then presents an
\par
independent administrative tribunal model that illustrates how the
\par
present structure might be modified to create a more effective system.
\par
The article proposes that a pilot project along the lines of the model be
\par
undertaken to test the practicability of channeling appropriate cases
\par
from the now exclusive \emph{de novo }federal court process into a system tai-
\par
lored to the needs of access dispute resolution. Alternatively, the article
\par
proposes an ombudsman-based administrative structure to ameliorate
\par
some of the burdens of an exclusivelyjudicial dispute processing system
\par
while maintaining the availability of court review.
