For the purposes of this article, the most significant case processing
\par
statistic is the time interval from case filing to disposition. In the six-
\par
year, post-1979 period, the annual median processing time for all
\par
cases terminated in the district courts ranged from 7 to \textbf{10 }months;
\par
for the D.C. District Court it ranged from 6 to 8 months.' The time
\par
interval was shorter for cases that were terminated without court
\par
action. In these cases, which account for roughly 40 percent of the
\par
total cases, the annual median time for all courts ranged from 5 to 7
\par
months and for the D.C. District Court ranged from 4 to 6 months. 7
\par
Correspondingly, the time interval was longer in cases that received
\par
court action. The largest category of these cases-those terminated
\par
before pretrial-constituted approximately 50 percent of the total
\par
cases. In these cases, the median time for all courts ranged over the
\par
period from 8 to \textbf{II }months and for the D.C. District Court from 7
\par
to 9 months.4 8 Excluding the fastest and slowest 10 percent, the range
\par
of processing time for all FOIA cases, averaged from the annual
\par
ranges over the six-year period, was from 2 months to 27 months. 4'
\par
\textbf{As }the processing time statistics indicate, a high proportion of FOIA
\par
cases are settled or are decided on summary judgment. Of all cases
\par
terminated in the six-year period, 92 percent were terminated before
\par
pretrial, only 7 percent reached pretrial and barely 1 percent were
\par
tried.5" Some form of voluntary "dismissal" was taken in 52 percent
\par
of these cases and "judgment on motion" was entered in slightly over
\par
40 percent.5 I For cases not settled, disposition by summary judgment
\par
was the norm. Of those cases decided by judgment, in 12 percent
\par
judgment was for the plaintiff; in 82 percent judgment was for the
\par
defendant, and in 6 percent judgment was for both. 52 While the gov-
\par
ernment thus prevails in the vast majority of cases that reach judg-
\par
ment, the weaker government cases would tend to be more heavily
\par
represented in the large group of cases that are settled beforejudgment.
