D. Existing Non-Judicial Compliance Mechanisms
\par
Under FOIA, after final agency denial, the only formal mechanism
\par
for relief for a requester is federal district court action. Other less
\par
case-specific mechanisms for promoting agency compliance do, how-
\par
ever, exist. Their presence and operation become part of the milieu
\par
in which individual disputes are decided, and some awareness of their
\par
role is helpful in understanding the overall issues of this article.
\par
\emph{1. Congressional Committees}
\par
The committees of Congress with oversight responsibility in the
\par
FOIA area have traditionally taken a close interest in case processing
\par
at the agency level, apart from their more general concern with leg-
\par
islative policy matters in the access field.3" 3 The case processing reports
\par
required by the Act to be filed with Congress are maintained by the
\par
Subcommittee on Government Information, Justice and Agriculture
\par
of the House Committee on Government Operations and the Sub-
\par
committee on the Constitution of the Senate Judiciary Committee.
\par
The staffs of the committees review these reports and make follow-
\par
up inquiries to the agencies. These committees, as well as individual
\par
members of Congress, also receive case-specific complaints in the FOIA
\par
area, which are handled in a manner similar to other constituent
\par
inquiries. Through these and other more traditional oversight func-
\par
tions, the agencies are made aware that their overall case-processing
\par
activities are subject to continuing congressional scrutiny. Neverthe-
\par
less, the cases that remain in dispute after agency processing and
\par
become subject to litigation would rarely benefit directly from this
\par
process.
\par
\emph{2. Department of Justice}
\par
Such cases are more likely to be influenced by the work of the
\par
Department ofJustice. In part out of its general role as legal counselor
\par
to the agencies and in part out of its responsibility for defending
\par
agencies in FOIA litigation, Justice serves, to a limited degree, as a
\par
centralized compliance authority. 4 For over ten years, there existed
\par
within the department a Freedom of Information Committee made
\par
up of representatives from the counseling and litigating divisions.6 5
