\emph{2. New York}
\par
The New York Committee on Open Government stands in sharp
\par
procedural contrast to the Connecticut Commission. It has no inves-
\par
tigatory or adjudicatory power. Its function is exclusively advisory."'
\par
The Commission is required by statute to give any person (including
\par
state and local government officials) an advisory opinion regarding
\par
the state freedom of information law.""11 It can issue its opinion orally
\par
or in writing, and bases its opinion on the characterization of the
\par
circumstances given by the person requesting the opinion. ]''
\par
The advisory opinions operate, in effect, to place the Committee
\par
in an ombudsman role in access disputes. \textbf{'"' }The advisory opinion can
\par
from the outset influence the agency decision and, in cases in which
\par
the initial agency denial is appealed, the Committee is formally given
\par
an opportunity to comment on the appeal prior to the agency deci-
\par
sion."12 The Committee's opinions are also given weight by the courts
\par
in interpreting the freedom of information statute."', The influence
\par
of the Committee's opinions is further enhanced by their careful in-
\par
dexing and wide distribution." 4
\par
Some members of the Committee are appointed by the Governor,
\par
some by the Legislature, and some serve by virtue of governmental
\par
position.'105 The members of the Committee serve part-time and the
\par
staff of the Committee is headed by an Executive Director who serves
\par
at the pleasure of the Secretary of State.""' While the administration
\par
of the New York freedom of information law, like FOIA, is based
\par
primarily upon agency case processing and judicial review, the om-
\par
budsman role of the Committee significantly augments the dispute-
\par
resolution process. \textbf{'0 7}
