\emph{4. Applicalion to FOIA}
\par
A comprehensive study of the Connecticut and New York systems
\par
captures well the dilemma posed by the comparative method in this
\par
area:
\par
Application of the experiences of Connecticut and New York to the
\par
federal Freedom of Information Act requires caution. Freedom of infor-
\par
mation provisions and practices vary fIrom state to state and often differ
\par
significantly from the provisions and practices of the federal government.
\par
The size of the federal bureaucracy creates difticulties and prob)Ilems that
\par
have not confronted New York and Connecticut. The federal government
\par
is also more likely to acquire or create politically sensitive records. The
\par
states possess little information related to Iational security, and state cr \textbf{im-}
\par
inal law enforcement agencies are likely to be significantly smaller than
\par
their federal cottnterparts. Also, the regulatory emphasis of federal agen-
\par
cies is more likely to generate documents or records that contain financial
\par
or commercial data of third parties. Furthermore, the political setting in
\par
which a freedom of information law operates reflects the geography, econ-
\par
omy, history, and po)utlation \textbf{of }each state.
\par
Yet, while the differences between state and federal agencies sutggest
\par
caution, the similarities offer insights into the federal Freedom of Infor-
\par
mation Act. \textbf{17}
