B. Delay
\par
The importance of timely decisionmaking in information access
\par
disputes is a matter about which there is little disagreement. For the
\par
purposes of this article the important question is where and to what
\par
extent delay now occurs. Delay at the agency level has been a widely-
\par
discussed problem. 2 11 \textbf{It }is generally attributed to the number of re-
\par
quests received, the volume of records requested, the complexity of
\par
the issues involved in the requests, the extent of resources devoted
\par
to processing requests and sometimes to agency intransigence.' 2 1 De-
\par
lay at the litigation stage has received less attention. It is generally
\par
attributed to the factors that affect all federal court litigation--case-
\par
load and limited judicial resources.
\par
Delay at the agency level can affect any case and therefore is a more
\par
prominent concern than post-agency delay, which affects only the
\par
relatively small number of cases litigated. There was a clear consensus
\par
among the persons interviewed that while agency delay remained a
\par
fundamental problem in the administration of \textbf{FOIA, }it generally was
\par
not a problem amenable to treatment through the use of a separate
\par
administrative structure. \textbf{122 }Here, the most commonly made point was
\par
that the speed of case processing at the agency level is affected \textbf{pri-}
