This broad agreement as to the significance of the courts' role in
\par
the development of FOIA policy is tempered somewhat by a number
\par
of subsidiary considerations that taken together bear importantly on
\par
the question of the present quality of decision-making in this area.
\par
First, the expertise that has developed in the courts over the years is
\par
concentrated in the District of Columbia.144 It was frequently sug-
\par
gested that adjudications in cases outside the district, particularly in
\par
districts that hear relatively few FOIA cases, are somewhat erratic
\par
substantively and tend to proceed more slowly. Secondly, it was ob-
\par
served that the document review function of the courts in FOIA case
\par
processing has been less important than the policy-making function.
\par
In turn, as the body of case law has expanded and been refined,
\par
judicial resources are seen as being used less appropriately to the
\par
extent they are being devoted to document review. This process-
\par
essentially a fact-finding function-was frequently viewed as one less
\par
suited to the capacities and resources of the federal courts than to
\par
those of a specialized adjudicator. Moreover, some viewed the \emph{ex parte}
\par
nature of the proceeding in cases in which heavy reliance is placed
\par
\emph{on in camera }review and advocacy as presenting a particularly awkward
\par
role for the courts. Finally, a more subtle, but extremely important
\par
observation was common throughout the range of perspectives rep-
\par
resented in the interviews. Given the mature state of the case law
\par
development and the administrative burden imposed by the undif-
\par
ferentiated mass of FOIA cases that reach the courts, judges are per-
\par
ceived by many close to the process as having "lost interest" in the
\par
area. Clearly, the basis for this perception is difficult to document and
\par
it was rarely expressed in terms of a failure of the courts to perform
\par
properly. Rather, it was generally a forward-looking concern that the
\par
courts might not reasonably be expected to perform indefinitely the
\par
major FOIA enforcement responsibility. \textbf{I5}
