F. The Significance of the Interviews
\par
Ultimately, the significance of the interviews lies not as much in tile
\par
insight they provide into the day-to-day functioning of the current
\par
access dispute resolution structure, as in their reflection of the range
\par
of perceptions and values that surround FOIA. Were no shared in-
\par
terests or concerns revealed, it would be difficult to proceed to the
\par
question of alternative mechanisms. The interviews suggest, however,
\par
enough common ground to guide a more focused inquiry.
\par
1II. ANALYSIS AND PROPOSAL
\par
A. The Problem and the Objectives of a Solution
\par
From the outset, a basic aim of the study was to evaluate whether
\par
exclusive reliance upon the \emph{de n1Vo }judicial proceeding is responsible
\par
for delay, cost, or qualitative deficiencies in the resolution of access
\par
disputes that could be minimized through tile use of an alternative
\par
mechanism. It is important to understand, however, that to identify
\par
burdens associated with the judicial process is not to suggest that all
\par
classes of users are affected equally by those burdens or would benefit
\par
equally from an alternative system. Nor is it to suggest that it will be
\par
possible to project with a high degree of confidence the extent to
\par
which a particular mechanism could reduce the overall burden. \textbf{At}
\par
this stage it is sufficient to recognize that both the quantitative and
\par
interview data indicate that the current dispute resolution structure
\par
imposes significant, though varying, burdens.
\par
To follow substantial agency processing delay with months and
\par
sometimes years of costly and delay-ladened federal court litigation
\par
hardly seems the most desirable method for disposing of a media
\par
request for timely information on an issue of vital interest to tile
\par
public. Nor does it seem the best use of the federal courts to impose
\par
with no limitation the duty to resolve the hundreds of less pressing
\par
claims for access to federal records. Similarly, from the viewpoint of
\par
the unsophisticated requester the specter of federal court litigation
\par
to resolve a relatively modest request for access seems unduly im-
\par
posing. And for the requester whose interest is in using the Act for
\par
harassment or to satisfy commercial interests, the judicial Forum is
