Aside from this tension in the choice of forums, virtually every
\par
other consideration militates in favor of using an administrative agency
\par
rather than a court for the primary resolution of FOIA disputes. First,
\par
an administrative structure permits the development and use of spe-
\par
cialized, expert decisionmaking. In the FOIA area, this expertise would
\par
relate not only to the large and complex body of access law, but also
\par
to agency programs and operations that generate the records at issue.
\par
Second, centralization of the adjudicative function should promote
\par
greater consistency in decisionmaking. Third, two key features of
\par
federal court practice-notice-pleading with procedures aimed at nar-
\par
rowing ill-defined issues over time and broad discovery-are simply
\par
inapt for FOIA cases. Fourth, the flexibility of administrative proce-
\par
dure would permit the development of rules and practices tailored
\par
to the particular needs of access disputes. Fifth, a specialized admin-
\par
istrative forum would free access cases from the crowded federal court
\par
dockets and at the same time provide a structure in which docket
\par
management could be used meaningfully to provide expedited treat-
\par
ment for cases warranting it. Finally, the use of traditional judicial
\par
review of administrative action would provide a means of preserving
\par
a law-interpreting role for the federal courts in FOIA disputes-as
\par
well as a check on arbitrary decisionmaking-while preserving the
\par
fact-finding and law-applying function for the specialized body. \textbf{76}
\par
This view of the choice between reliance upon the courts and re-
\par
liance upon an administrative entity for binding FOIA dispute res-
\par
olution draws heavily on the traditionally-perceived benefits of
\par
administrative agencies. It consciously omits, however, one aspect of
\par
the traditional view-that the agency serve as the primary policy-
\par
making body within its field. Policymaking in the FOIA area over the
\par
two decades of the Act's existence has reflected a unique blend of
\par
legislative, executive and judicial sharing of responsibility. \textbf{77 }In light
\par
of that history, it would be somewhat unrealistic to expect that a
\par
credible adjudicative agency could at the outset assume a significant
\par
part of that overall responsibility. Reform of the structure for general
\par
access policymaking is a question that deserves additional attention,
\par
but the central question of this article-the desirability and feasibility
\par
