\par
177See \textbf{INFORMATION DISCIOSURE, \emph{supra }1ole }3, at 3-f1 to 3-3 \textbf{1.FOIA }DISPUTE \textbf{RESOLUT ION }37
\par
of a mechanism for access dispute resolution-has independent
\par
importance. \textbf{1 78}
\par
In the final analysis this question may be one that can be answered
\par
only by a process of experimentation. Yet it seems clear from the
\par
evidence gathered here and the foregoing analysis of FOIA disputes
\par
that any such undertaking would require the observance of certain
\par
principles:
\par
1. The agency should be established in a manner that ensures the
\par
highest reasonable degree of independence.
\par
2. The process of the agency should preserve the valuable features
\par
of the current judicial proceeding while taking advantage of tile op-
\par
portunity to tailor procedural rules to the needs of access dispute
\par
processing.
\par
3. The agency should be structured primarily as a case processing
\par
entity rather than a policymaking body.
\par
4. The agency's docket should be restricted in some fashion that
\par
insures that its workload does not preclude prompt decisionmaking.
\par
5. The agency should have discretion to process cases of different
\par
classes on different procedural tracks.
\par
6. The staffing of the agency should ensure the development of
\par
substantial decisional expertise.
\par
7. The agency should be accessible to all requesters regardless of
\par
geographical location or resources.
\par
8. The agency should be designed to provide an opportunity for
\par
informal resolution of access disputes.
\par
9. The relationship between the agency and the courts should be
\par
structured to make the most appropriate use of judicial resources
\par
while preserving the integrity of the agency decisionmaking process.
\par
10. The agency should be structured with sensitivity to the need to
\par
avoid undue bureaucratization and administrative cost in access dis-
\par
pute resolution.
\par
B. An Agency Model for Analysis
\par
\emph{1. The General Framework}
\par
Most, if not all, of these objectives could be attained through the
\par
creation of an administrative authority either within a compatible
\par
existing agency or as a separate agency. The agency, hereafter referred
\par
to for convenience as the Information Access Authority, would be
\par
