\par
\textbf{IN AN }INFORMATlION SociEc \textbf{i }(1977). \emph{See also }H.R. 1721, 99th Cong.. \textbf{Ist }Sess. (1985)
\par
\textbf{("A }bill to establish \textbf{a }Data Ptrotection Board, and \textbf{tr otheC pot lxsCs.")}38 ADMINISTRATIVE LAW REVIEW
\par
empowered to adjudicate access disputes under FOIA. It would also
\par
be authorized to provide conciliation services in access disputes. The
\par
head of the Authority would be appointed by the President with the
\par
advice and consent of the Senate for a term of years and be subject
\par
to removal only for cause. The primary adjudicative function of the
\par
authority would be performed by administrative law judges. Apart
\par
from administrative support for the Authority head and for the ad-
\par
ministrative law judges, the only other personnel essential to the Au-
\par
thority's functions would be a staff of conciliators.
\par
The Authority would have two primary sources of jurisdiction for
\par
adjudications: (I) by consent of the parties and (2) by certification
\par
from a federal district court. The Authority's consent jurisdiction
\par
would arise after a final agency denial of access, upon the filing of a
\par
consent agreement by the parties. The Authority's certification juris-
\par
diction would arise upon the granting by the district court of a motion
\par
for certification filed by a party to an access action within the time
\par
provided for answer, if final agency processing has been completed.
\par
The status of third-party submitters in the Authority's proceedings
\par
would be resolved by the same principles now being developed in the
\par
area of "reverse" FOIA policy. The Authority's consent jurisdiction
\par
would be plenary; its certification jurisdiction would be limited to cases
\par
evaluated against the specified standards for certification. Access ac-
\par
tions in which no motion for certification is filed or in which the motion
\par
is denied would proceed in the district court. Upon the granting of
\par
the motion to certify, the underlying district court action would be
\par
dismissed. The Authority would then have exclusive jurisdiction to
\par
adjudicate the dispute, and its final order would be subject to limited
\par
review by a United States Court of Appeals.
\par
2. \emph{Submission by Consent}
\par
Any time after final agency processing, the agency and the requester
\par
could by agreement submit their remaining dispute for adjudication
\par
by the Authority. Submission of the matter to the Authority would
\par
preclude the filing of a court action during a fixed period of time.
\par
The time period would be subject to an opportunity for the agency
\par
and the requester to agree to a longer or shorter time in unusual
\par
circumstances. A final decision by the Authority in a consent adju-
\par
dication would have the same binding effect as a decision in an ad-
\par
judication following certification. If the Authority does not determine
\par
the matter within the required time period, the requester would be
\par
free to file the normal \emph{de novo }action in federal court, or could await
\par
the Authority decision. Should the requester sue after the expiration
\par
of the time period, the court would not require the requester to
\par
exhaust the consented-to Authority proceeding.FOIA DISPUTE RESOLUTION 39
\par
The provision for submission by consent accomplishes several ob-
\par
jectives. First, it reflects, for the particular controversy, the parties'
\par
recognition that the Authority is a desirable and appropriate forum
\par
for resolution of the dispute. This serves to legitimate the Authority's
\par
function and should assure each side of the other's willingness to
\par
respect the proceeding. Second, because the parties will be free to
\par
submit by agreement only so much of their controversy as remains
\par
in dispute, it provides an opportunity-short \textbf{of' }tile institution of
\par
suit-for the parties to attempt to narrow their disagreement. Put
\par
differently, the prospect of agreeing to submission provides an oc-
\par
casion to discuss compromise, with the outlet of non-judicial dieter-
\par
mination of those matters that cannot be settled. Finally, and perhaps
\par
most controversially, it places some "docket control" for the Authority
\par
in the agencies.
\par
By refusing to consent, an agency would force the requester who
\par
wished to pursue the matter, to file a federal court action. An agency
\par
would most likely take that position on one of two grounds-that the
\par
particular matter should be determined by a court or that the matter
\par
does not warrant further formal processing. While it may be desirable
\par
for the agencies to seek to eliminate further wasteful processing in
\par
frivolous cases by imposing upon requester the barrier of a federal
\par
court proceeding, the power to refuse to consent for that purpose
\par
would best be used sparingly if for no other reason than its exercise
\par
may lead the persistent requester to file the federal court action in
\par
any event. For those cases an agency believes should be determined
\par
by a court, the opportunity to refuse to consent provides a mechanism
\par
by which the agency can assert its position. The agency decision not
\par
to consent should not be directly reviewable. If after an agency refusal
\par
to consent the requester sues under the Act, the court will have the
\par
opportunity under the certification procedure, in effect, to overrule
\par
the agency's position.
\par
While the parties should not generally be permitted to control the
\par
scheduling of cases on the Authority's docket, it seems appropriate
\par
in unusual circumstances, most commonly where a prompt deter-
\par
mination is essential, that the parties be allowed to condition their
\par
submission on an expedited processing schedule. The parties' agree-
\par
ment could be made subject to acceptance by the Authority. Even in
\par
the case not requiring expedition, a fixed time limit after which the
\par
agency would lose jurisdiction would appear necessary to assure re-
\par
questers willing to submit their disputes to the Authority that they
\par
would not languish on the (locket. In the long run, of course, the
\par
general reputation of the Authority for timeliness in processing cases
\par
will be an important factor in determining its attractiveness to re-
\par
questers for voluntary submission.40 ADMINISTRATIVE LAW REVIEW
\par
3. \emph{Submission by Certification}
\par
Within the period for filing an answer, any party to an access action
\par
would be free to file with the district court a motion to certify the
\par
matter to the Authority for adjudication, if final agency processing
\par
has been completed. The motion to certify should be granted unless
\par
the case presents: \textbf{(1) }unusually complex legal questions, (2) unusually
\par
significant issues of access policy, or (3) is determined, in the discretion
\par
of the court, otherwise to be more appropriate for judicial resolution.
\par
The court should also be free to certify a matter, consistent with these
\par
standards, on its own motion. The court should also have authority
\par
to certify a case in part where to do so would promote efficiency in
\par
the resolution of the dispute. Upon certifying the matter, the court
\par
would dismiss the underlying action.
\par
The provision for submission by certification, like the provision for
\par
certification by consent, accomplishes several objectives. First, and
\par
most importantly, it represents a determination that the Authority
\par
should not be merely an available alternative to the traditional \emph{de novo}
\par
federal court action, but rather the preferred and primary mechanism
\par
to resolve access disputes. While it might be suggested that the Au-
\par
thority should attain that status by proving its fairness and impartiality
\par
through cases taken by voluntary submission, there is significant risk
\par
that if it functioned only at the sufferance of requesters, its credibility
\par
with agencies and third parties might be jeopardized. Likewise, it
\par
would be difficult for the Authority to attain stature generally and
\par
provide a source of consistent decisionmaking, if it were merely a
\par
"competitor" of the district courts.
\par
Secondly, and virtually as important, the arrangement preserves the
\par
opportunity for the \emph{de novo }court action in the special circumstances
\par
where that appears warranted. Concomittantly, in deciding the ques-
\par
tions raised under its certification authority, the district court would
\par
have some opportunity to assess the quality of the Authority's man-
\par
agement of its caseload. Ideally, over time, the number of cases in
\par
which certification is an issue would diminish, and even in the interim
\par
the certification proceeding itself should consume only a minimal
\par
amount of time and resources.
\par
Finally, the combination of the agency's power to refuse to submit
\par
a matter to the Authority voluntarily and the requester's continued
\par
right to bring a court action gives to both parties the opportunity to
\par
argue that the matter should remain in court, and where neither party
\par
objects, that would be the normal course. The court should remain
\par
free, however, to certify a matter on its own motion.
\par
Limiting certification to cases in which final agency processing has
\par
been completed avoids exacerbating the problem of agency backlogs,
\par
while reserving for court resolution those cases in which the "due
\par
diligence" standard is not being met. As in the case of submission by
\par
consent, after certification the requester should be free to refile andFOIA DISPUI"E RESOILUTION 41
\par
proceed \emph{de novo }if the Authority has not determined the matter within
\par
the fixed period of time. Also as in the case of consent, that right
\par
should not be subject to a "due diligence" claim. This approach reflects
\par
a balance of the Authority's primacy as the access decisionmaker against
\par
the need to ensure that its caseload is timely managed.
\par
\emph{4. Venue}
\par
The Authority should be headquartered in Washington, D.C. \textbf{IIn}
\par
adjudications, whether by consent or certification, the administrative
\par
law judge should preside-where a formal proceeding is necessary-
\par
in the geographical location most convenient for the particular case.
\par
The presumptively appropriate location should be the District of Co-
\par
lumbia. Upon a showing by either the requester or the agency that
\par
the District would be inconvenient, the administrative law judge should
\par
be assigned to preside at an appropriate alternative location. In con-
\par
ciliations, there would ordinarily be no need for the requester and
\par
the agency to be physically present, and these matters should be han-
\par
dled by correspondence and telephone to the greatest extent possible.
\par
Where in-person sessions are necessary, a conciliator should be as-
\par
signed for that purpose in the District of Columbia or at an alternative
\par
location.
\par
Concentrating the physical presence of the Authority in the District
\par
of Columbia, but not making the District the exclusive forum, serves
\par
several purposes. First, it accepts the primacy of the District as the
\par
forum for FOIA dispute processing, and the soundness of the reasons
\par
underlying that \emph{de facto }result. Second, it avoids the enormous cost
\par
and, in turn, the dilution of available resources that would result from
\par
establishing regional components throughout the country. Third, it
\par
nevertheless preserves the opportunity, in appropriate cases, to extend
\par
the presence of the Authority to distant forums. Finally, it helps ensure
\par
that the work of the Authority that can best be accomplished without
\par
in-person proceedings will not be compromised by the perception
\par
that an Authority representative must be present to perform a mean-
\par
ingful function.
\par
5. \emph{Adjudication}
\par
Adjudications should proceed \emph{de novo }in a manner essentially similar
\par
to the current district court proceeding, but with an effort to use the
\par
flexibility of the administrative process to minimize cost and delay
\par
and improve the quality of decision-making. In adjudications by con-
\par
sent, documents in the nature of a complaint and answer should
\par
accompany the submission agreement. In adjudications by certifica-
\par
tion, the complaint and the answer (if filed) would be transmitted to
\par
the Authority by the district court as part of the certification order.
\par
In either case, once the matter is lodged with the Authority, it would42 ADMINISTRATIVE LAW REVIEW
\par
be assigned to an administrative law judge for hearing. Either party
\par
would then be free to make preliminary motions, including motions
\par
to compel the production of a \emph{Vaughn }index and motions for summary
\par
judgment. Most importantly, however, by practice and by procedural
\par
rules the Authority should encourage the use of less formal techniques
\par
to determine the issues in controversy or promote settlement.
\par
The prospect of using relatively comprehensive document review
\par
by the administrative law judge as a substitute for detailed \emph{Vaughn}
\par
affidavits in appropriate cases illustrates one method of substituting
\par
substantive analysis for procedural formality. After the issue is joined,
\par
whether through pleadings or less formally, the case could be ad-
\par
vanced through flexible procedures. Certain cases would simply not
\par
require disposition on the merits. For cases reaching the merits, where
\par
characterization of the records is not at issue, the matter would pro-
\par
ceed by prompt summary disposition of the legal questions. Where
\par
the characterization of the records or portions thereof is in dispute,
\par
the necessary initial fact-finding could proceed by the preparation
\par
and submission of an oral or written \emph{Vaughn }index or by immediate
\par
submission of the records for \emph{in camera }review. At the same time, the
\par
proceeding must retain sufficient formality to provide the parties a
\par
well-defined "shadow" in which to attempt to settle their dispute and
\par
to provide inducement to do so. Put differently, the proceeding should
\par
be structured to preserve the well-understood change in the com-
\par
plexion of a case as it moves from agency processing to a neutral
\par
forum.
\par
The adjudication procedures should also be structured to provide
\par
for docket management consistent with the needs of access disputes.
\par
The most obvious illustration would be procedures for placing cases
\par
of different classes on different tracks for processing. Cases of par-
\par
ticular urgency could be given expedited treatment. Cases involving
\par
large volumes of records but serving an important public function,
\par
as in the case of historical research, could be given priority over large
\par
volume cases involving a personal or commercial interest without full
\par
scale expedition. At this point, the range of possibilities need not be
\par
enumerated. The important consideration is that the use of these
\par
techniques would be available in the administrative process.
\par
In cases requiring formal determination, the administrative law judge
\par
should decide, as in the district court proceeding, whether the records
\par
or any segregable portions thereof were improperly withheld and the
\par
burden to sustain the action should be on the denying agency. As
\par
suggested above, the administrative law judge should have authority
\par
to examine withheld records \emph{in camera. }The administrative law judge
\par
should also have authority to order the disclosure of improperly with-
\par
held records and to award litigation costs and attorneys fees to a
\par
requester who substantially prevails.FOIA DISPUTE RESOLUTION 43
\par
The decision of the administrative law judge would become the
\par
final decision of the Authority unless reviewed on the record by the
\par
head of the Authority pursuant to a discretionary appeals procedure
\par
available to the requester or the agency. The discretionary review
\par
opportunity comports with traditional administrative practice and
\par
provides a mechanism for fostering sound and consistent decision-
\par
making within the Authority. To ensure that the determination can
\par
proceed with the required dispatch, the standard should be strictly
\par
limited to exceptional questions of law or fact.
\par
After the period for seeking judicial review of an Authority decision
\par
has expired or after a judicial affirmance, a final decision requiring
\par
disclosure would, if not complied with, become enforceable through
\par
contempt proceedings in federal district court. This provides a mean-
\par
ingful enforcement mechanism in the unlikely event that an agency
\par
refuses to release the records.
\par
\emph{6. Judicial Review}
\par
The final decision of the Authority granting or denying, in whole
\par
or in part, access to records should be reviewable in the United States
\par
Court of Appeals for the circuit in which the adjudication took place
\par
or in the United States Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia.
\par
The requester, the agency, or both should be entitled to obtain review
\par
of an Authority decision, A petition for review should be required to
\par
be filed within a short period after the final decision of the Authority.
\par
An Authority decision ordering disclosure would ordinarily be stayed
\par
pending review. Review proceedings should be expedited in accor-
\par
dance with the "good cause" standard of the Federal Courts Improve-
\par
ment Act of 1984. The decision of the Authority should be reviewed
\par
under essentially the same standard now used by courts of appeals to
\par
review district court decisions in FOIA cases.
\par
Providing for court of appeals review of Authority decisions \textbf{not}
\par
only preserves for an Article \textbf{1II }body the determination of questions
\par
of law under FOLA, but also channels Authority decisions to the same
\par
forums that will review those cases that remain in the district courts,
\par
notwithstanding the availability of the Authority process. While the
\par
latter consideration alone might justify applying the same standard
\par
of review to both classes of cases, there is another important reason.
\par
The diversion of cases from the district courts to the Authority poses
\par
the uncertainty of a new system for requesters and agencies. Given
\par
the vagaries of the traditional formulations of the standards of judicial
\par
review of administrative agency decisions, it seems desirable to refer
\par
to a standard that is understood and accepted even if it is theoretically
\par
imperfect for describing judicial review of an administrative agency
\par
decision.44 ADMINISTRATIVE LAW REVIEW
\par
Review of final decisions of the Authority after certification should
\par
also include the question of the propriety of the certification by the
\par
district court. Any possible encumbrance of the process by the avail-
\par
ability of such review would appear justified by the need to ensure
\par
consistent application of the certification standard.
\par
\emph{7. Conciliation}
\par
The Authority should make available, at any stage in the \textbf{FOIA}
\par
process, the services of a trained conciliator upon the agreement of
\par
the agency and the requester that the informal assistance of a neutral
\par
party would aid in the resolution of the dispute. The services of the
\par
conciliator in each matter should be tailored to the needs of the par-
\par
ticular controversy. It could include, in appropriate cases, examination
\par
of the disputed records.
\par
The term "conciliation" is used here to distinguish the process from
\par
the similar process of "mediation":
\par
Conciliation is a weak form of mediation. Unlike the mediator, the con-
\par
ciliator has no authority to propose his own solutions or suggest new ideas
\par
as a means of breaking an impasse in negotiations. In essence, the concil-
\par
iator is a facilitator of negotiation between the parties. Like the mediator,
\par
the conciliator will pass on the parties' positions and priorities and will
\par
facilitate the search for common ground. \textbf{17"}
\par
Given the nature of the FOIA dispute-particularly its general non-
\par
amenability to formal compromise-the more subtle connotation of
\par
the conciliator function seems apt. This is best explained by illustra-
\par
tion. In many cases, a requester seeks records on the assumption that
\par
they contain certain information when in fact the information of in-
\par
terest to the requester is simply not there. The records may well be
\par
exempt, but the requester's strong interest in the presumed contents
\par
drives him to actively contest the case. If a neutral third party was
\par
informed of the requester's interest and had access to the records,
\par
the matter could likely be resolved by a report to the requester fiom
\par
the neutral that the records did not contain the information the re-
\par
quester was seeking. The role of the conciliator might also go beyond
\par
document review to probing with an agency representative the basis
\par
for an exemption claim. 8 4 t
