\par
Whatever the role of the conciliator, he must have the confidence
\par
of both the requester and the agency if the conciliation function is to
\par
be performed effectively. At a minimum this requires the agreement
\par
of both parties that the service would be useful in a particular case
\par
and, from the requester's perspective, that the service would be pro-
\par
vided by an individual truly independent of the agency. The concil-
\par
iation staff should maintain a separateness from the adjudication staff
\par
and nothing said or clone in the conciliation process should be ad-
\par
missible in a subsequent formal proceeding. Above all, the role of the
\par
conciliator should not be narrowly defined at the outset, but rather
\par
allowed to develop as the needs of particular cases are presented,
\par
bearing in mind that the purpose of the service would be not only to
\par
minimize the formality necessary to fulfill the basic policies of the Act,
\par
but also to protect the more formal dispute-resolution process from
\par
an unmanageable caseload.' 8 1
\par
\emph{8. Other Functions}
\par
In addition to the dispute-specific functions of adjudication and
\par
conciliation, the Authority ultimately could be assigned functions of
\par
a more general policy-oriented nature. These include most impor-
\par
tantly: (1) the formulation of government-wide policy through infor-
\par
mal rulemaking, (2) the provision of informal advice and guidance to
\par
agencies and requesters, and (3) the exercise of general investigatory
\par
authority with respect to government-wide FOIA compliance. Each
\par
of these functions offers promise for improving the overall admin-
\par
istration of the Act and thus minimizing the number of disputes re-
\par
quiring post-agency processing. Yet each also represents a substantial
\par
undertaking and could impair initially the effective implementation
\par
of the adjudication and conciliation functions. On balance, it would
\par
appear appropriate to consider assigning such additional functions to
\par
the Authority only after it has demonstrated its capacity to effectively
\par
discharge its primary dispute resolution duties.
\par
C. The Ombudsman Model as an Alternative
\par
The proposal for the establishment of an Information Access Au-
\par
thority contemplates an entity that would have both adjudicatory and
\par
conciliatory functions. This aspect of the proposal proceeds from the
\par
premises \textbf{(1) }that any formal administrative alternative to \emph{de novo }\textbf{\emph{ju-}}
\par
dicial determination could be overwhelmed by its workload without
\par
the availability of some mechanism for informal dispute processing,
\par
and (2) that certain forms of informal processing could have inde-
\par
pendent value in access disputes.' \emph{82 }A shift of the primary adjudicative
