\textbf{COMPUTERS AND VALUES}
\par
JOSEPH GRiINFELD
\par
I
\par
The essential difference between man and computers is not that man can
\par
do things which a computer cannot do, but that man constructs computers
\par
according to his values. The problem of whether and how far machines can
\par
"think" is thus wrongly put. Processes that are expected to achieve some-
\par
thing have a value that is inexplicable in terms of processes that have no
\par
such purpose. A "problem" as well as a "solution" is not in the machine
\par
as such but only in the conjunction of the machine and the human purpose.
\par
"Understanding" stems from \emph{our }contact with the world. Even our ideas of
\par
randomness reflect the ways of human perception: it is ourselves who prefer
\par
order to randomness, particularly in science. Our knowledge is not, and
\par
cannot be, wholly explicit - in short, we have problems because we have
\par
values. A mathematical theory (and therefore a computer) can be construct-
\par
ed only by relying on prior tacit knowledge. To form part of science, a state-
\par
ment of fact must not only be true but also "interesting." The process by
\par
which discovery will be brought to light will be acknowledged as a discovery
\par
precisely because it could not have been achieved by any persistence in ap-
\par
plying explicit rules to given facts. It is always we who have to provide the
\par
initial problem as well as the terms or reference by which a solution is
\par
recognized as such. A goal-seeking mechanism will not seek our goals unless
\par
we design it for that purpose.
\par
Indeed, among the most challenging scientific questions of our time are
\par
the corresponding analytic and synthetic problems: How does the human
\par
brain function? Can we design a machine which will simulate the brain?
\par
Speculation on these problems, which can \textbf{be }traced back many centuries,
\par
usually reflects in any period the characteristics of machines then in use. The
\par
plausibility of cybernetics, the science of information structure, is based on
\par
the observation that it is possible now to realize functions with technical
\par
instruments which, up to quite recently, were considered a monopoly of
\par
organic and particularly human behaviour. Typical examples for this are
\par
logical connections, storing of information, processes of perception and of
\par
learning. Actually, some robots with very complex behaviour patterns can
\par
be built; power devices which change the shape of the body or move its parts
\par
relative to each other can be constructed. Appropriate power devices for
\par
moving body parts or appendages can even be used by the robot for fixing
\par
its own flat tires or repairing various other damages external or internal -
\par
except of course disabling damage to some part required in fixing that same
\par
1
\par
part.
\par
\textbf{1 }James T. Culbertson, "Some Uneconomical Robots," in \textbf{\emph{Automata Studies, }}edited
\par
by C. E. Shannon and J. McCarthy, Princeton University Press 1956, p. 101.30
\par
\emph{The Journal of Value Inquiry}
\par
The basic idea of Norbert Wiener, the creator of cybernetics, was that
\par
humans and other organisms are not essentially different from any other
\par
type of control and communication system. All these function according to
\par
the feedback principle, a method of controlling a system by reinserting into
\par
it the results of its past experience. If the information which proceeds back-
\par
ward from the performance is able to change the general method and pat-
\par
tern of performance, we have a process which may well be called learning.2
\par
George has pointed out that man obviously operates on a gross feedback
\par
system in so far as he must have knowledge of the results of the actions that
\par
he performs in order that he may continue to act sensibly in his environment.
\par
A man who cannot see his results in a learning task will not improve, i.e. he
\par
will not learn; this fact has been well understood by experimental psy-
\par
chology. The great importance of the contribution made by cybernetics to
\par
biology and psychology is that, under certain circumstances, it can formalize
\par
theories and make them precise. It is commonplace that virtually anything
\par
can be made a model of anything else, and that description and explanation
\par
depend on this fact.
\par
Learning, thinking, perceiving and so on, are clearly influenced by language.
\par
Quite obviously, without language the accumulation of human learning
\par
would have been an impossibility, and it would seem that the high-speed
\par
change of strategies - high-speed learning - of which humans are capable,
\par
is due primarily to language. However, the words "learning," "motivation"
\par
and "perception" are hardly clear-cut in their meaning. The trouble is that
\par
we have reached a point where many of these problems can be understood
\par
in a general way, and yet the very size of the model-making undertaking is
\par
so large as to defy conceptual clarity.3 Nevertheless, what the feedback and
\par
the transistor have made possible is not the sporadic design of individual
\par
automatic mechanisms but a general policy for the construction of automatic
\par
mechanism of the most varied type as well as understanding related prob-
\par
lems in biology and psychology. In this the electronic devices have been
\par
reinforced by a new theoretical treatment of communication, and it is this
\par
conjunction of developments which now renders possible the new automatic
\par
age. A decisive condition for exact research is that the investigated objects
\par
be "isolated," i.e. that their situation and behaviour are not changed by
\par
conditions which are not under observation. Such "isolated systems" do not
\par
really exist in biology or in psychology where all units to be investigated are
\par
in a continuous process of energy and information exchange with their
\par
environment. Thinking in models is therefore a great help in understanding
\par
complicated biological and psychological units. At present automata per-
\par
form mainly what they have been programmed to do, but "learning auto-
\par
\textbf{mata" }are not restricted in this way. In a learning machine of this kind only
\par
the task and reactions to the environment are programmed. The machine
\par
\textbf{2 Norbert Wiener, \emph{The Human Use of Human Beings, }Eyre and Spottiswoode,}
\par
\textbf{London 1954, p. 61.}
\par
3 \textbf{F. H. George, \emph{The Brain as a Computer, }Pergamon, Oxford 1962, p. 380.}\emph{Computers and Values}
\par
31
\par
seeks itself the optimal solution to a problem by trial and error. In this
\par
manner there may appear results which the programmer has \emph{not }foreseen
\par
and which sometimes even surprise him. Learning, like more primitive forms
\par
of feedback, is a process which reads differently forward and backward in
\par
time. The creature that learns moves ahead from a known past into an un-
\par
known future and this future is not interchangeable with that past.4 Man,
\par
like all other organisms, lives in a contingent universe, but man's advantage
\par
over the rest of nature is that he has the physiological and intellectual equip-
\par
ment to adapt himself to radical changes in his environment.
\par
The process of selecting an action from a number of alternative courses
\par
of action is what is meant by decision. To a limited extent all living organ-
\par
isms encounter the problem of decision. Even a one-celled organism has to
\par
act, but the decision is made automatically by a biological mechanism. In
\par
the world of mammals most behaviour seems to be learned by the young
\par
either from parental behaviour or by trial and error. The advantage is in the
\par
greater flexibility, but the corresponding disadvantage lies in the necessity of
\par
transmitting the successful new response to the progeny, that otherwise are
\par
practically helpless. Many of the mammals have overcome this disadvantage
\par
through the mechanism of the family. The advantage of cultural patterns
\par
however, as opposed to a simple parent-child transmission, lies in the pooling
\par
of experience that is obtained. As the decision-problems presented by
\par
civilization became more complex, a class of specialists arose whose job was
\par
making decisions. These may indeed make better decisions because they can
\par
receive special training and gain experience. Most recorded history is a
\par
chronicle of these specialized decision-makers - kings, generals, priests and
\par
the like. Instead of dealing with a large number of specific responses, certain
\par
broad principles and models were developed which would enable a decision
\par
to be made in a large number of different situations. The devil theory of the
\par
world (to which even Wiener ascribes in a certain sense, as we shall see) has
\par
the advantage of being simple, comprehensive and easily understood, and it
\par
continues to be a very popular theory in the modern world. Unfortunately
\par
however, even the proponents of Reason confused consistency with truth.
\par
Truth involves the real world; consistency is a logical question that does \emph{not}
\par
involve the real world. This distinction was not realized until the discovery
\par
of several consistent geometries which led to theories contradicting those
\par
of Euclidean geometry. The fact that reason is divorced from the real world
\par
was thus not realized until comparatively recently (and many people even
\par
today are not aware of this gap). Only the new techniques introduced by the
\par
scientists closed the gap between reason and the real world by means of an
\par
\emph{inductive }logic, a procedure of going from observations to statements.5 But
\par
even so, all actions in the real world are shrouded in uncertainty and in ad-
\par
dition to that a realistic formulation of the structure of decision must take
\par
account of the problem of conflicting values. A given outcome will have both
\par
4 Wiener, \textbf{\emph{Human Use, }pp. 48-9.}
\par
5 Irwin D. J. Bross, \textbf{\emph{Design for Decision, }}Macmillan, New York \textbf{\emph{1953, p. }}17.32
\par
\emph{The Journal of Value Inquiry}
\par
desirable and undesirable aspects. To concentrate on survival, for instance,
\par
may turn out to be self-defeating.
\par
Whenever we find a new phenomenon which partakes to some degree of
\par
the nature of those which we have already termed "living phenomena," but
\par
does not conform to all the associated aspects which define the \textbf{term }"life,"
\par
we are faced with the problem whether \textbf{to enlarge }the word "life" so as to
\par
include them, or to define \textbf{it in a more }restrictive way so as to exclude them.
\par
It should be noted however that the nervous system and the automatic
\par
machine are fundamentally alike in that they are devices which make
\par
decisions on the basis of decisions they have made in the past. Ashby has
\par
shown that a machine of rather random and haphazard structure will have
\par
certain near-equilibrium positions, and certain positions far from equi-
\par
librium, and that the near-equilibrium patterns will by their very nature last
\par
for a long time, while the others will appear only temporarily. The result is
\par
that in Ashby's machine, as in Darwin's \textbf{nature, }we have the appearance of
\par
purposefulness in a system which is not purposefully constructed simply
\par
because purposelessness is in its very nature transitory. An organism or a
\par
society of organisms will tend to dally longer in those modes of activity in
\par
