\par
Man is intelligent because evolution has equipped him with a richly
\par
structured brain. A certain critical degree of structural complexity is re-
\par
quired of a network before it can become self-modifying in a way that we
\par
could consider intelligent. It is true that many differences between man and
\par
machine which, until recently have seemed immutably qualitative, have
\par
turned out to be merely quantitative. Nevertheless, human knowledge is
\par
not, and cannot be, wholly explicit.3 1 This fact itself seems obvious enough;
\par
but it is not easy to say exactly what it means. We know a person's face,
\par
