\par
that a machine had such capacities would presumably carry with it the com-
\par
mitment to speak about a machine as we speak about ourselves. It is not
\par
possible to produce a set of rules purporting to describe what a man should
\par
do in every conceivable set of circumstances. Similarly, our concepts have
\par
theories "built into them"; the new context may be the product of new
\par
theoretical knowledge or of a new technology. Intelligent behavior pre-
\par
sumably consists in a departure from the completely disciplined behavior
\par
involved in computation, but a rather slight one, which does not give rise to
\par
random behavior, or to pointless repetitive loops. In any case, a reproduction
\par
