\textbf{COMPUTERS AND VALUES}
\par
JOSEPH GRiINFELD
\par
I
\par
The essential difference between man and computers is not that man can
\par
do things which a computer cannot do, but that man constructs computers
\par
according to his values. The problem of whether and how far machines can
\par
"think" is thus wrongly put. Processes that are expected to achieve some-
\par
thing have a value that is inexplicable in terms of processes that have no
\par
such purpose. A "problem" as well as a "solution" is not in the machine
\par
as such but only in the conjunction of the machine and the human purpose.
\par
"Understanding" stems from \emph{our }contact with the world. Even our ideas of
\par
randomness reflect the ways of human perception: it is ourselves who prefer
\par
order to randomness, particularly in science. Our knowledge is not, and
\par
cannot be, wholly explicit - in short, we have problems because we have
\par
values. A mathematical theory (and therefore a computer) can be construct-
\par
ed only by relying on prior tacit knowledge. To form part of science, a state-
\par
ment of fact must not only be true but also "interesting." The process by
\par
which discovery will be brought to light will be acknowledged as a discovery
\par
precisely because it could not have been achieved by any persistence in ap-
\par
plying explicit rules to given facts. It is always we who have to provide the
\par
initial problem as well as the terms or reference by which a solution is
\par
recognized as such. A goal-seeking mechanism will not seek our goals unless
\par
we design it for that purpose.
\par
Indeed, among the most challenging scientific questions of our time are
\par
the corresponding analytic and synthetic problems: How does the human
\par
brain function? Can we design a machine which will simulate the brain?
\par
Speculation on these problems, which can \textbf{be }traced back many centuries,
\par
usually reflects in any period the characteristics of machines then in use. The
\par
plausibility of cybernetics, the science of information structure, is based on
\par
the observation that it is possible now to realize functions with technical
\par
instruments which, up to quite recently, were considered a monopoly of
\par
organic and particularly human behaviour. Typical examples for this are
\par
logical connections, storing of information, processes of perception and of
\par
learning. Actually, some robots with very complex behaviour patterns can
\par
be built; power devices which change the shape of the body or move its parts
\par
relative to each other can be constructed. Appropriate power devices for
\par
moving body parts or appendages can even be used by the robot for fixing
\par
its own flat tires or repairing various other damages external or internal -
\par
except of course disabling damage to some part required in fixing that same
\par
1
\par
part.
