\par
The basic idea of Norbert Wiener, the creator of cybernetics, was that
\par
humans and other organisms are not essentially different from any other
\par
type of control and communication system. All these function according to
\par
the feedback principle, a method of controlling a system by reinserting into
\par
it the results of its past experience. If the information which proceeds back-
\par
ward from the performance is able to change the general method and pat-
\par
tern of performance, we have a process which may well be called learning.2
\par
George has pointed out that man obviously operates on a gross feedback
\par
system in so far as he must have knowledge of the results of the actions that
\par
he performs in order that he may continue to act sensibly in his environment.
\par
A man who cannot see his results in a learning task will not improve, i.e. he
\par
will not learn; this fact has been well understood by experimental psy-
\par
chology. The great importance of the contribution made by cybernetics to
\par
biology and psychology is that, under certain circumstances, it can formalize
\par
theories and make them precise. It is commonplace that virtually anything
\par
can be made a model of anything else, and that description and explanation
\par
depend on this fact.
\par
Learning, thinking, perceiving and so on, are clearly influenced by language.
\par
Quite obviously, without language the accumulation of human learning
\par
would have been an impossibility, and it would seem that the high-speed
\par
change of strategies - high-speed learning - of which humans are capable,
\par
is due primarily to language. However, the words "learning," "motivation"
\par
and "perception" are hardly clear-cut in their meaning. The trouble is that
\par
we have reached a point where many of these problems can be understood
\par
in a general way, and yet the very size of the model-making undertaking is
\par
so large as to defy conceptual clarity.3 Nevertheless, what the feedback and
\par
the transistor have made possible is not the sporadic design of individual
\par
automatic mechanisms but a general policy for the construction of automatic
\par
mechanism of the most varied type as well as understanding related prob-
\par
lems in biology and psychology. In this the electronic devices have been
\par
reinforced by a new theoretical treatment of communication, and it is this
\par
conjunction of developments which now renders possible the new automatic
\par
age. A decisive condition for exact research is that the investigated objects
\par
be "isolated," i.e. that their situation and behaviour are not changed by
\par
conditions which are not under observation. Such "isolated systems" do not
\par
really exist in biology or in psychology where all units to be investigated are
\par
in a continuous process of energy and information exchange with their
\par
environment. Thinking in models is therefore a great help in understanding
\par
complicated biological and psychological units. At present automata per-
\par
form mainly what they have been programmed to do, but "learning auto-
\par
\textbf{mata" }are not restricted in this way. In a learning machine of this kind only
\par
the task and reactions to the environment are programmed. The machine
