desirable and undesirable aspects. To concentrate on survival, for instance,
\par
may turn out to be self-defeating.
\par
Whenever we find a new phenomenon which partakes to some degree of
\par
the nature of those which we have already termed "living phenomena," but
\par
does not conform to all the associated aspects which define the \textbf{term }"life,"
\par
we are faced with the problem whether \textbf{to enlarge }the word "life" so as to
\par
include them, or to define \textbf{it in a more }restrictive way so as to exclude them.
\par
It should be noted however that the nervous system and the automatic
\par
machine are fundamentally alike in that they are devices which make
\par
decisions on the basis of decisions they have made in the past. Ashby has
\par
shown that a machine of rather random and haphazard structure will have
\par
certain near-equilibrium positions, and certain positions far from equi-
\par
librium, and that the near-equilibrium patterns will by their very nature last
\par
for a long time, while the others will appear only temporarily. The result is
\par
that in Ashby's machine, as in Darwin's \textbf{nature, }we have the appearance of
\par
purposefulness in a system which is not purposefully constructed simply
\par
because purposelessness is in its very nature transitory. An organism or a
\par
society of organisms will tend to dally longer in those modes of activity in
\par
which the different parts work together according to a more or \textbf{less meaning-}
\par
ful pattern.6
\par
Developing organisms are exceedingly complex systems. Growth of size
\par
and differentiation will usually occur together in the organism, and can be
\par
regarded as the two fundamental processes of development. Development
\par
depends importantly on communication between cells, and is internally and
\par
not externally controlled, i.e. it is closed to information but open to energy.7
\par
At an early stage in the study of development Driesch argued that an
\par
organism is not a "machine" because a machine cannot develop. This is an
\par
argument which even today is often used, although some modern \textbf{automata}
\par
do develop in a significant sense. As far as concept-development is con-
\par
cerned, a relevant hypothesis is that of Paul Weiss who implies that control
\par
is mostly internal when he claims that knowledge grows like organisms,
\par
information acting as food which has to be assimilated rather than merely
\par
stored.s The essence of development is that the system as it were "deduces"
\par
itself. But the main thing to notice here is that if we have negative feedback
\par
in a closed loop (in other words, there is no meta-system to allow escape
\par
from the self-referential paradox), then the result is oscillatory behaviour
\par
in the loop. Wormell has argued that these paradoxes need not be so worry-
\par
\textbf{ing as }modern philosophers seem to find them, but that they should be
\par
treated simply as oscillating statements: in these terms the Cretan's state-
\par
ment, like the statement "this statement is false," when referring to itself,
\par
would be alternatively true and false. Such \textbf{a statement, }thus \textbf{"... oscillates}
