between two incompatible assertions and fails to settle on either of them." 9
\par
In the nineteenth century, scientific progress was always associated with
\par
order, system and coherent sequence of cause and effect. Even after Dar-
\par
win's work on the origin of species, the popular view of evolution was that
\par
the universe could now be regarded as a vast mechanism which automatical-
\par
ly, inevitably and systematically worked through the selection of favourable
\par
species to the attainment of desirable biological ends. The present view, on
\par
the other hand, is generally more ready to admit randomness as a decisive
\par
factor in development, and to stress similarities between evolution and
\par
artistic creation. The artist also applies some kind of feedback to his work,
\par
for if he changes one part of a painting or of a musical composition, it is
\par
almost inevitable that he must change other parts to balance this. In a work
\par
of art (and perhaps even in a scientific hypothesis) there must be not too
\par
much order nor too much disorder, and artistic thinking would seem to be
\par
neither altogether logical nor altogether random.
\par
The close, indeed essential relation between intelligence and selection is
\par
shown clearly if we examine the tests specially devised for its objective
\par
measurement. What seems to be remarkable in the genius is the rapid dis-
\par
crimination with which the possibilities are winnowed. Ashby gives a design
\par
for an intelligence-amplifier by showing that a system with a selection-
\par
amplifier can be more selective than the man who built it. Such a system
\par
would in principle be capable of solving problems which are beyond the
\par
intellectual powers of its designer. The intelligence-engineer allows the basic
\par
drive of entropy in nature to do the work of the intelligence-amplifier. But in
\par
order to reduce the time of search, it is conducted, not in the real physical
\par
world but in a model, the model being chosen so that the search can proceed
\par
much more rapidly. Mathematics itself provides a vast range of models
\par
which can be handled on paper, or made to "behave" faster than the systems
\par
to which they refer.' 0 Furthermore, continuity being common in the natural
\par
world, the time taken in the solution of problems coming from it may be
\par
substantially below that given by the exponential bound. It is also only
\par
realistic to consider, in this connection, the effect on the solving of problems
\par
of knowledge accumulated in the past. The effect of such knowledge is again
\par
to put a constraint on the possibilities, lessening the regions that have been
\par
searched. Finding a solution then becomes a matter of starting somewhere
\par
in this unknown distribution of states and trying to find one of the acceptable
\par
states.
\par
