\par
in them as being normative, i.e. of the form "the next axiom scheme should
\par
be no m" or "theory k should be dropped from consideration." However,
\par
not every worthwhile problem is well-defined.21 It is tempting to generalize
\par
Gdel's theorem to read that whenever we have a system dealing with itself,
\par
we may expect to encounter maladjustments and infelicities, if not down-
\par
right paradox. The brain that tries to understand, is itself part of the world
\par
that it is trying to understand. A "level of operation" may be roughly char-
\par
acterized by the things we leave unanalyzed. The best we can attain is
\par
relative rigour in a limited universe of discourse and operations. Computing
\par
machines are sometimes so constructed that they "verify" the result of their
\par
own operations and cease to function as soon as a "mistake" occurs. But the
\par
mistake is not in the machine, but only in the conjunction of the machine
\par
and the human purpose. No machine as such ever malfunctions or makes
\par
a mistake. The malfunctioning of a loose bearing, for instance, is not a mal-
\par
functioning from the point of view of the machine, but from the point of view
\par
of the man who wants it to do something for him2 2 There is no unique or
\par
uniform operation of verification that a computer could perform.
