We can state our conclusion also as follows. Most games discussed in the game-
\par
theoretical literature are games with immediate commitment, in the sense that each
\par
player is free to commit himself to some particular strategy before any chance
\par
move or personal move has taken place. (In the special case where the game is a
\par
cooperative game, the players are also free to enter into fully binding and en-
\par
forceable agreements at that point.)
\par
In contrast, the Bayesian games G* we are using in the analysis of games with
\par
incomplete information, must be interpreted as games with delayed commitment,5
\par
where the players can commit themselves to specific strategies (and can enter
\par
into binding agreements if the game they are playing is a cooperative game),
\par
only after certain moves have occurred in the game, viz., the chance moves
\par
determining the players' attribute vectors.6
\par
By the very definition of the normal form of a game, von Neumann and
\par
Morgenstern's Normalization Principle [61 can apply only to games with im-
\par
mediate commitment. Hence, in general, we cannot expect that the normal form
\par
Di (G*) = Di(G) of a Bayesian game G* will be a fully satisfactory representation of
\par
this game G* or of the incomplete-information game G Bayes-equivalent to G*.
\par
This is the reason why in the analysis of these games we have found it necessary
\par
to replace von Neumann and Morgenstern's Normalization Principle by our
\par
weaker Semi-normalization Principle (Postulate 2 in section 7 of Part I of this
\par
paper).
