1.1.2 The Probability Function. We will
\par
assume that each individual is able to rep-
\par
resent his beliefs as to the likelihood of
\par
the different states of the world (e.g., as
\par
to whether Nature will choose Rain or
\par
Shine) by a "subjective" probability dis-
\par
tribution [40, Irving Fisher, 1912, ch. 16;
\par
126, Leonard J. Savage, 1954]. That is, an
\par
assignment to each state of a number be-
\par
tween zero and one (end-points not ex-
\par
cluded) whose sum equals unity. Subjec-
\par
tive certainty would be represented by
\par
attaching the full probabilistic weight of
\par
unity to only one of the outcomes. The
\par
degree of subjective uncertainty is re-
\par
flected in the dispersion of probability
\par
weights over the possible states.
\par
Frank Knight [72, 1921] attempted to
\par
distinguish between "risk" and "uncer-
\par
tainty," depending upon whether proba-
\par
bility estimates are or are not calculable
\par
on the basis of an objective classification
\par
of instances. At times he suggested [72,
\par
1921, pp. 20, 226] that the probability con-
\par
cept is inapplicable under true uncer-
\par
tainty, for example, to such questions as
\par
whether or not a cure for cancer will be
\par
discovered in the next decade. It will not
\par
be possible to review here the philosophi-
\par
cal and operational underpinnings of the
\par
probability concept; for our purposes, it
\par
is sufficient that the "subjective" or "de-
\par
gree of belief" interpretation has proved
\par
fruitful even for Knightian uncertainty sit-
\par
uations. But elsewhere Knight's discussion
\par
is much more in line with modern devel-
\par
opments, as when he suggests [72, 1921,
\par
p. 227] that a man's actions may depend
\par
upon his estimate of the chance that his
\par
beliefs are correct-or, we shall say, upon
\par
his confidence in his beliefs. We will show
\par
explicitly in Part II that degree of confi-
\par
dence is an essential element in non-ter-
\par
minal (informational) actions; the esti-
\par
mated value of acquiring information
\par
varies inversely with prior confidence.
