ing at this is to note that, in principle, vari-
\par
ation of probability of loss is equivalent
\par
to variations in the amounts of loss under
\par
a suitably extensive specification of states
\par
of the world [57, Hirshleifer, 1970, p. 217].
\par
Thus, with perfect monitoring, insurance
\par
terms can always be based upon the fixed
\par
probabilities of the true underlying states.
\par
If insurers could offer suitably different
\par
premium/indemnity ratios for losses un-
\par
der perfectly observable contingencies
\par
like a 10-foot flood, a 20-foot flood, etc.,
\par
they need not be concerned with how
\par
high a dike the insured chooses to build.
\par
Realistically speaking, however, moni-
\par
toring of states will be imperfect. Insurers
\par
cannot be certain about the true flood haz-
\par
ards, fire hazards, or medical hazards ex-
\par
perienced by insureds. Consequently,
\par
very often contracts have to be written
\par
in terms of "result-states" [92, MarshalL,
\par
Mark Pauly, 1968; 148, Zeckhauser, 1970].
\par
The first is to require the insured party
\par
to bear some portion of the risk, for exam-
\par
ple, by a "deductible" provision (indem-
\par
nity will be less than the loss by a fixed
\par
amount) or by "coinsurance" (indemnity
\par
will be a proper fraction of the loss). Then
\par
insurance will be provided, but moral haz-
\par
ard persists in that insureds will engage
\par
in less preventive activity than would be
\par
efficient with costless monitoring. In addi-
\par
tion, risk-spreading through insurance is
\par
less than ideal. The second way of coping
\par
is to price insurance in accordance with
\par
the actual loss-prevention behavior of in-
\par
sureds (the height of the dike built), the
\par
idea being that to monitor behavior may
\par
be easier than monitoring the underlying
\par
states. Again, as this process is subject to
\par
slippage and uncertainty, there is less pre-
\par
ventive activity and less risk-spreading
\par
through insurance than would be optimal.
