2.1.2 Other Informational Activities. So
\par
far, under the heading of informational
\par
action we have only considered the
\par
acquisition of evidence-as by generation
\par
of sample data (the production of socially
\par
new" information) or the receipt of ex-
\par
pert advice (the interpersonal transfer of
\par
"old" information). But other types of in-
\par
formational activities can also be very im-
\par
portant. The possibility of acquiring in-
\par
formation from others, as discussed in
\par
connection with expert opinion above, im-
\par
mediately suggests the reverse activity-
\par
the dissemination of information to other
\par
economic agents. This might be done for
\par
a price, as when one is hired as an expert,
\par
but (as we shall see below) sometimes it
\par
may pay to disseminate gratuitously, or
\par
even to incur cost to "push" information
\par
to others [60, Hirshleifer, 1973]. Advertis-
\par
ing is an obvious example. There is also
\par
a choice between disseminating publicly
\par
("publishing"), or else privately to a select
\par
audience. As a question of authenticity
\par
might arise in all such cases, the receiver
\par
of information may devote effort to the
\par
process of evaluation, possibly assisted by
\par
authentication activities (or hampered by
\par
deception activities) on the part of the dis-
\par
seminator. There is also the possibility of
\par
unintended dissemination, achieved by
\par
espionage or monitoring on the part of
\par
information-seekers-possibly leading to
\par
countermeasures in the form of security
\par
(secrecy-maintaining) activities by the
\par
possessors of information.
\par
Finally, there are classes of activities,
\par
apart from those involved with acquisition
\par
or dissemination of information, that are
\par
indirect consequences of informational
\par
patterns. Wagering, for example, typically
\par
follows from differences of opinion in a
\par
situation where conclusive information is
\par
anticipated (so as to determine who wins
\par
and who loses). Speculation is a somewhat
\par
analogous activity, which turns on a pro-
\par
spective revision of prices in consequence
\par
of the arrival of information. Another
\par
form of activity, which will be considered
\par
in the next section below, involves the
\par
adoption of more or less "flexible" posi-
\par
tions in anticipation of ability to make use
\par
of future information when it arrives.
\par
2.1.3 Emergent Information and the
\par
Value of Flexibility. In the sections pre-
\par
ceding we thought of information as being
\par
newly generated by an informational ac-
\par
tion like a sampling experiment or, alter-
\par
natively, as acquired from others via a
\par
transaction like the purchase of expert
\par
opinion. But in some cases information
\par
may autonomously emerge simply with
\par
the passage of time, without requiiring any
\par
direct action by recipients. Tomorrow's
\par
weather is uncertain today, but the uncer-
\par
