Absence of Prior-Round Markets:
\par
Total absence of prior-round markets is
\par
of course the most extreme form of incom-
\par
pleteness. In effect, what has happened
\par
is that the information arrives before any
\par
exchanges have taken place, while indi-
\par
viduals are still at their endowment posi-
\par
tions.
\par
This situation has aroused considerable
\par
interest, as it implies the surprising result
\par
that incoming public information may be
\par
socially disadvantageous in the sense that
\par
everyone in the economy might be willing
\par
to pay not to have it! [58, Hirshleifer, 1971;
\par
90, Marshall, 1974; 149, Zeckhauser, 1974;
\par
50, Nils H. Hakansson, J. Gregory Kunkel,
\par
and James A. Ohlson, 1979.] As among a
\par
group of traders who would otherwise
\par
have mutually insured against fire, a con-
\par
clusive message (as to whose houses would
\par
actually burn down) would negate the pos-
\par
sibility of mutually advantageous risk-
\par
sharing through insurance. The prospec-
\par
tive arrival of such information prior to
\par
the opening of markets imposes an undi-
\par
versifiable wealth-redistribution risk on
\par
the economy; no one can hedge against
\par
the price impact of the message to be re-
\par
ceived. (On the other hand, if earlier
\par
arrival of information permits more effec-
\par
tive productive adaptations, as in loss-re-
\par
duction measures against fire, this socially
\par
valuable feature must be weighed against
\par
elimination of the ability to spread risks.)
\par
