Futures Markets (FM):
\par
The CCM and NCM regimes both allow
\par
trading in state-contingent claims. Such
\par
trading does take place to some extent in
\par
the actual world, directly as in some insur-
\par
ance transactions or indirectly via trading
\par
in assets like corporate shares that can be
\par
regarded as packages of state-claims. But
\par
most of the trading observed in the world
\par
represents exchange of unconditional
\par
claims to goods. In a market regime allow-
\par
ing only the exchange of unconditional
\par
claims to G consumptive goods, under
\par
conclusive emergent information the
\par
prior and posterior rounds can be respec-
\par
tively identified with current "futures"
\par
markets versus later "spot" markets.
\par
Under such a regime of unconditional
\par
or, as we shall say, Futures Markets (FM)
\par
there will be just G tradable claims in the
\par
prior round followed by possible re-trad-
\par
ing in the same claims in the posterior
\par
round. Since it is reasonable to assume
\par
that G {$<$} S (there are many more conceiv-
\par
able contingencies than goods), it is evi-
\par
dent that the G+ Gmarkets in two rounds
\par
under FM cannot in general achieve the
\par
same efficiency as the S + G markets un-
\par
der NCM (or, a fortiori, as the G- S plus
\par
G markets under CCM) [142, Robert M.
\par
Townsend, 1978].
\par
This negative conclusion is somewhat
\par
mitigated, however, once we allow for the
\par
fact that emergent information in the
\par
world is only rarely conclusive. If im-
\par
proved though not yet conclusive public
\par
information emerges repeatedly, the mul-
\par
tiplication of rounds of trading recreated
\par
after each informational input increases
\par
the effectiveness of FM relative to NCM
\par
and CCM. (Once again, the proviso as to
\par
correct conditional price forecasting re-
\par
tains its relevance and is indeed increas-
\par
ingly difficult of achievement.) Also, in
\par
general, multiple rounds can only partially
\par
offset market incompleteness, so that full
\par
Pareto efficiency is not achieved. More
\par
troubling, there may be different self-ful-
\par
filling predictions about prices in future
\par
trading rounds, leading to different equi-
\par
librium allocations-and these allocations
\par
may be Pareto-rankable [52, Oliver D.
\par
Hart, 1975].
