2.2.2 Speculation. The term "specula-
\par
tion" has caused a good deal of confusion.
\par
Some authors loosely apply the word to
\par
arbitrage between markets, or to storage
\par
of goods over time or carriage over
\par
space-activities that do not involve un-
\par
certainty in any essential way. For our
\par
purposes, speculation is purchase with the
\par
intention of re-sale, or sale with the intent
\par
of re-purchase, where the uncertainty of
\par
the future spot price is a source of both
\par
risk and gain. The probabilistic variability
\par
of price is in turn due to anticipated emer-
\par
gence of information. Each possible mes-
\par
sage (in the conclusive information case
\par
that we shall be assuming here, this is
\par
equivalent to the advent of a single possi-
\par
ble state) leads to an associated equilib-
\par
rium posterior price vector, benefiting
\par
agents who adopted trading positions gen-
\par
erating relatively high conditional wealths
\par
for that state.
\par
Among the possible determinants of
\par
speculative activity, John Maynard
\par
Keynes [70, 1930] and John Hicks [55,
\par
1946] followed by many others have em-
\par
phasized differential risk-aversion. In
\par
their view, in the prior round of a Futures
\par
Markets (FM) regime the relatively risk-
\par
tolerant speculators accept risks of price
\par
variability from relatively risk-intolerant
\par
"hedgers." In the prior trading round,
\par
speculators buy commodity futures,
\par
achieving on average a small gain (excess
\par
of later mean spot price over futures
\par
price), which represents the return they
\par
receive from suppliers unwilling to bear
\par
the price risk. For example, a wheat-
\par
grower hedges by accepting a firm price
\par
now from a speculator, both of them antic-
\par
ipating that the unknown spot price will
\par
on average be a little higher. Later devel-
\par
opments along this line [63, Hendrik S.
\par
