\par
Houthakker, 1957; 64, 1968; 26, Paul H.
\par
Cootner, 1968] have brought out that
\par
hedgers can be on either side of the fu-
\par
tures market; speculators need bear only
\par
the imbalance between "long" and
\par
"short" hedgers' commitments, so that the
\par
risk-compensating average price move-
\par
ment could go either way. In contrast with
\par
these views, Holbrook Working [146,
\par
1953; 147, 1962] has denied that there is
\par
any systematic difference as to risk-toler-
\par
ance between those conventionally called
\par
speculators and hedgers. Working empha-
\par
sizes, instead, differences of belief (opti-
\par
mism or pessimism) as motivating futures
\par
trading.
\par
The Keynes-Hicks concentration upon
\par
aversion to "price risk" is seriously mis-
\par
leading. Individuals' prior-round trading
\par
decisions are affected by "quantity risk"
\par
(variability of endowments over states) as
\par
well as by price risk [95, Ronald I. McKin-
\par
non, 1967]. Indeed, from the social point
\par
of view, price uncertainty is the (inverse)
\par
reflection of an underlying uncertainty as
\par
to the future aggregate commodity totals.
\par
And these risks tend to be offsetting, re-
\par
ducing the need to engage in prior-round
\par
hedging activity. For example, when the
\par
crop of a representative wheat-grower is
\par
big (good news) he will find that the wheat
\par
price tends to be low (bad news) and vice
\par
versa. In a Futures Markets (FM) regime
\par
it is in general not possible to divest one-
\par
self of quantity risk (since only certainty
\par
claims can be traded); consequently, trad-
\par
ers might well find it preferable not to
\par
hedge against the offsetting price risk.
\par
In a world where people have a spec-
\par
trum of beliefs as to probabilities of future
\par
states, an individual's speculative activity
\par
proper (his adoption of a trading position
\par
in anticipation of arriving public informa-
\par
tion that will change market prices) de-
\par
pends in a rather complex way upon the
\par
degree of deviation of his beliefs from
\par
average opinion,'upon his willingness to
\par
tolerate risks, and upon his endowment
\par
position conjoined with the trading limita-
\par
tions imposed by a regime of incomplete
\par
markets [61, Hirshleifer, 1975; 62, 1977].
\par
With regard to the first of these determi-
\par
nants, a speculator with strongly deviant
\par
beliefs thinks that others will be surprised
\par
by the incoming message (and thus will
\par
be forced to make unanticipated posterior
\par
transactions). He will in consequence have
\par
adopted a trading position enabling him
\par
to benefit from these transactions. With
\par
regard to the second determinant, degree
\par
of risk-aversion affects the scale of pre-
\par
ferred speculative exposure. As to the re-
\par
gime of markets, we saw in the preceding
\par
section that (assuming "correct condi-
\par
tional price forecasting") re-trading possi-
\par
bilities are not needed under Complete
\par
Conditional Markets (CCM). Thus, incom-
\par
pleteness of prior-round markets is also
\par
a necessary condition for speculation [122,
\par
Stephen W. Salant, 1976; 38, George
\par
Feiger, 1976].
