2.3 The Economics of Research and
\par
Invention
\par
Research and invention activities are
\par
prime instances of the informational ac-
\par
tions studied, on the level of the individual
\par
economic agent, in Section 2.1 above. We
\par
are not dealing here with situations like
\par
those examined in Section 2.2, where pub-
\par
lic information simply emerges with the
\par
passage of time. Since Nature will not au-
\par
tonomously reveal her secret, it must be
\par
sought out by costly (generally private)
\par
search for the still-unknown "message."
\par
The topic of concern here however is not
\par
the informational decisions of an isolated
\par
Robinson Crusoe, but rather of individuals
\par
in a market environment facing rivalrous
\par
competition from some agents, but also
\par
having opportunities for mutually advan-
\par
tageous exchanges with others. In particu-
\par
lar, as we shall see, successful private
\par
search generally leads to more or less uni-
\par
versal dissemination of the discovery, with
\par
