\par
The central problem considered by
\par
modern analysts [80, Fritz Machlup, 1968;
\par
5, Arrow, 1962] has been the conflict be-
\par
tween the social goals of achieving effi-
\par
cient use of information once produced
\par
versus providing ideal motivation for
\par
production of information. With regard
\par
to optimal use, already-produced informa-
\par
tion is a "public good" in the sense that
\par
its availability to any member of society
\par
does not reduce the amount that could
\par
be made available to others. Then any bar-
\par
rier to use, as may stem from legal en-
\par
forcement of patents or copyrights or
\par
property in trade secrets, is inefficient. On
\par
the other hand, as in the standard public-
\par
good situation, there will be inadequate
\par
motivation to invest in production of in-
\par
formation if the product cannot be re-
\par
duced to legally protected property.
\par
Under ideal conditions the efficient-use
\par
problem could be solved by charging per-
\par
fectly-discriminating fees to license (non-
\par
exclusively) all uses of a given idea. If the
\par
discoverer were granted full property
\par
rights in the idea, as by a perpetual copy-
\par
right or patent, he in turn would have the
\par
optimal incentive to produce (search for)
\par
ideas. But in practice owners of copyrights
\par
or patents cannot impose perfectly-dis-
\par
criminating royalty fee structures on li-
\par
censees. A patentee might instead maxi-
\par
mize returns by granting exclusive
\par
licenses (in which case the social value of
\par
the excluded uses is of course lost) or by
\par
imposing fee structures that distort the
\par
marginal production decisions of licens-
\par
ees. On the other side of the picture, be-
\par
cause of the elusiveness of property in
\par
ideas, there is uncertainty and unreliabil-
\par
ity in the legal protection of patents and
\par
copyrights, and even less protection for
\par
trade secrets not covered by patent or
\par
copyright. The result is that unlicensed
\par
uses often escape control. Short of ideal
\par
conditions there will be losses from both
\par
underproduction and underutilization,
\par
and in practice something of a trade-off:
\par
provision of greater legal protection to in-
\par
ventors tends to ameliorate the underpro-
\par
duction problem, but to worsen the un-
\par
derutilization problem.
\par
More recent investigations have indi-
\par
cated, however, that not all the important
