2.4 Informational Advantage and Market
\par
Revelation of Information
\par
In the preceding sections we have con-
\par
sidered situations in which an individual
\par
could profit by timely publication of
\par
knowledge in his private possession. For
\par
example, an agent obtaining new informa-
\par
tion might transact at existing prices (take
\par
a speculative position), planning to sell out
\par
at the revised prices that ensue once he
\par
publicly discloses his knowledge. But
\par
there are two problems here. First, the
\par
agent must be able to move to a trading
\par
position (make a speculative commitment)
\par
without thereby revealing his secret-this
\par
is the problem of information leakage.
\par
Second, at the disclosure stage he must
\par
be able to authenticate the information
\par
he is trying to publicize-that is the prob-
\par
lem of signalling. We shall consider these
\par
two problems, in reverse order, in the sec-
\par
tions following.
\par
2.4.1 Signalling. The particular signal-
\par
ling problem that has aroused greatest in-
\par
terest arises when sellers of a higher-qual-
\par
ity product or service are attempting to
\par
convey that message (that their product
\par
is high-quality) to buyers. Of course, any
\par
seller is motivated to claim that his is a
\par
high-quality product. Signalling as a solu-
\par
