\par
Prices in an economy will be related
\par
to the knowledge and beliefs of all partici-
\par
pating traders-possibly weighted by en-
\par
dowed wealths, degree of risk-aversion,
\par
etc. Under the heading of informational
\par
efficiency, the issue has been raised as to
\par
whether prices "fully reflect" people's
\par
current information [35, Fama, 1970].
\par
Unfortunately, the meaning of the term
\par
"fully reflect" has proved elusive. Mark
\par
E. Rubinstein has proposed that informa-
\par
tion be said to be already reflected in
\par
prices if, upon arrival of the message, trad-
\par
ers have no incentive to revise portfolios
\par
[121, 1975]. On this definition, he shows
\par
that prices can almost never "fully reflect
\par
information." As follows from our Section
\par
2.2 above, there are two main reasons: (1)
\par
even with agreed beliefs, incomplete re-
\par
gimes of prior markets generally make
\par
posterior trading (portfolio revision) un-
\par
avoidable, and (2) with diverging beliefs
\par
and consequent speculative prior trading,
\par
it will be necessary to close out speculative
\par
positions in the posterior round.
\par
The most widely held interpretation-
\par
what has been called the "Weak form" of
\par
the proposition-is that markets are "in-
\par
formationally efficient" if, as a pragmatic
\par
matter, there is no way to make a profit
\par
(more precisely, to achieve an expected
\par
utility gain) from information already in
\par
the public domain. In particular, there is
\par
no way to outsmart the stockmarket by
\par
detecting patterns of price movement in
\par
the historic record.
\par
This interpretation in effect asserts that,
\par
at least on average, individuals can process
\par
available information correctly. (While
\par
costs of processing may prevent perfect
\par
adjustment, errors tend to cancel out.)
\par
More specifically, this has been taken to
\par
imply that prices should follow a mar-
\par
tingale process. That is, except perhaps
\par
for time and risk adjustment factors, the
\par
price today should be the mathematical
\par
expectation of the price tomorrow [123,
\par
Paul A. Samuelson, 1965]. However, it has
\par
been shown that even supposing known
\par
or agreed probabilities (without which it
\par
would be impossible to calculate mathe-
\par
matical expectations), only under very
\par
special conditions does a martingale in
\par
prices result [145, Susan E. Woodward,
\par
1979]. First of all, prices are ratios, if a
\par
given ratio followed the martingale prop-
\par
erty, in general its reciprocal (the ratio
\par
taken the other way) would not. And even
\par
if expressed in terms of some standard
\par
numeraire commodity, prices would not
\par
follow a martingale unless there were no
\par
utility complementarities between the
\par
numeraire and other goods [122, Salant,
\par
1976]. It has also sometimes been argued
\par
that failure of prices to follow a martingale
\par
would by definition create an arbitrage-
\par
like profit opportunity in the sense of a
\par
positive expectation of gain from holding
\par
an asset, over and above the normal inter-
\par
est yield. But, as we have seen from our
\par
early discussion of "private and social risk"
\par
in Section 1.2, a higher mathematical ex-
\par
pectation of income does not in general
\par
