represent higher expected utility, so this
\par
argument is erroneous.
\par
The "strong form" of the efficient mar-
\par
ket hypothesis has been taken to mean
\par
that even private information cannot be
\par
profitably used (compare our discussion of
\par
"information leakage" in Section 2.4.2
\par
above). This form of the hypothesis does
\par
not seem consistent with the evidence-
\par
for example, of gains from insider trading
\par
(surveyed in Thomas E. Copeland and J.
\par
Fred Weston [27, 1979, chap. 9]). As for
\par
the "weak form," numerous econometric
\par
studies have concluded that it is not possi-
\par
ble to reject the hypothesis that price
\par
changes are independent of past prices.
\par
However, there does not yet seem to be
\par
a sufficiently well-specified model to allow
\par
testing the hypothesis that price changes
\par
are independent of all public information
\par
(but see Stephen Figlewski [39a, 1979]).
\par
Informational activities, finally, have an
\par
unusual relation to economic equilibrium.
\par
Information generation is in large part a
\par
disequilibrium-creating process [129, Jo-
\par
seph A. Schumpeter, (1911) 1936], and in-
\par
formation dissemination a disequilibrium-
\par
repairing process. The two are inter-
\par
twined, as we have seen, in very complex
\par
ways. It does not yet seem that we are
\par
very close to having an efficiency concept
\par
that can usefully be employed to measure
\par
the dynamically optimal level of such ac-
\par
tivities.
