\par
FORECASTING AND PLANNING: AN EVALUATION*
\par
ROBIN M. HOGARTHt AND SPYROS MAKRIDAKIS\$
\par
The formal practice of forecasting and planning (F \& P) has risen to prominence within a
\par
few decades and now receives considerable attention from both academics and practitioners.
\par
This paper explicitly recognizes the nature of F \& P as future-oriented decision making
\par
activities and, as such, their dependence upon judgmental inputs. A review of the extensive
\par
psychological literature on human judgmental abilities is provided from this perspective. It is
\par
argued that many of the numerous information processing limitations and biases revealed in
\par
this literature apply to tasks performed in F\&P. In particular, the "illusion of control,"
\par
accumulation of redundant information, failure to seek possible disconfirming evidence, and
\par
overconfidence in judgment are liable to induce serious errors in F \&P. In addition, insuffi-
\par
cient attention has been given to the implications of numerous studies that show that the
\par
predictive judgment of humans is frequently less accurate than that of simple quantitative
\par
models. Applied studies of F\&P are also reviewed and shown to mirror many of the findings
\par
from psychology. The paper subsequently draws implications from these reviews and suggests
\par
reconceptualizing F \&P through use of decision-theoretic concepts. At the organizational level
\par
this involves recognizing that F\&P may perform many, often conflicting, manifest and latent
\par
functions which.should be identified and evaluated through a multi-attribute utility frame-
\par
work. Operationally, greater use should be made of sensitivity analysis and the concept of the
\par
value of information.
\par
(PLANNING; FORECASTING APPLICATIONS)
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Intuitive forecasting and planning are not new phenomena. On the other hand,
\par
formal Forecasting and Planning (F\&P) activities have risen to prominence in busi-
\par
ness, nonprofit, and public organizations within only a few decades. Furthermore,
\par
annual expenditure related to F\&P now involves billions of dollars.
\par
The utility of these activities has, however, been questioned (e.g., [56], [173]). The
\par
purpose of this paper, therefore, is to assess forecasting accuracy and planning
\par
effectiveness in organizations and to provide guidelines to calibrate expectations.
\par
There are three sections. First, since F\&P concern future decisions, we explicitly
\par
recognize their dependence upon human judgment by reviewing findings from psychol-
\par
ogy. In ?3, we assess current approaches to F \& P. ?4 summarizes strengths and
\par
weaknesses of F\&P and proposes alternative conceptualizations.
\par
2. Human Judgmental Abilities: Implications for F \& P
\par
Although there is no generally accepted definition of planning, most writers agree
\par
that it involves attempts at purposeful, future oriented decision making. The distinc-
\par
tion between formal and informal planning in organizations can however be defined
\par
operationally by the degree of specificity of the former, as for example when plans are
\par
committed to writing (cf. [160]).
\par
As a decision making activity, planning requires (see, e.g., [1]): (1) The existence of
