\par
values and goals; (2) the generation of alternative courses of action; (3) the assessment
\par
of alternatives; and (4) the implementation of the alternative selected. (5) Forecasts are
\par
also needed in both the generation and assessment of alternatives and the prediction of
\par
future states of the environment. In addition, (6) monitoring and control are often
\par
mentioned as integral elements of planning.
\par
For problems of any complexity, planning clearly necessitates a wide variety of
\par
inputs and skills. Values and goals ,are difficult to communicate and often must be
\par
sensed by planners rather than directly obtained from policymakers [19]. The genera-
\par
tion of alternatives and many other aspects of forecasting require much imagination
\par
and creative thinking. On the other hand, the assessment of alternatives and certain
\par
aspects of forecasting call for high analytic competence and facility with the tools of
\par
management science. Implementation, monitoring and control require managerial
\par
skills not least of which is the ability to handle the political processes within organiza-
\par
tions.
\par
The evaluation of planning is problematic in that so many components contribute to
\par
its relative effectiveness. Good plans can fail through poor implementation. Erroneous
\par
forecasts can kill the best of plans, and accurate forecasts be offset through poor
\par
planning. Furthermore, goals and values can neither be "right" nor "wrong," although
\par
one could question their coherence.
\par
Judgment necessarily plays a crucial role in F \& P. Consider, for example, the
\par
specification of goals, intuitive predictions, choices concerning data sources, forecast-
\par
ing methodologies, adjustments to model-derived forecasts, and the assessment of the
\par
feasibility of implementation strategies.
\par
There are two key findings from cognitive psychology relating to human judgment:
\par
(1) ability to process information is limited; and (2) people are adaptive. Thus in order
\par
to understand the process of judgment it is necessary to specify the context in which it
\par
occurs [23], [145]. The importance of a person's cognitive system can be gauged by
\par
noting that the efficiency of purposeful behavior depends upon an understanding of
\par
the environment in which it occurs. People are strongly motivated to understand, and
\par
thus control the environment in which they live [172]. This search for understanding and
\par
control is, we maintain, the "raison d'etre" of F \& P activities, and when coupled with
