\par
The actual outcomes people experience, however, depend not only on their own
\par
actions, but on events outside their control (i.e., chance). This statement can be
\par
illuminated by considering Exhibit 1 which provides a conceptual diagram of stages of
\par
information processing.
\par
The model implicit in Exhibit 1 conceives of judgment taking place within a system
\par
involving three elements in mutual interaction [81]. First, there is the person; second,
\par
the task environment within which the person makes judgments; and third, actions
\par
that result from judgment and which can subsequently affect both the person and the
\par
task environment. In Exhibit 1, the person is represented by his or her "schema," by
\par
which is meant the person's belief and value system relative to the judgmental task.
\par
The operations of judgment are decomposed into: (a) acquisition of information; (b)
\par
processing of the information accessed; and (c) output. Output implies action which
\par
together with external factors yields an outcome. The outcome then feeds back into the
\par
person's schema and can also affect the task environment. At the same time, the
\par
person's schema affects the perception of the environment and its complexity, the
\par
problem identification, the tasks involved, the type of action required, the objectives
\par
and so forth.
\par
Much evidence indicates that superficial information search and processing biases
\par
cause gross errors in human decision making [78], [83], [101], [152], [155]. To relate this
\par
evidence to F\&P, Exhibit 2 summarizes the main sources of bias organized by the
\par
stages of information processing indicated in Exhibit 1. It also provides brief definitions,
\par
descriptions, illustrations, and references to the source literature.
