The list of references provided here cannot claim to be comprehensive; however, review papers are cited in which readers will be
\par
able to find additional references. It should also be added that individuals may vary on their susceptibility to different forms of bias.
\par
A large literature exists, for example, concerning "cognitive styles" [133].
\par
Of crucial concern to this paper are the conditions under which the biases described
\par
in Exhibit 2 occur, and their consequent effects on F \& P. Exhibit 1 indicates that
\par
understanding of a judgmental task-as represented in the person's schema-is
\par
mediated by several stages of information processing and links with the environment.
\par
In particular, links between the person's schema, actions, outcomes and feedback to
\par
the schema are crucial. If the action-outcome-feedback links are short and frequent, the
\par
individual is in a good position to learn about, and thus comprehend, the probable
\par
effects of actions on outcomes: short links enhance the ability to improve decision
\par
making by taking corrective actions. The opposite is true when the links are infrequent,
\par
long (in time) and subject to distortion. This suggests that people will have difficulty in
\par
providing adequate inputs in many F \& P activities where the action-outcome-feedback
\par
links are neither short nor clear.
\par
On the other hand, the judgmental performances people exhibit through motor skills
\par
are considerable. Imagine, for example, the series of complex judgmental tasks
\par
accomplished when driving a car, or skiing. However, note that these situations can be
\par
characterized by short and frequent action-outcome-feedback loops. Whereas evolu-
\par
