\par
tion has provided humans with considerable facility for making judgments that meet
\par
the characteristics of motor tasks, this is not true of many conceptual judgments (cf.
\par
[81], [149]). Indeed, most of the biases documented in Exhibit 2 have been demon-
\par
strated in situations where short feedback loops have either been lacking or, when
\par
present, have not necessarily been readily interpretable by the individual [52]. Interes-
\par
tingly enough, experiments which have examined intuitive decision making skills in
\par
dynamic environments involving interdependent, sequential judgmental tasks coupled
\par
with feedback on performance show people to be quite efficient [81].
\par
A further point concerning the results summarized in Exhibit 2 is the criticism that
\par
many biases found to operate in the unfamiliar circumstances of the psychological
\par
laboratory might not generalize to more naturalistic environments. However, in a
\par
rapidly changing world it is uncertain to what environment laboratory experiments
\par
should and can generalize. The lack of ability to handle unfamiliar tasks is clearly no
\par
cause for optimism [54], [152].
\par
A benchmark of judgmental ability is provided by numerous studies that have
\par
compared the relative predictive performance of experts and simple quantitative
\par
models. Almost all indicate the superiority of models [40], [66], [102], [143]. (For a
\par
review see [7, pp. 251-259 and pp. 363-372].) Although difficult to accept emotionally,
\par
such evidence must be considered seriously. Decision and policy makers should
\par
become aware of the nature of their inherent judgmental limitations.
\par
What implications do these findings have for F \& P? First, to avoid information
\par
acquisition biases it is necessary to sample deliberately from as wide a base as possible,
\par
to avoid accepting forecasts and plans in haste, and to strive to find information that
\par
could disconfirm hypotheses and forecasts. Too often people seek information to
\par
confirm their existing ideas, and often redundantly, rather than more potentially valid
\par
disconfirming evidence [170]. Institutionalizing procedures using counter-attitudinal
\par
role playing, dialectics, or "devil's advocates" could be helpful in this respect [34].
\par
Second, since people are inefficient at aggregating information, this should be done
\par
mechanically where possible [51]-see also ?4. Third, greater care needs to be exer-
\par
cised in interpreting the apparent causes of outcomes. Consider, for example, the
\par
so-called "illusion of control." Langer [96] has documented how even in chance
\par
determined situations (e.g., lotteries), observing an early sequence of "successes" can
\par
lead people to believe they have some control over outcomes. Similarly, if people are
\par
allowed to engage in cognitive activity about the outcome prior to its occurrence (e.g.,
\par
by choosing a ticket number), they are also inclined to believe they gain some control.
\par
These findings are entirely consistent with the need to master and control the environ-
\par
ment.
\par
Detecting patterns to control outcomes is functional. F\&P activities fall precisely
\par
into the types of situation studied by Langer [96]. People spend time on forecasting
\par
and elaborating plans with expectations of increasing control. Furthermore, in judging
\par
the effectiveness of F \& P, it does not seem unreasonable to attribute successful
\par
outcomes to accurate forecasting and good planning, but to discount failures (see also
\par
[97]). The use of F \& P to reduce uncertainty and gain control is both functional for
\par
organizations and understandable given human needs. The "illusion," however, con-
\par
sists of failing to attribute to outcomes the appropriate relative contributions of skill
\par
(including use of F \& P) and chance.
\par
Suggestions for overcoming some important judgmental biases enumerated in Ex-
\par
hibit 2 have been provided by Kahneman and Tversky [90] and Spetzler and Stael von
\par
Holstein [158]. In particular, these papers provide procedures to guard against over-
