3. Forecasting and Planning: Empirical Evidence
\par
The separation of forecasting accuracy and planning effectiveness in the overall
\par
assessment of F\&P is problematic. This section examines the predictive accuracy of
\par
F\&P divided into activities concerning long-, medium- and short-term horizons. We
\par
also make some general observations. In the subsequent section, other factors are
\par
considered.
\par
Long-range Forecasting and Planning. Long-range forecasting (two years or longer) is
\par
notoriously inaccurate. Ascher [10] has examined the predictive accuracy of forecast-
\par
ing (and indirectly, planning) in the fields of population, economics, energy, transpor-
\par
tation, and technology. His conclusions are pessimistic. Ascher found errors varying
\par
from a few to a few hundred percentage points, as well as systematic biases. He also
\par
stated that one could not specify beforehand which forecasting approach, or fore-
\par
caster, would have been right or wrong. Furthermore, because policymakers are
\par
supplied with so many, varying forecasts, the problem of "choosing" a forecast can be
\par
as difficult as making one's own. Parenthetically, it should be noted that the fields
\par
examined by Ascher are characterized by much experience and expertise in making
\par
forecasts as well as readily available data. One can therefore imagine the situation in
\par
other fields with data less "suitable" for forecasting (i.e., with less aggregation and
\par
greater fluctuations).
\par
Ascher's conclusions are echoed by opinions expressed in the long-term forecasting
\par
literature (e.g., [64]). It is difficult to assess the size of forecasting errors; unforeseen
\par
changes in trends can occur; discontinuities are possible; and new events or conditions
\par
emerge. Moreover, past data can provide contradictory clues to future trends [43]. For
\par
instance, while growth of some products in an industry can occur in one way, others
\par
follow different patterns [21]. As we argue below (?4), planning activities must accept
\par
the inaccuracy inherent in long-term forecasts. Even in the early seventies, for
\par
example, how many imagined the possibility of an oil embargo, a quadrupling of oil
\par
prices, severe shortage of raw materials, stagflation, high unemployment together with
\par
high inflation and interest rates, a near collapse of the stock market, and two
\par
recessions in a period of less than five years?
\par
Despite many fervent proponents, there is concurrently much disappointment in
\par
long-range planning as presently practiced [43], [56], [76], [137], [159], [173]. Long-
\par
range planning, it is claimed, has fallen short of its promises and fundamental changes
\par
are necessary both in conception and execution. This has given rise to "strategic
\par
planning" (see [5], [72]). In practice, however, "many managers use the phrases
\par
strategic planning and long-range planning interchangeably" [72, p. 24]. Long-range
\par
planning, it should be noted, grew and flourished in the sixties [110], a period
\par
characterized by relative stability and high growth rates. Furthermore, forecasting
\par
errors tended to be positive (i.e., actual values exceeded forecasts). Thus, even if plans
\par
proved to be "wrong," few complained of the direction of the errors. However, this did
\par
not occur in the seventies when forecasting was on occasion grossly in error in the
\par
opposite direction.
\par
Medium-term Forecasting and Planning. Medium-term plans (3 months to 2 years)
\par
are theoretically derived from long-term plans and incorporate medium-term forecasts,
\par
estimates of available resources, constraints and competitive considerations. The most
