\par
Considerable misconceptions exist concerning the ability of economists and business
\par
forecasters to predict important changes either in the general level of economic activity
\par
or for a given industry, firm or product. Cyclical turning points, in particular, are
\par
notoriously difficult to forecast [114], [119]. The problem faced by economists and
\par
planners is two-fold: first, unanticipated recessions occur; second, predicted recessions
\par
fail to materialize. Furthermore, the timing of economic recessions and accelerations is
\par
frequently missed. Finally, as with long-term forecasts, there are many different
\par
forecasts available from which people can choose those that best fit their preconcep-
\par
tions.
\par
How well have forecasters and planners performed in medium-term F \& P situa-
\par
tions? Exhibit 3 summarizes empirical findings and provides detailed references.
\par
Mirroring the findings documented in Exhibit 2, it should be noted that in these applied
\par
studies forecasters and planners have shown systematic deficiencies in their predictions
\par
and plans for the future; furthermore, in a majority of cases their estimates are less
\par
accurate than those of simple quantitative models (however, for exceptions see the
\par
finance literature, e.g., [21]).
