An important question concerns the extent to which operational plans are met either
\par
because forecasts were correct and plans appropriate or because forecasts and plans
\par
became the targets of self-fulfilling or self-defeating prophecies. In other words, could
\par
any reasonable forecasts and plans be attained without commitment and corrective
\par
actions? Alternatively, is it possible that effective forecasting and planning can invali-
\par
date the original forecasts (e.g., consider actions taken by a government to avoid a
\par
predicted recession)?
\par
Short-term Forecasting and Planning. There is considerable inertia in most economic
\par
and natural phenomena. Thus the present states of many variables are predictive of
\par
the short-term future (i.e., three months or less). Rather simple, mechanistic methods
\par
such as those used in time series forecasting can often make accurate short-term
\par
forecasts and even outperform more theoretically elegant and elaborate approaches
\par
used in econometric forecasting [6], [114].
\par
Short-term planning is characterized by several operations essential to basic business
\par
