\par
functions, e.g., establishment of schedules for production, distribution and employ-
\par
ment, cash management, etc. It is the only form of F\&P for which forecasts can be
\par
reasonably accurate and where real gains can be made consistently. However, it
\par
typically receives less attention than it merits.
\par
Some comparisons in Exhibit 3 involve short-term F \& P. These indicate both
\par
systematic biases and large errors; quantitative models outperform judgmental fore-
\par
casts; in addition, simpler models are often at least as accurate as sophisticated ones;
\par
even random walk models sometimes outpredict the alternative formulations. More-
\par
over, simple decision rules can often be as effective as elaborate F\&P procedures (see
\par
also below).
\par
General Examination of Forecasting and Planning. Comments in this section have
\par
concerned different time horizons. Other evidence, however, bears on F\&P irrespec-
\par
tive of horizon length.
\par
In an important study, Grinyer and Norburn [70] attempted to relate measures of
\par
corporate financial and management performance to planning activities. They exam-
\par
ined 21 companies in a variety of industries. No significant relationship was found
\par
between extent of planning and financial and management measures. Indeed, the only
\par
significant relationship detected was "between the number of items of information
\par
used in reaching decisions on appropriate action and financial performance." Further
\par
studies have also indicated lack of relationships between planning and various mea-
\par
sures of financial performance [94], [99]. On the other hand, studies by Karger and
\par
Malik [91], Herold [77], and Thune and House [160] concluded that formal planning
\par
was positively related to financial performance; in addition, a study by Ansoff et al. [4]
\par
indicates that mergers were managed more effectively with explicit planning. The
\par
evidence is unclear and was recently well summarized by Wood and LaForce [174]:
\par
"the review of the available empirical studies disclosed conflicting findings in the
\par
planning evaluation area" (p. 517). More studies are, therefore, urgently needed
\par
despite the inherent methodological problems of isolating the effects of formal plan-
\par
ning systems from other variables.
\par
Weaker and more difficult comparisons could also be made between organizations
\par
at different time periods. Is current performance better than in the past? It is difficult
\par
to state that there has been marked improvement because of planning per se. Indeed,
\par
some even claim the opposite [173]. Other possible comparisons concern countries with
\par
different levels of commitment to planning. Do, for instance, Eastern European
\par
countries with their planned, centralized economies fare better than their Western
\par
counterparts? Similarly, has a country like France, with its heavy commitment to
\par
planning, performed better (or have people been more satisfied) than countries with
\par
lower planned inputs, such as Germany or the U.S.A.?
\par
Marquand [1 16], a planning official in the English government summarizes precisely
\par
the essence of the dilemma between more or less planning activities:
\par
U.K. economic performance was inferior to that of, on the one hand, France with its formal 5
\par
year plans and its dirigiste politics, and on the other to that of Germany where Chancellor
\par
Erhard's economic miracle had produced similar effects by opposite means. How far were the
\par
methods adopted in each country a source of their success, and in which direction should the
\par
United Kingdom move? (p. 3)
\par
There are so many factors involved that F\&P can only play a secondary role in
\par
accounting for the relative performance of organizations and governments. Thus, it
\par
cannot be said that F\&P are not useful tools for decision and policy makers. On the
