\par
4. Forecasting and Planning: Implications and Reconceptualization
\par
We first emphasize the principal similarities between findings in the psychology of
\par
judgment and evidence and observations on F \&P. Subsequently we draw implications
\par
and make suggestions for reconceptualizing these activities.
\par
Similarities between Psychological Findings and Evidence on F\& P. The human need
\par
to master and control the environment is evident in F\&P. Furthermore, there is
\par
uncanny similarity between the history of F\&P and experiments concerning the
\par
"illusion of control" [96], [97] discussed in ?2. Observation of successes in predicting
\par
the outcomes of known random processes (e.g., coin-tossing) can lead to unfounded
\par
beliefs of control in experimental situations. Similarly, the successes met by F\&P in
\par
the sixties caused by under- as opposed to over-prediction seem to have led to
\par
analogous real-world illusions of control which have been shattered by subsequent
\par
events. Nonetheless, people have a tendency to attribute success to their own efforts
\par
and failure to external factors [82]. Indeed, both animals and humans have sometimes
\par
been shown to develop more effective decisions if they do have "illusions of control"-
\par
the illusion leads to more proactive and self-fulfilling behavior [132]. The issue that is
\par
not clear, however, is the extent to which such illusions are functional.
\par
Linked to the illusion of control are tendencies to see patterns where none exist
\par
[146]. These are further related to extensive findings indicating that intuitive notions of
\par
probabilistic concepts are deficient. Experiments show that people often lack the
\par
concepts of independence between random events and sampling variability [88]; in
\par
addition, they frequently underestimate the uncertainty inherent in the environment
\par
[78] which leads, inter alia, to mistaken confidence in judgment [103]. For F \&P, the
\par
implications involve the all too frequent surprises by unforeseen events which, in turn,
\par
discredit F \&P.
\par
Paul Samuelson has probably best captured the inability of forecasters and planners
\par
to understand the full extent of uncertainty: "I think that the greatest error in
\par
forecasting is not realizing how important are the probabilities of events other than
\par
those everyone is agreeing upon" [142, p. 51].
\par
A further important psychological finding is that although the availability of
\par
additional information increases confidence in judgment, it does not necessarily
\par
increase predictive accuracy [130], [151]. This has serious implications for F\&P given
\par
the tendency to consult and subscribe to many forecasting services, and to create huge
\par
data banks. Furthermore, since people have a tendency to retain information selec-
\par
tively in accord with their prejudices, and to reject possible disconfirming evidence, the
\par
potential dysfunctional consequences of collecting data from many, often differing
\par
sources is disturbing. As more information becomes available, it is increasingly easier
\par
to "prove" what one wishes. Emshoff and Mitroff [56] emphasize this point in relation
\par
to problems engendered by large, strategic MIS: "Access to more information results
\par
in its selective use to support preconceived positions . . . . They (managers) assume
\par
that the quality of decisions has improved because of the amount of information that
\par
support it" (p. 50).
\par
Finally, as can be verified by comparing Exhibits 2 and 3, there are many other
\par
similarities between the literatures on information processing biases and F\&P.
