\par
Suggestions for Reconceptualizing F\& P. We preface our suggestions by considering
\par
human conceptual skills from an evolutionary perspective. Skills have been developed
\par
over millenia for dealing with a relatively slowly changing environment. Furthermore
\par
such skills were necessarily first developed to ensure physical survival. Since humans
\par
adapted to the environment, it is environmental demands that preceded human
\par
development. However, within this century there has been dramatic acceleration in the
\par
rate of environmental change. Paradoxically, it is human conceptual development that
\par
has triggered these changes through technological breakthroughs.
\par
This emphasis on evolutionary processes stresses the need for organizations to have
\par
an adaptive approach to F \& P. This, we argue, involves: (1) acknowledgement of
\par
human limitations and possibilities; (2) explicitly recognizing the functions F\&P can
\par
serve within organizations; (3) issues concerning the precision of both goals and
\par
forecasts and the time-frame within which F \& P are considered; and (4) evaluating the
\par
benefits and costs of the often conflicting goals that F \&P attempt to meet.
\par
(1) We have already said much about human limitations. Of particular importance
\par
is the need to develop appropriate attitudes for facing the uncertainty inherent in the
\par
future. People should heed Russell's admonition to learn "how to live without
\par
certainty, and yet without being paralysed by hesitation" [140, p. 14]. Forecasting must
\par
therefore be used to identify sources of uncertainty in the environment. Planning
\par
should concern itself with developing policies which acknowledge the uncertainties and
\par
are on the efficient frontier. Planning cannot assume forecast accuracy.
\par
(2) Some argue that executives and policymakers do admit the inherent deficiencies
\par
of planning but recognize functional side-effects such as improvements in communica-
\par
tion and coordination, educating people to think explicitly about the future, and the
\par
use of planning as a mechanism for motivation and control. However, the question
\par
that should be raised is whether these side-effects could not be achieved more
\par
effectively through direct means. Consider, for example, specific programs to improve
\par
coordination. Two programs we believe planners should consider concern (a) the
\par
cognitive biases enumerated in Exhibit 2 and (b) training in imagination and creativ-
\par
ity. Furthermore, there are many advantages to helping people make explicit the
\par
causal chains (and thus assumptions) they use in confronting the future. For an
\par
appropriate methodology and example, see [75].
\par
Both the manifest and latent functions of F \&P need to be specified and, in the case
\par
of the latter, compared to other procedures that could meet the same goals.
\par
(3) The specificity of goals and forecasts should be linked to the time-frame. Long-
\par
and medium-term plans involve periods for which action-outcome-feedback loops (see
\par
Exhibit 1) are necessarily deficient and where learning is problematic. However,
\par
objectives that define direction are necessary. The precision with which such direction
\par
is specified should, we argue, be an inverse function of the length of the planning
\par
horizon. Whereas text-books advocate precise goals, there are many advantages to
\par
deliberate ambiguity. March [115] has made a penetrating analysis of this issue. He
\par
points out that the formulation of and adherence to specific goals at a particular point
\par
in time can hamper learning and adaptation since both experience and environmental
\par
changes modify values. Specific goals can be interpreted too narrowly leading to
\par
so-called goal displacement (where the objective to attain indicators of goals becomes
\par
more important than the actual goals); furthermore, the creative interpretation of
\par
ambiguous goals can be most important. Note that March does not state simplistically
\par
that goals should be fuzzy; rather there is what he calls an "optimal clarity problem."
\par
