\par
Analogous points can be made about the precision of medium- and long-term
\par
forecasts. One should use such forecasts (as aids to contemplate possible trends for
\par
example), but not believe them.
\par
In the context of economic planning, Bray [19] suggests a model which implies
\par
selecting a trajectory over time which can be adjusted en route. Taking the position
\par
that forecasts are necessarily inaccurate in noisy systems, he argues that planning
\par
should be based on the principle of setting control parameters to maintain the
\par
trajectory of the system within certain limits as opposed to basing periodic actions on
\par
predictions. In a simulation study, Bray compared this principle to the "stop-go"
\par
policies followed by successive U.K. governments in the post-World War II period;
\par
results indicated that the U.K. would be considerably better off today had she
\par
followed Bray's principles. Although this approach can be criticized-it assumes an
\par
excellent model of the underlying process-we believe it deserves further development.
\par
Given the limitations of F\&P, what should be done from a practical viewpoint?
\par
Would it be better, for example, to abandon formal F\&P and rely solely on intuitive
\par
procedures? We do not think so.
\par
First, intuitive procedures do not have an impressive record (recall Exhibits 2 and 3).
\par
Second, in the short-term, "traditional" F \& P are not only feasible but can be
\par
accurate. Indeed, we believe that few organizations avail themselves of the consider-
\par
able benefits to be had in this domain. For instance, evidence has been cited that
\par
quantitative, and particularly simple models can outperform humans in a wide range
\par
of situations (cf. Exhibit 3). In Exhibit 4 we further summarize studies showing that
\par
quite simple models can provide comparable, and often better results than more
\par
sophisticated models. Formal F \& P can and should be used in "traditional" mode for
\par
short-term situations. On the other hand, more careful analysis is needed concerning
\par
longer F \& P horizons.
\par
(4) It should be recognized that the choice of an F \& P system for the medium- and
\par
long-term is itself a decision. That is, organizations face an "optimal planning"
\par
problem and thus decision-theoretic principles are in order. At a conceptual level, it is
\par
convenient to distinguish between two kinds of costs and benefits of F \& P: (1) dollar
\par
gains/losses from accurate/inaccurate F \& P per se; and (ii) effects of F \& P proce-
\par
dures on the organization (e.g., on motivation and commitment). The operational
\par
decision-theoretic concepts we believe relevant to these issues are (a) sensitivity analy-
\par
sis, (b) assessing the value of information, and (c) the use of multi-attribute utility
\par
analysis as an aid to both planning and organizational design.
\par
First, whereas organizations must accept the inaccuracy of forecasts, the fact that
\par
losses from forecasting errors are often not symmetric also needs to be explicitly
\par
recognized. Planning alternatives have different risk profiles and sensitivity analysis can
\par
and should be used to test the relative robustness of alternative strategies to forecasting
\par
errors. Because the judgment literature indicates that people often lack imagination
\par
and are confronted by too many "surprises" even when attempting to think probabi-
\par
listically, we strongly recommend varying subjective inputs to simulation models and
\par
sensitivity analysis to a far greater extent than is currently done. Furthermore, one
\par
should also question whether the structure of the models used, and not just the values
\par
of some variables, is appropriate. That is, sensitivity analysis also needs to be applied
\par
to the manner in which planning problems are formulated. The "illusion of control," it
\par
should be recalled, tends to restrict one's beliefs to considering only certain scenarios
\par
as possible and people often fail to search actively for possible disconfirming evidence.
