\par
goals of F \& P activities, the extent to which they are compatible and/or one's
\par
willingness to make trade-offs. Consider, for example, the desire to have "flexible"
\par
plans but to use the F\&P system as a motivational device. Could a flexible plan
\par
advocating a growth rate between 2\% and 8\% really provide an adequate springboard
\par
for motivation? On the other hand, setting say a 10\% growth rate could also have
\par
dysfunctional consequences. Imagine, for instance, a manager under pressure offering
\par
discounts to meet targets in the short-term, but in so doing affecting the stability of
\par
prices in the medium-term and profitability in the long-term.
\par
Both the design of F\&P systems and the assessment of organizational and strategic
\par
consequences can be considerably facilitated by using the framework of multi-attribute
\par
utility analysis. Using this framework as a conceptual tool, different F\& P systems and
\par
plans can be considered as decision alternatives where the dimensions of evaluation
\par
(i.e., attributes) are both organizational consequences (e.g., flexibility, motivation,
\par
control) and the more traditional F\&P measures (e.g., relative expected performance
\par
in dollar consequences). Whereas one should clearly not expect precise answers from
\par
multi-attribute models, the wide range of situations to which they have been applied
\par
attests to their ability to provide insights into complex problems. Consider, for
\par
example, reports in this area that have covered complex issues such as the evaluation
\par
of social programs [49], air pollution control programs, the selection of computer
\par
systems, determining corporate strategy, and the siting of airports and nuclear power
\par
facilities [92], to name but a few. Advantages to be had by conceptualizing F\&P
\par
within the multi-attribute framework include: (1) A synthetic, evaluative overview of
\par
the issues; (2) recognition that explicit trade-offs need to be made between attributes
\par
of F\&P systems (e.g., organizational flexibility vs. profits from the realization of
\par
particular projects); (3) greater possibilities of detecting weaknesses in existing plans
\par
which, in turn, can lead to designing new plans and procedures; (4) recognition that
\par
specific knowledge about both organizational processes and the effects of F \& P
\par
systems is deficient. Furthermore, this can be significant in leading to more precise and
\par
relevant questions about these issues some of which could be amenable to research (cf.
\par
[127]); and most importantly (5) monitoring the F\&P system itself by updating the
\par
multi-attribute analysis over time on a continuing basis, i.e., by refining data inputs
\par
and considering new criteria and alternatives as information is received and situations
\par
evolve. Although the difficulties of achieving an adequate multi-attribute representa-
\par
tion of the F \& P problem should not be underestimated, we stress: (1) the choice of the
\par
best system available is relative rather than absolute; (2) although many subjective
\par
inputs to such analyses are necessarily of dubious precision, rough orders of magnitude
\par
are to be preferred to none. Furthermore, there are less illusions if one knows which
\par
inputs are based on questionable data; (3) the multi-attribute procedure can raise
\par
questions which are currently obfuscated; and (4) like long-term forecasts, this
\par
methodology should be used as a cognitive aid to overcome human limitations rather
\par
than believed to be an optimal model. Finally, although multi-attribute analysis is no
\par
panacea, what better alternatives have been suggested?
\par
One of our major arguments is for realism in F \& P. Illusions and limitations need to
\par
be recognized; the use of F\&P for control, motivation and communication accepted
\par
for what they are, and the extent of future uncertainties both appreciated and
\par
appropriately incorporated in plans. F\& P can be useful, but current practices need to
\par
be changed. A systematic balancing of the benefits and costs of F\&P within the
