  doi = {10.1111/j.1540-5915.1987.tb01500.x},
  urldate = {2024-05-03},
  abstract = {The failure of a free market system to attain socially optimal allocation of resources to research and development (R\&D) is a generally recognized problem. However, we are just beginning to understand the types of R\&D activities that receive relatively serious underinvestment from specific types of industries and the types of governmental intervention strategies that are likely to be effective and efficient in the correction of that underinvestment. Recently, Joglekar and Hamburg [16] [17] sought answers to these types of questions using models of the resource allocation behavior of firms considering investment in basic research related to their industry. It was assumed that the firms' benefits were exponentially distributed. In the present article, such benefits are assumed to be normally distributed, and an attempt is made to verify the earlier conclusions and policy implications of [16] and [17]. The results are similar for these two substantially different types of distribution, but the earlier conclusions and policy implications are clarified, qualified, and extended.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {R&D,Resource Allocation},
  note = {\textbf{Concepts, Theory, and Techniques}
\par
\textbf{INDUSTRY RESOURCE ALIDCATION}
\par
\textbf{TO BASIC RESEARCH UNDER}
\par
\textbf{NORMALLY DISTRIBUTED BENEFITS*}
\par
Prafulla Joglekar
\par
Management Department, La Salle University, Philadelphia, PA \textbf{19141}
\par
Morris Hamburg
\par
Department of Statistics, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania, Philadelphia, PA \textbf{19104}
\par
\textbf{ABSTRACT}
\par
The failure of a free market system to attain socially optimal allocation of resources
\par
to research and development (RCD) is a generally recognized problem. However, we are
\par
just beginning to understand the types of R\&D activities that receive relatively serious un-
\par
derinvestment from specific types of industries and the types of governmental intervention
\par
strategies that are likely to be effective and efficient in the correction of that underinvest-
\par
ment. Recently, Joglekar and Hamburg \textbf{[I61 [I71 }sought answers to these types of questions
\par
using models of the resource allocation behavior of firms considering investment in basic
\par
research related to their industry. It was assumed that the firms' benefits were exponential-
\par
ly distributed. In the present article, such benefits are assumed to be normally distributed,
\par
and an attempt is made to verify the earlier conclusions and policy implications of \textbf{[16]}
\par
and \textbf{[17]. }The results are similar for these two substantially different types of distribution,
\par
but the earlier conclusions and policy implications are clarified, qualified, and extended.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Subject Areas: Decision AnaIysis, Government, U\&Q Resource Allocation}}
\par
\textbf{INTRODUCTION}
\par
Economists long have contended that a free market system will fail to attain
\par
socially optimal allocation of resources to research and development (R\&D). Ar-
\par
row \textbf{[l] }identified two reasons for such underinvestment: \textbf{(1) }the risk inherent in
\par
any R\&D activity and \textbf{(2) }the inability to appropriate the benefits of many R\&D
\par
activities.'
\par
Not all R\&D activities possess the above two characteristics to the same degree.
\par
For example, basic research has a high degree of inability to appropriate benefits
\par
in addition to some risk; applied R\&D activities, on the other hand, are likely to
\par
be largely appropriable (under \textbf{US. }patent laws) but highly risky.' In any case, the
\par
presence of either one of these two characteristics is sufficient for underinvestment
\par
in a particular R\&D activity to take place in a free market system.
\par
*This paper was presented at the 1984 TIMS/ORSA Joint National Meeting in San Francisco.
\par
'For a detailed discussion of Arrow's argument and some counterarguments, see the text and notes
\par
in \textbf{[16, }pp. \textbf{997-9981.}
\par
'Strictly speaking, it is not cwrect to label all basic research as inappropriable or all applied re-
\par
search as appropriable. For a further discussion of this point and an understanding of the use of these
\par
terms, see \textbf{[16, }footnote I].
\par
1\textbf{2 \emph{Decision Sciences }}[Vol. 18
\par
Arrow's [ 11 discussion failed to distinguish between underinvestment because
\par
of inappropriability and underinvestment because of risk. Arrow also prescribed
\par
no governmental policies to correct underinvestment in different types of R\&D ac-
\par
tivities. Our research suggests that the effects of risk and inappropriability on the
\par
degree of underinvestment are not necessarily additive-particularly when inter-
\par
firm cooperation is permitted. It is important to distinguish between \textbf{\emph{basic }}(inap-
\par
propriable) research and \textbf{\emph{applied }}(appropriable) R\&D when analyzing the effec-
\par
tiveness and efficiency of federal policy instruments aimed at stimulating industry
\par
investment in R\&D. The appropriateness of certain federal policies for stimulating
\par
\textbf{\emph{applied }}R\&D has been discussed elsewhere [15]. Here we focus on government
\par
policies for stimulating \textbf{\emph{basic }}research.
\par
Recently, Joglekar and Hamburg [16] published a model of the resource alloca-
\par
tion behavior of a group of firms considering investment in basic research. The
\par
analysis showed that unaided industry allocation to basic (inappropriable) research
\par
is suboptimal, provision of government seed money generally is counterproductive,
\par
and the provision of matching subsidies is not cost efficient in stimulating industry
\par
allocation to basic research [ 161.
\par
In a follow-up article, Joglekar and Hamburg [17] showed that the degree of
\par
suboptimality of investment in basic research depends on several industry charac-
\par
teristics. Industries with one or more of the following characteristics are likely to
\par
have a higher degree of suboptimality of investment in basic research:
\par
Industries that involve a large number of firms,
\par
Industries with intense interfirm competition,
\par
Industries with less threat from other industries,
\par
Industries with smaller amounts of investable resources, and
\par
Industries with smaller degrees of risk aversion.
\par
It was argued that industries displaying these characteristics deserve more gov-
\par
ernment assistance in their basic research programs [17]. Both [16] and [17] assumed
\par
that a firm's benefits from its investments (whether or not those benefits were derived
\par
from basic (i.e., inappropriable) research or some other appropriable investment
\par
opportunity) were exponentially distributed. In justifying the choice of the exponen-
\par
tial distribution, Joglekar and Hamburg stated that
\par
R\&D managers would probably approve of the following properties of the expo-
\par
nential distribution in representing anticipated benefits of R\&D investments: (a) the
\par
exponential distribution rules out the possibility of ``negative benefits,'' (b) it
\par
assumes that the likelihood of a particular value of benefits increases as the value
\par
increases, with the most likely value (i.e., the mode of the distribution) being zero.
\par
However, an exponential distribution also implies that the standard deviation of
\par
the distribution is the same as its mean. Most R\&D managers may question that
\par
implication, often suggesting that the ``technical risk'' is far smaller. However,
\par
considering the commercial risk as well, it seems that benefits of a number of
\par
real life R\&D investments may be reasonably well represented by an exponential
\par
distribution (particularly in the context of our model, since we have imposed only
\par
the most reasonable restrictions on the values of the parameters of the distribu-
\par
tion). [ \textbf{16, }p. \textbf{10001}
