\par
it can be related to iterative models recently presented by hypergame and metagame
\par
theorists \textbf{[2] [4] [12] [13]. }The argument of these theorists can be restated as ``over
\par
repeated trials or in anticipation of repeated trials of the resource allocation game,
\par
the value of m (the perceived and actual matching rate) will go up even in a volun-
\par
tary group of firms; consequently, underinvestment in inappropriate activities (e.g.,
\par
basic research) will be wiped out.''
\par
As in \textbf{[16] }and \textbf{[17], }the model presented here confirms that if m increases,
\par
underinvestment will diminish. However, these models are not aimed at analyzing
\par
whether m will or will not change over repeated trials. They simply assume that
\par
in any specific short-run resource allocation trial the \textbf{\emph{perceived }}value of m also is
\par
its \textbf{\emph{actual }}value in the individually rational equilibrium. This assumption is equi-
\par
valent to Harsanyi's \textbf{[9] }principal of mutually expected rationality. While our ap-
\par
proach clearly represents a simplification, it is a justifiable simplification. Evans
\par
and Harris found it necessary to qualify their conclusion about the long-run opti-
\par
mality of investment in inappropriable goods by stating that
\par
