\par
greater risk aversion would invest more suboptimally in their basic research than
\par
those that display relatively smaller risk aversion. This hypothesis seems to be widely
\par
held by many people who study government policy toward R\&D. The analysis pre-
\par
sented here indicates that this popularly held hypothesis is incorrect. Instead, our
\par
analysis finds that greater risk aversion leads to a smaller degree of suboptimality
\par
of investment in basic research.
\par
Of course, Arrow's [l] major focus was to identify the variety of factors that
\par
could lead to suboptimal investment in R\&D on the part of a private enterprise
\par
system. The risk associated with R\&D activities was one of these factors. Implicit
\par
in Arrow's discussion (which considered only the allocation to R\&D and not the
\par
allocation of resources among R\&D and other investment opportunities) was the
\par
assumption that investment opportunities other than R\&D had relatively low risks
\par
associated with them. The model presented here clearly differs. It assumes that
\par
both the basic research activity and the alternative investment opportunities have
\par
specific risks associated with them. It finds that, in the most likely case, less risk
\par
aversion leads to greater suboptimality of investment in basic research.
\par
Note that Arrow's statementL`We expect a free enterprise economy to under-
\par
invest in invention and research (as compared with an ideal) because it is risky,
\par
because the product can be appropriated only to a limited extent and because of
\par
increasing returns in use'' \textbf{[l, }p. 6191-leaves the impression that these factors are
\par
\textbf{\emph{additive }}as far as their effect on the suboptimality of investment in R\&D is
\par
concerned.
\par
As in [17], the present analysis takes into account the \textbf{\emph{joint }}effects of inappro-
\par
priability of R\&D activity \textbf{\emph{and }}risk aversion on the part of individual firms. Our
\par
analysis suggests that if an industry is concerned with basic research investment
\par
opportunities, risk aversion on the part of member firms in fact may help the in-
\par
dustry to reduce the degree of its suboptimal investment in basic research.
\par
This is an important finding. Based on these findings we suggest that, con-
\par
trary to popular belief, a risk-averse industry calls for \textbf{\emph{less }}government interven-
\par
tion in order to encourage its basic research than an industry with less \textbf{risk }aversion.
