\textbf{CONCLUSIONS}
\par
Several recent works \textbf{[2] [4] }[ \textbf{121 }[ \textbf{131 }have suggested that, in the long run, the
\par
problem of underinvestment in an inappropriable good (e.g., basic research) may
\par
disappear because of repeated trials (or the anticipation of repeated trials) of the
\par
resource allocation game in a n industry. Hirshleifer \textbf{[l 11 }even argued that, because
\par
of ``pecuniary'' benefits associated with new technical information, a free economy
\par
may overinvest in R\&D.* Jn view of these works, a blanket conclusion about the
\par
suboptimality of investment in basic research is not warranted. At the same time,
\par
the possibility in some situations of long-term optimality or short-term overinvest-
\par
ment does not negate observed instances of underinvestment in other situations.
\par
Earlier models \textbf{[16] }[ \textbf{171 }have aimed at generating policy implicstions for situations
\par
of underinvestment. In this paper, an attempt was made to verify several of the
\par
findings in \textbf{[16] }and \textbf{[17], }assuming that a firm's benefits from investments in alter-
\par
native opportunities are distributed normally rather than exponentially. It is en-
\par
couraging that most of the earlier findings are confirmed.
\par
