\par
Some of the results obtained here suggest more accurate interpretations for
\par
the pertinent results in [17]. Because the algebra involved in the case of normally
\par
distributed benefits is rather complex, definitive verification of some of our results
\par
also must wait for further investigation. Overall, this model has not contradicted
\par
the primary findings from earlier work concerning the \textbf{effectiveness/efficiency }of
\par
the provision of seed money or matching subsidies. Together these models suggest
\par
that the federal government should avoid the use of seed money as a means of
\par
stimulating basic research in industry; such expenditures in fact \textbf{\emph{reduce }}the industry's
\par
own investment. The models further suggest that matching subsidies be made avail-
\par
able only beyond a minimum investment on the part of the industry and that where
\par
possible an optimal intervention instrument, involving a flat tax returned through
\par
a matching subsidy, be used [16] [17].
\par
The model presented here confirms the following important recommendations
\par
made in [16] and [17] for government policy toward the stimulation of basic research
\par
in industry. Where feasible, the government should
\par
1.
\par
attempt to increase an industry's cooperative spirit and predisposition
\par
toward R\&D investment (i.e., increase intraindustry matching rates).
\par
\textbf{2.}
\par
increase intervention in industries that involve large numbers of firms.
\par
Alternatively, it should reduce the number of firms in an industry (at least
\par
by not subsidizing smaller, marginal firms).
\par
\textbf{3.}
\par
tax the benefits from private investments \textbf{(Xi) }more heavily and those from
\par
joint investments less heavily.
\par
\textbf{4.}
\par
be aware that although industries involving smaller numbers of firms,
\par
greater investable resources, greater interindustry competition, less inter-
\par
firm competition, and greater risk aversion have relatively less need for
\par
government intervention, in most industries the degree of suboptimality
\par
of investment in inappropriate joint opportunities is fairly high.
\par
It would seem useful to employ the models and methods of analysis developed
\par
in these papers ([15], [16], [17], and the present paper) to determine degrees \textbf{of}
\par
suboptimality of investment and appropriate government intervention methods.
\par
