\textbf{\emph{b. }}\emph{Introduction to Substantive Analysis of Trade Restraints}
\par
Section one of the Sherman Act forbids all combinations in "restraint of
\par
trade."499 The courts recognized very early, however, that all contractual rela-
\par
tionships to some extent restrain trade, and thus that the law cannot mean
\par
what it seems to say. 5 00 The Supreme Court, in the early case of \emph{Chicago Board of}
\par
\emph{Trade v. United States, }set forth a distinction between reasonable and unreason-
\par
able or undue restraints of trade which the courts continue to apply:
\par
Every agreement concerning trade, every regulation of trade, restrains. To
\par
bind, to restrain is of their very essence. The true test of legality is whether
\par
the restraint imposed is such as merely regulates and perhaps thereby pro-
\par
motes competition or whether it is such as may suppress or even destroy
\par
competition. 501
\par
Under "rule of reason" analysis the courts applying section one of the
\par
Sherman Act examine concerted conduct to see if its purpose and effect is to
\par
promote or restrain competition, that is, to increase or decrease allocative effi-
\par
ciency.50 2 Thus, agreements that on their face restrain trade may be permis-
\par
sible because they expand a market, improve market functioning or achieve in-
\par
tegrated efficiencies, thus ultimately making the market more efficient.50 3 For
