\textbf{CONCLUSION}
\par
The preceding pages manifest how little is really known about
\par
the judicial process and, indeed, about decision-making in general.
\par
Most of the comments were negative: the relation of ability, moti-
\par
vation, beliefs and attitudes to problem-solving is not yet clear. The
\par
extent to which the cultural matrix, legal institutions, and even
\par
legal doctrine influence the judicial decision is not known. The op-
\par
eration of insight and creativity is beyond understanding, although
\par
simulation studies hold out great promise for illuminating the
\par
mysterious judicial hunch. The purpose of this article was merely
\par
to demonstrate that the phase or stage approach to problem-solving
\par
provides an admirable scheme for organizing and relating present
\par
and future studies relevant to the judicial process. It would seem
\par
that the purpose has been accomplished, and that, in addition, the
\par
utility of the phase theory for exemplifying areas and relationships
\par
that need more intensive investigation by both jurisprudents and
\par
behavioral scientists has been conclusively demonstrated.
\par
