II. DISINCENTIVES TO DATA PRODUCTION
\par
The market for chemicals will not produce and distribute data on
\par
toxicity and exposure unless an incentive structure is developed and
\par
maintained. Several factors work to prevent the current system from
\par
providing such incentives.
\par
The dearth of toxicity data is in part due to the "public good"
\par
nature of the information.51 The virtues of flexibility and ease of
\par
transfer that characterize public goods become liabilities in commerce,
\par
because public goods cannot easily be held for exclusive use. If only
\par
one person "buys" the information, others may still benefit; the costs
\par
of producing the data cannot be recouped by multiple individual sales.
\par
Because public goods are difficult to own and to control, the market
\par
produces them at lower levels than may be desirable.
\par
Information is an especially problematic public \emph{good, }because pur-
\par
chasers do not know its value until it has been acquired. 52 Research is
\par
affected by this dynamic. It is also a risky investment because one
\par
cannot know ahead of time whether the effort will yield anything of
\par
value. In spite of these handicaps, research and development are tra-
\par
ditionally a major area of investment. Private research has been sup-
\par
ported by legal protections, such as copyright and patent law.
\par
Cost-reducing inventions are one kind of information likely to be
\par
produced privately. But toxicological data has a less ready marketa-
\par
bility than many cost-saving inventions. It has value only to those
\par
who bear the costs that it reduces. Since even the individual victims of
\par
chemical-related disease can rarely identify its specific cause, little de-
\par
mand has developed for assurance of safe or low-risk chemical
\par
products. 53
\par
A further disincentive to private research is the fact that the infor-
\par
mation produced is often inexact. Toxicologists currently have three
\par
basic tools with which to gather data: laboratory cell analyses, bioas-
