III. THE FAILURE OF TRADITIONAL REGULATORY STRATEGIES
\par
Regulatory schemes that have attempted to correct these market
\par
dysfunctions have not succeeded. Neither the liability system nor reg-
\par
ulation has effectively located responsibility for the toxic side effects of
\par
chemicals. Indeed, the law not only fails to encourage the production
\par
and distribution of toxicity data, it has tended to discourage it.
\par
Liability rules increase the costs of private production of such
\par
knowledge. The tort system is predicated upon the existence of infor-
\par
mation linking cause with effect, but the manufacturer often is the
\par
only party in a financial position to perform the research necessary to
\par
prove a causal connection. Potential liability is therefore another in-
\par
fluence that discourages manufacturers from producing this informa-
\par
tion. Compensation schemes are similarly dependent on existing data
\par
to prove causation. 79
\par
Products liability rules have stimulated safety research where the
\par
reasons for plaintiffs' injuries are apparent or can be determined with
\par
relatively inexpensive expert assistance. The same dynamics could de-
\par
velop with respect to chemicals, if the means of producing toxicity
\par
data were accessible to parties other than manufacturers, particularly
\par
to those who are exposed or their representatives. If there were in-
\par
dependent sources of data, the potential costs to a manufacturer of not
\par
producing its own information might be high. Producing data and
\par
warning of negative effects would become a form of protection against
\par
the costs of liability. The existence of independent sources of data
\par
would also encourage higher quality in manufacturers' tests, because a
\par
third party would be able to check results. The liability system's de-
\par
terrent effect thus depends upon a developed information context, with
\par
independent researchers.
\par
Although courts are generally in no position to mandate major re-
\par
search initiatives and must await independent documentation of causal
\par
links, the need for testing has drawn some judicial attention. In
\par
\emph{Beshada v. Johns Manville Products Corp., }so the Supreme Court of
\par
New Jersey faced the fact that manufacturers control the production
\par
of data on toxicity. Using "cheapest cost avoider" reasoning, the
