IV. DISTRIBUTING DATA IN THE MARKET
\par
Traditional regulatory approaches have neglected to distribute the
\par
information they do collect. The information access provisions in
\par
most environmental statutes have been, at best, unambitious reformu-
\par
lations of the Freedom of Information Act.112 They have simply pro-
\par
vided for access to agency files, with exemption from disclosure for
\par
certain categories of information, such as trade secrets, and they have
\par
encouraged none of the potential uses of information to warn or guide
\par
private decisionmaking. Indeed, past laws have sometimes seemed to
\par
encourage behavior that has the characteristics of information
\par
hoarding.
\par
Right-to-know laws change existing information dynamics, mak-
\par
ing chemicals visible in a new way, by providing new information to
\par
actors in the marketplace. The laws direct disclosure of both the iden-
\par
tity of chemicals and information about chemicals' effects. Disclosure
\par
is currently limited to chemicals already known to have toxic
\par
potential.
\par
These disclosure requirements appear in both state and federal oc-
\par
cupational health and environmental regulations. Many states enacted
\par
right-to-know laws in the late 1970s and early 1980s.113 State laws
\par
covering workers are now partially preempted by OSHA's Hazard
