V. REsTRUCTURING PRIVATE RESEARCH INCENTIVES
\par
Dean Calabresi noted in \emph{The Costs of Accidents, }153 "[o]ne could
\par
build a whole theory of primary cost avoidance in terms of incentives
\par
to discover cures .... Usually, however, we apparently do not believe
\par
that a different allocation of most disease costs would do much to spur
\par
reduction of their number or severity."154 Incentives to discover cures
\par
can be supported in the chemical toxicity context, if the market's in-
\par
formation dynamics are taken into account.
\par
Among the laws which aim to stimulate private research, such as
\par
the patent system and product liability 'law, two models provide for
\par
direct funding of research. One might be called the "seed money"
\par
model, a variant of which has been proposed to encourage research on
\par
superconductivity.155 In this model, public money is provided as an
\par
inducement for research and development in a particular sector of the
\par
economy, in the hope that it will lead to the long-term development of
\par
marketable products. Another approach is to select a particular goal
\par
and fund efforts to attain it. For example, Congress has enacted a
\par
program to spur research on "orphan drugs," those which treat rare
\par
diseases and therefore have little commercial value.156 The orphan
\par
drug program finds sponsors for research on rare diseases and pro-
\par
