\par
ADMIN. NEWS 3577.1836 \emph{Michigan Law Review }(Vol. 87:1795
\par
vides them with financial incentives in the form of grants and tax
\par
breaks.157 These approaches are useful where the need for the infor-
\par
mation is limited or as yet undeveloped. That is not the case, however,
\par
with the need for chemical toxicity data.
\par
Chemicals in commercial use, far from being orphans, affect huge
\par
numbers of people and businesses. This circumstance provides oppor-
\par
tunities for adjusting research incentives in the chemicals market. The
\par
recent amendments to FIFRA and SARA recognize the option of
\par
making industry pay for the research required by its externalities, but
\par
these laws would simply tax the industry to finance limited govern-
\par
ment efforts and fail to take full advantage of market dynamics to
\par
stimulate private production of data.
\par
A more ambitious and promising approach would be to adapt the
\par
design of the national hazardous waste clean-up program to foster pri-
\par
vate research sponsored by the users of chemical products. The cur-
\par
rent deficit of toxicity and exposure data is analogous to the current
\par
hazardous waste problem. Both have hidden impacts and delayed ef-
\par
fects and require costly public initiatives to remedy, and both are ex-
\par
ternalities generated by the same group of industries. In addition,
\par
CERCLA attempts to involve all the parties who have an interest in a
\par
site (whether that interest is past or prospective) and to provide those
\par
who want to remedy the hazardous condition with the financial sup-
\par
port to do so. This type of mechanism is needed to mobilize latent
\par
forces in the market to generate more toxicity data.
\par
Hazardous waste cleanup is being carried out pursuant to
\par
CERCLA, commonly called "Superfund," and the 1986 amendments
\par
to the Superfund contained in SARA. Pursuant to CERCLA, the
\par
EPA has published a National Priority List, which identifies the hun-
\par
dreds of locations that require attention. The statute's Superfund and
\par
liability provisions provide funding for the actual cleanup. The basic
\par
fund itself is derived from taxes on the petrochemical industry and
\par
from general revenues. Cleanup operations may be financed from thi.s
\par
fund or by "potentially responsible parties" (PRPs), who, when identi-
\par
fiable, are held liable for clean-up costs. PRPs may be waste genera-
\par
tors, or haulers, or property owners, or tenants - virtually anyone
\par
associated with the presence of the waste at the site. The cleanup may
\par
be carried out by local, state, or federal agencies or by private parties,
\par
157. \emph{Id. }Biomedical research takes place in three contexts: the federal government; non{$\cdot$}
\par
profit medical centers, which are chiefly in medical schools and are largely funded through the
\par
federal grant system; and the pharmaceutical industry. In 1982, the FDA reported to Congress
\par
that it had approved approximately 960 drugs for rare diseases. But of these, only 34 were
\par
developed and marketed by companies; with 24 of these 34, a government agency or a university
\par
had assisted in either the financing or the research. \emph{Id. }at 3579-80.June 1989] \emph{Information Economics }1837
\par
who then may seek reimbursement from the fund or from identifiable
\par
PRPs. Most CERCLA cases are initiated by the EPA or state agen-
\par
cies which then negotiate for further involvement by PRPs.
\par
The Superfund program thus combines three elements: (1) a plan
\par
for action, formulated and published by the government; (2) a source
\par
of funding that attempts to link the costs of cleanup to the parties
\par
most responsible for the hazardous condition; and (3) open participa-
\par
tion in the process, with financial support made available to any party
\par
interested in undertaking to clean up a site. While the Superfund pro-
\par
gram has been criticized as unwieldy, some of its problems surely are
\par
attributable to the nature of its difficult task. In any event, the design
\par
of the Superfund is instructive as a model program to address current
\par
chemical research needs, a program which I will call "super study,"
\par
for lack of a better name.
\par
In a "super study" program, research would be conducted accord-
\par
ing to a general plan, by a combination of private and public institu-
\par
tions.158 Private laboratories or government agencies could conduct
\par
studies according to test protocols specified by the plan and then regis-
\par
ter the test results. The costs of different types of study could be stan-
\par
dardized. Documented research, with peer r\textasciitilde view completed, would
\par
be reimbursed from the general fund. Reimbursement to the fund by
\par
chemical producers would be mandated when studies showed adverse
\par
health effects from their individual chemicals or identified mixtures.
\par
Basic screening and research, and methodological research, would also
\par
be reimbursed from the general fund.
\par
Reimbursement actions against manufacturers would be managed
\par
by a separate custodian of the fund, so that research results would not
\par
be influenced by the need to support the fund. Responsible parties,
\par
producers comparable to the Superfund PRPs, could be identified
\par
through the TSCA, the new inventory provisions of the EPCRA, the
\par
registration provisions of the FDA, and the pesticide laws. Joint and
\par
several liability could be established by law, with liability apportioned
\par
on the basis of human exposure rates. Long-term epidemiological
\par
studies could also be funded by dischargers, based upon the extent of
\par
human exposure caused by their discharges.
\par
A national study plan would be published and managed by the
\par
National Toxicology Program, the National Institute of Environmen-
\par
