\par
managed as a more formalized version of the present national toxicol-
\par
ogy research agenda and grants programs, or it could adopt the Na-
\par
tional Research Council's recommendations for toxicological research
\par
published in \emph{Toxicity Testing. }159 The National Research Council has
\par
reviewed a variety of screening proposals and recommended a system
\par
that would operate in several stages, selecting chemicals for further
\par
research at each stage, based on their human exposure and potential
\par
toxicity.160 The \emph{Toxicity Testing }approach is not the only planning
\par
resource available, of course; the public health research community is
\par
a ready repository of expertise in research management.
\par
One basic appeal of a "super study" program, in contrast to the
\par
Superfund hazardous waste program, is that the investment would
\par
come at the beginning of the production process, when information
\par
can be useful in many environmental and health dimensions. A re-
\par
search program's benefits would also be broader than the Superfund's
\par
focus on hazardous dump sites, because much of the data produced
\par
would be applicable across different media. The current Superfund
\par
allocation of \$8.6 billion for waste site cleanup is addressing only part
\par
of a broader pollution problem. The amount of waste discharged into
\par
other media, particularly air, is greater than that disposed in landfills.
\par
The rationale for taxing the entire petrochemical industry to sup-
\par
port research is also stronger than it is for hazardous waste cleanup.
\par
Research would benefit the entire system, including the industry itself,
\par
by providing a better basis for product screening in the marketplace
\par
and by removing the pall cast over safe products by uncertainty about
\par
toxicity. Moreover, a tax that is imposed on future behavior, before
