A research super study also should be easier to administer than the
\par
current national Superfund program. CERCLA implementation must
\par
struggle with a number of unwieldy obstacles that would not be pres-
\par
ent in the super study program. Chief among these obstacles is the
\par
need to treat and to relocate millions of tons of poisonous and contam-
\par
inated material. A research program would have few such logistical
\par
problems because it would produce prospectively useful information,
\par
as opposed to trying to destroy or neutralize useless, dangerous waste.
\par
Each hazardous waste site is a complex ecological problem, unlike
\par
the relatively standardized procedures in toxics testing. Waste sites
\par
are located in a wide variety of natural and s\textasciitilde cietal settings, and can
\par
be handled by several different technologies, and treated to different
