VI. THE DATA SYSTEM AS A PUBLIC UTILITY
\par
The history of chemical information services in this country is a
\par
story of repeated flirtation with and hesitation over commitment to the
\par
concept of a national data system.164 In the late 1970s, in the wake of
\par
the enactment of TSCA, an integrated national data system was
\par
planned, but when the Reagan administration took office, information
\par
policy shifted in a different direction. The EPA in particular has suf-
\par
fered from inconsistent goals and support of its data project. Both
\par
private and public toxics data services are now evolving rapidly in re-
\par
sponse to new disclosure laws.165 Meanwhile, basic questions about
\par
what kind of long-term data resources the nation will have are being
\par
answered on an \emph{ad hoc }basis, without the benefit of a considered legis-
\par
lative policy.
\par
Two current developments outside the field of chemical regulation
\par
will shape the future of availability of this data. The first is the rapid
\par
diversification of telecommunications which has followed the restruc-
\par
turing of the telephone system and is fueled by the continuing evolu-
\par
tion of computer technology.166 The second is the reassessment of
\par
federal information policy, now taking place as government agencies
