VII. SOME PROBLEMS AND PRINCIPLES
\par
The information economics drawn upon here suggests that there
\par
are better ways of investing in information than the present law en-
\par
courages. This analysis has attempted to point out some directions the
\par
law might take, if information production and use were to be given a
\par
more central role in toxics control.
\par
One major issue that has not been addressed here is the question of
\par
how to treat trade secrecy in the context of a comprehensive informa-
\par
tion scheme. Current right-to-know laws provide some protection for
\par
trade-secret information. Neither OSHA nor the EPCRA require dis-
\par
closure of the identity of chemicals claimed to be trade secrets, ev:en
\par
though the exempted chemicals are hazardous.217 However, these ar-
\par
rangements do not seem satisfactory. On the one hand, firms would
\par
prefer not to disclose va,luable information at all, since secrecy is frag-
\par
ile and may easily be lost. On the other, confidentiality agreements are
\par
burdensome and may inhibit research and medical practice. {$\cdot$} Exempt-
\par
ing trade-secret chemicals on a basis unrelated to the purposes of the
\par
information system may also have systemic repercussions, affecting
\par
the development of research and information services.
