Separate from the problem of producing and distributing data is
\par
the question of how the law should use toxicity data. Do legal stan-
\par
dards for toxicity reflect rational and consistent recognition of the
\par
value of this information? Not surprisingly, the answer is definitely
\par
"no."
\par
Laws may require five types of actions with respect to chemicals:
\par
(1) mere disclosure of the identity of the chemical agent to which
\par
humans are exposed; (2) warning of its known hazards; (3) testing to
\par
determine hazards; (4) curtailing the exposure by reducing the dis-
\par
charge of the chemical; and (5) compensation for any injuries proved
\par
to be caused by chemical exposure. Each entails different costs and
\par
will affect differently the individuals exposed and the public health sys-
\par
tem. For instance, the monetary and social costs of disclosure, warn-
\par
ing, and testing normally will be less than the cost of reducing or
\par
eliminating discharges or fully compensating groups of injured indi-
\par
viduals. One would expect the law to establish separate but graduated
\par
tests of responsibility, tests that would call for different grades of proof
\par
and would reflect not only the costs and benefits inherent in each ac-
\par
tion, but also established jurisprudential norms. Tort law and chemi-
\par
cal regulation statutes contain legal standards for warnings, testing,
\par
exposure, and civil liability, but they do not form a coherent system.
\par
There are similar standards for very different activities and different
\par
standards for the same activity.
\par
For example, the standard incorporated in the TSCA which the
\par
EPA must meet before it may order a manufacturer to conduct testing
\par
on a chemical, is "may present an unreasonable risk of injury to health
\par
or the environment."220 This "unreasonable risk" test is similar to
