bution of information to workers in different businesses.
\par
The EPCRA uses two approaches to determine coverage. The
\par
emergency planning provisions, sections 301 and 312, incorporate
\par
OSHA's standard. The emergency releases provisions, section 303,
\par
limit reporting to one list of chemicals and section 313, on routine
\par
releases, uses another list. Using lists prepared by independent experts
\par
is preferable to employer assessment, as it assures credibility, clarity,
\par
and consistency. It also lightens the research burden on businesses
\par
and allows them to focus on the task within their bailiwick: identify-
\par
ing sources of exposure.
\par
However, now that these lists have been codified, they are unlikely
\par
to be revised appropriately in the future, since the EPCRA authorizes
\par
additions to its lists and to the incorporated OSHA lists only by
\par
rulemaking. To be added to EPCRA's lists for reporting to the na-
\par
tional inventory, chemicals must be shown to cause or to be reason-
\par
ably anticipated to cause "significant adverse acute human health
\par
effects at concentration levels that are reasonably likely to exist beyond
\par
facility site boundaries as a result of continuous, or frequent re-
\par
leases. "225 This is a high standard for warning and is inconsistent with
\par
the use of the right-to-know scheme. As with the TSCA's testing au-
\par
thority, the EPCRA standard for expanding coverage parallels the
\par
standards for setting permissible levels of human exposure.226 Stan-
\par
dards for warning - as for testing - need not be as high as those used
\par
to set exposure levels. Experience with the TSCA indicates that the
\par
EPA will not be able to add to these lists without engaging in lengthy
\par
review of the health effects of chemicals to be added and that private
\par
resources will be devoted to keeping chemicals off the lists.227
\par
