I. ORIGINS OF THE MODERN APPRAISAL RIGHT: THE DICK-
\par
\textbf{ERSON }COMMITTEE
\par
The enactment in \textbf{1975 }of a statutory appraisal right in the \emph{Canada}
\par
\emph{Business Corporations Act }\textbf{\emph{(CBCA) t }}that allowed shareholders to insist,
\par
upon the happening of certain events, that the corporation buy their shares
\par
was intended to substantially alter the balance of rights between majority
\par
and minority shareholders on fundamental changes. 2 Pointing to the
\par
reluctance of courts to intervene to protect minority shareholders where
\par
there is no fraud or bad faith, and the absence of any effective control
\par
over the behaviour of majority shareholders, the Dickerson Committee
\par
concluded that "the present state of the common law is at best unsa-
\par
tisfactory, at worst downright unjust."3 The appraisal right, adapted from
\par
similar provisions in New York's \emph{Business Corporation Law, 4 }was intended
\par
to strike a new balance between majority and minority shareholders.
\par
While the majority could, "if they go through the proper formalities,
\par
and if they pay any dissenting shareholders, effect almost any fundamental
\par
\textbf{1 }S.C. \textbf{1974-75, }c. 33, s. 184.
\par
2 The \emph{CBCA }appraisal provisions are far from being Canada's first. The earliest example
\par
of such a provision appears to have been in the reconstruction provisions adopted in a number
\par
of Canadian statutes in the latter half of the nineteenth century, including one adopted in Ontario's
\par
\emph{The Joint Stock Companies' Winding-up Act, }\textbf{S.O. }1878, c. 5, s. 13. In 1953, Ontario adopted an
\par
appraisal right in respect of private corporations in \emph{The Corporations Ac4 1953, }\textbf{\emph{S.O. }1953, }c.
\par
19, s. 99. The first modem appraisal provisions applying to a variety of fundamental changes
\par
in public corporations were adopted in the British Columbia \emph{Companies Act, }S.B.C. 1973, c. 18,
\par
s. 228.
\par
In the \emph{CBCA, }appraisal rights or procedures arise in a variety of contexts. Section 184 enumerates
\par
a number of fundamental changes in which a shareholder may insist on an appraisal as a matter
\par
of right (see also s. 217, sale of assets for securities or distribution of assets \emph{in specie }on winding
\par
up). A shareholder may also insist on an appraisal in respect of a compulsory acquisition of shares
\par
\emph{(ibid }s. 199). In at least two other contexts, an appraisal may arise not \emph{as of right }but pursuant
\par
to a court adjudication of entitlement. Under the oppression provision the court may, as a remedial
\par
tool, order the corporation or any security holder to buy the shares of the complainant \emph{(ibid s.}
\par
234(3Xf)). Similarly, where a statutory arrangement is undertaken, the court has authority to order
\par
that any shareholder or shareholders may dissent under s. 184 \emph{(ibid }s. 185.1(4)(d)).
\par
In the \emph{Bu.siness Corporations Ac 1982 (OBCA), }S.O. 1982, c. 4, the appraisal provisions are
\par
based on those in the \emph{CBCA }and are broadly similar \emph{(OBCA, }ss 181, 184, 187, 203, 247). However,
\par
the \emph{OBCA }also allows a shareholder to require the corporation to buy his or her shares if any
\par
security holder acquires 90 percent or more of that class of shares and provides an appraisal procedure
\par
similar to that in \emph{CBCA }at s. 184 to determine fair value \emph{(OBCA, }s. 188). In addition, although
\par
the \emph{OBCA }expressly allows "going-private" squeezeouts (other than under the "compulsory
\par
acquisition" provisions), it allows a dissenting shareholder to require an appraisal of his or her
\par
shares on such a transaction \emph{(bid }s. 189).
\par
The \emph{CBCA }provisions have been adopted in the same or substantially similar form not only
\par
in Ontario, but in Alberta, Manitoba, Saskatchewan, and New Brunswick. See Alberta \emph{Business}
\par
\emph{Corporations Act, (ABCA) }S.A. 1981, c. B-15; \emph{The Corporations Act, }(MCA) S.M. 1976, c. 40;
\par
New Brunswick \emph{Business Corporations Act, }\textbf{(NBBCA) S.N.B. 1981, }c. B-9.1; Saskatchewan \emph{Business}
\par
\emph{Corporations Act, }(SBCA) R.S.S. 1978, c. B-10.
\par
