is, in law, equivalent to a purchase of shares \textbf{by }a third party. The argument fails to withstand
\par
analysis on both policy and technical grounds. The policy considerations have already been made
\par
clear. In respect of the latter, the appraisal provisions appear to contemplate a \emph{Us }as between the
\par
shareholder and the pre-amalgamation corporation. For example, in the \textbf{\emph{CBCA, }}\emph{supra, }note \textbf{1 }at
\par
s. 184, the provision allows a shareholder to dissent in respect of shares of a "corporation," and
\par
s. 184(1) clearly identifies the "corporation" as the pre-amalgamation entity. Whether or not the
\par
amalgamated entity is different in law from the pre-amalgamation entities, the cause of action
\par
that arises from enlisting the appraisal right is one in respect of the pre-amalgamation entity, and
\par
the tax treatment of the cashout should reflect this reality.
\par
\textbf{127 }Where the shareholder does not purchase substitute shares, treating the disposition as an
\par
event giving rise to a capital gain \emph{may }result in a greater tax liability for the dissenter than the
\par
non-dissenter (see \emph{supra, }note 120). This has the salutary effect of ensuring that the appraisal right
\par
is not claimed solely for tax reasons. Since the dissenter has the option of purchasing substitute
\par
securities and receiving a tax-free rollover, there is no disincentive to claiming the right.
\par
\textbf{128 }This does not necessarily imply that the date of resale be fixed in the mind of the buyer
\par
at the time of purchase. An anticipated brokerage cost, as in the case of an anticipated tax cost,
\par
may be calculated probabilistically, taking into account the \emph{probable }time of disposition and
\par
reinvestmenL\textbf{1987]}
