This article critically reevaluates both the theoretical premises upon
\par
which the statutory appraisal right is based and the practical success it
\par
has had in achieving these goals. Part \textbf{II }of the article considers the theo-
\par
retical rationales for an appraisal right and suggests that the Dickerson
\par
Committee correctly identified the reasons why shareholders may desire
\par
to have this protection. In Parts III and IV these rationales are ex-
\par
changes \textbf{by }majorities \emph{(see supra, }note 4 and accompanying text), it clearly intended to preserve for
\par
minority shareholders potential resort to other remedial tools to \emph{block }fundamental changes that
\par
were unfair or in breach of relevant legal obligations. The non-exclusivity of the appraisal right only
\par
emphasizes that \emph{absent unfair discrimination or other unfair or inequitable conduct }the majority
\par
shareholders have a \emph{right }to proceed with the fundamental transaction. This view is confirmed \textbf{by}
\par
one of the principal drafters of the Dickerson proposals; see \textbf{J.L. }Howard, "The Proposals for a New
\par
Business Corporations Act for Canada: Concepts and Policies" \textbf{(1972) L.S.U.C. }Special Lectures \textbf{17}
\par
at 47-50.
\par
There is a potential inconsistency between the Committee's expressed desire of allowing a ma-
\par
jority to proceed with the fundamental change free of any hindrance \textbf{by }the minority and the policy
\par
of non-exclusivity. If the statutory appraisal right was intended to augment the existing entitlement
\par
of a majority of shareholders \textbf{by }allowing them to proceed \textbf{\emph{more }}\emph{easily }with fundamental changes,
\par
this logically leads in the direction of exclusivity. \textbf{By }relegating the minority shareholder's claim to
\par
the status of a liability (and dispensing with his or her entitlement to block a proposed transaction)
\par
the majority is afforded maximal freedom to engage in fundamental changes short of a taking with-
\par
out compensation.
\par
Exclusive resort to the appraisal right on fundamental changes may also result in substantial
\par
savings in litigation costs. See \emph{infra, }part V. Further, to the extent that exclusivity may result in
\par
increased risks to shareholders, one would anticipate that this would simply be incorporated in the
\par
price investors would be willing to pay for the company's shares, leaving shareholders with a normal
\par
risk-adjusted expected return. Fischel has suggested other reasons in favour of an exclusive ap-
\par
praisal right; where each shareholder can block the transaction, the corporation must buy out the
\par
entitlement of each, generating holdout problems and significant transaction costs. See D.R. Fischel,
\par
"The Appraisal Remedy in Corporate Law" \textbf{(1983) }4 A.B.F. Res. \textbf{J. 875 }at \textbf{898-901. }Although the
\par
point is well taken, Fischel appears to have overlooked the likelihood of the joinder of numerous
\par
plaintiffs as well as the effect that issue estoppel would have in barring successive litigations of the
\par
same issue \textbf{by }new plaintiffs. Fischel also suggests that an exclusive appraisal right would comport
\par
with the usual rule that injunctive relief is not available where damages are an adequate remedy. In
\par
cases involving public corporations, the "damages" awarded pursuant to the appraisal cashout will
\par
almost always be an adequate remedy. This may not be true, however, in cases involving private
\par
corporations.
\par
It has been suggested that the appraisal right transforms the shareholder's interest into a mere
\par
liability, de-individualizing his or her entitlement. B. Manning writes: \textbf{"[w]e }are all accustomed to
\par
observe, or to have pointed out to us, the rolling ground 'swell during this period [roughly the last
\par
century] from a law of fixity to a law of mobility, from a law centering on ownership to a law
\par
centering on claim, from a law focusing on the individual to a law focusing on groups." B. Manning,
\par
'The Shareholder's Appraisal Remedy: An Essay for Frank Coker" \textbf{(1962) 72 }Yale L.J. \textbf{223 }at \textbf{229.}
\par
From the above discussion it should be clear that this transformation of entitlement is only true if
\par
the appraisal right is the exclusive redress of the shareholder.
\par
