II. \textbf{WHAT INTERESTS }ARE PROTECTED BY \textbf{THE APPRAISAL RIGHT?}
\par
The interest of the majority of shareholders in maintaining the abil-
\par
ity to undertake fundamental changes is relatively clear. The exigencies
\par
of business often demand a great deal of flexibility in adapting the enter-
\par
prise to changing fortunes or business conditions. Such changing envi-
\par
ronments may demand an internal re-casting of the capital structure of
\par
the enterprise, perhaps including an alteration of the relative rights of
\par
outstanding securities, or the addition or even elimination of a class of
\par
securities. Business conditions may also dictate a rescaling of the enter-
\par
prise either \textbf{by }corporate combination or \textbf{by }increase or reduction in the
\par
size or scope of the enterprise. Finally, markets may suggest a partial or
\par
wholesale change in the nature of the business carried on \textbf{by }the
\par
enterprise.
\par
The potential interests of the minority are more problematic. The
\par
minority may desire protection against fundamental changes resulting in
\par
an alteration of the risk of the business but not impairing the value of the
\par
firm's securities, as well as unwise business decisions impairing enterprise
\par
value and thus diminishing the market value of the firm's securities. The
\par
minority may also value protection against discriminatory changes in the
\par
relative rights of security holders, which diminish the value of the securi-
\par
ties held. One form of discriminatory transaction that may be seen as
\par
posing particular dangers to the minority is the going-private
\par
transaction. 12
\par
In broad form this taxonomy of interests to be protected parallels
\par
that of the Dickerson Committee. In the two Parts that follow, these
\par
potential rationales for an appraisal right are examined in more detail in
\par
light of relevant insights supplied \textbf{by }modern capital theory.
\par
III. \textbf{FUNDAMENTAL CHANGES IN THE NATURE OF THE BUSINESS:}
\par
\textbf{ALTERATIONS OF RISK OR ENTERPRISE VALUE}
\par
Shareholders may wish to protect themselves against being forced
\par
"to participate in ventures beyond their initial contemplation"; 1 3 that is,
\par
to continue to invest in an enterprise that has altered its character in
\par
some material respect from the investment initially contemplated. The
\par
appraisal right may furnish such protection. It is therefore important to
\par
\textbf{12 }As with alterations of the terms (or relative terms) of outstanding securities that nevertheless
\par
leave minority shareholders within the enterprise, going-private transactions may be a way of effect-
\par
ing a discriminatory redistribution of values amongst classes of security holders in the enterprise.
\par
\textbf{13 F. }lacobucci, M.L. Pilkington \textbf{\& J.R.S. }Prichard, \emph{Canadian Business Corporations }\textbf{(Agin-}
\par
court, Ontario: Canada Law Book Ltd., \textbf{1977) }at \textbf{169.}\emph{SHAREHOLDERS' APPRAISAL RIGHT }\textbf{\emph{IN CANADA}}
\par
understand just what sorts of alterations investors may be concerned
\par
about (and in what situations).
\par
One such alteration is a change in the risk of the enterprise. Some
\par
investors may be more risk averse than others; for them, an increase in
\par
risk may make continued investment in the enterprise unattractive.
\par
Equally, a diminishing of risk may make the investment unattractive for
\par
shareholders who are less risk averse or are risk preferring.14 Further,
\par
views of shareholders may differ as to the efficacy and profitability of a
\par
given fundamental change. In the view of some investors, the change,
\par
whether or not it alters the risk of the enterprise, may threaten to dimin-
\par
ish the value of the enterprise. In either case, the appraisal right may
\par
provide an "exit" option to avoid the perceived undesirable consequences
\par
for the investor of remaining in the fundamentally changed enterprise. 5
\par
\textbf{\emph{A. }}\emph{Alterations in the Risk of the Enterprise not Diminishing}
\par
\emph{Security Values}
\par
Generally speaking, investors in public companies 6 \textbf{will }not be very
\par
concerned with shifts in the risk of the enterprise that do not diminish
\par
the market value of the class of securities held \textbf{by }those investors. 7 In
\par
contrast, investors in small, private firms may have good reason to be
\par
concerned, even where there is no adverse effect on the value of the class
\par
of securities held. In the former case the appraisal right is likely to be of
\par
little value, but in the latter it may be of great value.
\par
Two factors are central to this conclusion: modern capital theory,
