\par
controlled externally \textbf{by }the shareholder alone, rather than only internally \textbf{(by }means of adjusting the
\par
activities or structure of the enterprise), are discussed at \emph{infra, }note \textbf{23 }and accompanying text.
\par
\textbf{15 }Since the economic value of an investment may be completely characterized \textbf{by }its risk and
\par
expected return, investors will be primarily (and in the greatest number of cases, exclusively) con-
\par
cerned about these two determinants of value. Hence, concern about protecting shareholders on
\par
fundamental changes must necessarily focus on changes in either risk or expected return (or both)
\par
arising out of the change. Elaboration of specific shareholder protections must therefore take into
\par
account the effect on the investor of changes in risk or expected return and must as well be grounded
\par
in some normative theory of shareholder entitlement (or "just desert") against which the propriety
\par
of the remedy may be measured.
\par
\textbf{16 }In this article, the term "company" is used interchangeably with "corporation." The legal
\par
definition of a "private" corporation, as distinguished from a "public" or "publicly-traded" corpora-
\par
tion (again used interchangeably), will differ from one jurisdiction to another. However, a workable
\par
definition of a private corporation is an incorporated firm which does not offer (and has not offered)
\par
its securities for sale to the public, has a small number of shareholders, restrictions on the transfera-
\par
bility of shares, and a high degree of coincidence between those who are the officers, directors, and
\par
shareholders. \textbf{\emph{Cf. }}the Ontario \emph{Securities Act, }R.S.O. \textbf{1980, }c. 466, s. \textbf{l(l)(3 1).}
\par
\textbf{17 }Since the value of an enterprise (and its securities) can only be determined when both the
\par
risk and expected return are known, the effect of a change in risk, \textbf{by }itself, \textbf{will }produce an in-
\par
determinant effect on value.\emph{CANADA-UNITED STATES LAW JOURNAL}
\par
\textbf{Vol. 13:299 1988}
\par
including both portfolio diversification theory and the capital asset pric-
\par
ing model; and the existence or non-existence of a market-exit option.
\par
1. Changes in the risk of the enterprise, modem capital theory,
\par
and shareholder welfare
\par
Modem capital theory"' gives us some indication of when share-
\par
holders are likely to be concerned with shifts in the risk of the enterprise
\par
that do not adversely affect security values. Financial economists divide
\par
the risk facing investors into two types. This division reflects the fact
\par
that some risks are specific to a given company while other risks facing
\par
the enterprise are broadly derivative of general economic conditions and
\par
trends that affect the market as a whole. For example, in the case of a
\par
company engaged in manufacturing and selling children's toys, the com-
\par
pany (and its shareholders) face a risk that the market will not react
\par
favourably to a new product line. Entirely aside from the success of the
\par
new product line, however, the company's fortunes will be tied to the
\par
health of the economy as a whole, which will determine, for example,
\par
how much money is available for new investment, the level of consumer
\par
demand, and other factors affecting the company's profitability. The for-
\par
mer type of risk passes under a variety of names, including \emph{unsystematic,}
\par
\emph{company specific, }or \emph{diversfiable }risk. The second is referred to as \emph{sys-}
\par
\emph{tematic, market, or undiversifiable }risk.
\par
Unsystematic risk can be diversified away by holding a portfolio of
\par
securities. For example, if an investor holds a portfolio of the common
\par
shares of ten companies, some of these companies will succeed and some
\par
will not. The effect of a fluctuation in the price of any single security in
\par
the portfolio arising from unsystematic influences will, on average, be
\par
offset by contrary movements in the prices of the other securities in the
\par
portfolio. Diversification is, in a sense, little more than avoiding the
\par
hazards that extend from "putting all your eggs in one basket." The
\par
larger the number of securities in the portfolio, the less the investor will
\par
care about the unsystematic risk of any given security. In fact, it has been
\par
demonstrated that adequate diversification can be achieved with as few as
\par
ten or fifteen securities in a portfolio.1 9
\par
Thus, investors in a position to diversify their holdings will be sub-
