\par
in systematic risk, whatever the source,22 may be of concern to some
\par
investors-including those with diversified portfolios.
\par
The value of the appraisal right against pure changes in risk that do
\par
not adversely affect security values will therefore depend on the ability of
\par
investors to diversify their holdings. It will also depend, as the following
\par
sections make clear, on the existence of a "market-exit option"; that is,
\par
the ability of a shareholder to sell his or her holdings in the market in
\par
response to changes in risk that do not suit his or her risk preferences.
\par
2. Public companies with deep markets
\par
For public companies with well-developed markets, the appraisal
\par
right is likely to be of little value in protecting investors against risk shifts
\par
that do not adversely affect security value.
\par
On a practical level, a dissentient shareholder may simply sell his or
\par
her shares in the market without loss of capital and purchase a more
\par
satisfactory investment. In any case, there is little reason for a well-di-
\par
versified investor in a public company to be concerned about such risk
\par
shifts. As noted, a shift in \emph{unsystematic }risk is not of great concern to the
\par
diversified investor. At first sight, a shift in \emph{systematic }risk that does not
\par
alter the value of the security may nevertheless cause some investors to
\par
sell their shares if the investment no longer conforms to their risk prefer-
\par
ences. It is axiomatic both that investors have heterogeneous risk prefer-
\par
