\par
adequately diversified portfolio is the systematic risk of the \emph{portfolio.}
\par
Changes in the systematic risk of a single security in the portfolio are
\par
unlikely to have such a substantial impact that the portfolio requires
\par
rebalancing. Moreover, changes in the systematic risk of portfolio secur-
\par
ities occasioned \textbf{by }fundamental changes will be uncorrelated, leading to
\par
diversification against the danger of changes in systematic risk. \emph{Changes}
\par
\emph{in risk }will tend, over time, "to come out in the wash." An appraisal
\par
right providing protection against changes in systematic risk \textbf{will }there-
\par
fore have little value.2 '
\par
In those unlikely cases where a risk shift not affecting security value
\par
causes an investor to sell his or her holdings, the appraisal right could
\par
confer an advantage over the market-exit option only where the right is
\par
designed to afford superior tax consequences to the latter or a payment to
\par
mitigate the \emph{unexpected }tax burden or brokerage costs incurred \textbf{by }the
\par
shareholder on disposition. Modem statutory appraisal rights typically
\par
do not furnish such advantages.25
\par
\textbf{3. }Public companies with thin markets
\par
The vast bulk of Canadian publicly traded corporations are corpora-
\par
tions whose securities are inactively traded. 6 As in the case of public
\par
corporations with deep markets, investors in these corporations are likely
\par
\textbf{23 }The capital asset pricing model suggests an optimal investment strategy for investors with
\par
any given risk preferences. That strategy is to determine a desired level of risk experience and to
\par
adjust portfolio risk to that level by buying a combination of a "risk-free" asset (such as government
\par
bonds) and the "market" portfolio. The market portfolio consists of a weighted average of all capital
\par
investments available in the market in the proportion that the value of each bears to the aggregate
\par
value of all capital investments. Although transaction costs and indivisibilities clearly inhibit
\par
purchase of the market portfolio, the insight yielded is that comparatively risk-averse and risk-pre-
\par
ferring investors will, in theory, differ in their investment strategies only in respect of the portion of
\par
the portfolio invested (or disinvested, \textbf{by }borrowing) in the risk-free asset.
\par
\textbf{24 }The \emph{de minimus }character of a change in portfolio systematic risk resulting from a change of
\par
risk of an individual security, and the tendency for offsetting changes in risk to cancel out, will be
\par
especially true for investors with very large portfolios, such as institutional investors. It will also be
\par
true for individuals who invest through an institution such as a mutual fund.
\par
