\par
4. Privately held companies
\par
Investors in private businesses for which there exists no public mar-
\par
ket may have a very different attitude toward shifts in enterprise risk
\par
than investors in public companies. Such investors are often significantly
\par
underdiversified since a large part of their wealth (including their em-
\par
ployment) is tied up in the enterprise. In the face of significant un-
\par
derdiversification, individual investor welfare will depend on the \emph{total}
\par
risk of the enterprise. Therefore \emph{all }risk shifts, whether company specific
\par
and systematic, may be of profound concern whether or not such shifts
\par
affect security value.
\par
In most such enterprises, there is no reliable market-exit option.
\par
Shares of private companies will generally be difficult to sell and may in
\par
fact be subject to strict restrictions on transferability, reflecting the quasi-
\par
partnership status of many small, incorporated enterprises.29
\par
