\emph{C. Majorities and Minorities on the Exercise of the Appraisal Right}
\par
\emph{The potential }value of the appraisal right lies in holding a put option
\par
that arises on the happening of specified triggering contingencies. The
\par
put option enables the shareholder who would not otherwise be able to
\par
do so to cash out of the enterprise (in the case of private corporations) or
\par
to cash out at a better price than the current market price (in respect of
\par
public corporations) and (in both cases) to avoid the effects of the funda-
\par
mental change. This prospectively protects shareholders against certain
\par
risks. \emph{Prima facie, }this protection should have some value to
\par
shareholders.
\par
It must not be forgotten, however, that \emph{ex ante }it may not be entirely
\par
clear to a shareholder whether he or she will be in the majority or minor-
\par
ity in respect of any given fundamental change. If the shareholder is in
