IV. \textbf{CHANGES IN THE RELATIVE RIGHTS OF SECURITY }HOLDERS:
\par
\textbf{THE }APPRAISAL RIGHT \textbf{AS }PROTECTION \textbf{AGAINST}
\par
DISCRIMINATION
\par
\textbf{\emph{A. }}\emph{Discrimination Defined}
\par
Fundamental changes often involve discriminatory treatment of
\par
shareholders. Indeed, discrimination is a commonplace of corporate
\par
existence. Discrimination may arise in respect of shareholders of a single
\par
class or shareholders of different classes, and it may arise in a variety of
\par
contexts. In the life of an enterprise, business, economic, or financial
\par
considerations may dictate that the enterprise undergo reformation. This
\par
reformation may well involve, \emph{inter alia, }discriminatory cancellation of
\par
arrearages of dividends of a class of shares, removal of a retraction fea-
\par
ture, issuance of a class of securities superior to an existing class or
\par
classes (thus derogating from their relative interest in the enterprise), or
\par
even elimination of a class of securities. A corporate amalgamation may
\par
result in discriminatory treatment of shareholders, as may reincorpora-
\par
tion in a new jurisdiction, or virtually any other corporate fundamental
\par
change.
\par
Although the word "discrimination" usually carries with it a pejora-
\par
tive connotation, it is clear that the fact of discrimination by itself is not
\par
always objectionable. The discrimination may be of a formal but not
\par
substantive character. For example, where there is a single class of
\par
expert testimony, leading to difficult and expensive litigation. \textbf{Of }course, the appraisal right may also
\par
generate costly litigation (see \emph{infra, }Part V.B.6).
