\par
Even substantively unequal treatment is not necessarily \emph{unfair }treat-
\par
ment. Minority shareholders may be willing to accept substantively une-
\par
qual outcomes \emph{ex post }if this maximizes share prices \emph{ex ante }(which
\par
might be the case, for example, if productive fundamental changes are
\par
rendered more probable by a rule permitting substantively unequal treat-
\par
ment of minority and majority). The correct perspective from an eco-
\par
nomic point of view is the \emph{ex ante }bargain, and it is this perspective that
\par
will be used in this article.
\par
This Part addresses the extent to which the appraisal right may
\par
function as a device for preventing or affording relief from instances of
\par
substantive discrimination associated with fundamental changes. The
