full gain-sharing rule may conduce to a lower corporate cost of capital.
\par
The relative likelihood of opportunistic redistribution and the facilitation
\par
of gains will vary with different types of transaction and different corpo-
\par
rate types.
\par
\emph{B. A Rule of Unequal Distribution: Promoting Productive}
\par
\emph{Fundamental Changes}
\par
In respect of corporate control transactions, it has been vigorously
\par
argued by F.H. Easterbrook and D.R. Fischel that a rule permitting an
\par
unequal distribution of benefits-subject to the minority being no \emph{worse}
\par
off than before (as measured by the pre-transaction market price of the
\par
minority's shares)-will, \emph{ex ante, }make \emph{both }majority and minority
\par
shareholders better off." The unequal distribution makes changes of
\par
control more likely both because controlling shareholders are more likely
\par
to part with their interests when they can capture a larger share of the
\par
gain and because the acquiror's costs of gaining control will tend to be
\par
reduced. Such control transactions produce economic gains as control
\par
moves to the hands of those best able to manage the corporate assets.
\par
Inefficient management may be removed. Even where management is
\par
not actually removed, the \emph{threat }of removal promotes better manage-
\par
ment. This enlarges the size of the corporate pie available for
\par
distribution.
\par
\textbf{63 }Does it involve complex and difficult calculations? Difficult evidentiary questions? Is it
\par
likely to provoke difficult and expensive litigation, whether by minority shreholders acting \emph{bonafide}
\par
or opportunistically?
