\par
Protecting the pre-transaction value of minority shares while allowing
\par
the majority to capture the economic gains generated will arguably pro-
\par
duce more of all types of productive fundamental changes.
\par
\emph{C. The Rule of Unequal Sharing in Canadian Capital Markets}
\par
The argument advanced by Easterbrook and Fischel in favour of a
\par
rule of unequal sharing in order to foster economic gains rests in part on
\par
the assumption that both minority and majority have an opportunity to
\par
participate-whether directly or indirectly-in the gains arising from the
\par
non-sharing rule. To the extent that majority and minority shareholders
\par
are \emph{not }overlapping classes in the market but form discrete classes of
\par
capital contributors, the argument weakens.
\par
Canadian capital markets are characterized by a high degree of con-
\par
centration of share ownership. Few companies of significant size are
\par
"management controlled"; a small number of corporate and private
\par
shareholders hold a startlingly large portion of all marketable securities
\par
