\par
shareholders are apparently reduced.
\par
However, as a matter of theory, it is not clear that minority share-
\par
holders \emph{must }be able to hold the securities of parent companies in order
\par
that a non-sharing rule constitute the most efficient rule. Minority share-
\par
holders will be indifferent to the character of the rules as long as any
\par
change makes them no worse off. If a non-sharing rule does indeed en-
\par
hance the wealth of majority shareholders, while not detracting from the
\par
wealth of minority shareholders, moving to a non-sharing rule will effect
\par
a Pareto improvement. The degree of concentration of shareholdings
\par
may therefore only effect the \emph{distribution }of the gains resulting from a
\par
non-sharing rule as between majority and minority shareholders rather
\par
than the magnitude of the gains.
\par
\emph{D. The Economic Costs of a Rule of Unequal Distribution: The}
\par
\emph{Transaction Costs of Opportunistic Redistribution and}
\par
\emph{Potential Diminutions in Enterprise Value}
\par
1. The economic costs of opportunism
\par
Allowing majority shareholders to appropriate the gains flowing
\par
from fundamental changes may not have the uniform property of pro-
\par
ducing more productive fundamental transactions. It may also tend to
\par
produce a large number of unproductive (and possibly wasteful) transac-
\par
tions. Discriminatory alterations of the enterprise may have a purely
\par
redistributional motive. The change may be nothing more than a gambit
\par
by managers or majority shareholders to effect a redistribution between
\par
classes of shareholders, or between shareholders within a class, under the
\par
guise of a change putatively for the benefit of the company as a whole.
\par
The risks of opportunistic redistribution are heightened due to
\par
asymmetric possession of information regarding company value. The
\par
majority shareholders or managers may possess inside information dis-
\par
closing \emph{hidden values }that are not reflected in the market price of the
\par
company's securities and may wish to capture these values for themselves
\par
by excluding the minority.68
\par
