\par
There are a variety of economic losses that will result from under-
\par
taking purely redistributive fundamental changes. One is the direct costs
\par
of the transaction; the company will typically have to send out notices to
\par
shareholders, prepare proxy materials, hold shareholder meetings, and
\par
comply with other regulatory burdens associated with shareholder and
\par
other mandated approvals. Management time will be diverted from
\par
managing the corporation's business interests. Some transactions that
\par
may be undertaken with purely redistributive motives may adversely af-
\par
fect the corporation's capital structure, cash flows, or ability to raise cap-
\par
ital. A leveraged buyout, for example, might burden the corporation
\par
with excessive debt, exposing it to unreasonable risks of bankruptcy and
\par
co-opting cash flow to service the debt while depriving the corporation of
\par
access to the public equity markets.
\par
In a world that permits substantively unequal treatment, the antici-
\par
pation of a higher level of purely or partly redistributional fundamental
\par
changes will lead to a higher level of monitoring of the activities of the
\par
corporation by shareholders. This is also an economic cost.
\par
The familiar problem of adverse selection gives rise to another cost.
\par
Public shareholders may not be able, \emph{ex ante, }to observe the likelihood of
\par
any particular company or majority shareholder engaging in opportunis-
\par
tic redistribution. Thus, in setting a company's cost of capital, the mar-
\par
ket will assume that each issuer will engage in an \emph{average }amount of
\par
opportunistic redistribution. In fact, some companies will engage in \emph{less}
\par
and some companies in \emph{more }redistribution than the average. Those
\par
companies engaging in \emph{less }("high quality issuers") will be penalized by
\par
having a cost of capital reflecting an inappropriately large risk pre-
\par
mium.6 9 Coincidentally, companies engaging in \emph{more }opportunism ("low
\par
quality issuers") will have an inappropriately \emph{low }cost of capital.
\par
This inability of investors to differentiate the quality and character
\par
of corporate issuers will have both a distributive and an allocative effect.
\par
A redistribution of wealth will be effected from relatively high quality
\par
issuers to those of relatively low quality. This in turn will result in a
\par
diminution of investment in high quality enterprises (those with lower
\par
agency costs).
\par
In an efficient securities market,70 however, minority shareholders
\par
will be indifferent \emph{ex ante }to the increased prospects of redistribution. In
\par
such a market, the character and magnitude of redistributive events and
\par
their probability of occurrence will be fully anticipated and reflected in
\par
the price paid by shareholders for their shares.7' Thus, the full cost of
\par
and to classes of shares without voting power or whose votes are overmastered \textbf{by }those of other
\par
classes.
