\par
adopted since it will enlarge the size of the corporate pie available for
\par
distribution to all claimants.72 In this way, private ordering will answer
\par
the problem of adverse selection.
\par
If fundamental changes had a predominantly redistributive charac-
\par
ter, the preferred rule would be a prohibition of all fundamental changes
\par
in the interests of avoiding all the above costs. This alternative is, of
\par
course, untenable given the manifest gains that result from many funda-
\par
mental changes.73 Prohibition, which may be self-imposed or imposed
\par
by statute, will be a real alternative only in those cases where the pro-
\par
spective gains of a particular \emph{type }of fundamental change are dominated
\par
by the risks of redistribution and attendant economic costs.7 ' The focus
\par
must therefore be on the evolution of arrangements (whether statutory or
\par
extra-statutory) \emph{that permit }such discrimination as will facilitate produc-
\par
tive fundamental changes while eliminating or, more realistically, \emph{reduc-}
\par
\emph{ing the probability }of changes having purely redistributive effects.
\par
The appraisal right may be one such mechanism. An appraisal right
\par
that allows a shareholder to secure an independent appraisal of the fair
\par
value of the shares subject to the transaction can serve as both a cure for
\par
and prevention of the harms caused by redistributive fundamental
\par
changes. It is a cure in the sense that if redistribution occurs, the right
\par
will serve to make the shareholder whole. It is a prevention in the sense
\par
that an appraisal right that effectively protects shareholders from redis-
\par
tribution will remove the incentive to engage in redistribution.
\par
Thus, the firm may find it profitable to covenant against redistribu-
\par
tion by incorporating protections against discrimination in its constitu-
\par
tion. As suggested below, a corporate law statute can serve the same
\par
function (while simultaneously reducing transaction costs) in the form of
\par
enabling provisions that replicate the most usual form of private bargain.
\par
