\par
redistributed define the three types of valuation objective that this article
\par
focuses on.
\par
One standard protects against discriminatory fundamental changes
\par
that adversely affect the current market value of the securities with an
\par
appraisal right. A valuation objective geared to pre-transaction market
\par
value protects vested shareholder expectations based on publicly avail-
\par
able information bearing on the firm's earnings prospects. Another pos-
\par
sible standard is the protection of pre-transaction value based not only on
\par
publicly available information, but on insider information as well. This
\par
valuation objective protects against the appropriation of \emph{hidden values}
\par
known only to insiders.
\par
These two standards are similar in that they seek to protect \emph{pre-}
\par
\emph{transaction }values-in the former case, the values are known to the mar-
\par
ket, in the latter, they are not. A third standard is possible. This is a rule
\par
of full substantive equality. Under this rule, whatever gains arise from
\par
the transaction (in addition to any hidden values) must be shared pro \emph{rata}
\par
among all shareholders.
\par
The first and third standards are related. The market price of the
\par
firm's securities will reflect any anticipation of participating in future val-
\par
ues. Thus, a rule protecting current market value protects these expecta-
\par
tions and in effect awards the expected participation in transaction
\par
synergies. This rule yields no protection, however, in respect of \emph{unantici-}
\par
\emph{pated }transaction synergies. The third valuation principle goes the extra
\par
distance and awards participation in even these values. The second and
\par
third valuation principles are also linked. Transaction synergies that are
\par
not anticipated by the market may have been anticipated by insiders,
\par
drawing a connection between protection of hidden values and a rule
\par
allowing for full participation in all transaction synergies. These three
\par
valuation principles will be referred to respectively as "pre-transaction
\par
market value," "hidden values," and "transaction synergies."
