\par
\emph{F. A Rule of Full Substantive Equality}
\par
To sum up thus far, the non-sharing rule advanced by Easterbrook
\par
and Fischel has certain attractive properties. It would appear to generate
\par
incentives that maximize the probability of productive transfers of con-
\par
trol or other fundamental changes, benefitting all shareholders. The rule
\par
is simple and easy to apply and minimizes the costs of shareholder litiga-
\par
tion disputing the "fairness" or "business purpose" of the transaction or
\par
attempting to capture a larger share of the corporate pie. Shareholders
\par
are merely entitled to pre-transaction market value, which is readily as-
\par
certainable. Moreover, if the rule allows for some opportunistic redistri-
\par
bution of values, as long as the possibility of redistribution is fully
\par
anticipated, share prices will simply adjust to the point where minority
\par
shareholders earn a normal risk-adjusted return. The appraisal right
\par
serves a relatively limited (although not necessarily unimportant) role in
\par
constraining opportunism by protecting pre-transaction security values
\par
(as measured by market prices) from encroachment.
\par
This argument may be weaker in Canadian capital markets, where,
\par
owing to the considerable concentration of shareholding interests (and
\par
relatively large number of private parent corporations), the gains of the
\par
unequal sharing rule are divided unevenly between those who hold ma-
\par
jority positions and minority shareholders. In such a market, the risks of
\par
opportunistic redistribution may not be entirely diversifiable. A rule of
\par
unequal redistribution may allow for \emph{greater }variability in the returns of
\par
minority shareholders by allowing an opportunistic shifting of enterprise
\par
values to the hands of the controllers. Nevertheless, this may be antici-
\par
pated and factored into the price of the minority shares.
\par
However, as indicated above, if redistribution generates significant
\par
associated transaction costs or diminutions in enterprise value, an ap-
\par
praisal right with more extended protection may conduce to a lower cost
\par
of capital. Such protection may be supplied by an appraisal right that is
\par
designed to protect both pre-transaction market value and hidden values
\par
that are latent in the enterprise at the time of the fundamental change
\par
and discoverable only on the basis of inside information. This redefined
\par
valuation objective would tend to make opportunistic predations effected
\par
by managers or majority shareholders less profitable, reducing the inci-
\par
dence of such transactions and the associated dead weight social losses.
\par
This valuation procedure introduces its own set of difficulties and
\par
costs, which are explored at greater length below. For now suffice it to
\par
say that procedural, evidentiary, and conceptual difficulties attend the
\par
determination of hidden values. Objective evidence of hidden values will
\par
be hard to come by. These values will be discoverable only (if at all) in a
\par
careful search of the books and other documents of the company, and
\par
then only-particularly in respect of a large public company-with great
\par
effort and expense. Sometimes the inside information will only be known\emph{SHAREHOLDERS' }\textbf{\emph{APPRAISAL RIGHT IN CANADA}}
\par
to the insiders themselves and not subject to documentary or other
\par
discovery.
\par
The difficulties of calculating hidden values might well suggest that
\par
the costs of so doing exceed the benefits. However, difficulties of calcu-
\par
lating hidden values might also be used to argue in favour of a full gain-
\par
sharing principle. If the danger of opportunistic redistribution and asso-
\par
ciated economic costs is large, and it is difficult for a court to differentiate
\par
between transaction synergies and hidden values, it may be easy for the
\par
majority engineering the transaction to successfully characterize hidden
\par
values as transaction synergies. A rule allowing for the recovery of trans-
\par
action synergies by the dissenter would render this strategy of little avail
\par
and would thus operate as a strong disincentive to opportunistic plunder-
\par
ing of hidden values. 1 02 As has already been suggested, this may have
\par
the unfortunate property of discouraging productive fundamental
\par
changes where the realization of the gain depends on, or is more likely
\par
under a rule allowing for, unequal distribution of the gains of the transac-
\par
