\textbf{\emph{A. }}\emph{A Catalogue of Potential Problems}
\par
Thus far, I have identified some reasons why shareholders might
\par
value an appraisal right. However, whether or not a security with an
\par
appraisal right is an efficient financial contract also depends on other fac-
\par
tors. These include whether an appraisal right can in practice be
\par
designed to meet the theoretical needs of shareholders, and the extent of
\par
the cost of exercise of the appraisal right to shareholders and to the
\par
company.
\par
"The company" must be recognized as a proxy for all those whose
\par
claims on the enterprise may be affected by the payout of appraisal
\par
claims. For the most part, these will be those shareholders who choose
\par
not to exercise the appraisal right on any given fundamental change since
\par
the statutory solvency tests that govern the appraisal provision forbid
\par
appraisal payouts if the solvency of the enterprise (and hence the tickets
\par
held by fixed claimants) would be jeopardized by the payments.117 \emph{Ex}
\par
\emph{ante, }it may not be clear to an individual shareholder whether the ap-
\par
praisal right will represent a benefit (if exercised) or a cost (should the
\par
shareholder remain in the company). Therefore, the shareholder will
\par
want to know both the prospective benefits and the prospective costs.
\par
The shortcomings of the appraisal right include at least the follow-
\par
ing." 18 First, there is a tax cost. Cashing out of the enterprise via the
\par
appraisal route will give rise to a taxable event, the burden of which
\par
might have been reduced or avoided altogether by going along with the
\par
fundamental change in the form proposed. Second, most cashed-out in-
\par
vestors will reinvest their funds. This reinvestment will result in broker-
\par
age costs that would not have been incurred had the investment
\par
continued in the enterprise. Third, in the delay between dissenting from
\par
the resolution adopting the fundamental change and the cash payout, the
\par
shareholder may earn less than his or her investment, increasing the cost
\par
of the appraisal right relative to other alternatives (that is, going along
\par
with the transaction or cashing out in the market). Fourth, the proce-
\par
dural hurdles whereby the shareholder claims his or her appraisal right
\par
are often technical and treacherous for the shareholder, who may, by
\par
making any number of technical errors, lose his or her appraisal entitle-
\par
ment. Fifth, exercising the appraisal right is costly to the shareholder,
\par
who will likely have to hire experts to give valuations of the shares and
\par
the pay a lawyer to pursue the claim. Some or all of these costs may be
\par
