\textbf{\emph{B. }}\emph{The Canadian Experience}
\par
The \emph{CBCA }appraisal provisions have been more successful at meet-
\par
ing some of these criticisms than others. On balance, the Canadian expe-
\par
rience raises some serious questions about the value of the appraisal right
\par
to shareholders, at least in its current form.
\par
\textbf{1. }Tax cost
\par
Election of the appraisal option by a dissenting shareholder may
\par
trigger a taxable event for that shareholder. The fundamental change
\par
dissented from may or may not also result in a taxable event for a non-
\par
dissenting shareholder. It is clear that the decision of whether to elect
\par
the appraisal right will be coloured by the \emph{relative }tax treatment accorded
\par
dissenting and non-dissenting shareholders. In determining whether it is
\par
appropriate under the \emph{Income Tax Act }to provide the same or differential
\par
tax treatment to dissenters and non-dissenters (and whether, if the \emph{Act}
\par
fails to make the appropriate distinctions, a court should take the differ-
\par
ential tax treatment into account in appraising the value of the shares), a
\par
number of potential problems must be considered. The first is that \emph{less}
\par
\emph{favourable }tax treatment for dissenters may create an artificial disincen-
\par
tive to claiming the appraisal right, eroding the protection the right af-
\par
fords shareholders under either the bail-out or anti-discrimination
\par
rationales for the right. The second is that \emph{preferential }treatment of dis-
\par
senters may result in shareholders claiming the appraisal right only for
\par
tax reasons, a clearly wasteful and unproductive use of social resources.
\par
The third problem will arise only where the \emph{Income Tax Act }fails to em-
\par
body the theoretically appropriate distinctions (if any) between the tax
\par
treatment accorded dissenters and non-dissenters. In such a case, the
\par
court may wish to adjust the award of fair value in view of the relative
\par
tax consequences in order to achieve the appropriate \emph{after tax }award of
\par
fair value. As noted below, not all the benchmarks for the taxation of
\par
dissenters make it equally easy for a court to make the necessary adjust-
\par
ments to achieve a satisfactory after-tax outcome.
\par
In this section, I will suggest that, irrespective of the rationale and
\par
circumstances under which the right is claimed, the appropriate tax
\par
treatment of dissenters should be the same as that accorded non-
\par
dissenters.
\par
In the context of a freezeout, there are at least three possible\emph{SHAREHOLDERS' APPRAISAL RIGHT }\textbf{\emph{IN CANADA}}
\par
benchmarks for fixing the the tax treatment accorded to dissenters. The
\par
first is that of remaining in the enterprise as if the transaction dissented
\par
from had never occurred. The second is that accorded non-dissenting
\par
shareholders who accept the form and amount of consideration offered
\par
under the terms of the transaction. The third is that of selling the shares
\par
in the market. These will be dealt with in turn.
\par
Had the transaction never occurred, no taxable event would have
\par
arisen. To the extent that any tax is levied on the appraised amount, the
\par
shareholder is worse off by comparison. However, it is easy to overstate
\par
the amount by which the shareholder is disadvantaged. Very few (if any)
\par
shareholders will buy with the expectation of holding the shares indefi-
\par
nitely. In the normal course of events, the shares would have been dis-
\par
posed of at some future date, and on that date a taxable event would have
\par
arisen. Thus, the main component of what the shareholder has lost as a
\par
result of the premature termination of interest is not the full amount of
\par
the tax exigible on the appraised amount, but that amount less the pres-
\par
ent value of the expected tax costs on future disposition. The shorter the
\par
taxpayer's anticipated horizon to disposition at the date of the freeze out,
\par
the smaller the loss associated with the unanticipated cashout and the
\par
smaller the value of the appraisal right as protection against this loss.
\par
Especially where taxation of the proceeds of disposition results in a tax
\par
loss on the appraisal, the taxpayer may also suffer disadvantage as a re-
\par
sult of losing control over the timing of the taxable event (for example,
\par
by losing the ability to push tax losses forward into a year in which offset-
\par
ting taxable gains are expected).
\par
This benchmark appears to be an inappropriate one in any case. It
\par
makes an implicit but important assumption; that the appraisal right is
\par
designed at least in part to protect shareholders against the tax conse-
\par
quences of unanticipated termination of the investment. To a share-
\par
holder holding a diversified portfolio of securities, the risk of such an
\par
event and its resulting financial consequences can be reduced to a mini-
\par
mum. Further, the shareholder's ability to orchestrate his or her finan-
\par
cial affairs so as to minimize the effect of unanticipated termination
\par
cannot be replicated by the corporation. Any anticipated residual effect
\par
on the risk-adjusted financial return from holding the shares can be fac-
\par
tored into the price paid for the shares.
\par
Moreover, should the \emph{Income Tax Act }fail to accord dissenters tax
\par
treatment in accord with this benchmark, the court may wish to adjust
\par
the award of fair value to yield an after-tax. result similar to that exper-
\par
ienced by the shareholder had he or she not cashed out of the company.
\par
It may be difficult or impossible for a court to determine the particular
\par
dissenter's anticipated horizon to disposition had the freezeout not oc-
\par
curred in order to accomplish this good.
\par
Finally, and perhaps most importantly, according dissenters supe-
\par
rior tax treatment to that given non-dissenters will result in an incentive
\par
to claim the appraisal right purely for tax motives.\emph{CANADA-UNITED STATES LAW }\textbf{\emph{JOURNAL}}
\par
\textbf{Vol. 13:299 1988}
\par
If it is inappropriate to accord dissenters more favourable treatment
\par
than non-dissenters, it is also inappropriate to accord them \emph{less }favour-
\par
able treatment. According the dissenters inferior treatment will allow
\par
management to freeze shareholders out of the corporation at less than
\par
the true value of the shares, confident that few shareholders will elect the
\par
appraisal option.
\par
Thus, the second benchmark is a more suitable standard. That is,
\par
dissenters should receive the same tax treatment that non-dissenting
\par
shareholders will receive under the terms of the transaction. This will
\par
remove the tax system as a consideration either for or against electing the
\par
appraisal option, allowing the decision to be made solely on the basis of
\par
the anti-discrimination and bail-out rationales canvassed earlier. For this
\par
same reason, the third benchmark-that of a sale in the market-will be
\par
unsuitable unless non-dissenters receive the same tax treatment.
\par
The situation is more complicated in cases not involving shareholder
\par
freezeouts, under either the bail-out or anti-discrimination rationales. In
\par
this situation, two possible benchmarks present themselves for the taxa-
\par
