the company (aside from the potential competitive cost noted above) and
\par
the related point of what effect this will have on the undertaking of fun-
\par
damental changes. 162
\par
Manning suggests that "[fjrom the perspective of the company,
\par
these statutes can be a frightful nuisance, drain, and burden." He argues
\par
that a "sudden and largely unpredictable drain is imposed upon the cor-
\par
poration's cash position" at a time when "the enterprise is more apt to be
\par
in need of a blood transfusion than a leeching." 1 6 3 He further suggests
\par
that if there are sufficient dissenters, the transaction may be entirely
\par
aborted.
\par
Manning's argument has been met by Eisenberg with equal vigour
\par
on two fronts. First, there "may be a short-run need for a cash input, but
\par
it is seldom material.' ' "M Second, to the extent that the number of dis-
\par
senters grows or threatens to grow, this will exert a salutary effect upon
\par
