\textbf{162 }Earlier, it was suggested that a valuation rule yielding participation in hidden values or
\par
synergies of the transaction may tend to discourage productive fundamental changes and hence gen-
\par
erate economic losses. This must be separated from a concern that the cash drain generated by
\par
widespread exercise of the appraisal right may abort the fundamental change. In the former case,
\par
the concern is with the question of structuring \emph{appropriate incentives }to undertake productive funda-
\par
mental changes. In the latter case, the concern is primarily one of the effect of the payouts on
\par
working capital and the resulting illiquidity and increased financial risk.
