\par
for the shareholders. 65
\par
In the author's view, the Eisenberg arguments carry greater force.
\par
The author knows of no case involving a large public company where the
\par
number of dissenters was so great as to threaten the fundamental change.
\par
Should such a case arise, any fundamental change that succeeded in at-
\par
tracting so many dissenters is not likely to be the sort of beneficial funda-
\par
mental change that there is great concern about saving.166 Given the
\par
general apathy of shareholders, a vigorous dissent expressed through the
\par
widespread exercise of the appraisal right is likely to be symptomatic of a
\par
deep-seated infirmity in the fundamental change. As has already been
\par
suggested,167 the value of the appraisal right lies precisely in its potential
\par
to deter value-decreasing transactions, while permitting value-increasing
\par
transactions to go forward.
\par
\textbf{\emph{C. }}\emph{Summary}
\par
However theoretically attractive the appraisal right is, unless it can
\par
be made to work in practice, it cannot fulfil its potential function. Some
\par
relatively acute practical difficulties presently diminish the effectiveness
\par
of the appraisal right.
\par
It is clear that amendments can be made to the procedural and costs
\par
provisions of the \textbf{\emph{CBCA }}and cognate statutes that would improve the effi-
\par
cacy of the appraisal right. These changes would have the effect of re-
\par
ducing the delays and uncertainties as well as the costs of the appraisal
\par
right. Changes might also be made in the federal taxation legislation. \textbf{A}
\par
minor amendment is indicated in the provision allowing for payment of
\par
interest on the dissenter's shares. The awarding \textbf{by }the courts of a some-
\par
what more generous interest rate paid on appraised value prior to actual
\par
payment is suggested. These improvements would make the appraisal
\par
right more attractive as a remedial option.
\par
