\emph{A. The Appraisal Right as Cure for or Prevention of Discrimination}
\par
1. The alternatives
\par
The utility of a shareholder appraisal right depends to a considera-
\par
ble degree on the utility and scope of \emph{other }remedies available to prevent
\par
an opportunistic redistribution of enterprise values. These other tech-
\par
niques include subjecting the transaction to a test for business purpose or
\par
for fairness; requiring prior administrative or court approval; requiring
\par
management to submit to shareholders a valuation of terminated shares;
\par
putting in place a liability rule of mandated "full disclosure" in favour of
\par
shareholders; applying a liability for trading on inside information; or
\par
requiring a majority of the minority approval whether by means of a
\par
class or intra-class vote. In order to determine the role of the appraisal
\par
right in preventing or curing the harms caused by opportunism, the \emph{po-}
\par
\emph{tential }shortcomings of each of these techniques is examined as well as
\par
the potentially unique role that might be played by an appraisal right.
\par
Since a large part of the difficulty in drawing a line between produc-
\par
tive and unproductive fundamental changes consists in separating those
\par
transactions with a genuine business motive from those that have a
\par
purely or partly redistributional motive, the minority shareholder's first
\par
line of defence might be to subject the transaction to a test for a legiti-
\par
mate "business purpose." Such a requirement has not often been explic-
\par
itly resorted to in Canada although the presence or absence of some
\par
demonstrable business motive will undoubtedly colour a court's attitude
\par
to the transaction if challenged. 1 68
\par
A requirement to make out a business purpose may well eliminate
\par
those transactions that manifestly lack any business purpose. But, as in
\par
other contexts, subjecting fundamental changes to a business purpose test
\par
will not entirely protect minority shareholders against redistributional
\par
predation. An opportunistic redistribution may easily be engrafted upon
\par
a transaction with a legitimate business purpose to lend it an air of pro-
\par
priety. Alternatively, a transaction yielding real economic gains may be
\par
