\par
in order to disenfranchise the minority. Although courts and securities
\par
regulators are no longer as deferential to majority rule as they once were,
\par
nor as slow to review the \emph{bona fides }and the business purpose of corpo-
\par
rate fundamental changes, 16 9 it seems clear that the courts' natural reti-
\par
cence to sit as a review panel on business judgments renders a business
\par
purpose test a leaky sieve in preventing opportunistic redistribution.
\par
A business purpose test, in addition to being under-inclusive, may in
\par
some situations be over-inclusive. Where a court is called upon to review
\par
the business purpose of a transaction, there is the danger that a court
\par
anxious to preserve minority shareholder rights will strike down those
\par
transactions whose business purpose cannot-either because of complex-
\par
ity, uncertainty of attainment, or the danger of jeopardizing sensitive in-
\par
formation-be adequately communicated to the court. In either case, the
\par
difficulty with a business purpose test arises from the questionable insti-
\par
tutional competence of the courts to stand as a review tribunal in ques-
\par
