\par
Moreover, a strategy of reliance upon the invocation by disgruntled
\par
minority shareholders of fiduciary duties or the oppression remedy to
\par
prevent opportunistic transactions runs into the problem of collective ac-
\par
tion and free riders. An assertion of lack of business purpose presents a
\par
complex factual issue to a court. Trial of the issue is an expensive and
\par
time-consuming affair attended by much uncertainty. Few minority
\par
shareholders holding small stakes in the company (many of whom have a
\par
purely transient interest) will stand to gain sufficiently to make a suit
\par
worthwhile. Further, where the plaintiff is unable to secure contribution
\par
towards costs from other shareholders-but all shareholders stand to
\par
gain equally from success-each minority shareholder has a strong in-
\par
centive to let someone else sue. Although costs rules in the federal and
\par
Ontario legislation now allow a shareholder to seek an indemnity from
\par
the corporation in a derivative action, "costs follow the event" is still the
\par
rule of usual application without right of contribution from other share-
\par
holders or the benefit of an indemnity from the corporation. 1 71 A similar
\par
collective action problem plagues a requirement that insiders demon-
\par
