2. The appraisal right
\par
Adoption of an appraisal proceeding may confer benefits on both the
\par
company and dissenting shareholders by virtue of being a \emph{relatively }cheap
\par
dispute resolution mechanism. Although the appraisal right has been
\par
criticized as being slow, uncertain, and costly,185 it may be advantageous
\par
to both majority and minority shareholders if it is cheap relative to other
\par
available relief. While liability entitlements arising under common law
\par
or statutory fiduciary or oppression rules require a complex and expen-
\par
sive adjudication of right, the appraisal right is a strict liability entitle-
\par
ment that arises \emph{as of right }merely on the happening of stated
\par
occurrences." 8 6 Although trial of the issue of "fair price" has often
\par
proved difficult and expensive," 7 by reducing the arena of conflict to this
\par
single issue the appraisal procedure leads to a reduction in the joint com-
\par
pany-shareholder expenses of dispute resolution. In many cases, the re-
\par
sult of the determination of right under a relevant liability rule (for
\par
example, fiduciary duties or the oppression remedy) offers no greater pro-
\par
tection; the courts simply order the buyout of minority shares at an ap-
\par
praised value. 88 Thus, in many cases the appraisal right leads to
\par
comparable relief. It also often avoids the problems associated with mi-
\par
nority holdouts that attend the conferral of powers of veto on minority
\par
shareholders (either individually or as a group).18 9
\par
The strict liability character of the appraisal entitlement is impor-
\par
tant in relation to more than merely the issues of costs and relief. Where
\par
an adjudication of entitlement involves trial of complex issues of law and
\par
fact-as in the case of allegations of breach of fiduciary duty or of op-
\par
pression-a great amount of uncertainty is introduced in the determina-
\par
tion of right. Uncertainty is a cost to risk-averse plaintiffs-the greater
\par
the uncertainty surrounding the outcome, the less likely it is that a mi-
\par
nority shareholder will be willing to sue to defend his or her rights. Re-
\par
sort to the appraisal right restricts the ambit of uncertainty to the issue of
\par
cashout price and eliminates any uncertainty associated with entitlement
\par
(provided the minefield of procedural requirements is crossed without a
\par
fatal accident). All other things being equal, this renders the appraisal
\par
right a comparatively attractive entitlement.
