\par
Moreover, the appraisal right is an individualized entitlement. Class
\par
and majority of the minority requirements protect respectively discrete
\par
and fluid \emph{bodies }of minority shareholders in the company. The appraisal
\par
right, by contrast, protects \emph{individual }shareholders by allowing any
\par
shareholder to exercise his or her appraisal entitlement regardless of the
\par
opinion of \emph{any }other shareholder respecting the fairness or propriety of
\par
the transaction.
\par
The individualization of the entitlement answers to an extent the
\par
problem of collective action; it does not, however, entirely overcome it.
\par
\textbf{A }free-rider problem arises insofar as the court hearing to determine fair
\par
price joins and binds all dissenting shareholders. 90 Hence the valuation
\par
hearing confers joint benefits 9' but, at least in the \emph{CBCA }and most cog-
\par
nate statutes, without a right of contribution from other shareholders or
\par
payment of costs by the company.' 92 This problem might be remedied,
\par
along the lines suggested earlier, by redesigning the costs provisions so
\par
that the company routinely pays part or all of the costs of the
\par
appraisal. 193
\par
\emph{B. The Appraisal Right and the Bail-out Rationale?}
\par
In addition to those advantages already noted, the appraisal right
\par
has perhaps an even more special role to play where it is claimed under
\par
the bail-out rationale. In many situations where the shareholder claims
\par
the right under this rationale, the essence of the protection conferred will
\par
be against unwise business decisions. The decision dissented from will
\par
not typically attract liability under relevant fiduciary or fiduciary-like
\par
norms (like the oppression remedy) or for breach of the duty of care and
\par
skill. Similarly, where the reason for claiming the right under the bail-
\par
out rationale is \emph{opportunistic }risk shifting that diminishes enterprise
\par
value, it will frequently be difficult to convince a court that a simple shift
\par
in the risk of the enterprise constitutes oppressive conduct.' 94 The ap-
\par
praisal right thus confers protection that is not duplicated by the tech-
\par
niques explored above in relation to problems of discrimination. 9 5
\par
\textbf{190 }A court hearing need not arise unless the company and one or more dissentients fail to
\par
agree on a cashout price; however, the price offered to all such dissentients of any given class or a
\par
series must be the same \textbf{\emph{(CBCA, }}\emph{supra, }note 1 at s. 184(19)).
\par
\textbf{191 }The appraisal right may also confer joint benefits if sufficient shareholders dissent, threaten-
\par
ing to cause such a cash drain on the corporation that the fundamental change is abandoned. The
\par
author knows of no case where this has occurred.
\par
\textbf{192 }This problem has been overcome in the Alberta legislation by having the company pay the
\par
costs of the appraisal proceeding. \textbf{\emph{ABCA, }}\emph{supra, }note 2 at s. 184(11)(b) provides that "[a] dissenting
\par
shareholder.. except in special circumstances shall not be required to pay the costs of the applica-
\par
tion or appraisal." It is not entirely clear what license this gives minority shareholders to hire expert
\par
witnesses, at the cost of the company, to contest the company's asserted fair price.
\par
\textbf{193 }See \emph{ibid. }and Part V.B.5, \emph{supra.}
\par
194 Although see \emph{Re Mason and Intercity Properties Limited, supra, }note 188.
\par
\textbf{195 }This does not by itself mean that this protection has any value; only that, if the protection is
\par
otherwise valued, the appraisal remedy is one of a very few ways of conferring this protection.\textbf{\emph{SHAREHOLDERS' APPRAISAL RIGHT IN CANADA}}
