VII. A MENU \textbf{APPROACH TO A NON-MANDATORY STATUTORY}
\par
APPRAISAL RIGHT
\par
Before evaluating the impact of appraisal rationale, transaction type,
\par
and corporation type on the selection of a statutory appraisal right, it is
\par
appropriate to consider another fundamental question: Should the statu-
\par
tory appraisal right be mandatory or merely presumptive in character?
\par
The choice between these two alternatives reflects the choice between the
\par
two principal functions of corporate law.
\par
In this section,. it is suggested that the case for a mandatory statu-
\par
tory appraisal right is weak. However, a case is made for a non-
\par
mandatory (or "permissive") statutory appraisal right or a number of
\par
appraisal rights with alternative valuation objectives. These might be se-
\par
lected at will \textbf{by }any company for incorporation into the company's con-
\par
stitutional documents, with the option of declining to have any appraisal
\par
right at all.
\par
\emph{A. }\textbf{\emph{A }}\emph{Mandatory or Permissive Appraisal Right?}
\par
In one aspect, corporate law is "enabling"; that is, it supplies a type
\par
of standard form contract with terms that the parties in the greater
\par
number of corporations would in any case agree to in an environment
\par
where transactions costs were zero or negligible. Wearing its enabling
\par
mantle, corporate law supplies rules that are presumptive rather than
\par
prescriptive in character. The motivating reason for rules of this charac-
\par
ter is the reduction of the transaction costs of financial contracting; the
\par
statutory standard form renders it unnecessary to specifically contract to
\par
a given outcome. Under this rationale, there is no reason to prevent
\par
those involved in a corporation from contracting to other rules where
\par
they consider it appropriate to do so. The enabling function of corporate
\par
law seeks to assist in the process of private ordering.
\par
In another aspect corporate law is "regulatory," supplying mini-
\par
mum standards of performance or otherwise regulating the conduct of
\par
the affairs of the corporation. Regulatory law by its nature gives rise to
\par
mandatory rules. It is necessarily based on the presumption that private
\par
ordering-market contracting-is failing to produce an entirely efficient
\par
financial contract. \textbf{196}
\par
\textbf{196 Of }course, transaction costs may inhibit the process of bargaining to the same financial
\par
contract as we would see in a zero transaction costs environment. This is also an instance of market
\par
failure. However, the type of market failure that is sufficient to ground mandatory rules is generally
\par
some failure of the activities of the corporation, or the nature of its legal relationship with its security
\par
holders, to be reflected in its cost of capital. Even in the case of "last period problems," where those
\par
exiting the market (for example, by retirement or cessation of business) "take the money and run"
\par
without fear of market reprisals, presumptive rules allowing for contracting out (supplemented \textbf{by}
\par
anti-fraud rules) are adequate as long as these problems are anticipated by the market.
\par
It may sometimes be possible to justify mandatory rules under the enabling function. Mandat-
\par
ing a restricted number of standard form financial contracts the aggregate market costs of pricing
\par
securities. It is obviously expensive to determine and price each feature of the rather complex finan-\emph{CANADA-UNITED }\textbf{\emph{STATES }}\emph{LAW }\textbf{\emph{JOURNAL}}
\par
\textbf{Vol. 13:299 1988}
\par
It is difficult to make out a strong case for regulatory rules with
\par
respect to public companies with widely traded securities. The evidence
\par
is fairly strong that markets for the securities of such companies operate
\par
with a high degree of efficiency.1 9 7 Although there are undeniably
\par
cial contract between shareholder and corporation that is embodied in lengthy documents such as
\par
the articles, by-laws, and other constitutional documents of the corporation. \textbf{If }the degree of vari-
\par
ance in these financial contracts is reduced, securities may be priced at reduced cost, securing (other
\par
things being equal) a net social gain. Of course, other things do not remain entirely equal; there is
\par
loss resulting from restricting the scope of possible financial contracting. The case in favour of
\par
mandatory provisions under the enabling function thus depends on the relative magnitude of these
\par
efficiency gains and losses. It may well be that the argument has the greatest force in the case of
\par
thinly-traded public companies where the marginal reduction of information and pricing costs asso-
\par
ciated with mandating specific features of the company-shareholder contract might tend to be the
\par
greatest. Where, however, (as in the appraisal right) it is unclear what the most efficient form of
\par
contract is, it would be imprudent to mandate any specific form of appraisal right in the interests of a
\par
reduction of information costs.
