\par
suggesting, that the market inappropriately discounts any added risks of
\par
investing in such companies." The available evidence tends rather to
\par
suggest that Canadian markets are efficient in the semi-strong form de-
\par
spite the number of thinly traded companies.2 " 1 Thus, there are still po-
\par
tent incentives for thinly traded companies to select a form of financial
\par
contract that will minimize its cost of capital. It is likely that investors in
\par
such corporations demand and receive a risk premium for investing in an
