\par
\emph{B. The Optimal Form of the Statutory Valuation Principle and the}
\par
\emph{Indeterminacy of Pure Theory}
\par
It has been suggested that the two main functions served by an ap-
\par
praisal right are under the bail-out and anti-discrimination rationales. A
\par
close examination of the different rationales for the appraisal right sug-
\par
gests that the valuation objective might well vary depending on the ra-
\par
tionale under which the appraisal right is claimed.
\par
If, for example, a dissentient shareholder enlists the protection of
\par
the appraisal right in a situation where there has been, or is alleged to
\par
have been, substantive discrimination between shareholders, the optimal
\par
valuation objective \emph{may }include an assessment of hidden values captured
\par
by some shareholders to the exclusion of others or an assessment of the
\par
transaction synergies.
\par
If, on the other hand, the reason for claiming the right is to avoid
\par
the consequences of an unwise fundamental change, the more appropri-
\par
ate valuation objective is to value the dissentient's shares on a pre-funda-
\par
mental change basis in order to provide protection from the effect or
\par
anticipated effect of the change on the enterprise. 2 0 2
\par
The optimal valuation objective may also vary depending on the
\par
type of transaction under consideration. For example, as indicated
\par
above, going-private transactions present far greater dangers of opportu-
\par
nistic redistribution than two-step takeovers, and fewer opportunities for
\par
generating economic gains. Therefore, these transactions present a
\par
stronger case than other types of fundamental changes for a full gain-
\par
sharing rule. Finally, the appropriate valuation principle may depend on
\par
the type of corporation.
\par
Obviously, taking all of these factors into account at once might
\par
yield an extremely complex and unwieldy form of statutory appraisal
\par
right. Even more than this, it would appear that the question of what
\par
constitutes an optimal statutory appraisal form cannot be determined
\par
from pure theory alone. The answer will depend on several matters that
\par
are fundamentally empirical: the degree of opportunism in the market-
\par
place; the extent to which legal and market constraints operate to pre-
\par
vent opportunism; the extent to which a rule of unequal sharing is
\par
necessary to encourage productive fundamental changes; the magnitude
\par
of negotiating, monitoring, enforcement, and other transaction costs
\par
\textbf{202 }The distinction between awarding a post- and a pre-fundamental change value is not a
\par
distinction between looking backward and looking forward. Valuation is always looking forward,
\par
projecting a stream of enterprise earnings and applying an appropriate discount rate. The crucial
\par
difference between the two methods is the selection of the notional enterprise of which the dissentient
\par
shareholder is taken to be a part- that is, the pre- or post-fundamental change enterprise.\emph{SHAREHOLDERS' APPRAISAL RIGHT }\textbf{\emph{IN CANADA}}
\par
under each rule. Variation in any of these parameters may produce
\par
widely differing results.
\par
These are matters about which little hard (or at least systematic)
\par
empirical evidence exists. However, there is an important source of indi-
\par
rect evidence about the relative weights of these factors; private ordering
\par
arrangements in the marketplace. If appraisal rights are an efficient fea-
\par
ture of the shareholder's financial contract, we would expect to see them
\par
introduced in an attempt to reduce the cost of capital. By the same
\par
mechanism, we might gain some insights into the optimal form of the
\par
discrimination principle and hence the appropriate method of valuing
\par
dissentient shares on an appraisal. Other insights may be gained from
\par
the empirical investigations of economists.
\par
\emph{C. The Evidence from the Market}
\par
The record from the market is less than it might be. At common
\par
law, the rule in \emph{Trevor v. Wfhitworth 2 0 3 }made it impossible for a corpora-
\par
tion to repurchase its issued shares. This foreclosed the opportunity of
\par
offering appraisal rights. At the same time that this prohibition was
\par
lifted in Canada, mandatory appraisal rights were enacted in many Cana-
\par
dian jurisdictions. Thus, there is little opportunity to observe the mar-
\par
ket-generated contract.
\par
This does not mean, however, that there is no market record at all to
\par
consult. The federal legislation and cognate statutes allow for con-
\par
tracting out of the appraisal requirement in some situations. Further,
\par
although the statutory appraisal rights are otherwise mandatory, it is
\par
possible for a corporation to offer appraisal rights where not mandated
\par
by statute.
\par
The difficulty is that no systematic evidence exists to indicate the
\par
frequency of contracting out, nor as to how often appraisal rights are
\par
voluntarily given where not mandated. Anecdotal evidence suggests, at
\par
least, that contracting out is not an infrequent event. Such evidence also
\par
indicates that corporations sometimes offer appraisal rights where not
\par
mandated by statute.2"4 However, the implications of the latter type of
\par
event are not entirely clear; such occurrences may be a response to purely
\par
market forces, or an attempt to conform to perceived \emph{legal }norms, which
\par
may or may not be efficient.2 0 5
