ture to suggest that appropriation of hidden values is the key impetus
\par
behind a wide variety of fundamental changes. Of perhaps even greater
\par
interest, these same empirical investigations suggest that minority share-
\par
holders frequently participate in the synergies or other gains generated
\par
by these transactions by virtue of the terms offered them in the transac-
\par
tion.2 10 To the extent that this is true, a valuation objective that includes
\par
a calculation of transaction synergies (whether out of a concern to pro-
\par
tect appropriation of hidden values, from notions of what constitutes fair
\par
treatment of minority shareholders, or otherwise) may be entirely unnec-
\par
essary or even lead to double counting of transaction synergies in favour
\par
of dissenting shareholders. If the latter is true, the appraisal right will
\par
only serve as an invitation to opportunism for minority shareholders,
\par
who will find it worth their while to dissent only to capture a greater
\par
portion of the gains. This in turn will reduce the probability that produc-
\par
tive fundamental changes will occur. 21 1
\par
\textbf{\emph{D. }}\emph{The Menu Approach}
\par
What evidence there is from the market does not, therefore, strongly
\par
confirm the value of the appraisal right. 212 Neither does the market sug-
\par
gest a valuation objective more favourable than pre-transaction market
\par
value if the appraisal right is an efficient shareholder protection. How-
\par
ever, a statutory appraisal right might be capable of curing some of the
\par
deficiencies that stand in the way of an effective market-generated ap-
\par
praisal right or one with a valuation objective extending to hidden values
\par
or transaction synergies. In an unregulated market, appraisal rights
\par
might fail to develop only because the costs of exercise, as well as the
\par
difficulties of drafting and valuation, may overwhelm any benefits
\par
achieved by the right. Drafting a valuation rule is a difficult task that is
