\textbf{211 }The evidence also suggests that in some transactions (like negotiated mergers) one party to
\par
the transaction may capture all the gains which arise; indeed, in a competitive market for corporate
\par
acquisitions, one would expect all the gains to go to the "acquired" firm (whether pursuant to a
\par
negotiated acquisition or not). See Jensen \& Ruback, \emph{supra, }note 73. In these transactions espe-
\par
cially, awarding dissenting shareholders an appraised "fair value" that includes an assessment of
\par
transaction synergies will only encourage withholding of approval by individual shareholders in an
\par
attempt to secure a higher payoff. Such an award would be difficult to justify under either the bail-
\par
out or anti-discrimination rationales. See also K. Schipper \& R. Thompson, "The Impact of
\par
Merger-Related Regulations on the Shareholders of Acquiring Firms" (1985) 23 \textbf{J. }Acct. Res. 184;
\par
