\textbf{Vol. 13:299 1988}
\par
very often not something with which public accountants or corporate
\par
lawyers feel comfortable. Establishing procedures for carrying out the
\par
valuation may be both cumbersome and expensive. The benefits of con-
\par
tracting to an appropriate appraisal right and providing suitable mecha-
\par
nisms to secure effective implementation may not justify the effort.
\par
These infirmities could be overcome by the provision of a number of
\par
"off-the-rack" statutory standard forms with streamlined, low-cost pro-
\par
cedure and reliable, expert valuation. Providing more than one statutory
\par
standard form may serve a wider range of interests than is presently
\par
served by the unitary and mandatory statutory appraisal right. In addi-
\par
tion, it may achieve, by lowering the costs of the appraisal procedure,
\par
what private ordering is unable to do. At the very least, presenting the
\par
option of selecting from amongst a number of statutory standard forms
\par
can do little \emph{harm. }If the appraisal right has little or no value (or is
\par
actually harmful), a corporation may simply stipulate in its constitu-
\par
tional documents that no appraisal right will be available.
\par
These statutory standard forms might be cast as follows. Where a
\par
claim is made under the basic appraisal provision in respect of all public
\par
corporations and all types of fundamental transactions, and without con-
\par
sidering the rationale under which the right is claimed, the appraisal val-
\par
uation should yield only a pre-transaction market price exclusive of any
\par
hidden values or transaction synergies. However, this statutory right
\par
should be permissive in character. An alternative menu of valuation op-
\par
tions should be made available for adoption if desired. One option would
\par
include in the appraised fair value any hidden values arising from the
\par
transaction. 13 Another would include hidden values and a \emph{pro rata}
\par
share of transaction synergies; only this valuation principle seeks to en-
\par
sure full substantive equality of treatment. Any corporation would have
\par
the option of selecting any (or none) of the statutory valuation proce-
\par
dures or any other valuation procedure in respect of any type of funda-
\par
mental change. The costs of so doing would be minimal since the
\par
statutory procedure would specify the valuation objective and procedure,
\par
and the preferred rule would only need to be incorporated by reference
\par
into the corporation's constitution.
\par
Where the baseline valuation technique selected by the corporation'
\par
departs from a pre-fundamental change value, an additional election
\par
could be made in the corporation's constitutional documents, permitting
\par
shareholders to select a valuation based on pre-fundamental change
\par
value. This would protect against discrimination while preserving the
\par
ability of shareholders to elect the bail-out alternative.
\par
Thus, in every case, an appraisal procedure could be tailored to the
\par
transaction and corporation type, permitting the valuation objective to
\par
vary depending on the rationale under which the right is claimed.
\par
\textbf{213 }Thus, the valuation tribunal would be called upon to ferret out and evaluate any inside
\par
information bearing on security value as opposed to merely assessing publicly available information.\emph{SHAREHOLDERS' APPRAISAL }\textbf{\emph{RIGHT IN CANADA}}
\par
Where a case of oppression is made out, the court ought to be given
\par
discretion to apply whatever valuation objective it chooses based on the
\par
factors enumerated above, unless the parties have foreclosed the issue by
\par
appropriate private ordering arrangements. This recognizes the fact that
\par
parties cannot be expected to draft complete contingent contracts deter-
\par
mining the outcome in every possible event of discrimination, even where
\par
the statutory standard form makes contracting cheaper and easier. The
\par
oppression provision should therefore be seen as supplying a \emph{residual }cat-
\par
egory of appraisal event.
\par
With respect to private companies, all of the valuation objectives
\par
available to public companies should be equally available. However,
\par
given the probable expectations of shareholders in most private corpora-
\par
tions, the "default" rule (that which applies if the articles are entirely
\par
silent on the question of appraisal rights) would be a valuation yielding
\par
full substantive equality of treatment for all transactions, irrespective of
\par
the rationale under which the right is claimed and whether it is claimed
\par
under the basic appraisal or oppression provision. Because of the likeli-
\par
hood of unwise or opportunistic fundamental changes that have the effect
\par
of diminishing enterprise value, shareholders should presumptively be
\par
given the ability to elect pre-transaction market value. Again, the basic
\par
statutory appraisal provision would be permissive in character, allowing
\par
for contracting out.
\par
Thus, the question of the appraisal right's efficiency and of the opti-
\par
mal form of valuation principle would be left, in most cases, in the mar-
\par
ket itself. The statutory provision would serve an \emph{enabling }function,
\par
assisting private ordering by reducing the transaction costs of arriving at
\par
a specific contractual outcome.
