\par
there are strict restrictions on the transferability of shares in order to
\par
recognize and accommodate the interest of those who continue in the
\par
enterprise in selecting new "partners."' 6 Even where the continuing
\par
shareholders are receptive to admitting new partners, it will often be diffi-
\par
cult to find those willing to consign their money to an unknown enter-
\par
prise run by persons who are strangers. Thus, there is no "market" for
\par
the corporation's securities in any conventional sense and no "market"
\par
price to use as a benchmark of value.
