\par
for example, should the signing of a potentially unfavourable collective
\par
agreement or a general decline in the stock market caused by threat of
\par
nuclear war not trigger the appraisal right?
\par
There are unquestionably innumerable events in the life of the enter-
\par
prise that may affect the value of the shareholder's investment, and the
\par
appraisal right is triggered by only a very few of these. In the main, it is
\par
substantial \emph{structural }changes that trigger the appraisal right, such as an
\par
amalgamation, a sale of all the company's assets, or significant changes in
\par
the shareholder's financial contract with the company affected by altera-
\par
tion of the articles of incorporation.
\par
The statutory selection of triggering events can easily be justified by
\par
reference to a small number of defining principles: the likely magnitude
\par
of the effect of the change on either the risk or expected return of the
\par
investment; the frequency of the event; the risks of opportunism accom-
\par
panying the event; and the degree to which the company can affect the
\par
outcome of the event.
\par
The greater the magnitude of the effect on risk or return, the more
\par
likely it is that shareholders will value the protection yielded by the ap-
\par
praisal right. The greater the frequency of the transaction, the greater is
\par
the nuisance and probable expense to the company of maintaining, in
\par
effect, a standing order to purchase. With greater risks of opportunistic
\par
redistribution accompanying the transaction, the value of the protection
\par
afforded shareholders by the appraisal right will be greater. Finally, to
\par
the extent that the company cannot affect the outcome of the event, the
\par
corporation may become a general insurer against business risk, an event
\par
that would seriously destabilize the company and imperil its existence.
\par
As noted earlier, shareholders (at least those in public companies
\par
who are able to diversify their holdings) are generally unconcerned about
\par
changes in the level of \emph{unsystematic }risk of the company and it will be
\par
changes in \emph{systematic }or \emph{market }risk or in security value that will attract
\par
their attention. Changes in the financial contract are infrequent, with a
\par
potentially enormous effect on the market risk or expected return of the
\par
shareholder's investment. The risks of opportunism are great. The event
\par
is wholly within the control of the company. It is not at all surprising
\par
that such changes are typically included on the list of triggering events.
\par
The same logic would explain the inclusion of a continuation of the com-
\par
transaction for the appraisal right. Another is the inclusion in the Canadian statutes of continuance
\par
in another jurisdiction as a triggering transaction.
\par
\textbf{256 }The constitutional argument, needless to say, has little relevance in the Canadian context.
\par
Nevertheless, Canadian appraisal provisions draw their inspiration from the American statutes with
\par
the exceptions noted \textbf{\emph{ibil.}}\emph{SHAREHOLDERS' APPRAISAL RIGHT }\textbf{\emph{IN CANADA}}
\par
