\par
stantially all of the company's assets, or a winding-up are also relatively
\par
infrequent events of potentially great significance to the corporation and
\par
the shareholder. These events may either involve a recapitalization of
\par
the enterprise that has the same result as a change in the terms of out-
\par
standing securities or substantially alter the nature of the business carried
\par
on. In each of these cases, the event is an internal one over which the
\par
corporation has control. These changes may bring into play either the
\par
bail-out or anti-discrimination rationales for the appraisal right or both
\par
of them.
\par
External events such as a buildup of superpower tension that de-
\par
press the market are entirely beyond the control of the corporation. It
\par
makes little sense for the corporation to insure equity holders against
\par
these. A security with a generalized put option, exercisable virtually at
\par
will when external events threaten the shareholder's investment, would
\par
significantly destabilize the corporation and is not likely to represent part
\par
of any efficient financial contract. Of course, the risk of opportunism
\par
associated with events beyond the control of the corporation is, by defini-
