formal characteristics but substantively equivalent effects.25 8 It is there-
\par
\textbf{257 \emph{Cf. }}Eisenberg's response to Manning's criticisms, \emph{supra, }note 38 at 73-77.
\par
\textbf{258 }The \textbf{\emph{CBCA }}and cognate statutes fail to achieve complete consistency. For example,
\par
although shareholders of a company \emph{selling }all of its assets will get an appraisal right, shareholders
\par
of the company \emph{buying }the assets will not, even though, as an economic event, the purchase may
\par
have as much or more effect on the buying company's shareholders as the seller's \textbf{\emph{(CBC4, }}\emph{supra,}
\par
note 1 at s. 184). Similarly, where a parent company amalgamates a wholly owned operating sub-\textbf{\emph{CANADA-UNITED STATES }}\emph{LAW }\textbf{\emph{JOURNAL}}
\par
\textbf{Vol. 13:299 1988}
\par
fore appropriate to vest in the court a residual discretion to order an
\par
appraisal for those instances of discrimination that are not transactions
\par
or events that the statute or parties specifically designate as triggering
\par
events.25 9 In the \textbf{\emph{CBCA }and }cognate statutes, this has been achieved in
\par
connection with an application under the oppression provision \textbf{by }al-
\par
lowing the court to order2 60 that the corporation purchase the shares of
\par
the applicant at an appraised value.261
\par
One feature of the Ontario legislation, however, that cannot readily
\par
be explained \textbf{by }reference to any of the above defining principles is the
\par
unavailability of the statutory appraisal right to classes of shareholders
\par
that are not entitled to vote on the resolution approving the fundamental
\par
change.262 It has been suggested here that one of the primary \emph{raisons}
\par
\emph{d'etre }for the appraisal right is protection against discriminatory funda-
\par
mental changes initiated \textbf{by }shareholders or classes of shareholders exer-
\par
cise powers of control. The dangers of opportunistic predation are only
\par
enhanced in respect of those classes of shares that do not vote. One
\par
would therefore have thought that it is these classes to whom the ap-
\par
sidiary into another company, the interposition of the holding company deprives the true owners of
\par
the subsidiary (the shareholders of the parent) of the appraisal option. One mechanism for avoiding
\par
statutory appraisal rights-the effecting of what is in substance a merger by means of the issuance of
\par
a large block of shares by the target in return for the assets of the acquiror-has provoked some
\par
interesting jurisprudence and commentary in the United States. See \emph{Farris v. Glen Alden Corp.}
\par
(1958), 393 Pa. 427, 143 A.2d 25. See Folk, \emph{supra, }note 165. For a thorough review of the technical
\par
inconsistencies in the American legislation, see S.H. Schulman \& A. Shenk, "Shareholders' Voting
\par
and Appraisal Rights in Corporate Acquisition Transactions" (1983) 38 Bus. Law. 1529. See also
\par
Buxbaum, \emph{supra, }note 244 for a review of the California legislation. Although it is beyond the scope
\par
of this article to review the technical inconsistencies in the \textbf{\emph{CBCA }}and cognate legislation, suffice it to
\par
say that many of the technical inconsistencies noted \textbf{by }Schulman and Schenk are present in the
\par
Canadian legislation as well.
\par
\textbf{259 }This discretion might be of at least two varieties: a discretion which attaches only when
\par
\emph{some }form of appraisal right (whether a statutory standard form or otherwise) has been selected and
\par
is in conformity with the valuation objective chosen; or is a general residual discretionary power to
\par
order an appraisal with the valuation objective to be determined by the court. It is probably better to
\par
vest the broader discretion in the court, allowing it remedial flexibility where events arise that were
\par
not anticipated by the parties \emph{ex ante }and in respect of which the risk has not been allocated. Unfor-
\par
tunately, the broader discretion introduces a danger that a court may (by accident or design) upset a
\par
prior allocation of risk by the parties should it order an appraised cashout. The seriousness of this
\par
problem depends on the courts' sensitivity in divining the true ambit of the prior allocation of risk
\par
and the degree of willingness to resist tampering with this allocation. The \textbf{\emph{CBCA }}and cognate stat-
\par
utes currently vest the court with a virtually unrestricted discretion to order an appraisal (see \emph{infra,}
\par
notes 260-61).
\par
\textbf{\emph{260 CBCA, }}\emph{supra, }note 1 at s. 234.
\par
\textbf{261 }\emph{Ibid. }s. 234(3)(f). Where this has occurred, the court has usually ordered a trial of the
\par
issue of cashout price under s. 234(3)(n).
\par
\textbf{262 }\emph{OBCA, supra, }note 2 at s. 184(1) gives the appraisal right to "a holder of shares of any class
\par
or series entitled to vote on the resolution." By contrast, the \textbf{\emph{CBCA, }}\emph{ibid. }at s. 184(1) affords an
\par
appraisal right in respect of designated transactions to "a holder of shares of any class." See also
\par
\textbf{\emph{OBCA }}ss. 184(2), 169 and \textbf{\emph{CBCA }}ss. 184(2), 170 (furnishing an appraisal right to shareholders enti-
\par
tled to a class vote, such vote arising where the class is affected directly or indirectly whether or not
\par
the class is otherwise entitled to vote).\textbf{\emph{SHAREHOLDERS' APPRAISAL }}\emph{RIGHT }\textbf{\emph{IN }}\emph{CANADA}
\par
praisal right has greatest value. Should the statute continue to offer
\par
shareholders an appraisal right, logic and internal consistency suggest
\par
that the Ontario legislation should be amended to conform to the \textbf{\emph{CBCA}}
\par
in offering appraisal protection to non-voting classes of shareholders.
\par
X. CONCLUSION
\par
The Dickerson Committee set out to design a regime of rules for
\par
fundamental changes in which the appraisal right was the Archimedean
\par
pivot. The tool was designed to supplement existing remedies and to
\par
strike an appropriate balance between majority and minority sharehold-
\par
ers. In theory, there may well be reasons that shareholders would desire
\par
the protection afforded by the appraisal right. The protection may be
\par
against unwise business decisions that threaten to diminish security val-
\par
ues or against the effects of discriminatory treatment of a shareholding
\par
constituency. There are also reasons, in theory, for specifying a different
\par
valuation objective, depending on the rationale under which the appraisal
\par
right is claimed. Transaction and corporation type may also influence
\par
the optimal valuation objective.
\par
This theory cannot, however, be divorced from a host of practical
\par
considerations. The value of both the appraisal right as prevention of or
\par
cure for discriminatory action and the optimal form of valuation objec-
\par
tive will depend on the degree to which opportunism is a problem in the
\par
marketplace. This in turn depends on the adequacy of alternative rights
\par
and remedies to which shareholders may have resort. The appraisal
\par
right must be seen as part of a larger tapestry of shareholder protections
\par
both in the law and in the marketplace. In this constellation of protec-
\par
tions, the appraisal right may have particular advantages associated with
\par
the relative cost of its exercise and the manner in which it responds to
\par
collective action problems.
\par
It is clear that the appraisal right generates its own costs, not the
\par
least of which is the cost of the valuation itself, especially where a calcu-
\par
lation of hidden values or transaction synergies is involved. A generous
\par
valuation objective may also discourage the undertaking of productive
\par
fundamental changes. At least in freezeouts of public shareholders af-
\par
fected by a publicly traded parent, an appraised "right of re-entry" might
\par
overcome some of these problems. Pure theory alone can determine
\par
neither whether the appraisal right constitutes an efficient feature of the
\par
shareholder's financial contract nor the optimal form of valuation objec-
\par
tive. Although there is empirical evidence suggesting that pre-transac-
\par
tion market value may be the optimal valuation objective, the evidence is
\par
determinative neither of this question nor in deciding whether the ap-
\par
praisal right under any valuation objective has any value to shareholders.
\par
Thus, the case in favour of the appraisal right has not been conclusively
\par
affirmed or disproved.
\par
At the very least, however, it can be said that a mandatory statutory\textbf{\emph{CANADA-UNITED STATES }}\emph{LAW }\textbf{\emph{JOURNAL}}
\par
\textbf{Vol. 13:299 1988}
\par
appraisal right is likely to interfere with the process of private ordering
\par
through which an efficient financial contract is struck. A case can be
\par
made only for a presumptive appraisal right or for a menu from which
\par
the corporation may choose an appropriate appraisal right or no right at
\par
all. A presumptive right fulfills the enabling function of corporate law
\par
and serves to lower the transaction costs of financial contracting.
\par
It can also be said with some confidence that the current statutory
\par
appraisal right suffers from procedural, cost, taxation, and other opera-
\par
tional infirmities that render it a less attractive remedial option than it
\par
might be. The valuation process would be much improved by entrusting
\par
the task to an expert tribunal or valuator rather than (as at present) to
\par
the courts. 263 What is eminently clear from the brief history of the ap-
\par
praisal right in Canada is that it has largely failed to fulfill the role as-
\par
signed to it by the Dickerson Committee as the centrepiece of the
\par
fundamental change provisions.
