\emph{D. Market Self-Regulation of Information}
\par
In addition to enforcement mechanisms restraining false, misleading or de-
\par
ceptive advertisements, numerous economic incentives safeguard the marketplace
\par
from misleading information. For instance, the motivations underlying the pro-
\par
vision of false, misleading or deceptive information are limited to particular
\par
situations. First, sellers who do not rely on repeat sales have an incentive to
\par
increase current sales through misleading information.'93 Because there is no
\par
expectation for future sales, sellers are concerned only with maximizing short-
\par
term profits. Misled consumers have no effective opportunity to sanction these
\par
sellers. Second, sellers unconcerned with maintaining a particular reputation can
\par
successfully use misleading advertisements without fear of retribution. '" Sellers
\par
may increase profits through deceptive ads because consumers are minimally
\par
aware of the seller. Of course, most businesses are concerned with protecting
\par
their reputation, tradename, trademark or other measure of goodwill. An ef-
\par
fective market mechanism restraining false advertising claims is the potentially
\par
adverse effect an erroneous or untruthful claim may have on the advertiser's
\par
reputation.' 9 5 Finally, if the magnitude of loss is small, the consumer is less
\par
likely to pursue sanctions against the seller. 96 But, as the consumer's loss due
\par
to the deception increases, the possibility of litigation is greatly enhanced.
