\emph{B. Compulsion of Experts Under the Federal Rules}
\par
Whether the federal courts will compel cooperation of an expert,
\par
either at trial or in pretrial discovery, varies with the relationship
\par
of the expert to the parties and to the facts of the case. The expert,
\par
for example, may have acted in or observed the particular events
\par
that gave rise to the lawsuit. The expert may have systematically
\par
observed similar events and developed research data and drawn in-
\par
ferences from the data, but have no knowledge of the particular
\par
events that gave rise to the lawsuit. The expert may have been
\par
retained by a party and thereby have gained knowledge of the par-
\par
ticular events that gave rise to the lawsuit. Under some circum-
\par
stances the knowledge of any of these experts may be of great
\par
value to a litigant, and the litigant may wish to compel disclosure
\par
of the expert's information and knowledge.
\par
