\par
Paul MILGROM and Robert J. WEBER
\par
Northwestern University, Evanston, IL 60201, USA
\par
In this paper, we explore bidders' incentives to gather information in auctions, when there is one
\par
bidder with only public information and another with some private information. We find that the
\par
bidder with only public information makes no profit at equilibrium, while the bidder with private
\par
information generally makes positive profits. Moreover, the informed bidder's profits rise when he
\par
gathers extra information, and the increase is greater when the information is collected overtly than
\par
when it is collected covertly. When the uninformed bidder can observe some of the better-informed
\par
bidder's information, he prefers to make his observations covertly. If the seller has access to some of
\par
the better-informed bidder's information, or if he has affiliated information of his own, he can raise the
\par
expected price by adopting a policy of making that information public. However, there are cases
\par
where a policy of publicizing his information would lower the expected price. The distinguishing
\par
feature of these latter cases seems to be that the seller's information is complementary to the
\par
information of the better-informed bidder.
\par
1. Introduction
\par
Periodically, the United States Department of the Interior sells drilling rights
\par
for oil and gas on federally-owned properties by means of a sealed-bid auction.
\par
The sums involved in these sales are often large. For example, the total amount
\par
paid for the rights to 147 tracts in the Gulf of Mexico in September, 1980 was
\par
\$2.8 billion. Two individual tracts drew winning bids in excess of \$160 million.
\par
To a first approximation, the value of the rights on a tract --- as determined by
\par
the amount of oil in the ground, its depth and pressure, future prices for refined
\par
petroleum products, etc. - is the same to each bidder. However, the bidders may
\par
differ considerably in their information about this value. Some may have access
\par
only to publicly available geological data concerning the site. Others may have
\par
additional proprietary information, perhaps resulting from work on a nearby
\par
tract or from a privately commissioned survey. Our purpose in this paper is to
\par
study how the nature of the information available to a bidder affects his bidding
\par
strategy, his opponents' bidding strategies, the profits earned by the various
\par
bidders, and the revenue accruing to the seller.
\par
*This research was supported in part by National Science Foundation grant SES-800 1932,
\par
U.S. Office of Naval Research grants ONR-N00-14-79-C-0685 and ONR-N00-14-77-C-0518, and
\par
by the Center for Advanced Studies in Managerial Economics at Northwestern University.
\par
0304 4068/82/0000-0000/\$02.75 \copyright{} 1982 North-Holland
\par
106 P. Milgrom and R.J. Weber, Information in a sealed-bid auction
\par
In statistical decision problems, information never has a negative value to the
\par
decision-maker. At worst, irrelevant information can be ignored. More generally
\par
in multi-person settings, information that is gathered covertly cannot cause a
\par
competitive response, and therefore cannot have a negative value. However,
\par
information that is gathered overtly can have a positive, negative, or zero value.'
\par
Akerlof's (1970) well-known 'lemons' model provides an example of
\par
information with negative value. In that model, the seller of a used car knows
\par
more about the quality of his car than does the buyer, and the buyer knows that
\par
the seller is so informed. Consequently the buyer worries that the car is being sold
\par
because it is a 'lemon'. If the seller did not have superior information and the buyer
\par
knew that, then the car could be sold for its expected value,? but as things stand,
\par
the car can be sold only at a discount. Other similar examples include that of
\par
Spence (1974), where workers who know more about their ability than their
\par
employers do can be drawn into harmful competition, and that of Milgrom and
\par
Roberts (1982), where a firm with superior cost and demand data is trapped into
\par
playing a disadvantageous limit-pricing game.
\par
In these examples, when one agent is known to have extra information, the
\par
others alter their behavior in a way that is unfavorable to him. This kind of
\par
response does not reflect any underlying general principle; acquiring information
\par
can sometimes cause favorable changes in the behavior of others.
\par
For example, in this paper we study a two-bidder auction\textregistered{} in which bidder A
\par
has some proprietary information while bidder B has only public information
\par
concerning the value V of the object being sold. We show that A prefers to do any
\par
further information gathering overtly, rather than covertly, because, B's response
\par
to A's extra information is to bid more timidly. Bidder B, however, prefers to
\par
gather any additional information covertly.
\par
The intuition behind these results is as follows. Since A is better informed than
\par
B, B must worry that his bid will win only if A holds a low estimate of V (that is, B
\par
is subject to the so-called "Winner's Curse'). If A acquires additional information
\par
and B knows this, B must bid still more cautiously to avoid losing money. This
\par
reduction in B's bids benefits A. Of course, anticipation of B's response will cause
\par
A to adjust his strategy as well. However, the effects just described persist in the
\par
new equilibrium. An entirely different line of reasoning applies when the more poorly informed
\par
bidder B has a chance to observe some of A's information. We have shown
\par
elsewhere [Engelbrecht-Wiggans et al. (1981), Milgrom (1979)] that B has an
\par
'An exception arises in two-person zero-sum games, where even overtly gathered information
\par
cannot have a negative value. For three-person zero-sum games and two-person general-sum games,
\par
information can have positive, negative, or zero value to a player at equilibrium.
\par
*Actually, this argument requires that it be common knowledge [Aumann (1976), Milgrom (1981)]
\par
that the seller does not have superior information.
\par
Throughout this paper, we restrict our consideration to the standard sealed-bid auction, in which
\par
each bidder submits a non-negative bid and the object is sold to the highest bidder for the amount of},
