\textbf{4 Conclusion}
\par
By developing simple models of auditor behavior and auditor-client
\par
interaction, we have been able to obtain useful insights as to the likely
\par
consequences of changes in the legal system. Considering auditors in iso-
\par
lation, we find that increases in auditor liability lead to increases in both
\par
auditor effort and fees. The size of the impact depends, in part, on auditor
\par
size.
\par
Allowing for auditor-client interaction, we show that both the set of
\par
admissible defenses and the relative costliness of mistakes help determine
\par
the levels of effort chosen by auditors and client managers. We show that
\par
increased damage awards do not lead to increased effort under some liability
\par
rules. Finally, to the extent that recent changes in auditor liability can be
\par
characterized, in part, as a shift from scenario \textbf{(4) }to either scenario \textbf{(1) }or
\par
\textbf{(3), }our results suggest two reasons for future auditing fees increases: the
\par
change in the set of auditor defenses and the higher damages awarded to
\par
successful plaintiffs in suits brought against external auditors.
