\textbf{Multinational union-management}
\par
\textbf{consultation in Europe:}
\par
\textbf{Resurgence in the 1980s?}
\par
Herbert R. NORTHRUP, Duncan C. CAMPBELL
\par
and Betty J. SLOWINSKI *
\par
\textbf{O }ne of the expectations raised by the launching of the European
\par
Community was that economic integration would lead to consultation
\par
and eventually to collective bargaining between multinational unions and
\par
multinational managements. Several significant formal consultations did
\par
develop,' but by the beginning of the 1980s virtually all except those in the
\par
entertainment-communications industries2 had fallen victim to restructuring,
\par
unemployment or disagreement between the parties.' Some contacts con-
\par
tinued or even expanded as described below, but as a rule overt, formal
\par
multinational arrangements were conspicuous by their absence. By 1981 the
\par
severe recession and political changes in key European countries had led
\par
employers and unions to modify their approach to European or worldwide
\par
problems, and this of course has been reflected in the political agendas both
\par
of individual countries and of the European Community, as well as of other
\par
intergovernmental agencies.
\par
The changing character of these approaches, new initiatives towards
\par
multinational union-management consultation in the 1980s, and the pros-
\par
pects for the future are discussed in this article.
\par
\textbf{I. }The evolution of multinational relationships
\par
Over the past two decades, efforts to bring multinational enterprises
\par
within a system of international consultation have evolved from early union
\par
attempts to achieve this (and eventually bargaining as well) through the use
\par
of industrial muscle, to the inclusion of the multinational firm within a regime
\par
of voluntary codes of conduct, and finally to the proposed establishment of a
\par
comprehensive statutory system at the regional, if not global level. None of
\par
these efforts has been able to overcome entirely the resistance of one or more
\par
\textbf{* }Respectively Professor Emeritus of Management and former Director; Lecturer,
\par
Department of Management, and Associate Director; and French Translator and Senior
\par
Research Specialist, Industrial Research Unit, The Wharton School, University of Pennsylvania.
\par
The authors wish to thank Professor Richard L. Rowan for assistance with the section on
\par
developments in the automobile industry.
\par
Copyright \copyright{} International Labour Organisation 1988\emph{International Labour Review}
\par
of the parties that would be bound by an international regulatory framework,
\par
but consultation has developed in a quasi-formal manner - or has even been
\par
formalised by agreement - in some interesting and possibly precedent-setting
\par
developments.
\par
The decline of early consultation moves
\par
Union fears of multinational enterprises centre on their ability to
\par
transfer capital across national boundaries and to "play off" one subsidiary
\par
against another, thereby circumventing the constraints on management
\par
decision-making exerted by industrial relations machinery at the national and
\par
local levels. In the early 1970s it seemed that the way to enhance union
\par
control was to extend union-management relationships to the international
\par
level. This solution, however, collapsed on all except the entertainment-
\par
communications front for a number of reasons: employers feared that
\par
consultation might lead to bargaining, with the risks and limitations that
\par
implied; national union officials were reluctant to transfer power to an
\par
international body; employees demonstrated little or no interest; and laws,
\par
customs and bargaining structures in Europe remained anything but harmon-
\par
ised.' Moreover, whatever appeal the idea had was diminished by the
\par
downturn in the economy, for in a climate of uncertainty and decline,
\par
internationalism gives way to protectionism both for unions and for
\par
management. As early as 1981, Herman Rebhan, General Secretary of the
\par
International Metalworkers' Federation (IMF), declared that "the search for
\par
a system of common bargaining is a will of the wisp", and that he "would not
\par
dissent" from our conclusion "that there is no such thing as multinational
\par
collective bargaining".'
\par
By this time, as already noted, the formal arrangements for meetings and
\par
consultation were dead or severely wounded, whether by disagreement,
\par
disinterest or restructuring. The International Union of Food and Allied
\par
Workers' Associations (IUF) did achieve a noteworthy bargaining role with
\par
the Coca-Cola company over the status of the company's Guatemalan
\par
bottling affiliate. But even though the IUF won its objective in Guatemala, it
\par
did not achieve a lasting relationship with Coca-Cola.6
\par
The International Federation of Commercial, Clerical, Professional and
\par
Technical Employees (FIET) had numerous contacts with companies in the
\par
insurance and retail industries during this period, some involving just FIET
\par
officials, others including national union personnel as well. Although the
\par
significance of these meetings should not be discounted, we are unaware of
\par
arrangements to continue them on a permanent basis.'
\par
Guidelines as consultation
\par
As the 1970s wore on, many international union bodies turned to
\par
political channels; these included giving strong support to codes of conduct\emph{Multinational union-management consultation}
\par
for multinational enterprises promulgated by the Organisation for Economic
\par
Co-operation and Development (OECD) in 1976 and by the ILO in 1977.
\par
Most of the activity under the codes has concerned the OECD "guidelines"
\par
(especially those relating to industrial relations and employment), and the
\par
most contentious cases have involved plant closures within individual
\par
countries rather than co-ordinated union action. Nevertheless, a multina-
\par
tional union-management consultation device has been activated at the
\par
OECD through the mechanism of requests for "clarification" of individual
\par
guidelines by the Trade Union Advisory Committee (TUAC) or member
\par
governments - despite the fact that TUAC's management counterpart, the
\par
Business and Industry Advisory Committee (BIAC) has strongly resisted the
\par
transformation of a voluntary code into a quasi-regulatory process! For
\par
various reasons, less use has been made of the corresponding procedure for
\par
interpreting the ILO's Declaration on multinationals. The sentiment among
\par
national policy-makers that the elaboration of regulations may contribute to
\par
economic inefficiency seems, in any case, to have slowed moves to expand
\par
the reach of codes of conduct.
\par
Co-determination and hard times
\par
While formal multinational consultation arrangements were declining in
\par
the late 1970s, new types were being created. In the Federal Republic of
\par
Germany, after the co-determination law covering large companies was
\par
amended in 1976,1 four officials of international trade secretariats (ITSs),
\par
three of whom now remain, and one European regional union official were
\par
given seats on company supervisory boards."{$^\circ$} This provides multinational
\par
union bodies with the opportunity for high-level information and consulta-
\par
tion, and with the power to influence decision-making by emphasising
\par
international trade union concerns - for example, by opposing investments in
\par
low-wage environments.
\par
The severe recession of the early 1980s not only reduced union interest
\par
in multinational consultation arrangements, it also lessened employer
\par
opposition to meeting with international union officials. The wheel had
\par
turned full circle: employers no longer feared the concessions such meetings
\par
might extract from \emph{them, }they were eager to explain and to lobby for
\par
concessions they needed from the \emph{unions. }This has been particularly true for
\par
the automobile companies, which deal with strong unions and have been
\par
adversely affected by international competitive forces. Executives from
\par
Volkswagen, Renault, General Motors and Ford have attended conferences
\par
sponsored by the IMF. Thus in June 1987 Peter J. Pestillo, Ford's Vice-
\par
President for Employee and External Affairs, addressed a meeting of the
\par
IMF Ford Auto Council in Chicago" and answered questions from the
\par
audience. Pestillo analysed the competitive and quality aspects of the
\par
automobile industry and attempted to clarify the company's position in
\par
regard to out-sourcing. This is the first time that a Ford representative has\emph{International Labour Review}
\par
been willing to address an IMF council meeting and it is one indication that
\par
management's earlier reluctance to meet with the international union bodies
\par
for fear of establishing a bargaining relationship may now be breaking down
\par
in face of the need to secure union acceptance of industrial restructuring.
\par
Similarly, the meeting of the European Metalworkers' Federation
\par
(EMF) in Brussels in April 1987, dealing with "The impact of new
\par
technology on the world environment in industry", featured presentations by
\par
executives from General Motors, Ford, Nissan, Volkswagen and Peugeot.
\par
Management participants at the conference stressed that new technologies
\par
require new and better employee qualifications, and there was agreement
\par
that discussions on this question should be continued and efforts made at the
\par
European level to develop a common strategy.
\par
European Community employment law
\par
A clear objective of European Community (EC) law has been to
\par
harmonise social legislation and policy among the member States. Multina-
\par
tional union-management consultation may be considered a means to this
\par
end, on the one hand, and would be facilitated by such harmonisation, on the
\par
other. The evolution of EC social policy over the years has given rise to
\par
different approaches to such consultation. Thus, according to Hepple, EC
\par
social policy was at first harnessed to a philosophy of "economic liberalism",
\par
which assumed that the member States' harmonisation of social policies
\par
would be a natural outcome (rather than \emph{precondition) }of economic growth.
\par
Instead of promulgating laws that would equalise employment conditions
\par
among member States, "it was argued that as demand for labour increased
\par
where labour was cheapest, wage rates would tend to rise and free circulation
\par
of labour would facilitate an equalisation of conditions of employment". 2
\par
The second period of EC social policy began with the expansion of the
\par
Community from six to nine Members in the early 1970s, and led to the first
\par
Social Action Programme of 1974. For the rest of the 1970s the direction of
\par
the Social Action Programme was shaped by a prolonged period of economic
\par
downturn accompanied by ever higher levels of unemployment. During this
\par
period voluntary union-management consultation took a back seat in favour
\par
of direct government action and intervention. 3
\par
The third period, which began about 1980, has been one of "deregula-
\par
tion" ; this returns full circle to the premise of the "economic liberalism" of
\par
the first period, since a guiding assumption of centre-right governments and
\par
employers is that additional regulation in the labour area may increase firms'
\par
costs and thereby impede rather than promote social progress. Nevertheless,
\par
the period began with policy initiatives considerably at odds with this new
\par
climate. Most symbolic of the growing dichotomy between social policy
\par
formulation at the \textbf{EC }level and the interests of national governments and
\par
employers' groups was the proposed Vredeling directive of 1980. Named
\par
after the then Social Affairs Commissioner, the Dutch Socialist Henk
\par
\emph{528Multinational union-management consultation}
\par
Vredeling, the proposal was aimed at multinational enterprises in particular;
\par
it would have expanded the information and consultation rights of employee
\par
representatives and increased their access to decision-makers at multina-
\par
tional levels within the firm.
\par
Equally contentious, and perhaps equally symbolic of the way in which
\par
EC policy was to become increasingly blocked by member States, was the
\par
proposed Fifth Company Law directive, which had been proposed at the
\par
beginning of the "second period" of EC social policy but acquired
\par
considerable momentum only in the early 1980s. A key provision of the
\par
proposed directive would have extended board-level participation by
\par
employee representatives to the European-wide level as well. In addition to
\par
these, a host of other proposed labour laws pertaining to hours of work and
\par
the conditions of certain categories of workers - part-timers, temporary
\par
workers and women - were put forward by the Commission and subse-
\par
quently blocked in their legislative passage or downgraded in their degree of
\par
compulsion.
\par
A factor that may explain the disjunction of EC legislative activity with
\par
the mood of both employers and some member States is a structural one:
\par
unions are better organised than employers at the EC level, and the link
\par
between social policy formulation by the Commission and the European
\par
Trade Union Confederation (ETUC) is bolstered by an active and respected
\par
research arm, the European Trade Union Institute (ETUI), funded in part by
\par
the EC. The Union of Industries of the European Community (UNICE), on
\par
the other hand, which in the first place does not speak for all employers, is
\par
further hampered by their reluctance to cede authority to an organisation
\par
that may draw employers into unwanted arrangements or even collective
\par
bargaining agreements at the EC level. 4 UNICE's willingness to co-operate
\par
with the Commission and the ETUC in determining the shape of EC social
\par
policy had been clearly visible in the employment law developments of the
\par
1970s. With Vredeling, that willingness was seriously eroded: UNICE was
\par
now restrained by the hostility of its constituents towards the Commission's
\par
social policy intentions and by the vigour with which Commissioner
\par
Vredeling sided with the ETUC.'5 The subsequent failure of Vredeling and
\par
the Fifth directive meant that the drive for compulsory multinational union-
\par
management consultation was derailed, at least for the time being.
\par
As their relations with the other EC social partners cooled, the employers
\par
turned increasingly to lobbying against the direction the Commission's social
\par
policy was taking. United States and Japanese employer groups joined in the
\par
effort, which assumed unprecedented proportions. Another factor leading to
\par
the policy disjunction was the election in the early 1980s of several
\par
predominantly centre-right governments, which voiced varying degrees of
\par
disenchantment with the Commission's social policies. But it was the British
\par
Government of Margaret Thatcher that became the standard-bearer of
\par
opposition to the proposed employment laws, placing on record its "profound
\par
reservations" concerning the Vredeling and Fifth directive proposals. 6\emph{International Labour Review}
\par
Objections by employer groups and the British Government, among
\par
others, to the proposed Vredeling and Fifth directives were generally
\par
expressed in the form of opposition to increased regulation of business. The
\par
logic was that increased regulation could increase firms' costs and reduce
\par
their competitiveness. Moreover, some aspects of both the Vredeling and
\par
Fifth directives appeared to their opponents as the extension of a German-
\par
style system of co-determination to countries where tradition and law have
\par
forged rather different industrial relations patterns, and as an attempt to
\par
internationalise matters that are properly left to national and local decision-
\par
making.
\par
The stalemate in EC social policy is thus attributable to a conflict
\par
between two models of political economy. A sort of "Euro-corporatist" one
\par
manifested in the proposed Vredeling and Fifth directives, was championed
\par
in the early 1980s by EC legislators who favoured statutorily based systems
\par
of participation, including multinational consultation. This model, however,
\par
was increasingly undermined by employer unwillingness and the shift
\par
towards centre-right political regimes in the member States, which saw
\par
"economic liberalism", rather than increased regulation, as the best means
\par
of encouraging economic growth. Arguably marking an end to the period of
\par
stalemate is the current tendency of the European Commission to subordi-
\par
nate legislative programmes in the area of social policy to "informal
\par
dialogue" between the parties. This reorientation appears to constitute a
\par
fourth era of EC social policy that clearly provides for multinational union-
\par
management consultation, but is best discussed after some French initiatives
\par
have been analysed because these have a similar origin and ideological
\par
basis.
\par
\textbf{II. }The French socialist management initiatives
\par
In 1981 the election of a socialist administration in France committed to
\par
a greater role for workers in management made France a potential European
\par
leader in the advocacy of multinational union-management consultation. The
\par
new political environment had a direct impact on French industrial relations.
\par
In 1982 the passage of the four Auroux Acts radically changed the system of
\par
workers' representation. 7 Of particular importance was the second Auroux
\par
Act, which contains a major innovation: the creation of \emph{comites de groupe,}
\par
which are joint employer-employee committees that cover an entire group of
\par
undertakings, thereby extending the local works council to the intercompany
\par
or group level.
\par
A "group of undertakings" broadly encompasses the dominant parent
\par
of a holding company, its majority- or wholly-owned subsidiaries, as well as
\par
associate or other organisations in which the parent has a financial interest.
\par
Each group committee consists of the managing director of the dominant
\par
company or his representative plus two persons of his choice, and of\emph{Multinational union-management consultation}
\par
employee representatives from undertakings belonging to the group, who are
\par
,appointed by unions from among their elected members of the works or
\par
establishment committees. Group committees are entitled to receive infor-
\par
mation on the present and future activities, financial situation and employ-
\par
ment trends of the group and each of its component undertakings.
\par
As a result, French employees' representatives now have access to
\par
information on the global strategy of the employing group of companies.
\par
Thus, although the legislation applies only to operations based in France, it
\par
clearly sets a precedent for the extension of consultation to the multinational
\par
level. Moreover, the nationalisation of major companies by the 1981-86
\par
socialist Government encouraged receptivity to union requests for multina-
\par
tional relationships. In addition, a small number of French companies have
\par
traditionally been controlled by managers who have been open to, or have
\par
even advocated, multinational union-management consultation. BSN-
\par
Gervais Danone, discussed below, is a case in point.
\par
Saint-Gobain: Informal consultation
\par
\textbf{. }Prior to its nationalisation, the management of the glass and building
\par
materials concern, Saint-Gobain, declined to meet with the International
\par
Federation of Chemical, Energy and General Workers' Unions (ICEF).'8
\par
After Saint-Gobain was nationalised, however, its management agreed to a
\par
multinational meeting at the request of the chemical workers' union
\par
(Frddration unifide chimie \textbf{- }FUC) affiliated to the French Democratic
\par
Confederation of Labour (Confrddration frangaise drmocratique du travail -
\par
CFDT), -the confederation which is most closely aligned with the French
\par
Socialist Party.
\par
The meeting, held in Paris on 7 December 1983, was attended by the
\par
company's central management, including the President, and 33 union
\par
representatives from its glass factories in seven countries - Belgium,
\par
Denmark, France, the Federal Republic of Germany, Italy, the Netherlands
\par
and Spain. The discussion reportedly centred on employment. Although no
\par
concrete results were achieved, the CFDT hailed the meeting as the
\par
beginning of an international relationship. Saint-Gobain's management,
\par
however, made clear that it "did not intend to formalise these meetings or to
\par
hold them regularly") 9
\par
Neither the ICEF nor the French communist-led General Confederation
\par
of Labour (Confrdrration g6ndrale du travail - CGT) was involved in the Paris
\par
meeting, although a majority of Saint-Gobain's French unionised employees
\par
are CGT members. The CGT's future participation was deemed important by
\par
the FUC in order to start a truly effective dialogue with the company's central
\par
management, but it was opposed by most of the other unions which are
\par
traditionally reluctant to meet with communist organisations.
\par
This example of multinational union-management contact was clearly
\par
facilitated by the creation of a group committee within Saint-Gobain. An\emph{International Labour Review}
\par
official assessment of the group committees made by the French Parliament's
\par
Social Affairs Committee in mid-1985 showed that although certain
\par
agreements awarded the group committees the minimum jurisdiction defined
\par
in the 1982 Act, others allowed them rights not only to information
\par
disclosure but also to full consultation over company strategy. Saint-Gobain
\par
was listed among the second group and was singled out as having provided a
\par
special budget for its group committee work.2 0
\par
The fact that Saint-Gobain at that time was nationalised undoubtedly
\par
contributed to its willingness to have the meeting, although it did not let
\par
this interfere with such business decisions as the elimination of unneces-
\par
sary jobs.2 " Following the company's denationalisation by the 1986-88
\par
centre-right Chirac government, however, one wonders if any future
\par
meetings will be held. Without the involvement of an ITS or other
\par
multinational union organisation, and a favourably inclined management
\par
and/or political climate, national unions find international initiatives dif-
\par
ficult to sustain.
\par
Thomson-Grand Public: Creation of the first
\par
European works council?
\par
The Thomson group, also nationalised by the 1981-86 socialist Govern-
\par
ment, is headed by a very prominent French Socialist, Alain Gomez. Its
\par
Grand Public branch specialises in consumer electronic goods and has
\par
production units in France, the Federal Republic of Germany, Italy and
\par
Spain. It recently purchased the television business of RCA and General
\par
Electric in the United States.
\par
On 7 October 1986 Thomson-Grand Public (TGP) signed a two-year
\par
agreement, effective 1 January 1987, with the EMF, which was renewed
\par
indefinitely in February 1988. The agreement establishes two distinct bodies:
\par
\emph{A TGP-EMF Liaison Committee }composed of the TGP Chairman or his
\par
representative, assisted by the competent directors, and the EMF General
\par
Secretary plus 14 officials from EMF-affiliated unions: six French, four
\par
German, two Italian, two Spanish; since the renewal of the agreement,
\par
British representatives have been added. The Liaison Committee members
\par
meet every six months, at the expense of the company. They are to be
\par
informed of the economic, industrial and commercial activities of TGP and of
\par
all modifications in the economic and legal organisation of the company as
\par
well as of major structural and industrial changes prior to their implementa-
\par
tion, and particularly of plans for technical change. They also have the right
\par
to express opinions on all matters presented to them.
\par
\emph{A European Branch Committee }composed of the TGP Chairman or his
\par
representative, assisted by the competent directors, and 26 employee
\par
representatives elected on a proportional basis from among the members of
\par
the works councils of the company in the five countries. The Branch
\par
Committee meets annually at company expense, can establish ad hoc\emph{Multinational union-management consultation}
\par
subcommittees to deal with particular problems that arise at the European
\par
level, and has basically the same information rights as the Liaison Com-
\par
mittee.
\par
The agreement, which is the result of more than ten years of TGP co-
\par
operation with the EMF within a "working group" that was set up in 1973, is
\par
an important one for several reasons. First, this is the first time that a
\par
corporation in manufacturing industry has officially recognised an interna-
\par
tional union secretariat as an official representative of its workforce at the
\par
international level. Second, the agreement creates what an EMF press
\par
release refers to as "the first European works council", the first true
\par
information and consultation structure at the European level.22 Finally, it
\par
incorporates the spirit, if not the letter, of the proposed EC Vredeling
\par
directive. The committees, however, have no official bargaining status.
\par
Although their members have the right to be informed and to express their
\par
views, there is no specific commitment on Thomson's part to take their views
\par
into account. None the less, Thomson may find it difficult to limit the
\par
function of these committees to providing information and consultation.
\par
Several factors probably encouraged this cross-frontier pact. First, TGP
\par
was undergoing restructuring and the company seemed to believe that the
\par
European dimension could facilitate a smooth introduction of changes
\par
needed to compete with Japan and the Republic of Korea in the consumer
\par
electronics sector. Second, the move might have been prompted by ma-
\par
nagement's wish to improve the company's reputation in respect of
\par
labour practices, which had been tarnished in 1982 when Thomson closed
\par
Videocolor GmbH, a colour television tube manufacturer in the Federal
\par
Republic of Germany, and transferred production to Italy. Third, the
\par
Chairman of the company had publicly stated that the group had too many
\par
operations and that closures were therefore inevitable, thus attracting the
\par
attention of and giving ammunition to the EMF.
\par
The fact that Thomson was a nationalised company, headed by a
\par
Socialist, and that the French Government was supportive of industrial
\par
dialogue certainly played an important role in the decision. As the CFDT
\par
stated: "It would be inappropriate for a nationalised company not to
\par
participate in the European 'social space' called for by the Head of State,
\par
Franqois Mitterand. "23
\par
The elaboration of an EMF model
\par
The Thomson agreement is a clear example of an alert regional union
\par
secretariat seizing the opportunity to win a multinational agreement creating
\par
what the EMF seems justified in calling the first European works council.
\par
The establishment of such "Euro works councils" has become a major
\par
objective of the EMF, which hopes to arrange ten or 15 "precedents" with
\par
multinational enterprises over the next three years. Although Hubert
\par
Thierron, the EMF General Secretary, expressed his disappointment and\emph{International Labour Review}
\par
frustration at the June 1987 EMF Congress in Seville that most companies
\par
are not interested in such arrangements with the EMF, by May 1988 the
\par
EMF was attempting to finalise the details of two more agreements, one
\par
involving the Bull group of Honeywell-Bull, and the other the European
\par
operations of the American multinational, Continental Can.2"
\par
Representatives of the companies have been the motive force behind the
\par
development of both these tentative agreements. The managements' interests,
\par
however, appear to be more pragmatic or strategic than ideological:
\par
anticipating corporate restructuring, each company apparently perceived
\par
advantages to establishing employee representation on a company-wide basis.
\par
Indeed, according to Thierron, delays in clinching the agreements have arisen
\par
on the union side rather than from management resistance. In Bull's case, for
\par
example, one difficulty was how to include employee representatives from the
\par
company's Swedish subsidiary who are members of a union that is not
\par
affiliated to the EMF. The solution involved the EMF's seeking the mediative
\par
help of the FIET, the ITS to which the Swedish union is affiliated.
\par
Both the shape and the scope of these new agreements will differ from
\par
the Thomson model. In the first place, the new agreements will apparently
\par
establish a unitary structure of participation, rather than the dual structure of
\par
the Thomson agreement. A second distinction bears on the scope itself of at
\par
least one of the agreements. In the case of Continental Can, employee
\par
representatives will have 30 days in which to consider management proposals
\par
affecting employment, with the benefit of outside expert counsel when they
\par
so wish. The proposed agreement thus goes beyond mere information, and
\par
strengthens the consultative rights of the employee representatives.
\par
A major lesson to be drawn from these new constructions is that they
\par
must be built from within, rather than imposed from the top. Management
\par
support for the concept clearly seems to be a necessary condition, but not a
\par
sufficient one, since co-ordinating the methods of employee representation
\par
and even selecting which representatives are to participate appear to have
\par
presented major challenges.
\par
Establishing a Euro works council is thus by no means an easy task, even
\par
where both parties offer some support. The problem is further illustrated by
\par
yet another EMF-sponsored attempt to set up a Euro-wide consultative
\par
mechanism at Airbus Industrie. Talks continue between the EMF and the
\par
management of the aerospace consortium over the question of which
\par
employees are to be represented. As a consortium of five national aerospace
\par
companies, Airbus employs directly only 50 per cent of all employees who
\par
work on Airbus projects. The remainder are seconded to the consortium
\par
from their national companies. The EMF has favoured the creation of a Euro
\par
works council for all Airbus employees, including those seconded, but there
\par
are signs of management dissent. Disagreement over the value of establishing
\par
a cross-national mechanism for employee representation may arise because
\par
the constituent firms have greater autonomy vis-A-vis the consortium than do
\par
subsidiaries vis-A-vis their parent firms.\emph{Multinational union-management consultation}
\par
BSN-Gervais Danone: Establishment of an international
\par
union-management committee
\par
Antoine Riboud created BSN by merging several glass companies,
\par
initially in France and then in the Federal Republic of Germany, Austria,
\par
Belgium, the Netherlands and Spain. BSN later expanded into the food,
\par
beverage and packaging business not only in Europe but also in the Americas
\par
and Japan. In 1979 it decided to concentrate on the food, beverage and
\par
related businesses, and over the next few years sold all of its flat glass
\par
operations. BSN has never been nationalised.
\par
In 1972 the BSN takeover of Belgian flat glass operations precipitated
\par
opposition from unions there. BSN responded by establishing a multinational
\par
committee of labour and company representatives to meet regularly and
\par
discuss company plans. Except for one meeting, the ICEF was not involved.
\par
Moreover, neither the CFDT nor the CGT participated in these meetings,
\par
which were strictly informational in character.25
\par
During the 1970s BSN was the only multinational manufacturing
\par
concern that had a multinational consultative agreement based upon a
\par
written document. Moreover, the initiative for this arrangement came from
\par
the company, not from the unions or from an international trade union
\par
secretariat. It is, therefore, not surprising that BSN management has resumed
\par
such a practice, this time with the ITS of the food industry, the International
\par
Union of Food and Allied Workers' Associations (IUF), since BSN is now a
\par
food and food packaging concern. Employee and union representatives of
\par
BSN workers from several European countries, plus the General Secretaries
\par
of the \textbf{IUF }and of the European Committee of Food, Catering and Allied
\par
Workers' Unions within the IUF (ECF-IUF), met twice with BSN manage-
\par
ment representatives, first in Geneva on 21 April 19866 and then in Paris on
\par
9 June, to discuss the creation of a formal consultative agreement, which was
\par
signed in Brussels on 29 October 1986.27
\par
The agreement, which creates a permanent mechanism "for ongoing
\par
international consultations" within BSN-Gervais Danone, establishes a
\par
consultative committee consisting of 15 representatives from the ECF-IUF
\par
and its affiliated unions and 15 delegates from BSN plants, presumably works
\par
council members, who meet with general management representatives
\par
annually. Like the Thomson agreement, it is scheduled to run experimentally
\par
for a two-year period and the delegates' time off from work and their
\par
expenses are paid for by the company. If necessary, working group meetings
\par
or ad hoc bilateral meetings can be arranged between annual meetings. The
\par
agreement covers 40,000 workers at 100 plants in Austria, Belgium, France,
\par
the Federal Republic of Germany, Italy, the Netherlands and Spain.
\par
At the committee's first meeting, which also took place on 29 October
\par
1986 in Brussels, the company was represented by its Human Resources
\par
Director and by its Human Resources Co-ordinator; the union delegation
\par
was composed of national leaders of IUF affiliates from the seven countries
\par
\emph{535International Labour Review}
\par
mentioned, as well as representatives from the IUF and ECF-IUF sec-
\par
retariats. The management representatives not only gave an overview of past
\par
corporate performance, focusing particularly on G6n6rale Biscuit which had
\par
recently been taken over by BSN, but also dealt with future investment plans
\par
and plant closures. BSN's interest in strengthening its position in the pasta
\par
market in Italy was confirmed and its decision to close down four G6n6rale
\par
Biscuit plants in 1987 was announced. BSN representatives promised that
\par
they would take measures to lessen any negative impact on the workers
\par
involved. Management was urged to adopt in its foreign subsidiaries the same
\par
personnel policies and good relations with unions that prevail in France.
\par
At the second meeting, which took place on 30 March 1987 in Geneva,
\par
management representatives included, among others, the company's Manag-
\par
ing Director, the Director of Human Relations and the Social Relations Co-
\par
ordinating Manager. From the union side there were 15 delegates from
\par
BSN's European plants, national union officials from European countries
\par
where BSN operates, and IUF officials. Of considerable importance was the
\par
inclusion at the meeting of union officials from Japan and the United States
\par
and of representatives of the IUF regional secretariats for North and Latin
\par
America. In addition to hearing reports on the general outlook for the
\par
company, the participants were given detailed reports on its operations in the
\par
beer and fresh dairy products sectors. The company presented the familiar
\par
argument that although strategies are formulated at the corporate level, their
\par
implementation is a matter for subsidiary and plant levels. Union representa-
\par
tives expressed a special interest in the employment repercussions of BSN's
\par
acquisition of G6n6rale Biscuit. Management representatives declined to
\par
address the issue, but promised to do so at the next meeting in 1988. Some
\par
delegates also expressed concern at the fact that BSN's social policy does not
\par
always go beyond giving advance notice of impending layoffs.
\par
It appears, therefore, that for. the time being at least, the BSN-IUF
\par
relationship has retained the purely informational and consultative nature
\par
that characterised the earlier relationship in the flat glass industry. As in the
\par
Thomson case, the potential for multinational bargaining seems to exist. The
\par
IUF secretariat might well push for a bargaining arrangement, and some
\par
union committee members apparently are urging this course. Nevertheless,
\par
Dan Gallin, the IUF General Secretary, has urged caution.' Long efforts to
\par
achieve a permanent contractual relationship with Nestl6 and Unilever, and
\par
later with Coca-Cola, have failed to materialise, and the \textbf{IUF }would naturally
\par
not wish to disrupt the BSN arrangement by making premature demands that
\par
its character be changed.
\par
Comparison of the Thomson and BSN agreements
\par
As the first agreements of their kind, the Thomson and BSN agreements
\par
offer interesting similarities and contrasts. Both are based on written
\par
documents which establish formal joint structures with a regional or\emph{Multinational union-management consultation}
\par
international trade union secretariat. Both seem to have been initiated by
\par
socialist-sympathetic managements. Both are designed to limit relationships
\par
to consultation. Yet while both agreements eschew a bargaining relationship,
\par
they are both contractual arrangements that raise the level and extend the
\par
scope of employee representation.
\par
The differences between the two agreements are equally intriguing.
\par
BSN-Gervais Danone, unlike Thomson, was never nationalised. Riboud,
\par
who "has no time for the Patronat, the employers' association perennially at
\par
odds with the left-wing Government", 29 and is a close friend of such leading
\par
Socialists as Jacques Delors, the promoter of the EC social dialogue
\par
discussed below, established both the 1970s flat glass multinational consulta-
\par
tive agreement and the present food industry one during periods when
\par
conservative French governments were in power. Thomson, on the other
\par
hand, as a nationalised company, was coaxed into its agreement by the
\par
political orientation of the Government. The BSN agreement has only one
\par
consultative committee; Thomson recognises two such bodies. The specula-
\par
tion that the presence of two bodies was designed to permit the inclusion of
\par
communist-oriented unions, at least in the text of the agreement, was
\par
confirmed by EMF General Secretary Hubert Thierron in May 1987.' These
\par
unions were altogether excluded from the BSN deal because of pressures by
\par
Dutch and German unions, but the Thomson deal includes the French and
\par
Spanish communist-led unions. As they are not affiliated with the EMF,
\par
however, they are represented only on the European Branch Committee,
\par
which plays a less important role than the TGP-EMF Liaison Committee.
\par
The March 1987 meeting of the BSN committee saw the presence of
\par
American and Japanese, as well as European union representatives. The
\par
purchase by Thomson of the General Electric and RCA television operations
\par
in the United States raises the question whether union delegates from outside
\par
Europe will participate in this company's meetings too.
\par
\textbf{III. }The EC's fourth stage: The Val Duchesse informal
\par
consultative dialogue
\par
In addition to multinational labour-management consultation at com-
\par
pany level, recent initiatives at the EC level have encouraged broader
\par
consultative relationships. In early 1985 a new European Commission,
\par
headed by Jacques Delors, a former finance minister in the 1981-86 socialist
\par
Government, assumed control of EC legislative policy. Delors succeeded in
\par
reversing the procedural priorities in social policy formulation that had led to
\par
the stalemate. He had already advocated the sort of consultative arrange-
\par
ments described in the previous section.3 Rather than forwarding legislative
\par
proposals that would then become blocked "downstream", Delors sought as
\par
a preliminary step to revive the structure of informal tripartism at the EC
\par
level by encouraging the representatives of labour and management to\emph{International Labour Review}
\par
discuss their views on the direction of social policy in the EC. Consensus-
\par
building became a prior step in policy formulation. The ETUC nevertheless
\par
cautioned that the process should not supplant the promulgation of further
\par
legislation in the social field, while, on the employers' side, UNICE remained
\par
mindful of the risk that "dialogue" could lead to collective bargaining
\par
agreements at the EC level, and continued to oppose further employment
\par
legislation.
\par
Although the ETUC maintains that social dialogue is a rather weak
\par
replacement for the Commission's role in social policy formulation, both
\par
unions and management have been quick to credit the dialogue with ending
\par
the social policy impasse at the EC level. In November 1985 the ETUC and
\par
UNICE created two joint working groups, one of which addresses macro-
\par
economic policy in the Community, and the other the social aspects of new
\par
technology. Subsequently, agreements or "joint opinions" have emerged
\par
from each group. The macro-economic policy group found agreement by
\par
labour and management on the recognition by employers of the need to
\par
increase investment in return for understanding by unions of the need to hold
\par
wage increases below the rate of productivity increases. 2 The new technology
\par
group, meanwhile, arrived at a shared understanding of the value of
\par
information, consultation, motivation and training of workers in connection
\par
with the introduction of new technology. The joint opinion, moreover,
\par
contains the following definition of consultation:
\par
Consultation of the workers and/or their representatives, at the level concerned,
\par
means the action of gathering opinions and possible suggestions concerning the
\par
implications of such changes for the firm's workforce, more particularly as
\par
regards the effects on their employment and their working conditions.3
\par
The new technology group also agreed that information and consultation
\par
procedures should be governed by law and practice at the various national
\par
levels. Its "joint opinion" records only one area of disagreement, which
\par
pertains to the value of EC-wide legislation in this area. At present the
\par
dialogue continues, with the working groups having divided between them
\par
aspects relating to the flexibility and adaptability of the firm.
\par
It is noteworthy that Commission President Delors recently announced
\par
that, with the exception of occupational safety and health, no further
\par
legislation in the social affairs area will be proposed without the prior
\par
agreement of \emph{both }"social partners".' The latest stage of social policy
\par
formulation in the EC thus appears to be characterised by a process of informal
\par
consensus-building which subordinates the role of direct government inter-
\par
vention through regulation. In short, on the surface at least, the "economic
\par
liberalists" have won out over the more regulation-minded "Euro-corporat-
\par
ists". Several factors, however, call into question the permanence of the
\par
current inactivity in the field of employment law. For one thing, pending draft
\par
directives may be off the agenda of lawmakers, but they have not simply gone
\par
away. The Vredeling directive, in fact, is scheduled for revival in early 1989."5
\par
Other factors, as noted below, are more deeply rooted.\emph{Multinational union-management consultation}
\par
The internal market
\par
Despite major problems in forging a united Europe, the EC has adopted
\par
the ambitious objective of creating by 1992 an "internal market" free of
\par
non-tariff barriers to trade. The recent experience of recession and structural
\par
change has fostered awareness that the small size of European domestic
\par
markets, relative to those of the United States and Japan, impedes the
\par
development of competitiveness in European industry.
\par
The European Commission published its proposals for the creation of an
\par
internal market in the form of a White Paper in 1985. The spirit of the
\par
proposals was much in keeping with the earliest efforts at economic
\par
integration in the \textbf{EC }in the sense that no reference was made to
\par
accompanying measures required in the area of social policy: the document
\par
argued for "economic cohesion" with no mention of "social cohesion".36
\par
The ETUC was understandably quick to note the omission and has
\par
demanded that a framework of laws harmonising conditions in the social field
\par
must accompany the creation of an internal market. The basis of the ETUC's
\par
concern resides in the potential for firms to exploit those areas of the EC with
\par
less restrictive labour laws in the absence of a regulatory framework shared
\par
by all member States. As the intent of the creation of an internal market is to
\par
facilitate the movement of both goods and capital between member States,
\par
capital might flow naturally to those settings where labour is less well
\par
protected, thus creating a pattern of "social dumping", according to the
\par
ETUC.37 The proposals rely on much the same premise as that used in the
\par
EC's "first" period of social policy formulation, namely the correction of
\par
disequilibria through the free play of market forces.
\par
The European Single Act, which became law in member States in July
\par
1987, also furthers the aim of creating an internal market. In addition to
\par
opening up areas of social policy to qualified majority rather than unanimity
\par
voting, the Single Act also bears on social policy through its explicit
\par
encouragement of EC-level dialogue between labour and management as a
\par
means of probing areas of consensus between the two sides. The Act thus
\par
"institutionalises" a process that began as an informal method of ending the
\par
legislative stalemate in EC social policy. The balance in the \textbf{EC }social policy
\par
debate now lies between those who favour an expanded legislative
\par
framework and those who favour giving the social dialogue a chance to build
\par
consensus.
\par
The most recent initiative in legislating \textbf{EC }social policy was made at the
\par
conclusion of the Belgian presidency of the Council of Ministers in 1987.
\par
Belgian Employment Minister Michel Hansenne proposed that, in conjunc-
\par
tion with the completion of an internal market, the \textbf{EC }should draft a binding
\par
charter of basic labour rights harmonising social policy in the \textbf{EC }at some
\par
minimum level.3 " While the acceptability of Hansenne's proposal is the
\par
subject of current debate, one outcome may be that the social dialogue will
\par
be under more intense pressure to show continued progress in the form of\emph{International Labour Review}
\par
more agreements between unions and management at the EC level. The
\par
value of informing and consulting employees and their representatives, and,
\par
indeed, of building consensus between labour and management on the
\par
several challenges facing European industry, is questioned by no one. A
\par
resurgence of international labour-management consultation attests to this.
\par
The question remains whether these processes can or should be legislated.
\par
IV. Conclusion
\par
This article has attempted to analyse the changing character of multina-
\par
tional union-management consultation in Europe during the 1980s. The
\par
recent revival of multinational consultation was set against the backdrop of
\par
earlier efforts at multinational collective bargaining which never reached
\par
fruition. On the other hand, various forms of union-management consulta-
\par
tion have emerged, and it is quite possible that refinements will be made in
\par
this direction in the future. The major reason behind this prediction is that
\par
complex issues pertaining to human resource management, arising in the
\par
1980s and expected to continue into the 1990s, point to the need for labour
\par
and management to co-operate in exchanging information and ideas. The
\par
introduction of new technology, the restructuring of industry and the efforts
\par
to unite Europe provide the framework for extended union-management
\par
consultation in the years ahead where the parties desire it and find it
\par
helpful.
\par
Multinational consultation is thus one structural response to the new set
\par
of issues of the 1980s. But that response and the consultative structures that
\par
have been put in place are fragile and still experimental ones. The basic
\par
factors that worked against multinational collective bargaining remain as
\par
potential barriers to multinational consultation. Multinational employers,
\par
even those who have participated in such consultative mechanisms, are likely
\par
to resist any pressure, whether from union or governmental sources, that
\par
would transform consultation into negotiation. That resistance may be all the
\par
greater depending upon the country of origin of the multinational. Attempts
\par
to convert consultation arrangements into bargaining ones could easily end
\par
the multinational consultation efforts made by employers as it did some
\par
arrangements of the 1970s. Finally, political changes both at the national and
\par
at the EC levels could upset the tentative process of "social dialogue"
\par
initiated either by national policies or by the European Commission. For the
\par
present, however, the European Commission is unambiguously furthering
\par
the establishment of a true "European industrial relations area \textbf{9 }at the EC,
\par
industry and company levels, thereby repairing the omission in 1985 of any
\par
reference to a "social dimension" of the internal market.\emph{Multinational union-management consultation}
