INTRODUCTION
\par
Bargaining,I perhaps the oldest of decision-rendering processes, has
\par
taken on new life as scholarly2 and popular 3 writing alike have en-
\par
couraged penetrating examination and increased use of an already wide-
\par
spread process. The burgeoning interest in negotiation, however, has
\par
only begun to spark interest in the ethical dilemmas4 inherent in many
\par
negotiation strategies and tactics.5 The heightened interest in negotiation
\par
\textbf{I }The terms "negotiation" and "bargaining" are used interchangeably in this Article.
\par
Both refer to stylized reciprocal dealings, which are initiated, conducted, and monitored by the
\par
parties, and are intended to resolve a dispute or to reach an agreement concerning future
\par
action. Negotiation often involves the use of a mixture of strategies, but because most use
\par
some version of offers and counteroffers, positional bargaining is the paradigm for this Article.
\par
See notes 36-38 and accompanying text infra. However, to the extent that bargaining tech-
\par
niques are used to enforce ethics in other bargaining strategies, the functionalist model offered
\par
in this Article is pertinent.
\par
2 Scholarly writing on bargaining ethics has been sparse, but literature on the negotiation
\par
process itself has been voluminous and multidisciplinary. Frequently, writing on negotiation
\par
uses the framework of a single discipline, such as economics, anthropology, international rela-
\par
tions, mathematics, or psychology. This Article uses legal ideas and analogies to help eluci-
\par
date the role of self-interest in bargaining. For a detailed review of the legal literature
\par
concerning the negotiation process, see Menkel-Meadow, Legal Negotiation: A Study of Strat-
\par
egies in Search of a Theory, 1983 Am. B. Found. Res. J. 905 [hereinafter Menkel-Meadow,
\par
