\emph{BARGAINING ETHICS}
\par
points up the need for new knowledge about the ethics of an old process.6
\par
This Article is an examination of the sources of major ethical dilemmas
\par
in bargaining7 and of the way that the process resolves them in the ab-
\par
sence of an aspirational ethic.8
\par
The pervasive use of bargaining is reason enough for an examination
\par
of bargaining ethics. Negotiation is used more often than any other dis-
\par
crete dispute resolution or problem-solving process.9 Bargaining is an
\par
essential process in everyday life; in many situations, particularly com-
\par
mon business deals, there are no real alternatives. However, negotiation
\par
is critical to both dispute resolution and consensus-building alike) 0 In
\par
addition, the versatility of bargaining makes it useful in resolving issues
\par
