\par
ess controlled by the parties. The model developed here suggests that
\par
minimal truthfulness and fairness are functional ethical norms inherent
\par
in bargaining. The truthfulness and fairness associated with the process
\par
do not meet aspirational standards, but the practices that result do meet
\par
the minimal ethical requisite of an agreement. These practices also ac-
\par
count for the universality and longevity of the process despite the ab-
\par
sence of a consensus concerning an aspirational ethic.3 0 The resulting
\par
functionalism links bargaining ethics to the function they perform with-
\par
out assuming that the ethics that result are sufficiently aspirational in
\par
sorting out deception and unfairness. Thus, the functionalist model is
\par
not offered as an alternative to aspirational approaches. Rather, the as-
\par
sumption is that an adequately articulated aspirational ethic for bargain-
\par
ing must be preceded by an understanding of how the process operates in
\par
the face of ethical challenges.
\par
Part I of this Article discusses the role of ethics in negotiation and
\par
argues that although ethical decision-making is an important aspect of
\par
bargaining, deviations from ethical norms can be justified only by refer-
\par
ence to acceptable criteria. This Part then describes two core ethical
\par
norms, truthfulness and fairness, that will be used to analyze the opera-
\par
tion of ethics in bargaining.
\par
Part II first offers and evaluates four possible aspirational concep-
\par
tions of negotiating ethics and argues that such approaches standing
\par
alone are inadequate because they do not take sufficient account of the
\par
special operational aspects of the process into which ethical considera-
\par
tions must fit. This Part then offers an analytical model called function-
\par
alism for evaluating negotiation ethics. This "process model" relies on
\par
the characteristics and operational imperatives of the bargaining process
\par
itself, and thus provides an objective rather than a value specific basis for
\par
assessing bargaining ethics. As an analytical model, functionalism helps
\par
in understanding the minimal ethical needs of classical bargaining prac-
\par
tices but is not a replacement for indispensable ethical standards. Func-
\par
tionalism reveals a non-aspirational internal ethic and shows how
\par
bargaining, as traditionally structured and practiced,3' assumes that ad-
\par
versarial strategic actions will promote minimal truthfulness and fair-
\par
ness. Functionalism describes how the process receives and sorts ethical
\par
values using its own internal mechanisms-its market and adversarial
\par
features and its use of bargaining techniques.
\par
Part III draws upon the treatment of deception and unfairness in
\par
law to demonstrate how the functionalist model arrives at truthfulness
