I.
\par
IDENTIFYING NEGOTIATION ETHICS
\par
\emph{A. Challenges of the Process}
\par
In many areas of human endeavor practice and principle are often
\par
estranged. But the distance between practices that are commonly used
\par
and acceptable ethical standards is particularly great in negotiation. 32
\par
\textbf{32 }Formal ethical codes such as the Model Rules of Professional Conduct (1983) [hcreinaf-
\par
ter Model Rules] hold lawyers to high ethical standards. Yet, a national survey of lawyers
\par
conducted by the University of Michigan Law School revealed widespread deception in bar-
\par
gaining. See S. Pepe, Standards of Legal Negotiations, Summary of Preliminary Findings (on
\par
file at New York University Law Review). The survey reported that 75\% of the respondents
\par
said that incomplete information was a regular or frequent problem and 51\% said that "unfair
\par
and inadequate disclosure of material information" was a regular or frequent problem. \textbf{Id. }at
\par
3. Only about half (49\%) of the lawyers felt that if they answered a question during negotia-
\par
tion about deposition testimony they knew to be false, their response had to be truthful or
\par
complete. Id. at 2. Surveys and evidence of normative bargaining behavior are rare, method-
\par
ologically inadequate, and insufficiently probative of the details of particular bargaining con-
\par
texts to indicate the extent to which what is said, withheld, distorted, or otherwise done would
\par
indicate unethical or illegal bargaining conduct. The existing data on actual bargaining prac-
\par
tices, scarce and inadequate as they are, nevertheless raise important issues for the bar and for
\par
others who negotiate. Pepe concludes:
\par
Notwithstanding the questions of professional ethics involved, the data suggest that nu-
\par
merous attorneys could be held legally liable for fraud or misrepresentation under tort
\par
and contract law for settlement contracts arrived at after misrepresentation and fraudu-
\par
lent concealments. Attorney skill in choosing what misrepresentations might lead to
\par
liability coupled with the enormous problems of proof and the shields of confidentiality
\par
and privilege, generally allow attorneys to avoid such liability.
\par
