\textbf{\emph{B. }}\emph{Ethical Standards in Negotiation: Truthfulness and Fairness}
\par
Negotiation practices are numerous, varied, and complex, 49 but it is
\par
possible to identify some core ethical values implicated by these prac-
\par
tices.50 This Article focuses on two fundamental ethical values-truth-
\par
fulness and fairness.
\par
Truthfulness and fairness are part of the respect for others that ethi-
\par
cal standards are meant to encourage.5 1 The opportunities for departure
\par
from these ideals that regularly occur during bargaining capture the es-
\par
sential tension between negotiating opponents. The standard of truthful-
\par
ness may be diluted because of the temptation to misrepresent,
\par
considering the value of information in achieving the most advantageous
\par
result. Notwithstanding the ideal of fairness, strong pressure may be
\par
placed on an opponent in order to achieve the desired result.
\par
Truthfulness connotes the authenticity of the information conveyed.
\par
Without truthfulness, human relations would be impossibly cumber-
\par
some, because nobody could be believed and the trust essential to the
\par
accomplishment of all tasks would be impossible. In much the same
\par
way, truthfulness is vital to negotiations, but there are additional reasons
\par
for its necessity in this context. First, the assumption of truthfulness un-
\par
derlies the choice of negotiation by the parties; the parties would be un-
\par
likely to choose negotiation 5 2 without the belief that the process would
