As applied to negotiation, unfairness, like deception, is a trouble-
\par
some concept, because in some instances a pressure strategy may be legit-
\par
imate. In bargaining, it may be acceptable to pressure an opponent to
\par
agree to terms that she would like to avoid. Yet, when inappropriate
\par
pressure is applied, unfairness may result and the bargain may be uncon-
\par
scionable. For example, an agreement that was made under duress might
\par
be found to be unconscionable. A contemporary and controversial exam-
\par
ple that raises the issue of the limits on pressure is a threat to put a civil
\par
rights matter through the costly litigation process unless the plaintiff's
\par
attorney relinquishes her statutorily prescribed fees as part of the
\par
settlement.64
\par
The blurred lines between ethical and unethical deception and be-
\par
tween appropriate and unfair pressure are compounded by factors unique
\par
to negotiation. Ethical issues proceed from the frequent assumption in
\par
bargaining that pressing for advantage is the point of the process; from
\par
the need to differentiate tactical devices, such as bluffing, from unethical
\par
behavior such as misrepresentation; from the absence of clear and sys-
\par
tematic criteria for addressing the difference between the two; and from
\par
the necessity that the parties, in the absence of specific guidance or of a
\par
monitor, find their own way to ethical bargaining. Because common bar-
\par
gaining practices do not always coincide with the universe of ethics
\par
outside the process, the differences need to be explained and justified.
\par
