\par
used).
\par
The universalist concern with responsibility toward a negotiating
\par
opponent insinuates societal interests into the process, while the bargain-
\par
ing mentality is instinctively individualistic. A bargaining ethic that
\par
would substantially elevate society's interests thus contradicts the central
\par
individualist impulse of negotiation. Like legal ethics, negotiation ethics
\par
must always contend with the resiliency of partisan interests. The legal
\par
system condones the withholding of truthful information in some cir-
\par
cumstances because the legal process is driven by individual interests.
\par
The same is true for the negotiation process. At points in the barbells
\par
hypothetical, individual and societal interests may coincide. Both may
\par
be served by the misrepresentations that each side uses in the false offers
\par
and counteroffers, because this is one way to arrive at the market price.
\par
This societal interest may be undercut if the buyer does not ask and the
\par
seller does not volunteer the original purchase price.8' However, bar-
\par
gaining assumes that the tactics and strategies used by the parties, rather
\par
than ethical or other rules, will bring out the relevant information. The
\par
universalist conception is skeptical of alterations in ethical norms and
\par
would regard many widely accepted negotiation practices as unaccept-
\par
able. 8 2 Universalism does not sufficiently distinguish when bargaining
\par
techniques are more appropriate than ethical rules to regulate truthful-
\par
ness and fairness. Bargaining should be accountable to universal ethics;
\par
but exhortations to honesty and good faith that do not acknowledge the
\par
unique nature of the process do not pose a workable system of ethical
\par
accountability.
\par
Traditionalism, a specialized ethic that is based on zero-sum self-
\par
interest, is an alternative that specifically rejects universalism. Classical
\par
bargaining using offers and counteroffers can thus be viewed as tradition-
\par
alist. In the legal system, traditionalism is expressed in the oppositional
