\par
\emph{1. Structural Features of Negotiation}
\par
\emph{a. Bargaining as a Free-Form Market Process. }Bargaining is a self-
\par
regulated process in which parties with different goals engage in strategic
\par
dealings until they agree upon an outcome, or until one or more of them
\par
decides that agreement cannot be reached. However, this process has no
\par
prescribed form or rules. It is what the parties agree it is and may be
\par
conducted in any way that they decide. Bargaining, a self-directed mar-
\par
ket process, assumes that the parties will monitor the truthfulness and
\par
fairness of that process.
\par
To assure ethical dealings, negotiators have no choice but to rely on
\par
the resources of the negotiation process itself. These resources are the
\par
great assortment of bargaining techniques that fuel the process and give
\par
