\emph{2. Process Features, Goals, and Ethics: A Functionalist Hypothesis}
\par
This section discusses a functionalist model of ethics that relies on
\par
the features of the bargaining process that have been identified in this
\par
Article. The model is useful as an analytical tool to explain how the
\par
structure of the bargaining process, together with the bargaining tech-
\par
niques used by the parties, operate to produce an ethic that identifies the
\par
minimally acceptable forms of truthfulness and fairness that have sus-
\par
tained negotiation for centuries as a widely accepted process. This sec-
\par
tion shows how the model uses the structural characteristics of the
\par
