\textbf{\emph{BARGAINING ETHICS}}
\par
The assumptions of the functionalist model may be summarized as
\par
follows:
\par
1. Bargaining is indispensable to the functioning of society.
\par
2. The fundamental purpose of bargaining is to achieve a valid
\par
agreement. 156
\par
3. Practices that threaten the validity of an agreement violate
\par
the fundamental purpose of the process.
\par
4. Bargaining is an adversarial market process in which willing
\par
opponents use partisan strategic dealings (bargaining techniques) to ar-
\par
rive at accurate information and to obtain fair treatment.
\par
These assumptions underlie the use of the internal resources of the pro-
\par
cess to achieve truthfulness and fairness in bargaining. The result is a
\par
minimal but functional ethic. This ethic is best understood through an
\par
explanation of its operation to induce truthfulness and fairness and
\par
through an analysis of appropriate illustrations.
\par
Truthfulness is important in negotiations because it encourages trust
\par
between opponents and thus facilitates agreement. However, adherence
\par
to the ethical standard of truthfulness in bargaining does not require a
\par
negotiator to abandon the assumptions of the process. Parties to a nego-
\par
tiation may use bargaining techniques, including strategic dealings, in or-
\par
der to arrive at accurate information concerning factual matters and the
\par
true intentions of an opponent, as well as to limit information offered to
\par
an opponent.
\par
False information is not usually recognizable as a bargaining tech-
\par
nique and therefore closes off the opportunity for an opponent to arrive
\par
at accurate information through the use of his own bargaining tech-
\par
niques. Thus, giving false information in a negotiation violates the as-
\par
sumption that bargaining techniques will elicit accurate information.
\par
False information also undermines the basic purpose of the bargaining
\par
process, to achieve a valid agreement. However, false information neces-
\par
sary for a mode of bargaining to occur is an exception because of the
\par
assumption that bargaining is necessary to the functioning of society.
\par
Generally, though, information that is necessary for bargaining to occur
\par
is also recognizable as a bargaining technique. Such false information
\par
does not threaten the validity of an agreement because it may be uncov-
\par
ethics represented by universalism and traditionalism). See also notes 46, 141 supra (discuss-
\par
ing bargaining philosophies of Professors Fisher and White and contrasting Fisher's integra-
\par
tive, problem-solving approach with White's distributive, zero-sum approach).
\par
156 The concept of validity as used here does not necessarily refer to legal validity. Func-
\par
tionalism seeks to explain the internal ethic of generic bargaining, not only the ethic of bar-
\par
gaining used in countries with formal systems of law. Thus validity encompasses standards of
\par
truthfulness and fairness achieved by bargaining techniques which, because they are used
\par
throughout the world, give rise to an agreement that is meant to be honored \textbf{by }the parties and
\par
is recognized by the society and its institutions. See note 130 supra.
