\par
ered during the course of bargaining through the use of bargaining tech-
\par
niques so as to facilitate the eventual discovery of more accurate
\par
information. Thus, deceptive offers and counteroffers, as well as settle-
\par
ment point deception may be considered either bargaining techniques
\par
that invite counter-techniques or false information that is necessary for a
\par
form of bargaining to occur. The withholding of the name of a principal,
\par
however, cannot be uncovered readily through the use of bargaining
\par
techniques and therefore can be justified only as necessary for bargaining
\par
to occur. 157 Often deceptive exchanges are not misrepresentations as the
\par
term is commonly understood because the true asking price or the settle-
\par
ment point may become clear to the parties only as a result of the process
\par
of bargaining itself.
\par
Several examples may be helpful to show how the assumptions ap-
\par
ply to truthfulness. Avoiding a direct answer is a bargaining technique
\par
when it limits information to an opponent without undermining the va-
\par
lidity of an agreement. Failing to volunteer information is a bargaining
\par
technique when it limits information to an opponent unless, for example,
\par
silence about the particular facts undermines the validity of the agree-
\par
ment. Other examples of bargaining techniques are puffing concerning
\par
the quality of goods and bluffing concerning calling off negotiations. The
\par
process assumes that negotiators will use partisan strategic dealings to
\par
arrive at truthful information, and that the statements made will not
\par
keep opponents from using bargaining techniques to arrive at truthful
\par
information. Such deceptive information is not inconsistent with the as-
\par
sumptions of the process. When it is offered in such a way as to allow
\par
strategic responses that can arrive at more accurate information, it is nec-
\par
essary for bargaining to occur.
\par
Fairness is also important in negotiations. Like truthfulness, it en-
\par
courages trust between opponents and thus facilitates agreement without
\par
requiring a negotiator to abandon the assumptions of the process. Fair-
\par
ness is not a single concept but covers many abuses that can be judged
\par
only in the context in which they occur. For example, a lawyer in negoti-
\par
ations with another lawyer may take actions that might be considered
\par
unfair only if taken against a layperson. Many practices may be distaste-
\par
ful but not unfair, because they do not violate the assumptions of the
\par
process. For example, a strong negotiator may lower the asking price of
\par
a weak opponent, because the process assumes that willing opponents
\par
can depend on bargaining techniques to assure fair treatment.
\par
An appropriate test of functionalist assumptions is a recent addition
\par
to the Model Rules. This addition is the first explicit acknowledgement
\par
by the legal profession that the strict requirement of truthfulness in court
