The addition of the comment to the Model Rules is an important
\par
breakthrough because it is the first suggestion in the history of legal eth-
\par
ics that bargaining may require some adjustment in the application of the
\par
otherwise uniform requirement of truthfulness. However, the comment
\par
opens rather than settles the issue. This addition depends on a set of
\par
examples and does not purport to be a coherent principle. The stated
\par
rationale for the change-that deceptive information about price, value,
\par
settlement terms, and the existence of a principal are part of a widely
\par
accepted category-creates the impression of a self-evident and coherent
\par
group of exceptions to the requirement of truthfulness in bargaining.
\par
However, it is not clear what the category is. Price deception usually
\par
takes place in a bidding process; deception concerning a settlement point
\par
often is offered as a bluff; and the existence of a principal is deliberately
\par
withheld, although the comment does not indicate whether, upon in-
\par
quiry, the principal's existence could be denied.165 These are signifi-
\par
cantly dissimilar forms of deception, and placing them in a category
\par
called "accepted conventions" does not make their kinship any clearer or
\par
make it possible to analogize to other examples. The withholding of the
\par
existence of a principal, in particular, is a misrepresentation of a material
\par
fact and should therefore be the model for few if any similar exceptions.
\par
Thus reliance on "accepted conventions" in a field where the conventions
\par
have not been systematically examined injects pragmatism into the Rules
\par
without justifying this standard or examining its consequences.' 66
\par
Functionalism provides a preferable rationale for the new exceptions
\par
and for grouping them in the same category. First, disguising or with-
\par
holding the truth concerning estimates of value, a party's intentions as to
\par
settlement, or the name of a principal are all recognizable bargaining
\par
techniques that allow certain standard forms of bargaining to occur. If
\par
the real party in interest had to be disclosed, many transactions could not
\par
be submitted to bargaining at all. \textbf{1 67 }If parties had to state their real price
\par
or their intentions regarding settlement, most forms of bargaining could
\par
not occur because an exchange for money, for example, would then be-
\par
come a sale for a fixed price instead of a bargain. Moreover, since value
\par
estimates and settlement intentions are recognizable as bargaining tech-
\par
niques, an opponent may use bargaining techniques to arrive at more
\par
accurate information. Both of these bargaining techniques therefore con-
\par
firm functionalist assumptions concerning the use of bargaining to arrive
\par
at truthful information.
\par
