\par
Second, a functionalist explanation of the rule change has the ad-
\par
vantage of rationalizing the presence of the three examples in the same
\par
category. All involve deceptive statements without which bargaining
\par
could not occur. Without deception concerning price or value, intentions
\par
concerning settlement, and the existence of a principal, positional bar-
\par
gaining could not occur. In addition, of course, both price and settle-
\par
ment point deception (but not withholding the identity of a principal)
\par
can be categorized as recognizable bargaining techniques that stimulate
\par
an opponent to seek more accurate information. These functionalist cat-
\par
egories are less subjective and more rigorous than the pragmatist cate-
\par
gory of accepted conventions.
\par
Beyond rationalizing the Rule 4.1 exceptions, there are other advan-
\par
tages to a functionalist approach. The functionalist explanation is a more
\par
satisfactory rationale than the open-ended pragmatist examples used to
\par
explain the Rule 4.1 exceptions to truthfulness. Functionalism also is
\par
preferable because the pragmatist rationale requires documentation that
\par
does not presently exist. The inevitable vagueness of standards produced
\par
by pragmatism therefore could encourage unethical practices. By refer-
\par
ring to a "category" of such statements, the comment appears to envision
\par
a number of similar practices that would not be barred by Rule 4.1.
\par
Since no indication of the extent of similar conventions or of their identi-
\par
fying characteristics is given, the exception for accepted conventions
\par
risks subjective analogies drawn from each negotiator's experience. The
\par
existing conventions on which the Rules rely are self-policed, are unex-
\par
posed to public view or scrutiny, and have been subject to neither actual
\par
nor theoretical validation. Less obvious conventions than the examples
\par
given in the Rule 4.1 comment might prove to be more controversial.
\par
Finally, functionalism helps explain why certain standard negotia-
\par
tion tactics utilizing deception in some circumstances may not only be
\par
benign but may actually be helpful to the parties in reaching a valid
\par
agreement. Each negotiator, in putting forth several prices and responses
\par
or bluffs as offers and counteroffers and as settlement points, may be us-
\par
ing these amounts to draw out his opponent's price range; and each party
\par
in fact may change or adjust his price as he hears the bids of his oppo-
\par
nent. The process of offering and counteroffering in effect determines a
\par
market price. While it certainly is possible to arrive at a settlement with-
\par
out price exchanging techniques or settlement point deception, some ex-
\par
change processes that withhold or disguise the true or final intentions of
\par
the parties generally occur. Even if the point of offering several deceptive
\par
prices or deceptive information concerning the final settlement point is to
\par
drive an opponent to a fixed amount determined in advance, price bid-
\par
ding and similar exchanges, as well as disingenuous information concern-
\par
ing settlement terms, are essentially mechanical necessities for some
