forms of classical bargaining. Thus, such exchanges also meet the func-
\par
tionalist assumption that truthful information is not required if a stan-
\par
dard form of bargaining could not otherwise occur.
\par
The questions raised by the Rule 4.1 comment do not diminish the
\par
significance of the precedent it sets in forthrightly facing a discrepancy
\par
between a critical ethical requirement and an operational necessity for
\par
common and widespread forms of negotiation. Standing alone, the com-
\par
ment to Rule 4.1 cannot and does not purport to address negotiation
\par
ethics in a systematic fashion. It would have been remarkable if the pro-
\par
fession had achieved sufficient consensus to go further than the Rule 4.1
\par
comment. 1 68 With little theoretical or empirical work available to test or
\par
guide so consequential a change, the bar, faced with the unknown, not
\par
surprisingly retreated to the safety of the most familiar practices and
\par
stopped there.
\par
Functionalism is not a test characterized by mathematical accuracy,
\par
but it is useful because it requires that departures from truthfulness and
\par
fairness in negotiation be justified in a manner consistent with explicit
\par
and objective assumptions of the bargaining process. Functionalism ex-
\par
plains how the internal dynamic of the process sorts out gross deception
\par
and unfairness in order to protect the process from the invalidation of an
\par
agreement. A minimal ethic derived from the process itself is the result.
\par
Functionalism is not an aspirational ethic, but its descriptive mission en-
\par
dows it with an objectivity that can help discipline the search for an as-
\par
pirational ethic that will have more than subjective appeal.
\par
At the same time, the most serious shortcoming of functionalism is
\par
its ethical minimalism. Objective functionalist process criteria lack a
\par
deep moral dimension. At best, the minimalism of functionalism can
\par
separate etiquette from ethics and distinguish the distasteful from the
\par
disallowed and the inadvisable from the impermissible.
\par
The purpose of functionalism is not to assure ethical bargaining.
\par
Rather, functionalism is an operational model that can help explain how
\par
and why negotiators, because of salient features of the process, typically
\par
respond when confronted with ethical concerns. Even without an ex-
\par
plicit aspirational ethic, however, bargaining often rises above its own
\par
ethical functionalism. Some modes of negotiation, such as integrative
\par
bargaining, use cooperative approaches that minimize deception and un-
\par
fairness and therefore eliminate many ethical concerns.' 69 Standards
\par
that are aspirational in character and personal to the individual negotia-
\par
tor are used routinely in negotiations to dispose of ethical concerns.
\par
\textbf{168 }Professor Hazard concluded from the experience of trying to include ethical guidance
\par
for negotiation in the Model Rules that regulation beyond proscribing fraud was premature.
