\emph{A. Misrepresentation and Functionalism}
\par
Recall the important assumption that truthfulness is a functional
\par
necessity in bargaining. 179 Negotiators need truthful information in or-
\par
der to forge an enforceable or acceptable bargain. At the same time,
\par
accurate information is often so valuable that it becomes the basic cur-
\par
rency of bargaining. If the seller in the barbells hypothetical t80 can with-
\par
hold the original price, she may have greater latitude in bargaining for a
\par
resale. A developer interested in purchasing a particular tract for devel-
\par
opment may be willing to share her plans for developing the area, but
