\emph{B. Unconscionability and Functionalism}
\par
Fairness is a difficult concept to apply. Unlike misrepresentation, it
\par
lacks the concreteness and objectivity of specific violations. Sometimes
\par
in negotiation it is difficult to determine whether a harsh outcome to one
\par
party has resulted from her opponent's unethical action, or whether bar-
\par
gaining techniques, skill, or more favorable market conditions are re-
\par
sponsible for the unfair results. The latitude that a market process allows
\par
makes difficult the application of principled judgments concerning
\par
fairness.
\par
Moreover, fairness is an encompassing idea that can reach any and
\par
all ethical abuses, including misrepresentation and some forms of decep-
\par
tion.2 0 8 In judging pure issues of fairness in negotiation, then, what re-
\par
mains is conduct not encompassed by other conceptions of bargaining
\par
abuse. The concept of fairness, however, has little practical meaning
\par
apart from a specific set of rules or circumstances to which that concept
\par
is linked. There are no rules or circumstances for fairness that fit the
\par
entire expansive range of generic negotiations. Consequently, courts
\par
have judged fairness in negotiation on a case-by-case basis. Without spe-
\par
cific rules or principles against which to decide each case, courts seldom
\par
find unfairness in bargaining.2 0 9 Intervention by courts to judge the com-
\par
plex circumstances of each negotiation would compromise an inherent
\par
assumption of the process-that willing opponents will arrive at a fair
\par
solution using bargaining techniques.2 10 Thus, judicial intervention on
\par
fairness grounds has been largely limited to marginal or extreme situa-
\par
tions in relation to the ordinary circumstances surrounding negotiation.
\par
Drawing upon the doctrine of unconscionability, I argue in this sec-
\par
tion that, in the absence of statutory law or some other societal frame of
\par
reference, freedom of contract inhibits the institution of a definitive no-
\par
tion of fairness in bargaining. A generic definition of fairness would dis-
\par
place the legitimate operation of market forces and bargaining techniques
\par
in many situations. Thus, fairness in negotiation is exceedingly deferen-
\par
