\emph{C. Functionalism and Legal Regulation in Bargaining}
\par
The doctrinal reluctance in the law's approaches to regulation of
\par
truthfulness and fairness in bargaining conforms to the functionalist as-
\par
sumption that the needs of the process guide it toward its own internal
\par
ethic. To test the resilience of this ethic, it is useful to analyze areas of
\par
law in which important policy concerns might overcome functionalist
\par
assumptions. Considering the dominant role functionalism assumes for
\par
bargaining techniques in regulating ethical behavior, it is not surprising
\par
that the law has not readily or systematically embraced bargaining regu-
\par
lation. Through contract and tort law and doctrines such as assent, du-
\par
ress, concealment, and nondisclosure, the law has addressed bargaining
\par
sporadically, barring particular abuses as they arise, such as fraud or mis-
\par
representation.2 3 4 Thus the challenge is to discover law in which en-
\par
counters with issues of truthfulness and fairness are sufficient to make a
\par
judgment about the usefulness of the functionalist model.
\par
In labor law235 and domestic relations236 there is significant experi-
\par
ence to draw upon. This section examines the experience in these areas
\par
and concludes that, despite strong policy concerns, existing legal controls
\par
on bargaining are remarkably limited. Regulation of the bargaining pro-
\par
cess in both divorce and labor law has been tentative and deferential to
\par
the needs of the process. The latitude afforded contractual freedom as
\par
expressed through bargaining techniques corroborates the functionalist
