\par
ception and unfairness, judges have regarded techniques of bargaining as
\par
the appropriate tools for the protection of the parties.
\par
This experience does not mean that such results should be accepted.
\par
The deference that judges, though empowered t6 correct inequities, have
\par
given the process is comprehensible in functionalist terms. It is unlikely
\par
that courts on their own can overcome the contractual freedom doctrine
\par
that has made unconstrained negotiation the rule in marital bargaining.
\par
The most effective way to override functionalist assumptions that bar-
\par
gaining techniques will effect a fair agreement is through statutory re-
\par
form. By focusing on specific problems, as the NLRA does in its concern
\par
for information-sharing between labor and management, adjustments in
\par
ethical rules can be fine-tuned to particular concerns without displacing
\par
bargaining principles.3 0 2
\par
Even when the national economic stakes are high, as in assuring
\par
labor peace through effective collective bargaining, intervention through
\par
law has occurred to assure discussion among the parties, not fairness.
\par
Even when the public policy concerns are deep, as with divorce and fam-
\par
ily matters, the explicit concern for fair treatment of the parties has not
\par
resulted in significant alterations in bargaining. Courts have used neither
\par
the unconscionability doctrine nor the concept of good faith to invalidate
\par
agreements that appear unfair or one-sided in divorce or labor bargain-
\par
ing. Nor has bargaining deception in negotiation been effectively moni-
\par
tored in divorce law or barred in labor bargaining.
\par
Despite compelling concerns, the law has left parties in both areas to
\par
achieve truthfulness and fairness through manipulation of the bargaining
\par
process. In divorce, judicial power to upset unfair agreements has lain
\par
dormant or has been displaced by the contractual bargaining that has
\par
attended recent divorce reform. In labor bargaining, negotiators must
\par
provide some important information, but, in most vital areas, bargaining
\par
deception is unregulated. Further, government intervention to assure
\par
fairness, apparent in the early years of the labor statute, has been statuto-
\par
rily reduced. In both labor and divorce regulation, functionalist assump-
\par
tions have been asserted. In negotiations in both these areas, as well as in
\par
other bargaining, structural characteristics of the process-partisanship,
\par
the parties' adversarial posture, and strategic and other bargaining tech-
\par
niques-have become the safeguards of truthfulness and fairness.
\par
\textbf{302 }The U.C.C. standardizes many matters that arise in bargaining, thus eliminating oppor-
\par
tunity for unethical practices. See U.C.C. \S\S{} 2A-404 (1983) (requiring that substitute unload-
\par
ing facilites or carriers be used if agreed upon means become "commercially impracticable").
\par
Increasingly, there are requirements for mandatory disclosure during negotiations. See, e.g.,
\par
15 U.S.C. \S\S{} 1601-1693 (1982) (consumer credit transactions); id. \S\S{} 1705-1707 (interstate
\par
land sales).
