\par
\textbf{\emph{BARGAINING }}\emph{ETHICS}
\par
\textbf{CONCLUSION}
\par
The private world in which most bargaining occurs creates its own
\par
set of conditions and often its own rules. The ethics of bargaining, how-
\par
ever, must be reconciled with the ethics of the real world. Otherwise,
\par
bargaining ethics becomes a world unto itself, closed not only from pub-
\par
lic view but from public concern and criticism. In such a world the ine-
\par
quality that parties inevitably bring to the negotiating table might be
\par
accentuated. Power and resource disparities are tolerated in bargaining;
\par
ethics should be shared.
\par
This Article has argued with some irony that in the existing state of
\par
our understanding, bargaining ethics are premature. Ethics, the aspira-
\par
tional standards that should apply to bargaining, are the cart; this Article
\par
has focused on the horse, the bargaining process itself, and has argued
\par
that an understanding of its internal ethical radar must precede efforts to
\par
impose an aspirational ethic.
\par
This Article does not claim that bargaining is a self-regulating pro-
\par
cess that can depend upon its own internal resources to produce ethical
\par
behavior. If this were true, bargaining ethics would present fewer dilem-
\par
mas and would have less apparent need for aspirational standards. Nev-
\par
ertheless, the bargaining process does produce a minimal ethic. This is
\par
not because the process has a "conscience" but because the successful
\par
culmination of the process is an agreement which has not been under-
\par
mined \textbf{by }deception and unfairness. As its own structural features oper-
\par
ate using adversarial market and strategic dealings, the process
\par
encourages minimal levels of truthfulness and fairness. Often, depending
\par
upon the uses negotiators make of the features of the process and their
\par
own ethical standards, higher levels are achieved.
\par
Building upon an analysis of these features, this Article has offered a
\par
model for explaining how bargaining receives and disposes of ethical
\par
challenges. This model is called functionalism because it draws its as-
\par
sumptions about ethics from the way the process operates or functions.
\par
Because the purpose of bargaining is to achieve a valid agreement, the
\par
process guides the strategic maneuvers of the parties, at least minimally,
\par
to both seek and give accurate information and to accord and extract fair
\par
treatment.
\par
Functionalism embodies the imperfections of a market process, in-
\par
cluding its ethical minimalism. Yet it operates to distinguish the mini-
\par
mal truthfulness the process requires from the deception it disallows, and
\par
to distinguish permissible pressure tactics from intolerable unfairness.
\par
At the same time, it is the minimalism of the ethic of the market process
\par
that impels the search for a higher ethic.
\par
Nevertheless, the gross ethic of the process is remarkable consider-
\par
Imaged with the Permission of N.Y.U. Law Review\textbf{\emph{NEW }}\emph{YORK }\textbf{\emph{UNIVERSITY }}\emph{LAW REVIEW}
\par
\textbf{[Vol. 64:493}
\par
ing the ethical ambiguities negotiation invites. Minimal truthfulness is
\par
necessary for a valid agreement, but absolute candor is not. The process
\par
demands truthfulness about the original price of the barbells 3 0 3 but not
\par
about the ultimate settlement terms on which they might be sold. Simi-
\par
larly, fairness is necessary for a valid agreement, but the use of distasteful
\par
pressure tactics between roughly equal parties is not unfair. The bargain-
\par
ing process allows the seller to ask twice as much from an inexperienced
\par
negotiator as from an average one. On the other hand, the strategic deal-
\par
ings that produce minimal truthfulness and fairness can also raise ethical
\par
values to ideal levels. The process is responsive to both high and mini-
\par
mal ethical standards.
\par
Perhaps the best case for the functionalist explanation is law. Often
\par
law shapes its subject matter. Bargaining, however, has shaped law. In
\par
its rules and approaches, in common law and statutes, the law systemati-
\par
cally defers to bargaining. It declines to bar misrepresentation unless
\par
reliance is justified; to forbid many forms of direct and deliberate decep-
\par
tion; to force full disclosure; to penalize concealment unless there is ac-
\par
tive suppression; and to prohibit distasteful tactics unless the
\par
circumstances are such that a judgment of patent unfairness is unavoida-
\par
ble. Even when strong policy concerns have led to statutory standards
\par
for bargaining, they have been timid in approach and quick in retreat. In
\par
labor law, the NLRA regulates information-sharing concerning certain
\par
terms, but tolerates deception in most communications; it requires good-
\par
faith bargaining, but defines it minimally to require only the passing of
\par
words. In divorce law, the paternalism of the law has quickly withdrawn
\par
to the safety of contractual freedom and has given free rein to the bar-
\par
gaining techniques of the parties to produce truthfulness and fairness.
\par
The ethical endowments of the process also reveal its deficiencies.
\par
Bargaining requires more than ethics drawn from the minimal opera-
\par
tional needs of the process. Just as this Article has discussed ethics
\par
drawn from within, future work must now consider how external influ-
\par
ences and aspirational ethics might be made to fit the negotiation process.
\par
The next logical step is to submit bargaining ethics to broader dis-
\par
cussion and innovation. Ethics has gone mostly unnoted in scholarly
\par
literature, although negotiation itself has drawn widespread public inter-
\par
est and seen escalated use of the process and a large increase in books and
\par
commentataries on the subject. In this climate of interest, exposing the
\par
ethics of negotiation to the sunlight is likely to have salutary effects.
\par
Broader discussion may encourage negotiators to reflect on their ethics.
\par
Self-criticism as well as more systematic criticism by negotiators, schol-
\par
ars, and analysts may promote more ethical bargaining. Negotiators
\par
\textbf{303 }See text accompanying note 65 supra for the facts of the barbell hypothetical.
\par
Imaged with the Permission of N.Y.U. Law ReviewJune \textbf{1989]}
\par
\emph{BARGAINING }\textbf{\emph{ETHICS}}
\par
would probably welcome written commentary or informed guidance.
\par
Modes of negotiation that mitigate many ethical problems are be-
\par
coming more widespread. A pervasive and multi-purpose process such
\par
as bargaining, however, must accommodate legitimate strategies, tactics,
\par
and styles of all varieties, including classical positional bargaining that
\par
has been the paradigm for this Article. Nevertheless, traditional distrib-
\par
utive hard-bargaining and competitive approaches are increasingly mak-
\par
ing room for problem-solving negotiations and cooperative approaches.
\par
Such approaches in and of themselves displace many ethical concerns
\par
because problem solving is at odds with deception and unfairness. The
\par
popularization of problem-solving negotiation, such as Fisher and Ury's
\par
standard-based negotiation,30 4 suggests an eagerness for innovation in
\par
bargaining that departs from classical models.
\par
Quite apart from process driven ethics or a wholesale aspirational
\par
ethic, bargaining reform is possible in critical areas. Bearing in mind the
\par
operational needs of the process, reform should be discrete to particular
\par
concerns. While a statutory approach to the bargaining process is an
\par
unlikely remedy for most ethical concerns, carefully constructed law can
\par
remedy particular problems in areas of special concern, as in the regula-
\par
tion of collective bargaining, without unduly encumbering the process.
\par
These are steps that I believe can have a beneficial effect in the short
\par
term. Thus far, efforts to improve bargaining ethics have been an empty
\par
vessel. Thoughtful steps that begin to fill it, even preliminarily, are im-
\par
portant because of the increasing numbers of new negotiators. If left un-
\par
attended, the danger is that they and the countless others who use
\par
negotiation will perpetuate not only the process but also its unethical
\par
practices.
\par
Whatever steps are taken, an aspirational ethic for bargaining re-
\par
mains an important challenge. No process can be self-sufficient in creat-
\par
ing its own ethic. Truthfulness and fairness are values that derive their
\par
meaning from human experience and ideals. The development of a satis-
\par
factory aspirational bargaining ethic demands more systematic thinking
\par
not only about the process and its needs, but also about the needs of
\par
society. Such thinking might begin by focusing on the potential for an
\par
aspirational bargaining ethic in light of the functional requirements of
\par
the bargaining process.
