I. INTRODUCTION
\par
It is by now a commonplace in science that "the whole is greater than the sum of
\par
its parts"; in other words, that it is (usually) not possible to infer from detailed
\par
knowledge of subsystems similarly detailed knowledge of a system of which they
\par
are parts. With wholes and parts represented as probability distributions over,
\par
respectively, system states and system substates, the statement becomes: "a set of
\par
marginal probability distributions does not uniquely determine a joint distribu-
\par
tion." It is in essentially this form (variations include relations or possibility
\par
distributions." in place of probability distributions) that the problem of the
\par
relation between parts and wholes has been studied in system theory by a school
\par
of researchers in \emph{reconstructabi/ity analysis }who trace the origins of their field to
\par
W. R. Ashby's work on \emph{constraint analysis. }3.62
\par
The aim of Ashby's 1964 paper is to develop a technique whereby a relation can
\par
be decomposed into a set of subrelations from which it can then be reconstructed.
\par
(The problem of determining a set of subsystems from which a system is
\par
reconstructable is known as the \emph{reconstruction problem.) }Although not all relations
\par
are decomposable, many are, and there are advantages (reduction in storage
\par
requirements, indication of the structure of the system, etc.) to representing a large,
\par
decomposable relation as a set of subrelations. (An important area of computer
\par
science, relational database theory.?" has emerged to deal with issues of adequate
\par
representation of a relation by a set of subrelations. Although its origins are2 MICHAEL PITfARELLl
\par
attributed to Codd,18 many of the fundamentals of relational database theory were
\par
already worked out by Ashby.!")
\par
\textbf{In }1972, Ashby and Madden 73 considered the problem of identifying an
\par
unknown relation given knowledge of a set of its subrelations. (This type of
\par
problem, in the context of reconstructability analysis, is known as an \emph{identification}
\par
\emph{problem.) }The subrelations might correspond to the results of studies undertaken
\par
by different investigators, involving different aspects of some phenomenon, which it
\par
is desired later to integrate. (Such integration has come to be known in the social
\par
sciences as meta-analysis.P) Unfortunately for those who would want to perform
\par
this sort of analysis (and in many cases it is not, or is no longer, possible to study
\par
the phenomenon as a whole), Ashby and Madden concluded from their study that
\par
a relation ... that is chosen at random from the set of all possible relations on a large number of
\par
variables will almost certainly not be identifiable (p. 355).
\par
\textbf{In }other words, a whole -relation is rarely identifiable, in the sense that no other
\par
relation has exactly the same subrelations defined on some particular collection of
\par
subsets of its variables, from collections of its parts.
\par
The same is true when systems are represented as probability distributions; i.e.,
\par
the identification problem for discrete probability distributions is usually \emph{under-}
\par
\emph{determined. }However, some of those who employ concepts of probability and
\par
statistics are not aware that this is the case. As evidence, consider the following,
\par
from a recent collection of counterexamplesr'"
