\emph{3.6 Minimum description length}
\par
Maximum entropy estimation of probability distributions from frequency data has
\par
recently been shown to be a special case of minimum description length (MOL)
\par
estimation. 3 1 The motivation for MOL estimation is discussed by Risanen.f" who
\par
has shown'" that maximum likelihood estimation is also a special case of MOL
\par
estimation, under certain conditions.
\par
\emph{3.7 Summary}
\par
That so many apparently different (in motivation, at least) estimation criteria
\par
coincide with MEP, have MEP as a special case, or give approximately the same
\par
result as MEP, certainly provides some justification for use of MEP as a generally
\par
applicable estimation procedure. Of course, there are also independent arguments
\par
in its favor (e.g., the concentration theorem, despite its limitations; the fact that
\par
entropy maximization is a type of uncertainty maximization, and, hence, infor-
\par
mation minimization; etc.) that are very compelling. However, there do not seem
\par
to be enough good reasons to employ only a general purpose estimate of \emph{Po}
\par
(assuming that an estimate is called for at all). For example, if one is extremely
\par
cautious and believes that squared error is the best measure of the difference36 MICHAEL PITTARELLI
\par
between probability distributions, then the appropriate estimate is the squared
\par
Euclidean minimax estimate; if one uses \emph{e2 }but is more concerned about the long
\par
run, then \emph{Px }should be calculated; and so on. Furthermore, in some situations, it is
\par
neither necessary nor advisable to provide an estimate of the unknown
\par
distribution.
