In general the representation in terms of intervals seems superior to the representation in terms of
\par
point values, Even in the \emph{ideal }case, in which all of our measures are based on statistical inference from
\par
suitably massive quantities of data, it is most natural to construe these measures as being constrained
\par
by intervals. In confidence interval estimation, for example, what we get from our statistics is a high
\par
confidence thai a given parameter is contained in a certain interval. This translates neatly and
\par
conveniently into an interval constraint. The results of statistical inference should reflect indeterminacy
\par
or vagueness. What we can properly claim to know is not that a parameter has [a] certain value but
\par
(with probability or high confidence) that it lies within certain limits. This limitation of human
\par
knowledge should surely be mirrored in computer-based systems (p. 273).
\par
