6. CONCLUSIONS
\par
Principles of and techniques for making inferences and decisions from incomplete
\par
information regarding a discrete probability distribution have been presented.
\par
Their development was motivated primarily by the identification problem of
\par
reconstructability analysis, in which the partial information takes the form of
\par
known marginal distributions. Most of the concepts apply more generally, when
\par
information is available in the form of arbitrary linear constraints on the
\par
components of the joint distribution. (Such constraints arise when some of the
\par
probabilities are known exactly, or bounds on some of them are known; when an
\par
ordering on some or all of the probabilities is known; etc.)
\par
In Section 2, some new concepts of compatibility of a joint distribution with
\par
given marginals are explored. These lead to alternate definitions of the reconstruc-
\par
tion family associated with a given set of marginals, and to a more liberal view of
\par
local consistency of marginal distributions, which seems appropriate when the
\par
marginal distributions are determined empirically and are thus subject to error.
\par
Procedures for estimating a joint probability distribution from linear constraints
\par
(usually in the form of known marginal distributions) are examined in Section 3.
\par
The only estimation technique employed to date in the reconstructability analysis
\par
literature is maximum entropy estimation. A number of the arguments offered in
\par
thc past in favor of maximum entropy estimation are examined, and most are
\par
shown to be subject to more or less serious qualification. However, despite their
\par
limitations, taken collectively the arguments are somewhat compelling. Alternate
\par
estimation techniques, one of which, least-risk estimation, was proposed by
\par
Cavallo and Klir lJ in the context of reconstructability analysis but has not been
\par
implemented, arc also explored. The least-risk estimation problem is shown to be a
\par
special type of location problem, algorithms for the solution of which have been
\par
developed by operations researchers. It is also shown that maximum entropy
\par
estimation either coincides with, is a special case of, or gives approximately the
\par
same results as, each of these alternative criteria. Thus, for an automated
\par
identification procedure, maximum entropy estimation may be a reasonable default
\par
option.
\par
However, selection of a single estimate from the the reconstruction family of
\par
joint distributions compatible with given data is not always necessary in order to
\par
solve many types of problems; in fact, procedures that are based on such
\par
estimation may give worse solutions, in a well-defined sense dependent on the
\par
particular context, than procedures that are not. For procedures based on
\par
estimation, there is a potential for error, and for the others, there is a potential forRECONSTRUCTABILITY ANALYSIS 55
\par
nondecisiveness (Section 5), both of which are due entirely to the under-
\par
determination of the unknown joint distribution by the available data. In the ideal
\par
case, the data determine the joint distribution uniquely. In this case, no error or
\par
nondecisiveness can be attributed to underdetermination. Otherwise, there will be
\par
some potential for error or nondecisiveness which increases as the size of the
\par
reconstruction family compatible with the data increases. Measures of reconstruc-
\par
tion uncertainty for quantifying this potential for error are defined, and their
\par
properties explored, in Section 4.
\par
The relevance of these concepts to decision theory is demonstrated in Section 5.
\par
A method for decision making under partial uncertainty (i.e., when a probability
\par
distribution over states of nature is known to satisfy a set of linear constraints) is
\par
proposed and compared to other criteria. Measures of nondecisiveness for such
\par
decisions are shown to be measures of reconstruction uncertainty of the type
\par
