\par
PREFACE
\par
The purpose of this symposium on Modeling of Large-Scale Energy Systems
\par
was to review the scope, limitations, and current applications of state-of-
\par
the-art energy models. Significant progress was reported on the work in
\par
many countries of the world in the utilization of models to affect tactical,
\par
strategic, and policy decisions. Insights into future research needs were also
\par
presented.
\par
This symposium was organized by the International Institute for
\par
Applied Systems Analysis (IIASA) with cosponsorship by the International
\par
Federation of Automatic Control (IFAC) through the IFAC Systems
\par
Engineering Committee. IFAC is a federation of 40 national member orga-
\par
nizations representing the technical societies related to automatic control in
\par
the respective member countries. The IFAC Systems Engineering Committee
\par
(SECOM) is broadly concerned with the theory and application of systems
\par
engineering and focuses upon energy problems through the SECOM Working
\par
Group on Large-Scale Energy Systems Analysis.
\par
The Symposium Proceedings are presented according to the session
\par
organization:
\par
Opening Session
\par
Session I - Problems in Exploring Energy Demand and Conservation
\par
Session II - Integrated Sets of Models and Their Policy Applications
\par
Session III - Problems of Technology Assessment, Energy Supply,
\par
and Use
\par
Session IV - Questions of Distribution and Allocation of Resources
\par
Session V - Issues of Decision Making Under Uncertainty
\par
These papers represent the contributions of participants from 17 countries.
\par
As a general observation, impressive accomplishments have been
\par
achieved in the past several years in bringing models to a useful state of
\par
development. Many different aspects of modeling are treated in the papers.
\par
The models include physical models; time series models of economic
\par
representations; scenario writing in which models containing technical,
\par
economic, environmental, and social factors were all considered; as well as
\par
simulation representations covering decision making. The experience re-
\par
ported indicates that more and more governmental and corporate execu-
\par
tives are looking to economic and energy models as guides to improved
\par
v
\par
vi
\par
technology assessmen t and policies.
\par
A number of significant challenges remain in the development of large-
\par
scale energy system models. Areas for future work include modeling of:
\par
1. International energy markets
\par
2. Less-developed countries
\par
3. World oil policy
\par
4. Global transportation of fuels
\par
S. Energy and security
\par
6. Energy-GDP-inflation relationship
\par
7. Productivity-energy price relationship
\par
8. Discontinuous improvements
\par
Also additional efforts toward data collection and model validation as well
\par
as the treatment of uncertainty are warranted. The integration of economic,
\par
environmental, and energy models is a subject of increasing importance.
\par
We wish to acknowledge the key contributions of K. Hoffman, Co-
\par
chainnan of the Program Committee, as well as H. Porias and A. McDonald
\par
who served as Secretary and Acting Secretary of the Program Committee.
\par
Also to be noted are the major contributions of the members of the Program
\par
Committee: I.H. Abdel-Rahman, Institute of National Planning, Egypt;
\par
