ABSTRACT
\par
After dealing with the question of determining the complexity of a
\par
model, the Austrian experiences with the development and application
\par
of models for different purposes within the energy economy are
\par
described. Finally the importance of nonlinear models for the realistic
\par
representation especially of pclitical influences on the energy supply
\par
situation is underlined.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Complexity of Models; Analytical and Numerical Approach; Austrian
\par
ExpEriences with Models in Energy Economy; Necessity of Nonlinear
\par
Models.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Doubtless the greatest real system which we may consider is that of
\par
the Universe. It is worth to remember that, more than a century ago,
\par
it was already possible to make a distinct statement about the fate
\par
of this system. As a consequence of the just precisely formulated
\par
second law of thermodynamics one was able to assert that its ultimate
\par
destiny will be its heat death. The possibility of this statement is
\par
based on the following three assumptions:
\par
- the Universe is considered as a closed system;
\par
- there exists the entropy as a state function of
\par
this system;
\par
- only the peculiarity of this state has
\par
to be regarded.
\par
This example clearly illustrates the fact that any well posed model
\par
of a system is determined by the questions which should be answered
\par
by its aid and furthermore, that the complexity of a model is not
\par
determined by the physical extension of the real system which it
\par
- 3 -
\par
- 4 -
\par
should represent but by the nurr;ber of parameters "hich are necessary
\par
to describe its behaviour adequately with respect to the problems
\par
of which the solution should be found with its support.
\par
The term number of parameters needs some explanation. Here too I'll
\par
try to give the explanation by means of an illustrative example. A
\par
problem, familiar to mechanical engaged in the design of
\par
combustion engines, is the evaluation of the resonance frequencies
\par
of the torsional vibrations of the crankshaft of those engines. The
\par
execution of the evaluation is done by a simplified model of the
\par
vibrating system. According to the rules of mechanical equivalence,
\par
any piece of the crankshaft belonging to one piston can be reduced
\par
to a piece of a rigid straight shaft. Each of these pieces is
\par
connected with its neighbour by elastic forces. In the same way the
\par
fcrces acting on each piston as well as the iDertia forces of the
\par
moving piston and the rod can be recuced to a pericdi-
\par
cally varying force and a periodically varying momentum acting on
\par
each piece. The modE,l which we have constructed by this means
\par
consists therefore of n elastically coupled pieces of rigid shafts,
\par
if n is the number of pistons, subject to the action of periodically
\par
outer forces and momenta. So we have come to a system of n elasti-
\par
cally coupled masses - the masses of the rigid pieces. Its be-
\par
haviour is described by a system of n ordinary differer,tial equations
\par
of the second order of n time dependent variables which represent
\par
the relative angle position of the different pieces. The resonance
\par
frequencies of this model correspor-d to those experimentally ob-
\par
served in the real system within a range of less than 2 \%. But a
\par
further reduction of the number of variables - or pararr;eters - is
\par
possible. Though the differential equations of the system are of the
\par
same kind one can consider instead of n discrete pieces of rigid
\par
elastically coupled shafts on which n periodically forces and momenta
\par
are acting pointwise, a throughout elastic shaft cn which the mass
\par
and elastic forces as well as the acting forces and momenta are
\par
continously distributed. The behaviour of this syEtem is described
\par
by one single partial differential equation for the only variable of
\par
this system, which is the relative angle position of the cross
\par
section of the shaft, now on the time and the place of the
\par
cross section along the.axis of the shaft. The approximation of this
\par
simplified model to the original one is fast improving with the in-
\par
crease of n. The fact that the solution of this partial differential
\par
equation is a standard result of classical analysis allows an
\par
immediate estimaticn of the resonance frequencieE. This example does
\par
not show only that a drastic reduction of the original number of
\par
parameters - in the case if they describe states of the same kind -
\par
can be reached tut it demonstrates also that in our times, where we
\par
dispose over such powerful tools for the performance of complicated
\par
numerical calculations like electronic computers, we should not
\par
neglect the other powerful tool which mankind has developed to
\par
master complicated situations, namely the analytic In
\par
order to crack a nut we need a pair of tongs. The. same if, true for
\par
the solution of the problems which we are dealing with in our
\par
symposium. Both Computers and Analysis are essential.
