\par
AUSTRIAN EXPERIENCES WITH MODELS IN ENERGY ECONOMY
\par
From what I have said one might have got the impression - though al-
\par
ready great physical systems could be described with respect to a
\par
particular behaviour with only few variables - that the description
\par
of physically smaller systems create less problems than the des-
\par
cription of larger ones. But I shall demonstrate that this con-
\par
clusion is generally not true. In Austria, like in many other
\par
ccuntries, so far, we have had a rather tight correlation between
\par
the Gross National Product un the one side and the overall use of
\par
energy or, in particular, the use of electric energy on the other
\par
side. But this statistical fact does not explain the interplay of
\par
the different economic factors like labour, capital and energy in
\par
the creation of the Gross National Product. In some of the great
\par
countries it was possible to develop - based on the available
\par
statistical data - a simple model for the medium term planning of
\par
the energy economy namely a production function for the Gross National
\par
Product. This production function contains the variables capital
\par
expenditure for gross investments, labour fcrces and energy input
\par
- in aggregated totals or distinguishing between different kinds of
\par
energy. But for Austria the same endeavcur failed completely [1J
\par
for the national economy as a whole as well as for some important
\par
sectors. This was not due to the fact that the periods during which
\par
the economic evolution could be considered as relatively stable are
\par
not very long and therefore the result of smoothing of the statisti-
\par
cal data of relatively small number' of years is valid only within a
\par
rather broad range. The main reason for the mentioned disappointing
\par
result is the fact, that the law of the great number does not work
\par
within a small system. There the decisions of just one enterprise
\par
- or at least of a small number of economic entities - are often
\par
showing significant effects on the behaviour of the whole economic
\par
system. Therefore it is not possible to hide those entities behind
\par
a statistical aggregate ideally consisting of many such entities but
\par
one must take them explicitely into account i.e. one has to present
\par
them individually, which means that one needs an adequate number of
\par
variables for the description of the behaviour of the system. Further-
\par
more, external influences in general do affect smaller economic
\par
systems more than larger ones. In particular, in smaller systems the
\par
significance of the foreign trade compared to that of the home market
\par
is in general more important than in larger ones. Also international
\par
pclitical disturbances exert usually greater effects in smaller
\par
countries on the results of their economies than in larger ones. In
\par
the case of Austria this explains the situation of having only
\par
relatively short periods for which, under this aspect, statistically
\par
homogeneous data are available. Only beginning with 1955, the year
\par
of the state treaty, we can speak of a relatively long stable period.
\par
The fact that a small country like Austria has to use complex
\par
models for the description of its energy economy justifies that I
\par
speak today to you, at the side of eminent experts such as
\par
Academician Styrikovich and Prof. Hogan, who are both at the same
\par
time exponents of the greatest countries of our world.
\par
In Austria we have since about ten years an overall system of energy
\par
statistics published yearly by the Austrian Central Statistical
\par
Office which corresponds to the Input-Output Matrix of the
\par
National Economy. The idea to establish such a system of national
\par
- 6 -
\par
energy statistics was developed by S. Sagoroff, K. Schagginger,
\par
K. Turetschek and myself in a study concerning the relations which
\par
exist between the national economy as a whole and its energy supply
\par
These energy statistics show the input and output of 25 different
\par
energy carriers to 43 economic sectors into which the whole national
\par
economy is divided. These statistics serve as data base for two
\par
important models.
\par
The first one is a model for the simulation of the management cf the
\par
energy supply in caSES of emergency. If such a situation occurs
\par
an emergency council - set up by law [4J - has to advice the govern-
\par
ment the appropriate measures to be taken. The model is intended to
\par
facilitate the work of this council and is designed as a computer
\par
supported decision program. The procedure is as follows. By means
\par
of the data of the recent economic development the data of the last
\par
available yearly energy statistics are adapted tc those which may
\par
represent the present situation. With the aid of a set of index
\par
numbers on which an agreement among exrerts has been reached, one
\par
passes over from the yearly energy balance adapted to picture the
\par
present situation to monthly ones. Based on these data one is able
\par
to compare the unrestricted neEds of the forthcoming months with the
\par
amount of the different energy carriers which are expected to be tf,en
\par
available for supply. A first decision must now be taken concerning
\par
the quotas of the different kinds of energy carriers assigned to each
\par
sector of the economy order to arrive at a new equilibrum between
\par
the sum of imports and indigenous production of energy on the one side
\par
the deliveries of energy on the other side. This first decision
\par
has of course to be checked with respect to its intrinsic
\par
That means that the activities of the particular sectors of the
\par
economy, their production and ccnsumption of goods and services,
\par
which are possible with respect to thE granted quotas of supply with
\par
the different energy carriers, are compatible, i.e. that the industry
\par
for the production of building material should not come into a
\par
position to produce goods that the building industry is able to
\par
use and so on. This necessary check is done by a transition from the
\par
energy balance tc the monetary input-output balance of the whole
\par
econcmy wf.ich represents the activities of the particular sectors
\par
of the economy by monetary value of these with the
\par
aid of functions arE representing these activities in dependence
\par
on the respective of energy. These are
\par
pcstulated on the base of the experiences gained already in the
\par
particular sectors on the relations existing between the levels of
\par
economic activities and the levels of energy consumption as well as
\par
on the special technological conditions of these relations. Details
\par
about this whole procedure were published recently by M. Oettl and
\par
W. Teufelsbauer C5J . I may only mention here that this method
\par
doesn't allow only to identify the discrepancies caused by the
\par
primary supply decision but does also allow to correct this de-
\par
cision and to gain at last an equilibrum between the activities
\par
of the particular sectors of economy on the reduced level of
\par
overall energy supply. The comruter allows to find this solution
\par
rather fast and it will be now up to members of the emergency
\par
councilor at least to thE responsible politicians to decide whether
\par
this solution is acceptable from their point of view or should be
\par
replaced by another one is better suited for a compromise.
\par
In the latter case, the whole has to be repeated with a
\par
new set of initial values for the quotas and will be continued
\par
- 7 -
\par
since at least an optimal political solution has been reached. The
\par
first tests with this model are now way and we hope that we
\par
will be able to demonstrate that this model works well in the early
\par
summer of this year.
\par
The question rises of course if it would be advisable instead of
\par
working with such a simulation model with a model, which delivers
\par
directly a solution for the quotas with respect to the best fulfill-
\par
ment of a special socio-economic criteria. Examples for such criteria
\par
are for instance a maximum of employment or a maximum of national
\par
income. It may perhaps interest you that we indeed did occupy
\par
ourselves with such optimal models; this occupation was histori-
\par
cally previous to that with the simulation one. Prof. Tintner
\par
published already 1975 an excellent paper in which he dis-
\par
played in detail the structure of optimal models for the
\par
management of an energy emergency. Yet the disadvantage of thesE
\par
qptimal models lie in the fact that the necessary data for these
\par
models are not available. The Austrian Central Statistical Office
\par
has officially declared that it will last more than five years to
\par
produce these data and that then these data will refer to a
\par
si tuation at least seven years ago. Therefore on account of this
\par
lack of data we had to pass over from these perfect optimal models
\par
to the less perfect simulation one for which the data are more or
\par
less immediately on hand and which will allow us, as we hope, to
\par
reach at least a politically acceptable solution in any case of a
\par
possible energy supply emergency.
\par
The second application of the yearly energy balance consists in the
\par
use as initial data base for the evolution equations for the fore-
\par
cast model of the Austrian energy economy. Charged by the Austrian
\par
Federal Ministry of Trade, Ccmmerce and Industry, the Austrian
\par
Institute for Economic Research has developed a model for medium
\par
term forecast - i.e. for a period of 10 to 15 years - of the
\par
development of the Austrian energy economy. Every year a fore-
\par
cast is published by the Institute [7J taking into account the dis-
\par
crepancies between the former forecasts and reality in order to improve
\par
the statements on the base of the eXj:eriences so gained. The detailed
\par
structure of this IT.odel is published in the annexes to the "Plan of
\par
the Austrian Energy Economy" in 1975 and also in 1976, both edited
\par
by the Federal Ministry mentioned above [8J . This model is founded
\par
on the assumption of a more or less undisturbed international politi-
\par
cal and economic evolution and on a set of explicit assumptions
\par
about the further development of particular economic sectors with
\par
rather high energy intensity on the one hand as well as on the
\par
Gross National Product on the other hand. The relations between the
\par
quantities, which are characteristic for the economic activities of
\par
the relevant sectors of the economy and their energy inputs as well
\par
as the relations between the Gross National Product and the energy
\par
consumption of the whole economy, are deduced from the experience
\par
of the past. For the future the trends, resulting frem the histori-
\par
cal statistical data are modified - if necessary - in accordance to
\par
available information about possible changes in the technology, the
\par
behaviour of the population and so on. Up to now these modifications
\par
were dealt with great caution. Especially the possibilities of
\par
censervation of energy were only marginally taken into consideration,
\par
dLe to the lack of administrative measures and financial promotions
\par
for this purposes. Though the situation has altered new in this
\par
- 8 -
\par
respect, the future forecasts will take into account explicitly
\par
the measures for energy conservation. Two extreme cases will be
\par
demonstrated respectively: The expected development without the
\par
influence of any of those measures the development under the
\par
simultaneous influence of all those measures which may be realisti-
\par
cally considered as feasible.
\par
The experienceE gained up to now with these forecasts are showing
\par
that the estimation of needs in 1985 and 1990 were alw'ays too high
\par
and had to be revised dOwnwards. But this was mainly due to the un-
\par
stable general economic development,especially to the recession in
\par
the years 1975 and 1976. During the periods, wherein the assumption
\par
of the growth rate of tte Gross Naticnal Product was fulfilled the
\par
trend of the development of the actual energy consumption has
\par
shown good accordance with that of the forecast.
\par
For the Federal Ministry of Trade, Ccmmerce and Industry the fore-
\par
cast model serves as a standard of ccmparison in order to examine
\par
if the plans made up within the different branches of the energy
\par
economy by the respective companies are sufficient to meet the
\par
expected future needs which are to be expected without taking into
\par
account unawares, or if shortages are to be feared because of the
\par
insufficient arrangements by the companies, so that the administration
\par
should influence them to correct their initial plans.
\par
The forecast model has been submitted at several times to critical
\par
reviews cf a great number of experts on economic forecasts. Many
\par
proposals for improvements and refinements of the model have been
\par
made at these occasions. Yet the implementation of nearly all of
\par
these proposals proved to be impossible due to the lack of the
\par
availability of the necessary statistical data.
\par
Especially from the methodological point of view the deterministic
\par
feature of the present forecast model is very unsatisfactory. E.g.,
\par
in order to estimate the uncertainties in the result of the fore-
\par
cast caused by the ranges for the constants of the model it
\par
would be very desirable to work with a stochastic model for tte
\par
forecast. However, at present there does not exist any base for an
\par
assessment of the ranges of these constants and therefore the
\par
transition from the deterministic to a stochastic model is
\par
impracticable.
\par
Two conclusions may be drawn from these e'xperiences:
\par
1. By the existing statistical data base one ,is
\par
forced for practical purposes to work only with
\par
those models for which the base is adequate.
\par
2. That one cannot dispense with the development
\par
of advanced mcdels thcugh at present they are
\par
not applicable in practice. Because only with
\par
the knowledge of tte kind of data which is needed
\par
to make them operable one will be able to make
\par
the necessary provisions for the improvement of
\par
the statistics and to overcome their present
\par
lacks. Therefore advanced models are essential
\par
for any progress in modelling though beeing un-
\par
applicable at present.
\par
- 9 -
\par
For individual branches of the energy economy, as well as for the
\par
domain of great companies, a number of mathematical decision models,
\par
partially rather sophisticated ones, have been developed also in
\par
Austria. The reason for mentioning these models in the context of
\par
this conference is that they often show the same as
\par
those for whole energy supply system. A sample of such models,
\par
chosen by a committee of which I had the honour to act as chairman,
\par
has teen published within a series of publications of the Federal
\par
Ministry of Trade, Commerce and Industry devoted to problems of
\par
Energy Policy [9J . In general the possibility to work with such
\par
models within the scope of a company is much easier than within
\par
the frame of the whole socia-economic context tecause of the
\par
following reasons:
\par
a) it is simpler to define the goal of a company than
\par
the goal of the socia-economic development. In
\par
most cases the goal of a company is to achieve a
\par
maximum of profit and therefore the simple question
\par
is put which strategy has to be pursued to reach
\par
this aim.
\par
b) The necessary data for models on the level of
\par
companies are available much easier and with a
\par
higher degree of reliability than for models on
\par
the socia-economic level. This is due to the fact
\par
that at least a part of this data is of a technical
\par
nature, that means that they are derivable either
\par
from the technical disposition of the plants or
\par
from rather well known factors of costs, and that
\par
the range of the other part of the data can be
\par
appraised rather fairly in general.
\par
In Austria like in most other countries the use of models of large
\par
energy systems has started by dealing with the problems of the
\par
operation of the grid. 33 years ago the transmission capacity of
\par
the then existing grid under different load situaticns was
\par
systematically determined for the first time. This work was done
\par
with the aid of simple mechanical computers only and lasted some
\par
months. The results served as the base for the development of the
\par
grid. Three years later a lcw voltage electric model of the grid
\par
- in our modern terms an analogue computer - with a fidelity of
\par
at least 3 \% became a decisive aid for the off line control and
\par
for the further planning of the extension of the grid. Today this
\par
analogue computers are already repleaced by digital ones, which
\par
do serve for the planning of the grid as well as for the on line
\par
control of its operation. The new concept for the automatic load
\par
distribution for the Austrian grid, which will be fully
\par
in summer this year, is lead by the idea of a hierarchically
\par
organised system of local and regional computers and at last a
\par
central unit by which on line
\par
a) the control of the reliability
\par
b) the instantaneous optimal load distribution
\par
can be performed. Off line, by the central unit alone, the long term
\par
optimisation of the future operation of the different plants will
\par
constantly be prepared and adapted to the changing situations. This
\par
- 10 -
\par
is very important for a country like Austria, whose electric supply
\par
still is predominantly dependent on the production of its hydraulic
\par
power plants.
\par
As an example of application of a model for the best combined
\par
energy supply with electric power, natural gas and heat from the
\par
district heating system in Austria I may mention the model which
\par
is actually developec by the Salzburger Stadtwerke AC [10J . A
\par
mixed-integer program will be used for the of the further
\par
extension of the combined supply system as well as for its actual
\par
performance with different primary energy carriers: coal, natural
\par
gas, fuel oil, hydro power and the buyance in addition of electric
\par
energy.
\par
These examples may be sufficient to show that Austria takes an
\par
active role in the development and in the application of models
\par
of large-scale energy systems. Therefore it is also particularly
\par
interested in the results of the present IIASA Symposium.
\par
NECESSITY OF NONLINEAR MODELS
\par
Finally I want to come back to the question of socia-economic
\par
energy models. According to the Law on the Promotion of Energy
\par
Supply which has passed the parliament in December last year [llJ
\par
the Austrian gcvernment will have to present from now on to the
\par
parliament a comprehensive report on the situation of the energy
\par
supply and its further prospects every year. Without making use
\par
of a formalized model for the whole economy it will therefore be
\par
necessary to apply a model which gives an account of the impacts
\par
of measures taken or omitted in the field of energy on other parts
\par
of the economy. This model extends therefore in many respects the
\par
forecast model which has been mentioned previously, especially
\par
because it should also allow for the impact of political decisions.
\par
We in Austria have gained the practical experience that for the
\par
adequate representation of such relations linear models are not
\par
suited. The decision against the use of nuclear energy from
\par
fission for the supply of electricity has been taken by a
\par
majority of only 50,4 \% in the referendum. The converse result
\par
would have allowed to take the already finished nuclear power
\par
plant of Zwentendorf into operation at least in pricipal. This
\par
example shows that one has to consider the discontinous effects
\par
of politicaJ decisions in an appropriate model. The theory of
\par
catastrophes, developed in the last decade by R. Thom, Zemann
\par
and others and, an extended version, the theory of resilience
\par
to which special attention is given within the IIASA may help us
\par
to develop such realistic models. In finishing my exposition
\par
I would like to express my hope that this symposium will
\par
particularly stress the importance of these realistic non-
\par
linear models and that it will contribute to the development
\par
of those models which can be used with advantage by the men of
\par
practice in energy politics.
