INTRODUCTORY SPEECH
\par
M. Styrikovicb
\par
USSR Academy of Sciences, Moscow, USSR
\par
I am very grateful for the honor to present an introductory speech
\par
at such a highly representative forum of very competent specialists
\par
as gathered at this symposium.
\par
The very fact that IFAC--a most prominent federation of scientists
\par
specialized in the area of automatic control--and IIASA--well-known
\par
to the world from its work, in particular on global forecasting--
\par
have united their efforts on modeling of large-scale energy systems,
\par
is very significant.
\par
Any approach to modeling needs a profound knowledge, both in theory
\par
and technique of systems analysis, and in objects to which these
\par
analyses are applied. I myself am much more a specialist in general
\par
problems of energy than in methodology of modeling, and because of
\par
this I consider it reasonable to concentrate in this introductory
\par
speech on some complicated problems connected with prognoses of the
\par
world's as well as of regional energy development.
\par
The complexity in this field is great because today all of the
\par
world's energy systems are at a turning point from a rather simple
\par
situation at which growing energy demand was ensured mainly through
\par
the extension of the production of oil and gas which are suitable
\par
for virtually any consumer. At the same time, installations for
\par
energy conversion were not too capital-intensive and the largest
\par
part of transformed energy prices, mainly electricity, was con-
\par
nected with the payment for primary energy resources. Moreover,
\par
such prices were more or less stable with a given possibility to
\par
create an economic prognosis of good probability.
\par
Today, after an initial shock and some time of uncertainty, in which
\par
many individual options were considered as a probability, it is more
\par
or less clear--at least to the majority of specialists in the energy
\par
field--that several decades will be the transition period in which
\par
energy supply will be based on a mixed blend of various primary
\par
energy sources with a gradual switch from oil as a major source to
\par
nuclear and coal.
\par
- 13 -
\par
- 14 -
\par
It is also clear with rather high probability that in the field of
\par
demand the near future will be marked by a rather large effort to
\par
lower the demand for primary energy sources by more or less strong
\par
economy measures. But in spite of such measures energy demand will
\par
grow considerably, and the so-called soft energy path is' quite
\par
unrealistic. This was proven by IIASA's additional scenario for
\par
16 Terawatt energy consumption in year 2030. In my opinion this
\par
scenario demonstrates the improbability of such low demand at least
\par
from two points of view. First, energy consumption for Regions I
\par
and III--which include most developed free market regions--will be,
\par
in 2030, almost the same as today. Because it is quite impossible
\par
to stop energy demand growth in the next one to two decades, this
\par
means that in the first decades of the next century there will be
\par
a decrease of energy demand, which is a forecast with very low
\par
probability.
\par
Second, for less developed regions this scenario proposed very low
\par
energy consumption per capita even in the year 2030 which reflects
\par
the impossibility for such a region which represents the majority
\par
of the world population, to meet even the most essential needs for
\par
a proper level of living conditions.
\par
It is necessary to take into account also that the largest part of
\par
the measures for economy of primary energy sources needs additional
\par
capital investment which also includes additional amounts of
\par
and this initial energy consumption will be compensated by the energy
\par
economy only within a rather long period of time.
\par
For this reason we must go to choose, as the only reasonable per-
\par
spective, the so-called hard energy path, based in the next few
\par
decades on gradual sUbstitution of oil and natural gas by nuclear
\par
and coal. Of course, such a way of action must be combined with
\par
all reasonable measures for preventing wasteful energy consumption,
\par
safety, and prevention of large ecological side effects.
\par
It is worthwhile to mention that being at a turning point not only
\par
in the field of energy but practically in all fields of material
\par
culture implies the necessity to switch from traditional uses as
\par
general criteria level of life to a more broad quality of life. We
\par
have to re-evaluate almost all traditionally accepted points of view
\par
on optimal ways to solving many individual problems connected with
\par
energy in general.
\par
First, it is necessary to take into account that in a transition
\par
period the energy supply shall be based on a mixed blend of several
\par
types of energy resources, very different in their usability for
\par
various consumers and mainly very capital-intensive. Today it is
\par
clear that even at the present price for oil, base load production
\par
of electricity on a large scale in every part of the world shall be
\par
much cheaper in large nuclear electrical stations than in existing
\par
heavy oil fired stations. But nuclear stations--even today's thermal
\par
neutron stations--are very capital-intensive and expenses connected
\par
with fuel consumption comprise only 20-30\% of total electricity
\par
prices. That means that the price will change very significantly
\par
with the load factor. In future, with an increasing share of nuclear
\par
energy in total electricity production and especially with the intro-
\par
duction of fast breeder reactors, the difference between the prices
\par
for electricity consumed in intermediate load, base load, and off-peak
\par
- 15 -
\par
load, shall be very large. The same will take place for coal-fired
\par
electrical stations which will be more capital-intensive because of
\par
more stringent environmental restrictions. But very probably for
\par
coal stations the share of fuel expenses in electricity prices will
\par
remain larger than for nuclear, and consequently coal-fueled stations
\par
will be in many areas convenient also for intermediate load.
\par
Of course, such a situation will call for the introduction of various
\par
measures for energy storage and artificial methods to smooth the
\par
load curve. Because natural conditions for such measures are in
\par
some areas much more favorable than in others, it means that the
\par
difference in price of electricity connected with the weekly load
\par
curve will be in some areas significantly larger than in others.
\par
Very big changes will occur in the near future also in the area of
\par
heat consumption. In past times, the price for clean fuels--oil
\par
products and gas--which are acceptable for heat supply especially
\par
for small scattered consumers, was almost the same as the price for
\par
fuel for large electrical stations. But today the price of a unit
\par
of heat produced in a large nuclear or coal-fired electrical station
\par
is much lower than the price for oil products or natural gas, and in
\par
future the gap will most probably grow even further.
\par
At such conditions, the effectiveness to use large electrical sta-
\par
tions as a source for covering heat demand in place of using local
\par
installations for direct fuel combustion, will increase considerably.
\par
Of course, in countries (for example the Soviet Union) in which
\par
district heating systems based on co-generation of electricity and
\par
heat is commonly used, the problem can be solved in a simpler way.
\par
But also at such a condition we need a new approach because using
\par
nuclear energy means a very long and large pipeline for overheated
\par
water, or to use low pressure reactors for heat supply only. In the
\par
latter case it will be possible for such specially safe reactors
\par
near big cities.
\par
Of course, using nuclear energy for district heating has some ob-
\par
stacle connected with the change of heat load around the year. In
\par
this case, the problem can be solved by using natural gas for heating
\par
(if we have, like the Soviet Union, large reserves thereof), or by
\par
using coal for winter load (in which case the installation will be
\par
equipped with additional systems for exhaust gas cleaning).
\par
Because all these solutions have some obstacle, a zone of economical
\par
use of electricity for low temperature heat supply shall be broad in
\par
future, in form of cumulative of peak heating or use of heat pumps.
\par
Because natural (climatic, geographical, etc.) conditions of each
\par
specific area are different, it is necessary to prOVide a special
\par
submodel to investigate optimal solutions for several typical com-
\par
binations of events and large systems for choosing an optimal blend
\par
for each large region.
\par
In any case, seasonal changes of heat consumption is a very big
\par
problem because storage of heat for a season is much more expensive
\par
than for a day or a week. Today, only two methods of such storages
\par
exist--stockpiling of coal, or underground gas storage. In the case
\par
of cogeneration it is possible in some areas to use water storage of
\par
big hydrostations in combination with nuclear cogenerating plants;
\par
the latter, having a reactor of fixed thermal capacity, will work
\par
- 16 -
\par
at greater heat output, but lower electricity production in winter,
\par
while hydro-stations use accumulated water to increase output of
\par
electrical energy in winter.
\par
Especially complicated is the problem of replacing oil and natural
\par
gas from producing high temperature heat for industry (metallurgy,
\par
chemical industry, etc.). Several years ago it was almost common
\par
opinion that such a problem could be solved via HTGR. But in the
\par
last years, studies on the problem give some indication that such
\par
a combination will be rather expensive because of difficulties to
\par
develop not only HTGR itself, but mainly high-pressure-helium high
\par
heat exchanger. Besides, in many cases electrical heating has some
\par
technological preference which might compensate a loss connected
\par
with producing electrical energy from high temperature heat. Espe-
\par
cially promising in this field are several processes based on using
\par
plasmotrons in which practically the temperature level is unlimited.
\par
It is obvious that in many cases the replacement of direct com-
\par
bustion of oil and gas with nuclear energy through electricity may
\par
be preferable. It means that in future the already existing ten-
\par
dency of increasing the share of electrical energy in overall energy
\par
balance in the near future will be accelerated. A very special case
\par
is a problem to replace oil products in the field of transportation.
\par
Of course, in railways and pipeline transportation replacement of
\par
oil products or gas for electricity must be accelerated, and this
\par
can be done very easily.
\par
But for autonomous transportation the problem is very difficult.
\par
Today it is almost clear that using hydrogen for cars, lorries and
\par
buses will be economical as a small addition to liquid fuel only
\par
(mainly for decreasing air pollution). Some perspectives have
\par
electrical accumulators of new types, especially for intercity
\par
vehicles. But for universal cars a switch from natural to synthetic
\par
liquid fuel is most promising (for the near future mainly produced
\par
from cheap coal).
\par
Of course, all the examples mentioned above do not comprise a full
\par
list of new problems connected with the big change in all patterns
\par
of energy even for the near future. of course, a big part of
\par
these remains to'be solved and will require an extensive exchange
\par
of opinions such as I hope shall take place during this symposium.
\par
Many more problems shall arise if we go to long-term prognosis. But
\par
it is necessary to think about a very long term of energy because all
\par
energy systems are very inertial in producing, as well as in consum-
\par
ing parts. And future energy systems are growing more and more
\par
rigid, because of increasing capital investment requirements and
\par
consequently longer lead times, and long economically reasonable working
\par
lifetimes. Also we must take into account that the penetration time
\par
of any new technology is very long. For example, if we want to have
\par
synthetic fuel as a significant part of the liquid fuel world market
\par
in the second decade of the next century, we must have several com-
\par
mercial factories already working in the first decade of the 21st
\par
century. This means that several demonstration plants which could
\par
provide the basis for a proper selection from among several already
\par
proposed processes for coal liquefaction must be in operation in the
\par
90's, and consequently scientific work and experimental installations
\par
must be well established in the 80's. It means that we must work
\par
today very hard on future energy problems if we do not want to face
\par
very serious crises in future.
