ENERGY MODELS AND POLICY PROBLEMS
\par
W. W. Hogan
\par
Energy and Environmental Policy Center, Kennedy School of Government,
\par
Harvard University, 79 Boylston St., Cambridge, Massachusetts 02138, USA
\par
Abstract. Energy models 3prouted everywhere during the
\par
rush to analyze the problem of the seventies. A review of
\par
the major policy issues provides a setting for assessing
\par
the success of the modelers and for pointing to the
\par
problems to be faced in the next decade.
\par
Keywords. Energy models; energy policy; model assessment;
\par
energy modeling forum.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
My charge in these opening remarks was
\par
to overview energy modeling, covering
\par
the state of the art, the use of energy
\par
models, and the problems that occupy
\par
center stage in energy policy
\par
This is a broad mandate, more than I
\par
could hope to cover in the time availa-
\par
ble. Fortunately, this conference will
\par
provide a rich sample of the range of
\par
existing energy models. And for those
\par
interested in a more comprehensive sum-
\par
mary of energy models, we are indebted
\par
to my colleagues, Alan Manne, Richard
\par
Richels, and John Weyant,for their ex-
\par
cellent survey article in a \textbraceleft ecent is-
\par
sue of Operations Research.
\par
Faced with the competition, the best
\par
use of my time at the start of this
\par
conference is to examine the applica-
\par
tions of energy models. Speaking from
\par
the perspective of my experience in the
\par
United States, I would like to step
\par
back from the discussion of technical
\par
modeling problems, with the focus on the
\par
models, to describe where energy policy
\par
models, particularly large-scale models,
\par
are used, how a modeler perceives the
\par
policy problems that confront energy de-
\par
cision makers, and how the models have
\par
adapted over time, with varying degrees
\par
of success, to meet these problems.
\par
These introductory remarks on the uses of
\par
energy models may help motivate and set
\par
in context our many sessions over the
\par
coming days.
\par
This is a propitious time for examining
\par
energy policy and energy models. Spurred
\par
by events familiar to us all, we have
\par
seen over the last several years, a great
\par
deal of work in energy modleing and its
\par
applications to energy policy problems.
\par
Following the disruption of the world oil
\par
market in 1973, there was an explosion of
\par
analyses, including the development and
\par
use of many energy models, focused on
\par
helping deal with energy
\par
problems. The results of that
\par
burst of activity were summarized
\par
- 17 -
\par
recently in a remarkable sequence of
\par
more or less independent studies of
\par
energy policy and energy problems: 2
\par
Energy Future, by Stobaugh and Yergin
\par
Energy in America's Future, by Schurr
\par
et al. 3; Energy: The Next Twenty Years,
\par
by Landsberg et. al. 4 ; Energy in Transi-
\par
tion, by Brooks et al. s
\par
.
\par
You may be familiar with a few of these
\par
volumes. The first, Energy Future, a
\par
national bestseller in the United
\par
States, was edited by two colleagues
\par
from Harvard University, Robert Stobaugh
\par
and Daniel Yergin. The second, Energy
\par
in America's Future, was written by a
\par
team led by Sam Schurr at Resources for
\par
the Future in Washington, D.C. The
\par
third, Energy: The Next Twenty Years,
\par
was prepared by a Ford Foundation spon-
\par
sored study group of which I was one of
\par
twenty members. And the fourth,
\par
in Transition, which preceded the other
\par
three in execution if not publication,
\par
is the product of the Committee on Nu-
\par
clear and Alternative Energy Systems,
\par
chaired by Harvey Brooks and Edward
\par
Ginston, of the United States National
\par
Academy of Sciences.
\par
These four volumes are remarkable be-
\par
cause of consensus in their findings
\par
about the state of energy policy making
\par
and policy problems in the United
\par
States. This consensus provides a
\par
starting point for addressing the
\par
state-of-the-art of energy models in-
\par
directly by surveying the state-of-the-
\par
question in energy policy analysis. We
\par
can begin by summarizing the conven-
\par
tional wisdom, as I see it, held in
\par
common in the four studies which are
\par
renresentative of the mainstream of
\par
recent analysis in the United States.
\par
For convenience in summarizing this
\par
conventional wisdom on energy policy, I
\par
will borrow seven statements from Ener-
\par
gy: The Next Twenty Years, the seven--
\par
key realities of the energy situation.
\par
These seven realities of the energy si-
\par
tuation must be recognized and their
\par
challenges met if we are to move for-
\par
ward in the United States and in our
\par
relationships with the rest of the
\par
world.
\par
The world is not running out of energy.
\par
- 18 -
\par
Every responsible analysis recognizes
\par
the abundant sources of energy available
\par
to the United States and the world. But
\par
for many years the available supplies of
\par
energy will be increasingly expensive to
\par
obtain and difficult to use. The
\par
problems are not in absolute scarcity
\par
but rather in how to make energy availa-
\par
ble economically.
\par
Middle Eastern oil holds great risks but
\par
is so valuable that the world will re-
\par
main dependent on it for a lone time to
\par
come. A group at an international in-
\par
stitute such as IIASA, concerned with
\par
the international dimensions of energy
\par
problems, may find nothing remarkable
\par
in this observation, which is so obvi-
\par
ous as to be a commonplace. But this
\par
long-term dependence on Middle Eastern
\par
oil is still not accepted by many policy
\par
makers in the United States. Our most
\par
prominent official policy theme has been
\par
energy independence. Still daily we
\par
hear discussions of how to free our-
\par
selves from Hiddle Eastern oil. The
\par
prevalent view of the analysts, that
\par
this independence is an illusion, is
\par
only slowly seeping into the mainstream
\par
of energy policy making.
\par
Higher energy costs cannot be avoided
\par
but can be contained oy letting prices
\par
rise to reflect them. As you know, the
\par
United States has been unwilling to
\par
recognize and confront the consumer with
\par
the increased costs of energy that re-
\par
sulted from the turmoil in the world oil
\par
market. Our national policy has been
\par
directed at keeping domestic prices be-
\par
low world prices. The new conventional
\par
wisdom calls for facing the adjustments
\par
that are inevitable by removing the
\par
domestic barriers that may cost us more
\par
in the long run.
\par
Enyironmental effects of energy use are
\par
serious and hard to manage. Until re-
\par
cently, environmental impacts were
\par
thought to be controlling, that they
\par
would dictate the character of U.S.
\par
energy supply development, primarily by
\par
exclusion. But every careful analysis
\par
of the problems denies this view. We
\par
find that for most environmental
\par
problems, at least those critical over
\par
the next two decades, the difficulty is
\par
absorbing the costs of controlling
\par
- 19 -
\par
environmental effects. These costs will
\par
be high, and it will be important to
\par
mitigate damage to the environment, but
\par
the costs need not be so high as to pre-
\par
clude the development of indigenous
\par
energy supplies.
\par
Conservation is an essential source of
\par
energy in large guantities. This is the
\par
main theme of Energy Future, by Stobaugh
\par
and Yergin. The United States came to
\par
the conservation heresy rather slowly,
\par
starting with the doctrine of the abso-
\par
lute need for a growing level of energy
\par
usage. However, many analyses and the
\par
evidence of a few years have created the
\par
new orthodoxy of energy conservation.
\par
