ENERGY POLICY AND POLICY
\par
MODELS
\par
This is the core of the conventional
\par
wisdom in the United States, at the
\par
end of the seventies, after the in-
\par
tense amount of effort that went into
\par
both energy policy analysis and large-
\par
scale modeling for energy policy ana-
\par
lysis. What can we say about how
\par
modeling affected the formation of
\par
these opinions or provided the logical
\par
support for the policy conclusions. I
\par
will treat these questions by summari-
\par
zing the seventies and anticipating the
\par
eighties through a series of more de-
\par
tailed issues. This is a selective
\par
chronology of the use and development of
\par
large-scale energy policy models in the
\par
United States and, in part, an indict-
\par
ment of the failure of those efforts as
\par
we look to the problems before us.
\par
Allocation and Shortages
\par
The first analytical and policy response
\par
to the dramatic disruption of oil mar-
\par
kets, in 1973 and 1974, was to commis-
\par
sion a large-scale, intensive, and ac-
\par
celerated effort to develop the capa-
\par
bility for central management of the
\par
U.S. energy system. The charge was for
\par
the central government to allocate sup-
\par
plies and manage the effects of oil
\par
shortages. That effort was a dramatic
\par
failure. I suggest that we should not
\par
be surprised at this failure. Central
\par
management of the U.S. energy system is
\par
impossible during periods of emergency.
\par
Certainly large-scale modeling efforts
\par
have been of no help.
\par
The data requirements are prohibitive.
\par
Data are not available at the level of
\par
detail required for either the analysis
\par
or the execution of management control.
\par
Most likely, the effect of the emergency
\par
is to change the system so much--e.g.,
\par
change driving and vacation plans--that
\par
we cannot identify the data needed.
\par
Even if the data were available in prin-
\par
ciple, they would never be available in
\par
time to make decisions.
\par
It is remarkable how much effort has
\par
been spent on developing large models
\par
that purport to be able to manage an
\par
emergency in the U.S. oil economy,
\par
usually by controlling refineries
\par
through the distributivn of oil and the
\par
regulation of refinery yields. The idea
\par
has a surface appeal, particularly to a
\par
modeler. Unfortunately, true optimiza-
\par
tion and detached central management of
\par
the U.S. oil system is beyond the state-
\par
of-the-art. I attribute the repeated
\par
attzmpts to construct such models to the
\par
short tenure of the unfortunate govern-
\par
ment officials responsible for alloca-
\par
tion programs during emergencies. Since
\par
the job is impossible, model or no,
\par
turnover is rapid and we lose the ex-
\par
perience and knowledge that a large-
\par
scale modeling system won't help.
\par
During the tranquility between the
\par
chaos of emergencies, the real life
\par
data problems seem tractable and hope
\par
springs eternal. Few of us have a
\par
history of first trying to develop such
\par
models; then, counseling against them;
\par
and, finally, tiring of the discussion.
\par
With each succeeding emergency, however,
\par
our ranks grow.
