\par
The second major focus of U.S. energy
\par
policy centered on dealing with higher
\par
prices and the effects of the price
\par
controls imposed by the U.S. Congress.
\par
Modelers generally find price controls
\par
a great inconvenience. We have a good
\par
theory of the operation of unfettered
\par
competitive markets, without price con-
\par
trols, and poorer theories about how
\par
the system functions in the presence
\par
of price controls.
\par
The administrative control of prices
\par
is a divisive issue. There is no
\par
political consensus about the continua-
\par
tion of price controls or the associa-
\par
ted transfer of income. The modelers
\par
have been helpful, however, in showing
\par
how controls affect the predictions of
\par
their models. Controls depress domes-
\par
tic supply and expand demand. Unlike
\par
the case in the analysis of alloca-
\par
tions and shortages, models have
\par
played a role in describing for policy-
\par
makers the implications of removing
\par
price controls. It is our misfortune
\par
that the improved understanding that
\par
flowed from the analysis did not lead
\par
to a policy consensus. The models
\par
showed that the certain costs of decon-
\par
trol come early, and the uncertain
\par
benefits come much later. The models
\par
helped to clarify the gains of the win-
\par
ners and the sacrifices of the losers,
\par
but the models have not helped resolve
\par
the conflict in values. 6
