\par
pears from time to time, early model-
\par
ing efforts (e.g., in PIES) quickly
\par
disposed of this issue. Using no more
\par
than detailed accounting, which is a
\par
strength of large-scale models, we cal-
\par
culated the investment and employment
\par
demands of an aggressive domestic ener-
\par
gy expansion. By comparing such ener-
\par
gy forecasts with the predictions of
\par
macro-economic models of the full
\par
economy, it was easy to show the
\par
growth in the energy sector, although
\par
expensive and significant, would not
\par
be so dramatic as to substantially
\par
dominate the growth of the economy.
\par
In relative terms, the future require-
\par
ment would not be much greater than
\par
those of the past.
\par
This was an important result at the
\par
time; it is an example of one fre-
\par
quent contribution of modeling: to
\par
quickly isolate non-problems. Invest-
\par
ment capacity and employment bottle-
\par
necks quickly became non-problems in
\par
policy-making discussions; and, in my
\par
judgment, correctly so.
