Energy and the Economy
\par
After we considered the problems of
\par
the very short run--import reduction,
\par
shortages, price control, environmental
\par
constraints, the demand for investment--
\par
our analyses turned to longer-run ques-
\par
tions such as economic growth. At the
\par
start of the decade, all but a few spe-
\par
cialists assumed that there was an iron
\par
link between energy and economic growth.
\par
But by the end of the seventies, heresy
\par
had moved to become conventional wisdom:
\par
economic growth can be achieved even in
\par
a world of scarce energy resources.
\par
Energy modelers deserve much of the
\par
credit for this dramatic and important
\par
change in policy thinking. Hudson and
\par
Jorgenson, Manne, Nordhaus, Koopmans,
\par
and many others, with much of the work
\par
begun as part of the energy project here
\par
at IIASA, were at the core of the analy-
\par
sis and the debate that changed our
\par
of the role of energy in the economy.
\par
The modelers articulated the question
\par
better than anyone else, and they pro-
\par
duced the critical analyses which demon-
\par
strated that we could have economic
\par
growth even if we did have expensive
\par
energy. The key facts, as we know, were
\par
the small value share of energy expendi-
\par
tures in an equilibrium economy, and the
\par
high potential for substituting other
\par
factors of production for the newly
\par
scarce energy inputs. Almost all ener-
\par
gy models and energy analyses now recog-
\par
nize and incorporate this energy-economy
\par
flexibility.
\par
