\par
The single greatest obstacle to forming
\par
a consensus on an energy policy for the
\par
United States has been the problem of
\par
income distribution. Because of our
\par
large level of domestic energy produc-
\par
tion, compared to our level of total
\par
energy consumption, any increase in the
\par
price of imported oil which prompts an
\par
increase in the price of related energy
\par
products produces a dramatic shift of
\par
income, not only from the United States
\par
to the foreign suppliers of oil but al-
\par
so from energy consumers within the
\par
United States to energy producers with-
\par
in the United States. The control of
\par
this transfer must be part of any na-
\par
tional policy, and this makes energy a
\par
divisive issue.
\par
Many energy modelers have tried to con-
\par
front this problem. To date, however,
\par
I have seen nothing that amounts to a
\par
persuasive model of what happens to dif-
\par
ferent consumers and producers in the
\par
face of dramatic changes in the level
\par
and mix of energy prices. The equita-
\par
ble balancing of income distribution ef-
\par
fects is a central problem for US poli-
\par
cy. Some day modelers may have some-
\par
thing of value to contribute to the de-
\par
bate, but so far we have contributed
\par
preciou3 little.
