Energy and Security
\par
Intimately related to the economics of
\par
world oil pricing are the political and
\par
defense problems of energy and securi-
\par
ty. The events of 1979 reinforced our
\par
awareness of the dangerous state of the
\par
world oil supply system. The central
\par
problem of oil imports is the problem
\par
of security; the vulnerability to sup-
\par
ply interruptions is a great threat to
\par
our economic and political security.
\par
We now recognize that import reductions
\par
will not be sufficient to remove this
\par
threat. The potential for oil emergen-
\par
cies will be with us for the indefinite
\par
future. It is critical that we develop
\par
new characterizations of the oil mar-
\par
ket, with new policies for the United
\par
States and other countries to prepare
\par
to meet supply interruptions.
\par
What are the costs of measure supply
\par
interruptions? How do these costs dif-
\par
fer for different oil importing coun-
\par
tries? What policies might mitigate
\par
the size or impact of supply interrup-
\par
tions? What international cooperative
\par
measures could be adopted and enforced
\par
to meet the challenges of energy vul-
\par
nerability? The oil importing coun-
\par
tries must develop answers to these
\par
questions. And modelers must urgently
\par
provide the tools needed to help pro-
\par
duce and explain the answers.
