ENERGY MODEL COMPARISON
\par
During this evolution of energy policy
\par
models and policy studies, there has
\par
been a parallel development of insti-
\par
tutions and techniques for model veri-
\par
fication, validation, and ventilation.
\par
Verification is the demonstration that
\par
a model works as intended, that
\par
coding and data errors have been eli-
\par
minated. Validation is the demonstra-
\par
tion that a model reflects the es-
\par
sence of the system modeled, that the
\par
model predicts well. Vpntilation is
\par
the explanation of how a model
\par
works, the exposure of critical as-
\par
sumptions and structure sufficient to
\par
make the model transparent to the
\par
scrutiny of the user.
\par
Model developers and model users have
\par
been working on the ventilation phase
\par
to make large-scale energy models more
\par
accessible to policy-makers, and, in
\par
the process, to improve the quality of
\par
the models. These efforts at ventila-
\par
tion, at building bridges between the
\par
model developers and users, have been
\par
most visible in the work of the Energy
\par
Modeling Forum, the newly formed Uti-
\par
lity Modeling Forum, and the MIT Model
\par
Assessment Laboratory. In their own
\par
ways, these groups analyze the content
\par
of models and how the models might
\par
be used to improve policy making. A
\par
clear lesson of these studies is the
\par
importance of focusing on the policy
\par
decisions, the policy questions to be
\par
addressed with the model. No model
\par
can deal with all questions about any
\par
system; few questions would not yield
\par
a little to some modeling. And some
\par
problems can be dealt with only
\par
through models.
\par
As an example of an extreme case where
\par
models will be indispensable, but be-
\par
yond the reach of validation, consider
\par
the decision we now face regarding
\par
the disposal of waste from nuclear
\par
power plants. It is clear that this
\par
decision is going to be based on a
\par
model of how the depository will
\par
operate over thousands of years. No
\par
one is going to conduct an experiment
\par
of this process; more likely, lawyers
\par
in a courtroom will decide whether or
\par
not some particular technical model of
\par
the process is superior to another.
\par
Those lawyers are going to have to
\par
understand some of the technical de-
\par
tails; they will also have to
\par
