\par
understand how the model fits into peo-
\par
ple's perceptions of the problem; and
\par
they will have to explain their judg-
\par
ment to many people who know and care
\par
little about the details of large-scale
\par
modeling. Much of the work now under
\par
way to develop our ability to ventilate
\par
models will help in this and more pro-
\par
saic applications of energy models.
\par
One of the success stories of energy
\par
modeling is this gradual improvement
\par
in our ability to make energy models
\par
accessible and useful.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
Energy is pervasive in our economies.
\par
It affects all that we do. From the
\par
perspective of the economic and poli-
\par
tical policy-makers, we have seen over
\par
the last seven years energy emerge from
\par
the attention of a few specialists to
\par
attention at the center of public and
\par
private decisions. Everyone now feels
\par
obliged to be knowledgeable about ener-
\par
gy policy and energy analysis and, in-
\par
directly, energy modeling. From the
\par
perspective of the energy analyst, the
\par
changes of the last several years have
\par
moved energy problems to the core of
\par
the most difficult analytical issues
\par
confronting society. In large measure,
\par
energy policy analyses merge with the
\par
study of inflation, macro-economic
\par
management, income distribution, and
\par
the balance of trade. With the possi-
\par
bility of continued disruptions of oil
\par
markets, we find energy modelers
\par
wrestling with the problems of defense
\par
and foreign policy. While there will
\par
always be some special features, energy
\par
policy modeling will be more and more
\par
just policy modeling; the new
\par
challenges are far greater than the old.
\par
We have a new perception of the criti-
\par
cal issues as we anticipate the
\par
eighties. We will see energy policy
\par
analysts addressing less stable, less
\par
long-term, more dramatic issues dealing
\par
with energy and security, market mani-
\par
pulations, and international interde-
\par
pendence. Energy models have helped us
\par
organize our understanding of the many
\par
choices we have over the long run.
\par
Modelers must now turn their attention
\par
to describing the dangers and opportu-
\par
nities that are here today.
\par
