\par
ABSTRACT
\par
Different levels of energy demand are described, and the notion of negentropy is
\par
introduced to clarify the distinction between the thermodynemic conservation of
\par
energy and the consumption of highly-ordered (high quality) forms of energy. The
\par
substitutability of energy, labor, capital, and know-how in producing services is
\par
discussed. The actual mix that we use depends more on our culture and its evolu-
\par
tion than on physics. Three different approaches to assessing energy demand are
\par
described, the econometric approach, the scenario approach, and an approach based
\par
on projected settlement patterns and associated energy consumption densities. Fi-
\par
nally, some of the more salient insights gained from IIASA's set of global energy
\par
models are presented. Of particular interest are the economic implications of an
\par
extreme energy conservation strategy where primary energy requirements reach only
\par
16 TWyr/yr in 2030.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Developed regions; developing regions; energy conservation; global energy demand;
\par
long-range energy demand; negentropy.
\par
THE NATURE OF ENERGY DEMAND
\par
Energy demand is a hotly-debated subject these days. When exploring related prob-
\par
lems, it is most important to first clarify terms.
\par
One must distinguish between primary, secondary, final and useful energy, as is
\par
illustrated by Fig. 1. Primary energy, for example, is crude oil or coal. In order
\par
to obtain a readily usable form of secondary energy, a conversion process is needed,
\par
such as takes place in refineries or in electrical power stations. Gasoline at the
\par
mouth of a pipeline or electricity at the bus bar of a grid are such secondary energy
\par
forms. At the place of consumption, that is, after this energy has been transported
\par
to the user, it becomes final energy. Gasoline at the filling station or electricity
\par
at the plug are examples. Part of that final energy is converted into useful
\par
in end use facilities, such as a car or a washing machine. This is the mechanical
\par
energy in the car or the heat acting on the laundry in the washing machine.
