It is well known that energy cannot be consumed. Indeed, as indicated in Fig. 1,
\par
all conversion losses and waste heat add up; and the total rejected energy exactly
\par
equals the primary energy input to the process. What is then consumed is the qual-
\par
ity that goes along with energy, i.e., its negative entropy increments, for which
\par
no law of conservation holds. We call this negentropy. Engaging energy services
\par
by availing oneself of a warm room or a readable book to consume negentropy.
\par
It is useful to reflect on the fact that services created by the use of energy are
\par
balanced by services that are derived from the use of capital, labor, and know-how.
\par
It is know-how that is required, for example, when switching from a steam locomotive
\par
to a Diesel engine, by which some 80\% of the energy requirement of the steam engine
\par
can be saved. The mutual substitutability of services is a notable general phenom-
\par
enon.\_ The notion of services, as it is addressed here, is a very abstract and im-
\par
material quantity. One may relate these services to a generalized form of informa-
\par
tion, referring to them possibly as order states (Energy Systems Program Group of
\par
llASA, 1980a).
\par
The way in which the services derived from energy, capital, labor and know-how are
\par
actually divided among themselves is a matter of contingency. That is to say, the
\par
partitioning is not the outcome of a physical process, but reflects a certain state
\par
- 31 -
\par
in the intellectual, cultural and societal evolution. It is for this reason that,
\par
in the final analysis, energy demand and consumption are political issues, in the
\par
most profound and best sense of the word.
\par
Evolution takes time. Yes, energy conservation is possible; it is not an invention
\par
of our time but is observed to have occurred also in earlier centuries, and at a
\par
stunning regularity. This is exemplified by Fig. 2, which gives the second law
\par
efficiency for three specific conversion processes in a logarithmic scale. Note
\par
that the time scale extends from 1700 to 2000 and beyond.
