THREE APPROACHES TO ASSESSING ENERGY DEMAND
\par
If we assume that the services derived from energy, capital, labor and know-how are
\par
mutually substitutable we expect that a common denominator between these services
\par
exists. But there is no strictly thermodynamic nor a more general physical concep-
\par
tion of such a denominator, and so money has taken on this role. This works in the
\par
case of a perfect equilibrium between supply and demand, where the mere fact that
\par
such an equilibrium exists serves as a translator. There, the many informational
\par
dimensions of a real-world market situation in a given society, characterized by a
\par
certain culture and technology, are translated into a one-dimensional scalar, i.e.,
\par
the relative price.
\par
