\par
Inherent to this approach is the regression to elasticities, the idea that elastic-
\par
ities change more slowly than the actual variables of the demand function. In most
\par
cases, this assumption is fair for a limited time period. For longer time horizons,
\par
say five years or beyond, it is not appropriate to assume a relative constancy of
\par
elasticities, especially if the infrastructure underlying the balance of demand and
\par
supply is changing or is about to be changed. In this case, one has to go back to
\par
accounting of energy end uses by simulation, in order to obtain the information
\par
otherwise available from elasticities and prices. This is done by accounting of
\par
energy end uses in physical and engineering terms.
\par
The procedure works for the scenario writing approach, a way of simulating future
\par
economic conditions and lifestyles as they are conceived. The total demand for
\par
energy resulting from the scenarios can be accounted for in this fashion. It is
\par
generally felt that such an approach permits one to cover wider time horizons, of
\par
perhaps as many as 50 years. Here the constraining problem is internal consistency.
\par
It is possible to force consistency in by way of an input-output procedure, but this
\par
presupposes knowledge of how the input-output coefficients evolve over time and how
\par
to make them consistent.
\par
At IIASA we have followed such an approach (Lapillonne., 1978) to a great extent, as
\par
is illustrated by Fig. 5. Our initial assumptions involve economic growth rates,
\par
economic structures and lifestyles. Another set of assumptions relates to tech-
\par
nical factors, on the basis of which various end uses in the private, commercial
\par
and industrial sectors can be identified in detail, e.g., the heating requirements
\par
of a dwelling, the number of persons per car, person-kilometers, kilowatt-hours re-
\par
quired per dollar of value added in various industrial sectors, etc. The connection
\par
between energy end uses and final energy is made via technological efficiencies,
\par
which then close the link to the various kinds of primary energy.
