A case in point is low temperature uses of solar power. For bigger energy systems,
\par
and not just for certain single cases, it is largely the rate of introduction of
\par
such a new technology that matters. This is often referred to as a question of mar-
\par
ket penetration (Marchetti and Nakicenovic, 1979). The analysis leads to
\par
a menu of final energy demands, some of which appear mutually substitutable
\par
while others are not easily replaced.
\par
Only a few words need to be said about a possible third approach that might be taken
\par
to assess energy demand. It is radically different from the first two since it con-
\par
siders evaluations in space and not in time; it centers around the observation that
\par
the energy consumption density in urban areas is fairly constant. Compare Table 1
\par
(Energy Systems Program Group of IIASA, 1980b), which shows that the population
\par
densities in different places in the world vary widely--that of New York is half
\par
that of London--but the energy consumption densities are almost the same. Even for
\par
cities in India, with a population density ten times higher than that in New York,
\par
the energy density differs only by a factor of two from the U.S. city. It seems to
\par
be the use of more urban space per person that leads to a higher per capita energy
\par
consumption. The idea is, then, to consider present or future settlement patterns
\par
in order to judge the related energy demand. At IIASA, we have pursued this approach
\par
only in one respect (Hafele, 1979), also because, obviously, more basic research is
\par
needed to make this method operational and successful. But we think it relevant as
\par
a third approach.
