EXTREME ENERGY CONSERVATION
\par
We observed earlier that the actual partitioning of services derived from energy,
\par
capital, labor and know-how is of a contingent nature and not an outcome of a phys-
\par
ical process. Therefore, it is possible in principle to conceive scenarios of ex-
\par
treme energy conservation. Balancing supply and demand can be achieved also in
\par
rather extreme ways, either by going extremely to the supply end, which means facing
\par
all the troubles of enhanced supply, or by going extremely to the demand end, which
\par
means facing all the troubleS of enhanced conservation. The question is, then, what
\par
is extreme?
\par
In our IIASA studies, we therefore conceived a so-called 16 TW scenario, based on a
\par
proposal by Colombo and Bernardini (1979). 16 terawatts by the year 2030 means
\par
that the 8 billion people then assumed to live would have 2 kWyr/yr per person, the
\par
same global per capita average as today. Any increase in the per capita energy
\par
demand in developing countries would therefore require decreases in the demand in
\par
developed countries. It is along such lines that, for the year 2030, target values
\par
of primary energy of 8 kWyr/yr,cap in Region I and 3.2 kWyr/yr,cap in Region III
\par
were obtained. The corresponding 1975 values are 11.2 kWyr/yr,cap in Region I, and
\par
4.0 kWyr/yr,cap in Region III.
\par
It is crucial that such decreases be made consistent with the assumed positive GOP
\par
growth of 2\%/year. To that end, a number of assumptions had to be made, each of
\par
which is somewhat arbitrary, to ensure that the overall brackets of decrease in
\par
energy demand and of increase in GOP are met. Such assumptions relate to the struc-
\par
ture of an economy, as well as to the lifestyle.
\par
For the Low scenario, only a slight adjustment to the assumed GOP growth rate of
\par
2\%/year was necessary. The situation is entirely different for the 16 TW scenario.
\par
In Table 6 such major assumptions are compared with those of the Low case. It
