\par
ESTIMATION AND PROJECTION OF ENERGY
\par
DEMAND IN DEVELOPING COUNTRIES:
\par
CURRENT STATE AND MAJOR ISSUES
\par
B. J. Choe*
\par
Economist, The World Bank
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
Estimation and projection of developing countries' energy demand must cope with the
\par
problems of inadequate data and rapid changes in the underlying economic structure.
\par
Existing studies showed that income elasticity of total energy demand in
\par
countries is substantially greater than unity, and price elasticity is somewhat
\par
smaller than those obtained for industrialized countries. The studies also found
\par
that the manufacturing and transportation sectors require more rapid increases in
\par
energy consumption for a percentage increase in value-added than other sectors. As
\par
additional data become available, the important linkage between energy and economic
\par
development as well as structural changes should be investigated on a sounder basis
\par
than hitherto possible.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy demand; developing countries; econometric estimation; income and price elas-
\par
ticities.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
The purpose of this paper is to review the current state of estimating and projecting
\par
the demand for energy in developing countries, and also to discuss the important re-
\par
lated issues. Although a stock-taking may be premature at this stage, it may never-
\par
theless put the studies of developing countries' energy demand in a proper perspec-
\par
tive in relation to the progress made in this area for industrialized countries.
\par
* The views and interpretations in this paper are those of the author and should not
\par
be attributed to the World Bank or its affiliated organizations.
\par
- 45 -
\par
- 46 -
\par
The issues important for developing countries need to be thought through, so that
\par
the approaches taken so far can be evaluated in the context of developing countries.
\par
Unavailability or low quality of energy data are the major drawback in estimation
\par
and projection of developing countries' energy demand. The data problems are par-
\par
ticularly severe in the important areas like non-commercial energy, energy efficiency,
\par
sectoral demand and interfuel substitution. Significant efforts are being made to
\par
expand and improve the data base at the international and national levels.
\par
Within the scope of the available data, several studies have attempted to estimate
\par
the demand elasticities. Choe (1978 and 1979) estimated total commercial energy
\par
demand equations, alternatively assuming geometric and polynomial lags. Pindyck
\par
(1979) estimated geometric lag fuel demand equations for a limited number of develop-
\par
ing countries. Parikh (1978) estimated total energy demand equations (commercial
\par
plus non-commercial) with cross-section data, but left out the price variable.
\par
Hoffmann's (1979) on-going study attempts to separate out the impact of structural
\par
changes on energy demand.
\par
In the following sections, we critically review these studies, beginning with the
\par
studies of non-commercial energy. It is followed by the aggregate econometric
\par
studies of commercial energy demand. The fourth section deals with structural
\par
changes and sectoral demands for energy. The last section is devoted to conclusions
\par
and areas for future research.
\par
NON-COMMERCIAL ENERGY 1
\par
The fact that non-commercial energy still accounts for a large share of total
\par
primary energy consumption in many developing countries has prompted a number of
\par
studies in this area. A recent study by Hughart (1979) provides a useful summary
\par
of the state of this subject.
\par
Available data on non-commercial energy are limited, sketchy and often unreliable.
\par
The presently available data consist of the FAO data on firewood and charcoal,
\par
survey data of rural areas in several countries, and those indirectly constructed
\par
on the basis of crop production, forestry area and the number of animals. A sig-
\par
nificant part of the FAO data are also indirect estimates. These data are mostly
\par
cross sectional; time-series data, even if available, are usually not long enough
\par
for meaningful time-series analyses.
\par
The studies of non-commercial energy are largely aimed at identifying the nature
\par
and extent of non-commercial energy usage in developing countries. Despite the
\par
deficiencies of the data, the studies clearly indicate that non-commercial energy
\par
accounts for 20\% to 90\% of all primary energy consumption depending on the countries,
\par
mostly for household energy needs in the traditional rural areas. In the traditional
\par
setting, non-commercial energy is obtained and consumed as a part of delicate
\par
agrarian ecosystems. Examples abound that these systems are under severe strains
\par
in many parts of the developing world as rural energy needs begin to exceed the
\par
natural regeneration capacity of these systems. Efficiency characteristics of
\par
1 The term "non-commercial" is used here to represent such traditional sources as
\par
firewood, agricultural residues and animal power and dung, irrespective of whether
\par
they are commercially traded or not.
\par
- 47 -
\par
non-commercial energy usage are not well known. Generally, the traditional comb-
\par
ustion and utilization facilities are rudimentary, which normally result in subs-
\par
tantial heat losses. Modes of utilization and associated efficiencies, however,
\par
should vary widely across countries and localities.
\par
The dynamic aspect of non-commercial energy consumption, despite its obvious impor-
\par
tance, has not received much attention in the literature. Available intertemporal
\par
data (e.g., for India and Brazil) show dramatic declines in the share of non-com-
\par
mercial energy in total primary energy consumption. Rapid growth of the modern
\par
sector vis-a-vis stagnation of the traditional sector could account for the most
\par
of these changes, rather than penetration of commercial energy into rural areas to
\par
replace non-commercial energy. Transition from non-commercial to commercial energy
\par
should be understood as a part of the entire socio-economic transformation process
\par
of a traditional society.
\par
One can conceive of the demand for primary energy in a poor rural area as being
\par
determined primarily by the number of population; energy satisfies one of the basic
\par
human needs. Initially, practically all these needs would be met by non-commercial
\par
energy. Increases in rural population, however, would put pressure on the rural
\par
ecosystems and make it increasingly difficult to gather non-commercial energy. As
\par
rural incomes increase beyond the subsistence level, energy needs will increase and
\par
so will the opportunity cost of labor for collection of non-commercial energy.
\par
These developments have typically required a gradual transition from non-commercial
\par
to commercial energy. At the basic needs level, most rural population cannot afford
\par
commercial energy; when commercial energy is introduced as a matter of government
\par
policy, heavy subsidies are often required.
\par
In the absence of meaningful time-series data on rural energy consumption, the dy-
\par
namics can be gleaned from cross-country analyses of rural energy consumption, rural
\par
income and prices of commercial energy. The role of rural income as an explanatory
\par
variable of rural energy consumption is two-fold: it can be a proxy for the oppor-
\par
tunity cost of labor needed to obtain non-commercial energy, in addition to being
\par
the usual income variable in any demand functions. In areas where rural income
\par
increases fast and consumption of non-commercial energy is close to the limits of
\par
natural regeneration, the true (social) cost of non-commercial energy may be high
\par
and increasing very rapidly. Normally, this process led to penetration of commercial
\par
energy into the rural areas. It is a matter of great uncertainty how this process
\par
will develop in the future when the costs of commercial energy are already high and
\par
will increase further. One direction which could be pursued is to improve the
\par
efficiency of non-commercial energy usage and to harness the locally available non-
\par
conventional energy sources (wind, solar, small hydro), all at some costs. This
\par
course is likely to take a long time and much depends on the location-specific
\par
circumstances.
\par
AGGREGATE ECONOMETRIC STUDIES AND ELASTICITIES
\par
Although the available data on commercial energy are in a much better shape than
\par
those of non-commercial energy, quantitative analyses of developing countries' com-
\par
mercial energy consumption suffer from the lack of adequate data on several key
\par
variables. These are data on sectoral consumption, prices to the final users of
\par
various forms of energy and efficiency characteristics of energy-using capital stock
\par
in developing countries. Studies of developing countries' commercial energy con-
\par
sumption, so far, have been concerned with: (a) estimation of total commercial energy
\par
demand as a function of gross domestic product (GDP) and energy prices; (b) esti-
\par
mation of individual fuel demand equations; (c) attempts to estimate the effect of
\par
- 48 -
\par
structural changes on energy demand; (d) use of input-output tables to determine
\par
sectoral output and energy demand. In this section we look at the first two classes
\par
of studies.
\par
The common approach of the aggregative studies is to relate total energy consumption
\par
with GDP, energy prices and population, using time-series and/or cross-section data
\par
of these variables. When time series data are involved, the demand function is
\par
usually expressed in a dynamic form. Choe (1978 and 1979) estimated the following
\par
two dynamic forms on pooled time-series of cross-sections data of 40 developing
\par
countries for the 1960-75 period:
\par
