\par
where i and t are country and year subscripts respectively, E represents per capita
\par
energy consumption, Y is per capita GDP and P is energy price index, all expressed
\par
in natural logarithms.
\par
The two specifications above differ in the way demand adjusts over time to higher
\par
prices. The geometric lag specification (equation A) posits a dynamic adjustment
\par
process in which the impact on demand of a price increase declines geometrically as
\par
time goes on. The fact that energy is consumed through utilization of capital
\par
equipments suggests that higher energy prices will initially affect the capacity
\par
utilization rate and subsequently induce modification and replacement of capital
\par
stock, which normally takes a long time. The geometric lag distribution puts an
\par
implausible prior restriction on the relative magnitude of these adjustments. The
\par
geometric lag specification also assumes a symmetric time path of adjustments to
\par
income and price changes. The nature of adjustments to income could be entirely
\par
different from that to prices. The polynomial distributed lag model (equation B),
\par
on the other hand, assumes instantaneous adjustments to income changes and can
\par
accommodate any time path of adjustments to price changes. One problem, however,
\par
is that the time path of adjustments can hardly be elicited from short time-series
\par
observations usually available for developing countries.
\par
The models A and B above produced about the same estimate of the income elasticity;
\par
the long-run income elasticities estimated from model A average to about 1.3, which
\par
is practically the same as the estimate of the instantaneous income elasticity of
\par
model B. Model A, however, produced an average estimate of the long-run price elas-
\par
ticity of about -0.3, whereas model B results indicate a somewhat higher long-run
\par
price elasticity, in the neighborhood of -0.4 to -0.5. Model B results also indicate
\par
a time path of adjustments to prices which resembles a second degree polynomial;
\par
demand adjustments to price changes are small in the years immediately following
\par
the price change, gradually reaches a peak in 3 to 5 years and then tapers off after
\par
that.
\par
A number of problems, however, are embedded in such estimates, and those problems
\par
become acute when the estimates are used for projection purposes. Perhaps the most
\par
important is to understand the nature of the substantially higher-than-unitary esti-
\par
mate of income elasticity. It implies that developing countries have been shifting
\par
towards more energy-intensive economic activities and/or that technological changes
\par
have been energy-using. Since we have the energy price variable in the equations,
\par
these changes, to the extent reflected in the estimated income elasticity, must in
\par
principle have been occurring autonomously, that is, without being induced by energy
\par
- 49 -
\par
price changes. In practice, however, the weaknesses of the data, particularly the
\par
energy price data, and other statistical problems could produce a less-than-satis-
\par
factory separation of income and price effects. The question is to what extent it
\par
is in the nature of development process that economic structure shift toward more
\par
energy-intensive sectors.
\par
Projecting developing countries' energy demand on the basis of historical elastici-
\par
ties as estimated by the equations assumes in broad terms a continuation of the past
\par
trends in structural and technological changes. This is a sweeping assumption which
\par
may well turn out to be unwarranted. A way of approaching to this problem is to
\par
associate the nature and speed of structural changes to the stages of economic de-
\par
velopment. Income elasticities could be estimated separately for subgroups of
\par
developing countries, classified according to certain criteria. Stratification by
\par
per capita income, geographic regions, speed of industrial growth, etc., however,
\par
often do not produce the desired results. This may be taken as an indication that
\par
multiple criteria are required to adequately explain the differences in income elas-
\par
ticity.
\par
Estimates of price elasticities for industrialized countries showed a wide range of
\par
values and sometimes conflicting results. A priori reasoning does not tell whether
\par
the price elasticity of developing countries should be greater or smaller than that
\par
of industrialized countries. In household consumption of energy, the scope for
\par
"doing without" would be much less in developing countries than in developed coun-
\par
tries. However, the fact that developing countries have relatively small energy
\par
consuming capital stock would make it easier to adjust the total capital stock
\par
composition to changes in relative factor prices.
\par
Another important area of concern is the extent of energy conservation, price-
\par
induced or otherwise, which may be expected in developing countries. Efficiency
\par
characteristics and conservation potentials in these countries are not clear at this
\par
stage. Energy conservation has not yet received much emphasis in these countries,
\par
perhaps because of the fact that the level of energy consumption is low enough not
\par
to arouse concern in many countries. More fundamentally, the usually high cost of
\par
capital in developing countries could make conservation investments less economic
\par
than otherwise, and materials used in conservation investments (e.g., insulation
\par
materials) are often not available domestically. Research and development of new
\par
conservation technologies largely are beyond the capabilities of most developing
\par
countries. Developing countries' conservation policies relied primarily on domestic
\par
pricing measures. Mandatory energy conservation in the industrial and transportation
\par
sectors faces the problems of capital and technology mentioned above. Household
\par
energy consumption in the traditional and urban areas may be amenable to improvements
\par
in efficiency, by changing the modes of cooking and heating without requiring much
\par
investments, but the traditional way of life may be challenged in the process.
\par
Pindyck (1979) provides examples of estimating the demand for petroleum products in
\par
selected industrializing developing countries. Demand for a petroleum product is
\par
specified as a function of its own price, GDP, and the lagged demand variable, His
\par
estimates, although limited in coverage, show some interesting features. Generally.
\par
he finds that the residential and transportation demands for petroleum products are
\par
highly income elastic but price inelastic, compared with similar estimates for indus-
\par
trialized countries. On the other hand, the demand for heavy fuel oil, presumably
\par
the industrial sector, shows relatively low income elasticity but high price elas-
\par
ticity. Kerosene is found to be an inferior good (negative income elasticity) and
\par
price inelastic. The high price elasticity of the demand for heavy fuel oil is
\par
attributed to the availability of low-cost labor as a substitute for heavy fuel oil
\par
in the industrial sector of developing countries.
\par
- 50 -
\par
Pindyck's estimates can hardly be taken as representative of developing countries,
\par
since only five "advanced" developing countries are used in the estimates. In those
\par
five countries, kerosene may well have become an inferior source of household energy.
\par
However, in most of the remaining developing countries, kerosene still is preferred
\par
to non-commercial energy. It is also unconvincing that low-cost labor is a subs-
\par
titute for heavy fuel oil, but not for gasoline and light fuel oil. As a whole, it
\par
appears that more work needs to be done in this area, expanding the coverage and
\par
focusing on key petroleum products like gasoline, for which required data are more
\par
readily available and the associated substitution problems are less demanding.
\par
STRUCTURAL CHANGES AND SECTORAL DEMAND
\par
As noted in the preceding section, rapid changes in the underlying economic struc-
\par
ture of developing countries are recognized as an important determinant of energy
\par
demand. In fact, the share of industrial output in GDP increased from 29\% in 1960
\par
to 35\% in 1975 in developing countries as a whole. A significant part of the high
\par
estimated income elasticity may be accounted for by the increasing share of indus-
\par
trial output and accompanying urbanization. Although the proper way of quantifying
\par
the effects of industrialization on energy demand would be to estimate the sectoral
\par
demand equations, formidable data problems have thus far prevented this direct
\par
approach. A compromise approach is being attempted by Hoffmann (1978). Another
\par
conventional approach is the usual input-output method. This section deals with
\par
these two approaches.
\par
Hoffmann attempts to estimate the effects of structural changes on energy demand,
\par
without relying on sectoral energy consumption data. It is done by specifying the
\par
demand for total commercial energy as a function of GDP, energy prices and the per-
\par
centage shares in GDP of the major energy consuming sectors. Hoffmann uses four
\par
sectoral share variables: agriculture, mining and construction, manufacturing and
\par
electricity, and transport. The energy consumption and GDP variables are sometimes
\par
expressed in the per capita term.
\par
A fundamental assumption in Hoffmann's approach is that sectoral energy demand
\par
equations can be aggregated to a total energy demand equation, which resembles the
\par
form estimated by him. When sectoral energy demand equations are assumed to be
\par
linear functions of sectoral GDP and energy prices, it can be shown that total
\par
energy demand can be expressed as a linear function of sectoral GDP and energy prices.
\par
The identity that total GDP equals the sum of sectoral GDP can be used to replace
\par
one sectoral GDP variable by total GDP. The coefficient of the total GDP
\par
is then interpreted as representing the sector left out in the total demand
\par
equation. Aggregation of sectoral demand equations is not feasible when the demand
\par
equations are assumed to be non-linear. Hoffmann uses the log-linear form. The
\par
interpretations above in the linear case, therefore, would not apply to Hoffmann's
\par
log-linear form. However, we may at least presume that the log-linear model would
\par
have a reasonably close interpretation to that of the linear model.
\par
Preliminary results obtained by Hoffmann suggest that sectoral shifts have been an
\par
important determinant of total energy demand in developing countries. He finds that
\par
the GDP share of agriculture almost invariably has a negative relationship with total
\par
energy consumption, whereas the opposite is the case for the GDP shares of manufac-
\par
turing/electricity and transportation. The estimates for the GDP share of mining
\par
and construction are mixed, that is, sometimes positive and sometimes negative. Of
\par
interest are the surprisingly large positive coefficients for transportation, compared
\par
with those for manufacturing and electricity. The total GDP coefficients vary widely
\par
between the groups of developing countries, probably because of its interpretation
\par
as the coefficient of the left-out sector rather than GDP itself.
\par
- 51 -
\par
It apparently is not an easy task to elicit the implications of sectoral shifts
\par
without going directly into estimating sectoral demands. Apart from the aggregation
\par
problem mentioned above, there is the statistical problem of multicollinearity
\par
between the sectoral share variables, particularly between the manufacturing and
\par
transportation shares. It is also likely to be the case that the peculiar charac-
\par
teristics of energy consumption in different sectors call for different specification
\par
of the demand equation for each sector. One advantage of the input-output methodology
\par
is that it works in terms of greater sectoral details than the econometric approach.
\par
The input-output approach, however, also suffers from a serious problem: developing
\par
countries undergo rapid structural changes and also must adjust to higher energy
\par
prices. The input-output coefficients should be modified accordingly. To do this,
\par
the input-output coefficients can be related to relative prices. The relationships
\par
must come from econometric evidence. Another method of coping with this problem
\par
is to allow for a multitude of activities (input-output vectors) for each product
\par
or service, and let the model choose the optimal set of vectors. In either case,
\par
data problems effectively limit their applicability to only a few developing coun-
\par
tries for the near future.
\par
CONCLUSIONS
\par
The task of estimating and projecting energy demand in developing countries faces
\par
the twin problems of coping with rapid structural changes and limited data avail-
\par
ability, Existing studies are in fact largely a reflection of the data constraints.
\par
The studies conducted so far have indicated the broad range of aggregate income and
\par
price elasticities of energy demand, the effects of structural shifts on energy con-
\par
sumption and the demand elasticities for some of the individual fuels.
\par
Progress is being made to widen the energy data base of developing countries. The
\par
International Energy Agency (1978) has compiled the sectoral consumption data for
\par
16 developing countries, and the UN is also working on this area. The sectoral con-
\par
sumption data, combined with appropriate sectoral energy price data, will open up
\par
an important new area for research. The issues of structural changes and interfuel
\par
substitution can be addressed more meaningfully at the sectoral level.
\par
Price elasticities and energy conservation are the subjects which demand innovative
\par
approaches as well as more data. The low level of capital stock accumulation in
\par
developing countries makes it important to look at the conservation issue as a part
\par
of future investment policies. In this connection, the vintage capital approach
\par
would be more relevant to developing countries than to industrialized countries.
\par
Within the existing data, further analysis of cross-country differences in energy
\par
consumption seems desirable. Cross-country comparisons could focus on specific
\par
energy consuming sectors such as iron and steel, gasoline consumption for transport-
\par
ation and residential consumption of electricity. Additional data on non-commercial
\par
energy are likely to be hard to obtain. Sample surveys, however, can often provide
\par
an adequate basis for planning. Cross-country analysis of non-commercial energy
\par
with the existing data, despite all the deficiencies, could produce interesting in-
\par
sights into the relationship between rural energy demand, rural income and commercial
\par
energy prices.
