\par
ABSTRACT
\par
The gop model is an input-output one, driven by final demand for 24 different
\par
commodities including 4 fuels, and contains mechanisms to deal with fuel mix changes,
\par
technical progress in the use of energy and price-induced capital energy substitution,
\par
together with special demand sub-models of household heat consumption and car use
\par
and purchase. This paper describes in some detail the domestic heating sub-model
\par
and the constraints affecting the purchase of domestic conservation measures. As an
\par
illustrative example, a comparison is made between two projection runs, identical
\par
except that all 4 fuel prices are constant in the control run, and double linearly
\par
over the projection period in the second run. The aim of the experiment is not
\par
only to consider the total effect on domestic heat consumption but also to analyse
\par
the net savings into the contributions from reduced levels of comfort and increased
\par
use of conse-vation and to examine their relative shares over time.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
EP-ergy demand model; domestic conservation; behavioural constraints; contributory
\par
factors to net savings.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Part of the philosophy behind the EDP model is to investigate how people actually do
\par
respond to conservation signals such as fuel price increases and subsidies on the
\par
costs of conservation measures, to build a model of this behaviour and then to check
\par
how variations in particular signals affect the total demand for primary energy.
\par
Data for the UK over the 1958-1977 were analysed to determine parameter
\par
values, so the model may be described as a "behaviour as usual" one in that people's
\par
response in the future to eg fuel price increases is assumed to be the same as that
\par
in the past; however, larger increases will produce larger effects in general so
\par
the epithet "business as usual" does not apply, except for one scenario.
\par
