\par
Another feature of the real world is the number of interconnections
\par
which can produce various secondary and "knock-on" effects, and occasionally lead to
\par
counter-intuitive results. An important example is the set of constraints arlslng
\par
from the fact that at any time, consumers' disposable income is both fixed and dis-
\par
posed. Accordingly, increased fuel prices not only produce a reduction in fuel con-
\par
sumed directly but also cause expenditure on other goods and services to drop, lead-
\par
ing ultimately to lower industrial demand for energy as well.
\par
GENERAL MODEL STRUCTURE
\par
In order to maintain proper accounting procedures, the model is constructed around
\par
a 24-sector input-output table; this is used directly in the calculation of prices,
\par
and, via its Leontief inverse, in the calculation of gross output from the set of
\par
final demands. The block diagram in Fig. 1 shows the main mechanisms of the model
\par
and how they are linked together.
\par
For each of the. 24 industrial sectors there is a model of technical change, both
\par
autonomous and price induced; the former is driven endogenously by{$\cdot$}an experience
\par
factor, represented by the cumulative production of the sector in question. Each
\par
sector also has a market share model for the 4 fuels (solid, liquid, gas and electri-
\par
city) driven by their prices and the cumulative production term again.
\par
In the domestic fuel mix model, cumulative production is replaced by (disposable)
\par
income per household. Technical improvement in domestic heating appliances is
\par
treated exogenously while capital energy substitution involving each of the conserva-
\par
tion measures is triggered by the cost effective criteria being satisfied in each
\par
case, with the rate of uptake dependent upon income per capita, payback period and
\par
initial cost of the capital. Household demand for most of the 24 commodities
\par
(including the 4 fuels) is dealt with by a set of income and price elasticities
\par
with special sub models for domestic energy consumption (excluding appliance
\par
electricity) and for purchases and use of private cars. In these sub models, the
\par
effects of possible demand saturation are considered.
\par
A simple price model makes allowance for changes in labour productivity in each of
\par
the non-fuel sectors while the effects of one price upon another are mediated by the
\par
input output table. The whole model is demand driven with the main exogenous vari-
\par
ables being GDP, fuel prices (both wholesale and retail), purChase-type taxes,
\par
popUlation, number of households, trade and the ratio of primary to secondary
\par
production of each fuel.
\par
Details of each of these mechanisms are given by Danskin (1979) and a comprehensive
\par
description of the model and its background may be found in SARU (1980).
\par
In the remainder of this paper, the domestic heating model is explained in detail;
\par
then follows a description of a simple experiment to demonstrate how various factors
\par
contribute to the net reduction in demand for domestic heating when fuel prices are
\par
increased.
\par
