These results illustrate some of the effects which the model is designed to reveal.
\par
First of all, it appears that the extra sacrificing of some of the energy savings
\par
arising from the use of increased conservation measures is not trivial, but amounts
\par
to nearly 20\% of the potential total savings for the latter part of the projection
\par
period, or around 35PJ of primary energy in the year 2000. The other side of the
\par
coin, of course, is that the level of comfort in homes is correspondingly higher
\par
than would have been estimated without this effect, a useful point to remember
\par
when considering the possible depressing effects on the standard of living arising
\par
from fuel price increases.
\par
The large change over time in \_relative contribution of conservation measures
\par
to net savings highlights the problem of the long time constants involved in the
\par
domestic sector; however, because of the cumulative effect of conservation
\par
measures which allow comfort levels to be maintained with a lower energy input,
\par
conservation is important as a longer term investment. On the other hand,
\par
although reductions in desired comfort levels produce immediate energy savings, the
\par
reverse situation occurs just as fast; furthermore, as incomes rise, these
\par
depressed comfort levels will rise again with the net result that this particular
\par
factor merely delays the arrival of higher levels of energy demand.
\par
These results are specific not only to the comparison between doubling fuel prices
\par
and holding them constant but also to the basic scenario on which the experiment
\par
was carried out. For example, with much higher rates of growth of GDP, it might
\par
be expected that the share of savings from reduced comfort would be dominant for
\par
rather longer than in this case, although runs would have to be performed to check
\par
this. From a policy point of view, it might be useful to repeat this experiment
\par
on the same basic scenario but with the fuel price increases varying over some
\par
range in order to discover whether a threshold effect could exist whereby fuel
\par
price growth rates below a certain level produced negligible net savings over the
\par
period under consideration; alternatively, for very rapid growth of fuel prices,
\par
the secondary effects of the sacrificed savings could perhaps effectively cancel
\par
most of the original savings.
\par
Other policy issues on the domestic front which the could illustrate include
\par
the effects of subsidising conservation measures, the effects of producing the rise
\par
in fuel prices by means of a retail tax rather than by depletion in the energy
\par
sector, the different consequences for household expenditure and, ultimately,
\par
for national primary energy demand, of achieving the same household temperature
\par
profile against time by different means, e.g. different combinations of fuel taxes
\par
and conservation subsidies etc. In each case, there are further repercussions
\par
for national primary energy demand because consumer expenditure on other goods and
\par
services is altered; in addition, if there is an increase in the industrial price
\par
of fuels, the relative prices of other items change since the costs of energy
\par
intensive goods, e.g. chemicals, will rise faster than the average (although capital-
\par
energy substitution will reduce all energy intensities). As a result, the final
\par
demand for each commodity is altered, the gross output is different and the
\par
industrial demand for energy also changes. However, the strict accounting proce-
\par
dures enforced by the use of the input-output table allow the model to keep track
\par
of all these adjustments. Thus, the model is a convenient tool for carrying out
\par
"what-if" type experiments, the very kind which policy makers require.
