DEMAND FOR ENERGY IN MANUFACTURING:
\par
APPLICATIONS OF DYNAMIC MODELS OF
\par
FACTOR DEMAND TO U.S. AND CANADIAN
\par
DISAGGREGATED DATA
\par
M. Denny, M. Fuss and L. Waverman
\par
Institute for Policy Analysis, University of Toronto, Toronto, Canada
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
In this paper we utilize recent advances in the specification of dynamic models of
\par
the demand for energy to compare short run and long run responses in Canadian and
\par
U.S. manufacturing industries to energy prices shocks. We present short run and
\par
long run own and cross-price elasticities of demand for energy, labour, materials
\par
and capital for 18 2-digit Canadian and American manufacturing industries. Also
\par
contained in the empirical results are the speeds of adjustment between steady state
\par
equilibria. On average, it is estimated that about one-third of the ultimate adjust-
\par
ment occurs during the first year after the shock. In addition, we explore the
\par
nature of energy/capital and energy/labour complementarity or substitutability.
\par
Disaggregation by 2-digit industrial classification reveals considerable variation
\par
in the relevant production characteristics, so that no overall summary conclusions
\par
can be obtained. Complicating this situation is the fact that for a number of indus-
\par
tries, switching occurs in the complementarity/substitutability relationship as firms
\par
adjust from the short run to a new long run steady state equilibrium.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy demand; dynamic model; price elasticities; disaggregated data; energy/capital
\par
complementarity.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Many researchers have examined the nature of the demand for energy in the manufactur-
\par
ing sector. Knowledge of the characteristics of this demand for energy is important
\par
for many public policy decisions. For example, are energy and capital complements
\par
in production, so that energy conservation policies based on higher energy prices
\par
will lead to lower rates of capital accumulation? Most U.S. and Canadian studies of
\par
total manufacturing have indicated just such a complementarity relationship between
\par
energy and capital. l On the other hand, some international cross-section studies
\par
have indicated that there is a substitutability relationship between capital and
\par
1 For example, see Hudson and Jorgenson (1974), Berndt and Wood (1975) and Fuss
\par
(1977) {$\bullet$}
\par
- 61 -
\par
- 62 -
\par
energy in producing manufactured products2 that higher energy prices induce a sub-
\par
stitution of capital for energy generating greater investment and thus accelerating
\par
economic growth. Another important relationship is the cross-price elasticity
\par
between labour demand and energy prices. If energy and labour are substitutes as
\par
most studies have shown then higher energy prices will lead directly to an increase
\par
in employment. There are indirect effects to consider as well. Higher energy prices,
\par
if reflected in higher product prices reduce demand for the more energy intensive
\par
products thus affecting aggregate employment and investment.
\par
Most of the evidence to this point in time on the characteristics of the demand for
\par
energy in the manufacturing sector has emanated from research utilizing aggregate
\par
national manufacturing data estimated with static models of input demand. In this
\par
paper, we report the empirical results of two extensions to existing modeling of
\par
energy demand in the manufacturing sector - the use of disaggregated data by 2-digit
\par
industrial classification and the use of dynamic adjustment rather than static equili-
\par
brium models of factor demand. We show that these extensions add important informa-
\par
tion of the type required for the crucial policy decisions made necessary by the
\par
post 1973 changes in energy prices. We concentrate entirely on the production rela-
\par
tionships between energy and other inputs.
\par
THE MODEL
\par
The model used 3has been extensively described elsewhere, here we simply describe its
\par
main features. Explicit economic maximization principles are used in constructing
\par
a model of a firm's choice of input use. The parameters of this theoretical model
\par
are then estimated with past observations using econometric techniques. The dynamic
\par
model utilizes optimal control theory with a capital stock as the state variable and
\par
energy, labour, non-energy materials and investment as control variables. Utilizing
\par
an internal cost of adjustment model of capital accumulation, it is hypothesized
\par
that the objective of the firm is to minimize the present discounted costs of pro-
\par
ducing a flow of output. Included in production costs are the increasing marginal
\par
costs4of adjusting the capital stock along a path between two steady state equili-
\par
bria. The logic to the internal adjustment model is that as the firm attempts to
\par
grow more quickly, inputs must be increasingly devoted to investment activities
\par
instead of output production. This dynamic model contains a number of advances in
\par
modeling input demand: disequilibria in input markets is explicitly recognized, as
\par
spillovers between input demands are incorporated (i.e., if the firm is not employ-
\par
ing the desired capital stock because of adjustment problems, this disequilibrium
\par
in the capital market has important implications for the other input markets); the
\par
model involves a general equilibrium analysis of all factor demands simultaneously;
\par
the relationships between inputs can vary from the short to the long runs, i.e.,
\par
inputs can be substitutes in the short run and complements in the long run or vice
\par
versa.
\par
2 Examples include Griffin and Gregory (1976) and Pindyck (1979).
\par
3 The model was originally proposed by Fuss (1976) and has been used by Berndt,
\par
Fuss and Waverman (1977,1980), and Denny, Fuss and Waverman (1979). This present
\par
paper reports the basic empirical findings of the latter two reports. The theore-
\par
tical model developments have appeared as Berndt, Fuss and Waverman (1979), and
\par
Denny, Fuss and Waverman (1980).
\par
4 This conceptual model is based on the research of Treadway (1974).
\par
