\par
ESTIMATION AND RESULTS
\par
The dynamic model was estimated for 19 2-digit SIC manufacturing industries in Canada
\par
with data for the 1962-75 period and for 18 2-digit SIC manufacturing industries in
\par
the U.S. with data over the 1948-71 period. The Canadian data include regional
\par
as well as intertemporal observations, while the American data base consists of a
\par
time series of national aggregates.
\par
Dynamic Model Estimation with the U.S. Data
\par
In Table 1 we present a number of price elasticities of demand for 18 2-digit manu-
\par
facturing industries in the U.S .. The estimated cost function for each of these
\par
industries exhibit the correct curvature properties for cost minimization. In all
\par
of the 18 industries, the own-price elastiticies of demand are negative as implied
\par
by economic theory (columns 2 to 6, the percentage change in quantity used of input
\par
x with respect to a 1\% change in the price of x). The important elements for
\par
public policy are first, the magnitude of the long run price elasticities of demand;
\par
second, the differences between short and long run price elasticities of demand;
\par
and third, the cross-price elasticities of demand between labour and energy and
\par
capital and energy. Of the 72 long run own-price elasticities of demand, only nine
\par
are greater than unity indicating that input demand is inelastic in the long run.
\par
Long run energy own-price elasticities vary somewhat; in 11 of the 18 industries,
\par
this elasticity is less than -.5. Labour and capital own-price elasticities of
\par
demand appear to be slightly more elastic than those of energy, while the materials'
\par
response appears to be the least elastic.
\par
In all but one case there is very little difference between short run (one year) and
\par
long run energy own-price elasticities. This result is somewhat surprising for it
\par
implies that the firms are able to quickly adjust their energy demand to the level
\par
required. In the model, differences between long and short run own-price elastici-
\par
ties of demand for any variable input depend upon the absolute value of the elas-
\par
ticity of substitution between that input and capital. When energy and capital are
\par
neither highly substitutable nor highly complementary, the gradual adjustment of
\par
capital to its new equilibrium value has relatively little effect on the time path
\par
of energy demand. From column 7, we can see that the cross-price elasticities of
\par
demand between capital and energy are indeed small (the percentage change in demand
\par
for capital with respect to a 1\% change in the price of energy). There are much
\par
greater differences between short and long run own-price elasticities of demand for
\par
both labour and materials; which implies that the labour/capital and materials/
\par
capital elasticities of substitution are of more significance.
\par
In 14 of the 18 industries, the capital/energy cross-price elasticity is negative
\par
indicating that an increase in the price of energy leads to a reduction in the
\par
amount of capital used, i.e., capital and energy are complements in production.
\par
The weight of this evidence then is that higher energy prices tend to reduce invest-
\par
ment in the manufacturing sector and the rate of growth of capital accumulation.
\par
The estimates also indicate (column 6) that in 12 industries, labour and energy are
\par
long run substitutes and that in 6, labour and energy are long run complements.
\par
Therefore, any summary statement of the degree of complementarity or substitutability
\par
between energy and labour in aggregate manufacturing rests on substantial differ-
\par
ences in responses among specific industries. In the short run, only 8 industries
\par
exhibit complementarity between energy and labour. Moreover, in two industries
\par
energy and labour switch from being short run substitutes to being long run comple-
\par
ments while 2 industries show short run complementarity but long run substitutability
\par
between energy and labour.
\par
- 64 -
\par
An important parameter of the dynamic model is 8*, the variable representing the
\par
dlgree to which the gap between the desired capital stock and the actual stock is
\par
closed within one year. A 8* close to zero indicates a very slow response to
\par
exogenous shocks, while a 8* close to unity denotes very quick adjustment. The
\par
average adjustment coefficient for all 18 industries is .3, indicating that in the
\par
first year following an exogenous shock, 30\% of the difference between the' capital
\par
stock actually held by the firm and the desired stock of capital is closed. After
\par
five years, 81\% of the change to the desired level of the capital stock has been
\par
accomplished.
\par
Dynamic Model Estimated with Canadian Data
\par
The elasticities of demand estimated from Canadian data for the 1962-75 period are
\par
given in Table 2. Turning first to the last column, the average 8* (estimate of
\par
the adjustment parameter) is .38, a value very close to that (.30) estimated for
\par
the U.S. in a different time period. The long run energy own-price elasticities
\par
estimated with the Canadian data appear in most cases to be somewhat higher than
\par
those estimated for the U.S {$\bullet\bullet$} In most of the Canadian industries, there is very
\par
little divergence between short run and long run energy own-price elasticities.
\par
As we indicated earlier, this result is a signal that in the long run, energy and
\par
capital are neither strong complements nor strong substitutes; a result entirely
\par
consistent with our findings for the U.S.. 11 of the 18 industries show capital/
\par
energy substitutability; only 3 of these cross-price elasticities, however, are
\par
significantly above zero. The 7 industries which exhibit some capital/energy com-
\par
plementarity again have capital energy cross-price elasticities close to zero. In
\par
the U.S., we found that capital energy complementarity predominated. For Canada,
\par
however, it is impossible to summarize aggregate manufacturing as showing, in aggre-
\par
gate, either capital energy substitutability or capital energy complementarity. The
\par
labour energy cross-price elasticities also vary in sign across Canadian industries.
\par
These divergencies are similar to U.S. evidence. The dynamic model allows for
\par
short run energy/labour relationships to differ from and slowly adjust to some long
\par
run equilibrium value. Estimates with the Canadian data, indicate that in the short
\par
run only 4 industries exhibit labour/energy complementarity while in the long run
\par
9 industries exhibit such behaviour. These observations indicate that in the short
\par
run, as energy prices increase, firms tend to substitute labour for energy. In the
\par
long run however, the impact of higher energy prices is to reduce employment.
\par
Canadian industries exhibit twice as many shifts in energy/labour relationships
\par
between the short and long runs (6; 5 industries changing from short run substitu-
\par
tability to long run complementarity) as compared to the U.S. experience. In addi-
\par
tion, in 5 Canadian industries (as compared to 3 U.S. industries), 'overshooting'
\par
is found, i.e. a greater energy/labour substitutability is found in the short run
\par
as compared to the long run.
\par
It is our feeling that the evidence utilizing the Canadian data is more powerful than
\par
the evidence from the U.S. data for a variety of reasons. First, the Canadian data
\par
include both cross-sectional and time series observations. The additional cross-
\par
sectional evidence provides much valuable information. Secondly, the Canadian data
\par
have less flaws than the U.S. data.
\par
SUMMARY
\par
The evidence indicates that it is important to disaggregate aggregate manufacturing
\par
into its various industry components. Factor demand models, including energy demand
\par
models, estimated with aggregate data may be misleading for policy purposes since
\par
aggregating industries with very different production characteristics may result in
\par
- 65 -
\par
erroneous conclusions as to the nature and characteristics of important relationships
\par
among inputs. One of our major conclusions is that it is difficult to summarize
\par
'manufacturing' as conclusively showing energy/labour or energy/capital complemen-
\par
tarity or substitutability. The complementarity or substitutability between these
\par
inputs varies according to the production structure of the specific industry. Our
\par
second major conclusion is that utilizing dynamic models is important for public
\par
policy decisi0ns. The results indicated that, in fact, adjustment to exogenous
\par
shocks is fairly slow. Results for both the U.S. and Canada indicate that, on aver-
\par
age, in the first year after an exogenous change, only 30\%-40\% of the change to the
\par
new desired production techniques has been accomplished. Of course this speed of
\par
adjustment varies widely across specific industries. Utilizing the dynamic model
\par
also indicates that 'overshooting' can occur, and that complementarity or substitu-
\par
tability relationships change over time. The constraint of having fixed assets and
\par
a given technology may force firms in a short run to react to higher energy prices
\par
by employing more labour to produce a given level of output. However in the long
\par
run, when the firm is able to change its technology by using a new and different
\par
capital stock, it may use less labour than it did in the short run. Hence, evidence
\par
of employment gains in a specific industry shortly after large energy price increases
\par
should not be taken as necessarily representing the ultimate equilibrium response
\par
of firms in that industry. Our results indicate that the ultimate long run response
\par
of firms to the higher energy prices may well be to lower employment below the
\par
immediate short run response, perhaps even to a level less than that which existed
\par
in the previous steady state equilibrium. Finally, the evidence for the U.S. indi-
\par
cates that complementary relationships prevail for energy and capital. For Canada,
\par
energy/capital relationships are almost equally divided between substitutability
\par
and complementarity.
