SECTORAL ENERGY DEMAND FOR THE
\par
BELGIAN ECONOMY: THE CASE OF THE
\par
IRON AND STEEL INDUSTRY
\par
F. Bossier, D. Duwein, N. Gouzee and D. Rigauz
\par
Bureau du Plan-Planbureau, Brussels, Belgium
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
In this paper, an econometric model for the analysis of energy consumption in the
\par
Belgian iron and steel industry is presented. The model is used to simulate the
\par
energy needs of the iron and steel industry for the year 1990.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy consumption; stages of production price sensitiveness substitution be-
\par
tween energies; forecastings.
\par
INTRODUCT ION
\par
The purpose of this paper is to propose a model for the analysis of the energy con-
\par
sumption in the Belgian iron and steel industry. The econometric model disting-
\par
uishes the main stages of productlon of the sector and studies, for each of these
\par
stages, the evolution of the specific energy consumption of each product. The
\par
first part of the paper is devoted to a presentation of the Belgian iron and steel
\par
industry situation. This preliminary description shows the interest for a by sta-
\par
ge of approach. The second part of the paper is a short description
\par
of the model selected for the analysis of the sector. The first simulation results
\par
for the year 1990 are also introduced and briefly commented.
\par
THE BELGIAN IRON AND STEEL INDUSTRY AND ITS ENERGY CONSUMPTION
\par
The production of iron and steel is the most significant user of industrial energy
\par
in Belgium, with a share of 38,3 \% in the total industrial consumption for 1977.
\par
At a global level, the energy used by the iron and steel industry represents more
\par
than 15 \%of the national energy consumption.
\par
It is therefore justified to bring a special attention to the energy consumption
\par
process of this sector, given the increasing need for energy conservation. The
\par
iron and steel sector considered here includes the production of pig iron in the
\par
blast furnaces, the production of raw steel in the steel furnaces and the steel
\par
finishing process in the rolling mills. The production of coke in the coke ovens is
\par
treated in another study.
