The Belgian blast furnaces are in general relatively old and not equipped with mo-
\par
dern instrumentation and automatization. Yet, modernization efforts are being made.
\par
Most of the blast furnaces have been transformed to make possible gas or fuel injec-
\par
tions. This tendency to substitute gas and fuel partly to coke has been a result
\par
of a constant decrease of the relative prices of gas and fuel before 1974. Studies
\par
on that subject [Groupement (1977) ; Szekely (1975)] show that a continuous decrea-
\par
se in the mise au mille of coke has been brought by both improvements in. blast fur-
\par
nace working and substitution of gas and fuel to coke. In the case of Belgium,
\par
however, the value of 1976 is still high, though it dropped between 1965 and 1976
\par
from 650 kg to 540 kg coke per ton of steel produced.
