\par
The pig iron goes from the blast furnace to the steelmaking. In 1976, the oxygen
\par
processes (LD + LDAC and OBM) were the most important with a part of more than 95 \%
\par
of the steelmaking. Electrical steelmaking had a nearly constant part of 4 \%and
\par
did not penetrate further because of the high cost of electricity and the unstable
\par
market of scrap; it is principally devoted to the production of special steels.
\par
Belgian steelmaking is thus very advanced and low energy consuming, so that there
\par
is nearly no way in this field to improve energy efficiency.
\par
Finally in the steel finishing stage, continuous casting penetrates slowly. Only
\par
a few factories are already working with continuous casting. Most of the installa-
\par
tions are still high energy consuming because of repeated cooling and heating ope-
\par
rations. Thus, in this stage of production, the amount of energy saving which can
\par
be realized is important.
