\par
THE MODELIZATION OF THE IRON AND STEEL INDUSTRY ENERGY CONSUMPTION
\par
Energy consumption of the iron and steel industry is modelized in order:
\par
- to summarize the main energetical characteristics of the sector by the use of a
\par
limited number of relations; these relations should emphasize the most important
\par
factors for the explanation of the energetical behaviour (the prices of energies
\par
and of the other factors for instance). They also should include a complete ana-
\par
lysis of the possible relations existing between the aggregate factors of pro-
\par
duction (capital. labour. energy and other inputs). Finally. the model should
\par
permit to describe the potential limitations to the substitution between the
\par
energy products. by taking into account the origin (energy produced - by-product -
\par
or bought by the sector) and use of each form of energy ;
\par
- to simulate. on a ten year basis (1980-1990). the possible evolution of the steel
\par
industry energy mix. The study of different scenarios should be based on various
\par
price hypothesis. The influence of some technical choices should also be consi-
\par
dered (development of continuous casting technique. further development of elec-
\par
tric arc units ... ).
\par
The energetical model developed for the Belgian steel sector is presented shortly
\par
below. This model tries to incorporate the kind of information requested to clari-
\par
fy the energetical options of the sector. The main technical relations are brief-
\par
ly described in the first point. The second point is relative to the main results
\par
of the model simulation on the period 1978-1990.
\par
The Iron and Steel Industry Model
\par
The model developed for the analysis of energy consumption is made up of the follow-
\par
ing relations :
\par
- price relations aimed at analyzing the substitution possibilites between the ener-
\par
gies and the aggregate factors ;
\par
technical relations introducing limitations to the substitution between energies
\par
and explaining the origin and development of by-products (blast furnace and coke
\par
oven gases).
\par
The model distinguishes the main stages of production of the sector from one an-
\par
other. The price and technical relations are estimated for each of the stages con-
\par
sidered (blast furnace and steel making-steel finishing).
\par
The rice relations. The price relations are developed on a two-level way. The
\par
f1rs level allocates the total sectorial cost between the four main factors of pro-
\par
duction (capital. labour. energy and other non energetical inputs). each factor de-
\par
mand being a function of all factor prices. The second level shares out the ener-
\par
gy expenditure between the main energy products. each energy demand being a func-
\par
tion of all energies prices. All demand functions are derived from the minimiza-
\par
tion of translog cost functions at the two levels of the study 3.
\par
2 with the exception of by-product gases.
\par
3 See Berndt-Wood (1975). Fuss (1977).
\par
- 74 -
\par
The kind of price relations considered enables to study the sensibility of factors
\par
to price variations. In particular, the relations existing between factors can be
\par
specified from the cross-price elasticities estimates.
\par
The technical relations. Beside the set of price relations estimated for each sta-
\par
ge of productlon, technical relations are developed which bring two kinds of infor-
\par
mations :
\par
- first, technical relations permit to evaluate the quantities of by-product gases
\par
which could be produced by coke ovens and blast furnaces ;
\par
- another set of relations is aimed at controlling the evolution of the specific
\par
energetical consumptions (coke ratio, fuel oil and natural gas injections). The
\par
energy needed for the ore preparation is also calculated from this set of rela-
\par
tions.
\par
Accordingly, the technical relations may improve the information furnished by the
\par
price relations. They also complete the information requested for the evaluation
\par
of the total energy needs.
\par
The complete model is reproduced in figure 3. It shows how the different levels of
\par
modelization are imbricated in each other. Total energy demand (with the exception
\par
of by-products) is first calculated at the first level (KLEM level) of the price
\par
model. The total resulting energy demand is then allocated between the main forms
\par
of energies by the second level of the price model (energy sub-model). The techni-
\par
cal relations are used to improve the results coming out of the price models. They
\par
also calculate the demand for by-product gases and the energetical need of ore pre-
\par
paration. As a result, the total energy demand can be computed and the total ratio
\par
energy/ton of steel can be analyzed.
