\par
An Application of the Iron and Steel Industry Model : Energy Consumption
\par
Projection for the Year 1990
\par
A good example of the use of the energy model presented above is the simulation of
\par
the energy needs of the sector for some future year. This kind of exercise requi-
\par
res the choice of exogeneous hypothesis regarding the energy and other factors pri-
\par
ces and also the level of production expected for the various stages of the iron
\par
and steel industry. The simulation has been made for the year 1990 and for three
\par
energy price scenarios: a low scenario which is a constant energy prices hypothe-
\par
sis (in real terms), a middle scenario which selects a real energy price inflation
\par
of one to two percent and a high price scenario which selects a three to four per-
\par
cent real energy price inflation. As to the capacity of production for the year
\par
1990, it is expected not to exceed its level of 1974. Accordingly, the production
\par
of finished steel is fixed to about 16 millions of tons in 1990 (against 12 mil-
\par
lions of tons in 197\texteuro ). The main simulation results are summarized in table 4.
