- 77 -
\par
CONCLUSIONS
\par
In this short presentation, we have tried to show how the consumption of energy in
\par
the iron and steel industry can be explained by a model combining price relation-
\par
ships and technical information. The first results indicate a sensitiveness of
\par
the energetical consumption of the sector to the variation of the energy prices.
\par
High energy prices will lead to a decrease in total energy consumption and to a
\par
substitution of energy by other aggregate factors. The composition of the energy
\par
mix is also influenced by changing energy prices and substitutions between ener-
\par
gies, even if limited, will occur.
\par
Yet, the model has still to be completed. First, a more complete approach should
\par
include the study of coke ovens, which can be considered as practically integrated
\par
in the iron and steel sector. Further, the production of electricity by the sec-
\par
tor should be taken into account, given the important possibilities of self-produc-
\par
tion which exist already of which could be developed in the future. Finally,
\par
other technical relations could be included to improve the results obtained with
\par
our first exercise.
