ENERGY EXAMPLES
\par
A. R. Demirdache and R. H. Clayton
\par
Ministry of State for Science and Technology, Federal Government of Canada,
\par
Ottawa KIA IAI, Canada
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
One of the major tasks of technology assessment in Canada is to analyze and compare
\par
long-term energy options - nuclear fission, nuclear fusion, geothermal, tidal and
\par
solar - and to study the related transition from the present fossil fuel economy
\par
to a future non-fossil fuel economy. The purpose of this paper is to assess two
\par
energy options, nuclear and conservation, within the framework of technology assess-
\par
ment. This paper does not attempt to review the enormous current literature on the
\par
subject of energy, but rather to relate the energy question to the technology
\par
assessment process.
\par
Using the scenario approach, technology assessment is applied to two alternative
\par
energy options in detail. The critical supply and demand assumptions are high-
\par
lighted and the policy implications suggested. Above all, the sheer difficulty of
\par
ensuring adequate energy balances is emphasized.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Technology assessment; entropy; energy reference system; scenarios; nuclear
\par
controversy; conserver society.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
One of the major tasks of technology assessment in Canada is to analyze and compare
\par
long-term energy options - nuclear fission, nuclear fusion, geothermal, tidal and
\par
solar - and to study the related transition from the present fossil fuel economy
\par
to a future non-fossil fuel economy. Two points among others can be raised:
\par
i) The transition period is marked by great uncertainties arising from the
\par
trend in consumption, the trend of investment in the energy field, the
\par
state of resources and the world political equilibrium. Because of the
\par
system's slow-changing nature, if adequate strategies are not adopted,
\par
regardless of whether they may seem pointless in the short-term, various
\par
types of crises with serious consequences may emerge in the next twenty-
\par
five years.
\par
ii) During the transition which could take from some decades to possibly a
\par
couple of centuries, what can be the role of nuclear energy and
\par
conservation?
\par
The purpose of this paper is to outline some conditions likely to decide the
\par
latter question.
\par
- 79 -
\par
- 80
\par
It is clear that the energy problem is partly economic, partly technical and partly
\par
social. The conventional distinction between economics, engineering, geology and
\par
even biology gradually disappears. Many factors will affect the energy future. (l)*
\par
These include the following:
\par
i) The increasing role of information and knowledge
\par
The future patterns of energy growth will depend more on the exploita-
\par
tion of "information as a resource" and will be less dependent on ownership of
\par
traditional factors of production such as land, physical capital, resources and (2)
\par
mineral inputs. Knowledge may even become the most valuable property in society.
\par
The service-information economy will result in "restructuring the
\par
society" because it will bring profound changes to work and the home. The two
\par
places where people spend most of their lives may be profoundly altered. The
\par
working environment has already been changed for many people with the rise of
\par
white-collar work, but it is likely to change much more in the future. The intru-
\par
sion of energy technologies, mainly in the utility area, into the home is proceed-
\par
ing, although less rapidly than many technologists had expected.
\par
ii) Increasing social and technical complexity
\par
An important feature of the modern Canadian energy system is the sheer
\par
complexity of the institutions and technological structures. The increasing
\par
importance of knowledge has resulted in greater specialization, which has increased
\par
efficiency at the cost of increased interdependence among organizations, production
\par
processes and distribution networks. Modern energy industries have also tended
\par
toward extremely large scale, which achieve relative output economies but may lead
\par
to rigidity and unresponsiveness to further change. The technical complexity of
\par
the energy system has increased the cost of management and coordination. The energy
\par
industry now operates under numerous new constraints aimed at environmental protec-
\par
tion, worker {$\cdot$}safety and sales practices.
\par
iii) Increasing rigidity of interest group demands
\par
The increase in the number and power of special interest groups is a
\par
main cause of declining growth in the nuclear industry. These organizations
\par
include labour unions, professional associations, farmers' organizations, trade
\par
associations and other lobbying groups. These groups often have an incentive to
\par
block or delay nominations as well as to keep new entrants out of their industry
\par
or occupation. Members of a common-interest organization often can gain substan-
\par
tially from a policy that reduces the output of the society as a whole, because
\par
they can get most or all of the gains of the policy and bear little or none of
\par
the costs.
\par
iv) Entropy as the ultimate limits to energy growth
\par
In the face of this conflict between a complex and rather rigid energy
\par
system on the one hand, and social change on the other, the Law of Entropy now also
\par
may have to be taken seriously. The Law of Entropy states that the earth's energy
\par
and matter are being converted constantly from usable to unusable forms by combus-
\par
tion, wear and tear and rendering to waste. Just as heat always passes from the
\par
warmer to the cooler medium and never the reverse, the process of entropy cannot be
\par
reversed.
\par
This entropic process occurs at a gradual pace in nature but it is
\par
greatly accelerated by man in his quest for even more production technologies and
\par
higher levels of consumption. Since the amount of matter/energy from which all
\par
useful materials must be derived is limited, the speed by which this endowment is
\par
used up will determine how long energy growth and, ultimately, life itself, can be
\par
sustained on earth. Georgescu-Roegen(3) insists that living generations have a
\par
moral obligation to limit and indeed reduce population and entropy as to "minimize
\par
the regrets" of some future generation that must cope with a difficult and possibly
\par
violent adjustment, as the requirement of our materials-intensive and polluting
\par
society outstrips the remaining resources of the planet.
\par
