THE TECHNOLOGY ASSESSMENT PROCESS
\par
The easiest way to analyze energy policy is backward through hindsight. Unfortuna-
\par
tely, one has to live it forward. Technology assessment is directed towards helping
\par
adequate policy development in prospect. It isn't easy. For example, many techno-
\par
logy assessment studies have tried to deal with the future by extrapolating forward
\par
recent or existing past trends. "Many forecasts assume that the future is in some
\par
ways discernable from purely numerical evidence in the recorded past. Since fore-
\par
casting has shifted from the prophet and seer to the statistician, the visionary
\par
element has been downplayed and the numerical element has received nearly exclusive
\par
emphasis. ,,(3) The scenario approach, as will be applied in this paper, tries to
\par
blend the visionary and the numerical in a fruitful way.
\par
Technology assessment processes normally consider energy resources and energy demands.
\par
While the near-term future could be unsatisfactory in many instances - notably owing
\par
to shortages of liquid hydrocarbons - a number of options will lead to satisfactory
\par
solutions in the long-run. These options and their systems implications must be
\par
identified. Interest then focuses on the transitions from today's condition to one
\par
option or a combination of options for the long-term future. However, such transi-
\par
tions have certain constraints which determine strategies for managing such transi-
\par
tions. Capital costs and risk have immerged as major constraints in the consider-
\par
ation of energy systems.
\par
Two schools of thought on general energy strategy have evolved. One school, repre-
\par
sented by Amory Lovins (4) , favours a "soft technology path". This includes using
\par
direct solar energy, wind and biomass conversion; and careful resource conservation.
\par
The other, represented by the nuclear establishment, favours a "hard technology path",
\par
i.e., technological possibility to produce ample energy for our future while avoiding
\par
the use of non-renewable natural resource feedstocks as well as avoiding environmental
\par
problems.
\par
If one compares the assessment processes, as they have been applied to date in energy
\par
systems, different recommendations are observable regarding desirable future energy
\par
technologies. But even greater contrasts exist in the analysis that different groups
\par
use to support their recommendations for "hard" or "soft" energy paths.
\par
All methods of evaluation which compare favourable and unfavourable impacts of energy
\par
systems rest ultimately on how the assessments are made. Thus, they depend, at their
\par
foundation, on the type of data used and on the initial, and differing, premises.
\par
Very roughly, analysts fall into one of two groups with respect to their philosophy
\par
of assessment. The philosophy of the first group springs from the economic planning
\par
theory and views assessment as inference from market data. The second, which includes
\par
sociologists and systems analysts, views assessment as inference from the direct
\par
replies to an interviewer's questions.
\par
All models provide a breakdown of demand by economic sector and a disaggregation of
\par
supply by fuel type. Most often, nuclear electricity is not differentiated from
\par
other forms of generation and price elasticity of demand cannot be specified by fuel
\par
type. An example of a detailed demand breakdown for Canada is given in the "Refer-
\par
ence Energy System for Canada" presented in Figure 1. This description of the
\par
canadian energy system provides a complete physical representation of the technolo-
\par
gies, energy flows and conversion processes from extraction of primary energy
\par
resources through refining, transportation and storage of energy. In the reference
\par
system, energy supplies such as nuclear fuels, fossil fuels and hydropower are
\par
allocated to energy demands defined on a functional basis, such as space heating,
\par
transportation, etc.
\par
A satisfactory framework for the assessment of the full range of alternative energy
\par
policies requires an approach that encompasses both the economic techniques and
