\par
systems analysis. This is generally not done because analysts approach problems
\par
with a preselected methodology, carrying with it a philosophy of assessment. Recent-
\par
ly, however, Kenneth C. Hoffman of the Brookhaven Laboratory and Dale W. Jorgenson
\par
of Harvard University (5) , have developed a model which combines econometric and
\par
systems analysis techniques. The econometric model reflects economic impacts at the
\par
aggregate level that result from changes in energy policy. The systems analysis
\par
model determines the optimal use of resources for a given energy policy and a given
\par
economic environment.
\par
Technology assessment recognizes that an attempt must be made to imagine the differ-
\par
ent futures that could result from the behaviour of the actors involved within the
\par
limits of the systems through which they act. It attempts, therefore, to distinguish
\par
trends whose dynamics are almost invariant from those which can be affected by deci-
\par
sions of the actors concerned and by insignificant events. One of the main outcomes
\par
of this type of analysis has been the construction of global or partial scenarios
\par
worked out at different levels. These scenarios offer pictures of the future and
\par
certain time horizons, describe the paths that lead there, and illustrate the
\par
consequences of that possible situation or condition. Therefore, interest lies in
\par
the light they throw on the possible policies of certain actors, particularly
\par
governments.
\par
Scenarios are not forecasts, so much as attempts to cast light on possible or
\par
plausible futures. However, some of their characteristics which stand up compara-
\par
tively well in relation to the assumptions may naturally appear as forecasts. In
\par
addition, the various combinations of assumptions are not equally plausible.
\par
The two scenarios outlined in Parts 4 and 5 represent two of the productive avenues
\par
to pursue vigourously and simultaneously in Canada, while preparing for the emergence
\par
of new energy forms which may become available in the longer term. These scenarios
\par
are limiting cases. Their analysis becomes the basis for deriving the broad strate-
\par
gic thrusts of an energy policy. One is a "hard" energy path - an accelerated
\par
electricity scenario. The other is "soft" - a Conserver Society scenario.
\par
TECHNOLOGY ASSESSMENT AND THE NUCLEAR CONTROVERSY
\par
The use of nuclear technology has become very controversial in recent years. The
\par
growth of public opposition which has followed the expansion of nuclear power pro-
\par
grams has surprised electric utilities and governments. Public opposition has
\par
become a major consideration, perhaps a permanent constraint in the formulation of
\par
national policies. In Canada, a coalition of nuclear opponents (Canadian Coalition
\par
for Nuclear Responsibility) has emerged as a chief spokesman, and with a number of
\par
articulate scientists as technical advisors. This coalition sees in nuclear power
\par
installations the potential for catastrophic accidents, sabotage at nuclear plants,
\par
and for diversion of nuclear materials into the manufacture of terrorist weapons.
\par
On the other side, AECL (Atomic Energy of Canada Limited), AECB (Atomic Energy
\par
Control Board), the utilities and many independent scientists put forward the
\par
position that nuclear energy is relatively safe and that its potential for disaster
\par
can be contained with current technology. There is a strong case to be made for
\par
nuclear-generated electricity as an element in the national energy base. The
\par
Federal Government has recognized the contribution that nuclear technology can make
\par
to the nation's well-being. "It would be unconscionable under any circumstances to
\par
deny to the developing countries the most modern of technologies as assistance in
\par
their quest for higher living standards. But in a world increasingly concerned
\par
about depleting reserves of fossil fuels, about food shortages and about the need
\par
to reduce illness, it would be irresponsible as well to withhold the advantages of
\par
the nuclear age-- of power reactors, agricultural isotopes and cobalt beam therapy
\par
units." (Speech of the then-Prime Minister to the Annual Meeting of the Canadian
\par
Nuclear Association, Ottawa, June 17, 1975.)
\par
- 84 -
\par
If the current opposition to nuclear power merely follows the pattern of environmpntal
\par
concern which swept a number of industrialized countries a few years ago, and assu-
\par
ming no fatal accidents actually occur, one might expect it to die away in similar
\par
fashion as planning and regulatory procedures are tightened, as public attention is
\par
captured by new concerns and as people become accustomed to living with nuclear
\par
power stations. But, if the root cause lies in public fears about the peculiar
\par
dangers of radioactivity and an association in the public mind between civil nuclear
\par
power and the terrors of nuclear weapons, public opposition may intensify and
\par
increasingly curb the growth of nuclear power.
\par
Scientists and engineers playa dominant role in this controversy. The debate would
\par
long have died if it were not kept alive by a continuing intellectual input of scien-
\par
tifically-derived arguments. The scientists themselves engage in what could be
\par
called a purely scientific controversy, i.e., a debate which follows well institu-
\par
tionalized rules. (6)
\par
The areas of confusion and uncertainty are the following!6) (a) A prolonged debate
\par
on safety assumptions - radiation levels, probabilities of accidents, the safety
\par
of emergency cooling systems, toxicity of plutonium, waste disposal and storage of
\par
nuclear material (including theft and sabotage prevention). (b) Economic assump-
\par
tions, notably calculations of the economic rentability of construction and opera-
\par
tion of nuclear power plants. (c) Licensing procedures and legislative regulatory
\par
standards, particularly for adequate public participation in a field in which
\par
decision-making and planning processes require considerable technical and scientific
\par
expertise. Adequate forms of public participation have yet to be developed.
\par
The Canadian Nuclear Association recently surveyed the attitudes of Canadians towards
\par
the use of nuclear power in producing electricity. The main findings can be summa-
\par
rized as:(6) (a) Levels of public knowledge concerning the use of nuclear power to
\par
produce electricity are very low; (b) TWenty-one percent of the informed groups are
\par
opposed and 68\% are in favour of the use of nuclear power for generating electricity
\par
in Canada; (c) The public rated scientists as the most reliable source of informa-
\par
tion, then News" programs followed by AECB and the Federal Minister EMR;*
\par
the least reliable sources of information were viewed as the local
\par
reports and electric utilities; (d) Above all, if a nuclear power plant was planned
\par
in a local area, Canadians felt that technological experts, the Provincial Government,
\par
and the public at large should be the main participants in the siting decisions.
