Policy Implications
\par
This scenario corresponds to a forecast in which oil is not available in the future
\par
to meet the same energy share as at present and electricity has moved in to meet
\par
supply requirements. However, there are long-term problems of electricity: a
\par
certain inability to substitute directly for liquid fuels in transportation, build-
\par
ings and industrial plants. Technological developments in end use devices and
\par
changes in infrastructure are required. An important issue is the question of how
\par
rapidly electrification of land transportation could be achieved. Electricity does
\par
have the advqntage of being clean at point of use and has versatility in the resour-
\par
ces for its generation. It is as convenient as a wall plug or switch and virtually
\par
instantaneous in action. From an energy point of view, an "electrical society"
\par
would be a convenient, pleasant society. Electricity already has become associated
\par
with much that people regard as the better life. (7)
\par
No doubt the difficulties and disadvantages of electricity are likely to increase
\par
as substantial growth takes place in its use. However, they are likely to be out-
\par
weighed by the necessity of using more and more electricity. At least the problems
\par
associated with electricity are better known than those associated with new sources
\par
of energy supply such as frontier oil and gas, in situ oil sands production, and
\par
the new renewables such as solar, biomass or wind. Moreover, the problems to be
\par
overcome for an expanded electrical option are likely to be small compared with an
\par
over-reliance on world resources, such as oil, LNG and coal.
\par
Long-term future electrical costs might well increase much less rapidly than oil
\par
and natural gas, firstly because fuel costs for electricity are a smaller percen-
\par
tage of total costs, and secondly, because three basic resources for electrical
\par
generation (coal, water power and uranium) can establish costs and prices independ-
\par
ently of oil and natural gas. (7)
\par
However, the capital cost component of electricity is severe. One set of projec-
\par
tions for electricity, taking into account possible declines in "capacity factor"
\par
- percentage of time generating plant is used - showed electricity taking 7S\% of
\par
total energy capital in the year 2000, but providing only 24\% of total secondary
\par
energy. There is every reason for reducing electricity capital. One way to do
\par
this is by eliminating non-essential electricity and by load management. Hybrid
\par
heating systems, in which electric space-heating is supplemented by an alternative
\par
system, may be a solution.
\par
In the "hybrid" system(S), electricity would provide half the annual heat (or a
\par
different fraction if desired) during "off-peak" times and oil or gas the other
\par
half. For half the heat, the heating load factor would be SO\% and the overall
\par
electric generating plant capacity factor could be as high as 75\%. Thus,
\par
- 87 -
\par
electricity for heating would be produced at less than the power system's current
\par
average cost. The two heating sources could easily be controlled by two thermostats,
\par
one for the electric heater and one for the oil or gas furnace. The furnace ther-
\par
mostat would be set a few degrees below the heater thermostat. In this way, the
\par
heater would always have priority and the furnace would only come on when needed,
\par
that is in colder weather.
\par
This system has an important additional advantage. If widely used, the electric
\par
capacity dedicated to it could serve as a reserve which could be withdrawn by the
\par
power company on short notice by remote control. The auxiliary heat source would
\par
then take over and supply all heat till the heating capacity is released by the
\par
power company. There would thus be no need for any idle ready reserve (at present
\par
10\%) as kept by power companies to meet partial power failure or unexpected peak
\par
demand.
\par
with the "hybrid" system, nuclear electricity would become the cheapest form of
\par
power available to business and consumers, at least up to the limit of the "hybrid"
\par
system. The extra energy to give electric space heat within the system can be
\par
provided at essentially the marginal cost of fueling a nuclear reactor. So the
\par
nuclear controversy is not merely an academic discussion over the cost/benefit of
\par
different forms of electricity generation - it is an issue affecting how cheaply
\par
(perhaps even if at all) Canadians will keep their homes warm.
