\par
realistically attainable savings by 2000 for conservation measures which are believed
\par
to be technically feasible, economically justified and considered socially acceptable,
\par
in the major end-use sectors - buildings, transportation and industry.
\par
policy implications
\par
Planners increasingly concentrate on the scientific and technological implications
\par
of the growing need to more carefully conserve natural resources. Yet, it is clear
\par
that a trend towards conservation will have wide-ranging social effects. Neverthe-
\par
less, emphasis to-date is on technological development and it is assumed impli-
\par
citly that social adjustments will be made in the normal way. There are three
\par
policy implications stemming from the Conserver Society. The first concerns the
\par
conservation of resources. Much attention is already paid to energy conservation.
\par
However, less emphasis is being given to the conservation of materials. The dimen-
\par
sions of the economic threat due to materials scarcities are not well known. There
\par
is even less substitutability between different materials than there is between
\par
alternate energy sources. A shortage of a specific material - for example any of
\par
the ancient non-ferrous metals like lead, tin, zinc, copper, etc., which have risen
\par
in real price over the last few years without attracting commensurate increases in
\par
production - might cripple one industry yet leave another relatively untouched.
\par
Interestingly, if one assumed that conservation is necessary and imminent, and that
\par
a whole new line of "conserver" technologies and products will have to be invented,
\par
then it follows that whoever breaks the market first could stand to do a booming
\par
business. Nations which are slow to develop conserver technologies will more
\par
likely than not have to import them. In some cases such as solar energy technolo-
\par
gies, the importation of solar panels from the U.S. may result in Canada's using
\par
devices which are not ideally suited to our solar regime. In such an event, Canada
\par
would lose in two ways: through a decrease in domestic industry and through the use
\par
of sub-optimal technology. The recent U.S. announcement of its intention to develop
\par
more fully the conserver option makes more urgent the need to consider the industrial
\par
implications in Canada of a Conserver Society. Finally, it is desirable, technically
\par
feasible and economical to reduce the rate of primary energy growth in the years
\par
ahead, at least to the levels of a long-term average of about 2\% annually, as set
\par
forth in the EMR Study No. 11, or even less. Such a conservation-oriented energy
\par
policy provides benefits in every major area of concern-- avoiding shortages, pro-
\par
tecting the environment, avoiding problems with other nations and keeping real social
\par
costs as low as possible. In discussing the possibility of reduced energy demand,
\par
it is sometimes assumed that low growth and conservation might together form a
\par
single low demand future. In fact, there are good reasons for believing that these
\par
two conditions would, to some extent, conflict with one another. The reason is
\par
simple: conservation requires capital financing for new equipment and low economic
\par
growth lacks the money to pay for it. The converse is that, to some extent, econo-
\par
mic growth and conservation are complementary. Growth allows economies to replace
\par
old plants and old machines faster with newer, more efficient designs, a key requi-
\par
rement of conservation. There is no kidding ourselves - to one degree or another -
\par
economic growth must be paid for in energy. with no new energy sources of supply,
\par
an economic growth rate of (say) 2.0\% may well be about the maximum available and
\par
this may actually work against optimum conservation. There might come the day when
\par
governments have to sacrifice growth - and jobs - to save energy.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
In canada, as of the end of 1977, 4000 MW of nuclear power was already installed
\par
and operating and another 15,000 MW was under construction. Indeed, during 1977 the
\par
installed nuclear capacity in the province of Ontario accounted for 27\% of total
\par
electrical energy generated even on the basis of a comparatively low (70\%) capacity
\par
factor. Furthermore, based on ontario Hydro's latest system expansion program, about
\par
80\% of the province's electricity would be supplied by nuclear power stations by the
\par
- 89 -
\par
end of the century. The major concerns of critics of nuclear power relate to the
\par
health, environmental, safety, economic and political aspects of nuclear power rather
\par
than the scientific and technological. To many, nuclear power represents the embo-
\par
diment of "hard" as contrasted with "soft" technology, the increasing centralization
\par
of electric power, and concomitantly, the increasing centralization and dehumaniza-
\par
tion of society as well as threats to the biosphere. To the proponents, nuclear
\par
power is essentially an everlasting source of clean energy that will become increa-
\par
singly important as oil stocks rapidly dwindle towards the end of the century. They
\par
view it as a technology necessary for our social and economic well-being. Virtually
\par
all participants in the nuclear debate, however, have agreed with the conclusion of
\par
the World Council of Churches Study Group that "Pandora's Box cannot be closed. We
\par
cannot live as though nuclear power had not been discovered".
\par
The situation of the world economy will be precarious until the proportion of oil in
\par
the world supply of energy (and particularly the proportion of present OPEC oil) has
\par
been substantially reduced. Energy conservation, nuclear power development and use
\par
of coal - which is cheap and available - are the three avenues to pursue vigorously
\par
and simultaneously, while preparing for the emergence of new energy forms which may
\par
become available in the longer term, like solar energy, and without losing sight of
\par
safety and ecological considerations. In the energy field, each country should
\par
intensify its own national efforts. In other words, the energy scenarios are not
\par
alternates but rather must be conjoined. What is the rate of "energy growth
\par
in Canada"? How is this growth to be financed? How are the end-uses to be distri-
\par
buted? The main issue common to these questions is the balance between the present
\par
and the future. "How much does the present generation have to forego (in consumption)
\par
to ensure a higher rate of capital stock for future generations? As Robert M.
\par
Solow(12)has remarked: "We have actually done quite well at the hands of our ancestors.
\par
Given how poor they were and how rich we are, they might properly have saved less and
\par
consumed more."
\par
A recent report by Professor Wassily Leontief of Harvard University, "The Future of
\par
the World Economy" (13)makes clear the enormous scale of social and technological
\par
effort required to sustain global growth for another 25 years. The study was con-
\par
ducted under the auspices of the United Nations and was primarily funded by the
\par
Government of the Netherlands.
\par
The Leontief Study has demonstrated that, on balance and with some exceptions, the
\par
arguments are heavily against deliberate policies to halt or slow down the basic
\par
long-term technological trend, even if it could be done with safety. Indeed, one
\par
would prefer to accelerate some aspects of this trend, while being prudent and
\par
generally watchful in order to prevent or reduce the impact of the baneful possi-
\par
bilities. However, it is unlikely that growth can continue uncontrolled for more
\par
than another quarter century at the maximum. Thereafter, there will come a period
\par
marked by rapidly mounting needs for monitoring, restraining, and even reducing the
\par
economic process. Whether a more highly controlled, more commonly organized state
\par
to replace the society we now know will be better or worse than present-day society,
\par
few can yet answer.
\par
"
\par
... The first step is to measure whatever can be easily
\par
measured. This is okay as far as it goes. The second step
\par
is to disregard that which cannot be measured or give it an
\par
arbitrary quantitative value. This is artificial and mis-
\par
leading. The third step is to presume that which cannot be
\par
measured easily is not really very important. This is
\par
blindness. The fourth step is to say what cannot be
\par
measured really does not exist. This is suicide."
\par
Quoted in Hayes, 1974(1)
