\par
AND THEIR POLICY
\par
APPLICATIONS
\par
PROGRESS AND PROBLEMS OF A SYSTEMS
\par
APPROACH TO LONG-RANGE
\par
ENERGY DEVELOPMENT
\par
A. A. Makarov{$\cdot$} and A. A. Papin{$\cdot\cdot$}
\par
{$\cdot$}Siberian Power Institute of the USSR Academy of Sciences, Irkutsk, USSR
\par
"International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis, Laxenburg, Austria
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
In the paper an attempt is made, first, to assess both the potential and limits of
\par
systems analysis as applied to long-range energy development, and, second, to eval-
\par
uate past experience in the field, in light of such an assessment. Particular at-
\par
tention is devoted to: the premises underlying the systems approach; its means for
\par
reducing uncertainties about the future; temporal limits to its applicability; the
\par
state of the art in energy modeling (a principle tool of the systems approach); and
\par
possibilities for the further development of energy modeling. The paper also focus-
\par
es briefly on features of the systems approach that are particularly applicable to
\par
energy development under conditions of planned economies.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Systems analysis; energy development; long term; premises and means; temporal limits;
\par
energy modeling; energy-economy interconnections; energy supply system; resources;
\par
R\&D strategies; planned economy.
\par
PREMISES AND MEANS OF THE SYSTEMS APPROACH TO ENERGY
\par
Systems approach has already been widely acknowledged as the principle methodologi-
\par
cal basis for studying possible patterns of social and economic development, in
\par
particular, energy development. It plays a particularly great role in the so-called
\par
'normative' forecasting aiming not only at plotting possible scenarios of future
\par
development but also choosing a preferable one between them with regard to some cri-
\par
teria. However, in spite of the appreciation of the philosophy of systems approach,
\par
its initial premises and the resulting requirements to methods, as well as the tem-
\par
poral limits of its applicability have not been defined properly yet. Below an at-
\par
tempt is made to feel this gap.
\par
Outline of the Traditional Approach
\par
The principle theses underlying most of the energy studies carried out until recent-
\par
ly assumed:
\par
- 93 -
\par
- 94 -
\par
- the existence of objective regularities in the development of the phenomena under
\par
consideration;
\par
- the possibility of quantifying those regularities or, at least, of finding out
\par
quantitative measures for their visible images;
\par
- the validity of the past regularities or their quantitative images in the future.
\par
These theses can be observed most clearly in the simplest case--when a forecast
\par
offers an extrapolation of the past trends which are found out by means of analyz-
\par
ing statistical data about the phenomenon forecasted.
\par
The first thesis from those said above seems to be correct--energy as one of the
\par
most essential components of the productive forces is developing according to objec-
\par
tive laws inherent in them. However, the contents of those laws, and, all the more,
\par
their quantitative characteristics, are hardly explorable, and their visible quanti-
\par
tative images are far too mUltiple and heterogeneous to make the second thesis val-
\par
id. Finally, the third thesis can be accepted only for making short-range forecasts,
\par
and is evidently inadequate for long-term assessment of energy development.
\par
Premises of the Systems Approach
\par
Systems analysis is based on premises different from those applied in the tradition-
\par
al approach. Certainly, it rests on past experience and employs a comprehensive
\par
analysis of trends of the past development just as the traditional approach does.
\par
However, in this case external and internal relationships of the system (especially
\par
productional ones) which are observed more easily and therefore are more open for
\par
studying, become the principle subject of analysis. Exposure of those relationships
\par
and quantitative estimation of their parameters gives the ground to project develop-
\par
ment of the system.
\par
Thus the systems approach assumes:
\par
1. Possibility of exploring external and internal relationships of the systems
\par
studied.
\par
2. Visibility of those relationships and their parameters.
\par
3. Possibility of quantitative estimation of prospective values of the technological
\par
parameters characterizing the relationships of interest.
\par
The premises named above express the necessary and, it is believed, sufficient con-
\par
ditions for building a model (formal or partly mental) for the system studied that
\par
serves currently as the principle tool of systems analysis. But they do not imply
\par
satisfying the extremely important--and frequently forgotten--condition of the ade-
\par
quacy of a model and the modeled system that provides right use of models. As no
\par
formal methods of model verification exist yet, modeling continues to be a kind of
\par
art accessible for prominent experts in systems analysis, namely those who are capa-
\par
ble of selecting the essential relationships of the system from their innumerable
\par
number, and of finding the means to check whether the choice was right. One should
\par
not underestimate the role of that factor in systems approach to forecasting as well
\par
as the resulting 'humanization' or 'subjectivization' of conclusions. It would be
\par
incorrect to suppose that systems analysis allows to remove professional limits to
\par
the extent that an energy forecast can be worked out, say, by a mathematician or an
\par
economist with the same success as by an energy expert.
\par
Taking into account the above reservation one can consider the first premise of the
\par
systems approach to energy forecasting as a sufficiently feasible one. The second
\par
requirement that implies making models visible and matching modern computers is also
\par
usually met by reasonable aggregation of the system's relationships. The major dif-
\par
ficulties of systems analysis of energy development are connected with satisfying
\par
the third premise demanding for proper informational provisions.
\par
- 95 -
\par
The different kinds of technological parameters which make overwhelming share of
\par
input data necessary for systems forecasting of energy might be subdivided into the
\par
following three groups with regard to difficulties of their evaluation:
\par
- Parameters of existing or similar new energy technologies, evaluation of which
\par
does not offer any principle difficulties.
\par
- Parameters of new energy technologies or of existing ones applied under essential-
\par
ly new conditions (like oil production from deep offshore). The values of such
\par
parameters are estimated on the basis of technological, geological, and other
\par
projections fulfilled by specialists in connected areas with knowledge of the
\par
structure of new technologies and analogs for their main components. Thus, this
\par
part of the work on energy forecasting also cannot be made without wide partici-
\par
pation of energy experts--not system analysts but technologists.
\par
- Aggregated parameters characterizing complex connections of energy with other
\par
sectors of the economy, above all, connections on energy demand. The all-pene-
\par
trating character of energy makes those connections so fractional and numerous
\par
that their detailed modeling is hardly possible and, what is more, harmful be-
\par
cause in most cases it could break the consistency of the level of detail through-
\par
out the model. Therefore for purposes of long-range forecasting both straight
\par
and back energy-economy connections should be modeled in an aggregated way. But
\par
this, in turn, demands a determination of future values of a great number of ag-
\par
gregated indices of economic development the technological basis of which is sub-
\par
stantially unrecognizable. 50 far only one way exists of evaluation of such eco-
\par
nomic indices namely the way of extrapolation of their past trends for the future,
\par
that is of making use of traditional (not systems) methods of energy forecasting.
\par
50, the paradox of systems approach to energy forecasting lies in the fact that at
\par
some phase of its implementation the necessity has appeared to use extrapolation
\par
methods together with their controversial premises. In other words straightforward
\par
application of systems analysis to development of real systems is impossible as in
\par
that case one would have to study the whole set of interfaces inherent in the eco-
\par
nomic and social environments. On the other hand the necessity to distinguish the
\par
scope of the system studied, demands inevitably outside forecasting of its broken
\par
external connections involving extrapolation methods. This paradox seems to dis-
\par
credit the systems approach as one just adding its own hard-met conditions to ones
\par
of the traditional approach.
\par
In fact, however, that is not the case if systems analysis is used intelligently.
\par
On the one hand, the deeper the modeling of external connections of the system ex-
\par
pands outside it the less the errors of exogenous parameters definition affects the
\par
results of forecasting. For example, the results of projecting of the productional
\par
structure of energy supply systems show much less accuracy if demand for energy, and
\par
capital and material resources available for energy development, are given exogenous-
\par
ly than in the case when a combination of energy and economic intersector models is
\par
applied with exogenous setting the normatives of input-output matrices. In other
\par
words, the farther we move the line of exogenously given parameters from projected
\par
ones (that is the fuller the systems approach is realized) the less the possible
\par
errors in definition of exogenous parameters and, hence, shortcomings of the tradi-
\par
tional methods, influence the results.
\par
On the other hand, the errors of forecasting become smaller if a given number of
\par
exogenous parameters is greater because the impact of each of them to final results
\par
become weaker. Obviously, the latter is valid only if exogenous parameters are
\par
mutually independent and show about equal impacts to results. Coming back to the
\par
example given above it means that forecasting of the productional structure of the
\par
E55 on the basis of exogenously given demand for energy is dangerous as the param-
\par
eters of energy demand are highly dependent on each other and, getting together,
\par
influence the results on E55 development to a very high extent (almost definitely).
\par
- 96 -
\par
AS a result, all the shortcomings of traditional ways of energy demand projecting
\par
will be inherent in the systems approach to ESS development. On the contrary, join-
\par
ing of an energy model and an intersector model of the economy replace exogenous
\par
energy demand assessment (and exogenous assessment of values of economic resources
\par
directed to the energy development) by projection of a great number of mostly inde-
\par
pendent coefficients of input-output matrices of about equal potential impacts to
\par
results. Even substantial errors in definition of future values of those coeffi-
\par
cients lead to comparatively small total impact to results thanks to the effect of
\par
their mutual compensation. For example, if economic and energy development is de-
\par
scribed by linear models which is currently the case, then the errors in outputs
\par
appear to be a few times less than the errors in inputs (this is valid for models
\par
of dimension up to 1000 equations if above named conditions are satisfied)--see
\par
Table 1 (Makarov and Melentyev, 1973).
\par
