Thus a skillfully made approximation of external connections of the system consid-
\par
ered added with straightforward modeling of major ones let minimize--but not remove
\par
--the limitations of the traditional methods of forecasting and take the advantages
\par
of systems approach. But it should be stressed once again that the high profession-
\par
alism of forecast-makers who combine general and specific knowledge of the system
\par
with mathematical background and sufficient understanding of higher and adjacent
\par
systems is an indispensable condition for success.
\par
Means for Reducing Uncertainties in the Systems Approach to Energy
\par
In comparing the systems approach with traditional methods of energy forecasting it
\par
is necessary to show by what means the former is capable to provide more room to
\par
lower uncertainties of the future. In our opinion this is achieved by two principle
\par
means:
\par
First, by modeLing productionaL connections of the system, which due to high stabil-
\par
ity over time predetermine patterns of short-tomedium-term energy development (for
\par
up to 10 years) and reduce manyfold the number of long-term energy strategies to be
\par
analyzed, retaining only technologically feasible ones. The technological links of
\par
the system are especially important in this regard as they change only with the
\par
introduction of principally new energy technologies. As the large-scale employment
\par
of such technologies takes usually about 30 to 40 years then at least for that peri-
\par
od of time knowledge available and, hence, feasibility of modeling of technological
\par
connections in energy give a firm foundation to foresee the future.
\par
The problem of territorial connections especially those characterizing the alloca-
\par
tion of new fuel bases is more complicated. While geological data available for
\par
coal basins are, as a rule, sufficiently reliable for modeling of their long-range
\par
- 97 -
\par
development, the characteristics of new oil and natural gas deposits are not. How-
\par
ever, one should take into consideration firstly, more than 10-year lag between the
\par
time of discovery of a new oil or gas deposit and the phase of its large-scale de-
\par
velopment, and secondly, the fact that territorial (transport) connections in this
\par
case (especially in the case of oil) are much less important for adequate modeling
\par
than technological ones. Thus productional relationships in energy may be foreseen
\par
and modeled more or less reasonably for around 30 years ahead (depending on the
\par
level of the exploration densLty of a territory and the prevailing kinds of energy
\par
resources) {$\bullet$}
\par
But knowledge of the composition of productional interfaces of the system studied
\par
and an estimation of their parameters obtained with more or less reliability do not
\par
guarantee a trustworthy forecast of development of the system. In fact, a descrip-
\par
tion of cause-effect relationships of energy development in a model allows just to
\par
outline an extended over time cone-shaped range of feasible energy strategies. As
\par
a rule the dimension of that range is so large that it does not give much rapid in-
\par
formation about the future. To decrease the level of uncertainties in this case
\par
the second mean of systems analysis is applicableJ namelYJ the intentional selection
\par
of preferable or desirable strategies from the number of feasible ones.
\par
The introduction of an objective function in a model is a great methodological step
\par
forward makLng forecast normative and purposeful. While taken for granted by sys-
\par
tems analysts, the significance of this tool has often not been realized by forecast
\par
makers. Its meaning is that the subjects of forecasting come to include not only
\par
technological structure of the society but also its future aims that is its social
\par
order. In fact when for the purposes of global forecasting world energy development
\par
is optimized with regard to a single objective function (for instance, in a global
\par
LP model) complete world unity is assumed. Such an assumption would substantially
\par
affect the results of forecasting. For instance, runs of the gaming model for the
\par
world oil market have shown that the level of future oil price is 1.5 times higher
\par
in realistic situation of contradictions between oil producers and consumers than
\par
in a hypothetical case of full unity among oil producers and consumers (Makarov,
\par
1976). Then, if a model for national economic development operates with a single
\par
objective function it means in any way that the assumption of centralized planning
\par
is introduced, affecting to a large extent the results. At last, optimization of
\par
the energy development of a country with market economy with regard to a single ob-
\par
jective function is also an essential idealization--usually not estimated by its
\par
consequences--because accounting for competition between energy firms would likely
\par
lead to a different picture of energy development. It is curious that studies of
\par
some gaming models (Makarov, 1976) have shown that the situation of competition
\par
between a small number of large firms offers greater changes in energy development
\par
when compared with the situation of market equilibria while the situation of a great
\par
number of small competitors show almost the same results as those under condition of
\par
market equilibria.
\par
From what was said above it follows that the reduction of uncertainties of the fu-
\par
ture by the Lntroduction of objective functions into models for energy development
\par
is a very informative and binding act determining to a great extent the results of
\par
normative forecasting. Making use of this means of systems approach without com-
\par
prehensive analysis of its adequacy to real conditions of economic and social devel-
\par
opment would lead to, serious methodological error.
\par
Temporal Limits of Applicability of the Systems Approach to Energy
\par
In closing the discussion about the methodological foundations of systems approach
\par
to long-term assessment of energy development, it seems necessary to evaluate its
\par
general consistency with the problems to be solved. Let us try to do so in proceed-
\par
ing from the pragmatic goals of long-range energy forecasting.
\par
- 98 -
\par
In our opinion those goals can be outlined as follows (Makarov, 1977):
\par
1. Assessment of preferable long-term energy R\&D strategies with corresponding
\par
distribution of capital and material resources among R\&D programs.
\par
2. Finding out the necessity and timing of development of fuel production in bound
\par
areas with elaboration of measures for making proper technological and macro-
\par
structural provisions.
\par
3. Assessment of expedient
\par
with recommendations on
\par
economic, and, perhaps,
\par
levels
\par
proper
\par
social
\par
of energy conservation in the long-term future
\par
and timely changes in patterns of technological,
\par
development.
\par
What is the final time horizon that should be analyzed in order to reach the above
\par
goals? Are the methods of systems analysis capable of providing sufficiently trust-
\par
worthy estimations of energy development throughout that horizon? Let us try to
\par
answer these questions.
\par
In finding out the preferable energy R\&D strategies, forecasts of the energy de-
\par
velopment are necessary to estimate possible timing, scales, and impacts of deploy-
\par
ment of different technological innovations. Following Burkov, Irikov, and Makarova
\par
(1980), let us mark out three 'strata' of innovations being developed at any point
\par
of time: ones under industrial testing; designed ones; and ones under demonstra-
\par
tion on physical feasibility. According to trends in timing of development of R\& 0
\par
programs over the last few decades, studying the first 'stratum' would require in-
\par
formation about development of energy systems for 10 years ahead, the second 'stra-
\par
tum' for 15 to 20 years, and the third one for 30 to 40 years ahead. It follows
\par
from the earlier sections of this paper that studies of the first two 'strata' of
\par
innovations may be provided with necessary system's data. As far as the third
\par
'stratum' is concerned it appears under conditions of uncertainty--it is the short-
\par
age of data about costs and efficiencies of technologies being in initial stages of
\par
their development that imposes the temporal constraints on the modeling of techno-
\par
logical interfaces in energy. Thus systems analysis can help only to find out the
\par
demand for principally new technologies over the next 30 to 40 years and to estimate
\par
the utmost levels of their parameters at which they still can prove commercial fea-
\par
sibility. But it cannot help to make a reasonable selection among the new technol-
\par
ogies or even to range them in the order of priority (although there are no formal
\par
limits in doing so). Fortunately usually the share of such principally new technol-
\par
ogies in total R\&D spendings makes less than 10\%. Therefore, taken as a whole
\par
the problem of concentrating of economic resources on the most promisable R\&D
\par
programs may be solved by the methods of systems analysis (Burkov, Irikov, and
\par
Makarova, 1980).
\par
The matter of the second pragmatic goal--preparation for production of fuel from
\par
bound areas--stands a bit worse. The necessity of making such decisions when look-
\par
ing at past experience, must be determined up to 20 years in advance. Usually, this
\par
perspective is supplied rather sufficiently with technological data, and that allows
\par
to find out the very fact of necessity to come to bound areas. But for fuel re-
\par
sources with costly exploration (that is for oil and natural gas) the allocation
\par
and possible scales of fuel production in nonexplored bound areas stay out of cor-
\par
rect modeling, and, so, out of quantification. It means that in this case it is
\par
impossible to substantiate the timing of preparatory measures, especially, the mea-
\par
sures on regional macro-development. Resources of high level of exploration--coal
\par
and nuclear energy--play the role of marginal sources of energy and, because of
\par
that, estimations of their future development are affected by all possible errors
\par
in estimation of energy demand and development of other energy resources. There-
\par
fore making use of the methods of systems analysis one can succeed in estimating
\par
the allocation and establishing the priorities in development of new bases of coal
\par
and nuclear fuels production. But getting a trustworthy estimation of required
\par
scales of their development seems to be unrealistic, because of low reliability of
\par
existing geological estimations of hydrocarbons.
\par
- 99 -
\par
Thus, in regard to development of fuel production in bound areas systems analysis
\par
can give reasonable data about:
\par
- necessity and timing of development of bound areas that allows to judge about
\par
requirements in new exploratory and productional equipment.
\par
- sequence of priorities in development of new large fuel bases (for oil and gas
\par
such data is usually very rough) that gives a basis to start (or be prepared to)
\par
projecting activities on some of those bases.
\par
The rest of necessary preparatory measures including ones for development of macro-
\par
structure in bound areas, cannot be grounded on results of forecasting and, there-
\par
fore, is to be taken while watching the actual process of energy development.
\par
Finally, systems approach offers a basis for analyzing only principle components of
\par
energy conservation policies in a rather aggregated way. For instance it can help
\par
in examining the necessity of essential shifts in technological structure of energy
\par
consumption (like the shift from light motor fuel to diesel fuel and, later, to
\par
electricity in transportation sector). It should be noted here that the level of
\par
reliability of such estimations is believed to be about the same as of those con-
\par
nected with any other principle technological innovations in energy. Further, the
\par
systems approach can provide an estimation (in order of magnitude) of necessary
\par
shifts in the intersectoral structure of the economy, as well as in the patterns of
\par
final consumption of GNP. But at the same time an estimation of a whole mass of
\par
possible concrete changes in the economy and their potential contributions in energy
\par
savings does not seem possible by means of energy forecasting.
\par
So, comparing the requirements to informativity of long-term energy forecasts with
\par
feasibilities of systems approach one has to recognize that currently it cannot be
\par
considered as a methodology fully adequate to the goals of long-term energy assess-
\par
ment. And though systems analysis is considerably more appropriate for purposes of
\par
energy forecasting than traditional methods, improving of its feasibilities is an
\par
important scientific problem. In connection with this it is worthwhile to analyze
\par
the concrete tool of systems approach to energy--mathematical models--in ordez to
\par
outline major possible directions of its further development.
\par
LONG-TERM ENERGY MODELING: TODAY'S STATE AND FURTHER PROSPECTS
\par
At present there are some versions of the hierarchical set of mathematical models
\par
developed to assess long-range energy development at national/regional level! (see,
\par
for example, Behling, Cherniavsky, and Hoffman (1977); Connolly, Dantzig, and Parikh
\par
(1977); Hafele, and Basile (1978); Makarov (1976); Manne (1977); Scholer (1979>>.
\par
Their analysis shows a clear tendency of unification of the structure of the model's
\par
families, that is, of gradual adoption by more and more researchers of nearly the
\par
same composition of the models, however, with substantial differences in their con-
\par
tents. In our view that reflects the natural process of making a tool of research
\par
consistent with problems to be solved. The typical set of models is characterized
\par
below with more emphasis on its comparatively new elements.
\par
State of the Art in Long-Term Energy Modeling
\par
Not immediately, but quickly enough energy forecasters came to the conclusion of
\par
the impossibility of getting trustworthy estimates on energy development without at
\par
least aggregated estimation of development of the economy as a whole (Figure 1).
\par
!We do not touch on the problems of global energy modeling here.
