\par
Though very aggregated but direct modeling of the system of higher level of hierar-
\par
chy (for energy it is the economy) is an indispensable condition for systems
\par
analysis. Note, that the overwhelming majority of energy forecasts developed by
\par
traditional methods so far assumed exogenously given few hypotheses of economic
\par
development. At the beginning of the implementation of systems approach to energy
\par
attempts were made to do the same. But it became clear very soon that by doing so,
\par
accounting was missing for very important correlations between level of energy de-
\par
mand and amount of economic resources available for energy development. Besides,
\par
the possibility was missing for analysis of energy-economy feedbacks, that is, for
\par
quantifying the impacts of energy development to economic growth. The latter is
\par
extremely important on present and future stages of energy development because of
\par
the observed and anticipated sharp growth of its requirements to the economy. Ne-
\par
glecting potential impacts of any of the two-side energy-economy relationships to
\par
results would make major goals of energy assessment unachievable.
\par
Projections of demand for energy is an integral part of long-term energy assessment.
\par
The question is not whether such projections are necessary but which section along
\par
the chain of energy conversions can give most reliable estimates. Until recently
\par
almost all energy projections assumed energy demand given in terms of so-called
\par
secondary energy--electricity, heat, and directly used liquid, gaseous and solid
\par
fuels. Thereby the substitutability between different kinds of secondary energy
\par
that determine productional structure of energy in dependence on relative costs of
\par
secondary energy production, was left out of the analysis. Methodologically ful-
\par
filling long-term projections of energy demand in terms of useful energy (by energy
\par
consuming processes) seems, therefore, to be more correct.
\par
Estimation of energy resources availability is another traditional component of
\par
energy forecasting. A comparatively new element of this part of energy studies is
\par
the presentation of resources as functions of production costs and prices. That is
\par
what allowed to formulate the problem of optimization of productional structure of
\par
energy. In a broader sense energy resources projections must include separate cost
\par
estimates on resources exploration (especially for oil and natural gas) with find-
\par
ing out the geological features of deposits, and on fuel production with evaluation
\par
of optimized development of large fuel bases over time. The latter is closely con-
\par
nected with the optimization of the productional structure of energy.
\par
A very important component of long-term energy studies is the assessment of R\&D
\par
strategies. The necessity of such assessment is beyond doubt, but usually it is
\par
done by means of exogenous assumptions about the composition of new technologies to
\par
be considered, their build-up rates, and cost parameters, whereas systems approach
\par
requires straight analyzing of impacts of patterns of energy development to direc-
\par
tions and rates of scientific and technological progress in energy. Studying and
\par
modeling of these impacts is still a problem to be solved.
\par
Finally, assessment of dynamics of productional structure of energy (or, in other
\par
words, assessment of proportions of ESS development over time) is made on the basis
\par
of modeling mainly its technological interfaces while territorial connections are
\par
often neglected. For most countries and compact regions (like Western Europe) such
\par
an approach may be accepted but for countries like the USA or USSR, or for big re-
\par
gions like North America, characterized by great distances and high variety of geo-
\par
graphical conditions, modeling of territorial connections seems quite necessary.
\par
At the same time accounting for environmental constraints as well as conditions and
\par
limits of imports or exports of energy and some other factors is required.
\par
So, in our opinion, further efforts on improving the composition of the models
\par
should go in the following main directions:
\par
- 102 -
\par
- direct modeling of economic development with accounting for straight and back
\par
energy-economy relationships which determine parameters of energy demand and im-
\par
pacts of energy development to economic growth;
\par
- modeling of geological and economic aspects of long-term energy resources devel-
\par
opment (first of all, development of oil and natural gas resources);
\par
- studying and modeling the R\&D strategies in dependence on patterns of energy
\par
development.
\par
It would also be valuable to make an effort for further improvements of energy de-
\par
mand models in the direction of more adequate description of markets for different
\par
kinds of energy in the main consuming sectors, as well as of energy supply models
\par
for direct introduction of major territorial relations, and economic and environ-
\par
mental constraints.
\par
Below the possible approaches are considered to the realization of the above listed
\par
directions of improving the system of models for long-term energy assessment.
\par
Modeling of Energy-Economy Interactions
\par
By now there are two different approaches to economic modeling for purposes of
\par
energy forecasting. The first one prevails at energy studies under conditions of
\par
market economy. It was applied, for instance, in studies by Behling, Dullieu, and
\par
Hudson (1976); Manne (1977), and has been developed at IIASA (Hafele, and Basile,
\par
1978). This approach in general case makes use of three kinds of models, namely:
\par
a macroeconomic model describing the potential GNP growth as a function of capital
\par
and labor resources, labor productivity, and some other macroeconomic indices: inter-
\par
sectoral input-output models yielding balanced states of the economy in different
\par
points of the future and thereby providing inputs to energy demand calculations; and
\par
energy-oriented input-output model for assessment of impacts of different energy
\par
strategies to economic development.
\par
This approach should be appraised as methodologically correct for studying the eco-
\par
nomic development under strong monopolization of the markets. Its consistency with
\par
such a situation is explained by the fact that all three models lack objective func-
\par
tions, in other words, are not optimizing. Probably such an approach is more ade-
\par
quate to conditions of market economy than any other one assuming an exogenously
\par
given criteria of economic growth (like maximization of GNP or final consumption),
\par
as the latter would be equivalent to hypothesis of transition to planned way of run-
\par
ning the economy.
\par
However, the lack of optimization models, as was said, makes the quality of energy
\par
projections worse. In fact, an econometric macro-model for economic growth that
\par
serves as a central element of the above approach, is a classical example of tradi-
\par
tional methods of forecasting with their premises impracticable in long term. But
\par
this model is one that provides energy demand calculations with key parameters of
\par
economic growth and, thereby, predetermines the results on energy demand and affects
\par
to a large extent the results on energy supply. Therefore, the final results of
\par
forecasting appear to be in strong dependence on the a priori knowledge of character
\par
and parameters of the functions underlying the macro-model.
\par
Equally, the approach considered does not provide an analysis of the correlation be-
\par
tween the future levels of energy consumption and the sum of economic resources that
\par
may be directed to energy development (the latter is important for countries where
\par
energy is substantially state-owned), because none of the models used introduces
\par
investments by sectors of economy. Finally, the impacts of energy development to
\par
the economy are evaluated in a rather aggregated way--by total capital investments
\par
required not over the whole set of sectoral necessary outputs--due to the same dis-
\par
advantages of macro-economic model.
\par
- 103 -
\par
Thus, this approach to modeling of energy-economy interconnection does not realize
\par
all possibilities of systems analysis. That is explained not by the shortcomings
\par
of the systems analysis, but by objective difficulties of long-term forecasting
\par
under conditions of market economy.
\par
In planned economy an optimized dynamic intersector model is used for projecting
\par
economic growth. There are two existing modifications of such a model. The first
\par
one is an LP model (Zimin, 1980) and the second one represents a set of input-output
\par
submodels coordinated by special iterative procedure which simulates major stages of
\par
the economic analysis (Gershenzon, 1977). While not giving the features of those
\par
modifications it should be however noted that both of them describe in some detail
\par
the technological interfaces in the economy and maximize public consumption (with
\par
qualitative improvements of its structure) under some constraints, in particular,
\par
one on available labor force.
\par
The implementation of optimizing economic models is also connected with certain dif-
\par
ficulties. These difficulties result from the necessity to forecast the evolution
\par
of around two thousand coefficients of input-output matrices (in the models for
\par
market economy their number reaches 1,000). As it was said above, currently such
\par
forecasts can be done only on the basis of extrapolation. The reliability of such
\par
forecasts for planned economy might be a bit higher because the basis for extrapo-
\par
lation is composed not only of past meanings of the coefficient but also of their
\par
planned values for 10 to 15 years ahead. For instance, in the USSR in order to
\par
estimate values of input-output coefficients for long-term perspective, the histori-
\par
cal data from 1968, as well as the results of planning calculations for the period
\par
up to 1990, are used. On the basis of such 22-year statistics which, moreover,
\par
reflects partly changing conditions for energy development, one can set to forecast-
\par
ing the coefficients for another 20 to 25 years that is up to 2010-2015. It is also
\par
important that most of the extrapolated parameters are independent of each other,
\par
and impacts of each of them to rates and proportions of economic development are
\par
small; these facts allow to count on mutual compensation of errors in accordance
\par
with the Law of large numbers.
\par
Having an optimization model for the economy developed an iterative procedure of
\par
intercoordinated assessment of long-term development of energy and the economy may
\par
be organized in such a way that economic projections would give: inputs to energy
\par
demand calculations; dynamics of prices for economic resources demanded by the
\par
energy supply system development; and values of major kinds of economic resources
\par
allotted to energy development over time. On the other hand, assessment of long-
\par
term energy supply strategies would provide data for correction of the input-output
\par
coefficients for energy-correlated economic sectors.
\par
Certainly, the above named advantages of optimized models for the economy do not
\par
guarantee reliable results of energy forecasting by themselves, nevertheless they
\par
make forecasts more grounded--within the limits of our today's knowledge.
\par
Energy Resources Modeling
\par
Generally speaking, in order to estimate values and costs of any kind of energy
\par
resources available in a given region, three different processes have to be modeled:
\par
geological processes resulting in availability of potential resources; resources
\par
exploration providing additions to proven reserves; and fuel extraction that pro-
\par
vokes a necessity of accomplishing combined geological and techno-economic assess-
\par
ment.
\par
Modeling of those processes is at present in progress and assumes two essentially
\par
different approaches. The first one rests on the method of analogies that is, it
\par
- 104 -
\par
searches for resemblance of the characteristics of a given geological region to
\par
those of better explored regions, and thus estimating availability of potential
\par
energy resources in that region, and the levels of exploration and development costs.
\par
The data obtained compose a basis for the following, more formal, optimization of
\par
paths of resources development (as it is done at IIASA2 ). It is clear, that, as far
\par
as mental modeling exceeds formal one in this case, the confidence in such estima-
\par
tions is fully dependent on the proficiency of analysts.
\par
Second approach implies direct modeling of geological situation in the region, fol-
\par
lowed by formalized estimation of levels and costs of fuel production (Golovin,
\par
Kitaygorodsky, and Fainshtein, 1979). The relationships and the statements of the
\par
geological model introduce some a priori geological hypotheses and the numerical
\par
meanings of its parameters are simulated by the Monte Carlo method on the basis of
\par
available exploration statistics for the region.
\par
Estimates of potential fuel resources available in the region, as well as the total
\par
number of perspective geological structures underlying those estimates, represent
\par
major inputs to the model. Note, that those estimates may be specified while simu-
\par
lating the process of exploration in the model. Model runs give probabilistic esti-
\par
mates of additions to fuel reserves (by groups of deposit of the same size and deep-
\par
ness) versus exploration costs--see Figure 2.
\par
These estimates go as inputs to the linear-programing model which optimizes explora-
\par
tion activities and fuel production level over time, as well as defines dynamics of
\par
marginal production costs. Other inputs to the model include, in particular, deple-
\par
tion rates of reserves by groups of deposits, demand for fuel produced in this re-
\par
gion versus price, and, if necessary, total investments available for resources
\par
exploration and development. Obviously, two latter groups of inputs can be obtained
\par
only from assessment of ESS development strategies that necessitate rather tight
\par
linkage between energy resources models and a model for ESS development.
\par
An Approach to Energy R\&D Modeling
\par
Scientific and technological progress is one of the most important factors affecting
\par
long-term energy development. In energy studies conducted so far R\&D strategies
\par
are assumed as a rule, developing independently on general energy development. In
\par
fact, the course of scientific and technological progress is essentially determined
\par
by demand of ESS for technological innovations. At the same time, it submits to its
\par
own logic that gives a principle ground for direct modeling of R\&D strategies.
\par
The question is to describe the kind of interfaces in research and development of
\par
new technologies like influence of successful development of one technology to devel-
\par
opment of others if they have common physical or technological components. Explor-
\par
ing and modeling of such interfaces would allow us to find out internally consistent
\par
R\&D strategies and give a coordinated estimation of expenditures following their
\par
realization.
\par
Figure 3 gives a schematical representation of the system of models for R\&D fore-
\par
casting that has been developed on this ground in the USSR (Burkov, Irikov, and
\par
Makarova, 1980). In this system the energy supply model serves for estimating of
\par
rational scales of future deployment of technological innovations and their impacts
\par
on ESS strategies. Two other groups of models are'intended to estimate expected
\par
techno-economic characteristics of new technologies through assessment of possible
\par
alternatives for technological innovations with taking account of limited capital
