ABSTRACT
\par
Two sets of integrated energy models and their applications
\par
are described. The first set of integrated models is designed
\par
to address the need for additional electric power capacity
\par
in the face of uncertain future demand growth. Models of
\par
electric power operation, finance, and consumer preferences
\par
are integrated with methods for representing demand uncer-
\par
tainty. Using a common computer software package, integrated
\par
planning models have been constructed for dozens of U.S. elec-
\par
tric utilities, and the results have been employed in several
\par
regulatory proceedings.
\par
The second set of integrated models is designed to ad-
\par
dress medium- and long-range energy-economic policy and energy
\par
technology research and development issues. Models of all
\par
energy industries and processes and the rest of the economy
\par
are integrated to provide projections of energy supply and
\par
demand, capacity additions, financial flows, and economic im-
\par
pacts. Several modeling groups in the U.S. Department of
\par
Energy and its National Laboratories, industry-sponsored
\par
research institutions, and electric power companies presently
\par
use a common software system for constructing large-scale
\par
integrated models for their specific needs.
\par
The basic approaches of both sets of integrated models
\par
are presented, discussed, and summarized. References to
\par
detailed documentation of the models and methodologies are
\par
included.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy policy decisions; integrated energy models; eco-
\par
nomic models; power system planning; decision analysis; energy
\par
supply.
