INTRODUCTION
\par
Analysis of energy policy problems requires modeling of
\par
complex economic markets, technological change, resource deple-
\par
tion, energy-economy interactions, environmental impacts, and
\par
uncertainty. Integrated models that combine all of these
\par
relevant aspects of an energy decision problem are very help-
\par
ful in focusing information gathering and debate on the sensi-
\par
tive issues. This paper describes two sets of integrated
\par
models for energy decision making. The two sets of integrated
\par
models are quite different in terms of scope and detail and
\par
therefore illustrate how integrated models can be tailored to
\par
the problem.
\par
The first set of models is used extensively for electric
\par
power system planning, while the second set is used extensively
\par
for broad national and regional energy planning. The computer
\par
modeling systems used to construct each set of models are also
\par
different. Both computer modeling systems and the initial
\par
models were constructed by the author and his colleagues at
\par
Decision Focus Incorporated under contract to various organiza-
\par
tions.
\par
INTEGRATED ELECTRIC POWER CAPACITY PLANNING
\par
This first modeling system integrates simplified versions
\par
of accepted electric power utility models and methods of as-
\par
sessing electric demand uncertainty and consumer preferences
\par
(Cazalet, Clark, and Keelin, 1978; Clark and others, 1979).1
\par
The methodology emphasizes decisions concerning the alterna-
\par
tive levels of electric system capacity additions required
\par
to meet uncertain future electric demand. Detail in the model
\par
relevant to other decisions on such projects as electric power
\par
pricing and load management is included only where it is rele-
\par
vant to the determination of least-cost levels of capacity
\par
addition.
\par
The primary output of the methodology is the character-
\par
istic over/under capacity curve shown in Figure 1. The vertical
\par
axis of this curve is total consumer cost, which includes all
\par
costs of providing electricity to present and future electric-
\par
ity consumers, including environmental, electric service outage,
\par
and monetary costs. The horizontal axis of this figure is
\par
planning reserve margin, the percentage increment on expected
\par
peak demand in a future year used to determine planned total
\par
capacity in that future year.
\par
Figure 1 shows how total costs to consumers are affected
\par
by the level of planning reserve margin. As the planning re-
\par
serve margin increases, outage cost can diminish rapidly because
\par
system reliability improves. Variable costs (mostly fuel costs)
\par
may decline as a result of increased flexibility to ooerate
\par
low variable-cost units. Fixed costs increase with increased
\par
planning reserve margin, since a larger investment in new
