\par
Modeling Demand Uncertainty
\par
Perhaps the most important aspect of this integrated
\par
approach to capacity planning is the representation of demand
\par
uncertainty. In this methodology, demand in each future year
\par
is represented as a range of possible values, each with an
\par
associated probability. This range of uncertainty tends to
\par
become broader as the forecast lead time lengthens because
\par
of the unknown effects of the changing technical, political,
\par
and social conditions on the demand for electric power.
\par
Conversely, the range of uncertainty for a particular year
\par
tends to shift and narrow as the technical, political, and
\par
social conditions that will determine demand in that year
\par
become known.
\par
One of the several useful ways to represent uncertaintv
\par
in electric demand growth is with a probability tree of annual
\par
demand outcomes as shown in Figure 3. Extended and branched
\par
throughout the planning period, it characterizes a range of
\par
future electric demands.
