\par
At the left of the figure, the main inputs to the model
\par
are displayed. They consist of plant data, such as costs of
\par
new plants; system data, such as load duration curves; demand
\par
uncertainty in the form of probability distributions; and a
\par
description of the decision environment in terms of initial
\par
conditions of the system, planning lead times, and limits on
\par
the physical availability of new capacity such as those im-
\par
posed by the available hydroelectric sites.
\par
At the bottom of the figure are the two key decision
\par
variables of the study: the planning reserve margin and the
\par
planning mix. These two variables feed into the capacity ex-
\par
pansion decision model that determines the actual commitments
\par
of new units to various stages of licensing and construction.
\par
The planning reserve margin is the primary focus of the models;
\par
planning mix is considered to the extent that it affects plan-
\par
ning reserve margin.
\par
- 114 -
\par
In the center of the figure is the probability tree
\par
described earlier. This probability tree is determined by
\par
the two continuous probability distributions provided as in-
\par
puts on the left. These two distributions provide short-
\par
and longer-term measures of demand uncertainty. The model
\par
user specifies how many stages and branches per stage will
\par
be used in constructing the demand probability tree. Some
\par
approximation techniques are used to reduce the size of the
\par
probability tree.
\par
An input to the capacity expansion decision model at the
\par
base of the figure is the conditional demand forecast at each
\par
time point on each path in the probability tree. The condi-
\par
tional capacity expansion decisions are recomputed at each
\par
time point on each path in the probability tree. This gives
\par
us the inputs to the electric system model along each path
\par
in the tree.
\par
The electric system model is composed of the fixed charge
\par
(financial) model and production simulation model. The pro-
\par
duction simulation model is a probabilistic simulation of
\par
system operation with the capacity of each unit treated as
\par
uncertain. The outputs of the electric system model are the
\par
fixed and variable monetary costs of service, the outage
\par
(unserved) energy, and the environmental effects as measured
\par
by the energy production by each type of plant. These out-
\par
puts of the electric system model are computed for every
\par
time point on every path through the probability tree.
\par
The representation of values and preferences is shown
\par
at the right of the figure. First the consumer preference
\par
model reduces the various consumer outcomes for a single path
\par
through the tree to a single nurnber--the equivalent levelized
\par
consumer cost. The value assignments required to perform
\par
this calculation are the consumer discount rate, environmental
\par
charge rates, and outage charge rates. These value assign-
\par
ments are usually determined on the basis of studies of con-
\par
sumer behavior and expressed preferences. As with all model
\par
inputs, the effect on the least-cost decisions of changes in
\par
value assignments is determined through sensitivity analysis.
\par
Next, the consumer risk preference model computes the
\par
certain equivalent levelized cost. The calculation weights
\par
the equivalent levelized cost along a path through the prob-
\par
ability tree by the probability of that path. The certain
\par
equivalent levelized cost is the constant annual known payment
\par
that has the same perceived cost to the consumer as the un-
\par
certain streams of costs computed by the model along each
\par
path in the probability tree.
\par
This certain equivalent levelized cost is calculated
\par
for a range of planning reserve margins. By varying the plan-
\par
ning reserve margin over the range, we can trace out the
\par
characteristic U-shaped over/under curve shown in Figure 1.
\par
As we have described, this single curve summarizes a large
\par
amount of economic and technical information including many
\par
value judgments and a representation of demand uncertainty.
\par
- 115 -
\par
Application of Over/Under Models
\par
First used in late 1978, over/under capacity planning
\par
models have been constructed for dozens of utility and regional
\par
electric service areas. Over eighty copies of the computer
\par
software have been acquired by U.S. electric utilities and
\par
regulatory bodies. Over/under models have played important
\par
roles in several regulatory hearings (Cazalet and Keelin, 1978).
\par
More importantly, however, the over/under methodology is
\par
facilitating a trend in power system planning away from heavy
\par
reliance on single number econometric demand forecasts and
\par
arbitrary loss-of-load probability reliability criteria. The
\par
trend is towards evaluating generation capacity additions, con-
\par
servation programs, system reliability, and environmental im-
\par
pacts on a comparable basis. Integrated modeling is the only
\par
effective way to analyze the trade-offs and uncertainties
\par
required for current power system planning.
\par
The extensive use of over/under capacity planning models
\par
has required development of extensive user documentation
\par
(Clark and others, 1979). Many user workshops have been held
\par
to introduce new users to the methodology and computer soft-
\par
ware.
\par
INTEGRATED ENERGY MARKET MODELING
\par
This second modeling system integrates models of energy
\par
supply, conversion, transportation, and end use with models
\par
of the rest of the economy (Cazalet and others, 1979, Clark,
\par
Keelin, Shur and Warthman). The modeling system is used to
\par
construct comprehensive medium- and long-range models of
\par
international, national, and regional energy markets. The
\par
outputs of these models are price and quantity projections
\par
used as inputs to analyses of specific energy policy decisions
\par
or as background projections used to insight into energy
\par
problems.
\par
Typical outputs of these models are price or quantity
\par
projections for energy products as shown in Figure 5. However,
\par
the specific projections are of much less interest than the
\par
sensitivity analysis results that show, for example, how
\par
future U.S. oil imports vary under different assumptions as in
\par
Figure 6.
\par
