Process Models
\par
The process models linked together by the system need
\par
satisfy the network conventions only as to definition of the
\par
price and quantity flows on the linkages and certain other
\par
conventions related to the solution algorithm. In practice,
\par
however, a set of generic process models is used. The generic
\par
process models represent generic resource extraction industries,
\par
conversion technologies such as electric power and synthetic
\par
fuels technologies, transportation of energy products, and
\par
end-use conversion technologies. These models are made spe-
\par
cific by providing data or process parameters, such as capital
\par
costs, thermal efficiency, and construction lead time.
\par
Generic process models have been developed at various
\par
levels of detail; the modeler selects the level of detail
\par
appropriate to his problem. All of the process models
\par
establish economic relationships between prices and quantities
\par
of outputs and inputs over time. Within a process model, con-
\par
- 120 -
\par
struction of capital facilities is distinguished from the
\par
operation of those facilities. In addition, the changes in
\par
the design of facilities over time and with respect to prices
\par
are modeled. Thus each vintage of plant can be distinguished,
\par
and explicit technical models of production are utilized. The
\par
financial structure of the firm is also characterized in a
\par
generalized equilibrium model. Behavioral rules for invest-
\par
ment, operating, and pricing decisions are usually based on
\par
profit-maximization (but need not be) and are endogenously
\par
determined at all levels.
\par
In modeling nonrenewable resource processes, economic
\par
rents (Hotelling rents) associated with exhaustible resources
\par
such as oil and gas are calculated on the basis of producer
\par
and consumer expectations of future prices.
\par
Markets are characterized as perfect, imperfect, or reg-
\par
ulated using available generic process logic. Competitive
\par
and centrally planned markets can be represented. Unlike
\par
linear programming models, the share of the market demand
\par
allocated to various supply sources can be characterized as
\par
smooth functions of the relative prices of each source, thus
\par
avoiding the bang-bang behavior of linear programming alloca-
\par
tions.
\par
Methodology
\par
The methodology used to construct these models has been
\par
called generalized equilibrium modeling even though market
\par
disequilibrium is often represented in the models (Cazalet,
\par
1977; Cazalet, 1979). Mathematically, a generalized equil-
\par
ibrium model is a large system of simultaneous nonlinear
\par
equations or relations and unknowns. The basic approach
\par
used is similar to the Gauss Seidel successive approximations
\par
method normally used for the solution of linear systems of
\par
equations (Synfuels Interagency Task Forces, 1975). In this
\par
method, an initial guess at the solution is made, and succes-
\par
sive improvements in the solution are made until each equation
\par
in the model is satisfied. Convergence of the procedure is
\par
greatly enhanced by salving the relations and variables in
\par
the order that most rapidly reduces the errors in satisfying
\par
each relation. The procedures have been effectively employed
\par
in dozens of generalized equilibrium models often with 100,000
\par
or more fully simultaneous nonlinear equations.
\par
Applications of Generalized Equilibrium Energy Models
\par
The major models and studies carried out using generalized
\par
equilibrium modeling systems are listed in Table 1. In some
\par
cases, the models are used to directly assist in public policy
\par
decision making, such as in 1975 when a generalized equilibrium
\par
model was used to analyze U.S. synthetic fuels strategy
\par
(Synfuels Intieragency Task Force, 1975). The analysis ult-
\par
imately influenced Presidential and Congressional decisions.
\par
In other cases, the models are used by such organizations as
\par
the U.S. Department of Energy and its National Laboratories,
\par
the Tennessee Valley Authority, and the Electric Power Research
\par
- 121 -
\par
Institute for energy policy and R\&D planning and analysis.
\par
Generalized equilibrium modeling studies have been published
\par
in Cazalet and others (1976), Stanford Research Institute
\par
(1977), National Academy of Sciences (1978), Sussman and
\par
others (1976), Henry and others (1979), Sussman and Rousseau
\par
(1978), and Balson and Barrager (forthcoming).
