\par
SUMMARY OF THE INTEGRATED APPROACH
\par
The essence of integration is to tailor the models to
\par
the analysis of specific decision problems or a class of
\par
related decision problems. All variables and relationships
\par
that influence the evaluation decisions should be explicitly
\par
- 122 -
\par
represented in an integrated model; detail that does not
\par
influence the evaluation of decisions should be eliminated
\par
from the integrated model.
\par
An integrated approach should explicitly represent un-
\par
certainty using sensitivity and probabilistic analysis. A
\par
modular approach is necessary when precoded submodels are
\par
assembled or activated to rapidly create new models. Other-
\par
wise the cost of tailoring a model to a decision problem
\par
can be excessive. Methodologies, as well as models, must
\par
be integrated. Most modeling methodologies can be viewed
\par
ultimately in terms of systems of equations. When these
\par
equations have special structure (linearity, diagonal depend-
\par
ency), special techniques can be used. However, an integrated
\par
model should begin with the formulation of the problem (spe-
\par
cification of the equations). Techniques for solving the
\par
equations should take advantage of model structure and not
\par
impose a structure on the model.
