LINKING LARGE-SCALE MODELS AND
\par
ENERGY-ENVIRONMENTAL CASE
\par
P. W. House
\par
Director, Office of Technology Impacts, Office of the Assistant Secretary for
\par
Environment, U.S. Department ofEnergy, Washington, D.C., USA
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
This paper is a discussion of the linking of the two major assessment models used
\par
by the Department of Energy (DOE): The Project Independence Evaluation System
\par
(PIES)--recently renamed the Midrange Energy Forecasting System (MEFS)--and the
\par
Strategic Environmental Assessment System (SEAS). PIES is used to project energy
\par
and economic forecasts. SEAS then uses these forecasts to predict environmental
\par
impacts over the same time period. These linkages are made at the national and
\par
regional levels.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Project Independence Evaluation System (PIES); Strategic Environmental Assessment
\par
System (SEAS); Department of Energy (DOE); Regional.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Environmental Impacts of Energy Policy
\par
Ever since Habitat in Vancouver, B.C., in the Seventies, environmental issues have
\par
been formally recognized as transnational or global. The concerns in the environ-
\par
mental area include those where the international implications are obvious such as
\par
acid rain, C02, and the pollution of rivers that flow across national boundaries.
\par
Of less obvious significance are cases where differential pollution control laws
\par
encourage the relocation of "dirty" industries to countries who will accept the
\par
pollution. Or, a world-wide concern for the preservation of site of particular
\par
national beauty or species of wildlife that is threatened by extinction.
\par
Recently, still another issue has come upon the scene which also has significant
\par
international ramifications: energy. Because there is clearly a limited supply of
\par
energy sources (particularly oil) and because these supplies are not evenly distri-
\par
buted across the earth but are controlled by individual sovereign states who are
\par
generally free to dispose of them as they see fit. The politics which have tran-
\par
spired between the nations that have energy and those that do not has been front
\par
page news.around the world for years now.
\par
- 125 -
\par
- 126 -
\par
Finally, the interplay between the strategies of environmental and energy policies
\par
has been of varying intensity, ranging from high interest in specific issues at
\par
specific locales to more generalized concern at the global level.
\par
This paper will be about the efforts made at the U.S. Department of Energy to ad-
\par
dress the environmental impacts of energy policies. More particularly, the regional
\par
implications for the environment. The models discussed which have been used to per-
\par
form the regional environmental impacts are the Mid-Range Energy Forecasting System
\par
(formerly known as PIES) and the Strategic Environmental Assessment System (SEAS).
\par
Both models are of the "top-down" variety.
\par
Top-down analysis suggests methodologies which attempt to get a global perspective
\par
of the relationship of variable in question and other variables which might im-
\par
pact it or that it impacts. Normally, the analyses produced are relatively holistic
\par
in nature and attempt to investigate strategic questions of national policy. Data is
\par
collected at a relatively course grain (State or Federal region as opfosed to city
\par
block, for example) and relationships between the variables given certain actions are
\par
represented by average coefficients of groups of people, processes, or actions rather
\par
than specific activities. Such models are used both for research and analysis pur-
\par
poses to test the first order consequences of various strategy alternatives and to
\par
look for unanticipated effects of various courses of action.
\par
Before going on to discuss these models in detail, I would like to degress and talk
\par
to some points concerning global issues and models in generaL The various confer-
\par
ences held at IIASA and elsewhere around the world ( in addition to numerous articles
\par
and books on the subject) attest to the belief that it would be useful to add the
\par
analytic power of models to the investigation of global issues. Unfortunately,
\par
several factors have continually surfaced which have frustrated the attempt to really
\par
use the potential power of these analytic techniques. One, for example, is the lack
\par
of consistently, high quality data bases throughout all nations. In some countries,
\par
data is just not available or, if available, of questionable validity. is
\par
that the relationships between the economics of the various nations are so poorly
\par
understood that the specification of them in a model is impossible--or only possible
\par
at so high a level of aggregation that the results are of questionable value. Fi-
\par
nally, it is clear that there is no real client for the results of global models.
\par
The lack of such clientele means that both the specification and the use of the re-
\par
sults is left to chance and the builder of the model.
\par
Obviously, I am not going to be able to do much with this collection of problems in
\par
any definitive fashion. However, the use of a national-to-regional linkage of model
\par
results has some analogy to global-national models. The problems of linking models
\par
and data bases were addressed as these models were put together and the experience
\par
gained can be used by those interested in global issues in thinking through their
\par
issues.
\par
Let us now turn to a description of the model systems (PIES and SEAS) and to their
\par
regional components.
\par
- 127 -
\par
Project Independence Evaluation System (PIES) Model*
\par
Description: General
\par
By way of background, Project Independence was the generic title used to describe
\par
activities organized by the Federal Energy Administration (FEA) (and later the De-
\par
partment of Energy (DOE)) in the continuing development and implementation of a
\par
national energy policy for the United States. The PIES system generates planning
\par
estimates of possible energy system variables, taking into consideration the effect
\par
of relative prices, the potential for fuel substitution, and the technological con-
\par
straints inhibiting increased energy supplies.
\par
Among the several objectives considered by the PIES model in analyzing alternative
\par
strategies are:
\par
1. Price sensitivity -- the impact of relative prices.
\par
2. Fuel competition -- the substitution of one energy source for another.
\par
3. Technology -- the variation of the production and conversion technologies
\par
within the energy system.
\par
4. Resource limitations -- the physical capacities and other resource limitations.
\par
S. Externalities -- the by-products or side-effects of energy production and
\par
consumption.
\par
6. Economic impact -- the interaction with the total energy production and
\par
consumption.
\par
7. Regional variations -- the uneven geographic distribution of energy production
\par
and consumption.
\par
8. Dynamics -- the lead times, capacity in previous periods, and other time-
\par
dependent conditions.
\par
9. Modularity -- the possibility of expansion of major components of the energy
\par
system or the introduction of new components.
\par
10. Judgment -- the capability of incorporating information and making approxima-
\par
tions and estimates.
\par
Thus, the energy system is depicted as a kind of network wherein production, pro-
\par
cessing, conversion, distribution, transportation and consumption activities take
\par
place. The prices and capacities for these activities are presented so as to be con-
\par
sistent with the dual objectives of preserving price sensitivity and recognition of
\par
potential constraints on the system. This framework is structured by separating the
\par
supply and demand sectors.
\par
Details of the National-Level PIES System
\par
The supply system which produces, processes, and converts activities becomes nodes
\par
within an energy network. These nodes are then connected with links which depict
\par
the transportation and distribution system.
\par
Potential energy production system activities are described by a set of supply curves,
\par
identifying prices to be paid and nonenergy resources to be consumed at each possible
\par
level of operation.
\par
*Peter W. House, and John McLeod, Large Scale Models for Policy Evaluations, New
\par
York: John Wiley and Sons, 1977.
\par
- 128 -
\par
Important physical or technological limitations affecting the production of energy
\par
are described within the transportation and distribution network. The refining and
\par
conversion sectors are included as the intermediate nodes of the network, each with
\par
a description of its capacities and conversion technologies. The refining and con-
\par
version activities are joined with the demand or consumption sectors through an addi-
\par
tional set of transportation and di8tribution links. These links are subject to
\par
capacity restrictions which can be modified if sufficient key resources are available
\par
(when compared with alternative uses in the production or distribution of energy).
\par
The supply curves, conversion technologies, transportation possibilities, costs and
\par
resource requirements are produced by supply submodels of the evaluation system and
\par
linked within this framework.
\par
The estimates of the demand for energy are produced by a demand model. The demand
\par
for energy products takes place in different geographical regions and varies with
\par
energy prices. The choice of the activities to be described as demand is somewhat
\par
arbitrary, but can be thought of as the final demands for energy. Fuel substitution
\par
is simulated by the demand model through the empirical development of the relation-
\par
ships between demands and relative prices.
\par
Given the prices, resource requirements, and capacity constraints, an integration
\par
model is utilized to construct a feasible set of energy flows that satisfies the
\par
final demands for energy. The energy supply activities and the demand prices are
\par
adjusted during this market simulation to obtain a balanced solution which is in
\par
equilibrium. This equilibrium balance is a point where no consuming sector would be
\par
willing to pay more for an additional unit of any energy product and no supplier
\par
would provide an additional unit of any energy product for less than the prevailing
\par
market prices.
\par
For an arbitrary selection of prices and demands, the least cost balancing solution
\par
may not be an equilibrium solution: there is no means of guaranteeing that an ar-
\par
bitrary price for estimating product demand will be equal to the prices at which
\par
the product is supplied. However, the necessary adjustments in the prices are iden-
\par
tified and these adjustments are repeated until the equilibrium solution is obtained.
\par
The supply demand and equilibrium balancing components describing the energy system
\par
are combined with models of the economy, assessments of nonenergy resource avail-
\par
ability, and report writers that evaluate energy solutions in terms of the environ-
\par
mental, economic, or resource impacts. Econometric, simulation, accounting, and
\par
optimization models are included in this system, each exploiting special capabilities
\par
for the relevant components of the problem. The model of macroeconomic activity is
\par
the well-known system developed by Data Resources, Inc.,* and is one of the several
\par
major econometric models of the United States economy.
\par
The resource constraint elements consist of a large data base which records the co-
\par
efficients of demand for nonenergy resources for the alternative energy activities
\par
included in the system. These coefficients are employed to construct constraints
\par
for the equilibrium solution of capacities are known or to prepare ex-pose summaries
\par
for off-line evaluation of potential bottlenecks. The supply model component consists
\par
of a variety of procedures used to construct stepwise approximations to the energy
\par
supply curves. These range from nonautomated engineering analysis in the case of coal
\par
to the complex software of the National Petroleum Council for the estimation of the
\par
supply of oil and gas.
\par
The demand model is a behavioral econometric model. The demand for energy is not
\par
represented in terms of its final use as energy but as the demand for the variety of
\par
energy products in the using sectors. The structure of demand and substitution is
\par
* Data Resources, Inc., The Data Resources Quarterly Model, Econometric Forecasting
\par
System. Equation Specifications, varied dates.
\par
- 129 -
\par
postulated in terms of relative prices and the parameters of these relationships
\par
are estimated using econometric techniques. The resulting system consists of over
\par
800 behaviora relationships governing the demand for energy in 40 product and
\par
sectoral combinations.
\par
The demand, supply, and resource assessments models are combined through the series
\par
of programs, constituting the integrating model. A partial equilibrium is obtained
\par
by balancing prices and quantities for all energy products. Through this model,
\par
the quantity flows and prices of the equilibrium energy sector solution provide the
\par
input to a series of evaluation or report writing programs that relate the solution
\par
to particular problems under consideration. These reports, over 20 in total, range
\par
from an executive summary of the energy balance to detailed classification and com-
\par
pilation of associated environmental residuals, water requirements, or implied non-
\par
energy resource usage for those inputs which have not been considered directly in
\par
the energy system network.
\par
The Strategic Environmental Assessment System (SEAS) Model
\par
Description: General
\par
SEAS has been described* as "a system of special-purpose models linked to a macro-
\par
econometric model and an interindustry input-output of the United States economy."
\par
Economic forecasts to estimate pollutant levels and associated abatement costs, such
\par
as pollution abatement benefits, energy demands, solid waste generation and associ-
\par
ated recycling, land use requirements, mineral use and virgin stock status, processed
\par
ore inventories, transportation demand, and relative commodity price changes, are
\par
used by other models in this system. At present, this model is used to build on
\par
economic and energy policies and forecasts made primarily by government agencies
\par
in order to predict pollution loadings, costs of cleanup and associated direct and
\par
indirect economic impacts which are likely to flow from these policies and forecasts.
\par
In the Department of Energy, SEAS has a very particular use. In DOE's Office of
\par
Environment, SEAS is used to predict the environmental impacts of energy policies
\par
and scenarios produced by other parts of the Agency. To accomplish this, it is
\par
necessary for the analysts to map the energy and economic forecasts onto the SEAS
\par
system of models and to then use the system to calculate the environmental residuals
\par
and impacts from these forecasts.
\par
The model, as presently used: forecasts up to a 25-year period; produces forecasts
\par
at national and regional levels; provides for regional variation of industrial pollu-
\par
tants; allows for variation in assumptions regarding pollution control regulations;
\par
has a detailed breakdown of economy for environmental analyses; emphasizes pollution
\par
from energy industry in conjunction with major polluting nonenergy industries; permits
\par
users to trace effects throughout the system; has a very rich data base containing a
\par
comprehensive set of environmental, energy, and economics variables and parameters;
\par
and finally, allows for extensive user options, particularly in output report selections.
\par
SEAS is designed to operate as a set of integrated submodules which can be run either
\par
alone or as a part of the overall system. These submodels can be divided into four
\par
general areas: energy, economics, regional disaggregation, and environment. Within
\par
each of these major categories, there is a further breakdown. The economics section
\par
has four models: energy investment, interindustry input/output, sector disaggregation
\par
and pollution abatement costs. Energy consists of three submodels: electric utilities,
\par
industrial combustion and an energy supply network similator. Regional disaggregation
\par
is carried out using a single subsection, and the environmental portion uses four
\par
models: transportation, residual generator, land use, solid waste, and environmental
\par
quality indicators.
\par
* Peter W. House, Trading Off Environment, Economics, and Energy, Lexington Books,
\par
Lexington, Massachusetts, 1979.
\par
- 130 -
\par
Details of the Component Submodels
\par
The Economic Models
\par
The SEAS economic forecasts are developed at several levels of detail. The first,
\par
the macroeconomic forecast, provides the general parameter projection on an annual
\par
basis through 1985. These general values include employment, general production
\par
sector output volumes, personal consumption, disposable income, capital investment,
\par
etc., and allow projections of the general subaccounts of the GNP. Basic structural
\par
relationships between these economic variables are developed from Bureau of Labor
\par
Statistics and the Commerce Department models and data bases. The macroeconomic
\par
model used in SEAS was developed and is maintained by Chase Econometric Associates
\par
(CEA).
\par
The second level of forecast is the calculation of interactions among industries
\par
in order to meet the levels of output in the demand forecast of the macro model.
\par
This economic input/output model provides the yearly economic projections for 185
\par
sectors of the economy, and statistics for each sector such as employment, output
\par
sold for final demand, total output, durable goods and construction expenditures,
\par
export, imports and inventories. The model, called INFORUM, is a rigorous input-
\par
output system that provides great detail and balanced accounting of sectors and
\par
to the final consumers. The INFORUM model is maintained by a staff at the
\par
University of Maryland and linked to the macroeconomic model by CEA.
\par
The third level of forecast detail of economic activity is produced by the SEAS
\par
design and system teams and deals with procedures that break the specific (185)
\par
industry forecasts into richer information (where required) for specific analysis
\par
questions, using off icial estimates from the Departments of Commerce, Agriculture
\par
and the Interior for more detailed economic activity levels. This last level of
\par
detail in SEAS is readily expanded and now represents three times the detail
\par
available from the original INFORUM sectors.
\par
A fourth level is found in the abate cost model, ABATE. ABATE estimates the in-
\par
vestment, operating, and maintenance costs associated with abating the emissions
\par
of air and water pollutants by each economic sector. It also feeds back to INFORUM
\par
the increased monetary and goods demands placed by pollution control investment and
\par
operating purchases on the industrial sectors which supply construction materials
\par
and labor, abatement equipment, chemicals for abatement, energy sources, and oper-
\par
ating manpower requirements. INFORUM uses this information to rebalance its fore-
\par
casts of sector economic activity. Nonindustrial consumption and disposal pro-
\par
cesses, such as utilities, sewage treatment plants, and commercial and residential
\par
space-heating consumption, are handled by INFORUM, RESGEN, and ABATE in concert,
\par
in the same manner as the industrial sectors.
\par
The fifth and final level of detail is in the areas of energy investment. The
\par
energy supply demand, the unit capital costs, the forecasted construction period,
\par
and the materials requirements are loaded into the model as inputs. These are
\par
then fed into an energy investment model which calculates the impact on the com-
\par
position of GNP, output of selected sectors, the indirect energy use for capital
\par
resources, and the sectoral employment. These calculations are then fed back into
\par
INFORUM, used to update the economic forecasts.
\par
The Energy Models
\par
There are three energy models in SEAS: electric utilities, industrial combustion,
\par
and an energy supply network similator (ESNS). Each is considered briefly in turn.
\par
- 131 -
\par
The electric utilities model forecasts the air emissions and fuel consumption
\par
levels for electric utilities operating before 1976, provide state-level location
\par
of electric utilities operating after 1976 by fuel type, and regionalize state
\par
electric utility fuel mixes to the county level. It considers various fuel types
\par
and takes as inputs items such as total demand for electric power, fuel mix,
\par
capacity factors and retirement factors, and environmental considerations.
\par
The industrial combustion model has as its purpose the regionalization of industrial
\par
fuel combustion and the calculation of regional air pollution emissions. It takes
\par
as inputs future coal distribution, boiler size, various assumptions as to the state
\par
of coal technology and relevant environmental factors. The forecasts are made at
\par
the county level and normally aggreLated to a higher level, such as Air Quality
\par
Control Region (AQCR).
\par
Finally, the energy system network similator (ESNS) is designed to determine the
\par
environmental/economic consequences of a specific energy policy the candidate
\par
energy policy is translating into changes in both the demand and energy and ad-
\par
justments in various stages of the fuel cycle. It is divided into sections
\par
containing energy demands, intermediate activities and energy supply sectors.
\par
The network detail is determined by environmental issues, investment implications,
\par
and the calibration requirements fostered by mapping it onto other models (i.e.,
\par
PIES). The changes forecast by this model lead to adjustments in other models
\par
in SEAS; for example, in the energy activities both nationally and regionally,
\par
economic forecasts and pollution forecasts.
\par
The Environmental Models
\par
There are five environmental modules in SEAS: transportation, residuals genera-
\par
tion (RESGEN), land use, solid waste, and environmental quality indicators. The
\par
transportation module forecasts the demand for automobiles, buses, trucks, rail-
\par
roads, and airplanes in terms of miles traveled for both passenger and freight
\par
transportation purposes, using the Department of Transportation forecasted total
\par
of miles traveled. It estimates the annual volume of controlled emissions produced
\par
by mobile sources. It has the ability to vary emission standards by model year,
\par
average age of the auto fleet, model splits for SMSA, occupancy ratios, and the
\par
MPG of various model years. It can also take into consideration the transportation
\par
control plans of regions.
\par
The residuals module estimates annual emissions of air and water pollutants and of
\par
solid wastes for the most significant polluting industries. It estimates first
\par
the potential environmental emissions before abatement, and then the emissions
\par
actually reaching the environment; the latter depends on the degree of pollution
\par
abatement in each sector and its subprocesses. The released emissions include not
\par
only the untreated primary pollutants, but also the significant secondary pollu-
\par
tants produced by the pollution treatment processes themselves (e.g., sludges).
\par
The Solid Waste-Recycling Module estimates the amounts of solid wastes from non-
\par
industrial sources, the expected method of disposal, and the costs associated with
\par
each method. For incineration and open-burning disposal methods, it also estimates
\par
the annual levels of air pollution emissions. Recycling levels are applied to the
\par
product classes which comprise significant elements of the solid waste stream to
\par
calculate total amounts of materials available for recycling and the levels
\par
actually recycled.
\par
The Land Use Module estimates amounts of land used in broad categories as a func-
\par
tion of forecasts of economic activity. It also calculates nonpoint sources of
\par
water pollution. For nonpoint rural pollution sources, such as agriculture, con-
\par
struction, forestry, mining and drilling, it calculates soil loss, sedimentation,
\par
- 132 -
\par
and pesticides/fertilizer loading. For urban runoff as well as naturally occurring
\par
pollution sources, it forecasts levels of air, water, and solid waste pollution
\par
emissions.
\par
The final module, environmental quality indicators, is designed to translate air
\par
emissions and water effluents into indicators of environmental quality to allow
\par
regional and temporal comparisons of air and water pollutant levels. For air
\par
pollution, for example, the indicators used are concentration ranges and ratios,
\par
emissions per square mile, emissions per cubic mitre of ventilated flow, and
\par
population at risk for both TSP and SOX.
\par
The Regional Disaggregation Model
\par
In order to enrich the forecast of economic and environmental impacts, this model
\par
provides a forecast of distributions of industry outputs and the associated envi-
\par
ronmental emissions to a number of geographical subdivisions. The subdividions
\par
include states, SMSA (Standard Metropolitan Statistical Areas), river basins, air
\par
quality control areas and "basic economic areas." The OBERS projections developed
\par
by the Departments of Commerce and Agriculture are employed in this model.
\par
The analyses performed outside the computer system are most important and are made
\par
up of two elements: (1) the analysis used to develop the experimental design of
\par
alternate scenarios to modify the specific input data for each scenario, and
\par
(2) the analysis of the computer outputs to show impacts of scenario changes,
\par
and, hence, insight into probable effects of specific national policies and regu-
\par
lations, including public and institutional reactions.
\par
The purpose of the regionalization model is to allocate the present and future
\par
industrial, commercial, and consumer activity to lower levels of aggregation.
\par
The model has four separate regionalization modules: (1) a permanent share file
\par
based on OBERS* projections; (2) an ability to manually override these coeffi-
\par
cients; (3) a dynamic forecasting capability; and (4) special regionalization
\par
programs for utilities, boilers, and coal mining.
\par
The model has several special features which make it unique as a regionalization
\par
system. It reflects changing pollution control technologies or standards over
\par
time. It also incorporates changing process technologies within sectors over
\par
time. In addition to allowing for differences in pollution per unit of output
\par
for the same sectors in different regional locations, it allows user override
\par
of all the above. Finally, it automatically identifies secondary residuals
\par
produced by the treatment of process streams.
\par
Using a Top-Down Approach to Energy and Environmental Policy
\par
Having described SEAS and PIES** and alluded to the fact that such systems could be
\par
and have been used to forecase regional impacts of energy policy and environmental
\par
impacts, it remains for us to demonstrate how these are done. We shall provide
\par
examples from the National Energy Plan required of the Department of Energy every
\par
two years. In general, the energy and economic forecasts used are accepted as given
\par
