\par
The largest domestic supply increases are expected in coal and nuclear energy. The
\par
production from coal would more than double under the low price scenario and almost
\par
triple under the high price scenario. Nuclear energy was expected to increase by a
\par
factor of roughly eight to nine times by the year 2000.
\par
Under the low price scenario, energy imports would nearly triple from 1975 to 2000.
\par
At higher oil prices, however, imports would actually decline by about 30 percent.
\par
Total U.S. demands for energy in 2000 are projected to be about five percent lower
\par
under the high oil price scenario than under the low price scenario.
\par
Given the energy future projected, the environmental analysis could proceed as in
\par
the earlier case having first received estimates of the economic and regional impacts
\par
of the Plan. These latter estimates were not a part of the published results.
\par
Following are some selected summary environmental impacts that were published
\par
given the above forecasts.
\par
Air Emission Trends.
\par
Air pollutants are released from a variety of processes. Of interest to the Depart-
\par
ment of Energy are those emitted by the combustion of fossil fuels by electric
\par
utilities and industrial boilers and the operation of motor vehicles. Because of
\par
improved controls and stricter emission standards, emission levels for three of
\par
these criteria air pollutants--hydrocarbons, carbon monoxide, and particulates--
\par
are generally projected to remain at the 1975 levels or to decrease between 1975
\par
and 2000 under both scenarios. Sulfur oxide levels in 2000 are anticipated to re-
\par
main at the 1975 level under the low world oil price scenario and decrease slightly
\par
under high world oil prices. Nitrogen oxide emissions are expected to increase by
\par
a third in both the low and high price cases over the twenty-five year period
\par
examined due to increased fuel burning.
\par
Each pollutant and each region were subjected to more detailed analyses. Rather
\par
than try and summarize them all further and instead of attempting to either repro-
\par
duce or paraphrase the results here, we have chosen to give a capsulized discussion
\par
of air emissions of TSP--total suspended particulates.
\par
Projections of particulate emissions at the Federal Region Level show a fairly sub-
\par
stantial decrease between 1975 and 2000 in the Mid-Atlantic, Southeast, and Great
\par
Lakes Regions, where emissions were highest in 1975. This decline is due to the
\par
forecasts compliance of existing energy facilities and industrial sources with SIP
\par
emission standards. Decreases are also seen in the New York-New Jersey and Central
\par
regions for similar reasons. The regions that exhibit increases in TSP emissions by
\par
2000 were those with fairly low emissions in 1975. In general, reductions in TSP
\par
are projected to occur in highly industrialized regions that had fairly substantial
\par
emission levels in 1975 as a consequence of the use of better emission controls,
\par
whereas increases were seen in regions with fairly low 1975 TSP levels as a conse-
\par
quence of increased industrial activity or coal use.
\par
The regional trends indicate that air quality improvement may occur in areas, such
\par
as the Great Lakes Region, which are highly industrialized and, as of 1975, emitted
\par
large quantities of TSP. The projected increases do not appear alarming at the
\par
Federal Region level, but may present localized problems within some regions.
\par
Similar assessments were carried out by pollutant for air, water, solid waste, and
\par
nuclear emission streams. With this as an example, let us just briefly summarize
\par
some of the other environmental impacts found by the analyses.
\par
- 135 -
\par
Water Consumption Trends.
\par
Water consumption by the Nation's energy industry is expected to increase almost
\par
four-fold between 1975 and 2000. This increase would be caused primarily by growth
\par
in steam electric generation by coal and nuclear fuels facilities and by more
\par
stringent water pollution control regulations, which encourage the use of cooling
\par
towers which consume larger quantities of water than current once-through cooling
\par
practices.
\par
Water Pollution Trends.
\par
National efforts to improve water quality during the last decade are beginning to
\par
show general success. Energy use would be responsible for the major share of point-
\par
source releases of total dissolved solids, sulfates, and other pollutants. Releases
\par
from coal mining and processing operations and electric utilities would be the main
\par
source of total dissolved solids and are projected to contribute about three-fourths
\par
of the national sulfate effluents by 2000.
\par
Energy industries would be responsible for a minor share of the other water pollu-
\par
tants analyzed (biochemical oxygen demand, total suspended solids, nutrients, and
\par
chlorides). Total suspended solids from energy sources are projected to decrease
\par
slightly over the projection period while nutrients and biochemical oxygen demand
\par
from energy sources are projected to remain at their 1975 levels.
\par
Solid Waste Trends.
\par
Solid wastes, currently produced at a level of 487 million tons per year, include
\par
industrial solid wastes and sludges, municipal refuse, and municipal wastewater
\par
treatment sludges. Noncombustible solids remaining after conversion of solid fuels,
\par
and sludges, from pollution control devices and from wastewater treatment currently
\par
represent about 17 percent of all solid wastes. That portion resulting from fuel
\par
use is expected to almost triple during this time as a result of electric utility
\par
and industrial use of coal. For example, sludges from flue gas desulfurization
\par
systems show a dramatic growth due to the projected impact of revised regulations
\par
for new plants. Another area of projected solid waste of interest to the Depart-
\par
ment of Energy is oil shale retorting. This emerging fuel source is expected to
\par
result in from 372 to 537 million tons of noncombustible wastes annually by 2000
\par
for the low and high world prices, respectively.
\par
Regional Trends.
\par
The discussion of environmental trends presented above has emphasized expected
\par
changes at the national level. Regional variations will occur that depend on local
\par
energy supply and demand patterns. Figure 1 is a map summarizing the major regional
\par
environmental trends anticipated under both world oil prices cases.
\par
Lessons Learned
\par
In one sense, this paper demonstrates that it is technically possible to link the
\par
very large policy model, PIES, used to help make national energy policy, with SEAS,
\par
the also large environmental model, in such a fashion that impact assessments are
\par
possible. Further, to the extent that regional forecasts of national policy have
\par
meaning when done from the top down, it is equally technically possible to do
\par
regional environmental impact analyses at the same level of detail as the energy
\par
analysis is done. Although these findings are of some comfort to those who would
\par
have empirical analyses as a major input into national energy/environmental policy,
\par
it should be noted that even this significant step was not done without cost, nor
\par
is the process complete. Let us look to a couple of the more obvious technical
\par
difficulties yet to be addressed adequately.
