\par
One class of problems yet to be adequately resolved is those associated with the
\par
building of any model: the calibration of the data used in the model. It is well
\par
known that if the assumptions used in the model data are not consistent and if the
\par
data is not calibrated to a single norm, then the resulting output is so much garbage.
\par
This requirement for consistency is made more difficult when, in addition to the data
\par
calibration requirement, there is a need to assure that the assumptions made in the
\par
models to be linked are consistent also. The larger the models being linked (larger
\par
in the sense of numbers of substantive forecasting unique variables), the more dif-
\par
ficult this requirement is to meet.
\par
With two models the size of PIES and SEAS, this goal is far from being realized.
\par
The econometric models used to generate the national growth, for differ-
\par
ent and even though the SEAS model does not actively use its economic forecasting
\par
capability, the mapping of the PIES forecast onto it is made more difficult because
\par
of the difference in the subroutines themselves. For example, SEAS uses INFORUM
\par
which is basically an I/O model with about 190 sectors. These sectors are further
\par
disaggregated with several hundred subsectors. The PIES model uses DRI's Macro-
\par
Economic Model which is built on a very different logic structure from the I/O
\par
approach. Consequently, the mapping techniques chosen require that the GNP final
\par
demand components provide the bridge between PIES and SEAS models in the economic
\par
areas. The most obvious loss this engenders is in the area feedbacks into the
\par
system. One specific case is that PIES does not explicitly account for the expendi-
\par
ture of pollution control on interindustry decisions including energy costs and
\par
investments. Although this is only about 2\% of the total GNP, it is a significant
\par
percent of specific industrial sector expenditures. The electric utility sector,
\par
for example, is important to both energy and environmental policy makers, spending
\par
about 10-15\% of its annual expenditures on such items. However, the linkage between
\par
PIES and SEAS is much more straightforward when national and Federal region levels
\par
of energy demand and supply are used to drive SEAS.
\par
In another area, the regional forecasts are subject to criticism. In the beginning
\par
of this paper, I discussed top-down vs. bottom-up forecasting. Proponents of the
\par
latter school hold that no macro-modeling technique can possibly accurately reflect
\par
the richness and uniqueness of individual regions of our Nation. The methods used
\par
in the PIES and SEAS models are based on logic structures which generally assume
\par
partitioning of data according to historical patterns. A few of the more analyti-
\par
cally sophisticated subroutines may go so far as to adjust these regional shares
\par
according to some preordained decision rules. There is some truth to allegations
\par
that such rules cannot possibly represent reality and suffer most as one becomes
\par
more regionally-specific or moves further ahead in time.
\par
At DOE, we have recognized this deficiency but have not been able to solve it in a
\par
strictly'empirical fashion. Although space does not permit complete exposition of
\par
the technique, I can capsulize it as a non-computer interface.
\par
Specifically, the state-level forecasts of both the energy supply and demand and the
\par
resulting environmental impacts are sent to six research centers located across the
\par
country who, using a mix of models, interviews, and other analyses, add local per-
\par
spectives, institutions, and laws to the model forecasts. In some cases, then, feed-
\par
back causes a change in the models themselves; in most it merely enriches the analysis
\par
of the data.
\par
It is doubtful whether any forecasting or impact system can ever be designed or
\par
perfected to cover all the difficulties discovered. On the other'hand, the steps
\par
taken to link these two very large systems are significant indeed and the recogni-
\par
tion that problems exist and solutions are sought is equally encouraging. There
\par
is still a long way to go. These experiences, however, are of enormous benefit
\par
for those in the global policy or modeling communities as the technical questions
\par
addressed are normally transferable (albeit often only in a conceptual sense) to
\par
the similar difficulties with creating analytical tools to deal with global issues.
\par
FORMULATION OF LONG-RANGE
\par
POWER-ENGINEERING DEVELOPMENT
\par
CONCEPT
\par
V. I. Danilov-Danilyan and A. A. Ryvkin
\par
Institute for Systems Study of the USSR Academy of Sciences, USSR
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
The attempts to peer into a distant future and, as far as possible, influence its
\par
structure are already becoming more and more serious and persistent. The fact that
\par
the long-range forecasts are developed at present not by futurologists alone but by
\par
experts well-known for their significant contribution to natural, economic and so-
\par
cial sciences, illustrates the urgency of the long-range development problems fac-
\par
ing mankind. It is no accident that energy and power resources are in the focus
\par
of attention and have turned out to be the area of the most intensive research in-
\par
volving sophisticated techniques and methods of modeling and systems analysis.
\par
Today as never before it becomes abundantly clear that the future of mankind, real-
\par
ization of its ancient aspirations to progress and justice are closely linked with
\par
penetration into the secrets of a huge laboratory--nature, with the introduction of
\par
material resources, developed and being developed in this laboratory, in social and
\par
economic turnover. Accordingly, the energy transformed from its natural state to a
\par
force controlled by man assumes a special role, becomes one of the decisive compo-
\par
nent of his might and to a great measure determines the reproduction capacities of
\par
the entire human civilization.
\par
The current acute energy problems have arisen due to a shift from a long and seemingly
\par
the only possible period of "energy collection", wherein man made a merit of "un-
\par
sealing" the natural energy endowment and intensive drawing of deposits that have
\par
been accumulating for thousands of years, to a new era of mass production and rational
\par
exploitation of natural energy sources. This transition has just begun; it is by
\par
no means definite as the natural "depots" are far from exhausted and much that is
\par
stored there is just starting to attract our attention. But from social and economic
\par
points of view, however, one is safe in saying that the transition process has gained
\par
momentum and is in full swing as the distant future state of world power engineering
\par
is determined by today's ideas and developments carried out on their basis. And
\par
these ideas call for an ever-increasing intrOduction into the economic circulation
\par
of "inexhaustible", i.e., sufficiently large energy sources.
\par
We observe that the very consumerism with regard to nature is more and more rejected
\par
by progressive mankind, though it is still strong in economic and social structures
\par
tracing its origin to the past. However, the influence of these structures on the
\par
current decision making, including the decisions relating to distant future, is very
\par
strong. They influence also those serious and undoubtedly unbiased research projects
\par
- 139 -
\par
- 140 -
\par
that underlie the long-range strategic decisions in the area of power engineering.
\par
In this paper we shall discuss a number of generally accepted conceptual hypotheses
\par
which are behind numerous research projects of both power engineering and of a more
\par
general nature, hypotheses which, routine though they are, do not seem indisputable
\par
at all. We shall touch upon only those assumptions, however, that essentially af-
\par
fect the selection of development strategies and whose rejection may question many
\par
a conclusion on alternative ways of overcoming natural difficulties of the interim
\par
period.
\par
CONCEPTUAL HYPOTHESES BEHIND THE PRESENT NOTIONS
\par
a) Commenting on the reasons for the world energy crises that burst out in the
\par
early 70s, the majority of experts place the emphasis on the fact that the era of
\par
cheap energy, that led to wasteful energy consumption, has gone. The sources of
\par
cheap energy are limited, and the exploitation of new deposits and application of
\par
new types of energy imply greater expenses. Such an approach to describing reality
\par
is quite customary for economists. Most of them have developed their theories on
\par
the basis of the hypothesis of the limited nature of resources suitable for economic
\par
exploitation and of primary utilization of the best factors of production available.
\par
Formally this hypothesis is realized in economic models in different ways, in par-
\par
ticular, by choosing the production functions, linking the output and resources, so
\par
that the first partial derivatives with respect to each of the resources are posi-
\par
tive and the second partial derivatives, negative. Thus, the use of such production
\par
functions means that, first, the set of considered economic factors is strictly
\par
fixed. Second, the very factors are internally homogeneous, and their values form
\par
a linear space wherein the production function is specified. Third, the use of
\par
such functions for long-range forecasting presupposes additional hypotheses both
\par
on commensurability of factors and output at considerably separated time intervals
\par
and on the nature of changes of the factor space or the very functional dependence
\par
over time. The production functions theory, developed in econometrics, employs
\par
approaches based on the notions of the so-called technology space where the tech-
\par
nological advance is interpreted as the change in the function parameters over time.
\par
In any case, in the existing mathematical models of economic growth technological
\par
progress is interpreted either as an explicit function or as an implicit dependence
\par
on the structural parameters of the model, which is a function of time too. Be
\par
that as it is, this implies relationships affecting the rate of output, though the
\par
influence may be indirect (e. g., through the dynamics of a real and registered de-
\par
parture of factors, etc.).
\par
b) The ideas concerning ways of solving energy and more general problems of world
\par
development are based mostly on the hypothesis of reproducibility of advanced coun-
\par
tries' technologies in developing countries. Accordingly, a conclusion is made that
\par
the developing countries should mostly adopt and utilize the scientific and tech-
\par
nological achievements offered by the advanced countries. The logic of this approach
\par
leads to an assertion that the industrially developed countries have to extend their
\par
technologies, including the energy ones, to backward countries while the latter,
\par
through economic relations established with the former, must not only compensate
\par
the rendered assistance with supplies of natural resources but also assist further
\par
technological progress in the developed world. As a result, the assistance in
\par
technologies and equipment rendered by the consumers of natural resources is pro-
\par
claimed to be a decisive source of industrial growth in the third world countries
\par
for the whole period of their adaptation to the established forms of international
\par
division of labor. According to the proponents of this concept of scientific and
\par
technological progress the countries most rich in natural resources will henceforth
\par
specialize in their extraction while the consumers will continue specializing in
\par
development of new technologies.
\par
- 141 -
\par
c) In the condition where the reserves of the most effective energy resource--oil--
\par
are near to exhaustion, the entire world should exercise a gradual transition to new
\par
energy sources consuming the remaining reserves of cheap energy just for maintaining
\par
the economic activity during the interim period. This thesis is partially derived
\par
from the two preceding ones and is supported by the arguments on the growing inter-
\par
dependence of all the partners in the world economy, which is becoming ever more
\par
unified.
\par
d) The established trends in the dynamics of world market prices are quite natural
\par
and reflect the objective socio-economic laws. In other words, at the time of
\par
cheap energy one could observe a justified tendency towards a relative reduction
\par
of raw material prices while the new stage, involving the more expensive energy,
\par
must bring about a reverse trend to higher natural resource prices.
\par
e) The current world price structure is in principle (i.e., with regard to the con-
\par
tinuing trends) acceptable for comparing the costs and effects of extensive future
\par
introductions of various new technologies, and the economic effect calculated on the
\par
basis of these prices may be used for comparing the long-range decision alternatives.
\par
f) The traditional economic measures like the Gross National Product, Gross Domes-
\par
tic Product, National Income and so on are reliable guidelines for measuring the
\par
real long-run dynamics of national economies of countries and regions. The economy
\par
featuring higher rates of macro indicators growth catches up with the economy de-
\par
veloping at a slower rate.
\par
g) The comparison of economic evaluations (criteria) may be used for operational
\par
purposes too, i.e., as sufficient conditions for making actual economic decisions
\par
at the macro level.
\par
ANALYSIS OF TRADITIONAL HYPOTHESES AND ALTERNATIVE ASSUMPTIONS
\par
Almost all of the hypotheses mentioned above are perceived by the majority of fore-
\par
casters as laws of socio-economic development reflecting not only the generally
\par
accepted point of view but the essence of the actual development processes. But
\par
are these assertions truly indisputable, is the line of development that is the
\par
logical consequence of these assumptions as straightforward as is implied?
\par
a) The assumption concerning the limited volume of resources suitable for economic
\par
application and the primary utilization of the best factors of production, essen-
\par
tially limits the area of possible development options.
\par
First, this fixes the structure of the considered technological factors. The in-
\par
troduction into the analysis of quite new resources, in particular of new energy
\par
sources, is possible only through an application of respective changes to the old
\par
structure. In other words, alteration of the object when its development in cer-
\par
tain directions has slowed down while the new dimensions, non-essential for the
\par
former structure, tend to grow, is either not taken into account at all or (at best)
\par
is accounted for as applied to the space specified by the structure that existed at
\par
the time of forecast development. Wi thin the framework of long-range and super-
\par
lonq-range analysis this hypothesis may exert a strong distorting influence.
\par
Second, the dynamic models based on such hypothesis, even if the dimensions of the
\par
considered space are set a priori, are capable of reflecting just "trifle" changes
\par
interpreting the general development trajectory as an integral curve which appeared
\par
as a result of stagewise current decisions. Hence, long-range (structural) laws
\par
are identified with the sequential realization of short-term interactions similarly
\par
to the solution of differential equations.
\par
- 142 -
\par
Third, our notions of economic systems are based to a measurable degree on the
\par
postulate of the existence of not only fixed finite space, within which the object
\par
develops, but of the presence in this space of a normal coordinate system. It is
\par
worth pointing out that this normalization exists objectively independently of the
\par
state of the considered system. Unfortunately, this postulate is applicable only
\par
to a short-range analysis as the system of cost economic measures depends essen-
\par
tially on the reproduction structure of economy, i.e., not only on the effort ex-
\par
pended by each of the independent elements of the system but on how these elements
\par
are isolated from each other, and how they interact in production, exchange, and
\par
reproduction.
\par
b) The hypothesis of the global reproducibility of technologies, prevalent in the
\par
advanced capitalist countries, closes up with the assumption of the only way of
\par
technological advance in our civilization, of independence of technical change from
\par
the existing reproduction structure of world economy. To prove the flimsiness of
\par
such hypotheses, suffice it to analyse the situation that would have formed in the
\par
world should the developed capitalist countries be completely cut off from raw
\par
materials in other parts of the globe. What are the major structural changes that
\par
would have followed the deep crisis? Probably, many a directions viewed at present
\par
as technological progress would have been abandoned as wasteful and inefficient.
\par
But Why are they not considered as such today?
\par
In order to fully comprehend the problem we may analyze the possible consequences
\par
of yet another assumption. Let us assume that it became possible to instanta-
\par
neously disseminate the most advanced technologies, possessed for example by the
\par
US, and set up enterprises equipped with respective machinery and staffed with
\par
highly-skilled personnel allover the world. Would the gap between industrialized
\par
and developing countries be bridged in this case? No, there is no solution to this
\par
problem by this route. The problem is not confined to the fact that some countries
\par
have already passed certain stages in their development while others have still to
\par
make it. The most sophisticated technologies reproduced across the world will re-
\par
main idle as the type of technical advance we observe has taken place in specific
\par
socio-economic environments.
\par
Numerous achievements of technical thought in the US were realized owing exlusively
\par
to specific features of the reproduction structure of the world economy. This
\par
structure happened to be stratified and its lower layers, connected with extraction
\par
of natural resources, shift at an ever-increasing rate to the developing countries
\par
while the upper ones involving development of technologies and manufacture of
\par
equipment turn to the most advanced countries. The laws of the "free trade" world
\par
are such that they dictate the selection of the only long-range development strategy
\par
based on the concept of comparative costs. Following this strategy implies that
\par
each region must strive to specialization in the field where its efficiency will
\par
be the highest, i.e., the developing countries should henceforth concentrate on
\par
turning out raw materials and semi-products and the advanced ones, on the develop-
\par
ment of technologies and manufacture of equipment. With surprising unanimity
\par
this point of view is shared by the majority of authors of well known global pro-
\par
jects. M. Mesarovic and E. Pestel give it the form of "humanism" when speaking
\par
of organic growth. G. Kahn upholds it from the positions of a blunt reactionary
\par
pointing out that though the US does allocate a third of world resources in its
\par
interests, it is only fair as the destiny of civilization is determined by those
\par
who are capable of ensuring the technological change and, consequently, it is only
\par
to the benefit of mankind if the poor and unskilled do dirty and unpleasant work
\par
for them. Now we are interested, however, not in the social aspects of such a
\par
long-range development strategy but in the feasibility of other strategies that
\par
do not lead to stratification of the reproduction structure of the world economy
\par
but provide conditions for the comprehensive development of each participant of
\par
international division of labor and integrated employment of his natural and other
\par
- 143 -
\par
economic resources. The strategies oriented at internal evaluation of developing
\par
countries and the establishmenn of an economic reproduction structure adequate to
\par
this problem, will result in a different type of world technological progress. In
\par
such conditions the technical advance in the most developed capitalist countries
\par
will no longer rest upon a huge resource base. Incentives will appear for an ac-
\par
celerated transition to technologies penetrating into new, deeper and wider spheres
\par
of nature.
\par
The assistance from the industrialized world to the developing countries must not
\par
at all aim at reproduction on the part of the latter of or, as referred
\par
to at present, adaptive technologies. The assistance will really enhance the de-
\par
velopment only if the developing regions are assisted in setting up their own re-
\par
production structure and, in this case, if they find ways of satisfying their basic
\par
requirements with internally reproducible technologies.
\par
c) It is difficult to oppose the thesis of the necessity for a gradual transition
\par
to new energy sources as it becomes clear that the traditional sources can no longer
\par
meet our requirements. Today, however, the world does not yet face the shortage of
\par
oil. It is available and will surely last for some 20 to 30 years of intensive ex-
\par
ploitation. That is why the problem of gradual transition to new sources must be
\par
approached simultaneously with the questions of how best to dispose of the available
\par
reserves and whether the entire world will undergo this transition at one time.
\par
At present the advanced capitalist states pursue a policy aimed at immediate con-
\par
sumption of the best reserves available in the world, as this is allegedly the
\par
optimal strategy for the globe as a whole. Indeed, such a strategy would have been
\par
really optimal if the world economy were first, a unified nonconflicting whole and
\par
second, consisted of homogeneous regions. As none of the conditions are met in
\par
reality the proclamation of such a strategy equals the slogan: let us first eat
\par
yours together and then each will eat his own. The arguments that in exchange for
\par
their resources the developing countries receive technologies (as a rule intended
\par
for extractive industries or primary processing of raw materials) and other modern
\par
commodities (mostly for current consumption) do not change the essence of the
\par
matter.
\par
New ways of energy generation will inevitably be discovered. But irrespective of
\par
the combination of specific technologies that would be applied, the general law
\par
inherent in the progress in the man-nature relationship will remain intact. Ac-
\par
cording to this law the transition to the next technological level involves gener-
\par
ally a sharp rise in capital investments and creation of much more powerful arti-
\par
ficial systems supporting the man-nature interaction. The of the system
\par
introduction measured in terms of increased output must, with their wide-spread
\par
application, surpass the system size growth. Only in this case may one speak of
\par
progress. Thus, at the initial stages of new technology introduction the capital
\par
intensiveness of produce will grow (if it may be reliably measured) and then as
\par
technology becomes commonly applied, it will go down. No doubt that out of the
\par
multitude of proposed alternatives mankind will choose only those that, first,
\par
require feasible systems consistent with the given level of development and,
\par
second, offer lower capital-intensiveness of the generated energy compared to its
\par
current level.
\par
At the same time it may be near to impossible for the developing countries to con-
\par
struct energy transfer systems which require sizable initial investments. Hence,
\par
it is these countries rather than the advanced capitalist regions that need the
\par
remaining reserves of traditional energy sources at the initial stages of develop-
\par
ment. Accordingly, the gradual transition to new energy sources may be exercised
\par
only with due account of the mentioned need of developing countries. They will
\par
be unable to meet this demand within the present structure 0: world economic re-
\par
lations as the few countries possessing huge oil reserves realize their exports in
\par
