d,e) Recently economists have extensively discussed the long-run dynamics of raw
\par
material and finished product prices. Some presume that in conditions of inflation
\par
the relative raw material prices tend to go down as the finished product prices,
\par
primarily machinery, grow at a faster rate. Others, following the official price
\par
indices, assert that the dynamics of raw material prices and those of finished
\par
product prices are approximately equal and, moreover, the raw material prices rise
\par
even at a faster rate.
\par
In our opinion the comparison of price indices for different aggregate products under
\par
the conditions of the stratified reproduction structure of the world economy, given
\par
its extreme heterogeneity and high trade barriers, can hardly clarify the nature of
\par
exchange between regions with different levels of development.
\par
The mechanism of price formation for new equipment in the advanced capitalist coun-
\par
tries is such that the price is set on the basis of the cost of the additional liv-
\par
ing labor replaced by this equipment, on the basis of the effect gained through its
\par
utilization (replacement of existing technologies) and with due account to costs
\par
incurred in its manufacture. The contribution of each of the mentioned factors
\par
may be different depending on specific market conditions. The present patent sys-
\par
tem enables the manufacturers to set the price on the basis of replacement and ac-
\par
cordingly gain a sizable inventor's profit. Further on, as production expands, the
\par
price would not actually increase which, given the general inflationary background,
\par
would seem as its reduction while the mass of profits will grow on as the price
\par
considerably exceeds the level of costs. What is more, the frequent artificial
\par
innovations practiced in the majority of industrialized capitalist states (linked
\par
with the introduction of some additional quality or the simple additon of a new
\par
part to the product) and frequent revisions of the base for calculating the price
\par
indices indicate that the dynamics of indices cannot cover the considerable share
\par
of quite inflationary price growth.
\par
The developing countries are in a quite different position. They have either no
\par
alternatives at all to the purchases of equipment (when they start or run national
\par
production) or, at best, solve this problem by comparing the costs involved in the
\par
manufacture of machinery on their own. In any case the arguments in favor of pur-
\par
chasing the equipment usually dominate. Irrespective of the equipment price its
\par
cost would be included in the cost of products manufactured using it, etc. Should
\par
it be used for resource output, then the raw material cost will again be taken into
\par
account in setting the price of new equipment and so on. However, the raw material
\par
producer and its consumer, i.e., equipment manufacturer (or any other modern product)
\par
are in different conditions. The cost structure of the former is open for study
\par
while that of the latter is a commercial secret. That is why the comparison of
\par
actual efforts of partners (be it possible in principle) is quite impossible in
\par
reality. It is clear though that the decisive word in the inflationary race rests
\par
with the second partner as it is he who is less controlled. But when the second
\par
overstates his effort at a faster rate, we may but spot it when reviewing the dy-
\par
namics of price indices. Moreover, as the equipment cost is a component of raw
\par
material price, while the equipment itself is rated as a new product, these indices
\par
may even Show a faster rise in raw material prices.
