ABSTRACT
\par
The approach to evaluating the long-range energy development, which has been
\par
worked out in the USSR, stipulates the application of a set of mathematical models.
\par
Recent efforts provided inclusion to that set of some new elements designed for
\par
the assessment of scientific and technological progress on the energy sector,
\par
namely: models for the assessment of parameters of an alternative to a new energy
\par
technology; models for choosing between alternatives to a new technology; an
\par
aggregate model for the assessment of Research and Development strategies. The
\par
paper describes the approach to the build-up and structure of these models. Be-
\par
sides, the problems of interfacing of new models within the whole set of energy
\par
models are discussed.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Scientific and technological progress; R+D program; R+D strategy; R+D planning
\par
and forecasting; new energy technology; life-time; research; design, testing; com-
\par
mercialization; R+D modeling; model interactions.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
The growth of demand for technological advancement over the coming transitional
\par
period of the energy systems development, as well as the anticipated growth of
\par
capital and material intensiveness of energy, are expected to reinforce the inter-
\par
dependence between patterns of energy supply systems development and energy R+D
\par
activities. Such an observation was made in particular in recent papers by
\par
Hafele and others (1975), Kononov (1977), Makarov and Melerttyev (1973), and
\par
Melentyev (1979).
\par
Undoubtedly this requires a more comprehensive assessment of energy R+D strategies
\par
in the process of energy planning and forecasting, which would provide consistency
\par
of R+D strategies with the general energy systems development, and vice versa. In
\par
this paper we describe a possible approach to planning and forecasting in the
\par
sphere of scientific and technological activities on the energy sector which would
\par
meet such a The approach implies wide modeling of the systems under
\par
consideration.
\par
- 149 -
\par
- 150 -
\par
In the first section of the paper the place of R+D activities in the whole process
\par
of energy development is determined, and their principle characteristics are given.
\par
Also, a classification of tasks of R+D planning and forecasting is proposed,
\par
stressing the connections of these tasks to those related to projecting future
\par
energy development.
\par
The next section reviews the scope and functions of the models we propose for use
\par
in energy R+D assessment. A special set of models is to be used for completing
\par
each R+D planning/forecasting task. The composition of the models and their
\par
interactions, as well as their connections and feedbacks with other models for
\par
energy development are described.
\par
The final section of the paper shows the place and role of the proposed models in
\par
the assessment efforts of long-term R+D strategies.
\par
PROBLEMS OF R+D PLANNING AND FORECASTING
\par
In a somewhat conventional wayan R+D strategy may be determined as an over-
\par
lapping set of concrete R+D programs developing over time, each of which is de-
\par
scribed by a set of indices characterizing its results and the process of fulfill-
\par
ment. Here, an R+D program means a large project resulting in the creation of a
\par
new complex energy technology (like the MHD-plant) but not of a particular equip-
\par
ment (like a turbine or generator).
\par
The results of an R+D program are described by the following two groups of output
\par
indices:
\par
- techno-economic parameters of a new technology (e.g., energy
\par
efficiencies, specific capital investments);
\par
- scales of deployment of a new technology measured in rates of its
\par
installed capacities over time.
\par
The process of fulfilling an R+D program may be described in terms of projects,
\par
tasks, and problems to be completed and solved, and of the necessary resources.
\par
our purposes let us consider this process as a sequence of different stages of
\par
life-time of a new energy technology from its conception to the end of operation,
\par
namely; fundamental and applied research; projecting and designing; experimental
\par
and industrial testing; and commercialization (large-scale production of equip-
\par
ment for the new technology, construction and operation of new facilities).
\par
Let us agree that the stages of the life-time of a new technology from research to
\par
reaching the projected levels of its output characteristics were regarded as a part
\par
of the R+D activities, and that the stages from development of the leading commer-
\par
cial sample to the start of operation of a given technology were regarded as part
\par
of the industrial activities. In this case the major target of R+D planning and
\par
forecasting is an identification of a prclerable R+D strategy, including time-
\par
schedules of different R+D activities of R+D programs to be fulfilled
\par
throughout a given time horizon, their timing, and the distribution of the limited
\par
economic resources among them) and their expected results (levels of output
\par
characteristics and scales of deployment of new technologies).
\par
Keeping in mind the approach to the R+D programs described above, the process of
\par
energy development can be considered as a continuous flow of life-cycles of
\par
different technologies. The heterogeneity of this flow makes the whole problem of
\par
energy planning and forecasting significantly more complex, and necessitates its
\par
decomposition into a set of comparatively homogenous interconnected tasks. Let us
\par
take the USSR scheme for planning and forecasting as a basis for such a decomposi-
\par
tion.
\par
