\par
years; they should therefore he assessed by their impacts to the ESS development
\par
within the framework of the medium-term forecasting of energy production in order
\par
to provide for proper and timely preparatory measures in related industrial sectors.
\par
In this connection the most important tasks to be undertaken at the long-term R+D
\par
planning stage include: making an ultimate choice between the alternatives for
\par
each new technology design and construction, as well as for the values of its out-
\par
put characteristics; elaboration of technological tools, terms, and scales of
\par
manufacturing of the necessary equipment; distribution of responsibilities among
\par
expeEimental industries and, partially, projecting firms; estimation of necessary
\par
scales and terms of capital construction and reconstruction in related industrial
\par
sectors, and distribution of capital investments and building capacities among
\par
them.
\par
For technologies of class 3, which are shifting from research and development to
\par
industrialization within some 20 years and are thus covered by long-term fore-
\par
casting of ESS development, the problems to be solved consist in the choice of the
\par
preferable alternatives for R+D programs on the basis of a wide sensitivity
\par
analysis. Consequently, the follwoing tasks should be considered the major ones
\par
in R+D forecasting: narrowing down the range of studied alternatives
\par
for a new technology (to 2-3 alternatives); specifying the ranges of possible
\par
values of the output characteristics and the schedules of the work; identification
\par
of the key problems which require enforced efforts (e.g., development of new
\par
materials and manufacturing technologies); estimation of the scales of commercial-
\par
ization of new technologies with complex accounting for the demands of ESS develop-
\par
ment and constraints from machinery and other related sectors; substantiation of
\par
the distribution of projecting and, partially, research capacities among R+D
\par
programs for the first 5 years; forecasting of the patterns of development of
\par
eneEgy related industrial sectors; forming the R+D programs in those industrial
\par
sectors supporting the energy sector, including distribution of research and pro-
\par
jecting capacities among them. The completion of these tasks results in the
\par
formation of the long-term energy R+D strategies.
\par
The technologies of class 4 are expected to reach the stage of industrial develop-
\par
ment in more than 20 years from now (that is beyond 20001. Here, the major point
\par
is to explore and specify possible ways of utilization of new scientific con-
\par
ceptions in the field of energy. Accordingly, the ta3ks to be completed at the
\par
stages of long- and super-long-term R+D forecasting include: analysis of possible
\par
directions of research for the creation of new energy technologies on the basis of
