When looking at the above sets of R+D planning/forecasting tasks from the viewpoint
\par
of choosing between strategic directions of the scientific and technological pro-
\par
gress, it becomes apparent that the tasks connected with class 3 of energy
\par
technologies and long-term forecasting of the ESS development (see darker boxes in
\par
Table 2) and, to some extent, those connected with class 3 of energy technologies
\par
and medium-term forecasting of the ESS development are of primary significance.
\par
So, in the following we will center our attention on those tasks, while considering
\par
a set of models for R+D assessment.
\par
A SYSTEM OF MODELS; DESIGN AND STRUCTURE
\par
A systems approach to energy (see, in partiCUlar, W. Hafele and others (1975),
\par
Kononov (1977), Marakov and Melentyev (1973), Melentyev (1979)) and to planning
\par
of R+D activities (developed, e.g., by Irikov, Shabunin and Sheverov (1975),
\par
Komkov (1978), Martino (1977), Pospelov and Irikov (1976), Yanch (1979)) is avail-
\par
able to solve methodological problems connected with the assessment of energy R+D
\par
strategies. The approach which has been developed in the USSR on this basis
\par
stipulates the modeling of the main internal and external connections in the
\par
energy field. However, the models are regarded as an auxiliary tool which ar-
\par
ranges and amplifies the creativity of the specialists.
\par
Let us first outline briefly the system of models used for long-term energy
\par
assessment (see Figure 1). The core of the system is the dynamic model for the
\par
ESS development (M-4 in Fig. 1) which describes major internal (technological,
\par
territorial and other) energy links. Its chief objective is the estimation of
\par
impacts of new technologies to the total ESS development and an analysis of the
\par
effects of the limited extent of high-grade fuel resources.
\par
A dynamic inter-sectoral model is intended to:
\par
