\par
chief objective is the elimination of bottlenecks arising in energy production.
\par
This requires recognition and simulation of both, internal links in R+D activities
\par
which integrate R+D programs into a unified system and provide possibilities of
\par
management, and external links, primarily on the energy production sector.
\par
Until recently energy modeling tended to neglect these requirements. A typical
\par
approach was that estimates of output characteristics were made for each new tech-
\par
nology; then an energy supply model was investigated to yield an approximate
\par
estimate of the scale of commercialization of new technologies. The adequacy of
\par
such an approach is questionable because of the underlying assumptions that R+D
\par
programs are independent, that the economic resources for the programs are un-
\par
limited, and that the R+D activities are independent of the general energy develop-
\par
ment.
\par
There are the following factors (links) combining separate R+D programs into a
\par
system:
\par
l. Links through common resources for all R+D programs. The resources necessary
\par
for completing all potentially feasible and useful projects are several times
\par
larger than those available. Deficiency of resources significantly affects the
\par
list of projects under way and their time-schedules.
\par
- 155 -
\par
2. Links through cornmon elements of different technologies. Such elements make
\par
R+D programs mutually useful in terms of cost and time reductions, a growing
\par
probability of success and improved values of some output characteristics (espe-
\par
cially specific costs) of new technologies due to unification, standardization,
\par
and facilitation of serial production.
\par
3. Links through the final objective which may either lead to a competition of
\par
technologies (if they perform similar functions) or to their mutual benefit by
\par
elimination of individual disadvantages (e.g., a combination of nuclear power
\par
plants and hydrogen production plants).
\par
The existence of controlled factors which determine the course of scientific and
\par
technological progress is useful for organizing an iterative of inter-
\par
action of R+D models and the model for ESS development, in such a way that the
\par
system of R+D models provides the ESS model with data on the potential techno-
\par
logical advancement (a list of feasible technologies and their characteristics),
\par
while the data on the demand of the energy sector for new technologies are pro-
\par
vided by the energy supply model for the R+D models (with due regard to the in-
\par
ternal and external connections of the ESS).
\par
Let us first outline the possible structure of R+D models and their functions, and
\par
then take a more thorough look at the system of these models (see Figure 1).
\par
M0del M-5.li recognizes the physical and technological links among the subsystems
\par
(elements) of technology "i". Its major functions are: (1) estimation of the
\par
values of the basic characteristics for a given design; (2) search for a design
\par
which offers a better set of values of output characteristics.
\par
At the initial stages of fulfilling an R+D program, the models based on morpho-
\par
logical methods of analysis can serve as M-5.li models (Yanch, 1979), while at the
\par
final stages the models optimizing the technological parameters (such as the one
\par
described by Levental and Popyrin, 1972) may be used.
\par
Model M-5.2i, describing the development process of technology "i", considers the
\par
internal technological and organizational links in performing its activities. Its
\par
functions are: (1) to provide an optimistic estimate of the time of completion of
\par
the i-th program and a pessimistic estimate of its resources requirements; (2) to
\par
establish the resources-to-time dependences; (3) to schedule the activities in the
\par
development of the i-th technology.
\par
Model M-5.3 for R+D strategies considers the above mentioned links between R+D
\par
programs to be implemented while neglecting the alternatives to an R+D program.
\par
Its objectives are: (1) specifying time and cost estimates with regard to the
\par
links between R+D programs; (2) allocation of resources among R+D programs and
\par
estimation of their start-up and completion dates.
\par
Model M-5.4 for R+D programs considers the links between new energy technologies
\par
and their development options. Its chief function is to narrow down the number
\par
of alternatives to an R+D program, taking mutual utility of R+D programs and the
\par
limited resource situation into consideration.
\par
For a detailed description of the M-5.2i, M-5.3, and M-5.4 models, their under-
\par
lying algorithms, and their results, see Irikov, 1977; Irikov and Larin, 1978,
\par
Irikov, Shabunin, and Sheverov, 1975; Komkov, 1978; pospelov and Irikov, 1976.
\par
