If the criterion of achieving the objective cannot be set as a scalar function in
\par
the M-5.3 and M-5.4 models, the approach given below is helpful in projecting
\par
selection of and resource allocation to R+D programs. The constraints and links of
\par
the models are all retained. To replace the goal function, a number of meaningful
\par
assumptions are made on the properties of the decisions and procedures within a set
\par
of decision rules, which can be corrected in the course of a dialogue and result in
\par
the choice of the most preferable decision. For the discrete problem of project
\par
selection the tools of a set theory and the Boolean choice functions (Irikov, 1977;
\par
Pospelov and Irikov, 1970) are used. For the continuous problem of resource
\par
allocation, the simpler and more effective algorithms of the gradient for multi-
\par
criterial choice (Irikov and Larin, 1978) lead to iterative dialogue procedures
\par
which converge into the most preferable decision in five through seven iterations.
