\par
Now let us consider the systems of models intended to assist in completing the R+D
\par
planning and forecasting tasks, which were pointed out in the previous section
\par
(Figure 1).
\par
In medium-term R+D forecasting all the models mentioned are used. The M-S.li and
\par
M-S.2i models are used to produce alternative projects for each i-th technology
\par
and estimate their output characteristics. The M-S.4 model selects and transmits
\par
to the energy supply model technological data with regard to the mutual utility of
\par
technology alternatives and the limited resource situation. The energy supply
\par
model is used for further selection of alternatives to technologies, taking the
\par
connections of the ESS into consideration, as well as for specification of the re-
\par
quirements for the output characteristics of the new technologies. The M-S.3 model
\par
specifies the schedules of completion of the selected projects in view of the con-
\par
straints on available resources.
\par
In long-term R+D planning the same set of models is used but the resources are
\par
measured more thoroughly, and an ultimate selection of alternatives to each tech-
\par
nology is made. Note that the M-S.3 model is also used for scheduling the respec-
\par
tive activities.
\par
Now, in medium-term R+D planning the ultimate choice between the alternatives has
\par
already been made and only the "resources-versus-time" task has to be undertaken;
\par
the application of the M-S.4 model, or of the M-S.li models is no longer necessary.
\par
So, in this case only part of the models is used, as shown in Figure 1.
\par
When solving the problems of long-term R+D forecasting (and those of super-long-
\par
term forecasting) an analysis of resources and organizational links in R+D activi-
\par
ties does not seem necessary; estimation and approximate selection of alternatives
\par
may be made on the basis of the energy supply model runs. This leads to the imple-
\par
mentation of only a simplified version of the M-S.l models for the estimation of
\par
the utmost values (ranges) of output characteristics, informally accounting for
\par
mutual utility of projects with cornmon elements.
\par
APPLICATION OF THE SYSTEM OF MODELS
\par
Let us consider briefly the possible implications of R+D models in the long-term
\par
R+D assessment. The place of the models and their informational links may be seen
\par
by passing through the major stages of the informal procedure for forming the R+D
\par
strategies.
\par
1. Analysis of "bottlenecks" in energy development including those of the long
\par
term (the M-l, M-2, M-3, and M-4 models are used).
\par
2. Formation of the list of potential technological innovations as possible tools
\par
of filling anticipated gaps in energy development. Estimation of output charac-
\par
teristics and completion dates of R+D programs (simplified versions of the M-S.li
\par
and M-S.2i models).
\par
3. First-order estimation of scales and dates of commercialization of new tech-
\par
nologies (interlinking of the M-4 model with the M-S subsystem of R+D models to be
\par
used).
\par
4. Detailed specification of foreseeable alternatives to R+D programs. Specifica-
\par
tion of the structure of the system and mutual utility of the programs (detailed
\par
versions of the M-S.4 and M-S.2 models and the M-4 model).
\par
S. Analysis of possibilities in related industries (first of all in machinery).
\par
Updating cost estimates, taking account of the unification of equipment and the
\par
- 158 -
\par
needs of the industries in capital formation and technological advancement (asso-
\par
ciated sectoral models).
\par
6. Updating the scales and anticipated dates of commercialization of new technol-
\par
ogies after allocation of the limited resources (interaction of detailed versions
\par
of the M-4 and M-5 models).
\par
7. Composition and approval of R+D strategy and development of five-year- and
\par
long-term R+D plans (detailed versions of the M-5.3 and M-5.2 models).
\par
The procedure is iterative. For coordination of the inputs and outputs of R+D
\par
models and the energy supply model a converging algorithm is available, which re-
\par
lies on a sensitivity analysis of the M-4 model. At each iteration a solution of
\par
the energy supply model results in finding the gradient of its objective function
\par
in the field of input characteristics, whose components are used as coefficients
\par
in the objective function of the model for selection of R+D alternatives. The
\par
solution of the latter model is put back into the energy supply model in an up-
\par
dated form, and a converging procedure is run.
\par
CONCLUSIONS
\par
This approach to the assessment of scientific and technological progress contri-
\par
butes to achieving the main objective of forecasting, i.e., to a reduction of
\par
uncertainty of possible outcomes by a more realistic incorporation of the follow-
\par
ing groups of factors: links which define R+D activities as a system; criterial
\par
links with the sphere of energy production; and the possibility of stimulating
\par
the credibility of expert information through the use of models for active systems.
