ABSTRACT
\par
The original formulation of the Brookhaven energy system models was directed
\par
toward technology assessment for new and competing energy technologies. The
\par
Hudson-Jorgenson econometric model was originally formulated to identify the
\par
economic impacts of energy futures where energy use projections departed marked-
\par
ly from historical trends. The two models were married so that the feedback ef-
\par
fects of energy and nonenergy demand levels and nonenergy prices genera ted by
\par
the economic model could be reflected in the technology and fuel-mix-selection
\par
solutions of the energy model. In turn, the engineering-based energy costs, en-
\par
ergy prices, and capital requirements for energy systems characterized in the
\par
energy model are used to override the econometric estimates based on historical
\par
data in the economic model. Recently, the coupled models have been used to ad-
\par
dress questions concerning the macroeconomic impacts of accelerating the imple-
\par
mentation of renewable energy technologies in the United States. Of particular
\par
interest were the scenarios where 1) renewables were included which cost more
\par
than conventional alternatives now and in the future, and 2) some renewables
\par
that are initially more costly are characterized by a learning curve so that in
\par
time their costs come to equal conventional alternatives. A further analysis
\par
was done for the first case (renewables always more expensive) under conditions
\par
where 1) the incremental costs were paid by the government through deficit fi-
\par
nancing, and 2) the incremental costs were paid by consumers. This paper will
\par
present the formulation of the analysis using the combined energy system -
\par
economic model and the results of the study.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Technology assessment; renewable energy; energy-economy interactions; models;
\par
hierarchical system.
