INTRODUCTION
\par
This paper presents the results of a study of energy/economy interactions, which
\par
were generated by the concurrent solution of three complex models. The study is
\par
discussed in the context of the limitations and constraints imposed on the anal-
\par
ysis by the decision-making environment. Thus, the operational process of de-
\par
fining and clarifying the needs of decision makers is explici tly presented.
\par
Descriptions of the analytical models, the nature and current status of the
\par
linkages between the models, and plans for future development are documented
\par
elsewhere (Groncki and Marcuse, 1979). The underlying approach is that of a
\par
hierarchical, decomposed structure with the outputs of one model fed as inputs
\par
to the others. The methodologies for the interfaces have evolved over time and
\par
the transformations have become routine. The analyst still oversees the inter-
\par
faces to be sure that the models behave reasonably during the solution routine
\par
and to reduce the risk of surprise outcomes.
\par
Most systems analysis reports are on work performed at universities or research
\par
centers and are complete in the sense that all possible pathways and outcomes
\par
are examined and discussed. In contrast, much work performed directly for deci-
\par
sion makers and that enters into the decision process is not reported at alL
\par
When it is documented, it is often presented as completely finished even though
\par
much of the polishing and detailed analysis was done after the fact. This paper
\par
presents a policy analysis that was performed under conditions that are, unfor-
\par
tunately, all too typicaL Not all of the analytical questions raised during
\par
the study were completely addressed; the "optimal" formulation of the problem
\par
was discarded in favor of a "timely" one; and several shortcuts were employed to
\par
provide the decision maker with access to the best available information When he
\par
needed it, instead of fully refined information when it was no longer useful,
\par
This paper presents an unembellished account of a study that was executed under
\par
severe constraints.
\par
The study was commissioned by Bert Mason and Greg Ferris of the Solar Energy
\par
Research Institute (SERI) and in response to a request from Bennett Miller,
\par
Director, Solar and Geothermal Energy Programs, U.S. Department of Energy (DOE).
\par
Mason played a seminal role in translating the concerns of Miller into the for-
\par
mulation of the analysis. The technological data were provided by Ferris. The
\par
study was executed by a team consisting of Richard J. Goettle IV, Paul J.
\par
Groncki, and Joan Lukachinski of Brookhaven National Laboratory; and Edward A.
\par
Hudson and David C. O'Conner of Dale W. Jorgenson Associates. The analysis was
\par
prepared for Bennett Miller to be used in testimony before the United States
\par
Congress in support of his program. The original presentation to the sponsor
\par
was in the form of an oral report accompanied by briefing charts. The only doc-
\par
umentation to date has been an unpublished research memorandum.
\par
BACKGROUND
\par
Departmental Policy Review
\par
In the fall and winter of 1978, the United States Department of Energy (DOE)
\par
launched a comprehensive departmental policy review (DPR) of solar energy op-
\par
tions. The DPR recommended a solar energy "maximum practical" implementation by
\par
the year 2000, wi th solar energy sources providing about 21. 5 quadrillion Btu
\par
(quads) of primary energy (fossil fuel equivalents). The DPR base case (which
\par
assumes no special solar initiatives above those already legislated plus the
\par
effect of higher energy prices) projects a solar sector supplying 14.7 quads of
\par
primary energy in 2000. In February 1979, the Office of the Assistant Secretary
\par
for Energy Technology in DOE asked SERI to assess the employment implications of
\par
- 163 -
\par
the DPR scenarios. It is widely believed that solar and conservation alterna-
\par
tives to fossil fuels are job creating, and thus, the possible effects of energy
\par
policies on employment are of primary concern to Congressional staffs. As dis-
\par
cussed below, this question was reformulated.
\par
Methodology
\par
The methodology, developed to analyze the effect of alternative energy policies
\par
and technologies on the U.S. energy and economic systems, involves the integra-
\par
tion of three different models (Groncki and Marcuse, 1979). A macroeconomic
\par
growth and interindustry model (econometric in nature) is used to estimate the
\par
aggregate energy/economy interactions and the long-run path of the economy
\par
(Jorgenson and Hudson, 1974). A linear programming process model is used to es-
\par
timate the least cost method of meeting specified energy service demands and
\par
captures the substitution possibilities within the energy system (Kydes and
\par
Rabinowitz, 1979). A disaggregate input-output model is used to assess the im-
\par
pacts of the aggregate energy/economy interactions on detailed industrial sec-
\par
tors (Tessmer et al., 1975; Fraser, 1978).
\par
Formulation of the Issue
\par
The original request was to examine the employment-generating effects of the DPR
\par
"maximum practical" solar implementation. The DPR scenario depicts the penetra-
\par
tion of solar technologies over the next twenty-five years so that the impact on
\par
the labor force occurs gradually during that period. Since the forces affecting
\par
the economy-wide demand and supply of labor far exceed the impact of a gradual
\par
substi tution of solar for conventional energy technolog ies, it is obvious that
\par
the direct and indirect impacts of the DPR scenario might be to change the occu-
\par
pational structure, the skill mix, and, to a small degree, the productivity of
\par
the labor force. The level of employment will be a function of the supply and
\par
demand for labor and a market-clearing wage rate. This does not mean that sub-
\par
stantial unemployment may not exist at any given moment during the period caused
\par
by either a non-market clearing wage rate that is slow to respond to the excess
\par
supply or a pool of functional unemployables (structurally unemploYed). Govern-
\par
ment policies designed to offset these types of unemployment must meet three
\par
tests: the expenditure stream must be timed such that it has a large and quick
\par
impac t, the occupational mix of those to be employed should conform to the mix
\par
of currently unemployed and the policy alternative must prove preferable to
\par
other short-run employment generating programs. Since the DPR initiatives do
\par
not meet all three conditions, the question of their aggregate, economy-wide
\par
employment effects is not relevant.
\par
Although the solar alternatives may generate an increase in demand for certain
\par
types of labor, the supply of this labor can only come from the labor pool
\par
available for all economic activity. Since a general equilibrium model incorpo-
\par
rates labor supply as well as demand, wage rates will adjust to clear the mar-
\par
ket. Hence, no excess labor is available and the additional demand must be met
\par
by diverting labor from other activities, and thus changing the mix of occupa-
\par
tional skills. Of course, if the DPR technologies increase labor productivity,
\par
wages will rise and additional labor will be forthcoming. If productivity is
\par
decreased, the reverse will happen. Only if long-run equilibrium is achieved
\par
with a chronic excess supply of labor will labor-absorbing technologies increase
\par
employment. However, under these circumstances policy makers could turn to any
\par
other labor-intensive ac tivity to absorb the excess labor. Excess labor supply
\par
may also result from structural unemployment, but there is little reason to
\par
- 164 -
\par
believe that solar technologies will eliminate this pool more effectively than
\par
other measures aimed directly at the structural problem.
\par
The sponsors were alerted to the fact that it is not meaningful to seek solu-
\par
tions to short-run problems through long-run policies nor to analyze shortrun
\par
responses with long-run policy models. The sponsor accepted the suggestion that
\par
two important labor-related issues should be considered: (l) the changes tha t
\par
are likely to occur in the sectoral and occupational structures of employment
\par
resulting from the DPR scenario; (2) the change in labor productivity and conse-
\par
quently in real wages associated with the enhanced solar scenario.
\par
CASE SPECIFICATION
\par
The solar penetrations for the base case and the DPR "maximum practical" cases
\par
were given. The life-cycle costs for solar technologies, supplied by SERI, were
\par
higher than the lifecycle costs of conventional technologies, even with rising
\par
fuel prices. It was assumed that the introduction of the higher cost solar
\par
technologies would be encouraged by a government subsidy or that consumers might
\par
voluntarily decide to pay the higher costs. On the basis of these assumptions,
\par
two cases were analyzed: "subsidy" and "user pays." A third scenario was cho-
\par
sen which is characterized by decreasing costs of solar technologies to levels
\par
making them competitive with conventional technologies. This analysis case is
\par
labeled "competitive."
\par
ECONOMIC IMPACTS
\par
Table 1 presents the values for GNP and its components for the base case and for
\par
the three DPR "maximum practical" cases. It is clear that if the cost of solar
\par
technology does not exceed that of conventional energy sources, economic activ-
\par
i ty is not affected. On the other hand if solar energy is more expensive, fu-
\par
ture economic output and the rate of growth of the economy are reduced. Al-
\par
though this reduction seems small, the cumulative effect of even a small annual
\par
percentage change can become quite large over a period of decades.
\par
Competitive Case
\par
The competitive case shows no change from the base case in GNP or its compo-
\par
nents. This is expected if the resource costs of the competitive case are no
\par
greater than those in the base case. The calculated values, carried out to more
\par
than three significant figures. show a slight decline in GNP, almost all of it
\par
in consumption. This is consistent with the necessity to free up more output in
\par
the early years for capital which characterizes the case with more solar pene-
\par
tration. Thus the time phasing of capital expenditures in the base case com-
\par
pared to the competitive case leads to more investment for the cOOlpetitive solar
\par
options in the first few decades and greater consumption later.
\par
Subsidy Case
\par
Both the absolute level of GNP and growth rate are lower in the subsidy case
\par
than in the base case because the solar technologies are less efficient per dol-
\par
lar cost in providing energy than the lower cost conventional alternatives. The
\par
required labor and capital inputs per unit of energy output are increased and
\par
this reduction in productivity is reflected throughout the ecouOOIY in the form
\par
of lower wages, decreased returns to capi tal, and a fall in out put. In the
