Disaggregated EmplOyment Effects
\par
The BNL input-out put/ linear programming model contains 110 sectors. The labor
\par
coefficients for these sectors have been projected to the year 2000. The con-
\par
vergent energy/economy solution provides activity levels for each sector. The
\par
level of sectoral employment is estimated by the product of these activity lev-
\par
els and the projected labor coefficients. Sectoral occupational-mix coeffi-
\par
cients are then applied to the employment projections for each sector, and the
\par
change in occupational mix is calculated. These coefficients, supplied by
\par
Lawrence Berkeley Laboratory, describe the 1972 occupational skill mix. When
\par
they are applied to the 2000 activity level the employment for each skill is
\par
generated. The employment mix is based on the working assumption that the skill
\par
mix in each sector will not change between 1972 and 2000. The change in skill
\par
mix over the whole economy is the result of the change in the structure of em-
\par
ployment across industries and not of changes of skill mixes within industries.
\par
The results should be viewed with these caveats in mind. The employment re-
