that allocated much of the solar construction to the solar supply sectors rein-
\par
forces the construction employment loss due to the decrease in utility and manu-
\par
facturing activity.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
This paper discusses a study in the context of the real life constraints under
\par
which policy analysts operate. The results are significant in that they are
\par
counter to the conventional wisdom that solar technologies create employment and
\par
shift the occupational mix toward blue collar jobs. They also indicate that in-
\par
creased capital intensity in the future may not lead to increased labor produc-
\par
tivity. The results imply that the differences in the time paths of capital ex-
\par
penditure between solar and conventional energy technologies, even if they are
\par
equal in cost, are an area for worthwhile research. The assumption used in this
\par
study was that life-cycle costs are equal. The case of equal capital costs has
\par
not yet been investigated.
\par
These findings are unusual. They are heavily dependent on two characteristics
\par
of the modeling structure. First, the international trade balance mechanism as-
\par
sumes that exports fall in response to lower fuel imports and that these exports
\par
have the same sectoral composi tion in 2000 as in the recent pa st. Second, the
\par
econometric formulations reflect the increasing fraction of output arising from
\par
tertiary industries that has been true historically and assumes that this trend
\par
continues through 2000.
