\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
As interest on energy matters mounts the development of models as
\par
well as the number of scientists which are diverted to the subject,
\par
increase exponentially. Energy modelling can be approached from
\par
either the supply side or the demand side. In retrospect it seems
\par
that the emphasis has tilted from supply to demand and hopefully
\par
somewhere in the middle now. No doubt the ultimate resolution lies
\par
in the point where the demand curve meets the supply curve. Still
\par
it is crucial to lay down our priorities from the beginning.
\par
The supply of fuels is planned on the anticIpation of likely demands
\par
and acts, in the short and medium term, as a constraint that
\par
activates price changes in order to clear market imbalances. In the
\par
longer term, unless the signals from the market are interpreted
\par
clearly overcapacity might be the result, which means that we have
\par
been wasting scarce resources. It follows then that the endeavour
\par
to balance desired supply and demand should start by an assessment
\par
of the likely demands for future fuel sources.
\par
ENERGY AND THE MACRO-ECONOMY
\par
The traditional macro-economic analysis has become in the post-1973
\par
era more and more energy conscious. The usual "macro-parts" of the
\par
real, monetary and labour markets must now be supplemented with a
\par
fourth one, the energy market. Energy and oil have by now gained a
\par
legitimate place in macro-economic planning and forecasting. Hence
\par
the view in this paper is that the energy market forms part of a
\par
general macro-economic equilibrium solution.
\par
A typical macro model would yield estimates for GOP, aggregate
\par
consumption expenditure and industrial output. These three
\par
variables are the basic activity indicators that are needed to
\par
"drive" a three-sector energy market. Industrial output represents
\par
the income effect for energy consumed in industry, while aggregate
\par
consumption expenditure will likewise be the income effect for
\par
energy consumption in the domestic sector. In the transportation
\par
sector, since a portion of energy is used for residential usage and
\par
- 179 -
\par
- 180 -
\par
the rest is used for commercial or industrial usages, a dual income
\par
effect is needed. Thus both the industrial output and consumers'
\par
expenditures could be used.
\par
There is also an autonomous conservation element which stems from
\par
the progress in technology and has been re-enforced from the 1973-74
\par
energy crisis. Autonomous conservation is directly linked to the
\par
performance of the economy, and should not grow smoothly overtime.
\par
Instead it should be a function of the economic cycle. Thus
\par
periods of stagnation would exert a negative pressure on
\par
the abIlity to save energy.
\par
The area of interest to many economists nowadays is on the feedback
\par
effects from the energy market to the macro-economy. Even more
\par
crucial is the impact on economic activity of an increase in the
\par
price of oil. The initial effect of such an increase is felt in the
\par
economy directly from the price of oil to the level of domestic
\par
prices. There is a secondary effect, however, which stems from the
\par
fact that the rest of the fuel prices rise in "sympathy" to the
\par
initial rise in the price of oil. Since oil is the fuel in
\par
the energy market it assumes a leading role in determinIng the
\par
prices of the rest of the fuels. Thus a given percentage increase
\par
in the price of oil has a present and lagged impact on energy prices
\par
that extends to a few years.
\par
Another linkage between the energy market and the macro economy,
\par
which operates through the supply side of the system, is fuel
\par
availability. Any production function in the macro-model should
\par
allow for the by now all important factor, energy. Capital, labour
\par
and energy become the basic inputs in the production process that
\par
define the potential output of the economy. When the supply of
\par
energy and oil is limited or equivalently, if people anticipate that
\par
energy or oil is in limited supply, then the potential output of the
\par
economy is restricted. It is especially this last factor that would
\par
explain, at least in part, the sluggish economic growth in the OECD
\par
are in the period since 1974. The difference between potential and
\par
actual GDP, being a measure of the "overheating" of the economy
\par
should be included in the inflation equation.
\par
An increase in the price of oil, however, at the Arabian Gulf is an
\par
increase for all rather than anyone country in isolation. Thus,
\par
although there is recycling of money from the producers to the
\par
consumers of oil, world trade incurs a setback and export volumes of
\par
every single country decrease. In order to measure this
\par
international trade multiplier effect simulations with linked models
\par
of the major world economies are needed.
\par
Another effect which is easy to describe but very difficult to
\par
quantify is the impact on investments of frustrated expectations
\par
which spring from turmoils in the oil market. Oil is probably the
\par
world's most important traded commodity; and as such it is bound to
\par
affect expectations of governments, firms and households alike.
\par
Since expectations are in turn important in investment
\par
expenditures and durable good purchases, theIr impact on investment
\par
activities could be quite significant. Measuring, however,
\par
expectations has always been a hurdle in econometric modelling.
\par
The following flow chart describes how the various linkages
\par
between the energy market and the overall economy fit together.
