THE BASIC RELATIONSHIPS IN THE ENERGY MARKET
\par
The analysis of energy demand is more appropriately done at the
\par
final consumption level. Any attempt to forecast energy on a
\par
primary input basis disregards reality where ordinary decisions
\par
about how much energy to consume and which fuel type to use are
\par
based on final or end-use energy. In this context it is firstly
\par
important to identify the different types of behaviour that exist in
\par
the industrial, residential/commercial and transportation sectors.
\par
Once we have established the level of final energy consumption in
\par
these basic sectors it is not difficult to arrive at the primary
\par
energy level through a set of technological relationships.
\par
The two main market forces influencing final energy demand are
\par
income and price changes, each one having a different time profile.
\par
Income is primarily a short run phenomenon while price is assumed to
\par
encompass both a short and a long term reaction. Energy demand,
\par
being essentially a derived demand, in the short term responds
\par
directly to an income variable. In the industrial sector, for
\par
example, although firms plan their production for the short and long
\par
run on expectations about future output levels, energy consumption
\par
(like other production inputs) will aepend on the proauction level
\par
that will actually be demanded at each time period. In the short
\par
- 182 -
\par
term - when the technology and substitution between the factors of
\par
production remains constant - demand will vary with output levels
\par
and possibly efficiencies associated with the utilization rate of
\par
capacity. In the longer term, energy prices relative to prices of
\par
other factors of production will further influence industrial energy
\par
demand; this effect is assumed to be captured by the change in the
\par
"real" price of energy.
\par
The reasoning is similar for the transportation and residential/
\par
commercial sectors of the economy. Since energy demand derives from
\par
the demand for services that the various durable goods yield, any
\par
level of consumption expenditure will be associated with a given
\par
rate of energy consumption. Here again there is a proportionality
\par
between the activity indicator, consumption expenditure, and energy
\par
consumption. Although the purchases of durable goods may depend on
\par
both present and expected incomes, the actual utilisation of such
\par
durable commodities - and hence energy consumption - will depend on
\par
consumer expenditure at each time period. Thus the income effect of
\par
energy demand on transportation and the residential/commercial
\par
sectors can be viewed again as a short term phenomenon.
\par
Fuel price changes by producing short and long term reactions have
\par
implications on both energy consumption and interfuel substitution.
\par
When the price of energy increases, consumers will in the short term
\par
try to cut consumption but they will, in general, keep their
\par
existing capital equipment or durables stock. In the longer term,
\par
conservation can be achieved by sWitching to more efficient
\par
equipment, retrofitting houses, reorganising the production process,
\par
etc.
\par
The long term response of energy consumption to price changes has a
\par
different time response in every sector. In industry long term
\par
conservation, which reflects the life of capital stock and the
\par
ability to invest in more efficient process, should be achieved a
\par
lot faster than in the household, where appliances once bought stay
\par
for years. In the transportation sector the existence of a long
\par
term reaction is not clear. A short term reaction of course does
\par
exist but motorists tend to go back to old habits quite soon.
\par
In order to arrive from final energy demand to the primary energy
\par
level, an efficiency factor for the conventional thermal power
\par
stations should be assessed. The same factor, of course, would be
\par
used to measure output from nuclear and hydro stations into
\par
equivalent primary terms. Energy consumption by the energy sector
\par
together with losses are essentially determined by final energy
\par
requirements and the state of technology. Simple functional
\par
relationships between the total requirements of various fuels and
\par
the actual consumption of these fuels (or losses), in the energy
\par
sector, can be computed. The resulting technical coefficients can
\par
be variable over the forecast horizon depending on the likely
\par
movements of technological progress. This can be performed
\par
judgementally. Refinery fuel consumption can likewise be made a
\par
function of total demand for oil products. As regards manufactured
\par
gas, an efficiency factor - like in power generation - can be
\par
assessed and used for conversion to primary input units.
\par
- 183 -
\par
Thus, from the final energy consumption stage, through some
\par
essentially technical adjustments for electricity generation, energy
\par
consumption by the energy sector, losses, refinery consumption and
\par
manufactured gas, primary energy consumption can be ascertained.
\par
THE BALANCE OF DEMAND AND SUPPLY
\par
The balance between supply and demand for energy can be carried out
\par
both at the national and the world level. In the past years,
\par
however, the practice in most countries has been to increase
\par
indigenous energy sources to a certain level and then meet excess
\par
demand by importing the rest of the energy needs primarily in the
\par
form of oil. This means that the most crucial balance is at the
\par
world rather than at the country level. 1979 has indeed been a
\par
testimony of how fragile the world system is to shortfalls in oil
\par
supply anywhere in the globe.
\par
Thus, within every country the balance of supply and demand for
\par
every fuel will be important in determining prices at the final
\par
market. At a second stage inter-regional trade in coal and natural
\par
gas should be considered, while the worldwide exportable oil
\par
supplies should be treated as the residual fuel that tops up world
\par
energy needs. This is not the same as assuming that the supply of
\par
oil is unlimited. The assumption instead is that the price of crude
\par
oil will rise enough to keep demand within the confines of desired
\par
production.
