CONCLUDING REMARKS
\par
The analysis so far was an effort to describe the specification of
\par
an energy model that simultaneously interacts with a macro-economic
\par
model. Or alternatively a macro-economic model that treats energy
\par
as one market within the rest of the markets in the economy. At the
\par
same time an effort was made to pinpoint the broad differences in
\par
behaviour among the energy sectors. Thus the aim has been to
\par
specify the boundaries where a modelling exercise can take place
\par
given the availability of data and the empirical evidence so far.
\par
The strong tendency, or desire, to plan the demand and supply of
\par
energy, demonstrated by the interest shown by governments and other
\par
major economic organisations in intervening to control and contain
\par
market forces, necessitates the introduction of a number of
\par
exogenous elements in such a model. Autonomous conservation,
\par
government policies that induce fuel substitution, ?rice controls,
\par
exogenous assumptions with regard to nuclear electrIcity, to name a
\par
few. However, the market mechanism should be allowed to playa
\par
significant role in a model because it is crucial to know the
\par
modification of these "planned" changes according to the direction
\par
of market forces. In this respect a simulation model, as the one
\par
here described, is particularly appropriate in testing the
\par
feasibility or consequences of such plans. The blend of the energy
\par
reality with the macro-economy is a modelling task of great
\par
importance and it merits more attention than what economists have
\par
devoted until now. The changes in the energy market - and the
\par
economy as a whole - that we have experienced since 1974, have
\par
revealed that mere extrapolation of past trends is an inadequate
\par
tool to capture the future or even plan the present. It is
\par
especially through the interplay of fuel prices that a model can
\par
capture the subtleties of the energy market. The various
\par
relationships in the model can be econometrically estimated wherever
\par
this is possible or meaningful. However, in many ways energy
\par
economics has undergone a qualitative chanRe in recent years so that
\par
judgment is also essential to enhance the realism" of any modelling
\par
exercise.
