\par
ABSTRACT
\par
The EC energy modeling project includes several national and multinational models
\par
linked between themselves. They have econometrically estimated equations, input-
\par
output tables, simulation modules and a linear programming energy flow optimisa-
\par
tion model. This system of models is built in a modular form and the users can
\par
easily work with each of its single components. Policy applications of the models
\par
are starting after a period of development and validation. The paper describes a
\par
set of possible partial or global applications of the models, at national or
\par
multinational levels.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy; modeling; simulation; forecasting; policy; condensed forms.
\par
TIlE EC ENERGY MODELING PROGRAMME
\par
The development of a comprehensive EC energy policy is still at a very early stage
\par
despite the fact that its usefulness has been often stressed in the recent years.
\par
Undoubtedly the development of such a policy encounters many obstacles and practical
\par
constraints. National characteristics of the energy systems of the EC countries
\par
dictate specific differences both in strategies and in policy decisions: these
\par
differences are particularly strong in the area of nuclear energy.
\par
All member countries, however, feel the need for studies of the future in order to
\par
better assess the implications of alternative decisions; but there again methodo-
\par
logical differences appear as the administrations of some countries favour normative
\par
approache; while others prefer exploratory approaches.
\par
The Commission of the EC, conscious of these initial difficulties, started four
\par
years ago a relatively large programme of systems analysis applied to the energy
\par
sector, in the framework of the activities of the Research and Development
\par
Directorate.
\par
- 187 -
\par
- 188 -
\par
Essentially the approach is modular, treating countries individually in order to
\par
progressively build a multinational system. It calls upon the modeling capabi-
\par
lities existing in the nine EC countries aiming at a harmonisation of modeling
\par
techniques in this area. The situation of this programme is described in a
\par
special issue of Energy Policy (Sept. 1979) to which most of the teams that are
\par
currently participating in the programme have contributed.
\par
The set of models used includes:
\par
medium-term models (next ten years)
\par
macro-economic (EURECA)
\par
sectorial, input-output (EXPLOR)
\par
energy demand (EDM)
\par
flow optimisation (EFOM)
\par
long-term models (next thirty years)
\par
macro-economic (SLT)
\par
energy demand (EDM)
\par
flow optimisation (EFOM)
\par
All these models have been developed and tested for each single EC country. At
\par
this national level they are all linked (the linkage between the medium-term
\par
models and the long-term models is made in the flow optimisation models that
\par
require specification of energy demands up to the year 2020).
\par
As pointed out earlier, one of the objectives of this programme is to develop
\par
alternative multinational futures as opposed to the simple sum of national futures.
\par
The multinational approach has been chosen from the start at the macro-economic
\par
level; it is clear that the process of economic integration in the EC has been
\par
developing during the last two decades and that at present there is a considerable
\par
degree of interdependence that makes it imperative to treat explicitly in the
\par
model the inter-country e"conomic relations. Thus EURECA is a multinational model
\par
with trade linkages and the longer term model national SLT models are driven by
\par
a supranational model SLT model with national desaggregation.
\par
The complexity and high degree of uncertainty of multinational modeling at the
\par
sectorial and energy demand levels has led to a postponement to a later stage of
\par
the programme, of this type of linkages. The approach remains national but with
\par
due consideration in the trade vectors of possible future evolutions of the inte-
\par
gration process, and adopting harmonious classifications allowing for intra-
\par
country comparisons.
\par
Finally at the level of the energy flow optimisation it has been felt that, although
\par
the volume of intra-European energy trade is still relatively small it may well
\par
develop in the future as a result of European energy policies, and therefore
\par
together with the national EFOM models a multinational model has been developed
\par
(EFOM 12 C).
\par
This later effort has had to face considerable difficulties as a result of the
\par
existence of substantial differences in data and statistical practices even in
\par
- 189 -
\par
relatively technical areas as those related to cost coefficients.
\par
Although the process of validation of these models is not yet completed, by the
\par
end of 1979 all of them were technically operational and were ready for concrete
\par
applications. A small operational group in Brussels coordinates the national
\par
groups of implementers and performs multinational exercices: this group is working
\par
in relation with other EC Directorates (in particular the Directorates for Energy
\par
and for Economic Policy).
\par
The national groups of implementers have strengthened their energy modeling capa-
\par
bilities during this programme and are working in close relation with the national
\par
agencies involved in energy policy making.
\par
As a result of the work performed so far in this programme the energy modeling
\par
community has been consolidated in Europe and national studies are being increasingly
\par
made in a harmonised framework. This is perhaps an essential first step for further
\par
coordination of the energy policies of the European countries and for a future
\par
European energy policy.
\par
CONSTRAINTS IN THE MODELING APPROACH
\par
As already mentioned earlier, the EC system of energy models had to be modular in
\par
order to allow for both national and multinational applications.
\par
It was also essential to build the system using the capabilities existing in
\par
Europe when the programme was started; this constraint implied that, at the
\par
national levels, the best modeling capabilities had to be identified and con-
\par
siderable effort had to be made in order to integrate them into a coherent frame-
\par
work. Rather than to start a new model system, the programme evolved towards the
\par
more complex and less rewarding process of linking existing models mainly through
\par
their exogenous variables.
\par
In itself this process was enriching for all the teams as, since the beginning, it
\par
introduced as a major constraint for the model builders, a perfect transparency of
\par
their models: all teams of implementers had to be aware of both the possibilities
\par
and the limitations of each single model. Furthermore, from the software point of
\par
view, this initial methodological choice imposed a very high level of flexibility;
\par
each single model had to be treated in separate modules thus allowing later on for
\par
a possibility of using each model either separately or jointly (when calling upon
\par
the multiple linkage programmes).
\par
The evident loss in mathematical elegance of the EC modeling system has therefore
\par
been overcompensated by the gains in transparency and flexibility, and last but
\par
not least, by the gain in time resulting of the acceleration of the implementation
\par
process.
\par
THE EC ENERGY MODELING SYSTEM AND THE MODEL USER
\par
Transparency of the models (of the structure of the relations, of the data used, of
\par
the values given to exogenous variables and parameters) and great flexibility are
\par
essential for the user. Besides these national assets of the modeling approaches
\par
adopted by the EC, a substantial effort is envisaged to improve the conversational
\par
- 190 -
\par
characteristics of the software and thus facilitate the technical relation between
\par
the user and the models.
\par
The models included in the EC system are conceptually simple, but they call upon
\par
at least four types of modeling techniques: econometric, input-output linear
\par
programming and simulation. Many of the models are very large and disaggregated.
\par
It can hardly be said that the task of the user is easy; indeed those who fully
\par
grasp all the details of the structure are very few, even among the team of model
\par
builders. Thus the model user may be tempted to call upon the flexibility aspects
\par
of the system and to use only the part of it that he can easily understand; this
\par
is particularly the case for those familiar with linear programming methods or
\par
with input-output models. In doing so the user certainly will gain insight into
\par
his particular problem but he may lose other interesting aspects of it that could
\par
have been better illustrated by other parts of the system of models.
\par
Conscious of this difficulty the EC team is planning to develop in the next stages
\par
of the programme "condensed forms" of the entire system. The condensed forms, or
\par
"models of the models", should in particular summarize the main reactions of the
\par
large model to changes in the world environment (e.g. changes in petroleum prices)
\par
or in specific national policies (e.g. nuclear investment programmes).
\par
The development of condensed forms opens new areas of modeling of potential interest
\par
to the users such as optimal control, decentralised optimal control and gaming, a
\par
line of research which is also included in the second EC programme (1980-84).
\par
THE EC ENERGY MODELING SYSTEM AND THE SOCIO-ECONOMIC ENVIRONMENT
\par
The energy system cannot be treated in isolation and this is the reason that
\par
justified the inclusion in it of economic models (macro-economic and sectorial),
\par
both for the medium term and for the long term.
\par
When using the models with the purpose of exploring the future, economic scenarios
\par
can be elaborated by giving specific values to the exogenous variables or to
\par
technical coefficients and parameters. For the medium term this procedure is
\par
considered to be valid owing to the inertia of the socio-economic structures.
\par
However, when looking into the longer term, major socio-economic changes may take
\par
place and a more careful and systematic analysis of the scenarios has to be made.
\par
For the EC energy modeling system two procedures have been adopted for the long
\par
term: cross-impact analysis, as a way of computing probabilistic aggregated
\par
scenarios considering major future events and developments both in the EC and in
\par
the rest of the world and a hierarchic scenario technique with the demand model
\par
MEDEE. This latter technique forces the user to specify many detailed conditions
\par
of future situations, and thus allows for use of the models both in an exploratory
\par
and in a normative way (and in this way it answers one of the initial problems
\par
created by national differences in methodological approaches).
\par
POLICY APPLICATIONS OF THE EC MODELING SYSTEM
\par
With the characteristics described above, it is obvious that the EC modeling system
\par
is particularly useful for the simulation of both events and policy decisions and
\par
the assessment of their possible future impacts on a national and multinational
\par
level, in aggregate and sectorial terms.
\par
- 191 -
\par
During 1979 work has concentrated in the different countries on the drawing up of
\par
a central projection or reference scenario towards which all other simulations
\par
could be compared. Some first tests have been made of the impacts of events
\par
(mainly changes in petroleum prices) or policies (in GB, simulations have been
\par
made bringing the prices of gas and electricity to the same level with a thermal
\par
equivalent; in France, simulations have been made for energy saving measures).
\par
In each country the teams of implementers working together with national energy
\par
agencies are preparing new simulations of policies of potential interest for their
\par
countries in a common EC context and considering national constraints; these may
\par
include price controls, rationing, investment policies, subsidies of new energies
\par
among others are resulting in a constant energy demand forecast.
\par
In so far as each of these teams is using a similar instrument, it will be possible
\par
from now on to compare country results and to develop a common European language.
\par
For the central team, it will be possible to develop multinational solutions and
\par
to bring them into the discussions between national teams, thus contributing again
\par
to a clarification of policy issues.
\par
The EC has recently stated some overall energy objectives like bringing the
\par
elasticity of energy consumption to the GDP to 0.7 in 1990 or making a converging
\par
movement of the relative prices of the different energy products (thermal equi-
\par
valents). It is envisaged at present to evaluate with the multinational system
\par
the possible impacts of these evolutions.
\par
Similarly, case studies are underway to assess the impacts of alternative invest-
\par
ment policies including a sizable increase of energy investments.
\par
In all these cases, many of the impacts are related to socio-economic magnitudes
\par
(GDP, inflation rate, employment, {$\bullet$}.. ). It is quite obvious that, at the present
\par
stage of our knowledge of essential processes of substitution among production
\par
factors, many of these results should be treated with great care. The EC energy
\par
modeling system is, however, attempting to bring together the best knowledge
\par
available and is making the user conscious of its unavoidable limitations.
\par
For the time being, and considering both the complexity of the system and the
\par
limitations of both the data and the model structure, the contact between the final
\par
user (the policy decision maker at the legislative or executive levels) and the
\par
model has to be filtered by the expert teams (at the EC and at the national levels).
\par
But it is essential that a communication is established between the final user and
\par
the expert team of the utmost transparency both at the level of the drawing up of
\par
hypothesis and of the interpretation of results. This transparency is the essential
\par
pre-condition if one wants to avoid the black-box characteristics of modeling that
\par
have limited its credibility, its development and its final usefulness.
\par
Let us now examine the different possible uses of the EC energy models, uses for
\par
which the present stage of development of the programme is sufficient, provided
\par
extreme care is paid to the evaluation of future exogenous variables and para-
\par
meters.
\par
1. Uses of Single Modules at National Level
\par
As pointed out earlier the great flexibility of the EC system of models allows for
\par
specific applications using only parts of the system.
\par
- 192 -
\par
The macro-economic models: the Eureca model includes a production function
\par
with capital, labour and energy. Thus this specific model can be used in each
\par
country in order to:
\par
assess the impact on economic growth of changes in energy prices and
\par
availability,
\par
study the possibilities of substitution between energy and labour at
\par
an aggregate level.
\par
The international linkage existing for these macro-economic models facilitates
\par
the study of the international transmission of economic trends.
\par
Eureca also identifies separately petroleum imports and it is therefore possible
\par
to compute growth consequences of constraints in petroleum imports.
\par
The input-output models: the input-output tables available in the EXPLOR model
\par
allow for an assessment of the structural implications for any single national
\par
economy of technological changes in production technologies induced by changes
\par
in the prices and availability of energy. As, for the moment, the input-output
\par
models are static, this type of simulations requires specific sectorial techno-
\par
economic studies analysing available production processes and their probable
\par
evolution. A project is underway to introduce dynamic behavioural functions
\par
into EXPLOR.
\par
The methodology is also available to compute the structural effect of import or
\par
production constraints using, for instance, consumer expenditures maximisation
\par
as an objective function.
\par
The input-output tables have been used in many countries already to compute the
\par
impact on prices of changes in the price of petroleum, considering both the
\par
direct and indirect effects; this computation is practically standardised.
\par
EXPLOR is, however, something more than a simple input-output model; it includes
\par
a number of other functions explaining both the formation of final demand and
\par
of factor costs and prices. Therefore it is possible to compute with EXPLOR
\par
the third order effect on prices of changes in petroleum prices when taking into
\par
consideration the relation existing in all countries between the cost of living
\par
indices and the wage rates. Studies conducted in this direction have clearly
\par
shown that the final impact of petroleum prices on national prices is much
\par
higher than the direct and indirect effects indicated and basically depends upon
\par
the strength of the unions when defending the purchasing power of the workers.
\par
The energy demand models: Both the EDM (medium term) and MEDEE (long term) can
\par
be used to analyse in depth the possibilities of substitution among energy pro-
\par
ducts to meet demand requirements. For the medium term, the analysis is mainly
\par
done using the relative prices of the energy products and sectorial activity
\par
levels; for the long term, the analysis is made in terms of energy consumption
\par
processes at a very detailed techno-economic level. It is indeed felt that, in
\par
the long term, the range of possibilities of substitution is much wider and much
\par
less conditioned by past situations. The great flexibility of the MEDEE approach
\par
allows for simulations not only at the technical level but also at the social
\par
level.
\par
It has often been said that the energy cannot be solved in the long term
\par
without substantial socio-economic changes (implying new social structures
\par
corresponding to new value systems). The MEDEE models can be run with scenarios
\par
- 193 -
\par
portraying the essential characteristics of this new society (favouring, for
\par
instance, soft energy choices, non-polluting industries, decentralized
\par
industries {$\bullet\bullet\bullet$} ) and may suggest government policies oriented towards lower
\par
energy consumption standards.
\par
The linear programming models: Energy modeling efforts in most countries have
\par
for some years been concentrated in this area as a result of the previous
\par
application of linear programming in the optimisation of electrical networks.
\par
The general outcome of these models are optimal resource allocation under given
\par
constraints cost calculations and investment needs. It is therefore possible
\par
with EFOM to compute the implicit costs of environmental constraints or of
\par
import constraints.
\par
These models include a detailed description of energy production processes and
\par
it is therefore possible to compute conditions for use of new technologies
\par
(e.g. solar) and to derive a policy of subsidies or of research and develop-
\par
ment expenditures.
\par
2. Use of all the Modules together in a National Context
\par
When using all the modules together it is possible to provide a more comprehensive
\par
answer to all the questions treated with each single module. One should, however,
\par
keep in mind the complexity of the system and it is necessary for the final user
\par
to specify a priori his areas of interest in order to extract from the information
\par
of a complete run only, those aspects which are specifically related to them.
\par
It is also possible, when using the entire system at national level, to run more
\par
complex scenarios including events (e.g. petroleum price changes, import cons-
\par
traints {$\bullet\bullet\bullet$} ) and a policy mix (e.g. energy saving measures, rationing, subsidies
\par
to new technologies, public investment in petroleum substitution processes {$\bullet\bullet\bullet$} ).
\par
For reasons already pointed out, it is essential that this type of complex simula-
\par
tion be preceded by a thorough study of the system and that each result be explained
\par
with due reference to the structural relations of the models from which it is de-
\par
rived.
\par
3. Use of all the Modules together in a Multinational Context
\par
This is by far the most ambitious application of the EC systems of models that can
\par
be envisaged at present. It'is, however, essential if the EC models are to be in
\par
the future of any use for the development of EC energy policies.
\par
The problems to be dealt with are the same as those described for national appli-
\par
cations. But furthermore it is possible in multinational applications to simulate
\par
the impacts of common policies (e.g. in energy saving or in the taxes and subsidies
\par
policies) or of a coordination of national policies (e.g. in the investment area
\par
leading to modifications of production specialisation in the different countries).
\par
FINAL REMARKS
\par
The EC system of energy models has kept up to now the rest of the world as exo-
\par
genous (with the single partial exception of the EURECA module). In principle
\par
- 194 -
\par
this situation could limit some of its possible applications in a very inter-
\par
dependent world. Collaboration with research groups in the United States and
\par
in international organisations has, however, been established since the beginning
\par
in order to overcome this potential limitation. It would practically be impossible
\par
to further develop the linkage process of the existing models in order to include
\par
explicitly other areas of the world. This is another reason for which the deve-
\par
lopment of condensed forms of the existing system appears as an essential need of
\par
the Programme for the years to come.
\par
