\par
A L.P. MODEL OF THE DUTCH
\par
ENERGY SECTOR
\par
F. van Oostvoom, P. G. M. Boonekamp and N. J. Koenders
\par
Energy Study Centre, Netherlands Energy Research Foundation ECN,
\par
Petten, The Netherlands
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
The main objective of the model is to use it as a tool for long term energy plan-
\par
ning and evaluation of energy policies. After discussing the main policy issues
\par
and charachteristics of the Dutch energy system an attempt is made to clarify the
\par
choice of the adopted modelling technique. Although we stress the major advantages
\par
of the L.P. modelling approach we pay particular attention to the limitations of
\par
the adopted class of models. We indicate how to remove some limitations and thus
\par
enlarge the scope and credibility of the L.P. model of the Dutch energy sector,
\par
called SELPE. Furthermore the specification of SELPE will be discussed and an
\par
application is presented.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Linear-programming; energy sector; objective functions; energy policy of the
\par
Netherlands; applications; network-model description.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
A few years ago the national Energy Study Centre was established with the objec-
\par
tive to perform system analysis studies in the energy field. The main part of the
\par
modelling activities is devoted to develop tools for energy planning and policy
\par
analysis for the government. In this paper we will concentrate our attention on a
\par
long term energy model of the Dutch energy sector. Before focusing on the choice
\par
of modelling technique we mention some special features of the Dutch energy economy
\par
and some important energy policy issues. Firstly an import feature is the openness
\par
of the Dutch national economy and energy system. This means a strong domestic
\par
dependence on world market developments and a high import vulnerability. Secondly
\par
the dependence on foreign energy resources will strongly increase in the future
\par
when domestic natural gas is depleted. Thirdly the cheap crude oil imports and the
\par
large natural gas deposits made the Dutch economy very energy intensive.
\par
The major energy policy goals are:
\par
- conservation of rapidly depleting natural gas resources,
\par
- restricted use of nuclear energy,
\par
- increasing imports of LNG and the reintroduction of coal as an important energy
\par
source,
\par
- 207 -
\par
- 208 -
\par
. last but not least energy demand conservation.
\par
It is clear that long term energy policy and planning is needed to tackle the pro-
\par
blems that go along with the profound shifts that will take place in the near and
\par
the far future. Evidently an important policy maker and decision maker like the
\par
government a policy and planning tool to comprehend these transitions and to
\par
deal effectively with the problems involved.
\par
WHY LINEAR PROGRAMMING FOR MODELLING THE NATIONAL ENERGY-SECTOR?
\par
The fact that technological features dominate the energy sector suggests the use of
\par
a process analysis type of model. A L.P. network description of the many production
\par
functions composing the energy network model offers a number of advantages over the
\par
alternative approach of statistical production or supply functions and is therefore
\par
commonly used in energy sector modelling (see for instance models like BESOM, DESOM,
\par
Markal etc.).
\par
In a joint-product-multiprocess sector like the energy sector, statistical estima-
\par
tion is difficult in view of the problems of simultaneous equations bias and multi-
\par
collinearity. On the other hand the L.P. network description is easily adaptable to
\par
joint production and capital intensive processes. It offers easy incorporation of
\par
technological processes by using detailed engineering data.
\par
The general structure of SELPE, an accronym for Static Linear
\par
model, is like most of the L.P. models of the energy sector.
\par
minimize cx
\par
subject to Ax {$>$} b
\par
and x {$>$} 0
\par
where c is an I x n vector of costs associated with the various activit-
\par
ies. x is an n x I vector of endogenous energy flows. A is an m x n matrix of con-
\par
version, transportation, distribution and allocation coefficients. b is an m x I
\par
vector of exogenous energy demand.
\par
The heart of the network model is the set of constraints describing the Dutch energy
\par
sector as close as possible for the period 1976-2000. In the network representation
\par
of the energy sector energy forms and fuels are represented by nodes. Processes,
\par
important ancillaries and by-products are represented by links. For the network
\par
description we did not use a single stage specification of energy supply till end-
\par
use like (Cherniavsky, 1974).
\par
Instead we used a multi-stage specification of energy supply till end-use like
\par
(Finon, 1976). This last approach has the advantage of identifying separately each
\par
stage of the transformation process from primary energy till end-use. We used many
\par
different types of constraints and no strict network convention was adopted.
\par
The SELPE model provides a complete physical representation of the Dutch energy sec-
\par
tor for the period from 1976 till 2000.
\par
Energy resources are: nuclear power, solar heat, wind power, imports of LNG, NG,
\par
coal, coke, crude oil and oil products and extraction of NG and small amounts of
\par
crude oil.
\par
New technologies like coal gasification, fluidized bed combustion and combined heat-
\par
power production etc. are incorporated. Also centralized and decentralized electric-
\par
ity and heat production on a coal, oil, gas or nuclear base are included. We distin-
\par
guish peak and base load electricity production and the capacity variables in the
\par
electricity sector are endogenous.
\par
The definition of exogenous demand is partly in useful energy and partly in final
\par
energy demand. A fuel independent definition of the exogenous demand variables was
\par
not adopted, because of lack of data, linking problems with demand models and the
\par
weak applicability of the L.P. technique in this sector definition. To indicate the
\par
size of the model it contains about 220 endogenous variables, 60 processes and 50
\par
other constraints.
\par
- 209 -
\par
LIMITATIONS OF THE L.P. DESCRIPTION AND WAYS TO REMOVE THEM
\par
It is a well-known fact that the L.P. models are of an overtly normative nature.
\par
Thus the qualities in describing the actual behaviour of the energy sector in a
\par
realistic way can be rather poor.
\par
Using a single objective function like minimization of cost in the model assumes
\par
perfect competition on all energy markets. However in the real world the physical
\par
energy-supply responses are not always functions of market prices only. Besides
\par
energy-prices other factors influence the energy supply and distribution. The capi-
\par
tal intensive energy sector contains a lot of market imperfections and often its
\par
production functions are subjected to economies of scale. For that reason appro-
\par
priate constraints were added to the SELPE-specification and partly removed these
\par
drawbacks. So probably the L.P. objective of cost minimization or maximization of
\par
economic efficiency is rather close in reflecting the decision making process in
\par
the energy sector.
\par
The definition of cost in the model raises problems too.
\par
Energy markets are characterized by price regulation and rate structure economics
\par
that weaken the general accepted relation between price and economic cost. For
\par
example many models use the concept of supply cost in their objective functions.
\par
Supply costs are more pertinent to supply decisions and (demand) prices are more
\par
pertinent to demand decisions. So for the computation of a market equilibrium with
\par
a static L.P. model we have to incorporate market prices to guide the decisions in
\par
the model. This price-cost distinction is overlooked in many large L.P. energy
\par
sector models. To improve the behavioral performance of SELPE we used the concept
\par
of market prices by incorporating taxes, regulation effects and rate differences
\par
in the specification of the objective function. For example public utility rate-
\par
making for electricity and gas are included for final demand categories.
\par
We think the network description can easily handle these cost-price distinctions
\par
Another improvement in modelling the decision making proces is attained by using
\par
other objective functions. The changing environment of the energy policy making
\par
and the existence of conflicting objectives linked to different decision makers or
\par
groups of decision makers is obvious. In other words energy policies and objectives
\par
cannot be placed in the context of cost minimization only.
\par
We have to take into account other political dimensions such as uncertainty of
\par
crude oil imports or the availability of resources in the long run. Different
\par
pressure or interest groups, for example environmentalist and entrepreneurs, have
\par
conflicting objectives.
\par
As a matter of fact we specified other objectives for SELPE too, viz.:
\par
- minimization of oil imports,
\par
- minimization of an environmental index,
\par
- minimization of resource use,
\par
- minimization of capital cost.
\par
Clearly some of the above mentioned objectives are conflicting and this is reflec-
\par
ted in the decision making process. A promising method developed to handle the
\par
conflicting decision making within an L.P. model is the so called multi-objective
\par
analysis (Zionts, 1979; Nijkamp, 1976). Probably there are two important benefits
\par
to the use of multiple criteria analysis. Firstly the model describes decision
\par
making of the real world more realistically. Secondly the decision maker, for
\par
example the government, can be forced to reveal his priorities in a more consistent
\par
way than is otherwise attainable.
\par
The energy demand is treated as an exogenous variable rather than as a demand
\par
function. This concept of zero price elasticity of final demand works reasonably
\par
well during an era of no abrupt discontinuities in energy prices and demand is not
\par
seriously affected by prices. But nowadays and in the future price effects on
\par
energy demand will be important.
\par
- 210 -
\par
Besides the zero price elasticity causes a bias towards high technology and capital
\par
intensive energy systems and thus blurs a fair comparison betweem competing tech-
\par
nologies.
\par
There are several ways to remove the zero price elasticity of demand. Shadow prices
\par
of demand constraints can be compared with assumed market prices, complementory to
\par
the assumed exogenous final demand of energy till market equilibrium is attained.
\par
For this exercise price elasticities of demand are needed. Another apporach is
\par
linking the sector model to an (interindustry) energy demand model.
\par
The deterministic system description of the L.P. model overlooks the fact that the
\par
parameters or coefficients of the model contain statistical errors. The user must
\par
know how accurately the model represents the energy sector, because changes of the
\par
values of the parameters would change the calculated optimal values of decision
\par
variables and so influence the energy policy recommendations.
\par
Main sources of errors are:
\par
- specification errors,
\par
- input data errors,
\par
- parameter errors.
\par
The coefficient of the constraints of SELPE are estimates for the optJ.mJ.zation (plan-
\par
ning) years 1980, 1985, 1990, 1995, 2000. These estimates are based on a combina-
\par
tion of engineering and economic (statistical) data. The statistical data consists
\par
of annual data for the years: 1973, 1974, 1975, 1976, 1977.
\par
Probably the most suitable way to deal with the statistical errors in the para-
\par
meters of a large scale L.P. model is:
\par
I. Use appropriate L.P. software (e.g. Apex III) and one gets information about
\par
the sensitivity of parameters, input variables etc.
\par
2. Select the most sensitive parameters and input variables. Generally model sol-
\par
utions are most sensitive to variations in the values of the cost parameters.
\par
3. Estimate the uncertainty of the selected parameters and input variables.
\par
So for a specific year tolerance margins for each variable can be calculated. Thus
\par
point estimates of sensitive parameters and input variables are replaced by margin
\par
estimates and so the calculated optimal solutions are band-width values.
\par
Furthermore for a proper assessment of the capability of SELPE to reflect the rela-
\par
tions of a real world system we also validate our model. This is very important
\par
for giving reliable policy answers to the policy maker. We did validate SELPE only
\par
for a few historical years. These ex-post forecasts for 1976 and 1977 were very
\par
illuminating. In fact they led to some respecification of constraints of SELPE.
\par
We admit that proper validation would need a longer reference period, but valida-
\par
tion of a rather big L.P. model needs a lot of time. Therefore in spite of the
\par
rather "good fit" of the calculated values of the endogenous variables to the ac-
\par
tual values we feel that further study is needed in this direction.
\par
AN APPLICATION
\par
The model SELPE described previously will be illustrated by an empirical applica-
\par
tion. We will present some preliminary results of a larger analysis of a set of
\par
long term energy scenario's, of which more computation results are underway. Our
\par
first step was to establish a reference or base case scenario representing a pro-
\par
jection of the developments in the Dutch energy sector till the year 2000. The
\par
period 1976-2000 was subdivided in intervals of 5 years.
\par
So the planning years for optimization were 1980, 1985, 1990, 1995, 2000.
\par
Input data representing the national economic framework for the reference scenario
\par
were projections of the Central Planning Bureau of the Netherlands.
\par
