\par
{$\cdot\cdot\cdot\cdot$}Tokyo Gas Co., Ltd., Tokyo, Japar
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
The objective of this simulation model of the energy system is intend-
\par
ed to quantitatively support the formulation of development strategies
\par
for Japan's energy technology.
\par
We shall discuss the characteristics of the energy system in Japan
\par
and outline three submodels - namely the supply, demand and energy
\par
flow models.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy R\&D strategy; world energy model, energy flow in Japan; quality
\par
of life; industrial structure; security of an energy system, roles of
\par
new technologies.
\par
PREFACE
\par
The development of energy technologies not only takes time, but in-
\par
volves vast amounts of money and numerous trained personnel. There-
\par
fore, it is necessary to establish a strategy on energy development.
\par
Through the use of models we have attempted to clarify the importance
\par
of new energy technologies, in order to assess its impact on Japan's
\par
future supply and demand for energy. The model has incorporated 3
\par
interesting features.
\par
Energy data was collected and processed in such a way so that it con-
\par
formed to the parameters of the model. However, not only forecasted,
\par
but historical data concerning the conversion, storage, transportation
\par
and distribution costs of energy, that takes place from the time of
\par
origination or acquisition until final consumption was difficult to
\par
obtain. We were successfully able to construct a data base.
\par
It was also important to perceive changes in the needs of the people.
\par
Food, clothing, shelter, social life styles and industrial activities,
\par
- 215 -
\par
- 216 -
\par
all affect the quality and quantity of energy required. The model
\par
takes into consideration these changes in consumption patterns.
\par
Another aspect was determining the impact of the amount of money and
\par
resources spent in energy development. Self-supply and internal
\par
technological development of energies are deemed essential for national
\par
security. Thus, a method of evaluating energy security has been
\par
devised in connection not only with self-supply of energies, but also
\par
with imported energies, availability of energies from various energy
\par
producing countries and diversification of energy sources.
\par
BRIEF DESCRIPTION OF JAPAN'S ENERGY SYSTEM AND THE
\par
ANALYTICAL MODEL
\par
In reviewing the energy system of any country, it is important that
\par
the characteristics and peculiarities of the country be studied, so
\par
the model simulates a "real world" situation. The characteristics
\par
listed below are not only important as a starting point for the
\par
analysis, but identify the Japanese energy system. The model assumes
\par
that they will not undergo drastic changes in the future.
\par
(1) The level of domestic energy supplies and resources are limited.
\par
Therefore, Japan depends on imports for the majority of its
\par
energy needs.
\par
(2) Oil accounts for a large portion of total energy supplies.
\par
(3) Energy is consumed in large quantities in a concentrated area,
\par
where environmental regulations are very severe.
\par
(4) The use of energy by industry accounts for 60\% of the total
\par
energy consumption, while the residential consumption is small.
\par
(5) Most of the energy-related plant and equipment are modern, due
\par
to many years of high economic growth and investment.
\par
(6) Electricity is available allover the country. However, only
\par
half of the nation's population consumes city gas, while the
\par
other half uses LPG.
\par
The simulation model has the following major features.
\par
(1) The model has been designed to closely scrutinize energy imports,
\par
while rough estimates are given for domestic resources.
\par
(2) The demand for oil by industry was classified by end use in the
\par
industry's facilities.
\par
(3) In order to measure the effect on the environment, energy
\par
consumption has been categorized by both sulphur content and
\par
whether or not the facility was equipped with desulfurization
\par
or denitrating devices.
\par
(4) With respect to residential energy demand, kerosene and LPG
\par
consumption has been considered, in addition to electricity and
\par
city gas.
\par
SCOPE OF STRATEGY REVIEW
\par
The following studies were made using the analytical models.
\par
(1) External factors of the Japanese energy system were forecasted
\par
on the basis of mUltiple scenarios. (i.e. Energy prices, amount
\par
- 217 -
\par
of energy supply available to Japan and energy demands.)
\par
(2) According to multiple criteria, the optional amount of energy
\par
for each demand sector and the optimum rates of operation of
\par
the facilities were determined in 5 year intervals.
\par
(3) The effect of the technological development, such as improvement
\par
of the system's vulnerability, energy saving, reduction of energy
\par
costs, reduction of energy imports were discussed in the review
\par
of the outputs. Especially, analysis on end-use markets were
\par
emphasized where new energies are thought to be suitable.
\par
OUTLINE OF THE MODEL
\par
Structure of the model:
\par
The model includes the following three submodels and a control model.
\par
(1) Supply model
\par
(2) Demand model
\par
(3) Energy flow model
\par
Supply model:
\par
The supply model may be regarded as the world's energy supply and
\par
demand balance model. It forecasts energy prices and amount of
\par
energy suppliable to Japan under the predicted economic and energy
\par
situation of the world. This model is called supply model, because
\par
its results are used as restrictions of primary energies for the
\par
energy flow model.
\par
Basic assumptions are listed below.
\par
(1) The world is divided into 9 demand/supply regions.
\par
(2) Each region has its own standard economic growth rate which
\par
depends on energy prices and energy demand elasticity based on
\par
economic growth rates.
\par
(3) OPEC's oil prices are affected by the supply/demand gap of traded
\par
oil.
\par
(4) Demand in each region is divided into 4 categories, substitutable
\par
demand, non-competitive electricity, non-competitive oil and
\par
non-competitive coal.
\par
(5) The amount of substitute energy production depends on the market
\par
price mechanism.
\par
(6) The amount of each energy source used is determined by its
\par
substitutability function.
\par
Fig. 1 shows the basic structure of the model.
\par
Fig. 2 shows an example of a substitutability function which determines
\par
the amount of a substitute energy according to its relative price to
\par
oil.
