Energy flow model:
\par
The energy flow model was used for evaluating the effects of various
\par
policies. The output estimated the percentage contribution for each
\par
primary and secondary energy source and how technological development
\par
of energies would improve the future energy situation in Japan.
\par
The model includes about 40 primary energies, over 60 secondary
\par
energies and more than 50 end use demands. These are connected by
\par
about 400 processes such as conversion facilities, transportation
\par
measures and end use appliances to form an energy flow.
\par
The forecasted time period was from 1975 to 2025, though it is to be
\par
noted that the model itself can be applied for any given period.
\par
Optimization is carried out only for a single fiscal year at one time.
\par
However, in order to connect all the fiscal involved, the results
\par
obtained in any fiscal year were reflected to the constraints in the
\par
following year.
\par
- 220 -
\par
The major algorithm that determines the amount of energy supply
\par
required at each level of the energy flow is a linear programming
\par
technique. A maximum of 10 criteria can be used for the objective
\par
function. The significant ones were the following.
\par
(1) To maximize the total thermal efficiency in the whole system
\par
(2) To minimize the amount of energy imports
\par
(3) To minimize energy-related costs in the whole system
\par
(4) To minimize the total emission of air pollutants in the whole
\par
system
\par
(5) To minimize the vulnerability of a nation's energy structure
\par
As for the last criteria the vulnerability is defined as a combination
\par
of a degree of reproduceability and regional divergence of resources.
