CONCLUSION
\par
The models were originally developed in 1974. During the period of
\par
the past six years, data was updated repeatedly and the models were
\par
modified, and the results obtained were made public every year.
\par
They have been used to support a new governmental project of so called
\par
new energies. (i.e. Solar energy, oceanic energy, hydrogen, etc.
\par
excluding nuclear power.) Now the resources and techniques related
\par
to these new energies have been given more important roles than ever
\par
in the long term energy plan in Japan.
\par
We believe the most distinguishing characteristic of this research
\par
lies in the data used. With respect to demand forecasting, data on
\par
residential use was based on a detailed analysis by type of use for
\par
period of more than ten years. We also investigated more than 500
\par
processes to get information on characteristics of respective facili-
\par
ties. They involve average scale or capacity, efficiency, construction
\par
costs, time of construction, possibility of energy saving, coefficients
\par
of environmental pollutants, operating rates, sites, etc. In the case
\par
of the flow model, the volume of data used in a simulation exceeded
\par
100,000.
\par
Data is still being collected and various new scenarios are being
\par
tested. We expect to obtain more concrete analyses and useful
\par
suggestions for Japan's new energy policy in 1980.
