\par
ABSTRACT
\par
JES - The Jillich Energy Model System is presented. An important part of this
\par
system is LESS (Long Term Energy Simulation System), a dynamic energy simulation
\par
model. LESS and-the support ing program packages IRECA (I nterface for REgress ion
\par
and Correlation Analysis), DAIMOS (DAta Interface for tlOdular Simulatior1) and OASIS
\par
(2ptTmization .!.ntegrated are described.-
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy model, Simulation, Optimization, Systems analysis.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
The present situation in the energy planning field is characterized by uncertainty,
\par
complexity, a large quantity of data which needs to be considered and the long-term
\par
nature. In many areas of pol itics and economics, precarious decisions have to be
\par
made when discussions and debates concerning the choice of future directions of the
\par
energy economy arise. One must recognise that the basic characteristics of energy
\par
planning mentioned above do make the formulation of adequate decision aids very
\par
difficult. There is therefore an increasing interest in the application of systems
\par
analysis in the energy field to overcome these difficulties. One aspect of systems
\par
analysis concentrates on the construction and development of computer-aided energy
\par
models and to date a large number of such models have been produced (Beaujean and
\par
Charpentier; 1978). It appears that systems analysis based investigations, parti-
\par
cularly in the field of energy modelling, can yield improved approaches by virtue
\par
of their interdisciplinary nature which provides an integrated point of view for
\par
solving the energy planning problem.
\par
JES - THE JULiCH ENERGY MODEL SYSTEM
\par
At Jill ich, STE has been engaged in developing energy models of the Federal Republic
\par
of Germany which can be regarded as tools for pursuing new ways in energy planning
\par
(Voss, 1977; Rath-Nagel, 1977; Schmitz and colleagues, 1977; Scholer and colleagues,
\par
1978; Schmitz, 1979; Egberts, 1979).
\par
