\par
LESS - LONG TERM ENERGY SIMULATION SYSTEM
\par
Today LESS consists of four main modules for
\par
the macroeconomics,
\par
- the final energy demand,
\par
- the energy supply, and
\par
- the environmental aspects.
\par
Another module for the useful energy demand will be added in the near future. Now
\par
some aspects of useful energy are integrated within the final energy demand module
\par
The main goal in the development of LESS was the integrated mathematical formula-
\par
tion of the interdependencies between economic development, energy demand, energy
\par
supply and its economic impacts with the possibility of consistent and reproduc-
\par
ible analyses.
\par
The task of the macroeconomic module is to provide values for the economic growth
\par
- 225 -
\par
in the different areas of the economy, which are:
\par
- the four defined industrial sectors (iron and steel production, chemical indus-
\par
try, stone, cement and clay industry, and other industries as a residual),
\par
- the commercial sector, and
\par
- the energy industry.
\par
Furthermore for private households the disposable income as an economically deter-
\par
mining factor for the energy demand is computed.
\par
By means of these economic indicators within the energy demand module the final
\par
energy demand is calculated. This is done mainly by using regression and correla-
\par
tion techniques. As an output of these computations we obtain the final energy in
\par
the following sectors: the four industrial sectors mentioned, the transport sector
\par
with a subdivision into road, rail, water and air transport systems, the commer-
\par
cial branch, private households and the non-energetic consumption in the petro-
\par
chemical industry. As a basis for these analyses, in some cases the useful energy
\par
demand is determined, e.g. space heating demand, goods or passenger transport
\par
volume, and iron and steel production in the steel industry. The energy demand
\par
module allows the simulation of energy saving by introducing energy saving meas-
\par
ures, such as better insulation of buildings, and the implementation of decentra-
\par
1ized technologies, for example, solar equipment and heat pump for warm water and
\par
space heating appl ications. Thus an assessment of decisions in this field can be
\par
simulated.
\par
The energy supply module has the task of meeting the energy demand calculated by
\par
the er.ergy demand module. It therefore accounts for the energy flows on the
\par
supply side, with its conversion capacities, transformation losses, imports,
\par
exports, stockpi ling, indigenous mining, distribution losses etc. These are
\par
described by a set of simultaneous equations which are solved for every year.
\par
Within the energy supply module there is a power plant construction routine which
\par
computes the annual erection and decommissioning of the different types of elec-
\par
trical power plants required and the total installed capacity to meet the electri-
\par
city demand for the calculated years. It is worth mentioning that the energy
\par
supply module al lows for the simulation of different new energy supply technolo-
\par
gies like coal gasification, coal 1iquefaction, hydrogen and methanol production
\par
etc.
\par
The environmental module is an emission module which calculates the emissions of
\par
different types of pollutants due to energy consumption and energy conversion in
\par
the various sectors. Thus the emissions by sectors, by pollutants and the overall
\par
emissions can be derived.
\par
OPERATION OF LESS (IRECA, DAIMOS, OASIS)
\par
With the growing complexity of LESS and the enormous quantities of data to be
\par
handled, the software data management problems increased. To overcome these pro-
\par
blems a special FORTRAN programmed package of tools has been developed to facili-
\par
tate the construction testing and operation of LESS (Drepper and Hermes, 1979).
\par
Figure 2 shows the data and software environment of LESS. These are
\par
- the model base containing the above mentioned modules of LESS,
\par
- the data base, and
\par
- the software packages DAMOS, IRECA and OASIS.
\par
Their application is not restricted to LESS and may be extended also to other
\par
large dynamic simulation systems (Drepper, Heckler and Schwefel, 1979).
