- OASIS contains all important derivative-free parameter optimization techniques
\par
as described and tested by Schwefel (1977) which can be superimposed on the DAIMOS
\par
guided simulation model (Heckler and Schwefel, 1978). No changes are necessary
\par
within the model itself. The variables to be changed are given to the optimiza-
\par
tion techniques by name and can even be individual items of time series. The same
\par
holds for the objective function and the constraints to be incorporated. The
\par
starting point given does not have to be feasible and during a search for a maxi-
\par
mum or minimum the otpimization method used can be changed interactively. There
\par
are two ways of combining a simulation model, e.g. LESS, with OASIS:
\par
- Simulation within the optimization, which has already been achieved.
\par
- Optimization within the simulation, a technique which wi 11 be available in the
\par
near future.
\par
Normally the second way does not lead to an overall optimum, because optimization
\par
at certain time steps does not necessarily lead to a global optimum over the full
\par
time range. Therefore the first way wi 11 be preferred.
\par
IRECA, DAIMOS and OASIS operate with identical input and output formats. Thus
\par
they form a coupled system working with the same data bases. The three interfaces
\par
are programmed in FORTRAN, so that LESS, or any other simulation model, has to be
\par
written in FORTRAN too. The advantage is an easy transportabil ity from one compu-
\par
ter to another and the possibility that a model builder may calIon all available
\par
library subroutines and is therefore no longer restricted to special subsets of
\par
numerical algorithms.
