\par
CONCLUSIONS
\par
The above described techniques of simulation, optimization and analysis, are now
\par
based on the threefold system.
\par
IRECA
\par
answering questions such as: "How are system variables inter! inked
\par
with each other?"
\par
(systems analysis)
\par
LESS/DAIMOS
\par
answering questions such as: "What will happen, if ....?"
\par
(systems simulation)
\par
LESS/OASIS
\par
answering questions such as "By what action can a given goal be
\par
reached?"
\par
(systems synthesis)
\par
The transition from a merely simulation oriented analysis to an optimization of
\par
simulation models can be seen as a step from systems analysis and systems simula-
\par
tion to systems synthesis. It is clear that this latter way of investigation
\par
needs an active participation of decision makers in the sense that goals must be
\par
formulated. The passive role characterized by looking at a system when asking
\par
"What happens, if ....?" is not longer sufficient and must be supplemented by the
\par
normative way. Both ways together allow answers to be given to a broad range of
\par
different questions concerning alternative energy strategies for the future with
\par
one single model.
\par
Using the tools described above at their best, would be done by asking for those
\par
decisions which have to be taken now for certain, and those which could be
\par
in order to avoid unrepairable mistakes.
