ABSTRACT
\par
This work has a purpose to release every work about energy modeling that
\par
have been taking place in Mexico mentioning briefly what it consists on and
\par
the results. Finally it will discuss problems gained from having tried to
\par
implement this kind of systems.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
The Mexican energy sector represents roughly 6\% of the Gross Domestic Prod-
\par
uct and has been growing at a historical annual rate of 6.8\%. Total energy
\par
demand for 1978 was 713 x 1012 Kcal and was distributed roughly in the fol-
\par
lowing way: 26\% industrial sector; 30\% transportation sector; 8\% residential
\par
and commercial sectors and 30\% consumption by the energy sector itself, and
\par
the remaining to others such as agriculture, etc. Of this demand, 87\% was
\par
supplied by oil and gas; 7.2\% by hydroelectricity and geothermal* and the re-
\par
maining 5.07\% by coal. These proportions have been almost constant during
\par
the last two decades, with a small increase in the participation of oil and gas
\par
due to the relative decrease in the participation of hydroelectricity.
\par
In Mexico, nationalized enterprises are responsible of energy supply. While
\par
Petr61eos Mexicanos provides hydrocarbons, and Comisi6n Federal de Electri-
\par
cidad electricity, both of them are coordinated and supervised by Secretarfa
\par
de Patrimonio y Fomento Industrial. Mainly these enterprises have worked on
\par
developing models to the energy sector. The most important goal of this pa-
\par
per is to show all the efforts that have been done on this field, briefly describ-
\par
ing the systems and works generated, particularly those general systems use
\par
to determine energy consumption in Mexico.
\par
