\par
Development of the Energy Sector
\par
Energy supply in Mexico is the responsibility of government owned enterprises.
\par
Under the present organization scheme of the public administration the respon-
\par
sibility to coordinate and supervise the energy supply is an important faculty
\par
of Secretarra de Patrimonio y Fomento Industrial.
\par
The oil industry was nationalized in 1938 by integrating the foreign owned oil
\par
private enterprises into the recently created PetrOleos Mexicanos (Pemex),
\par
which became one of the largest oil industries in the world. Actually Pemex
\par
is the most important industry in the country, it is organized as an integrated
\par
industry, covering all the aspects from exploration to production, refining and
\par
distribution of products, including primary petrochemicals.
\par
In 1978, Pemex processed 485.3 million barrels of crude, condensates and
\par
gas liquids and produced 2,561. 4 million cubic feet of gas per day. Of its
\par
total production of crude and liquids, 365, 000 barrels per day were for ex-
\par
portation.
\par
ComisiOn Federal de Electricidad (CFE) is the single electriC utility in the
\par
country. It was established in 1937 as a small utility to promote the electri-
\par
fication of those regions of the country that were not economically attractive
\par
for the private utilities. In the early 1960's the private utilities were pur-
\par
chased by the Government and integrated into CFE. In 1978, the total in-
\par
stalled capacity was 13,992 MW out of which 5,225 MW came from hydro-
\par
electric plants. In that year some 8.6 million consumers were connected,
\par
being approximately 26, 000 of the industrial category. Electricity generated
\par
in that year was 55 TWH.
\par
The ComisiOn Nacional de Energia AtOmica was established in January 1979,
\par
in order to coordinate the two nuclear entities that were established at the
\par
same time: Uranio Mexicano (Uramex), responsible for exploration, mining
\par
and milling of uranium, and the Instituto Nacional de Investigaciones Nuclea-
\par
res (ININ) responsible for research and development work in the nuclear field,
\par
as well as for the promotion of the peaceful uses of atomic energy.
\par
MexicO does not have an integrated coal industry. Actually coal is mined es-
\par
sentially by the steel industry which is both government and private owned
\par
through a concession system administered by Secretarra de Patrimonio y Fo-
\par
mento Industrial.
\par
The Energy Commission was established in February 1973, as a mechanism
\par
to assist in the coordination of the energy sector. Its main objective is to
\par
analyze and propose long range energy policies on the basis of the knowledge
\par
available about primary resources within the overall economic framework
\par
defined by the government.
\par
This Commission is also a forum to deal with matters common to Pemex,
\par
CFE, and the nuclear institutions.
\par
With respect to the renewable energy systems, research and development is
\par
carried out essentially by academic institutions but operating without a coor-
\par
dinating group to these activities.
