ISSUES IN MODELLING ASPECTS OF THE
\par
GREEK ENERGY PROBLEM
\par
J.-E. Samouilidis
\par
Chair for Industrial Management, National Technical University of Athens,
\par
28is Oktovriou 42, Athens, Greece
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
This paper highlights the main aspects of the Greek energy problem and reviews the
\par
current state of the research effort undertaken in order to model these aspects.
\par
An energy POlicy model is under development, which can be used to access the impact
\par
of energy policies on the economy, in a long term perspective. The model includes
\par
four submodels: an energy demand model;an energy-economy model; an energy system
\par
model; and an economic growth model. The paper presents the structure of the ener-
\par
gy policy model and gives some details on the energy-economy and energy system
\par
models.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy modelling; energy-economy interaction; energy system model; linear program-
\par
ming; energy policy.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
The Greek energy problem, though not basically different from that of many other
\par
countries, has its own peculiarities. These are mainly due to the development sta-
\par
ge of the economy, which is somewhere between the post-industrial societies arid
\par
the developing agricultural economies. The income elasticity of energy demand has
\par
been very high in the last twenty years, due to the rapid industrialisation pro-
\par
cess; and prospects are not optimistic, given that the industrial sector, and in
\par
particular the high-energy-intensity industries, are expected to be the leading
\par
growth sectors of the economy. On the other hand, increasing prices are
\par
exercising a heavy pressure on the already sensitive balance of payments, in an
\par
economy which is roughly 70\% dependent on foreign sources of energy supply. Deve-
\par
lopment of indigenous resources, like lignite oil and natural gas, though expected
\par
to relieve existing pressures, is tied to high capital expenditure, Which is also
\par
required for industrial expansion.
\par
A modelling effort, centered around the abOve aspects, has been undertaken at the
\par
Chair for Industrial Management of the National Technical University of Athens.
\par
The aim of the work, which is still ongoing, is to develop an Energy PoZiay ModeZ-
\par
GREPOM, intended to give a better understanding of the interrelashionship among
\par
the factors that are shaping the energy policy formulation.
\par
