\par
ASPECTS OF THE GREEK ENERGY PROBLEM
\par
The Greek economy has been a fast growing economy for the last twenty five years.
\par
The per capita gross national product has overtripled during that period and
\par
reached the level of 3500 \$, at current prices, in 1978, Which corresponds to 50\%
\par
of the average income per capita in the European Community. This development
\par
process was accompanied, as one could expect, by considerable structural changes
\par
in the economy, which were particularly reflected in two ratios: (1) the ratio of
\par
the product of the industrial sector to the GDP, which has gone up from 14 \% (1958)
\par
to 22 \% (1977); (2) the ratio of all energy intensive industries to the product of
\par
the industrial sector, which has also increased by 50 \% in the last twenty years.
\par
Despite the high degree of uncertainty which is characteristic of future growth,
\par
government plans indicate that the country's growth rate should, and could, be
\par
higher than the expected average in the European Community. It is forecasted, and
\par
also intended, that industry will be the leading growth sector, with particular
\par
emphasis given to some metal extracting and processing industries, which could
\par
thus contribute to a higher utilization rate of indigenous natural resources.
\par
As a result of the above, it \textpm s more than obvious, that energy should be a factor
\par
of primary consideration in all economic development plans. It could be argued,
\par
that the role of energy in economic growth is more critical in the Greek economy,
\par
than is the case with the developed economies in which the composition of the GDP
\par
will remain either constant, or even change in favour of the service sector.
\par
Following the trends which were observed in the GDP, energy demand in the Greek
\par
economy has been growing with high rates, in the last twenty five years. Average
\par
annual rates were in the range of 10-12 \%before 1973 and decreased to 8-10 \% the-
\par
re after. In 1978, energy consumption reached at 1.5 tons of oil equivalent per
\par
capita, which is still very low when compared to the corresponding figure in the
\par
U.S. or the E.C. Income elasticity of demand has remained in the past rather
\par
stable on a level which is characteristic of developing economies, ie 1.5-1.\&
\par
Total expenditure for energy, which amounted to only 5 \%of the GDP in 1965,
\par
reached 9 \% in 1973 and overpassed the 10 \% level in 1976. This overdoubling in
\par
the energy contribution to GDP is due both to an increase of the energy use per
\par
unit of product and to an increase of the energy prices. In relation to the total
\par
expenditure for energy, it must also be pointed out that 90 \%of it represents
\par
foreign exchange payments. Thus expenditure for imported energy which was only 90
\par
million US \$ in 1966, reached the level of 1.3 billion in 1978. This last figure
\par
corresponds to 20 \% of the value of imported goods.
\par
The share of the various carriers of primary energy has been gradually changing,
\par
in favour of indigenous natural resources. Today oil accounts for 70 \%, coal
\par
lignite-- for 26 \%, and hydroenergy for 4 \%of total primary energy con-
\par
sumption. Significant changes, have taken place in the final energy side. Thus
\par
the share of electricity has increased from 27 \%in 1965 to 35 \% in 1978. At the
\par
same period the share of lignite in electricity generation has gone up from 44 \%
\par
to 58 \%. Natural gas is not used today in the Greek energy system.
\par
- 241 -
\par
Considerable changes have also occurred in the structure of energy demand. In
\par
1978, the industry's share was roughly 45 \%, transportation accounted for 24 \%,
\par
the rest 33 \%having been covered by domestic and miscellaneous users.
\par
The country "50 main energy resources, under exploitation today, are lignite and hy-
\par
droenergy. Known lignite reserves are estimated at 5.500 million tons of low
\par
calorific value fuel.
\par
Hydroenergy potential is estimated at 20.000 GWh, out of which 19 \%is nOw under
\par
utilization. There is no production of oil and gas, at present. Oil and associa-
\par
ted gas reserves were discovered in Northern Greece. Oil and gas production.
\par
planned to begin in 2 years, will reach a peak, which is equivalent of 12 \% of
\par
current demand for oil. Major exploration activities are underway in various
\par
parts of the country for hydrocarbons, uranium and geothermal energy. Indicative
\par
results seem promising.
\par
Major structural decisions for the Greek energy system are today: (1) The introdu-
\par
ction of natural gas; negotiations are on the way with the major gas suppliers.
\par
(2) The use of nuclear technology for electricity generation; it is expected that
\par
the first nuclear power station of 600-800 MW will be in operation at the end of
\par
the 1980's; two or three units of similar size will follow in the neMt decade.
\par
(3) The utilization of very poor lignite deposits to be burnt in thermal power
\par
stations.
\par
THE ENERGY POLICY MODEL
\par
The Energy Policy Model, actually a set of models, provides a tool for the analy-
\par
sis of energy policies, which can be used to access the impact of these policies
\par
on the Greek economy, in a long term perspective. The model includes four (sub)
\par
models, each being in a different stage of development: an energy demand model,
\par
already operative; an energy-economy model, in the stage of result evaluation; an
\par
energy system model, in the final stage of formulation; and an economic growth mo-
\par
del, in the stage of conception.
\par
The model, which is shown in Fig. 1, presents some conceptual similarities with
\par
the set of energy models at the International Institute of Applied Systems Analy-
\par
sis, as described by Hafele and Basile (1978).' Three growth parameters are in use
\par
at this stage: gross national product, the participation of industry in GNP, and
\par
the participation of energy intensive industry in the product of the industrial
\par
sector. The latter two, having proved to be decisive factors influencing energy
\par
demand, are in continuous change in the Greek economy, unlike the stability which
\par
they have shown in the post-industrial economies. These growth parameters are
\par
combined with the exogenous crude oil prices to generate scenarios, which drive an
\par
energy demand model.
\par
The Energy Demand Model is based on a combined econometric and optimization
\par
approach, as described by Samoulidis and Pappas (1979). A set of classical econo-
\par
metric models is used to forecast energy demand for each one of the twenty sectors
\par
of the economy, based on historical data. These are fed into a goal
\par
programming model, which functions as an "information integrator". Besides the e-
\par
conometric models, the goal programming model retrieves also information from other
\par
sources such as: government reports on energy policy objectives; feasibility re-
\par
ports on new uses of energy; and the forecaster's judgemental analysis. All the-
\par
se pieces of information are integrated by the goal programming model, to provide
\par
a set of consistent energy demand forecasts.
\par
- 242 -
\par
I' These forecasts are, however, open loop forecastB'in the sense that they are ba-
\par
sed on the assumption of zero energy-economy interaction. Thus they are revised
\par
to take into consideration the existing interaction between the economic and the
\par
energy systems. The revision is performed through a very simple Energy-Eaonomy
\par
Model, based on a conceptual framework described by Sweeney (1978). This model is
\par
summarized in the following section.
