THE ENERGY SYSTEM MODEL
\par
The energy system model, described in this section, follows the already established
\par
tradition of using linear programming to represent the energy system.
\par
The planning horizon of the model covers a 35-year period starting in 1980 and
\par
ending in 2015. The planning horizon is divided into four periods 1980-85;
\par
1985-90; 1990-2000; 2000-1015; thus giving the LP model a dynamic structure. We
\par
are primarily concerned with the system structure in the first three periods, the
\par
fourth period playing the role of a "buffer". The variables of the model are ex-
\par
pressed in period averages of annual quantities.
\par
There are two types of decision variables in the model. The strategia of struatu-
\par
variables represent new capacity or expansion of existing capacity in the
\par
energy system: eg electricity generating capacity, refinery capacity, gas pipeline
\par
capacity, or distribution network capacity. These variables are associated with
\par
investment costs. The r;aatiaaZ or variables represent how the capacity
\par
of the various installations is utilized; eg electrical energy produced, diesel oil
\par
produced, natural gas transported or aistributed etc. These variables are asso-
\par
ciated with operation costs.
\par
The model constraints are structured in five modules: the electricity module; the
\par
oil module; the coal module; the natural gas module; and the energy demand module.
\par
The model considers three alternative objective functions, which can be either
\par
used one at a time in the simple LP versions of the model, or might be used simul-
\par
taneously in a goal programming version.
\par
One of the objective functions represents total cost extending over the planning
\par
horizon. Total cost for meeting the demand in energy is analysed in two compoIJ:!nts:
\par
investment cost, which is expressed as a linear function of the strategic variables
\par
of the model; operation cost, which is expressed as a linear function of the tacti-
\par
cal variables of the modeL All costs, incurring during the course of the planning
\par
horizon, are discounted to a present value. Residual values of equipment are also
\par
taken into consideration.
\par
The two other objective functions to be also minimlaed express respectively: the
\par
quantity of primary energy consumed; the quantity of imported energy.
