CONCLUDING REMARKS
\par
This paper presented the present stage of the ongoing research work on energy mo-
\par
delling, at the Chair for Industrial Management of the National Technical Univer-
\par
sity of Athens. The structure of an energy policy model was described, and some
\par
details of two of the component models were given.
\par
Within the context of a simplified energy-economy model, an effort was made to ana-
\par
lyse the impact of elasticities of demand, both price and income elasticities, on
\par
NNP and energy changes, caused by energy price increases. It has been confirmed
\par
that, higher price elasticities of demand produce greater energy reductions, for a
\par
given energy price increase; but on the other hand tend to have a smaller impact
\par
on the national product. Income elasticity of demand which is assumed equal to 1.0
\par
for the industrialised economies, is higher for the developing economies, taking
\par
values as high as 1.6. It has been shown that for a given price increase, the
\par
greater the income elasticity, the greater will be the reduction of energy demand,
\par
but the smaller will be the reduction of the NNP. It should be noted, however,
\par
that NNP is not very sensitive to income elasticity changes, and relevant redu-
\par
ctions tend to be very small. On the other hand energy seems to me more sensitive.
\par
Given the complexity and the great uncertainty that are characteristic of the
\par
nergy future, we are trying to keep our modelling effort modular and flexible as
\par
much as possible, so that quick answers are given to the ever arising questions.
\par
We also make an effort to develop models that are small and simple, capable of
\par
capturing the basic interrelationships between the main underlying factors. Not
\par
only because this is more appropriate for small countries, having at their dispo-
\par
sal only iimited modelling resources; but also beca\textbackslash ise recent experience in
\par
advanced countries has indicated that small, well thought, models do not necessa-
\par
rily say less than big models (Energy Modeling Forum, 1977).
