PLANNING AND ANALYSIS OF ENERGY
\par
RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT PROGRAMS
\par
K. C. Hoffman
\par
MA THTECH, Inc., Princeton, New Jersey, USA
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
In response to higher prices and projected shortages of energy, particularly liquid
\par
fuels, many nations have launched large research and development programs. For max-
\par
imum effectiveness, these programs must deal with new technologies for both the sup-
\par
ply and utilization of energy--particularly liquid fuels--in a balanced way. While
\par
the outcome of research and development is, by definition, uncertain, it is neces-
\par
sary to plan national and international programs that are balanced and effective.
\par
The approaches to R\&D planning and analysis vary according to the stage of a tech-
\par
nology in the research development and commercialization cycle. This paper reviews
\par
analytical approaches to the planning and analysis of national and international
\par
energy research and development programs. Among the special topics discussed are
\par
the market-oriented, total energy system concept, the coupling of energy and econo-
\par
mic models, and milti-objective analysis. The methods may also be employed by
\par
industrial groups involved in significant R\&D investments and by government sponsors
\par
of energy R\&D.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Research and development; planning; assessment; modeling.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
The management of an energy research, development, and demonstration program re-
\par
quires the explicit definition of a set of goals and objectives for technologies
\par
under development, and extremely close monitoring of progress in the R\&D program
\par
towards meeting these goals and objectives. Strategic goals and objectives define
\par
the need for liquid fuels, electricity, and environmental controls consistent with
\par
a desired or projected pattern of economic development. Alternative supply and end-
\par
use technologies may be evaluated against these objectives by comparing their tech-
\par
nical, economic, and environmental characteristics in terms of such parameters as
\par
efficiency of performance, reliability, maintainability, first cost and operating
\par
cost, and emissions to air and water. R\&D priorities may be established by evalu-
\par
ating alternative systems in the context of projections of energy markets, as well
\par
as economic and environmental development. All of these factors, as well as the
\par
ultimate characteristics of the technology under development, are, of course, highly
\par
uncertain. These uncertainties and risks must be evaluated to the extent possible
\par
and factored into the R\&D planning and analysis process.
\par
The research, development, and demonstration cycle for a new technology includes the
\par
following steps:
\par
- 251 -
\par
- 252 -
\par
1. Conceptual phase: definition of needs or requirements, or more
\par
likely, generation of new ideas or concepts;
\par
2. Survey of applicable concepts;
\par
3. Basic and applied research;
\par
4. System development, demonstration, and commercialization.
\par
It is important to note that following the initiation step, Step 1, which leads to
\par
the perception that a new technological concept can fulfill a need in the private
\par
or public sector, a selection process must occur, based on technical and/or econo-
\par
mic factors. Up to the point of commercial or practical implementation, systems
\par
which are conceptually feasible may encounter technical, economic, or social bar-
\par
riers which either render them unacceptable for further development when considered
\par
alone, or when compared to alternatives which appear more viable as the R\&D cycle
\par
proceeds.
\par
In addition to the technical and economic analyses performed during the R\&D cycle,
\par
energy technologies must be scrutinized within the overall energy system itself.
\par
This implies that a credible analysis must rely upon a basic reference (conven-
\par
tional) system to serve as a benchmark against which a new or advanced technology
\par
may be gauged in terms of technical, economic, and environmental attributes. Such
\par
comparisons can provide a required measure of consistency in terms of system costs,
\par
resource constraints, technology characteristics. A uniform set of assumptions
\par
regarding fuel prices, material costs, labor costs, and productivity, and infla-
\par
tion rates for the time frames of interest is necessary for such comparisons.
\par
These factors, which affect the economic viability of new technologies, may be
\par
taken as parameters permitting a series of sensitivity analyses to be performed
\par
regarding the assumptions used for R\&D planning. All too often, new technologies
\par
are compared with other advanced concepts without ever addressing a comparison with
\par
conventional, well characterized, and perhaps superior alternatives. The following
\par
discussion deals with the topics to be addressed in R\&D planning and analysis, cri-
\par
teria that may be applied in R\&D assessments, and information needs for the manage-
\par
ment of R\&D programs.
\par
The questions to be addressed in R\&D planning, and the analytical approaches to be
\par
used, vary considerably through the steps of the R\&D cycle from the definition of
\par
requirements through research and development to commercialization.
\par
CRITERIA FOR R\&D ASSESSMENT
\par
The criteria used to establish priorities among energy research and development
\par
options generally involve a complex mix of technical, economic, environmental,
\par
social, and institutional factors. The relative weights given to each of these
\par
factors are closely related to overall national objectives for security of resource
\par
supply, protection of the environment, and economic development. An energy R\&D
\par
program must address these issues at the near, intermediate, and long term in a
\par
balanced way.
\par
The technical options to be pursued in an R\&D program are classified according to
\par
the time frame that they impact upon; and their contributions to the energy system
\par
over time may be analyzed. These options may also be classified according to their
\par
current stage of development, i.e., research, development, or demonstration. The
\par
criteria by which options are evaluated will vary according to this stage of devel-
\par
opment. The selection among promising options in the early development stage,
\par
where technical and economic characteristics are poorly defined, is quite different
\par
from that among candidates for large demonstration projects where a technological
\par
base has been established. The current stage of development of a given technology
\par
is closely correlated with the time frame in which it will impact, and thus, this
\par
- 253 -
\par
classification may be made with respect to either attribute.
\par
The priorities established on the basis of these criteria are not necessarily rela-
\par
ted to the level at which a program is funded. There are several program elements
\par
that may show a very high priority rating but that simply do not require the same
\par
expenditure as a more complex technology having a much lower priority. The demon-
\par
stration of a number of solar heating systems, for example, is much less costly
\par
than a single coal gasification plant. High priority options should be funded on
\par
the same basis to the extent permitted by budgetary constraints. When funding lev-
\par
els must be reduced below the level required to ensure successful development of
\par
all options, the lower priority options are either reduced to a minimum viable
\par
level, or dropped from the R\&D plan entirely.
\par
It is also misleading to determine priorities on the basis of energy provided (or
\par
saved) per dollar invested in R\&D. The analysis must include cost savings attri-
\par
butable to implementation of the newly developed technology compared with the alter-
\par
native technology that would have been used instead, as well as the cost of the R\&D
\par
programs. For example, a technology requiring a larger R\&D program may contribute
\par
fewer energy units, but at a significantly lower cose than the technology it re-
\par
places. Thus, this technology could have an advantage when all costs and savings
\par
were considered, but would look rather poor when compared on the quantity of energy
\par
delivered per dollar invested in R\&D.
\par
Conservation options must be considered in a similar fashion by accounting for
\par
energy saved, along with dollars invested in R\&D and dollars invested in extra cap-
\par
ital equipment associated with the conservation measure. Further, to estimate all
\par
of the benefits, any cost savings attributable to the effect of reallocating scarce
\par
resources released for other uses by the conservation technology should be accoun-
\par
ted for.
\par
Following is a summary of the criteria that apply among technical options at each
\par
of the stages of development. An overlaying set of criteria and evaluations apply
\par
to the assignment of priorities between the time frames; e.g., fusion compared with
\par
higher efficiency end-use devices. The problem of addressing the appropriate allo-
\par
cation of priorities between stages of development or time frames deals with the
\par
desire to resolve immediate problems as compared with the protection of future gen-
\par
erations. There is no firm analytical basis for such comparisons and a research
\par
and development program must deal with these objectives in as balanced a way as
\par
possible.
\par
Demonstration Projects to Impact in Near Term (to 1985)
\par
The new technical options that will impact in the near term are those that are now
\par
in the advanced development and demonstration phase. The program objectives gener-
\par
ally involve the demonstration of engineering and economic feasibility. Some of
\par
the projects in this category deal also with technologies that have been developed
\par
and are now being implemented, R\&D problems that are constraining the rate
\par
of implementation of the technology. In this latter area, the R\&D program should
\par
enhance the implementation of developed systems by removing barriers to public ac-
\par
ceptance, providing the means of satisfying recent codes and standards, and provid-
\par
ing an improved basis for the establishment of new codes and standards. Included
\par
in this area is research on reactor safety, environmental control technology, and
\par
biomedical effects of pollutants.
\par
The technical and economic characteristics of near term technologies are generally
\par
well defined and are important criteria. The role of the technology in supporting
\par
current national objectives is of equal importance in this time frame. Clean energy
\par
systems, such as solar-storage combinations, must be developed and demonstrated
\par
despite the possibility of their having a higher current cost than conventional
\par
- 254 -
\par
options. Further, demonstration projects that are candidates for wider, near term
\par
implementation should be compatible with the existing energy infrastructure in order
\par
to make effective use of existing facilities.
\par
In the evaluations performed on large projects approaching the latter stage of de-
\par
velopment, it is important to consider the economics of the system as well as the
\par
policy background. Such policies as peak load pricing of electricity, energy taxes,
\par
and subsidies have a strong influence on the market penetration of technologies.
\par
The influence of such policies on the economic competitiveness of new technologies
\par
must be understood. This understanding is necessary both from the viewpoint of
\par
judging the commercial viability of a system against known policies and from the
\par
viewpoint of estimating the policy actions necessary to bring a system into the
\par
market in order to exploit its potential environmental or fuel saving benefits.
\par
These considerations are especially pertinent to the solar options.
\par
The availability of labor, materials, resources, and an industrial base to imple-
\par
ment these technologies in the near term are very important. If these are projec-
\par
ted to be available, then there almost certainly will be a sufficient base for the
\par
construction of demonstration facilities.
\par
Development Projects to Impact in Intermediate Term (1985-2000)
\par
Most technical options that will have an impact in the intermediate term are cur-
\par
rently in the development phase. The technical and economic characteristics of
\par
these technologies are not well defined at their present state of development, hence
\par
they must offer some other potential advantages to be assigned a high priority. A
\par
R\&D program must be designed to achieve the appropriate scientific and engineering
\par
basis for eventual demonstration and implementation, and to obtain an improved def-
\par
inition of the technical and economic characteristics of the technology. The ob-
\par
jective of this work is primarily the demonstration of engineering feasibility.
\par
The critical national objectives and attendant energy R\&D objectives that will hold
\par
in this time frame are also less clear than in the near term. uncertainties exist
\par
with regard to future trends of energy demands, the ultimate availability of domes-
\par
tic resources, and the need to address specific national needs. The R\&D priorities
\par
have to be tested for sensitivity to these future developments. In view of the un-
\par
certainties that exist, the portion of the program directed at technologies in this
\par
category serves to increase the available options. This serves to remove the con-
\par
straints that energy problems place on national economic and social objectives, and
\par
thereby broadens the feasible range of policy alternatives.
\par
In this category, there is sufficient lead time to develop an appropriate manpower
\par
and industrial base for implementation, but the availability of R\&D manpower at the
\par
present time may be limiting.
\par
Research and Early Development, Impact in Long Term (2000 and Beyond)
\par
Most basic research projects and some complex development programs fall into this
\par
category. The important criteria for basic research programs deal with the oppor-
\par
tunities to add to basic knowledge and understanding of energy-related phenomena
\par
and the availability of researchers with sufficient background or experience to work
\par
effectively in the field. These may include people now in the field, or those that
\par
may transfer from other fields of research. This area of energy R\&D must be sup-
\par
ported to the extent with which funds can be effectively utilized and receives this
\par
priority because of its importance in providing the basis for future breakthroughs,
\par
and for technological supremacy of the nation. R\&D also serves to generate options
\par
that will provide flexibility and protection against future risks or problems. In
\par
this regard, it is useful to consider potential risks or problems, such as a possi-
\par
- 255 -
\par
ble C02 catastrophe, that could dictate either a change in policy or the most ag-
\par
gressive pursuit of alternative technologies.
\par
The complex development programs that may be implemented in this time frame must be
\par
judged against the promise that they hold as an abundant resource base, a clean
\par
form of energy, or any other special distinguishing characteristics. These criteria
\par
emphasize the requirement that long term programs must represent significantly nif-
\par
ferent options from those in the near and intermediate term.
\par
The uncertainties regarding energy requirements and availability and national needs,
\par
are much greater in this time frame than in earlier periods. Similarly, technical
\par
and economic characteristics are poorly defined. Programs in this category deal
\par
with proof of scientific feasibility. Formal analysis is not really relevant to
\par
the determination of priorities at this stage of research.
\par
General Criteria for R\&D Program Elements
\par
Following, is a statement of general criteria applied to various program elements.
\par
Regardless of the time frame, all development and demonstration projeats must con-
\par
sider these criteria:
\par
- Environmental impacts of technology and related factors
\par
affecting public acceptability;
\par
- Need for Federal support (related to work in the private
\par
sector);
\par
- Probability of technological success;
\par
- Cost of achieving R\&D objective.
\par
Near term demonstration projects must also evaluate:
\par
- Projected level of implementation and contribution to energy
\par
requirements (special emphasis on substitution for, and
\par
conservation of, oil);
\par
- Technical efficiency of process, including analysis of energy
\par
inputs to plant construction (net energy);
\par
- Projected capital and operating cost of commercial size
\par
plant and economic benefits attributable to implementation;
\par
- Compatibility with existing energy infrastructure;
\par
- Adequacy of industrial base to support implementation and
\par
severity of any other barriers (legal-political);
\par
- Adequacy of resource base to support the implementation
\par
(energy resources, water, other resources);
\par
- Adequacy of manpower base to support the implementation
\par
(must communities be developed and people be relocated?);
\par
- Adequacy of scientific and engineering base (is demonstra-
\par
tion premature?).
\par
General criteria that apply to intermediate term development programs are:
\par
- Projected level of implementation and contribution to energy
\par
requirements (special emphasis on substitution for, and
\par
conservation of, oil);
\par
- Availability of energy resources to support implementation
\par
and long term operation of commercial plants or devices
\par
based on this development program;
\par
