\par
- Potential for broadening range of national policy options;
\par
- Diversity that would be added to the energy system;
\par
- Need for additional information on technical and economic
\par
characteristics;
\par
- Availability of trained personnel to perform R\&D.
\par
Long term program elements (2000 and beyond) should assess:
\par
- Potential for significant augmentation of energy resource
\par
base to substitute for scarce fuels;
\par
- Potential to assist in end-use substitution of abundant
\par
resources for scarce fuels;
\par
- Potential to reduce environmental damage, or to avoid such
\par
contingency problems as a C02 crisis;
\par
- Potential to add to base of scientific knowledge;
\par
- Opportunity cost of research (value of near and
\par
mediate term programs that cannot be supported if this
\par
long term research is
\par
In the planning and management of energy research, development, and demonstration,
\par
analysis is performed at several levels for a variety of purposes. Strategic an-
\par
alysis is carried out at the higher management levels on a range of technological
\par
and policy strategies. While such analysis must capture the essential economic,
\par
technical, and environmental characteristics of alternative technologies, it gener-
\par
ally does not involve a detailed process description of individual technologies
\par
(e.g., solar power tower) and their components. Such analysis at the strategic
\par
level usually deals with an array of technologies that are evaluated against broad
\par
national goals and objectives, and must treat the entire energy system embedded
\par
in the economy and ecosphere.
\par
Analysis at the Program Level, however, must deal with the mechanical and process
\par
description of technologies and major components. It must be designed to feed
\par
appropriate information on the essential characteristics of the energy technology
\par
or system under development to the strategic analysis and, in turn, accept con-
\par
straints on, and objectives for, the economic, technical, and environmental charac-
\par
teristics as determined by energy and economic or environmental policy. A good
\par
deal of energy process and technology modeling is in progress to address this need.
\par
Ideally, the results of this design process should be captured in the strategic
\par
level analysis. In the reverse direction, the strategy and policy goals must be
\par
reflected in the design of the specific technical process. The nuclear technology
\par
area is an example of one that has progressed to this point. Early systems analy-
\par
ses of alternative nuclear reactors and fuel cycles concentrated almost exclusively
\par
on minimum cost systems. Recently, the new strategic objectives of risk aversion
\par
and nonproliferation have been factored into the analyses. along with conventional
\par
economics.
\par
ANALYTICAL METHODS
\par
The large number of technical options that exist or are under development for the
\par
supply, conversion, and utilization of energy, as well as the complexity of the
\par
overall energy system, has led to the widespread use of analytical models and data
\par
bases for the assessment of energy technologies. A number of these analytical
\par
models are reviewed and summarized by Hoffman and wood!
\par
A more detailed review of the use of a selected set of analytical models in energy
\par
policy has been conducted by weyant. 2 With respect to R\&D assessment, Weyant's
\par
- 257 -
\par
review includes the Gulf-SRI Model developed by Cazalet 3 and its application to a
\par
synthetic fuels assessment, and the Brookhaven-Hudson-Jorgenson energy-economic
\par
modeling system that was utilized by the united States Energy Research and Develop-
\par
ment in its formulation of national R\&D plans.' In the former case,
\par
the Gulf-SRI Model was used to address a specific decision problem, the level and
\par
mix of a synthetic fuel demonstration program. The Brookhaven-Hudson-Jorgenson
\par
modeling system was used in a different way, as a framework for the overall R\&D
\par
plan to ensure that a sufficiently broad set of options was being pursued and to
\par
estimate national benefits of that plan. This latter R\&D planning process made sub-
\par
stantial use of the Reference Energy System as a framework for analysis encompas-
\par
sing the entire energy system.
\par
The Total Energy System Perspective
\par
The Reference Energy System (RES), Figure 1, is a network representation of the
\par
overall energy system encompassing all of the physical activities required in the
\par
supply and utilization of various forms of energy used to deliver energy services.
\par
Technologies are represented for all operations involving specific fuels, including
\par
their extraction, refinement, conversion, transport, distribution, and utilization.
\par
Each of these activities is represented by a link in the network for which effi-
\par
ciency, environmental impact, and the cost coefficients may be specified. The net-
\par
work is quantified for a given year with the level of energy demands, and the energy
\par
flows through the supply activities that are required to serve those demands. The
\par
total environmental effects, resource consumption, and costs for the energy system
\par
are tabulated for each planning year. It provides a compact information system
\par
that can be used directly, in a manual fashion or with computer assistance, for the
\par
evaluation of alternative policies and technologies. Projections of demands and
\par
flows represented in the Reference Energy System have been developed for future
\par
years using a variety of analytical techniques, ranging from a judgmental approach
\par
to rather sophisticated energy and economic models. The Reference Energy System
\par
is designed to permit the assessment of individual technologies, or a broad set of
\par
technolgies and policies ranging across all supply and utilization options, with
\par
emphasis on the potential markets for the technologies.
\par
A major feature of the RES is the treatment of end-use devices as a part of the
\par
energy system, and the correlation of energy demands in these devices with func-
\par
tional definitions of energy services. That is, demands are defined in terms of
\par
vehicle-miles traveled, households (of various types and in different climatic
\par
regions) to be heated, and tons of steel and aluminum to be produced, as examples.
\par
The levels of services, defined in a base year and projected to future years, thus
\par
serve as demand drivers for the energy system and are translated into energy units
\par
(Btus per year) needed to provide the required service levels. Market oriented
\par
energy services are generally estimated in relation to projections of population,
\par
economic activity, and income.
\par
The high level of technical,detail is necessary to permit the assessment of inter-
\par
fuel substitution strategies, analysis of conservation options, and analysis of the
\par
impacts of introduction of advanced technologies. Additionally, this comprehensive
\par
framework provides the ability to analyze both centralized and decentralized tech-
\par
nologies, since the detail associated with energy transmission and distribution
\par
systems is also included. Thus, the RES framework permits the comparative assess-
\par
ment of:
\par
- Single technologies, delineating the associated impact upon
\par
the cost of energy, environmental emissions, and the use of
\par
oil and other resources;
\par
- Groups of technologies (a strategy), producing net impacts for
\par
the group;
\par
- Policy options, including taxes, standards, incentives, etc.
