Benefit-Cost Analysis
\par
A series of benefit-cost analyses of individual R\&D options were performed at
\par
Brookhaven National Laboratory using the Reference Energy System. Base case pro-
\par
jections were developed for 1985 and 2000. Individual technologies were then inser-
\par
ted into the system, competing against other technologies for two sets of oil
\par
prices. The system benefit (if system cost decreased) or deficit was calculated.
\par
By interpolation, each intermediate-year benefit was calculated and annual
\par
were discounted to obtain present value. Similarly, estimated R\&D expenditures
\par
were also discounted. The results of the analysis are presented in Table 1, which
\par
are based on an oil price increasing to \$23/bbl in the year 2000 (\$'s refer to 1975
\par
dollars) .
\par
Modeling Approaches
\par
A variety of energy models using different analytical approaches may be employed to
\par
analyze energy supply, demand, and the market potential for new technologies in the
\par
context of the total energy system. The major classes of analytical models that
\par
have been employed for energy R\&D planning and technology assessment in the united
\par
States include:
\par
Mathematical Programming:
\par
ETA Macro (Stanford, Alan Manne) 5
\par
Brookhaven Energy System Optimization Model and TESOM
\par
MARKAL-International Energy Agency6
\par
PILOT (Stanford)7
\par
Dynamic Simulation:
\par
Fossil 2 (Dartmouth, Roger Naill) 8
\par
LEAP (Successor to Gulf-SRI and Decision Focus Models) 9
\par
The mathematical programming models use a process or activity analysis approach,
\par
where a wide variety of technologies are incorporated and appear in the solution in
\par
response to the optimization.of a specific objective function. Some of the models
\par
listed include a linkage to a macroeconomic model.
\par
The optimization routine in ETA-Macro deals with the supply technologies that de-
\par
liver either electric or nonelectric energy. The demand for electricity and non-
\par
electric fuels are estimated in relation to Gross Domestic Product (GDP) by a set
\par
of econometric relationships. The Brookhaven Models, and MARKAL, include an activ-
\par
ity description of end-use technologies which permit the assessment of specific R\&D
\par
options in the end-use sectors in balance with supply technologies.
\par
Linkages to the economy are included in the Brookhaven models through a coupling to
\par
the Hudson-Jorgenson econometric mOdel of the economy which includes the variable
\par
coefficient interindustry matrix. The PILOT model incorporates a fixed coefficient
\par
input-output model with an econometric demand model for goods and services to couple
\par
the energy system to the overall economy. The analysis of energy options in the
\par
context of the overall economy is extremely important to determine the effects of
\par
alternative technical strategies on such economic variables as growth of GDP,
\par
ployment, and structural inflation attributable to changes in the cost of energy.
\par
A variety of objectives may be used in the mathematical programming models. For
\par
example, the Brookhaven model can accept the following objectives to be minimized:
\par
- Capital cost;
\par
- Total system cost;
