\par
Conservation Technology Assessment
\par
The planning and analysis of a Federal research and development program on end-use
\par
technologies requires an information base at the individual process or technology
\par
level. Estimates must be made of the prospects for significant improvement in the
\par
efficiency of specific end-use devices, and of the likely pace of industrial re-
\par
search and development toward these objectives. The effects of a Federal program
\par
may then be estimated and the benefits compared with the R\&D cost, similar to the
\par
methods used to evaluate alternative supply options. It should be noted that the
\par
conservation technologies rank rather high in the cost-benefit results illustrated
\par
in Table 1.
\par
The problem of distinguishing between the role of improved technology and that of
\par
alternate policy options in achieving energy conservation arises quite frequently
\par
in energy R\&D planning. It frequently is argued that certain policy options involv-
\par
ing taxes, incentives, and/or standards can achieve specific goals without the de-
\par
velopment of new, more efficient technology, or can force new developments. The
\par
purpose of analysis here is to establish a conceptual framework for the assessment
\par
of both policy options and R\&D to determine, in specific cases, whether they are
\par
indeed alternative options or actually are mutually supporting options. The actual
\par
situation will vary, depending on the specific energy-utilizing activity (residen-
\par
tial space heat, auto transport, aluminum manufacture, etc.), and thus the metho-
\par
dology must be applied using information on specific utilizing devices (e.g., fur-
\par
nace) or systems (e.g., building including insulation, furnace, etc.)
\par
CONCLUSIONS
\par
The major planning and analysis requirement is the disciplined comparison of the
\par
many technical options for the supply, conversion, and utilization of energy. De-
\par
cisions taken by private firms and/or government to accelerate the research and
\par
development pace on specific options requires a judgment of the market potential,
\par
or a determination that a particular technology supports an important national or
\par
regional objective. In the latter case, the decision is a political one, and once
\par
made, must be supported by regulatory and tax policies to ensure that the tech-
\par
nology will be competitive in markets. Standards may also be employed to force a
\par
technological change into the marketplace. In either case, the technology assess-
\par
ment must be conducted in the context of the overall energy system. All of the
\par
infrastructure requirements to supply, deliver, and utilize energy from the new
\par
technology must be identified and analyzed, and the benefits and costs of the al-
\par
ternatives considered.
