Formal analysis and modeling can support the R\&D planning process in a number of
\par
ways. Given the many uncertainties and subjective parameters that operate in the
\par
energy analysis, care must be taken to ensure that the results of the analysis are
\par
properly used. In any given policy analysis of technology assessment, quantitative
\par
results may provide only a small portion of the information required to support de-
\par
cision making. In other instances, quantitative analysis may playa larger role in
\par
the decision. Regardless of the extent of its impact, it is still important to do
\par
the analysis correctly to the highest standards of documentation and validation of
\par
the methodologies employed. The major benefit of quantitative analysis is the dis-
\par
cipline and structure that it provides to the analysis and discussion of complex
\par
relationships between energy, economic, and environmental factors. In general, the
\par
structure and relationships among elements of a policy or decision will be of
\par
greater importance than the actual numerical results. In this spirit, formal analy-
\par
sis and modeling can support the R\&D planning process in the following ways:
\par
1. Rank technology options with respect to economic,
\par
technical, and environmental criteria;
\par
2. Determine R\&D targets for technical, economic, and
\par
environmental characteristics of a new technolgy, so
\par
that it will be competitive in commercial markets;
\par
3. Identify those technologies that can support national
\par
plans and policy in the most effective way;
\par
4. Estimate the regulatory and financial incentives
\par
required to commercialize a desirable technology;
\par
5. Develop long term projections of technological
\par
change and substitution.
\par
It is clear that formal R\&D assessment methods are not applicable to the early
\par
stages of research in a new technology. At that stage the new technology is not
\par
sufficiently characterized to make the comparison with alternative approaches.
\par
Further, the costs of research at this early stage are low, and it is desirable to
\par
encourage the innovative process. At the advanced research and development stage,
\par
however, the program costs increase significantly and a formal screening and deci-
\par
sion process is needed. At this point in the R\&D cycle, it is extremely important
\par
to demonstrate that the program will lead to an improvement, in comparison with
\par
conventional and currently available systems.
\par
Future development of analytical methods for R\&D assessment will probably continue
\par
to use a combination of methods, including mathematical programming, dynamic simu-
\par
lations, and econometrics. No single methodology can address the variety of issues
\par
that must be analyzed. Specific problems in the current state of the art that must
\par
be addressed through further research include:
\par
1. Probabilistic and stochastic approaches that can handle
\par
the uncertainties in technology characterizations and
\par
market descriptions;
\par
2. Methods of evaluating risks in the R\&D portfolio;
\par
3. Standardized methods of cost estimating for new technologies;
\par
4. Consistent characterization of the technical efficiences,
\par
material inputs, and labor requirements for the construc-
\par
tion, operation, and maintenance of both supply and end-use
\par
technologies.
\par
It should be noted that, in the Bechtel Energy Facility Data character-
\par
izes the cost and inputs to a number of energy supply technologies, an excellent
\par
start has been made on the last item above. This work should be extended to cover
\par
- 264 -
\par
end-use devices, as well as to include the range of uncertainty that exists for the
\par
descriptive parameters.
