\par
SUPPLY-DEMAND MODEL
\par
Z. Falecld and J. Ordwa
\par
Energy Economy Research and Development Centre, Katowice, Poland
\par
The fundamental factors influencing the overall energy econ-
\par
omy of Poland are considered in a complex concept. Principles are
\par
formulated for the construction of the OBPE models system permit-
\par
ting effective analysis of the national energy demand/supply pro-
\par
gramme, with the aim of coordinating existing energy demand and
\par
supply potential. The system proposed enables the structUEe of the
\par
national energy balance to be rapidly corrected to allow for vari-
\par
ous strategies adopted in plans for the development of the natio-
\par
nal economy and also enables feasibility evaluation for choice of
\par
most advantageous technologies and energy carriers. A straight-
\par
forward calculation echnique is employed, suitable for application
\par
with virtually every type of computer currently in use.
\par
This model is a valuable instrument making it pocsible to
\par
assess the effect on the fuel-energy economy of feasible options
\par
in substitution of energy carriers and of energy-intensive tech-
\par
nologies.
\par
1. INTRODUCTION
\par
To find satisfactory solutions to problems of national energy
\par
strategy it is essential to take a complex view, envisaging various
\par
time horizons, of the total factors governing requirements for fuel
\par
and energy and also the feaSible methods of satisfyIng this re-
\par
quirement.
\par
Hence the basis for decision making in matters of energy econ-
\par
omy must be an analysis of energy consumption considered in con-
\par
- 265 -
\par
- 266 -
\par
junction with methods of obtaining energy from domestic production
\par
or from import. It is a sign of the times that in virtually all in-
\par
dustrialised countries energy policies have become of first prior-
\par
ity, mobilising administrative efforts at all levels and also ma-
\par
jor scientific research efforts. Inextricably bound up with this
\par
undertaking is another aspect of the problem, that is confrontation
\par
of the effects of energy policy implementation with the social re-
\par
qUirements for environment protection.
\par
A mutli-variant evaluation of this kind should provide the
\par
necessary data for establishing development tactics for the coun-
\par
try's energy economy, and hence for both management and design de-
\par
cisions. It is understood that this process of establishing devel-
\par
opment lines for the economy in general and for the energy industry
\par
in particular is one with no finite end, since both time and the
\par
advance of knowledge have their inter-related influence on the
\par
state and development of energy programmes.
\par
This process, by its very nature, is a reflection of the mut-
\par
ual relations between relevant component elements and occurrences,
\par
together governing the ultimate development of the system: energy -
\par
environment. It is of signal importance to consider the problem
\par
matter in its entirety, not only from the aspect of balancing mater-
\par
ial substance and energy produced, but allowing also for organisa-
\par
tional and legislative conditions which are frequently the deciding
\par
factors in matters of priorities and basic values.
\par
It is essential to include the relations between the econ-
\par
omic, technological, ecological and organisational sectors with
\par
particular allowance for the elements of uncertainty and changes
\par
occurring with time, while determining definitively the information
\par
flow both input and output /results/.
\par
The purpose of the system described is to provide a method
\par
for analysis of the energy economy enabling a comparison of prac-
\par
tically feasible strategies, which can then serve as a basie for
\par
decision making.
\par
Since the total energy-environment system comprehends a very
\par
large number of human activities and also organisational structures
\par
it needs to be presented in a manner practically applicable for the
\par
user, that is for those responsible for making executive decisions.
\par
The contemporary approach requires that the energy issue
\par
sho.ld be treated in a complex manner, that is considering the
\par
- 267 -
\par
total of problems associated with the obtaining, processing, trans-
\par
port/transmission and consumption of fuels and energy in all forms,
\par
In the search for optimum solutions one of the known approa-
\par
ches is to analyse the possibilities of mutual substitution of the
\par
particular energy carriers and also to choose the most advantageous
\par
of the feasible applicable technologies for supplying the varied
\par
production and services demands. The asseosment enabling the cor-
\par
rect solution to be selected must be developed using suitable opti-
\par
mation parameters, which will be discussed in a further part.
\par
The constituent elements forming together the solution to the
\par
problem as posed above are described here together with principles
\par
for the construction of a models system given the name OBPE.
\par
1. SYSTEM PRINCIPLES
\par
The specific nature of the energy economy means that it appe-
\par
ars in the total national economy in two forms:
\par
a/ as a distributed set of elements forming constituents of part-
\par
icular sectors, departments, brancnes or production/services
\par
sequences /direct consumption of fuel and energy/,
\par
b/ as a compact economic system forming a part of the whole nati-
\par
onal economy /the energy winning and processing industries to-
\par
gether with a complex of distribution systems/.
\par
This formulation is chosen to stress the fact that the energy
\par
economy can be separated from the total national economy only to a
\par
certain extent. In this context there can be distinguished three
\par
fundamental and reciprocally inter-related spheres of operation de-
\par
termining the energy supply/demand programme for the whole country,
\par
i.e.
\par
- choice of energy carriers at the user's end /taking into acco-
\par
unt substitution possibilities and choice of basic technologies/
\par
- programming of generation and winning of fuels and energy and
\par
also energy exchange with foreign countries,
\par
- establishing a rational scheme for energy supply/demand balance
\par
and also costs of its implementation fusing, among others, the
\par
parameters as in the WELMM method/.
\par
Figure 1 shows the relations between the constituent elements
\par
forming the basis for programming of the energy supply/demand patt-
\par
ern for the whole country. It is clear that such a programme correc-
\par
- 268 -
\par
tly determined must embody a balance between energy requirements and
\par
the possibilities of satisfying these requirements, assuming the
\par
current national energy base and taking into cons ideration the sys-
\par
tem of energy supply and distribution. Investigations on the devel-
\par
opment of economy must be conducted using successive
\par
and step by step balance analysis as a function of time, allowing
\par
for multi-variant set of inptu data and multi-variant results para-
\par
meters.
\par
The multi-variant nature of the solution found will result
\par
from the varied probable development strategies for the national
\par
economy /inout datal and the varied probable structures of
\par
fuel-energy economy /results parameters/.
\par
Both the number of input paraneters and the values of indices
\par
obtained from these calculations on projected energy supply/demand
\par
programmes are time-related, and decrease as the time horizon be-
\par
comes more distant.
\par
Moreover the qualitative and quantitative structure of the
\par
fuel-energy balance is subject to changes and any assessment be-
\par
comes increasingly problematical as further time horizons are con-
\par
sidered. In the same way the costs relations vary as the time hori-
\par
zon recedes and become increasingly difficult to determine with
\par
any precision.
\par
3. DETER\textbraceright UNING ENERGY REQUIREMENTS
\par
The set of input data necessary for establishing energy reqUi-
\par
rements in the given time horizon are determined from the programme
\par
for national socio-economic 4evelopment, eliborated by the appro-
\par
priate central government offices. Taking into consideration the
\par
feasible variants of this programme an all-round assessment of the
\par
future situation may be achieved. On Figure 1 the area of these
\par
topics is indicated in the upper part of the diagram, distinguishing
\par
two basic groups of topics, i.e. natural conditions and human acti-
\par
vity.
\par
After establishing a set of sUitably selected indices charac-
\par
terising unit energy consumption it is possible to determine both
\par
quantitative and qualitative energy reqUirements. It is important
\par
at this point to programme the directions of energy utilisation so
\par
as to avoid excessive detail while maintaining a sufficiently re-
\par
presentative profile.
\par
- 269 -
\par
4. NATIOBAL ENZRGY BASE
\par
The national energy base is indicated in the lower part of the
\par
diagram on Figure 1 and comprehends the Polish energy production
\par
and processing industries plus the whole area of energy exchange
\par
with foreign partners together with all the associated issues. The
\par
actual magnitude of the national energy base, and in particular its
\par
development, is very largely decided by the possibilities of con-
\par
struction of new or redevelopment of existing energy sources and
\par
distribution systems, which again is governed by the size of the in-
\par
vestment funds made available and also foreign currency questions.
\par
The most requirement is that the energy base should
\par
be programmed in such a way as to ensure full availability, i.e.
\par
that the total energy needs of the national economy are met both
\par
quantitatively and qualitatively.
\par
5. ENERGY SUPPLY AND DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM
\par
This system is an element of signal importance for ensuring
\par
availability as defined above. On Figure 1 this element is indicated
\par
on the left side of the system must be suitably devel-
\par
oped and organised to be able to guarantee effective energy supply
\par
to the users. The most important distribution systems are:
\par
for electric energy - the electricity transmission network
\par
for gas fuels - long distance piped transport, local networks and
\par
individual containers
\par
for liquid fuels - rail, inland waterways, road and piped transport
\par
for solid fuels - rail, inland waterways, road-and hydraulic trans-
\par
port
\par
for heat energy - municipal and local thermal network systems
\par
6. OBPE METHOD FOR ANALYSIS OF NATIONAL FUEL-ENERGY BALANCE
\par
As stated previously, to find a rational solution to the prob-
\par
lems involved in the future predicted national fuel-energy balance
\par
demands a complex evaluation of the total factors determining energy
\par
requirements and feasible means of satisfying these reqUirements.
\par
Hence the basis for making decisions concerning energy economy must
\par
be an analysis of energy consumption plus methods of obtaining energy
\par
- 270 -
\par
from domestic production or alternatively from import.
\par
The principal aim of the studies conducted at the Energy Econ-
\par
omy Research and Development Centre /Katowice - Paland/ was to co-
\par
ordinate the eXisting or predicted /multi-variant/ schemes for
\par
social-economic development of the country with the national energy
\par
economy, basing on the available data obtained from practical ex-
\par
perience and from official predictions.
\par
xhe three-stage OBPE method was proposed for modelling the
\par
national energy economy, i.e.
\par
stage \S{} - determining the structure of the outgoings side of the
\par
energy balance, allowing for variations in direct energy
\par
requirements /model OBPE-1/
\par
stage 2 - determining the structure and also quantitative and qual-
\par
itative conditions on the income side of the energy bal-
\par
ance, i.e. winning the energy carriers /model OBPE-2/
\par
stage 3 - inte8rating these two models in order to balance the two
\par
sides of the energy account.
\par
lbe solution algorithm is presented in Figure 2. If there is
\par
no solution to the system, this signifies that for the assumed pot-
\par
ential production and import of energy the energy requirements
\par
cannot be covered. In this case it is necessary to correct the input
\par
data as indicated on Figure 2, i.e. correction of elements 1.01 or
\par
1.03 for model OBPE-1, 2.01 and 2.03 for model OB1'E-2, or elements
\par
1.01 and 2.01 for the whole.
\par
If the system can be solved, then for the given assumptions
\par
for socio-economic development of the country /1.01 and 2.01/ the
\par
following results are obtained:
\par
a - energy balance for the country
\par
b - magnitude of expenditure necessary to meet energy supply
\par
c - optimum distribution of energy carriers among the energy users.
\par
The proposed system of models to be solved by linear pro-
\par
gramming is a valuable instrument for rapid determination of the
\par
basic constituents of the national fuel-energy balance, allowing
\par
for varying models of socio-economic development of the c;untry.
\par
The system described was checked by means of appropriate
\par
calculations which showed its operational effectiveness, that is
\par
- 271 -
\par
its suitability fbr practical prediction techniques applicable with
\par
a time horizon of 15 years.
\par
The model matrices are of sparse type. The assumed aggregation
\par
depth for strategic analysis of the national energy balance appears
\par
to be sufficient /90 groups of energy consumers, 26 types of energy
\par
siurces, 31 types of energy carriers/ and relates to macroscale
\par
consideration of the problem, i.e. for the whole country.
\par
In this proposed system it is of vital importance to take
\par
natural /commercial/ measurement units for the various energy car-
\par
riers, which considerably facilitates all types of substitution
\par
analysis.
\par
Figure 3 shows the diagram of the proposed system of two
\par
i.e. OBPE-1 and OBPE-2. The accurate mathematical and fac-
\par
tual descriptions are given in /6/.
\par
Model OBPE-1 deals with the so-called direct energy require-
\par
ments, comprehending both production and services sectors by means
\par
of indices for energy consumption and energy requirements. For
\par
optimation parameters economic, work-intensity, capital-intensity
\par
and similar coefficients may be or alternatively complex in-
\par
dices, as for instance in the systerr..
\par
The model OBPE-1 forms a matrix with the elements: set of
\par
equations describing energy needs for selected directions of utili-
\par
sation, at the same time taking into account the possibility of
\par
energy carriers substitution, dovetailing with a set of equations
\par
allowing for possible limitations in the available carriers. In
\par
this system it is also possible to take into account substitution
\par
of technologies applied for the implementation of necessary prod-
\par
uction or services duties.
\par
Model OBJ'E-2 comprehends total energy preperation, i.e. con-
\par
siders the processes of winning the primary energy, energy trans-
\par
formation and import of energy carriers. Suitable coefficients
\par
similar to those in OB?E-1 are used for optimation parameters.
\par
This model forms a matrix with the elements: set of equations
\par
describing volume of production from the various energy carrier
\par
sources, dovetailing with a set of equations comprehending limita-
\par
tions in supply of these carriers. The probleu to be solved in this
\par
.odel is to cover the requirements resulting from the solution
\par
found fromOBPE-1 and also to cover energy carriers consumption to
\par
