this system feasible substitution in choice of energy carriers both
\par
in winning and in processing of fuels and energy is allowed for.
\par
The set of input data is compiled from outside, i.e. from
\par
data prepared by central planning, and then fed to the model by
\par
means of which the calculations are performed.
\par
The calculations procedure, as indicated in Figure 2, is per-
\par
formed for each alternative choice of socio-economic development,
\par
the results obtained forming the basic data for decision making
\par
on development lines for the national economy.
\par
Bearing in mind that the economic life of the country is
\par
developing both in time and .pace it become. essential to consider
\par
these problems for various time horizons, taking into account elem-
\par
ents involved in spatial development of the country. To get a
\par
clearer view of the reciprocal relations in the national economic
\par
structure the model needs to be broken down, initially in two
\par
directions, i.e.
\par
territorially - allowing for differentiated natural background and
\par
associated human material activities
\par
sector-branchwise - to give a closer review of energy carriers
\par
choice, considering substitution feasibilities in
\par
various types of production and services.
\par
7. CONCLUSIONS
\par
The proposed system described here is based on previous re-
\par
search plus currently applied methods of planning and prediction,
\par
using relevant accessible statistical data, and aims to coordinate
\par
energy requirements with energy production and import potential,
\par
for all energy sectors existing in the country.
\par
Simultaneously this system allows the structure of the energy
\par
balance to be rapidly corrected depending on various strategies in
\par
the planned development of the national economy.
\par
The essential features of the proposed system are:
\par
a/ complex approach to the problem on a nation-wide scale for an-
\par
alysis of energy economy development with a time horizon of up
\par
to 15 years
\par
b/ creation of an instrument facilitating raoid review of the nat-
\par
c/ d/ e/ f/ g/ - 273 -
\par
ional fuel-energy balance for each variant of planned economic
\par
development, simultaneously choosing the optimum solution for
\par
distribution of available fuel and energy, at the level of
\par
supply
\par
adjusting the system of models to the accessible input data
\par
taking into consideration mutual substitution of the various
\par
energy carriers
\par
enabling choice of applied technology most advantageous from
\par
the aspect of energy economy, for the implementation of prod-
\par
uction or services duties
\par
uSing a straightforward and universally applied calculation
\par
technique based on known of modelling and programming
\par
/simplex/, making it possible to employ virtually every type
\par
of currently used computer
\par
allowing for a number of reciprocal relations resulting from
\par
the basic propositions of national development, and affecting
\par
the structure of the energy balance.
\par
The proposed system was checked by test calculations, both for
\par
a simplified example and for a variant emboJying the full range of
\par
relevant data. Test results showed that the OBPE system of models
\par
is operationally sound and suitable for practical utilisation.
\par
Correct choice of the models system, both central and in
\par
breakdown, makes it possible to achieve a reasonably comprehensive
\par
assessment of variants arising due to the various practically acc-
\par
eptable structures both of energy consumption and of energy winning,
\par
and also due to siting of individual industrial projects.
