ENERGY SYSTEMS AND ENERGY
\par
DEPENDENCE: A COMPARATIVE STUDY IN
\par
TECHNOLOGY ASSESSMENT FOR VARIOUS
\par
lEA COUNTRIES
\par
G. Egberts, K. Leimkiihler and St. Rath-Nagel
\par
Programmgruppe Systemforschung und Technologische Entwicklung,
\par
Kernforschungsanlage Jillich, Federal Republic of Germany
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
The dependence on imported oil in many countries clearly shows that strong efforts
\par
become necessary to provide economies with new energy technologies that draw on
\par
different resources. Examples of these are Shale Oil and Tar Sands, Enhanced
\par
Recovered Domestic Oil and Gas, Coal Derived Liquids and Gases, Electricity genera-
\par
ted from Breeders or Advanced Converters, Application of Solar, Wind, Ocean and
\par
Geothermal Energy, Fuels from Biomass, Heat Pumps, etc.
\par
Research, Development and Demonstration (R,D\&D) for these technologies has typi-
\par
cally long lead times and requires large investments that may be difficult to
\par
allocate for a single country. The International Energy Agency (lEA) in Paris aims
\par
at stimulating, pooling and coordinating these activities in multi-national cooper-
\par
ative projects. To support these, the agency elaborates an energy R,D\&D strategy,
\par
that uses systems analysis results obtained from a large scale energy model. The
\par
model, which is applied by 15 member countries, some important input parameters,
\par
the generated scenarios and the approach for technology assessment are presented
\par
in the article.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy Systems Analysis, Energy Research Development \& Demonstration, Technology
\par
Assessment, New Energy Technologies, Linear Programming Model, International Study.
\par
INTRODUCTI ON
\par
New energy technologies play an increasingly important role in many countries'
\par
efforts to reduce the dependence on imported oil. But technology development typi-
\par
cally has very long lead times and often has large investment requirements that may
\par
compete with other national objectives or cannot be carried by a single country.
\par
Technology development may, however, become more effective and rapid through inter-
\par
national cooperation and multi-national projects. This principle led the 20 member
\par
countries of the International Energy Agency (lEA)! in Paris to call for establish-
\par
I Member countries of the lEA are: Australia, Austria, Belgium, Canada, Denmark,
\par
Germany, Greece, Ireland, Italy, Japan, Luxembourg, Netherlands, New Zealand, Nor-
\par
way, Spain, Sweden, Switzerland, Turkey, United Kingdom, United States.
\par
- 279 -
\par
