\par
ing an international strategy for energy development and demonstration
\par
(R,D\&D).
\par
The objective of the lEA R,D\&D strategy project is to provide guidance as to which
\par
new energy technologies should be made available at economically and environment-
\par
ally acceptable cost, when and to what extent.
\par
The elaboration of the strategy follows a systems analysis effort that has been
\par
underway since 1976 with the objective of developing the methodology, collecting
\par
the necessary data and producing the projections of future energy technology im-
\par
pacts. The systems analysis work is carried out by a staff seconded from 15 part-
\par
icipating member countries, working at two national research laboratories as host
\par
institutions, one at Brookhaven, USA and the other at JU1ich, Germany.
\par
The objective of the systems analysis part of the project is to obtain quantita-
\par
tive estimates of the market penetration of individual new energy technologies
\par
over the 1980 to 2020 time period under a variety of strategic assumptions, so
\par
that through analysis their effects on total energy system costs, oil import
\par
dence and environment could be assessed. In the conduct of this work an analyti-
\par
cal model has been developed, which uses linear programming techniques to generate
\par
national scenarios.
\par
CHARACTERIZATION OF TECHNOLOGIES
\par
One of the most important factors in the application of the model for energy R,D\&D
\par
planning is the data base on technologies. Two categories of technologies can be
\par
distinguished:
\par
a) Demand Device Technologies
\par
These are technologles that provide a useful service, such as oil
\par
burners, heat pumps or electric motors. The input data consists of
\par
efficiency, a fractional allocation of each energy type required by
\par
the device, investment cost, operating and maintenance cost and fuel
\par
delivery cost. In the case of improved insulation measures, this
\par
"service" has to be specified with the energy savings and installation
\par
costs.
\par
b) Technologies for Converting Energy Carriers from one Form to
\par
These technologles lnclude systems such as reflnerles, coal gaslflca-
\par
tion plants, power plants. Input data comprises the type of energy
\par
input and output, efficiencies, investment cost, operating and main-
\par
tenance cost and fuel delivery cost.
\par
The 34 generic new technologies that were characterized for the model are dis-
\par
played in Fig. 1. All countries' runs are required to use all 34 technologies
\par
unless excluded by a policy constraint or by a separate analysis indicating that
\par
the technology excluded would never be a viable
\par
MODEL CHARACTERISTICS
\par
The model used (called MARKAL, an acronym for MARKet ALlocation) is a
\par
linear programming (LP) model of a generalized energy-System. Thus instead of
\par
just optimizing an energy system at a certain time and in a certain state, MARKAL
\par
optimizes the system's development within a time period of up to 40 years. The
\par
total time span to be considered is divided into 5-year intervals. For intervals
\par
centered at 1980, 1985, ... up to 2020, the total energy system (including energy
\par
supply and energy use) is optimized; a set of additional constraints is responsi-
