few important categories of assumptions, which distinguish particular scenarios.
\par
The input variations compared with the 'reference scenario' are:
\par
- accelerated implementation and faster growth rates for new technologies
\par
(accelerated scenarios)
\par
- varying assumptions on the development of the price of imported oil and oil
\par
products (higher and lower oil price scenarios)
\par
- restrictions on the total installed nuclear capacity (limited nuclear scenarios)
\par
- restrictions on the total fossil fuel use (limited fossil scenarios)
\par
- restrictions on the total net oil import ('supply security' scenarios), and
\par
- implementation levels of renewable technologies specified exogenously to their
\par
upper limits (renewable scenario).
\par
Usually with an LP-prob1em every non-constrained row may serve as an objective
\par
function; the ones used most commonly with MARKAL were
\par
P = PRICE INDICATOR
\par
total discounted cost of the energy system for the time considered
\par
S SECURITY OF SUPPLY INDICATOR
\par
total net oil import for the time considered
\par
The reference scenario has been obtained by minimizing the objective function P
\par
with no constraints on S. Constraining the total net oil import to lower values
\par
which must not be exceeded subsequently leads to higher system costs (while more
\par
expensive technologies will enter the solution) until a lower limit of oil import
\par
will be reached where solutions will go'infeasible due to a shortfall in the system!;
\par
oil supply. The corresponding values of system cost and oil import in between
\par
those extreme scenarios constitute a 'trade-off curve'. Trade-off curves have
\par
been computed for individual countries which show individual degrees of flexibi-
\par
lity between both variables. Figure 4 shows trade-off curves for two acceleration
\par
levels of new technologies (levelland level 4) for all 15 lEA member countries
