For this reason the approaches described below should not be used without simul-
\par
taneously making judgements about those factors that have not been quantified.
\par
Three approaches were studied for individual countries using MARKAL to measure the
\par
relative benefits of new technologies. In the first one an index had been defined
\par
measuring the economic attractiveness of new technologies. It assesses the impact
\par
of each technology on the total energy system cost at marginal prices. In another
\par
case a competitiveness indicator for technologies had been used which shows the
\par
marginal profitability of technologies in terms of the objective function applied.
\par
In the third case the contribution of new technologies to a particular policy
\par
objective was studied. The latter approach has been adopted for the particular
\par
case of Germany, which is summarized in the following as one example:
\par
a) The sixteen scenarios computed for Germany are all cost minimum cases. All
\par
demands are satisfied and the technology mix and resource consumption depend on
\par
the particular constraints implied in the scenario. In order to differentiate
\par
among new technologies in these scenarios from an energy R,D\&D policy viewpoint,
\par
additional policy criteria have been applied.
\par
c) The application of these criteria leads to the selection of a particular
\par
'supply security scenario' as a preferred scenario among the set of sixteen scen-
\par
arios computed for Germany.
\par
d) If the oil consumption target is applied to technologies that are implemented
\par
in this scenario the grouping as displayed in Fig. 7 can be inferred.
\par
Vital Technologies are those that would cause a violation of the oil policy target
\par
in case they were unavailable and had to be replaced by other technologies.
\par
Essential, but Non-Vital Technologies are those that would allow the achievement
\par
of the 011 P011CY target but at a slgnificant cost increase, in case they were
\par
unavailable and had to be replaced by other technologies.
