INTRODUCTION
\par
In a few years, Turkey will reach a point at which certain key decisions must be
\par
taken concerning the future of the energy sector in general, and the electricity
\par
system, in particular. The basic problem is that there is no obvious strategy:
\par
soon, the hydro potential will have been almost fully developed; hard coal produc-
\par
tion levels can hardly be maintained; brown coal (lignite) resources have to be
\par
shared among several consuming sectors; petroleum reserves are small and the actual
\par
potential is unknown. In about ten years, an additional resource is probably going
\par
to be required so as to avoid a shortage of electricity, as well as severe short-
\par
ages of other fuels.
\par
One of the most urgent decisions involves the electricity generation mix. If no
\par
new conventional sources are discovered, extensive buildup of nuclear power plants
\par
seems unavoidable. In order to analyze future possibilities, the "energy mini-mode1"
\par
described below is currently being used to assess the role that nuclear energy can
\par
play in Turkey in the future. The study is commissioned by the Turkish Electricity
\par
Authority.
