MODELING THE NATIONAL ENERGY SYSTEM
\par
It is possible to model the national energy sytem in many different ways.The impor-
\par
tant factor that will influence the final choice;i.e,the level of aggregration, the
\par
method of approach, type of mathematical representation of the relations, etc. are
\par
largely dependent on the characteristics of the system as well as on the type of
\par
policy decisions that are to be analyzed. The approach taken here is to keep the
\par
model as small as possible while retaining all the elements that are needed for a
\par
meaningful analysis of the system as a whole. Furthermore, since the model will be
\par
applied to Turkey, it should reflect all likely developments in the energy sector
\par
of the country.
\par
The energy flow diagram of the model is shown in Fig. 1. The primary energy sources
\par
include only oil, coal, nuclear and hydro potential. Other sources such as natural
\par
gas, geothermal, and solar energy are used in negligible quantities. Noncommercial
\par
sources like fire wood and cow dung are utilized locally and are not really influen-
\par
ced by policy decisions.
\par
Major conversions take place in the refining of petroleum and in the generation of
\par
electricity. Transportation of petroleum or coal and the transmission of electri-
\par
city have associated energy losses. The final form of energy reaches the consuming
\par
sectors as solid fuels, liquid fuels, and electricity.
\par
Investment activities are in the areas of coal and oil m1n1ng; refining facilities;
\par
power plants utilizing coal, petroleum (fuel oil and diesel oil), nuc1ear,and hydro
\par
sources; and transmission of electricity.
\par
The time horizon is lS-years, with another six years for end-effects. There are
\par
- 291 -
\par
five planning periods of three years duration.
\par
Fuel demands are treated exogenously, as are quantities that are decided on outside
\par
of the energy system. The import prices of fuels and energy technology equipment;
\par
unit local costs of energy investments; operating and maintenance costs; conversion
\par
efficiencies: time lags between payments and investments, and between investments
\par
and actual production; and calorific values of energy sources and carriers consti-
\par
tute the "technology coefficients" of the dynamic linear progralIDning model. The
\par
objective of the model is to minimize the weighted sum of: fuel import costs; energy
\par
technology import costs; and costs of unsatisfied demand. The details of the model
\par
are given in the following,
