MODEL VALIDATION
\par
In order to validate the model, it was applied to the Turkish energy sector for the
\par
years 1960-1975. Since the details of validation trials have already been described
\par
elsewhere (1,2,3), only the crucial points will be mentioned here. In the valida-
\par
tion runs, the following assumptions were made:
\par
i. The (physical) technology coefficients were taken from average
\par
aggregate values;
\par
ii. Fuel demands and primary resources were assigned values equal to
\par
their observed or officially recorded values;
\par
iii. Values of 2.5, 1.5, 1.0, 1.0, and 10.0, respectively, were assigned
\par
to WI through W 5 {$\bullet$}
\par
During 1960-1975, the energy sector did not develop very smoothly; cer-
\par
tain abrupt changes took place which had to be reflected to the model. The first
\par
such change was a halt in hydroelectricity increase, while the second one was a
\par
rise in oil prices. The halt in hydroelectricity growth was the result of a 4-5
\par
year delay in a large hydro project.
\par
In order to take account of these two events, the model was run interactively, much
\par
like a simulation model. For the first run which covered the IS-year horizon, oil
\par
prices were assumed constant at the 1960 price.
\par
The results of this run were assumed to be valid up to 1968, when the delay in the
\par
hydro project began. For the second run, which covered the years 1968-1975, a 6-
\par
year delay was introduced, and the results up to 1973 were assumed to be valid, at
\par
which time the oil prices went up. In the third run where the oil prices were
\par
assigned their higher costs, the model was run for the 1974-1979 period. The re-
\par
sults of these runs are summarized in Figures 2 and 3 where primary energy mix
\par
and electricity generation mix are given for the 1960-1975 period. The actual data
\par
for the same variables are shown in Figures 4 and 5.
