It is difficult, and probably futile, to judge models on the basis of their mathema-
\par
tical formulation. The structure and size of a model depend on the expected use
\par
of the model. Furthermore, it is difficult to categorically ascribe potential uses
\par
for different types of models. For example, a simulation model can be structured
\par
in order to yield near-optimal solutions, while an optimization model can be so for-
\par
mulated as to represent system behaviour much like a simulation model.
\par
Finally, a point must be made concerning the ultimate objectives of formal models.
\par
Since the final decisions are actually based on mental models, probably the best
\par
use of a formal model is to help improve the capahilities of that mental model.
