\par
Energy planning; energy management; energy policy; Austria; energy models; energy
\par
demand; comparative energy studies.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
The main purpose of this paper is to show how a system of models can be used in a
\par
systematic way to describe alternative energy futures and the environmental effects
\par
related to energy use. The assumptions and major results of a case study for
\par
Austria l are presented here to illustrate the possible role of the general ap-
\par
proach as an instrument to solve problems of resource management. The contribu-
\par
tion does not aim at a detailed discussion of methological aspects of the work;
\par
IFor a detailed documentation of the IIASA Energy/Environment Case Study for Austria,
\par
see (Foell, 1979).
\par
- 299 -
\par
- 300 -
\par
they are described in several of the references.
\par
This work is the fourth of a series of IIASA regional case studies of energy/
\par
environment management; the first three were conducted in 1975/76 on three greatly
\par
differin2 regions, namely, the German Democratic Republic, the Rhone-Alpes region
\par
in France, and the State of Wisconsin in the U.S.A. They are described in detail
\par
in (Foell, 1979).
\par
The Austrian study had two primary objectives: (1) To examine alternative energy
\par
futures and strategies for Austria and to consider some of their environmental
\par
implications; and (2) to investigate concepts and methodologies for energy/
\par
environment management and policy design in Austria.
\par
The establishment of these objectives was based upon the conviction that in Austria,
\par
as in most regions and nations of the world, there is an urgent need for the
\par
development and application of methods for studying energy systems and for testing
\par
the impact of alternative policies. In view of the major role which energy plays
\par
in the determination of environmental quality, this study was designed to aid in
\par
the integration of energy and environmental management from a systems perspective.
\par
The issues and strategies studies were chosen through an iterative procedure,
\par
beginning with suggestions at a three-day workshop attended by 28 members of
\par
Austrian energy and environmental communities (private and public sectors),
\par
followed with exploration by the IIASA team to see whether they could be analyzed
\par
within the time and resource limitations of the study. One major decision was that
\par
this study would address broad mid- to long-term strategies and pOlicies. Con-
\par
sequently, the time horizon of the study was 2015. The major issues fell into the
\par
following categories: Energy Demand; Energy Conservation; Energy Supply Options
\par
and Strategies; and Environmental Impacts and Protection Strategies.
\par
BASIC CONCEPTS
\par
Scenario Writing
\par
The Austrian case study used scenario building and energy system modelling as a
\par
formal quantitative approach to policy analysis and the examination of energy/
\par
environment strategies. To present a more comprehensive picture of the use of the
\par
energy/environment models described in the following paragraphs, it is appropriate
\par
to summerize here the scenario framework.
\par
In order to specify a policy set or a framework within which a scenario could be
\par
built, it was necessary to develop a means for expressing a policy in terms of a
\par
limited number of characteristics. Each characteristic is associated with one of
\par
four main categories of Scenario Properties, namely:
\par
1) Socio-Economic Structure; 2) Lifestyle;
\par
3) Technology; 4) Environment
\par
Within the framework of these four general categories, a large number of assump-
\par
tions about future events and/or policies and strategies can be combined. This
\par
framework then provides the exogenous functions, boundary conditions, and con-
\par
straints for the models and data bases with which the details of the energy/
\par
environment scenario are calcUlated.
