Characteristics such as those in Table 1 provided the major inputs to the models.
\par
Some of the models have direct links to several of the characteristics in the table.
\par
For example, inputs to the personal transportation model are directly specified
\par
by several of the characteristics in Table 1, including 1) human settlement pat-
\par
terns, 2) transportation lifestyle (car ownership), 3) technical efficiency of
\par
cars, and 4) car emission standards.
\par
A System of Energy/Environment Models
\par
The overall system of models is a set of submodels which combine data and infor-
\par
mation about energy flows to describe or simulate the energy system and its rela-
\par
tionship to other characteristics, e.g., demography, the economy and the environ-
\par
ment. An overall simulation framework integrates the variety of analytical
\par
techniques employed.
\par
The system of models has five major components:
\par
I Socioeconomic Activity Models
\par
II End-use Energy Demand Models
\par
III Energy Conversion and Supply Models
\par
IV Environmental Impact Models
\par
V Preference and Decision Models
\par
The general flow of information between these models, shown in a highly simplified
\par
manner in Figure 1, is summarized as follows.
\par
I) Regional socio-economic information (e.g., population settlement patterns,
\par
economic activity) is provided exogenously and/or by models.
\par
II) The socio-economic information serves as input to energy demand models which
\par
are structured according to economic sector (e.g., industrial, service, agriculture,)
\par
or by technological process (e.g., heating, cooling, lighting).
\par
III) The outputs of the energy demand models form the input to energy supply
\par
models which in turn are used to calculate primary energy requirements, needed
\par
conversion and transport facilities, supply system costs, etc. Supply requirements
\par
are directly matched to demand or related to demand within a framework of con-
\par
straints; in addition, a resource allocation model based on minimization of a cost
\par
function was used in the Austrian study.
\par
IV) The energy flows in the supply system and the end-use energy serve as inputs
\par
to the environmental impact models; these models calculate a broad spectrum of
\par
impacts, including human health and safety, on both a systemwide and subregional
\par
localized basis.
\par
V) Indicators from the demand, supply and environmental models are linked to for-
\par
mal quantitative "Preference models" which allow the use of a mul tiattribute
\par
objective function for the evaluation of alternative strategies (Buehring, 1978).
\par
There are additional flows of information between the major components as indicated
\par
by the dashed lines in Figure 1. In general, although not in all cases, the
