dashed flows (feedbacks) are implemented by intervention of the model user and not
\par
by formal mathematical links. Some of the models in the system are highly for-
\par
malized; others are of rather simple structure, depending on the relative impor-
\par
tance of the aspect covered and on the availability of data. The following
\par
paragraphs illustrate briefly the diversity of approaches within the system.
\par
For a fuller description see (Foell, forthcoming, and Foell, 1979).
\par
Socio-economic development. Socio-economic development was simulated by the help
\par
of a demand-oriented input-output model (Richter, 1973) which was originally de-
\par
signed for medium-term simulations. Based on specific assumptions about the over-
\par
all economic development of Austria's foreign trade partners and on technological
\par
changes in Austria, this model provides for each year full input-output tables
\par
at constant prices and thus consistent forecasts for all the 31 sectors distin-
\par
guished. This great sectoral detail has three advantages for deriving energy
\par
demand projections: (1) one can capture the change in the level and structure of
\par
energy demand that is attributable to different rates of expansion of the inter-
\par
mediate sectors (it has been shown (Bayer, 1975) that the elasticity of energy
\par
consumption in Austrian industry observed between 1960 and 1974 would have been
\par
considerably smaller if all branches had grown at the same rate as the average for
\par
the whole industry); (2) the introduction of assumptions about technological
\par
changes and changes in the evolution of final demand is transparent; and (3) it
\par
facilitates the analysis of environmental effects associated with the production
\par
and consumption of energy.
\par
- 304 -
\par
Energy demand. The energy demand modeling and assessment was carried out at the
\par
point of final or end-use demand. A four-sector classification of energy demand
\par
was used for Austria: Residential, Commercial and Service, Industrial, and Trans-
\par
portation. Each user class is the basis for a demand model which examined the
\par
energy consumption within the sector. Further sector disaggregation within each
\par
demand model is determined by policy issues of the region and by data availability.
\par
Analysis of energy consumption was done by fuel type and, when possible, by
\par
physical process.
\par
The models are simulation models with one year time steps. The models might best
\par
be characterized as technological process models, with socio-economic variables as
\par
the exogenous inputs. Socio-economic feedbacks from the demand models are in
\par
general not implemented formally within the models but rather by intervention of
\par
the model user. The general structure of these relationships is shown in Figure
\par
1. Brief overviews of the models are given below.
\par
The residential model (Poenitz, 1978 and Buehring, 1979) focuses on the household,
\par
which is analyzed in terms of base (space and water heating, and central air
\par
conditioning, if appropriate) and secondary appliances (e.g. refrigerator, stove,
\par
television). Important aspects of the model included determination of the number,
\par
type, and quality of housing units, their heating source as well as the number of
\par
appliances and the energy use of these appliances.
\par
In the commercial and service, industrial, and agricultural sectors, the
\par
models were based upon 1) the level of economic activity in the sectors or sub-
\par
sectors (as described by the input-output model); and 2) the energy intensity per
\par
unit of activity. In a first step the overall energy intensity was determined by
\par
sectors; in the second step the fuel mix in these sectors was projected.
\par
In the transportation sector (Hanson, 1979) the model divides the sector into per-
\par
sonal travel and freight components. The procedure for projecting personal travel
\par
energy use was to estimate personal travel in terms of person kilometers by mode,
\par
convert this to vehicle kilometers based on vehicle use characteristics, and
\par
finally to convert vehicle kilometers to energy use based on vehicle technological
\par
characteristics. For the freight component, ton kilometers by mode were directly
\par
related to industrial, commercial and service, and agricultural activity. In the
\par
Austrian study, transportation activity was included as a sector in the input-
\par
output model.
\par
Energy supply. In the Austrian case study two approaches were used to examine
\par
energy supply questions. First, a demand/supply balance technique was employed,
\par
in which energy supply was matched to energy demand; however, it was necessary to
\par
evaluate supply options for electricity and district heat generation. Although no
\par
formal computer-implemented model was used in this analysis, it reflects Austria's
\par
historical experience and future plans.
\par
To complement the demand/supply balance approach, a formal resource allocation
\par
model was applied to the 51, 52 and 53 1990 end-use demands. This analysis was
\par
performed using the Brookhaven Energy System Optimization Model, BESOM (Hoffman,
\par
1973), which is a resource allocation model developed at Brookhaven National
\par
Laboratory and designed to examine interfuel substitution within a framework of
\par
constraints on the availability of competing resources and technologies and their
\par
associated costs. For this study, a version of the Brookhaven model was modified
\par
to reflect the specific characteristics and structure of the Austrian energy
\par
system, including appropriate technologies, coefficients, and costs. Three al-
\par
ternative reference energy systems were developed for Scenarios 51, 52, and 53, for
\par
the year 1990. These reference energy systems were based on a minimization of
\par
total system costs in that year.
\par
- 305 -
\par
Environmental Impact. A large set of environmental impacts due to energy use in
\par
Austria was calculated for each scenario by the environmental simulation models.
\par
The models, developed at the University of Wisconsin and IIASA for regional
\par
studies, have been reparameterized and adapted to Austrian conditions.
\par
As shown in Figure 1, both end-use energy demand and primary energy requirements
\par
are inputs to the environmental models. Impacts at each point in the fuel chain
\par
are calculated, i.e. extraction, transportation, processing, conversion and direct
\par
use. This means that impacts occurring both inside and outside Austria are con-
\par
sidered.
\par
These fuel chin impacts were calculated for reference coal, nuclear, oil and gas
\par
systems, including impacts associated with electricity generation. A reference
\par
energy system represents the average characteristics of the fuel chain for the
\par
entire region; these so-called "system impacts" are not site-specific (Buehring,
\par
1975; Buehring, 1976; Foell, forthcoming). In contrast, localized impacts were
\par
calculated for air pollution due to end-use of fuels, taking spatial
\par
characteristics of the region into account. The air pollution impacts are pri-
\par
marily concerned with human health at the urban level (Dennis, 1976; Dennis, 1978)
\par
The impacts calculated by the environmental models are called "quantified impacts".
\par
They do not represent all of the impacts known to occur; the quantified environment-
\par
al impacts are the impacts which we judge to have an adequate scientific basis.
\par
Since not all quantified impacts were calculated, these models present only one
\par
perspective on the system of impacts. Impacts are for each simulation year;
\par
time-dependent calculated changes stemming from regulations or technological ad-
\par
vances are taken into account.
\par
MAJOR FINDINGS
\par
The findings are not the result of any single scenario but rather were deduced
\par
from the analysis of the entire set of scenarios and sensitivity studies. Al-
\par
though the findings, in general, are applicable to the long run (2015), more
\par
attention has been devoted to the initial two decades of the study. The major
\par
findings of the study are as follows:
\par
Energy Demand
\par
(1) Total energy demand will probably increase at a rate considerably lower than
\par
in the past two decades. If these lower demand estimates are valid, they imply
\par
major rethinking of Austrian energy supply policies that are based on higher over-
\par
all proj ections.
\par
(2) An overall "societal energy intensiveness," defined roughly as primary energy
\par
use per unit of GNP, will probably decrease over the coming few decades.
\par
(3) Electricity will supply an increasing fraction of total end-use energy.
\par
Nevertheless, growth of electricity generation will be much lower than historical
\par
rates. Needs for future facilities should be examined in light of their depend-
\par
ence on the structure and rate of Austria's economic development.
\par
Total electricity generation requirements for the four scenarios are illustrated
\par
in Figure 2, along with historical data for the 1945-1971 period. A comparison
\par
between the high growth scenario (S2) and the conservation scenario (S4) shows
\par
that S2 requires over twice as much electricity in 2015. This large increase
\par
would lead to extremely high levels of fuel imports. In all scenarios except S2,
\par
the growth in electricity is less than historical trends.
