\par
(4) The continued dominance of the industrial sector in energy use suggests that
\par
policy measures for altering energy-use patterns must focus largely on this sector.
\par
However, energy demand growth in the next few decades appears to be greatest in
\par
the service sector of the economy, with the lowest growth likely in the transpor-
\par
tation sector. .
\par
(5) There is considerable potential for energy conservation by means of improved
\par
insulation practices in the residential sector. Because of the institutional
\par
barriers related to initial costs, the realization of the economic benefits of
\par
this potential may require vigorous government support of conservation measures.
\par
Energy Supply
\par
(6) Nuclear power could playa major role in electricity generation over the next
\par
several decades. However, during the time period considered, the continued slowing
\par
of electricity demand growth, coupled with further vigorous conservation measures,
\par
could make future nuclear plants unnecessary if hydropower were exploited fUlly*.
\par
(7) Coal is generally considered to be an unattractive long-term supply option
\par
for Austria. However, our economic analysis of the Austrian energy supply system,
\par
based on a resource ahlocation model for the year 1990, indicated that a shift to-
\par
ward increased reliance on lignite, coupled with decreased reliance on gas and
\par
petroleum, would be cost effective. However, the associated environmental impact
\par
would be significant.
\par
(8) Extrapolation of most previous Austrian energy forecasts yields a continuation
\par
of the trend toward greater reliance on petroleum and natural gas. However, as-
\par
*In a national referendum on November 5, 1978, a majority voted against putting
\par
Austria's first nuclear plant (Zwentendorf) into operation. As a result, plans
\par
for developing nuclear power in Austria have been laid aside. In the study reported
\par
here, Scenario S4 describes a non-nuclear future.
\par
- 307 -
\par
sessment of world petroleum resources and future demands demonstrates a serious gap
\par
between potential petroleum demand and supply in Austria in the 1990's, even under
\par
the assumption of low growth (Conservation Scenario S4). It would be possible,
\par
from a technical standpoint, to close the gap in the conservation case by shifting
\par
to coal in the industrial sector. In the commercial and residential sectors, a
\par
continued reliance upon, or shift back to, coal and wood could help in avoiding
\par
shortfalls in rural areas. In urban areas a shift to district heat (possibly coal-
\par
fired) could decrease the reliance on petroleum.
\par
Environment
\par
(9) Potential system-wide environmental impacts due to energy use and supply in
\par
Austria are appreciable. Because of continuing energy demand growth, the system-
\par
wide impacts would not significantly decrease over the time period studied, despite
\par
introduction of improved pollution control technology.
\par
(10) Air pollution will be the largest contributor to energy-related impacts on
\par
public health. These air pollution impacts will be concentrated in the five major
\par
urban areas of Austria, namely, Vienna, Salzburg, Graz, Linz, and Innsbruck.
\par
(11) Desulfurization of fuel oil for use in urban residential and commercial
\par
buildings would be an effective and important measure for protecting the public
\par
from health effects of sulfur emissions.
\par
(12) Regional and local environmental effects due to Austria's energy system are
\par
significant. However, there is also a family of effects whose significance is
\par
better assessed from a global perspective. Examples of these are long term climate
\par
modifications due to C02 emissions from combustion of fossil fuels, and potential
\par
dangerous flows of fissile material within the nuclear fuel cycle. Such global
\par
concerns can and must be addressed from an international perspective to avoid the
\par
"tragedy of the commons" on a global scale.
\par
Sensitivity Studies
\par
In addition to the scenarios studied, a large number of sensitivity analyses were
\par
conducted. Several of these proved to be of great interest to government and in-
\par
dustrial organizations in Austria; two of these are summarized here.
\par
Petroleum shortfalls - closing the petroleum gap. The present uncertainty surround-
\par
ing world petroleum reserves and production rates, and its impact on future supply
\par
availability, was an extremely important concern in developing a comprehensive
\par
energy strategy. Results of recent studies forecast potential petroleum shortfalls
\par
developing in the mid- to late-1980's. This study estimated Austria's future oil
\par
supplies using a world forecast developed by the Workshop on Alternative Energy
\par
Strategies (WAES) (Wilson, 1977). The petroleum supply curve was based on the 33
\par
Million Barrels of Oil Per Day (MBOD) production limit (non-communist countries)
\par
scenario with a reserve addition rate of 10 billion barrels per year and 3\% economic
\par
growth. The WAES supply curve peaks shortly after 1990.
\par
As a basis of comparison of supply and demand in Austria, petroleum imports to
\par
Austria were assumed to decrease at the same rate as total world production. This
\par
leads to a petroleum shortfall of 20 million barrels of oil per year (about 0.05
\par
MBOD) by 2015. In scenario S4, as illustrated in Fig. 3, this sizeable shortfall
\par
develops despite the low growth in energy demand resulting from conservation
\par
measures, lower economic growth, and the phasing out of petroleum for electrical
\par
generation.
