\par
A sensitivity study was developed for Scenario S4 in the year 2015, in order to study
\par
the feasibility of relieving the petroleum "shortfall" through shifts to other fuels.
\par
In the sensitivity study, fuel shifts were assumed to occur in the industrial, com-
\par
mercial, and residential sectors. The rationale for shifts in the industrial
\par
sector was based upon a 1975 Viennese investigation of fuel substitutability in
\par
major industries (Bundeskammer, 1976). This study considered technical changes
\par
which must accompany such fuel shifts, as replacement of boilers, storage of solid
\par
fuels, and the construction of gas pipelines. The investigators found that the
\par
largest Austrian industries, which consume over 80\% of the nation's heavy fuel oil,
\par
would be capable of reducing their reliance on this fuel by 40\%, through shifts to
\par
coal, natural gas, and coke. In the S4 sensitivity study, similar substitutions
\par
were assumed for the industrial sector (with the exception of natural gas, because
\par
of uncertainty about the availability of this fuel in 2015).
\par
A decrease in petroleum consumption in the residential and commercial sectors could
\par
be achieved by 2015 through stepped-up construction of waste- and coal-fired dis-
\par
trict heating plants, as well as through a slowing of the shift away from coal-
\par
burning ovens in urban apartments. In rural areas greater use of coal and wood for
\par
space-heating is feasible. It was also anticipated that new technologies, such as
\par
solar heating, could replace as much as 20\% of petroleum requirements in the
\par
residential sector by 2015.
\par
Figure 4 shows the results of the sensitivity study in terms of an alternative pri-
\par
mary energy fuel mix. Because of the need to decrease the absolute quantity of
\par
petroleum by 30\% in order to relieve the petroleum shortfall, the importance of
\par
this fuel dropped from 43\% of primary energy requirements in the regular S4 case in
\par
2015 to 31\% in the sensitivity study. At the same time, the percentage of total
\par
primary energy requirements met by hard coal and lignite increased from 13\% in the
\par
regular S4 case in the year 2015 to 24\% in the sensitivity study.
\par
