CONCLUDING REMARKS
\par
The assessment of alternative energy/environment futures up to 2015 has, because of
\par
the broad systems perspectives that it provides, contributed to a better understanding
\par
of the overall energy and environmental problems of Austria. This perspective is
\par
particularly inportant in the current period of transition in Austria's energy use
\par
patterns. At the same time, the Austrian case study demonstrated the feasibility
\par
and usefulness of the adopted approach: building alternative scenarios on the
\par
basis of a system of linked quantitative models.
\par
The dominent philosophy underlying the development of this system of models has
\par
been one of continous evolution and refinement and an insistance upon the maintain-
\par
ance of flexibility. This strategy was an outgrowth of the perception of the energy
\par
problem as a rapidly changing one in a period of great uncertainty. This uncertainty
\par
led to a research approach which stresses flexibility and room for innovation. It
\par
also has underscored the importance of a continuing process moving through the
\par
sequential phases of modelling: 1) Model conception, 2) model development and
\par
testing, 3) implementation and use in policy formation and decisionmaking, and
\par
finally, 4) feedback bringing about refinement and further conceptualizing.
\par
To avoid inconsistency, the general concept of combining submodels of various
\par
degrees of refinement in a flexible way requires careful consideration by the model
\par
user of all the interrelationships and feedbacks which are not implemented formally.
\par
On the other hand the same concept allows the general use of models developed for
\par
different countries and regions, and achieves a degree of flexibility which prob-
\par
ably never can be achieved in one large model covering all aspects of the energy
\par
system. Although it is unlikely there will ever be a universal system of energy/
\par
environment models, a long range goal of this research continues to be the
\par
- 311 -
\par
generalization of the approaches into a coherent process for sound resource man-
\par
agement in all regions of the world.
