ABSTRACT
\par
uncertainty in energy policy modeling is usually handled by the technique of esta-
\par
blishing scenarios. Various assumptions are made on the uncertain elements and
\par
deterministic models are run under the corresponding scenarios. A strategy is then
\par
derived based on the results of these runs.
\par
In this paper we consider a specific model for the power generation planning pro-
\par
blem when some future data are uncertain. The model is based on a particular non-
\par
linear programming formulation of the power generation problem which leads to a
\par
very compact mathematical program. As a consequence, rather complex event trees
\par
can be considered at reasonable computational cost. A realistic application is
\par
presented and its solution analyzed. It is shown that, even for problems where the
\par
value of information is expected to be low, the structure of the solution may be
\par
more satisfactory than that usually obtained from deterministic models.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Stochastic programming, decision under uncertainty, power generation planning,
\par
piecewise quadratic programming.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Tackling uncertainty is probably one of the stumbling blocks of energy policy mode-
\par
ling. The difficulty of dealing adequately with an uncertain future is illustrated
\par
by the numerous energy planning studies done under various scenarios, and by the
\par
ongoing effort to update these scenarios as soon as new information becomes availa-
\par
ble. Notwithstanding its interest, the subject does not seem to have received,
\par
among energy modelers, the methodological attention that it would seem to deserve
\par
from its practical importance. Apart from the work of Manne and his co-workers
\par
(Manne, 1974) (Hung-po Chao, 1979) (Richels, 1979), stochastic energy models are
\par
seldom refered to in the literature (Manne, 1979) : instead the current approach
\par
of dealing with uncertainty is to run deterministic models under various assump-
\par
tions and to derive, using more or less formalised approaches, a strategy from the
\par
set of obtained results.
\par
- 313 -
\par
- 314 -
\par
Several reasons can probably be put forward to explain this apparent lack of inte-
\par
rest in systematic techniques dealing with uncertainty. Among them it seems clear
\par
that the complexity, and in particular the enormous size, of the optimization pro-
\par
blems that one is naturally lead to when dealing directly with uncertainty is a
\par
strong deterrent from using formalized approaches to the problem. It should also
\par
be added that the value of information is often low in the usual planning models
\par
(Manne, 1974) and that it is mainly for the study of R-D strategies that stochas-
\par
tic models are most relevant.
\par
In this paper we consider the case of a power generation system and provide a spe-
\par
cific nonlinear stochastic model adapted to that problem. A major advantage of
\par
our formulation is that it leads to a mathematical program of much smaller size
\par
than the deterministic equivalent which would result from usual linear programming
\par
approaches. The model is then solved using a particular algorithm for piecewise
\par
quadratic programming which we think also presents definite advantages compared to
\par
more classical approaches (Louveaux, 1979).
\par
The practical interest of focusing on the power generation system for illustrating
\par
the problems related to uncertainty is clear : this sector is characterized by im-
\par
portant substitution possibilities in its inputs (oil-coal-nuclear), and hence ex-
\par
hibits quite broad options; on the other hand, the very long lead time and service
\par
life of its plants require the planning process to be based on rather long term
\par
projections where uncertainty can play an important role. The power system is also
\par
susceptible of significant developments in the future, as new technologies which
\par
are still in the realm of research and development (for example FBR, underground
\par
gazification, photovoltaic cells {$\bullet\bullet\bullet$} ) come to maturity.
\par
Because of the limited space allowed for this paper, the expos1t1on will concentra-
\par
te on the presentation of the model and on a relatively simple application. More
\par
involved problems are discussed in a companion paper (Louveaux and Smeers, 1979)
\par
and will be alluded to in the oral presentation. Although the example dealt with
\par
here does not belong to the class where stochastic models are most useful, its
\par
solution already exhibits some interesting features which are difficult to obtain
\par
by purely deterministic approaches.
\par
