\par
A few additional notions will be useful before stating the complete deterministic
\par
model. Besides unit investment and operating costs, power plants are usually cha-
\par
racterized in generation planning models by technico-economic data such as the
\par
annual availability, the investment cost and the service life. In order to make
\par
our model as compact as possible (and hence to allow for more complex event trees
\par
when dealing with stochastic problems) we shall make the following assumptions.
\par
Following the tradition of power generation planning models, we shall assume the
\par
availability of each type of plant to be defined by a single coefficient which takes
\par
into account both planned maintenance and forced outages. This coefficient is stric-
\par
tly between 0 and I; the product of the rated capacity of the plant by the coef-
\par
ficient is the available capacity. In order not to cloud the presentation with too
\par
many symbols, we did not include these coefficients in the written equations.
\par
Power generation planning models usually deal with a time horizon which is of the
\par
same order of magnitude as the service life of the plants. No great error is then
\par
introduced in the model if one assumes that plants that are commissioned and dis-
\par
mantled during the planning horizon are replaced, at the end of their service life,
\par
by equipment of the same type. Making this assumption, the investment cost of each
\par
set of equipment will then depend on the year of beginning of operation and will
\par
be equal to the present value at time t of the sum of the annual values of the in-
\par
vestment cost from t until the end of the horizon.
\par
Let The be the unit investment cost of plant beginning its operation at time t.
