INTRODUCING UNCERTAINTY
\par
Data used in the power generation planning process are often uncertain. A common
\par
approach used to overcome this difficulty is toset up various scenarios which one
\par
studies using some deterministic model; a final strategy is then arrived at more or
\par
less formally, taking into account the results obtained from these various runs.
\par
In the following we shall assume that the union of the paths representing these
\par
scenarios form an event tree. We shall suppose that the decision maker has some
\par
idea about the probability of occurrence of these various scenarios. The determi-
\par
nistic model can then be extended to the event tree by proceeding as follows : at
\par
each node k t of period t in the tree is associated a set of information about dif-
\par
