A TEST PROBLEM
\par
Various problems dealing with uncertain elements can be thought of in the context
\par
of power planning. Because of the lack of space, in this paper we shall only con-
\par
sider the classical problem of choosing the best mix of known equipment sets (i.e.
\par
assuming no new technology) in order to satisfy some future demand. Besides exis-
\par
ting capacities which are assumed to remain in operation until the end of their
\par
service life, four types of plants can be commissioned in the planning horizon :
\par
namely, coal and fuel-gas fired plants, nuclear plants, and gas turbines. A main
\par
problem in this context is the treatment of uncertain future costs and demands.
\par
As already mentioned before, the value of information is usually low for this
\par
type of problem. Notwithstanding this fact the example can illustrate some interes-
\par
ting features of the approach compared to a purely deterministic approach as we
\par
- 319 -
\par
briefly discuss now. Experience with classical linear programming models dealing
\par
with this problem shows, that very small modifications of the costs can result in
\par
drastic changes in the mix of plants covering the base load. One of the aims of
\par
this test problem is to show that much smoother transitions are obtained by dealing
\par
directly with uncertainty using a model such as the one presented in the preceding
\par
section. Similarly moratoria have often been advocated for nuclear plants in order
\par
to acquire some new information. We shall see that this type of behavior comes
\par
quite naturally in the solution of the problem. We consider an horizon from 1979
\par
to 2007, decomposed in seven periods of four years. We assume nuclear plants and
\par
fossil fuel fired plants to require respectively two and one period as construction
\par
lead times; gas turbines can be put in operation with a negligible delay. Demand
\par
is assumed to grow at 4.9 percent, per year, although that percentage could also
\par
be made random. We focus on the evolution of the various fuels that we represent
\par
on the event tree displayed on Fig. 3; it can be seen that the tree involves eight
\par
terminal branches and a total of 43 nodes. To each node is associated one or two
\par
vectors (N,C,F) and [N,C,GT). The components of (N,C,F) correspond to the opera-
\par
ting costs in Belgian Francs/KWh of nuclear, coal and oil plants respectively. The
\par
operating cost of gas turbine is proportional to of oil fired plants.
