not represented because, as expected, they do not appear in the solution. Turning
\par
to the optimal solution represented on Fig. 3, we can see that it is much more
\par
- 320 -
\par
diversified than what is usually obtained in deterministic studies. We shall not
\par
consider the decisions made after 87 : indeed from that period on the tree reduces
\par
to a set of paths and a deterministic behavior can then be expected. For the de-
\par
cision taken in periods 79, 83 and 87, the obtained solutions usually lead to a
\par
mix of nuclear and coal (only in scenarios H7 and H3 do we have purely nuclear
\par
stategies). An interesting feature of the solution is illustrated by thedecisions
\par
taken in period 83. Transforming the new capacities represented in Fig. 3 to com-
\par
missioned capacities we see that when the fuel costs are given by (39, 56, 73) the
\par
optimal strategy is to abstain from any new decision concerning coal or nuclear
\par
plants and to wait until the next period when new information is available.
\par
CONCLUSIONS
\par
A model for the stochastic power generation planning problem is presented and a
\par
simple application discussed. Because of the compact representation of the pro-
\par
blem allowed by the formulation, it is expected that the model will permit the
\par
treatment of rather complex event trees at reasonable cost. As illustrated by the
\par
example, the method arrives at solutions that are more diversified than the usual
\par
deterministic approach.
