ENERGY MODELING AND AGGREGATION
\par
OF REFINING
\par
D. Babusiaux and M. Valais
\par
Institut Francais du Petro/e, France
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
The "Institut Fran..ais du Petrole" has compiled several dynamic linear
\par
programming models (energy planning model with the assistance of "Electricite
\par
de France", refining and petrochemicals models) that need an aggregate
\par
representation of the refining industry.
\par
In order to simplify, it might be conceivable to assimilate the whole set of
\par
French refineries with a single refinery. In fact, this can hardly give a
\par
satisfactory formulation. As a matter of fact, the value of the dual variables
\par
found in this case may be rather different from the values of marginal costs
\par
that can be observed in real-life. This is due to structural and regional
\par
differences from one refining center ot another.
\par
The accuracy of an aggregation method (taking into account the above-mentioned
\par
differences) is evaluated by studying the marginal costs of products. Such a
\par
study is always important; especially, in the case where the model (refining)
\par
is to be connected with the model of another sector (electricity production
\par
for instance) employing as an input the output of the first sector (heavy
\par
fuel oil).
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy model; Refinery model; Linear programming.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
For several years now, the Institut Fran..ais du Petrole has owned and has been
\par
using models representing the refining industry. These models are used either
\par
for modeling one or several specific refineries (process evaluation, inserting
\par
a unit into an existing complex) or on an aggregate level (e.g. national) for
\par
examining the desirable evolution of the structure of the refining industry as
\par
a function of forecasts concerning the consumption demand which will have to be
\par
fulfilled in the years to corne.
\par
- 321 -
\par
- 322 -
\par
As part of a research project for CNRS (Centre National de la Recherche
\par
Scientifique), IFP built a model integrating all the energy production and
\par
transformation sectors in France (oil, electricity, coal, gas). The aim was
\par
to analyze the consequences of variations in the demand for electricity and
\par
petroleum products, variations quite different from the ones used in the
\par
most frequently used scenarios, as the result of poli-
\par
cies for French industry. The formalization of the submodel relating to
\par
electricity production was performed by the Service of Economic and General
\par
Studies of EDF (Electricite de France) and was calibrated on more sophisticated
\par
models.
\par
Building a model representing the French refining industry raises aggregation
\par
problems at the level of supplies, products, regions, etc. Dual variables
\par
make up an indispensable indicator for evaluating the validity of the simpli-
\par
fications adopted. The analysis of these variables (mainly marginal production
\par
costs) is of particular importance when the refining model has to be coupled,
\par
as mentioned above, with representative models of other energy sectors. We
\par
begin by describing a few elements relating to the aggregation of crude oil
\par
supplies and finished product demands and then we take up the problem of
\par
aggregating different refining structures.
\par
I - REPRESENTING THE REFINING INDUSTRY BY A SINGLE-REFINERY MODEL
\par
The models used in the refining industry are linear programming models. For
\par
a single-period model, the main variables are product throughputs (amounts
\par
of crude oils and intermediate products) to which must be added, for long-
\par
range planning models, variables corresponding to the capacities of units to
\par
be built. The main equations are:
\par
{$\bullet$} material-balance equations (e.g. for intermediate products,
\par
indicating that the sum of the amounts used downstream is equal to the amounts
\par
available at the outlet of the upstream units;
\par
{$\cdot$} demand equations, explaining the fact that the sum of the amounts
\par
of intermediate products used in making up a finished product must be able
\par
to meet the consumption demand for this product;
\par
{$\cdot$} quality equations expressing the need to respect, for each
\par
finished product, the legal or technical specifications (e.g. specific gravity,
\par
vapor pressure, octane number of automotive fuels, sulfur content, viscosity
\par
for distillates and fuel oils).
\par
The models used for refinery management, corresponding to a fairly detailed
\par
representation, generally include several hundred or even several thousand
\par
constraints and variables. In building a dynamic (multiperiod) model and
\par
integrating other energy sectors while maintaining a reasonable size, the
\par
representation of refining over one period of time must be done in a simplified
\par
manner.
\par
The planning of investments does not require a model to distinguish each
\par
finished product manufactured by a refinery. For example, engine diesel fuel
\par
- 323 -
\par
and home heating oil can be manufactured from the same base stocks. The only
\par
difference between these two products (apart from coloring additive used for
\par
tax reasons) is in the specification for the cetane number. But selecting
\par
base stocks of sufficient quality to manufacture engine diesel fuels is
\par
generally done without any additional cost. Engine diesel fuel and home
\par
heating oil are thus considered as a single product in long-range models.
\par
Likewise, various special products produced in relatively small tonnages will
\par
be assimilated with the major products having the most similar properties.
\par
The number of products to be taken into consideration is thus relatively
\par
limited (e.g. half a dozen: automotive fuels, naphta, heating oil, heavy
\par
fuel oil, liquefied petroleum gas).
\par
French refineries process several dozen types of crude oil. But the size
\par
of a linear programming model is approximately proportional to the number
\par
of crude oils considered. Therefore, it is impossible to introduce all the
\par
crude oils corresponding to French supplies into a refining model. These
\par
supplies must thus be represented by a restricted number of crude oils so as
\par
to lose as little accuracy as possible.
\par
In the industry, specialists in crude oil evaluation usually make this reduction
\par
empirically as the result of their experience, and they consider a few
\par
"typical" crude oils to which others can be assimilited by analogy. A more
\par
systematic method can also be used, involving multidimensional data analysis
\par
techniques l
\par
.
\par
A crude oil is defined by a certain number of properties, i.e. qualities
\par
(specific gravity, sulfur content), yields in distillation products (naphtha,
\par
middle distillates, heavy distillates, residue) and their qualities (octane
\par
number of gasoline, PONA analysis of feedstocks for catalytic reforming units,
\par
viscosity and freeze points of distillates, etc.).
\par
Let US consider the problem of representing any supply by means of a number>>
\par
of given basic crude oils (e.g. from 3 to 5). Each crude oil will be represen-
\par
ted by the vector of the properties defined above. An effort can then be made
\par
to define a crude oil not belonging to all the>> basic crude oils by means of
\par
a linear combination of the vectors associated with the basic crude oils. The
\par
linear combination coefficients enabling the errors committed to be reduced
\par
will be defined by linear regression2
\par
.
\par
In practice, a regression method under constraints is generally used (positive
\par
coefficients) so as to avoid having to define a crude oil on the basis of
\par
amounts of one or several basic crude oils. (In some cases, if the
\par
has to be represented by negative amounts, it cannot be introduced
\par
a model. Furthermore and in all cases, the introducing
\par
makes it more difficult to interpret and present
\par
of results).
\par
A more detailed presentation of this method is available in Bond, Majorga,
\par
Offant (1977).
\par
2 Properties are standardized to prevent any bias introduced by the use of
\par
heterogeneous physical units.
\par
- 324 -
\par
The selection of the basic crude oils set is very important. This set will be
\par
chosen so as to minimize the weighted sum of the errors obtained during the
\par
regressions defined above.
\par
This might lead to a large-size combinatory problem (analyzing all combinations
\par
including n crude oils). A principal components analysis of all the crude oils
\par
may be useful in this search. It makes it possible to spot the crude oils most
\par
liable to represent a group of crude oils after having isolated groups of
\par
similar crude oils. At the same time, in making this selection it is natural
\par
to retain only the crude oils representating a significant proportion of
\par
national supplies.
\par
The number n of basic crude oils is the result of a compromise between the
\par
objective of accuracy and that of the size of the model. The quality of the
\par
representation obtained can be tested by introducing, into a refining model
\par
(linear programming), a reduced supply by means of this method and by comparing
\par
the results with those obtained by introducing a supply described in greater
\par
detail.
\par
For long-range planning models, a set of three or four basic crude oils can
\par
generally be considered satisfactory.
\par
II - AGGREGATION OF REFINING STRUCTURES
\par
A process often used in energy optimization models consists in representing
\par
the entire refining industry of a country as if it were a single refinery.
\par
However, in France there is some disparity between existing refinery types.
\par
Some are simple (and include distillation, hydrodesulfurization and reforming
\par
units), while others have one or several conversion units (catalytic cracking,
\par
visbreaking, etc.).
\par
These disparities occur on two levels:
\par
On a national level: different refining zones are equippedqutte differently
\par
in catalytic cracking capacities. This can be explained by the existence of
\par
larger or smaller regional markets for heavy fuel oil. For example, the
\par
location of heavy industries in the North and in Alsace-Lorraine results in
\par
a high regional demand for heavy fuel oil, and this reduces the local need
\par
for conversion.
\par
On a regional level, the distribution of conversion units is no longer
\par
homogeneous among refineries in the same zone. The example of the Seine-
\par
Maritime department illustrates this situation.
\par
If the long-range marginal costs of production corresponding to different
\par
types of refinery are analyzed, very great differences can be seen.
\par
A primordial factor explaining the values of these marginal costs (dual values
\par
in linear programming) is the proportion of heavy fuel oil in total production.
\par
If the demand for heavy fuel oil is parametrized and if the value of the marginal
\par
costs of finished products is considered as a function of the demand for heavy
\par
fuel oil, the following observations can be made :
\par
- 325 -
\par
For a simple refinery (distillation and reforming) there is considerable
\par
discontinuity, i.e. when heavy fuel oil production drops below a given
\par
threshold (approximately 30\%), the marginal cost of this product decreases
\par
very quickly since the excess amounts cannot be upgraded by conversion.
\par
Conversely, for a refinery having conversion units, the long-range marginal
\par
cost, with suitable capacities, evolves much less abruptly.
\par
To illustrate a few analysis elements of the mechanism of price setting on the
\par
market, let us consider a simplified diagram. Let us assume that there are only
\par
two types of refinery (complex and simple; simple refineries being not equipped
\par
with conversion units).
\par
Let us neglect costs of transportation and let us discount any possibilities of
\par
exporting and importing products.
\par
If a demand pattern similar to that of France is to be met by each type of
\par
refinery, the marginal cost of heavy fuel oil from the simple refinery will
\par
be quite appreciably lower than that from the refinery having conversion units.
\par
