\par
II - APPLICATION EXAMPLES
\par
As part of the project it was carrying out for CNRS, IFP with the assistance
\par
of EDF built a linear model for the optimization of the energy production and
\par
transformation sectors4
\par
. The dynamic (multi-period) structure of this model is
\par
similar to those of models built by other organizations.
\par
4 Babusiaux, Offant. Valais (1978)
\par
- 327 -
\par
Among the specific features of the model (beyond the techniques used to define
\par
an aggregate representation of the refining industry), mention can be made of
\par
the formulation of the electricity production sector (formulation worked out
\par
by EDF). This formulation includes the random nature of the demand for electri-
\par
city, with the objective function being an expected value of the production cost.
\par
Possibilities of supply default are introduced, and high costs are associated
\par
with them.
\par
The raw materials requirements for organic chemistry (plastics, synthetic fibers,
\par
synthetic rubber, detergents, etc.) and for nitrogen fertilizer chemistry are
\par
taking an increasing share of the supplies of primary fossil fuels. At the
\par
same time, the rapidly rising prices of hydrocarbons, and especially those of
\par
crude oil, coupled with the progressive widening of the price differentials
\par
between light and heavy oil fractions are resulting in doubts as to the validity
\par
of traditional schemes for supplies of raw materials for the chemical industry.
\par
The coupling of oil, gas and petrochemical linear models enables the basic
\par
petrochemical sector (production of basic intermediates) to be linked to its
\par
upstream hydrocarbon supplies with an extremely wide range of feedstocks (going
\par
from the lightest components of natural gas all the way to vacuum distillates
\par
from oil refining). The static or dynamic models thus built can be used more
\par
effectively for analyzing the consequences, for the petrochemical sector of
\par
events or energy trends, or, on the contrary, the secondary effects of a given
\par
evolution of the petrochemical sector on the energy sector. Lastly, this
\par
coupling provides a particularly effective tool for analyzing the importance of
\par
new raw manufacturing processes with regard to both refining and petrochemicals.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
The increasing importance of energy problems in the economies of the leading
\par
industrialized countries makes it necessary to built aggregate energy model on
\par
a nationwide scale. However the construction of such models raises particularly
\par
acute problems with regard to their degree of representativity.
\par
In the field of oil refining in particular, special care must be taken to
\par
integrate not only quantitative phenomena and structural upheavals (drastic
\par
changes in the patterns of demand for finished products and refining processes)
\par
but also the intensification of qualitative problems (increasing difficulties in
\par
manufacturing low-sulfur products, products having reasonable viscosity, etc.).
\par
It should be borne in mind that a rough aggregation in the modelling of the oil
\par
refining sector on a nationwide scale can only lead to unsatisfactory or even
\par
dangerous representations.
