A MATHEMATICAL PROGRAMMING MODEL
\par
FOR ASSESSING ADVANCED OIL
\par
TECHNOLOGIES
\par
A. Suzuki{$\cdot$}
\par
Department ofNuclear Engineering, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan
\par
1. INTRODUCTION
\par
In the long-term perspective, it seems unavoidable that light and easy-to-handle
\par
crude oil will decline because of the limited nature of its reserve and heavy and
\par
hard-to-handle one will take its place instead. In the demand side, however, the
\par
higher quality of oil products is required from environmental safety. The demands
\par
for fuel oil, for instance, are changing qualitatively as well as quantitatively;
\par
the market share of heavy residual oil with high sulfur content and/or high pour
\par
point is going down while the demands for light fuel oil with low sulfur content
\par
and/or low pour point are increasing.
\par
A number of alternative advanced technologies for refining heavy oil is now con-
\par
sidered so that they may fill up this qualitative gap between supply and demand
\par
aspects. Most of those technologies are highly capital-intensive and therefore a
\par
long-range decision making is required for choosing the alternatives. This paper
\par
describes a dynamic mathematical programming model of the petroleum industry in
\par
Japan as a whole, being intended to provide an analytical tool for simulation
\par
studies on the question as of when, how and which kind of the technologies should
\par
be introduced and thereby to serve as a framework for that long-term planning.
\par
2. SPECIFICATION OF THE MODEL
\par
2-1. Basic Structure
\par
The basic structure of the model is quite the same as the one being now used at
\par
many petroleum industries. 1. e., the constraints which must be satisfied at eaLh
\par
point of time are the followings;{$\cdot$}
\par
(1) supply constraints: the upper bounds are imposed upon the availability of
\par
each imported oil,
\par
(2) crude oil balance: once imported, crude oil goes either to refinery or to
\par
direct use,
\par
(3) intermediate product balance: intermediate products of each refinery units
\par
are produced with given yield and then used as raw materials for other products or
