\par
The objective function (9) is useful if we are interested in the price-responsive
\par
demand projection where marginal production cost increase results in some reduction
\par
of demand level, while the other (6) is useful for the price-inelastic demand condi-
\par
tions. The reason why the criterion (9) is price-responsive is as follows. If q
\par
is optimal, then the relation:
\par
dU/dq = 8(eR + vX + kY)/8q (10)
\par
must be valid. Since the left-hand-side of this equation implies the market price
\par
and the right does the marginal production cost, the above relation indicates that
\par
an optimal demand level be determined so that it may equal the price to the marginal
\par
production cost. There are two modes of production cost rise; one is the price
\par
increase of oil (8(eR)/8q) and the other is the refining cost increase expressed as
\par
(8(vX + kY)/8q). According to the equation (10), both effects cause the price in-
\par
crease (dU/dq) and result in some reduction of demand level q, since usually the
\par
price elasticity 0 is negative.
\par
2-4. Mathematical Programming Formulation
\par
Methodologically, the mathematical problem considered here is very similar to the
\par
long-term energy planning problems treated by the investigations (1-3), where the
\par
mathematical programming approach was employed for optimization. Almost all the
\par
constraints and the mathematical equations mentioned above can be formulated in
\par
terms of the linear programming without special modifications. The only equation
\par
that needs the modification is one of the candidates of objective function (9).
\par
With the aid of piece-wise linear approximation, however, the use of the linear
\par
- 332 -
\par
programming can be made, too. Figure-l is the fundamental structure of the final
\par
tableau.
\par
3. COMPUTATIONAL RESULTS
\par
3-1. Reference Case
\par
The schematic description of the reference system of petroleum refinery is shown in
\par
Figure-2, where the following refining units are included as the alternative
\par
technologies for heavy oil processing which at the moment are relatively easy to
\par
introduce: (1) thermal cracking unit for producing cokes from vacuum bottom, (2)
\par
gasification unit for producing gaseous fuel from cokes, (3) catalytic cracking unit
\par
for processing atmospheric residue, (4) hydrocracking unit for processing vacuum
\par
bottom, and (5) advanced type of desulferization of atmospheric residue with very
\par
high sulfur content.
\par
At present, these technologies are not used widely. It mainly rests upon the extent
\par
of heaviness of the imported crude oil whether these are required in the future.
\par
Figure-3 represents one of the reference cases estimated by the Japanese petroleum
\par
industry, indicating that the shift toward heavier oil seems inevitable especially
\par
after the year 1990. Thus, if this is the case, it is expected that some of the
\par
advanced technologies listed above will be introduced around that year.
\par
3-2. Illustrated Examples
\par
The model provides a cost-minimal or utility-maximal dynamics of the refinery system
\par
for given conditions on future oil supply and demand. Figure-4 is used to show its
\par
typical example for atmospheric distillation unit, which implies that the initially
\par
existing capacity will be large enough to the 1983, and thereafter some new instal-
\par
lation will be required every year to meet growing demands.
\par
With respect to the advanced technology units, the introduction schemes are heavily
\par
dependent upon whether the cost-minimal or the utility-maximal. In Figure-s, the
\par
marginal production costs of final products for the cost-minimal case where the
\par
price-inelastic demand projection is made is shown, and in Figure-6, for the utility-
\par
maximal case where it is assumed that the demands are price-responsive with constant
\par
elasticity. The difference between the two cases is striking; in the price-
\par
inelastic demand case the petroleum industry must meet the demand by any means, and
\par
therefore the marginal production costs will rise remarkably in particular for the
\par
products with relatively high growing rates, such as kerosene, gas oil and fuel oil
\par
(A). The fact that the costs for these products will increase rapidly since the
\par
year 1990 is obviously due to the introduction of the advanced technology units.
\par
Except for the gasification unit, all the alternatives considered in the reference
\par
system are introduced with fairly high rates since around that year.
\par
As shown in Figure-6, in case of price-responsive demand projections, however, the
\par
marginal production costs do not change significantly but rather will be stabilized.
\par
The reason is that the price elasticity a priori fixed there is large enough for
\par
consumers to behave in such a way that the utility decrease from reducing the con-
\par
sumption of products is better than the cost increase associated with such high
\par
market price hike as in Figure-s. Naturally, the introducing manner of the advanced
\par
technology units is far modest compared with the other case; the hydrocracking unit
\par
disappears as well as the gasification unit and as for the remaining three technolo-
\par
gies their contribution is rather minor.
\par
4. CONCLUDING REMARKS
\par
A dynamic mathematical programming model was constructed to assess advanced tech-
\par
- 333 -
\par
nologies for processing heavy oil. As long as it is inevitable that the quality
\par
of crude oil will worsen to a certain extent, we cannot avoid to introduce some of
\par
those technologies in some years. The results obtained here suggest that it rests
\par
closely upon the future market condition of oil products, particularly whether
\par
effectively price-responsive or not. It is known that the past structure of
\par
Japanese oil business was of the special feature that is unable to for
\par
the future. Our country is now at the turning point in this regard as well.
