AL-EDIS: AN ENERGY INFORMATION SYSTEM
\par
FOR DEVELOPING COUNTRIES
\par
V. Mubayi, R. Chatterjee and T. D'Errico
\par
Institute for Energy Research, State University of New York, Stony Brook,
\par
New York, USA
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
This paper provides a brief description of an interactive, graphics-oriented, com-
\par
puterized energy information system intended for use as "a tool in energy sector
\par
planning in developing countries. A brief review of the methodology used for
\par
constructing the system framework is followed by a description of its current capa-
\par
bilities and planned future extensions as warranted by user requirements.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Energy; information system; developing countries.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
In September 1978, under a grant from the aL Dir'iyyah Institute, The Institute
\par
for Energy Research at the State University of New York, Stony Brook, began a
\par
project to develop an energy information system designed to help energy sector
\par
planning in developing countries. The project was initiated in response to the
\par
urgent need felt by energy sector planners in these countries to obtain updated
\par
information and suitable analytical tools in order to project future energy re-
\par
quirements, evaluate various energy options and carry out national energy assess-
\par
ments.
\par
The information system, AL-EDIS, that has been developed is designed to provide a
\par
system of access to relevant energy information specifically directed to the needs
\par
of policymakers. A special emphasis has been placed on graphics capability to
\par
provide information in a form readily understandable to policy and other non-
\par
technical decisionmakers. Since the requirements of energy sector planning go
\par
beyond just information on energy supplies and demands, AL-EDIS has also been
\par
designed to offer users a methodology for carrying out national energy assessments
\par
in addition to containing a data bank on energy resources and technologies.
\par
Two broad categories of information are designed to be contained in the system.
\par
First, generic data on energy resources worldwide, energy technologies, process
\par
efficiencies, capital and operating costs, etc,. Second, country-specific data
\par
on domestic energy resources, consumption patterns (including non-commercial fuel,,),
\par
- 339 -
\par
- 340 -
\par
and related ancillary data important for energy planning such as national econom-
\par
ic, industrial, demographic, geographic and sociocultural characteristics.
\par
The users of AL-EDIS are expected to be, primarily, national planning organiza-
\par
tions or similar agencies in developing countries. The system is initially de-
\par
signed to be used in conjunction with a national energy assessment for which a
\par
basic set of data on energy supply and consumption will be generated. AL-EDIS
\par
will help in categorizing and organizing the data collected into a form suitable
\par
for further analysis. As the data base expands and improves, the system may be
\par
used by policy analysts for answering more detailed and specific questions related
\par
to the assessment process.
\par
THE AL-EDIS FRAMEWORK
\par
The principal form of access to information contained in AL-EDIS is through a
\par
computer-graphic energy network simulator. This network is based on the Reference
\par
Energy System (RES), developed at Brookhaven National Laboratory, which displays
\par
a nation's energy system in the form of a link-node network diagram of energy
\par
flows from primary energy resource sectors through conversion systems to final
\par
end-use sectors. An illustrative network suitable for developing countries is
\par
Fig. 1. (A more complete discussion of the RES framework and its use can be
\par
found in Beller, 1975; Reisman and Malone, 1978; Mubayi, Palmedo, and Doernberg,
\par
1979.) This framework provides a complete policy-oriented description of a
\par
country's energy system. The simulator is designed for on-line, interactive use
\par
by which the user queries the system obtaining an immediate response.
\par
In using the system for energy assessments, one first constructs a "reference" or
\par
"base case" network by making projections of the demand for energy services in
\par
various end-use sectors. Depending on the available data, the level of detail
\par
involved in making projections can vary, from those making simple assumptions be-
\par
tween energy use and sectoral output to more sophisticated approaches based, for
\par
example, on input-output analysis. The allocation of fuels and technologies to
\par
supply these energy services can be performed either on informed engineering-
\par
economic judgment or, in more sophisticated methods, through the use of energy
\par
optimization models. Once the reference case network is constructed, the impact
\par
of introducing new technologies or new energy resources on the energy system as a
\par
whole and its implications for some important national policy goal, e.g., reducing
\par
the level of oil imports, can be quantitatively assessed by making perturbations
\par
to the reference case.
\par
CURRENT STAGE OF DEVELOPMENT
\par
A demonstration of the country-specific component of the information system has
\par
been a major goal of the first year of the project. The major categories of
\par
national data contained in the data base are shown in Fig. 2. The data base
\par
assembled for demonstration has been programmed with data from the Peru Energy
\par
Assessment (U.S. DOE, 1979).
\par
Two major considerations guided us in constructing the graphic displays. The
\par
amount of information which can legibly be displayed on a graphics terminal is
\par
limited. Thus a physical constraint is imposed on the amount of information con-
\par
tained in a given network diagram. A second consideration was related to the
\par
"human engineering aspects" which govern the use of an interactive computer system.
\par
The amount of information displayed at any given time should be only as much as
\par
can usefully be absorbed by the user. Our solution to this particular problem has
\par
been to provide views of the national energy system at varying levels of aggrega-
\par
tion allowing a user to select the level of detail determined necessary for the
\par
- 34\textbackslash{} -
\par
purposes at hand.
\par
Three levels of aggregation are provided for in the graphic displays. At the
\par
highest level of aggregation, a summary view of the entire national energy net-
\par
work describes energy flows from extraction of fuel resources to end-use demands.
\par
Figure 3 displays this for the year 2000 "business as usual" or reference case
\par
projection for Peru. At a medium level of aggregation, under each of the major
\par
activity headings shown in Fig. I, (extraction, refining and conversion activities,
\par
central station conversion, transmission, distribution and storage, decentralized
\par
conversion), the user selects a particular fuel or demand sector for viewing. For
\par
example, he may look at the oil subsystem of a country to examine the flow of oil
\par
into each major end-use. Alternatively, a particular demand sector may be exam-
\par
ined in greater detail to obtain an immediate visualization of the fuels that
\par
feed it. It is also possible at this level of aggregation to select groups of
\par
fuels, or groups of demands for review. At the highest level of aggregation,
\par
the fine details of each fuel supply subsystem or demand sector are shown. In the
\par
case of fuel subsystems, detailed views are available for: coal, oil, nuclear,
\par
hydro, gas,geothermal, wind/solar, agricultural/animal waste, wood and animal/
\par
human labor. In the case of demand, for each of the seven demand sectors (indus-
\par
try, transport, commercial, urban households, rural households, agricultural,
\par
and government) fuels flowing into each end-use are detailed. The networks avail-
\par
able in AL-EDIS for displaying various levels of aggregation are shown in Fig. 4.
\par
Development of the necessary computer software to support the graphic network
\par
simulator has been completed. This software is composed of a number of computer
\par
programs which, in conjunction with the data base, provide the interactive capa-
\par
bility described earlier. These programs are depicted in Fig. 5.
\par
Color graphic capabilities designed into the system include the ability to view
\par
selected trends, shown in the networks, in the form of bar charts. For example,
\par
having viewed the reference-case national energy system, and selected future pro-
\par
jections, a user may request a review of trends in national oil consumption under
\par
each scenario contained in the system. Figure 6 shows an example of this capa-
\par
bility in which Peru's oil consumption projected under various strategies is
\par
displayed in the form of bar charts.
\par
For simulation purposes, the software allows users to review data on which exist-
\par
ing networks are based. It is possible for the user to select particular cases
\par
for manipulation, or, for the user to construct a scenario using his own data.
\par
(In Fig. 5 the set of programs allowing this capability are shown under the head-
\par
ing of "perturbation analysis.")
\par
In addition, the completed software allows users to request selected output in
\par
printed form.
\par
FUTURE DEVELOPMENTS
\par
Work in progress is aimed at adding to the system a set of models which will al-
\par
low more sophisticated simulation exercises in the network format. For example,
\par
it would be useful to allow users the capability of projecting energy require-
\par
ments within particular sectors on the basis of econometric or input-output
\par
models of the economy whose output can be linked to the energy system network
\par
for projection purposes. Similarly, it is proposed to incorporate energy system
\par
optimization models to allocate the various fuel forms within the network on the
\par
basis of minimizing total system costs or any particular component such as
\par
capital costs. In addition, the country-coverage of the data base will be
\par
substantially expanded.
\par
- 342 -
\par
The data base on energy reserves and resources as well as on the characteristics
\par
of specific energy technologies will be expanded. The system will allow technical
\par
users to querj it dirtctly for such stored information and respond to such re-
\par
quests with output in both printed and graphic form.
\par
A useful information system to support decisions pertaining to both energy systems
\par
and to energy and economic development linkages must successfully combine the
\par
technical language of energy engineering systems with the numerous socioeconomic
\par
parameters used in expressing development objectives. These are of primary con-
\par
cern to policymakers. The categories of information to be included in this system
\par
and the network simulator provided for in the system design are intended to effect
\par
such a synthesis.
\par
Finally, Fig. 7 shows the various steps in the use of AL-EDIS as a tool for nation-
\par
al energy planning.
