\par
ABSTRACT
\par
The crude oil price increases of 1974 and 1979 are examined qualitatively
\par
with respect to their impact on final consumer demand for oil products. It is
\par
argued, that recent statistics suggest that price signals do influence the
\par
marketed consumption of energy much stronger and faster than sometimes thought.
\par
This evidence is used to suggest some plausible extrapolations of current trends
\par
which would imply that the world would use some 7 to 13 million bdoe less oil by
\par
1985 than if 1979 consumption patterns were maintained and the economy grew at
\par
p.a. 1979-1985.
\par
In the second part, some comments are made concerning the costing of
\par
alternative forms of energy. It is argued that costing of future technology
\par
should be done on a dynamic basis, distinguishing an "estimation phase", a
\par
"learning curve" phase and a "maturity" phase.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
As the invited paper on the topic of 'Allocation of Resources' this article
\par
will attempt to contribute some thoughts on the subject of the
\par
effectiveness of price signals in the market place. It reflects the underlying
\par
philosophy of the authors that energy production and consumption are much too
\par
complex processes to be effectively centrally regulated in any detail. Thus, the
\par
aim of a "resource allocation" policy should be to ensure a maximum benefit to a
\par
maximum number of participants with a minimum number of regulations. Such a
\par
policy would recognise that the relative price of any commodity has an important
\par
role as carrier of information, pervasively reaching all participants.
\par
This article will not attempt to expound a comprehensive and waterproof
\par
"theory of optimum pricing", it merely aims at presenting some recent price
\par
and consumption statistics in such a way that a pattern suggests itself. The
\par
story is told mainly by the graphs and their legends, and the text is mainly
\par
intended for "asides".
\par
- 353 -
\par
- 354 -
\par
The article consists of two, loosely interrelated, parts. The first traces
\par
qualitatively the impact of the crude oil Drice increases in the seventies on
\par
oil product consumption, the second floats some observations on the desirability
\par
of dynamic rather than static costing of new technologies.
\par
PART I THE IMPACT OF CRUDE OIL PRICE RISES ON OIL PRODUCT CONSUMPTION
\par
The crude oil price over most of this century (that is up to 1970) never
\par
exceeded in money of the day. (See Fig. 1). This implied that from
\par
1948-1970 the crude oil price decreased steadily in real terms. Most of the
\par
infrastructure currently in place in the OECD countries was designed with this
\par
comfortable prospect of falling real prices in mind. Since 1974, this has
\par
obviously changed (See Fig. 1). It should be noted, that the technical
\par
potential for (further) energy efficiency improvements is very substantial
\par
(say 20-40\% of 1973 average practice) and that much of the existing stock of
\par
buildings and industrial plant can successfully and gainfully be improved.
\par
As this potential has been well documentated elsewhere it will not be further
\par
elaborated upon (this article has no reference list, due to difficulties of
\par
"embarras du choix" and for fear of discrimination). It should suffice, that
\par
few experts disagree nowadays that considerable savings would be possible at
\par
economical cost, provided the energy consumers "get the message".
\par
It is generally believed that the "elasticity" of oil consumption is very
\par
low. This article attempts to illustrate the authors' notion that this
\par
elasticity is not so very low after all. It should be said before hand, that
\par
the material shown is not conclusive proof, and much of the assertions made
\par
in this article have yet to rely on "hunch" and "notion" techniques.
\par
If we trace the impact of the ten fold crude oil price increase (in real
\par
terms) between 1970 and 1980 on the final consumer selling price, than we see
\par
that the selling price, in real terms, increased by a much lower factor than
\par
ten-for instance in the EEC by only a factor of roughly two across the barrel.
\par
We should now distinguish two main components of the price mechanism: the
\par
income effect and the price effect (See Fig. 2).
\par
As the price of imported crude oil shoots up, direct effects (such as loss
\par
of spending power in the non oil sector, recycling delays etc) and indirect
\par
effects (consumer's increased savings ratio, loss of business confidence, a
\par
switch in government policies towards deflationary measures, etc) will cause
\par
a recessionary slow-down in economic growth of consuming countries. This
\par
results in a lower economic output than otherwise would have resulted. This is
\par
referred to as the income effect. One can estimate this impact: some detailed
\par
studies suggest that every additional crude oil price increase reduces
\par
economic growth in the World Outside the Communist Areas (WOCA) by i to over
\par
the next year or so.
\par
One can then also calculate the loss of economic output in money terms and
\par
divide this by the amount of oil thus saved. This illustrates that imported
\par
crude oil has a very high "nuisance value", in the order of Jrc'50-\$500/bbl.
\par
The second link in the price mechanism is the price effect, i.e. the
\par
reduction in consumption per constant unit of income. This is expressed by a
\par
decrease in oil/GDP for an increase in the oil price. It is interesting to note
\par
that the costs of substitution and conservation are significantly lower than the
\par
"nuisance value" indicated above. Although few energy consumers will realise
\par
this consciously, it is nevertheless an illustration of the strong incentive
\par
that is implicity administered by the "invisible hand" of the imported crude oil
\par
- 355 -
\par
price.
\par
If we now examine how the crude oil price increases hit the consumer in the
\par
main oil consuming markets, then - again for the EEC - we see that the spread
\par
has not been even at all (See Fig. 3). The transport sector saw hardly any
\par
increases in fuel prices, whereas the industrial sector saw a virtual doubling
\par
(in real terms) of the fuel oil price.
\par
(As a humorous aside, we could compare the erosion of the consumption tax
\par
per barrel in real terms in the EEC with the sharp increase in taxation of oil
\par
and gas production. For policies that profess to aim at curbing consumption
\par
and maximizing indigenous supplies, such a taxation policy makes obvious
\par
sense).
\par
Focussing now on the price effect (as opposed to the income effect) we
\par
can examine the consumption of the main oil products per unit of GDP (See Fig. 4).
\par
Qualitatively, we see - again in the EEC- a very good agreement between the
\par
reductions in consumption per unit of GDP (in constant money) and the
\par
corresponding increases in relative prices.
\par
Without wanting to draw too strong conclusions from such a short time series,
\par
and recognising that roughly half of the reduction shown is substitution rather
\par
than conservation, we still would like to submit this evidence as an indication
\par
that Adam Smith's "invisible hand" is still as active as ever, and still very
\par
effective.
\par
A similar analysis (not presented here) has been carried out for the USA.
\par
There the price signals were not as clear as in the EEC, for well known reasons,
\par
but qualitatively, the same conclusions can be drawn. In real terms, 1979 was
\par
the first year that gasoline prices really went up significantly since 1968 or so
\par
in the US, and the reaction was clear: a 5\% reduction in gasoline consumption in
\par
1979, (i.e. a 7\% reduction in consumption/GDP) over one year, in response to a
\par
real price increase 1978/1979 of not more than 30\%.
\par
In 1978, the US consumers spent roughly 5\% of total Consumer Private
\par
Expenditure on gasoline. With oil price deregulation now a firm fact one can
\par
predict that, in real terms, the gasoline selling price will go up by at least
\par
60\% and possibly as much as 100\% as compared with 1978. This would imply that
\par
the percentage of CPE spent on gasoline would go up to 8-10\%.
\par
However, at such a percentage level, the consumer will have to take notice,
\par
and the signs are everywhere loud and clear that the US motorist is taking notice.
\par
We can therefore now confidentally predict that the US government's
\par
compulsory miles-per-gallon legislation will be able to boast some spectacular
\par
results over the next five years.
\par
Another little example of how price signals can work fast in the market
\par
place comes from the Netherlands. During the car-less Sundays in 1973 (as a
\par
result of the Arab oil embargo) vehicles powered by LPG and diesel fuels were
\par
exempt. This made the public suddenly much more aware of the existence of such
\par
fuels. Added to that, the fact that LPG is not taxed in the Netherlands
\par
ensured a rapid penetration of LPG powered passenger cars. A loss of 10\% in
\par
kilometres driven on gasoline over just 5 years is a very fast reaction indeed
\par
(See Fig. 5).
\par
Finally, one can examine the expenditure on fuels as a percentage of total
\par
expenditure. After all, if the price of a commodity doubles and your income
\par
- 356 -
\par
doubles too (all in real term of course) then you are not all that much worse off,
\par
in fact, none at all. Plotting expenditure on oil products as \%of GDP, CPE, and
\par
Manufacturing Output in the EEC (See Fig. 6), reveals a surprising tendency
\par
"back towards the norm", (a similar tendency can be observed in the US). If it
\par
were indeed true that there are certain universal percentage ranges that consumers
\par
of different income levels tend to spend on certain categories of fuel
\par
consumption, then the corollary would be that the long term price elasticity of
\par
oil products is one, and that "long term" in this sense would mean only some five
\par
years or so.
\par
The above observations, (together with all the little signs in the
\par
newspapers) have led the authors to the notion that over the next five years,
\par
simply due to the fact the "the consumer will get the message", the world could
\par
be in for a surprise on oil consumption. Rather than an inelastic demand that
\par
grinds inexorably into the inevitable medium term oil supply constraints, a
\par
scenario could be foreseen in which the world finds itself (rather to its own
\par
surprise) in a situation where the calIon OPEC oil has fallen below 25 million
\par
barrels per day by 1985, whereas WOCA GDP still has managed an average growth of
\par
some p.a. (See Fig 7 and Fig. 8).
\par
Although this scenario still has all the chances of sliding into a cr1S1S
\par
situation at any time over the next five years, it is interesting to note that
\par
it presents a plausable solution to the current dangerous situation (by
\par
allowing Saudi Arabia, for instance, to come down to more comfortable levels of
\par
production), and that the makings of this solution are, unbeknownst, already
\par
emerging all around us. A careful monitoring of oil/GDP over 1980/81 will be a
\par
rewarding task for virtually all policy makers.
\par
PART II THE COSTING OF ALTERNATIVE ENERGY SUPPLIES
\par
In terms of energy resources substitution (i.e., coal gasification,
\par
liquefaction etc.), the result of the 1973 oil crisis was anything but a crash
\par
program in the OECD countries. EEC expenditures for research in this field
\par
increased by 66\% between 1974 (which was budgeted before the crisis) and 1976.
\par
While this is not much in relative terms as a reaction to the tripling of crude
\par
oil prices, the absolute figure of less than million in 1976 demonstrates
\par
even more the lack of significant response. Overall expenditure on energy R\&D
\par
went up by 40\% in the same period (these figures are not deflated:). Half of
\par
this figure went into nuclear energy, an energy conversion system which was well
\par
into its disappointment scenario.
\par
US government energy R\&D exceeded that of EEC member states from 1976
\par
onwards. Between 1975 and 1978, expenditures doubled. Industry figures are
\par
available only to 1976, at which time they amounted to half of government
\par
expenditures. Even with the above increases it must be realized that total
\par
energy R\&D expenditures amounted in recent years to no more than money spent
\par
on the space program, and one quarter of defence R\&D costs.
\par
A number of factors specific to the development of new energy resources and
\par
conversion systems and to their environment have contributed to the 'much-talk,
\par
little action' situation prevailing between 1975-79 in the three regions:
\par
USA, Europe, Japan.
\par
1. Oil prices remained
\par
for the time being.
\par
declined in terms of
\par
indigenous crude oil
\par
stable in dollar terms and supplies appeared sufficient
\par
For Japan and some European countries, prices actually
\par
local currencies. In the USA, price controls on
\par
dampened the effect of OPEC price rises.
\par
- 357 -
\par
2. The technology necessary was, with few exceptions, basically known - although
\par
(as in coal conversions) it had lain dormant for decades. In reality, very
\par
few people had actual experience with them. This led to underestimation of
\par
costs in time, money and manpower needed to establish viable modern
\par
processes.
\par
3. Classically, the system size of a new technology serving a new (self-created)
\par
market grows in parallel to the growth of that particular market. The
\par
situation concerning energy conversion systems (coal, gas,geothermal) is
\par
different in that they will supply existing markets. Therefore, after
\par
having gone through the development stages of pilot, demonstration and/or
\par
pioneer plant, they are likely to arrive at their ultimate size more or less
\par
immediately on commercialization.
\par
The very large initial size and cost of installations for a new technology
\par
leads undoubtedly to hesitation in decision-making. This hesitation is
\par
exacerbated by an unusual degree of uncertainty, due to the unprecendented
\par
influence of politics on this particular market.
\par
4. Most importantly, cost estimates of alternatives appear to float upwards
\par
over time ahead of conventional energy costs (See Fig. 10 and 11), while
\par
individual cost estimates reckon with a fixed cost (See Fig. 9).
\par
The present situation for coal gasification and liquefaction in particular
\par
shows, for the reasons given above, overruns on lead time and cost
\par
estimates.
\par
Rand Corp., in a report to the US Dept. of Energy (August 1979), assesses
\par
the cost of a 'first-of-a-kind' energy process plant at times initial
\par
cost estimates. Not the least of reasons for optimistic cost estimates.is
\par
to attract money for a project - particularly when external funds are sought.
\par
Nevertheless, it is wrong to expect the cost trend to continue into the
\par
future: as the body of know-hOW has accumulated over the years, the date of
\par
possible innovation has come nearer and areas of uncertainty have shrunk, so
\par
the growth in costs has receded in newer estimates. The Iranian supply
\par
short-fall and subsequent price rises have led to a flurry of activities
\par
which are reminiscent of the oil crisis of 1973. However, given recent
\par
development work - and after deduction of rhetoric - a good deal more is
\par
now expected to be converted into actual hardware-and with reduced lead
\par
timee-than in the period 1974-1978.
\par
We have already alluded to the escalation of cost estimates for alternative
\par
hydrocarbon fuels over time. In addition, there is a myriad of sources of cost
\par
estimates, differing in basic assumptions, so that a 'marker' cost in terms of a
\par
league table is nearly meaningless. Moreover, factors other than purely economic
\par
considerations playa decisive role in determing which feedstock to use in what
\par
process to produce which products.
\par
Necessary briefness leads in the following analysis unavoidably to superficiality
\par
and incompleteness:.
\par
Fig. 9 (for coal-derived liquids) exemplifies the conventional approach to costs
\par
of alternative energies - i.e. a time-independant level of costs versus an
\par
increasing price of imported/OPEC crude, leading to a breakeven point.
\par
Unease about the reality of escalating cost estimates has led the US Department of
\par
Energy to commission studies investigating the underlying reasons, and to
\par
attempts by cost engineers to include factors perceived by them to contribute to
\par
- 358 -
\par
the escalation. The conclusions reached can best be divided into factors external
\par
and internal to the particular estimate.
\par
External factors include:
\par
Change of scope and objective.
\par
Post-facto changes of governmental rules.
\par
Management by committee, especially where non-economic actors are involved
\par
(government).
\par
Internal factors are:
\par
Deliberate underestimation in order to procure funds.
\par
Insufficient project and process contingencies.
\par
'Static' estimation.
\par
The conventional approach (static estimation) to project evaluation using
\par
discounted cash-flow (DCF) calculation can be briefly typified as consisting of:
\par
an assessment of process parameters and their uncertainties, resulting in a total
\par
erected cost; and an assumption of feedstock and other operating costs. A
\par
required DCF return is then used to calculate sales revenue, i.e. product values -
\par
the latter being cited as the cost of a "barrel of oil equivalent". In order to
\par
avoid underestimation of future costs, use has occasionally been made of a
\par
general inflation factor (e.g. a.a.i.) for all costs and for sales revenue.
\par
However, since costs are smaller than sales revenue, this only boosts cash-flow
\par
artificially and thus the resultant DCF return. A better approach is where
\par
capital costs of a future plant are escalated along past cost index curves.
\par
However, this has not been applied to operating costs and sales revenue, and
\par
furthermore it is a purely mechanical trend extrapolation.
\par
Instead, this paper suggests the use of scenarios for such cost estimates.
\par
In such a "scenario dependent project evaluation", historic trend lines for costs
\par
and prices are modified to be consistent with a particular scenario.
\par
To demonstrate the principle, a coal liquefaction study was used evaluating
\par
the supercritical gas extraction process for a us site. Without giving
\par
quantitative data we will briefly describe the methodology.
\par
For simplicity's sake the scenario-dependent variables can be lumped
\par
together under 4 headings:
\par
Total Erected Cost
\par
Feedstock Cost
\par
Operating Cost
\par
Product Values
\par
Total erected cost was assumed to follow the Chemical Plant Index (Chemical
\par
Engineering, continously updated). Between 1970 and 1978, this index seemed to
\par
follow the GDP in an anti-cyclical fashion. It can be argued that this anti-
\par
cyclical mode is in reality a time-lag of half a business cycle, which
\par
coincides with the 2-3 years between an investment decision and actual conclusion
\par
- 359 -
\par
of contracts and equipment orders. Over the longer term, low economic growth
\par
results in higher unit prices due to a lack of innovative investment.
\par
Feedstock (coal) Costs can be assumed to follow oil prices, as they have
\par
over the period of 1970-8. Other Operating Costs, mainly labour, can be
\par
expected to follow the general GDP/cap increases. Finally, Product Values can be
\par
linked to the oil price index. The resultant cash-flows are then used to
\par
determine the DCF returns.
\par
Our findings were that the static approach can overestimate the profitability
\par
of a venture considerably (15\% rate of return versus 2-9\% depending on plausable
\par
scenario assumptions).
\par
SUMMARY
\par
Scenario Dependent Project Evaluation
\par
