WORLD FOSSIL RESOURCES: AVAILABILITY
\par
AND CONSTRAINTS
\par
M. Grenon
\par
International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis, Laxenburg, Austria
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
World oil resources assessments lack, to a certain extent, scientific foundation
\par
inasmuch as the methods used and data utilized are most often not disclosed. The
\par
changing prices also call for an urgent reappraisal of world oil resources. The
\par
situation is the same, or even worse, for unconventional oil resources. These must
\par
be examined not only with regard to their potential occurrence in the ground, but
\par
also in connection with their systems interactions with other natural resources,
\par
which may constrain future availability.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Oil; fossil; resources; unconventional oil; WELMM.
\par
The growing awareness of the importance and irreplaceability of liquid fuels in the
\par
next four or five decades leads us to concentrate especially on these in this short
\par
review. In fact, part of our comments could also be applied to gaseous fuels and,
\par
partially, to solid fuels.
\par
Fast increasing prices of energy, and especially of oil, in the world market since
\par
1973 anticipate, in reality, the irreversible trend towards higher and higher
\par
energy production costs. Briefly st3ted, man has generally first, and sometimes at
\par
an unprecedented speed, exploited his cheapest fuels (and mineral resources) after
\par
the end of World War II. This trend is illustrated for the liquid fuels by what
\par
we have called the "three paths to costly oil".
\par
Concerning conventional oil, the author has recently pointed out that there are,
\par
unfortunately, no reliable and scientific estimates of world energy resources
\par
(Grenon, 1979). Few of the estimates which have been published are really indep-
\par
endent. Most of them do not disclose the method which was used, and still fewer
\par
the data which was utilized to obtain the results. A somewhat "magic" figure of
\par
2000.109 bbl (or about 300 billion tonnes) has attracted some "consensus", which,
\par
in fact, hardly stands up to a close examination. Moreover, among the assumptions
