\par
exploit their huge oil shale reserves (apart, maybe, from the special case of
\par
Brazil), although present oil prices could, in our opinion, justify at least the
\par
exploration and/or initiation of such developments.
\par
Important characteristics of these unconventional oil resources are the growing
\par
interaction, in the phase of extraction (and possibly upgrading) with other natural
\par
resources, especially water and land; the huge extent of their exploitation, inv-
\par
olving major problems of materials handling; and their large manpower requirements.
\par
This means that, although these new energy resources are very large indeed, they
\par
appear as being possibly constrained by the availability of the other natural (or
\par
human) resources, and by the impacts on these other resources.
\par
To better understand these constraints, as well as to bypass some difficulties of
\par
economic forecasting in a long term perspective, we have developed at IIASA a new
\par
systems approach, WELMM (standing for Water, Energy, Land, Materials and Manpower)3
\par
This approach assesses the WELMM resources requirements for various energy strat-
\par
egies, and analyzes the relationships (or systems aspects) between these different
\par
resources.
