\par
The development in the Athabasca Oil Sands, in Alberta, Canada, provides a good
\par
example of the requirements of a big unconventional oil program, in this case a
\par
major tar sands project. Alsands, a new project led by the Shell group, proposes
\par
to establish a \$4.9 billion oil sands mining plant near the centre of the Athabasca
\par
deposit. The 140,000 barrel-per-day capacity facility, scheduled for operation in
\par
1986, would extract 1.25 billion barrels of bitumen over a period of 25 years. The
\par
quantity of materials handled could amount to 2.50 to 3 billion tons over the same
\par
period. The mining, upgrading and support facilities would disturb an area of
\par
almost 20,000 acres of wildland. The plant, which would use the hot-water separa-
\par
tion process, would extract up to 1,500,000 barrels of water from the Athabasca
\par
River daily, which would amount to about 2.8\% of the minimum winter flow. (Inci-
\par
dentally, this is a strong motivation for a major change in technology.) To pro-
\par
duce about 1 million barrels per day, seven similar plants would be required
\par
(Grenon, GrUbler, Merzeau, 1979).
\par
Such a plant would also involve 7000 to 8000 construction workers, which could
\par
mean a local population of 20,000 people in regions normally scarcely populated.
\par
In addition to public opposition to such a dramatic change, this population would
\par
also induce indirect WELMM requirements (drinking water, housing materials, energy
\par
facilities, etc.), the future of which would have to be planned for the time when
\par
the exploitation of the tar sands declines, etc ....
\par
The availability of water is already proving to be a bottleneck for coal or oil
\par
shale development in Colorado.
\par
CONCLUSIONS
\par
Growing constraints may limit the future availability of energy resources. Our
\par
studies point to a definite need for big improvements in technology, particularly
\par
with regard to in situ developments. so as to decrease environmental impacts on
\par
the other natural resources.
