DIVERGENCIES OF MARKET AND OPTIMUM
\par
PRICES FOR ENERGY RESOURCES
\par
H. Uzawa
\par
Faculty of Economics, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan
\par
Introduction
\par
In this paper, I should like to discuss some of the more important factors which
\par
account for the divergence of market and optimum prices for energy resources and
\par
their implications to the allocative mechanism in a market economy. Market
\par
prices are established at those levels at which demand for and supply of scarce
\par
resources are equated at each moment of time, while optimum prices are those
\par
which result in an optimum time pattern of distribution and allocation of scarce
\par
resources, the concept of the optimum being defined in terms of an intertemporal
\par
preference ordering from a social point of view.
\par
It is one of the main propositions in the neoclassical theory of welfare economics
\par
that market and optimum prices are identical, provided that scarce resources are
\par
all malleable, the period of production is zero, ,there exist neither internal
\par
nor external (dis-) economies, in the process of production, competition is
\par
perfect, and perfect foresight prevails.
\par
In an actual economy, whether a purely capitalist economy or a mixed, none of
\par
these qualifying assumptions are fullfilled. In particular, the malleability of
\par
scarce resources, the absence of external (dis-) economies, and the perfect
\par
foresight hypothesis are hardly satisfied even in a purely hypothesised economic
\par
model.
\par
The divergence of market and optimum allocations has particularly important
\par
implications for the situation where energy resources are mainly derived from
\par
crude oil and other non-renewable resources and are distributed unevenly among
\par
the nations or the regions. In this paper, I should like to examine in theoretical
\par
terms the circumstance under which such divergence occurs and single out the
\par
factors which account for it.
\par
The Optimality of Market Allocation
\par
It is the salient feature of a market economy that, while each economic unit,
\par
either a business enterprise or a household, seeks the pattern of economic
\par
behaviour which is optimum from his own private point of view, the resulting
\par
- 379 -
\par
- 380 -
\par
allocation of scarce resources is optimum from the social point of view. Indeed,
\par
it has become one of the basic propositions in the neoclassical theory of welfare
\par
economics to establish the conditions under which the optimality of market
\par
allocation, prevails. However, a number of qualifying assumptions concerning
\par
the institutinal premises of the market economy have to be made in order to
\par
prove the optimality of market allocation. Before I proceed with the discussion
\par
of the main theme, I should like to examine some of the more crucial conditions
\par
accountable for the identity of private and social optima.
\par
The neoclassical theory of welfare economics has focussed its attention upon a
\par
market economy in which all the scarce resources that are limitational to economic
\par
activities are privately appropriated and transacted in the market. The assumption
\par
of private ownership, which has been regarded as a crucial element in the insti-
\par
tutional framework of capitalism, precludes the existence of the class of scarce
\par
resources usually termed as social overhead capital. In most of contemporary
\par
capitalistic societies, even in its pure form, a significant portion of scarce
\par
means of production are not privately appropriated and are managed by the society
\par
as social overhead capital. Those scarce resources which are classified as
\par
social overhead capital are either produced collectively by the society, as is
\par
the case with highways, bridges, harbour, etc., or simply endowed within the
\par
society, as in the case of air, water, soil, etc. The optimality of market
\par
allocation crucially hinges upon the institutional premises of the private
\par
ownership of all scarce means of production. Otherwise, a complicated set of
\par
administrative and economic measures have to be introduced concerning the manage-
\par
ment of social overhead capital to ensure the optimality of market allocation
\par
(see, e.g., Uzawa [1,2]). This point has particularly importance bearings upon
\par
the present discuss'ion where both energy production and consumption require the
\par
services to be derived from social overhead capital and at the same time have
\par
implications upon the institutional arrangements concerning the private ownership
\par
10f means of production.
\par
INext I should like to focus my attention upon the assumption of malleability of
\par
Imeans of production which are indispensable to the neoclassical theory. It is
\par
lassumed that each economic unit can change the way it is using factors of production
\par
laccording to the conditions then prevailing in the market, and that neither time
\par
I nor additional costs is required to change the manner by which they are used in
\par
!the processes of production. This assumption of malleability, which is crucial
\par
iin the neoclassical proposition, implies that the time required in production
\par
processes is zero.
\par
- 381 -
\par
The assumption of malleability of productive factors seems to have particularly
\par
important implications when we deal with the problems concerning energy resources.
\par
The nature of equipments and machineries in the productive processes and the
\par
mode of consumption are determined by the type of energy resources to be employed
\par
and it is almost impossible to change them even if they become unsuitable either
\par
due to the changes in the market conditions or in social circumstances.
\par
The third assumption made in the neoclassical theory which is crucial in the
\par
present discussion concerns itself with the assumption of market equilibrium.
\par
Namely, it has been assumed that, at each moment of time, the price system under
\par
which demand and supply are equated for all goods and services is established in
\par
the economy. Indeed, under the assumptions of malleability and individual
\par
rational behaviour, the situation of market disequilibrium never occurs, since
\par
adjustments by individual economic units and markets are made instantaneously to
\par
reach a market equilibrium.
\par
While the neoclassical theory has been formulated under such unrealistic assumptions
\par
concerning institutional arrangements and individual behaviour, the alternative
\par
approach which would be free from these deficiencies has been not yet developed.
\par
In the present paper, I should like to present a simple model of energy resources
\par
where some of the points raised above may be partly taken care of.
