\par
Introduction
\par
In the world oil market the price of one crude oil is related to the prices of all
\par
others. There is a complex logic which defines the relationship between the prices
\par
of different crudes and between the overall price of crude oil and other energy
\par
sources. The fundamental nature of the world energy system is determined by its
\par
having, at equilibrium, only one degree of freedom - it is unidimensional in price
\par
(see reference). With one price fixed (the price of the 'marker' fuel) all others
\par
are determined through the various complex mechanisms that link different enerey
\par
sources.
\par
At present this marker fuel is Saudi Arabian light crude oil. In the 1950's it was
\par
Iran's Agha Jara; in the 1960's it was Kuwait's crude. Far into the future it will
\par
not be crude but an alternative energy source.
\par
The amount of any energy source being produced will depend upon its price. A rise
\par
in the price of the marker crude would stimulate demand for alternatives and lift-
\par
ings of crude oil would be reduced. At an extremely hieh price, production of
\par
alternative energy, say synthetic crude oil from coal, would be stimulated to such
\par
an extent as to replace completely imports of crude oil into a particular country.
\par
On the other hand, if the price of the marker crude is set very low, say near cost,
\par
there will be no economic incentive to produce any alternative form of energy. In
\par
this case liftings of oil will be maximised, but revenue will be minimal.
\par
Between these two extremes, there is an optimum price which will result in maximum
\par
revenue (net of cost) accruing to the producer. WEML's long-range World Energy
\par
Model will determine this optimum price. The logical reasoning behind the Model
\par
can be understood by considering the mechanics of inter-fuel substitution in a
\par
highly simplified case.
\par
Inter-fuel substitution
\par
Why do consumers choose, for example, oil rather than coal; or substitute natural
\par
gas instead of oil? What factors control the choice and what would cause them to
\par
change?
\par
- 387 -
\par
- 388 -
\par
Let us assume a closed, isolated energy system (i.e. one with no imports or exports)
\par
with only two energy sources:
\par
crude oil, and
\par
coal
\par
Reserves of both are adequate to meet demand, but the crude is the cheaper to
\par
produce. In this closed system there is a demand for secondary energy:
\par
20 tons of motor gasoline
\par
30 tons of kerosene
\par
50 tons of heavy fuel oil
\par
All the heavy fuel oil is burnt to generate electricity. Only three processing
\par
routes are available (figure I) to achieve this mix: one using oil only; one part
\par
oil and part coal; and the third all coal.
\par
Within this system the government encourages the energy industries to choose the
\par
processing route which the total energy bill. The producer of crude can
\par
determine which route the energy industries will select by his pricing policy, as
\par
we shall see.
\par
Considering Alternatives I and II, the consumer will choose whichever is the lower
\par
bill of
\par
110 x Price of crude + \$110
\par
and 70 x Price of crude + 70 x Price of Coal + \$900
\par
If both bills were the same, the consumer would be indifferent as to choice of
\par
alternative routes. In this case:
\par
110 x Price of crude + \$110 Price of crude 70 x Price of crude +
\par
70 x Price of coal + \$900
\par
1.75 x Price of coal + \$19.75
\par
The coal producer cannot lower his price below cost, so to ensure that the consumers
\par
follow Alternative I, the crude producer would set his price just below Price A
\par
where Price A = 1.75 x Cost of coal + \$19.75
\par
If the crude producer wished Alternative II to be chosen, he would put his price
\par
above Price A. The question is: how much above? If he sets the price too high,
\par
Alternative III will be chosen and he will have no crude production. Alternative II
\par
will be preferred to Alternative III, when the consumer's bill for II is just below
\par
III:
\par
70 x Price of crude + 70 x Price of coal + \$900
\par
{$<$}320 x Price of coal + \$6450
\par
or price of crude {$<$}3.5 Price of coal + \$79.30
\par
Since the crude producer wishes to ensure that Alternative II is followed he would
\par
set his price just below the cost of coal, stifling competition from coal:
\par
Price of crude must be slightly less than
\par
Price B = 3.5 x Cost of coal + \$79.30
\par
The crude producer therefore has two alternative policies:
\par
Price A: to price crude at 1.75 x cost of coal + \$\textbackslash 9.75,
\par
in which case he sells 110 tons of crude
\par
Price B:
\par
to price crude at 3.5 x cost of coal + \$79.30,
\par
in which case he sells 70 tons of crude.
\par
Being a rational person, he will select that pricing policy which maximises his
\par
net revenue, i.e. the larger of:
\par
110 (1.75 Cost of coal + \$19.75 - cost of crude)
\par
or 70 (3.5 Cost of coal + \$79.30 - cost of crude)
\par
- 389 -
\par
This situation can be shown graphically as in Figure Z, where the price of crude is
\par
plotted (horizontally) against the volume of crude lifted (vertically).
\par
For any price up to PA, 110 tons of crude are produced. Obviously, the maximum
\par
revenue occurs when the price is at its limit (1.75 cost of coal + \$19.75). At any
\par
price between PA and PB, 70 tons of crude are produced. The maximum revenue occurs
\par
when the price is set at the upper limit. The production of an alternative energy
\par
source is triggered at its substitution price.
\par
This simple closed system illustrates the principles and the logic of the argument.
\par
The real world is, of course, much more because:
\par
there are numerous alternative energy sources
\par
extraction costs vary from place to place
\par
relative geographical locations of the source and the consumer have to be
\par
considered
\par
there are many proven conversion processes
\par
there are many crude oil sources
\par
environmental laws apply
\par
political considerations overlay the techno-economic factors.
\par
All these parameters have to be considered in analysing the real world. And our
\par
highly simplified illustration has shown the importance of refinery costs in the
\par
determination of PA and PB{$\bullet$}
\par
The real-world system is so highly interactive that only by using the World Energy
\par
Model can meaningful answers be obtained.
\par
The results from the model can be represented graphically as shown in Figure 3,
\par
plotting price against volume of crude lifted.
\par
At a price PI, it is just profitable for the cheapest alternative to be produced
\par
in volume equivalent to (VI - VZ). At PZ, the second cheapest alternative is
\par
produced in volume equivalent to (VZ - V3) and so on. The Model's solutions give
\par
full details of these alternatives at each discontinuity.
\par
Having the price elasticity 'curve' for the marker crude, we know the revenue at
\par
each discontinuity, e.g. PIVI, PZVZ' P3V3 and so on. These can be plotted against
\par
the price of crude (PJ, PZ, P3, etc), the highest peak occurring when the price
\par
maximises revenue to the producer as shown in Figure 4. (In practice, net revenue
\par
is plotted.)
\par
It is fortunate that this price the net revenue to the producer and
\par
minimises the total fuel bill to the consumer in the long run. If crude oil is in
\par
short supply it should get its substitution value (see later).
\par
To summarise, the marginal energy source at any time (currently the crude oil, Saudi
\par
Arabian Light) has an upper and a lower price limit. The lower price corresponds
\par
to its cost (currently estimated at about 10.15 t per barrel for Saudi Arabian
\par
Light). Should the price fall below its cost, no oil would be lifted. Should the
\par
price rise above that of an alternative marginal source (syncrude, tar sands or
\par
whatever) then in due course, when sufficient of the alternative is being made, the
\par
production of the original marginal source would cease. Thus the realistic long-
\par
term price of light Arabian crude lies between these two very wide limits. The
\par
energy analyst's problem is to decide what will be the next marginal source of
\par
energy. One price in the energy system - that of the marginal source - determines
\par
all other prices.
\par
- 390 -
\par
Natural gas
\par
Natural gas is a strong candidate to be the next marginal energy source in the
\par
medium term, bridging the gap until other forms of energy can be developed. Its
\par
attractions are twofold:
\par
1. It is available in substantial quantities in many geographical regions
\par
(see Table I).
\par
2. It can be transported through pipelines, or as Liquefied Natural Gas, or it can
\par
be converted into 'methyl fuel' through catalytic reaction with steam, Methyl
\par
fuel is an approximately 2/3 to 1/3 mixture of methanol (methyl alcohol, CH30H)
\par
and higher alcohols, particularly iso-butanol.
\par
Reserves of natural gas have not been so rigorously proved as reserves of Middle
\par
East crude oil. However, present proven exportable reserves of gas are equivalent
\par
to two thirds of Middle East crude oil reserves and they are widespread.
\par
The relationship between the price of crude oil and natural gas is quite complex.
\par
One possible relationship in the international market is via substitution of No 2
\par
furnace fuel oil by 'methyl fuel' as a burning fuel provided they are at the same
\par
BTU equivalent price.
\par
