\par
The case for blending methyl fuel with motor spirit
\par
There is now a considerable body of practical evidence to support the additio
\par
up to 30\% methyl fuel to motor spirit. Although pure methanol does not blend
\par
- 391 -
\par
with hydrocarbons, methyl fuel does. It has a high octane rating and is volatile.
\par
In traditional gasoline blends the straight-run front end components, which are
\par
good petrochemical feedstock have low octane ratings and require the addition of
\par
lead to raise the octane number to acceptable levels. A methyl fuel/hydrocarbon
\par
blend does not require lead, and this should reduce the necessity for constructing
\par
further octane-improving and cracking plants.
\par
Methyl fuel gives more miles BTU than pure hydrocarbon mixtures. Extensive bench
\par
tests with a British Leyland Marina engine show that a 15\% blend of methyl fuel,
\par
whilst improving power, gives about the need for adjustment of the engine. Even
\par
a 30\% mixture, after optimum setting of carburet tor and ignition tuning, reduces
\par
miles/gallon by only 8\%. In the event that constraints are placed on the addition
\par
of methyl fuel to motor spirit, methyl fuel could be substituted for furnace fuel
\par
oil, again giving gas a higher value.
\par
Methyl fuel vs LNG
\par
There are, in the literature, many calculations of the relative merits of LNG and
\par
methyl fuel as means of transporting large volumes of natural gas over large
\par
distances. But they suffer from one or more of the following defects:
\par
1. It is assumed that the price of gas is a necessary input to the calculation,
\par
and since this price can only be guessed at, the comparison is necessarily
\par
imprecise.
\par
2. The possibility is ignored that the optimal supply routes for LNG and methyl
\par
fuel might be very different.
\par
3. The comparisons are carried out on a calorific value basis.
\par
The price of LNG or methyl fuel is fixed - at equilibrium - by the price of the oil
\par
products for which they are substituting (the energy system is unidimensional in
\par
price); the price of gas is that price less the prices charged for conversiun and
\par
transport.
\par
With regard to supply patterns, either the LNG or the methyl fuel route might be
\par
favoured in particular circumstances. For example, methyl fuel manufactured in
\par
Algeria might be absorbed in Europe and the oil products which it displaces exported
\par
to the USA, thus saving on shipping costs per BTU. Such a route might not be
\par
available for LNG in view of the indigenous gas production in Europe and the
\par
possibilities of importing pipeline gas from the USSR. WEML's model, with its
\par
representations of alternative supply possibilities and local demand patterns, per-
\par
mits us to analyse these complex interactions.
\par
The WEML Model
\par
The Model is a complex representation of the international oil and gas industry,
\par
based on linear programming to determine the least-cost strategy to meet a specified
\par
world demand, a technique used widely in the petroleum industry (see "Understanding
\par
Energy: a rational approach to Energy Planning" for a fuller description).
\par
Refining and marketing are represented as taking place in 22 locations throughout
\par
the world, crude oil production at 25 (more than 50 crudes are taken into account)
\par
and natural gas production at 19 locations. Oil and liquefied-naturai-gas transport
\par
is included using eight categories of ships and about 30 major pipelines are
\par
considered.
\par
Natural gas from the 19 locations is considered to be transported by one of the
\par
following routes:
\par
- 392 -
\par
by pipeline to meet local gas demand and to exports
\par
by exporting as LNG, which is regasified to meet a distant gas demand
\par
by conversion to methyl fuel, which may either be used locally or exported for
\par
use as a component of motor spirit, or as a substitute for middle distillates
\par
and fuel oils.
\par
The operating costs of these facilities and the investment costs pertaining to their
\par
expansion are supplied as input data to the model.
\par
Using the reserves figures in Table I, a reserves/production ratio of 25: I was
\par
assumed. A price was chosen for light Arabian which was high enough to cause its
\par
production rate to fall to zero - an exaggerated and unrealistic case which could
\par
then be moved away from or towards a meaningful value.
\par
With a stated future demand for petroleum, the model will determine the theoretical
\par
programme (in capacity and capital expenditure) for refinery construction worldwide,
\par
giving details of the type, size and location of plant, the optimal crude supply
\par
pattern and the necessary tanker construction by size category, to meet this demand.
\par
With a specified price for light Arabian crude - the current marker crude -
\par
equilibrium prices for all other crudes and products and for natural gas at each
\par
location are determined. The model system also determines the cost of harbour and
\par
pipeline constraints and the equilibrium spot charter rate of vessels by class
\par
category. The results from running this model are given in Tables 3 and 6. This
\par
shows a time in the future when demand has increased and when oil refinery plant
\par
and tankers would need to be built if the methanol route were not used. With the
\par
high price chosen for light Arabian, its production falls to zero. Refinery
\par
capacity unused leaps to 35-40\%: none is built. LNG is made in competition with
\par
methyl fuel up to the existing capacity of LNG tankers: no further tankers are
\par
constructed. Oil tanker construction is non-existent in this case and some 19 m dwt
\par
of 25,000 ton vessels are left idle - stemming from the relative closeness of methyl
\par
fuel to the market. Some 640 mta of methyl fuel is made in eight different
\par
locations, 470 mta being used as burning fuels. All motor spirit worldwide is at
\par
maximum methyl fuel content.
\par
Although this is not a realistic solution, since a cutback in OPEC production would
\par
never be allowed to fall entirely on one member, it gives us the starting point from
\par
which we can move towards the optimum price of light Arabian crude.
\par
By reducing the price of this crude in stages, and running the model, natural gas
\par
liftings are found to decrease and crude liftings increase. The revenue to the
\par
crude producer, volume lifted multiplied by price, follows a saw-tooth curve passing
\par
through a global maximum (see Figures 5 and 6). At this point, the producer's
\par
revenue is maximised and the consumer's total cost minimised. If the producer sets
\par
his price too low, the alternative sources will not be developed. The industry will
\par
build too much conventional refining and shipping facilities. Since alternatives
\par
take time to build and commission, thete is a period in which the producer has no
\par
competitive alternative sources available in significant volumes, and therefore no
\par
upper limit to his price. As he capitalises on this situation, the consumer's bill
\par
increases and previously uneconomic alternatives are developed, and slack appears
\par
in crude refining and shipping capacities reflecting previous over-building.
\par
This single demonstration gives the flavour of the technique and the logic behind
\par
it. To give a definitive price, further runs are to cover a range of
\par
scenarios including variations in:
\par
possible OPEC prorationing schemes
\par
gas production rates and potential discoveries
\par
gas processing costs (LNG and methyl spirit plants, pipelines)
\par
demand assumptions
\par
- 393 -
\par
for the longer term other forms of energy (coal, shale oil, nuclear etc.) have
\par
to be included
\par
discounting over time (the current model is static).
\par
Conclusions
\par
It is in the best long-term interest of both the producer and the consumer of oil
\par
that an economic price be established for crude oil. The producer should wish to
\par
avoid risking lower future revenues which would result from competition from
\par
substitution by other energy sources which would become relatively economic at
\par
higher prices. The consumer will wish to avoid being forced into uneconomic capital
\par
expenditure connected with these energy substitutions. WEML's model can help in
\par
the analysis of possible future consequences of today's political decisions.
\par
It is possible that in the future a monopoly of gas producers (OGEC?) could replace
\par
the existing monopoly of crude oil producers. But this situation could only arise
\par
when energy demand has increased to the point where the crude producer's maximum
\par
revenue corresponds to the physical limits of production of the marginal source.
\par
The price of gas would then be subject to lower and upper limits - the cost of
\par
production and the cost of the next alternative energy source - in the same way as
\par
crude is now. The next alternative might then be coal, tar sands, shale oil,
\par
nuclear power, or some as yet undiscovered form of energy.
