APPLICATION OF GENERALIZED TRANS-
\par
SHIPMENT AND INTEGER PROGRAMMING
\par
ALGORITHMS IN REGIONAL ENERGY
\par
PLANNING MODELS
\par
J. Debanne
\par
Faculty ofAdministration, University of Ottawa, Canada
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
Regional energy policy and planning models can be efficiently represented and solved
\par
using two constructs: a generalized network to represent and simultaneously solve for
\par
supply, distribution, transformation and demand of energy in every region and time
\par
period considered in the planning study; and a binary integer program to represent
\par
and solve for all discrete variables relevant to the planning study, such as invest-
\par
ment and technology options, subject to investment sequencing constraints as well as
\par
other constraints that cannot be represented in a network cpnstruct.
\par
The global optimization algorithm alternately generates investment and technologi-
\par
cal option plans with the binary program; then evaluates these plans with the gene-
\par
ralized network optimization algorithm. This network optimization produces a total
\par
discounted cost for the plan as well as activity levels and opportunity costs. These
\par
activity levels, opportunity costs as well as the total plan cost are in turn used
\par
to set up the integer program in order to produce an improved investment plan, and
\par
so on {$\bullet\bullet\bullet$} until no further reduction in the total discovered cost can be achieved.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Programming, Integer Programming, Generalized Networks, Generalized
\par
Trans-shipment, Decomposition Algorithm, Energy Modelling, Regional Modelling, Multi-
\par
Period Models. Energy Planning, Binary integer programming, zero-one programming.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
Network flow models to solve single period, regional energy supply-distribution pro-
\par
blems for oil (Debanne 1969, 1971), natural gas (Brooks, 1976), oil and natural gas
\par
(Debanne, 1975), oil, natural gas and coal (Debanne,1977), oil, natural gas, coal,
\par
syncrude, nuclear and renewable power (Debanne 1979, 1980) use either standard trans-
\par
shipment network optimization algorithms or generalized network algorithms. Multi-
\par
period aspects of the problem at hand, such as expansion of facilities or the choice
\par
of technological options are solved for "myopically" in all above models, using the
\par
minimum cost criterion at the period or time step when a particular investment or
\par
option becomes economical to introduce, without regard for conditions prevailing in
\par
subsequent time steps. A demonstration pilot energy model featuring optimization
\par
over a multi-period planning horizon was developed (Debanne, 1976) in which integer
\par
variables are solved for by a specially designed "generalized" network flow optimi-
\par
zation algorithm. This technique proved to be inefficient for large problems and
\par
- 401 -
\par
- 402 -
\par
proved to be too restrictive in terms of integer programming formulation.
\par
Progress in the field of decomposition techniques, (Benders, 1962), has led to the
\par
development of single period optimization models (Geoffrion 1974, 1979) in which
\par
that part of the problem featuring the 0-1 variables, i.e. the investment options
\par
and corresponding fixed charges, are solved by an integer programming code, while
\par
the trans-shipment part of the problem is solved for with a standard network opti-
\par
mization code. One of the most successful applications of this decomposition scheme
\par
is a management support system currently on stream at General Foods Inc. and other
\par
enterprises. (Geoffrion, 1979) The efficiency of the solution algorithm combined
\par
with a well designed supporting data base, report generator and problem formulation
\par
package has considerably enhanced the utility of this application as a planning and
\par
decision aid for production and distribution managers of multi-product, multi-plant,
\par
multi-customer operations such as General Foods. This particular application of
\par
Benders' decomposition algorithm (1962) disconnects the trans-shipment portion of
\par
the problem into as many subnetworks as there are commodities to store (or manufac-
\par
ture) in the various warehouses (or plants) and allocates these commodities to the
\par
various consumption centers. Expansion and investment options are solved for with
\par
a binary integer program for the period of interest. This application imposes how-
\par
ever a "single sourcing constraint", whereby a consumption center is supplied with
\par
all products consumed at this center, from one and only one warehouse or plant.
\par
Adaptation of Geoffrion's management support system to regional energy planning
\par
models could not however be implemented despite the strong similarities between a
\par
food products supply-distribution operation and an energy supply-distribution system.
\par
The main difficulty is the single sourcing constraint which is the cornerstone of
\par
Geoffrion's decomposition algorithm and which definitely does not apply in energy
\par
supply-distribution systems. For example, oil imported to the United States or
\par
Eastern Canada originates from a variety of overseas supply sources. Moreover,
\par
other energy commodities such as natural gas or hydro power originate wherever na-
\par
tural gas fields and hydro sites happen to be located... In other words all energy
\par
sources are not available like all food products at every production or warehousing
\par
facility.
\par
Another peculiarity of regional energy supply distribution systems is the long term
\par
implications of certa1n decisions such as the building of a tar sand plant, the
\par
expansion or building of a pipeline or of a hydro power generation facility. Ac-
\par
cordingly, a "look ahead" capability would considerably enhance the value of a re-
\par
gional energy planning model.
\par
Moreover, energy supply distribution systems featuring electric power generation
\par
and distribution and/or energy tranformation processes incurring energy losses cannot
\par
adequately be represented in standard networks, i.e. networks in which flow is con-
\par
served around all nodes, hence that preclude losses proportional to throughput or
\par
flow. This aspect of total energy supply-distribution systems has two implications:
\par
Firstly, a generalized network optimization algorithm must be used for the "eva-
\par
luation" phase of the procedure instead of a standard network flow optimization
\par
algorithm; and secondly it becomes highly cumbersome and impractical to disconnect
\par
the multi-commodity network into single commodity sub-networks.
\par
In summary, in order to adequately represent and solve for optimal energy supply-
\par
distribution-demand plans over a multi-period planning horizon, it is necessary to
\par
combine the binary integer program that generates investment plans, with a genera-
\par
lized network flow optimization algorithm that evaluates these investment plans.
\par
Moreover the decomposition scheme to solve the global optimization problem must
\par
not feature the single sourcing constraint imposed in Geoffrion's management sup-
\par
port system and cannot easily be decomposed into single commodity sub-networka.
\par
- 403 -
\par
The model described in this paper answers all above requirements using a novel de-
\par
composition algorithm which follows in broad terms the familiar alternating sequence
\par
of investment plan generations and investment plan evaluations initially proposed
\par
by Benders (1962), and adapted by Geoffrion (1974), and Debanne, Beaubien and \&aohy
\par
Laleh (1980) for particular industrial supply-distribution systems. However, the
\par
substitution of a generalized network to the standard network flow algorithm and
\par
the lifting of the single sourcing constraint precluded the adoption of computational
\par
strategies comparable to those featured in Geoffrion's management support system
\par
(1979) or even Debanne et aI's algorithm (1980) for multi-period supply-distribution
\par
systems where the single sourcing constraint is lifted. This is due to the impos-
\par
sibility of devising (as yet) efficient commodity demand constraints to guide the
\par
binary integer program, if losses (or gains) in commodity flow occur between the
\par
sources or production nodes of the network and the sinks or consumption nodes.
