\par
Guaranteed Feasibility
\par
The above difficulty is resolved by constructing the generalized network flow re-
\par
presentation of the problem in a manner that ensures problem feasibility for any
\par
investment plan produced by the integer program, including the case where all in-
\par
vestment options Yk t are "out", Le. are equal to zero. Network feasibility is
\par
"guaranteed" by replacing all energy demand constraints in the network by price-
\par
demand elasticity mappings, using the method described by Debanne (1976, 1979, 1980)
\par
This method essentially consists in constructing as many "dummy flow" arcs as needed
\par
for a proper representation of every price-demand elasticity curve. These arcs
\par
originate at the source node of the network and terminate at the appropriate sink
\par
or demand node as illustrated in Fig. 2.
