Session V
\par
ISSUES OF DECISION MAKING
\par
UNDER UNCERTAINTY
\par
RISK STUDIES FOR NUCLEAR POWER PLANTS-
\par
MERITS AND LIMITATIONS
\par
A. Birkbofer
\par
Lehrstuhl fUr Reaktordynamik und Reaktorsicherheit, Technische
\par
Universitllt MUnchen, Federal Republic of Germany
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
Main results of the German Risk Study for nuclear power plants are presented. On
\par
the basis of these results merits and limitations of risk analyses are discussed.
\par
It is shown that such analyses can give objective criteria for the design of en-
\par
gineered safeguards and help to harmonize the safety concept.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Reactor safety; risk analysis; technology assessment.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
It has always been a most important aspect in the development and the application
\par
of technology, to avoid hazards to man and environment as far as possible. It is
\par
in the nature of new developments to extend the scope of applications into areas
\par
where not all potential problems are fully understood. In this situation it is
\par
important to recognize new hazards on the basis of the available experience. In
\par
general, however, this goal can be reached only partially. Therefore, the principle
\par
of "trial and error" has always played an important role in technology, in addition
\par
to the well-directed protection against hazards already recognized. This learning
\par
process is acceptable, as long as the loss resulting from a single technical fai-
\par
lure is relatively small. Corrective actions are feasible in due time to avoid
\par
large extent damages.
\par
A basically different situation exists in case of big industrial installations with
\par
inherent high hazards potential. Large damage could result from a single accident.
\par
In such cases the higher hazards potential has to be compensated for by higher
\par
graded safety measures. This reflects a basic principle in technology that the
\par
hazards potential is a measure of the amount of safeguards required. As long as the
\par
safeguards are proberly operating, severe damages will very rarely occur as a re-
\par
sult of an accident. The still remaining hazard - one might also call it risk - can
\par
then hardly be assessed on the basis of really occurred damages.
\par
Proper decision making requires objective information on hazards. While in the past
\par
the solution of problems of this kind has always a task of the experts in the
\par
technical field, it has recently become an issue of great public interest. This is
\par
particularly true for problems of air pollution posing health hazards and other
\par
- 413 -
\par
- 414 -
\par
environmental impacts. Increasingly, also accidental risks from large industrial
\par
installations are attracting public attention. If risks cannot directly be quanti-
\par
fied from experience, there remains the possibility to assess potential hazards by
\par
means of analytical methods.
\par
In the following, efforts are made to outline merits and limitations of such ana-
\par
lyses. This is done mainly on the basis of the methods developed for WASH-1400 and
\par
applied also to the "German Risk Study" for nuclear power plants with pressurized
\par
water reactors.
\par
RESULTS OF THE GERMAN RISK STUDY FOR NUCLEAR POWER PLANTS
\par
Each risk can be characterized by probability and extent of possible damages of
\par
various kinds (e.g. health damage, loss of life, property damage). The safety con-
\par
cepts applied in nuclear power plants ensure that accidents do not cause dangerous
\par
release of radioactive material into the environment as long as the engineered
\par
safeguards are properly operating. Therefore, only those events contribute signi-
\par
ficantly to the risk which result from failure of systems required to cope with an
\par
accident.
\par
The sequence of events, starting from an "initiating failure" is dependent on the
\par
functioning or failure of actuated engineered safeguards. Since a number of diffe-
\par
rent systems are actuated, a multitude of different courses of events is concei-
\par
vable, depending on the possible combinations of system success and system failure.
\par
The frequency of a specific sequence of events is determined by the frequency of
\par
the initiating failure and by the probabilities of success or failure of the
\par
different systems required. For highly reliable systems these probabilities are
\par
frequently not known from direct experience. Therefore, they have to be calculated
\par
analytically.
\par
This is done mostly by means of fault tree analyses. A fault tree represents the
\par
logical structure of the functional interaction between different system components.
\par
On the basis of this structure the probability of system failure is calculated as a
\par
function of probabilities of component failures. In doing so, also the influence of
\par
human behaviour and of external events on system reliability may be considered.
\par
To analyze the risk from a nuclear power plant mainly events leading to the melt-
\par
down of the reactor core have to traced. Only in this large amount activity re-
\par
leases could happen.
\par
In the German Risk Study a core melt frequency of about I to 10,000 per year was
\par
estimated, using a nuclear power plant with pressurized water reactor as a model.
\par
Quite a number of accident sequences contribute to this number. About 70 have been
\par
analyzed in some Fig. I shows the influence of the different initiating
\par
events on the overall core melt frequency. The loss main coolant through a small
\par
leak in a reactor coolant pipe dominates all other contributions. In comparison,
\par
a large break in the main coolant piping is of very little significance, although
\par
it could be deemed much more serious in the first instance. This fact has mainly
\par
two reasons.
\par
- Small leaks may occur more frequently than medium or large breaks.
\par
- In order to remove the decay heat, the reactor has to be cooled down by the
\par
secondary system. For this task operator action is necessary. In this case
\par
the influence of manual actions reduces system availability significantly.
\par
Fig. 2 shows, that consequently about two third of the overall core melt fre-
\par
- 415 -
\par
quency result from human errors.
\par
The risk analysis thus finds great significance in seemingly unimportant operatio-
\par
nal disturbances which lead to insufficient core cooling as a result of multiple
\par
technical and human failures. On the other hand, more spectacular initiating events,
\par
like the guillotine break of a main coolant pipe, is of less importance.
\par
After fission products have been released into the containment, their impact onto
\par
the environment depends on the state of containment. Containment integrity may be
\par
lost due to fast or delayed overpressure failure, or due to failure of containment
\par
isolation. By combining results of core melt analysis with the analysis of con-
\par
tainment failure modes, amount and frequency of fission product released from the
\par
plant to the atmosphere are obtained. Further more, the atmospheric dispersion of
\par
fission products, and the resulting radiation exposures are calculated.
\par
Finally, health effects and - according to the population data - the number of
\par
individuals afflicted by health damage have been estimated. Emergency procedures
\par
like evacuation of contaminated areas have been taken into account based on govern-
\par
ment recommendations existing in Germany.
\par
Fig. 3 shows the correlation between number and frequency of acute fatalities
\par
which could be caused by radiation exposure to the public after a nuclear accident.
\par
With 25 plants in operation a frequency of about 10- 5 per year has been estimated
\par
that acute fatalities are caused. The study has made an attempt to quantify con-
\par
fidence intervals of the results. These are shown at selected points. From this
\par
figure it can be concluded that large consequence events are extremely unlikely.
\par
Fig. 4 shows that these low frequencies result as the product of several factors.
\par
Considering 25 plants, calculations show a core melt frequency of I to 400 per year.
\par
Given a core melt down, fission product release to the atmosphere is limited by
\par
the containment in most cases very effectively. There is only a chance of 1 to 16
\par
that potentially lethal doses would appear after severe containment failure. In
\par
this case, consequences depend on weather conditions and population distribution.
\par
The chance for this situation is 1 to 10 that acute fatalities are caused. Given
\par
a core melt accident, the probability is higher than 99\% that no acute fatality
\par
will occur. A great number of fatalities could only occur if after the most severe
\par
accident unfavourable weather conditions coincide with specific site conditions.
\par
Given a lethal activity release, the probability is in addition less than 1\% that
\par
2,000 or more acute fatalities are caused.
\par
Besides acute fatalities, similar to WASH-1400, late health effects were calculated.
\par
Late health effects reflect the possibility of an increased risk of cancer or
\par
leukemia due to radiation. These effects may show up after a latent period of some
\par
decades. They have therefore been traced over several generations. From Fig. 5 it
\par
can be concluded that late effects are estimated also for less severe accidents.
\par
With a frequency of about 1 to 200 per year for 25 plants a considerable number of
\par
late fatalities has been calculated. It has to be born in mind that a linear
\par
dose-risk relationship has been used by the study. That means that even the smallest
\par
radiation exposure is assumed to cause an increase of risk of cancer. Late health
\par
effects which have been calculated would appear over large areas. As an average
\par
about half of these effects may occur outside the Federal Republik of Germany.
\par
This emphasizes the international importance of reactor safety. Applying the
\par
assumptions of the study - the linear dose-risk relationship - it can be calculated
\par
that about half a percent of all cancer fatalities are caused by natural radiation.
\par
Although this influence is rather samll, the absolute numbers amount to more than
\par
50,000 for Germany and about 600,000 for Europe, if the whole period of life is
\par
considered.
\par
- 416 -
\par
The study also estimated the number of people and the extension of areas affected
\par
by evacuation or relocation. However, the models are very crude in this respect so
\par
that the results can only be considered as rough estimates and are not presented
\par
here.
\par
UNCERTAINTY MARGINS OF RISK ANALYSIS
\par
If all quantities influencing a risk analysis were either deterministic or subject
\par
to stochastic variations according to a known law, then the probability of occuren-
\par
ce of a certain event could be calculated as a single number, just like the pro-
\par
bability that a thrown dice shows a certain face can be determined as J/6. However,
\par
risk analysis of complex technical systems has to account for other kind of influ-
\par
encing quantities which cannot be determined exactly because of incomplete know-
\par
ledge.
\par
For illustration, consider the failure probability of an electric circuit consis-
\par
ting of components like transistors, diodes, resistors etc. The failure probabili-
\par
ty of each of the components will depend on operating and environmental conditions,
\par
and will be subject to nonstochastic variations, influencing the uncertainty mar-
\par
gins. In order to explicitly exhibit this kind of uncertainty, density distribu-
\par
tions reflecting the existing knowledge are used for the description of such para-
\par
meters. Consequently, a density distribution is obtained for the circuit failure
\par
probability.
\par
In complex systems there are many other uncertain parameters besides component
\par
failure probabilities, which influence the calculated probabilities. However, the
\par
general way of treatment follows the pattern outlined above. Thus, finally, sub-
\par
jective density distributions are obtained for the probability of a certain damage.
\par
In various problems arise, which restrict the accuracy of the analysis
\par
and widen the range of uncertainty beyond the theoretically unavoidable measure.
\par
Problem of completeness
\par
It is practically impossible, to analyze all conceivable initiating events and
\par
their consequences. Thus, the detailed analysis has to investigate such initiating
\par
event which cover all the others with regard to consequences. Accordingly, the
\par
estimated frequency of an initiating event has to include contributions from other
\par
subsumized events. The possible sequences of accident resulting from an initiating
\par
event are systematically arranged and their frequencies and consequences are
\par
assessed. Of course, there remains in principle the eventuality to miss essential
\par
effects or to underestimate frequencies.
\par
At the time of the TMI-accident the German Risk Study had been almost completed.
\par
The question has been asked, if the sequence of events occuring at TMI had been
\par
considered in sufficient detail.
\par
The transition from a transient to a small leak, like in TMI, had been recognized
\par
as an important contribution to risk in the early stage of the work. At TlfI the
\par
leak could be closed after some time. However, significant portions of the coolant
\par
had already been lost. Thus, the reactor had to be cooled with only a portion of
\par
its normal coolant inventory.
\par
Because the study pessimisticly did not take account for the reestablishment of
\par
failed system functions, the actual sequence of events occuring at TMI has not
\par
been considered, but it has been assumed a total core meltdown for this case. Ex-
\par
plicit treatment of time-dependent effects would require much more detailed simu-
\par
- 417 -
\par
lation of the physical phenomena during an accident. A systematic approach renders
\par
omissions improbable. However, a positive proof of completeness is not possible.
\par
In any case, the qualification of the analyst and the detailed knowledge of the
\par
respective systems play an essential role. Certain risks may not be considered
\par
deliberately. So, the German Risk Study did not attempt to quantify frequency and
\par
extent of damages, which could be caused by military actions or by acts of sabotage.
\par
Data
\par
Another problem results from the fact that reliability data are required for all
\par
components considered in reliability analysis. Since these data can only be derived
\par
from statistical evaluations of ecperience or by experts estimate, they are in-
\par
evitably affected with uncertainties. It is obvious, that uncertainty margins are
\par
larger, the less experience is available. Frequently data have to be used which
\par
have been determined not for the component considered but for one of similar de-
\par
sign and similar environmental conditions. In this case, the calculated reliabili-
\par
ties are, strictly speaking, not valid for a specific plant, but serve as model
\par
for plants of the type analzyed.
\par
Human influences
\par
The safety design of nuclear power plants also provides measures against human
\par
failures. Safety actions, especially if they are required within a short time, are
\par
actuated automatically, as far as possible. This provides for the operators the
\par
opportunity to become acquainted with an upset plant condition and - if necessary
\par
- to respond with well planned actions. A number of other measures, mainly inten-
\par
sive training of operators is aimed to reduce the probability of false actions.
\par
Despite such provisions operator errors cannot be fully excluded. Therefore, they
\par
have to be considered in reliability analyses. Though it is rather straight forward
\par
to identify cases in which human errors could lead to system failure, it is diffi-
\par
cult to quantify probabilities of such erroneous actions. Since it is unfeasible
\par
to frame human behaviour into a rigid pattern, one has to operate with estimates.
\par
For example, the failure probability is rated the higher, the less time is avail-
\par
able to react, the more complicated the required action is, or the less the
\par
"personal redundancy" is.
\par
Particular problems may be encountered in the identification and quantification
\par
of operator actions not planned in operator manuals. The effects of such actions
\par
could be adverse as well as favourable. It is likely, however, that primarily the
\par
frequencies of events will be influenced by such actions. Basicly new accident
\par
sequences are not to be expected.
\par
Common-Mode Failures
\par
This term means cases, in which several components and one or several systems are
\par
put out of order by a single cause. A fire, impairing several redundant system
\par
at the same time, could be a typical example. Analysis of common-mode failures
\par
poses some serious problems. Reliability analysis is particularly suitable, to
\par
recognize system configurations, which could given rise to common-mode failures.
\par
A portion of such failures will be picked up by the systematics of fault tree ana-
\par
lysis. However, for other cases it depends on the knowlegde of the analyst to re-
\par
cognize potential common-mode failures which could have been missed by the analysis.
\par
Probability assessment often is only possible on the basis of estimates, since
\par
there is only little experience available with common-mode failures. Moreover,
\par
frequently experience is not applicable to actual design, since recognized causes
\par
of common-mode failure will lead to design modifications.
\par
- 418 -
\par
Accident simulation
\par
Risk analyses have not only to calculate system reliabilities, but also to simulate
\par
the physical course of accidents by means of computer codes. In the first place,
\par
this is necessary to determine requirements for the safety systems. However, the
\par
German Risk Study - just like WASH-1400 - in this point relies mainly on the acci-
\par
dent analyses, which have been performed for the safety assessment for the refe-
\par
rence or similar plants during the licensing procedure. That means, that for the
\par
risk anaysis safety systems are considered as totally failed, if fewer redundant
\par
subsystems are available than have been assumed as functionable in the safety
\par
assessment.
\par
In the next step, the further course of accidents, which follow from system fai-
\par
lures, have to be simulated. The models, on which the computer codes are based,
\par
usually describe the real events only approximately. Therefore, one tries to model
\par
the phenomena in such a way that the calculations always give pessimistic results.
\par
For risk analyses, which should supply realistic results, this is unsatisfactory.
\par
Presently, these uncertainties can hardly be quantified.
\par
Calculation of damage, caused by radiation exposure, plays an essential role in
\par
risk analysis. Also this calculation depends on various parameters, which either
\par
can be described only statistically or which have to be estimated. The first ca-
\par
tegory contains for instance the meteorological conditions, which prevail at and
\par
after an accident. Examples of the second category are the biological-medical para-
\par
meters, like dose-effect-relations, or parameters of the emergency procedures. In
\par
the German Risk Study it was attempted to assess uncertainty margins. The corres-
\par
ponding confidence intervals have already been shown together with the results of
\par
the study.
\par
USE OF RISK ANALYSIS TO ASSIST DECISION MAKING
\par
On the background of the situation described one may ask to what extent risk ana-
\par
lyses are an appropriate instrument to assist decision making.
\par
In the Federal Republic of Germany, like in many other countries, today courts have
\par
the last word on licensing of nuclear plants. They have to decide whether the pro-
\par
visions are sufficient to exclude undue hazards to the public from the operation
\par
of nuclear power plants. In evaluating the required provisions the state of science
\par
and technology has to applied.
\par
Problems always arise, when the undefined legal terms "state of science and techno-
\par
logy" and "required provisions" have to be concretized. The idea is self-suggesting
\par
to fix by law a permissible risk and to prove that this risk will not be exceeded.
\par
The proof, that the risk from a specific plant does not exceed a specified level,
\par
is a technical problem. The discussed difficulties in the methods of risk analysis
\par
suggest that it is presently not feasible to furnish legally unquestionable eviden-
\par
ce about the absolute level of risk remaining inspite of all safety measures. With
\par
our present knowledge it is doubtful if the problems mentioned can be solved in a
\par
satisfactory way.
\par
The problem of fixing a permissible level of risk is primarily of political nature.
\par
Legislatory action of perliament would be required and it must be doubted that the
\par
willingness to do so could be stimulated. Presently, the public does not seem to
\par
be used to proper dealing with and interpretation of risk numbers. As long as this
\par
situation persists, it is understandable that politicians have reservations about
\par
declaring certain risks, and thus implicity certain damages, as acceptable.
\par
However, there are indications of change of opinion. From the discussion of risks
\par
- 419 -
\par
it has been learned, that the eventuality of large extent damages, although expec-
\par
ted with very low probabilities, could playa role in decision making. The demand
\par
seems to arise, to limit frequencies as well as the potential extent of damage by
\par
inherent engineered safeguards.
\par
The situation is complicated by the fact that the individual perception of risk
\par
may largely differ from actual or estimated risk. I believe IIASA to be one of the
\par
most experienced institutions in this field. Also recent investigations conducted
\par
at MIT have shown that the quantitative measure of accepted risks may differ by
\par
several orders of magnitudes. According to this work, individual risks of death by
\par
accident resulting form well known causes, being tolerated voluntarily, considered
\par
controlable, and affecting only a few persons simultaneously with some time delay,
\par
are accepted if their probability is 10-2 to 10- 3 per year and person. For risks of
\par
death by accident of complementary nature, like novel causes, involuntarily tole-
\par
rated, immediate consequences assumed to be uncontrolable and catastrophic as a
\par
single event, the acceptance level is as low as 10-9 per year and person.
\par
Below the level of formal decision making, risk analyses, may invluence public
\par
opinion. In this process, two opposite reactions become visible: On the one hand
\par
the intention to 2nalyze risks, and the potential to minimize them, is appreciated
\par
by the public. On the other side, the public may be made conscious of a certain
\par
risk through its detailed analysis which conflicts with the human reaction to
\par
suppress the notion of risk. The evolution of technology has always taken account
\par
of this attitude. Technical products are declared safe, and also considered safe
\par
by the public, if they are produced according to the current safety standard. In
\par
many instances, the "amount of safety" is expressed in terms of a safety factor.
\par
However, also by determination of safety factors, one implicity accepts failure
\par
probabilities and, in consequence, certain damages.
\par
Normally, this becomes evident only through the occurence of accidents resulting in
\par
damages. The public seems to be largely unaware of these interrelations. Consequent-
\par
ly, the determination of safety factors and similar safety requirements is left to
\par
the experts. Yet, if the expert expresses in the same situation the amount of safe-
\par
ty in terms of corresponding failure probabilities and of possible consequences of
\par
failure, he may easily be accused to decide over life or death.
\par
It will become necessary to make the public better acquainted with the approach
\par
of risk analyses (which is also new to many engineers and scientists). Among other
\par
things, this requires to make available suitable measures of comparison in order
\par
to enable realistic evaluation of the analyzed risks. Obviously, comparisons are
\par
accepted more easily, if they are made mainly between technical systems serving
\par
equal purposes. This could provide a basis for decisions between several alterna-
\par
tive technologies.
\par
However, one of the problems of such comparisons is that damages of different kinds
\par
may occur. It is still unclear how a uniform and commonly accepted measure of
\par
evaluation can be defined for such damages as early and late health effect, large
\par
area contamination, and long range meterological effects - to name a few typical
\par
kinds of damages. On the other hand, comparison of risks of technologies serving
\par
different purposes, like nuclear energy systems or big chemical plants, could be
\par
used primarily to show the level of risk we are living with. Another point is, to
\par
what extent can probabilistic investigations influence decisions in the technical
\par
field considering nuclear plants, mainly three areas may be distinguished:
\par
Determination of accidents and accident sequences which have to be accounted for
\par
in the safety design
\par
In the assessment of safety of nuclear plants one always has made use of probabili-
\par
- 420 -
\par
ties - at least implicitly. This is already indicated by the use ofe terms like
\par
"maximum credible accident" in the 60's. Also the concept of "design basis accident",
\par
as it is used today, is partly based on probabilistic considerations. Probabilistic
\par
aspects, however, are mostly transposed into deterministic criteria. So, assumptions
\par
about the safety systems to be used in the analysis of an accident, are determined
\par
by convention.
\par
By quantitative probabilistic methods a well-balanced concept can be reached, since
\par
possible sequences of event are not assessed according to rigid criteria, but
\par
judged according to their respective contribution to risk.
\par
Comparison and optimization of systems
\par
In order to optimize systems design it is necessary not only to analyze the per-
\par
formance of individual systems, but to consider the interactions of the different
\par
systems required to cope with accidental situations. This is particularly im-
\par
portant because system design in general as well as later system modifications can
\par
cause undesirable effects in other systems. Here, a quantitative probabilistic ana-
\par
lysis advantages over rigid deterministic crieteria, applied mostly only to indi-
\par
vidual systems. Also the licensing procedure for nuclear plants makes use of pro-
\par
babilistic analyses when evaluating systems design. Yet, this is frequently done
\par
only for selected systems, thus loosing the advantage of a systematic global ana-
\par
lysis.
\par
Test and maintenance strategies
\par
Maintenance is a crucial aspect for the reliability of safety systems. On the basis
\par
of reliability analysis test intervals and permissible repair times are fixed. In
\par
all three cases it is sufficient to perform reliability analysis. Consequence cal-
\par
culations, necessary to determine the risk, are not required in this context. Risk
\par
analysis, however, could be employed in order to obtain reference values for the
\par
required systems reliabilities.
\par
Probabilistic methods have been used in various technical disciplines for de-
\par
signing systems of high reliability. E.g., in licensing commercial airplanes, many
\par
countries require the applicant to demonstrate that the probability of failure of
\par
vital systems does not exceed predetermined values. In determining the probabili-
\par
ties of system failure human errors seem to be considered only to a limited extent.
\par
A complete risk analys risk analysis is not performed. Contrary to nuclear techno-
\par
logy, cases concerning licensing of commercial airplans have rarely been taken to
\par
court. Therefore, it is not so urgent to furnish legally unquestionable evidence on
\par
the level of risk.
\par
CONCLUS IONS
\par
Merits of risk analyses lay mainly in the field of technical assessment. They can
\par
give objective criteria for the design of engineered safeguards and help to har-
\par
monize the safety concept. In many instances, however, also reliability analysis
\par
could give the required information. For a proper interpretation and use of results
\par
of risk analyses it is essential to provide for comparitive numbers.
