\par
SOME PRINCIPAL ASPECTS OF FORECASTING
\par
AND DECISION-MAKING AND THE ISSUES OF
\par
DECISION-MAKING UNDER UNCERTAINTY IN
\par
THE AUSTRIAN ELECTRICITY SUPPLY SYSTEM
\par
F. Pober
\par
VerbundgesellschaJt, A-lOlO Vienna, Am HoJ 6A, Austria
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
The forecast of demand and the planning of electricity supply systems
\par
have to be made in a political and economical environment of growing
\par
uncertainty. In the first part some basic notions related to systems,
\par
operations and models are mentioned. The concept of probability fore-
\par
cast is discussed and a new notion is defined, the preciseness of
\par
forecasts to evaluate different kinds of forecasts like point fore-
\par
casts, band forecasts and probability forecasts. The basic weakness of
\par
the forecasting models is also indicated. In the second part some data
\par
of the Austrian electricity supply system are given and indications of
\par
the planning problems in the short, medium and long term and of the
\par
uncertain factors. Coordination, flexibility, diversification and
\par
regular revision of expansion programs are some of the answers to
\par
uncertainty.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Forecast; probability; demand; preciseness; model; planning; Austria;
\par
electricity; supply; system; uncertainty.
\par
PART 1: SOME PRINCIPAL ASPECTS OF FORECASTING AND DECISION-MAKING
\par
As an introduction some fundamental notions are to be mentioned. The
\par
real world is extremely complex. A first step towards a weltanschauung
\par
would be to divide into the world and the persons as bearers of cer-
\par
tain roles. A language is for communication between persons. The words
\par
of the language are codes for things and are an appeal to experience
\par
and conceptions. The sciences determine domains of the world, as there
\par
are economy or energy. On synthesis it is possible that existing
\par
relations between the domains are omitted. Specialized languages are
\par
used. The domains still are extremely complex. Therefore only certain
\par
aspects which are essential for the problems are considered. At first
\par
the description of the problem is done verbally. A model comprises
\par
only a subset of all aspects. Various domains show common properties
\par
as for example electricity, gas and water supply. This leads to
\par
- 425 -
\par
- 426 -
\par
systems theory. One can also speak of systems research. The aspect of
\par
language is especially important and systems theory could be justified
\par
by the creation of standardized conceptions. The keyword is system. It
\par
is possible to speak about a pre-model, a general model or universal
\par
model. Systems theory is an interdisciplinary tool, a weltanschauung.
\par
Basic terms are system, element, component, hierarchy of systems,
\par
organisation, input, output, auxiliaries (resources, capital, per-
\par
sonnel), objective function, environment, complementary system.
\par
Functional notions applied in supply systems are transport, trans-
\par
formation, storage. Systems exist in time. Besides of a generally
\par
spatial dimension the time dimension also has to be regarded: past,
\par
presence and future. The notion of process has to be added. Something
\par
will be changed within a certain span of time (dynamics of systems).
\par
When a system is constructed, components will be added. When a system
\par
is operated, the states (of components) are changed. Transitions take
\par
place. A system is static only under a certain view. In order to
\par
define the task of systems theory in a short way one has to say that
\par
systems theory should provide the transition from a verbal description
\par
of a problem to a formal (mathematical) model.
\par
Some fundamental operations can be defined when persons are dealing
\par
with systems. The operations theory (operations research) lists the
\par
following operations:
\par
Identification: Perception and description of components and of the
\par
design of the system. Recently, in case of uncertainty, the notion of
\par
fuzzy systems has been introduced.
\par
Analysis: Perception and description of processes, that is, the opera-
\par
tion of systems. Prognosis: It is related to the future design of the system and to the
\par
future operation of the system (process) or to the input and the
\par
output of the system.
\par
Planning: A list of alternatives has to be assumed. As not all can be
\par
known about future possibilities, the list of alternatives will always
\par
be incomplete.
\par
Decision-making: A choice between several alternative plans has to be
\par
made. A sensitivity analysis will support a certain choice.
\par
An analysis of the final decision and plan will end a sequence of such
\par
operations which usually are iteratively applied.
\par
Models are a tool of planning and decision-making. Models comprise
\par
restrictions on the variables of the problem and an objective
\par
function. Models make possible simulations of design and operation of
\par
systems. Optimisation is a choice out of many simulations. A sensiti-
\par
vity analysis shows the dependence of solutions on certain parameters.
\par
A robust solution would be an interesting result. and con-
\par
science of the persons participating in these operations (analyst,
\par
forecaster, planner, decision-maker) are the foundations for the final
\par
decision. A certain subjectivity is always involved.
\par
Data and data collection are also very important. Data collection can
\par
be very costly. Availability and accuracy of data can strongly in-
\par
fluence model building. A choice can be made between available data.
\par
The objective methods of statistical analysis cannot prevent sUb-
\par
jective manipulation by selection of data. But also for the models of
\par
planning subjective influences cannot be excluded. This may be true
\par
- 427 -
\par
for the choice of restrictions or for the choice of the objective
\par
function.
\par
Therefore the acute question of approval and validation of the models
\par
always presents itself.
\par
Event and utility are two concepts which are very important in the
\par
theory of decision under uncertainty. In the models of electricity
\par
supply systems a minimum cost objective function is included (invest-
\par
ment and operating costs). Sometimes, outage costs to the economy and
\par
even indicators for environmental and health factors are also in-
\par
cluded. The notion of utility is still discussed in theory and prac-
\par
tice. The notion of (uncertain) events and forecasting of future
\par
events will be discussed even more. The object of these discussions
\par
will be some fundamental considerations which often are disregarded. A
\par
forecast is a statement about a future event or about several (mutual-
\par
ly exclusive) events only one of which will occur. It is charac-
\par
teristic that the truth of the statement can only be checked in the
\par
future. The statement, the next throw of a dice will show a six, can
\par
only be checked after the throw and the statement is true or false.
\par
The knowledge of the conditions which lead to the throw of a six can
\par
only be inconlplete. Therefore, it follows that no accurate forecast is
\par
possible. This is confirmed especially in case of repetition. This
\par
situation leads to an extension of the language, to the introduction
\par
of the concept of probability. The statement, the next throw will show
\par
a six, is probable and will be assessed by a probability P. Incomplete
\par
knowledge and probability are related and therefore the generally
\par
subjective character of probability is understood. A probability
\par
theory which to a greater extent takes into consideration the needs of
\par
forecasting than the needs of analysis, as it is almost exclusively
\par
the case today, would be more valuable. Notions like conditional pro-
\par
bability which can be directly related to the notion of conditional
\par
forecast or the adaptive theorem of Bayes can be more easily under-
\par
stood in relation to the notion of information. The forecasting of
\par
electricity demand should be considered as an example of application.
\par
But this should not be a restriction on the considerations. It is
\par
necessary and usual to classify forecasts. According to time there are
\par
short-term, medium-term and long-term forecasts. The short-term fore-
\par
cast of the load dispatcher reaches from hours to days, eventually to
\par
months (maintenance plan). The medium-term forecast of the energy
\par
planner reaches from nonths to several years and is the base for the
\par
planning of supply, especially imports and exchanges, for estimation
\par
of costs and income and for the consideration of tariffs. The long-
\par
term forecast of the energy planner is the base for the investment
\par
program for new power stations and the transmission and distribution
\par
system, for estimations of resources (primary energy) and extends to a
\par
horizon of 10 or 15, eventually 20 or 30 years. Horizons beyond these
\par
figures were subjects of sophisticated studies by IIASA. The notion of
\par
prognosis generally provokes the question of accuracy which even
\par
experts cannot guarantee. Therefore, instead of long-term forecasts
\par
the notion of scenarios is now used. An analysis of such long-term
\par
forecasts or scenarios should give some idea of the future. According
\par
to the kind of statements there are point forecasts, band forecasts
\par
and probability forecasts. The latter are given by a probability
\par
distribution. Point and band forecasts can also be viewed as probabi-
\par
lity forecasts. Band forecasts implicitely define a uniform distri-
\par
bution. Point forecasts imply a paradox. As these would have the
\par
probability zero, nothing should happen. They would never be true.
\par
- 428 -
\par
This is certainly not the case. In order to avoid this paradox (which
\par
obviously could also be done by the use of the mathematical concept of
\par
a distribution function) discrete variables defined on intervals will
\par
be used. The size of the intervals should be adapted to the problem. A
\par
point forecast should be treated like a band forecast (with a small
\par
band). The use of discrete variables also allows for the introduction
\par
of the notion of entropy which will be used for the definition of the
\par
preciseness of a forecast.
\par
The question arises how the planner and decision-maker appreciates
\par
these different kinds of statements, for example in case of the elec-
\par
tricity demand. A builder of power stations only can be told to build
\par
a certain power station or not, but not to build it with a certain
\par
probability. Therefore it is clear that the point forecast which could
\par
also be denoted as a bold forecast will be preferred by the planner
\par
and decision-maker as it is the keenest statement and the forecaster
\par
is most strongly bound by his responsibility for accuracy. It has to
\par
be stated that in case of the prognosis of electricity demand the
\par
investments are already determined to a high degree by the forecast,
\par
at least according to quantity. Only the quality has to be evaluated
\par
in detail by the planner. Sometimes, it is maintained that only proba-
\par
bility forecasts are admissible. It is completely overlooked that
\par
especially in case of forecasting electricity demand it is practically
\par
impossible to assess a probability distribution. It can only be stated
\par
that there is a probability of demand. Beyond this it seems to have
\par
been completely omitted that on principle a probability forecast is of
\par
a not-binding character and therefore the forecaster is more or less
\par
released from his responsibility for accuracy. What is really in-
\par
teresting is the next event, the demand of the next year, but probabi-
\par
lities (of intervals) are given. Any demand is possible. The statement
\par
(forecast) is really related to probabilities and not to demand.
\par
Probabilities cannot be checked in one step as the measure of probabi-
\par
lity is the relative frequency in a large number of repetitions. As
\par
there is a growth process the character of repetition is missing and
\par
therefore probabilities are not measurable. This criticism shows that
\par
the concept of preciseness of a forecast (statement) should be intro-
\par
duced and a numerical value should be evaluated. The notion of entropy
\par
will be the base of such a measure for forecasts. The notion of entro-
\par
py is derived from the measure of information. When a probable event
\par
occurs, a dice shows six, the observer can transmit the relevant
\par
information to a person who knows the forecast. The transient charac-
\par
ter of information should be emphasized in comparison to knowledge.
\par
The value of information can be measured by the probability of the
\par
event: The smaller the probability of the event the greater the value
\par
of information to the interested person, the greater the surprise. The
\par
value of information has additive property. This can be seen when the
\par
information to the person is divided into two parts. The first part of
\par
the information would be, the dice shows an even number. This partial
\par
information induces a new forecast for the possible events according
\par
to the theorem of conditional probabilities. Then the second part of
\par
the information follows, the dice shows a six. From this it follows
\par
without difficulty that the measure of information is ld(1/p). The
\par
(non-negative) entropy is the expectation of the information. In case
\par
of the uniform distribution and a given number n of intervals the
\par
entropy H is ld n. This is also the maximum value of the entropy. For
\par
any other distribution the entropy is smaller. For the point forecast
\par
it is zero. The entropy is related to the expected information. For
\par
the notion of preciseness of a forecast it should be regarded that for
\par
- 429 -
\par
a greater probability of an event there is a greater expectation that
\par
the event will occur and this forecast will be more useful for the
\par
user of the forecast. Moreover, a uniform distribution can always be
\par
quoted according to the of indifference (missing knowledge
\par
induces a uniform distribution). Therefore the preciseness R of a
\par
forecast can be defined by Id n -H. In case of a uniform distribution
\par
R=O and in case of a point forecast R=ld n, the maximum value. From
\par
this it follows that it is of no great value to increase the number of
\par
intervals beyond a certain level.
\par
Moreover, one should put the question what a decision-maker should do,
\par
if he has at hand two forecasts about the same event. In relation to
\par
this question there appear notions like expert and trust. Especially
\par
the nuclear debate shows the importance of these notions. In case of
\par
the existence of two equally ranking forecasts, that is, two probabi-
\par
lity distributions, one could use the notion of information to escape
\par
from the dilemma. Suppose a certain event has occured. The probabili-
\par
ties were p(1) and p(2) according to the two forecasts. There are also
\par
two information values. It is obvious that the interested person would
\par
prefer to pay only for the information according to the smaller of
\par
both information values. This means that the greater of both probabi-
\par
litiy values is assumed to be valid. Thus the adaption of two or more
\par
probability forecasts can be made by the acceptance of the maximum of
\par
the probability values related to the same event. Finally a factor has
\par
to be applied to make the sum of the probabilities equal to 1. It
\par
follows that the adaption of the forecasts always is directed to a
\par
more uniform distribution.
\par
In general a forecast will not be made for only one future date but
\par
for a sequence of dates. The forecast is related to a process
\par
(stochastic process). In such a case one also speaks of a model (of a
\par
development). For example, in case of a Gaussian (stochastic) process,
\par
mean and standard deviation should be given as functions of time. A
\par
diagram of a past time-series shows a rather irregular trend still
\par
giving the notion of a certain underlying curve, for example a
\par
straight line. The deviations of the actual values from this line are
\par
thought to be distributed at random (not explained). By the principle
\par
of least-square estimation or by stochastic considerations it is
\par
possible to evaluate a special curve out of a given class of curves.
\par
This is the method of stochastic interpolation. The future development
\par
is interesting. In physics given three points of the parabola of a
\par
thrown stone, the parabola and its development are determined by the
\par
theorems of mechanics (but also under the assumption that the forces
\par
will not change or that they are given). There are no such fundamental
\par
theorems for the behaviour of persons, especially the future be-
\par
haviour. Thus the future development of social systems cannot be
\par
predicted by extrapolation of the development in the past. Also it is
\par
not possible to predict the future development based on the knowledge
\par
of other predictions. There is only the conditional forecast: If there
\par
is no change in the forces of the development in the past, then the
\par
future development will be the continuation of the development in the
\par
past. Clearly, this is a permanent source of failures of forecasts.
\par
One need not even bring about such striking examples as the crisis of
\par
crude oil supply in 1973/74 or the Austrian law of 1979 forbidding the
\par
commissioning of the nearly finished nuclear power station near
\par
Zwentendorf. Generally, short-term forecasts - the type of model will
\par
not be too critical - are of a better quality than long-term fore-
\par
casts. On the other hand, in case of long-term forecasts there should
\par
- 430 -
\par
be greater chances for an adaption of the forecasts avoiding wrong
\par
decisions following a cybernetic analogy while pericdically revising
\par
the forecasts.
\par
Summarizing, it can be said that in principle there is uncertainty
\par
about the future on account of incomplete knowledge. is
\par
basically related to forecasts and a forecast is only a synopsis of
\par
other forecasts. A probability forecast is of a rather non-obligatory
\par
character when compared to a point forecast. Taking this point fore-
\par
cast as expectation of a distribution the actual value will deviate
\par
from it at random. Remembering the basic property of irregularity of a
\par
sequence of stochastic events and the consequent principle of the
\par
impossibility of a system of gambling according to R.v.Mises the
\par
principle of the impossibility of accurate forecasts could be
\par
as the deviations from the model values are at random (not explained)
\par
or even the models are doubtful, at least for long-term forecasts.
