Pumped storage power plants (with a small storage volume) are of minor
\par
importance in Austria. There are pumping installations in several
\par
power plants which are used for seasonal storage and/or in a daily
\par
cycle of pumping and generation. The total installed pumping capacity
\par
amounts to 1124.2 MW.
\par
The electricity demand shows seasonal character. The demand of the
\par
winter period (October-March) is higher than the demand of the summer
\par
period. Also the hydraulic production shows a seasonal variation even
\par
much stronger than the demand. There are important differences between
\par
summer and winter, the winter production being smaller than in summer.
\par
The consequence is a high share of thermal production and of imports
\par
during winter time and hydraulic surplus production in summer time
\par
which will be exported or exchanged against imports in winter time.
\par
The hydraulic projuction can strongly deviate from the mean values.
\par
These deviations are measured by a production coefficient which is
\par
distributed according to empirical probabilities. Other stochastic
\par
parameters which determine the necessary reserve and the reliability
\par
of supply are the unavailability of power plants, especially of ther-
\par
mal power stations and first of all the demand. It is very difficult
\par
to find a probability distribution for the demand. The same is true
\par
for the guarantees of imports to be assessed as probabilities. There-
\par
fore an analysis of the risk not to meet the demand is very doubtful
\par
in terms of probability. During winter time long-lasting periods of
\par
low temperature increase the risk of supply outages.
\par
The potential of hydro resources is assessed to 49.2 TWh/a. Thereof
\par
67\% are for run-off river and 33\% for storage power stations. Present-
\par
ly 50\% of the potential are used. At the end of the current planning
\par
period of ten years this percentage will be 76\% of the total, 85\% of
\par
the run-off river plants and 57\% of the storage plants. For the cur-
\par
rent planning period the growth rate of demand is assumed to be 5\%.
\par
After this period there will still be a (reduced) growth of demand.
\par
Therefore the question arises how a growing demand could be met in
\par
future as Austria has no appreciable resources. The application of
\par
nuclear energy is prevented by law and a further increase of imports
\par
of electricity seems to be doubtful for reasons of system reliability.
\par
Besides, it remains to be doubted that in future there will be enough
\par
options for new import contracts at a high level of guarantee.
\par
Before entering on the details of the planning and decision-making
\par
process for energy planning (power stations, imports, exports) it will
\par
be remarked that the planning of the transmission system faces diffi-
\par
culties in an increasing measure (uncertainty of licences). Also the
\par
- 433 -
\par
problems of short-term planning by the load dispatcher for the sche-
\par
duling of plants can only be entered upon in a short way. The task of
\par
energy planning is to provide the load dispatcher with the means for
\par
meeting the actual demand. These are power plants and feasible import
\par
contracts. It is up to the decision of the load dispatcher to schedule
\par
the operation of power stations and pumps and to make arrangements for
\par
exports or imports at short notice or to arrange for the operation of
\par
the thermal power stations other than those he is responsible for. The
\par
uncertainties which render difficult the task of the load dispatcher
\par
are related to demand (weather, television, scheduling of power
\par
stations of resellers), hydraulic situation, forced outages of power
\par
station and transmission lines. The necessary reserve could be provi-
\par
ded by the international interconnected system and by the seasonal
\par
storage plants which would take over load during the start-up period
\par
of an idling thermal power station. For the control of demand the load
\par
dispatchers of the prOVincial companies could also use the means of
\par
peak shaVing, that is, the disconnection of certain consumers. As the
\par
load dispatcher is aware of the dependence of consecutive states
\par
depending on the time elapsed the stochastic element diminishes for
\par
him the more, the shorter the period of foresight is chosen. There
\par
must always be sufficient reserve to cope with the forced outage of
\par
the biggest unit. This will be provided by the seasonal storage power
\par
stations which have also to match the biggest variations of the daily
\par
load.
\par
The base for the cooperation between the Verbundgesellschaft and the
\par
prOVincial companies are the coordination contracts and also the
\par
contracts of the provincial companies for participation in certain
\par
power stations of the Verbundkonzern. The coordination contracts refer
\par
to the quota of energy which the provincial companies have to buy from
\par
the Verbundgesellschaft, to the validity of the tariffs of the Ver-
\par
bundgesellschaft, to the observance of the contractual maximum load
\par
during winter time, to the readiness of reserve and to the frequency
\par
control by the Verbundgesellschaft. The coordination contracts are
\par
also the base for a common forecast of the demand and the planning of
\par
the system expansion for a period of ten years. This is done by the
\par
coordination committee of the Austrian association of the electric
\par
utilities and its experts. On account of many uncertainties of the
\par
electricity demand forecast and of the projects (delayed begin of
\par
construction and/or delayed commissioning of projects due to the
\par
obstruction of various parties for different motivations) the expan-
\par
sion program will be thoroughly revised every one to two years. If an
\par
enlarged increase of demand is foreseen, some of the projects of the
\par
expansion program will be constructed earlier and in case of a reduced
\par
increase the begin of construction will be postponed. Thus the funda-
\par
mental principle of control is introduced into this dynamic process of
\par
planning in order to adapt to the uncertainties. This generally suffi-
\par
ces -at least up to now- to maintain the reliability of supply.
\par
as for the coordinated expansion planning the system in its entity is
\par
investigated (matching of demand, matching of demand separated into
\par
base-, intermediate- and peak-load, necessary reserve, base load
\par
reserve, reserve at short notice, pumping, exports, imports) the
\par
preparation of projects requires intensive and detailed planning. On
\par
the level of system planning only recommendations for planning of
\par
subsystems (for example storage povrer plants) can be given which are
\par
not binding for a certain project. This that the individual
\par
project can be designed and optimized more or less independent of the
\par
system. The projects are prepared by the planning staff of the com-
\par
- 434 -
\par
panies. In the Verbundkonzern reports upon the projects are given by
\par
the project council and his experts. The maturity of a project for
\par
construction will also be declared by this project council. Expert
\par
reports are given upon geological problems, upon the areas influenced
\par
by the project (environment), buildings, mechanical and electrical
\par
integration of the project into the system (energy plan-
\par
ning) and upon the economy of the project. For multipurpose projects,
\par
joint power station projects in border areas political and economical
\par
issues and also fiscal and other legal promotions by the state are
\par
involved. Therefore the planning and the construction of power
\par
stations depend on the state for many aspects.
\par
Energy planning in the medium term cannot be based on power stations
\par
on account of their long design and construction periods. To ensure
\par
system reliability in the medium term it is necessary to contract for
\par
imports or exchange of electric energy (base load in summer time for
\par
base load in winter time depending on a certain rate of exchange,
\par
peak-load for base-load in the same or in the forth-coming period).
\par
This is often done within the framework of existing contracts. On
\par
account of the central position of Austria within Europe and on
\par
account of a great number of international high capacity transmission
\par
tie lines which connect the Austrian electricity supply system to the
\par
neighbour systems there are many opportunities for the exchange of
\par
electricity. Not on1y neighbour countries but also countries without
\par
common borders like Poland or Roumania had been or are contractual
\par
parties of Austria. These import contracts provide manifold options
\par
ranging from the guaranteed continuous supply of band energy to the
\par
energy offered at random and at short notice and to mutual assistance
\par
contracts which have the character of insurances in kind and could be
\par
claimed in case of a major event in one of the systems to the best of
\par
the abilities of the other contractual parties. As far as there are no
\par
events comparable to force majeure which would simultaneously in-
\par
fluence all parties which are operating an interconnected bulk power
\par
grid these exchange contracts give expectation of high reliability of
\par
imports. Thus the principle of diversification reduces the risk of not
\par
meeting the demand and contributes to a reliable electricity supply.
\par
These contracts as a total imply a high degree of fleXibility to adapt
\par
to changing situations. Another means which increase the flexibility
\par
and reliability of the Austrian system in an especially high degree
\par
are the seasonal storage power stations. Austria is rather well
\par
equipped with seasonal storage power stations which on account of
\par
large energy storage capacity are preferable to pumped storage power
\par
stations.
\par
For carrying out its planning the Verbundgesellschaft applies several
\par
demand and planning models which at least partially take into con-
\par
sideration the random nature of the future and therefore consider
\par
decision-making under uncertainty. A simulation- and optimisation-
\par
model based on the principle of dynamic optimisation had been de-
\par
veloped. The model is rather detailed. In spite of this the appli-
\par
cation is limited to general statements. A recent application of this
\par
model supplied the result of increasing economy of hydro-power due to
\par
rising fuel costs. The result shows a high sensitivity to the rate of
\par
interest. A stochastic model for the determination of the necessary
\par
reserve incidentally had been applied to evaluate the reliability of a
\par
given expansion program or to assess a guaranteed output of the sub-
\par
system of thermal power stations. Finally a planning model has to be
\par
mentioned which could be called a plan adaption model according to its
\par
- 435 -
\par
application. This model is frequently applied because it considers the
\par
knowledge about the system and its components in many details and on a
\par
monthly base. Contractual details of demand including the partici-
\par
pation contracts of the provincial companies are considered, the
\par
extended commissioning time of hydro-power stations with several
\par
aggregates (as these aggregates are individually set to operation at
\par
certain times the total commissioning time can last longer than one
\par
year), import and exchange contracts, seasonal storage, reasonable
\par
assumptions about base-, intermediate- and peak-load, pumping, sche-
\par
duling of storage power stations, hydraulic situation like mean, mean-
\par
dry and extreme-dry situation, as well as the scheduling of thermal
\par
power stations (under different definitions of the order of merit:
\par
fuel costs, priority of kind of fuel) and assumptions about outages.
\par
The plan adaption model is implemented on a compact computer. This
\par
allows for a flexible and quick application of model. It is impor-
\par
tant to be aware of the fact that these models are only an auxiliary
\par
means of calculation. They cannot supply automatic decisions.
\par
Referring to decision-making under uncertainty there are several other
\par
important factors to be mentioned which scarcely could be evaluated in
\par
terms of models and of probabilities:
\par
The political development in Austria and in the world.
\par
The economical development in Austria and in the world.
\par
The problems of resources.
\par
The technological developments.
\par
The development of demand (this will be influenced by micro- and
\par
macro-decisions, by the behaviour of persons, by voluntary or legal
\par
measures).
\par
The development of costs (capital, personnel, fuel).
\par
The financial situation (tariffs, capital market, rate of inflation).
\par
Summarizing, it has to be stated that the planning of the Austrian
\par
electricity supply system takes place in an environment of growing
\par
uncertainty in the short, the medium and the long term. Austria, a
\par
small country, not abundant with resources depends on the political,
\par
economical and energy developments in the world which also influence
\par
the Austrian electricity supply system, directly or by means of its
\par
political and economical environment. The incalculable attitude of the
\par
politicians and their lack of comprehension for the real energy
\par
situation generate this uncertain environment which cannot be pre-
\par
dicted. The same is true for a certain press. This causes increased
\par
difficulties for the construction and operation of the system. In
\par
spite of this uncertain and difficult situation the Austrian electri-
\par
city supply companies will try their best to fulfil the legal ob-
\par
ligation to provide for a reliable and economical supply of electric
\par
energy to the population and the economy of Austria.
