\par
DISTRIBUTION WITH AN
\par
THE HEALTH RISKS OF
\par
FUZZY STATE
\par
APPLICATION TO
\par
NUCLEAR POWER
\par
E. Kofler and P. Zweifel
\par
Institute for Empirical Research in Economics, University of Zurich,
\par
Switzerland
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
The traditional theory of decision-making under uncertainty does not allow for the
\par
formal incorporation of weak a priori knowledge such as, "Among the three states
\par
A, B, and C, A is more likely than B". This can be done using Linear Partial
\par
Information (LPI) analysis. Being a natural generalization of uncertainty as a
\par
risk, LPI also calls for the introduction of a generalized decision rule, the
\par
MaxEmin criterion. The elements of the analysis are combined and put to work in an
\par
evaluation of the relative health risks of nuclear power, where information
\par
concerning risks is very incomplete indeed.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Decision theory, uncertainty, fuzzy systems, technological assessment; cost-
\par
benefit-analysis, nuclear safety; energy supply.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
When faced with an actual decision problem, the economist often finds that his
\par
tool kit is incomplete. His advice is most needed and heeded for non-routine
\par
decisions. But these are precisely the situations where uncertainty and not risk
\par
prevails. This means that information is insufficient to establish a well-defined
\par
probability function over the relevant states of Nature. Traditionally, there have
\par
been two ways out of this difficulty. Either additional information must be sought
\par
or even fabricated with very rough guesses. Or a minimax strategy of complete
\par
uncertainty is adopted, which throwing away the fuzzy information there
\par
may be.
\par
In this paper, we propose a method for evaluating linear partial information (LPI)
\par
that allows the analyst to exploit Whatever knowledge he has. The basic idea of
\par
LPI analysis is that a finite number of different probability functions over states
\par
is relevant under conditions of fuzziness (Kofler, 1974; Kofler and Menges, 1976).
\par
It constitutes a natural generalization of the concept of uncertainty as a risk
\par
(Knight, 1921). In our example, LPI analysis enables a policy-maker to reach a
\par
final judgment on the relative health risks of nuclear power, although information
\par
