EVALUATING THE RELATIVE HEALTH RISKS OF NUCLEAR POWER
\par
Nuclear power from light water reactors (LWR) and coal will be the two most import-
\par
ant sources of energy in the intermediate future. Public debate has been focused on
\par
the health risks of nuclear power, with less concern for the loss of lives associated
\par
with the mining and combustion of coal. For simplicity, we disregard the issues
\par
relating to the economic costs of foregoing the LWR alternative as well as the issues
\par
relating to radioactive waste, proliferation of nuclear weapons, and nuclear terror-
\par
ism. They have been analyzed by the Nuclear Energy Policy Study Group (Keeney, 1977);
\par
