\par
SUMMARY AND CONCLUSION
\par
This paper presents a new tool for exploiting linear partial information (LPI) in an
\par
arbitrary decision problem. While the traditional theory of decision under risk
\par
admits of one single state distribution only, a set of admissible probability
\par
distributions is derived. In a second step, this set is reduced and made finite.
\par
Fuzziness regarding the set of actions at the disposal of the decision-maker, the
\par
payoffs associated with actions, and the credibility of partial information itself
\par
can also be modeled. These generalizations of LPI analysis, not reported here, are
\par
relevant to the energy debate because e.g. alternative sources of energy may
\par
become economically efficient, depending on choices made now. Hence, the set of
\par
strategies is fuzzy (Kofler and Zweifel, 1979). The transition from risk to uncert-
\par
ainty calls for the introduction of a generalized choice criterion to replace the
\par
maximization of expected utility. The new MaxEmin rule leads to a warranted minimal
\par
expected payoff. It also enables the decision-maker to assess the value of low-
\par
quality information that had to be disregarded in traditional decision theory.
\par
In an illustrative application of LPI analysis to the energy choice problem, it is
\par
shown how risk estimates of a fuzzy nature can be exploited to derive a clear-cut
\par
recommendation for action.
