THE EFFECT OF UNCERTAINTIES ON
\par
ESTIMATES OF THE ECONOMIC BENEFIT
\par
OF INTRODUCING ALTERNATIVE REACTOR
\par
TYPES INTO AN ELECTRICITY GENERATION
\par
SYSTEM
\par
C. Scrogg
\par
Risley Nuclear Power Development Establishment, United Kingdom Atomic
\par
Energy Authority, Risley, Warrington, Cheshire, UK
\par
ABSTRACT
\par
One way of assessing the economic advantage of a nuclear station is to determine
\par
the financial benefits from incorporating stations of that type into a computerised
\par
model of the electricity generating system. Such a model will give the number of
\par
stations of the different types installed at different times, partly as a result of
\par
input data and partly as a result of imposed conditions. The computer code could
\par
then be used to calculate the present worth of expenditure on the system so that if
\par
the calculation is repeated with one type of station replaced by another the
\par
economic benefit of this replacement can be determined. However, uncertainties in
\par
factors affecting expenditure and capacity commissioned introduce uncertainties in
\par
the total system expenditure and therefore in any estimate of the economic benefit
\par
of one strategy over another. A computer code, RISKAN, has been developed which
\par
carries out a large number of system benefit calculations for a pair of alternative
\par
strategies, each calculation incorporating perturbations in some or all of a set of
\par
items of input data (ie fabrication costs, capital costs, generating system growth
\par
rate, reactor introduction date, etc); the perturbations are selected individually
\par
from freely specified frequency distributions. The set (order 10,000) of results
\par
is then processed to determine the frequency distribution of the cost benefit and
\par
to indicate the probability of exceeding different levels.
\par
KEYWORDS
\par
Nuclear strategy; economic benefits; economic uncertainties; RISKAN; DISCOUNT;
\par
MONTE CARLO.
\par
INTRODUCTION
\par
With a suitable choice of input data the computer program DISCOUNT (I1iffe, 1973)
\par
can be used to obtain the total discounted expenditure associated with a particular
\par
reactor commissioning pattern and therefore to compare the cost benefits of one
\par
pattern over another. Initially the reactor commissioning pattern is determined to
\par
meet a given long-term programme of electricity demand, the different types of
\par
reactors commissioned at different times depending partly on the input data and
\par
partly on imposed constraints.
\par
