CALCULATION METHODS
\par
It is convenient to consider first the calculation of the effect of cost
\par
uncertainties only. Sufficient information from DISCOUNT runs is entered into the
\par
program to allow for the calculation of the A' k as defined in Equation 2 for each
\par
station type and commissioning pattern. The cost components, IV, FV, RC, OC and
\par
HV are each divided into at most 5 fractions, representing, for example, the
\par
contributions of capital costs, fabrication costs, uranium value etc, by
\par
associating multipliers (C. 'k) with each which usually, but not necessarily, total
\par
to unity; associated with of these fractions are "uncertainty" distributions
\par
K.. k and K.. To allow for long-term changes the K. and K ..k may be complete ly
\par
at any 2 dates during the period uncrer The K" k and K.
\par
distributions are expressed in the form of cumulative probability
\par
where the chance of getting a value of the variable less than, or equal to, a given
\par
value is represented on a scale from 0 to 100. Each distribution is represented by
\par
up to 21 pairs of "x, y" values so that a wide range of distributions can be
\par
represented reasonably accurately. In particular there is no restriction to
\par
distributions of a particular form (usually Gaussian) as is often the case in
\par
- 450 -
\par
analytic methods, Random numbers between 0 and 100 are generated by the computer
\par
and, by linear interpolation within the pairs of "x, y" points, the corresponding
\par
random values of the uncertainty are obtained. It is a feature of this method of
\par
sampling that repeated application generates the form of the original frequency
\par
distribution of the variables. If an uncertainty distribution is assumed to be
\par
exactly normal then it is only necessary to indicate this by the appropriate marker
\par
in the code and input the mean and standard deviation of the distribution.
\par
Provision is also made for a variable to be specified at any desired constant
\par
value, if this is appropriate. The use of the last 2 facilities significantly
\par
reduces computer running time.
\par
For the non-zero elements of each C.. matrix random values of Ki and Ki {$\cdot$}k are
\par
selected from the appropriate distfff;utions. The perturbed values of -N. k as
\par
calculated using Equation 4 are then summed over station types to givJ the
\par
perturbed expenditures for each commissioning pattern. The difference between the
\par
2 expenditures gives the perturbed value of system cost benefit (Equation 1). This
\par
procedure is repeated until a satisfactory representation of the distribution of
\par
uncertainty in cost benefit has been set up. Provision is made for up to
\par
10 "blocks" of samples to be taken, the number of samples within each "block" being
\par
unlimited. The first hundred sample values are used to set up a histogram to
\par
represent the distribution of both those values, and subsequent values, as they are
\par
generated. The overall mean and standard deviation of the expenditures associated
\par
with each commissioning pattern, and of the system cost benefits, are calculated as
\par
each sample block is completed.
\par
At the end of each sample block the updated histogram is processed to determine
\par
values corresponding to a number of percentage points of the distribution thus
\par
providing extra information which is particularly useful when the resulting
\par
distribution is non-normal. A simple test of convergence is carried out on the
\par
standard deviation calculated at the end of each sample block and this may be used
\par
to terminate the calculation.
\par
Consider now those factors such as programme size, reactor type introduction date
\par
and early installation rate, etc which may introduce uncertainties in system cost
\par
benefit through the effect of their uncertainties on the precise manner in which
\par
electrical demand is satisfied. The approach adopted in RISKAN is to determine the
\par
appropriate A.k's from a library of cases in the form of DISCOUNT results. For
\par
example, 3 va\textbackslash ues of programme size could be studied using the DISCOUNT code and
\par
the results entered into RISKAN together with the probability levels assigned to
\par
each of the 3 programmes and some quantitative measure of programme size, (eg
\par
discounted capacity commissioned; total number of stations installed at a given
\par
date). The A'k are then calculated from the data for each station type, of each
\par
at each programme size, and quadratic curves fitted to them
\par
wi thin the code. In a similar way the effects of uncertainties in up to 4 more
\par
variables can be considered in a simple linear relationship, each variable
\par
requiring 2 sets of DISCOUNT results to provide the necessary information to
\par
determine the coefficients. As in the case of the K. and K. 'k' numbers between 0
\par
and 100 are selected at random to give corresponding of the programme
\par
size and each of the other variables. The values of A, k are then determined by
\par
substitution in the quadratic and linear expressions.J At each selection the
\par
perturbation due to uncertainties in the cost data are incorporated as in Equation
\par
5. The results are tabulated in histogram form and processed as described above in
\par
the treatment of cost uncertainties.
