SUMMARY
\par
A computer program has been described which relies on the repeated sampling of
\par
freely specified probability distributions to generate fluctuations in, and
\par
therefore a distribution of, possible values of the economic benefits of
\par
introducing particular reactor types into an electricity generating system. The
\par
importance of particular variables can be easily investigated, as can the effect of
\par
various assumptions about the possible distribution of their uncertainties.
\par
The example which has been given above, the levels of the variables considered, and
\par
their effect on benefits are purely illustrative for the purpose of this
\par
presentation.
