DISCUSSION AND DECISION IN THE CASE OF
\par
POWER PLANT OBERJAGERWEG IN WEST
\par
BERLIN. MODELS, EXPERIENCE,
\par
CONCLUSIONS 1976-1978
\par
H. Maier
\par
Fachhochschule fUr Wirtschaft Berlin und Institut fUr Stadt-und Regionalplanung,
\par
Technische Universitlit Berlin, Berlin (West), Federal Republic of Germany
\par
Abstract. The paper contains items of the decision process
\par
of a power plant in West Berlin the realization of which
\par
was stopped in 1977. It shows mathematical models for
\par
energy demand and supply have no high priority in the whole
\par
decision process. which is a game between institutions and
\par
persons inside and outside of public administration. The
\par
conflict cutting about 35000 trees to provide more energy
\par
for the eighties versus keep the trees to provide good
\par
air was solved by law-court. Result: Keep the trees and
\par
provide more energy. Consequence: Population in central dis-
\par
tricts has to suffer more pollution in the air.
\par
Keywords. Air pollution; decision theory; environment con-
\par
trol; game theory; modelling; pollution; social and behaviour-
\par
al sciences; urban systems.
\par
GOALS
\par
Object of this paper is to examine the
\par
so-called power plant in
\par
West Berlin. a power plant which has
\par
been planned until 1976 but for which
\par
realization was stopped in 1977.
\par
First goal is to show that models for
\par
energy demand and supply are only one
\par
element in the field of planning power
\par
plants. They appear in the planning
\par
phase of a power plant in the form of
\par
expert opinions. Their effect should not
\par
be overestimated. because for each ex-
\par
pert opinion there will exist a differ-
\par
ent one.
\par
Second goal is to show that the whole
\par
process leading to the
\par
realization of a power plant is one
\par
which can well be modeled by means of
\par
systems analysis. In the past this has
\par
not been done to the extend possible.
\par
There is no reason why it should not be
\par
done. because the rules laid down for
\par
public administrations (on national le-
\par
vel) are laid down in written form.
\par
The persons. groups. departments of pub-
\par
lic administration. and law-courts which
\par
have to co-operate are clearly defined;
\par
their interests may be in opposition.
\par
The power plant Oberjagerweg is an exam-
\par
ple which shows how the different admini-
\par
strative levels and groups may act and
\par
react in the Federal Republic of Germany
\par
to provide or to prevent the realization
\par
of a particular power plant. It is not an
\par
atomic power plant and therefore the dis-
\par
- 453 -
\par
- 454 -
\par
cussion is free from many of the emotio-
\par
nal arguments usually found in those
\par
projects. On the other hand, it is my
\par
hypothesis that the problems of attai-
\par
ning special investments in the energy
\par
sector are the same. To make this hypo-
\par
thesis plain is the third goal.
\par
APPROACH
\par
The approach used to verify the three
\par
goals in this report is the following:
