CONCLUSIONS
\par
The previous example should not be
\par
overestimated because West Berlin has
\par
a special geographical and political
\par
situation. But it shows evidently:
\par
(1] Citizens or citizen groups. using
\par
legal instruments. have a possibi-
\par
lity to control large planning
\par
projects on the field in
\par
the Federal Republic of Germany.
\par
In the case of the power plant
\par
Oberjagerweg this control lead
\par
to a quite different solution of
\par
the power plant than either the
\par
administration nor the citizen
\par
groups had thought before.
\par
- 459 -
\par
(2] The conflict 35000 trees to be cut
\par
versus less energy in the eighties
\par
was not accepted in this form by
\par
the law-court oberverwaltungsge-
\par
richt. The oVG solved the conflict
\par
at a very early For this
\par
court the goods need of energy
\par
and interest in the forest (who
\par
provides 800 tons of oxygen) were
\par
both high economic values. The so-
\par
lution was to provide both. and
\par
population has in consequence to
\par
suffer more pollution of the air.
\par
(3) The senate of West Berlin followed
\par
hints of the law-court oVG and
\par
planned the location of new power
\par
plants near to the present power
\par
plant in Ruhleben (so-called Ernst-
\par
Reuter-Kraftwerk). One is obliged
\par
to say. the law-court is respon-
\par
sible for urban and regional plan-
\par
ning; but this argument is merely
\par
superficial. Rather the law-court
\par
has stated that normal planning
\par
procedures have not been observed.
\par
The more it has stated that ad-
\par
ministration is not allowed to
\par
agree with pre-decisions. here:
\par
cutting of trees. which give a
\par
point of no return in the decision
\par
process before its end.
\par
(4) According to the first goal of the
\par
report the items show. where
\par
(simple) models for prognosis of
\par
energy demand and supply have been
\par
used. They appear in the expert
\par
opinions of Schafer. Kisker. Bauer.
\par
Knigge et.al {$\bullet\bullet$} and are in details
\par
opposite. Surprising is. that the
\par
law-court oVG could refer to all
\par
these expert opinions because no
\par
expert denied the need of a future
\par
supply of additional energy in
\par
West Berlin. only the year of this
\par
supply was in dispute. Therefore
\par
we can say that these studies had
\par
only second priority in the de-
\par
cision process.
\par
(5] According to the second goal the
\par
items of the discussion process
\par
show indeed that there is a chance
\par
to model this discussion process.
\par
Not in this sense that the result
\par
is predicted. but in the sense
\par
that all stages of the decision
\par
are well known. One can make
\par
- 460 -
\par
checklists of all licences which are
\par
necessary in the process of planning.
\par
of all administration departments which
\par
have to cooperate. and so on. The
\par
sources for these informations are the
\par
laws for administration. laws for buil-
\par
dings like Bundesbaugestz (BBauG) in
\par
the Federal Republic of Germany. They
\par
may be different in different countries.
\par
but they exist. Having this list one
\par
can make simple systems analysis. in
\par
form of flow diagrams or in form of
\par
lists with activities. to show what is
\par
possible in which time. Time delays in
\par
project planning are then normal and
\par
necessary in the realization process of
\par
a power plant. when the planning phase
\par
is superficial. In some sense the flow
\par
diagramm of ongoing of the planning
\par
process is an interface between models
\par
for future demand and supply of energy
\par
on the one side. and the realization
\par
of a special project on the other side.
