\par
deals with the costs of achieving informational and allocational efficiency. \emph{See }Friend, \emph{The Economic}
\par
\emph{Consequences of the Stock Market, }\textbf{62 }AM. \textbf{ECON. REV. 212 }(May \textbf{1972). }The mandatory disclosure
\par
system of the securities laws and rules promote informational efficiency directly, and allocational
\par
efficiency indirectly. Because the market will incorporate false information as well as true information,
\par
the ability of investors to determine the credibility of the information directly affects the allocational
\par
efficiency of the market.\textbf{1989] }\emph{Management Forecasts }\textbf{\emph{1}}
\par
Article examines the research on forecasting methods and the applicability of
\par
statistical models to company financial forecasts. It surveys the newly promulgated
\par
guidelines for accountant's review of forecasts and discusses their value. Finally,
\par
the Article offers suggestions for expanded disclosure accompanying a forecast
\par
to assist investors in assessing its reliability. Improving the reliability of financial
\par
forecasts will enhance their usefulness, reduce the potential to mislead investors,
\par
and improve allocational market efficiency. Because company forecasts convey
\par
important information to shareholders, public disclosure of such forecasts should
