II. \textbf{STANDARDS }FOR LEGAL \textbf{LIABIITY }FOR FORECASTS-CASE LAW
\par
\textbf{\emph{A. }}\emph{Historical Evolution of Opinions and Predictions as Fraudulent: Do}
\par
\emph{Predictions Constitute Material "Facts"?}
\par
Statutory and common-law prohibitions on fraudulent misrepresentations
\par
typically require communication of a "material fact." Material fact, however,
\par
can be an elusive concept because the content of statements can involve diverse
\par
types of information characterized \textbf{by }various levels of concreteness. At one end
\par
of the spectrum are hard facts such as past events or certain verifiable items.
\par
The middle level includes subjective, present intentions concerning future actions
\par
within the control of the speaker. The other end of the spectrum consists of
\par
opinions or predictions of future events substantially beyond the control of the
\par
speaker. The latter two levels are "soft information," which the courts have
\par
treated differently from the first level or so-called "hard information."
\par
The early law of deceit or fraudulent misrepresentation evolved out of a
\par
milieu where commercial business transactions were thought to occur at arms
\par
length and, therefore, required few legal protections. Consequently, the early
\par
common-law rule held that only misleading statements of facts could constitute
\par
fraud. Courts did not impose liability on speakers for their opinions because
\par
opinions were considered only assertions of individual beliefs."2 Concomitantly,
\par
predictions regarding future events also were not considered sufficiently factual
\par
to support a claim for fraud. 3 Some recent federal securities law cases have
