\par
of the parties-such as the speaker's status as an agent or fiduciary,- tle
\par
presence of cautionary language, and the speaker's expertise or special knowledge
\par
in the area.? These factors affect the possibility that a listener will accept the
\par
statement as credible, and therefore rely on it. When it is probable that an
\par
investor would accept a statement as believable, such a statement should not
\par
be excluded from the securities laws, whether it involves hard or soft information.
