\emph{B. Misrepresentations or Untruthful Statements}
\par
\emph{1. Good Faith and Reasonable Basis}
\par
Many courts refused to accept predictions in support of fraud claims believing
\par
that because no one can guarantee the future, such statements were mere opinions
\par
that could not be relied upon by another.30 As this absolutist approach began
\par
to break down, courts became more willing to acknowledge the possibility of
\par
justifiable reliance with regard to statements of future events. Once the possibility
\par
of justifiable reliance was accepted, judges had to determine the veracity of an
\par
opinion. Soft information, which lacks the objective verifiability of hard facts
\par
and involves various levels of certitude, necessarily has been treated as conveying
\par
a different sort of message to recipients. Consequently, the courts have applied
\par
the concept of implied representations. At common law, an opinion or prediction
\par
implied that the speaker knew of no facts that would prevent its being fulfilled, 3 '
\par
and that there were facts warranting the prediction.32
