\par
These cases demonstrate the variety of possible approaches for determining
\par
the level of assurance properly anticipated by an investor. The high probability
\par
standard attempts to promote greater certainty-approaching that of hard
\par
information-by requiring a high level of support for the forecaster's reasonable
\par
basis for the predictions. This standard retains vestiges of the early era of
\par
securities regulation, which attempted to protect even naive investors. The high
\par
probability standard, however, is unsuitable for a forecast disclosure policy for
\par
several reasons. First, numerous factors affect forecast accuracy, and the impact
\par
of specific factors varies for individual firms. Second, forecasting ability differs
\par
with respect to particular factors or events that affect different industries. Third,
\par
accuracy varies with the forecast horizon. Consequently, among a large number
\par
of firms and different forecast horizons, forecast accuracy is likely to be quite
\par
dispersed. Imposing a high level of certainty on company forecasters is unrealistic
\par
and would subject some companies to an unacceptably high level of scrutiny.
\par
The more lenient reasonable basis standard attempts to accommodate the
\par
uncertain nature of prospective information. Nevertheless, adopting a low level
\par
of accuracy or enabling a company easily to disavow assurances with vague
\par
terms would seriously undermine the value of forecasts. Obviously, the standard
\par
for the degree of confidence and expected error rate attached to a forecast
\par
affects the good faith and reasonable basis factors. As the accepted level of
\par
certainty is lowered, the disciplining character of the two tests decreases. For
\par
a forecast to convey useful information to the shareholder, it must have some
\par
reasonable measure of reliability. Where determining good faith and reasonable
\par
basis becomes more difficult because the standards for measuring the meaning
\par
of a projection are too permissive, the reliability of the forecast is weakened.
\par
\emph{(b) Suggested Standards}
\par
\emph{(i) Confidence Level}
\par
The best solution to this problem is to embellish the forecast statement
\par
with more specific information about expected accuracy.1 0 Where this information
\par
is absent, the courts should fashion a more definite standard than that of
\par
reasonableness. Under the "best efforts" or moderate reasonableness standard,
\par
it is unclear whether the projected result is expected to be more likely than all
\par
of the other possible outcomes combined, or merely more likely than any \emph{one}
\par
of the possible outcomes on an individual comparison of pairs basis. 7' To enhance
\par
the value of forecasts, the courts should adopt a "more likely than not" standard
\par
for the confidence level attached to a forecast. In other words, the basis for
\par
the forecast should be sufficient to support a conclusion by the forecaster that
\par
it is more likely than not that the forecast will be achieved. This standard is
\par
less onerous than the high probability standard, yet high enough to insure that
\par
predictions are useful to investors. The vast majority of companies should be
\par
able to generate forecasts in the form of specific figures or a range that are
\par
\textbf{70. }\emph{See }\textbf{\emph{infra }}notes \textbf{300-12 }and accompanying text.
\par
\textbf{71. }The current \textbf{SEC }policy on this issue is that a company projection should represent
\par
management's opinion on "the most probable specific amount or the \textbf{most }reasonable range for
\par
each financial item projected." \textbf{17 }C.F.R. \S{} \textbf{229.10(b)(2) (1988). }This formula offers little real
\par
improvement over that used in the case law. \textbf{\emph{See infra }}notes \textbf{150-51 }and accompanying text.\textbf{1989]}
\par
\emph{Management Forecasts}
\par
more likely than not to occur. The predicted figure should have a \textbf{fifty }percent
\par
probability of occurring or of falling within the predicted range. When this level
\par
of certainty cannot be achieved for a particular forecast, a company can and
\par
should so indicate, and provide a specific probability figure. In this way,
\par
companies will not be prevented from issuing forecasts with a less than \textbf{fifty}
\par
percent probability, but they will have to provide explicit information on their
\par
confidence level.
\par
Such a rule would provide several benefits. First, both investors and forecasters
\par
would have a clearer standard on which to base their actions. Investors could
\par
justifiably rely on a forecast being sufficiently probable that it could be used
\par
in investment decisions. Reliability would be enhanced \textbf{by }preventing forecasters
\par
from escaping liability \textbf{by }creating ambiguity through the use of vague cautionary
\par
language. Companies would have a clearer rule for additional disclosures.
\par
Second, it would clarify a chaotic and equivocal aspect of the law and
\par
facilitate application of the good faith and reasonable basis tests. The "more
\par
likely than not" rule would provide a standard for evaluating the strength of
\par
the factual support for the forecast, which would aid both companies in their
\par
initial development of the forecast and the courts in determining whether the
\par
basis was reasonable. It would facilitate assessing the sincerity or good faith of
\par
companies \textbf{by }creating a guideline understandable to management. The failure
\par
of the courts to establish a more exact standard of certainty or confidence level
\par
for projections has contributed to the ambiguity in this area of the law.
\par
\textbf{\emph{(ii) }}\emph{Expected and Actual Error Rate}
\par
Unlike the issue of confidence levels, the variety of potentially valid levels
\par
of expected error rates cautions against setting a rigid inaccuracy figure for this
\par
element. The variances in expected error rates for different industries and forecast
\par
horizons prevents setting an arbitrary single expected error rate for establishing
\par
the existence of a reasonable basis for a forecast. Companies in \textbf{highly }stable
\par
industries should be held to a higher standard for an expected error rate than
\par
companies in \textbf{highly }volatile industries where forecasting is more difficult. \textbf{All}
\par
companies' forecasting systems should be designed to minimize potential forecast
\par
error. However, attempting to require all firms to have their forecasts be within
\par
a uniform error rate level is unrealistic and could deprive investors of relevant
