\par
or reckless conduct because it was so exiguous that it amounted to practically
\par
no care at all would eviscerate the reasonable basis test. This approach applies
\par
a single scienter standard to the defendant's action. A more appropriate approach
\par
would harmonize the two criteria by applying the scienter standard to the initial
\par
reasonable basis standard. In other words, a defendant should be liable when
\par
the facts provide an inference that he knew, or was reckless in not realizing,
\par
that he did not have a reasonable basis for the forecast. This approach
\par
accommodates the different concerns of the misrepresentation and scienter elements
\par
and is based on the content of the forecast statement. The broad approach
\par
would revise the meaning of the statement to that of an implied representation
