The SEC intended to foster voluntary forecast disclosure by companies with
\par
its lenient policy. While a certain amount of flexibility is necessary with pro-
\par
spective information, the amorphous standards currently applied by the courts
\par
and followed by the \textbf{SEC }impede the statutory goal of full and fair disclosure.
\par
Moreover, companies are reluctant to issue projections voluntarily because of
\par
the uncertainty surrounding the vague SEC policy. Arguably the courts are not
\par
equipped to promote disclosure aggressively, but the SEC, on the other hand,
\par
is charged with implementing the securities laws. The current SEC position on
\par
projections has failed to increase investors' access to this important information.
\par
The SEC should adopt a mandatory disclosure system in conjunction with specific
\par
guidelines designed to enhance the reliability of this information.
