IV. IMPROvING THE AccuRAcY OF MANAGEMENT FORECASTS
\par
\emph{A. The Use of Statistical Models}
\par
Although management and analyst's forecasts have been shown generally
\par
to be more accurate than those derived from statistical models, models still may
\par
be useful in improving the reliability of public forecasts. First, improvements
\par
in developing statistical models may lead to increased accuracy in management
\par
forecasts where firms utilize models as one input to generate their forecasts.
\par
Second, models can serve as an additional objective source of forecasts for
\par
comparison with management forecasts, thereby providing a check on manipulation
\par
by company management. Third, objective models employ established statistical
\par
methods for determining uncertainty in a forecast that are lacking in purely
\par
subjective methods of forecasting.
\par
\emph{1. Actual Practice in Corporate Forecasting}
\par
Although corporations employ some objective forecasting methods, they tend
\par
to rely most heavily on judgmental (subjective) methods for financial forecasting.
\par
In an early survey of large firms, eighty-nine percent utilized judgmental methods
\par
alone or in combination with other methods to forecast sales. 15 2 More than twice
