\par
macroeconomic financial data. 63 Improvements in the choice and use of statistical
\par
models would increase the accuracy of corporate forecasts. Mandatory forecast
\par
disclosure would provide an added incentive for firms to improve their forecasting
\par
systems. There is some evidence of a positive relationship between the degree
\par
of accuracy desired and the degree of accuracy actually achieved in forecasts.' 6'
\par
Forecast accuracy may be improved without extensive additional financial
\par
expenditures, by more efficient utilization of the data and resources already used
\par
internally, and adequate awareness of the research on forecasting techniques.
\par
\emph{2. Judgmental Forecasting}
\par
Research on judgmental forecasting reveals several problems associated with
\par
human psychological limitations. Recognition of these problems and appropriate
\par
use of objective methods could improve financial forecasting. This is consistent
\par
with a realistic view of the role of forecast disclosure policy, which should
\par
provide relevant information to investors in a manner that allows them to assess
\par
its reliability.
\par
Potential bias leading to erroneous predictions can arise in various stages
\par
of decision making including the acquisition, processing, and output of
\par
information. 6 Biases in acquisition of information include selective perception,
\par
illusory correlation of variables, and favoring concrete information over abstract
\par
information.' 66 When humans process information, it creates problems such as
