Biases particularly relevant to financial forecasting include overconfidence,
\par
underestimating uncertainty, creating illusory correlation or patterns, and anchoring.
\par
The availability of additional information increases forecasters' confidence in
\par
their predictions but does not lead necessarily to increased accuracy. 69 People
\par
often seek additional information that supports existing beliefs rather than
\par
disconfirming information. Anchoring occurs when the forecaster is predisposed
\par
toward an opinion. 70 For instance, a predisposition toward conservatism or
\par
optimism may be based on recent experience, which is related to the tendency
\par
to see illusory patterns in data. The combination of these biases may cause
\par
forecasters to underestimate uncertainty. \textbf{'7' }Corporate executives, however, typically
\par
have a keen awareness of uncertainty in financial forecasting.
