\par
(507o). Meanwhile, \textbf{MSE }assumes a quadratic loss function with large errors being considered worse
\par
than small errors, so squaring the error severely penalizes larger errors. While MSE has been used
\par
in comparisons of forecasts, the most common measure is MAPE because, as the percentage of
\par
realized value, MAPE provides a relative error measure useful for cross-sectional comparisons and
\par
is understood easily. ME is used primarily to test systematic error (bias) because it is affected by
\par
the fact that errors of different signs can cancel each other out. For a discussion of accuracy
\par
measures, see \textbf{S. WHEELwRi-r }\& \textbf{S. }MARInsuA\textasciitilde s, \textbf{FoRcASINo METHODS FOR MANAoEmENT }43-47
