Long-range forecasting generally covering time horizons over two years
\par
involves somewhat different considerations than shorter term projections. The
\par
three major components of the basic pattern in economic time-series are trend,
\par
cycle, and seasonality. Unexplained randomness constitutes a fourth factor. Long
\par
horizons are dominated by the trend factor with some influence from the cycle
\par
factor, whereas seasonality and randomness are most important in the short-
\par
term. Therefore, some of the simpler methods that do not take trend into account
\par
do well with short forecasts but perform poorly with longer period data and
\par
longer horizon forecasts. On the other hand, some sophisticated methods such
\par
as Box-Jenkins and Bayesian forecasting apparently extrapolate too much trend,
\par
resulting in poor long-term forecasts. Moreover, deseasonalizing data through
\par
a simple classical decomposition procedure reduces the accuracy variances of
\par
different methods. This procedure leads to very similar performance by both
\par
simple and sophisticated models that employ seasonal data for shorter periods
\par
(monthly or quarterly), provided seasonality is a factor.'8 9 Randomness is a
\par
