\textbf{\emph{3. }}\emph{Regression Methods}
\par
The financial forecast studies mentioned above concentrated on extrapolation
\par
methods, which rely on past data of the item being forecasted. An alternative
\par
approach is to use causal methods, which develop forecasts from the relationships
\par
of dependent and independent variables.238 These methods have not been used
\par
frequently in cross-sectional comparative studies of firm financial forecasts partly
\par
because they must be developed from individual firm-specific characteristics.
\par
Nonetheless, some research indicates that they represent a viable alternative to
\par
time-series models for earnings forecasting.
\par
For instance, Chant found that at least two simple causal models competed
\par
favorably with pure extrapolation methods. He analyzed three time-series models
\par
(an average growth model, a single exponential smoothing model, and the
\par
martingale model), and three lead-indicator models (a money supply model, a
\par
stock index model, and a bank loan model). 2 3 9 Comparing 2180 forecasts by
\par
each model between 1968 and 1977, Chant found that the money supply model
\par
was statistically significantly better than the others, while the bank loan model
\par
was the worst.2 4 The stock index, martingale, and average growth models were
\par
not significantly different, but were much better than the single exponential
\par
smoothing model.24' The money supply model had the lowest MAPE in six of
\par
the nine years, and the stock index model had the lowest MAPE in three years. 2 4
