\textbf{\emph{A. }}\emph{The Feasibility of Long-Term Forecasting}
\par
Critics of mandatory management forecasts fear that this policy would focus
\par
too much attention on the short-term. For example, some argue that management
\par
would forego long-term projects in favor of enhancing short-term earnings, and
\par
investors might be misled into investing in firms with favorable short-term but
\par
poor long-term prospects. Either circumstance could lead to an inefficient allocation
\par
of economic resources.2 This argument is overstated given that current public
\par
forecasts tend to be short-term, a year ahead or less. In addition, corporate
\par
disclosure of quarterly financial performance already fosters a short-term market
\par
mentality. Therefore, management forecasts would not substantially shorten
\par
investment time horizons.
