\par
\emph{B. New Models}
\par
The results of the Forecasting Competition has stimulated additional research
\par
in new forecasting methods. In particular, two new approaches to handling the
\par
trend component appear to provide some improved accuracy in certain situations
\par
compared to the better models in the Forecasting Competition. First, Gardner
\par
and McKenzie have developed a variant of the Holt model (the Holt-D model)
\par
that adds a parameter for dampening the trend component. 25 9 The addition of
\par
this parameter permits varying levels of extrapolation of trend based on the
\par
past volatility of the time-series, making it more adaptable to different types
\par
of data and various forecast horizons.
\par
Utilizing the same data as the Forecasting Competition, the authors compared
\par
the accuracy of the Holt-D model to that of Lewandowski's FORSYS, Parzen's
\par
method, and the standard Holt model. The accuracy of the two Holt models
\par
was similar for the shorter horizon forecasts, but Holt-D was more accurate
\par
for longer horizons.m Some differences also existed according to the type of
\par
data, with Holt-D exhibiting superiority over the standard Holt for quarterly
\par
and monthly data, but the two models had very analogous accuracy for yearly
\par
data.21 This occurred because the yearly data was less erratic and contained a
\par
strong trend characteristic, which necessitated much less dampening of the trend.
\par
In comparison with the more sophisticated models, Holt-D was substantially
\par
more accurate for short horizon forecasts than Lewandowski's or Parzen's
\par
methods.26 2 Differences between the three methods were not dramatic for the
\par
middle horizons (two to eight-steps ahead). For the longer horizons, both
\par
Lewandowski's and Parzen's tended to be somewhat more accurate.2 6 3 These
\par
findings indicate that Holt-D may be especially useful for short and middle
\par
horizon forecasts for companies with more volatile performance characteristics.
\par
The benefits of the Holt-D model include its less complex nature relative to the
\par
more sophisticated extrapolation methods such as Parzen's, a general familiarity
\par
with its basic elements (because it is derived from the standard Holt model),
\par
and its competitive accuracy over several forecast horizons.
\par
Similarly, Carbone and Makridakis recently proposed a new extrapolation
\par
method (the C-M method) for use especially with business and economic time-
\par
