\textbf{VI. }SELECTING \textbf{MODELS}
\par
Research on the numerous statistical models demonstrates that their abilities
\par
vary with the particular situation. Using an appropriate model can improve
\par
greatly the accuracy of forecasting, but one first must identify the better models.
\par
A traditional test of the quality of a model is how well the model "fits" the
\par
data used to estimate (calibrate) its components. Unfortunately, a model that
\par
closely fits past data may not provide superior \emph{ex ante }forecasts because of
\par
changing patterns and relationships among the economic variables. 272 A superior
\par
test for assessing a model's quality is to imitate the forecasting situation.
\par
