\par
successive updating. Under this technique, historical data covering a period long
\par
enough to generate forecasts for the required horizon is withheld from use in
\par
calibrating each model being evaluated. Then individual forecasts within that
\par
period are produced sequentially, using actual results to update the data used
\par
to recalibrate the model for the next period forecast. This procedure results in
\par
a series of forecasts for each model for different horizons that is then compared
\par
to the actual results for accuracy.
\par
There are two variations of this successive updating technique that can be
\par
used. The first variation, called \emph{ex post }actual data updating, involves holding
\par
out data for a certain period, then generating individual forecasts with similar
\par
horizons using updated \emph{actual }data to calibrate the model after each forecast
\par
is produced. The second variation, called \emph{ex post }forecast data updating, involves
\par
utilizing \emph{forecasted }values to recalibrate the model for each successive forecast
\par
within the holdout period. Several forecasts for the desired horizon are generated
\par
given the amount of historical data available using both methods, with the
\par
average error rate being calculated from the known actual results. The error
\par
rates of the models tested then can be compared easily. The two variations
\par
differ in that with the \emph{ex post }forecast data update method, the model is not
\par
recalibrated with updated actual data after each forecast. As a result, the forecast
\par
data update method tends to give a better indication of the range of errors
\par
with longer term forecasts. These techniques give a better evaluation of how a
\par
model handles the type of changing patterns that previously have been experienced,
\par
as well as the typical forecast error.
\par
\emph{B. Combined Forecasts}
\par
Another option for selecting models is to utilize several models to produce
\par
a combined forecast. Combining forecasts often results in accuracy superior to
\par
the use of a single model forecast.2 73 The main techniques for combining forecasts
\par
include simple averages, weighted averages, and judgmental merging. Judgmental
\par
merging relies on the forecaster's subjective ability to evaluate the precision of
\par
the various models to be combined. Although this method takes advantage of
\par
the broader information base of human forecasters, it is still susceptible to some
\par
of the biases and weaknesses of judgmental forecasting. 274 The other techniques
\par
are objective methods that eliminate some potential bias.
\par
The simple averages method is easy to apply, but gives equal weight to the
\par
forecasts of each model. In an attempt to improve the combination procedure,
\par
various methods for assigning weights to the individual forecasts have been
