\textbf{\emph{C. }}\emph{Correcting Forecasts}
\par
In addition to using appropriate models, forecasts may be improved \textbf{by }a
\par
systematic evaluation of past forecasting performance. Statistical analysis of the
\par
error patterns of prior forecasts affords forecasters insight into some of the
\par
causes for inaccuracy. Utilizing the mean squared error \textbf{(IvSE) }measure, forecast
\par
inaccuracy can be broken down into errors attributable to bias, association
\par
inefficiency, and random causes. Bias involves the tendency to overestimate or
\par
underestimate relative to the average actual results. Association inefficiency
\par
concerns a propensity to overestimate when the values are low or below average,
\par
and underestimate when the values are high or above average. \textbf{By }decomposing
\par
the historical forecasting error series, the extent of influence of these three factors
\par
may be identified and future forecasts revised to reduce the two controllable
\par
factors-bias and association inefficiency.
\par
