\textbf{\emph{A. }}\emph{Assessing Uncertainty}
\par
The potential for misleading investors represents a major concern of opponents
\par
to mandatory forecast disclosure. This problem may be combated \textbf{by }the inclusion
\par
of additional disclosure items that will aid recipients in assessing the reliability
\par
of the forecast. Additional disclosure also would mitigate potential claims of
\par
fraud against companies. Cautionary statements on the inherent uncertainty of
\par
forecasts have been recognized universally as beneficial \textbf{by }the courts,30 {$^\circ$} the
\par
\textbf{SEC,30' }and commentators.3 2 These general statements concerning the inability
\par
of management to guarantee absolute accuracy and the general nature of uncertainty
\par
in forecasting future events should be aimed primarily at the more unsophisticated
\par
shareholders. Moreover, a specific cautionary statement as to greater inaccuracy
\par
with increasing forecast horizons should accompany long-term projections. In
\par
addition, assumptions concerning major factors that could affect forecast accuracy
\par
should be discussed.
