produce fewer large errors; and (2) the accuracy of an overall better model will
\par
tend not to greatly exceed that of a combined forecast. Second, rarely will an
\par
individual model have a narrower error range than a combination of models.
\par
Third, most of the benefits of combined forecasts may be achieved \textbf{by }a simple
\par
averaging of relatively few of the less sophisticated models. 320 Fourth, the combining
\par
process creates a body of forecast estimates of various model combinations from
\par
which statistical uncertainty measures such as variance and standard deviation
\par
can be developed with less reliance on possibly outdated historical data.
\par
Consequently, managements' potential liability for disclosure of inaccurate or
\par
misleading information would be greatly diminished. The difficulty of selecting
\par
the best model for comparative purposes would decrease because accuracy becomes
\par
less sensitive as the number of models combined increases.
\par
Statistical models represent a potentially important source of valuable
\par
information for shareholders, as well as for management. Statistical models can
\par
furnish objective alternative forecasts that serve as benchmarks for comparison
\par
with management forecasts. Further, statistical variance and error measures for
\par
model forecasts provide information on uncertainty generally applicable to the
\par
specific firm's forecasting situation. This type of supplementary information also
\par
benefits management because it reduces the threat of potential liability from
\par
misleading disclosures. Fuller disclosure demonstrating the uncertainty of the
\par
forecasting process would dissuade shareholders from placing inordinate credibility
\par
on management's forecast. In addition, this information supplies useful parameters
\par
from which shareholders can derive reasonable expectations. Moreover,
\par
management should be creating model forecasts as inputs to their forecasting
