The most difficult issue involving disclosure of assumptions is not whether
\par
it should be mandatory, but rather the scope of disclosure. Despite the SEC's
\par
reluctance to require mandatory disclosure of assumptions for purposes of its
\par
safe harbor rule, the statutory half-truth principle prohibiting omission of
\par
information needed to make published information not misleading, applies to
\par
forecasting assumptions. Assumptions must be disclosed if they involve "material"
\par
information. The courts have eschewed establishing specific criteria, but have
\par
identified a two-prong probability/impact test involving the probability of
\par
occurrence and the magnitude of the impact as major factors in determining
\par
