\emph{2. New AICPA Position}
\par
The AICPA recently promulgated new guidelines for accountants' involvement
\par
with forecasts. In \textbf{1985, }the AICPA issued a Statement on Standards \textbf{(SOS)}
\par
entitled \emph{Financial Forecasts and Projections, 56 }and a companion \emph{Guide.3 1 7 }The
\par
\emph{Guide, }which incorporates the \textbf{SOS, }establishes presentation and performance
\par
guidelines for prospective financial information, and supersedes the prior standards.
\par
These new guidelines attempt to create a comprehensive set of standards to
\par
facilitate understanding by users of financial statements of the accountant's role
\par
with respect to prospective information. This action signals the accounting
\par
profession's recognition of the increased use of future-oriented financial data.
\par
The new guidelines utilize procedures mentioned in the old guidelines, but
\par
further clarify an auditor's responsibilities. The new guidelines provide new
\par
definitions for a "financial forecast" and a "financial projection." A financial
\par
forecast consists of "prospective financial statements that present, \emph{to the best}
\par
\emph{of the responsible party's knowledge and belief, }an entity's \emph{expected }financial
\par
position ... . \textbf{"" }A financial projection is defined as "prospective financial
\par
statements that present, to the best of the responsible party's knowledge and
\par
belief, \emph{given one or more hypothetical assumptions, }an entity's expected financial
\par
position . \textbf{. . . 359 }A financial forecast is now a presentation of management's
\par
actual expectations; whereas a financial projection represents a what-if scenario
\par
that management does not necessarily believe will occur. Under the new standards,
\par
only financial forecasts are approved for "general use," that is where third
\par
parties not directly negotiating with the company are expected to use the prospective
\par
information, such as general public disclosure.3 1 Financial projections may be
\par
used to supplement a forecast for "general use," but otherwise, they are restricted
\par
to "limited use," that is when third parties have direct contacts with the
\par
361
\par
company.
\par
The new guidelines eliminated the "most probable results" and "possible
\par
but not necessarily the most probable" terminology contained in the old definitions
\par
of financial forecast and financial projection because these terms created confusion
\par
and were difficult to apply in practice. 362 Many companies interpreted the old
