\emph{B. Empirical Evidence on the Importance of an Accountant's Review}
\par
\emph{1. Stock Market Reaction Studies}
\par
In an attempt to determine the usefulness or informational content of an
\par
accountant's review of company financial statements, researchers have examined
\par
stock price movements surrounding the release of qualified audit reports. Although
\par
the belief that independent review offers benefits to investors is well established,
\par
the empirical evidence based on market reaction to auditor report announcements
\par
is equivocal. Several studies failed to discover significant price movements associated
\par
with disclosures of qualified audit reports. For example, one review of weekly
\par
stock price returns for 145 firms between \textbf{1973 }and \textbf{1978 }found no statistically
\par
significant abnormal returns associated with "subject to" audit reports expressing
\par
certain qualifications. 3 Another review of daily stock returns for 147 firms with
\par
qualified reports between \textbf{1968 }and \textbf{1975 }reported no significant abnormal
\par
performance for the period immediately surrounding disclosure based on the
\par
filing date of the Form 10-K.39 4
\par
Meanwhile, other research does reveal significant stock price reaction to
\par
release of unfavorable audit reports. In a \textbf{1978 }study of 247 British firms, Firth
\par
discovered statistically significant negative price reactions related to qualified
\par
audit reports involving general, going concern, and asset value problems. 39 5 \textbf{A}
\par
\textbf{1982 }survey of ninety firms for \textbf{1973 }and 1974 \textbf{by }Chow and Rice also found
\par
significant downward price movements surrounding disclosure of questionable
\par
audit reports.39
