However, the reliability of these information content studies has been
\par
challenged. One criticism is that the methodology used in the studies weakens
\par
their conclusions. For instance, one commentator contends that the pre-1980
\par
research, including Firth's study, suffers from problems such as the failure to
\par
identify a precise release date for the announcement, and the presence of
\par
confounding financial information. 3 97 Because audit reports usually accompany
\par
the financial statements, there is often a simultaneous release of various kinds
\par
of information about the firm. Also, earnings announcements can occur near
\par
the disclosure date of the audit reports. These additional disclosures make it
\par
difficult to determine whether the market reaction was in response to the audit
\par
report or to other information..
\par
The later studies attempted to mitigate some of the methodological problems
\par
encountered in the pre-1980 research. They identified the announcement date
\par
more accurately and measured stock returns over a longer period to include
\par
possible prior price movements based on investor expectations of a qualified
\par
report. They tried to offset the effects of contemporaneous earnings information
\par
by eliminating firms whose unexpected actual earnings change differed in sign,
\par
positive or negative, from expected earnings, which could cause an offsetting
\par
opposite stock price reaction. Nevertheless, these studies utilized different methods
\par
for deriving the "unexpected" earnings change and may not have sufficiently
\par
eliminated confounding effects because of the failure to account adequately for
\par
the magnitude of "unexpected" earnings. 3" In addition, the levels of confidence,
\par
\emph{i.e. }statistical significance, associated with the measured market reactions varied
\par
with the type of audit qualification. The low levels of statistical significance for
\par
some qualifications support only a weak conclusion of information content.
\par
Two current studies utilizing improved methodology also present conflicting
\par
results. In a review of 283 firms between 1973 and 1980, Dodd, Dopuch,
\par
Holthausen, and Leftwich found little evidence of significant abnormal stock
\par
price reactions associated with disclosure of qualified audit reports. 3" The authors
\par
attempted to identify more accurately the date of public disclosure, and deal
\par
with confounding earnings information and existing market expectations. After
\par
determining that qualified opinions are generally not reported in public news
