\par
of independence and quality of performance.
\par
Accountants clearly can perform a useful role in the public disclosure of
\par
company forecasts. The practice of auditor review in other areas of financial
\par
disclosure has created a general public expectation of independent review. There
\par
is some evidence that shareholders want review of forecasts by accountants. 421
\par
Review of forecasts will help deter management from attempting fraud, and
\par
enhance the mathematical reliability of forecasting systems. Earlier questions on
\par
the responsibility and expertise of accountants relating to forecasts have now
\par
generally been resolved with the promulgation of new standards by the AICPA.
\par
The new guidelines create standardized procedures for comparability of reports
\par
across firms, and clarify for users of reports the extent and type of review
\par
conducted in the audit of forecasts. Accountants are much more familiar with
\par
this kind of information because of the growing use of future-oriented information
\par
in business, and thus are better able to evaluate its reliability. Third party review
\par
is cost effective because it provides a more extensive, thorough review by a
\par
single entity of the support for assumptions and the forecast generating process
\par
than could be conducted by individual shareholders. The potential that investors
\par
will give excessive credibility to a forecast that has been reviewed by accountants
\par
can be handled with disclosure of accompanying information and cautionary
\par
statements. The variety of stock market responses to qualified reports shown
\par
in the empirical research indicates that investors have a realistic appreciation
\par
for the limitations of audit reviews. A mandatory disclosure policy can provide
\par
sufficient reliability of forecasts by combining direct presentation of a reasonable
\par
amount of accompanying information and third-party review.
\par
IX. \textbf{CONCLUSION}
\par
The current treatment of prospective information clearly is inadequate.
\par
Future-oriented information concerning the financial performance of a firm relates
\par
directly to the investment decision. The value of a firm's stock depends on the
\par
future earnings stream of the firm. Forecasts of future performance are the
\par
most explicit type of future-oriented information; and the lively market in analyst
\par
forecasts demonstrates investors' desire for this kind of information. Company
\par
forecasts represent potentially valuable information for investors. Indeed, both
\par
the SEC and the courts acknowledge the relevance of company forecasts.
\par
While the courts developed a basic approach that permits public company
\par
forecasts made in good faith and with a reasonable basis, these criteria suffer
\par
from ambiguity. The good faith test involves elements that conflict with con-
\par
gressional intent and statutory law, and should be abandoned. The courts also
\par
should establish clearer rules on the acceptable confidence level in the forecast
\par
to satisfy the reasonable basis test.
\par
Given the relevance of company forecasts, some commentators have called
\par
on the SEC to adopt a mandatory disclosure policy. Other commentators,
\par
however, caution that disclosure of company forecasts could be detrimental. The
\par
