\emph{Management Forecasts}
\par
\textbf{SEC }proposed an ambitious program designed to foster more extensive and
\par
equitable disclosure of company forecasts, but soon abandoned it.
\par
The problem of reliability represents perhaps the chief objection to mandatory
\par
disclosure. There are several ways to enhance the reliability of management
\par
forecasts. For instance, accuracy may be improved if companies utilized statistical
\par
models to a greater extent than in the past. Research shows that use of simpler
\par
models, linear correction, and combined forecasts can lead to more accurate
\par
predictions. Further, new models have been developed that are adaptable to
\par
various types of data series and forecast horizons. Modern computers make
\par
generating forecasts with statistical models easier and less expensive.
\par
More importantly, the \textbf{SEC }could require disclosure of additional information
\par
that will allow investors to assess the reliability of the company forecast. One
\par
extremely useful category of information is a probability distribution associated
\par
with the forecast. Supplying probability and variance figures gives investors clear,
\par
direct information on the likelihood of achieving a forecast and the risk that
\par
it may be inaccurate. Second, the company should furnish a supplementary
\par
alternative forecast derived from a statistical model or models. The combining
\par
of forecasts from various models would be especially appropriate for this purpose
\par
because of the lower variance of combined forecasts. This alternative forecast
\par
would serve as an objective benchmark suitable for comparison with the com-
\par
pany's judgmental forecast. The model forecast. also would complement the
\par
probability distribution data. Third, the company should outline and describe
\par
material assumptions underlying the forecast. Materiality of assumptions can be
\par
determined according to the probability/impact formula with the impact prong
\par
being based on an average variance of past years.
\par
Independent third-party review of forecasts constitutes another important
\par
method of enhancing reliability. The AICPA recently promulgated a Statement
\par
on Standards and comprehensive guidelines for accountant review of prospective
\par
information. Earlier concerns about the lack of standards or accountant's un-
\par
familiarity with future-oriented information are no longer valid. The new guide-
\par
lines have evolved from knowledge gained by accountants through increasing
\par
experience with this information, particularly since the \textbf{SEC }changed its original
\par
restrictive policy. Fears that an accountant's review would create an unwarranted
\par
aura of credibility for forecasts are unfounded, and this potential problem would
\par
be mitigated by providing additional disclosure items on reliability.
\par
The current \textbf{SEC }policy is an abdication of the Commission's obligation to
\par
assist investors. The safe harbor rule does little more than mirror existing case
\par
law. The SEC's reliance on voluntary disclosure combined with the ambiguity
\par
of its policy has contributed to minimal and inequitable disclosure of company
\par
forecasts in the United States. The time has come for the \textbf{SEC }to re-evaluate
\par
its experimental position. Adoption of a mandatory disclosure policy is more
\par
feasible when combined with required disclosure of additional information relating
\par
to the reliability of the forecast and independent third-party review. Expanded
\par
release of reliable future-oriented information will improve the stock market's
\par
allocational efficiency by allowing investors to make more informed investment
\par
decisions. The present \textbf{SEC }position on soft information has resulted in insuf-
\par
ficient disclosure of company forecasts. The adoption of more explicit standards
\par
focused on furnishing information that permits investors to assess the reliability
\par
of the forecast will benefit both investors and company managements, and reduce
\par
the potential for misleading shareholders.},
