\par
\textbf{III. FORECAST ACCURACY}
\par
Any thorough discussion of forecast disclosure policy cannot deal with
\par
management forecasts in isolation. One indication of the importance of fore-
\par
cast information is the lively market in forecasts \textbf{by }securities analysts. To
\par
the extent analysts already provide prospective information, any recommen-
\par
dation of mandatory disclosure of forecasts \textbf{by }companies must demonstrate
\par
the benefits of such a policy.22 1 A major issue affecting all forecasts is accu-
\par
racy. Traditionally, the accuracy of forecasts has been used-and misused-
\par
as a measure of their value. Accuracy is also relevant to the proper alloca-
\par
tion of social resources because investor's capital investment decisions are
\par
heavily influenced \textbf{by }forecast information. The courts focus on accuracy as
\par
the primary element in determining whether disclosure of existing company
\par
forecasts should be compelled under the federal securities laws.2 22 Examina-
\par
tion of the accuracy of analyst as well as of management forecasts provides
\par
some insight into the problems of forecasting.
\par
\emph{A. General Accuracy of Analyst and Management Forecasts}
\par
\textbf{\emph{1. }}\emph{Analysts' Forecast Accuracy}
\par
Attempts to determine the accuracy of analyst forecasts have involved
\par
various methods including surveys and empirical analysis. In an early re-
\par
search study \textbf{by }the Financial Analysts Federation \textbf{(FAF), }the group of port-
\par
folio managers surveyed indicated that 41\% of the analyst annual forecasts
\par
were accurate within \textbf{10\%, }and \textbf{93\% }were within 20\%.223 \textbf{A }review of fore-
\par
casts \textbf{by }several large institutions conducted for this same research project
\par
showed a \textbf{10-15\% }error rate in annual earnings forecasts for the "average"
\par
firm.224 The errors, however, varied greatly based on the particular indus-
\par
try.225 \textbf{A }review of analyst forecasts for the period \textbf{1972 }to \textbf{1976 }involving
\par
ninety-two New York Stock Exchange \textbf{(NYSE) }companies revealed error
\par
frequency rates of \textbf{30\% }or less in \textbf{84.8\%, }20\% or less in 71.4\%, and \textbf{10\% }or
\par
less in \textbf{50\% }of the forecasts. 226 \textbf{A }simple analysis of earnings forecasts ap-
\par
pearing in Standard \textbf{\& }Poor's \emph{Earnings Forecaster }between \textbf{1977 }and \textbf{1979}
\par
