\par
establishment of benefit structures for health
\par
insurance and other health payment systems that
\par
are contingent on the entry of specific clients
\par
in the system. Such health payment programs,
\par
in addition to having benefit structures that
\par
act as constraints, also can have policies that
\par
further constrain the actions of population
\par
intervention managers.
\par
Examples: the U.S. Senate may create a national
\par
health insurance plan with specific benefit
\par
structures. Blue Cross/Blue Shield may create
\par
specific benefit structures and have policies
\par
that population intervention units must live
\par
up to in order to receive these funds.
\par
3. Full accountability: this is a unidirectional
\par
relationship in which one unit delegates re-
\par
sponsibility to another which, as a result,
\par
becomes accountable to the first.
\par
Examples: a community mental health center
\par
creates an aftercare clinic and delegates to it
\par
the responsibility for services to patients
\par
discharged from state hospitals and other in-
\par
patient services. A community mental health
\par
center forms a relationship with an existing
\par
child guidance clinic such that the clinic will
\par
receive state or federal grant funds allocated
\par
to the mental health center if it becomes fully
\par
accountable to the mental health center for
\par
providing child services for an indefinite time
\par
interval.
\par
4. Negotiated accountability: any two units may
\par
create a contract or quasi-contract that is
\par
revocable that requires each to perform some
\par
service, provide funds, etc. for clients or
\par
for another unit.
\par
Example: a community mental health center and
\par
a family service agency prepare and sign a con-
\par
tract in which the community mental health cen-
\par
ter will allocate funds provided the family
\par
service agency will use these funds to make
\par
housekeeping and home visits for elderly,
\par
chronically mentally ill clients living at
\par
home.
\par
5. No accountability: two units are independent
\par
and one unit cannot require the other to do
\par
anything.
\par
Example: two independent, voluntary, semi-
\par
private hospitals.
\par
A mental health delivery system, defined in
\par
this specific fashion, made up of units of the
\par
types specified, all of which are interrelated by
\par
the types of relationships we have described,
\par
exists for every geographic area in the United
\par
States. Most of these MHDS's were not designed
\par
with much conscious planning. They have evolved
\par
as a result of the extension of the private prac-
\par
tice of medicine and psychiatry, the development
\par
of state and local inpatient and outpatient ser-
\par
vices, and by the development of community mental
\par
health systems. It is our belief that in the
\par
future, considerably more conscious planning will
\par
go into the design of such catchment-wide MHDS's
\par
and that the design of MIS will be important in
\par
this planning and in the coordination and main-
\par
tenance of the systems. We are particularly in-
\par
terested in MHDS in which there are units that are
\par
not fully accountable to one another.
\par
The objectives of any actual MHDS are very
\par
complex. The following list does not cover all
\par
possible objectives exhaustively, and it does not
\par
cover alternative ways of wording the objectives
\par
that are based on different value orientations. It
\par
also lists clusters of objectives without speci-
\par
fying the precise subobjectives in detail. The
\par
list was made up by recording every objective we
\par
have heard mentioned in psychiatric and mental
\par
health circles. These were then simplified and
\par
clustered for convenient discussion. The list is
\par
intended to provoke discussion rather than to
\par
establish standards or force agreement.
\par
The major objectives of all MHDS's we as-
\par
sume to be:
\par
I. To minimize the severity weighted average point
\par
prevalence of all mental illness and/or psycho-
\par
social maladjustment, for all the people in a
\par
catchment area, over a time scale long enough
\par
to include the lifetime of the clients in-
\par
volved, with concern for clients in all cate-
\par
gories defined by a set of important socio-
\par
demographic variables and by a set of diag-
\par
nostic or problem identification variables.
\par
For purposes of simplification, we shall some-
\par
times call this objective the severity weighted
\par
average point prevalence objective (SWAPP).
\par
This major objective is usually thought of
\par
as being attained through a set of subobjectives,
\par
which can be regarded as means to that end. We
\par
can often pursue these subobjectives in parallel
\par
but they sometimes compete with one another for
\par
resources.
\par
Subobjecti ves:
\par
I.a. Minimizing incidence through primary pre-
\par
vention.
\par
I.b. Maximizing the conversion of non-appli-
\par
cants to applicants (outreach, case-
\par
finding, advertising, etc.).
\par
I.c. Maximizing the conversion of applicants
\par
to cases receiving care, by maintaining
\par
adequate capacity (beds, outpatient
\par
slots, manpower, etc.).
\par
I.d. Intervention to treat illness or solve
\par
adjustment problems to minimize the du-
\par
ration of illness or disability (pro-
\par
duce cures).
\par
I.e. Intervention to treat illness or solve
\par
adjustment problems to minimize severity
\par
of uncured cases.
\par
209I.f. Minimizing severity of illness on psycho-
\par
social maladjustment by preventing in-
\par
creases in severity (tertiary prevention).
\par
Other objectives are:
\par
II. Distributing care evenly over all groups de-
\par
fined by "important" sociodemographic va-ri-
\par
ables.
\par
III. Maximizing human contact, support, and social
\par
services for all clients whether or not ob-
\par
jective I is pursued.
\par
IV. Minimizing social disruption and social dan-
\par
ger in all communities due to the presence
\par
there of people who are different, bizarre in
\par
behavior, assaultive or homicidal.
\par
V. Minimizing deaths associated with mental ill-
\par
ness or psychosocial maladjustment (suicide
\par
prevention).
\par
VI. Maximizing the profit of private practice and
\par
the self interest rewards of providers.
\par
VII. Minimizing total costs of services for men-
\par
tally ill whether these costs are met through
\par
subsidies (taxes or gifts from individuals),
\par
insurance, medicare, etc. (premiums or taxes),
\par
or self insurance (client payment).
\par
VIII. Minimizing the dumping of clients when the
\par
insurance money runs out.
\par
IX. Minimizing the providing of unnecessary ser-
\par
vice for clients just to obtain insurance
\par
money.
\par
X. Minimizing the stigma of the labels associ-
\par
ated with mental illness and/or psychosocial
\par
maladjustment and minimizing the deprivation
\par
of civil rights associated with care.
\par
XI. Minimizing iatrogenic disability secondary
\par
to treatment.
\par
XII. Maximizing the efficiency of client services
\par
(peer review to make patient needs match ser-
\par
vices rendered; other efficiency activities).
\par
XIII. Maximizing the efficiency of support ser-
\par
vices.
\par
XIV. Maximizing the quality of, and range of,
\par
medical services that deal with medical prob-
\par
lems other than mental illness or psycho-
\par
social maladjustment.
\par
XV. Individualizing client problem solving. Pro-
\par
viding a range of services and an appropriate
\par
timing of services and a matching of detailed
\par
services to precise needs to deal with a very
\par
wide set of major and minor problems of in-
\par
dividuals that go beyond those defined in I
\par
and III.
\par
We assume all units are trying to reach some
\par
subset of these objectives. Each unit will do
\par
this by identifying a set of options open to it.
\par
In order to make our description of decision
\par
making in mental health more detailed we have found
\par
it useful to follow the analysis of decistonmaking
\par
based heavily on the wogk of Bayesian theorists
\par
such as Howard Raiffa4s However, I would liketo
\par
stress that decision theory can be used in at least
\par
two ways; as a technical tool in making a particu-
\par
lar decision, and as a model that provides some
\par
anatomical structure for the process of decision
\par
making. In this paper, and in the work of our
\par
laboratory, we are not at all arguing that all de-
\par
cisions in the mental health field should use de-
\par
cision theory as a tool in practical decipi9n
\par
fnaking. It might be useful in some cases ; but
\par
on the whole, the decisions that need to be made
\par
are so complex and the features of the situation so
\par
difficult to quantify that decision theory is not
\par
likely to be used in most settings. However, de-
\par
cision theory is useful for talking about decision
\par
making, particularly about how to conceptualize
\par
information within the decision makinq orocess.
\par
A decision can be regarded as a series of
\par
steps that begin with a vague recognition of the
\par
problem and proceed to a tentative identification
\par
of the objectives of the organization that are
\par
likely to be influenced by the decision and a ten-
\par
tative identification of options among which a
\par
choice is to be made.
\par
Consider a typical decision in a mental
\par
health unit. Suppose we are considering removing
\par
\$10,000 from the budget of one cost center (and
\par
in so doing reducing the funding of some functions
\par
of that cost center) and using it to add to some
\par
functions-in a second cost center. The options we
\par
are considering are A' and A'', let us say, leaving
\par
\$10,000 where it is, and placing it in the second
\par
cost center, respectively. The choice between A'
\par
and A'' is up to the decision maker, but this does
\par
not determine the outcome. It is not certain that
\par
any single outcome, say that twenty-five people
\par
will be cured, will follow from choosing A'. Af-
\par
ter a decision maker chooses A' or A'', nature re-
\par
sponds with one or another outcome, each of which
\par
has a probability attached to it. Knowing the
\par
values assigned to each of the consequences, and
\par
knowing the probabilities of the different outcames
\par
for any option, we can, in principle, compute the
\par
desirability of option A' compared with option A''.
\par
This suggests that for convenience we can think of
\par
decision making as consisting of five steps: pre-
\par
analysis; structural analysis, in which options are
\par
identified, constraints identified, and conse-
\par
quences clarified associated with options; uncer-
\par
tainty analysis, in which the probabilities of con-
\par
sequences associated with options are estimated;
\par
utility or value analysis, in which the desirabil-
\par
ity of the consequences are assessed in a more or
\par
less orderly fashion; and lastly, an optimization
\par
analysis, in which the choice among the options is
\par
made .
\par
In decisions of some complexity, each conse-
\par
quence resulting from the choice of one option
\par
over another may have more than one important at-
\par
tribute. For instance, in the example we have
\par
210chosen, in which we have considered the wisdom of
\par
moving money from one cost center to another, for
\par
both options we must consider the effect on the
\par
number of admissions, the type of clients treated,
\par
the number of cures, the cost, the revenues gener-
\par
ated and perhaps the incidence of undesirable side
\par
effects, among others.
\par
Information is needed at several of the
\par
stages of decision making.
\par
(1) Preanalysis: information of various sorts can
\par
help in the preliminary formulation of the
\par
probl em.
\par
(2) Structural analysis: information can be used
\par
to identify objectives; to list performance
\par
variables relevant to specific attributes of
\par
objectives; to describe the quality of data
\par
available using performance variables; to
\par
identify options already tried and to suggest
\par
alternatives; and to identify constraints
\par
limiting the outcome possibilities.
\par
(3) Uncertainty analysis: information should be
\par
able to help us predict the probability of the
\par
system performing within certain limits pro-
\par
vided we adopt one option or another.
\par
(4) Value analysis: information could be made
\par
available on the preferences for outcomes and
\par
the value tradeoffs made by decision makers.
\par
(5) Optimization: the actual optimization will
\par
usually be made informally in most presentday
\par
MHDS units (i.e. computational approaches to
\par
optimization through linear programming, etc.,
\par
are probably out of place at this time). The
\par
results of optimization decisions can, how-
\par
ever, be stored as information for use in
\par
future decisions.
\par
We have described a MHDS as a network of units
\par
linked by different accountability relationships.
\par
Each unit becomes a "locus of decision making."
\par
The types of decisions made at each type of unit
\par
are different and the decisions also differ de-
\par
pending on the nature of the accountability re-
\par
lationships. For instance, although a legislator
\par
establishes subsidy support for a community mental
\par
health center and the manager of that community
\par
mental health center may, in one sense, have the
\par
same objective, let us say, to reduce the severity
\par
weighted average point prevalence of psychosis in
\par
a specific community, the options open to them to
\par
achieve this objective are different. The legis-
\par
lator must decide how much money to approve to sup-
\par
port the budget request of the community mental
\par
health center (made through the channels of astate
\par
department of mental health), and he must further
\par
decide whether he wishes to make the subsidy he
\par
approves conditional on the manager following
\par
specific policies laid down by the legislator.
\par
The manager must decide what options are open to
\par
him in spending the money, and how to allocate
\par
funds to specific populations and to specific in-
\par
tervention functions, etc.
\par
The fact that efforts to reach the same ob-
\par
jective require totally different decisions at dif-
\par
ferent loci in a MHDS is partly responsible for the
\par
difficulty in deciding what decisions are in fact
\par
being made. Societal decision makers, catchment
\par
managers, population intervention managers, and
\par
case workers describe the work of MHDS's in very
\par
different terms. We have a situation very like the
\par
blind men examining an elephant. Those handling
\par
the tail don't understand the verbal reports of
\par
those handling the trunk or ears. Case workers of-
\par
ten talk as if the only important decisions being
\par
made in a MHDS are the choices of the details of
\par
individualized treatment plans. They totally ig-
\par
nore the need for policy decisions on how popu-
\par
lations with specific problems are to be handled
\par
and yet these population decisions are critical for
\par
planning facilities and budgets. Population inter-
\par
vention managers are concerned about populations
\par
but they often do not need to worry about popula-
\par
tions not under their direct care and they often
\par
are not concerned with prevention of illness inthe
\par
general population. Catchment managers must be
\par
very concerned about allocating funds between pre-
\par
ventive and therapeutic services or between in-
\par
patient and outpatient facilities, but they often
\par
sound as if their job is completed once services
\par
are made available.
\par
This description of decision making in aMHDS
\par
is a preamble to suggesting that an orderly cate-
\par
gorization of decision types is possible and that
\par
given such a categorization of decision types and
\par
a statement of the loci in the MHDS where the de-
\par
cision is made, it should be possible to describe
\par
the information needs of each unit in a MHDS.
\par
A tentative list of decision types is dis-
\par
played in Table 1, with an indication of the types
\par
of units in a MHDS that are likely to make such
\par
decisions. This is insufficient to describe the
\par
actual decisions made in each unit. The actual
\par
decision depends on the constraints operating, on
\par
the policies determined at higher levels in the
\par
system, or based on negotiation with other units,
\par
etc.
\par
The details of a full list of decision types
\par
would also depend on the specific description of
\par
an actual MHDS. Let us suppose that some unit in
\par
the system is admitting patients selectively based
\par
on their revenue generating capacity (only insured
\par
patients are admitted) and patients are discharged
\par
when their insurance money runs out, whether they
\par
are still sick or not. These policy decisions
\par
must be made at some specific decision point in
\par
the system, and we must assume the policies are
\par
adopted to achieve some specific objectives: any
\par
specific detailed description of the location of
\par
decision types in this MHDS would identify the lo-
\par
cus of the decision making, the type of unit making
\par
the decision, the accountability relation with
\par
other units, and specify the information needed by
\par
that decision maker to decide whether the policies
\par
should be continued another year in order to meet
\par
unit objectives and to conform with the values of
\par
the appropriate value establishing manager or
\par
board.
\par
