\par
Provocative Structured Learning Workshops
\par
I would like to devote particular attention to
\par
our activities using simulations of mental health
\par
decision making processes. It has been our ex-
\par
perience that mental health workers of many types
\par
become bored when issues of administration are
\par
raised. The usual picture in the mind of a clini-
\par
cian of an administrative problem has to do with
\par
sending out bills or arguing about the levels at
\par
which fees should be set. Therefore, it is not
\par
easy to interest such individuals in evaluation
\par
and information systems through standard lecture
\par
techniques. Over the years, we have found that
\par
workshop approaches are much more acceptable, part-
\par
ly because they do not make the pretense there is
\par
one expert who is prepared to impart knowledge to
\par
others who know nothing about it. The workshop is
\par
an honest effort to get the best knowledge from
\par
every participant and,in a sense, everyone is both
\par
teacher and learner. The major difficulty with
\par
such workshops is that they are often so unstruc-
\par
tured that it is very difficult to anticipate what
\par
ideas will emerge and what type of learning will
\par
take place. We have, therefore, tried to combine
\par
the workshop notion with the use of some highly
\par
structured experience, making use of computer simu-
\par
lations. We have conducted pilot studies on these
\par
methods with individuals from both community mental
\par
health settings and the Department of Psychiatry.
\par
In each of the simulations, students work in
\par
pairs and are given a case study problem to solve.
\par
The reason for the work in pairs is that this per-
\par
mits us to ask each participant to clarify his
\par
ideas, and it permits us to make a tape recording
\par
of the team efforts at problem solving. In each
\par
of the simulations, information is available about
\par
the system that would be useful in the decision
\par
making, but there is always the possibility of ob-
\par
taining more information at a cost. The indivi-
\par
duals must therefore evaluate many different levels
\par
and types of information and decide which types are
\par
relevant to their purposes and which not, and
\par
which are worth the cost. Then the individual
\par
makes a decision and the computer tells him what
\par
the outcome is. The computer is thus really used
\par
as a way of generating complex data as a conse-
\par
quence of different decisions. In addition to
\par
this, we have simulated decision making in the com-
\par
puter, using linear programming optimization mo-
\par
dels, so that we can, if we wish, ask the computer
\par
itself to make a decision using values adopted by,
\par
and the information available to, the subjects.
\par
One of these simulations is an evaluation of
\par
the effectiveness of different types of treatment.
\par
It raises problems that are often associated with
\par
the research designs used in the evaluation of
\par
drugs or psychotherapy but it does this in a set-
\par
ting that is as much as possible like that faced
\par
by the average clinician.
\par
A second simulation is concerned with the al-
\par
location of funds to different functions in a
\par
large system. It particularly stresses the issues
\par
involved in decisions to allocate money either to
\par
prevention or to treatment for the same condition.
\par
The issues include the fact that it is obviously
\par
foolish to put large amounts of money into either
\par
prevention or treatment unless it can be determined
\par
that it is effective. It is also possible that
\par
putting large amounts into prevention would make
\par
treatment unnecessary even if treatment is effec-
\par
tive. It also raises the issue of comparing the
\par
desirability of putting money into one illness com-
\par
pared with another.
\par
A third simulation is primarily concerned
\par
with the development of admiission, discharge, and
\par
treatment assignment policies in order to achieve
\par
outcomes that are consistent with one's values.
\par
This simulation takes into account a wide range of
\par
sources of revenue and permits decision making to
\par
be based on many different insurance and subsidy
\par
situations.
\par
The "Insurance Subsidy Game" places pairs of
\par
players in the role of U.S. senators designing
\par
national health insurance policy, state senators
\par
designing subsidies for community mental health
\par
213and inpatient facilities, and facilities managers
\par
who must use resources from these sources to maxi-
\par
mize patient benefits at reasonable cost levels.
\par
All of these players call for and use mental health
\par
utilization data to help them make optimization de-
\par
cisions based on their objectives and values.
\par
A "Data Base Manipulation Game" uses a simu-
\par
lated data base in MICRO, a University of Michigan
\par
Time Sharing System data base management language.
\par
This gives experience in formulating questions and
\par
answers as sequences of routinely requested sets
\par
of data based on highly selected populations of
\par
patients, selected in many different ways, and in
\par
formulating and asking spontaneous and unusual
\par
questions that might occur to mental health workers.
\par
Description of one simulation: the Five-Value
\par
Trade-off Game
\par
I shall go into a little more detail in de-
\par
scribing the third simulation because it clarifies
\par
the use of these games in teaching. A group of
\par
eight or ten potential students is divided into
\par
pairs. An effort is made to include both clini-
\par
cians and individuals with some administrative in-
\par
terests or skills so that they can learn from one
\par
another. A pair of individuals acts as a decision
\par
making team in setting admission, discharge, and
\par
treatment policies. Before the decision making oc-
\par
curs, a questionnaire is used to clarify the values
\par
of the individuals. We are particularly interested
\par
in getting the participants to see that trade-offs
\par
are necessary among the objectives they may have.
\par
They may be interested in providing service to as
\par
many people as possible without concern for any im-
\par
provement that may be expected. On the otherhand,
\par
they may be interested in achieving cure rates that
\par
are high. Still others may be interested in cost
\par
containment or in achieving profits of one sort or
\par
another. It is also possible that the decisions
\par
they make could harm the patient by keeping him
\par
after treatment has become unnecessary, or perhaps
\par
by discharging him before he has benefitted from
\par
treatment because his insurance money has run out.
\par
The problem is to pick an admission strategy that
\par
will help meet the set of values chosen. This
\par
might mean that only insured patients would be ac-
\par
cepted, that the ratio of insured to uninsured
\par
would equal that in the community, or that onlyun-
\par
insured patients would be admitted. A discharge
\par
policy then is chosen which states whether patients
\par
will be discharged only when they are cured or
\par
when it is determined that treatment will no longer
\par
be effective; whether they will be dischargedwhen
\par
the money runs out, even if these other conditions
\par
are not met; or whether they will be exploited by
\par
being given unnecessary hospitalization just for
\par
their insurance money. Clients can be assigned to
\par
treatment at three levels. Each treatment carries
\par
with it a mean cure rate provided individuals are
\par
kept as long as it is necessary for them to stay
\par
for improvement. Patients get better at different
\par
rates and both the time of the first recovery and
\par
the time of the last recovery are dependent on the
\par
treatment. This means that the analysis of theef-
\par
fectiveness of treatment is dependent not only on
\par
the treatment chosen, but on the duration of time
\par
that a population is, on the average, kept in a
\par
unit. As a result, relatively complicated strate-
\par
gies must be worked out. To do this, a variety of
\par
forms of information are available. Players can
\par
receive information free that is roughly equivalent
\par
to the standard utilization data available in most
\par
agencies, but they must pay money for more detailed
\par
information about the effect of treatment on simi-
\par
lar groups of patients in the past, both with the
\par
same and with different treatments.
\par
In practice, we have found that students en-
\par
gaged in these decision making processes clarify
\par
most of the issues that we wish to have discussed
\par
that are concerned with the use of management in-
\par
formation systems. When the simulation is over,
\par
the results are displayed on a chart which indi-
\par
rectly teaches much of the conceptual material in-
\par
volved in decision analysis. No effort is made to
\par
teach the formal structure of decision theory.
\par
There is then a debriefing session in which the
\par
students discuss their decision and discuss there-
\par
lationship that this task has to the type of de-
\par
cision making they do in their own agency. At a
\par
later date, all of the individuals are gatheredto-
\par
gether and there is further discussion of the simu-
\par
lation. By this time, the tapes of the discussions
\par
between the decision makers have been analyzed,
\par
using a content analysis procedure that separates
\par
those discussions that are relevant to decision
\par
making from those discussions that are relevant to
\par
the strategies for use of information. Issues
\par
which may have been raised by one pair but not
\par
others can now be raised for discussion by the
\par
group. In this way, all of the major items which
\par
we think should be discussed can be brought in in
\par
some way.
\par
When the workshop is over, each individual
\par
participates in an interview with staff and in a
\par
semistructured way is led to make comments about
\par
the entire experience. This provides anadditional
\par
opportunity for learning, but it also is a useful
\par
evaluation procedure for getting at what has been
\par
learned from the experience, particularly with in-
\par
dividuals who have widely varying value systems.
\par
It is instructive to have a pair in which one in-
\par
dividual, who strongly believes in providing social
\par
services to all patients and has relatively little
\par
concern for either cures or cost containment, has
\par
an opportunity to interact with a business oriented
\par
person interested primarily in both cures and cost
\par
containment. Because the discussion is focused,
\par
it brings out the relationship of values to objec-
\par
tives and clarifies many of the issues which we
\par
have identified as problems in the earliersections
\par
of this paper.
\par
Concl usions
\par
1) Professional personnel in mental health de-
\par
livery systems use management information sys-
\par
tems with reasonable efficiency in operating
\par
support functions, but they do not regard in-
\par
formation as useful and do not use it very ef-
\par
ficiently to make decisions that allocate re-
\par
sources and set policies to reach major objec-
\par
tives for populations of clients.
\par
2) Somewhat arbitrary categorizations of decision
\par
214types assigned to specific loci in complex
\par
mental health delivery systems can be prepared.
\par
These categorizations can be used to provoke
\par
discussion among mental health professionals
\par
and might contribute to better organization of
\par
management information system data bases and
\par
better design of question formulation and ques-
\par
tion answering capabilities.
\par
3) Political differences among mental health pro-
\par
fessionals over system objectives, concerning
\par
the value of information, and over the contri-
\par
bution of information systems to confidenti-
\par
ality leaks and troubles due to labeling,are
\par
barriers to efficient design of, and use of,
\par
mental health delivery system management in-
\par
formation systems.
\par
4) Workshops that provoke creative thinking about
\par
the value of information in decision making
\par
and about the structure of important decisions
\par
may be useful in changing attitudes and skills
\par
of mental health professionals.
\par
5) Such workshops, supported by computer based
\par
simulations of decision problems could become
\par
a routine component of the training efforts
\par
of most mental health delivery system units
\par
that have management information systems.
