\par
\textbf{J. Edward Russo}
\par
\textbf{Toward Intelligent Product Information}
\par
\textbf{Systems for Consumers}
\par
ABSTRACT. Telecommunication, video, and artificial intelligence technologies are
\par
supporting new product information systems capable of an increasingly intelligent
\par
dialog with consumers. The success of these new systems, just as with all past
\par
systems, depends on consumers' perceiving more benefits than costs from using
\par
them. Many of the new sophisticated systems may not succeed, and some have
\par
already failed, because their benefits cannot be made to exceed their costs. Espe-
\par
cially important are nonmonetary costs like information processing effort and
\par
frustrating or tedious use experiences. The impact of the new technologies on
\par
information providers is discussed, as is the role of consumer-oriented policy
\par
makers in influencing the development of new product information systems.
\par
New telecommunication and computer technologies are changing the
\par
delivery of product information to consumers. In this paper I
\par
consider the impact of these new technologies and the role of
\par
consumer-oriented policy makers in influencing the emerging product
\par
information systems. The paper begins with the distinction between
\par
systems that are knowledgeable but passive and those that are
\par
actively intelligent. This is followed by a benefit-cost analysis for
\par
predicting consumers' acceptance of product information systems.
\par
The remainder of the paper addresses the important behavioral
\par
and technological questions facing those concerned with consumer
\par
welfare.
\par
PRODUCT INFORMATION SYSTEMS
\par
\emph{A product information system consists of information content and a}
\par
\emph{delivery vehicle. }For example, a U.S. government agency uses crash
\par
tests to measure the safety of automobiles. The resulting safety
\par
information is disseminated (a) by the government, (b) in a privately
\par
published and commercially successful book (Gillis, 1986), and (c)
\par
\emph{in Consumer Reports' }evaluation of automobiles. Similarly, the same
\par
motion picture reviews are broadcast on television and printed in
\par
newspapers. It is the combination of content and delivery method
\par
that constitutes a distinct product information system.
\par
\emph{Journal of Consumer Policy }10 (1987) 109--138.
\par
\copyright{} 1987 \emph{by D. Reidel Publishing Company.}12"0 J. Edward Russo
\par
\emph{Intelligent Systems}
\par
Intelligent product information systems should be distinguished from
\par
those that are merely knowledgeable. A product information system
\par
is \emph{knowledgeable }if it contains a sufficient information base in terms
\par
that consumers can readily comprehend. Familiar examples are a
\par
guidebook to restaurants, a telephone directory, or a \emph{Consumer}
\par
\emph{Reports }article on foreign compact cars. Note that the information
\par
provided in a more technical, engineering analysis of foreign com-
\par
pact cars might be sufficient but not comprehensible by ordinary
\par
consumers. Similarly the \emph{Consumer Reports }article could be compre-
\par
hensible but provide insufficient information to a consumer con-
\par
sidering more than foreign compact cars (e.g., domestic compacts as
\par
well). Both sufficiency and comprehensibility are necessary for an
\par
information system to qualify as knowledgeable.
\par
In knowledgeable product information systems all the work of
\par
extracting relevant knowledge is done by the consumer. The system
\par
itself is passive; it makes the same information available to everyone.
\par
For instance, consumers using a printed telephone directory must do
\par
all the work of knowledge extraction themselves. This is simple if the
\par
correct name and address are known. But if there is uncertainty
\par
about the spelling of the last name or only an approximate address is
\par
known, using a telephone directory can be frustrating, time-consum-
\par
ing, and sometimes unsuccessful.
\par
In contrast, a product information system is \emph{intelligent }if it is able,
\par
at a minimum, to answer questions posed by consumers and, ideally,
\par
to ask questions that link consumers' needs, preferences, and other
\par
characteristics to alternative products. Intelligent systems are capable
\par
of active problem solving to help bridge the gap between consumer
\par
information needs and the system's contents.
\par
Returning to the example of a telephone directory, consider an
\par
active, intelligent version of this printed information. A computerized
\par
directory could display variations of the last name such as Rousseau
\par
or Rosso for Russo, or show full names and addresses to prompt
\par
recognition. It might go further and provide the names of all
\par
telephone subscribers at a given address or a list of all businesses by
\par
category (say, plumbers) and location (in Cambridge, Massachusetts).
\par
Such an electronic telephone directory is now available in France
\par
(Mayer, 1986). Similarly, a restaurant guidebook to Boston could be
\par
reformatted as a computerized database. This new mode wouldIntelligent Information Systems 111
\par
permit a user to actively search for restaurants by type of cooking
\par
(say, Northern Italian), location (within a 15 minute walk), availa-
\par
bility of service (open on a Sunday evening), etc.
\par
Systems with greater intelligence can do more than help con-
\par
sumers find what they are looking for; they can help consumers
\par
determine what they should be looking for. Thus, an intelligent
\par
system for assisting automobile purchasers might do more than find
\par
all cars with (a) a price under \$12,000, (b) a 4-cylinder engine, (c) 4
\par
doors, (d) excellent crash safety, (e) very low frequency of repair,
\par
and (f) high fuel efficiency. It might ask whether there is a preference
\par
for domestic or foreign cars, whether the user would consider
\par
spending slightly over \$12,000 for a car scoring very well on all five
\par
other attributes, or whether highway or city fuel efficiency is more
\par
important. It might go further and check whether a 4-cylinder engine
\par
is adequate, e.g., asking whether there is a need to tow a boat or
\par
house trailer. Systems with even higher levels of intelligence might
\par
have explanatory, tutorial, and learning capabilities.
\par
Because of their active search ability, intelligent systems can
\par
\emph{personalize }the available information. They adapt to the user by
\par
displaying only the information that is requested and, in the ideal
\par
case, can help to determine the most useful information even if the
\par
consumer is not knowledgeable enough to ask for it directly.
\par
All of this manifests itself in a \emph{strong information dialog }between
\par
system and user. This strong dialog can be initiated by either party,
\par
reflects an exchange of information between the system and user,
\par
and takes place in a (shared) language that is natural enough for
\par
consumers to comprehend easily and rich enough to communicate
\par
complex or fuzzy descriptions of consumer needs and characteristics.
\par
Ideally, a strong information dialog is terminated when the con-
\par
sumer's information needs have been satisfied, not when the system's
\par
ability to provide information is exhausted. Note that typical passive
\par
information systems are capable of only the weakest form of dialog.
\par
Knowledgeable and intelligent systems can be placed on a con-
\par
tinuum of system activity. As we move from the knowledgeable and
\par
totally passive end, the system becomes increasingly capable of an
\par
active role in helping consumers determine and find the specific
\par
information they need. At the intelligent end of the continuum we
\par
approach the ideal of a trusted human expert with the important
\par
advantage of access to a rich database of product information. This
\par
hypothetical expert maintains a strong dialog and delivers person-112 J. Edward Russo
\par
alized product information. Note that knowledgeable information
\par
systems may contain extensive information, as in a first-rate library,
\par
or sophisticated communication, such as TV reviews of motion
\par
pictures or in-store videos displaying merchandise. However, neither
\par
the size of the knowledge base nor the richness of the communica-
\par
tion medium makes a system intelligent. It is only the active, problem
\par
solving, personalizing capacity of a system that qualifies it as
\par
intelligent.
\par
\emph{Can Knowledgeable but Passive Systems Succeed?}
\par
The distinction between knowledgeable but passive product informa-
\par
tion systems and the new actively intelligent ones inevitably high-
\par
lights the exciting potential of the latter. Yet it is important to
\par
recognize how effective traditional product information systems can
\par
be, even single printed pages. Two examples from my own research
\par
will suffice. Figure 1 shows a proposed monthly energy bill for
\par
residential consumers. It provides personalized feedback on changes
\par
in energy consumption from the previous year. This enables con-
\par
sumers to determine whether conservation actions like adding insula-
\par
tion or lowering the winter thermostat have been successful. In 1975
\par
a U.S. electric utility adopted a similar format for their monthly bills,
\par
and the average energy consumption of its customers decreased
\par
about 3\% (Russo, 1977a).
\par
The second example is a list of the added sugar in breakfast
\par
cereals as shown in Figure 2. The market share of the brands lowest
\par
