Nolan, Russell, \& Metcalf, 1986). In compensation, those brands
\par
highest in this to-be-avoided component of cereal lost significant
\par
share, dropping from 18.2\% to 15.9\%. The same technique of listing
\par
information for all brands in a product category was used for unit
\par
prices (Russo, 1977b). Posting such lists in supermarkets resulted in
\par
a 3\% decrease in the average unit price over six different product
\par
categories. The list format has also made it easier for consumers to
\par
compare the mortgage rates of different lending institutions (Canner
\par
\& EUiehausen, 1986).
\par
The point of these examples is that, although restricted to a weak
\par
information dialog, passive systems can be quite successful. If the
\par
information is valuable and the presentation format convenient, the
\par
system can have a major impact on consumer choice. Such passive
\par
systems show that the success of information systems is not neces-
\par
sarily dependent upon advanced technology. We return to this
\par
important point after an overview of the new information systems.
\par
\emph{The Technology of Intelligent Systems}
\par
What are the new technologies that will become incorporated into
\par
intelligent product information systems? Three types are reviewed:
\par
telecommunication, video, and artificial intelligence. This overview
\par
illustrates current and potential applications of each of these technol-
\par
ogies to product information systems. Its purpose is to flesh out the
\par
concept of intelligent systems and set the stage for the guiding
\par
criterion of a benefit-cost analysis by information users and for the
\par
policy questions that follow. The review is not meant to be compre-
\par
hensive. Additional technologies are considered in reports by Butler
\par
Cox (1984), Mitchell and Murley (1984), and Sepstrup and Olander
\par
(1986).
\par
\emph{Telecommunication. }Videotex is probably the most common pro-
\par
duct information system based on telecommunication technology. A
\par
videotex signal containing both text and graphics originates at some
\par
central location. It is transmitted via telephone or cable to homes
\par
equipped with a decoder and specially modified TV monitor. A
\par
keypad enables the at-home consumer to send signals back to a
\par
computer at the central location. (This user hardware can be
\par
replaced by a personal computer and the appropriate videotex
\par
software.) The promise of videotex is enormous (Scholl, 1982;Intelligent Information Systems 115
\par
Grunert, 1984; Talarzyk, Widing, \& Urbany, 1984). The reality has
\par
been disappointing. In the U.S. several commercial services have
\par
failed to attain their projected market sizes (Talarzyk, 1986).
\par
Potential subscribers were discouraged by the cost of the special
\par
equipment and the monthly subscription fees. But as much as
\par
anything else videotex systems suffer from a "critical mass" problem.
\par
Until there is a large enough subscription base, there is insufficient
\par
revenue to support an increase in the product information and other
\par
services offered. But until more services are offered the subscription
\par
base will not grow. Some European systems have faced similar
\par
problems. For example, the British system, Prestel, has failed to meet
\par
its projections for subscription and use. It does, however, appear that
\par
Prestel's subscriber base is growing at a slow but steady rate
\par
(Mitchell, 1986).
\par
Some U.S. videotex systems have proved commercially successful,
\par
but these are not systems whose sole value is product information.
\par
For instance, COMP-U-STORE is a successful videotex catalog
\par
service with over a million paid subscribers. These subscribers can
\par
access a text-only catalog in the company's central computer via
\par
telephone lines (and a home terminal and modem). With standard
\par
menu-structured software they can examine a particular product
\par
category, select types and brands within this category, compare
\par
alternative brands on objective product characteristics, and order the
\par
product. The prices of products ordered through COMP-U-STORE
\par
are typically 20--40\% below retail. We do not know how much the
\par
current success of this and similar systems depends on product
\par
information versus deeply discounted prices. However, commenting
\par
on consumers' use of Comp-U-Card, a system similar to COMP-U-
\par
STORE, Talarzyk (1986) says, "In most cases members seem to be
\par
using the system for pricing information and then using that data to
\par
make deals with local retailers" (p. 16).
\par
It is worth noting that the problems videotex has encountered in
\par
the consumer arena are not inherent to the technology. The cost and
\par
critical mass problems are solvable in principle, e.g., via govern-
\par
mental support. When these difficulties are avoided, as they are in
\par
field tests, consumer acceptance of videotex is achieved (Grunert,
\par
1984; Mayer, 1986). These problems are also greatly attenuated in a
\par
corporate setting. In-house videotex systems can run on the many
\par
personal computers already in place, and the company has an
\par
incentive to offer many forms of information (Burstyn, 1985). These116 J. Edward Russo
\par
include personnel benefits, "smart" telephone directories, details of
\par
training courses, and, using graphics, the locations of hotels in cities
\par
to be visited.
\par
That videotex networks can be successful has been shown by the
\par
French Teletel system (Mayer, 1986). The critical mass and cost
\par
barriers were overcome by the involvement of the national tele-
\par
communication organization. It offers terminals free to any tele-
\par
phone subscriber as a substitute for the printed telephone directory.
\par
Although not yet available everywhere in France, the current total of
\par
almost two million subscribers is more than enough to attract and
\par
support many (over 2000 in March 1986) on-line services from
\par
private companies. Although the idea of a state-supported videotex
\par
network is less acceptable in some other countries, the success of the
\par
French system validates the early enthusiasm for videotex once the
\par
cost and critical mass problems are solved.
\par
\emph{Video technologies. }Among the new video technologies are video-
\par
discs and computer generated graphics, as well as conventional
\par
videotapes (which become more attractive as more households own
\par
videotape players). 1 focus mainly on one of these technologies,
\par
videodiscs, and on one example of their use, electronic kiosks. The
\par
latter are stand-alone systems in public places like shopping malls
\par
and airports. They consist of a micro-computer with menu-struc-
\par
tured software, a display monitor with touch-sensitive screen and/or
\par
keyboard, a videodisc player with its own monitor for full color and
\par
motion presentations, and a hard-copy printer. Future kiosks may
\par
use speech recognition technology to make their use more like a
\par
natural dialog with a human (Foderaro, 1986).
\par
Self-contained kiosks are currently being tested by various sellers,
\par
among them several automobile manufacturers (e.g., General Motors
\par
in the U.S. and Austin Rover in the U.K.). Using these latter systems
\par
consumers can obtain information about available models and
\par
options, view advertising-like video of the cars being driven, obtain
\par
information on prices, warranties, and local dealers, and even
\par
configure a car to their own specifications. The manufacturers see
\par
such kiosks as marketing tools that build interest in their products
\par
through interactive involvement.
\par
A more elaborate version of the electronic kiosk is connected to a
\par
central facility, often via videotex. Such a system can be used to
\par
demonstrate and order a wide range of merchandise. EssentiallyIntelligent Information Systems 117
\par
such kiosks are electronic shopping stations (Hurly, 1986; Vloyanetes
\par
\& Magel, 1986). For consumers they reduce travel between stores
\par
yet allow for comparison of products that might only be stocked at
\par
different stores. Electronic kiosks can provide detailed information
\par
on products that is beyond the knowledge of typical sales people.
\par
For example, the auto systems can potentially offer brief explana-
\par
tions of new features. Then consumers can, if they wish, access more
\par
detailed explanations of improved product performance, maintenance
\par
requirements, engineering details, etc.
\par
At present most electronic kiosks support their interaction or
\par
dialog with consumers through menu-structured software. That is,
\par
consumers request information from the system by choosing one of
\par
several options from a listed "menu." The resulting software has a
\par
tree-like structure of branches that terminate only at the most
\par
detailed information. Although this menu structure is constrained
\par
compared to artificial intelligence, it still enables a relatively strong
\par
dialog between the system and consumers. In field tests these
\par
electronic kiosks have proved commercially successful and several
\par
firms are marketing them aggressively (ElectroniStore, 1985; Reiling,
\par
1985).
\par
\emph{Knowledge-based systems. }These systems utilize artificial intelligence
\par
techniques. Their emphasis is on intelligent software rather than
\par
video or telecommunication hardware -- though the latter may play
\par
an important role. Of the relatively few such systems now in use,
\par
none that I know of provide product information to consumers.
\par
Among the closest to a consumer-oriented system is one being
\par
built for the French national agricultural research agency (Gallagher,
\par
1985). Its goal is to serve individual French farmers as an expert on
\par
crop diseases. Farmers can access this information system via a
\par
telephone fine, specialized terminal, and a videodisc player. The
\par
latter is essential for visual matching of the condition of the crop
\par
with disease symptoms. An existing system can diagnose 60 tomato
\par
diseases based on 200 different symptoms. Like a human expert it
\par
realizes that few diagnoses are certain, so it provides a reliability
\par
value between 0 and 100 to qualify whichever disease it decides on.
\par
When completed in late 1986 or early 1987, it is expected that 30
\par
different knowledge-based systems (for 30 different crops) will be
\par
offered to French farmers.
\par
A similar expert system for cotton crop management has been1 18 J. Edward Russo
\par
constructed in the U.S. (Lemmon, 1986). It advises cotton farmers
\par
on daily decisions such as irrigation and fertilization levels (based, in
\par
part, on weather information from sensors in the cotton fields). The
\par
system performed very well in a pilot test.
\par
Knowledge-based systems come closest to what may be the ideal
\par
intelligent product information system: a trusted human expert.
\par
Whether this tec,hnology is developed to serve consumers is an
\par
open issue that we consider shortly. At the moment expert systems
\par
are only available for more specialized applications like medical
\par
diagnosis (Duda \& Shortliffe, 1983) and configuring complex com-
\par
puting systems (Harmon \& King, 1985). These are applications with
\par
government or industry support where the users are themselves
\par
professionals, e.g., physicians (but not experts) in a particular
\par
disease. No knowledge-based information systems have yet been
\par
built for nonprofessional consumers.
\par
A BENEFIT-COST ANALYSIS OF INFORMATION SYSTEMS
\par
The success of any information system for consumers depends upon
\par
consumer acceptance and use. The decision to use a system depends,
\par
in turn, upon a benefit-cost analysis \emph{from the consumer's perspective.}
\par
That is, the benefits anticipated by the consumer, both tangible and
\par
intangible, must exceed the expected costs.
\par
For researchers the success of a benefit-cost analysis depends
\par
upon their ability to identify the benefits and costs \emph{as perceived by}
\par
\emph{consumers. }The economic benefits and costs are tangible and more
\par
apparent. Those that are psychological and intangible are more
\par
difficult to ascertain and measure. However, they are no less
\par
important, and the listing that follows pays special attention to them.
\par
The benefit-cost viewpoint proposed here is similar to the
\par
economic analysis of the search for product information (Ratchford,
\par
1980; Stigler, 1961). The economic principle is that consumers
\par
search for product information as long as its benefits exceed its costs.
\par
In the present analysis of consumers using information systems, the
\par
search process is broadened to include the "processing" as well as
\par
acquisition of the information. Similarly, the benefits and costs
\par
explicitly include the intangible and psychological. That is, they go
\par
beyond purely monetary considerations or those that can be trans-
\par
formed into monetary units like travel time (using marginal wage rate
\par
as a measure of the monetary value of time).Intelligent Information Systems 119
\par
A list of the benefits and costs of information use is presented
\par
below. In a few cases it is difficult to separate a benefit from a cost
\par
since the same consideration can appear as either depending on
\par
consumers' judgment. For instance, the completeness of the informa-
\par
tion provided by the system may be seen as satisfactory (a benefit) or
\par
unsatisfactory (a cost) depending on consumers' expectations. The
\par
various benefits and costs have also been identified by whether they
\par
are related to the outcome of the purchase decision or to the
\par
decision process itself. Finally, where relevant I distinguish con-
\par
siderations that are immediate from those that are delayed and those
\par
that occur once only from ones that are repeated. The presented list
\par
of benefits and costs is not claimed to be complete. Its main virtue is
\par
that it recognizes the important role of noneconomic factors in a
\par
consumer's benefit-cost analysis of the value of product information.
\par
\emph{Benefits of Information Use}
\par
The most obvious benefit is the selection of an alternative -- a
\par
brand, model, optional accessories, price, vendor, and so on, includ-
\par
ing the option not to purchase -- that maximizes personal utility. The
\par
term utility is used here in the narrow sense of the utility of the
\par
chosen alternative. Thaler (1985) calls this acquisition utility. He
\par
distinguishes it from transaction utility which includes not only the
\par
chosen alternative itself but also how it was chosen, e.g., in a risky
\par
versus riskless setting. Other tangible benefits of an information
\par
system include optimal use of the product (e.g., its full range of uses)
\par
and advice on cost minimization (e.g., the cost of repair, ongoing
\par
maintenance, insurance, disposal).
\par
There may also be less tangible but no less real benefits. One is
\par
the increased certainty that the best alternative has been chosen or,
\par
conversely, the reduction in the risk of making a suboptimal selec-
\par
tion. Another is instruction on how to use the product safely. A third
\par
is the knowledge gained about the product category so that future
\par
purchase decisions can be made more efficiently.
\par
All of the above benefits, tangible and intangible alike, are related
\par
to the outcome of the process that uses the product information to
\par
decide which product to purchase (or to decide not to purchase).
\par
Another class of benefits is more closely related to the process itself.
\par
Consumers may be more or less satisfied with the adequacy (com-
\par
pleteness) of the product information from the system. Depending
\par
on their specific expectations, they may find the level of complete-120 J. Edward Russo
\par
ness satisfactory (a benefit) or unsatisfactory (a cost). Similarly the
\par
personalization, comparability, etc., of the product information
\par
enables a choice process that consumers are able (a benefit) or
\par
unable (a cost) to justify to themselves and/or others.
\par
Finally, there is \emph{experiential value }in the process itself. Using the
\par
information system may be pleasant, entertaining, etc. (a benefit), or
\par
it may be annoying, frustrating, or tedious (a cost). Experiential value
\par
is an underappreciated component of the decision to use product
\par
information. To a large extent, it explains why people watch TV
\par
commercials and in-store videos. Horne (1982) discriminated
\par
between potential users and non-users of an electronic funds transfer
\par
system at non-bank locations like grocery stores. Of the perceived
\par
attributes of this service, the one that far and away most successfully
\par
discriminated between potential users and non-users was "enjoyment
\par
of using." (Second and third were "easing of personal routine" and
\par
"time saving.") Although enjoyment is not identical to entertainment,
\par
it captures the notion of experiential value or the pleasure associated
\par
with the process of using the system. In sum, experiential value,
\par
especially the capacity to entertain as well as inform, may well play a
\par
major role in the success of intelligent information systems.
\par
This catalog of benefits is summarized in Table I. What may be
\par
most important to appreciate about this list are those consumer
\par
benefits besides the utility of the chosen alternative. Normative
\par
economic analyses, in practice if not in theory, too often focus on
\par
this latter benefit to the exclusion of intangible, psychological
\par
benefits. The latter are just as real to consumers, and play just as
\par
important a role in their acceptance or rejection of systems for
\par
providing product information.
\par
\emph{Costs of Information Use}
\par
The costs to users of information parallel the benefits, and are also
\par
listed in Table I. Monetary costs are direct payments whether for
\par
hardware like videotex terminals, a subscription to \emph{Consumer}
\par
\emph{Reports, }the annual membership fee for COMP-U-STORE, or travel
\par
costs to a vendor. Note that some product information is free, at
\par
least in the sense of no direct monetary cost. The nutrition informa-
\par
tion on food packages is one example.
\par
Note also that some monetary costs recur on an event basis, i.e.,Intelligent Information Systems 121
