\par
discussed the cost of mental effort in some detail, partitioning it into
\par
three components: collection, comprehension, and computation
\par
(Russo, 1981; Russo et al., 1986). For practical purposes, we can
\par
think of mental effort as indexed by time. That is, we assume that
\par
using product information so fully occupies thinking capacity that no
\par
other task can be accomplished in parallel. Given full absorption of
\par
thinking capacity, "time on task" is a good index of the total mental
\par
effort. Note that mental effort must be expended each time informa-
\par
tion is acquired and used. There is no avoiding some effort cost, a
\par
fact that becomes increasingly important as other costs are elimi-
\par
