\par
Experiential costs refer to all negative experiences associated with
\par
the process of using the information system. One example occurs in
\par
teletext -- a broadcast, television-based version of videotex widely
\par
spread in Europe (Sepstrup \& Olander, 1986) which however has
\par
attracted little interest in the U.S. (Carey \& Moss, 1985). In this
\par
system users can select a desired "page" of information but have
\par
to wait for its appearance in the transmission cycle. That is, unlike
\par
current videotex consumers cannot "order" a page of information
\par
and have it appear more or less immediately on their terminal
\par
screens. They have to wait until the desired page appears in a fixed
\par
sequence of transmitted pages. The resulting delay could take many
\par
seconds, and it grows longer as the system becomes bigger and
\par
better through the addition of more pages of information. These
\par
delays are surprisingly annoying. In a laboratory study, Elton and
\par
Carey (1984) judge delays of 6 seconds or longer to be unacceptable
\par
to users.
\par
The preceding catalog of benefits and costs is not complete. For
\par
instance, additional delayed benefits and costs could be enumerated.
\par
It would also be possible to categorize those listed here in different
\par
taxonomies (e.g., Sepstrup, 1980; see also Capon \& Lutz, 1979\textasciitilde. My
\par
goal is not a catalog that is definitive, but one that is representative.
\par
That is, it illustrates the full range of \emph{kinds }of benefits and costs.
\par
Then a benefit-cost analysis can be performed for the new actively
\par
intelligent information systems just as for traditional passively knowl-
\par
edgeable ones. For example, any annoying delay in receiving requestedIntelligent Information Systems 123
\par
information is an important consideration for most new systems but
\par
may be largely irrelevant to printed information.
\par
Especially important is recognizing the role of intangible, psycho-
\par
logical factors that are less prominent in popular theories, differ
\par
more widely across consumers, and are harder to quantify. This is
\par
particularly pertinent to public policy decisions where monetary
\par
considerations are often undeservedly prominent and any other
\par
consideration should be translated into monetary units (e.g., the
\par
monetary value of a human life or of retaining a manufacturing job).
\par
My point is that consumers themselves will base their decision to use
\par
or not use information on a full range of benefits and costs.
\par
Researchers and policy makers must do the same.
\par
\emph{Do Consumers Perform a Benefit-Cost Analysis?}
\par
I believe that consumers generally perform a benefit-cost analysis of
\par
the value of using a product information system. I do not claim that
\par
this analysis is complete, precise, or universal. Usually it is incom-
\par
plete and crude in its precision, and sometimes it may not be
\par
performed at all. I also recognize that some benefits and costs may
\par
be harder to estimate than others (e.g., the value of knowledge for
\par
future purchases). This may bias the analysis away from these
\par
considerations toward those that are more specific or immediate like
\par
monetary costs or frustration with delays. And the analysis is
\par
undoubtedly subject to some of the judgmental biases exposed by
\par
behavioral decision researchers (e.g., Bazerman, 1986; Kahneman,
\par
Slovic, \& Tversky, 1982).
\par
Nonetheless, sooner or later, precisely or imprecisely, consumers
\par
are going to base their decision to use (or reuse) a product
\par
information system on whether the benefits of information use
\par
exceed its costs. Monetary costs, when present, are obvious to nearly
\par
all consumers, but even more so are psychological costs like the time
\par
and effort expended (which are always present) and experiential
\par
negatives like annoyance or frustration. The immediacy of these
\par
costs calls our attention to them. This leads naturally to a considera-
\par
tion of the compensating benefits and to a judgment as to which is
\par
greater. I believe that this benefit-cost comparison is applied equally
\par
to passively knowledgeable and actively intelligent systems. It is used
\par
by both educated and uneducated consumers, the rich and poor,
\par
ignorant and informed, old and young, and those in the Third World124 J. Edward Russo
\par
as well as in industrialized societies. In short, the success or failure of
\par
any information system for consumers can be understood and
\par
explained in terms of a benefit-cost analysis from the consumer's
\par
perspective.
\par
OPEN QUESTIONS FOR CONSUMER POLICY
\par
Two questions seem fundamental to the effective planning of
\par
consumer policy: What kinds of information \emph{should }be provided to
\par
consumers via the new technologies, and what kinds of information
\par
\emph{can }be provided? These two issues correspond roughly to informa-
\par
tion demand and information supply.
\par
\emph{What Information Should Be Provided?}
\par
Suppose that we try to anticipate the kinds of product information
\par
that, available in the ideal world of the new information technologies,
\par
consumers would want and use. To avoid being anchored on current
\par
availability, we might perform a "zero-based" analysis of the demand
\par
for product information. In other contexts this task is called a needs
\par
assessment. This major undertaking is beyond the scope of this
\par
paper, though it is an important exercise for policy makers. Instead,
\par
let me outline how such a needs assessment might be performed.
\par
An obvious strategy is to use personal interviews of consumers,
\par
i.e., a standard survey technique. This approach is problematical,
\par
however, because consumers themselves may have difficulty imagin-
\par
ing new types of information enabled by technologies with which
\par
they have little or no experience.
\par
Instead, a two-stage process might be used. The first stage consists
\par
of concept generation, where the concepts are possible kinds of
\par
product information. Concepts might be produced by experts in the
\par
technology, consumer advocates, existing information providers, and
\par
so on. They might also be generated by consumers' imagining how
\par
they would use a trusted human expert. That is, consumers can be
\par
asked: What information do you get from the experts you know, or if
\par
experts were available what questions would you ask them? This first
\par
stage might yield the following kinds of product information: (a)
\par
instructions for installation, use, or repair of a purchased product onIntelligent Information Systems 125
\par
videotape or videodisc; (b) advice on auto route planning including,
\par
e.g., information on time-of-day or day-of-week traffic patterns, road
\par
repairs, and current conditions such as weather or accidents, via
\par
videotex and an expert system; and (c) whether a family new to a city
\par
should purchase a second car or could rely On public transportation
\par
from buses, taxis, airport limo services, etc.
\par
In a second stage interview with consumers these information
\par
concepts could be tested, ideally with access to demonstrations of
\par
the new technology. Admittedly this kind of concept testing is
\par
difficult, but it can be productive and it may be necessary to the
\par
planning of policy.
\par
This empirical approach should be complemented by a benefit-
\par
cost analysis. Whether we are evaluating an existing system or the
\par
prototype of a new system, the question of the optimal design can be
\par
cast in benefit-cost terms. Specifically, system builders should ask:
\par
How can benefits be increased or costs reduced most efficiently? In
\par
my own experience with passive (knowledgeable) systems, I have
\par
found it most effective to reduce the time and effort costs devoted
\par
to information processing (Russo et al., 1986). In contrast, the
\par
approach of enhancing benefits, e.g., by persuading consumers to
\par
value nutritious food more highly, has been relatively ineffective. Of
\par
course, whether it proves more effective to increase the perceived
\par
benefits of information use or to decrease its costs will vary with the
\par
specific context. Further, some new technologies will prove more
\par
successful at increasing benefits (e.g., videodiscs and computer
\par
graphics), while others like telecommunication are probably better at
\par
decreasing costs.
\par
One target benefit that I fear may be overlooked by consumer-
\par
oriented information providers (but not sellers) is the experiential
\par
value of using the systems. The new actively intelligent systems have
\par
a great capacity to be \emph{informative }but an even greater capacity to be
\par
\emph{entertaining. }Color and full motion video, sound from music or voice
\par
synthesis, artificial intelligence for timely answers to the consumer's
\par
most pressing questions, and computer graphics to simulate the
\par
appearance or operation of the product, all have a capacity for
\par
holding attention that is matched only by TV advertising or in-store
\par
demonstrations.
\par
While we tend to think of product information in utilitarian terms,
\par
I am arguing for the value of thinking of product information systems126 J. Edward Russo
\par
in both utilitarian and entertainment terms. The greater the enter-
\par
tainment benefit, other things equal, the greater the use of the system
\par
-- and the more product information communicated.
\par
Children's television may provide a useful analogy. The U.S.
\par
Children's Television Workshop developed and produced Sesame
\par
Street, which holds children's attention through entertainment while
\par
educating them at the same time. Similarly, different professors can
\par
teach the same topic in an entertaining or boring way. The informa-
\par
tion content may be nearly identical, but the vividness of examples,
\par
the ability to involve students, the animation of the speaker, and
\par
so on, can make one lecturer much more effective. Many people
\par
associate entertainment with advertising and think of information
\par
as the antidote to this subtle, arational (Russo, 1978) and non-
\par
informative (Sepstrup, 1985) method for influencing consumers. I
\par
believe that this traditional separation between entertainment and
\par
information is unnecessary and counter-productive. There is no
\par
reason why product information cannot be delivered in an enter-
\par
taining way via the new technologies. If consumer-oriented informa-
\par
tion providers choose not to exploit the potential experiential
\par
benefits, they can be sure that sellers will. They already are, with
\par
electronic kiosks one example.
\par
\emph{Increased Information Seeking?}
\par
The new technologies are expanding the range of available product
\par
information systems. For any given information need this can only
\par
increase (or leave unchanged) the benefit-cost difference since the
\par
consumer has more alternative information sources/systems to
\par
choose from. The end result ought to be an increase in information
\par
u s e .
\par
Part of the improved situation for consumers will result from
\par
better \emph{targeting. }This refers to the ability of consumers to target the
\par
information they need and not have to waste money and effort
\par
sorting through mostly irrelevant information. Videotex enables
\par
efficient targeting of the information topic, and expert systems will
\par
enable targeting at the level of specific questions. It should be noted
\par
that targeting may be a two-way street with sellers trying to target
\par
potential buyers with their selling messages.Intelligent Information Systems 127
\par
\emph{The Information Poor." Will the Poor Get Poorer?}
\par
In each country there are consumers disadvantaged by illiteracy,
\par
poverty, rural isolation, and unfamiliarity with computer technology.
\par
Will the new technologies further impoverish this segment or will
\par
they enable more informed participation in the marketplace? It is too
\par
early to know with certainty, but some things are becoming apparent.
\par
Certainly those who own personal computers and other equipment
\par
will obtain earlier access to the new product information systems.
\par
But in the longer run disadvantaged consumers may benefit as much,
\par
if not more, from the new technologies. For instance, illiteracy (or
\par
low literacy) will be less of a handicap as audio/video devices
\par
augment printed material. Telecommunication should mitigate rural
\par
isolation.
\par
Even the cost and familiarity problems can be overcome (if the
\par
benefits justify it) by facilities staffed with counsellors and open to
\par
consumers. These \emph{information intermediaries }may be sponsored by
\par
labor unions, senior citizens clubs, and social service agencies.
\par
Besides access to the new product information systems, these
\par
intermediaries may offer counselling. That is, if the new systems are
\par
highly valuable, but cost or familiarity barriers hinder broad usage,
\par
product information counsellors may serve as the interface between
\par
the systems and ordinary consumers. Such counsellors might be
\par
regular employees of the sponsoring organization (i.e., of the labor
\par
union, co-operative, church group, etc.), or they might be specialists,
\par
similar to home economists, who are hired by the organization
\par
possibly on a part-time basis.
\par
I acknowledge that my view of new product information systems
\par
is optimistic and largely speculative. There are not even many
\par
parallel experiences to turn to. One may be the Children's Television
\par
Workshop's Sesame Street program for preschool children men-
\par
tioned earlier. Using the technology of television it has successfully
\par
reached the advantaged and disadvantaged alike, e.g., teaching read-
\par
ing skills to children whose parents are marginally literate. (However,
\par
for a critical view of Sesame Street, see Cook, 1975.) Further, there
\par
seems to be little evidence that the new information technologies
\par
currently in place have widened the gap between advantaged and
\par
disadvantaged consumers. The Working Group on Consumers and
\par
the New Information Technologies of the International Organization
\par
of Consumers' Unions has reported: "Experience does not bear out\textbf{1 2 8 J. Edward Russo}
\par
the argument of pessimists that the new information technology
\par
devices are confusing and frightening to old people, to people living
\par
in remote or rural areas, to the unsophisticated" (Mitchell \& Murley,
\par
1984, p. 14).
\par
\emph{What Information Can Be Provided?}
\par
I see three types of constraints on the kinds of product information
\par
that can be usefully offered to consumers via the new technologies.
\par
These are technological, economic, and behavioral.
\par
The technological constraints can affect the quantity, quality, or
\par
speed of information. As one example, current capabilities restrict
\par
the size of expert systems. It is possible to build a knowledge-based
\par
consumer advising system for a specific product like a washing
\par
machine. However, the current technology cannot support an expert
\par
system for legal advice on product grievances. The breadth of
\par
knowledge needed to provide legal advice is too great to be
\par
incorporated into a knowledge-based system (Prerau, 1985).
\par
Economic constraints include the monetary costs of the new tech-
\par
nology, obsolescence, and the critical mass problem. Obsolescence is
\par
particularly tricky. Some technologies may fail because consumers
\par
see them as being replaced by a superior form in the near future. To
\par
take one current example, are compact (audio) disc sales lower that
\par
they might be because consumers, especially audiophiles, are aware
\par
of the direct audio to digital technology that will be introduced in the
\par
near future? Investment in a new technology depends, in part, on its
\par
expected commercial life.
\par
Behavioral constraints refer to the benefit-cost difference dis-
\par
cussed earlier. It is safe to say that the new technologies are relaxing
\par
this constraint, possibly in a revolutionary way. There are some
\par
kinds of product information for which benefits may exceed costs for
\par
the first time in history (e.g., video catalogs for at-home shopping).
\par
And there are others for which this may happen in the next few
\par
years (e.g., expert systems for advising on home appliance pur-
\par
chases). The new technologies are especially powerful at lowering
\par
effort and time costs. Via a personal computer one can examine,
\par
compare, and order products from home. The time to drive to a
\par
vendor, or to several vendors to make product comparisons, has
\par
been eliminated. Computer databases are already more convenient
\par
in time and effort than visits to conventional libraries. As computer-Intelligent Information Systems 129
\par
implemented product advisors become available, consumers can
\par
obtain authoritative advice (a) at low cost, (b) even if they live in a
\par
rural area, (c) on Sunday at 9 PM, (d) with superior video (e.g.,
\par
computer graphics) to educate as well as explain, and (e) still get a
\par
second opinion from another computer expert -- all before 10 PM
\par
that same evening. Although all information systems are constrained
\par
to have the benefits to consumers exceed their costs, the rapid
\par
relaxation of this behavioral constraint is one of the most striking
\par
aspects of the new intelligent product information systems.
\par
\emph{Who Should Provide Product Information via the New Technologies ?}
\par
To decide who should provide active information systems to con-
\par
sumers a second benefit-cost analysis is needed, this one from the
\par
information providers' perspective (Russo, 1978). If the providers'
\par
economic benefits exceed their costs, a system is economically
\par
viable. It does not require an economic subsidy to survive. If it is not
\par
economically viable, then government or some other organization
\par
must subsidize the system. Both options are considered, with the role
\par
of government covered at greater length in a later section.
\par
\emph{Retailers. }Retailers are among those marketplace actors who are
\par
most threatened by these new systems. Printed (mailed) catalogs
\par
already make retail outlets places for inspection rather than purchase
\par
for some buyers. Electronic and video catalogs, both at-home and
\par
via public kiosks, will further erode retailers' role in the marketplace.
\par
Thus, they may need to participate in the new information provision
\par
game or be squeezed out. For instance, retailers may want to offer
\par
their customers access to services such as expert systems for the
\par
product categories they sell.
\par
\emph{Wholesalers. }Wholesalers (or retailers) who market through catalogs
\par
and never have face-to-face contact with buyers are poised to be
\par
major beneficiaries of the new technology. Existing mail-order firms
\par
may expand to electronic catalogs, electronic kiosks, or computerized
\par
expert advisors. Alternatively, entirely new firms may enter the
\par
market. Since a strength in the new information technology may
\par
outweigh traditional wholesaling skills, new entrants like COMP-U-
\par
STORE may prove successful.130 J. Edward Russo
\par
\emph{New information providers\textasciitilde advisors. }A third type of potential infor-
\par
mation provider is a profit making enterprise that offers the con-
\par
sumer nothing but product information. Such firms may specialize in
\par
a product area like home appliances, food products, or automobiles,
\par
or in an information technology like videotex or artificial intelli-
\par
gence. Alternatively, they may cover a full range of products and
\par
services much like \emph{Consumer Reports }and similar magazines. An
\par
interesting version of the non-selling information provider is an
\par
industry trade association that does not sell directly to consumers
\par
(though its supporting members do). Such a group would have the
\par
advantage of access to vast amounts of information from its manu-
\par
facturer members. There would be pressure on all members to
\par
participate or risk lost sales -- the way an airline cannot afford to
\par
withhold its flight schedule from centralized reservation systems. Its
\par
main disadvantage for consumers is a restriction on the information
\par
provided, probably the omission of negative attributes like injury
\par
rates and not providing overall recommendations. A trade associa-
\par
tion's members would resist disseminating negative attributes that
\par
might reduce overall sales or create a negative feeling toward the
\par
product. They might also resist head-to-head comparisons based on
\par
overall evaluations as too risky compared to competing on con-
\par
sumers' vague impressions of product quality that can be more or
\par
less controlled through advertising.
\par
\emph{Consumer organizations. }Organizations like Consumers Union in
\par
the U.S. are a fourth possible information provider. Indeed, it seems
\par
essential that such non-profit consumer-oriented organizations par-
\par
ticipate in the new product information systems. First, they need to
\par
protect their current position against erosion by competing commer-
\par
cial information providers. Magazines and the broadcast media may
\par
no longer be the major vehicles for delivering product information.
\par
Second, such organizations can capitalize on existing advantages
\par
such as credibility, expertise, a mature organization, and testing
\par
facilities. This makes them strong competitors against retailers or
\par
wholesalers who expand into information provision and neq\textasciitilde{} in-
\par
dependent information (but not selling) services. Nonetheless, to the
\par
extent that expertise in the new active systems (including techno-
\par
logical, economic, and behavioral expertise) is important to success,
\par
consumer organizations that delay in acquiring this expertise may
\par
lose out to other market actors.Intelligent Information Systems 131
\par
\emph{What Is the Role of Government?}
\par
The final potential actor is the government itself, most likely through
\par
a government-sponsored consumer organization. Such an alternative
\par
is nearly unthinkable in the U.S. except where natural (i.e., profit-
\par
oriented) market mechanisms do not provide information that is
\par
seen as essential, e.g., health and safety information. However, as
\par
national economies are based less on free market mechanisms and
\par
are more centrally planned and controlled, the need increases for
\par
a government organization to provide active product information
\par
systems. I take up the role of government in such systems more fully
\par
below.
\par
Government has at least two major roles it can play in the arena
\par
of new product information systems, financial aid and standards
\par
setting. Most directly, it may choose to offer its own systems via a
\par
government department whose clients are consumers. Teletel, the
\par
French national videotex system, was created by the state-owned
\par
monopoly that controls the country's postal and telephone services.
\par
In the face of the high level of activity in the marketplace and the
\par
current political climate both in Europe and the U.S., this govern-
\par
ment-sponsored approach seems less likely. However, Teletel's
\par
apparent success may overcome this resistance, especially in Europe.
\par
A more likely role involves subsidies, direct or indirect. A direct
\par
subsidy would provide monetary assistance. An indirect subsidy
\par
might take the form of support for the development of the technol-
\par
ogy. To the extent that the U.S. government has supported research
\par
in artificial intelligence, information retrieval systems, and all the
\par
fundamental technology on which intelligent information systems are
\par
based, it has already provided subsidies. Of course, this indirect
\par
subsidization could go much further and take many forms. T h e
\par
expert systems for farmers may "spin off" expertise useful for
\par
general consumers. If it built systems for military personnel, e.g., for
\par
information on transportation alternatives in new cities, the govern-
\par
ment would develop systems that could be readily transferred to the
\par
general consumer. The critical mass problem could also be solved by
\par
government participation. By providing hardware or free subscrip-
\par
tions to government employees, a large enough user base might be
\par
created to reach critical mass, i.e., to support a supply of information
\par
services large enough to attract paying consumer subscribers.132 J. Edward Russo
\par
\emph{Standards setting. }A second role for government is standards setting.
\par
This task has already been faced for videotex transmission standards.
\par
The Japanese government has set a full range of hardware and
\par
software standards that it hopes will facilitate complete compatibility
\par
among traditional and novel telecommunication devices.
\par
However, I want to suggest a second, less-well recognized area for
\par
standards setting. Should there be standards for accuracy of informa-
\par
tion and advice? As we move from simple information provision like
\par
videotex toward knowledge-based advice-giving systems, we move
\par
from facts to judgments. Should intelligent information systems be
\par
treated like passive information or like human experts? ff the former,
\par
the mechanisms of consumer redress for false information are mainly
\par
reactive, e.g., lawsuits for recovery of damage, withdrawal of patron-
\par
age, and negative publicity (even if only word-of-mouth). If these
\par
new active systems are treated as human experts should there be
\par
government certification? Lawyers and physicians must pass govern-
\par
ment examinations, and may lose their licenses to practice if their
\par
performance does not measure up to some standard. In their analysis
\par
of the policy implications of the new telecommunication and video
\par
technologies, Sepstrup and Olander (1986) emphasize standards
\par
setting. They assert (p. 16) that "greater priority must be given in
\par
the field of consumer policy to rules, standards and limitations for
\par
\emph{commercial }consumer information" [emphasis in original].
\par
To what extent should governments protect information suppliers
\par
in order to encourage the development of active product information
\par
systems? Consider the possibility of government licensing, which can
\par
affect the vulnerability of new advising systems to legal harassment
\par
by manufacturers. If such systems are effective, manufacturers of
\par
non-recommended products will lose sales, and the short-term option
\par
of attacking the information provider may seem very attractive. It
\par
may also prove chillingly effective if the information provider is a
\par
newly established organization without the resources to mount an
\par
expensive and protracted legal defense. Government certification
\par
would offer some support against such harassment by manufacturers.
\par
It would also provide some credibility to new organizations that are
\par
unknown to the consumer.
\par
Closely related is the issue of legal liability and insurance cover-
\par
age. Like any human expert (lawyers or physicians, for instance) an
\par
expert advising system cannot be perfect. Yet its imperfections are
\par
very public. The logic that leads to an error is completely traceable,
\par
which is quite different from the private (unrecorded) discussionIntelligent Information Systems 133
\par
between physician and patient. This openness makes the system
\par
unusually vulnerable to legal harassment. An immediate consequence
\par
may be intolerable insurance rates from malpractice. Here again
\par
government certification might help.
\par
The free riding problem is another vulnerability of information
\par
suppliers that may only be solved by government action. Briefly, free
\par
riding occurs when one information provider benefits from informa-
\par
tion originated by another provider and does not compensate the
\par
latter. When providers can free ride on others' costly information,
\par
the originators cannot recover all of its value and are economically
\par
discouraged from producing such an unprotected commodity. The
\par
situation is not unlike what would face book publishers if there were
\par
no copyright protection. The free riding problem has been examined
\par
by Eovaldi (1985) who advocates protection similar to literary
\par
copyrights.
\par
At the same time that the "public good" nature of product
\par
information exposes it to free riding, the problem may be less severe
\par
for intelligent information systems than for traditional ones. With
\par
incieasing intelligence comes increasing personalization of the infor-
\par
mation. For example, an expert system for advising on the purchase
\par
of an automobile would personalize its recommendation based on
\par
the driving habits, price range, and many other characteristics of a
\par
particular user. If this information were reproduced and distributed,
\par
the advice would fit relatively few other consumers. Indeed, instead
\par
of obviating the need of other auto buyers to use the information
\par
system, encountering a copy of one user's advice might stimulate
\par
system use by others. Just the opposite happens with copies of
\par
\emph{Consumer Reports }or other printed information. Because it is not
\par
personalized a \emph{Consumer Reports }article applies to all potential
\par
purchasers, and Consumers Union suffers from free riding.
\par
The possible functions of government in the service of the new
\par
active product information systems are summarized in Table If. Note
\par
that any permanent action includes the possibility of termination. For
\par
example, a subsidy to a private information provider could be
\par
terminated whenever that provider was judged to be economically
\par
viable. Although the list of options in Table II may not be complete,
\par
it covers many of the important alternatives facing policy makers.
\par
