\par
for consumer organizations is as lobbyists influencing government in
\par
both its economic and legislative initiatives. There are important
\par
issues of both public policy and consumer protection (Jones, 1984;
\par
Shapiro, 1986). For instance, on the policy side is the question of
\par
access: Should all information providers and all consumers be
\par
guaranteed access to a videotex system? On the protection side are
\par
problems like impulse purchasing through a videotex service and the
\par
regulation of computer-based artificially intelligent advisors. Com-
\par
paring the complexity of policy versus protection problems with
\par
respect to videotex, Shapiro (1986) concludes that the public policy
\par
issues are more difficult to resolve. Protection problems benefit from
\par
regulatory practices governing analogous consumer transactions that
\par
can, with appropriate modification, be applied to promotion, pur-
\par
chasing, and payment via videotex. Shapiro's analysis, however, is
\par
confined to the Canadian environment. His conclusions may not
\par
apply to Europe where the new technologies promise to lowerIntelligent Information Systems 135
\par
national trade barriers and create new problems in cross-national
\par
consumer protection.
\par
The third and last role for consumer-oriented organizations is
\par
outreach to disadvantaged consumers. They may want to sponsor
\par
central facilities with the necessary equipment staffed by counsellors.
\par
Such a facility might contain videotex terminals, electronic kiosks
\par
and other video technologies, or a library of expert systems. These
\par
central facilities might be open to the public or only to the spon-
\par
soring organization's members. They might be free to users or charge
\par
fees in various ways.
\par
Outreach capability can be extended to distant locations. The
\par
central facility can be made mobile by putting it in a truck.
\par
Individuals can even be reached in their homes. For example, expert
\par
systems require only a portable computer that can easily be brought
\par
by a counsellor into the homes of the elderly, handicapped, isolated,
\par
etc.
\par
CONCLUSION
\par
There are new telecommunication, video, and artificial intelligence
\par
technologies. New intelligent product information systems based on
\par
these technologies have been built, and more are under develop-
\par
ment. These systems have the potential to serve the consumer
\par
interest. It is too early to predict whether this interest will be
\par
adequately served through natural market mechanisms such as the
\par
emergence of a new class of commercially viable consumer-oriented
\par
information providers -- the consumer equivalent of business'
\par
management consultants. Policy makers should monitor the develop-
\par
ment of intelligent product information systems and be prepared to
\par
take action if the consumer's interests are not being met.
