\par
nature and causes of occupational disease. Part III analyzes the consequences
\par
of the lack of information about occupational disease and concludes that while
\par
there are many reasons for the failure of the labor markets and government
\par
regulation to protect workers from occupational disease, lack of information is
\par
the key element. Part IV analyzes recent attempts to force the disclosure of
\par
information held by employers in order to improve the performance of labor
\par
markets and government regulation and to increase scientific research into oc-
\par
cupational disease. Finally, Part V discusses proposals to shift the financial
\par
consequences of risk posed by lack of information about occupational disease
\par
away from workers, where it is presently located, to employers and the public.
