\textbf{OCCUPATIONAL }DISEASE
\par
\textbf{C. }LIMITS OF THE DATA
\par
While government reports claim to have documented the epidemic propor-
\par
tions of occupational disease, the statistical evidence is so equivocal that the
\par
extent of the problem remains unknown. Scientists do agree, however, that
\par
there have been significant outbreaks of occupational disease affecting
\par
thousands of workers. Moreover, they agree that thousands of potentially haz-
\par
ardous chemicals remain in the workplace. Unfortunately, most of these
\par
chemicals have not yet been studied, and information about those that have
\par
been studied is often sketchy. Part II of this article explores the reasons for the
\par
lack of adequate information about occupational disease.
\par
\textbf{II. }PRODUCTION OF INFORMATION ABOUT \textbf{OCCUPATIONAL }DISEASE
\par
An efficient market requires that both buyers and sellers have complete and
\par
equal information about the subject matter of a bargain. Employer and em-
\par
ployee demand for information about occupational disease, however, is lim-
\par
ited because the cost of its acquisition often exceeds the benefits from its
\par
purchase. Further, it is often financially disadvantageous for employers to
\par
share information they obtain with their employees. Finally, even when em-
\par
ployees demand the information, suppliers will not always find it profitable to
\par
provide it.
\par
An employer will produce the information necessary to recognize and abate
\par
workplace hazards if the costs of abatement are less than alternative costs.
\par
Those alternative costs are \emph{ex ante }compensation, \textbf{in }the form of wage premi-
\par
ums, or \emph{expost }compensation, under the workers' compensation and tort sys-
\par
tems. Demands for wage premiums, however, are limited by the general lack
\par
of information. Even when hazards are known, workers may lack sufficient
\par
bargaining power to obtain wage premiums,46 and the workers' option to quit
\par
may be limited by the unavailability of other employment.47 Further, regula-
\par
tion in the form of workers' compensation, tort awards, or OSHA emission
\par
standards, has only a limited impact on many employers.4 8
\par
To the extent that employers have an incentive to obtain information about
\par
health effects, there are corresponding incentives to keep the data confidential
\par
and to prevent others from obtaining similar information.49 Numerous in-
\par
stances of employer "cover-ups" of research revealing adverse health effects
\par
have been reported. 50 Similarly, employers have prevented independent re-
