\par
III. \textbf{MARKET PERFORMANCE}
\par
\textbf{A. LABOR MARKETS}
\par
Private markets work on the basis of self-interest. Buyers demand the goods
\par
or services they most desire and sellers obtain the greatest financial reward \textbf{by}
\par
providing those products. Economists regard this result as efficient because
\par
scarce resources are not utilized to provide products that are less desirable to
\par
consumers.63 In a truly efficient market, the cost of a product must reflect the
\par
value of all the resources that are used for its manufacture and production.
\par
Therefore, if a manufacturing process produces toxic substances that cause ill-
\par
nesses, the manufacturer should pay for all of the costs associated with those
\par
illnesses. 6 4
\par
Ordinarily, there would be no incentive for a manufacturer to pay for these
\par
