\par
to a purchaser with sufficient resources is plagued \textbf{by }a second problem. Any potential purchaser of
\par
information will have trouble determining its value without knowing the results of the research. \textbf{If }the
\par
researcher reveals the information to the potential buyer, the information cannot then be sold. As a
\par
consequence, the sale price of any information will be discounted either because the buyer is assuming
\par
some risk that the information will not be useful, or because part of the information was revealed in
\par
order to make the sale. \textbf{\emph{Cf. }}Arrow, \emph{supra }note \textbf{56, }at \textbf{615 }(use of information in any productive way is
\par
bound to reveal it, at least in part). Rather than engage in expensive research in the hope that it can be
\par
sold under these circumstances, research firms will sell only their services and not a final product.
