\textbf{B. }REGULATORY \textbf{MARKETS}
\par
There is little evidence that workers, even when aided by organized labor,
\par
obtain the wage premiums that economists predict they should receive for haz-
\par
ardous jobs. The federal government and the states have responded to this
\par
failure by regulating the labor market. In adopting this regulation, however,
\par
legislators failed to consider adequately that where there is inadequate infor-
\par
mation, the degree of protection will depend on the nature of the legal rules
\par
under which the regulation is imposed.
\par
Legal rules can authorize a manufacturer to pollute until the public interest
\par
requires that the pollution be abated. In this scheme, the government, or any
\par
worker seeking government action, has the burden of proving that an emission
\par
is hazardous. If there is insufficient information to prove this, workers will
\par
continue to be exposed to the hazard until the information is developed. On
\par
the other hand, legal rules can authorize the government to restrain the use of
\par
a manufacturing process until the employer proves that it is safe. In this situa-
\par
tion, workers will not be exposed to a chemical until sufficient information
\par
about its safety is developed.
\par
Regulatory responses to occupational disease have authorized employers to
\par
pollute until the government has sufficient information to act. To be effective,
\par
however, this type of regulation requires the same missing information that in
\par
part caused the labor markets to fail. The following section examines the con-
\par
sequences of this discrepancy for regulation of the labor market.
\par
