\par
workers' compensation could be federalized, 54 but congressional action is
\par
stymied by the same interest groups that have slowed state reform.
\par
Opponents of reform represent an impressive political force. Typically, the
\par
insurance industry, workers' compensation lawyers, and employers have com-
\par
bined to oppose reform efforts. Support for such efforts has come from labor
\par
unions, workers' compensation administrators, and judges who dislike the re-
\par
sponsibility of interpreting standards that produce harsh results. The limited
\par
reform that has occurred has usually been adopted with the agreement of op-
\par
ponents, who compromised to prevent more drastic changes.' 55
