\emph{The Tort System. }As a result of the ineffectiveness and inefficiency of
\par
the workers' compensation system, workers who suffer from occupational dis-
\par
eases have attempted to bring common law tort actions. Workers who are suc-
\par
cessful in these actions can recover all of their economic losses, plus
\par
compensation for pain and suffering and punitive damages, both of which are
\par
unavailable under workers' compensation. Companies that are responsible for
\par
causing occupational illnesses will, in theory, be made to bear the full cost of
\par
those illnesses. Thus, they should undertake remedial efforts until the cost of
\par
those efforts is greater than the cost of defending tort suits and paying the
\par
resulting settlements or judgments. The actual outcome, however, is quite
\par
different.
\par
Few workers disabled by occupational disease receive a full measure of
\par
compensation under tort remedies. Just as with workers' compensation claims,
\par
ignorance about the causal relationship between workplace exposure and la-
\par
tent illness keeps many workers from filing suit. For instance, the Selikoff
\par
study found that only sixteen percent of eligible survivors filed third-party tort
\par
suits for the asbestos-related deaths of their relatives. 56 Those who do file suit
\par
rarely recover all of their economic losses. The Selikoff study found that most
