\textbf{A }fourth obstacle to tort suits is that plaintiffs may have no idea who manu-
\par
factured the toxic substances to which they were exposed. In fact, workers
\par
often do not even know the names of the chemicals in the workplace,' 7 8 much
\par
less their manufacturer. In a suit involving the drug \textbf{DES, }the California
\par
Supreme Court adopted the concept of market share liability, which shifts the
\par
burden of proof on the issue of identification from plaintiffs to defendants and
\par
subjects defendants to liability in proportion to their market share unless they
\par
can show they did not manufacture the product that harmed the plaintiff.1 79
\par
Widespread adoption of this legal theory would be helpful to workers but
\par
would also be enormously expensive and slow since it can only occur on a
\par
state-by-state and product-by-product basis.
