Hazard warnings would apprise workers that they were being exposed to
\par
dangerous substances and that they should take the precautions recommended
\par
in the warnings to protect themselves. Armed with this knowledge, workers
\par
could attempt to secure \emph{ex ante }compensation for these dangers. In addition,
\par
employees are more likely to relate serious illnesses to workplace hazards
\par
when they have been previously warned that their workplace may be danger-
\par
ous. Warnings therefore should also increase the rate at which \emph{ex post }com-
\par
pensation is sought. Employees could use their access to medical and exposure
\par
records to obtain evidence for workers' compensation, tort, and \textbf{OSHA }pro-
\par
ceedings and to monitor employer compliance with applicable health regula-
\par
tions. The usefulness of this information, however, depends on how readily it
\par
is available.
\par
Under existing statutory authority workers will not always receive the infor-
\par
mation they require. Under federal and some state legislation, employers can
\par
be ordered to provide hazard warnings and to retain and disclose records as
\par
part of the regulation of employee exposure to a specific hazard.3 10 Workers
\par
will benefit, however, only when a health agency is in a position to issue such
\par
regulations. OSHA's history strongly suggests that promulgation of regula-
\par
tions concerning exposure records is a time-consuming and difficult task.31'
\par
Under federal and some state legislation, employers can also be ordered to
\par
give employees access to medical and exposure records that the employers vol-
\par
untarily maintain.3 12 Nevertheless, employees still may not obtain much infor-
\par
mation. Many employers do not voluntarily keep medical and exposure
\par
records concerning their employees, probably because of the expense involved.
\par
Employers may also wish to avoid providing employees with information that
\par
could assist them in making compensation claims against the employer.3 13 In
\par
the absence of a legal order, employers would be compelled to give access to
\par
records only if enough other employers provided similar access and, as a re-
\par
sult, competition for workers required that employers offer the information as
\par
part of their fringe benefits.31 4
\par
These loopholes in statutory authority can be remedied by authorizing regu-
\par
