\textbf{B. }THE LEGALITY OF REQUIRING \textbf{DISCLOSURE }OF INFORMATION
\par
Opponents have challenged the legality of OSHA's records access and haz-
\par
ard warning rules. The records access rule was challenged by an industry
\par
group which claimed that OSHA lacked the statutory and constitutional au-
\par
thority to order the disclosure of information. The hazard warning rule was
\par
challenged by labor unions and by states with their own disclosure laws. Both
\par
groups argued that the agency had issued its warning rule in order to preempt
\par
stronger local laws and that the rule ignored the plight of workers in non-
\par
manufacturing industries.328 The legality of requiring disclosure of non-trade-
\par
secret information is considered in the next section and that of requiring dis-
\par
closure of trade secret information in the following section.
\par
\textbf{1. }Disclosure of Non-Trade-Secrets
\par
Some states have clearly authorized the forced disclosure of information,329
\par
but for most agencies, including OSHA, the authority to require disclosure is
\par
governed by an ambiguous patchwork of statutes and recent judicial decisions.
\par
For these agencies, it is arguable that the authority to order disclosure of infor-
\par
mation exists, although additional legislation may be necessary to clarify the
\par
matter.
\par
An agency's authority to order disclosure of private information is also re-
\par
stricted by constitutional constraints. If forced disclosure of information can be
\par
considered to be a governmental search or seizure, fourth amendment limita-
\par
tions would apply. Moreover, the disclosure of medical records could impli-
\par
cate privacy concerns. Nevertheless, under prevailing authority, regulations
\par
requiring information disclosure should be considered to be constitutional.
