\par
such experimentation by forcing states to adopt only rules requiring indirect
\par
disclosure. Moreover, OSHA should have adopted a disclosure policy that
\par
would allow consideration of the danger of disclosure based on the particular
\par
situation. Disclosure should be required or prohibited on an industry-by-in-
\par
dustry basis as the facts warrant. In some industries, especially those that are
\par
not unionized, the need for direct disclosure may be especially compelling.
\par
Moreover, the danger of unauthorized disclosure in particular industries may
\par
be slight. For example, the trade secret may involve only a limited number of
\par
employees, or only those employees who are directly employed by the owner
\par
of the trade secret. A flexible rule would give OSHA more discretion to accu-
\par
mulate evidence concerning the advantages and disadvantages of direct disclo-
\par
sure and to offer greater protection to workers in appropriate circumstances.
