been determined that requiring disclosure of the identity of a substance is le-
\par
gal, employers and manufacturers will be unable to refuse to comply with re-
\par
quests for chemical identities. This approach, however, may not sufficiently
\par
protect the confidentiality of the information. The Reagan administration, on
\par
the other hand, has offered employers and manufacturers more protection by
\par
requiring disclosure only under certain terms and conditions, but it has
\par
thereby invited intransigence and delay.443 These more detailed procedures
\par
will undoubtedly limit the amount of information available. The current
\par
OSHA policy imposes the burden of establishing the need for disclosure and
\par
the unreasonableness of any demand for liquidated damages on the individual
\par
seeking disclosure. One reform that might discourage employer exploitation
\par
of this approach would be to shift the burden of proof on those issues to the
\par
trade secret owner.
\par
\emph{Legality of Disclosure. }There are significant statutory and constitutional
\par
questions concerning the legality of requiring disclosure of trade secrets. One
\par
statutory issue is whether OSHA or a similar agency is authorized to divulge
\par
trade secret information. Another issue is whether OSHA can preempt state
\par
regulation in the area of hazard communication and, if so, to what extent. A
\par
constitutional issue is whether the disclosure of trade secrets constitutes a tak-
\par
ing of property without just compensation in violation of the fifth amendment.
\par
Each of these questions is considered below.
