V. REFORM OF INFORMATION \textbf{REGULATION}
\par
Recent interest in the forced disclosure of information concerning occupa-
\par
tional disease has produced a complex scheme of laws and regulations and has
\par
raised a number of statutory and constitutional issues concerning their legality.
\par
Workers in manufacturing industries are protected by OSHA's hazard warning
\par
rule. The millions of workers who are not covered by the hazard warning rule
\par
are protected by those state rules which have not been preempted by OSHA,
\par
by OSHA's records access rule if their employer voluntarily maintains expo-
\par
sure or medical records, and by disclosure requirements that are part of the
\par
few exposure standards promulgated by OSHA. The agency's warning rule
\par
contains a definition of hazardous chemicals that is narrower than that used by
\par
the records access rule. As a consequence, the coverage of the warning rule is
\par
limited. A similar restriction on the records access rule has been proposed.
\par
Information disclosure is also limited by restrictions put on the disclosure of
\par
trade secrets. OSHA's warning rule limits disclosure of trade secrets to health
\par
professionals with a considerable number of restrictions. The agency's records
\par
access rule orders disclosure to employees of trade secret identities, imposing
\par
only the condition that a confidentiality agreement be signed. OSHA, how-
\par
ever, has proposed to amend the records access rule to make its provisions
\par
concerning disclosure of trade secrets similar to those in the warning rule.
\par
State provisions vary, but some states apparently allow direct disclosure to em-
\par
ployees under certain circumstances. OSHA has attempted to preempt any
\par
direct disclosure requirements in the state laws for manufacturing industries.
\par
For workers in unionized industries, NLRB regulation could offer some
\par
\textbf{530. \emph{I }}at \textbf{362.19841 1295}
\par
\textbf{OCCUPATIONAL DISEASE}
\par
hope of additional information disclosure. While employers generally are re-
\par
quired to provide relevant information to unions, recent decisions have given
\par
them grounds for denying access to information they consider confidential.
\par
Although unions will probably prevail if they insist on obtaining access to the
\par
information under appropriate confidentiality orders, the opportunity for de-
\par
lay by employers is great. The high cost of prolonged litigation may make it
\par
difficult if not impossible for some unions to obtain information by this
\par
method.
\par
OSHA's attempts to limit the scope of disclosure regulation, including its
\par
preemption of state laws, have been motivated by the agency's desire to reduce
\par
the costs to industry of those regulations. OSHA may have gone too far in this
\par
direction. There appear to be significant gaps where workers are unlikely to
\par
obtain necessary information or to receive appropriate compensation for occu-
\par
pational illnesses. Additional OSHA regulation should tailor the regulatory
\par
response to the danger perceived in a particular industry. Workers should at
\par
least be given the identity of any chemical to which they are exposed, unless it
\par
is a trade secret. Further obligations should be based on a determination of
\par
what is necessary to protect workers. These obligations might include provi-
\par
sion of hazard warnings, disclosure of voluntarily kept records, and retention,
\par
maintenance, and disclosure of required records. Similarly, decisions about
\par
whether there will be direct or indirect disclosure of trade secrets should be
\par
based on an assessment of the workers' need to know the information.
\par
It is possible that a graduated regulatory scheme would involve the same
\par
delay OSHA has faced in the promulgation of exposure standards. On the
\par
other hand, OSHA should be able to implement this type of regulation without
\par
the information about chemical hazards needed to establish an exposure regu-
\par
lation. The standard for requiring regulation of an industry to produce addi-
\par
tional information should be the degree of suspicion concerning the possible
\par
dangers of its workplaces.
\par
It is also questionable whether OSHA now has the legal authority to order
\par
disclosure of information, especially if the information is a trade secret. The
\par
OSHA Act appears to give OSHA the necessary authority, but if pending law
\par
suits go against the agency, legislation will be necessary to reinstitute many of
\par
the aspects of its regulation. Legislation would then probably also be required
\par
to achieve the reforms proposed here. OSHA's ability to regulate information,
\par
however, may be limited even if appropriate legislation is enacted. If employ-
\par
ers' claims about the constitutional limitations on such regulation succeed, the
\par
ability to regulate information will be significantly limited.
\par
VI. \textbf{CONCLUSION}
\par
A fundamental flaw exists in the way in which labor markets have been
\par
regulated in order to prevent occupational disease. Labor markets have failed
\par
to produce an efficient distribution of the risk of occupational disease in signif-
\par
icant part because of a lack of adequate information about such illnesses. Reg-
\par
ulation intended to encourage or require employers to engage in greater
\par
preventive efforts has also failed because of the absence of the same informa-
\par
tion that is missing from the labor markets.THE GEORGETOWN LAW \textbf{JOURNAL}
\par
\textbf{1296 }[Vol. \textbf{72:1231}
\par
Efforts to reform workers' compensation, tort law, and OSHA regulation
\par
have been largely unavailing or counterproductive. Recommendations
\par
designed to improve workers' compensation, particularly through the use of
\par
presumptions and expert panels to determine difficult issues of causality, re-
\par
main unadopted. "Reforms" proposed for tort law, if adopted, would effec-
\par
tively reduce the amount of compensation for occupational diseases below the
\par
rather meager amount now available. OSHA's attempt to speed its approval
\par
process by adoption of a generic cancer policy was dealt a death blow by the
\par
Supreme Court's insistence that the agency quantify the extent of the danger
\par
posed by each substance on a case by case basis. In addition, difficult ques-
\par
tions of costs and benefits continue to slow OSHA decision-making despite the
\par
Supreme Court's holding that a cost-benefit decision is unnecessary for adop-
\par
tion of exposure limitations.
\par
The failure to improve regulatory performance in situations where inade-
\par
quate information exists has generated recent interest in regulations that force
\par
employers to disclose information that would be useful to market and regula-
\par
tory decisions and to scientific research. The disclosures that have been re-
\par
quired range from a limited obligation to issue nonspecific warnings about
\par
chemical hazards to an extensive obligation to reveal the identities of the sub-
\par
stances to which a worker has been exposed, even if they are trade secrets, and
\par
the medical records concerning that exposure. These regulations, however,
\par
cover only a small number of workers and may be vulnerable to employer
\par
claims that they are without statutory authority or violative of constitutional
\par
limitations. Nevertheless, they are a start toward resolving the fundamental
\par
problem of lack of information.
\par
Two additional reforms should be considered that would reallocate the risk
\par
of inadequate information from unprotected workers to the public or employ-
\par
ers, although their implementation may not be politically feasible. The first
\par
reform would be to increase governmental support for the production of infor-
\par
mation concerning occupational disease. This would be appropriate because
\par
of the public goods characteristics of such information. An analysis of the in-
\par
centives of the private market for the production of information about occupa-
\par
tional disease reveals that neither employers nor, to a lesser extent, labor
\par
unions, are likely to demand such information and that individual workers are
\par
unable to afford it. Even if employers purchase the information, they are un-
\par
likely to make the information public in the absence of regulation, because to
\par
do so would involve them in regulatory problems that otherwise could be
\par
avoided. If a significant amount of additional information were available,
\par
both the market and current regulatory schemes would function more
\par
productively.
\par
The second reform would be to require employers to prove the safety of a
\par
substance at the planned level of worker exposure before it could be used in a
\par
workplace. Drugs and pesticides are currently regulated in this manner.
\par
Adoption of this approach would wreak havoc with the country's industrial
\par
system, however, since thousands of chemicals are currently in industrial use
\par
and hundreds more enter into use each year. Presumably, it was this fact of
\par
life that caused Congress and state legislatures to allow the use of substances\textbf{OCCUPATIONAL DISEASE}
\par
\textbf{1984] 1297}
\par
until workers or regulatory agencies prove that they are unsafe at current expo-
\par
sure levels.
\par
A more feasible alternative would be to authorize OSHA to require employ-
\par
ers to prove the safety of any chemical for which there is sufficient data to
\par
suspect that the chemical might be hazardous at current exposure levels. EPA
\par
has been given an analogous authority under the Toxic Substances Control
\par
Act to regulate the manufacture and shipment of toxic chemicals.53' Workers
\par
would receive somewhat more protection under such a system because an em-
\par
ployer would not be allowed to continue using a suspect chemical unless suffi-
\par
cient information existed to prove that it was safe at current exposure levels.
\par
The determination of what evidence is sufficient to require a manufacturer to
\par
prove the safety of a substance could involve OSHA in the same time-con-
\par
suming and controversial litigation in which it presently finds itself. OSHA
\par
would be more likely to prevail, however, if it could create a rebuttable pre-
\par
sumption that a substance is not safe and thereby shift the burden of proving
\par
safety to employers.
\par
A change to even a limited licensing system would produce considerable
\par
costs, especially in lost production and delay in implementing new technology
\par
that used possibly hazardous substances. Whether the benefits of this reform
\par
would exceed the costs is impossible to calculate because the existing data is
\par
insufficient to estimate accurately the degree of danger posed by existing chem-
\par
icals. Nevertheless, the change should be made because it shifts some of the
\par
risk of occupational disease from employees to employers and the public that
\par
buys their products. This redistribution of risk is in keeping with an American
\par
tradition of preferring prevention to \emph{expost }compensation. This tradition is the
\par
basis for the creation of OSHA and for the decision that the agency should
\par
protect workers without consideration of whether the benefits of a regulation
\par
exceed the costs.
\par
Such a fundamental change in the regulation of labor markets is unlikely to
\par
occur unless there is a public scandal resulting from an epidemic of occupa-
\par
tional disease. In the meantime, American workers will continue to bear the
\par
risk of being exposed to chemicals that may be found to be highly toxic. In-
\par
terim reforms can help to lessen this danger, but it is unlikely to disappear in
\par
the near future. The problem of occupational disease is one of life's risks that
\par
workers will continue to bear.
\par
