\par
\textbf{Peter Seipel}
\par
\textbf{The Swedish Law \& Informatics Research Institute, University of Stockoim}
\par
Computer and data communications put their mark on
\par
society. In many sectors the impact has already been
\par
profound but, nevertheless, there is a common belief that
\par
we have so far merely seen the beginnings of a future,
\par
full-fledged world of information technologies. Not
\par
surprisingly, the development has been accompanied
\par
\textbf{by }repeated suggestions that the various legal issues
\par
associated with the technology ought to be treated in a
\par
co-ordinated and systematic fashion.'
\par
The field has been given names such as 'electronic
\par
law' or, more often, 'computer law'. Eventually, names
\par
such as 'information law' and 'data law' have also come
\par
into use. In this article we shall look at some of the
\par
characteristics of this field of law as well as the associated
\par
basic methodological concerns. In particular, an attempt
\par
will be made to explain why a notion such as information
\par
law is ofvalue and how the field may be further developed.
\par
The principal argument is that we seem to be dealing not
\par
with an obscure and short-lived phenomenon but rather
\par
with a change of perspective as to how certain legal issues
\par
of information handling and information systems ought
\par
to be treated.
\par
The early interest in the matter was primarily practical.
\par
It concerned disputes, claims, and conflicting interests
\par
which were engendered \textbf{by }the rapidly growing use of
\par
computing machinery. Among them was the protection
\par
of personal data which were being collected and stored in
\par
large volumes onto computer media. There were con-
\par
tracts for computer hardware and software and for data
\par
processing services. There was the protection of pro-
\par
prietary rights in computer programs.
\par
This practical interest in various aspects of substantive
\par
law associated with automated computing and data
\par
communications has certainly not dwindled away. Quite
\par
the contrary, what came into being as an engagement of a
\par
few specialists has now become an essential element of
\par
the information policies of many nations.2 Professional
\par
organisations and international institutions of many
\par
kinds presently pay attention to a broad variety of regula-
\par
tory issues associated with modern information tech-
\par
nology.'
\par
At the outset, the theoretical interest in computer law
\par
as an independent field of law was rather weak. Practical
\par
concerns dominated and descriptions or definitions of
\par
the field tended to rely on simple enumerations of legal
\par
issues or problem areas which were considered to be of
\par
interest. However, many experts no longer find this
\par
handbook approach sufficient.
\par
Already certain early studies had indicated the
\par
existence of more fundamental concerns with regard to
\par
the legal consequences of automation. For example, the
\par
introduction of computers in public administration
\par
necessitated a theoretically orientated discussion of the
\par
possibilities and consequences of automated handling of
\par
matters. Briefly, how should automated decision-making
\par
be accomodated within the system of procedural rules,
\par
liability rules, rules on competence, rules on access to
\par
\textbf{MEDIA LAW \& PRACTICE. JULY 1986 }government files and so forth?' And the legal treatment of
\par
computer software involved issues not only of an
\par
immediate practical nature. For example, computer pro-
\par
grams and compilations of data generated \textbf{by }automatic
\par
means began to attract theoretical interests as a new form
\par
of 'information goods'. Should a computer program be
\par
regarded as some sort of 'soft machine' or 'instant hard-
\par
ware'? Can a computer be the creator of a literary or
\par
musical work? Does the execution of a series of machine
\par
instructions on computing machinery constitute a copy-
\par
right infringement?
\par
Thus, increasingly often attention was focused on
\par
various theoretical and methodological issues associated
\par
with the law of automation and data communications. \textbf{A}
\par
parallel development concerned the design of courses in
\par
'computer law', 'law and technology' and similar topics in
\par
law schools and other institutions for legal training.
\par
Efforts of this kind were not natural reasons accom-
\par
panied \textbf{by }attempts to define and delimit the subject of
\par
study.5
\par
In Europe there are now many signs that the question
\par
of the place and role of the law of information technology
\par
is beginning to be taken seriously. Thus, in April \textbf{1980}
\par
the Council of Europe adopted a Recommendation Con-
\par
cerning Teaching, Research and Training in the Field of
\par
Computers and Law (No. R \textbf{(80) 3). }The recommenda-
\par
tion was accompanied \textbf{by }an explanatory discussion of the
\par
subject, albeit of a rather superficial nature. In \textbf{1985 }the
\par
Council organised a conference for university depart-
\par
ments and other institutions responsible for teaching and
\par
research in the field. Progress and problems were dis-
\par
cussed as well as the need for continued efforts,
\par
including a revision of the \textbf{1980 }recommendation. The
\par
various papers presented at the conference describe
\par
courses and research programs in many countries.
\par
Special chairs in 'law and informatics' are beginning to
\par
be created. On the whole, one gets the impression that
\par
the subject is slowly beginning to gain academic
\par
recognition and that one is now entering into a stage of
\par
more widespread implementation in the form of univer-
\par
sity courses and doctoral programs.
\par
Nevertheless, there is uncertainty with regard to the
\par
concept of 'computer law' or 'data law' or 'information
\par
law'. Is there at all a need for such a construction? Does
\par
'computer law' and 'information law' mean the same
\par
thing? What is there to be said about the relationship
\par
between, on the one hand, 'information law' and, on the
\par
other hand, the various legal issues concerning the use of
\par
information technology \textbf{- }for information retrieval, for
\par
automated decision-making, for administrative pur-
\par
poses, and so forth?
\par
\textbf{The nature of the question}
\par
'Does information law exist?' This way of posing the
\par
question is questionable in itself. \textbf{A }metaphysical quest
\par
\textbf{51}for an answer is hardly meaningful. Neither is a purely
\par
practical and enumerative attempt to describe how the
\par
notion of information law is presently understood \textbf{by}
\par
various experts and institutions. The task consists in
\par
evaluating, theoretically as well as practically, the useful-
\par
ness of grouping legal norms together in a certain way, of
\par
emphasising certain interrelationships among prob-
\par
lems, of using certain methodological approaches, and so
\par
forth. Thus it may be assumed that for a discussion of the
\par
notion of information law, criteria of existence are of less
\par
concern than criteria of value.'
\par
To summarise, it is certainly not possible to 'prove' in a
\par
formal and conclusive sense the existence of information
\par
law. Instead, the task consists in defining a framework
\par
which permits rational reasoning and clarification of the
\par
moot points. Such an approach leads to a discussion of
\par
possible bases and alternative structures of information
\par
law. As pointed out above, it leads to an attempt to
\par
establish the practical and theoretical merits of develop-
\par
ing and using the concept. In particular, the various
\par
possible criteria for evaluation must be made explicit: for
\par
example, the value of information law when it comes to
\par
formulating, predicting, explaining, and solving prob-
\par
lems of a certain type.
\par
In this context, a theoretical note of a general nature
\par
may be added. The system of legal norms may be looked
\par
upon as a 'steering model' of reality. \textbf{By }a 'system of legal
\par
norms' we then mean not only the collection of norms in a
\par
simple sense but also the way in which norms and groups
\par
of norms are delimited, structured, and interrelated. In
\par
order to be efficient, the steering model must constantly
\par
be adapted to changes in the world that it is intended to
\par
depict and to regulate. Thus, if information is associated
\par
with new characteristics \textbf{- }with regard to its processing,
\par
communication, marketing, and so forth \textbf{- }it is well worth
\par
considering the question of the implications with regard
\par
to the (desired) structural characteristics of the system of
\par
legal norms.'
\par
As the preceding discussion indicates, there are two
\par
types of arguments associated with information law. The
\par
first approach may be called the conceptual one. It places
\par
emphasis on theoretical and methodological issues and
\par
on developments within legal science. The second one is
\par
the functional or practical approach. Briefly, it evaluates
\par
information law with regard to its usefulness and stability
\par
as a practically orientated field of law to be compared
\par
with existing fields such as building law and maritime
\par
law.
\par
\textbf{The conceptual approach}
\par
There are a number of interrelated ways of pursuing the
\par
conceptual approach. One of them places emphasis on
\par
the notion of information, that is, it directs attention to
\par
the growing number of situations where information as
\par
such is the subject or focal point of legal regulation. This
\par
development is furthered \textbf{by }a number of factors. For one
\par
thing, information is beginning to be regarded as an
\par
independent resource which should be evaluated and
\par
administered in a manner similar to other assets.! More-
\par
over, as is emphasised \textbf{by }Bing,' information technology
\par
has 'liberated' information from the medium. This
\par
means, among other things, that needs arise for legal
\par
\textbf{52}
\par
rules which deal directly with information \textbf{- }not, for
\par
example, with the medium on which it is stored.
\par
Such a regulation of information is not without prob-
\par
lems. They are located in almost every area of the law.
\par
Among other things, they concern the different pos-
\par
sibilities with regard to the placing of information into
\par
categories such as 'goods', 'tangible property', and
\par
'service' in the fields of sales law and tax law. Some-
\par
times, the introduction of information as a key concept
\par
seems to require a fundamental rethinking of a whole
\par
legal structure. An example may be found in the Swedish
\par
legislation on access to documents held \textbf{by }public
\par
authorities and the associated rules on secrecy. Informa-
\par
tion available from computer databases poses quite dif-
\par
ferent problems than do traditional collections of
\par
documents. Among other things, a need arises for
\par
advance regulation of the information handling of public
\par
authorities. It becomes necessary to decide what the right
\par
of access should mean in terms of, for example, retrieval
\par
facilities in a database management system.o
\par
Thus the regulation (control, protection, etc.) of infor-
\par
mation requires a deeper understanding of the concept
\par
as such and one of the goals of information law ought to
\par
be to accomplish the necessary clarifications, definitions,
\par
and common denominators. On the other hand, it may be
\par
put in question whether these needs are sufficient to
\par
bring about the development of a new and coherent field
\par
of law."
\par
In consequence, one is induced to shift attention to the
\par
broader framework within which information may be
\par
handled. In doing so, either (a) the basic features of
\par
modern information technology or \textbf{(b) }the concept of
\par
information systems may be placed in the foreground.
\par
(a) One of the key characteristics of information tech-
\par
nology has been mentioned already, viz. the new
\par
independence of information in relation to the storage
\par
medium. However, let us attempt to construct a more
\par
complete (but in no way exhaustive) list:
\par
\textbf{(1) }Automated selection, matching, transformation, etc.
\par
of data can be performed at a previously unknown scale
\par
and speed.
\par
(2) Human participation in the form of controls, \textbf{judg-}
\par
ments, evaluations, etc. disappears or is replaced \textbf{by}
\par
automated procedures.
\par
\textbf{(3) }Small'data atoms' may be handled in numerous ways
\par
and may be combined and re-combined to generate all
\par
kinds of messages and compilations. The 'information
\par
pattern' is changeable rather than stiff. Changes may
\par
leave no traces.
\par
(4) There is a tendency to freeze information into pre-
\par
defined schemes, procedures, formats, and so forth. The
\par
room for \emph{ad hoc }solutions shrinks. \textbf{A }contradiction exists
\par
between this tendency and the freedom to handle 'data
\par
atoms' mentioned above.
\par
\textbf{(5) }Computer programs and data management methods
\par
have a capacity to handle alternative logical paths and
\par
information structures that \textbf{by }far exceed the human
\par
ability to remember and to comprehend such matter.
\par
\textbf{(6) }Once information technology has been introduced
\par
into an activity it often turns out that the activity has
\par
become more or less completely dependent on its proper
\par
functioning. For example, it is not possible to use manual
\par
\textbf{MEDIA LAW \& PRACTICE, JULY 1986}methods to handle the same data volumes, achieve the
\par
same data processing speeds, etc. Thus, automation is
\par
often an irreversible process.
\par
\textbf{(7) }The use of and the reliance on traditional visual
\par
media (such as paper documents) for data storage and
\par
communication diminishes. Paperless information
\par
handling systems become a reality or, at least, a possibil-
\par
ity. The telephone conversation becomes a model for
\par
many kinds of information (data) interchange.
\par
\textbf{(8) }Short-lived (volatile) representations of information
\par
occur frequently, e.g. a collection of texts presented on a
\par
computer display terminal once and never more.
\par
\textbf{(9) }Compact storage of large data volumes. \textbf{A }small disc
\par
which may be held in the hand can contain billions of
\par
characters.
\par
\textbf{(10) }Better and better possibilities to work with (geo-
\par
graphically) distributed information resources (texts,
\par
formatted data, pictures, computer programs, etc.).
\par
Interconnected (permanently or momentarily) com-
\par
puters and computer work stations form continuous 'in-
\par
formation spaces' independent of distances and of the
\par
geographical and organisational dispersion of the
\par
participating subjects.
\par
\textbf{(11) }Media integration. Digital data (ones and zeroes, as
\par
is now common knowledge) are beginning to constitute
\par
the basic form for registration, processing, communica-
\par
tion, and storage of all kinds of information, including
\par
telephone conversations and pictures. Telecommunica-
\par
tion of all kinds, data processing, radio and television
\par
broadcasting, and publishing or printed works are on
\par
their way to becoming closely interrelated and inter-
\par
dependent activities within the framework of an
\par
integrated information technology.
\par
These characteristics, combined or in isolation, often put
\par
their mark on today's legal regulation and control of
\par
information and information handling. For example,
\par
they create a new vulnerability situation and necessitate a
\par
new kind of co-ordinated legal analysis and treatment of
\par
information security problems which occur in different
\par
contexts. At the evident risk of over-simplifying, it may be
\par
said that the purpose is to achieve consistent and uniform
\par
legal standards with regard to such features as data
\par
quality, record-keeping (logging) of events in computer
\par
networks, and authorisation and authentication schemes.
\par
Thus, an important concern of information law can
\par
be said to consist in studying the characteristics of
\par
modern information handling systems and their legal
\par
consequences.12 Sometimes the task may consist in
\par
transforming these characteristics into well-structured
\par
legal problems which also have practical solutions. For
\par
example, legal standards for automatic error detection
\par
and correction must be developed for many kinds of
\par
information handling, not least the ones involving auto-
\par
mated applications of legal norms (compare with
\par
proposition \textbf{(5) }above).
\par
\textbf{(b) A }systematic approach to information handling of
\par
this nature can be broadened and applied regardless of
\par
the particular technology used for information handling.
\par
Attempts are then made to construct problem categories
\par
which are well-suited to co-ordinated and broad
\par
legal analysis of information systems. For example,
\par
Steinmilller \emph{et al., }have directed their efforts to legal
\par
\textbf{MEDIA LAW \& PRACTICE, JULY 1986 }norms which regulate information processes, that is
\par
prohibit, permit or prescribe certain information
\par
processes." There are, of course, other possibilites of
\par
this kind. Consider the notion of legal liability for infor-
\par
mation handling. It may be divided into liability for
\par
communication services and liability for information
\par
services. Both of these may then be discussed in such
\par
terms as: (a) performance liability, \textbf{(b) }data protection
\par
liability, (c) recording and logging liability, \textbf{(d) }notifica-
\par
on liability, (e) time-keeping liability and similar
\par
categories." Briefly, there seem to be numerous
\par
possibilities to explore conceptual schemes of this kind
\par
orientated towards information systems and data pro-
\par
cessing and communication. They may be associated
\par
with different perspectives such as the distribution of
\par
power in society, ethics of information handling, or the
\par
notion of freedom of information. The question is; what
\par
are their merits as criteria for delimiting and structuring a
\par
proposed new branch of the law?
\par
\textbf{The functional approach}
\par
There now exists both general and special treatises of the
\par
law associated with computers and related information
\par
technologies. Typically, they have titles such as 'Com-
\par
puter Law, 'Computer Contracts', 'The Law of
\par
Electronic Funds Transfer Systems', and 'Telecom-
\par
munications Law'." It can hardly be denied that a
\par
number of interrelated problem areas of considerable
\par
practical interest have emerged and that there exist
\par
associated needs for co-ordinated treatment in hand-
\par
books and as well as more ambitious studies.
\par
Thus, regardless of one's opinion of information law
\par
as a theoretical construction, it is \textbf{by }now difficult to deny
\par
that there has been a development of a field of law related
\par
to information technology which is of considerable
\par
practical and economic importance. Moreover, this field
\par
requires of the people who work in it a significant degree
\par
of specialisation and expert knowledge about apparatus,
\par
activities, products, services, and so forth. It also requires
\par
specialist knowledge about the particular types of legal
\par
problems and complications which occur.
\par
From this point of view, then, it seems apparent that
\par
information law is of value and ought to be developed as a
\par
special field \textbf{- by }expert lawyers, \textbf{by }professional
\par
organisations, and in academia. But the question is: what
\par
is the structure and scope of information law if we con-
\par
sider it to be equivalent with a set of problem areas of the
\par
above mentioned kind? It is narrowed down to 'the law of
\par
the computer'? Or should one talk about 'the law of data
\par
processing' or, perhaps, 'the law of information tech-
\par
nologies'? Should the methodological concerns which we
\par
explored in the foregoing be completely set aside?
\par
The answer is, of course, no. To begin with, certain
\par
areas of practical interest are complicated from a
\par
methodological point of view and can hardly be
\par
developed without theoretical efforts associated with the
\par
concept of information and the characteristics of infor-
\par
mation systems. Consider as an example the regulation of
\par
the citizens' access to computerised databases and com-
\par
puter networks operated \textbf{by }state and municipal organs.
\par
Not least, practical experience has demonstrated that the
\par
\textbf{53}design of rules on secrecy, registration of recordings, and
\par
examination of requests for access require theoretical
\par
progress in the borderland between law and information
\par
system design.
\par
More important, there are whole problem areas where
\par
practical needs offer but insignificant guidance in disen-
\par
tangling the legal complications. There may exist little
\par
more than a fragmented view of the actual problems
\par
which cause the uncertainty. Automation and computer
\par
support of legal decision-making may be mentioned in
\par
this context. What procedural rules are and will be
\par
needed to handle this phenomenon? What kinds of legal
\par
safeguards are required to preserve the rule of law? How
\par
should competence be distributed with regard to rule-
\par
making and application of the law which involve com-
\par
plete or partial implementation through automated pro-
\par
cedures?
\par
\textbf{Conclusions}
\par
Certain conclusions may now be drawn. To begin with,
\par
the conceptual and the functional approaches to infor-
\par
mation law are mutually supporting. Perhaps, one may
\par
say that relevant problem areas of a practical nature, such
\par
as computer contracts, ought to be fitted into a broader
\par
framework where conceptual or methodological aspects
\par
are of importance. Conversely, the conceptual founda-
\par
tions of information law should be developed in close
\par
contact with practical realities such as automation of
\par
public administration and usage of international com-
\par
puter networks.
\par
What are the consequences for our present view of the
\par
notion of information law? Five main areas may be
\par
distinguished:
\par
\textbf{1. }Issues of vulnerability, security, and liability.
\par
\emph{Sample problems: }Analyses of legally acceptable error
\par
rates and data quality standards in different types of
\par
automated information handling (e.g. banking, tax
\par
administration, and retrieval of legal information associ-
\par
ated with decision-making). Principles of protection of
\par
personal data and their practical implementation. Formal
\par
and actual secrecy in large-scale government informa-
\par
tion systems. The possible introduction of the notion of
\par
'information damages' into the relevant fields of law (tort
\par
law, insurance law, etc.).
\par
Main methodological thrust: legal theory of risk
\par
management and security linked to information handling
\par
and information systems with particular emphasis on
\par
automation and formalisation.
\par
2. Marketing and procurement of information products
\par
and services.
\par
\emph{Sample problems: }The development of standard contracts
\par
for \textbf{EDP }systems (that is, packages of hardware, software,
\par
know-how, maintenance, instruction and other services).
\par
Protection on consumers who use various 'data utilities'
\par
via computer networks, e.g. advertisements, personal
\par
mail, home banking, and legal advice. Protection of pro-
\par
prietary rights in information. Database licenses.
\par
Main methodological thrust: an improved legal
\par
understanding of the nature of emerging new types of
\par
information 'goods and services'.
\par
54
\par
\textbf{3. }Telecommunications and the information market.
\par
\emph{Sample problems: }Legal dividing lines and regulation of
\par
data communications as opposed to data processing. On-
\par
line contracting procedures for services in international
\par
data networks. Regulation of on-line information
\par
services provided \textbf{by }public authorities. Regulation of
\par
international teleconferencing systems. Conflicts among
\par
different legal regimes associated with the ongoing
\par
integration of information technologies (telephone, telex,
\par
\textbf{EDP, }cable TV, radio, print media, etc.)
\par
Main methodological thrust: a contribution to the
\par
design of a legal framework for tomorrow's global infor-
\par
mation utility systems. Particular emphasis ought to be
\par
placed on databanks and data communications.
\par
4. The use of information technology in the administra-
\par
tion of justice and application of law.
\par
\emph{Sample problems: }Regulations of responsibilities, data
\par
flows, and databases in the area of law enforcement.
\par
Automated application of legal norms in public adminis-
\par
tration. Legal issues associated with mixed (overlapping
\par
and communicating) public and private information
\par
systems in the area of, for example, personal data. The
\par
dependency of various regulatory solutions on informa-
\par
tion systems, and in particular on automated application
\par
of the rules. Problems of upholding the rule of law in
\par
automated administrative information systems.
\par
Main methodological thrust: a legal theory of the
\par
consequences of large scale automation of public
\par
administration and of the judiciary.
\par
\textbf{5. }The role of the information order for freedom,
\par
participation, and control in society.
\par
\emph{Sample problems: }The regulation of access to data in
\par
government databases and data networks. Industrial
\par
democracy vis-a-vis automation of offices and industry.
\par
Division of power over information resources as a
\par
problem of constitutional law. Ethical guidelines for
\par
information systems used in social planning and political
\par
work.
\par
Main methodologal thrust: a normative theory of
\par
checks and balances with regard to the design and usage
\par
of information systems in society.
\par
Clearly, the five suggested main areas are associated as
\par
well as overlapping. The centre and the periphery of
\par
information law are indicated \textbf{by }the view of what
\par
constitutes 'the main methodological thrust' of each area,
\par
albeit in a rather sketchy fashion.
\par
We must also return to an issue which was alluded to in
\par
the introduction viz. the relationship between infor-
\par
mation law and the study of the legal usage of information
\par
technology.
\par
Different answers have been given and are still being
\par
given to this question. There seem to be three principal
\par
alternatives. (a) Information law has nothing to do with
\par
the usage aspects. \textbf{(b) }There are some links between
\par
information law and the legal usage of information tech-
\par
nology. Such a link may be, for example, the common
\par
interest in social effects of the technology. Therefore,
\par
information law, or at least certain elements of it, should
\par
be regarded as an appendix to'law and informatics' in the
\par
strict sense (Rechtsinformatik, Rettsinformatikk, etc.).
\par
(c) Usage and regulation aspects overlap in important
\par
\textbf{MEDIA LAW \& PRACTICE, JULY 1986}respects. For example, security and vulnerability prob-
\par
lems are to a large extent of this nature. Consider the
\par
problem of replacing messages forwarded on paper with
\par
messages forwarded electronically. Here we are faced
\par
both with various matters of 'security technology' and
\par
with matters of adaptation of the traditional legal regula-
\par
tion to a new situation. From the point of view of law and
\par
informatics these matters are not disparate but rather two
\par
sides of the same coin. In practice, questions such as the
\par
following one may be raised: how can we make it
\par
possible for public authorities to receive various
\par
messages from business enterprises etc. via data net-
\par
works and accept them as true and legally binding state-
\par
ments about various facts and conditions? 6
\par
\textbf{My }personal preference is the third alternative, that is
\par
the view that information law \textbf{- }as the concept is
\par
presented here \textbf{- }may best be developed within the
\par
framework of a broader discipline of law and informatics.
\par
This means, among other things, that problems will often
\par
be approached as if no distinction existed between
\par
information system design and regulation. For example,
\par
a discussion of valid law with regard to the notion of
\par
computer matching of personal data may fruitfully be
\par
accompanied \textbf{by }an analysis of possible ways of safe-
\par
guarding data quality based on theories and concepts
\par
developed \textbf{by }information system science. The main
\par
reason to hesitate seems to be the possibly too
\par
high demands for interdisciplinary training. Swedish
\par
experience indicates that it takes several years of engage-
\par
ment in law and informatics, including the information
\par
law aspects, before a young scholar begins to see the
\par
whole and had developed a natural sense for the
\par
paradigms of the field. But is this really so different from
\par
other areas of science?
\par
To conclude, a few words should be said about other
\par
possible approaches to information law than the one
\par
presented here. Perhaps, it is possible to talk about a
\par
more traditional view of information law. It is primarily
\par
concerned with human rights aspects of freedom of in-
\par
formation (freedom of speech etc.) and with media
\par
regulation. Terms such as media law and mass com-
\par
munications law are commonly used to denote this
\par
approach." Typically, studies of this kind concern such
\par
topics as the restraint of expression, the freedom to
\par
gather news, and the regulation of commercial speech.
\par
Thus, there exist at least two views of information law.
\par
One is associated primarily with data processing systems
\par
and decision making, with automation and data com-
\par
munications, with security and vulnerability aspects, and
\par
with the administration and marketing of information
\par
goods and services. The other view focuses on regulation
\par
of mass communications and places issues of freedom of
\par
expression, protection of privacy, copyright protection
\par
etc. in the foreground. Evidently, the two views share a
\par
number of interests, for example the interest in the
\par
regulation of technologies of communication and the
\par
protection of personal data.
\par
Clearly, the two views are not mutually exclusive.
\par
Information law is not a narrow concept, neither is it
\par
associated with one single idea about the delimiting and
\par
structuring of the field. But information law as it may be
\par
developed within the framework of law and informatics
\par
seems to be both particularly challenging from the
\par
theoretical point of view and practically important with a
\par
view to our ability to control the information tools we now
\par
have at our disposal.
\par
