\par
Contemporary commentators have focused on the premise of Chief Justice
\par
Marshall's opinion, that "the means of intelligence are equally accessible to both
\par
parties," and analyzed it in terms of economic theory. Information concerning
\par
future material changes in the supply and demand of goods will affect the alloca-
\par
tion of resources. The Treaty of Ghent, for example, could be expected to lead to
\par
an increase in the demand for American tobacco. This, in turn, initially would
\par
increase the price of tobacco, and ultimately because of higher price, likely
\par
would lead to increased production. More of the limited total of resources would
\par
be devoted to the production of tobacco.48 Thus, society has an interest in the
\par
accurate reception of market signals regarding future levels of supply and de-
\par
mand at the earliest possible moment. If signals are delayed or not accurately
\par
received, tobacco might be priced too low relative to other goods and services,
\par
leading to its underproduction and a lower total value received for the produc-
\par
tion of all goods and services.
\par
Consistent with the economic theory of allocational efficiency, \emph{Laidlaw }en-
\par
couraged traders to seek out new information about future supply and demand
\par
levels. \textbf{By }rewarding those who first learn of information concerning future sup-
\par
\textbf{ply }and demand levels, \emph{Laidlaw }created a financial incentive for traders to speed
\par
information concerning future supply and demand level to the marketplace. The
\par
opposite legal result, imposing liability on those who trade while in possession of
\par
material nonpublic information concerning market conditions, would discourage
\par
traders and analysts from expending resources on search costs, slow the acquisi-
\par
tion of material information concerning changed market conditions, and lead to
\par
an inferior allocation of resources.49
