Modem economic theory thus provides an intriguing rationalization of the
\par
result in \emph{Laidlaw. }But this rationalization does not support the most important
\par
application of the \emph{Laidlaw }doctrine in corporate and securities law, that is, \textbf{appli-}
\par
cation to corporate officers and directors who possess material "inside" informa-
\par
tion.50 In this instance, the corporate officers or directors possess information
\par
that is not "equally accessible to both parties." The information, typically about
\par
such matters as mineral discoveries, new products or product research, or pay-
\par
ment of dividends, concerns decisions that would be produced inside the corpo-
\par
ration in any event. The common law hardly needed to create an incentive for
\par
insiders to undergo search costs to inspire the insiders to learn such data.
\par
The "majority" common law rule concerning insider trading ignored eco-
\par
nomic effects. Instead, it held that a corporate executive or director could trade
\par
securities without informing the shareholder with whom he traded of material
\par
nonpublic information obtained while inside the corporation because the corpo-
\par
rate insider was not a "fiduciary" or "trustee" of the shareholder.51 The com-
\par
mon law focus on whether a fiduciary or similar relationship existed, as a
\par
practical matter, ended thoughtful judicial consideration of the purpose of the
\par
rule. The invocation of the term "fiduciary duty" was an outcome determinative
\par
label. Once invoked, few judges felt compelled to explain why it was desirable
\par
that corporate officers and directors not be held liable. Most courts simply indi-
\par
cated that the directors' or officers' conduct was in a zone that did not impose
\par
liability.
\par
