In the period from the rule's adoption until the early 1970s, many courts often analyzed the legislative
\par
history of \S{} \textbf{10(b) }in a different fashion than did the Court in \emph{Chiarella. }Rather than focusing narrowly on
\par
the legislative history of one statutory section, these courts interpreted each statutory section in light of
\par
the general purposes of the securities laws. \emph{See }SEC v. C.M. Joiner Corp., 320 U.S. 344, 350-51 (1943)
\par
("courts will construe the details of an act in conformity with its dominating general purpose, will read
\par
text in the light of context and will interpret the text so far as the meaning of the words fairly permits so as
\par
to carry out in particular cases the generally expressed legislative policy"); \emph{see also }Landis, \emph{Statutes and}
\par
\emph{the Sources of Law, }2 HARV. J. \textbf{ON }LEGIs. 7, 25-26 (1965) (scholarly articulation of this theory of interpre-
