II. THE INTEGRITY OF THE MARKET THEORY
\par
The primary policy reason for proscribing trading while in possession of mate-
\par
rial nonpublic information is to make investors confident that they can trade
\par
securities without being subject to informational disadvantages. 15 7 The goal is to
\par
guarantee the integrity of the market. This integrity of the market theory is the
\par
key analytical distinction between state and federal securities fraud laws. Where
\par
state law focuses on the direct relationship between specific buyers and sellers of
\par
securities, federal law also addresses the general impact an individual securities
\par
fraud may have on overall investor confidence.15 8
\par
The basis of the integrity of the market policy is both historical and theoreti-
\par
cal. In recommending passage of the \textbf{1933 }securities \textbf{bill, }Roosevelt emphasized
\par
that \textbf{by }putting "the burden of telling the whole truth on the seller" the proposed
\par
act "should give impetus to honest dealing in securities and thereby bring back
\par
public confidence." 1 59 At the time President Roosevelt wrote these words, the
