\textbf{E. }TENDER OFFERS
\par
Congress already has legislated one exception to the parity of information ap-
\par
proach. Under the Williams Act, any person who acquires five percent or less of
\par
the beneficial ownership of any equity security as part of a plan to ultimately
\par
control the firm need not publicly disclose the interest or the ultimate plan.245
\par
However, the Williams Act does require disclosure of a more than five percent
\par
stock interest or a tender offer for more than five percent of the beneficial owner-
\par
ship of any equity security.2 4 6 The Act's limited exemption to the parity of infor-
\par
mation approach reflects Congress' belief that takeover bids provide "a check on
\par
entrenched but inefficient management.' 2 4 7 \textbf{By }giving bidders an opportunity to
\par
purchase shares before complying with the Act's disclosure requirements, it can
\par
be argued that Congress slightly reduced the time period and cost of a takeover
\par
attempt, with a concomitant slight increase in allocative efficiency; for example,
\par
better managers obtain control of a firm and increase its rate of returns at a
\par
slightly lower initial cost.248
\par
However, Congress emphasized in enacting the Williams Act that its primary
\par
purpose was to provide "adequate disclosure to investors" in an area where the
\par
disclosure requirements of securities law in 1968 were deemed "inadequate." 2 49
