\par
The 1970 amendments to the Williams Act also gave the SEC specific author-
\par
ity to deal with the practice of "warehousing," by which a potential bidder gave
\par
advance notice to institutional investors of the impending tender offer. To the
\par
bidder, "warehousing" may "lock up" the target's shares in friendly hands; to
\par
the institution, warehousing offers the opportunity for a swift premium when the
\par
shares are resold in the tender offer.251 But, as the SEC pointedly noted, ware-
\par
housing could be criticized on parity of information grounds. The Commission
\par
believed that regulatory action was necessary to prevent powerful institutions
\par
from being treated more favorably than individual investors. 252 Acting pursuant
\par
to the 1970 amendments to the Williams Act, the SEC in 1980 adopted rule 14e-
\par
3 specifically to prevent warehousing.2 5 3
