\textbf{F. "INNOCENTS"}
\par
Besides purchases \textbf{by }large investors of less than five percent of a firm prelimi-
\par
nary to a tender offer, a second major area of concern involving the parity of
\par
information approach involves individuals who euphemistically might by termed
\par
"innocents." Innocents are outsiders who blamelessly obtain material informa-
\par
tion concerning a specific corporation. There are several different fact paradigms
\par
involving innocents trading on the basis of nonpublic information.
\par
One type of fact pattern concerns the eavesdropper; for example, the outsider
\par
who just happens to be riding in the same elevator with the corporate chief exec-
\par
utive officer and overhears him mention material information to an associate. In
\par
\emph{SEC v. Platt, 26{$^\circ$} }a football coach sitting in the stands at a track meet claimed to
\par
have overheard a business executive tell his wife of certain material nonpublic
\par
information. Under current law, the football coach may only be successfully
