\par
or short-sales are made during the negotiations, but before execution of an agree-
\par
ment, presumably there also would not be a duty creating liability but rather
\par
"arm's length negotiations.'266 Assuming that knowledge of the invention was
\par
material and concerned a fact, not an opinion,2 67 exoneration in either scenario
\par
involving an inventor is troublesome. In both fact patterns, the inventor is free
\par
to negotiate for the fair value of the new product in a competitive market. The
\par
inventor also may be rewarded with the equivalent to a monopolist's profits by
\par
securing a patent, and, in any event, has the opportunity to start his own firm
\par
and directly capture the potential profits associated with the new product. With
\par
all of these incentives, it cannot seriously be argued that the inventor also needs
\par
the incentive of stock trading profits to stimulate inventive activity. On the other
