mer public officials may make speeches that move the overall market or some
\par
segment by a material amount. May these individuals buy or short-sell stock
\par
immediately before a speech is delivered? These individuals can be distinguished
\par
from journalists engaged in scalping. Unlike scalpers who make recommenda-
\par
tions concerning a particular stock, these speeches typically refer to market-wide
\par
events such as future interest rate levels or national defense needs. In addition,
\par
while scalpers often base their recommendation on "facts" about a specific firm,
\par
these speakers typically stress that they are communicating opinions. Thus, one
\par
basis for not prosecuting this type of case is to characterize the celebrity speaker
\par
as rendering an "opinion," not stating "facts." This distinction is not persuasive.
\par
A scalper can also assert that a news column merely contains opinions. What
\par
makes the scalper's conduct actionable is the high probability that a column will
\par
cause a market reaction. With a very small number of speakers, an equal
\par
probability of a market reaction exists. The "fact" that the opinion was commu-
\par
nicated in a column or by a speaker who normally produces a market reaction
\par
should make the communication actionable.
\par
There is, however, a second basis for exonerating the celebrity that almost
\par
always would be persuasive. Even the most celebrated economists speaking
\par
about a probable future change in interest rate levels rarely, if ever, can be identi-
\par
fied as the "cause" of a material change in market levels. It is true that influen-
\par
tial speakers may be associated with a rapid one or even two percent movement
\par
in market indices. But that level of change normally is too small to be consid-
\par
ered material and is dwarfed by the considerably larger stock price movements
\par
found in SEC "scalping" litigation against investment advisers or journalists.26 8
\par
III. A \textbf{LEGISLATIVE }PROPOSAL
\par
The case law that has developed in the period after the Supreme Court's 1980
\par
\emph{Chiarella }decision is confused. It does not clearly warn securities traders what
\par
conduct violates current law. It cannot be justified by reference to traditional
\par
common law standards, by analogy to other sections of the securities laws, or by
\par
consideration of the economics of the securities markets.
\par
In contrast, amendment of the federal securities laws to prohibit any person
\par
from trading while in possession of material nonpublic information has clear
\par
advantages. Unlike current law, which has left the scope of rule \textbf{lob-5 }applica-
\par
bility highly unsettled, a parity of information approach would constitute a
\par
bright-line rule. It should deter violations and reduce litigation. In addition, a
\par
\textbf{268. }\emph{See supra }note 197 and accompanying text (approximately 20 percent price movement found in
\par
\emph{Zweig v. Hearst).}\textbf{1138 }[Vol. \textbf{73:1083}
\par
THE GEORGETOWN LAW \textbf{JOURNAL}
\par
parity of information approach should reassure investors that the securities mar-
\par
kets generally prohibit information advantages. This should increase levels of
\par
investments, reducing capital costs of issuing firms. Nor would a parity of infor-
\par
mation approach be a novel one. Many commentators believed that \emph{Texas Gulf}
\par
\emph{Sulphur }adopted this approach.
\par
Nonetheless, in order to harmonize the new standard with other parts of the
\par
securities law, there would have to be several exceptions to a prohibition on
\par
trading while in possession of material nonpublic information. First, the new
\par
statute would need to parallel the Williams Act and exempt potential takeover
\par
bidders who possess less than five percent of a target from the disclose or abstain
\par
rule.269 Second, the new statutory section should legitimate the "Chinese Wall"
\par
defense, by which securities broker-dealer firms that receive nonpublic informa-
\par
tion from firms for underwriting or other purposes will not be held liable if their
\par
brokers simultaneously make recommendations concerning the firms, as long as
\par
the broker-dealer firms have implemented procedures to prevent the brokers
\par
from knowing the information provided to their underwriting or other depart-
\par
ments. The "Chinese Wall" defense has been recognized in both rule 14e-3270
\par
and rule \textbf{lOb-5 }case law.271 Third, the new statutory provision, either in its text
\par
or in the accompanying legislative report, should affirm that securitites analysts
\par
may discover immaterial nonpublic information and may form opinions or make
\par
recommendations based on such data without risk of liability. Fourth, the new
\par
provision should permit stock or options exchange specialists, market-makers or
\par
floor traders to trade while in possession of material nonpublic information
\par
about trading activity on the floor of an exchange to the extent that such trading
\par
is permitted by section 11 of the Securities Exchange Act or Stock Exchange
\par
rules (which are adopted after SEC approval).
\par
Congress, no doubt, would also consider other exceptions to the general prohi-
\par
bition on trading while in possession of material nonpublic information. The key
\par
to this statutory proposal, however, is that all trading while in possession of
\par
nonpublic information would be illegal unless permitted by an exception. The
\par
new provision explicitly should state that there will be no burden on a plaintiff to
\par
prove a violation of a fiduciary or similar duty. The essence of the plaintiff's
\par
burden will be in proving that defendants traded while in possession of material
\par
nonpublic information. The legislative reports accompanying the new statutory
\par
section should emphasize that Congress finds trading while in possession of ma-
\par
terial nonpublic information illegal regardless of whether the information was
\par
"inside" or "outside" information. At the least, the legislative reports should
\par
make it clear that trading while in possession of material nonpublic information
\par
by securities analysts, journalists engaged in scalping, government officials or
\par
employees, insiders (directors, officers, employees, agents or outside consultants
\par
or a corporation), misappropriators of information, eavesdroppers, other outsid-
\par
ers and tippees of any of the above types of persons is illegal. The standard
\par
269. \emph{See supra }text accompanying notes 245 to \textbf{259 }(discussion of Williams Act).
\par
270. \textbf{17 }C.F.R. \S{} 240.14e-3(b)(2)(1984).
\par
271. \emph{See }Slade v. Shearson, Hammill \& Co., 517 F.2d 398, 401 (2d Cir. 1974) (Shearson failed to
\par
disclose material information either to general public or to customers or to its own retail sales force by
\par
virtue of "Chinese Wall"). \emph{See generally }Lipton \& Mazur, \emph{The Chinese Wall Solution to the Conflict}
\par
\emph{Problems of Securities Firms, }50 \textbf{N.Y.U. }L. REv. 459 (1975).\textbf{1985] 1139}
\par
REFORMING INSIDER TRADING LAWS
\par
should apply to trading in securities-including debt securities and stock op-
\par
tions-and should not be limited to trading in stock. While this is a comprehen-
\par
sive list, it should be emphasized that the new statutory provision should retain
\par
the materiality and scienter requirements of current rule \textbf{lOb-5 }law. A defendant
\par
will be liable (or guilty) only if the information is material under \emph{TSC Industries}
\par
\emph{v. Northway }\textbf{272 }and the misconduct was deliberate or at least "reckless. 273
\par
Within those limits, however, a prohibition on trading while in possession of
\par
material nonpublic information would be consistent with the "integrity of the
\par
market" theory generally employed in the federal securities laws.
\par
A general prohibition on trading while in possession of material nonpublic
\par
information is superior to other alternative approaches recommended for the re-
\par
vision of the federal securities fraud laws. One current proposal involves explic-
\par
itly naming the types of persons who can violate the law (i.e., insiders,
\par
fiduciaries, misappropriators of information, tippees and tippers). 2 74 The diffi-
\par
culty with this approach is the risk that it will prove underinclusive. New cate-
\par
gories of traders may emerge who are not covered by the list. Alternatively,
\par
courts might interpret terms such as "fiduciaries" to limit the scope of the pro-
\par
posed section. At the least, the proposal would require considerable litigation to
\par
establish its scope. In contrast, the general prohibition proposed in this article
\par
has the advantage of greater certainty of application.
\par
A second current alternative would declare illegal the "unfair use of material
\par
nonpublic information. 275 The legislative report accompanying this proposal
\par
apparently would cite several \emph{pre-Chiarella }cases and include language alluding
\par
to an existing body of law preceding \emph{Chiarella and Dirks }that suggests that
\par
nearly any trading on the basis of material nonpublic information is an "unfair
\par
use."276 Nonetheless, there is considerable risk in employing the term "unfair
\par
use." An unsympathetic judiciary might interpret the term in an unpredictable
\par
way. The initial judicial constructions of the Sherman Act typify the risk in
\par
employment of such a term.277 If the proponents of this alternative intend to
\par
prohibit "nearly any trading on the basis of material nonpublic information,"
\par
then their purpose can be achieved without the risk of employing a term such as
\par
"unfair use" by supporting the general prohibition proposed in this article.
\par
A final legislative concern should be noted. Other elements of the securitites
\par
fraud laws also could be addressed by legislation. Would it be wiser to offer a
\par
comprehensive package of proposals concerning the federal securities laws or
\par
merely proceed with a single isolated reform? While a reasonable case can be
\par
made for either approach, the recent success enjoyed by the SEC in securing
