THEORY
\par
\emph{Divorcing Commercial Speech from the First Amendment}
\par
Many first amendment commentators would counsel the courts to
\par
stay out of the commercial speech area. They include people who are
\par
profoundly committed to freedom of speech, like \textbf{C. }Edwin Baker,74
\par
Archibald \textbf{Cox, 75 }Thomas Emerson,76 Alexander Meilejohn,77 and
\par
emphasize that \textbf{\emph{labor }}speech is not \emph{commercial }speech. Even with this understanding, the com-
\par
plexity of the Court's categorization scheme has not been exhausted, for the strength of protection
\par
afforded to an employer's speech is a function of context, a context that the Court has recognized
\par
as quite different from that of an ordinary political speech. \emph{See }NLRB v. Gissel Packing Co., \textbf{395}
\par
U.S. 575, 617-18 (1969).
\par
\textbf{73 }In any event, it is clear that the Court has not allowed doctrine to interfere with its intu-
\par
itions. This is not necessarily bad. The wisdom of the common law might inhere in its willingness
\par
to reformulate principles when new fact situations suggest that its prior formulations have been
\par
misguided. Such flexibility might be particularly important in constitutional law when the conse-
\par
quences of decisions are nationwide with small possibilities for change. As Thomas Nagel has
\par
observed:
\par
[J]udgment-essentially the faculty Aristotle described as practical wisdom-reveals itself
\par
over time in individual decisions rather than in the enunciation of general principles .
\par
We should [not] abandon the search for more and better reasons and more critical insight in
\par
the domain of practical decisions. It is just that our capacity to resolve confficts in particular
\par
cases may extend beyond our capacity to enunciate general principles that explain those reso-
\par
lutions. Perhaps we are working with general principles unconsciously, and can discover
\par
them \textbf{by }codifying our decisions and particular intuitions.
