FREE \textbf{SPEECH }METHODOLOGY \textbf{AND }COMMERCIAL \textbf{SPEECH}
\par
In this section, I propose to pursue three objectives. First, I want
\par
to describe a first amendment methodology, make some observations
\par
about how I might defend it, and discuss some of its general strengths
\par
and weaknesses. Second, I want to defend the claim that marketplace
\par
theory should play an important but limited role in commercial speech
\par
analysis. Third, I aim to advance a number of normative claims about
\par
the relationship of freedom of speech and commercial speech, specifi-
\par
cally with respect to the regulation of true speech, of false speech, and
\par
of time, place, and manner regulations that discriminate against com-
\par
mercial speech. I reach the second objective in order to pursue the
\par
third. That is, I hope to explore the important but limited place of
\par
marketplace theory while discussing several different commercial
\par
speech problems. In pursuing the third objective, I seek to make my
\par
proposed first amendment methodology more attractive, but I also ar-
\par
gue that the methodology is appealing even if the conclusions \textbf{I }recom-
\par
mend in the commercial speech area are unpersuasive.
\par
