\par
\emph{Mathematical models of the judge. }As we have seen, the lens model
\par
was developed to study the effects of the decision maker's environment on
\par
his performance. Because of this environmental emphasis, the focal
\par
components of the model are \textasciitilde, the achievement index, and the factors
\par
such as R,, G, and C that determine achievement. Workers following the
\par
other stream of correlational research have had a different emphasis,
\par
They have been more interested in the judge's weighting process, his
\par
policy. In contrast with the Brunswikian tradition, they have placed less
\par
importance upon modeling the environment and, instead, have stressed
\par
the need to control the environmental situation. They tend to make the
\par
stimulus dimensions explicit and to vary their levels systematically, even
\par
though some degree of realism may be lost in the process.
\par
A wide variety of mathematical models have been developed to capture
\par
judgmental policies. The first and most prominent of these is the \emph{linear}
\par
\emph{model }(Hoffman, 1960) which is exemplified by Eq. (2) of the lens
\par
model. Alternatively, when the judge is classifying stimuli into one of
\par
two categories, the \emph{linear discriminant function, }rather than the multiple-
\par
regression equation, can be used to analyze the way that cues are
\par
weighted (Rodwan \& Hake, 1964). In either form, the model captures
\par
the notion that the judge's predictions are a linear combination of each
\par
of the available cues. When .iudgment is represented by the linear model,
\par
the b\textasciitilde.,, values of Eq. (2) and the utilization coefficients, r\textasciitilde,\textasciitilde, are used to
\par
represent the relative importance given each cue. Hoffman (1960) pro-
\par
posed an alternative index, "relative weight," designed for this purpose.
\par
Relative weights are computed as follows:
\par
\emph{' \textasciitilde 7\textasciitilde S2}
\par
Since the sum of relative weights is 1.0, Hoffman's index describes the
\par
relative contribution of each of the predictors as a proportion of the pre-
\par
dictable linear variance.
\par
However, as Darlington (1968) has recently emphasized, all indices
\par
of relative weight become suspect when the factors are intereorrelated.
\par
This problem has led many judgment researchers to work with sets of
\par
stimuli in which the cues are made orthogonal to one another. One device
\par
used to insure orthogonality has been to construct stimuli by producing
\par
factorial combinations of the cues. Of course, this practice is anathema
\par
to Brunswikians, since it is the antithesis of representative design.
\par
Brunswik observed (1955; pp. 204-205) that factorial designs may pro-
\par
duce certain combinations of values that are inherently incompatible or
\par
otherwise unrealistic and disruptive of the very process they were meant
\par
to disclose. This criticism cannot be taken lightly and some evidence does
\par
exist that judgment processes differ as a function of cue interrelationshipsBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN JUDGMENT 659
\par
(Slovic, 1966; Dudyeha \& Naylor, 1966a). But to the researcher who is
\par
primarily interested in relative weights, rather than achievement, orthog-
\par
onal designs often seem preferable to designs in which the cues are corre-
\par
lated in a representative fashion. Attempts are usually made, however,
\par
to mitigate potential disruptive effects by telling the judge that he will be
\par
dealing with a selected, rather than a random, sample of eases and by
\par
eliminating combinations of factors that are obviously unreal (see, for
\par
example, Hoffman, Slovic, \& Rorer, 1968).
\par
As we shall see, the linear model does a remarkably good job of pre-
\par
dicting human judgments. However, judges' verbal introspections indicate
\par
that they believe they use cues in a variety of nonlinear ways, and
\par
researchers have attempted to capture these with more complex equa-
\par
tions. One type of nonlinearity occurs when an individual cue relates to
\par
the judgments in a \emph{curvilinear }manner. For example, the following quota-
\par
tion from a leading authority on the stock market suggests a curvilinear
\par
relation between the volume of trading on a stock and its future
\par
prospects :
\par
If you are driving a ear y o u can get to y o u r d e s t i n a t i o n more quickly at. 50 m p h
\par
than at 10 mph. But you may wreck the car at 100 mph. In a similar way, increasing
\par
volume on an advance up to a point is bullish and decreasing volume on a rally
\par
is bearish, but in both eases only up to a point (Loeb, 1965, p. 287).
\par
Such functions can be modeled by including exponential terms (i.e., X J ,
\par
X\textasciitilde{} '\textasciitilde, etc.) as predictors in the judge's policy equation.
\par
A second type of nonlinearity occurs when cues are combined in a
\par
\emph{eonfigurag }manner. Configurality means that the iudge's interpretation or
\par
weighting of an item of information varies according to the nature of
\par
other available information. An example of configural reasoning involving
\par
price changes, volume of trading, and market cycle was given by the
\par
same stock market expert:
\par
Outstanding- strength o1" weakness (',an have precisely opposite meanings a\textasciitilde{} different
\par
times in the market cycle. For example, consistent strength and volume in a
\par
particular issue, occurring after a long general decline, will usually turn out to be
\par
an extremely bullish indication . . . . On the other hand, after an extensive
\par
advance which finally spreads to issues neglected all through the bull market,
\par
belated individual strength and activity not only are likely to be shortlived but
\par
may actually suggest the end of the general recovery . . . (Loeb, 1965, p. 65).
\par
When decision makers state that their judgments are associated with
\par
complex, sequential, and interrelated rules, it. is likely that they are
\par
referring to some sort of configural process. It is important, therefore,
\par
that techniques used to describe judgment be sensitive to eonfigurality.
\par
The C coefficient, described earlier,, is unsatisfactory from a descriptive
\par
standpoint because of its lack of specificity.6 6 0 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
\emph{The ANOVA Paradigm}
\par
One way of making the linear model sensitive to eonfigural effects has
\par
been to incorporate cross-product terms into the policy equation of the
\par
judge. Thus, if the meaning of factor X1 varies as a function of the level
\par
of factor X2, the term \emph{bl2X\textasciitilde X\textasciitilde{} }can be added to the equation. When
\par
models become this complex, however, the proliferation of highly-inter-
\par
correlated terms in the equations becomes so great that estimation of the
\par
weighting coefficients is unreliable unless vast numbers of cases are avail-
\par
able (Hoffman, 1968). For this reason, investigators such as ttoffman,
\par
Slovie, and Rorer (1968), Rorer, Hoffman, Dickman, and Slovie (1967),
\par
and Slovic (1969) have turned to the use of analysis of variance
\par
(ANOVA) to describe complex judgmental processes.
\par
The structural model underlying ANOVA is quite similar to that of
\par
multiple regression, both being alternative formulations of a general
\par
linear model (Cohen, 1968). Although the factors that describe the cases
\par
can be either continuous or categorical, each must be partitioned into a
\par
relatively few discrete levels. In addition, the factors are typically made
\par
orthogonal to one another, although this is not a necessity. In return for
\par
these restrictions, the ANOVA technique provides a statistically efficient
\par
mechanism for detecting eurvilinear and eonfigural use of information.
\par
When judgments are analyzed in terms of an ANOYA model, a signifi-
\par
cant main effect for cue X1 implies that the judges' responses varied
\par
systematically with X\textasciitilde{} as the levels of the other cues were held constant.
\par
If sufficient levels of the factor were included in the design, and if these
\par
levels can be assigned interval scale values, the main effect may be
\par
divided into effects due to linear, quadratic, cubic, etc., trends. Similarly,
\par
a significant interaction between cues X\textasciitilde{} and X2 implies that the judge
\par
was responding to particular patterns of those cues, i.e., the effect of
\par
variation of cue X\textasciitilde{} upon judgment differed as a function of the corre-
\par
sponding level taken by cue X\textasciitilde.
\par
The ANOVA model thus has potential for describing the linear, eurvi-
\par
linear, and eonfigural aspects of the judgmental process. Within the
\par
framework of the model, it is possible to calculate an index of the im-
\par
portance of individual or patterned use of a cue, relative to the impor-
\par
tance of other cues. One such index is based upon the degree to which the
\par
mean judgment shifts as the levels of a factor are varied (see Slovic,
\par
1969). Another is simply a transformation of these mean effects into an
\par
estimate of the proportion of the total variation in a person's judgments
\par
that can be predicted from knowledge of the particular levels of a given
\par
cue or pattern of cues (see Hays, 1963, p. \emph{324, }or Hoffman \emph{et al., }1968).
\par
The latter index includes linear and nonlinear variance and, therefore,RAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN JUDGMENT 661
\par
it is analogous to, but more general than, Hoffman's index of relative
\par
weight.
\par
In an ANOVA design, the usual way to produce orthogonal stimulus
\par
dimensions is to construct all possible combinations of the cue levels in
\par
a completely crossed factorial arrangement. Such an arrangement be-
\par
comes unmanageable when the number of cues is large, or when it. is
\par
desirable to include many levels of each cue. However, if one is willing
\par
to assume that some of the higher-order interactions are negligible, then
\par
it is possible to employ a fractional replication design and evaluate the
\par
importance of the main effects and lower-order interactions with a eon-
\par
siderably reduced number of stimuli (Anderson, 1964; Coehran \& Cox,
\par
1957; Shanteau, 1970; Slovie, 1969).
\par
\emph{Integration theory. }Integration theory can be considered one extension
\par
of the regression approaches described above. As such, it has formed the
\par
basis of an intensive program of research in the areas of clinical and
\par
psyehophysieal judgment, personality impression formation, and decision
\par
making. The essential ideas stem from the work of Norman Anderson,
\par
and are summarized in Anderson (1968b, 1969, 1970, in press).
\par
Integration theory is concerned simultaneously with two problems.
\par
The first is sealing the stimulus items and determining the weighting
\par
parameters. This component of the theory is called "valuation." The
\par
second concern, called "integration," tests theories about the specific
\par
composition rules used by the subjects. Particular attention has been
\par
given to tasks in which a simple algebraic model, involving adding,
\par
averaging, subtracting, or multiplying the informational inputs, serves
\par
as the substantive theory of judgment that is being tested.
\par
Technically, integration theory relies upon factorial designs, due
\par
to the fact that the substantive theories studied thus far have almost
\par
always been reducible to an ANOVA model. Therefore, ANOVA has
\par
been the principal analytical tool, serving to represent the theoretical
\par
postulates and providing a goodness-of-fit test of the models. An invalid
\par
response seale could cause a valid model to fail the test of fit. There-
\par
fore, an important feature of Anderson's approach is the use of mono-
\par
tone resealing procedures for the response variable. If the model is
\par
correct, it serves as a frame on which to rescale the response. Failure
\par
to, obtain an adequate fit after rescaling argues against the model, and
\par
success argues for it. Once the model and response scale are established,
\par
the subjective values of the information items can be derived. Anderson
\par
has used the term "functional measurement" to describe this interplay
\par
between theory and sealing.
\par
According to integration theory, each piece of information is repre-
\par
sented by two properties: A subjective seale value, s, and a weight, w.662 SLOVIC AND L I C H T E N S T E I N
\par
The weight represents the salience or importance of the information.
\par
The basic theoretical model is:
