LEAP\textasciitilde NING TO USE INFORMATION
\par
There has been considerable investigation into the learning of informa-
\par
tion processing and judgmental skills. Our focus here will be on studies in
\par
which the subject has to learn to use information to make a prediction or
\par
judgment. We shall neglect a rather sizable literature that explores
\par
whether subjects can learn to detect correlational or probabilistic con-
\par
tingencies among events but does not require that this knowledge be used
\par
in decisions.
\par
\emph{Regression Studies of Learning}
\par
l\textasciitilde esearchers working within the regression framework, and in particu-
\par
lar with the lens model, have been quite interested in learning. In fact,
\par
learning could be categorized, along with the problem of modeling, as a
\par
focal topic within the correlational paradigm. One way to partition the
\par
studies that have been conducted is according to whether subiects had
\par
available only one cue or multiple cues in the learning task.
\par
\emph{Single-cue learning. }Research with single cues has focused upon what
\par
Carroll (1963) has called "functional learning." Carroll attempted to dis-
\par
cover whether subjects could learn the functional relationships between a
\par
scaled cue or stimulus variable, X, and a scaled criterion, Y. The environ-
\par
ment was deterministic; i.e., there was a perfect 1-1 correspondence be-
\par
tween all values of X and Y. Across tasks, Carroll varied the mathe-
\par
matical complexity of the functions as determined by the number of
\par
parameters needed to describe them. He found that subjects' responses
\par
seemed to follow continuous subjective functions, even when the stimuli
\par
and criterion feedback were randomly ordered. Not surprisingly, simple
\par
functions were learned more accurately. Later work by BiSrkrnan (1965)
\par
and Naylor and Clark (1968) centered around the relative ease of learn-
\par
ing positive \emph{vs. }negative linear functions both in deterministic and prob-
\par
abilistic settings. The results of these studies indicated that positive
\par
relationships between cue and criterion are learned much more readily
\par
than negative ones.BAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES I N J U D G M E N T 707
\par
BjSrkman (1968) defined "correlat\textasciitilde ion learning" as functional learning
\par
where error (Re {$<$} 1.00) was involved. He observed that correlational
\par
learning requires a subiect to learn both the function relating stimulus
\par
and response, and the probability distributions around this function. In
\par
one experiment he found that the variance of a subject's responses about
\par
his own regression curve decreased as a consequence of training. A second
\par
experiment varied the extent to which there was a definite function to
\par
learn. Conditions with less pronounced cue-criterion trends resulted in
\par
larger ratios of subjects' response variance to criterion variance. From
\par
these results, BjSrkman concluded that correlational tasks are learned
\par
through a two-stage process involving both functional learning and
\par
probability learning, with the former occurring temporally prior to the
\par
latter.
\par
\emph{Conservatism in s\textasciitilde 'ngle-cue tearrzing. }Do subjects in single-cue learning
\par
experiments exhibit conservatism such as occurs in Bayesian studies of
\par
performance? The results of several studies have been brought to bear
\par
upon this question but. they must be viewed cautiously because of the
\par
problems in assessing conservatism in correlational tasks. For example,
\par
Naylor and Clark (1968) measured conservatism by dividing the stimulus
\par
distribution into thirds and computing the variance of each subject's re-
\par
sponses within each third of the range. These variances were compared
\par
with the variances of the criterion values computed over the same sub-
\par
ranges. The assumptions underlying this measure are (a) that the criterion
\par
distribution reflects the true probabilities of the various hypothesis states
\par
within each subrange of cue values and (b) that a subject's distribution
\par
of point responses represents an adequate picture of his perceived subjec-
\par
tive probabilities for each of these hypothesis states. Given these assump-
\par
tions, Naylor and Clark's subjects were conservative, inasmuch as the
\par
average dispersion of their judgments was found to exceed the dispersion
\par
of the criterion values in the upper and lower thirds of the cue distribution.
\par
Naylor and Clark also proposed that the standard error of estimate
\par
(W / 1 --R\textasciitilde{} 2) could be taken as an index of conservatism. Conservatism
\par
was presumed to increase this index, leading subiects to scatter their
\par
responses rather than consistently predicting the same criterion value,
\par
given a particular cue value. By this measure, Naylor and Clark's sub-
\par
jeers, as well as subjects in studies by BjSrkman (1965), Gray (1968),
\par
Gray, Barnes, and Wilkinson (1965), were not conservative. In these
\par
studies, R\textasciitilde{} typically exceeded Re and the discrepancy (R\textasciitilde --Re) was
\par
inversely related to R\textasciitilde. Thus the two measures proposed by Naylor and
\par
Clark lead to opposite conclusions about conservatism.
\par
Brehmer and Lindberg (1970) have criticized the above eonelusions,
\par
arguing that conservatism really means that subjects do not change their
\par
inferences as much as they should when the cue values change. They708 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
argued that the indices used by Naylor and Clark confound two sources
\par
of variance--the consistency of the subjects and their conservatism or
\par
extremeness. Therefore, Brehmer and Lindberg proposed that con-
\par
servatiSm be assessed by the relationship between b\textasciitilde{} and b\textasciitilde, the slopes of
\par
the regression lines relating the criterion values and judgments to the cue
\par
dimension. 5
\par
The experiments by Gray (1988), Gray et \emph{al. }(1965), and Naylor and
\par
Clark (1968) found that b, exceeded b\textasciitilde{} for low values of R\textasciitilde{} (and be) but
\par
not for high values. Since R\textasciitilde{} and be were confounded in these studies,
\par
Brehmer and Lindberg decided to vary Re, holding b\textasciitilde{} constant. Lower
\par
values of R\textasciitilde{} simply had greater deviation about a regression line that
\par
was the same for each condition. They found that subjects' judgments
\par
were consistently more extreme than the criterion values; i.e., b, was
\par
greater than be. This was especially true when R\textasciitilde{} was low. This result,
\par
along with similar findings by Gray, and Naylor and Clark, was inter-
\par
preted as indicating that subjects are not conservative in this type of
\par
task.
\par
\emph{Multiple-cue learning. }Multiple-cue research is assumed to have
\par
relevance for a varieW of "real-world" situations in which an individual
\par
must integrate information from several sources. Most of the studies rely
\par
upon the lens model for conceptual and analytical guidance. Independent
\par
variables are the number of cues, their r\textasciitilde,\textasciitilde{} values and the multiple
\par
correlation, R\textasciitilde, the forms of \textbraceleft he functional relationships between cues
\par
and criterion, and the intercorrelation between cues. Typically, the sub-
\par
ject is presented with a set of cues, he makes a quantitative judgment
\par
on the basis of these cues, and then receives the criterion value as
\par
feedback. Among the major results are (a) subjects can learn to use
\par
linear cues appropriately (Lee \& Tucker, 1962; Smedslund, 1955; Sum-
\par
mers, 1962, and Uhl, 1963) ; (b) learning of nonlinear functions occurs but
\par
is slower and less effective than learning of linear relationships (Brehmer,
\par
1969a; Hammond \& Summers, 1965; Sheets \& Miller, in press; Summers,
\par
1967; and Summers, Summers, \& Karkau, 1969) and is especially difficult
\par
if subjects are not properly forewarned that the relations may be non-
\par
linear (Earle, 1970; Hammond \& Summers, 1965; and Summers \& Ham-
\par
mond, 1966); (c) subjects can learn to detect changes in relative cue
\par
weights over time although they do so slowly (Peterson, Hammond, \&
\par
Summers, 1965a; Summers, 1969); (d) it is easier for subjects to learn
\par
which cue to use than to discover which functional rule relates a known
\par
valid cue to the criterion; learning both of these simultaneously is
