\par
especially difficult (Summers, 1967, 1969); (e) in a two-cue task, pairing
\par
a cue of low or medium validity with one of high validity is detrimental
\par
to performance (a distraction effect), while pairing a cue of low validity
\par
with another of medium or low validity is facilitative (Dudyeha \&
\par
Naylor, 1966b) ; and (f) subjects can learn to use valid cues even when
\par
tlhey are not perceived with perfect reliability (Brehmer, 1970).
\par
Cue intereorrelations (redundancies) have been varied in several learn-
\par
ing studies (Armelius \& Lenntoft, 1970; Miller \& Sarafino, in press;
\par
Naylor \& Sehenek, 1968). The major result is that subjects' beta weights
\par
match the cue-criterion correlations rather than the cue-criterion beta
\par
weights. Thus subjects fail to take appropriate account of redundancies.
\par
Conservatism has not been an explicit concern in many multiple-cue
\par
learning studies. However, Peterson, Hammond, and Summers (1965b)
\par
found that subjects failed to weight the most valid of three cues heavily
\par
enough and slightly overweighted the cue with lowest validity. Peterson
\par
\emph{et at. }noted the similarity of these results to those of Bayesian perform-
\par
anee tasks in which conservatism and data diagnosticity are positively
\par
related.
\par
A few studies have investigated the effects of different modes of feedback
\par
upon correlational learning. When subjects are given the correct answer
\par
on every trial ("outcome feedback"), learning is relatively slow. Lens
\par
model feedback, indicating how a subject's cue utilization coefficients
\par
compare with the ecological validities, is far more effective (Hammond
\par
\& Boyle, 1970; Newton, 1965; Todd \& Hammond, 1965). Magnusson and
\par
Nystedt (1969) found that providing subjects with the ecological
\par
validities was more effective than providing feedback about their cue-
\par
utilization coefficients.
\par
The lens model paradigm has also been extended to the problem of
\par
analyzing interpersonal learning and conflict between pairs of individuals
\par
(Hammond, 1965; Hammond \& Brehmer, in press; Hammond, Todd,
\par
Wilkins, \& Mitchell, 196.6; Hammond, Wilkins, \& Todd, 1966; Rappoport,
\par
1965). A typical experiment trains pairs of subjects to use one of two cues
\par
in either linear or nonlinear fashion. Each subieet learns to use a different
\par
cue, perhaps in different ways as well. After training, subjects are
\par
brought together to learn to predict a new criterion, using the same cues.
\par
Typically both cues must be used in this second task, and the subjects'
\par
training leads them initially to disagree with one another and with the
\par
outcome feedback they receive from the task. Lens model analysis of
\par
each subject's individual iudgments and the pair's ioint judgments pro-
\par
vides a great deal of information about the mechanisms whereby subjects
\par
learn from the task and from one another. A study by Brehmer (1969b)
\par
found that the differences between subjects' weighting policies are rapidly710 SLOVIC AND LICtITENSTEIN
\par
reduced in the joint task but this reduction is accompanied by increased
\par
inconsistency such that overt discrepancies are not very much diminished
\par
by the end of the conflict period. Because of such findings, Hammond and
\par
Boyle (1970) and HIammond and Brehmer (in press) argued that it is
\par
necessary to invent methods to display to the subjects the real sources
\par
of their disagreement and both of these studies describe a computer
\par
system which does this. Another interesting result from this area is that
\par
persons initially trained to have nonlinear policies are more likely to
\par
change than are persons with more linear policies (Brehmer, 1969b;
\par
Earle, 1970').
\par
\emph{Bayesian Studies of Learning}
\par
Bayesian researchers have been notably uninterested in the topic of
\par
learning. Many Bayesian studies have used situations like bookbags and
\par
poker chips, with which the experimenters assume the subject is already
\par
familiar. Others (e.g., Lichtenstein \& Feeney, 1968) have given initial
\par
training trials, with feedback; however, this training data is usually not
\par
analyzed. The epitome of indifference to learning is illustrated in a study
\par
by Peterson (1968). Though his subjects responded to more than 8000
\par
four-data sequences, Peterson did not mention whether feedback was
\par
given (presumably it was not), and all analyses were based on all the
\par
data, without any attention paid to changes over time. Peterson, like
\par
most other Bayesian researchers, was interested in how subjects \emph{behave--}
\par
not how they learn. Nonetheless, a few Bayesian studies do consider
\par
learning and thus merit attention.
\par
\emph{The effects of ]eedbaclc. }Edwards, Phillips, Hays, and Goodman (1968)
\par
reported a study which compared two groups of subjects who gave likeli-
\par
hood ratio responses; these responses were then cumulated, that is, con-
\par
verted into posterior odds estimates, by the experimenters, using Bayes'
\par
theorem. One group received feedback of these cumulated posterior odds
\par
after each estimate; the other group received no feedback. This type of
\par
feedback was found to degrade the cumulated posterior odds--making
\par
them more conservative--although changes over time were not reported.
\par
Martin and Gettys (1969) gave subjects either nominal feedback
\par
(e.g., I-I\textasciitilde{} generated the data) or probabilistic feedback (e.g., the posterior
\par
probabilities that each hypothesis generated the data are .769 for H\textasciitilde,
\par
.108 for HI2, and .123 for Ha) in a multinomial task. These authors found
\par
that probabilistic feedback produced more optimal responses than nomi-
\par
hal feedback, but they found no evidence that learning had occurred,
\par
either across four blocks of 50 trials, within the first 50 trials, or in a 20-
\par
trial replication. However, learning may have occurred in the five-pre-
\par
experimental practice trials.BAYESIAN AND REGRESSIO,N APPROACHES IN JUDGMENT 711
\par
\emph{The e\#ects oJ p,a.go\#. }Phillips and Edwards (1966) presented 20
\par
sequences of binomial data to three groups, each with different payoff
\par
schemes, and to one group which received no payoff. They found that the
\par
no-payoff group showed a small amount of learning (decreasing discrep-
\par
ancy from optimal responses); all payoff groups showed more learning,
\par
with no evidence of asymptote by the end of the experiment. Performance
\par
showed greater improvement in the latter half of these 20-item sequences
\par
than in the first half, suggesting that the subjects learned to use large
\par
probabilities as the evidence for one hypothesis mounted.
\par
\emph{Learning specific aspects of a probabilistic setting. }Schum (1966a)
\par
showed that subjects can learn and utilize existing conditional non-
\par
independence in multinomial data. The subjects were warned which data
\par
sources might be nonindependent, but they were not told the form of the
\par
relationship, nor which of the hypotheses mediated the relationship. They
\par
were taught to tabulate the frequencies with which the data occurred in
\par
such a way that the nonindependenee could be seen. Thus the outstanding
\par
achievement of the subjects was not that they could learn what inter-
\par
dependencies existed, but that they could utilize this information appro-
\par
priately in their posterior probability estimates; their responses more
\par
closely matched a model utilizing the nonindependence than a model in
\par
which independence was falsely assumed.
\par
Two additional learning studies were oriented to the misperception
\par
explanation of conservatism. In order to strengthen the point that sub-
\par
jects' conservatism resulted from their misunderstanding of the kinds of
\par
samples to expect from a given population, Peterson, DuCharme, and
\par
Edwards (1968) found that subjects were less conservative after they
\par
had been shown 100 illustrative samples of data from a binomial popula-
\par
tion. Wheeler and Beach (1968) not only showed their subjects 200
\par
binomial samples, but they asked the subjects to make a bet on which
\par
population generated the data, for each sample. Outcome feedback was
\par
given immediately after each bet. The effects of such training were seen
\par
in increased accuracy of subjects' estimated sampling distributions and
\par
decreased conservatism.
