AIDING THE DECISION MAKER
\par
Experimental work such as we have iust described documents man's
\par
difficulties in processing multidimensional and probabilistic information.
\par
Unfortunately, there is abundant evidence indicating that these diffi-
\par
culties persist when the subject leaves the artificial confines of the
\par
laboratory and resumes the task of using familiar sources of information
\par
to make decisions that are important to himself and to others. ExamplesBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION A P P R O A C H E S I N J U D G M E N T 717
\par
of overly simplistic use of information have been found in business
\par
decision making (Katona, 1951), military decision making (Wohlstetter,
\par
1962), governmental policy (Lindblom, 1964), design of scientific experi-
\par
ments (Tversky \& Kahneman, in press), and management of our natural
\par
resources (Kates, 1962; Russell, 1969; White, 1956). Agnew and Pyke
\par
(1969, p. 39) note that a decision maker left to his own devices
\par
\emph{" . . . }uses, out of desperation, or habit, or boredom, or exhaustion,
\par
whatever decision aids he can--anything that prepackages information."
\par
Among the vast assortment of decision aids described by Agnew and
\par
Pyke are rumors, cultural biases and self-evident truths, common sense,
\par
appeals to authority, and appeals to experts who, themselves, are all
\par
too fallible.
\par
The need for effective decision aids has not gone unnoticed, however.
\par
This is an age of technological advancement that creates more difficult
\par
and more important decision problems as it provides man with ever
\par
more power to manipulate his environment. It is not surprising, there-
\par
fore, that this same technological bent has been focused upon the
\par
decision-making process itself. One interesting new development is the
\par
cognograph, a computer system that provides lens model feedback to
\par
the judge (Hammond \& Boyle, 1970; Hammond, in press).
\par
The a.im of this section is to describe two other recent and distinctive
\par
contributions to the regression and Bayesian approaches to the improve-
\par
ment of decision making.
\par
\emph{Probabilistic Information Processing Systems}
\par
A great deal of Bayesian research has centered about the use of
\par
probability assessments in applied diagnostic systems. Edwards (196.2)
\par
introduced the notion of a probabilistic information processing (PIP)
\par
system because of his concern about the optimal use of information in
\par
military and business settings. He distinguished two types of proba-
\par
bilistic outputs for such a system. The first was diagnosis (what is
\par
the probability that this activity indicates an enemy attack?), and the
\par
second was parameter estimation (how rapidly is that convoy moving
\par
and in what direction?). Edwards proposed the following design for a
\par
PIP system: let men estimate \emph{P(D/H), }the probability that a particular
\par
datum would be observed given a specified hypothesis, and let machines
\par
integrate these \emph{P(D/tt) }estimates across data and across hypotheses
\par
by means of Bayes' theorem. After all the relevant data have been
\par
processed, the resulting output is a posterior probability, \emph{P(H/D), }for
\par
each hypothesis. Edwards originally designed the PIP system with the
\par
intention of using Bayes' theorem as a labor-saving device. However,
\par
when re\textasciitilde earch subsequently indicated that difficulties in aggregating718 SLOVIC AND LICI-ITENSTEIN
\par
data led subjects' unaided posterior probability estimates to be markedly
\par
conservative, the need to develop an antidote for conservatism added
\par
considerable impetus to the development, of such systems.
\par
Edwards and Phillips (1964) promoted the PIP system as a promising
\par
alternative to traditional command and control systems. They hypothe-
\par
sized that this system would produce faster and more accurate diagnoses
\par
for several reasons. First, Bayes' theorem is an optimal procedure for
\par
extracting all the certainty available in data. It automatically screens
\par
information for relevance, filters noise, and weights each item appro-
\par
priately. In addition, PIP systems promise to permit men and machines
\par
to complement one another, using the talents of each to best advantage.
\par
Sometimes \emph{P(D/H) }values are readily calculable from historical in-
\par
formation or from some explicit model of the data-generating device.
\par
ttowever, in many cases, no such probabilities exist. For example, what
\par
is the probability that Russia would have launched 25 reconnaissance
\par
satellites in the last 3 days if she planned a missle attack on the United
\par
States? As Edwards and Phillips observed, only human judgment can
\par
evaluate this type of information; PIP systems attempt to obtain and
\par
use such judgments systematically.
\par
The basic idea of a PIP system suggested a number of questions for
\par
research. Edwards and Phillips discussed the need to verify the basic
\par
premise that men can be taught to be good estimators for probabilities.
\par
One question concerned the most effective method for making such
\par
estimates. For example, should men estimate \emph{P(D/H) }values directly
\par
or estimate other quantities from which \emph{P(D/H) }can be inferred?
\par
Subsequent research indicated that it is easier to estimate likelihood
\par
ratios than to estimate \emph{P(D/H) }values themselves, because the latter
\par
are influenced by many irrelevant factors such as the level of detail
\par
with which the datum is specified (Edwards, Lindman, \& Phillips, 1965).
\par
Perhaps the most important research need was to evaluate the effec-
\par
tiveness of PIP systems in realistically complex environments. A number
\par
of such studies have been completed in recent years. One of the most
\par
extensive studies was by Edwards, Phillips, Hays, and Goodman (1968).
\par
They constructed an artificial future world (complete with \emph{"history"}
\par
up to 1975). The subjects related sequences of data to six hypotheses
\par
concerning war within the next 30 days, e.g., H\textasciitilde{} was "Russia and China
\par
are about to attack North America," while H6 was "Peace will continue
\par
to prevail." Four groups of subjects received intensive training in the
\par
characteristics of the "world," and then each group was trained in a
\par
particular response task. The \emph{PIP group's }responses were likelihood
\par
ratios. To each datum, five ratios were given, comparing in turn the
\par
likelihood of the datum given each of the war hypotheses against.BAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES I N J U D G M E N T 719
\par
the likelihood of the datum given the peace hypothesis. The responses
\par
were registered on log-odds scales. The \emph{POP group }responded with
\par
posterior odds, estimated upon receipt of each new datum. Again, each
\par
of the war hypotheses was compared in \textasciitilde urn to the peace hypothesis.
\par
The \emph{PEP group }responded by naming, for each war hypothesis, the fair
\par
price for an insurance policy that would pay 100 points in the event of
\par
t;hat particular war, and nothing in the event, of peace. The \emph{PUP group}
\par
gave probability estimates comparable to the PEP group's price esti-
\par
mates. Thus, of the four groups, only the PIP group, who gave likeli-
\par
hood ratios, were relieved of the task of cumulating evidence across
\par
the data in each sequence. In this group, the aggregation was done by
\par
machine to compute posterior odds.
\par
No optimal model could be devised for this simulation. The "true"
\par
hypothesis for any sequence of data was not known. Results showed
\par
that the PIP group arrived at larger final odds than other groups.
\par
When the PIP system showed final odds of 99:1, other groups showed
\par
final odds from 2:1 to 12:1. Because of this greater efficiency, the authors
\par
concluded that PIP was superior to the other systems.
\par
The problem of finding a \textasciitilde a.sk complex enough to warrant the com-
\par
parison of \emph{P(D/H) }responses (PIP) with \emph{P(H/D) }responses (POP),
\par
while still providing an optimal model against which to evaluate both
\par
methods, was tackled by Phillips (1966; also reported in Edwards, 1966).
\par
The data were thirty bigrams, combinations of two letters such as "th"
\par
or \emph{"ed." }The hypotheses were \%hat the bigrams were drawn either from
\par
the first two letters of words, or from the last two letters of words. The
\par
bigram \emph{"ed" }might thus be viewed as beginning a word (like editor)
\par
or ending a word (like looked). Phillips' subjects were six University
\par
newspaper editorial writers; data came from their own editorials.
\par
Frequency counts using the subjects' editorials (not shown to them)
\par
provided the veridical probabilities against which their responses could
\par
be compared. For the PIP task, all subjects estimated the likelihood
\par
ratio \emph{(P(D/Ha)/P(D/H\textasciitilde )) }for each bigram. Then, for the POP task,
\par
they were asked to imagine that the bigrams had been placed in two
\par
bookbags according to their frequencies of use, i.e., if \emph{"my" }had occurred
\par
20 times at the beginning of words and 40 times at the end of words, the
\par
20 "my" bigrams were placed in bag B, and 40 in bag E. One bag was
\par
chosen by the flip of a coin, and 10 bigrams were successively sampled.
\par
The subjects gave posterior odds estimates after each draw. Following
\par
this POP task, they repeated the PIP task. Results showed that in the
\par
PIP task subjects were modestly successful at estimating the relative
\par
frequencies of their own use of bigrams, but five of the six subjects
\par
were conservative. In the POP task they were much more conservative;720 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
they treated all but two of the bigrams as if they provided little or
\par
no diagnostic information.
\par
Kaplan and Newman (1966) reported the results of three experiments
\par
designed to evaluate PIP in a military setting. In two of these studies
\par
the PIP technique showed a definite superiority over a POP condition.
\par
This superiority was particularly evident early in the data sequence.
\par
The authors speculated that the relatively poor performance of the PIP
\par
system in the other experiment, may have been due to the faet that
\par
subjects there were provided with the output of Bayes' theorem after
\par
each datum was presented, making it difficult to evaluate each item of
\par
information on its own merit. Edwards, Phillips, Hays, and Goodman
\par
(1968) and Schum, Southard, and Wombolt (196.9) also found a detri-
\par
mental effect from showing \emph{P(D/H) }estimators the current state of
\par
the system.
\par
A maior effort to evaluate the idea of a PIP system within the context
\par
of threat evaluation has been carried out at Ohio State University under
\par
the direction of David Sehum and his colleagues. The results are de-
\par
scribed in Briggs and Schum (19\textasciitilde 5), Howell (19\textasciitilde 7), \$ehum (1967, 1968,
\par
1969), Sehum, Goldstein, and Southard (1966), and Sehum, Southard,
\par
and Wombolt (1969). The Ohio State research employed a situation in
\par
which the experimenters specified an arbitrarily constructed \emph{P(D/H)}
\par
matrix that governed the sampling of data. Subjects had to learn the
\par
import of various data items by accumulating relative frequencies linking
\par
data and hypotheses. The subjects were intensively trained in making
\par
probabilistie judgments and were quite familiar with the characteristics
\par
of the information with which they were dealing. Howell (1967) has
\par
summarized the first 6 years of research at Ohio State, concluding that
\par
automation of the aggregation process (i.e., PIP) can be expected to
\par
improve the quality of decisions in a wide variety of diagnostic condi-
\par
tions. He also observed that the superiority of a PIP system is most
\par
pronounced under degraded, stressful, or otherwise difficult task
\par
conditions.
\par
In contrived or simulated diagnostic situations, the PIP system seems
\par
to be a promising device for improving estimates of posterior probabili-
\par
ties. Future work will undoubtedly see the extension of the system \textasciitilde co
\par
nonmilitary settings along with greater attention to the practical details
\par
of implementing such systems in applied contexts. PIP systems have
\par
already been proposed for medicine (Lusted, 1968; Gustafson, 1969;
\par
Gustafson, Edwards, Phillips, \& Slack, 1969) and probation decision
\par
making (McEaehern \& Newman, 1969), and applications to weather
\par
forecasting, law, and business seem imminent.
\par
As promising as the PIP idea seems to be, however, a number ofBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES I N J U D G M E N T 721
\par
serious problems have yet to be faced. Some problems of particular
\par
importance noted by Schum, Southard, and Wombolt (1969) include
\par
hypothesis definition, source unreliability (uncertainty about which
\par
datum is being observed), nonstationarities of the environment, and
\par
nonindependence of data. Schum (1969) observed that. in systems where
\par
data accumulates rapidly, experts who assess \emph{P(D/H) }may have to
\par
aggregate their judgments over a series of data (i.e., judge \emph{P(D1, D2,}
\par
\emph{D s , . . . D,\textasciitilde/H\textasciitilde ). }When data items are nonindependent, these con-
\par
ditional probabilities can become quite complex. Three experiments re-
\par
ported by Schum, Southard, and Wombolt (1969) found that highly
\par
trained subjects could adequately aggregate diagnostic import across
\par
small samples of conditionally nonindependent data. However, the sub-
\par
jects were given access to the relative frequencies of the lower order
\par
conditional probabilities and, as the experimenters' noted, the results
\par
gave little indication about "how one uses his educated intuition in
\par
assigning import to evidence in the absence of a relative frequency
\par
'crutch' " (Schum \emph{et al., }1969, p. 44).
\par
Tversky and Kahneman have recently scrutinized subjects' "educated
\par
intuitions" about, probabilities in a series of experiments (Tversky \&
\par
Kahneman, 1970, in press; Kahneman \& Tversky, 1970) and their results
\par
imply that man may be as poor at estimating \emph{P(D/H) }values as he is at
\par
estimating posterior probabilities. One of their hypotheses is that the
\par
number of instances of an event that are readily retrieved from memory
\par
or the ease with which they come to mind are major clues used for
\par
estimating the probability of that event. The "availability" of instances
\par
is affected by many subtle factors such as recency, salience, and imagin-
\par
ability, all of which may be unrelated to the correct probability. Tversky
\par
and Kahneman point out the implications of these biases for PIP systems
\par
and they suggest, that informing the decision maker of his susceptibility
\par
to these influences might be valuable, although by doing so one risks
\par
imparting new biases.
\par
\emph{Bootstrapping}
\par
Can a system be designed to aid the decision maker that is based
\par
on his own judgments of complex stimuli? One possibility is based on
\par
the finding that regression models, such as the linear model, can do a
\par
remarkably good iob of simulating such judgments. An intriguing hy-
\par
pothesis about cooperative interaction between man and machine is that
\par
these simulated judgments may be better, in the sense of predicting
\par
some criterion or implementing the judge's personal values, than were
\par
the aetuaI judgmen\textbraceleft s themselves. Dawes (1970) has termed this phe-
\par
nomenon \emph{"bootstrapping."}722 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
The rationale behind the bootstrapping hypothesis is quite simple.
\par
Although the human judge possesses his full share of human learning
\par
and hypothesis generating skills, he lacks the reliability of a machine.
\par
As Goldberg (1970, p. 423) has noted:
\par
He 'has his days': Boredom, fatigue, illness, situational and interpersonal dis-
\par
tractions all plague him, with the result that his repeated judgments of the exact
\par
same stimulus configuration are not identical, tie is subject to all these human
\par
frailties which lower the reliability of his judgments below unity. And, if the iudge's
\par
reliability is less than unity, there must be error in his judgments--error which can
\par
serve no other purpose than to attenuate his accuracy. If we could . . . [eliminate]
\par
the random error in his judgments, we should thereby increase the validity of the
\par
resulting predictions.
\par
Of course, the bootstrapping procedure, by foregoing the usual process
\par
of criterion validation, is vulnerable to any misconceptions or biases
\par
that the judge may have. Implicit in the use of bootstrapping is the
\par
assumption that these biases will be less detrimental to performance
\par
than the inconsistency of unaided human judgment.
\par
Bootstrapping seems to have been explored independently by at least
\par
four groups of investigators. Yntema and Torgerson (1961) reported a
\par
study that suggested its feasibility. Their subjects were taught, via out-
\par
come feedback, to predict a criterion that was nonlinearly related to
\par
the cues. After 12 days of practice, their average correlation with the
\par
criterion was found to be .84. Then a linear regression model was com-
\par
puted for each subject on the basis of his responses during the final
\par
practice day. When these models were used to predict the criterion, the
\par
average correlation rose to .89. Thus consistent application of the linear
\par
model improved the predictions, even though the subjects had pre-
\par
sumably been taking account of nonlinearities in making their own
\par
judgments. Yntema and Torgerson saw in these results the possibility
\par
that "artificial, preeomputed judgments may in some eases be better
\par
than those the man could make himself if he dealt with each situation
\par
as it arose" (p. 24). More recently, Dudycha and Naylor (1966b) have
\par
reached a similar conclusion on the basis of their observation that sub-
\par
jeers in a multiple-cue learning task were employing the cues with
\par
appropriate relative weights but were being inaccurate due to the in-
\par
consistency of their judgments. They concluded that. although humans
\par
may be used to generate strategies, they should then be removed from
\par
the system and replaced by their strategies.
\par
Bowman (1963) outlined a bootstrapping approach within the context
\par
of managerial decision making that has stimulated considerable empirical
\par
research (see Gordon, 1966; Iturst \& 3/IeNamara, 1967; Jones, 1967; and
\par
Kunreuther, 1969). Kunreuther, for example, developed a linear modelBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION A P P R O A C H E S I N J U D G M E N T 723
\par
of production scheduling decisions in an electronics firm. Coefficients
\par
were estimated to represent the relative importance of sales and inven-
\par
tory variables across a set of decisions made by the production manager.
\par
Under certain conditions, substitution of the model for the manager was
\par
seen to produce decisions superior to those the manager made on his own.
\par
At about the time that Bowman was proposing his version of boot-
\par
strapping, Ward and Davis (1963) were advocating the same kind of
\par
approach to man-computer cooperation. Although they presented no
\par
data, Ward and Davis outlined several applications of the method in
\par
tasks such as estimating the time it would take to retrain 500 people,
\par
who now hold 500 existing jobs, to 500 new, possibly different jobs.
\par
Here a model would be built to capture an expert judge's policy on
\par
the basis of a relatively small number of eases. The model could then
\par
be substituted for the expert on the remaining cases. Ward and Davis
\par
also outlined an application of bootstrapping for the purpose of assigning
\par
personnel to jobs so as to maximize the payoff of the assignments.
\par
Goldberg (1970) evaluated the merits of bootstrapping in a task where
\par
219 clinical psychologists had to predict the psychiatric diagnoses of 861
\par
patients on the basis of their MMPI profiles. A linear model was built
\par
to capture the weighting policy of each clinician. When models of each
\par
clinician were constructed on the basis of all 861 eases, 86\% of these
\par
models were more accurate predictors of the actual criterion diagnoses
\par
than the clinicians from whom the models were derived. There was no
\par
instance of a man being greatly superior to his model. When a model
\par
was constructed on only one-seventh of the eases and used to predict
\par
the remaining eases, it was still superior to its human counterpart 79\%
\par
of the time. While the average incremental validity of model over man
\par
was not large, the consistent superiority of the model suggested con-
\par
siderable promise for the bootstrapping approach.
\par
Another demonstration of bootstrapping comes from a study of a
\par
graduate student admissions committee by Dawes (1971). Dawes
\par
built a regression equation to model the average judgment of the four-
\par
man committee. The predictors in the equation were overall under-
\par
graduate grade point average, quality of the undergraduate school, and
\par
a score from the Graduate Record Examination. To evaluate the validity
\par
of the model and the possibility of bootstrapping, Dawes used it to
\par
predict the average committee rating for his sample of 384 applicants.
\par
The R\textasciitilde{} value for predicting the new committee ratings was .78. Most.
\par
important, however, was the finding that it was possible to find a cutting
\par
point on the distribution of predicted scores such that no one who scored
\par
below it was invited by the admissions committee. Fifty-five percent of
\par
the applicants scored below this point., and thus could have been elim-\emph{724 }8LOVIC AND L I C H T E N S T E I N
\par
inated by a preliminary screening without doing any injustice to the
\par
committee's actual judgments. Furthermore, the weights used to predict
\par
the committee's behavior were better than the committee itself in pre-
\par
dicting later faculty ratings of the selected students. In an interesting
\par
cost-benefit analysis, Dawes estimated that the use of such a linear
\par
model to screen applicants to the nation's graduate schools could result
\par
in an annual savings of about \$18,000,000 worth of professional time.
\par
A recent paper by Dawes and Diller (1970) derives a formula based
\par
on observable properties of judgments that indicates when bootstrapping
\par
with linear models may be expected to occur. In addition, a procedure
\par
is presented for amalgamating the judge's predictions with the predic-
\par
tions of his linear model in such a way that the amalgamation is superior
\par
to both the judge and model.
\par
CONCLUDING REMARKS
\par
\emph{Some Generalizations about the State of our Knowledge}
\par
What have we learned about human judgment as a. result of the efforts
\par
detailed on the preceding pages? Several generalizations seem appro-
\par
priate. First, it is evident that the judge responds in a highly predictable
\par
way to the information available to him. Furthermore, much of what
\par
we call "intuition" can be explicated in a precise and quantitative man-
\par
ner. When this is done, the iudge's insight into his own cognitive processes
\par
is often found to be inaccurate.
\par
Second, we find that judges have a very difficult time weighting and
\par
combining information, be it probabilistic or deterministic in nature.
\par
To reduce cognitive strain, they resort to simplified decision strategies,
\par
many of which lead them to ignore or misuse relevant information.
\par
The order in which information is received affects its use and integra-
\par
tion but the specific form of sequential effects that occur is dependent
\par
upon particular circumstances of the decision task. Similarly, the manner
\par
in which information is displayed and the nature of the required response
\par
greatly influence the use of that information. In other words, the struc-
\par
ture of the judgment situation is an important determinant of informa-
\par
tion use.
\par
Finally, despite the great deal of research already completed, it is
\par
obvious that we know very little about many aspects of information use
\par
in iudgment. Few variables have been explored in much depth-even
\par
such fundamental ones such as the number of cues, cue redundancy, or
\par
the effects of various kinds of stress. And the enormous task of infe-
\par
grating this area with the mainstream of cognitive psychology-work
\par
on concept formation, problem solving, memory, learning, attention,
\par
etc.-remains to be undertaken.BAYESIAN AND REGRESSIO,N APPROACHES IN JUDGMENT 7 2 5
\par
\emph{Does the Paradigm Dictate the Research?}
\par
One of the objectives of this chapter was to determine whether the
\par
specific models and methods characteristic of each research paradigm
\par
tend to focus the researcher's attention on certain problem areas while
\par
causing him to neglect others. Such focusing has obviously occurred.
\par
For example, the Bayesians have been least concerned with developing
\par
descriptive models of subjective composition rules, concentrating instead
\par
on comparing subjects' performance with that of an optimal model,
\par
Bayes' theorem. They ha\textasciitilde e paid little attention to the learning of
\par
optimality, however. Researchers within the correlational paradigm have
\par
spent a great deal of effort using correlational methods to describe a
\par
judge's idiosyncratic weighting process and researchers using ANOVA
\par
designs to study impression formation have concentrated on distinguish-
\par
ing various additive and averaging models and delineating sequential
\par
effects at the group level.
\par
These different emphases are further illustrated by the fact that
\par
experimental manipulations which are similar from one paradigm to the
\par
other have been undertaken for quite different purposes. For example,
\par
the Bayesians have studied sequence length to gauge its effects on con-
\par
servatism; set size was studied in impression formation in order to
\par
distinguish additive and averaging models; and the number of cues was
\par
varied by correlational researchers to study the effects upon consistency
\par
and complexity of subieets' strategies.
\par
Can these differences in focus be attributed to the influence of the
\par
model used? Is a researcher inevitably steered in a particular direction
\par
by his chosen model? To some small extent, this is certainly true. A
\par
eorrelationalist would find it difficult to use, as his cues, intelligence
\par
reports such as: "General Tsing was seen last Monday lunching with
\par
Ambassador ttsieh." Instead, he will feel more comfortable with cues
\par
such as MMPI scores, or grade point averages. Similarly, a Bayesian
\par
is most comfortable working with a small number of hypotheses, while
\par
the eorrelationalist can work conveniently with many, provided they
\par
are unidimensionally scaled.
\par
In general, however, we believe that the maior differences in research
\par
emphasis cannot be traced to differences between the models. On the
\par
one hand, we see neglected problems for which a model is well suited.
\par
For example, the Bayesians neglect learning, although they have a
\par
numerical response, which can easily be compared to a numerical optimal
\par
response, for every trial; they need not partition the da\textasciitilde a into blocks
\par
(as eorrelationalists must in order to compute a beta weight). On the
\par
other hand, we see persistent, even stubborn, pursuit of topics for which
\par
the model is awkward. Correlationalists have devoted much effor\textasciitilde{} to726 SLOVIC AND LICttTENSTEIN
\par
..o
\par
the search for configural cue utilization, yet the linear model is extra-
\par
ordinarily powerful in suppressing such relationships, and interactions
\par
in ANOVA must be viewed with suspicion because the technique lacks
\par
invarianee properties under believable data transformations.
\par
Several research paradigms have been wound up around common
\par
points of interest and are chugging rapidly down diverging roads. Since
\par
any study almost always raises additional questions for investigation,
\par
there has been no dearth of interesting problems to fuel these research
\par
vehicles. Unfortunately, these vehicles lack side windows, and few in-
\par
vestigators are looking far enough to the left or right.. Of several hundred
\par
studies, only a handful indicate any awareness of the existence of com-
\par
parable research under another paradigm. The fact remains, however,
\par
that all these investigators are interested in the same general problem,
\par
that of understanding how humans integrate fallible information to
\par
produce a judgment, or decision. Singleminded dedication to one paradigm
\par
is disturbing since it suggests a lack of concern with basic, substantive
\par
issues. As Platt (1964) put the matter:
\par
To paraphrase an old saying, Beware of the man of one method or one instrument,
\par
either experimental or theoretical, lie tends to become method oriented rather
\par
than problem oriented. The method oriented man is shackled; the problem
\par
oriented man is at least reaching freely toward what is most important. (Platt,
\par
1964, p. 351).
\par
\emph{Applied vs. Theoretical Objectiv,es}
\par
If one is problem oriented, the distinction between applied and theo-
\par
retical objectives becomes relevant to the selection of an experimental
\par
design. Methods suited for one of these general aims may be inadequate
\par
for the other. Thus correlational research, with its emphasis on predict-
\par
ability, may be quite useful for certain applied work but less adequate
\par
for theoretical endeavors which require sharper hypotheses and tests of
\par
fit. Similarly, in an applied man-machine system, the response scale is
\par
often valid by definition. But when the research emphasis shifts from
\par
practical problems and normative models to theoretical issues, the choice
\par
of a response scale may become critical. A clear awareness of these
\par
distinctions would seem to be important for both theoretical and applied
\par
research.
\par
\emph{Towards an Integration of Research Efforts}
\par
We suggest that researchers should employ a multiparadigm approach,
\par
searching for the most appropriate tasks and models to attack the sub-
\par
stantive problems of interest to them. We will try to show, for several
\par
such problems, how such a broader perspective might be advantageous.BAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES I N J U D G M E N T 727
\par
\emph{Sequential e]]ects. }The potential value of a diverse approach is
\par
illustrated by research on primacy and recency effects. Hendriek and
\par
Costantini (1970a) found no effect of varying information inconsistency
\par
in an impression formation task, where adjectives served as cues. They
\par
argued that attention decrement, not inconsistency, accounts for the
\par
primacy commonly found in studies of impression formation. Yet a
\par
number of Bayesian studies did obtain primacy when early and late
\par
data were, to varying degrees, inconsistent (Dale, 1968; Peterson \&
\par
DuCharme, 1967; Roby, 1967) and recency when later data were not
\par
inconsistent (Pitz \& Reinhold, 1968; Shanteau, 1970). The discrepancy
\par
between the Hendriek and Costantini data and the Bayesian results
\par
would seem to be worth investigating.
\par
The study by Shanteau (1970) provides a nice example of the utility
\par
of applying methods and tasks from different paradigms when studying
\par
sequential effects. Shanteau used an ANOVA design with a Bayesian
\par
task. He presented subjects with sequences of data constructed according
\par
to factorial combinations of binary events. Their task was to estimate
\par
\emph{P(H/D) }after each datum was received. Sequential effects appear as
\par
main effects of serial position in such a design. Two experiments clearly
\par
showed that recency was operating throughout all stages of sequences
\par
as long as 15 items.
\par
The inertia effect in Bayesian research can be viewed as a type of
\par
primacy effect. The fact that. inertia is so dependent upon the degree
\par
to which subjects' previous judgments are displayed or otherwise high-
\par
lighted suggests that this same factor might also be operating in studies
\par
of primacy and recency. It is perhaps relevant that most of the studies
\par
of impression formation that employed intermittent responding and
\par
obtained recency effects used spoken ratings, slash marks, or required
\par
subjects to fill out detailed questionnaires. None of these formats gives
\par
particular salience to previous judgments. The one study that exhibited
\par
primacy effects (Anderson, 1959) employed a more standard written
\par
response, although subjects did have to turn the page for each new
\par
item of information. In addition, in each of the Bayesian studies that
\par
obtained primacy (Dale, 1968; Peterson \& DuCharme, 1967; Roby,
\par
1967), subjects were asked to make estimates on some mechanical device
\par
that preserved the previous response and required it to be physically
\par
manipulated when changes were made. While all this is obviously \emph{post}
\par
\emph{hoc }analysis, future research on primacy and recency effects should t.ake
\par
a close look at the manner in which the previous response in the sequence
\par
is made and stored.
\par
\emph{Novelty. }How do subjects handle data that are rare or novel? Wyer
\par
(1910) examined the effects of novelty, defined in terms of the un-7\textasciitilde 8 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
conditional probability of an adjective, upon impression formation. Novel
\par
adieetives were seen to carry greater weight, making impressions more
\par
polarized. This increased weight attached to rare data appears to be
\par
in contradiction with findings from Bayesian research on rare events
\par
(Beach, 1968; Vlek, 19'65; Vlek \& van der Heijden, 1967). These studies
\par
have presented evidence that rare events are viewed as uninformative,
\par
i.e., they are not given enough weight in the decision process.
\par
\emph{Learning. }Hammond and his colleagues (e.g., Hammond \& Brehmer,
\par
in press; Todd \& Hammond, 1965) have long contended that specific
\par
feedback derived from the lens model (i.e., feedback about the weight
\par
the subject gives to each cue, and the weight the environment gives to
\par
each cue) is more effective than nonspecific feedback (i.e., the "correct"
\par
answer). How does this result relate to the finding by Martin and
\par
Gettys (1969.) that probabilistic feedback is better than nominal feed-
\par
back, or to the evidence from Wheeler and Beach (1968) and Peterson,
\par
DuCharme, and Edwards (1968.) that subjects give more optimal
\par
\emph{P(H/D) }estimates after they have received training in \emph{P(D/H)? }If
\par
specific feedback enhances performance, why then did Pitz and Downing
\par
(1967) find that subject.s' binary predictions were not improved by de-
\par
tailed information about the sampling distributions?
\par
\emph{Diagnosticity and conservatism. }Both the Bayesian and the correla-
\par
tional models have well-defined measures of the diagnosticity of data-
\par
\emph{P(D/H) }and b\textasciitilde,\textasciitilde, respectively. A unified approach to this topic seems
\par
natural. In the past, eorreIationalists have done little exploration in
\par
performance (nonlearning) studies where diagnosticity was varied.
\par
Bayesian research on this topic has been extensive and has demonstrated
\par
the difficulties subjects have in integrating probabilistic information.
\par
The different data and response formats possible within the correlational
\par
paradigm would seem to provide an excellent opportunity to investigate
\par
the generality of these difficulties. Subjects could be taught how to use
\par
various linear and curvilinear cues individually and could then be
\par
asked to integrate them into their judgments.
\par
From a theoretical standpoint the question of conservatism rests upon
\par
the validity of the response scale. We have already seen that different
\par
response modes produce different degrees of eonservatism, but these
\par
responses have been selected on the basis of practical, not theoretical
\par
considerations. Response rescaling and model testing, as practiced within
\par
integration \textasciitilde heory and conjoint measurement, could be quite useful in
\par
evaluating conservatism.
\par
\emph{Sell-insight. }The analysis of the iudge's insight into his own sub-
\par
ieetive composition function is a fascinating and important area that.
\par
has been investigated only within the correlational paradigm. And inBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION A P P R O A C H E S IN J U D G M E N T 729
\par
these studies, only one rather arbitrary technique has been used to
\par
scale the judge's perceptions of a cue's importance-the "distribute-100-
\par
points" technique. Besides the obvious step of employing new scaling
\par
techniques in correlational studies, it would seem valuable to test the
\par
accuracy of self-insight in a variety of tasks, using Bayesian and
\par
ANOVA models as well.
\par
\emph{Decision aids. }The idea of bootstrapping, which was developed in
\par
the context of regression equations, has some interesting relationships
\par
with the PIP system designed by Bayesians to improve human judg-
\par
merit. Both view human judgments as essential and attempt to blend
\par
them optimally (see Pankoff \& Roberts, 1968, for an elaboration of this
\par
point). However, advocates of the PIP system assume that the aggrega-
\par
tion process is faulty and attempt to circumvent this by having subjects
\par
estimate \emph{P(D/H) }values and letting a machine combine them. They
\par
decompose the judgment task into a number of presumably simpler
\par
estimation tasks. Bootstrapping assumes that subjects can aggregate
\par
information appropriately except for unreliability that must be filtered
\par
out. The success of bootstrapping and PIP systems suggests that the
\par
assumptions of both have some validity; judges are biased \emph{and }un-
\par
reliable in their weighting of information. Perhaps a system can be
\par
designed to minimize both these sources of error or, at least, to differ-
\par
entiate situations where PIP might excel bootstrapping or vice versa.
\par
\emph{New Directions}
\par
Although a diverse program, integrating Bayesian and regression
\par
paradigms, might be quite valuable in increasing our understanding of
\par
information processing, some new approaches might be even more
\par
illuminating. Our own inclination is to move towards more molecular
\par
analyses of the heuristic strategies that subjects employ when they
\par
integrate information, along the lines we discussed earlier in this article.
\par
At this level, the evidence to date seems to indicate that subjects are
\par
processing information in ways fundamentally different from Bayesian
\par
and regression models. Thus, if we are to pursue this line of research we
\par
wilI have to develop new models and different methods of experimenta-
\par
tion. Use of eye movements, introspection, and tasks where subjects
\par
]nave to search for or request information as they need it may help
\par
provide insights into molecular strategies. The search for new models
\par
should bring judgment research into closer contact with the more tra-
\par
ditional areas of psychology. For example, Bruner, Goodman, and
\par
Austin's (1956) work on the role of cognitive strain in determining
\par
concept formation strategies, and Simon's analyses of cognitive limita-
\par
tions and their influence on problem solving (Simon, 1956, 1969), seem730 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
likely to have much relevance for our understanding of composition
\par
strategies in judgment.
