\textbf{Comparison of Bayesian and Regression Approaches to}
\par
\textbf{the Study of Information Processing i,n Judgment 1}
\par
\textasciitilde AUL SLOVIC AND SARAH LICHTENSTEIN
\par
\emph{Oregon Research Institute}
\par
In recent years there have been several hundred studies within the
\par
rather narrowly-defined topic of information utilization in judgment and
\par
decision making. Much of this work has been accomplished within two
\par
basic schools of research, which we have labeled the "regression" and
\par
the "Bayesian" approaches. Each has its characteristic tasks and character-
\par
istic information that must be processed to accomplish these tasks. For
\par
the most part, researchers have tended to work strictly within a single
\par
approach and there has been minimal communication between the re-
\par
sultant subgroups of workers. Our objective here is to present a review
\par
and comparative analysis of these two approaches. Within each, we
\par
examine (a) the models that have been developed for describing and
\par
prescribing the use of information in decision making; (b) the major
\par
experimental paradigms, including the types of judgment, prediction, and
\par
decision tasks and the kinds of information that have been available to
\par
the decision maker in these tasks; (c) the key independent variables tha\textasciitilde{}
\par
have been manipulated in experimental studies; and (d) the major
\par
empirical results and conclusions. In comparing these approaches, we seek
\par
the answers to two basic questions. First, do the specific models and
\par
methods characteristic of different paradigms direct the researcher's atten-
\par
tion to certain problems and cause him to neglect others that may be
\par
equally important? Second, can a researcher studying a particular sub-
\par
stantive problem increase his understanding by employing diverse models
\par
and diverse experimental methods?
