\par
precedes and determines these activities. The distinction between judg-
\par
ments and decisions is a tenuous one and will not be maintained here;
\par
we shall use these terms interchangeably.
\par
Regardless of terminology, one thing is certain: Judgment is a
\par
fundamental cognitive activity that vitally affects the well-being, or
\par
more accurately, the survival of us all. Decisions are frighteningly more
\par
important and more difficult than ever before. Ancient man's most
\par
important decisions concerned his personal survival, and only a limited
\par
number of alternatives were available to him. Technological innovation
\par
has placed modern man in a situation where his decisions now control
\par
the fate of large population masses, sometimes the whole earth, and
\par
his sights are now set on outer space. Even the personal decisions that
\par
direct an individual's daily life have become increasingly complicated.
\par
To cite but one example, consider the bewildering array of career
\par
choices that confront today's bright youth. And consider the extreme
\par
commitment of time, effort, and money necessary to obtain the specialized
\par
training most of these opportunities require. The result is a high-gain-
\par
high-risk decision, much more difficult and complex than that faced by
\par
his parents.
\par
The difficulties attendant to decision making are usually blamed on
\par
the inadequacy of the available information, and, therefore, our techno-
\par
logical expertise has been mobilized to remedy this problem. Devices
\par
proliferate to supply the professional decision maker with an abundance
\par
of data. The physician, for example, has access to sophisticated electronic
\par
sensors, and satellites now relay masses of strategic data for military in-
\par
telligence. However, the problem of interpreting and integrating this
\par
information has received surprisingly little attention. At this point, the
\par
decision maker is typically left to his own devices. More likely than not
\par
he will proceed, as will the physician, businessman, or military com-
\par
mander, in much the same manner that has been relied upon since
\par
antiquity, and when you ask him what distinguishes a good judge from
\par
a poor one he will reply,
\par
"It's a kind of locked in concentration, an intuition, a feel, nothing
\par
that can be schooled" (Smith, 1968, p. 20).
\par
However, things have begun to change. Specialists from many dis-
\par
ciplines have started to focus on the integration process itself. Their
\par
efforts center around two broad questions, "What. is the decision maker
\par
doing with the information available to him?" and "What should he
\par
be doing with it?" The first is a psychological problem, that of under-
\par
standing how man uses information. The second problem is a more
\par
practical one and involves the attempt, to make decision making more
\par
effective and efficient.BAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES I N J U D G M E N T 653
\par
\emph{The Focus of This Paper}
\par
Information processing occurs at several levels. Our concern here is
\par
not with events at the neural level but rather with cognitive operations
\par
performed on such grosser phenomena as symbols, signs, and facts. We
\par
shall focus on the processes and strategies that. humans employ in order
\par
to integrate these discrete items of information into a decision. These
\par
are the deliberative processes commonly referred to by the terms \emph{"in-}
\par
tegrating," \emph{"weighing," "balancing," }"trading off," or "combining"
\par
information.
\par
Prior to 1960 there was relatively little research on information
\par
processing at this molar, judgmental level. However, the intellectual
\par
groundwork had already been laid by studies such as Brunswik's
\par
pioneering investigations of inference in uncertain environments
\par
(Brunswik, 1956; Hammond, 1955); the work on "probability learning"
\par
(Estes, 1959); investigations of gambling decisions, utility, and sub-
\par
jective probability (Edwards, 1954); Miller's (1956) elaboration of the
\par
limitations on the number of conceptual items that can be processed
\par
at one time; the concept formation studies by Bruner, Goodnow, and
\par
Austin (1956); and the research on computer simulation of thought by
\par
Newell, Shaw, and Simon (1958).
\par
Since 1960, this early work has been supplemented by several hundred
\par
st.udies within the rather narrowly-defined topic of information utiliza-
\par
tion in judgment and decision making. The yearly volume of studies
\par
has been increasing exponentially, stimulated by a growing awareness
\par
of the significance of the problems and the aid of the ubiquitous com-
\par
puter. The importance of the latter cannot be overestimated. When
\par
Smedslund (1955) published the first multiple-cue probability learning
\par
study, he bemoaned having to compute 3200 correlations on a desk
\par
calculator. It's not surprising that the next study of its kind was not,
\par
forthcoming for 5 more years.
\par
Much of the recent work has been accomplished within two basic
\par
schools of research. We have chosen to call these the "regression" and
\par
the "Bayesian" approaches. Each has its characteristic tasks and char-
\par
acteristic information that must be processed to accomplish these tasks.
\par
For the most part., researchers have tended to work strictly within a
\par
single approach and there has been minimal communication between the
\par
resultant subgroups of workers.
\par
Our objective in this chapter is to present a review and comparative
\par
analysis of these two approaches. Within each, we shall examine (a)
\par
the models that have been developed for describing and prescribing
\par
the use of information in decision making; (b) the major experimental65Ax SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
paradigms, including the types of judgment, prediction, and decision
\par
tasks and the kinds of information that have been available to the
\par
decision maker in these tasks; (c) the key independent, variables that
\par
have been manipulated in experimental studies; and (d) the major
\par
empirical results and conclusions.
\par
In comparing these approaches, we seek the answers to two basic
\par
questions. First, do the specific models and methods characteristic of
\par
different, paradigms direct the researcher's attention to certain problems
\par
and cause him to neglect others that may be equally important? Second,
\par
can a researcher studying a particular substantive problem, such as
\par
the use people make of inconsistent or conflicting information, increase
\par
his understanding by employing diverse models and experimental
\par
methods ?
\par
\emph{Areas of Omission}
\par
Space limitations have forced us to omit several other paradigms that
\par
have made significant contributions to the study of human judgment.
\par
One of these is the process-tracing approach described by Hayes (1968)
\par
and exemplified by the work of Kleinmuntz (1968) and Clarkson (1962).
\par
Researchers following this approach attempt to build sequential branch-
\par
ing models of the decision maker based upon his verbalizations as he
\par
works through actual decision problems. Yet another important approach
\par
to the study of judgment uses multidimensional scaling procedures to
\par
infer the cognitive structure of the judge. For coverage of this work the
\par
reader is referred to the chapter by Wiggins (in press). There have
\par
been several attempts to apply information theory to the study of human
\par
.iudgment. One of the most notable recent efforts along these lines is
\par
the work of Bieri, Atkins, Briar, Leaman, Miller, and Tripodi (1966)
\par
which examines the transmission of information in social judgment along
\par
the lines of Miller's (1956) well-known paradigm. Another area we shall
\par
omit. here is that of probability learning. A recent and thorough review
\par
emphasizing the information processing implications of this work is
\par
presented by Jones (in press). Finally, we have not attempted to review
\par
signal detection theory, an approach that. has produced a great deal of
\par
research concerning the integration of sensory information into decisions.
\par
The reader is referred to books by Swets (1964) and Green and Swets
\par
(1.966) for detailed coverage of this area.
\par
THE REGRESSION APPROACH
\par
The regression approach is so named because of its characteristic
\par
use of multiple regression, and its close relative, analysis of variance
\par
(ANOVA), to study the use of information by a judge. Within thisBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN JUDGMENT 655
\par
broad approach we shall distinguish two different paradigms which we
\par
have labeled the "correlational" paradigm and the "ANOVA" paradigm.
\par
\emph{The Correlational Paradigm}
\par
In the correlational paradigm, a judge's integration of information
\par
is described by means of correlational statistics. The basic approach
\par
requires the judge to make quantitative evaluations of a number of
\par
stimuli, each of which is defined by one or more quantified cue dimen-
\par
sions or characteristics. For example, a judge might be asked to predict
\par
the grade point average for each of a group of college students on the
\par
basis of high school grades and aptitude test scores. Sarbin and Bailey
\par
(1966) elaborate the aims of the correlational analyst in a study such
\par
as this:
\par
He correlates the information cues available to the inferring person with the
\par
judgments or inferences... What usually results is that the coefficients of
\par
correlation between cues and judgment make public the subtle, and often un-
\par
reportable, inferential activities of the inferring person. That is, the coefficients
\par
reveal the relative degrees that the judgments depend on the various sources of
\par
information available to the judge (pp. 193-194).
\par
The development of the correlational paradigm has followed two
\par
streams. One stream has focused on the judge; its goal is to describe
\par
the judge's idiosyncratic method of combining and weighting information
\par
by developing mathematical equations representative of his combina-
\par
torial processes (ttoffman, 1960).
\par
The other stream developed out of the work of Egon Brunswik, whose
\par
philosophy of "probabilistic functionalism" led him to study the organ-
\par
ism's successes and failures in an uncertain world. Brunswik's main
\par
emphasis was not on the organism itself, but on the adaptive inter-
\par
relationship between the organism and its environment. Thus, in addi-
\par
tion to studying the degree to which a .iudge used cues, he analyzed the
\par
manner in which the judge learned the characteristics of his environ-
\par
ment. He developed the "lens model" to represent the probabilistie
\par
interrelations between organismic and environmental components of
\par
the judgment situation (Brunswik, 1952, 1956).
\par
Because of his concern about the environmental determinants of
\par
judgment, Brunswik was also the foremost advocate of what he called
\par
"representative design." The essence of this principle is that. the organism
\par
should be studied in realistic settings, in experiments that are repre-
\par
sentative of its usual ecology. The lens model provides a means for
\par
appropriately specifying the structure of the situational variables in
\par
such an experiment.
\par
\emph{The lens model. }The lens model has proved to be an extremely656 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
