\par
equation. It. is not assumed that the objective values of the stimulus
\par
dimensions are linearly related to the responses. If, for example, the
\par
judgment task involved the rating of occupations, and salary was one
\par
of the factors, the actual salary levels would enter into the linear modelBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN J U D G M E N T 663
\par
of Eq. (2) as predictors of the judgments. But it is quite likely that
\par
the judge perceives salary in a nonlinear fashion. The subjective dif-
\par
ference between \$20,000 and 825,000 is probably less than the difference
\par
between \$5,000 and \$10,000. Integration theory attempts to discover
\par
these subjective scale values and to determine rules of composition
\par
based on \emph{these }values, whereas the regression and ANOVA approaches
\par
described earlier attempt to discover the combination rule based on
\par
the objective dimensions.
\par
Equation (5) implies that the row by column interaction should be
\par
zero in principle and nonsignificant in practice. Therefore, ANOVA
\par
serves to test the model's goodness of fit. If the model passes this test,
\par
it may be used to estimate the subjective values sR\textasciitilde{} and sej. When Eq.
\par
(5) is averaged over columns, the mean response for row i is:
