\par
the column means constitute an interval scale of the column stimuli.
\par
The above results hold for an additive model. Similar analyses for
\par
subtraetive and multiplicative models may be found in Shanteau and
\par
Anderson (1969) and Anderson and Shanteau (1970). Anderson (in
\par
press) summarized these results and presented averaging versions
\par
of these models in which the weights are constrained to sum to unity.
\par
In some of these models, wl\textasciitilde{} can be scaled but not s\textasciitilde,, or vice versa. In
\par
others, both parameters can be scaled.
\par
Anderson (1969) noted that caution is required in interpreting the
\par
meaning of significant interactions when these occur. Interactions may
\par
result from cognitive eonfigurality that is theoretically meaningful or
\par
from defects in the response scale, such as floor and ceiling effects. In
\par
some cases, a monotonic rescaling of the iudgments can be used to
\par
