interpretation.
\par
Anderson and colleagues have also applied integration theory to
\par
study t.he effects of serial position in information integration. In these
\par
studies the stimuli were presented successively and the serial positions
\par
corresponded to factors in the design. The weights indicated by the
\par
main effects thus produced a serial-position curve that was used to
\par
assess whether information was given more salience earlier (primacy)
\par
or later (recency) in the sequenee (Anderson, 1965b, 1968a; Shanteau,
\par
1970). Anderson (1965b) noted that when information is presented
\par
serially, the weighted average model can be reformulated in a manner
\par
that makes it particularly valuable for studying the step-by-step buildup
\par
of a judgment, in response to each item. This form, called the proportional
\par
change model, asserts that the iudgment, R\textasciitilde, produced after receipt
\par
of the kth item of information, is given by:
