\emph{Conjoint measurement. }The theory of conjoint measurement (Luee
\par
\& Tukey, 1964; Tversky, 1967b; Krantz \& Tversky, 1971) is analogous
\par
in many respects to integration theory. Both are concerned with dis-
\par
covering the psychological laws that. govern the composition of several
\par
attributes (for our purposes, several items of information), tIowever,
\par
integration theory deals with \emph{quantitative }laws, whereas conjoint
\par
measurement is concerned with \emph{qualitative }laws.
\par
When the stimuli and the judgments can be measured independently
\par
on interval scales, the rule of combination can be tested directly. How-
\par
ever, when the assumption of interval-scale measurement is of dubious
\par
validity, conjoint measurement is valuable, since it. uses only \emph{ordinal}
\par
properties of the judgments to test the proposed combination rule.
\par
Krantz and Tversky delineated the testable ordinal relationships
\par
among judgments that can be used to diagnose which, if any, of several
\par
polynomial composition rules is appropria\textbraceleft e. For example, one class of
\par
testable properties, called independence conditions, serves a valuable
\par
diagnostic function. The essence of ordinal independence is that the
\par
ordering of the judgments within any row of the factorial matrix is
\par
constant across rows.
\par
Krantz and Tversky argued that the ordinal approach to the studyBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES I N J U D G M E N T 665
\par
of composition rules, exemplified by conjoint measurement, should be
\par
regarded as complementary to numerical approaches such as the lens
\par
model or integration theory. From a practical standpoint, they noted
\par
that direct tests of ordinal properties are generally more powerful in
\par
their ability to discriminate alternative composition rules than are
\par
overall tests of goodness of fit. From a theoretical standpoint, they con-
\par
tended that qualitative properties may sometimes lead to a more funda-
\par
mental understanding of psychological principles than do numerical
\par
analyses.
\par
Conjoint measurement has been applied in only a few judgment
\par
studies thus far (see, for example, Coombs \& Huang, 1970; Tversky,
\par
1967a, 1967c; Tversky \& Krantz, 1969; Wallsten, 1970) but the explica-
\par
tion of its analysis techniques by Krantz and Tversky should stimulate
\par
greater use of this approach in the future.
\par
THE BAYESIAN APPROACH
\par
Brunswik proposed the use of correlations to assess relationships in
\par
a probabilistic environment, lie could have used conditional probabilities
\par
instead; had he done so, he undoubtedly would have built his lens model
\par
around Bayes' theorem, an elementary fact about probabilRies described
\par
in 1763 by the Reverend Thomas Bayes. The modern impetus for what
\par
we are calling the Bayesian paradigm can be traced to the work of
\par
yon Neumann and Morgenstern (1947) who revived interest in maximi-
\par
zation of expected utility as a core principle of rational decision making,
\par
and to L. J. Savage, whose book \emph{The Foundations of ,Statistics }fused
\par
the concepts of utility and personal probability into an axiomatized
\par
theory of decision in the face of uncertainty, \emph{\% }highly idealized theory
\par
of the behavior of a 'rational' person with respect to decisions" (Savage,
\par
1954, p. 7). The Bayesian approach was communicated to businessmen
\par
by 8chlaifer (1959) and to medical diagnosticians by Ledley and Lusted
\par
(1959). Psychologists were introduced to Bayesian notions by Ward
\par
Edwards (Edwards, 1962; Edwards, Lindman, \& Savage, 1963) and
\par
much of the empirical work to be discussed was stimulated directly by
\par
the ideas in the latter two papers.
\par
The Bayesian approach is thoroughly embedded within the framework
\par
of decision theory. Its basic tenets are that opinions should be expressed
\par
in terms of subjective or personal probabilities, and that the optima1
\par
revision of such opinions, in the light of relevant new information, should
\par
be accomplished via Bayes' theorem. Edwards (1966) noted that, al-
\par
though revision of opinion can be studied as a separate phenomenon,
\par
it is most interesting and important when it leads to decision making
\par
and action. Because of this concern with decision making, the output of666 SLOVIC AND L I C H T E N S T E I N
\par
a Bayesian analysis is not a single prediction but rather a distribution
\par
of probabilities over a set of hypothesized states of the world. These
\par
probabilities can then be used, in combination with information about
\par
payoffs associated with various decision possibilities and states of the
\par
world, to implement any of a number of decision rules, including the
\par
maximization of expected value or expected utility.
\par
Bayes' theorem is thus a normative model. It specifies certain in-
\par
ternally consistent relationships among probabilistic opinions and serves
\par
to prescribe, in this sense, how men should think. Much of the psycho-
\par
logical research has used Bayes' theorem as a standard against which
\par
to compare actual behavior and to search for systematic deviations
\par
from optimality.
\par
