\emph{Information-seelcing ezperiments. }The decision maker often has the
\par
option of deferring his decision while he gathers relevant information,
\par
usually at some additional cost. The information presumably will increase
\par
his certainty about the true state of the world and increase his chances
\par
of making a good decision. In seeking additional information, the decision
\par
maker must weight the relative advantage of the new information againstBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN JUDGMENT 671
\par
its cost. When the probabilistic characteristics of the task are well-
\par
defined, an optimal strategy can be specified that will, in conjunction
\par
with the reward for making a correct decision, the penalty for being
\par
wrong, and the cost of the information, determine a stopping point that is
\par
optimal in the sense of maximizing expected value (Edwards, 1965;
\par
Raiffa \& Schlaifer, 1961; Wald, 1947). This task is a natural extension
\par
of the probabilistic inference tasks described above, since it requires the
\par
decision maker to link payoff considerations with his inferences in order
\par
to arrive at a decision. A large number of studies have investigated man's
\par
ability to make such decisions. For example, one commonly studied task
\par
uses the bookbag and the poker chip problem described earlier. As before,
\par
a sequence of chips is sampled, with replacement, from a bag with pro-
\par
portion of red chips equal to P\textasciitilde{} or P2. Instead of estimating the posterior
\par
probabilities for each bag, the subject must decide from which bag the
\par
sample is coming. In some cases, he must decide, prior to seeing the first
\par
chip, how many chips he wishes to see (fixed stopping). In other cases, he
\par
samples one chip at a time and can stop at any point and announce his
\par
decision (optional stopping). Space limitations prohibit further analysis
\par
of this body of research here. The interested reader is referred to papers
\par
by Fried and Peterson (1969), Pitz (1989b, 1969e), Rapoport and Burk-
\par
heimer (1970), and Wallsten (1968) for examples of this research.
