\par
titular, each paradigm is based on a theoretical model of the composition
\par
rules whereby informational input is integrated into a judgmental re-
\par
sponse. The schematic diagram in Fig. 2 depicts some general relation-
\par
ships between input, process, and response, and will serve as the organiza-
\par
tional framework for the present discussion.
\par
\emph{Input}
\par
The information that serves as input to the decision maker varies
\par
somewhat both within and between each approach. The correlational
\par
paradigm typically involves dimensions of quantitative information.
\par
Data presented within the ANOVA and Bayesian studies, by contrast,
\par
are often discrete, categorical, or qualitative items, although these ap-
\par
proaches can also process dimensional data. As to the relationships among
\par
data sources, ANOVA techniques require factorially combined informa-
\par
tion elements, and workers within the descriptive stream of correlational
\par
research also prefer orthogonaI structure. Lens model research often uses
\par
items that are correlated in a fashion representative of the real world.
\par
Bayes' theorem, in its analytically convenient forms (Eqs. (8) and (9)),
\par
requires conditionally independent, data. A rough translation of this
\par
requirement in correlational terms would be that the residual correlations
\par
between cues, with the criterion dimension partialled out, must be zero.
\par
\emph{The Subject's Response}
\par
The response required of the subject also differs across paradigms. The
\par
correlational and ANOVA approaches usually deal with a single-valued
\par
prediction (point estimate) about some conceptually continuous hypoth-
\par
esis. Bayesians would say that there is a probability distribution over this
\par
continuous distribution and that the subject's single judgment must
\par
represent the output of some covert decision process in which some im-
\par
plicit decision rule is applied (e.g., the response may be interpreted as
\par
specifying the criterion value having the largest, probability of occur-
\par
rence), based on some implicit payoff matrix. Some Bayesian studies also
\par
require subjects to make predictions, usually concerning discrete hypoth-
\par
eses. When they do, the payoffs accompanying correct and incorrect pre-
\par
dictions are usually made explicit to the decision maker. Most often, how-
\par
ever, subjects in Bayesian studies estimate the posterior or conditional
\par
probability distribution (or some function thereof, such as odds) across
\par
various hypotheses. Although Bayes' theorem can, in principle, be ap-
\par
plied to continuous hypotheses, the emphasis on probability distributions
\par
rather than point estimates makes such a task experimentally awkward
\par
(see, however, Peterson \& Phillips, 1966).BAYESIAN AND REGRESSION A P P R O A C H E S IN J U D G M E N T 673
\par
\emph{Subjective Composition Rules}
\par
From the standpoint of psychological theory, the rule by which the
\par
subject combines or integrates the input information is the most im-
\par
portant element being investigated. Each paradigm is based upon a
\par
theoretical model of this composition process. Furthermore, these various
\par
algebraic models are closely related. The simple additive model plays a
\par
key role in both correlational and ANOVA studies and more complex
\par
polynomial rules can also be studied within these paradigms. Bayes'
\par
theorem is multipticative in form, much like the multiplieative models
\par
within integration theory and conjoint measurement. In addition, the
\par
proportional change model of integration theory (Eq. (7)) and the
\par
Bayesian model both conceptualize the step-by-step buildup of judgment
\par
in terms of a weighted combination of the present datum and prior
\par
opinion.
\par
Each of these composition models contains descriptive parameters
\par
which assess the subjective impact or importance of information. Corre-
\par
lational studies rely on global measures which reflect importance across
\par
an entire dimension or data source, such as correlations (r\textasciitilde,\textasciitilde ), regression
\par
weights \emph{(b\textasciitilde,\textasciitilde ) }or relative weights \emph{(RW\textasciitilde,\textasciitilde ). }In contrast, Bayesians are
\par
usually interested in the subjective impact of each individual datum, as
\par
measured by its subjective likelihood ratio. In the Bayesian approach,
\par
the source or dimensionality of the datum is irrelevant. Integration
\par
theory makes a conceptual distinction between two kinds of impact which
\par
are confounded in the above measures: wk reflects the importance of one
\par
dimension or source of data relative to other sources, and sT\textasciitilde{} measures the
\par
subjective value of a single datum relative to other data from the same
\par
source.
\par
The Bayesian paradigm looks at fixed hypotheses and examines the
\par
manner in which the subjective probabilities of these hypotheses are
\par
revised in the light of new information. For this reason it has sometimes
\par
been called a "dynamic" paradigm. In contrast, most of the correlational
\par
research deals with "static" aspects of information processing: When a
\par
subjective weight is inferred from a subject's responses over 50 trials, it
\par
is assumed that the subject's view of the world remains unchanged over
\par
this period. However, the static \emph{vs. }dynamic distinction is not inherent in
\par
the models. A good example of this point is found within integration
\par
theory, where the weighted average model takes both a static form (Eq.
\par
(4)) and a dynamic form, the proportional change model of Eq. (7). In
\par
like manner, a regression equation could process information sequentially,
\par
and the item-by-item revisions of the judgments could be compared to
\par
optimal revisions specified by the equation although such a study has not
\par
been reported.674 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
\emph{Objectiwe Composition Rules}
\par
Objective composition rules can be thought of as representing the
\par
environmental aspect of the judgment situation, and they encompass the
\par
same sorts of models as are applied to the subjects' judgments. In some
\par
cases, logical considerations suggest a normative rule (such as Bayes'
\par
theorem). In other situations, the rule is determined primarily on em-
\par
pirical grounds (e.g., the use of multiple regression in the lens model to
\par
predict some criterion variable).
\par
Both the lens model and the Bayesian approach share a deep concern
\par
about the relationship between the decision maker and his environment.
\par
Both models compare what the decision maker does with what he should
\par
be doing. The optimality of multiple regression rests on the acceptance
\par
of a built-in payoff function: the least-squares criterion of goodness of
\par
fit. The posterior probabilities of the Bayesian model, however, can be
\par
combined with any payoff function to determine the best action. In
\par
certain circumstances, Bayesian and multiple regression models lead to
\par
identical solutions, as in the ease of determining an optimal decision
\par
boundary between two hypotheses on the basis of normally distributed
\par
and standardized data (Koford \& Groner, 1966).
\par
\emph{Testing the Composition Rules}
\par
Validation of composition rules can take a number of different forms.
\par
Most important are comparisons between predicted responses (or other
\par
critical properties of subjective and objective rules) and the observed
\par
responses of the judge. These have been designated as validation types
\par
Nos. 1 and 2 in Fig. 2. The predictions of a particular subjective rule can
\par
also be compared with those of a particular objective rule. We have
\par
designated this as validation type 3. An example of this type of valida-
\par
lion is the G index in the lens model.
\par
With regard to validation types 1 and 2, researchers differ in their
\par
attitudes towards testing the models. Workers within correlational and
\par
Bayesian settings have typically been satisfied that. high correlations
\par
between their model's predictions and the subject's responses provide
\par
adequate evidence for the validity of the model. For example, Beach
\par
(1966) observed correlations in the .90's between subjects' \emph{P(H/D)}
\par
estimates and Bayesian values that were calculated using subjects' earlier
\par
\emph{P(D/H) }estimates. He concluded thai:
\par
. . . Ss possess a rule for revising subjective probabilities that they apply to
\par
whatever subjective probabilities they have at the moment . . . .
\par
As has been amply demonstrated, the Ss' revision rule is essentially Bayes'
\par
theorem. That is to say, Ss' revisions can be predicted with a good deal of pre-
\par
cision using Bayes' theorem as the model (Beach, 1966, p, 36).BAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN JUDGMENT 6 7 5
\par
However, Anderson, working within the framework of integration
\par
theory, has chided researchers for neglecting to test goodness of fit:
\par
Tests of quantitative predictions clearly require evaluation of tile discrepancies
\par
from prediction. Much of the earlier work . . . is unsatisfactory in this regard
\par
since it is based on regression statistics and goes no further than reporting correla-
\par
tions between predicted and observed (Anderson, 1969, p. 64).
\par
Anderson goes on to note that high correlations may occur despite a
\par
seriously incorrect model. As evidence he cites a study by Sidowski and
\par
Anderson (1967), which found a correlation of .986 between the data and
\par
a simple additive model, despite the fact that the ANOVA showed a
\par
statistically significant and substantively interpretable interaction.
\par
Finally, as we noted earlier, conjoint measurement provides qualitative,
\par
localized tests that may be quite powerful for discriminating among alter-
\par
native composition models.
\par
\emph{The paramorphic representation problem. }Hoffman (1960, 1968) raised
\par
an issue particularly germane to the discrimination of alternative sub-
\par
jective composition functions. He observed that: (a) Two or more models
\par
of judgment may be algebraically equivalent yet suggestive of radically
\par
different underlying processes; and (b) two or more models may be
\par
algebraically different yet equally predictive, given fallible data. Draw-
\par
ing an analogy to problems of classification in mineralogy, Hoffman
\par
introduced the term "paramorphic representation" to remind psycholo-
\par
gists that "the mathematical description of judgment is inevitably in-
\par
complete . . . , and it is not known how completely or how accurately
\par
the underlying process has been represented" (Hoffman, 1960, p. 125).
\par
Although Hoffman raised the paramorphic issue in connection with
\par
models based upon correlational techniques, all of the models we have
\par
discussed face the same problem when they are used descriptively.
\par
