FOCAL TOPIC OF CORRELATIONAL RESEARCH:
\par
MODELING A JUDGE'S POLICY
\par
\emph{The Linear Model}
\par
In a large number of studies, researchers have attempted to represent
\par
the judge's idiosyncratic weighting policy by means of the linear model
\par
(Eq. (2)). Examination of more than 30 of these studies illustrates the
\par
tremendous diversity of judgmental tasks to which the model has been
\par
applied. The tasks include judgments about personality characteristics
\par
(Hammond, Hursch, \& Todd, 1964; Knox \& Hoffman, 1962); perform-
\par
ance in college (Dawes, 1970; Einhorn, 1971; Newton, 1965; Sarbin,
\par
1942) or on the job (Madden, 1963; Naylor \& Wherry, 1965) ; attractive-
\par
ness of common stocks (S]ovic, 1969) and other types of gambles (Slovic
\par
\& Lichtenstein, 1968); physical and mental pathology (Goldberg, 1970;678 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
Hoffman, Slovic, \& Rorer, 1968; Oskamp, 1962; Wiggins \& Hoffman,
\par
1968) ; and legal matters (Kerr, 1968; Ulmer, 1969).4 In some cases, the
\par
stimuli were artificial and the judges were unfamiliar with the task.
\par
Typical of these is a study by Knox and Hoffman (1962), who asked
\par
college students to judge the intelligence of other students on the basis
\par
of grade point average, aptitude test scores, credit hours attempted, etc.,
\par
and a study by Summers (1968), who asked students to rate the potential
\par
for achieving minority group equality as a function of legislated oppor-
\par
tunities and educational opportunities. At the other extreme are studies
\par
of judgments made in complex but familiar situations by skilled de-
\par
cision makers who had other cues available besides those included in the
\par
prediction equation. For example, Kort (1968) modeled judicial decisions
\par
in workmen's compensation cases using various facts from the cases as
\par
cues. Brown (1970) modeled caseworkers' suicide probability estimates
\par
for persons phoning a metropolitan suicide prevention center; the cues
\par
were variables such as sex, age, suicide plan, etc., obtained from the
\par
telephone interview. And Dawes (1970) used a linear model to predict
\par
the ratings given applicants for graduate school by members of the
\par
admissions committee.
\par
In all of these situations the linear model has done a fairly good job of
\par
predicting the judgments, as indicated by R\textasciitilde{} values in the .80's and .90's
\par
for the artificial tasks and the .70's for the more complex real-world
\par
situations. Although most of these models were not cross-validated, the
\par
few studies that have applied the linear model derived from one sample
\par
of judgments to predict a second sample have found remarkably little
\par
shrinkage (Einhorn, 1971; Slovic \& Lichtenstein, 1968; Summers \&
\par
Stewart, 1968; and Wiggins \& Hoffman, 1968).
\par
\emph{Capturing a Judge's Policy}
\par
The various cues that define a stimulus are certainly not equally ira-
\par
portant and judges do not weight them equally. One of the purposes of
\par
using a linear model to represent the judgmental process is to make the
\par
judge's weighting policy explicit. Large individual differences among
\par
weighting policies have been found in almost every study that reports in-
\par
dividual equations. For example, Refer, Hoffman, Dickman, and Slovie
\par
4Drs. Arthur Elstein and Lee Shulman have recently brought to our attention a
\par
study by I-Ienry A. Wallace (who was later to become Secretary of Agriculture and
\par
Vice President) that used linear equations to model corn judges, and, in anticipation
\par
of the lens model, compared the judges' models with a model of the environment.
\par
Published in 1923, this study predates all other known work of this kind by several
\par
decades. The reference is:
\par
WALLACE, tt. A. What is in the corn judge's mind? \emph{Journal o] the American Society}
\par
\emph{o] Agronomy, }1923, 15, 300-304=.BAYESIAN AND REGRESSION A P P R O A C H E S IN J U D G M E N T 679
\par
(1967) examined the policies whereby hospital personnel granted week-
\par
end passes to patients at a mental hospital. For five of the six cues there
\par
was at least one judge for whom that cue was the most important item
\par
and at least one judge for whom it was nonsignificant. A striking example
\par
of individual differences in a task demanding a high level of expertise
\par
comes from a study of nine radiologists by Hoffman, Slovie, and Rorer
\par
(1968). The stimuli were hypothetical ulcers, described by the presence
\par
or absence of seven roentgenological signs. Each ulcer was rated accord-
\par
ing to its likelihood of being malignant. There was considerable disagree-
\par
ment among radiologists' judgments as indicated by a median interjudge
\par
correlation, across stimuli, of only .38. A factor analysis of these correla-
\par
tions disclosed four different types of judges, each of which was asso-
\par
ciated with a particular kind of policy equation.
\par
Even when expert judges don't disagree with one another, an attempt
\par
to model them can be enlightening. For example, seven of the nine
\par
radiologists studied by Hoffman et \emph{al. }viewed small ulcer craters as more
\par
likely to be malignant than large craters. Yet a follow-up study by
\par
Slovie, Rorer, and Hoffman (1971) described statistical evidence
\par
obtained by other researchers indicating just the opposite, that large
\par
craters are more likely than small ones to be malignant.
\par
The ability of regression equations to describe individual differences
\par
in judgment policies has led to the development of a number of tech-
\par
niques for grouping or clustering judges in terms of the homogeneity of
\par
their equations (Christal, 1963; Dudyeha, 1970; Maguire \& Glass, 1968;
\par
Naylor \& Wherry, 1965; Wherry \& Naylor, 1966; Williams, tIarlow,
\par
Lindem, \& Gab, 1970). Although a few of these studies have compared
\par
the methods, their relative utility remains to be demonstrated.
\par
In summary, it is apparent that the linear model is a powerful device
\par
for predicting quantitative judgments made on the basis of specific cues.
\par
It is capable of highlighting individual differences and misuse of informa-
\par
tion as well as making explicit the causes of underlying disagreements
\par
among judges in both simple and complex tasks.
\par
\emph{Nonlinear Cue Utilization}
\par
Despite the strong predictive ability of the linear model, a lively
\par
interest has been maintained in what GoIdberg (1968) has referred to as
\par
"the search for eonfiguraI judges." The impetus for this search comes
\par
from Meehl's (1954) classic inquiry into the relative validity of clinical
\par
\emph{vs. }actuarial prediction. Meehl proposed that one possible advantage of
\par
the clinical approach might arise from the clinician's ability to make use
\par
of configural relationships between predictors and a criterion.
\par
A clue to one outcome of the search was provided by Yntema and680 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
Torgerson (1961) who hypothesized that, whenever predictor variables
\par
are monotonically related to a criterion variable, a simple linear com-
\par
bination of main effects will do a remarkably accurate iob of predicting,
\par
even if interactions are known to exist. Yntema and Torgerson demon-
\par
strated their contention by present.ing an example in which they showed
\par
that 94\% of the variance: of a truly eonfigural function could be predicted
\par
from an additive combination of main effects.
\par
Early work by Hoffman, some reported in Itoffman (1960) and some
\par
unpublished, indicated that eonfigural terms based on the judge's verbali-
\par
zations added little or no increment of predictable response variance to
\par
that contributed by the linear model. The R\textasciitilde{} values were approximately
\par
as great as the retest reliabilities would permit, thus casting additional
\par
doubt about the existence of meaningful nonlinearities. Hursch, Ham-
\par
mond, and Hursch (1964); Hammond, Hurseh, and Todd (1964); and
\par
Newton (1965) reported unsuccessful attempts to find evidence of non-
\par
linearity using the C coefficient. However, these findings do not preclude
\par
the possibility of eonfigural judgment processes, since either a lack of
\par
nonlinearity in the environment or a difference between the nonlinearity
\par
in the environment and in the judgmental systems is sufficient to produce
\par
low C values.
\par
In light of \textasciitilde he simple but compelling arithmetic underlying Yntema
\par
and Torgerson's "main effect, hypothesis" the results of this early research
\par
should not have been too surprising. Yet the search continued, buoyed by
\par
(a) the repeated assertions of iudges to the effect that their processes
\par
really were complex and configural; (b) the possibility that previous
\par
experimenters had not yet studied the right kinds of tasks, tasks that
\par
were "truly eonfigural," and (c) the possibility that the experimental
\par
designs and statistical procedures used in previous studies were not
\par
optimally suited for uncovering the existing eonfigural effects.
\par
For example, Wiggins and Hoffman (1968) used a more sophisticated
\par
approach in their study of the diagnosis of neurotieism \emph{vs. }psychoticism
\par
from the MMPI. These 861 MMPI profiles were selected because MMPI
\par
lore considered this task to be highly configural with respect to this type
\par
of diagnosis. In addition to criterion diagnoses, the iudgments of 29
\par
clinical psychologists were available for each profile. Besides using the
\par
linear model, Wiggins and Hoffman employed a "quadratic model," which
\par
included the 11 MMPI scale scores (X\textasciitilde ), as in the linear model, along
\par
with all 11 squared values of these scales (X\textasciitilde{} 2) and the 55 cross-product
\par
terms \emph{( X i X j ) . }The third model tested was a "sign model" which included
\par
70 diagnostic signs from the M3/IPI literature, many of which were non-
\par
linear. The coefficients for each model and each judge were derived using
\par
a stepwise regression procedure. Cross validation of the models in a newBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES I N J U D G M E N T 681
\par
sampIe indicated that 13 subjects were best described by the sign model,
\par
three by the quadratic model, and 12 by the linear model. But even for the
\par
most nonlinear judge the superiority of his best model over the linear
\par
model was slight (.04 increase in R\textasciitilde ).
\par
Studies in which judges predicted the effects of various foreign
\par
policies (Summers \& Stewart, 1968), rated the attractiveness of gambles
\par
(Slovie \& Liehtenstein, 1968), evaluated the quality of patient care in
\par
hospital wards (tIuber, Sahney, \& Ford, 1969), and made decisions about
\par
workmen's compensation cases in a court of law (Kort, 1968) also found
\par
only minimal improvements in predictability as a consequence of includ-
\par
ing eurvilinear and configural terms.
\par
In an attempt to demonstrate the existence of configural effects, a
\par
number of investigators dropped the regression approach in favor of
\par
ANOVA designs applied to systematically constructed stimuli in tasks
\par
ranging from medical diagnosis to stock market forecasting (Hoffman,
\par
Slovic, \& Rorer, 1968; Rorer, Hoffman, Dickman, \& Slovic, 1967; Slovic,
\par
1969). These studies did succeed in uncovering numerous instances of
\par
interaction among cues but the increment in predictive power contributed
\par
by these eonfigural effects was again found to be small.
\par
This line of research, employing both correlational and ANOVA tech-
\par
niques, can be summarized simply and conclusively. The hypothesis of
\par
Yntema and Torgerson has clearly been substantiated. The linear model
\par
accounts for all but a small fraction of predictable variance in judgments
\par
across a remarkably diverse spectrum of tasks.
\par
However, the ANOVA research and other recent studies aimed at
\par
assessing the predictive power of nonlinear effects have exposed a differ-
\par
ent view of the problem, one that accepts the limited predictive benefits
\par
of nonlinear models but, simultaneously, asserts the definite, indeed wide-
\par
spread, existence of nonlinear judgment processes, and emphasizes their
\par
substantive meaning. To illustrate the complexity inherent in judgments
\par
that are quite predictable with a linear model, consider the data from
\par
the study of ulcer diagnosis conducted by Hoffman \emph{et al. }An ANOVA
\par
technique showed that each of the nine radiologists who served as subjects
\par
exhibited at least two statistically significant interactions. One showed
\par
13. Across radiologists there were 24 significant two-way, 17 three-way,
\par
and 14 four-way interactions. A subset of only 17 cue configurations, out
\par
of a possible 57, accounted for 43 of the 57 significant interactions. Thus
\par
numerous instances of eonfigurality were evidenced and a subset of
\par
specific interactions occurred repeatedly across radiologists. Hoffman
\par
et al. did not attempt to probe into the content of the interactions they
\par
observed but Slovie (1969), in his study of stockbrokers, and Kort
\par
(1968), in his study of workmen's compensation decisions, did and both6\textasciitilde 2 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
uncovered information about the rationale behind their judges' nonlinear
\par
use of the cues.
\par
Anderson has also paid careful attention to interactions obtained in
\par
his ANOVA studies of impression formation and has found several of
\par
substantive interest. For example, Anderson and Jacobson (1965) asked
\par
subjects to judge the likableness of persons described by sets of three
\par
adjectives. They found an interaction which implied that the weight
\par
given a particular adjective was less for sets where that adjective was
\par
inconsistent with the implications of the other adjectives than for sets
\par
in which it was consistent.. Sidowski and Anderson (1967) asked subjects
\par
to judge the attractiveness of working at a certain lob in a certain city.
\par
While the judgments of each city-job combination were found to be a
\par
weighted sum of the values for the two components, the attractiveness
\par
of being a teacher was more dependent upon the attractiveness of the
\par
city than were the other occupations, perhaps because teachers are in
\par
more direct contact with the cities' socioeconomic milieu. Other interest-
\par
ing examples of interactive cue utilization have been found by Lampel
\par
and Anderson (1968) and Gollob (1968).
\par
tIoffman (1968) has observed that an undirected search for eonfigural
\par
relations within a finite set of data is fraught with statistical difficulties.
\par
Green (196.8) eoneurred and criticized standard regression and ANOVA
\par
techniques for being essentially fishing expeditions. A better strategy,
\par
he suggested, is to form some specific hypothesis about eonfigurality and
\par
seek support for it. In this vein, Slovie (1966) hypothesized and found
\par
differences in subjects' strategies for combining information as a function
\par
of whether cues were in conflict. When the implications of important cues
\par
were congruent, subjects seemed to use both. When they were incon-
\par
sistent, subjects focused on one of the cues or turned to other cues for
\par
resolution of the conflict. This study, and related experiments reported in
\par
Hoffman (1968) and in Anderson and Jacobson (1965), indicate that the
\par
linear model may need to be amended to include a term sensitive to the
\par
level of incompatibility among important cues.
\par
Tversky (1969) and Einhorn (1971) also hypothesized, and found,
\par
specific nonlinear uses of information. Tversky's subjects sometimes
\par
chose among a pair of two-dimensional gambles by a lexicographic pro-
\par
cedure in which they selected the gamble with the greater probability of
\par
winning, provided that the difference between gambles on this dimension
\par
exceeded some small value e. If the probability difference was less than or
\par
equal to e, these subjects selected the gamble with the higher payoff.
\par
In contrast to the linear model, this sort of strategy is noneompensatory,
\par
inasmuch as no amount of superiority with regard to payoff can overcome
\par
a deficiency greater than e on the probability dimension.B A Y E S I A N A N D REGRESSIO,]N " A P P R O A C H E S I N J U D G M E N T 6 8 3
\par
Einholna developed mathematical functions that could be incorporated
\par
into a prediction equation to approximate conjunctive and disjunctive
\par
judgmental processes as postulated by Coombs (1964) and Dawes
\par
(1964). Dawes described the evaluation of a potential inductee by a
\par
draft board physician as one example of a conjunctive process. The
\par
physician requires that the inductee meet an entire set of minimal cri-
\par
teria in order to be judged physically fit. A disjunctive evaluation, on
\par
the other hand, depends solely on the attribute with highest value. For
\par
example, a scout for a professional football team may evaluate a player
\par
purely in terms of his best specialty, be it passing, running, or kicking.
\par
Neither the conjunctive nor the disjunctive models balances one attribute
\par
against another as does the linear modeh Einhorn pitted his conjunctive
\par
and disjunctive models against the linear model in two tasks, one where
\par
faculty members ranked applicants for graduate school, the other where
\par
students ranked jobs according to their preferences. He found that many
\par
subjects were fit better by the conjunctive model than by the linear
\par
model, particularly in the job preference task. Einhorn concluded by
\par
criticizing the notion that cognitive complexity and mathematical com-
\par
plexity go hand in hand. He argued that nonlinear, noncompensatory
\par
strategies may be more simple, cognitively, than the linear model, despite
\par
their greater mathematical complexity.
\par
At this point, it seems appropriate to conclude that notions about
\par
nonlinear processes are likely to play an increasing role in our under-
\par
standing of judgment despite their limited ability to outprediet linear
\par
models.
\par
\emph{Subjective Policies and Self-Insight}
\par
Thus far we have been discussing weighting policies that have been
\par
assessed by fitting a regression model to the judge's responses. We think
\par
of these as "computed" or "objective" policies. Judges in a number of
\par
studies were asked to describe the relative weights they had used in
\par
the task. The correspondence between these "subjective weights" and
\par
the computed weights serves as an indieant of the judge's insight into
\par
his own policy. Martin (1957) and Hoffman (1960) proposed a technique
\par
that has been used to examine these "after the fact" opinions--that of
\par
asking the judge to distribute 100 points according to the relative
\par
importance of each attribute. Martin found that the linear model based
\par
on subjective weights produced a mean R\textasciitilde{} of .77 in predicting evalua-
\par
lions of a student's sociability. A linear model computed in the usual
\par
way, but not cross-validated, did better, producing a mean R\textasciitilde{} of .89.
\par
Koepfl and Huber (1970), Hoffman (19fi0), Oskamp (1962), Pollack
\par
(19fi4), Slovie (1969), and Slovic, Fleissner, and Bauman (in press)684 SLOVIC AND I.ICHTENSTEIN
\par
all found serious discrepancies between subiective and computed relative
\par
weights for their judges.
\par
One type of error in self-insight has emerged in all of these studies.
\par
Judges strongly overestimate the importance they place on minor cues
\par
(i.e., their subjective weights greatly exceed the computed weights for
\par
these cues) and they underestimate their reliance on a few major vari-
\par
ables. Subiects apparently are quite unaware of the extent to which
\par
their judgments can be predicted by only a few cues. Across a number
\par
of studies, varying in the number of cues that were available, three
\par
cues usually sufficed to account for more than 80\textasciitilde o of the predictable
\par
variance in the judge's responses. The most important cue usually
\par
accounted for more than 40\textasciitilde 'o of this variance.
\par
Shepard (1964, p. 266) presented an interesting explanation of the
\par
subjective underweighting of important cues and overweighting of minor
\par
cues. He hypothesized:
\par
Possibly our feeling that we can take into account a host of different factors comes
\par
about because, although we remember that at some time or other we have attended
\par
to each of the different factors, we fail to notice that it is seldom more than one
\par
or two that we consider at any one time.
\par
Slovic, Fleissner, and Bauman (in press), studying the policies of
\par
stockbrokers, examined the relationship between number of years as a
\par
broker and accuracy of self-insight. The latter was measured by cot-
\par
relating a broker's subjective weights with his computed weights across
\par
eight cue factors. Across 13 brokers, the Spearman rank correlation
\par
between the insight index and experience was --.43. Why should greater
\par
experience lead to less valid self-insight? Perhaps the recent training
\par
of the young brokers necessitated an explicit awareness of the mechanics
\par
of the skill that they were attempting to learn. Skills generally demand
\par
a great deal of conscious attention as they are being acquired. With
\par
increasing experience, behaviors become more automatic and require
\par
much less attention. Because of this they may also be harder to describe.
\par
It may be that the most experienced judges produce verbal rationales
\par
for their evaluations that are less trustworthy than those of their in-
\par
experienced colleagues. This hypothesis is an intriguing one and needs
\par
to be investigated with more precision than was done in this study.
\par
In fact, all of the studies described in this section need to be replicated
\par
using a variety of psyehophysieal methods to assess subjective weights.
\par
Some methods that could be used in such studies are suggested by
\par
Eekenrode (1965), Klahr (1969), Yntema and Klein (1964), and Yntema
\par
and Torgerson (1961).BAYESIAN AND REGRESSIO'N APPROACHES IN JUDGMENT \textasciitilde 85
\par
TASK DETERMINANTS IN CORRELATIONAL RESEARCH
\par
\emph{Cue Interrelationships}
\par
Several studies have examined the role of intercorrelational structure
\par
arid conflict among cues in determining the weighting of those cues.
\par
Slovic (1966; see also Hoffman, 1968) found that, when important cues
\par
agreed in their implications, subjects typically used both and weighted
\par
them equally. When they disagreed, subjects focused on one of the
\par
cues or turned to other cues for resolution of the conflict. Also, in
\par
situations of higher cue conflict, linear consistency (R\textasciitilde ) was much lower.
\par
These effects were found both when cue conflict and cue intercorrelations
\par
"varied together and when conflict was varied while keeping the cues
\par
orthogonal (uncorrelated). Dudycha and Naylor (1966a) have also
\par
studied the effect of varying the intereorrelations among cues upon
\par
policy equations. They found that profiles of cue-utilization correlations
\par
(r\textasciitilde,\textasciitilde ) showed more variance and R\textasciitilde{} decreased as the correlation between
\par
cues decreased. However, the regression weights (b\textasciitilde.,s values) remained
\par
relatively stable across sets varying in cue correlation and, therefore,
\par
much of the decrease in R\textasciitilde{} may have been a statistical artifact (see
\par
Schenck \& Naylor, 1968).
\par
\emph{Cu, e Variability azzd Cue Utilization}
\par
Uhl and Hoffman (1958) hypothesized that an increase in the vari-
\par
ability of a salient, cue, across a set of stimuli, would lead to greater
\par
weighting of this cue by a judge. This increased weight would persist,
\par
they proposed, even among subsets of stimuli for which this cue was
\par
no\textasciitilde{} unusually variable. Underlying this hypothesis was the assumption
\par
that the judge is motivated to make differential predictions and that
\par
cues which increase his ability to differentiate will reinforce his use
\par
of those attributes. The variability of a salient cue is one such feature
\par
that correlates with differentiability. Presumably judges will focus their
\par
attention on the more highly variable cues, other things being equal.
\par
Uhl and Hoffman tested this hypothesis in a task where subjects
\par
iudged IQ on the basis of profiles made up of nine cues. Each subject
\par
judged several sets of profiles on different days. The variability of a
\par
particular cue was increased on 1 day by providing a greater number
\par
of extreme levels. On a following day, the cue was returned to normal
\par
and its relative weight was compared with the weight it received prior
\par
to the manipulation of variability. The hypothesized effect was found
\par
in seven out of ten subjects when a strong cue was manipulated. In-
\par
creasing the variability of a minor cue had no effect upon its subsequent686 SLOVIC AND L 1 C H T E N S T E I N
\par
use. The authors concluded that the judge may alter his system of
\par
judgment because of the characteristics of samples he sees.
\par
Morrison and Slovie (1962) independently tested a version of the
\par
variability hypothesis in a different type of setting. Each of t.heir
\par
stimuli consisted of a circle (Dimension 1) paired with a square (Dimen-
\par
sion 2). Subjects had to rank order a set of these stimuli on the basis
\par
of their total area (circle and square combined). The results indicated
\par
that if the variability of circle area was greater than the variability
\par
of square area in the set of stimuli, then circle area would be assigned
\par
much heavier weight in the judgment. If the variability of square area
\par
was higher in the set, then square area became the dominant dimension.
\par
\emph{Cue Format}
\par
Knox and Hoffman (1962) examined the effect of profile format, on
\par
judgments of a person's intelligence and sociability. Each subject based
\par
his judgments on profiles of cues. In one condition, the cues were pre-
\par
sented as T scores with a mean of 50 and SD of 10. A second condition
\par
presented the information in percentile scores. The percentile profiles
\par
showed considerably more scatter and had more extreme values than
\par
did the T scores, which tended to appear rather "squashed." Judgments
\par
were made on a stanine scale with a normal distribution suggested but
\par
not. forced. Judgments made to percentile scores were found to be much
\par
more variable. It appeared that judges were responding not only to
\par
the underlying meaning of the scores but to the graphical position of
\par
the points on the profile in an absolute sense. Subjects were reluctant
\par
to make ratings on the judgment scale that were more extreme than
\par
the stimulus scores. Being statistically naive, they were unable to gauge
\par
the true extremeness of certain T scores. Judgments made to percentile
\par
scores were also more reliable and produced higher values of R\textasciitilde{} when
\par
linear models were fitted to them. Regression weights did not differ
\par
significantly between formats.
\par
\emph{Number of. Cues}
\par
There has been surprisingly little correlational research on the effects
\par
of varying the number of cues. A pilot study by ttoffman and Blanehard
\par
(1961) obtained some interesting results but was limited by a small
\par
number of subiects. Hoffman and Blanchard had subjects predict a
\par
person's weight on the basis of either two, five, or seven physical char-
\par
acteristics. Increased numbers of cues led to lower R\textasciitilde{} values, decreased
\par
accuracy, lower test-retest reliability, and lower response variance. This
\par
latter finding may be the cause of some of the other results and mayBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN JUDG\textasciitilde IENT \textasciitilde 87
\par
itself be due to an increased number of conflicts among cues in the
\par
larger data sets.
\par
Hayes (1964) and Einhom (1971) also found that linear consistency
\par
decreased as the number of cues increased. Einhorn interpreted this
\par
decrease in R\textasciitilde{} as indicating that his subjects were using more complex
\par
models in the high information conditions--models whose variance was
\par
not predictable from the linear and nonlinear models he tested. However,
\par
the reliability of his subjects' judgments was not assessed and it is
\par
possible that greater information merely produced more unreliable rather
\par
than more complex judgments. Hayes found that increased numbers
\par
of cues also led to a reduction in decision quality when subjects were
\par
working under a time limit.
\par
Oskamp (1965) had 32 judges, including eight experienced clinical
\par
psychologists, read background information about a case. The informa-
\par
tion was divided into four sections. After reading each section of the
\par
case the judge answered 25 questions about the attitudes and behaviors
\par
of the subject and gave a confidence rating with each answer. The
\par
correct answers were known to the investigator. Oskamp found that,
\par
as the amount of information about the case increased, accuracy re-
\par
mained at about the same level while confidence increased dramatically
\par
and became entirely out of proportion to actual correctness.
\par
In summary, there is a small amount of evidence that increasing the
\par
amount of information available to the decision maker increases his
\par
confidence without increasing the quality of his decisions and makes
\par
his decisions more difficult to predict.
\par
\emph{Cue-Response Compatibility}
\par
FiRs and Deininger (1954) introduced the concept of stimulus-
\par
response compatibility to explain the results of several studies of paired-
\par
associates learning and reaction time. Compatibility was defined as a
\par
function of the similarity between the spatial position of the stimulus
\par
in a circular array and the position of the correct response in the same
\par
sort of array. High compatibility produced the quickest learning and
\par
fastest reaction time. In an experiment concerned with risk-taking judg-
\par
ments, Slovic and Lichtenstein (1968) observed a related type of com-
\par
patibility effect that influenced cue utilization. They found that when
\par
subjects rated the attractiveness of a gamble, probability of winning
\par
was the most important factor in their policy equations. In a second
\par
condition, subjects were required to indicate the attractiveness of a
\par
gamble by an alternative method, viz., equating the gamble with an
\par
amount of money such that they would be indifferent between playing6S8 SLOVIC AND L[CHTENSTEIN
\par
the gamble and receiving the stated amount. Here it was found that
\par
attractiveness was determined more by a gamble's outcomes than by
\par
its probabilities. The outeomes, being expressed in units of dollars,
\par
were readily commensurable with the units of the responses, also dollars.
\par
On the other hand, the probability cues had to be transformed by the
\par
subiect into values commensurable with dollars before they eould be
\par
integrated with these other cues. It seems plausible that the cognitive
\par
effort involved in making this sort of transformation greatly detracted
\par
from the influence of the probability cues in the second task.
\par
This finding suggests the general hypothesis that greater compatibility
\par
between a cue and the required response should enhance the importance
\par
of that cue in determining the response. Presumably, the more complex
\par
the transformation needed to make a cue commensurable with other
\par
important cues and with the response, the less that cue will be used.
\par
FOCAL TOPIC OF THE ANOVA PARADIGM:
\par
MODELS OF IMPRESSION FOP\textasciitilde MATION
\par
There is a substantial body of literature concerned with the problem
\par
of understanding how component items of information are integrated
\par
into impressions of people. Much of this research can be traced to the
\par
work of Aseh (1946) who asked subjects to evaluate a person described
\par
by various trait adjectives. In one of Aseh's studies, the adjective
\par
"warm" was added to the set of traits. Another group saw the trait
\par
"cold." All other adjectives were identical. The subjects wrote a brief
\par
description of the person and completed an adjective checklist. Asch
\par
found that substitution of the word "warm" for "cold" produced a
\par
decided change in the overall characterization of the person being
\par
evaluated. He interpreted this as being due to a shift, in meaning of
\par
the traits associated with the key adjectives "warm" and "cold." This
\par
view has much in eommon with the notions of eonfigurality and inter-
\par
action we have been discussing.
\par
More recent endeavors have centered around the search for quantita-
\par
tive models of the integration process. That is, they attempt to develop
\par
a mathematical function of the scale values of the individual items to
\par
predict the overall impression. Although Aseh explicitly denied that
\par
an impression could be derived from a simple additive combination of
\par
stimulus items, the additive model and its variations have received the
\par
most attention (Rosenberg, 1968). Most of these studies have used
\par
rigorous experimental control, factorial designs, and statistical tech-
\par
niques such as ANOVA as the basis for their analyses.
\par
One of the first studies to test the additive model was carried out by
\par
Anderson (1962). His subjects rated a number of hypothetical persons,BAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN J U D G M E N T 689
\par
each described by three adjectives, on a 20-point scale of likableness.
\par
Within each set there was one item each of high, medium, and low scale
\par
value as determined in a separate normative study. An additive model
\par
gave an excellent fit to the data.
\par
The additive model serves as a more general case for two derivative
\par
models--one based on the principle of summation of information, the
\par
other an averaging formulation. In the summation model, the values
\par
of the stimulus items are added to arrive at an impression. The averaging
\par
model asserts that an impression is the mean, rather than the sum, of
\par
the separate item values. The adding \emph{vs. }averaging question has funda-
\par
mental importance in integration theory. It governs the theoretieal
\par
interpretation of the results and it affects the sealing of the stimuli
\par
(Anderson, in press).
\par
The study by Anderson (1962) did not attempt to distinguish be-
\par
tween the averaging and summation formulations. To do so requires
\par
careful attention to subtle facets of stimulus construction and experi-
\par
mental design. Recent research has taken on this challenge, varying
\par
task and design characteristics in an attempt, to determine the validity
\par
of these and other competing models. The following section will review
\par
briefly the types of situational manipulations that have been brought
\par
to bear on this problem.
\par
TASK DETERMINANTS OF INFORMATION USE
\par
IN IMPRESSION FORMATION
\par
\emph{Set Size}
\par
The number of items of information in a set is one factor that has
\par
been varied in attempts to distinguish summation and averaging models.
\par
Fishbein and Hunter (1964) provided four groups of subjects with
\par
different amounts of positively evaluated information about a fictitious
\par
person. The information was presented sequentially in such a way that
\par
the total summation of effect increased as a function of the number
\par
of items while the mean decreased, i.e., the more highly evaluated items
\par
came first. The subjects used a series of bipolar adjective scales to
\par
evaluate the stimulus persons. The judgments became more favorable
\par
as the amount of information increased, presumably supporting the
\par
summation model. The Fishbein and Hunter study has been criticized
\par
by Rosenberg (1968) who argued that presenting the most favorable
\par
adjective first permitted possible sequential effects to influence the
\par
results. Also, the intended decrease in mean evaluation of the individual
\par
adjectives as a function of set size was not statistically significant. As
\par
Anderson (1971 in press) observed, increased extremity of judgment690 SLOVIC AND L I C H T E N S T E I N
\par
with sequential presentation of essentially equal adjectives can be
\par
accounted for by a version of the averaging model.
\par
Anderson (1965a) also used set size to contrast the two models, tie
\par
had subjects rate the likableness of persons described by either two or
\par
four traits. He found that sets consisting of two moderately valued
\par
traits and two extremely valued traits produced a less extreme judgment
\par
than sets consisting of the two extreme traits alone. This result was
\par
taken as support for the averaging model and was later replicated by
\par
Hendrick (1968). Another result of Anderson's study, that sets of four
\par
extreme adjectives were rated more extreme than sets with two extreme
\par
adjectives, confirmed earlier findings by Anderson (1959), Podell (1962)
\par
and Stewart (1965) to the effect \textasciitilde hat increased set size produces more
\par
extreme ratings. While this result seems to support a summation model,
\par
Anderson showed how it could be accommodated using an averaging
\par
model that incorporates an initial impression with nonzero weight and
\par
scale value So. Anderson (1967b) provided further support for this model.
\par
\emph{Extremity of Information}
\par
The adjectives in Anderson's (1965a) study were presumed to be of
\par
equal weight. Thus the averaging model predicted that the judgment of
\par
a stimulus set containing four items having extreme scale values averaged
\par
with the judgment of a set containing four items of moderate value
\par
would equal the iudgment of a set containing two extreme and two
\par
moderate items. Anderson found that this prediction did not hold for
\par
negatively evaluated items. The discrepancy suggested that. the extreme
\par
negative items carried more weight than did moderately negative
\par
information.
\par
Studies by Himmelfarb (1970), Kerrick (1958), FIanis, Gleason,
\par
and Dawes (1966), Oden and Anderson (in press), Osgood and Tannen-
\par
baum (1955), Podell and Podell (1963), Weiss (1963), and Willis (1960)
\par
also found indications that the weight of an information item is asso-
\par
ciated with the extremity of its scale value. Manis \emph{et al. }found that two
\par
positive or two negative items of information of different value would
\par
lead to a iudgment less extreme than the most extreme item but more
\par
extreme than that predicted by a simple averaging of the items. At
\par
the same time these judgments were not extreme enough to be produced
\par
by the summation model. To account for these results, \textasciitilde he authors
\par
suggested a version of the averaging model that weights items in pro-
\par
portion to their extremity, ttimme]farb found support for an averaging
\par
model in which neutral information received less weight than more
\par
polarized information. Oden and Anderson found that. the importance
\par
of a stimulus dimension depended on its value but the direction of
\par
dependence varied across different types of iudgment tasks.BAYESIAN AND REGRESSIO:N APPROACIKES I N .JUDGMENT 691
\par
\emph{Redundancy}
\par
Both summation and averaging models assume that the values of
\par
the stimulus items are independent, of the other items in the set. This
\par
assmnption has been the focus of concern for a number of studies. Dustin
\par
and Baldwin (1966), for example, had subjects evaluate persons described
\par
by single adieetives, A and B, and by the combined pair AB. Ratings of
\par
AB pairs tended to be more extreme than the mean of the individual
\par
items; this tendency was dependent upon the degree of redundancy or
\par
implication between A and B as measured by their intereorrelation in
\par
a normative sample. Schmidt (1969) did a similar study but varied
\par
the relatedness of the items differently. He combined trait sentences
\par
(Mr. A is kind) with instance sentences (Mr. A is kind to B). The two
\par
sentences just given are obviously highly redundant. By changing the
\par
trait adjectives this redundancy can be greatly reduced. Schmidt found
\par
that judgments based on less redundant sets were consistently more
\par
extreme than those based on more redundant information. Wyer reported
\par
similar findings in studies where redundancy was measured by the con-
\par
ditional probability of A given B (Wyer, 1968) and by the degree to
\par
which the joint probability of occurrence (Pa\textasciitilde ) exceeded the product
\par
of the two unconditional probabilities (Pa and P\textasciitilde ) (Wyer, 1970). It
\par
seems apparent that models in this area will need further revision to
\par
handle the effects of redundancy.
\par
\emph{Inter-Item Consistency}
\par
The data just described indicate that highly redundant information
\par
has less impact. But information with too great a "surprise value" shares
\par
a similar fate. Anderson and Jaeobson (1965) found that an item whose
\par
scale value is highly inconsistent with its accompanying items (as is
\par
the trait "gloomy" in the set "honest-considerate-gloomy") was likely
\par
to be discounted, i.e., given less weight. The discounting was slight when
\par
subjects were told that all three traits were accurate and equally
\par
important, but increased when subjects were cautioned that one of the
\par
items might be less valid than the others. Anderson and Jacobson
\par
argued that the averaging model might have to include differential
\par
weights to accommodate the reduced impact of inconsistent information.
\par
Wyer (1970) defined inconsistency among two adjectives as the degree
\par
to which their joint probability (PA\textasciitilde ) was less than the product of their
\par
unconditional probabilities (PA and PB). Note that this places high
\par
inconsistency at the negative end of a continuum defined by \emph{PA\textasciitilde --PAPB,}
\par
with maximum redundancy at the positive end. After constructing stimuli
\par
according to this definition, Wyer found that inconsistency produced a
\par
discounting of the less polarized of a pair of adjectives, leading to a692 SLOVIC AND L I C I \textasciitilde{} T E N S T E I N
\par
more extreme evaluation. However, when inconsistency became too
\par
great, both adjectives appeared to be discounted, producing a less extreme
\par
evaluation.
\par
Himmelfarb and Senn (1969) studied the effects of stimulus incon-
\par
sistency in experiments concerned with judgments of a person's social
\par
class. The stimulus persons were described by dimensional attributes,
\par
occupation, income, and education. Surprisingly, discounting of incon-
\par
sistent information was not found. The authors speculated that their
\par
failure to find discounting might have been due to the lack of directly
\par
contradictory information or to the possibility that social class stimuli,
\par
being objective aspects of an individual, might be less easily discounted
\par
than personality traits.
\par
\emph{Other Contextual Effects}
\par
Anderson and Lampel (1965) and Anderson (1966) had subjects form
\par
an impression based on three adjectives and then rate one of the com-
\par
ponent traits alone. Both studies produced context effects, judgments
\par
of the single trait being displaced towards the values of the other traits.
\par
A natural interpretation of this effect is that the value or meaning of
\par
the test word has changed as a function of the impression formation
\par
process, much as Asch originally suggested. Wyer and Watson (1969)
\par
argued in favor of this change-of-meaning interpretation over several
\par
competing hypotheses but their data are equivocal. Anderson and
\par
Lampel (1965) hypothesized that positive context effects are due to
\par
a generalized halo effect rather than a change of meaning. According
\par
to this theory, once a component has been integrated into the whole,
\par
it no longer has a distinct individual meaning. The subject then rates
\par
the individual component according to a weighted average of its context-
\par
free meaning and the overall impression. Anderson (1971) describes two
\par
experiments supporting this position.
\par
\emph{P\textasciitilde imacy and Recency Effects}
\par
Several dozen studies of impression formation have attempted to de-
\par
termine whether information presented early in a sequence is more or
\par
less influential than information presented later, other things being
\par
equal. Greater influence of early information is called primacy. Its
\par
opposite effect is called recency.
\par
These studies can be categorized according to whether the subiect's
\par
task was to average verbal items of information such as adjectives, or
\par
quantitative and perceptual items such as numbers, weights, lines, and
\par
loudness of sounds. A typical experimental design in such studies goes
\par
as follows: First the items are scaled individually with respect to theBAYESIAN AND REGRESSIO,N A P P R O A C H E S I N J U D G M E N T 693
\par
criterion. These items are then sorted into homogeneous subsets having
\par
high (H) or low (L) scale values. Then blocks of H and L items are
\par
presented in varying order. For example, primacy would lead the final
\par
judgment for an HHLL sequence to be higher than that for an LLHH
\par
sequence, while recency would produce the opposite effect.
\par
The results of these investigations indicate that order effects are
\par
highly pervasive phenomena, appearing in studies that employ quite
\par
diverse stimuli and response modes. However, whether primacy or
\par
recency effects occur is dependent upon task characteristics. When
\par
adjectives are used as stimuli and the subject responds only at the
\par
end of the information sequence, primacy is usually found (Asch, 1946;
\par
Anderson, 1965b; Anderson \& Barrios, 1961; Anderson \& Norman, 1964;
\par
Luehins, 1957). Primacy effects in these studies have been attributed
\par
to decreased attention being given the later adjectives and to discounting
\par
of the inconsistent information provided by the later adjectives (Ander-
\par
son, 1968a). When attentional demands were changed by having subjects
\par
recall the adjectives after making their rating, pronounce each adjective,
\par
or make a rating after each new item was presented, recency pre-
\par
dominated (Anderson, 1959, 1968a; Levin \& Schmidt, 1969; Luchins,
\par
1958; Anderson \& Hubert, 1963; Hendrick \& Costantini, 1970a; Rhine,
\par
1968; Rosenkrantz \& Crockett, 1965; Stewart, 1965). When numbers,
\par
weights, lines, or sounds are used as stimuli, the information items are
\par
homogeneous and not likely to create feelings of incongruity. In these
\par
studies recency is observed, regardless of whether the judgments are
\par
made during the sequence or only at the end (Anderson, 1964, 1967a;
\par
Anderson \& Jacobson, 1968; Hendrick \& Costantini, 1970b; Parducci,
\par
Thaler, \& Anderson, 1968; Weiss \& Anderson, 1969). Although many
\par
hypotheses have been proposed to account for these effects, their causes
\par
remain to be determined precisely.
\par
FOCAL TOPIC OF BAYESIAN I\textasciitilde ESEARCI-I: CONSEtgVATISM
\par
The most common Bayesian study deals with probability estimation,
\par
often in some variant of the bookbag and poker chip experiment
\par
described earlier. The primary finding has been labeled \emph{conservatism:}
\par
Upon receipt of new information, subjects revise their posterior prob-
\par
ability estimates in the same direction as the optimal model, but the
\par
revision is typically \emph{t,oo small; }subjects act as if the data are less
\par
diagnostic than they truly are. Subjects in some studies (Peterson,
\par
Schneider, \& Miller, 1965; Phillips \& Edwards, 1966) have been found
\par
to require from two to nine data observations to revise their opinions
\par
as much as Bayes' theorem would prescribe for one observation.
\par
Much of the Bayesiart research has been motivated by a desire to694 SLOVIC AND L I C H T E N S T E I N
\par
discover the determinants of conservatism in order that. its effects might
\par
be minimized in practical diagnostic settings. A spirited debate has
\par
been raging among Bayesians about which part of the judgment process
\par
leads subjects astray. The principal competing explanations as to the
\par
"locus of conservatism" are the misperception, misaggregation, and arti-
\par
fact hypotheses (Edwards, 1968).
\par
\emph{Misperception}
\par
In order to perform optimally, subiects must have some understanding
\par
of the data generator, the model, device, equations or other assumptions
\par
used by the experimenter to generate the stimuli shown to the subject.
\par
If the subject misunderstands the data generator, he may misperceive
\par
the conditional probability of the data given the hypothesis, \emph{P(D/H);}
\par
this misperception is hypothesized by some to be the source of con-
\par
servatism. For example, Lichtenstein and Feeney (1968) showed that
\par
sub iects performed very poorly when dealing with a circular normal
\par
data generator despite 150 training trials with feedback. But subjects'
\par
data and comments suggested an entirely different (and incorrect)
\par
model regarding the meaning of each datum, and reanalyses of their
\par
responses showed them to be quite consistent with this simpler yet
\par
incorrect view of the data generator. Does such a simple and popular
\par
data generator as the binomial distribution also lead to misperceptions
\par
about the meaning of data? Vlek and Beintema (1967) and Vlek and
\par
van der Heijden (1967) showed that it does. Vlek and Beintema pre-
\par
sented subjects with samples (e.g., five black and four white) drawn
\par
from an urn whose constituent proportions were known to the subject,
\par
and asked them how often such a sample might be expected to occur
\par
in 100,000 samples of the same size. Vlek and van der Heijden asked
\par
for the probability that such a sample would occur in 100 trials. Both
\par
studies showed that subjects had poor understanding of the likelihood
\par
of data.
\par
If such mispereeptions are the cause of conservatism, then one would
\par
expect estimates of posterior probabilities to be consistent with, and
\par
predictable from, estimates about the data generator, that is, estimates
\par
of \emph{P(D/H). }Peterson, DuCharme, and Edwards (1968) had subjects in
\par
a binomial task estimate \emph{P(H/D), }then \emph{P(D/H). }Then they were in-
\par
structed in \emph{P(D/H) }by being shown several theoretical sampling dis-
\par
tributions and discussing them with the experimenter. For example, they
\par
observed how the distribution became more peaked as the number of
\par
draws and the dominant proportion increased. Finally they were again
\par
asked to estimate \emph{P(H/D). }Peterson \emph{et al. }found that subieets' coI\textasciitilde -
\par
servative \emph{P(H/D) }estimates could be explained by the deviations ofBAYESIAR r AND REGRESSION APPROACHES I N J U D G M E N T 695
\par
their \emph{P(D/H) }estimates from the optimal values. They also found
\par
that instruction about the sampling distributions reduced conservatism
\par
in. the final stage, but the reduction was small in relation to the amount
\par
of conservatism.
\par
Subjects in the study by Peterson \emph{et at. }did not have the theoretical
\par
sampling distribution available a t the time they made post, instruction
\par
\emph{P(H/D) }estimates. Pitz and Downing (196/) gave subjects similar
\par
instruction and, in addition, allowed them to refer to histogram displays
\par
of the theoretical sampling distributions as they made predictions about
\par
which of two populations was generating the data. However, their pre-
\par
dictions were not improved by this instruction. Wheeler and Beach
\par
(1968) trained subjects by having them observe samples of eight draws,
\par
make a bet on which of two populations generated the data, and then
\par
observe the correct answer. Prior to training the subjects' sampling
\par
distributions were too fiat, their betting responses were conservative,
\par
and these two errors were consistent with one another. After training,
\par
the subjects' sampling distributions were more veridical, their betting
\par
responses were less conservative, and again the two sets of responses
\par
were consistent.
\par
A particular kind of misperception error relates to the impact of rare
\par
events. Vlek (19'65) suggested that unlikely events, when they occur, are
\par
seen as uninformative. He argued for the compelling nature of this error
\par
by giving an exaggerated example,
\par
The posterior probability that a sample of 2004 chips, 1004 of which are red, is
\par
takel\textasciitilde{} from bag A \emph{(Pr }z .70), and not from B (P,. z .30), is equal to .967. But who
\par
will accept hypothesis A as a possible generator of these data, and, if forced to
\par
do so, who dares to base an important decision of such a small difference in
\par
the--seemingly biased sample? (p. 15).
\par
The answer to his question is, of course, that Bayes' theorem dares. In
\par
the optimal model, it matters not at all that a datum may be highly
\par
unlikely under both hypotheses. The only determinant of its impact is the
\par
relative possibility of its occurrence: the likelihood ratio. The violation of
\par
this likelihood principle has been demonstrated by Vlek (1965) and Vlek
\par
and van der Heijden (1967), who showed a systematic increase in con-
\par
servatism as a function of the rarity of the data, and this violation can
\par
serve as an explanation for Lichtenstein and Feeney's (1968) results.
\par
Beach (1968) directly tested Vlek's hypothesis. Beach constructed decks
\par
of cards, each with a letter, from A to F, written on it in green or red
\par
ink. The task of the subjects was to estimate the posterior probability
\par
that the letters sampled were drawn from the green deck rather than the
\par
red deck, given complete information about the frequency of each letter696 SLOVIC AND, LICHTENSTEIN
\par
in each deck. Two groups of subjects used different decks of cards; the
\par
likelihood ratios were the same between groups, but the relative fre-
\par
quencies of the letters differed between groups. This permitted a test of
\par
whether the impact of rare events was mispereeived, with likelihood ratio
\par
held constant. The results verified Vlek's hypothesis; subjects were more
\par
conservative when responding to less likely events.
\par
\emph{Misaggr, egation}
\par
Another explanation of conservatism is that subjects have great diffi-
\par
culty in aggregating or putting together various pieces of information to
\par
produce a single response. Proponents of this view draw support from
\par
several sources (Edwards, 1968). First, they point out that in the studies
\par
just reported as supporting the misperception hypothesis, subjects were
\par
shown samples of several data at once. When shown a sample of, say, six
\par
red and three blue chips, and asked to state the probability that such a
\par
sample might occur, the subject must, in a sense, aggregate the separate
\par
impact of each chip, even though the sample is presented simultaneously.
\par
Viewed in this light., both estimation of \emph{P(H/D) }and of \emph{P(D/H) }in
\par
studies like Wheeler and Beach (1968) are aggregation tasks; thus the
\par
consistency between the two tasks does not provide a discrimination be-
\par
tween the mispereeption and misaggregation hypotheses. Beach (1968),
\par
testing the rare event hypothesis, did present subjects with only one
\par
datum at a time, but he presented three data per sequence. Gettys and
\par
Manley (1968.) reported two experiments in which five levels of frequency
\par
of data and five levels of likelihood ratios were faetorially combined in
\par
100 binomial problems. For each problem the subject was shown the
\par
contents of two urns and the result of a single sampling of one datum.
\par
In this situation with no aggregation required, the rare event effect was
\par
not found. The subjects were sensitive to changes in likelihood ratio but
\par
not to differing event frequencies. The authors argued that the rare event
\par
effect found in other studies is attributable to aggregation difficulties.
\par
A related source of support for the misaggregation hypothesis comes
\par
from the finding that subjects perform best on the first trial of a
\par
sequence. DuCharme and Peterson (1968) reported this finding based on
\par
a task using normal data generators. The subjects were shown samples
\par
of heights and asked the posterior odds that the population being
\par
sampled was of men or women. They were virtually optimal for single-
\par
datum sequences and for the first, trial of four-data sequences, but
\par
conservative on subsequent trials. Similar results were obtained by
\par
Peterson and Swensson (1968).
\par
It might be noted that Peterson and Miller (1965) found conservatism
\par
with just one datum per problem, but this presents no special problem forBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN J U D G M E N T 697
\par
the misaggregation hypothesis, since in that study the one datum had to
\par
be aggregated with a varying value for the prior probability of the
\par
hypothesis. Peterson and Phillips (1966) also found first-trial eonserva-
\par
tism. However, they, like Peterson and Miller, used a probability
\par
response mode. This mode, as will be discussed later, is highly susceptible
\par
to a nonoptimal but simple strategy which produces artifactual results.
\par
DuCharme and Peterson (19.68) and Peterson and Swensson (1968)
\par
avoided this criticism by asking for responses in terms of posterior odds
\par
rather than probabilRies, and found first-trial optimality.
\par
Both the misperception and misaggregation hypotheses received sup-
\par
port in a study by Phillips (1966; also reported in Edwards \emph{et al., }1968).
\par
His subjects misperceived the impact of each datum, and, in addition,
\par
were not consistent with that misperception in a subsequent aggregation
\par
task.
\par
Additional evidence for the misaggregation hypothesis was given by
\par
Hammond, Kelley, Schneider, and Vancini (1967) who found that the log
\par
likelihood ratios inferred from subjects' \emph{P(D/H) }estimates did not
\par
match the log likelihood ratios inferred from these subjects' \emph{P(H/D)}
\par
estimates. Similar results were recently obtained by Grinnell, Keeley, and
\par
Doherty (1970).
\par
Finally, man's difficulties in aggregating data have been demonstrated
\par
in a series of man-machine systems studies. A system where men estimate
\par
\emph{P(D/H) }separately for each datum and the machine combines these into
\par
posterior probabilities via Bayes' theorem has consistently been found
\par
superior to a system where the man, himself, must aggregate the data
\par
into a \emph{P(H/D) }estimate (Edwards, Phillips, Hays, \& Goodman, 1968;
\par
Kaplan \& Newman, 1966; and Schum, Southard, \& Wombolt, 1969).
\par
\emph{Artifact}
\par
The third explanation of conservatism, that conservatism is artifactual,
\par
was originally suggested by Peterson (see Edwards, 1968.). A similar
\par
explanation, called \emph{response bias, }was advanced by DuCharme (1970).
\par
DuCharme hypothesized that subjects are capable--and optimal--when
\par
dealing with responses in the odds range from 1:10 to 10: 1, but are con-
\par
servative when forced, either by the accumulation of many data or by
\par
the occurrence of one enormously diagnostic datum, to go outside that
\par
range. He pointed out that such a response bias would explain many of
\par
the conservatism effects reported in the literature, including increased
\par
conservatism attributed to increasing diagnosticity and the superiority
\par
of first-trial performance. DuCharme tested his hypothesis directly in a
\par
task where subjects had to determine whether observed samples of
\par
heights came from a male or female population. His subjects gave698 SLOVIC AND L I C H T E N S T E I N
\par
sequential posterior odds estimates to sequences varying in length from
\par
one to seven data. The results supported the response bias hypothesis.
\par
First-trial estimates and later-trial estimates in the same probability
\par
range were similarly optimal. Second- and third-trial estimates were more
\par
conservative following a highly diagnostic first datum (Ll\% = 99) than
\par
were estimates to those same data following an undiagnostie first trial
\par
( L R = 1.3). Optimality of response was exhibited within a central range
\par
of posterior odds, while conservatism occurred outside this range.
\par
TASK DETERMINANTS IN BAYESIAN RESEARCH
\par
\emph{The Effects of Response Mode}
\par
\emph{Direct estimation methods. }Direct estimation of posterior probabilities
\par
has several drawbacks. First, the amount of change in \emph{P(H/D) }induced
\par
by a single datum decreases as the probabilities prior to the receipt of
\par
that datum become more extreme. Subjects may have difficulty coping
\par
with this nonlinear relationship between stin'mlus and response. In addi-
\par
tion, there is a potential problem with floor and ceiling effects because of
\par
the boundedness of the probability scale at zero and one. The subject
\par
may be reluctant to give an extreme response early in the sequence, for
\par
fear of "using up" the scale before the last data arrive. If subjects
\par
estimate odds or log odds (odds spaced logarithmically on a scale), the
\par
above dii\textasciitilde eulties are avoided. Phillips and Edwards (1966) compared
\par
these various response modes in a binomial task and found, as expected,
\par
that "odds" and "log odds" responses showed less conservatism than did
\par
estimates of probabilities.
\par
\emph{Indirect methods. }Instead of asking the subject for probabilities, in-
\par
direct methods infer his probabilities from some other response. Sanders
\par
(reported in Edwards, 1966) used bookbag and poker chip situations to
\par
compare a direct response, verbal odds, with two different, indirect re-
\par
sponses, choice among bets and bidding for bets. He found substantial
\par
agreement, as measured by similarity of accuracy ratios, between the
\par
direct, verbal estimates of odds and the estimates inferred from choices
\par
among bets. The bidding mode produced considerably more optimal be-
\par
havior than the other two modes.
\par
Beach and Phillips (1967) compared direct probability estimates with
\par
probabilities inferred from choices among bets, and found high correla-
\par
tions between the two responses. Strong agreement, between probability
\par
estimates and probabilities inferred from bids has also been found in two
\par
studies by Beach and Wise (1969a, 1969b). However, Beach and Olson
\par
(1967) have shown that probabilities inferred from choices among bets
\par
were highly susceptible to the gambler's fallacy (e.g., subjects over-
\par
estimated the probability of a red after four greens were sampled, andBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION- APPROACHES I N J U D G M E N T 699
\par
underestimated it after four reds occurred), while direct estimates of
\par
probabilities were much more optimal. Geller and Pitz (1968) have
\par
explored the use of decision speed, measured without the subject's
\par
knowledge, as an indirect measure of probability in a bookbag and poker
\par
chip task. A high correlation was found between the speed of decision
\par
and the Bayesian probability that the decision was correct. In addition,
\par
relative changes in decision speed approximated optimal changes in
\par
probability more closely than did changes in subjects' confidence
\par
estimates.
\par
\emph{Effects of intermitten\textasciitilde{} responding. }Perhaps the very act of making
\par
repeated responses, once after each datum is presented, affects the final
\par
response of the subject. This hypothesis was tested by Beach and Wise
\par
(1969b), who compared verbal estimates of posterior probabilities made
\par
only at the end of a sequence of three data with estimates made after
\par
each datum. They found satisfactory correspondence between the two
\par
estimate methods. Pitz (1969a), however, did find differences attributable
\par
to repeated responses. When subjects responded after each new datum
\par
and their previous responses were displayed, confidence increased with in-
\par
creased sample size, holding diagnosticity constant. However, when the
\par
responses made after each datum were not continuously displayed, or
\par
when the subject made confidence responses only at the end of the
\par
sequence, such nonoptimal tendencies were not observed. Halpern and
\par
Ulehla (1970), using a signal detection task, also found differences be-
\par
tween repeated responses and a single, final response.
\par
\emph{Nominal vs. probability responses. }Is there any difference between a
\par
nominal response (yes-no; predominantly red-predominantly blue) and a
\par
probability response which is later converted to a nominal response by the
\par
experimenter? Swets and Birdsall (1967), using an auditory detection
\par
task, found that the probability-response data provided a better fit to the
\par
signal detection model than the nominal-response data. Similar results
\par
were found by Ulehla, Canges, and Wackwitz (1967). However, tIalpern
\par
and Ulehla (1970) found exactly the opposite results in a visual dis-
\par
crimination task.
\par
Using a Bayesian task with three hypotheses, Martin and Gettys
\par
(1969) found better performance using a nominal response than using a
\par
probability response. Attaching probabilities to \emph{two }less likely hypothe-
\par
ses as well as to the favored hypothesis was apparently difficult, enough
\par
to degrade subjects' performance.
\par
\emph{The Effects o/Payoffs}
\par
The use of payoffs in probability estimation tasks may have a motiva-
\par
tional effect, persuading the subjects to try harder, and an instructional
\par
effect, helping subjects to understand what the experimenter wants from700 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
them (Winkler \& Murphy, 1968). These effects were explored by Phillips
\par
and Edwards (1966), who used three different payoff schemes and a
\par
control group in a bookbag and poker chip task. The subjects estimated
\par
the posterior probability of each bag for 20 sequences of 20 draws each.
\par
The control group received no payoff but were told which hypothesis was
\par
correct after each sequence. The three payoff groups were paid \emph{v(p)}
\par
points, later converted to money, where p was the subject's estimate for
\par
the correct hypothesis, and v (p) was calculated as follows:
\par
Quadratic: \emph{v(p\textbraceright{} }= 10,000 - 10,000 (1 - p\textbraceright 2.
\par
Logarithmic: v(p) = 10,000 -\textasciitilde - 5,000 logl0p.
\par
Linear: \emph{v(p) }= 10,000p.
\par
The quadratic and log payoffs share the characteristic that the only
\par
way the subject can maximize his expected winnings is by reporting his
\par
true subjective probability (Toda, 1963). For the linear payoff, the
\par
subject should always estimate 1.0 for the more likely hypothesis. The
\par
results indicated that payoffs help to decrease conservatism, but do not
\par
eliminate it. The instructional value of payoffs was reflected in more
\par
learning by the payoff groups than the control groups, and by the lower
\par
between-subject variance for the payoff groups.
\par
These findings were amplified in a study by Sehum, Goldstein, Howell,
\par
and Southard (1967) using a complex multinomial task with six
\par
hypotheses and 4, 8, or 12 data, of varying diagnostieity, in each of 324
\par
sequences. A log payoff group was found to be conservative. A linear
\par
payoff group was not conservative, but their responses were highly
\par
variable: their posterior odds were as likely to be 50 times too great or
\par
too small as they were to be accurate. When the responses were simply
\par
scored as "eorreet," meaning that the true hypothesis received the
\par
largest estimated posterior probability, or "incorreet," differences among
\par
the payoff groups were eliminated.
\par
Pitz and Downing (1967) manipulated payoffs in a binary prediction
\par
task, to test subjects' sensitivity to changes in strategy required by the
\par
optimal model. Subjects were asked to guess which of two specially
\par
constructed dice was being rolled, after five data were presented. Five
\par
different payoff matrices were used. The first matrix was symmetric, in
\par
that rewards and penalties were the same for both dice. The other
\par
matrices were biased. In order to maximize their expected winnings,
\par
subjects should alter their strategies when payoff matrices are biased. For
\par
example, they should guess the less likely die when the reward for being
\par
correct is great and the cost for being wrong is small. The subieets were
\par
highly optimal when using the symmetric matrix. With the biased payoff
\par
matrices, they altered their predictions as a function of varying payoffs,
\par
but they did not change nearly enough; they were unwilling to makeBAYESIAN AND REGRESSIOIN \textasciitilde{} A P P R O A C H E S I N J U D G M E N T 7 0 1
\par
responses which had a smaller probability of being correct, even though,
\par
because of the biased payoffs, these responses would have increased
\par
their expected gains. Pitz and Downing suggested that subjects have a
\par
high utility for making a correct guess. A similar suggestion was made
\par
by Ulehla (1966), who found essentially the same result in a study of
\par
perceptual discrimination of lines tilted left or right. With a symmetric
\par
payoff scheme, subjects closely fit the signal detection model, but biased
\par
payoffs led to insufficient change in strategy.
\par
\emph{The E\#ec\textasciitilde s of Diagnosticity}
\par
One of the simplest ways of varying the diagnosticity of the data in a
\par
probability estimation task is to change the data generator. In a bookbag
\par
and poker chip experiment, the diagnostic impact of a sample of one red
\par
chip is greater when the bag being sampled contains 80 red and 20 green
\par
\emph{vs. 20 }red and 80 green than when the possible contents of the bag are
\par
more similar, say 60 red and 40 green \emph{vs. }40 red and 60 green. In several
\par
experiments (Peterson, DuCharme, \& Edwards, 1968; Peterson \& Miller,
\par
1965; Phillips \& Edwards, 1966; Pitz, Downing, \& Reinhold, 1967; and
\par
Vlek, 1965) diagnosticity has been manipulated in this way and all have
\par
shown greater conservatism with more diagnostic data. Very low levels
\par
of diagnostieity sometimes produce the opposite of conservatism: sub-
\par
jects' responses are more extreme than Bayes' theorem specifies (Peter-
\par
son \& Miller, 1965).
\par
When the data generator is a complex multinomial system, different
\par
samples can differ greatly in total diagnosticity, i.e., in the certainty with
\par
which the sample points to one of several hypotheses. Studies by Martin
\par
and Gettys (1969), Phillips, Hays, and Edwards (1966), and Sehum,
\par
Southard, and Wombolt (1969) all showed that samples of higher overall
\par
diagnosticity lead to greater conservatism. Martin and Gettys found that
\par
their least diagnostic samples produced the same extremeness of response
\par
(opposite of conservatism) as found by Peterson and Miller (19'65) in a
\par
binomial task.
\par
Another way of varying diagnosticity is to vary sample size. In general,
\par
the larger the sample, the more diagnostic it is. Vlek (1965), Pitz, Down-
\par
ing, and Reinhold (1967), and Peterson, DuCharme, and Edwards
\par
(1968), using binomial tasks, and Schum (1966b, also Schum, Southard,
\par
\& Wombolt, 1969), using a multinomial task, have shown that larger
\par
sample sizes yield greater conservatism. Diagnosticity can be held con-
\par
stant across different sample sizes, however. In any symmetric binomial
\par
task, diagnostieity is solely a function of the difference between the
\par
number of occurrences of one type and of the other type. Thus the
\par
occurrence of four reds and two blues in a sample of six chips has the
\par
same diagnosticity as the occurrence of 12 reds and 10 blues in a sample702 Sl\textasciitilde OVlC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
of 22 chips. Studies by Vlek (1965) and Pitz (1967) showed that when
\par
this difference was held constant, the larger sample sizes yielded lower
\par
posterior estimates, hence greater conservatism. However, when Sehum,
\par
Southard, and Wombolt (1969) held diagnosticity constant in a multi-
\par
nomial task, variations in sample length had no effect upon the size of
\par
subjects' final posterior probability estimates. The method used for
\par
holding sample diagnostieity constant as sample size increases differed
\par
in the binomial and multinomial task. In the binomial task, diagnostieity
\par
is held constant by holding constant the difference between red and blue
\par
chips, as the sample size increases. But in the multinomial task used by
\par
Sehum \emph{et al., }the total diagnostieity of a large sample was equated with
\par
that of a small sample by using data in the large sample each of which
\par
was, on the average, less diagnostic than the average datum used in the
\par
small sample. Since data of low diagnostieity have been shown to produce
\par
less conservatism, this may account for the discrepancy between Schum's
\par
findings of no sample-size effect and the finding of large effects by Vlek
\par
(1965) and Pitz (1967).
\par
Sample size and diagnostieity can also be varied by holding the total
\par
number of data constant and varying the number of data presented to the
\par
subject at any one time. Peterson, Schneider, and Miller (1965) presented
\par
subjects with 48 trials of one datum each, with 12 trials of four data each,
\par
with four trials of 12 data each, and with a single trial containing 48 data.
\par
Conservatism was large when subjects responded after each single datum,
\par
but was even larger when the number of data (and hence the average
\par
diagnosticity) per trial increased. Vlek (1965) also found poorer perform-
\par
ante with larger blocks of data presented simultaneously.
\par
All these studies tell the same story: Increased diagnosticity, no matter
\par
how produced, increases conservatism. The sole exception to this state-
\par
ment. is reported by Schum and Martin (1968), who used a multinomial
\par
task-six hypotheses and six data per sample. They used two different
\par
data-generating models, Model A and Model B. Diagnosticity was varied
\par
both within and between the two models. The results for Model A were
\par
typical of diagnosticity studies--subjects were sensitive to changes in
\par
diagnosticity within the model, but as diagnostieity increased, subjects
\par
became increasingly conservative. The results from Model B represented
\par
a unique finding; as diagnosticity increased in Model B samples, the
\par
responses deviated further from Bayes' theorem, but in a nonconservative
\par
direction (i.e., they became increasingly more extreme). This finding is
\par
unexplained by Schum and Martin. One possible explanation is that
\par
subjects completely disregarded the difference between Model A and
\par
Model B, responding solely to the number of items favoring the most
\par
likely hypothesis. The subsequent comparison of such responses with the
\par
optimal responses derived from the two different models would makeBAYESIAN AND REGRESSION APPROACHES IN JUDGiV[ENT 7 0 3
\par
similar responses look conservative in one ease and extreme in the other
\par
c a s e .
\par
\emph{The Effects of Manipulating Prior Probabilities}
\par
A decision makers' beliefs about the state of the world before he
\par
receives relevant information are expressed in the prior probabilities he
\par
attaches to the hypotheses. Prior probabilities may be manipulated in a
\par
bookbag and poker chip experiment, e.g., by showing the subject not two,
\par
but ten bookbags, n of which are predominantly red and ( 1 0 - n) pre-
\par
dominantly blue. When the experimenter chooses one bag at random, the
\par
subject has reason to believe, before the first chip is drawn, that the prior
\par
probability of the bag being predominantly red in \emph{n/lO. }Variation of n
\par
thus constitutes variation of prior probabilities.
\par
When subjects' responses are analyzed in terms of inferred likelihood
\par
ratios or Accuracy Ratios, such measures should not change when prior
\par
probabilities are varied. Phillips and Edwards (1966) and Sehum (1966b)
\par
reported such invariance. However, Peterson and Miller (1965) did find a
\par
systematic relationship between prior probability and Accuracy Ratio
\par
in a binomial task. Across nine levels of prior probability, from .1 to .9,
\par
subjects' Accuracy Ratios increased (the subjects beeame less conserva-
\par
tive) as the priors became more extreme (departed from .5). This finding,
\par
however, may be an artifactual result, of the response mode--probabilities
\par
expressed with a sliding pointer on an equal-interval scale. If subjects
\par
simply moved the slider a constant amount., up for a black datum, down
\par
for a white datum, regardless of its initial setting, the reported relation-
\par
ship between the Accuracy Ratio and prior probabilities would occur.
\par
The one general characteristic of the Bayesian research summarized
\par
so far is that subjects are never as sensitive to the experimental condi-
\par
tions as they ought to be. This statement characterizes conservatism
\par
itself, as well as the effects of payoffs and diagnosticity. Partial sensitivity
\par
to variations in prior probabilities has been found using signal detection
\par
models by Ulehla (1966) and Galanter and tIolman (1967). Wendt
\par
(1969) found partial sensitivity to prior odds. tie asked his subjects to
\par
bid for each datum; this bid was interpreted as the value of the datum
\par
for the subject. Wendt found that the bids were closer to optimal when
\par
the prior odds were 1 : 1 than when the prior odds were extreme.
\par
\emph{The Effects of Sequence Length}
\par
Several studies have found that subjects are more hesitant to commit
\par
themselves fully to a probability revision when they know that there
\par
will be opportunity for additional revision on later trials than when they
\par
know any revision taking place must be made immediately. Vlek (1965)
\par
compared \emph{P(H/D) }estimates made after the ninth trial in a 19-trial704 SLOVIC AND L I C H T E N S T E I N
\par
sequence with estimates made after the simultaneous presentation of nine
\par
data items (no more were to be presented). The probability estimates
\par
were less extreme in the former condition where subjects knew they had
\par
ten additional opportunities for revisions. This effect might be attributed
\par
to the difference between simultaneous \emph{vs. }serial presentation in the
\par
above study. However, Pitz, Downing, and Reinhold (1967) used serial
\par
presentation with responding after each item and found the average
\par
revision of \emph{P(H/D) }to be greater for shorter sequences than for longer
\par
ones. Similarly, Shanteau (1970) found that shorter sequences produced
\par
more extreme \emph{P(H/D) }responses at any serial position, holding the
\par
evidence constant. Although none of the above studies put any pressure
\par
on subjects to make their intermediate responses maximally accurate,
\par
R.oby (196.7) used a payoff system to motivate subjects to be accurate at
\par
every response point and he, too, found that they tended to delay for
\par
several trials before modifying their estimates. These results should be
\par
viewed with caution, however, because each of these studies employed a
\par
response mode highly susceptible to ceiling effects. Replication of these
\par
findings using odds or log odds response modes is needed to discover if
\par
subjects are affected by sequence length even when they feel comfortable
\par
that they cannot "use up" the response scale.
\par
\emph{Primacy and Recency Effects}
\par
Studies investigating sequential use of probabilistie information have
\par
generally required subjects to make judgments after each new datum was
\par
presented. Three of these studies have reported primacy effects (Peterson
\par
\& DuCharme, 1967; Dale, 1968; Roby, 1967) and two have obtained
\par
recency (Pitz \& Reinhold, 1968; Shanteau, 1970). Both primacy and
\par
recency violate the Bayesian model. The occurrence of primacy here
\par
contrasts with the recency effects generally found when subieets make
\par
intermittent judgments upon receipt of verbal or perceptual information.
\par
One possible explanation for this is the fact that studies showing primacy
\par
effects each presented subiects with a long sequence of items of informa-
\par
tion that first pointed strongly to one hypothesis and then suddenly
\par
changed in character so that the less favored hypothesis became at least
\par
as probable as the first. The resulting inconsistency of the latter data is
\par
extremely implausible in a stationary environment, and it is not surpris-
\par
ing that subiects tended to discount those data. Neither of the two studies
\par
obtaining recency effects used such inconsistent, sequences of data.
\par
\emph{An Inertia E)\textasciitilde ec\textasciitilde{} in Bayesian Research}
\par
Anderson (1959) invoked the concept of inertia in discussing the "basal
\par
component" of an opinion--that part which becomes increasingly resist-BAYESIAN AND REGRESSION A P P R O A C H E S IN J U D G M E N T 705
\par
ant to change as information accumulates. More recently Pitz and his
\par
associates have conducted a series of studies demonstrating the existence
\par
of inertia in opinions that are formed and revised on the basis of prob-
\par
abilistie evidence. Pitz, Downing, and Reinhold (1967) found that sub-
\par
jeers revised their \emph{P(H/D) }estimates much less following evidence
\par
contradictory to their currently favored hypotheses than they did after
\par
confirming evidence. Revision should have been equal in either direction.
\par
Especially interesting was the finding that probability estimates some-
\par
times moved towards greater certainty after a single disconfirming datum
\par
was observed. This phenomenon, labeled an "inertia effect," was also
\par
found by Geller and Pitz (1968).
\par
Geller and Pitz investigated two possible explanations of the inertia
\par
effect. The first was that inertia stems from strong commitment to a
\par
hypothesis whereby subjects become unwilling to change their stated
\par
level of confidence even though their opinions might change. This hypoth-
\par
esis was suggested by findings in studies by Gibson and Nichol (1964),
\par
Brody (1965), and Pruitt (1961). Pruitt found that subjects required
\par
more information to change their minds about a previous decision than
\par
to arrive at that decision in the first place. Brody found that initial
\par
commitment to an incorrect decision slowed down the rate of increase in
\par
confidence for the correct choice. Geller and Pitz obtained data indicating
\par
that subjects \textasciitilde{} speed of decision decreased markedly following disconfirm-
\par
ing evidence even though the stated confidence in that decision had not
\par
decreased. They argued that this supported the commitment hypothesis
\par
and also concluded that. stated confidence may not indicate the subject's
\par
true opinions. A second hypothesis tested by Geller and Pitz was that
\par
subjects may expect an occasional disconfirming event to occur when
\par
information is probabilistic. For example, if the task is to determine
\par
whether the samples of marbles are coming from an urn that is 60\% red
\par
and 40\% blue or vice versa and the first nine draws produce six red and
\par
three blue marbles, the drawing of a blue on the next. trial may not be
\par
upsetting to subjects who believe the urn to contain 40\% blue marbles.
\par
When subjects were asked to predict the next event in the sample, Geller
\par
and Pitz found that the inertia effect was greater following predicted
\par
diseonfirming events than nonpredieted diseonfirming events, and this
\par
was taken as support for the second hypothesis.
\par
Further evidence for the commitment hypothesis comes from a study
\par
by Pitz (1967). His subjects stated their confidence in their opinions only
\par
after an entire sample was presented. When confidence was plotted as a
\par
function of increasing sample size, with Bayesian probabilities held con-
\par
stant, mean confidence judgments decreased, rather than increasing as
\par
would be predicted from the inertia effect. This lack of inertia was at-7"06 SLOVIC AND LICHTENSTEIN
\par
tributed to the fact that there was no prior judgment, to which subjects
\par
would have been committed. A later study (Pitz, 1969a) found that when
\par
subjects were not allowed to keep track of their trial-by-trial responses,
\par
inertia was eliminated.
\par
Pitz (1966) had subjects make sequential judgments of the proportion
\par
of particular events in a sample. When subjects' previous iudgments were
\par
displayed to them or could be recalled, their estimates showed a delay in
\par
revision towards .5 that seems analogous to the inertia effect found in
\par
studies of confidence or subjective probability. Here, too, a group whose
\par
previous judgments were not displayed showed no such effect.
