\par
to such functions is either the standard APL call procedures or a table-like function
\par
presentation.
\par
Although \textbackslash DAMS has been implemented largely in APL, the data base management system
\par
resides in a non-APL environment. Currently, two data base management systems are
\par
supported: Extended Relational Management System (XRM) \textbraceleft/974\textbraceright{} or System R \textbraceleft/976\textbraceright. As
\par
with the programs written in PL! I or other non-APL languages, the interface to the data base
\par
is carefully managed so that users can conveniently move back and forth between the two
\par
environments.
\par
To provide the reader with some feeling for using \textbackslash DAMS, a sample terminal session is
\par
included in the Appendix. It shows how a user with little or no knowledge of \textbackslash DAMS might
\par
obtain plots of stock quotations of firms listed on the German Stock Exchange. The displays
\par
shown in the Appendix are taken directly from a Tektronix display device which is used in
\par
conjunction with an IBM 3277 terminal. On the right side of each display is a description of
\par
the activity shown on the left.
\par
\textbackslash DAMS is currently being used in several applications in Germany for actual problem solving.
\par
Logging of usage patterns is built into the system to allow for subsequent evaluation. The
\par
analysis of these usage patterns in real-life applications may help not only to pinpoint
\par
possible future improvements in \textbackslash DAMS but also to improve the general understanding of
\par
human problem solving. The "human factors" are a critical aspect of any decision support
\par
system and it is here that most of the current developmental work in \textbackslash DAMS is being done.
\par
SUMMARY
\par
\textbackslash DAMS is an experimental system; and, as such, it is still undergoing development. Although
\par
designed for use by non-programmers, it is an even more powerful tool in the hands of an APL
\par
programmer. In a way, IDAMS may be viewed as an enhancement to APL in several respects:
\par
{$\bullet$} It provides access to an underlying non-APL data base system (currently XRM, but this
\par
could be replaced by System R without changing the user interface).
\par
{$\bullet$} It provides efficient access to non-APL programs and thus opens new opportunities for
\par
the use of APL for interactive data analysis in combination with existing libraries of
\par
compiled programs.
\par
{$\bullet$} It provides an easy-to-use means of data analysis and management for non-programmers
\par
via its non-procedural language, which is translated into executable APL.
\par
The major differences between \textbackslash DAMS and many other query languages and implementations
\par
are:
\par
{$\bullet$} \textbackslash DAMS supports a data base with a tabular data model, supporting array structures on
\par
the field level; i.e., a table entry may be a scalar, vector, matrix, or even an array of
\par
higher dimensions, whatever is most appropriate.
\par
{$\bullet$} \textbackslash DAMS offers a guidance component which enlarges the facilities of the data dictionary
\par
through descriptively oriented information.
\par
{$\bullet$} The high level query language of \textbackslash DAMS derives its power from the possibility to draw
\par
on the full data manipulation facilities of APL within a query.
\par
{$\bullet$} \textbackslash DAMS allows existing program libraries to be embedded into the system, thus providing
\par
for customization of program libraries as well as of the high level query language.
\par
\textbackslash DAMS is not a perfect DSS generator. Its many features, some of which are quite powerful,
\par
are not all uniformly easy to use. Similarly, although a knowledge of APL is not needed to
\par
use \textbackslash DAMS, there are many occasions when such knowledge is of considerable advantage.
\par
Finally, there are many specific applications which a user may wish to undertake which are
\par
not possible without the direct intervention and assistance of a skilled \textbackslash DAMS systems expert.
\par
196 J. W. Bergquist and E.R. McLean
\par
However, all of these issues are currently being investigated; and, by the end of the 30-month
\par
IBM-UCLA joint study, it is expected that a number of changes and improvements will have
\par
occurred.
