\par
In recent years, researchers and practitioners are paying increasing attention to the phenome-
\par
non of manufacturing strategy. However, there exists no formal theory of manufacturing
\par
strategy to explain the phenomenon. There is a real need for empirical studies for the develop-
\par
ment of such a theory. This study takes a step in that direction by clarifying, organizing and
\par
integrating terms and concepts relevant to manufacturing strategy in the process of conducting
\par
an empirical investigation of key manufacturing strategy variables.
\par
The empirical section of this study based on data gathered from 35 manufacturers found that
\par
environmental uncertainty influenced manufacturing strategy vanables such as Manufacturing
\par
Flexibility, and the Role of Manufacturing Managers in Strategic Decision Making. The man-
\par
ufacturing strategy variables, in tum, influenced business performance.
\par
(DECISION ANALYSIS-SYSTEMS; INVENTORY/PRODUCTION-SURVEYS; PLAN-
\par
NING-CORPORATE)
\par
Background
\par
Strategy denotes actions or patterns of actions intended for the attainment of goals.
\par
In an organizational setting, the term strategy covers more than just intended or
\par
planned strategy; it also includes the "sequence of decisions" that exhibit a "posteriori
\par
consistencies in decisional behavior" (Mintzberg 1978, p. 935). Manufacturing strategy
\par
concerns itself with questions such as, "Should a given manufacturer choose a produc-
\par
tion strategy that emphasizes flexibility, consumer choice, and quality or one that
\par
emphasizes cost. . ." (Buffa 1980, p. 4).
\par
As a working definition, manufacturing strategy is viewed as the effective use of
\par
manufacturing strengths as a competitive weapon for the achievement of business and
\par
corporate goals. Manufacturing strategy reflects the goals and strategies of the business,
\par
and enables the manufacturing function to contribute to the long-term competitiveness
\par
and performance of the business (Skinner 1985; Wheelwright and Hayes 1985).
\par
Manufacturing strategy is part of the widely accepted hierarchy of strategy suggested
\par
by Hofer (1975), and Hofer and Schendel (1978). The hierarchical view of strategy
\par
visualizes at least three levels of strategies. First, within large multi-business corpora-
\par
tions, corporate strategy involves the selection of product markets or industries and the
\par
allocation of resources among them. Second, each business unit within a multi-business
\par
corporation could have its own specially tailored business strategy designed to use its
\par
distinctive competences as competitive weapons. Third, Manufacturing strategy forms
\par
a part of a cluster of functional area strategies such as marketing strategy, financial
\par
strategy, etc., which complement higher level business and corporate strategies.
