\par
The phenomenon of manufacturing strategy is only beginning to be understood. In
\par
the evolution of manufacturing strategy theory, some variables, components, and de-
\par
terminants of manufacturing strategy have been identified by operations management
\par
writers, notably, Skinner (1969, 1978), Wheelwright (1978, 1984), Miller (1981), Buffa
\par
(1984) and Hayes and Wheelwright (1984). However, certain factors of critical impor-
\par
tance to manufacturing strategy have not received sufficient attention in operations
\par
management area literature. For example, environmental uncertainty, whose impor-
\par
tance to strategy is explicitly recognized in the organization theory and business policy
\par
literatures, has received little attention in manufacturing strategy literature.
\par
Objective
\par
Within the broader purpose of developing a partial model of manufacturing strategy,
\par
the thrust of this study is to investigate the impact of environmental uncertainty on
\par
manufacturing strategy content and process, and manufacturing strategy's influence on
\par
business performance. An incidental purpose of the study is to review, classify, clarify,
\par
define, and integrate ideas and concepts from diverse disciplines that are relevant to the
\par
study of manufacturing strategy.
\par
Manufacturing Strategy Literature
\par
At the outset, there is a need to clarify the term "manufacturing strategy," because it
\par
has been used rather loosely to refer to the content as well as the process of manufac-
\par
turing strategy. Manufacturing strategy content refers to the distinctive competences of
\par
the manufacturing function employed in the pursuit of competitive advantage.
\par
In recent manufacturing strategy literature there is a tendency to call manufacturing
\par
strategy content the dimensions of manufacturing strategy. They are (1) cost, (2) qual-
\par
ity, (3) flexibility, and (4) dependability (Buffa 1984; Wheelwright 1984). Each of these
\par
competitive dimensions of manufacturing strategy at the aggregate level is the result of
\par
a number of contributing variables as shown in Figure 1. For example, variables such as
\par
economies of scale, learning, etc. contribute to the cost dimension of manufacturing
\par
strategy. Further, as shown in the figure, the use of techniques such as JIT production
\par
may contribute to more than one dimension of manufacturing strategy.
\par
The other use of the term manufacturing strategy in POM literature refers to the
\par
issues surrounding the formulation and implementation of manufacturing strategy
\par
thereby focusing on the process of developing and implementing manufacturing strat-
\par
egy (Skinner 1969, 1978 and 1985). Skinner's model of manufacturing strategy process
\par
is essentially a top-down view of manufacturing strategy. Other writers have added
\par
support to this view including Miller (1981) and Wheelwright (1978). Key element\$ of
\par
the top-down manufacturing strategy process are (1) the establishment of manufactur-
\par
ing task-a statement of goals and means, (2) alignment of the policies and actions of
\par
the manufacturing infrastructure with the task established earlier, and (3) the involve-
\par
ment of manufacturing managers/executives in the strategic decision process. While the
\par
top-down view of manufacturing strategy is very prevalent, in certain firms, Hayes and
\par
Wheelwright (1984) see a need for an interactive (top-down-bottom-up) development
\par
of manufacturing strategy.
\par
It can be inferred from literature that for the successful practice of manufacturing
\par
strategy, its content as well as its process are important. However, it should be noted
\par
that POM literature does not formally dichotomize manufacturing strategy variables
\par
into content and process variables. But the use of such a dichotomy found in the
\par
business policy and organization theory literature brings greater clarity to the study of
\par
manufacturing strategy.
