\par
Thus far, in the evolution of manufacturing strategy theory (particularly in the area
\par
of manufacturing strategy process), with the exception of the work by Wheelwright
\par
(1978), and Van Dierdonck and Miller (1980), case histories, anecdotal experiences and
\par
conjectural writings have played the central role with very limited contributions from
\par
empirical research. Much empirical analysis is now essential for the continued growth
\par
and development of manufacturing strategy theory.
\par
Manufacturing strategy literature lacks some of the essential ingredients that can
\par
stimulate empirical research. It lacks the benefits of an accumulation of empirical
\par
findings over a period of time. Consequently, although many concepts have been
\par
identified, it lacks the scientific development of these concepts, integration of relevant
\par
literature from several disciplines, operationalization of manufacturing strategy vari-
\par
ables for empirical analyses, empirically validated models of manufacturing strategy,
\par
and empirical findings relating to manufacturing strategy which can sprout new hy-
\par
potheses for investigation.
\par
The Model
\par
From industrial organization (IO), marketing (M), and administrative behavior (AB)
\par
literature it could be inferred that strategy is a major determinant of a business's
\par
performance, and the environment in turn is a determinant of strategy (Jemison 198 la;
\par
White and Hamermesh 1981). Thus there exists a sequential relationship among the
\par
variables, external environment, strategy, and business performance.
\par
In Figure 2, which represents this study's basic premise, this sequential relationship
\par
amongst the variables, Environment, Strategy and Performance is expressed in terms of
\par
the variables Environmental Uncertainty (which is a consequence of the external envi-
\par
ronment), Manufacturing Strategy (which is a subset of corporate and business strate-
\par
gies), and Performance.
\par
Figure 2 shows that manufacturing strategy content as well as process are influenced
\par
by environmental uncertainty. For the purpose of this study, manufacturing strategy
\par
was operationalized through two variables namely, Flexibility (content variable) and
\par
