\par
variables) not explicitly included in the structural (i.e., path analytic) model in Figure 3.
\par
For example, at the node representing the variable Flexibility, the disturbance term D1
\par
accounts for the effects of business strategy, corporate strategy, product and process
\par
technologies, etc. Thus all variations in the variable Flexibility not accounted for by the
\par
variable Environmental Uncertainty will be assigned to the disturbance term by the
\par
model. This enables researchers using path analysis to study the effects of selected
\par
variables on other variables of interest to them.
\par
Therefore, the path analytic model in Figure 3 does not deny the existence; or the
\par
effects of variables such as competitive context, competitive imperatives, organiza-
\par
tional context, industry, process, size, structure, technology, etc., which may be relevant
\par
to manufacturing strategy and performance, but not included in the model. The effect
\par
of these variables, if any, are captured by the three disturbance terms mentioned earlier.
\par
In the structural model in Figure 3, assumptions of causal order deduced from literature
\par
are represented by arrows. The absence of arrows between pairs of variables signifies
\par
that there is insufficient reason to hypothesize a relationship between the variables.
\par
Path analysis offers some distinct advantages including the identification of direct as
\par
well as indirect effects in a complex system of variables, and the convenience with
\par
which intervening variables could be included in the model. Path analysis uses ordinary
\par
