\par
least squares regression to test proposed ". . . causal relationships among a set of
\par
variables" (Billings and Wroten 1978). In adopting the path analytic approach, two
\par
injunctions by methodologists were kept in mind. The first injunction was to include in
\par
the model only those variables and relationships which have theoretical or logical
\par
support (Asher 1981; Heise 1975), and the second was to keep the number of variables
\par
included in the model to a minimum (Young, 1977).
\par
The variables and relationships included in the structural model in Figure 3 have
\par
varying levels of support in the literature which is reviewed in the following paragraphs.
\par
Environmental Uncertainty
\par
In the AB area literature, external environment of organizations is viewed as the
\par
source of events and changing trends which "create opportunities and threats for indi-
\par
vidual firms" (Lenz 1980). At least two opposing perspectives on the external environ-
\par
ments of organizations can be discovered in literature.
\par
One perspective which is characterized as the information perspective considers the
\par
"impact of uncertainty on the ability of organizational participants to make decisions,
\par
. l. ." (Aldrich 1979, p. 110). The opposing perspective, which is characterized as the
\par
resource perspective, focuses on the dependency of organizations on environments for
\par
vital resources (Pfeffer and Salancik 1977). In a recent work, Lawrence and Dyer (1983)
\par
proposed that the concept of adaptation suggested by them could bridge the long-
\par
standing differences between the two perspectives. They defined adaptation as the
\par
"process by which organization and its environment reach and maintain an equilib-
\par
rium ensuring the survival of the system as a whole."
\par
In the context of information and resource dependency views of organization-envi-
\par
ronment relationship, there can be two kinds of environmental uncertainty; one that is
\par
based on resource scarcity and the other based on information complexity (Lawrence
\par
and Dyer 1983, p. 300).
\par
Environment and Strategy
\par
The adaptive view of organizations found in literature emphasizes the need to adapt
\par
to external forces to maintain organizational viability (Emery and Trist 1965; Lawrence
\par
and Lorsch 1969; Terreberry 1968; Thomson 1967). Strategic decision making is a
\par
vehicle for adapting to the environment.
\par
Unfortunately, POM area literature pays little attention to uncertainty-manufactur-
\par
ing strategy connection. The work of Van Dierdonck and Miller (1980) is a lone
\par
example of an empirical study in the POM area which investigated the influence of
\par
environmental factors on the design of production planning and control systems. While
\par
the use of stochastic, chance constrained, or other models that acknowledge uncertain-
\par
ties are prevalent in POM research, research on the strategic importance of environ-
\par
mental uncertainty is wanting.
\par
Measuring Environmental Uncertainty
\par
There exists an on-going debate on the nature of the appropriate measure for envi-
\par
ronmental uncertainty. The debate concerns the adequacy of perceptual vs. objective
\par
measures for environmental uncertainty. Several researchers including Downey and
\par
Slocum (1975), Miles et al. (1974), and Tosi, Aldag and Storey (1973) emphasized that
\par
only through managerial perception does environmental uncertainty become known
\par
to the organization. As a natural consequence, perceptual measures for environmental
\par
uncertainty found support among these researchers. Some studies (e.g., Lawrence and
\par
Lorsch 1969; Duncan 1972) depended entirely on perceptual data in measuring envi-
\par
ronmental uncertainty. The perceptual measure of environmental uncertainty used in
\par
this study is described later.
