Manufacturing Flexibility
\par
Manufacturing flexibility is a new and emerging theme in POM literature (Buffa
\par
1984; Schonberger 1982; Wheelwright 1984). Japanese manufacturers deserve credit
\par
for exploring new avenues for achieving it as well as exploiting it for purposes of
\par
competitive advantage. Manufacturing flexibility is attained and maintained by several
\par
coordinated policies and actions. While discussing Japanese manufacturing systems,
\par
Schonberger provided a description of some of the ingredients of manufacturing flexi-
\par
bility. He said:
\par
Western production lines are unlikely to be very flexible because they are planned "off-line" by
\par
specialists using cumbersome analyses, because of labor and equipment inflexibility, because of
\par
line shape, because of quality and inventory buffer policies, and because of basic strategies. .
\par
Japanese strategies require flexibility, and get it, by means of procedures and policies that are
\par
rather opposite to western tendencies. (pp. 133-134)
\par
In the above quote, although Schonberger addressed production lines, the idea that
\par
flexibility is achieved through the use of several policies and activities is true regardless
\par
of whether the production is on lines or not.
\par
Hall's (1983) definition of flexibility recognizes the value of flexibility in adapting to
\par
changes. He observed, "Flexibility means that plants should be capable of switching
\par
very quickly from one product to another, or from one part to another . . . almost
\par
instantly" (p. 2). Therefore, it should not be difficult to see the value of flexibility under
\par
conditions of environmental uncertainty. Of the four dimensions of manufacturing
\par
strategy discussed earlier, flexibility offers the capability to cope with environmental
\par
uncertainty. In this context, coping with environmental uncertainty means that perfor-
\par
mance is unaffected by environmental uncertainty.
\par
Since manufacturing flexibility permits a manufacturing firm to cope with environ-
\par
mental uncertainty, manufacturing flexibility is treated as an important representative
\par
of manufacturing strategy content in the model. The foregoing discussions on the
\par
relationships among the variables Environmental Uncertainty, Flexibility, and Perfor-
\par
mance are reflected in the relationships shown in Figure 3.
\par
In this study, the degree of flexibility as expressed by the goals of the unit for the
\par
preceding two years was used as the measure of flexibility. The instrument used in this
\par
study to measure flexibility is described later.
\par
Role of Manufacturing Managers in Strategic Decision Making (RMMSDM)
\par
An item frequently noted by researchers studying manufacturing strategy is the
\par
importance, yet neglect of the Role of Manufacturing Managers in Strategic Decision
\par
Making (Rafii 1984a, b; Rafii and Miller 1983; Skinner 1978; and Wheelwright 1978).
\par
The RMMSDM is a variable that is critical to the formulation and implementation of
\par
manufacturing strategy, i.e., it is important to manufacturing strategy process.
\par
Rafii (1984), in a study using the Boston University's Manufacturing Futures Re-
\par
search Project data, reported "some evidence of the negative consequences of not
\par
integrating the manufacturing function in strategic planning." Similar findings from
\par
case studies were reported by Skinner (1978) and Wheelwright (1978). These reports are
\par
the basis for the link hypothesized in the model between the variables RMMSDM and
\par
Business Performance.
\par
The RMMSDM is a function of numerous variables such as size, structure, intra-or-
\par
ganizational power and influence, environmental factors, the strategic planning system,
\par
top management coalition, resource and information constraints, formalization of
\par
strategic planning, etc. In literature, all these variables are categorized as either organi-
\par
zational context (internal), or environmental context (external).
\par
After simultaneously investigating internal and environmental sources of influence
