\par
on strategic decision making, Jemison (1981b) concluded that "departments that are
\par
responsible for dealing with the environment are more influential in strategic decision
\par
making" (p. 86). Thus marketing executives may be better suited to cope with certain
\par
strategic contingencies than other executives (Hambrick 1981). The foregoing discus-
\par
sion is the basis for the hypothesized relationship and its direction between Environ-
\par
mental Uncertainty and RMMSDM in the model in Figure 3. While it is evident from
\par
the above discussions that RMMSDM is a function of organizational as well as environ-
\par
mental contexts, this study concerns itself with the effect of the external environment
\par
alone on the variable RMMSDM. The instrument used to measure RMMSDM is
\par
discussed later.
\par
Business Performance
\par
Numerous variables influence a business unit's performance; however, the thesis of
\par
this paper is that manufacturing strategy could make a measurable impact on the
\par
performance of a business. The variations in performance due to variables not included
\par
in the model are accounted for by the disturbance term D3 in Figure 3.
\par
While the inclusion of performance in strategy related studies is desirable, the mea-
\par
surement of performance in empirical studies is a thorny issue. Consider the statement
\par
by Bourgeois (1980) who described the difficulty in selecting performance measures
\par
thus, ". . . the adoption of any particular set of indicators embroils the researcher in
\par
the quagmire of problems of quantification and dimensionality, not to mention the
\par
issue of validly choosing the set of indicators which meets universal acceptance .
\par
(p. 235).
\par
The appropriateness of the performance measure to use may depend on the circum-
\par
stances unique to the study. Growth is one frequently used measure of performance
\par
(Dess and Beard 1984). In this study, growth was the favored measure of performance.
\par
However, instead of using growth in a single aspect of performance, growth in three
\par
major aspects of performance were aggregated into a composite measure of growth:
\par
growth in return on assets, growth in sales, and growth in return on sales.
\par
The use of growth as the measure of performance had a special appeal for this study.
\par
In the years preceding the summer/fall 1982, when data were collected for this study,
\par
the industries represented in this study were faced with severe recession as well as
\par
increasing competition from abroad. Under these circumstances, growth provided a
\par
more rigorous test of performance than perhaps any other measure of performance that
\par
could have been devised.
\par
Return on assets was not used as the measure of performance in this study due to the
\par
following reason. The industries covered by this study were generally very mature.
\par
Many firms that participated in this study tended to delay new investment in their
\par
facilities and preferred to operate with a very small asset base. Thus given the unequal
\par
pace of modernization among the participants, if a ROA measure were to be used, firms
\par
with smaller asset bases may erroneously appear to be performing better than those
\par
with relatively modern facilities and larger fixed assets.
\par
The access to accurate financial and other performance data often pose real chal-
\par
lenges to the empiricist. The firms participating in this study were mostly privately-held
\par
and the rest were business units of multi-business, publicly-held firms. Most of the
\par
participants of this study politely declined to furnish objective performance data re-
\par
quested in the questionnaire.
\par
Although objective performance measures are preferable to perceived measures of
\par
performance, the latter have been used and recommended as a substitute when objec-
\par
tive measures are unavailable (Dess and Robinson, Jr. 1984; Venketraman and Ra-
\par
manujam 1985). The heads or equivalents (hereafter referred to as chief executives) of
\par
the business units participating in this study were asked to provide perceptual informa-
