\par
Method
\par
Sample
\par
The data for the empirical investigation of the model were obtained through a field
\par
study. Data were collected from participating firms predominantly via face-to-face
\par
interviews with chief executives or equivalents (General Managers, Plant Managers or
\par
Vice-Presidents) and executives/managers in-charge of the manufacturing function.
\par
The questionnaires used in the interviews were so structured that they permitted a mail
\par
survey when interviews were not possible.
\par
Careful selection of the sample was necessary to reduce the influence of suspected
\par
contaminants. Since process type and industry are two variables which could pro-
\par
foundly influence manufacturing strategy, these two variables were treated as potential
\par
contaminants whose effects had to be controlled for the success of the study. The
\par
following paragraphs describe the controls instituted in this study.
\par
Broadly, processes used in the manufacture of discrete (as opposed to homogeneous
\par
commodity) products could be categorized into one of the following: (1) unit or small
\par
batch, (2) large batch, (3) line production, or (4) mass production. The dominant
\par
process used by a manufacturer is a function of the output, product varieties, stability of
\par
demand, etc. Therefore, manufacturing process could have profound influence on the
\par
manufacturing strategy of the firm. Consequently, to eliminate any unwanted influence
\par
of the process on the manufacturing strategy of the firms studied, it was decided to
\par
control for the manufacturing process variable by limiting the study to a single
\par
process type.
\par
Further, in order to eliminate, or reduce the influence of industry type, the sample of
\par
firms for this study was drawn from a similar group of industries. Once it was decided
\par
that the sample would be controlled for process type and industry, the next step in-
\par
volved the choice of a specific process type and industry for the study. Since face-to-face
\par
interviews were planned with the executives of the participating firms, the sample of
\par
firms for this study was chosen from the region in the proximity of Seattle where this
\par
research was conducted.
\par
At the outset, to permit statistical analyses on the empirical data, a target of at least 30
\par
participating firms was established. In order to attract the targeted number of partici-
\par
pants from the region, special attention was paid to the potential population of firms
\par
from which the sample was to be obtained. The machinery and machine tools industry
\par
offered one of the largest population of manufacturers in the region with at least 50
\par
employees per manufacturing unit. This industry group represented by a selected group
\par
of 15 four-digit SIC categories provided the necessary control for industry type. Further,
\par
this industry group, which predominantly used small batch production, offered the
\par
necessary control for process type as well.
\par
Seventy-seven firms fulfilling the preconditions, mostly from the Pacific Northwest,
\par
were invited to participate in the study during the summer of 1982. Among the 35
\par
responding firms, 27 firms participated in face-to-face interviews, and 8 firms re-
\par
sponded to a mail survey. The average manufacturing unit participating in the study
\par
had an employment of 210 during 1982.
\par
Instruments and Measures
\par
Interviews for gathering data were conducted using a pair of questionnaires-one was
\par
administered to chief executives and the other to heads of the manufacturing function.
\par
The questionnaires included multi-item instruments to measure the four variables
\par
