\par
included in the model. The procedure for selecting the items included in the measures
\par
for each of the four variables is described later. Appendix I contains the list of items
\par
ultimately selected for inclusion in each of the four measures.
\par
Chief executives rated, (1) on a 13-item instrument using a five-point scale, the
\par
perceived uncertainty in the environment (environmental uncertainty items were rated
\par
between extremes of always predictable-never predictable), (2) on a five-point scale,
\par
the importance of each of the 13 items in the uncertainty instrument, (3) on a three-
\par
item instrument using a ten-point scale, and performance of their firms (three measures
\par
of growth relative to industry average over the preceding five years), (4) on a four-item
\par
instrument using a five-point scale, the involvement of manufacturing managers in
\par
strategic decision making, and (5) on a eleven-item instrument using a ten-point scale,
\par
the goals of the business unit during the preceding two years (the measure of flexibility).
\par
The instrument used to measure environmental uncertainty was a modified version
\par
of the one used by Duncan (1972) and Bourgeois (1978). Ratings assigned by the chief
\par
executives to the items in the uncertainty instrument were weighted by the average
\par
importance (across all participants) assigned to the respective item in a separate
\par
instrument.
\par
To assess flexibility, the respondents were asked to rate on a eleven-item instrument
\par
their business unit's goals relevant to flexibility over the previous two years. In measur-
\par
ing flexibility, in addition to the chief executives, manufacturing managers/executives
\par
were also asked to express their perception of their business unit's goals on an instru-
\par
ment identical to that administered to chief executives. For every firm participating in
\par
the study, a single measure of flexibility was computed by averaging the ratings by the
\par
two executives.
\par
Churchill's (1979) recommendations for developing reliable measures from multi-
\par
item instruments were followed in developing measures for the variables in the study.
\par
According to this procedure, items with low inter-item correlation were dropped from
\par
the instruments. The subprogram "reliability" in the SPSS software package was used
\par
to identify items of low inter-item correlation. It should be noted that the exclusion of
\par
an item from an instrument meant that the item was not relevant to the sample of firms
\par
considered, but it may be a useful part of the measure while studying a different sample
\par
of firms. For each variable, item scores were summed and averaged to compute the
\par
measure.
\par
The advantage of Churchill's procedure is that. it improves the reliability of the
\par
instruments usually assessed by the reliability index, Cronbach's Coefficient Alpha.
\par
Coefficient Alpha is an estimate -of the homogeneity of the measure and assesses the
\par
"internal consistency of items" included in an instrument (Peter 1979). The Cron-
\par
bach's Alphas for the four measures ranged from 0.69 to 0.88. The alpha values were
\par
remarkably high for new instruments measuring complex constructs (Churchill 1979).
\par
Analysis
\par
The initial step in the analysis applied the Simon (1954)-Blalock (1964) technique to
\par
test for the existence of linkages not included in the recursive model in Figure 3 by
\par
computing the zero-order correlations between pairs of variables hypothesized to be
\par
unrelated (Asher 1976).
\par
On applying the Simon-Blalock technique, the model in Figure 3 was not eliminated
\par
from further consideration because the zero-order correlations, between pairs of vari-
\par
ables hypothesized to be unrelated, were near zero (Table 1).
\par
The next step involved the use of path analysis to empirically ascertain the direction
\par
and magnitude of causal relationships between variables hypothesized to be related.
\par
This step used regression analysis of standardized antecedent and dependent variables
