The findings in Figure 4 indicate that the higher the flexibility, the better the perfor-
\par
mance. The fact that the high-low dimension of Manufacturing Flexibility correlated
\par
significantly with both Environmental Uncertainty and Performance in the directions
\par
hypothesized adds validity to the flexibility measure used in this study.
\par
Figure 4 shows that the Role of Manufacturing Managers in Strategic Decision
\par
Making (RMMSDM) tends to decrease with increasing environmental uncertainty.
\par
Further, the findings in Figure 4 show that RMMSDM is positively related to perfor-
\par
mance. Thus reduced involvement of manufacturing managers in the strategic decision
\par
making process would have an adverse effect on performance and vice versa.
\par
Based on the square of the multiple correlation coefficients (R2) reported in Figure 4,
\par
about ten percent of the variations in the variables Flexibility and RMMSDM are
\par
explained by environmental uncertainty. In an ex post facto study, such as this, the
\par
explanation of about ten percent of the variations in the dependent variables Flexibility
\par
and RMMSDM by the independent variable Environmental Uncertainty is not a trivial
\par
contribution. It is just about what might be reasonably expected given the several
\par
factors besides environmental uncertainty that influence the said dependent variables.
\par
Again based on the R2, 36 percent of the variations in performance are explained by
\par
the two manufacturing strategy variables in the model. This is an important finding of
\par
this study. The magnitude of the explanation far exceeded the expectations of the
\par
researchers.
