Conclusions
\par
This study faced the same dilemma common to most ex post facto studies. A mini
\par
mum requirement for the success of such studies is the control of key extraneous
\par
variables which may contaminate the relationships being studied. Herein lies the di-
\par
lemma; while key variables are controlled (i.e., not allowed to vary) to filter-out their
\par
effects on the dependent variables, such controls reduce the generalizability of the
\par
findings.
\par
In this study, by controlling for industry and process variables, the effects of these two
\par
variables on the dependent variables of the study were filtered-out. The implications of
\par
this is that the generalizability of this study in the purest sense may be limited to the
\par
industries and process covered with some room for cautious generalizations. In the
