\par
findings reported in literature to detect emerging patterns of generalizable nature.
\par
The sample of 35 firms, although not very large, yielded statistically significant
\par
results. Statistical significance of the findings offered a degree of protection against
\par
spurious and unreliable findings. There were three major conclusions resulting from
\par
this study.
\par
The first conclusion concerns manufacturing flexibility. The finding that the greater
\par
the flexibility the better the performance confirms reasonable intuitive expectations
\par
and the literature on flexibility and uncertainty. Although, this finding seems to con-
\par
firm the obvious, the obviousness of the finding should not diminish its importance. In
\par
the light of the fact that U.S. manufacturers are belatedly recognizing the competitive
\par
value of flexibility from their counterparts in Japan (Abeggelen and Stalk 1985; Schon-
\par
berger 1982), it appears that sometimes we need to "find" the obvious before its
\par
importance is recognized.
\par
There is a special significance to flexibility related findings of this study which was
\par
based on a sample of firms using the small batch manufacturing process. It adds to
\par
reports that Japanese manufacturers gained significant competitive advantage by de-
\par
signing their repetitive production lines for more flexibility than U.S. manufacturers
\par
(Schonberger 1982). Thus, flexibility seems to contribute competitive advantage to a
\par
manufacturer regardless of the manufacturing process used.
