\par
The second conclusion of this study concerns the Role of Manufacturing Managers
\par
in Strategic Decision Making (RMMSDM). Although arguable, the findings concerning
\par
RMMSDM are more generalizable than the findings concerning flexibility because the
\par
former is less likely to be a function of the process, industry, and economic conditions
\par
unique to the sample.
\par
This study found that the RMMSDM was a function of Environmental Uncertainty,
\par
and the higher the RMMSDM the better the performance. These findings, at least
\par
partially, parallel the findings reported by Rafii (1984a, b). Further, the positive rela-
\par
tionship found between the variable RMMSDM and performance is at least a partial
\par
response to the question raised by Jemison (1981b) ". . . how does the distribution of
\par
internal power effect the performance of the organization" (p. 87).
\par
Several POM area researchers have identified the problem of the inadequate involve-
\par
ment of manufacturing managers in strategic decision making. However, solutions to
\par
the problem remain anecdotal. This study offers an explanation for the problem, which
\par
may lead us to a solution. The findings showed that there was a tendency to reduce the
\par
RMMSDM with increasing Environmental Uncertainty. Taken together with another
\par
finding of this study that the higher the RMMSDM, the better the performance, a
\par
prescriptive conclusion from this study is: regardless of environmental conditions,
\par
efforts should be made to maintain the involvement of manufacturing managers in
\par
strategic decision making.
\par
The third conclusion from this study concerns the role of environmental uncertainty
\par
in manufacturing strategy. A prescriptive conclusion based on this study's findings
\par
concerning environmental uncertainty is: an organization may find at least some help
\par
in coping with the high uncertainties imposed by the environment by increasing man-
\par
ufacturing flexibility and by maintaining or ensuring the role of manufacturing man-
\par
agers in strategic decision making.
\par
The contributions of this study may fall into two categories, the specific and the
\par
general. At the specific level, this paper draws attention to the importance of studying
\par
environmental uncertainty, manufacturing flexibility, and RMMSDM to manufactur-
\par
ing strategy research and practice. Further, it has used proven investigative methods in
\par
the study of a relatively new area.
\par
At the general level, this study contributes to the development of a manufacturing
\par
strategy theory by adding to the existing body of literature on the subject. Additionally,
\par
it takes an important first step in the direction of the empirical development of such a
\par
theory. Finally, the integration of literature from several related fields, and the classifi-
\par
cation and organization of terms and concepts relevant to the study of manufacturing
\par
strategy sets the stage for more future work.'
