  publisher = {Wiley Online Library},
  doi = {10.1111/j.1540-5915.1982.tb01176.x},
  langid = {english},
  note = {\textbf{State of the Art}
\par
\textbf{RESEARCH DIRECTIONS IN}
\par
\textbf{DECISION MAKING UNDER UNCERTAINTY*}
\par
Robert L. Winkler, \textbf{\emph{Indiana }}University
\par
\textbf{ABSTRACT}
\par
This paper considers the modeling of decision-making problems under uncertainty.
\par
indicating current gaps in knowledge and promising research directions. Technical details
\par
are omitted, and no attempt is made to review past work since excellent recent reviews are
\par
available. The discussion of research topics is divided into four categories: model formula-
\par
tion, modeling uncertainty, modeling preferences, and modeling competitive and group
\par
decisions. The primary focus is normative, although descriptive work that can \textbf{be }valuable
\par
from a normative perspective is included. The suggested research directions cover the en-
\par
tire spectrum from theory \textbf{to }application.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Subject Areas: Dtckion Analysis, Statistical Dec\&ion Theory, and Game Theory.}}
\par
\textbf{INTRODUCTION}
\par
The foundations for the modeling of decision-making problems under uncer-
\par
tainty date back over two centuries to, for example, Bernoulli and Bayes. Subse-
\par
quently, some very important contributions in this century prior to the late \textbf{1950s}
\par
were made by Ramsey, de Finetti, von Neumann and Morgenstern, Wald, Ar-
\par
row, Blackwell and Girshick, Savage, and others. It is primarily in the past two
\par
decades, however, that increasing interest in decision making under uncertainty
\par
(DMUU) has effected \textbf{a }rapid growth of this area and led to the development of a
\par
sound structure built upon these early foundations. Indeed, the area is currently
\par
enjoying a fair degree of popularity as evidenced by measures such as the number
\par
of actual applications; the number of articles appearing in professional journals;
\par
the existence of sessions at major professional meetings, conferences, and sym-
\par
posia; the increasing willingness of granting agencies to support research; the
\par
presence on editorial boards of individuals specializing in the area; and the devel-
\par
opment of courses (and textbooks for such courses). Moreover, although much
\par
of the \textbf{work }in \textbf{DMUU }has been theoretical or methodological in nature, interest
\par
in applications, running the gamut from various managerial problems \textbf{\emph{to }}impor-
\par
tant public policy decisions, has increased considerably in the past decade. Cur-
\par
rent work in DMUU represents a healthy mix of \textbf{theoretical/methodological }work
\par
and applications-oriented work.
