An important feature of \textbf{DMUU }is its strongly interdisciplinary nature,
\par
which requires it to draw heavily upon mathematics, statistics, economics, psy-
\par
chology, operations research, management science, philosophy, engineering,
\par
computer science, and various areas of application. This interdisciplinary nature
\par
has important implications for the sort of graduate training that might be useful
\par
for students who hope to work in the area in the future. When asked to label
\par
themselves, some \textbf{DMUU }scholars might call themselves statisticians, others
\par
might claim to be economists, and so on. In fact, many researchers do concen-
\par
trate on the interface of decision making under uncertainty with a single field
\par
such as statistics, economics, or psychology. Others, although they may have
\par
been trained primarily in one of these fields, are interested in many different
\par
aspects of decision making under uncertainty and read and contribute to various
\par
types of journals, On the one hand, then, keeping up with the area requires an in-
\par
dividual to look at many different journals from different fields. Obtaining re-
\par
search support, on the other hand, often requires an individual to relate proposed
\par
work to the ``mainstream'' of a particular field. Granting agencies tend to be or-
\par
ganized along traditional lines with programs in fields such as psychology and
\par
mathematics but not specifically in decision making under uncertainty.
\par
The purpose of this paper is to indicate current gaps in knowledge and hence
\par
future research needs in the modeling of problems of decision making under un-
\par
certainty. The area of ``decision making under uncertainty'' is often called deci-
\par
sion theory, decision analysis, or statistical decision theory. These labels have dif-
\par
ferent connotations to different people. Thus, although (or perhaps because) the
\par
label in vogue at the moment appears to be decision analysis and I tend not to
\par
favor acronyms, the relatively neutral \textbf{``DMUU'' }will be used in this paper.
\par
There are many interesting and important directions that research in \textbf{DMUU}
\par
might take. To keep the paper's length within tolerable bounds, I will provide
\par
only brief, general discussions of promising research topics while omitting techni-
\par
cal details and resisting the temptation to review and critique past work, including
\par
my own. \textbf{As }the Editor has \textbf{so }perceptively pointed out, this will be a refreshing
\par
change for both me and the journal.
\par
The absence of a detailed bibliography and a plethora of citations should not
\par
be taken as an indication that \textbf{a }topic has been ignored in the past. Indeed, work
\par
has been and is being conducted on most if not all of the topics mentioned in the
\par
Paper. Keeney \textbf{I31 }presents a thorough look at the current state of the field in deci-
\par
sion analysis; Fishburn [1 \textbf{J }gives an extensive review with an orientation toward
\par
theoretical aspects; and Hogarth \textbf{[2) }provides a wide-ranging discussion with a be-
\par
havioral orientation. These three references are very recent, are well written, are
\par
not at all parochial in outlook (within their respective orientations), and provide
\par
numerous references. Taken together, they present an excellent picture of past de-
\par
velopments and of the current state of the art in \textbf{DMUU.}
\par
In preparing this paper, I have benefitted from discussions with and com-
\par
ments from other researchers in the area, Nonetheless, a paper of this nature in-
\par
evitably reflects the prejudices of its author. The listing of topics provided here is
\par
not exhaustive by any means. Others might include additional topics, omit some
\par
of those mentioned here, place emphasis differently, and \textbf{so }on.
