\par
useful information about the model-building process and how it might be im-
\par
proved. Also, descriptive studies of actual decision-making behavior without
\par
modeling can provide better knowledge of how individuals (or groups) actually
\par
do make decisions, and this knowledge can in turn help provide recommenda-
\par
tions about what they could do to improve their decisions and the role that
\par
models could play in such improvements. Key issues are how much improvement
\par
is likely to be attributable to \textbf{DMUU }models and where the gains are likely to be.
\par
Related methodological work might concentrate on the development of evalua-
\par
tion measures and the investigation of the characteristics of these measures.
\par
From a practical viewpoint, the development of more sophisticated decision
\par
aids to assist unsophisticated decision makers in applying decision analysis would
\par
be valuable. One promising possibility involves the use of convenient, user-
\par
oriented computer software for modeling decision-making problems under uncer-
\par
tainty, preferably in an interactive mode. Various sensitivity checks could be built
\par
into the software, and the direct interface with the computer makes it possible to
\par
consider diagnostic questions concerning factors that might be considered for in-
\par
clusion in the model and flexible parametric representations of uncertainty and
\par
preferences that would be too difficult to deal with otherwise.
\par
Decision aids designed for specific classes of decision-making problems
\par
could be even more detailed than decision aids intended for general use. Generic
\par
structuring also may yield valuable information about the limitations of various
\par
simple rules of thumb that are used (or might be considered) for some types of
\par
problems. Studying specific classes of problems should provide useful insights
\par
into the problems themselves as well as contributions \textbf{to DMUU }in general. \textbf{No }at-
\par
tempt will be made to provide a detailed list of classes of problems here, but some
\par
examples involve bidding, trading, incentives. diagnosis, forecasting, informa-
\par
tion acquisition, resource allocation, investment, bargaining, siting, standard set-
\par
ting, and regulation.
\par
Before suggested research needs in model formulation are listed to sum-
\par
marize the preceding discussion, a brief reminder of where we now stand might
\par
help to place the suggestions in perspective. We have a solid axiomatic framework
\par
for \textbf{DMUU }modeling, and with this framework we can formulate and analyze a
\par
wide variety of problems. \textbf{DMUU }models are used in practice, and the process of
\par
model formulation seems to help decision makers to understand and think about
\par
their problems. Given this background, work in the following directions, which
\par
arc not listed in any particular order and are not mutually exclusive, seems espe-
\par
cially promising:
\par
\textbf{\emph{1 . }}the role of the creative process in \textbf{DMUU }modeling,
\par
\textbf{2. }the selection of the appropriate level of detail for a model and the handling
\par
of factors not included in the model,
\par
3. \textbf{4. }the evaluation of decisions (with or without models),
\par
the development of decision aids.
\par
\textbf{Modeling Uncertainty}
\par
During the model-formulation stage, events or variables of interest for a
\par
decision-making problem are identified. The modeling of uncertainty refers to
\par
15405915, 1982, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1540-5915.1982.tb01176.x by Cory Whitney - Universit\"ats-U Landesbibliothek Bonn , Wiley Online Library on [03/05/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License\textbf{522 }Decision Sciences [Vol. \textbf{13}
\par
the quantification of uncertainty in terms of probabilities based on all available
\par
information about those events or variables. Considered separately from the un-
\par
derlying decision problem, modeling uncertainty is an inferential problem. When
\par
past data are available, methods of probability and statistics may be useful in re-
\par
lating the past data to the future uncertainties. However, much to the dismay of
\par
those hoping for ``objective'' models free from contamination by humans, the in-
\par
formation often is largely in the form of subjective judgments of the decision
\par
maker or of an expert consulted by the decision maker. Thus, methods for quan-
\par
tifying such judgments (often called methods of probability assessment or elicita-
\par
tion) and combining them with other information are crucial in \textbf{DMUU.}
\par
In the area of probability assessment, various assessment procedures have
\par
been developed, studied experimentally, and used in actual decision-making
\par
problems. Multivariate probability assessment deserves more attention, with em-
\par
phasis on dependence among variables. Even when only one event or variable is
\par
of interest, decomposition of the problem to help the assessor think about the un-
\par
certainty involved may create a multivariate assessment situation. In cases where
\par
it is very difficult to assess probabilities for the events or variables of interest but
\par
much easier to assess probabilities for other events or variables, the decision
\par
maker could assess the latter probabilities and then make inferences about the
\par
former probabilities, For example, predictive distributions for the dependent var-
\par
iable in a regression analysis can be used to make inferences about probability
\par
distributions for the coefficients of the regression equation (and even about the
\par
form of the regression equation), The predictive distributions are much easier to
\par
understand intuitively and hence to assess than are the distributions of the coeffi-
\par
cients. What are some other situations in which this indirect assessment can be
\par
beneficial? The study of cognitive processes involved in the use of information to
\par
form opinions should provide some insights useful in the development of assess-
\par
ment procedures.
\par
The assessment of probabilities for rare events is of concern because events
\par
with small probabilities can be very important (e.g., consider the probability of a
\par
serious accident at a nuclear power plant) and past data are frequently not avail-
\par
able. It is difficult for individuals to think about small probabilities, and the
\par
study of models of the assessment process may provide improved procedures that
\par
relate probabilities of rare events to other probabilities that are easier to assess.
\par
The consideration of rare events is, of course, related to the assessment of proba-
\par
bilities in the tail areas of probability distributions. Questions concerning very
\par
small probabilities are \textbf{also }related to questions concerning small differences in
\par
larger probabilities, but the latter issue is generally less crucial insofar as the un-
\par
derlying decision-making problem is concerned. 1ncidentally;the modeling of un-
\par
certainty in complex situations, usually but not always involving some low-prob-
\par
ability, undesirable events, is sometimes referred to \textbf{as }``risk assessment,'' which
\par
has received increasing attention recently.
\par
Assessors sometimes indicate a vagueness, or shakiness, about some proba-
\par
bilities, even though they do provide assessments. In such cases, more extensive
\par
use of second-order probabilities to quantify such vagueness seems warranted, as
\par
15405915, 1982, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1540-5915.1982.tb01176.x by Cory Whitney - Universit\"ats-U Landesbibliothek Bonn , Wiley Online Library on [03/05/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License\textbf{19821 \emph{Winkler }523}
\par
does the further study of such probabilities. Second-order probabilities could be
\par
especially useful in indicating where further information might be of value and in
\par
providing some guidelines for sensitivity analysis.
\par
In probability assessment, the role of the analyst/interviewer in directing the
\par
assessment process is generally a major one except in repetitive situations (e.g.,
\par
weather forecasting). However, if probabilities are to be elicited from unsophisti-
\par
cated assessors on a widespread basis without the availability of a multitude of
\par
``probability interviewers,'' aids such as interactive computer programs for prob-
\par
ability assessment are needed. Further development of such programs should
\par
make it easier for individuals to quantify their judgments and to use more sophis-
\par
ticated probability models. This is related to the role of approximations in the
\par
quantification of uncertainty. For example, the assessment of an entire density
\par
function for a continuous random variable and the use of such a density function
\par
in a decision-making model can be very difficult, and the difficulties are increased
\par
considerably in a multivariate situation. One solution is to use a discrete approxi-
\par
mation, and another solution \textbf{is }to use a member of a parametric family of distri-
\par
butions as an approximation. The potential error introduced by such approxima-
\par
tions can be reduced by using finer (as opposed to coarser) discrete approxima-
\par
tions or by developing parametric families of distributions that are quite flexible
\par
and capable of providing good fits to a wide variety of types of distributions. If
\par
interactive probability assessment programs are developed, various approxima-
\par
tions (and checks on the goodness of the approximations) might be built into
\par
these programs.
\par
\textbf{A }selling point for DMUU modeling is that it permits the decomposition of
\par
the problem \textbf{so }that, for example, the modeling of uncertainty can be separated
\par
from the modeling of preferences, When a probability assessor has a vested inter-
\par
est in the eventual outcomes, this separation may be hard to maintain. How
\par
serious is this problem, and how can assessment procedures be designed to mini-
\par
mize the problem?
\par
Despite the desire for the assessor to separate the uncertainty side of a deci-
\par
sion problem from other aspects of the problem, the details of the decision prob-
\par
lem dictate what needs to be assessed. In particular, in some cases, a simplified
\par
description of the uncertainty (e.g., a mean or a fractile) is sufficient for decision-
\par
making purposes. How can circumstances under which full information about
\par
the probabilities is not needed be identified? \textbf{Also, }the axioms underlying decision
\par
analysis require the use of probabilities, but foundational work involving weaker
\par
axioms might be valuable. For example, under what circumstances might it be
\par
sufficient to have just a ranking of the probabilities instead of actual numerical
\par
probabilities?
\par
If the decision maker hires an expert \textbf{\emph{to }}assess probabilities, should the as-
\par
sessments be taken at face value? Models of the assessment process could enable a
\par
decision maker to make inferences, possibly including second-order probabilities,
\par
on the basis of an expert's assessmehts. For example, a decision maker could cali-
\par
brate an expert's probabilities on the basis of past evidence or subjective judg-
\par
ments about the expert as a probability assessor. How can experts be identified,
\par
15405915, 1982, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1540-5915.1982.tb01176.x by Cory Whitney - Universit\"ats-U Landesbibliothek Bonn , Wiley Online Library on [03/05/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License\textbf{524 \emph{Decision Sciences }[Vol. 13}
\par
and how can their assessments be calibrated? The study of various evaluation
\par
measures such \textbf{as }scoring rules and various models of the information-gathering
\par
process (e.g., hierarchical inference structures) should be helpful in this regard.
\par
Descriptive studies of probability assessment can be helpful for both assess-
\par
ment and evaluation purposes. Psychological studies have provided useful infor-
\par
mation concerning the use of different response modes, possible response biases,
\par
the ability of individuals to express their uncertainty in terms of probabilities, and
\par
\textbf{so }on. Much of this work has involved nonexperts (e.g., students providing prob-
\par
abilities for almanac-type questions), and the generalization of the results to ex-
\par
perts has been questioned. For example, extensive data involving probabilities as-
\par
sessed by weather forecasters in an operational setting indicate that their proba-
\par
bilities are much more reliable and accurate than are probabilities assessed by
\par
nonexperts in the aforementioned psychological studies. Further descriptive
\par
work, particularly with experts, should cast additional light \textbf{on }how individuals
\par
understand uncertainty and process information \textbf{and }should stimulate more basic
\par
psychological research concerning the underlying cognitive processes.
\par
It was noted above that the probabilities used in DMUU should be based on
\par
all available information. Often information is available from several different
\par
sources (e.g., different experts, past data, forecasts generated by different fore-
\par
casting models). \textbf{How }\emph{can }information from different sources be combined?
\par
Bayes's theorem can, of course, be used to revise probabilities \textbf{on }the basis of new
\par
information. \textbf{Most }applications of Bayes's theorem involve independent, identi-
\par
cally distributed observations from a well-defined data-generating process. In
\par
DMUU, the various sources of information concerning a particular event or vari-
\par
able are unlikely \textbf{to }be independent. For example, two experts may have similar
\par
training or experience, and their probabilities may be based at least partially on
\par
the same data that are used to generate another input (i.e., information from
\par
another source), such as a forecast from, say, an econometric model. Bayes's
\par
theorem can be used when the information sources are dependent, but further
\par
work \textbf{on }Bayesian models for various types of situations with dependence is
\par
needed. This includes situations with data that relate to, but are not directly
\par
about, the event or variable of interest.
\par
' \textbf{In }decision-making problems, it is useful \textbf{to }contemplate the acquisition of
\par
additional information. The value of information from different sources can be
\par
compared, and changes in the value of information from a particular source \textbf{as }a
\par
function \textbf{\emph{of }}the information that has already been observed can be investigated.
\par
\textbf{Also, }the revision \textbf{Of }probabilities and the value of information when the data-
\par
generating process is ``noisy': are of interest. ``Noisy,' is being used in \textbf{a }broad
\par
sense here, including various types of nonstationarity, censoring, randomization
\par
to provide privacy, errors in data handling, unreliable information sources, and
\par
\textbf{so }on.
\par
Where do we currently stand in terms of modeling uncertainty? Probability
\par
theory provides an appropriate framework within which to work, and methodol-
\par
ogy for Probability assessment and evaluation complements the extensive meth-
\par
odology of Probability and statistics. These methodologies have been used widely
