\par
somewhat elusive concepts. In terms of both foundations and assessment, the
\par
consideration of value functions (representing preferences under certainty) and
\par
their relationships with utility functions (representing preferences under uncer-
\par
tainty) may yield useful insights. Further work involving concepts such as
\par
strength of preference and relative risk aversion should help to provide a better
\par
understanding of preference and how different components of preference affect
\par
decision making. To what extent can different components of preference (e.g.,
\par
risk aversion and decreasing marginal value) be separated? \textbf{To }what extent can
\par
``risk attitude'' be defined independently of the numeraire?
\par
Frequently it is possible to eliminate potential actions from consideration
\par
without having to assess an entire utility function, and this is obviously of great
\par
importance from a practical standpoint. For example, when the outcomes are ex-
\par
pressed in terms of money, knowledge that the decision maker is risk averse may
\par
be sufficient to establish the dominance of one particular action over several
\par
other actions, and the elimination of actions in this manner is important because
\par
it provides important insight for the decision maker and reduces the size of the
\par
model. The further study of dominance and admissibility (sometimes referred to
\par
as efficiency analysis) is a promising research topic, both in terms of additional
\par
theoretical developments and in terms of the formulation of efficient algorithms
\par
to detect inadmissible decisions.
\par
\textbf{A }related topic concerns foundational work involving weaker axioms than
\par
the standard \textbf{DMUU }axioms and decision-making models involving criteria other
\par
than expected utility. Descriptive studies indicate that other criteria sometimes
\par
describe actual behavior quite well and thus provide insights into such behavior.
\par
The behavior observed in the descriptive studies generally \textbf{can }be explained within
\par
the expected-utility approach. For example, the consideration of additional attri-
\par
butes (e.g., ``regret'') may resolve some seemingly paradoxical results. Can all
\par
such paradoxes be resolved within the expected-utility framework? What can be
\par
said about very basic psychological issues such \textbf{as }how people form preferences
\par
and evaluate alternatives?
\par
Most of the discussion involving single-attribute utility functions applies
\par
equally well \textbf{to }multiattribute utility functions, but everything becomes more
\par
complex in the multiattribute case. Nevertheless, multiattribute utility has recent-
\par
ly received more attention than any other topic in \textbf{DMUU, }and its potential im-
\par
portance is unquestioned since consequences in the more vital and complex deci-
\par
sion-making problems simply cannot be meaningfully expressed in terms of a
\par
single \textbf{\emph{dimension. }As }a result, the development \textbf{\emph{of }}multiattribute utility theory
\par
(and a related area known as multicriteria decision making) has played a major
\par
role in the increased application of decision theory, and multiattribute utility re-
\par
mains an important research topic in \textbf{DMUU.}
\par
Applications involving multiattribute utility generally involve simplifying as-
\par
sumptions, such as utility independence, that make it possible to decompose a
\par
multiattribute utility function into a relatively simple function (e.g., a weighted
\par
average) of single-attribute utility functions. Such decompositions are approxi-
\par
mations that simplify the analysis, and just as in the case of the other approxima-
\par
tions discussed in this paper, it is important to be able to tell how ``good'' the
