\par
veloped, and comparisons with other decompositions are desirable. Also, the ex-
\par
tension of the study of dominance and admissibility from the single-attribute case
\par
to the multiattribute case is of interest.
\par
Many decision-making problems yield consequences that consist of streams
\par
of outcomes (which may be unidimensional, such as a stream of monetary pay-
\par
ments, or multidimensional, such as consumption/income streams) received over
\par
time. The assessment of utility functions for such streams of outcomes has re-
\par
ceived some, albeit inadequate, attention. For example, does the simple, typically
\par
used procedure of discounting future streams of monetary values provide \textbf{a }rea-
\par
sonable approximation to a wide range of preferences? \textbf{Is }discounting reasonable
\par
for nonmonetary attributes? How important is the timing of the resolution of un-
\par
certainty? What about the impact of preferences changing over time? If outcomes
\par
at different times are considered as separate attributes in a multiattribute utility
\par
function, do various decompositions of multiattribute utility functions provide
\par
good approximations?
\par
In multiattribute situations, certain attributes are difficult to analyze. One
\par
such question involves the value of a life. Some decision-making problems (par-
\par
ticularly, but not exclusively, problems pertaining to public policy) have out-
\par
comes that include increases in the death rate for certain classes of individuals.
\par
From the standpoint of an individual and from the standpoint of society, how
\par
can utilities be assessed for changes in death rates and similar factors? In a more
\par
general sense, how can consequences affecting future generations (life-and-death
\par
consequences or otherwise) be evaluated and utjljties assigned to such conse-
\par
quences? What are the implications with respect to preferences of how attributes
\par
are defined? (For example, using the number of deaths as the only fatality-related
\par
attribute implies that all individual lives are valued equally.) How well do the at-
\par
tributes and their utilities reflect the decision maker's underlying goals and objec-
\par
tives?
\par
The current state of affairs in the modeling of preferences is that utility
\par
theory provides a framework for such modeling, that assessment procedures have
\par
been developed, and that both single-attribute and multiattribute utility have
\par
been used in many applications of DMUU. Suggestions for the most valuable
\par
topics to pursue are as follows:
\par
\textbf{1. }the futher study of value versus utility functions and components of prefer-
\par
ence,
\par
\textbf{2. }the study of methods \textbf{to }handle value and utility dependence,
