\par
Can \textbf{DMUU }modeling help to identify and resolve conflicting opinions and
\par
values? Although the discussion here has focused on the modeling of group deci-
\par
sions in terms of individual assessments of probabilities and utilities, which can
\par
be considered without any interaction among group members, the possibility of
\par
such interaction should be considered.
\par
If all of the individuals in a particular community or state or country are
\par
thought of as members of a group, then public decision making can be thought of
\par
in terms of group decision making. Corporate decision making might be thought
\par
of in the same way, with a corporation's shareholders as group members. In some
\par
cases, decisions are actually made by the group (e.g., by voting), and the proce-
\par
dures used to make the decisions are of interest. In many other cases, decisions
\par
are made by a particular individual (a public official, a manager) or a subgroup,
\par
and the information upon which the decisions are based is of interest. It is not
\par
feasible to assess probabilities or utilities for all of the members of a large popula-
\par
tion, but partial information might be gathered concerning individuals' judg-
\par
ments and preferences. How can the interested parties be identified? How can a
\par
public or corporate utility function be formulated? What role might opinion polls
\par
or interest groups play in public decision making, and what type of information
\par
from opinion polls or interest groups would be most useful to those responsible
\par
for the ultimate decisions? In other words, how can the group decision-making
\par
problem be modeled when the available input from the group members is limited?
\par
How can the decision-making process be improved in such situations?
\par
The emphasis in the discussion of competitive and group decisions has been
\par
normative, but it should be obvious that descriptive studies can be valuable. For
\par
example, knowing something about how individuals or groups make decisions in
\par
practice can help decision makers to predict the decisions of competitors (or the
\par
behavior of fellow group members, for that matter). \textbf{As }is the case with individual
\par
decision making, knowledge of typical competitive or group decision-making
\par
processes can point to directions for potential improvements in these processes.
\par
Where do we stand insofar as the modeling of competitive and group deci-
\par
sions is concerned? Extensive modeling of competitive situations has been done
\par
by economists and game theorists, and work on group decisions has come primar-
\par
ily from economics and behavioral science. The role of \textbf{DMUU }modeling \textbf{of }com-
\par
petitive and group decisions is more hazy at the moment than is the case with
\par
\textbf{DMUU }modeling of decisions by individuals against nature, partially because the
\par
normative framework is not as solid and partially because the models rapidly be-
\par
come more complex. Research along the following lines might help to make the
\par
situation less hazy:
\par
\textbf{1. }the further investigation of normative theories of competitive and group de-
\par
cision making;
\par
2. 3. the study of the decision-making processes of individuals and groups;
\par
the study of the special role of information in competitive and group deci-
\par
sion making;
\par
\textbf{4. }the impact of organizational structures on \textbf{DMUU }modeling, with special at-
\par
tention to public and corporate decision making.
