\textbf{CONCLUDING COMMENTS}
\par
If \textbf{DMUU }is to be regarded as a unique and meaningful discipline, it has to
\par
make unique and meaningful contributions to our understanding of and to the
\par
solution of the important and complex decision-making problems of the real
\par
world. Keeney \textbf{131 }notes that ``the techniques and procedures.. .are sufficiently
\par
developed to make substantial contributions on many complex decision prob-
\par
lems,** It seems safe to say that \textbf{DMUU }is a reasonably mature field. It is past the
\par
growing pains of the \textbf{1950s }and \textbf{1960s. }when basic issues such as the use of subjec-
\par
tive judgments as inputs to quantitative models caused considerable opposition in
\par
some circles; and it is past the ``preapplication'' stage, when a theoretical frame-
\par
work was available but doubts were expressed as to whether \textbf{DMUU }modeling
\par
would ever be used in practice. Yet maturity need not imply dullness. Some fac-
\par
tions and controversies still exist, forcing reexamination of basic issues while
\par
helping to keep the field lively and stimulating. Given the interdisciplinary nature
\par
of \textbf{DMUU, }however, there is surprisingly widespread agreement on the normative
\par
framework for modeling individual decision-making problems. There is less
\par
agreement with respect to the important areas of competitive and group decision
\par
making, and more emphasis is needed on basic research concerning foundations,
\par
decision-making processes, the role of information, and the impact of organiza-
\par
tional structures in situations with multiple decision makers.
\par
Research in \textbf{DMUU }can, and doubtless will, take off in many varied if inter-
\par
related directions. Some of the suggested research is oriented toward theory and
\par
some is oriented toward application. The overlap is considerable, however, and
\par
new theory may lead to improvements in application just as applications may sug-
\par
gest valuable directions for theoretical work. The primary thrust of some sugges-
\par
tions (e.g., the development of decision aids) is toward more widespread and im-
\par
proved implementation of \textbf{DMUU. }Theoretical and methodological research
\par
(e.g., the study of components of preference or the development of improved
\par
methods to handle characteristics such as stochastic or preferential dependence)
\par
can help to make \textbf{DMUU }models more realistic while at the same time providing
\par
insight into the impact of various problem characteristics. Research of a behav-
\par
ioraf nature can help \textbf{us }to understand cognitive processes relating to \textbf{DMUU }and
\par
can indicate \textbf{how DMUU }modeling can be most effective in improving individual
\par
and group decision making.
\par
By its very nature, a paper such as this gives the impression of a shopping
