\textbf{RESEARCH TOPICS IN DECISION MAKING UNDER UNCERTAINTY}
\par
The discussion of research topics is divided into four categories:
\par
\textbf{1. }Model formulation
\par
\textbf{2. }Modeling uncertainty
\par
3. Modeling preferences
\par
\textbf{4.}
\par
Modeling competitive and group decisions.
\par
Unfortunately, some appealing topics cross boundaries and cannot be readily
\par
compartmentalized. Such topics are included by categorizing them as seems most
\par
appropriate.
\par
In modeling a decision-making problem, the first step is to provide the basic
\par
structure of the model. For example, what actions are available, what events or
\par
variables are of interest, what consequences might result, and how are these ele-
\par
ments related to each other? Model structuring, or model formulation, is the first
\par
category to be discussed. The second and third categories involve two important
\par
considerations in decision making: uncertainty and preferences. Much of the
\par
work in DMUU centers around the modeling of uncertainty and the modeling of
\par
preferences. The final category covers important problems that arise when more
\par
than one decision maker is involved, as is the case in competitive situations and in
\par
group decision making.
\par
The primary focus in this paper \textbf{is }on the normative aspects of decision
\par
modeling. That is, the emphasis is prescriptive rather than descriptive. However,
\par
to the extent that descriptive studies of actual decision-making behavior can be
\par
valuable from a normative perspective, they are included in the discussion. Simi-
\par
larly, implementation-related issues, while not listed as a separate category, are
\par
by no means ignored.
\par
\textbf{Model Formulation}
\par
Perhaps because it \textbf{seems }less ambiguous and more ``scientific,'' the analysis
\par
of decision-making models that have already been formulated has received con-
\par
siderably more attention than the model-formulation stage. The identification of
\par
a problem and the structuring of a model for the problem raise questions con-
\par
cerning the link between the creative process and DMUU. This link has not been
\par
explored adequately. Correctly posing a problem \textbf{goes a }long way toward solving
\par
it, yet solving has received more attention than posing. The difficulty in structur-
\par
ing is illustrated by the tendency of students in introductory \textbf{to }intermediate quan-
\par
titative courses to have more trouble ``setting \textbf{up'' }a problem than solving the
\par
problem once it has been set up. Interesting real-world problems, of course, are
\par
almost always much more complex than problems assigned in these courses,
\par
despite what the students might think.
\par
Consider, for example, the listing of possible actions that might be taken.
\par
\textbf{Not }only is it desirable to avoid omitting any readily available actions, but it is
\par
perhaps even more desirable \textbf{to }create new actions. Similarly, there is an element
\par
of creativity in the identification of appropriate goals and objectives and of rele-
\par
vant events that could influence the eventual consequences. How can this creative
\par
15405915, 1982, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1540-5915.1982.tb01176.x by Cory Whitney - Universit\"ats-U Landesbibliothek Bonn , Wiley Online Library on [03/05/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License\textbf{\emph{520 Decision Sciences }}[Vol. \textbf{13}
\par
process be stimulated and directed? What role can DMUU itself play in this stim-
\par
ulation and direction?
\par
Generating new potential actions and trying to imagine all of the possible
\par
ramifications of a decision (e.g., all events that may have a bearing on the final
\par
consequences, all attributes of such consequences, or all future choices that may
\par
be opened or precluded) can provide long lists of elements to be considered for in-
\par
clusion in the model. Interesting real-world decision-making problems involve
\par
complexities associated with factors such as the number of uncertain events or
\par
variables, the number of attributes making up the outcomes, the number of avail-
\par
able actions (including information-purchasing actions), and the interrelation-
\par
ships among different decision-making problems (both in a static sense, at a given
\par
time, and in a dynamic sense, over time). Further theoretical and computational
\par
advances are very important in the sense that they permit the analysis of larger
\par
problems. If the problem becomes too large for current capabilities, how can it be
\par
bounded and how can the appropriate level of detail for the model be deter-
\par
mined?
\par
The cost of modeling is certainly relevant in choosing a level of detail, as is
\par
the effect of various simplifying assumptions on the final output generated by the
\par
model. Further research concerning sensitivity analysis might yield some general
\par
guidelines that could help answer questions such as: What is the impact of includ-
\par
ing in the model only the few variables considered to be most crucial to the deci-
\par
sion or reducing the number of attributes in the consequences? To what degree
\par
can different decision-making problems faced by the same decision maker be ana-
\par
lyzed separately? What is the impact of the choice of a time horizon in a dynamic
\par
decision-making problem, and how can future actions beyond that time horizon
\par
be considered, either formally or informally, in the modeling process? Can fac-
\par
tors omitted from the model be allowed for in part by minor adjustments to cer-
\par
tain inputs?
\par
Model formulation should be thought of as an iterative process. While it is
\par
useful to begin the formulation process by generating long lists of possible ac-
\par
tions, relevant events, and so on, it often seems best in terms of formal modeling
\par
to start with a relatively simple model that includes only the factors considered to
\par
be most crucial to the decision. If one action clearly outperforms the others in this
\par
simple model, then further modeling might be deemed unnecessary; or if a small
\par
subset. of the original actions looks especially promising, then further modeling
\par
might be limited to these actions or other similar actions that `might be created.
\par
How likely is it that the cumulative effect of the factors not considered in the sim-
\par
ple model would be to change significantly such apparently clear-cut results?
\par
How stable are the results? How can sensitivity analysis best provide guidance for
\par
the iterative process of model building?
\par
Questions about the appropriate level of detail in a model can be viewed as
\par
aspects of a more general issue: the evaluation of decision-making models. For
\par
repetitive decision-making problems, perhaps data comparing model-generated
\par
decisions with various rules of thumb or subjective decisions could be collected
\par
and analyzed. For one-of-a-kind problems, the evaluation problem is more diffi-
\par
cult. However, descriptive studies of the application of DMUU can still provide
\par
