\textbf{Modeling Preferences}
\par
In modeling a decision-making problem, \textbf{a }decision maker must determine
\par
the consequences associated with the possible act/event combinations. Once the
\par
consequences have been identified, the decision maker's preferences for the vari-
\par
ous consequences are quantified in terms of value functions or utility functions,
\par
In many situations the consequences are multidimensional, necessitating the con-
\par
sideration of multiattribute value or utility functions, The case of unidimensional
\par
consequences will be considered first, both because it is of interest in its own right
\par
and because it is relevant for the multiattribute case as well when the multiattri-
\par
bute function can be expressed as a function of single-attribute functions.
\par
The assessment of utility functions poses some questions that differ from
\par
those posed by the assessment of probabilities. In utility assessment, everyone is
\par
an ``expert'' in the sense that as individuals we each assess our own preferences.
\par
Thus, while a probability assessor can be evaluated after the events or variables of
\par
interest are observed (e.g., if the events actually occurring are consistently events
\par
that have been assigned very low probabilities, the assessor might not be viewed
\par
\textbf{as }a ``good'' assessor), it is more difficult to think about evaluating a utility as-
\par
sessor.
\par
In utility assessment, the impact upon assessed utility functions of various
\par
response modes and the study and further development of utility assessment pro-
\par
cedures, particularly interactive computer programs, are of considerable impor-
\par
tance. \textbf{As }in the case of probability, certain assessments (generally choices) might
\par
be used to make inferences about other preferences, and approximations can play
\par
a key role with respect to utility. Various families of utility functions (e.g., expo-
\par
nential, quadratic, or logarithmic functions) have been used as approximations,
\par
but more work is needed on the development of families of utility functions that
\par
are flexible \textbf{and }capable of providing good fits to a wide variety of utility func-
\par
tions (e.g., families involving spline functions) and on the sensitivity of decision-
\par
making models to errors introduced by the use of approximations. In some cases
\par
such families might be designed for use with particular probability distributions
\par
since some probability/utility combinations can simplify the analysis consider-
\par
ably.
\par
The motivation for assessing and using utility functions often is related to
\par
the notion of attitude toward risk. But risk and attitude toward risk seem to be
\par
