\textbf{Modeling Competitive and Group Decisions}
\par
Much-of \textbf{DMUU }involves a single decision maker, but situations with multi-
\par
ple decision makers are sufficiently widespread and important to warrant sepa-
\par
rate coverage in this paper. Furthermore, it is convenient to distinguish between
\par
competitive decision making and group decision making. The former refers to sit-
\par
uations in which each decision maker chooses actions that may affect one or more
\par
of the other decision makers, running the gamut from strictly competitive situa-
\par
tions (e.g., zero-sum games) to cooperative situations. Group decision making re-
\par
fers to situations in which the decision makers must make a joint decision.
\par
Competitive decision making has been studied by economists and game theo-
\par
rists, and their results provide useful insights into the special features of competi-
\par
tive situations. \textbf{DMUU, }however, provides a different approach and hence a dif-
\par
ferent perspective from which to study competitive decision making. The compe-
\par
titive nature of the situation adds many complexities not found in decision mak-
\par
ing against nature, and the work done to date has involved fairly simple situa-
\par
tions.
\par
Considering the competitive situation from the perspective of one of the de-
\par
cision makers, the uncertainty facing the decision maker includes uncertainty
\par
about the opponents' actions. Assuredly, probabilities for the opponents' actions
\par
can be assessed directly, taking into account any information about the oppo-
\par
nents' past decision-making behaviors and current situations. \textbf{As }an aid in assess-
\par
ing such probabilities, a decision maker might want to model the opponents' deci-
\par
sion-making problems, attempting to estimate the opponents' possible actions,
\par
probabilities, utilities, and so on, keeping in mind that opponents cannot be ex-
\par
pected to share their information and their preferences truthfully. The impact of
\par
the competitive nature of the situation in a decision maker's optimal strategy,
\par
with particular emphasis on the role of the opponents' states of information, risk
\par
attitudes, decision-making abilities, and other characteristics, is of interest. The
\par
competitive situation also offers opportunities for new types of actions, such as
\par
the formation of coalitions, the offering of bribes, the use of threats, and \textbf{so }on.
\par
Indeed, the role \textbf{\emph{of }}creativity in generating alternatives and in the entire model-
\par
formulation process seems more important in competitive situations than in non-
\par
competitive situations.
\par
With respect \textbf{to }information-buying decisions in competitive situations,
\par
many different types of information might be considered, including secret infor-
\par
mation (information obtained by the decision maker without the opponents being
\par
aware that it has been obtained), private information (information obtained by a
\par
decision maker where the opponents are aware that it has been obtained but do
\par
15405915, 1982, 4, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1540-5915.1982.tb01176.x by Cory Whitney - Universit\"ats-U Landesbibliothek Bonn , Wiley Online Library on [03/05/2024]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License\textbf{19821 \emph{Winkler }529}
\par
not learn the actual information), public information (information that is availa-
\par
ble to all competitors), and information shared by members of a coalition. What
\par
can be said regarding the value of different types of information in competitive
\par
situations? It is known that many standard results from decision making against
\par
nature (e.g., the result that the expected value of information can never be nega-
\par
tive) do not carry over into decision making in competitive situations, but the re-
\par
search to date in this area has barely scratched the surface. \textbf{Is }a decision maker
\par
better off with or without information? How should information-revelation deci-
\par
sions be handled in competitive situations?
\par
An alternative to considering the competitive situation from the perspective
\par
of one of the decision makers is the possibility of studying the situation from the
\par
viewpoint of an outsider looking in on the situation, as might be done, for exam-
\par
ple, by a mediator or arbitrator. The outsider can attempt to model the competi-
\par
tors' decision-making situations, and the modeling is similar to that done by a
\par
single decision maker concerning the decision-making situations of a set of oppo-
\par
nents. The actions, probabilities, payoffs, utilities, and other factors must be
\par
considered for the set of competitors, and the model must allow for possible de-
\par
pendence (e.g., an outsider's judgments about the probabilities of one competitor
\par
for an uncertain event are most likely not independent of the outsider's judg-
\par
ments about the probabilities of other competitors for the same event). The possi-
\par
bility of ``bad'' decision making on the part of some competitors should be con-
\par
sidered, as should the formation of coalitions or the existence of bargaining
\par
among the competitors and the use of arbitration procedures to balance prefer-
\par
ences of different competitors (or groups of competitors).
\par
In the study of competitive decision-making situations, different solution
\par
concepts must be considered. The normative theory \textbf{\emph{is }}not as clear cut in competi-
\par
tive decision making as it is in individual decision making against nature. The sta-
\par
bility of solutions can be investigated, as can the existence of equilibrium solu-
\par
tions in competitive situations. This, of course, relates to the study of the forma-
\par
tion of and the nature of markets and the transmission of information in market
\par
situations. Furthermore, a one-shot situation, in which the competitors' decisions
\par
are intertwined for one time only, may have considerably different implications
\par
than a dynamic situation, in which the competitors repeatedly make decisions
\par
that affect themselves and their opponents. In the latter case, the role of changes
\par
in the situation over time, including the possibility of competitors entering or
\par
leaving the scene from time to time, deserves careful study.
\par
Group decision making, in which a group must make a joint decision and the
\par
eventual outcome received by the group will be allocated in some fashion among
\par
the group members, also involves multiple decision makers and has widespread
\par
applicability. Here each member of the group can assess probabilities for any un-
\par
certain events or variables and values of utilities for possible outcomes (either for
\par
outcomes to the group or just for outcomes to himself or herself). \textbf{As }in the com-
\par
petitive decision-making situation, the question of the appropriate solution con-
\par
cept must be addressed and the normative theory is not as clear cut as in individ-
\par
ual decision making. Should the group members attempt to aggregate their
