probabilities and utilities in some fashion to arrive at ``group probabilities'' and
\par
``group utilities,'' with the group then behaving as would an individual with
\par
probabilities and utilities equal to the group probabilities and utilities? If \textbf{so, }how
\par
should the group perform the necessary aggregations? Should the group members
\par
focus on a criterion such as Pareto optimality instead of beginning with the as-
\par
sumption that aggregation is desirable? Should the group simply make decisions
\par
by some voting rule?
\par
\textbf{As }the above questions suggest, an important aspect of research into the
\par
modeling of group decision making concerns foundational matters. Much of the
\par
existing research in the area concerns the existence of group probability functions
\par
or group utility functions (e.g., the well-known ``impossibility theorem,'' which
\par
shows that, given certain assumptions, a group ordinal value function consistent
\par
with the ordinal value functions of the individuals comprising the group does not
\par
always exist). Further research is needed concerning the types of situations under
\par
which group probability functions and group utility functions do exist and types
\par
of situations under which the group can behave (for external decisions) like an in-
\par
dividual decision maker. Different axiomatic structures concerning the group de-
\par
cislon-making situation should be explored.
\par
For a given solution concept, the nature of the group's decision is of interest.
\par
Under what conditions, for instance, might the group appear to be risk averse or
\par
risk seeking or risk neutral? How do the characteristics of the group relate to the
\par
characteristics of the individual group members? For example, how do group
\par
information-buying decisions relate to individual information-buying decisions?
\par
The internal decision-making problem facing the group is also of interest,
\par
both in its own right and in terms of its relationship to the group's external deci-
\par
sion-making problem. The internal decision-making problem involves sharing
\par
rules for dividing the outcome received by the group (if it is divisible), side pay-
\par
ments among members of the group, and side bets among members of the group.
\par
An important question concerns the impact on a group member of the character-
\par
istics (e.g., information, risk attitudes) of other members of the group. This
\par
relates to questions involving the initial formation of the group. With what sorts
\par
of colleagues would an individual like to be associated for the purpose of making
\par
group decisions? It also relates \textbf{to }the creation of new actions for individual deci-
\par
sion makers, as exemplified by the possibility of forming a syndicate for risk-
\par
sharing purposes.
\par
Other internal questions facing the group concern organizational issues.
\par
What are the impacts of different organizational structures upon the group as a
\par
whole and upon the individual group members? \textbf{Do }individual value structures
\par
and organizational structures mesh or clash? How are group members motivated,
\par
and what is the role of incentive and reward systems? What \textbf{sorts }of procedures
\par
should be set up for \&he communication of information and the delegation of au-
\par
thority within a group? Within an organization, individuals may have much com-
\par
mon information and, to some degree, common objectives; but they also may
\par
have the usual intragroup conflicts, disagreements, and jockeying for position.
