\par
DECISION ANALYSIS *
\par
Detlof von WINTERFELDT **
\par
\textbf{\emph{University of Southern California, Los Angeles, CA 90007, USA}}
\par
Structuring decision problems into a formally acceptable and manageable format is probably
\par
the most important step of decision analysis. Since presently no sound methodology for struc-
\par
turing exists, this step is still an art left to the intuition and craftsmanship of the individual
\par
analyst. After introducing a general concept of structuring, this paper reviews some recent
\par
advances in structuring research. These include taxonomies for problem identification and new
\par
tools such as influence diagrams and interpretative structural modeling. Two conclusions
\par
emerge from this review: structuring research is still limited to a few hierarchical concepts and
\par
it tends to ignore substantive problem aspects that delineate a problem it its real world context.
\par
Consequently structuring research has little to say about distinctions between typical problem
\par
classes such as regulation, siting, or budget allocation.
\par
As an alternative the concept of ``prototypical decision analytic structures'' is introduced.
\par
Such structures are developed to meet the substantive characteristics of a specific problem
\par
(e.g., siting a specific Liquid Natural Gas plant) but they are at the same time general enough to
\par
apply to similar problems (industrial facility siting). As an illustration, the development of a
\par
prototypical analytic structure for environmental standard setting is described. Finally, some
\par
typical problem classes are examined and some requirements for prototypical structures are dis-
\par
cussed.
\par
An introduction to problem structuring
\par
Decision analysis can be divided into four steps: structuring the
\par
problem; formulating inference and preference models; eliciting proba-
\par
bilities and utilities; and exploring the numerical model results. Prac-
\par
