\par
extensively with Helmut Jungermann. The present version owes much to his thought.
\par
Please don't take footnote 3 too seriously. It is part of a footnote war between Ralph Kee-
\par
ney and me.
\par
** Presently with the Social Science Research Institute, University of Southern California,
\par
University Park, Los Angeles, CA 90007, (213) 741-6955.\textbf{\emph{12 D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems}}
\par
titioners of decision analysis generally agree that structuring is the most
\par
important and difficult step of the analysis. Yet, until recently, decision
\par
analytic research has all but ignored structuring, concentrating instead
\par
on questions of modeling and elicitation. As a result, structuring was,
\par
and to some extent still is, considered the `art' part of decision analysis.
\par
This paper examines some attempts to turn this art into a science.
\par
Trees are the most common decision analytic structures. Decision
\par
trees, for example, represent the sequential aspects of a decision
\par
problem (see Raiffa 1968; Brown \emph{et al. }1974). Other examples are goal
\par
trees for the representations of values (Keeney and Raiffa 1976) and
\par
event trees for the representation of inferential problem aspects (Kelly
\par
and Barclay 1973). In fact, trees so much dominate decision analytic
\par
structures that structuring is often considered synonymous to building
\par
a tree. This paper, however, will adopt a more general notion of deci-
\par
sion analytic structuring.
\par
According to this notion, structuring is an imaginative and creative
\par
process of translating an initially ill-defined problem into a set of well-
\par
defined elements, relations, and operations. The basic structuring activi-
\par
ties are identifying or generating problem elements (e.g., events, values,
\par
actors, decision alternatives) and relating these elements by influence
\par
relations, inclusion relations, hierarchical ordering relations, \emph{etc. }The
\par
structuring process seeks to formally represent the environmental (ob-
\par
jective) parts of the decision problem and the decision makers' or
\par
experts' (subjective) views, opinions, and values. Graphs, maps, func-
\par
tional equations, matrices, trees, physical analogues, flow charts, and
\par
venn diagrams are all possible problem representations. In order to be
\par
useful structures for decision analysis, such representations must facili-
\par
tate the subsequent steps of modeling, elicitation, and numerical
\par
analysis.
\par
Three phases can be distinguished in such a generalized structuring
\par
process. In the first phase the.problem is identified. The elements which
\par
are generated in this phase are the substantive features of the problem:
\par
the decision maker(s); the generic classes of alternatives, objectives, and
\par
events; individuals or groups affected by the decision; characteristics of
\par
the problem environment. This list is pruned by answering questions
\par
such as: what is the purpose of the analysis? For whom is the analysis
\par
to be performed? Which alternatives can the decision maker truly
\par
control? At this stage only very rough relations between problem
\par
elements are constructed. Examples include organizational relations\textbf{\emph{D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems 73}}
\par
among decision makers, influence relations between classes of actions
\par
and events, and rough groupings of objectives. Products of this problem
\par
identification step are usually not very formal, and are seldom reported
\par
in the decision analytic literature. They may be in the form of dia-
\par
grams, graphs, or ordered lists. Among the few documented examples
\par
are Hogarth \emph{et al. }(1980) for the problem of city planning and Fischer
\par
and von Winterfeldt (1978) for the problem of setting environmental
\par
standards.
\par
In the second structuring step, an overall analytic structure is devel-
\par
oped. The elements generated in this step are possible analytic problem
\par
representations. Besides tree structures, these may include more com-
\par
plex structures previously developed for similar problems such as
\par
screening structures for siting decisions or signal detection structures
\par
for medical decision making. Paradigmatic structures of alternative
\par
modeling approaches (e.g., systems dynamics or linear programming)
\par
which could fit the problem should also be examined at this step [ 1 I. A
\par
creative activity in this structuring phase is to relate and combine part
\par
structures, e.g., simulation structures with evaluation structures, or
\par
decision trees of different actors. From the candidate structures and
\par
their combinations an overall structure is selected which is judged most
\par
representative of the problem and manageable for further modeling and
\par
elicitation. Only a handful of analytic structures have been developed
\par
which are more complex than decision trees. Gardiner and Ford (in
\par
press) combined simulation and evaluation structures. Keeney (in press)
\par
developed decision analytic structure for the whole process of siting
\par
energy facilities. Von Winterfeldt (1978) constructed a generic struc-
\par
ture for regulatory decision making.
\par
The third structuring phase coincides with the more traditional and
\par
limited notion of structuring. In this step the parts of the overall
\par
analytic structure are formalized in detail by refining the problem
\par
elements and relations identified in the first step. This includes a
\par
detailed construction of decision trees, event trees, and goal trees.
\par
Linkages between part structures are established, e.g., between simula-
\par
tion and evaluation structures. Decision makers and groups affected by
\par
possible decisions are specified together with events or actions linking
\par
[l] Although alternatives to decision analytic structures should be considered, I will ignore
\par
such structures in the remainder of this paper.14 \textbf{\emph{D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems}}
\par
them. Examples of this structuring step can be found in most decision
\par
analytic textbooks.
\par
This three step structuring process of identifying the problem,
\par
developing an analytic structure, and formalizing its detailed content
\par
seldom evolves in strict sequence. Instead, the process is recursive, with
\par
repeated trials and errors. Often the analyst decides on a specific struc-
\par
ture and later finds it either unmanageable for modeling or non-repre-
\par
sentative of the problem. The recognition that a structure needs refme-
\par
ment often follows the final step of decision analysis, if numerical com-
\par
putations and sensitivity analyses point to places that deserve more
\par
detailed analysis. Knowing about the recursive nature of the structuring
\par
process, it is good decision analysis practice to spend much effort on
\par
structuring and to keep an open mind about possible revisions.
\par
The above characterization of the structuring process will be used as
\par
a format to review the structuring literature. First, the use of problem
\par
taxonomies for the step of problem identification is examined. Methods
\par
to select analytic approaches are then reviewed as possible aids for the
\par
second structuring step. Finally, some recent advances in formalizing
\par
part structures are discussed. *
\par
Two conclusions emerged from this review and motivated the sub-
\par
sequent sections of this paper:
\par
(1) Although structuring research has much to say about analytic dis-
\par
tinctions between decision problems and structures (e.g., whether a
\par
problem is multiattributed or not), it has little bearing on substan-
\par
tive problem distinction (e.g., the difference between a typical regu-
\par
lation problem and a typical investment problem).
\par
(2) Structuring research is still limited to a few, usually hierarchical
\par
concepts and operations. Emphasis is put on simple, operational and
\par
computerized structuring. Little effort is spent on creating more
\par
complex combinations of structures that represent real problem
\par
classes.
\par
As an alternative, the concept of prototypical decision analytic struc-
\par
tures is introduced. Such structures have more substance and com-
\par
plexity than the usual decision trees or goal trees. They are developed
\par
to meet the substantive characteristics of a specific problem, but are at
\par
the same time general enough to apply to similar problems. As an illus-
\par
tration, IIASA's [21 development of a prototypical decision analytic
\par
\textbf{\emph{[2] }}International Institute for Applied Systems Analysis, Laxenburg, Austria.\textbf{\emph{D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems 75}}
\par
structure for environmental standard setting will be described. Finally,
\par
several typical classes of decision problems will be examined and some
\par
requirements for prototypical structures will be discussed.
\par
\textbf{Taxonomies for problem identification}
\par
The taxonomies described in the following typically classify decision
\par
problems by analytic categories (e.g., whether a problem is multiattri-
\par
buted or not) and they attempt to slice the universe of problems into
\par
mutually exclusive and exhaustive sets. The purpose of such taxono-
\par
mies is twofold: to facilitate the \textbf{\emph{identification }}of an unknown element
\par
(e.g., a medical decision problem) with a class of problems (e:g., diag-
\par
nostic problem); and to aid the process of \textbf{\emph{matching }}classes in the prob-
\par
lem taxonomy (e.g., diagnostic problems) with an analytic approach
\par
(e.g., signal detection structures). Thus, by their own aspiration, prob-
\par
lem taxonomies should be useful for the early phases of structuring
\par
decision problems.
\par
MacCrimmon and Taylor (1975) discuss on a rather general level the
\par
relationship between decision problems and solution strategies. Deci-
\par
sion problems are classified according to whether they are ill-structured
\par
or well-structured, depending on the extent to which the decision
\par
maker feels familiar with the initial state of the problem, the terminal
\par
state, and the transformations required to reach a desired terminal
\par
state. Three main factors contribute to ill-structuredness: uncertainty,
\par
complexity, and conflict. For each category MacCrimmon and Taylor
\par
discuss a number of solution strategies. These strategies include, for
\par
example, reductions of the perceptions of uncertainty, modeling
\par
strategies, information acquisition and processing strategies, and meth-
\par
ods for restructuring a problem.
\par
Taylor (1974) adds to this classification scheme four basic types of
\par
problems: resource specification, goal specification, creative problems,
\par
and well structured problems (see fig. 1). Problem types are identified
\par
by the decision maker's familiarity with the three subparts of the prob-
\par
lem. Taylor discusses what types of decision strategies are appropriate
\par
for each of these problem categories, for example, brainstorming for
\par
creative problems and operations research type solutions for well struc-
\par
tured problems.
\par
Howell and Burnett (1978) recently developed a taxonomy of tasks16 \textbf{\emph{D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems}}
