\par
Uncertain events are classified according to three dichotomies:
\par
frequentistic - not frequentistic;
\par
known data generator - unknown data generator;
\par
process external - internal to the observer.
\par
Task characteristics are complexity, setting (e.g., real life us. labora-
\par
tory), span of events, and response mode characteristics. For each
\par
event/task combination Howell and Burnett discuss how different cog-
\par
nitive processes may be operating when making probability judgments.
\par
For example, in estimating frequentistic events with unknown data
\par
generators, availability heuristics may be operative.
\par
Brown and Ulvila (1977) present the most comprehensive attempt
\par
yet to classify decision problems. Their taxonomy includes well over
\par
100 possible characteristics. Decision problems are defined according to
\par
their substance and the decision process involved. Substantive
\par
taxonomic characteristics are mainly derived from the analytic proper-
\par
ties of the situation, \emph{i.e., }amount and type of uncertainty, and amount\textbf{\emph{D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems 71}}
\par
and types of stakes, types of alternatives. Only a few elements of this
\par
part of the taxonomy can be directly related to problem content, \emph{i.e.,}
\par
current vs. contingent decision, operating vs. information act. The
\par
taxonomic elements of the decision process refer mainly to the con-
\par
straints of the decision maker, e.g., reaction time, available resources.
\par
The taxonomy by Brown and Ulvila incorporates most previous prob-
\par
lem taxonomies which tried to define decision problems by categories
\par
derived from decision analysis. These include taxonomies by von Win-
\par
terfeldt and Fischer (1975), Miller et al. (1976), and Vlek and Wage-
\par
naar (1979).
\par
To be useful for problem identification, the above taxonomies
\par
should lead an analyst to a class of problems which has characteristics
\par
similar to the decision problem under investigation. Unfortunately, the
\par
existing problem taxonomies are ill-suited for this purpose, because
\par
they use mainly analytic categories to distinguish problems. Such
\par
categories are derivatives of the decision analytic models and concepts,
\par
rather than characteristics of real world problems. For example, the
\par
analytic categorizations of problems into risky vs. riskless classes is
\par
based on the distinction between riskless and risky preference models.
\par
Analytic categories create more or less empty classes with little or no
\par
correspondence to real problems. For example, none of the above
\par
taxonomies allows distinguishing between a typical siting problem and
\par
a typical regulation problem in a meaningful way. It appears that sub-
\par
stantive rather than analytic characteristics identify real problems. Sub-
\par
stantive characteristics are generalized content features of the prob-
\par
lems belonging to the respective class. For example, a substantive
\par
feature of regulation problems is the involvement of three generic deci-
\par
sion makers: the regulator, the regulated, and the beneficiary of regula-
\par
tion. To become useful for problem identification, taxonomies need
\par
to include such substantive problem characteristic\&
\par
\textbf{Methods for selecting an overall analytic structure}
\par
Most taxonomies include some ideas or principles for matching prob-
\par
lems with analytic structures or models. MacCrimmon and Taylor
\par
attempted to match their basic type of decision problems with cogni-
\par
tive solution strategies, Howell and Burnett speculated on which cogni-
\par
tive processes may be invoked by typical task/event classes in proba-18 \textbf{\emph{D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems}}
\par
bility assessment; von Winterfeldt and Fischer identified for each prob-
\par
lem category appropriate multiattribute utility models. But in none of
\par
these papers explicit matching principles or criteria for the goodness of
\par
a match are given. Rather, matches are created on the basis of a \textbf{\emph{priori}}
\par
reasoning about the appropriateness of a strategy, model, or a cognitive
\par
process for a particular class of decision problems.
\par
Brown and Ulvila (1977) attempted to make this selection process
\par
more explicit by creating an analytic taxonomy in correspondence with
\par
the problem taxonomy. The analytic taxonomy classifies the main
\par
options an analyst may have in structuring and modeling a decision
\par
problem. The taxonomy includes factors such as user's options (amount
\par
to be expended on the analysis), input structure (type of uncertainty),
\par
elicitation techniques (type of probability elicitation). These categories
\par
identify options, both at a general level (optimization, simulation, and
\par
Bayesian inference models) and special techniques (e.g., reference
\par
gambles, or Delphi technique).
\par
To match problems with analytic approaches Brown and Ulvila
\par
created a third taxonomy, called the ``performance measure taxono-
\par
my''. This taxonomy evaluates analytic approaches on attributes like
\par
``time and cost measures'', ``quality of the option generation process'',
\par
``quality of communication or implementation'', \textbf{\emph{etc. }}Different problem
\par
classes have different priority profiles on the performance measure
\par
categories. Similarly, different analytic approaches have different scor-
\par
ing profiles on the performance measures. The analytic approach
\par
chosen should perform well on the priority needs of a particular prob-
\par
lem, Brown and Ulvila discuss the `goodness of fit' of several analytic
\par
approaches to a number of decision situations in terms of these per-
\par
formance measures. For example, they argue that a contingency type
\par
analysis (an element of the analytic taxonomy) is appropriate for deci-
\par
sion problems that occur repeatedly and require a fast response (ele-
\par
ments of the decision situation taxonomy) because contingency type
\par
analysis allows fast calculations (elements of the performance measure
\par
taxonomy).
\par
Several authors have developed logical selection schemes, which can
\par
identify an appropriate analytic approach or model based on selected
\par
problem features. MacCrimmon (1973), for example, developed a
\par
sequential method for selecting an appropriate approach for multiattri-
\par
b\&e evaluation. The first question to be answered is whether the
\par
purpose of the analysis is normative or descriptive. Further questions\textbf{\emph{D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems 79}}
\par
include whether the type of problem has occurred frequently before, if
\par
there are multiple decision makers with conflicting preferences, and
\par
whether alternatives are available or have to be designed. All questions
\par
are of the yes-no type and together create a flow chart for selecting
\par
among 19 possible approaches. For example, if the purpose of the
\par
analysis is normative, if direct assessments of preferences (e.g., ratings)
\par
are valid and reliable, and if the type of problem has frequently
\par
occurred before, regression models or ANOVA type approaches would
\par
be appropriate.
\par
Johnson and Huber (1977) and Kneppreth et \textbf{\emph{al. }}(1977) discuss a
\par
three step procedure for selecting a multiattribute utility assessment
\par
approach. In the first step, the characteristics of the multiattribute
\par
problem are listed, including discreteness vs. continuity of dimensions,
\par
uncertainty vs. no uncertainty, and independence considerations. In the
\par
second step the evaluation situation is characterized on the basis of
\par
judgments about the task complexity, amount of training required for
\par
assessment, face validity required, assessment time, accuracy and flexi-
\par
bility. In the third and final step the profile describing the evaluation
\par
problem is compared with a profile characterizing five different generic
\par
assessment models or methods. The technique that best matches the
\par
situation profile is selected. For example, lottery assessment methods
\par
and models would be appropriate if the evaluation problem involves
\par
uncertainties, does not require high face validity, and allows for a good
\par
amount of training of the assessor.
\par
Both the taxonomy oriented and the sequential selection methods
\par
for matching problems and analysis suffer from certain drawbacks. As
\par
stated earlier, problem characteristics used in taxonomies typically
\par
neglect substantive aspects of the decision problem. Consequently, an
\par
analyst may choose an analytic approach based on a match with a
\par
spuriously defined problem class. For example, when facing a medical
\par
diagnosis problem, an analyst may find that some detailed substantive
\par
characteristics of the problem (e.g., the way doctors process informa-
\par
tion, the physical format of information, \textbf{\emph{etc.) }}suggest a signal detection
\par
structure. Yet, as far as I can see, none of the above matching processes
\par
would directly lead to such a structure.
\par
Advances in formalizing structures
\par
Influence diagrams are a recent development in decision analytic struc-
\par
turing (see Miller \textbf{\emph{et al. }}1976). Influence diagrams draw a graphical\textbf{\emph{80 D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems}}
\par
picture of the way variables in a decision model influence each other,
\par
without superimposing any hierarchical structure. For example, a deci-
\par
sion variable (price) may `influence' a state variable (demand) and thus
\par
`influence' a final state (successful introduction of a new product into
\par
a market). Influence diagrams have been conceived mainly as an initial
\par
pre-structuring tool to create a cognitive map of a decision maker's or
\par
expert's view of a decision problem. In the present stage influence
\par
diagrams are turned into hierarchical structures and analyzed with tradi-
\par
tional tools. But research is now underway at SRI International on the
\par
use of influence diagrams directly in EV or EU computations.
\par
Another generalization of the tree approach is Interpretative Struc-
\par
tural Modeling (ISM) developed, for example, in Warfield (1974) and
\par
Sage (1977). In interpretative structural modeling, matrix and graph
\par
theory notions are used to formally represent a decision problem. First,
\par
all elements of the problem are listed and an element by element matrix
\par
is constructed. The structure of the relationships between elements is
\par
then constructed by filling in the matrix with numerical judgments
\par
reflecting the strength of the relationship, or by simply making O-l
\par
judgments about the existence/non-existence of a relation. Computer
\par
programs can then be used to convert the matrix into a graph or a tree
\par
that represents the problem.
\par
Influence diagrams, value trees, decision trees, and inference trees
\par
can all be thought of as special cases of ISM. For example, in value tree
\par
construction, the analyst may begin with a rather arbitrary collection of
\par
value relevant aspects, attributes, outcomes, targets and objectives.
\par
Using alternative semantic labels for the relationships between these
\par
elements (e.g., `similar', `part of'), an element by element matrix can be
\par
filled. Finally, the analyst can explore whether a particular relational
\par
structure leads to useful goal tree structure.
\par
Besides these generalizations of traditional hierarchical structuring
\par
tools, several refinements of special structuring techniques have been
\par
suggested, particularly for evaluation problems. Keeney and Raiffa
\par
(1976) devoted a whole chapter to the problem of structuring a value
\par
tree. They suggest a strategy of constructing a value tree by beginning
\par
with general objectives and disaggregating by using a pure explication
\par
logic (i.e., what is meant by this general objective?). This approach has
\par
previously been advocated by Miller (1970) and others. Mannheim and
\par
Hall (1967) suggest in addition the possibility of disaggregating general\textbf{\emph{D. van Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems 81}}
\par
objectives according to a means-ends logic (how can this general objec-
\par
tive be achieved?). Other disaggregation logics (problem oriented, pro-
\par
cess oriented, \emph{etc.) }could be analyzed in the ISM context. There are a
\par
number of papers that suggest more empirical or synthetic approaches to
\par
value tree construction. Of particular interest is a repertory grid tech-
\par
nique described by Humphreys and Humphreys (1975) and Humphreys
\par
and Wisuda (1979). In this procedure similarity and dissimilarity judg-
\par
ments are used to span the value dimensions of alternatives.
\par
Several computer aids have been developed recently to aid decision
\par
makers or experts in structuring decision problems. Some of these are
\par
discussed in Kelly ( 1978), and Humphreys (1980). These aids typically
\par
rely on empty structuring concepts (decison trees, value trees, inference
\par
trees, or influence diagrams) and they guide the decision maker/expert
\par
in the analytic formulation of his/her problem. Special aids are OPINT
\par
for moderately complex problems which can easily be formulated into
\par
a decision tree or matrix structure, the decision triangle aid for
\par
sequential decision problems with a focus on changing probabilities,
\par
and EVAL for multiattribute utility problems (Kelly 1978). In addition
\par
to these structuring and assessment aids, there are now computerized
\par
aids under development exploiting the idea of influence diagrams and
\par
fuzzy set theory.
\par
Influence diagrams, ISM, and computer aids are indicative of a trend
\par
in structuring research and perhaps in decision analysis as a whole. This
\par
trend turns the fundamentally empty structures of decision trees, goal
\par
trees, and inference trees into more operational, computerized elicita-
\par
tion tools, without adding problem substance. There are clear advan-
\par
tages to such an approach: a wide range of applicability, flexibility, user
\par
involvement, speed, limited training, and feedback, to name only a few.
\par
It also reduces the demands on the decision analyst's time.
\par
There is, of course, the other extreme, the prestructured, precanned
\par
problem specific version of decision analysis applicable to essentially
\par
identical situations. A military example is Decisions and Designs Inc.\%
\par
SURVAV model (Kelly 1978) which applies to routing decisions for
\par
ships to avoid detections by satellites. Such a structure and model can
\par
routinely be implemented with almost no additional training. In turn it
\par
gives up generalizability.
\par
Neither extreme is totally satisfactory. Empty general structures
\par
must consider each problem from scratch. Substantive specific struc-\textbf{\emph{82 D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems}}
\par
tures have limited generalizability. The middleground of problem driven
\par
but still generalizable structures and models needs to be filled. Problem
\par
taxonomies may help here by identifying generic classes of problems.
\par
But as was discussed earlier, existing taxonomies are ill equipped for
\par
this task since they neglect substantive problem features. The question
\par
of filling in the middleground between `too general' structures and `too
\par
specific' structures thus becomes a question of searching for general-
\par
izable content features of problems that identify generic classes of deci-
\par
sions. These generic classes can then be modelled and structured by
\par
``prototypical decision analytic structures'' which are specific enough to
\par
match the generalizable problem features and general enough to
\par
transfer easily to other problems of the same class.
\par
At the present stage of research this search process will necessarily
\par
be inductive because too little is known about problem substance to
\par
develop a problem driven taxonomy and matching analytic structures.
\par
An inductive research strategy may attempt to crystallize the general-
\par
izable features of a specific application, .or compare a number of similar
\par
applications (e.g., with siting problems), or simply use a phenomenolog-
\par
ical approach to delineate problem classes in a specific application area
\par
(e.g., regulation). In the following two sections some possibilities for
\par
developing prototypical decision analytic structures will be discussed.
\par
