\par
\textbf{Towards a kit of prototypical decision analytical structures}
\par
Not every decision analysis can afford to be as broad and time consum-
\par
ing as the previous study. Decision analysis usually has a much more
\par
specific orientation towards producing a decision rather than develop-
\par
ing a generic structure. Still I think that it would be helpful if analysts
\par
were to make an effort in addressing the question of generalizability
\par
when modeling a specific problem, and in extracting those features of
\par
the problem and the model that are transferable. Such an inductive
\par
approach could be coupled with more research oriented efforts and
\par
with examinations of similarities among past applications. Such an
\par
approach may eventually fill the middleground between too specific
\par
and too general models and structures. But rather than filling this
\par
middleground with analytically specific but substantively empty struc-
\par
tures and models, it would be filled with prototypical structures and
\par
models such as the above regulation model, more refined signal detec-
\par
tion models, siting models, \emph{etc.}
\par
In the following, four typical classes of decision problems (siting,
\par
contingency planning, budget allocation, and regulation) are examined
\par
and requirements for prototypical structures for these problems are dis-
\par
cussed.
\par
\emph{Facility siting }clearly is a typical decision problem. Keeney and other
\par
decision analysts have investigated this problem in much detail and in a
\par
variety of contexts (see the examples in Keeney and Raiffa 1976). A
\par
typical aspect of such siting problems is sequential screening from
\par
candidate areas to possible sites, to a preferred set, to final site specific
\par
evaluations. Another aspect is the multiobjective nature with emphasis
\par
on generic classes of objectives: investment and operating cost,
\par
economic benefits, environmental impacts, social impacts, and political
\par
considerations. Also, the process of organizing, collecting, and evaluat-
\par
ing information is similar in many siting decisions. Thus, it should be
\par
possible to develop a prototypical structure for facility siting decisions,90 \textbf{\emph{D. von Winterfeldt /Structuring decision problems}}
\par
simply by assembling the generalizable features of past applications [ 31.
\par
\textbf{\emph{Contingency planning }}is another recurring and typical problem. Deci-
\par
sion and Design Inc. addressed this problem in the military context, but
\par
it also applies to planning for actions in the case of disasters such as
\par
Liquid Natural Gas plant explosions or blowouts from oil platforms.
\par
Substantive aspects that are characteristic of contingency planning are:
\par
strong central control of executive organs, numerous decisions have to
\par
be made simultaneously, major events can drastically change the focus
\par
of the problem, no cost or low cost information comes in rapidly, and
\par
organizational problems may impede information flows and actions.
\par
Although, at first glance, decision trees seem to be a natural model for
\par
contingency planning, a prototypical decision model would require
\par
modifying a strictly sequential approach to accommodate these aspects.
\par
For example, the model should be flexible enough to allow for the
\par
`unforeseeable' (rapid capacity to change the model structure), it
\par
should have rapid information updating facilities without overstressing
\par
the value of information (since most information is free), and it should
\par
attend to fine tuning of simultaneous actions and information inter-
\par
linkages.
\par
Budget \textbf{\emph{allocation }}to competing programs is another typical prob-
\par
lem. In many such problems different programs attempt to pursue
\par
similar objectives, and program mix and balance has to be considered
\par
besides the direct benefits of single programs. Another characteristic of
\par
budgeting decisions is the continuous nature of the decision variable
\par
and the constraint of the total budget. MAU looks like a natural struc-
\par
ture for budget allocation decision since it can handle the program
\par
evaluation aspect (see Edwards \textbf{\emph{et al. }}1976). But neither the balance
\par
issue nor the constrained and continuous characteristics of the budget
\par
are appropriately adressed by MAU. A prototypical decision analytic
\par
structure would model an evaluation of the budget apportionment, or
\par
the mix of programs funded at particular levels. Such a structure would
\par
perhaps exploit dependencies or independencies among programs much
\par
like independence assumption for preferences.
\par
