\textbf{CANADIAN TRANSBORDER DATA FLOWS}
\par
The closest approximation to data protection law in Canada is
\par
the Canadian Human Rights Act, which arises from a fundamen-
\par
tally different legal philosophy. 1 0 6 Common law countries tradition-
\par
ally view legislation as necessary only if a demonstrable mischief
\par
exists which customary law or judicial case law cannot remedy.1 0 7
\par
The contrast in legal philosophies is manifested in the differing
\par
types of enforcement mechanisms designed to reduce data abuse.
\par
Canadian control over data abuse is both internal and external. In-
\par
ternally, ultimate "in-house" supervision is conferred on the minis-
\par
ters of the Crown. Externally, control is subjective; the data sub-
\par
ject must initiate any controlling activity. In contrast, European
\par
data protection law establishes an external objective rather than
\par
subjective controlling mechanism; a tribunal or board regulates all
\par
data processing and initiates enforcement activities.1 08 The role of
\par
the Canadian Privacy Commissioner is somewhat analogous to that
\par
of a European data protection agency, but the Privacy Commis-
\par
sioner has a limited ombudsman duty and no enforcement powers
\par
equivalent to those of a European data protection agency.0 '
\par
Canadian concerns over the privacy of personal data mainly
\par
emerged from the potential of governmental abuse, 110 making it
\par
doubtful that the Canadian Human Rights Act would be expanded
\par
to give a government bureaucracy as much power as a European
\par
data protection board. It is suggested that the concept of data pro-
