\par
VI. \textbf{CONCLUSION}
\par
The information revolution, with its attendant increasing trans-
\par
border data flow, is a two-edged sword. While holding out the
\par
promise of a mutually beneficial interconnected world, TDF
\par
threatens some of the basic notions about nation-states. TDF
\par
makes present legal systems obsolete \textbf{by }raising issues that cut
\par
across traditional institutional frameworks. Canada is actively try-
\par
ing to construct a legal framework that recognizes these issues. But
\par
as Canada moves toward a comprehensive informatics policy it
\par
faces a Hobson's choice: unregulated TDF could pose a threat to
\par
economic independence while overregulation could estrange Ca-
\par
nada from current technologies and emerging potential world mar-
\par
kets. As a result Canadian policy-makers have left the flow of data
\par
more or less unchecked.
\par
Canada can choose from a variety of domestic and foreign mod-
\par
els to regulate TDF; however, the present developmental trend of a
\par
Canadian legal framework for TDF either targets legislation at key
\par
sectors such as banking or uses the FIRA. This trend may effec-
\par
tively protect vulnerable sectors of Canadian industry and promote
\par
the information industry, while causing few political repercussions
\par
at home or abroad. In Canada's effort to promote interdependence
\par
rather than dependence, stimulation \textbf{of }domestic research and de-
